window.RV_LOADED['ib-biology-hl'] = {"name":"IB Biology HL","topics":[{"name":"Continuity & Change","subtopics":[{"name":"Cell & Nuclear Division","questions":[{"ref":"BI0026","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0026\n\nDuring which stage of mitosis does the nuclear envelope break down?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Prophase\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Metaphase\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Anaphase\n\n- D$\\hspace{1em}$ Telophase","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe nuclear envelope is the membrane that separates the nucleus from the cytoplasm. For mitosis to proceed it must be broken down in the first stage of mitosis which is prophase so that the spindle fibres are able to attach to the centromeres. **Choice A** is correct.\n\nMetaphase (**Choice B**), Anaphase (**Choice C**) and Telophase (**Choice D**) all follow Prophase (**Choice A**) in the sequence of mitosis and do not involve the breakdown of the nuclear envelope."},{"ref":"BI0027","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0027\n\nWhich statement(s) about mitosis is/are true?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | Sister chromatids are pulled apart during metaphase. |\n  | II. | The nuclear envelope reforms during telophase. |\n  | III. | Chromosomes supercoil and become visible during prophase. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe four stages of mitosis are prophase, where chromosomes supercoil and become visible (**Statement III**); Metaphase, where chromosomes line up at the equator of the cell; anaphase, where sister chromatids are pulled apart and telophase, where the nuclear envelope reforms (**Statement II**) and two new daughter nuclei are formed. **Choice C** is, therefore, the correct answer.\n\n**Statement I** is not true as sister chromatids are not pulled apart during metaphase.  They are pulled apart during anaphase.\n**Choices A** and **D** are incorrect as they include the untrue **Statement I**. **Choice B** is incorrect as it is missing **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI0028","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0028\n\nIn the micrograph below, which letter points to a cell in anaphase?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n[Source: Adapted from Reischig, J. (2014, March 11). *File:mitosis (261 13) pressed; root meristem of onion ... - wikimedia*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved May 13, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mitosis_(261_13)_Pressed;\\_root_meristem_of_onion\\_(cells_in_prophase,_metaphase,_anaphase,_telophase).jpg. Copyright under CC BY-SA 3.0]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\nDuring Prophase, chromosomes supercoil and become visible (**Choice A**). In Metaphase, chromosomes line up at the equator of the cell (**Choices B and C**). In Anaphase, sister chromatids are pulled apart (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0029","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0029\n\nThe table shows the number of cells in different stages of the cell cycle in four samples of lung tissue from four patients. Which tissue has the lowest mitotic index?\n\n|| **Interphase** | **Prophase** | **Metaphase** | **Anaphase** | **Telophase** | **Total** |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.  | 80 | 30| 4 | 5 | 1 | 120 |\n| B.  | 100 | 9 | 1 | 7 | 8 | 125 |\n| C.  | 30 | 15 | 5 | 10 | 0 | 60 |\n| D.  | 70 | 20 | 4 | 0 | 6 | 100 |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe mitotic index is calculated by adding together all the cells undergoing mitosis (the cells in prophase, metaphase, anaphase and telophase) and dividing that value by the total number of cells.\n\nFor **Choice A**, the mitotic index is 0.3, ((30 + 4+ 5+ 1)/120).\n\nFor **Choice B**, the mitotic index is 0.2, ((9 + 1+ 7 + 8)/125).\n\nFor **Choice C**, the mitotic index is 0.5, ((15 + 5 + 10 + 0)/60).\n\nFor **Choice D**, the mitotic index is 0.3, ((20 + 4+ 0 + 6)/100)."},{"ref":"BI0030","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0030\n\nThe image shows a micrograph of a cell.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/0030.png\" alt=\"0030\">\n[Source:  Adapted from Reischig, J. (2014, March 11). *File: mitosis (261 13) pressed; root meristem of onion ... - wikimedia*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved May 13, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mitosis_(261_13)_Pressed;\\_root_meristem_of_onion\\_(cells_in_prophase,_metaphase,_anaphase,_telophase).jpg. Copyright under CC BY-SA 3.0]\n\nWhat explains the appearance of the cell in the micrograph?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The cell is undergoing interphase.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The sister chromatids are being pulled apart.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The cell is getting larger.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The cell is in metaphase.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nChromosomes in metaphase are aligned at the equator of the cell.\n\nDuring interphase, chromosomes are not visible (**Choice A**) and the cell grows in size (**Choice C**).  Sister chromatids are pulled apart during anaphase (**Choice B**)."},{"ref":"BI0031","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0031\n\nThe mitotic index of throat tissues taken from 4 different patients is shown below, calculated as a percentage. Which patient is most likely to have cancerous throat tissue?\n\n| | Mitotic index of throat tissue / % |\n| --- | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 10.0 |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 2.3 |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 3.2 |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 5.6 |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCancerous cells have a high rate of mitosis, so would have the highest mitotic index.\n\n**Choices B**, **C** and **D** are below 10, so are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0033","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0033\n\nWhich word describes the formation of secondary tumours from primary tumours as a result of the movement of cells?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Malignant\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Carcinoma\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Benign\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Metastasis","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nTerm “malignant” (**Choice A**) describes cancerous tumours which can metastasize. “Carcinoma” (**Choice B**)  is another word for a malignant tumour. The term “benign” (**Choice C**) describes a tumour that is unlikely to cause significant harm to the body."},{"ref":"BI0034","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0034\n\nWhich statement is a similarity between cytokinesis in animal and plant cells?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ A new cell wall is formed.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Two daughter cells are formed from one parent cell.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ A cleavage furrow is formed.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Vesicles are used to form two layers of membrane.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA new cell wall is formed (**Choice A**) only in plant cells as animal cells do not have cell walls. A cleavage furrow does not form in plant cells  (**Choice C**).  Vesicles are used to form two layers of membrane (**Choice D**)  in plant cells only."},{"ref":"BI0035","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0035\n\nIf a cell with a diploid number of 36 undergoes mitosis, how many chromosomes will be present in each daughter cell after cytokinesis?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 36\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 18\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 72\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 9","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nMitosis forms two genetically identical daughter cells with the same number of diploid chromosomes as the parent cell.\n\n18 (**Choice B**) is incorrect as it is the haploid number of chromosomes. 72 (**Choice C**) is incorrect as it is twice the diploid number of chromosomes. 9 (**Choice D**)  is incorrect as it is a quarter of the diploid number and an odd number."},{"ref":"BI0036","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0036\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nThe graph below shows the relative amount of DNA in one cell during one complete cell cycle. Each letter represents one stage of the cell cycle.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI0036.png\" alt=\"BI0036\">\n\n1.\n 1. State which stage of mitosis or interphase is represented by stage B in the graph. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Explain your answer to part (i). [2 marks]\n\n 3. Bortezomib is an anticancer medication and arrests cells in the G:sub[2] phase. If a cell shown in this graph began the cell cycle at A with 3 relative units of DNA, and Bortezomib was applied to the cell culture at stage A as well, estimate the relative amount of DNA in the cell at the end of C. [1 mark]\n\n2. Explain why the amount of DNA in one cell remains the same in stages C and D. [3 marks]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI0036-2.png\" alt=\"BI0036-2\">\n[Source: Reischig, J. (2014, March 11). *File:mitosis (261 13) pressed; root meristem of onion.. - Wikimedia*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved May 13, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mitosis_(261_13)_Pressed;\\_root_meristem_of_onion\\_(cells_in_prophase,_metaphase,_anaphase,_telophase).jpg. Copyright under CC BY-SA 3.0]\n\n3. The diagram above shows cells in different stages of mitosis. Label one cell that is in prophase and one cell that is in anaphase. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1.\n $\\hspace{0.2em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ S phase, **OR** synthesis phase, of interphase [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n 2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the amount of DNA in the cell has doubled [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ this occurs in the S phase to prepare the cell for mitosis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA polymerase replicates the DNA [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n :u[Sample answer]:\n\n Stage B is the S phase because, during the S phase, the amount of **DNA is doubled** ($\\checkmark$) by **DNA polymerase** ($\\checkmark$) to **prepare the cell for mitosis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n 3.\n $\\hspace{0.2em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 6 **OR** SIX [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n :u[Sample answer]:\n\n There would be **6 units** ($\\checkmark$) of DNA in the cell.\n\n2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ stage C represents the G:sub[2] phase of the interphase [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ stage D represents the M phase **OR** mitosis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ during G:sub[2], the cell prepares for mitosis with duplication of organelles, **OR** other detail of G:sub[2] [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA was replicated in stage B, and its amount is not changed in G:sub[2] [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ during the M phase, **OR** mitosis, there is no reduction in DNA **OR** chromosome number [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ only during cytokinesis, **OR** stage F, does the cell split into two cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cytokinesis, **OR** stage F, results in halving the DNA content of one cell [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\n\nStage C represents **G:sub[2]** ($\\checkmark$), and stage D represents **mitosis** ($\\checkmark$). During this time, there is no reduction in the number of chromosomes. During G2, the cell **prepares for mitosis** ($\\checkmark$) by doubling the amount of the organelles and producing additional protein. During mitosis, sister chromatids are pulled to opposite poles of the cell, but they are still within the confines of the same cell. In anaphase, the amount of chromosomes doubles, but not the amount of the total DNA, as chromosomes are counted by the number of centromeres. The amount of DNA per cell is only reduced **(halved) during cytokinesis** ($\\checkmark$), as this is the stage where the cytoplasm containing the two daughter nuclei is **divided into two new cells** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ one cell correctly labelled as prophase [1 mark]\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ one cell correctly labelled as anaphase [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">"},{"ref":"BI0195","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0195\n\nThis karyogram is for an individual with Down syndrome.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI0195.png\" alt=\"BI0195\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from File: Abedi M., Salmaninejad A. and Sakhinia E. (13th November 2017). *File: Peripheral-blood-karyotype-result-belonging-to-the-subject-compatible-with-48-XYYY.png.* Wikimedia commons. Retrieved 15th May 2022 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peripheral-blood-karyotype-result-belonging-to-the-subject-compatible-with-48-XYYY.png.Copyright under Creative Commons 4.0 International.]\n\nWhich statement is **not** correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Down syndrome is caused by trisomy 21\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    Down syndrome is caused by a trisomy of sex chromosomes\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    Down syndrome is caused by a trisomy of autosomes\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    Down syndrome is caused by an incorrect number of chromosomes","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDown syndrome is not caused by a trisomy of sex chromosomes (**Choice B**). The other statements are correct. Down syndrome is caused by trisomy 21 (**Choice A**). It is caused by a trisomy of autosomes (**Choice C**) because chromosome 21 is an autosome. Down syndrome is caused by an incorrect number of chromosomes (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0202","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0202\n\nWhich row in the table below is true of meiosis?\n\n| | The number of cells produced | Type of cells produced | Function |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| Two | Haploid | Sexual reproduction |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Four | Haploid | Sexual reproduction |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Four | Haploid | Asexual reproduction |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Two | Diploid | Sexual reproduction |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nMeiosis produces four haploid gametes, which are involved in sexual reproduction.  **Choice A** is incorrect because four haploid cells are produced, not two. **Choice C** is incorrect because the function is not asexual reproduction. **Choice D** is incorrect because four cells are produced and they are haploid not diploid."},{"ref":"BI0203","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0203\n\nWhich stage of meiosis is represented in the diagram below?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI0203.png\" alt=\"BI0203\">\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Metaphase II\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Metaphase I\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Anaphase I\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Telophase I","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because there are pairs of homologous chromosomes lined up on the equator, and each chromosome is attached to only one pole. **Choice A** is incorrect because in metaphase II, chromosomes are not in homologous pairs. Each chromosome, composed of sister chromatids, is attached to both poles. **Choice C** is incorrect because in anaphase I, homologous chromosomes are separated, and one chromosome from each pair is pulled to one pole. **Choice D** is incorrect because in telophase I, there are two sets of chromosomes that are already separated."},{"ref":"BI0204","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0204\n\nThe diploid number of the chimpanzee (*Pan troglodytes*) is 48. Which row shows the correct chromosome number for different cell types and cell division stages?\n\n| | Sperm cell | Brain cell | Cell in prophase I | Cell in metaphase II |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| 48 | 24 | 24 | 48 |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 24 | 48 | 24 | 48 |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 24 | 48 | 48 | 24 |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 48 | 24 | 48 | 24 |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct, because sex cells, such as sperm cells, are haploid and body cells, such as brain cells, are diploid. Cells in prophase I still have pairs of homologous chromosomes (diploid number) and cells in metaphase II have one set of chromosomes (haploid). **Choice A** and **Choice D** are incorrect because sperm cells are haploid. **Choice B** is incorrect because cells in prophase I are diploid and are haploid in metaphase II."},{"ref":"BI0205","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0205\n\nWhich stage of meiosis involves the separation of homologous chromosomes?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Metaphase I\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$  Anaphase I\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$  Metaphase II\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$  Anaphase II","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nHomologous chromosomes are separated during meiosis 1, specifically during anaphase I (**Choice B**).  In metaphase I, homologous chromosomes line up on the equator (**Choice A**). In metaphase II, individual chromosomes line up on the equator (**Choice C**). In anaphase II, sister chromatids (copies of the chromosome) are separated (**Choice D**). **Choice B** is therefore correct."},{"ref":"BI0206","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0206\n\nWhat is the most likely cause of the following human karyogram?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI0206.png\" alt=\"BI0206\">\n[Source: Adapted from Abedi, M., Salmaninejad, A., Sakhinia, E. (2017, November 13). Peripheral-blood-karyotype-result-belonging-to-the-subject-compatible-with-48-XYYY.png. Wikipedia Commons. Retrieved May 13, 2022, from https:// commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peripheral-blood-karyotype-result-belonging-to-the-subject-compatible-with-48-XYYY.png. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n- A.    $\\hspace{1em}$ Crossing over occurred during mitosis\n\n- B.    $\\hspace{1em}$ Crossing over occurred during meiosis\n- C.    $\\hspace{1em}$ Non-disjunction occurred during mitosis\n- D.    $\\hspace{1em}$ Non-disjunction occurred during meiosis","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn the karyogram there are 3 copies of chromosome 21, this is known as Trisomy 21. The most common cause of Trisomy 21 is nondisjunction during meiosis I or meiosis II, resulting in gametes that contain an abnormal number of chromosomes; **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choices A** and **B** are incorrect as crossing over is not associated with trisomy. Although Trisomy 21 can also arise due to nondisjunction during mitosis in the developing zygote or foetus, it is much less likely; therefore **Choice C** is not the correct answer."},{"ref":"BI0207","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0207\n\nWhich of the following statements is/are correct for meiosis?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1em}$ It is a reduction division\n- II.  $\\hspace{.7em}$ It involves random orientation\n- III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ It produces genetically identical cells\n\n- A.    $\\hspace{1em}$  I only\n\n- B.    $\\hspace{1em}$  II only\n- C.     $\\hspace{1em}$  I and II\n- D.    $\\hspace{1em}$  I and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nMeiosis reduces the number of chromosomes by half (**Statement I**), and random orientation of chromosomes along the equator occurs during metaphase I and II (**Statement II**), so **choice C** is correct because both statements are correct.\n\nMeiosis produces genetically different cells, so **Statement III** is not true, making **Choice D** incorrect. **Choices A** and **B** are both missing one of the true statements."},{"ref":"BI0208","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0208\n\nThe diagrams below show a cell, with a diploid number of 8, in different stages of meiosis. Which row correctly names each stage?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| I. | | | |II.|\n|<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI0208_A.png\" alt=\"BI0208_A\"> | | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI0208_B.png\" alt=\"BI0208_B\">|\n|III. | | | |IV. |\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI0208_C.png\" alt=\"BI0208_C\"> | | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI0208_D.png\" alt=\"BI0208_D\">|\n\n| | I | II | III | IV |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| Metaphase I | Metaphase II | Anaphase I | Prophase II |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Metaphase I | Metaphase II | Prophase II | Prophase I |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Metaphase II | Metaphase I | Prophase I | Anaphase II |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Telophase I | Metaphase II | Anaphase I | Prophase I |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Diagram I** is metaphase one, because pairs of homologous chromosomes are lined up at the equator and are attached, by spindle fibers, to only one pole. **Diagram II** is metaphase II, because there are individual chromosomes, composed of sister chromatids, lined up along the equator of the cell. **Diagram III** is prophase II because there are no homologous chromosomes, and each individual chromosome is composed of sister chromatids that have not been separated. **Diagram IV** is prophase I because there are four pairs of homologous chromosomes visible, which have not aligned at the equator and have yet to be separated."},{"ref":"BI0209","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0209\n\nThe graph below shows the relationship between maternal age and the incidence of non-disjunction. What relationship is suggested by the data in the graph?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/incidence%20of%20non-disjunction%20against%20age.png\" alt=\"incidence of non-disjunction against age\">\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ There is a positive correlation between age and incidence of non-disjunction.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ There is a negative correlation between age and incidence of non-disjunction.\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ There is no correlation between age and incidence of non-disjunction.\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Non-disjunction only happens when maternal age is above 35.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe graph shows an overall positive correlation, as maternal age increases, the incidence of non-disjunction increases (**Choice A**). **Choice B** is incorrect because there is a positive correlation not a negative correlation. **Choice C** is incorrect because there is a correlation. **Choice D** is incorrect because non-disjunction does occur below 35 years, but the incidence is lower when compared to above 35 years."},{"ref":"BI0210","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0210\n\nWhich of the following processes is characteristic of the meiotic and not the mitotic cell cycle?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Reduction division\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Nuclear division\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Interphase\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Cytokinesis","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nMeiosis is a reduction division (**Choice A**) because it reduces the number of chromosomes by half, but mitosis maintains the chromosome number. Nuclear division (**Choice B**), interphase (**Choice C**) and cytokinesis (**Choice D**) all happen in both the meiotic and mitotic cell cycle."},{"ref":"BI0211","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0211\n\nThe diagram below represents a cell with a diploid number of 4. Which stage of cell division is the cell currently undergoing?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI0211.png\" alt=\"BI0211\">\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Metaphase I\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Anaphase II\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Metaphase in mitosis\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Prophase I","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because there are four chromosomes, and each chromosome is composed of sister chromatids. **Choice A** and **choice C** are incorrect because, in metaphase, chromosomes are lined up on the equator. **Choice B** is incorrect because, during anaphase II, chromosomes are being pulled apart and towards opposite poles."},{"ref":"BI0212","marks":"13","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0212\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\nMitosis and meiosis are two ways in which cells can divide. The diagrams below show two cells from an organism with a diploid number (2n) of 8.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BIO212_A.png\" alt=\"BIO212_A\">\n\n1. Deduce if Cell A is a gamete or a somatic cell. Justify your answer. [1 mark]\n\n2. Identify the stage of meiosis represented in Cell B. Justify your answer. [1 mark]\n\n3.\n 1. Draw a diagram which represents Cell A during metaphase I. [4 marks]\n\n 2. Draw a diagram which represents Cell A during prophase II. [3 marks]\n\n4. Compare and contrast meiosis and mitosis. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. Justification has to be present to earn the mark.\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ gamete **BECAUSE** there are 4 chromosomes which is half the diploid number **OR** has one set of chromosomes **OR** is haploid [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nCell A is a **gamete** because it is **haploid** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Justification has to be present to earn the mark\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ anaphase II **BECAUSE** sister chromatids have been separated **OR** individual chromosomes are pulled to opposite poles [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nCell B is in **anaphase II** because the **sister chromatids have been separated already** ($\\checkmark$) and the **individual chromosomes are being pulled to opposite poles**.\n\n3.\n 1. All **four** of the following clearly shown on a diagram:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ no nucleus or nucleus shown breaking down [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ spindle fibers present **AND** each is attached to one homologous chromosome [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ eight chromosomes composed of sister chromatids [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chromosomes arranged in the homologous pairs [1 mark]\n\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BIO212_Ci.jpeg\" alt=\"BIO212_Ci\">\n\n 2. All **three** of the following clearly shown on a diagram:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chromosomes are composed of sister chromatids attached at the centromere [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ four chromosomes (composed of sister chromatids) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ no nucleus **OR** the nuclear membrane is shown as breaking down [1 mark]\n\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BIO212_Cii.png\" alt=\"BIO212_Cii\">\n\n4. Any **four** of the following (maximum of **three** marks awarded for **only** similarities or differences provided in the answer):\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ both meiosis **AND** mitosis involve prophase, metaphase, anaphase and telophase [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ both meiosis **AND** mitosis are followed by cytokinesis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ both meiosis **AND** mitosis are preceded by DNA replication [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mitosis involves one cycle of prophase, metaphase, anaphase and telophase, **BUT** meiosis involves two cycles of prophase, metaphase, anaphase and telophase [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mitosis produces two cells, **BUT** meiosis produces four cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mitosis produces diploid cells, **BUT** meiosis produces haploid cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mitosis produces genetically identical cells **BUT** meiosis produces genetically distinct cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mitosis is involved in growth and repair **OR** asexual reproduction **WHEREAS** meiosis is involved in sexual reproduction **OR** gamete formation. [1 mark]\n\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n **Both** meiosis and mitosis involve **prophase, metaphase, anaphase and telophase** ($\\checkmark$). In addition, **before both** meiosis and mitosis **DNA is replicated** ($\\checkmark$), and **after both cytokinesis** occurs ($\\checkmark$).\n\n Meiosis and mitosis are different in the following ways:\n\n - - meiosis produces **genetically different cells**, whereas mitosis produces **genetically identical cells**. ($\\checkmark$)\n\n - - meiosis produces **haploid cells**, while mitosis produces **diploid cells**. ($\\checkmark$)\n - - meiosis produces gametes (sperm and eggs) for **sexual reproduction**, while mitosis is used to produce new cells for **growth and repair**.($\\checkmark$)\n - - meiosis produces **four cells**, while mitosis produces **two cells**.($\\checkmark$)\n - - **meiosis has two cycles of prophase, metaphase, anaphase and telophase**. In mitosis, these stages only happen **once**. ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"BI0221","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0221\n\nWhich row correctly states the number of alleles of a single gene which would be present in the nucleus of a cell as a result of each process?\n\n| | Mitosis I | Meiosis II | Fertilisation |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| Two | Two | Two |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | One | One | Two |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Two | One | Two |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Two | One | One |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because meiosis halves the number of chromosomes, mitosis maintains the diploid number and fertilisation restores the diploid number.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because meiosis separates pairs of alleles and produces nuclei with one allele for each gene.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because mitosis produces genetically identical cells that have two alleles for each gene.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because, in fertilisation, two gametes fuse, and each gamete has one allele for each gene."},{"ref":"BI0240","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0240\n\nHow does meiosis result in variation?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ New combinations of gametes fuse together.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ New combinations of alleles are produced in body cells.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ New combinations of alleles are produced in gametes.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ New combinations of alleles are produced by base substitution.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because meiosis produces new combinations of alleles in gametes via random orientation of bivalents (independent assortment) and crossing over.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it describes fertilisation (mixing of gametes), not how meiosis creates new allele combinations.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because meiosis produces gametes, not new allele combinations in body (somatic) cells.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because base substitution is a mutation, not a feature of meiosis (mutation is a separate source of genetic variation)."},{"ref":"BI0314","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0314\n\nWhich statement highlights the importance of nucleosomes in DNA supercoiling ?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ To fit the length of DNA into the nucleus\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ To access specific DNA sequences\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ To form a circular chromosome\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ To hold the information needed for coding amino acids","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nA nucleosome helps to supercoil the DNA in order to package the DNA into a more condensed and compact structure. With this packaging, a length of DNA, totalling 2m, would be able to fit into the nucleus. **Choice A** is correct.\n\nEven though the DNA is packed into the nucleus, nucleosomes can still allow access to specific DNA sequences for the transcription process (**Choice B**). DNA can be accessed with or without the nucleosomes present.\n\nThe circular chromosome found in bacterial cells is shorter than the chromosomes of  eukaryotic cells so they do not require any DNA packaging, hence the absence of nucleosomes (**Choice C**). \n\nDNA acts as a template for RNA transcription, which then is used to code for amino acids. A nucleosome is used to supercoil the DNA and does not hold the information to code for amino acids (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0351","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0351\n\nThe photomicrograph below shows a root tip meristem. Which of the following sentences correctly defines a meristem?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI0351.png\" alt=\"BI0351\">\n[Source: Berkshire Community College Bioscience Image Library (2014 February 10th) *Apical meristem in onion root tip (34886438370 .jpeg*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved August 15th 2022 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Apical_Meristem_in_Onion_Root_Tip_(34886438470).jpg. Copyright under CC0]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ A region of a plant with differentiated cells that undergo cell division\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ A region of a plant with undifferentiated cells that undergo cell division\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ A region of a flower that undergoes cell division\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ A region of a plant where cell differentiation occurs in response to a change in day length","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because a meristem is a part of a plant with undifferentiated cells that undergo cell division. **Choice A** is incorrect because meristems do not have differentiated cells that undergo cell division. **Choice C** is incorrect because a meristem is not defined as a region of a flower. **Choice D** is incorrect because cell division in a meristem is not influenced by day length."},{"ref":"BI0354","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0354\n\nWhich process does **not** happen in the shoot apex?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Meiosis\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mitosis\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cell elongation\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Primary growth","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because meiosis happens in the anther and ovule of flowers, it does not happen in the cells of the shoot apex. **Choice B**, **Choice C** and **Choice D** are incorrect because mitosis and cell elongation happen in the shoot apex to produce new cells and allow for primary growth (plant lengthening)."},{"ref":"BI0358","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0358\n\nThe diagram below represents the structure of a shoot apical meristem. Which row in the table correctly identifies the processes occuring in the structures labeled in the diagram?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI0358ii.png\" alt=\"BI0358ii\">\n\n| | I | II |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Cell elongation | Cell division |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Cell differentiation | Cell elongation |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Cell division | Cell elongation |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Cell differentiation | Cell division |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because cell division happens at the stem tip and cell elongation happens in the new cells below the stem tip. **Choice A** is incorrect because cell elongation happens below the stem tip and cell division happens in the stem tip. **Choice B** is incorrect because cell production happens in the stem tip, cell differentiation happens below the stem tip. **Choice D** is incorrect because cell division happens below the region where cell elongation happens and cell elongation and differentiation happen below the stem tip."},{"ref":"BI0362","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0362\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe diagram represents a bud with a shoot apical meristem.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BIO362.png\" alt=\"BIO362\">\n\n1. State the type of cell division that occurs in the shoot apical meristem. [1 mark]\n\n2. Outline what happens to the cells produced in the shoot apical meristem. [2 marks]\n\n3. State two types of tissue that are produced by the differentiation of cells found at the shoot apical meristem [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{->}$ mitosis [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ **Mitosis** ($\\checkmark$) is the type of cell division which occurs in the stem tip.\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{->}$ new cells can differentiate [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{->}$ new cells can elongate [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{->}$ some cells remain undifferentiated **OR** remain as apical meristem cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{->}$ new cells become new tissues **OR** new cells differentiate to produce leaves **OR** dermal tissue **OR** vascular tissue [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n The cells produced by cell division in the apical meristem can **differentiate** ($\\checkmark$) and become **dermal tissue** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{->}$ dermal tissue [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{->}$ vascular tissue **OR** phloem **OR** xylem [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{->}$ pith [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{->}$ cortex [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n The new tissues produced by differentiation include **dermal tissue** ($\\checkmark$) and **vascular tissue** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0364","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0364\n\nWhich statement(s) are true of meiosis I but **not** meiosis II?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    |  I. | Crossing over occurs during prophase |\n    | II. | Sister chromatids are pulled apart during anaphase |\n    | III. | Chromosomes supercoil and become visible during prophase |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCrossing over occurs between homologous pairs during prophase I, but not during prophase II, as homologous chromosomes have already been separated during meiosis 1. **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Statement II** is incorrect as sister chromatids are pulled apart during anaphase II (**Choices B, C** and **D**). **Statement III** is incorrect, as chromosomes supercoil and become visible during prophase I and II (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0365","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0365\n\nWhich allele combination can result in a gamete, from the crossing over of non-sister chromatids shown in the diagram below?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI0365.png\" alt=\"BI0365\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ bj\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ bJ\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Bj\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Jj","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct as crossing over could result in a gamete containing the “b” allele and a “j’ allele. **Choices B** and **C** are incorrect as they are the same as the original combination of alleles, so crossing over hasn’t occurred. **Choice D** is incorrect because the gamete should contain one copy of each gene."},{"ref":"BI0366","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0366\n\nHow does meiosis promote variation?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Crossing over of sister chromatids\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Independent assortment during telophase I\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Crossing over during prophase I\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Fusion of gametes during sexual reproduction","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCrossing over occurs during prophase I. (**Choice C**)\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect as non-sister chromatids on homologous chromosomes cross over. **Choice B**  is incorrect as independent assortment occurs as a result of random orientation during metaphases I and II. **Choice D** is incorrect as the fusion of gametes does not occur during meiosis but during the fertilization."},{"ref":"BI0367","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0367\n\nThe photomicrograph shows a pollen grain from *L. candidum* (Lily). Which stage of meiosis is shown?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI0367.png\" alt=\"BI0367\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Company, C. B. S. (2011, October 13). *Lily pollen meiosis*. Flickr. Retrieved June 5, 2022, from https://www.flickr.com/photos/carolinabio/6240931651.Copyright free]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Telophase I\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Anaphase I\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Telophase II\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Anaphase II","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nMeiosis produces four daughter cells from one parent cell over two cell divisions, during anaphase II the sister chromatids are being pulled to either pole of the cell (**Choice D**).\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as during Telophase I, homologous chromosomes are pulled to either pole of the cell, producing two new cells. **Choice B**  is incorrect because during Anaphase I, homologous chromosomes are beginning to be pulled to either pole of the cell leading to formation of two new cells. **Choice C**  is incorrect as during Telophase II, sister chromatids (now chromosomes in own right) are at each pole of the cell, the chromosomes are unwinding, the spindle is disassembling and the nuclear envelopes are forming, resulting in the formation of 4 haploid cells."},{"ref":"BI0368","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0368\n\nIn the cells of a horse (*E. caballus*) during prophase I of meiosis I there are 64 chromosomes. What is the haploid number of chromosomes?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 64\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 128\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 32\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 16","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCells in prophase I of meiosis I have the diploid number of chromosomes. Thus 64 is the diploid number. The haploid number is half the diploid number. Half of 64 is 32. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect as 64 is the diploid number of chromosomes. **Choice B**  is incorrect as it is double the diploid number of chromosomes. **Choice D** is incorrect as it is half the haploid number."},{"ref":"BI0369","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0369\n\nWhich arrow is pointing to a chiasma?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI0369.png\" alt=\"BI0369\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is incorrect as it is pointing to the telomere. **Choice B** is wrong as it is pointing to the centromere. **Choice C** is incorrect as the chromatids here do not show evidence of physical linkage and hence crossing over; therefore, no chiasma is present."},{"ref":"BI0370","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0370\n\nHow many potential crossing-over points are present in the diagram?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI0370A.png\" alt=\"BI0370A\">\n\n[Adapted from: Source Images, I. A. B. (2015, August 23). *Image from page 172 of \"cytology\" (1961)*.  Flickr. Retrieved June 5, 2022, from https://www.flickr.com/photos/internetarchivebookimages/20640324138 Public domain]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 2\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 3\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 4\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 8","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCrossing-over typically occurs at points called chiasmata, where two non-sister chromatids overlap and exchange genetic material. The diagram above shows three distinct crossing-over points where the chromatids intersect. These points represent potential sites of genetic recombination during meiosis;**Choice B** is the correct answer.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D**  are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0371","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0371\n\nWhen does the random orientation of homologous chromosome pairs occur?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Metaphase I\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Prophase I\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Metaphase II\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Prophase II","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because during metaphase I homologous chromosomes align at the equator and are subject to random orientation. **Choice B** is incorrect as, during Prophase I, homologous chromosomes condense and crossing over occurs. **Choice C** is incorrect as, during Metaphase II, chromosomes align on the cell's equator, and there is random orientation of the sister chromatids and not homologous chromosomes. **Choice D** is incorrect as, during Prophase II, chromosomes align on the cell's equator."},{"ref":"BI0372","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0372\n\nWhich stage of meiosis is matched to the correct description?\n\n| | Stage | Description |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Anaphase I | Sister chromatids are pulled to either side of the cell. |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Metaphase II | Homologous chromosomes align at the equator of the cell. |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Prophase I | Crossing over between non-sister chromatids occurs. |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Telophase I | Four haploid nuclei are formed. |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDuring prophase I, homologous chromosomes pair up and crossing over can occur between non-sister chromatids at sites called chiasmata (chiasma singular) (**Choice C**). During Anaphase I, homologous chromosomes are pulled to either pole of the cell; thus, **Choice A** is incorrect. During Metaphase II, chromosomes align on the cell's equator; thus, **Choice B** is incorrect. During Telophase I, two nuclei are formed; thus, **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0373","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0373\n\nWhich process does not occur during meiosis?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Crossing over\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Formation of two identical daughter cells\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Random orientation\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Separation of homologous chromosomes","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because meiosis results in four genetically unique haploid cells, not two identical ones. The formation of two genetically identical daughter cells is the defining characteristic of mitosis, which is used for growth and tissue repair rather than gamete production.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because crossing over occurs during Prophase I of meiosis, where non-sister chromatids exchange genetic material to increase variation.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because random orientation occurs during Metaphase I and II, ensuring that maternal and paternal chromosomes are distributed independently to the daughter cells.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the separation of homologous chromosomes is the primary event of Anaphase I, which reduces the chromosome number from diploid to haploid."},{"ref":"BI0374","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0374\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nThe diagram below shows chromosomes during meiosis.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI0370A.png\" alt=\"BI0370A\">\n[Source: Adapted from Images, I. A. B. (2015, August 23). *Image from page 172 of \"cytology\" (1961)*. Flickr. Retrieved June 5, 2022, from https://www.flickr.com/photos/internetarchivebookimages/20640324138 Copyright free]\n\n1. Identify the stage of meiosis from the diagram. [1 mark]\n\n2.\n    1. State one process, other than the one shown in the diagram that promotes variation during meiosis. [1 mark]\n\n    2. State when the process you named in part (i) occurs in meiosis.[1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{->}$ prophase I [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ **Prophase I,** is when crossing over occurs. ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    1. $\\hspace{1em}$\n    $\\ce{->}$ random orientation (of homologous chromosomes) [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ **Random orientation** of homologous chromosomes ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    2. $\\hspace{1em}$\n        $\\ce{->}$ metaphase I          [1 mark]\n        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ Occurs during **metaphase I** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0375","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0375\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe diagram below shows chromosomes during meiosis.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI0375i.jpg\" alt=\"BI0375i\">\n[Adapted from: Source Images, I. A. B. (2015, August 23). *Image from page 172 of \"cytology\" (1961)*. Flickr. Retrieved June 5, 2022, from https://www.flickr.com/photos/internetarchivebookimages/20640324138 Copyright free]\n\n1.\n    1. On the diagram above, label a potential crossing-over point with the letter R.     [1 mark]\n\n    2. On the diagram above, label a chromatid with the letter Q.[1 mark]\n\n2. Describe the process of crossing over.     [3 marks]\n\n3. Explain how, after crossing over has occurred, genetically unique nuclei are formed in the gametes. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. $\\hspace{1em}$\n    $\\ce{->}$ a crossing-over point (the place where two non-sister chromatids overlap) is correctly identified as R [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/bi0375A.png\" alt=\"bi0375A\">\n\n    2. $\\hspace{1em}$\n    $\\ce{->}$  a chromatid  is correctly identified as Q      [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI0375C.png\" alt=\"BI0375C\">\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ happens during prophase I     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ homologous chromosomes pair up **OR** bivalents are formed     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ non-sister chromatids form chiasmata **OR** crossing-over points         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ (during crossing-over) exchange of alleles, **OR** genetic information, **OR** DNA fragments between non-sister chromatids         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ locations of chiasmata are random         [1 mark]\n[3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Crossing over occurs during **Prophase I** ($\\checkmark$). During this stage, the chromosomes condense and **homologous chromosomes** pair up to form **bivalents** ($\\checkmark$).  Non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes form **chiasmata** ($\\checkmark$) at **random** sections ($\\checkmark$), **exchanging alleles** between chromosomes ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ crossing-over produces recombinant chromatids **OR** chromatids with new combinations of maternal and paternal alleles OR sister chromatids are no longer genetically identical         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$  random orientation of homologous chromosome pairs occurs in metaphase I     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ independent segregation of chromatids in meiosis II increases the variety of allele combinations              [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$  each gamete receives a different combination of recombinant and non-recombinant chromatids, resulting in genetically unique nuclei     [1 mark]\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Crossing over produces **recombinant chromatids** or **chromatids** with **new combinations of maternal and paternal alleles** ($\\checkmark$). **Random orientation** of **homologous chromosome** pairs in **metaphase I** ensures that each gamete receives a **different combination of chromosomes** ($\\checkmark$). **Independent segregation** of chromatids in **meiosis II** increases the **variety of allele combinations** in the gametes ($\\checkmark$), resulting in genetically unique gametes."},{"ref":"BI0393","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0393\n\nWhich of the following is the typical type of cell division seen in prokaryotic cells?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Mitosis\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    Meiosis\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    Binary fission\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    Budding","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because prokaryotic cells divide through a process called binary fission. In binary fission, the DNA is replicated, and the cell divides into two genetically identical daughter cells. This is a simpler form of cell division compared to the processes in eukaryotic cells, and it does not involve a spindle apparatus or mitotic structures.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because mitosis is the process of cell division in eukaryotic cells, where the cell divides into two genetically identical daughter cells. Prokaryotic cells, which lack membrane-bound organelles and a nucleus, do not undergo mitosis.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because meiosis is a process of cell division that produces haploid gametes in eukaryotes. Prokaryotic cells do not undergo meiosis because they are not involved in sexual reproduction and do not have paired chromosomes like eukaryotic organisms.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because budding is a form of asexual reproduction in certain organisms, such as yeast or some multicellular organisms, where a new individual forms from a small outgrowth (bud) from the parent organism. This is not the typical mode of division for prokaryotic cells."},{"ref":"BI0503","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0503\n\nThe image shows an abnormal human karyotype.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI0503.png\" alt=\"BI0503\">\n[Source: Adapted from Wessex Reg. Genetics Centre. (n.d.) *Down syndrome human karyotype 47,XY*. Retrieved March 29, 2022, from https://wellcomecollection.org/works/wmcdanw6. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\nWhat is the abnormality in this karyotype and its cause?\n\n| | Abnormality | Possible Cause |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | X and Y chromosomes are different in size | Non-disjunction in anaphase I or II |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | X and Y chromosomes are different in size | Crossing-over in prophase I |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 3 copies of Chromosome 21 | Non-disjunction in anaphase I or II |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 3 copies of Chromosome 21 | Crossing-over in prophase I |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA karyotype shows the chromosomes in an individual. Humans have 23 paired chromosomes, meaning there are two copies of each chromosome. In the karyotype, chromosome 21 is shown to have 3 copies rather than 2 copies. So, the 3 copies of chromosome 21 is the abnormality in this karyotype.\n\nAn abnormal chromosome number can arise due to non-disjunction which is the failure of chromosomes to separate during meiosis. Non-disjunction can occur in anaphase I, the phase of meiosis I in which homologous chromosomes separate, or anaphase II, the phase in meiosis II in which sister chromatids separate. So, the cause of the abnormality is non-disjunction in anaphase I or II.\n\nThe karyotype shows unequal X and Y chromosomes (**Choice A** and **B**), but this is normal for a human karyotype. The sex chromosomes are not homologous. Crossing over in prophase I (**Choice B** and **Choice D**) exchanges genetic material between two nonidentical sister chromatids. It is not related to a failure of chromosomes to separate."},{"ref":"BI0525","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0525\n\nThe diagram below shows the arrangement of alleles on the sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes before and after prophase 1 of meiosis.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI0525A.png\" alt=\"BI0525A\">\n\nWhat single crossover event could have occurred to produce the final arrangement of alleles shown?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n | | |\n | :-- | :-- |\n | I. | Crossover occurring in a region found between genes A and B of non-identical chromatids |\n | II. | Crossover occurring in a region found between genes B and C of non-identical chromatids |\n | III. | Crossover occurring in a region found between genes C and D of non-identical chromatids|\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI0525-screenshot.png\" alt=\"BI0525-screenshot\">\n\nWhen crossing over occurs, all the alleles below the position of the chiasma are exchanged. The homologous parent chromosomes are shown on the left (before the first arrow). As a result of crossing over, two allele changes occurred between non-sister chromatids to produce a new arrangement of alleles (see yellow highlights). The alleles that were exchanged were c with C and d with D, so the crossover event producing this combination must have been a result of chiasma between genes B and C (**Statement II**).\n\nIf chiasma formation had occurred between genes A and B, the alleles for all three genes, B, C and D, would have been swapped (**Statement I**). If chiasma formation had occurred between genes C and D, then just the alleles for gene D would have been swapped (**Statement III**)."},{"ref":"BI0544","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0544\n\nThe micrographs show four phases of meiosis I in a plant. In what order do these phases occur?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI0544.png\" alt=\"BI0544\">\n[Source:  Adapted from Windham, M. D., Pryer, K. M., Poindexter, D. B., Li, F.-W., Rothfels, C. J., and Beck, J. B. (2020). *A step-by-step protocol for meiotic chromosome counts in flowering plants: A powerful and economical technique revisited*. Retrieved January 4, 2023, from Applications in Plant Sciences 8( 4): e11342.  © 2020, Windham et al. Licensed under CC BY 4.0]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ X-Y-W-Z\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ W-Z-X-Y\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Y-X-W-Z\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Z-W-X-Y","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nMeiosis is the process through which a diploid cell divides twice to produce four haploid cells. Meiosis I is the first round of cell division that occurs during meiosis. In meiosis I, homologous chromosomes separate. There are four phases of meiosis I:\n- Prophase I: In this phase, the chromosomes condense, the nuclear membrane dissolves, and homologous chromosomes pair up.\n- Metaphase I: In this phase, spindle fibers from the centromeres on either side of the cell align homologous chromosomes on the metaphase plate.\n- Anaphase I: In this phase, the spindle fibers contract to separate the homologous chromosomes and pull them to opposite sides of the cell\n- Telophase I: In this phase, the homologous chromosomes have finished moving to opposite sides of the cell, the chromosomes decondense, and the cell prepares for cytokinesis.\n\nThese stages are labeled on the image below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI0544A.png\" alt=\"BI0544A\">\n[Source:  Adapted from Windham, M. D., Pryer, K. M., Poindexter, D. B., Li, F.-W., Rothfels, C. J., and Beck, J. B. (2020). *A step-by-step protocol for meiotic chromosome counts in flowering plants: A powerful and economical technique revisited*. Retrieved January 4, 2023, from Applications in Plant Sciences 8( 4): e11342.  © 2020, Windham et al. Licensed under CC BY 4.0]\n\nSo, the correct order in which the phases occur is Y-X-W-Z."},{"ref":"BI0546","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0546\n\nAt the end of which phase of meiosis I does crossing over occur, and what is the outcome of crossing over?\n\n| | Meiotic phase | Outcome |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Prophase I | increased genetic variation |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Prophase I | halved chromosome number |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Anaphase I | increased genetic variation |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Anaphase I | halved chromosome number |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCrossing over is the process through which homologous chromosomes exchange genetic material.\nCrossing over occurs during the first phase of meiosis I, prophase I. During prophase I, homologous chromosomes are each made up of two identical sister chromatids.  Homologous chromosomes pair up to form a tetrad. Crossing over occurs if two non-sister chromatids on either side of the center of this tetrad exchange genetic material. This process breaks up allele combinations on the chromatids to produce non-identical sister chromatids. Since crossing over results in sister chromatids that are no longer identical, it increases genetic variation.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the total number of chromosomes before crossing over occurs is the same as the total number of chromosomes after crossing over occurs.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because crossing over occurs during prophase I, not anaphase I.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because crossing over occurs during prophase I, not anaphase I, and also because the total number of chromosomes remains the same after crossing over as before crossing over."},{"ref":"BI0695","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0695\n\nWhich statement(s) about homologous chromosomes is/are **not** true?\n\n-    I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Homologous chromosomes have the same centromere position.\n-    II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ Homologous chromosomes have genes with the same loci.\n-    III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ Homologous chromosomes are identical to each other.\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement III**  is not true, as homologous chromosomes often have different alleles (forms of genes), so are generally not identical to each other.\n\n**Statements I** and **II** are true, as homologous chromosomes are pairs of chromosomes that have their centromeres and genes at the same positions. Therefore, **Choice C** is correct."},{"ref":"BI0880","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0880\n\nIf the DNA in a somatic cell is changed in an animal as a result of a mutation, which of the following could most significantly affect other tissues in the animal?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mechanisms involved in the cell cycle are altered.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Apoptosis occurs in the cell.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ A start codon is altered which deactivates a gene.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Transcription of one or more genes becomes impossible.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because if enough mechanisms involved in the cell cycle are altered, the cell may begin to divide uncontrollably leading to the formation of a metastatic tumour which can spread to other tissues of the body.  **Choice B** is incorrect because if a single cell undergoes apoptosis, it will not affect other tissues of the body.  **Choice C** is incorrect because deactivation of a single gene in one cell is very unlikely to affect other tissues.  **Choice D** is incorrect because the inability to transcribe genes in one cell is very unlikely to affect other tissues."},{"ref":"BI1124","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1124\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe graph shows the relationship between parental age and incidence of Down syndrome in offspring. The study was conducted using chorionic villus sampling (CVS).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI1124.png\" alt=\"BI1124\">\n[Source: Adapted from Vashist, M., Neelkamal, & Kumar, A. (2016, May 5). *Whether paternal age effect exists as a risk factor in trisomy 21*. Biosciences Biotechnology Research Asia. Retrieved April 15, 2023, from https://www.biotech-asia.org/vol8no1/whether-paternal-age-effect-exists-as-a-risk-factor-in-trisomy-21/. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n1. Describe the pattern between paternal age and incidence of Down syndrome.  [2 marks]\n\n2. A researcher concluded that increasing maternal age has a greater effect on the incidence of Down syndrome in offspring than increasing paternal age. Comment, giving a reason, whether you agree with this conclusion. [1 mark]\n\n3. Using karyotyping, it is possible to test whether an unborn child has Down syndrome. State how a karyogram would identify a child with Down syndrome.   [1 mark]\n\n4. Explain how the production of ova by meiosis can lead to Down syndrome. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ between 16 - 35 years, the chances of offspring having Down syndrome increases    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ after 35 years, the chances of offspring having Down syndrome decrease [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ between 35 - 50 years, the chances of offspring having Down syndrome stabilises    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Between the ages of **16 -35**, the chances of offspring having Down syndrome **increases** ($\\checkmark$) from 1 to 31%, however, **after 35 years**, the chances of having offspring with Down syndrome **decrease** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **one** of the following (answer must include a reason to gain the mark):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ yes **BECAUSE** there is positive relationship between increasing maternal age and risk of Down syndrome            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ no **BECAUSE** up until the age of 35, increasing paternal age is correlated with a much higher risk of Down syndrome **OR** data might suggest trend but without statistical testing cannot come to this conclusion                [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Yes, because as maternal age increases, there is also a consistent increase in the incidence of Down syndrome in offspring, whereas there is only an increase in incidence with paternal age up to the age of 35 ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 3 chromosome 21s **OR** trisomy 21    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    If the unborn child had Down syndrome, **three chromosome 21s** ($\\checkmark$) would be visible in the karyogram.\n\n4. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ spindle apparatus errors    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ non-disjunction        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous chromosomes do not separate during anaphase I                             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sister chromatids do not separate during anaphase II            [1 mark]\n\n     $\\ce{ -> }$ results in gametes OR sex cells OR ova with an extra chromosome **OR** abnormal number of chromosomes     [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Spindle apparatus errors** ($\\checkmark$) can lead to **non-disjunction** ($\\checkmark$) of **homologous chromosomes** during **anaphase I** ($\\checkmark$), or of **sister chromatids** during **anaphase II** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1140","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1140\n\nA human pancreatic cell divides three times by mitosis. What is the end result of these divisions?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Two genetically identical daughter cells.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Four genetically distinct daughter cells.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Eight genetically identical daughter cells.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sixteen genetically distinct daughter cells.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because each successive round of mitosis doubles the number of cells, assuming all daughter cells divide in the next round. Each parent cell becomes two daughter cells, which can then divide themselves:\n\n1 --> 2 --> 4 -->8\n\nThe number of cells therefore doubles in each round, rather than increasing by two. This gives \\(2:sup[3]  = 8\\) cells, not \\(3 x 2 = 6\\). The daughter cells are genetically identical to the original cell, assuming no mutations occur.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because two genetically identical daughter cells would be produced after one round of mitosis, not three.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because four daughter cells would be produced after two rounds of mitosis, not three. Additionally, mitosis produces genetically identical cells, not genetically distinct cells.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because sixteen daughter cells would be produced after four rounds of mitosis, not three. These cells would also be genetically identical, not genetically distinct."},{"ref":"BI1148","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1148\n\nAn unfertilised egg cell belonging to the mangrove horseshoe crab (*Carcinoscorpius rotundicauda*) contains 16 chromosomes. How many chromosomes would be found in a cell from this species about to undergo meiosis?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 8\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 16\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 32\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 64","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe diploid number for this species is 32. At the start of meiosis, each chromosome has been replicated to form two sister chromatids, held together at the centromere. Each pair of sister chromatids is still regarded as one chromosome so the total number of chromosomes after replication will still be 32; **Choice C** is correct\n\n**Choice A**, 8 chromosomes, is incorrect as it would represent a condition not typical for any stage in the life cycle of this species. **Choice B**, 16 chromosomes, incorrectly suggests the haploid number, which is characteristic of the gametes post-meiosis, not cells about to undergo meiosis. **Choice D**, 64 chromosomes, incorrectly implies a tetraploid number, which suggests an error in chromosome number doubling beyond the typical diploid state."},{"ref":"BI1167","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1167\n\nWhich is a potential cause of Trisomy 21 or Down syndrome?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Improper alignment of homologous chromosomes during metaphase II\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Formation of multiple potential crossing-over points on chromosome 21 during prophase I\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Formation of a polar body in telophase I\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Failure of sister chromatids to separate during anaphase II","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDown syndrome or Trisomy 21  is caused by a person having three copies of chromosome 21, due to a failure of homologous chromosomes to segregate properly during anaphase I or a failure of sister chromatids to separate during anaphase II; **Choice D**  is correct.\n\nBefore metaphase II, homologous chromosomes have already been separated into 2 different nuclei, therefore, in metaphase II, individual chromosomes are aligned on the equator of the cell, to prepare for the sister chromatids to be pulled apart; **Choice A**  is incorrect.  Potential crossing-over points on chromosomes, where genetic material may be exchanged, increase variation, but do not lead to Trisomy 21 or Down syndrome; **Choice B** is incorrect. Forming a polar body during telophase I occurs during oogenesis and does not contribute to Trisomy 21 or Down syndrome; **Choice C** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1179","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1179\n\nWhich of the following generate genetic variation during meiosis?\n\n| | |\n| :-- | :-- |\n| I. | Crossing over during prophase I |\n| II. | Independent assortment of homologous chromosomes during metaphase I |\n| III. | Random fertilisation |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because crossing over occurs during prophase I when homologous chromosomes pair together. Non-sister chromatids exchange segments of DNA, producing new allele combinations and increasing genetic variation.\n\n**Statement II** is true because homologous chromosome pairs align randomly during metaphase I. This independent assortment results in different combinations of maternal and paternal chromosomes in gametes, increasing variation.\n\n**Statement III** is false because random fertilisation does increase genetic variation in offspring, but it does not occur during meiosis. It occurs during sexual reproduction when gametes fuse.\n\n**Choice B** is the correct asnwer to the question as it only includes the true **Statements I** and **II**."},{"ref":"BI1211","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1211\n\nWhich is the correct definition of cytokinesis?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Division of the nucleus of a cell to form two identical nuclei\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Splitting of cytoplasm in a parent cell between two daughter cells\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Pinching off of a daughter cell from a parent cell\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The pulling away of the plasma membrane from the cell wall","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A**  is incorrect as division of the nucleus of a cell to form two identical nuclei describes mitosis. **Choice C** is incorrect as pinching off a daughter cell from a daughter cell describes budding, a form of asexual reproduction carried out by organisms such as yeast. **Choice D**  is incorrect as it describes plasmolysis, a process that happens in plant cells when they lose too much water via osmosis."},{"ref":"BI1212","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1212\n\nWhich is an anucleate cell?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Striated muscle cell\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ovum cell\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Spongy mesophyll cell\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Red blood cell","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAn anucleate cell does not contain a nucleus. Mature red blood cells do not have a nucleus; **Choice D**  is correct.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect as striated muscle cells are multinucleated, so they contain many nuclei. **Choices B** and **C**  are incorrect as they both contain a nucleus."},{"ref":"BI1213","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1213\n\nWhat is the role of microtubules during mitosis?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Align homologous chromosomes on the equator\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Supercoil DNA\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Move sister chromatids to opposite poles\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Separate daughter chromatids","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nMicrotubules are the proteins that form spindle fibres and move sister chromatids to opposite poles during mitosis; **Choice C**  is correct. **Choice A**  is incorrect because although microtubules are involved in aligning the pairs of sister chromatids at the cell's equator, they do not align as homologous pairs in mitosis - that occurs in meiosis 1. In mitosis a single line of pairs of sister chromatids, joined together at the centromere, are arranged along the equator of the cell by microtubules. **Choice B**  is incorrect because microtubules are not involved in this process. **Choice D**  is incorrect because microtubules separate sister chromatids, not daughter chromatids."},{"ref":"BI1326","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1326\n\nWhich is the correct definition of metastasis?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ A tumour that is formed when cancerous cells detach from a primary tumour\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ An aggressive tumour that grows rapidly, invades and destroys nearby tissues\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ A cell that undergoes uncontrolled cell division due to cell cycle errors\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The process by which cancer cells spread to other parts of the body","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nMetastasis is the process by which cells break off from a primary tumour and move to other parts of the body where they can set up secondary tumours; **Choice D**  is correct.\n\nMetastasis is a process, all the other choices are descriptions of different tumours. A tumour that is formed when cancerous cells detach from a primary tumour is called a secondary tumour; **Choice A**  is incorrect. An aggressive tumour that grows rapidly, invades and destroys nearby tissues is called a malignant tumour; **Choice B** is incorrect. A cell that undergoes uncontrolled cell division due to cell cycle errors is the general description of a cancerous cell; **Choice C**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1327","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1327\n\nThe micrograph shows onion cells undergoing mitosis. What is the mitotic index of the cells?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI1327.jpg\" alt=\"BI1327\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Laurent, E. (2022, July 15). File:onion diagram.svg. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved November 11, 2023 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Onion_diagram.svg. Copyright under CC BY-SA 4.0]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 0.25\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 0.9\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 4\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 0.02","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI1327%20answer.jpg\" alt=\"BI1327 answer\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Laurent, E. (2022, July 15). File:onion diagram.svg. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved November 11, 2023 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Onion_diagram.svg. Copyright under CC BY-SA 4.0]\n\nThe mitotic index is calculated by adding together all the cells undergoing mitosis (the cells in prophase, metaphase, anaphase and telophase) and dividing that value by the total number of cells. There are three cells undergoing mitosis and twelve cells in total, therefore, the mitotic index is 3 $\\div$ 12 $=$ 0.25. **Choice A  is correct**.\n\n**Choices B**, **C** and **D**  are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1386","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1386\n\nThe gametes of a wolf (*Canis lupus*) contain 39 chromosomes. How many chromosomes would be found in the cells of a wolf as meiosis begins?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 156\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 117\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 78\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 39","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nGametes are haploid cells that contain half the normal number of chromosomes (n). They are formed from diploid (2n) cells during meiosis, which is a reduction division. Since the wolf’s gametes, which are haploid cells, contain 39 chromosomes, its diploid number must be twice that, or 78. During interphase, DNA replication takes place. This means that each of the 78 chromosomes is made up of two sister chromatids joined at the centromere. Thus there are 156 chromatids making up 78 chromosomes. The sister chromatids will then separate during meiosis to form chromosomes in their own right. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect because even though there are 156 sister chromatids at the start of meiosis, each pair is considered a single chromosome, so the number of chromosomes remains the same as it did before replication. **Choice B** is incorrect because it would not be possible to have a cell with this number of chromosomes for this particular species. **Choice D** cannot be correct because this is the haploid chromosome number, and at the start of meiosis, homologous chromosomes have not yet been separated."},{"ref":"BI1400","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1400\n\nWhich of the following correctly describes events occurring in meiosis I and meiosis II?\n\n| | Meiosis I | Meiosis II |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | DNA replication | Crossing over |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Independent assortment of chromosomes | Separation of recombinant sister chromatids |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Crossing over | Independent assortment of chromosomes |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Random orientation of sister chromatids | Cytokinesis |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because the independent assortment of homologous chromosomes during meiosis I refers to the process by which homologous chromosome pairs align independently at the metaphase plate randomly and independently of each other. This random assortment results in the mixing of maternal and paternal chromosomes, leading to genetically unique daughter cells. Separation of recombinant sister chromatids occurs along the metaphase plate during metaphase II.  The sister chromatids, now possibly carrying different genetic information due to crossing over in meiosis I (recombinants), are pulled apart towards opposite poles of the cell.\n\nDNA replication occurs during the S phase of interphase, not during meiosis. Crossing over occurs during meiosis I, not meiosis II. **Choice A** is, therefore, incorrect. **Choice C** is incorrect because the independent assortment of chromosomes only takes place during meiosis I. **Choice D** is incorrect because it is the random orientation of homologous pairs that takes place during meiosis I, and cytokinesis is not part of meiosis, it occurs straight after meiosis I and II."},{"ref":"BI1480","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1480\n\nThe horse threadworm (*Parascaris equorum*) has a chromosome number of 2n=4. Which diagram represents a haploid nucleus?\n\n| $\\text{A}$. $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI1480%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1480 fig1\"> | $\\text{B}$. $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI1480%20fig2.png\" alt=\"BI1480 fig2\"> |\n\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| C. $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI1480%20fig3.png\" alt=\"BI1480 fig3\"> | D. $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ $\\\\$ $\\hspace{0em}$ | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI1480%20fig4.png\" alt=\"BI1480 fig4\"> |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nA haploid nucleus contains a single set of chromosomes and has half the number of chromosomes of a diploid nucleus. The diploid number for horse threadworms is four, therefore the haploid number is two. **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choices B, C** and **D** are incorrect as they do not contain exactly two different chromosomes."},{"ref":"BI1481","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1481\n\nThe diagram shows a process that occurs during prophase I of meiosis. What does this process directly result in?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI1481%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1481 fig1\">\n\n[Adapted from: Source Images, I. A. B. (2015, August 23). Image from page 172 of \"cytology\" (1961).  Flickr. Retrieved June 5, 2022, from https://www.flickr.com/photos/internetarchivebookimages/20640324138. Copyright free]\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Exchange of genes between sister chromatids\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Exchange of telomeres between non-homologous chromosomes\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Exchange of centromeres between non-sister chromatids\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Exchange of alleles between homologous chromosomes","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nThe diagram shows the process of crossing over. Homologous chromosomes are chromosomes with the same sequence of genes but different alleles. Crossing over results in the exchange of alleles between homologous chromosomes. **Choice D**  is correct.\n\nSister chromatids are found on the same chromosome and are connected by a centromere. Crossing over does not occur between sister chromatids; **Choice A**  is incorrect. Crossing over does not occur between non homologous chromosomes; **Choice B**  is incorrect. Centromeres are what hold sister chromatids together, but they are not involved in crossing over; **Choice C**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1539","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1539\n\nBelow is a diagram representing the cell cycle. At which point A-D does CDK combine with cyclins to form MPF?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/Cell%20cycle%20labelled%20A-Di.png\" alt=\"Cell cycle labelled A-Di\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAs shown in the diagram, the cell cycle includes two primary stages, interphase and mitosis. Interphase is divided into three phases: the G${_1}$, S, and G${_2}$ phases. There are three main checkpoints: G${_1}$, G${_2}$ and M. At these checkpoints specific signals are required for the cell to proceed to the next stage. For the G${_2}$ checkpoint to be passed, cyclins must combine with CDK (cyclin-dependent kinase) molecules to form the mitosis-promoting factor (MPF). Once MPF is active, the cell can proceed from G${_2}$ to mitosis. **Choice A** is therefore the correct answer.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/cell%20cyle%20with%20G1%20G2%20and%20S%20phase%20marked%20on%20i.png\" alt=\"cell cyle with G1 G2 and S phase marked on i\">\n\n**Choice B** indicates the end of mitosis when all the MPF will have been broken down. **Choice C** indicates the G${_1}$ checkpoint before the cell goes into the S phase. **Choice D** indicates the end of the S phase and the beginning of the G${_2}$ phase."},{"ref":"BI1540","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1540\n\nThe diagram below shows the concentrations of different cyclins during the cell cycle.  Deduce which cyclin is involved in preparing for DNA replication?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/cyclins%20graph%20i.png\" alt=\"cyclins graph i\">\n[Source:  Adapted from CNX OpenStax. (2016, May 27). *File: Figure_10_03_02.jpg- wikimedia commons*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved May 13, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Figure_10_03_02.jpg. Copyright under CC BY 4]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cyclin D\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cyclin E\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cyclin A\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cyclin B","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nFrom the graph it is possible to work out that **Choice B** is correct. This is because levels of Cyclin E peak at the beginning of the S phase, which is when the DNA is replicated.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because Cyclin D is high throughout mitosis, triggering the movement of cells from G${_0}$ to G${_1}$ and to the S phase.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because cyclin A peaks in the G${_2}$ phase after the DNA has been replicated.\n\n**Choice D**  is incorrect as Cyclin B peaks at the beginning of mitosis, and DNA replication has already happened by then."},{"ref":"BI1560","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1560\n\nCyclins are a class of proteins that control the cell cycle by binding with and activating cyclin-dependent kinases (CDKs). Their concentrations vary throughout the cell cycle, as seen in the graph.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI1560ii.png\" alt=\"BI1560ii\">\n\nDeduce which event is initiated by the increased concentration of cyclin E?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Condensation of homologous chromosomes\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Duplication of organelles\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ DNA replication\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Appearance of spindle fibres","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because the concentration of cyclin E peaks during the transition between the G1 and S phases of the cell cycle. Cyclin E helps initiate DNA replication by phosphorylating target proteins involved in DNA synthesis.\n\n**Choices A** and **D** are incorrect because the chromosomes do not condense and the spindle fibres do not appear until prophase of mitosis. **Choice B** is incorrect because the organelles are copied during the G1 and G2 phases of the cell cycle which are controlled by cyclins A and D, not cyclin E."},{"ref":"BI1571","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1571\n\nThe data in the table was obtained from a study on the causes of Down syndrome (trisomy 21). The researchers were interested in whether Down syndrome is associated with maternal age, errors originating from maternal or paternal sources, or different meiotic phases. 300 cases resulting in Down syndrome were compared to a control group of 410 unaffected individuals.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI1571.png\" alt=\"BI1571\">\n\nBased on the data in the table, what can be concluded regarding the risk of a child being born with Down syndrome?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ It decreases with maternal age.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ It increases with paternal age.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ It is more likely to be caused by non-disjunction in meiosis II.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ It is less likely to be caused by non-disjunction in male gametes.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because Down syndrome (trisomy 21) is caused by non-disjunction of chromosomes (a pair of chromosomes moving to the same pole) during meiosis. According to the data, paternal causes are less likely, suggesting that male gametes are less likely to contribute to cases of the disease.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the data indicates that older women are more likely to give birth to a child with Down syndrome. **Choice B** is incorrect because paternal age is not mentioned in the table. **Choice C** is incorrect because non-disjunction appears to occur more frequently in meiosis I rather than in meiosis II."},{"ref":"BI1716","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1716\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1.  Explain how mitosis is regulated in eukaryotic cells.    [5 marks]\n\n2.  Describe the stages of interphase and their importance to the process of mitosis.    [4 marks]\n\n3.  Discuss the importance of mitosis to living organisms using named examples.     [7 marks]","markScheme":"1.  any **five**  of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cyclins are proteins involved in the regulation of mitosis    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ levels of cyclins rise and fall at specific times in cell cycle        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ kinases  **OR** cyclin-dependent kinases are enzymes activated by cyclins    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ signalling occurs at specific checkpoints **OR** stop points of cell cycle    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ G$_2$ checkpoint controls whether cell goes into mitosis    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as cyclin level rises, it attaches to specific kinase or cyclin-dependent kinase **OR** CDK        [1 mark]\n\n    \n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ acts as mitosis-promoting factor    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ As mitosis-promoting factor **OR** MPF **OR** cyclin-kinase complex builds up switches on mitosis **OR** triggers nuclear membrane breakdown [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    **Cyclins** are **proteins** that **regulate mitosis** ($\\checkmark$), with their **levels rising** and **falling** at **specific times** ($\\checkmark$) in the cell cycle. They **activate cyclin-dependent kinases** ($\\checkmark$) (CDKs), forming a **complex** that acts as a **mitosis-promoting factor** ($\\checkmark$) (MPF). This complex **triggers** processes such as **nuclear membrane breakdown**, enabling **mitosis** to **proceed** ($\\checkmark$). The **G$_2$ checkpoint** ensures **favourable conditions** before the **cell enters mitosis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.  Any **four**  of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in G$_1$ **OR** G$_2$  the cell grows larger    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in G$_1$ the cell performs its normal metabolic functions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in S phase, the cell replicates its DNA [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in S phase, the cell replicates its centrosomes **OR** centrioles    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in G$_2$ phase, the cell duplicates its organelles **OR** synthesises proteins required for cell division    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the replication of DNA ensures that each daughter cell receives an identical set of chromosomes during mitosis    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in animal cells the replication of centrosomes **OR** centrioles is critical for forming the mitotic spindle during mitosis        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the growth and preparation during G$_1$ and G$_2$ phases provide the necessary cellular components and energy for successful cell division during mitosis    [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Interphase is split into three stages. In **G$_1$**, the **cell grows larger** ($\\checkmark$)  and performs its **normal metabolic functions** ($\\checkmark$), while in the **S phase**, it **replicates** its **DNA** ($\\checkmark$)  and **centrosomes** ($\\checkmark$). During **G$_2$**, the cell grows, **duplicates** its **organelles**, and **synthesises proteins** ($\\checkmark$)  necessary for cell division. These stages ensure the cell has **enough components** and an **identical set of chromosomes** to successfully **divide into two daughter cells** ($\\checkmark$)  during mitosis.\n\n3.  Any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ necessary to produce genetically identical cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ happens only in eukaryotes   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ provides new cells for growth    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ suitable examples of growth include embryonic development **OR** organ growth    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tissue repair **OR** wound healing    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tissue repair can protect harmful microorganisms from entering a wound    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ essential for regeneration of limbs in certain organisms  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ asexual reproduction    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ suitable examples of asexual reproduction include budding in hydra **OR** budding in yeast **OR** runners in plants **OR** tubers in plants    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ replacement of old **OR** dying cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ suitable example of replacement of cells include renewing of skin cells **OR** renewing intestinal lining [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ uncontrolled mitosis can result in the growth of tumours    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ maintains chromosome number in somatic cells.    [1 mark]\n\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Mitosis is essential for producing **genetically identical cells** ($\\checkmark$), a process **exclusive to eukaryotes** ($\\checkmark$). It enables **growth** ($\\checkmark$), for example, **embryonic development** and **organ growth** ($\\checkmark$) and **tissue repair**, such as in **wound healing** ($\\checkmark$), which **protects against** harmful **microorganisms** ($\\checkmark$).  **Asexual reproduction** ($\\checkmark$) relies on mitosis, for example, budding in **Hydra** and **yeast**. Mitosis **replaces old** or **dying cells** ($\\checkmark$) and **maintains chromosome number** in **somatic cells** ($\\checkmark$). However, **uncontrolled mitosis** can lead to **tumour** formation ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2342","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2342\n\nWhich of these is an example of unequal cytokinesis?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mitosis in root tips\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Spermatogenesis in testes\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Binary fission in bacteria\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Budding in yeast","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because during budding in yeast, the cytoplasm is not divided equally between the parent cell and the bud. The bud receives less cytoplasm than the parent cell, making it an example of unequal cytokinesis.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because mitosis in root tips produces many small cells that are approximately equal in size, with equal cytoplasmic division.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because spermatogenesis in the testes produces sperm cells that are equal in size and have nearly identical cytoplasmic volumes.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because binary fission in bacteria divides the cytoplasm equally to produce two daughter cells with approximately the same volume of cytoplasm."},{"ref":"BI2614","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2614\n\nPlant meristem cells are typically smaller than mature plant cells. A ball of meristem cells on a plant shoot was measured to be 1.50 mm in radius. The meristem cells within the ball were determined microscopically to have a mean diameter of 5.0 $\\mu$m. If all of the available space in the meristematic ball is occupied by cells, how many cells were present?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ $3 \\times 10^2$\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ $6 \\times 10^2$\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ $2.7 \\times 10^7$\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ $2.16 \\times 10^8$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because:\n\n**Calculation Method 1**\n\n*Convert both to the same linear measurement of length:*\nBall radius $\\times$ 2 = ball diameter\n1.5 mm $\\times$ 2 = 3 mm diameter\n\n*Convert mm to $\\mu$m so both structures are expressed in the same units:*\\\nBall diameter = 3 mm $\\times$ 1000 = 3000 $\\mu$m\\\nCell diameter = 5 $\\mu$m\n\n*Calculate the linear scale factor between the ball and an individual cell:*\\\nLinear scale factor =  $\\dfrac{\\ce{3000 \\mu m}}{\\ce{5 \\mu m}}$ = 600 x\n\n*Area scale factor = (linear scale factor)$^2$*\\\n*Volume scale factor = (linear scale factor)$^3$*\n\n*Because there is no unoccupied space in the ball, we can calculate the number of cells in the whole ball by converting this linear scale factor to a volume scale factor by raising it to the power of 3*\\\n$600^3$ = 216 000 000 cells\n\n*Convert to standard form:*\n\n216 000 000 = $2.16 \\times 10^8$\n\n**Calculation Method 2**\n\nCalculate the volumes of the ball of cells and of a single cell using the formula for the volume of a sphere, $\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3$\\\n*Convert both radii to the same linear measurement of length ($\\mu$m):*\\\nBall radius = 1.5 mm $\\times$ 1000 = 1500 $\\mu$m\\\nCell radius = $\\dfrac{\\ce{Cell diameter}}{2}$ = $\\dfrac{\\ce{5 \\mu m}}{2}$ = 2.5 $\\mu$m\n\n*Because we are dividing the volume of the ball by the volume of one cell to calculate the number of cells present, we can cancel the $\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi$ term from top and bottom and be left with the division:*\n\nNumber of cells in the ball = $\\dfrac{\\ce{volume of ball}}{\\ce{volume of one cell}} = \\dfrac{\\ce{\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3 (ball)}}{\\ce{\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3 (cell)}}$\n\nCancel the $\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi$ term from the numerator and denominator:\n\n$\\dfrac{\\ce{\\cancel{\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi} r^3 (ball)}}{\\ce{\\cancel{\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi} r^3 (cell)}} = \\dfrac{1500^3}{2.5^3} = \\dfrac{3.375 \\times 10^9}{15.625}$ = 216 000 000\n\n*In standard form:*\n\n216 000 000 = 2.16 $\\times 10^8$\n\n**Choices A, B** and **C** are incorrect because they all contain calculation errors."},{"ref":"BI2616","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2616\n\nHuman skin cells are replaced frequently; therefore, they undergo regular cell cycles. \\\nWhich row correctly identifies a phase of a skin cell’s cycle and the events that take place within that phase?\n\n| | Phase | Event |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\ce{G_1}$ | The cell remains the same size |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | S | DNA replication and transcription |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\ce{G_2}$ | Synthesis of proteins necessary for meiosis |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Cytokinesis | Chromosomes move to the poles of the cell |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because the S phase stands for ‘synthesis’ (of DNA, by replication). Transcription also occurs during the S phase, although is carefully regulated in order to prevent DNA that is being replicated from being transcribed at the same time, which may cause errors in either process. \\\n**Choice A** is incorrect because, during $\\ce{G_1}$, the cell increases in size as do some of the organelles within it.\\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because, during $\\ce{G_2}$, the proteins necessary for mitosis (not meiosis) are synthesised. This is a skin cell, which cannot produce gametes, so does not need to perform meiotic division. \\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because cytokinesis occurs when a membrane forms between two halves of a cell, causing two daughter cells to be produced. ‘Chromosomes move to the poles of the cell’ describes an event of anaphase (part of mitosis)."},{"ref":"BI2619","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2619\n\nThe table shows the concentrations of three types of cyclin (X, Y and Z) at three different stages of cell proliferation.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Cell%20%26%20Nuclear%20Division/images/BI2619.png\" alt=\"BI2619\">\n\nWhich hypothesis is supported by the data in the table?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cyclin X maintains the undifferentiated state of stem cells.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cyclin Y inhibits the process of cell differentiation.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cyclin Z is required for the maintenance of fully differentiated cells.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cyclin concentrations remain constant regardless of the stage of differentiation.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct  because Cyclin X is at its highest concentration (18 units) in totipotent stem cells and decreases as cells become more specialised, reaching 0 in fully differentiated cells. This strongly suggests that Cyclin X plays a role in maintaining an undifferentiated state.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because Cyclin Y concentration is at its highest (11 units) in fully differentiated cells. This suggests that Cyclin Y promotes or maintains the differentiated state, rather than inhibiting it.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because Cyclin Z levels drop significantly (from 15 to 1 unit) once the cell becomes fully differentiated. This suggests Cyclin Z is more likely involved in the transition between stem cell stages (proliferation) rather than the maintenance of differentiated cells.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the table shows significant fluctuations in the levels of all three cyclins (X, Y, and Z) as the cell changes state."},{"ref":"BI2621","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2621\n\nWhich of the following features of the P53 gene identify it as a tumour suppressor gene?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | The protein it codes for binds directly to DNA. |\n    | II. | It converts to an oncogene in the presence of carcinogens. |\n    | III. | It determines whether a cell needs to undergo apoptosis. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because the P53 protein is a transcription factor. This binds directly to DNA and suppresses the cell cycle, preventing uncontrolled cell division. \\\n**Statement II** is false because this statement describes proto-oncogenes, not tumour suppressor genes. \\\n**Statement III** is true because when DNA is irreversibly damaged, the P53 protein initiates apoptosis (controlled cell death) to eliminate cells with badly damaged DNA that should not divide further.\n\n**Choice C** is correct because it contains only the correct statements (**I** and **III**).\\\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it contains the false statement (**II**) and omits one of the true statements (**III**).\\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because it omits one of the true statements (**I**).\\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because it contains the false statement (**II**)."}]},{"name":"Climate Change","questions":[{"ref":"BI0129","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0129\n\nWhich of the following statements about methane in the atmosphere are true?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | Methane is emitted during the extraction of natural gas, coal and oil |\n  | II. | Methane is released into the atmosphere from cattle farms and other agricultural practices |\n  | III. | Methane is short-lived in the atmosphere and is converted to carbon dioxide and water |\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAll the statements are true, **Choice D** includes all the statements. Methane is emitted during the production and transport of fuels and is emitted from cattle farms. Atmospheric concentration of methane is limited because it is converted to carbon dioxide and water.\n\nThe other choices (**Choice A, Choice B, and Choice C**) do not include all the statements."},{"ref":"BI0137","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0137\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nForest fires occur naturally in various parts of the world and contribute to the health of ecosystems. With an increase in global warming, forest fires have become more frequent.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Climate%20Change/images/BI0137.png\" alt=\"BI0137\">\n[Source: Strandberg, C. (2009, June 2). *Forest fire*. Commons Wikimedia. Retrieved May 7, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Fire-Forest.jpg.  Copyright under Creative Commons Attribution 2.0 Generic]\n\n1. State the name of the process that describes how forest fires are involved in the carbon cycle.      [1 mark]\n\n2. Outline how forests can benefit from forest fires. [2 marks]\n\n3. Describe the effects of increased forest fires on the carbon cycle. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ combustion **OR** burning of biomass [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ :u[Sample answer]:\n$\\hspace{1em}$ :br\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Forest fires are part of the carbon cycle and fall under the category of **combustion** ($\\checkmark$). The burning of fossil  fuels by humans is also part of the combustion process.\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ forest fires can clear out underbrush **OR** destroy climax communities [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ open areas make light available to smaller plants and trees for photosynthesis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ new growth takes place in burned areas      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ may increase biodiversity [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some seeds need exposure to fire to break out of dormancy   [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Forest fires are useful in **opening up** dense areas of forest  **to make light available** for smaller plants and trees ($\\checkmark$), the undergrowth in the floor of the forests. Some seeds on the ground **need exposure to fire to break the dormancy** ($\\checkmark$) and start germinating.\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in combustion **OR** burning of biomass       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in carbon dioxide in the atmosphere   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can lead to **OR** enhance global warming  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ immediately after forest fires, there is a decrease in the amount of photosynthesis because there are fewer trees     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as the forest regrows there is an increase in the total amount of photosynthesis  [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Increased forest fires are detrimental, they cause an **increase in carbon dioxide in the atmosphere** ($\\checkmark$). This can subsequently **lead to global warming** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0139","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0139\n\nWhich of the following statements about greenhouse gases is NOT true?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Greenhouse gases are needed to keep the Earth’s atmosphere warm.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Greenhouse gases absorb the majority of incoming shortwave radiation from the Sun.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ The majority of longer-wavelength radiation emitted by the earth is absorbed by greenhouse gases.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Nitrous oxide and industrial halocarbons are greenhouse gases.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nGreenhouse gases only absorb a small percentage of incoming shortwave radiation from the Sun.\n\nGreenhouse gases warm the earth (**Choice A**) because they let most incoming radiation through, which heats the surface of the earth and then absorbs the re-emitted longer wavelength radiation (**Choice C**).\n\nNitrous oxide, which is anthropogenic in origin, as well as industrially generated halocarbons, are also greenhouse gases (**Choice D**) and enhance the greenhouse effect."},{"ref":"BI0141","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0141\n\nWhich of the following greenhouse gases is correctly paired with its source?\n\n| | Greenhouse gas | Source |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| A. $\\hspace{1em}$ | Methane | Melting of permafrost in frozen tundra |\n| B. $\\hspace{1em}$ | Nitrous oxide | Landfills |\n| C. $\\hspace{1em}$ | Carbon dioxide | Production of aluminium and magnesium |\n| D. $\\hspace{1em}$ | Fluorinated gases | Melting of permafrost in frozen tundra |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe melting of permafrost releases methane gas into the atmosphere. Other sources of methane include landfills, oil and natural gas systems, and agriculture.\n\nNitrous oxide (**Choice B**) is emitted from the combustion of fossil fuels, from solid waste, and also from agricultural sources. Carbon dioxide (**Choice C**) is released by the combustion of fossil fuels, solid waste, trees and other biological materials. Fluorinated gases like hydrofluorocarbons\n(**Choice D**) are emitted from household and industrial processes; even if present in small amounts, they trap much more heat than carbon dioxide."},{"ref":"BI0142","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0142\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Climate%20Change/images/BI0142.png\" alt=\"BI0142\">\n[Source: Adapted from  Inaglory, B. (2005, September). *File: permafrost pattern.jpg - Wikimedia Commons*. Retrieved May 2, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Permafrost_pattern.jpg Copyright under CC BY-SA 3.0]\n\nScientists have been studying the effects of global warming for some time now, and are worried about the thawing of frozen ice and permafrost in the Arctic region. The image above was taken from a helicopter and shows the crack pattern in permafrost.\n\nWhich of the following statements best describes the concerns of scientists?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | There is frozen organic matter in the Arctic which contains ancient bacteria and viruses  |\n  | II. | With the thawing of ice and organic matter, more carbon dioxide will be released into the air |\n  | III. | The melting of permafrost in the Arctic will cause the release of methane |\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDead plants and animals have been frozen in permafrost for millions of years. When permafrost thaws, so will the ancient bacteria and viruses which could infect other organisms (**Statement I**).  The thawing of ice and permafrost will lead to the release of both carbon dioxide  (**Statement II**) and methane  (**Statement III**)  from the permafrost.\n\nAll statements are true. The other choices (**Choices A, C and D**) do not include all statements."},{"ref":"BI0143","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0143\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Climate%20Change/images/BI0143.png\" alt=\"BI0143\">\n[Source: Adapted from  Semhur, S.(n.d.). *File: Mauna Loa carbon dioxide-en svg.* Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved April 27, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Mauna_Loa_Carbon_Dioxide-en.svg. Copyright free]\n\nThe above data obtained from Mauna Loa show trends in atmospheric CO$_{2}$ concentrations. What conclusions can be made from this graph?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | There is an annual fluctuation of carbon dioxide concentration in the atmosphere. |\n  | II. | There is a continuous increase in the average concentration of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere over the years. |\n  | III. | Increased use of fossil fuels has caused an increase in carbon dioxide in the atmosphere as recorded in Mauna Loa. |\n\n- A. I only\n\n- B. I and II only\n\n- C. I and III only\n\n- D. I, II and III","markScheme":"Explanation:\n\nThe data show an annual fluctuation in the concentration of carbon dioxide **(I)** and a constant increase in average concentration over the many years measured **(II)**.\n\n**Choice A** includes annual fluctuations but not a constant increase in carbon dioxide.\n\n**Choice C** includes a conclusion about annual fluctuations but also the conclusion that says increased use of fossil fuels has caused the increase in carbon dioxide in the atmosphere **(III)** which cannot be determined based on this graph.\n\n**Choice D** includes all three conclusions and since conclusion III is incorrect, it is not the right choice."},{"ref":"BI0144","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0144\n\nWhich of the following shows the effect of rising atmospheric carbon dioxide concentrations on marine ecosystems?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Expansion of habitat for large predators like killer whales.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Increase in fish populations.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Ocean acidification.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Reef-building organisms such as corals have increased in numbers.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCarbon dioxide reacts with water to form carbonic acid. The carbon dioxide released by humans through the burning of fossil fuels has been dissolving in the oceans, causing acidification.\n\nRising temperatures cause the loss of breeding grounds (**Choice A**) for marine fish and mammals.\n\nLocal populations of fish and other marine animals are disappearing at double the rate of land-based species. (**Choice B**)\n\nThe number of reef-building organisms has been reduced considerably.  The concentration of carbonate ions which are needed to build the calcium carbonate shells of reef-building corals (**Choice D**) has decreased. The acidified oceans cause the dissolution of existing reefs and could make the corals disappear"},{"ref":"BI0145","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0145\n\nScientists analyzed the main contributors to greenhouse gas emissions from human-related activities. The resulting data is presented in the charts below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Climate%20Change/images/bi0145.png\" alt=\"bi0145\">\n[Source: Adapted from Rohde, R. A. (2019, March 14). *File: greenhouse gas by Sector.png - Wikimedia Commons*. Annual Greenhouse Gas Emissions by Sector. Retrieved May 4, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Greenhouse_Gas_by_Sector.png\nCopyright under Creative Commons Attribution-Share Alike 3.0]\n\nWhich contributor is most responsible for the production and release of methane and nitrogen oxides into the atmosphere?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Land use and biomass burning\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Industrial use\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Residential sources\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Agricultural production","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe largest area in the pie charts showing sources of methane and nitrogen oxides is agricultural production.\n\nLand use and biomass burning (**Choice A**) are a source of both methane and nitrous oxide but the area in the pie charts is less than agricultural production.\n\nIndustrial use (**Choice B**) releases mainly nitrous oxide.\n\nResidential sources (**Choice C**) also release both methane and nitrous oxide but in lesser amounts than agricultural production."},{"ref":"BI0146","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0146\n\nScientists predict that global warming will have devastating effects in the future. Which of the following are the consequences of global warming?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | Coastal flooding and loss of coastal cities  |\n  | II. | Bleaching of coral reefs |\n  | III. | Increased spread of tropical diseases  |\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nScientists predict that global warming will have devastating effects which include coastal flooding, loss of coastal cities, and loss of atolls in places like the Maldives.  Scientists predict a majority of corals will experience bleaching as a result of climate change.   As biomes shift in latitude more tropical diseases will spread towards the north and south poles because pathogens thrive in warmer environments.\n\nAll other choices (**Choice A, Choice B and Choice C**) do not include all the predicted effects of global warming."},{"ref":"BI0147","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0147\n\nWhich statement is **not** true regarding greenhouse gases?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Heat is not trapped equally by all greenhouse gases.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The greenhouse effect resulting from naturally occurring greenhouse gases is needed for life on Earth.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Sulfur dioxide is a greenhouse gas.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Methane is more potent than carbon dioxide in trapping heat energy.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSulfur dioxide (**Choice C**)  is not a greenhouse gas.  It is a pollutant in the atmosphere resulting mainly from the combustion of coal and causes acid rain.\n\nIt is true that heat trapped by different greenhouse gases varies (**Choice A**).  Some gases trap more heat than others and the average time that these gases stay in the atmosphere varies as well.\n\nGreenhouse gases are necessary because they increase the temperature of the earth to a level (**Choice B**) required by organisms to survive on Earth.\n\nMethane is more potent than carbon dioxide by almost 20 times but stays in the atmosphere for a shorter time compared to carbon dioxide (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0148","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0148\n\nWhich of the following would not delay or decrease the severity of the effects of global warming?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Increased use of renewable energy.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Decreased deforestation and increased forestation.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Increased use of natural fertilizers and decreased use of nitrogen fertilizers.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Forests converted to farms.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCutting down forests and clearing land for agricultural purposes causes a decrease in the intake of carbon dioxide from the atmosphere which is a leading cause of global warming.\n\nBy using more renewable energy (**Choice A**), there will be a decrease in the burning of coal and fossil fuels for generating electricity in power stations. Reducing deforestation (**Choice B**) will enhance the retention of carbon dioxide by trees through photosynthesis. The use of natural fertilizers like compost (**Choice C**) instead of fertilizers enriched in nitrogen will reduce the release of nitrous oxide which is a greenhouse gas."},{"ref":"BI0149","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0149\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\n1. Outline what is meant by the greenhouse effect. [2 marks]\n\n2. List two of the main greenhouse gases present in the atmosphere. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ greenhouse gases **OR** CO$_2$, methane, water vapour and nitrous oxides are responsible for the greenhouse effect[1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ warming of Earth by gases is called the greenhouse effect [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ greenhouse gases trap the Sun’s heat **OR** longwave radiation [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ short wave radiation from the Sun passes through the atmosphere and reaches the surface of the earth [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the greenhouse effect results in a warmer planet **OR** more suitable environment for life  [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Greenhouse gases in the atmosphere **trap the Sun’s heat** ($\\checkmark$) and **make the Earth warmer and more suitable for living things** ($\\checkmark$). This is called the greenhouse effect.\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Carbon dioxide [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Water vapour [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Methane [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Nitrogen oxides [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Halocarbons [1 mark]\n[2 marks]"},{"ref":"BI0150","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0150\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n1. Describe the method used by scientists to deduce carbon dioxide concentrations from thousands of years ago.         [2 marks]\n\n2. Explain why rising levels of atmospheric carbon dioxide are the main cause of climate change. [2 marks]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Climate%20Change/images/bi0150.png\" alt=\"bi0150\">\n\n[Source: Lum, W. H. W. (2014, October 4). *Great Barrier Reef*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved May 9, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Great_barrier_reef.JPG Copyright- CC BY-SA 4.0]\n\n3. How have increased carbon dioxide levels affected reef-building corals in marine waters?  [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ columns of ice **OR** ice cores have been drilled in the Antarctic   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ice from deeper layers is older than ice closer to the surface [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as new ice forms on the surface it traps air bubbles in the newly formed ice [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ bubbles of air trapped in the ice can be used to deduce atmospheric gas concentrations at the time the ice formed [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Ice that has been frozen over the years in the Antarctic **has been drilled and extracted** for analysis ($\\checkmark$). The ice from **deeper down is older than the ice that is towards the surface** ($\\checkmark$). The **bubbles of air trapped in the ice can be used to determine past concentrations of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ global temperature and CO$_2$ fluctuations are strongly correlated over long periods of time **OR** show a similar pattern of increase and decrease [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ since the start of the industrial revolution the use of fossil fuels has increased  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ since the start of the industrial revolution, more carbon dioxide has been released into the air  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ CO$_2$ is abundant in the atmosphere **OR** CO$_2$ is a stable atmospheric gas     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ CO$_2$ has been shown to absorb (and re-emit) long-wave radiation **OR** CO$_2$ is a greenhouse gas [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    CO$_2$ is a known **greenhouse gas** ($\\checkmark$). Over the last 800,000 years, the patterns of global temperature and carbon dioxide have been cyclical, **showing similar patterns of increase and decrease** ($\\checkmark$). In the last 200 years, since the start of industrialization, **the use of fossil fuels has increased** ($\\checkmark$). Consequently, **more carbon dioxide is being released into the air** causing temperatures to rise ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ CO$_2$ from the air dissolves in water [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some CO$_2$ combines with water to form the carbonic acid  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lowers the pH **OR** makes it acidic   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ seawater has a buffer capacity that combines the dissolved CO$_2$ with H$_2$O to make HCO$_3$:sup[-] **OR** increased CO$_2$ leads to the decrease of carbonate ion (**OR** CO$_{3}$ $^{2-}$) concentration [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbonate ions in the water are needed for the building of the coral  CaCO$_3$ skeletons [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased acidity will cause dissolving of the calcium carbonate from the coral skeleton [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ threat to the existence of corals **OR** slow growth of corals  [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Increased carbon dioxide in the air leads to more **dissolution of carbon dioxide in oceans causing the formation of carbonic acid** ($\\checkmark$). This leads to increased acidity **and a decrease in ocean water pH** ($\\checkmark$). This **lowers the availability of carbonate ions in the water** ($\\checkmark$), so corals **cannot make the calcium carbonate** needed to make their skeletons ($\\checkmark$). Also, **the lower pH can dissolve the calcium carbonate in corals** which is threatening their existence ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0549","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0549\n\nWhich characteristics of carbon dioxide make it a greenhouse gas of concern?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Absorbs long wave radiation and has a significant concentration in the atmosphere.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Emits shortwave radiation and has a significant concentration in the atmosphere.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$  Absorbs shortwave radiation and has a high stability.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Blocks infrared radiation and has a high stability.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA greenhouse gas is a gas that contributes to the greenhouse effect. Greenhouse gases absorb the long wave infrared radiation (heat energy) emitted from Earth’s surface and can reradiate it back to Earth’s surface. So, one factor that determines whether a gas acts as a greenhouse gas is its ability to absorb long wave radiation: gases that are better able to absorb long wave radiation are more likely to act as greenhouse gases. A second factor that qualifies carbon dioxide as a greenhouse gas of concern is its relatively high concentration in the atmosphere: gases that are in higher concentration in the atmosphere have a larger effect on Earth’s temperature."},{"ref":"BI0564","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0564\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe image shows the observed surface temperature change in the Northern Hemisphere from 1979 to 2019.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Climate%20Change/images/bi0564a.png\" alt=\"bi0564a\">\n[Source: Adapted from Buis, A. (2022, April 22). *Making Sense of \"Climate Sensitivity.\" National Aeronautics and Space Administration Goddard Institute for Space Studies*. Retrieved January 4, 2023, from https://www.giss.nasa.gov/research/features/202009_sensitivity/. Copyright under Public Domain.]\n\n1. State the name and source of the greenhouse gas primarily associated with the observed temperature increases. [2 marks]\n\n    Name:\n\n    Source:\n\n2. Outline the process through which greenhouse gases increase Earth’s global temperatures. [2 marks]\n\n3. The bar-tailed godwit (*Limosa lapponica*) nests and breeds on Arctic coasts and tundra and overwinters in the Southern Hemisphere. Predict how the increase in surface temperatures in the Northern Hemisphere may affect the survival of such a migratory bird. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Each of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ name: carbon dioxide **OR** CO$_2$   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ source: combustion of fossil fuels **OR** burning of fossil fuels for energy **OR** combustion of coal/oil/gas for energy generation [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Type: **carbon dioxide** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n    Source: **combustion of fossil fuels for energy** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ earth’s surface emits longer wavelength radiation **OR** infrared radiation as heat [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ longer wavelength radiation **OR** infrared radiation is absorbed by greenhouse gases [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ longer wave **OR** infrared radiation is reradiated by greenhouse gases  [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The Earth’s surface receives shorter wavelength radiation (electromagnetic radiation) from the sun and **emits longer wavelength radiation** ($\\checkmark$) (infrared radiation) as heat. **Longer wavelength radiation is absorbed by greenhouse gases** ($\\checkmark$) in Earth’s atmosphere and is **reradiated** back to the Earth’s surface ($\\checkmark$). These processes mean heat is retained rather than released to space, which increases the Earth’s temperature.\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ survival decreases because of loss of resources **OR** loss of food **OR** loss of habitat  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ survival decreases because of reduced reproductive success caused by changes in resource availability **OR** loss of food **OR** loss of habitat  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ survival decreases because of mistiming of migration **OR** godwits arriving in Arctic breeding ground after key food sources have emerged   [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    As temperatures increase in the Northern Hemisphere, the availability of food and habitat may change.  The **survival** of godwits will **decrease** if these changes result in a **loss of food or habitat** ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, global temperature increases may result in the start of spring/summer in the Arctic occurring earlier in the year. If godwits do not change the timing of their migration back to their breeding grounds to keep pace with the start of spring, they may miss the **emergence** of their **food sources** ($\\checkmark$), meaning less food would be available for breeding birds and their chicks. This **change in the food availability** for breeding birds and their offspring would **lower reproductive success** ($\\checkmark$) and therefore threaten the survival of the bird population."},{"ref":"BI0806","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0806\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nThe following graphs show the concentration of atmospheric carbon dioxide over time at Mauna Loa Observatory in Hawaii, United States.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Climate%20Change/images/BI0806A.png\" alt=\"BI0806A\">\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Climate%20Change/images/BI0806B.png\" alt=\"BI0806B\">\n[Source: Adapted from US Department of Commerce, NOAA. *“Global Monitoring Laboratory - Carbon Cycle Greenhouse Gases.”* GML, ( Oct. 2005). Retrieved 18 May from gml.noaa.gov/ccgg/trends/. Copyright under CC0 4.0]\n\n1. Describe the overall trend in atmospheric carbon dioxide levels over time. [1 mark]\n\n2. State one factor that has significantly contributed to increases in atmospheric carbon dioxide concentration as seen in the graphs.    [1 mark]\n\n3. Explain why the concentration of atmospheric carbon dioxide fluctuates annually. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. The following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide levels in the atmosphere have been rising continuously since the 1960s [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Carbon dioxide levels in the atmosphere **have increased from 1960 to 2020** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increasing use **OR** increase in combustion of fossil fuels to provide energy    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in the numbers of cars and other vehicles [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ deforestation **OR** land use changes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ improper waste management **OR** decomposition of organic waste in landfills   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ industrial activities    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The main cause has been the **increased number of cars** and other vehicles ($\\checkmark$) being used.\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ during the summer (in the northern hemisphere), **OR** spring (in the northern hemisphere) **OR** growing season, an increase in vegetation **OR** biomass **OR** temperature causes an increase in photosynthesis which results in increased fixation of carbon dioxide, causing a decrease in atmospheric concentration     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ during the autumn (in the northern hemisphere) **OR** winter (in the northern hemisphere), there is a decrease in photosynthesis and an increase in respiration due to cooler temperatures which results in an increase of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vegetation decay  during the dormant season **OR** autumn **OR** winter (in the northern hemisphere) and carbon dioxide is released        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fluctuations in ocean carbon exchange in response to temperature changes **OR** cooler temperatures more carbon dioxide absorbed by oceans, reducing atmospheric carbon dioxide **OR** warmer temperatures ocean releases carbon dioxide into the atmosphere [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The annual fluctuations in atmospheric carbon dioxide concentrations are primarily driven by the **seasonal patterns** of **photosynthesis** and **respiration in plants**.  In the northern hemisphere, there is an **increase** in **plant growth** in the **summer**, which **increases carbon dioxide fixation** by **photosynthesis** ($\\checkmark$). In **winter**, there is a **decrease in photosynthesis**, so **reduced carbon dioxide uptake** by plants ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0807","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0807\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe image below shows global sea levels from the years 1880 to 2020.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Climate%20Change/images/BI0807.png\" alt=\"BI0807\">\n[Source: Adapted from Wikipedia, ( May 2023), *“Sea Level Rise.”*\nRetrieved 17 May 2023, from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea_level_rise Copyright under CC0 3.0]\n\n1. There is a trend line showing changes in sea level from 1920 to 1960 and another trend line showing changes in sea level between 1980 and 2020. Describe the differences in sea level change between these two periods of time. [1 mark]\n\n2. Outline the reasons for linking atmospheric carbon dioxide levels to the rise in sea levels. [2 marks]\n\nEcosystems growing in salt marshes are able to help reduce the effects of climate change by the sequestration (capture) of carbon. Sea level rise (SLR) has affected these ecosystems by flooding and eroding coastal areas.\n\nThe results from a recent study modelling the effects of a one-metre sea level rise on carbon sequestration by salt marshes in New York, United States, are shown in the graph below. The data shows the predicted effects by the year 2100.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Climate%20Change/images/BI0807B.png\" alt=\"BI0807B\">\n[Source: Adapted from Tanner, Katherine, and Aaron L. Strong. ( Mar, 2023) *Sustainability 2023, 15(6), 4733; “Assessing the Impact of Future Sea Level Rise on Blue Carbon Ecosystem Services on Long Island, New York.” MDPI*, Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute. Retrieved May 15, 2023, from doi.org/10.3390/su15064733, Copyright under CC0 4.0]\n\n3. Suggest an explanation for the difference in carbon sequestration between no SLR and one meter SLR, shown in the prediction graph. [1 mark]\n\n4. Draw a diagram of the carbon cycle for a terrestrial ecosystem, and include carbon sinks, and processes associated with the cycle. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. The following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the trend lines show that the increase in sea levels for the years 1920 to 1960 is not as steep as seen for the years 1980 to 2020 [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The increase in sea levels (as shown by the trend lines) **is steeper for the years from 1980 to 2020** ($\\checkmark$) compared to the years from 1920 to 1960.\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increasing carbon dioxide levels cause an increase in the temperature of the atmosphere [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increasing atmospheric temperatures cause glaciers **OR** ice sheets to melt more rapidly which raises sea levels [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increasing ocean temperatures cause thermal expansion of the ocean waters causing sea levels to rise [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Increasing carbon dioxide levels **cause an increase in atmospheric temperature** ($\\checkmark$) which can lead to **increased melting of ice sheets** ($\\checkmark$) and a rise in sea levels.\n\n3. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the sea level rise may damage photosynthesising organisms **OR** producers, in the salt marshes **OR** coastal ecosystems so decrease the capture of carbon [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ erosion may release carbon into the atmosphere [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ rising sea level increases the depth of water in salt marshes resulting in less light reaching aquatic photosynthesising organisms' habitats [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Sea level rises may kill some photosynthesising organisms and so **decrease the capture of carbon by photosynthesis** ($\\checkmark$) thereby affecting the carbon cycle.\n\n4. Any **three** of the following sinks or storages with at least **three** different connecting processes relevant to those sinks or storages:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ CO$_2$ in the air **AND** combustion **OR** respiration **OR** photosynthesis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon compounds in producers **AND** photosynthesis **OR** respiration **OR** consumed by **OR** death [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon compounds in consumers **AND** respiration **OR** consumed by **OR** death [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon compounds in saprotrophs OR decomposers **OR** detritivores **AND** decomposition **OR** respiration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon compounds in dead organisms **AND** fossilisation **OR** death **OR** decomposition [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon compounds in fossil fuels **AND** combustion **OR** fossilisation [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Climate%20Change/images/BI0807BII.png\" alt=\"BI0807BII\">"},{"ref":"BI1103","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1103\n\nWhich term refers to research into the timing of biological events?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Phenology\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Synchrony\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Photoperiod\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Periodontology","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because phenology is the study of cyclic and seasonal changes, especially in relation to climate and living organisms. It involves observing and recording the timing of events such as flowering, migration, and hibernation. **Choice B** is incorrect because synchrony, in biology, refers to events occurring simultaneously or in coordination. **Choice C** is incorrect because photoperiod refers to the duration of light exposure in a 24-hour period and how it influences the behaviour and physiology of organisms. **Choice D** is incorrect because periodontology refers to a speciality in dentistry."},{"ref":"BI1104","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1104\n\nReindeer feed on mouse-ear chickweed in the Arctic. What is likely to result from a trophic mismatch in the Arctic?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ More Arctic mouse-ear chickweed seeds germinate.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Fewer reindeer calves are born.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Greater reproductive success of migrating reindeer.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Fewer Arctic mouse-ear chickweed seeds germinate.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA trophic mismatch can occur when climate change causes shifts in the timing of plant growth. If, for example, the Arctic mouse-ear chickweed, a key food source for reindeer, experiences a change in its growth cycle due to warming temperatures, it might peak earlier or later than the period when reindeer need it most for calving. If the timing is not synchronised with the needs of pregnant reindeer, it can reduce food availability for the mothers and their calves, potentially leading to fewer reindeer calves being born.\nMore seeds may develop if conditions improve, such as temperatures increase and there is enough precipitation, however, this is not a result of a trophic mismatch, so **Choice A** is incorrect.\n\nA trophic mismatch usually leads to challenges in finding sufficient food, which can negatively impact reproductive success, therefore, **Choice C** is incorrect.\n\nFewer seeds may germinate if conditions are not right to break the dormancy of the seeds; a lower germination success is not to do with a trophic mismatch, therefore, **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1105","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1105\n\nClimate change can impact which of the following events?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Growth of *Cerastium arcticum* (Arctic mouse-ear chickweed) |\n    | II. | The arrival of migrating *Rangifer tarandus* (reindeer) |\n    | III. | The mating of *Parus major* (great tit) |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III\n\n*Note: Starting May 2028, this question may be assessed differently due to changes to the Biology guide.*","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statements I, II**, and **III** are all correct as they can all be influenced by changes in temperature, which are influenced by climate change. Rising temperatures, changing precipitation patterns, and altered growing seasons can affect the availability of suitable plant habitats and resources (**Statement I**). As temperatures and ecosystems change, the availability of food sources and suitable habitats for migratory species can be affected, leading to changes in the timing of migrations as animals respond to shifts in resource availability (**Statement II**). Mating behaviours, including courtship and breeding, are often influenced by environmental cues such as temperature, food availability, and day length (**Statement III**). Therefore, **Choice D** is correct because it contains all three true statements.\n\n**Choices A**, **B** and **C** are incorrect as they are missing one or two of the true statements."},{"ref":"BI1106","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1106\n\nSpruce bark beetles burrow under the bark of spruce trees and other conifers. The tawny owl has two colourations, brown and white. Climate change can have which of the following impacts?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Decrease in the number of life cycles per year of the spruce bark beetle\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increase in the number of life cycles per year of the spruce bark beetle\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Decrease in the fitness of the brown colour variant of the tawny owl\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increase in the fitness of the white colour variant of the tawny owl","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because warmer temperatures can accelerate the life cycle of many insect species, including the spruce bark beetle, as they can reproduce and develop more rapidly, making **Choice A**  incorrect. **Choices C** and **D** are incorrect because the fitness of the brown variant of the tawny owl is increasing, and the fitness of the white variant is decreasing because, as snow cover decreases, the brown variant is better camouflaged."},{"ref":"BI1107","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1107\n\nWhich of the following is/are primarily influenced by photoperiod?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Bird migration |\n    | II. | Bird nesting |\n    | III. | Hatching of eggs |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because only **Statement I** is correct, as many bird species rely on the changing day length as a signal for the timing of their migratory journeys.\nBird nesting (**Statement II**) is primarily influenced by factors such as food availability and temperature, and the hatching of eggs (**Statement III**)  is influenced mainly by the warmth of the nest and embryonic development. Therefore, **Choices B, C** and **D** are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1108","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1108\n\nWhich row in the table is correct for variables affecting the timing of biological events in an arctic ecosystem?\n\n| | Migration of Reindeer | Germination of Arctic Mouse-Ear Chickweed Seeds  |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Temperature | Temperature |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Photoperiod | Temperature |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Temperature | Photoperiod |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Photoperiod | Photoperiod |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because Reindeer (Rangifer tarandus) use day length as a cue to begin their journey to their breeding grounds, and the Arctic mouse-ear chickweed (Cerastium arcticum) uses temperature as a cue to germinate. **Choice A, C** and **D** are incorrect because the variables do not match up correctly with the biological events."},{"ref":"BI1109","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1109\n\nWhich of the following is an example of evolution due to climate change?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increase in brown tawny owl morphs in Finland in response to change in snow levels\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increase in the number of life cycles of spruce bark beetle on north-facing slopes of a boreal forest in one year\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increase in numbers of bees in spring in temperate regions in response to increased temperatures breaking hibernation early\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increase of reindeer calves during one breeding season due to warmer temperatures","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nMicroevolution involves changes in the genetic makeup of a population over generations in response to selective pressures. **Choice A** is an example of such a change, as it shows an increase in the brown morph of owls due to better camouflage in areas with reduced snow cover caused by increasing temperatures.\n**Choice C** is incorrect as it represents a phenological response to climate change, meaning a change in the timing of life cycle events, but it does not involve genetic changes in the bee population. The reduction in the number of bees is due to a lack of food caused by flowers not being open. Flowers in temperate zones open in response to the length of the day, not temperature.  **Choice D** is incorrect; is not an example of microevolution, but rather a disruption of a phenological event caused by climate change. Climate change is likely to reduce the number of calves due to the disruption to the synchrony of the growth of plants that the reindeer eat and the return of the reindeer to their breeding sites. **Choice B** is also incorrect as it is a response to temperature caused by the position of the tree where the spruce bark beetle is living. The spruce bark beetle tends to go through one life cycle a year on the north-facing slopes, and two on south-facing slopes which get more sun."},{"ref":"BI1110","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1110\n\nWhich change contributes to a positive feedback cycle that amplifies global warming?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increased absorption of atmospheric carbon dioxide by oceans as water temperature rises\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$\nIncreased reflection of solar radiation as ice cover decreases\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increased decomposition of previously frozen organic matter as permafrost thaws\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ ncreased carbon storage in forest vegetation as the frequency of forest fires rises","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C**  is correct because rising temperatures cause permafrost to thaw, allowing microorganisms to decompose previously frozen organic matter. This releases carbon dioxide and, under suitable anaerobic conditions, methane. These greenhouse gases strengthen the greenhouse effect, causing further warming and additional permafrost thawing. This is positive feedback because the initial temperature increase is amplified.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because increased absorption of atmospheric carbon dioxide would tend to oppose warming rather than amplify it. Furthermore, increasing water temperature reduces carbon dioxide solubility, so warming alone does not increase the ocean’s capacity to absorb carbon dioxide.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because decreasing ice cover reduces the reflection of solar radiation. The darker surfaces exposed absorb more radiation, contributing to further warming.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because forest fires release carbon stored in vegetation into the atmosphere rather than increasing carbon storage in that vegetation. Greater carbon dioxide emissions from fires can contribute to further warming."},{"ref":"BI1117","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1117\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Define the term phenology, and describe the impact of climate change on phenological events associated with a named species.        [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline the anthropogenic causes and positive feedback cycles associated with climate change.                                   [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain the impacts of climate change on organisms and ecosystems, other than those already mentioned.        [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phenology is the study of the timing of biological events    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phenological events refer to recurring, seasonal events or stages in the life cycles of organisms that are influenced by environmental factors  [1 mark]\n\n    **AND** any **one** named example with **one** correct impact of climate changefrom  the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Arctic mouse-ear chickweed **OR** *Cerastiun arcticun*    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ spring growth is advancing **OR** grows earlier in the year due to a warmer climate     [1 mark]\n\n    *Reindeer* \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reindeer **OR** *Rangifer tarandus*  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ migration  is advancing **OR** occurs earlier due to reduced snow cover **OR** warmer temperatures                         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ not enough food as increased temperature means growth of mouse-ear chickweed OR vegetation **OR** plants is out of synch with migration patterns [1 mark]\n\n    *Great tits* \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ great tits **OR** *Parus major*  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ breeding earlier **OR** laying eggs earlier due to earlier peak mass of caterpillars as a result of warmer temperatures         [1 mark]\n\n    *Bees* \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Bees **OR** *Apidae* sp. [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ higher temperatures mean they break hibernation early before there are enough flowers open to provide food [1 mark]\n\n    *Spruce bark beetle* \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ spruce bark beetle **OR** *Ips typographus* **OR** *Dendroctonus micans* [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ higher temperatures increase number of life cycles per year [1 mark]\n\n    *Any other correct example* \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ correctly named organism  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ example of disruption to phenological events due to temperature **OR** photoperiod            [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Phenology is the study of the **timing** of biological events which are **affected by environmental changes** ($\\checkmark$). Climate change can disrupt the synchronisation of events such as the arrival of **migrating reindeer** ($\\checkmark$)  to their **breeding ground** which **arrive earlier** due to **higher temperatures** ($\\checkmark$); the earlier availability of vegetation due to **higher temperatures** means **less food** is available ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following (*answer must include reference to anthropogenic causes and positive feedback cycles to gain full marks*):\n\n    ***Anthropogenic Causes*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ combustion of fossil fuels **OR** anthropogenic increase in carbon dioxide concentrations     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ land use change **OR** deforestation **OR** increased cattle farming [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ many industrial processes release greenhouse gases, contributing to climate changes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ landfills **OR** waste treatment facilities generate methane as organic waste decomposes in anaerobic conditions         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ urbanisation as urban areas generate heat **OR** urban heat island effect, contributes to localised temperature increases and can impact local weather patterns     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the destruction of natural carbon sinks **OR** forests **OR** wetlands **OR** peatlands, reduces the Earth's ability to absorb carbon dioxide [1 mark]\n\n    ***Positive Feedback Cycles*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ocean temperatures increase and they release stored carbon dioxide into the atmosphere. This release of CO:sub[2] amplifies the greenhouse effect     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is a loss of snow **OR** ice cover **OR** arctic ice **OR** glaciers **OR** arctic ice-albedo feedback leading to an increase in the absorption of solar radiation, which contributes to global warming **OR** as polar ice sheets melt due to warming temperatures, sea levels rise, which contributes to further melting of ice sheets          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased rate of peat **OR** undecomposed organic matter decomposition in permafrost, which releases CO:sub[2] into the atmosphere, further enhancing the greenhouse effect          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ methane is released into the atmosphere from melting permafrost, which further enhances the greenhouse effect       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased incidence of forest fires, which release carbon dioxide into the atmosphere, which further enhances the greenhouse effect, **OR** there is an increased incidence of droughts, which can increase the chance of forest fires, which release carbon dioxide into the atmosphere when they burn    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the air becomes warmer, which can hold more water vapour, a greenhouse gas, contributing to further warming [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The major anthropogenic causes of climate change are the **combustion of fossil fuels** ($\\checkmark$) and the change in land use, such as **deforestation** ($\\checkmark$), to clear land for cattle farming. Positive feedback cycles that enhance climate change include the release of **carbon dioxide** from the oceans due to increasing ocean temperatures ($\\checkmark$), the increase in the rate of **decomposition of peat** leading to the release of carbon dioxide into the atmosphere ($\\checkmark$), and the melting of permafrost which releases **methane** into the atmosphere ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ climate change is the long-term change in weather patterns    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased temperatures **OR** reduced snowfall increases the incidence of drought **OR** forest fires in boreal forests **OR** taiga    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **WHICH** reduces primary productivity (in boreal forests **OR** taiga) [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased temperatures lead to the melting of land-fast ice and sea ice [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **WHICH** reduces the breeding grounds of emperor penguins (*Aptenodytes forsteri*) **OR** habitat for walruses (*Odobenus rosmarus*)     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased surface water temperatures can prevent nutrient upwelling     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **WHICH** reduces ocean primary productivity **OR** energy flow [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased global average temperatures can affect the distribution of habitats **OR** ecosystems **OR** communities             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **WHICH** could lead to an upslope shift for montane bird species **OR** range contraction **OR** northward spread of tree species found in North America     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in carbon dioxide concentrations causes ocean acidification     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **WHICH** can lead to the suppression of calcification in corals [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in ocean temperatures can cause coral bleaching     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **WHICH** can lead to the reef ecosystems collapsing [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ shifts in the geographical distribution of species could lead to species disappearing from previously inhabited areas **OR** local extinctions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ species might struggle to adapt to rapidly changing conditions, leading to decreased biodiversity [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rising sea levels threaten coastal habitats        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ expanding range of disease vectors enabling spread of diseases    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased temperatures **OR** droughts can lead to more frequent forest fires **OR** tree diseases  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reduced water availability can lead to reduced habitat for aquatic organisms    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ecosystem changes can affect human communities that rely on them for resources     [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Climate change is the long-term change in **weather patterns** ($\\checkmark$), which can have different impacts on different ecosystems. In boreal forests, increasing temperatures leads to an increased incidence of **drought** and forest fires ($\\checkmark$). This leads to a reduction in **primary productivity** ($\\checkmark$) and a net carbon loss due to the combustion of carbon compounds. Increased temperatures also have an impact on habitats such as sea ice as they reduce the coverage of sea ice because it **melts** ($\\checkmark$), which **reduces the available habitat** for organisms such as the walrus ($\\checkmark$). Increased temperatures also warm the surface of the ocean waters, which can cause **coral bleaching** ($\\checkmark$) and prevent **nutrient upwelling** ($\\checkmark$). A reduction in available nutrients near the surface can reduce the **primary productivity** of ocean ecosystems ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1118","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1118\n\nThe spruce bark beetle (*Ips typographus*) is an invasive pest species of spruce and pine trees in Europe. They are one of many species that are impacted by climate change. Females burrow through the bark into phloem tissue, where they lay eggs. Once hatched, the developing larvae feed on phloem sap and burrow through the bark as they emerge as adults.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Climate%20Change/images/BI1118.png\" alt=\"BI1118\">\n[Source: San Martin, G. (2019).*File: Ips typographus (female).jpg*.\n Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved August 18, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Ips_typographus_%28female%29.jpg.Copyright under CC BY-SA 2.0\n]\n\n1.\n    1. Describe the impact of climate change on the spruce bark beetle (*Ips typographus*). [2 marks]\n\n    2. Explain how climate change can lead to the evolution of a species.    [3 marks]\n\n    3. Climate change can impact the timing of biological events. State the term used to describe the study of these events.     [1 mark]\n\n    4. Outline a **named** method of carbon sequestration that can be employed to mitigate the impacts of climate change on organisms and ecosystems.    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. **BOTH** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ climate change leads to an increase in temperature **OR** warmer summers and winters    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in the number of spruce bark beetle life cycles per year **OR** reproductive cycles **OR** increase in the spruce bark beetle population **OR** number of outbreaks of spruce bark beetle **OR** increased infestations **OR** extend the period during which spruce bark beetles are active **OR** less severe winters mean beetles have longer to feed, reproduce, and lay eggs [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Climate change can increase the **temperature** in some ecosystems, leading to warmer summers and winters ($\\checkmark$). As spruce bark beetles are sensitive to cold temperatures, warmer temperatures mean they can increase the number of **life cycles** per year ($\\checkmark$). This would lead to an increased population of spruce bark beetles or more outbreaks per year.\n\n1.\n    2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ new environmental conditions **OR** changes in temperature **OR** precipitation **OR** habitat availability cause selective pressure **OR** favour individuals within a species that are better adapted [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ individuals with alleles providing advantageous characteristics are more likely to survive, reproduce, and pass on their advantageous traits to their offspring\nover generations, these adaptive traits become more common in the population, leading to evolutionary changes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ climate change can exert rapid and strong selective pressures, leading to relatively quick evolutionary responses in some species [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ significant shifts in habitats **OR** geographic ranges can create isolated populations, leading to speciation [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **WHICH** can lead to changes in behaviour, such as shifts in migration patterns, breeding times, or feeding behaviours and over time, these can influence the genetic makeup of a population [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Climate change can lead to new environmental conditions, creating new **selective pressures** ($\\checkmark$). Individuals better adapted to these new conditions are more likely to survive, reproduce and pass on their **advantageous traits** to their offspring ($\\checkmark$). Over many generations, these traits become more common in the population, which leads to **evolutionary changes** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    3.\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phenology         [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Phenology** ($\\checkmark$) is the study of the timing of biological events.\n\n1.\n    4.\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ named technique, e.g. afforestation, forest regeneration, and peat-forming wetland restoration   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ description of the technique, e.g. afforestation is the process where trees are planted in deforested areas  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ impact on carbon concentration, e.g. act as carbon sinks, reducing atmospheric carbon dioxide concentrations         [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Afforestation** ($\\checkmark$) and **forest regeneration** ($\\checkmark$) are two techniques that can be used to sequester carbon, which can help mitigate the impacts of climate change. Afforestation is when trees are planted in areas that have suffered from deforestation. The newly planted trees act as **carbon sinks** ($\\checkmark$), which can help to reduce atmospheric carbon dioxide levels. Forest regeneration is somewhat similar to afforestation, but trees were removed due to forest fires or disease. These trees are replaced with new trees and also act as **carbon sinks** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1119","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1119\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe tawny owl (*Strix aluco*) has two colour variants, a brown form and a grey/white form. Studies have shown that the grey/white form survives better during winter periods, with heavy snow cover.\n\n|<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Climate%20Change/images/BI1119ai.png\" alt=\"BI1119ai\"> | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Climate%20Change/images/BI1119bi.png\" alt=\"BI1119bi\"> |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| [Source: Trimming, P. (2011). File: Tawny Owl (5946073610).jpg. Wikimediacommons Retrieved August 17, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tawny_Owl_(5946073610).jpg. Copyright under CC BY 2.0] | [Source: Tambako the Jaguar (2023). Tawny Owl. Flickr. Retrieved August 17, 2023, from https://www.flickr.com/photos/tambako/52870363361/in/photostream/. Copyright under CC BY-ND 2.0] |\n\n1.\n    1. Outline the impact of global warming on tawny owl colour variant fitness. [3 marks]\n\n    2. Describe two positive feedback cycles associated with global warming.  [2 marks]\n\n2.\n    1. Define the term phenology.     [1 mark]\n\n    2. Using a named species, describe the impact climate change can have on the timing of phenological events.     [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ global warming is the increase in the average global temperature    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the increase in global average temperatures leads to changing weather patterns **OR** climate change         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ changes in climate lead to changes in snow cover **OR** reduced snow cover (in the tawny owls' environment)             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tawny owl brown variant has a selective advantage **OR** is fitter **OR** is better adapted to reduction in snow **OR** is better camouflaged in habitats with less snow **OR** vice versa             [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Global warming refers to the increase in the Earth's global **average temperature** ($\\checkmark$). This increase in temperature changes the long-term **weather patterns** of different climates and environments ($\\checkmark$). This could lead to a **reduction** in the snow cover of the tawny owl's habitat ($\\checkmark$). This means that the grey/white colour variant isn’t as well camouflaged, and therefore, the brown variant has a **selective advantage** ($\\checkmark$) and is more fit to survive and reproduce.\n\n1.\n    2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as ocean temperatures increase, they release stored carbon dioxide into the atmosphere **WHICH** amplifies greenhouse effect **OR** environmental temperature [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ loss of snow **OR** ice cover **OR** arctic ice **OR** glaciers **OR** arctic ice-albedo feedback leading to an increase in the absorption of solar radiation **WHICH** contributes to global warming **OR** sea levels rise **WHICH** causes further melting  of ice sheets [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased rate of peat **OR** undecomposed organic matter decomposition in permafrost releases more CO:sub[2] into the atmosphere **WHICH** increases greenhouse effect **OR** environmental temperature                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ methane is released into the atmosphere from melting permafrost **WHICH** increases greenhouse effect **OR** environmental temperature     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased incidence of forest fires releasing carbon dioxide into the atmosphere **WHICH** increases greenhouse effect **OR** increases temperatures further **OR**  increased incidence of droughts increase the chance of forest fires which release carbon dioxide into the atmosphere when they burn **WHICH** increases greenhouse effect **OR** environmental temperature    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as the air becomes warmer, it can hold more water vapour **WHICH** is a greenhouse gas **OR** increases greenhouse effect **OR** contributes  to further warming [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Because of global warming, the temperature of the oceans has increased. **Warmer oceans** have a lower capacity for dissolved gases, so they **release** stored **carbon dioxide** into the atmosphere, which further enhances the greenhouse effect ($\\checkmark$). **Warmer oceans** due to global warming also **melt arctic ice**, which reduces the Earth’s **albedo**. This leads to an increase in the **absorption of solar radiation** and enhances global warming ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    1.\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phenology is the term used to describe research into the timing of biological events      [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Phenology is research into the **timing of biological events** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    2.\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ named example, e.g. reindeer **OR** *Rangifer tarandus* [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ correct impact of climate change on phenological event, e.g. climate change leads to increased snow cover in some regions, which changes the migration patterns of reindeer     [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Reindeer** ($\\checkmark$) can be affected by climate change because changes in **snow cover** as a result of climate change can lead to a change in their **migration patterns** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1168","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1168\n\nThe graph shows trends in atmospheric CO:sub[2] concentrations in Mauna Loa. What conclusions can be made from this graph?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Climate%20Change/images/BI1168.png\" alt=\"BI1168\">\n[Source: Adapted from Semhur S (n.d).*File: Mauna Loa carbon dioxide-en svg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved April 27.2022. from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Mauna_Loa_Carbon_Dioxide-en.svg. Copyright-free]\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | The concentration of carbon dioxide is always highest in March |\n    | II. | Increasing carbon dioxide concentration is directly correlated with climate change |\n    | III. | Since 1960, the atmospheric carbon dioxide concentration has increased in Mauna Loa |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe data shows that since 1960, the atmospheric carbon dioxide concentration has increased in Mauna Loa (**Statement III**); **Choice C**  is correct.\n\nThe graph for the annual fluctuations of carbon dioxide concentration shows the carbon dioxide concentrations are highest in May (**Statement I**); **Choices A** and **D**  are incorrect. The graph does not refer to climate change, therefore, it cannot be concluded that increasing carbon dioxide concentration is correlated with climate change (**Statement II**); **Choice B**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1389","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1389\n\nThe following graph shows how the pH of seawater, obtained from Aloha station in Hawaii, has changed between 1988 and 2021. What could account for the decrease in pH?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Climate%20Change/images/BI1389.png\" alt=\"BI1389\">\n[Source: University of Hawaii (2021, April 16). *Conserve and sustainably use the oceans, seas, and marine resources*. Our World in Data. Retrieved December 30, 2023, from https://ourworldindata.org/sdgs/life-below-water#targets. Copyright BY SA]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increased ocean salinity\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increased ocean temperatures\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increased atmospheric carbon dioxide\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increased sea level","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because increased atmospheric carbon dioxide leads to increased diffusion of $\\ce{CO_2}$ into seawater. When $\\ce{CO_2}$ dissolves in seawater, it reacts with water molecules to form carbonic acid.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect since increased salinity will generally lead to an increase in pH rather than a reduction. **Choice B** is incorrect since pH is largely unaffected by ocean temperature. **Choice D** is incorrect since sea level changes are unlikely to have an effect on pH."},{"ref":"BI1427","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1427\n\nWhich of the following is **NOT** a source of methane?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Decomposition of organic matter in landfill sites\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Digestive systems of ruminants\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Combustion of fossil fuels\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Melting permafrost","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because the combustion of fossil fuels produces carbon dioxide and water, not methane. Methane is consumed during combustion rather than released. Although methane can be emitted during the extraction, transportation, and storage of fossil fuels (particularly natural gas), it is not a product of the combustion process itself.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because organic matter decomposes anaerobically in landfill sites, producing methane as a by-product of microbial activity.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because ruminants such as cattle produce methane during digestion through enteric fermentation carried out by microorganisms in the rumen.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because thawing permafrost can release methane that has been trapped in frozen soils and can also allow anaerobic decomposition of organic matter, both of which contribute methane to the atmosphere."},{"ref":"BI1536","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1536\n\nWhich of the following are **NOT** associated with the tipping point from net carbon storage to net release in boreal forests?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1.6em}$ The influence of higher temperatures and reduced winter snow cover on the increased occurrence and severity of wildfires.\n\n- II. $\\hspace{1.3em}$ Increased incidence of drought and decrease in the growth rate of forest vegetation in taiga, leading to forest browning.\n\n- III. $\\hspace{1em}$ The accelerated growth of tropical forests as a result of higher levels of atmospheric carbon dioxide.\n\n- IV. $\\hspace{1em}$ The combustion of stored organic carbon due to more frequent and severe wildfires in boreal forests.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and IV only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II, III and IV only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation:}}$\\\n\nHigher temperatures and decreased winter snowfall lead to drier conditions, increasing the frequency and severity of wildfires. These fires contribute to a net carbon loss as trees, which normally act as carbon sinks, are destroyed.  **Statement I** is, therefore, a true statement.\n\nWarmer temperatures and reduced precipitation, leading to drought conditions. Drought conditions reduce forest vegetation growth, causing forest browning, which weakens the ability of the forest to absorb and store carbon. This contributes to the shift from net carbon storage to net carbon release. **Statement II** is, therefore, a true statement.\n\nIncreased frequency and intensity of wildfires result in the burning of stored carbon in the form of dead wood, leaf litter, and soil organic matter. This combustion releases significant amounts of carbon dioxide back into the atmosphere, increasing the shift from carbon sink to carbon source in these forests. **Statement IV** is, therefore, a true statement.\n\n**Statement III** describes the accelerated growth of tropical forests due to higher CO₂ levels, which has **NO** direct impact on the tipping point of boreal forests.\n\n**Choice B** is therefore correct as it includes the only statement of conditions **NOT** associated with the tipping point of net carbon storage to net carbon release in boreal forests.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are incorrect as they all include statements about conditions that **ARE** associated with the tipping point."},{"ref":"BI1576","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1576\n\nThe photo shows a coral reef affected by coral bleaching.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Climate%20Change/images/BI1576.png\" alt=\"BI1576\">\n[Source: Llaguno, S. (n.d.). *Blue-Green Chromis around bleaching coral*. Dreamstime.com. Retrieved April 24, 2024, from https://www.dreamstime.com/stock-photo-blue-green-chromis-around-bleaching-coral-image94425527. Royalty free image]\n\nWhat conditions cause stress to the coral that could lead to reef bleaching?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Overfishing and ocean acidification\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Overfishing and invasive species\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Climate change and ocean acidification\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Climate change and invasive species","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because both rising temperatures due to climate change and ocean acidification from increased atmospheric carbon dioxide stress corals. When temperatures become too high, corals expel the zooxanthellae living within their tissues, which provide them with colour and essential nutrients through photosynthesis. This expulsion causes coral bleaching, turning corals white and potentially leading to their death if the stress continues. At the same time, increased carbon dioxide diffuses into the ocean, forming carbonic acid and lowering ocean pH. This process weakens coral skeletons by reducing the availability of carbonate ions needed for calcium carbonate formation. The additional stress from acidification further compromises coral health and structural integrity, making them more susceptible to damage and bleaching.\n\nAlthough overfishing and invasive species are significant problems, they do not contribute to coral bleaching. **Choices A, B**, and **D** are incorrect because they contain one or more of these processes."},{"ref":"BI1623","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"?","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1623\n\nWhich of the following are the **three** major carbon sinks in a boreal forest?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n\n    | | |\n\n    | :-- | :-- |\n\n    | I. | Peat deposits |\n\n    | II. | Forest biomass |\n\n    | III. | Lake water |\n\n    | IV. | Soils |\n\n| $\\text{A.}$ $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\text{II, III and IV}$ $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n| --- | :-- |\n\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ I, III and IV $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and IV $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III $\\hspace{1em}$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\n**Statements I, II** and **IV** are all important carbon sinks. \n\nPeat deposits (**Statement I**) accumulate partially decayed organic matter in waterlogged conditions. They are significant carbon sinks because the anaerobic conditions slow down the decomposition process, allowing carbon to be stored for long periods.\n\nForest biomass (**Statement II**) refers to the total mass of all living plants and trees in the forest. Plants absorb carbon dioxide from the atmosphere (for photosynthesis) and form organic compounds, which are stored in their biomass. Thus, forests are important carbon sinks.\n\nSoils (**Statement IV**), rather like peat deposits, contain dead and decaying organic matter (leaf litter and humus), microorganisms such as fungi and bacteria and small animals such as worms and soil insects, all of which add up to a large carbon sink held in the soil. The low temperatures in boreal regions slow down the rate of decomposition, allowing carbon to be stored for long periods. \n\n**Choice C** is, therefore, the correct answer to this question.\n\nWhile boreal forests are characterised by numerous lakes (**Statement III**), they are not considered major carbon sinks. Lakes can store carbon in the form of dissolved carbon dioxide, organic carbon (like plant debris), and sediments that accumulate at the bottom. However, the amount of carbon stored is relatively small compared to other components of the boreal forest ecosystem.\\\n\n**Choices A, B,** and **D** are incorrect as they all include **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI1624","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1624\n\nThe emperor penguin (*Aptenodytes forsteri*) lives and breeds on land-fast ice in Antarctica. Their primary diet is fish and crustaceans caught in the nearby ocean. Satellite imagery has been used to count their populations accurately. The Antarctic population declined from 252,000 in 2009 to 226,000 in 2018.\n\nThe following factors are thought to have contributed to this population decline.\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n\n    | | |\n\n    | :-- | :-- |\n\n    | I. | Increased competition from other penguin species |\n\n    | II. | More difficulty accessing their food sources |\n\n    | III. | Melting of land-fast ice and consequent loss of habitat |\n\nWhich of the following gives a rank order of the likely level of impact (highest to lowest) of each of these factors on the decline of the population?\n\n$\\hspace{3em}$ Highest impact $\\to$ lowest impact\n\n| | |\n\n| :-- | :-- |\n\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | III $\\to$ II $\\to$ I |\n\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | I $\\to$ III $\\to$ II |\n\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | II $\\to$ I $\\to$ III  |\n\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | III $\\to$ I $\\to$ II |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\n**Statement III** is the most impactful factor because emperor penguins use land-fast ice (ice fastened to the coast) for breeding and raising their chicks. Loss of the ice due to melting deprives them of their important breeding grounds, forcing them to seek land-based areas that are inherently more hazardous due to predation.\n\n**Statement II** has a moderate impact as changes in sea ice can also affect the availability and accessibility of fish and crustaceans, their primary diet. Difficulties accessing food due to ice melt or changes in ocean currents can lead to decreased survival and breeding success.\n\n**Statement I** has the lowest impact. While competition from other penguin species could have some impact, emperor penguins have unique breeding and feeding habits compared to other species. Therefore, this factor will likely have a lower impact than habitat loss and food accessibility issues.\n\nTherefore, the order is **Statement III → II → I**, making **Choice A** the correct answer to the question."},{"ref":"BI1625","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"?","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1625\n\nWhich set of conditions gives the best combination of nutrients for oceanic food chains?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" }\n\n    | | $\\text{Temperature of deep water}$ | $\\text{Salinity of water}$ | $\\text{Direction of movement of water}$ |\n\n    | :-- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n    | A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Cold | Low  | Down |\n\n    | B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Cold | High | Up |\n\n    | C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Warm | High | Down |\n\n    | D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Warm | Low  | Up |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\n**Choice B** is correct because the ideal combination is for cold water with high salinity to move upwards in the process of upwelling. Generally, cold water is more nutrient-rich because it can hold more dissolved gases, including oxygen and nutrients, which are crucial for marine life. Water with high salinity tends to be denser and so often sinks, bringing nutrients from the surface down to deeper layers. The movement of water upwards, known as upwelling, will bring the nutrients from the deep ocean to the surface. These nutrients often come from decaying carcasses of dead sea organisms or minerals that enter the water from hydrothermal vents on the deep ocean floor. These nutrients are used by phytoplankton in photosynthesis, which act as producers for oceanic food chains. \n\n**Choices A** and **C** are incorrect because nutrients need to be channelled upwards towards the surface of the ocean by water movement. This enables photosynthetic organisms to utilise them. \n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because cold water is better than warm water for nutrient supply."},{"ref":"BI1626","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1626\n\nWhich of the following statements is/are example(s) of poleward range shifts of species associated with climate change?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n\n    | | |\n\n    | :-- | :-- |\n\n    | I. | A species of butterfly native to North Africa appearing in Southern Europe and becoming less abundant in Africa. |\n\n    | II. | Expansion of a species’s active latitude range from 45 $-$ 60 $\\text{\\textdegree}$N to 40 $-$ 65 $\\text{\\textdegree}$N. |\n\n    | III. | A mammal normally found at an altitude range of 500 $-$ 600 m above sea level, occupying ground at 800 m above sea level. |\n\n| $\\text{A.}$ $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\text{I only}$ $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n| --- | :-: |\n\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III $\\hspace{1em}$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\n**Statement I** is true because the active range of this butterfly species has moved northward towards the North Pole, which is a clear example of a poleward range shift. The information about the species becoming less abundant in Africa suggests that climate change has made conditions in Africa less habitable for this species. As a result, the butterfly has migrated to find its niche further north in Europe. **Choice A** is correct because **Statement I** is the only true statement.\n\n**Statement II** is not true because this species' active latitude range has widened, but not only in a poleward direction; it has also moved equatorward (towards latitude 40$\\text{\\textdegree}$N), so this is not an example of a poleward range shift. There are many reasons why a population may be able to spread in this way, such as an improved availability of food and habitat. \n\n**Statement III** is not true because it is an example of an upslope range shift, not a poleward one. For this question, it is important to understand the difference between the two similar words, “altitude” (a place’s vertical height above sea level) and “latitude” (a place’s angular distance from the equator). \n\n**Choices B, C** and **D** are incorrect because they all include one or more untrue statements."},{"ref":"BI1627","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1627\n\nThe image shows an example of silvopasture, which integrates trees and plants with the grazing and foraging of domesticated animals.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Climate%20Change/images/BI01627%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI01627 fig1\">\n\n[Source:  United States Department of Agriculture, USDA NRCS Texas, ”Cattle grazing in a silvopasture”.  Retrieved May 2024, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Cattle_grazing_in_a_silvopasture._(25112780735).jpg, Copyright-free]\n\nIt is claimed that pastures with trees can sequester 5 to 10 times as much carbon as an equivalent-sized piece of treeless pastureland. \n\nWhich of the following statements could be claimed as additional benefits of silvopasture?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n\n    | | |\n\n    | :-- | :-- |\n\n    | I. | Greater biodiversity in the silvopasture than in traditional treeless pastureland |\n\n    | II. | Ability to plant any kind of tree in a silvopasture |\n\n    | III. | Greater combined productivity from silvopasture |\n\n    | IV. | Protection of livestock animals from wind and intense sunlight |\n\n| $\\text{A.}$ $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\text{I, II and IV}$ $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n| --- | :-: |\n\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ I and IV $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ I, III and IV $\\hspace{1em}$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\n**Choice D** is correct because **Statement II** is the only untrue statement.\n\n**Statement I** is true because trees in silvopasture systems significantly increase biodiversity. Trees provide habitats for various species, such as insects, birds, and small mammals. Additionally, tree shade creates microclimates that support a wider range of ground-level plants and grasses. This diversity in flora and fauna enhances the overall ecosystem biodiversity. \n\n**Statement II** is untrue because not all tree species will be suitable. Livestock are likely to feed on certain species of trees, especially young saplings. Care should be taken to select trees of the right species and manage those trees through their early growth with techniques such as ring-fencing and pollarding.  \n\n**Statement III** is true because the greater productivity will be proportional to the increased carbon sequestration by photosynthesis. Silvopasture systems combine the productivity of trees, forage, and livestock. Trees contribute to carbon sequestration through photosynthesis and can produce timber, fruits, or nuts. The forage provides food for livestock, and integrating these components can lead to higher overall productivity compared to a single-use system.\n\n**Statement IV** is true because trees can shade livestock and protect them from the worst excesses of wind and rain. This can reduce instances of wind chill and heatstroke, thereby improving the animals’ mortality."},{"ref":"BI1628","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1628\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\n1.  The table contains data about the storage of carbon within different biomes globally.\n\n    | | Global carbon storage   | / kg of carbon | per m$^2$ |\n\n    | --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n    | Biome                     | Vegetation | Soil | Total |\n\n    | Boreal forests            | 6 | 34 | 40 |\n\n    | Croplands                 | 0 | 8 | 8 |\n\n    | Deserts and semideserts   | 0 | 4 | 4 |\n\n    | Grasslands/savannahs      | 2 | 7 | 9 |\n\n    | Temperate forests         | 6 | 10 | 16 |\n\n    | Tropical forests          | 12 | 12 | 24 |\n\n    | Tundra                    | 1| 13 | 14 |\n\n    | Wetlands                  | 4| 64 | 68 |\n\n    | Total                     | **31** | **152** | **183** |\n\n    1.  Boreal forests are in danger of passing their carbon storage tipping point. Using the data in the table, predict which **other** biome is at the highest risk of passing its carbon storage tipping point. [1 mark]\n\n    2.  Explain your choice in part (a) (i). [2 marks]\n\n2.  Climate change is believed to be the cause of the phenomenon of forest browning in some heavily wooded areas of central Europe. \n\n    1.  Outline the direct link between climate change and forest browning. [1 mark]\n\n    2.  Suggest **two** consequences of forest browning on the organisms in woodland ecosystems. [2 marks]\n\n3.  Place the following five events into chronological order.\n\n    - A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Phytoplankton photosynthesise and act as producers in the ocean food chain.\n\n    - B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cold water from the deep ocean moves upwards.\n\n    - C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Fish stocks increase.\n\n    - D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The rotation of the Earth and the action of winds drive surface water away from coastal areas.\n\n    - E.$\\hspace{1em}$ Nutrients are transported from nutrient-rich waters to more nutrient-poor waters.\n\n    | $\\text{Event order}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\text{1st}$ $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\text{2nd}$ $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\text{3rd}$ $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\text{4th}$ $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\text{5th}$ $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n    | --- | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n    | Letter | | | | | |\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n4.  Certain species that inhabit the slopes of mountains in tropical areas have been said to be ‘riding an elevator to extinction’.\\\n\nUse your knowledge of climate change to discuss the implications of this statement.  [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  \n\n    1.  $\\ce{→}$ wetlands [1 mark]\n\n        [1 mark]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n        **Wetlands** are most likely to reach their tipping point ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    2.  any **two** of the following:\\\n\n        $\\ce{→}$ the difference between the mass **OR** amount **OR** kg of carbon in soil and vegetation **OR** below and above ground is the largest, **OR** there is a large gap between carbon in soil and carbon in vegetation        [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{→}$ as vegetation is lost to drainage for agriculture **OR** land clearance **OR** peat extraction    [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{→}$ this will lead to wetlands becoming a carbon source, not a sink **OR** a net loss of carbon from the biome        [1 mark]\n\n        [2 marks]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n        The **difference** between the mass of carbon held **in** the **soil** and the mass held above ground in the form of **vegetation** is the **largest** of any of the biomes ($\\checkmark$). Wetlands are under pressure from **agriculture, peat extraction**, and **climate change-induced drying**, all of which can **release large masses** of carbon. The **high mass** of **soil carbon** combined with the susceptibility to **water level changes** and **warming** puts wetlands at a high risk of surpassing their carbon storage tipping point, i.e. **becoming a net carbon source** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.  \n\n    1.  any **one** of the following:\\\n\n        $\\ce{→}$ increased temperatures **OR** rising global temperatures **OR** prolonged drought conditions **OR** reduced water availability CAUSE withering **OR** wilting **OR** dying **OR** browning **OR** of leaves **OR** needles      [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{→}$ heavy rainfall can cause waterlogged soil **CAUSES** root damage    [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{→}$ frequent **OR** intense extreme weather events **CAUSE** physical damage to trees, resulting in browning    [1 mark]\n\n        [1 mark]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n        Forest browning occurs in areas where there have been **prolonged periods of drought**, usually over more than one single year and consecutively, which has led to the **wilting of leaves** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    2.  any two of the following:\\\n\n        $\\ce{→}$ forests become dry **OR** desiccated     [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{→}$ so are at greater risk of fire        [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{→}$ trees may become more susceptible to pests        [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{→}$ trees die **OR** tree dieback occurs    [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{→}$ a reduction in biodiversity    [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{→}$ loss of canopy cover can lead to higher ground temperatures, affecting plants and animals that live at ground level    [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{→}$ pathogens can more easily infect and spread among stressed trees    [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{→}$ alteration in forest succession patterns, potentially leading to the dominance of less desirable or invasive species    [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{→}$ loss of habitat OR food sources for animals    [1 mark]\n\n        [2 marks]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n        Forest browning causes leaves to **become desiccated**, leading to a lack of photosynthesis, which causes the **leaves to wither and die** ($\\checkmark$). Dead trees are drier than live ones, so they are at a **greater risk of catching fire** and **spreading wildfires** rapidly ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.  $\\ce{→}$ D, B, E, A, C [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Climate%20Change/images/1628.png\" alt=\"1628\">\n\nThe Earth's rotation and wind patterns cause surface water to move away from the coast **(D)**, which is the initial event that triggers the entire sequence. As surface water is pushed away, it is replaced by cold, nutrient-rich water from the deep ocean in a process known as upwelling **(B)**. The upwelled deep water brings essential nutrients (like nitrates and phosphates) to the surface, enriching the nutrient-poor surface waters **(E)**. The influx of nutrients supports the growth of phytoplankton, which photosynthesise and form the base of the oceanic food chain **(A)**. As phytoplankton populations grow, they provide a food source for zooplankton and small fish, supporting larger fish populations and increasing fish stocks **(C)**.\n\n4.  any **three** of the following (must include at least **one** statement in favour and **one** statement against the phrase)\\\n\n    ***Statements in favour of the phrase:***\\\n\n    $\\ce{→}$ climate change **OR** global warming is causing upslope range shifts **OR** warmer temperatures are pushing certain species to higher altitudes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{→}$ the higher up the mountain, the less available habitat area [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{→}$ higher elevations often have fragmented habitats, so it is harder for species to find areas large enough to support viable populations        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{→}$ upward migration of species can disrupt existing ecological communities and interactions [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{→}$ species that are less competitive **OR** have more specialised habitat requirements may be displaced **OR** outcompeted by more adaptable species, pushing them closer to extinction    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{→}$ changes in precipitation **OR** rain patterns can alter the availability of water resources    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{→}$ lack of niches will prevent the species from reproduction, hence extinction    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{→}$ some evidence exists to support the effect in certain species only        [1 mark]\n\n    ***Statements against the phrase:***\\\n\n    $\\ce{→}$ there is only a small body of evidence **OR** very few examples exist to prove the integrity of the phrase        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{→}$ the species could exist in other countries **OR** mountain ranges so might not necessarily become extinct        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{→}$ some species have adaptive potential to help them survive even as their environment changes **OR** species may evolve quickly enough to cope with changing conditions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{→}$ mountains can have a variety of microclimates where species can find suitable conditions even as the broader climate changes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{→}$ conservation efforts by humans can help mitigate the impacts of climate change    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{→}$ species with mutualistic relationships may be better able to survive    [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n    This phrase illustrates the phenomenon of **upslope range shift**, in which species occupy niches at higher and higher elevations as **climate changes** ($\\checkmark$). As such, it can be likened to an elevator in that the species themselves are getting higher. The higher up the mountain, the **less available habitat area** there is because the circumference of the mountain decreases with altitude. The species will have **reduced availability of locations** that can provide a niche ($\\checkmark$), and so they will become extinct.\\\n\n    In countering the phrase, the species in question **could live perfectly well in another country** or mountain range in which the **effects of climate change are not so severe**; this will not lead to ‘extinction’ because the **species will remain viable** in the more suitable habitat ($\\checkmark$). Also, **conservation** work by humans may have recognised the threat and proved an **alternative habitat** for the species ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2379","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2379\n\nWhich of the following is/are possible consequences of a climate change-induced temporal shift in the reproduction cycle of the great tit (*Parus major*)?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Earlier hatching and fledging of great tit chicks. |\n    | II. | Change of food sources sought by great tit parents to feed their young.|\n    | III. | Extinction. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III\n\n*Note: Starting May 2028, this question may be assessed differently due to changes to the Biology guide.*","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because one of the environmental cues for egg-laying and hatching is temperature. The current climate change trend is for warmer, wetter winters that are likely to give great tit parents earlier cues for spring laying, and hence the eggs may hatch earlier in the season, with earlier fledging times for the juvenile birds.\n\n**Statement II** is true because many species, including great tits, are highly adaptable and can find alternative food sources if their preferred sources are unavailable. For example, when their preferred food sources of caterpillars, ants and grubs might not be present in larger numbers, great tits often switch to beech mast (nut-like fruits of the beech tree) as an alternative food source.\n\n**Statement III** is true because, whilst great tits are an extant species, they might not be able to withstand continued selective pressure from climate change, which could lead to their disappearance from habitats and eventual extinction.\n\n**Choice D** is correct because it contains all three true statements (**I, II** and **III**).\n\n**Choices A, B** and **C** are incorrect because they omit one or more of the true statements."}]},{"name":"DNA Replication","questions":[{"ref":"BI0048","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0048\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nGel electrophoresis is a technique often used to analyse DNA molecules. It includes a gel and an electric field. The diagram below shows the outcome of a gel electrophoresis run.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI0048.png\" alt=\"BI0048\">\n[Source: Adapted from Vedran. (2019, October 30). *Agarose gel*. Agarose Gel | Free SVG. Retrieved May 08, 2022, from https://freesvg.org/agarose-gel. Copyright under CC BY.]\n\n1. DNA is a type of nucleic acid. State one location where DNA is found in cells. [1 mark]\n\n2. Deduce, with a reason, which direction the DNA fragment moves through the gel. [2 marks]\n\n3. Distinguish between the functions of DNA and RNA in unicellular organisms. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n$\\hspace{1.5em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleus **OR** nucleoid **OR** mitochondria **OR** chloroplasts **OR** plasmids [1 mark]\n\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1.5em}$:u[Sample answer]:\n\n$\\hspace{1.5em}$ One place where DNA is found in the cell is in the **nucleus**($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ towards the positive electrode **OR** from negative to positive electrode **OR** downwards [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA is negatively charged **OR** phosphate group of DNA is negatively charged **OR** DNA is an acid [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    DNA stands for deoxyribonucleic acid; therefore, as an acid, it is **negatively charged** ($\\checkmark$). The phosphate group present in nucleotides also gives DNA a negative charge.  In an electric field, opposites attract; therefore, negatively charged DNA would be travelling **towards the positively charged electrode** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA stores genetic information **WHILST** RNA carries this information from DNA to ribosomes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA replicates before cell division so genetic information can be passed to next generation **WHILST** RNA does not replicate, it carries information within the cell, not between generations [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA is used as a template for RNA synthesis, **WHILST** RNA has a variety of functions such as carrying information to the ribosome for translation (any described role for tRNA, rRNA or any other correct function is acceptable) [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA is transcribed **WHILST** RNA is translated [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    DNA is the cell’s long-term **storage of genetic information** and it **replicates** before cell division, to allow it to transmit this information from **one generation to the next**. RNA does not transmit information from one generation to the next but instead carries the genetic information from the **DNA to the ribosomes** ($\\checkmark$) of the **same cell** ($\\checkmark$).  DNA is the **template for RNA** and the RNA is made during **transcription**, This RNA  is then used during the process of **translation** ($\\checkmark$). Translation involves ribosomes, containing **rRNA**, that read the **code**, and **tRNA** which brings in the **amino acids** to form the polypeptide chain ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0088","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0088\n\nWhat is the function of the Polymerase Chain Reaction?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ To show that DNA replication is semi-conservative\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ To determine the sequence of bases in DNA\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ To make many copies of a sample of DNA\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ To incorporate Taq DNA into the sample","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nPCR is used to amplify samples of DNA so they can be used for analysis.\n\nPCR replicates DNA but does not show that replication is semi-conservative (**Choice A**).\n\nPCR does not determine the base sequence of DNA.  It produces copies of short sections of DNA that can then be further analyzed (**Choice B**).\n\nTaq polymerase is an enzyme used in PCR but no Taq DNA is incorporated.  (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0089","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0089\n\nWhich of these answers is true in relation to the polymerase chain reaction?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | High temperatures are used |\n  | II. | It produces exact copies of a segment of DNA |\n  | III. | It uses *Taq* polymerase |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe Polymerase Chain Reaction is used to amplify a  specific DNA segment (**Statement II**). High temperatures are used (**Statement I**)  to separate the two complementary DNA strands during denaturation. A heat-resistant Taq polymerase (**Statement III**) allows replication to proceed at an optimal temperature. **Choice A**  is correct because all three statements are true.\n\n**Choices B, C** and **D** are incorrect as they are missing one or more true statements."},{"ref":"BI0090","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0090\n\nThe diagram shows a DNA profile taken from a crime scene.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI0090-1.png\" alt=\"BI0090-1\">\n\nFollowing are the DNA profiles of the suspects in this crime. Which one is the guilty individual?\n\n| | | | |\n| --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A. | | |B.|\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI0090A.png\" alt=\"BI0090A\">| | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI0090b.png\" alt=\"BI0090b\">|\n|C. | | |D. |\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI0090c.png\" alt=\"BI0090c\"> | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI0090d.png\" alt=\"BI0090d\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDiagram **B** has bands in the same positions as the sample taken from the crime scene.\n\nThe bands in **Choice A**, **Choice C** and **Choice D** are not in the same positions as the DNA from the crime scene. Therefore they are from different people."},{"ref":"BI0096","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0096\n\nWhich row represents the steps of the polymerase chain reaction (PCR) in the correct order?\n\n| | I | II | III | IV |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Sample is heated to 95 ⁰C | Temperature is reduced to 53⁰C | At 72⁰C Taq DNA polymerase produces two double-stranded copies | Primers attach to both strands |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Sample is heated to 95 ⁰C | Temperature is reduced to 53⁰C | Primers attach to both strands | At 72⁰C Taq DNA polymerase produces two double-stranded copies |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Primers attach to both strands | Sample is heated to 95 ⁰C | At 72⁰C Taq DNA polymerase produces two double-stranded copies | Temperature is reduced to 53⁰C |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Primers attach to both strands | At 72⁰C Taq DNA polymerase produces two double-stranded copies | Sample is heated to 95 ⁰C | Temperature is reduced to 53⁰C |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B**  is correct. In PCR, the sample is first heated to approximately 95 °C to separate the two strands of the DNA double helix (denaturation). The temperature is then lowered to around 53 °C so that primers can anneal to complementary sequences on each DNA strand. Finally, the temperature is raised to 72 °C, the optimum temperature for Taq DNA polymerase, which extends the primers by adding nucleotides to synthesise two double-stranded DNA molecules.\n\n**Choices A, C**, and **D** are incorrect because although they contain the correct PCR stages and temperatures, the steps are arranged in the wrong order."},{"ref":"BI0098","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0098\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe image shows a basic principle behind the polymerase chain reaction (PCR). The use of PCR revolutionized the biotechnology industry and opened up possibilities for many applications.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI0098.png\" alt=\"BI0098\">\n[Source: Adapted from Forluvoft, & Leyo. (2009, September 23). *File: DNA simple.svg.* Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved May 13, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: DNA_simple.svg. Copyright-free.]\n\n1. List three purposes for the DNA copies that are produced using PCR. [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline the function of DNA primers in the polymerase chain reaction. [2 marks]\n\n3. Explain the reason for using Taq polymerase in the polymerase chain reaction. [2 marks]\n\n4. Describe one application of the DNA profiles that are produced by PCR and visualized by gel electrophoresis. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ forensics [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ paternity tests [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ testing for genetic diseases [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ human genome project [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ detecting mutations in viral diseases e.g. Covid and HIV [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ any other valid use [1 mark]\n[3 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    The copies of DNA that are produced by PCR are used in **paternity testing** ($\\checkmark$), **forensic analysis** ($\\checkmark$), **analysis of mutations in viruses and bacteria** ($\\checkmark$), and were used in the **Human Genome Project** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ primers attach to the separated strands of DNA [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ primers prevent hydrogen bonds between the separated bases from reforming **OR** prevent original helix from recombining [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ provide a starting point for replication as Taq polymerase attaches the first nucleotide to the 3'-end of the primer [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ primers can be designed to target specific sequences in the genome [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Primers are short sections of DNA that **attach to specific separated strands of DNA** ($\\checkmark$). Primers **stop the original strands from combining** to re-form ($\\checkmark$) the double helix. Primers then serve as the **starting point for replication as DNA polymerase attaches the first nucleotide** ($\\checkmark$) to the 3'-end of the primer.\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it can withstand higher temperatures than human DNA polymerase [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a temperature of 95 $^o$C is necessary in PCR to separate the DNA strands [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in PCR a DNA polymerase is required to synthesize the complementary strands [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Taq polymerase is isolated from thermophilic bacteria [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Taq polymerase is isolated from **thermophilic bacteria** ($\\checkmark$). It is used instead of human DNA polymerase as it is **heat resistant** ($\\checkmark$). This prevents the polymerase from denaturing when the **solution is heated to 95 $^o$C** **to separate strands of DNA** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gel electrophoresis separates DNA fragments based on size to produce a unique pattern of bands [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ PCR is used to amplify alleles with STR sequences [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ each individual has a unique combination of allele lengths with STR sequences [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ unique patterns of bands can be used in paternity testing **OR** forensics [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in **paternity** testing, the pattern of an individual is compared with the patterns of possible  parents [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in **forensics**, the pattern from a crime scene is compared with the patterns of victims and suspects [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in a **paternity** test, half of the child's bands match half of the mother’s bands and half of the child’s bands match half of the father’s bands [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in a forensic investigation,  investigators look for an exact match between all of the bands from the crime scene and the suspect [1 mark]\n[3 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    DNA profiles are used in **forensics** to identify suspects. DNA profiles are made from samples from the **victim, crime scene and suspects**. To create forensic profiles **alleles containing STR sequences are amplified using PCR** ($\\checkmark$). Each individual has a **unique combination** of allele lengths with **STR sequences** ($\\checkmark$) creating a **unique**  “DNA fingerprint”($\\checkmark$). **These profiles are compared** ($\\checkmark$) to identify the profile matches."},{"ref":"BI0317","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0317\n\nTandem repeats are non-coding regions of DNA which are used in DNA profiling. An example of the application of DNA profiling would be in paternity testing. The diagram below shows two chromosomal regions with tandem repeats obtained from a child.\n\n| Locus A\n\n| :-\n\n| :br\n\n| ACTACTACTACT\n\n| :br\n\n| ACTACTACTACTACT\n\n| :br\n\n| **Locus B**\n\n| :br\n\n| GCGCGCGCGCGC\n\n| :br\n\n| GCGCGC\n\n$\\small{\\textrm{[\\copyright\\ 2022]}}$\n\nWhich of the following individuals could be a paternal match to the child?\n\n| | $\\hspace{3em}$ Locus A $\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ Locus B $\\hspace{3em}$ |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ A. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | ACTACTACT | GCGCGC |\n\n| | ACTACTACT | GCGCGC |\n\n| :br\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ B. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | ACTACTACTACT | GCGCGCGC |\n\n| | ACTACTACT | GCGCGCGCGC |\n\n| :br\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ C. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | ACTACTACTACT | GCGCGC |\n\n| | ACTACTACTACT | GCGCGCGCGC |\n\n| :br\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ D. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | ACTACTACT | GCGC |\n\n| | ACTACTACT | GCGCGCGCGC |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nThe sequence of the tandem repeat found in the two alleles of the child are:\\\n\nLocus A - ACT sequence \\\n\nAllele 1 $-$ 4 repeats\\\n\nAllele 2 $-$ 5 repeats\n\nLocus B $-$ GC sequence\\\n\nAllele 1 $-$ 6 repeats\\\n\nAllele 2 $-$ 3 repeats\n\nOne allele came from mother and one from father, potential parents have to be a match for at least one out of two alleles at each locus.\n\nThe closest paternal match to the child would be **Choice C**, because this individual has 4 repeats on locus A and 3 repeats on locus B.\n\n**Choice A** is not a match, because it has 3 repeats on locus A.\\\n\n**Choice B** is not a match, because it has 4 and 5 repeats on locus B.\\\n\n**Choice D** is not a match, because it has 3 repeats on locus A."},{"ref":"BI0319","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0319\n\nWhich event happens in the leading strand but not in the lagging strand?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ DNA polymerase I removes the RNA primer and replaces it with DNA. \n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ DNA polymerase III adds free nucleotides in the direction of the replication fork.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Ligase joins DNA fragments together.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Helicase unwinds the strands by breaking hydrogen bonds.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nOn the leading strand, DNA polymerase III synthesises continuously because DNA polymerase III adds free nucleotides to the 3' end in the direction toward the replication fork. In contrast, on the lagging strand, DNA polymerase III synthesises in short segments (Okazaki fragments) away from the replication fork. **Choice B** is, therefore, the correct answer, as this continuous addition in the same direction as the replication fork occurs only on the leading strand.\n\nRNA primers are initially laid down on both strands to initiate synthesis. DNA polymerase I then removes these RNA primers and replaces them with DNA nucleotides on both strands after synthesis by DNA polymerase III. **Choice A** is incorrect.\n\nAs the lagging strand is synthesised in fragments (Okazaki fragments), DNA ligase is needed to join these fragments together. **Choice C**  is therefore incorrect as it only occurs on the lagging strand.\n\nHelicase unwinds the DNA strands by breaking hydrogen bonds between the base pairs, an essential process that occurs on both the leading and lagging strands. It does not selectively work on only one strand but rather unwinds both in preparation for replication. **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0323","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0323\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe diagram below shows a replication fork which forms during DNA replication.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI0323.png\" alt=\"BI0323\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from LadyofHats Mariana Ruiz. (2014, December). *File: DNA_replication_hy.svg*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved May 22nd, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: DNA_replication_hy.svg. Copyright under CC-0]\n\n1. On the diagram above, label the following structures.\n\n    1. The 5' and 3' ends of both template strands. [1 mark]\n\n    2. With the letter **E**, label the strand in which replication is continuous. [1 mark]\n\n    3. With the letter **P**, label the protein that keeps both strands separated. [1 mark]\n\n2. Compare and contrast the role of DNA polymerase I and DNA primase. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. \n\n    1. $\\hspace{1em}$\n\n        $\\ce{->}$ correct identification of 5' **AND** 3' ends [1 mark]\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI0323ai.png\" alt=\"BI0323ai\">\n\n        [1 mark]\n\n    2. $\\hspace{1em}$\n\n    \n\n        $\\ce{->}$ correct identification of the leading strand (E) [1 mark]\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI0323aii.png\" alt=\"BI0323aii\">\n\n        [1 mark]\n\n    3. $\\hspace{1em}$\n\n    \n\n        $\\ce{->}$ correct identification of single strand binding protein (P) [1 mark]\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI0323aiii.png\" alt=\"BI0323aiii\">\n\n        [1 mark]\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ **BOTH** work on **OR** are involved in building the lagging strand [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ **BOTH** work in a 5' to 3' direction [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ DNA polymerase I removes **OR** replaces RNA primer, **WHEREAS** DNA primase adds **OR** synthsizes, RNA primers [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ DNA polymerase I primarily works on the lagging strand **WHEREAS** DNA primase works on both the lagging and the leading strands [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    DNA polymerase I and DNA primase are enzymes which are both involved in building the **lagging strand** ($\\checkmark$) during DNA replication and they both work in **5' to 3' direction** ($\\checkmark$). However while DNA primase adds **RNA primers**, DNA polymerase I **replaces** ($\\checkmark$) them with DNA."},{"ref":"BI0462","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0462\n\nUnlike the majority of other DNA polymerases, Taq polymerase, which is used in PCR reactions can withstand high temperatures without denaturing. Which of the following is a likely source for Taq polymerase?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Bacteria living in the Arctic\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    Bacteria from the hot springs in Yellowstone National Park\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    Bactweria in Siberia's deep permafrost\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    Bacteria found in the undergrowth of rainforests","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nTaq polymerase comes from Thermus aquaticus, a thermophilic bacterium that thrives in hot springs like those in Yellowstone National Park. Unlike most other DNA polymerases, it can withstand the high temperatures used in PCR (typically around 95°C during denaturation) without denaturing. Therefore, **Choice B** is the correct answer.\n\nBacteria living in the Arctic and bacteria in permafrost are adapted to cold temperatures, not heat; **Choices A and C** are not correct.\nBacteria in rainforest undergrowth live in warm but not extreme high-temperature environments. **Choice D** is  not correct."},{"ref":"BI0466","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0466\n\nWhat distinguishes DNA polymerase from RNA polymerase?\n\n| | DNA polymerase | RNA polymerase |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| Synthesises a new DNA strand in the 5' to 3' direction | Synthesises an RNA strand in the 3' to 5' direction |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Binds thymine-containing nucleotides to adenine-containing nucleotides | Binds uracil-containing nucleotides to adenine-containing nucleotides |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Binds uracil-containing nucleotides to adenine-containing nucleotides | Synthesises an RNA strand in the 3' to 5' direction |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Used in transcription | Used in RNA replication |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDNA contains the bases thymine, guanine, adenine, and cytosine.  RNA contains all of these bases except for thymine, which is replaced by uracil.  Therefore, DNA polymerase adds thymine-containing bases to the growing DNA strands, whereas RNA polymerase adds uracil to growing RNA strands, so **Choice B** is correct.\n\nDNA polymerase is an enzyme that synthesises new DNA strands during DNA replication, whereas RNA polymerase is an enzyme that synthesises RNA strands during transcription; therefore, **Choice D** is incorrect. Both enzymes synthesise in the 5' to 3' direction therefore **Choice A** is incorrect.\n\nDNA polymerase binds thymine to adenine and RNA polymerase synthesises in 5’ to 3’\ndirection therefore **Choice C** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0467","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0467\n\nThe figure below illustrates the results of an experiment based on the methods used by Meselson and Stahl. Initially, bacteria were cultured in a medium containing only the heavy isotope of nitrogen ($^{15}$N) over multiple generations. The DNA extracted from these bacteria formed the band shown in the centrifuge tube labelled as Generation 0. Subsequently, these bacteria were transferred to a medium with only the light isotope of nitrogen ($^{14}$N). After undergoing one round of binary fission, some of the bacteria were removed, their DNA was extracted, and this underwent centrifugation. The results of the centrifugation are shown as Generation 1. The process was repeated after another round of binary fission in the $^{14}$N medium, with the results displayed as Generation 2.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/meselson%20%26%20stahl%20semi-conservative%20centrifugation%20results.png\" alt=\"meselson & stahl semi-conservative centrifugation results\">\n\nWhat evidence in Generation 2 supports the theory that DNA replication is semi-conservative?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ There are two bands; a band for the heavy $^{15}$N and a band for the light $^{14}$N\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ There are two bands; both bands contain half $^{14}$N and half $^{15}$N\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ There are two bands; a band for a hybrid of $^{15}$N - $^{14}$N DNA and a band for the light $^{14}$N molecule\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ There are two bands; both bands only contain $^{14}$N","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThis experiment refers to Meselson and Stahl's investigation that demonstrated that DNA replication is semi-conservative. Based on the diagram, there are two distinct bands in the second generation. The relative weight of this band pattern indicates that DNA replication is semi-conservative.\n\nIf replication were conservative, then the two bands would have consisted of one band with only heavy $^{15}$N and one band with only light $^{14}$N (**Choice A**).\n\nSemi-conservative replication results in two bands where one band contains only light $^{14}$N and the other contains both $^{14}$N and $^{15}$N. The band containing both $^{14}$N and $^{15}$N can be seen in the diagram between $^{14}$N and $^{15}$N (**Choice C**).\n\nIf replication were dispersive, there would be only one band which would consist of both $^{14}$N and $^{15}$N (**Choice B**).\n\nIt is not possible to have two bands containing only $^{14}$N at generation 2 because they would have the same mass which would result in only one band of DNA (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0468","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0468\n\nThe table below shows the percentage of nitrogenous bases found in the DNA of *Escherichia coli* bacteria.\n\n| Adenine | Cytosine | Guanine | Thymine |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| 32\\% | 18\\% | 18\\% | 32\\% |\n\nWhat conclusion can be made based on the data shown in the table above?\n\n-     A. There is complementary base pairing between adenine and thymine, and between cytosine and guanine\n\n-    B. The total amount of cytosine and guanine is less because there are fewer hydrogen bonds between cytosine and guanine\n\n-    C. The bacteria contain a circular chromosome which prevents errors when nitrogenous bases pair up\n\n-    D. The percentage composition of base pairs found in chromosomes and plasmids is different","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe percentage of adenine bases equals that of thymine, and the percentage of guanine equals that of cytosine. These percentages support the rules of complementary base pairing where adenine pairs with thymine and cytosine pairs with guanine.\n\nEven though the total amount of cytosine and guanine is less than the total amount of adenine and thymine, this does not mean that the hydrogen bonds between cytosine and guanine are fewer. There are three hydrogen bonds between cytosine and guanine compared to two hydrogen bonds between adenine and thymine (**Choice B**).\n\nBacterial chromosomes are circular. However, this does not mean that there are fewer errors in base pairing compared to linear chromosomes (**Choice C**).\n\nBacteria are prokaryotes which generally have DNA in the form of a single chromosome, and also plasmids. The base pair rules apply to both forms of DNA, but this cannot be concluded from the table (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0469","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0469\n\nThe diagram below shows the replication of DNA.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI0469.jpeg\" alt=\"BI0469\">\n[Source: Adapted from Version 8.25 Textbook OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology. (2016, May). *File:0323 DNA Replication.jpg*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved May 12, 2022, from https:// commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0323_DNA_Replication.jpg. Copyright under CC-BY 4.0]\n\nWhich of the following are correct functions of structures P, Q and S?\n\n| | $\\hspace{3em}$P$\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$Q$\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$S$\\hspace{3em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| Unwinds and separates the DNA | New strand made in fragments | Adds free nucleotides to the 3' end of the new DNA strand |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Adds free nucleotides to the 5' end of the new DNA strand | The template strand,  running from 3' to 5' | Carries amino acids to the growing strand of DNA |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Carries amino acids to the growing strand of DNA | New sense strand | Adds free nucleotides to the 5' end of the new DNA strand |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Adds free nucleotides to the 3' end of the new DNA strand | New strand running from 5' to 3'| Unwinds and separates the DNA |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nStructure P is DNA polymerase which adds free nucleotides to the 3' end of the DNA strand being produced. Structure Q is the newly synthesized strand which runs (is produced) in the 5' to 3' direction whereas structure S is helicase which unwinds and separates the DNA strand (**Choice A**).\n\nFree nucleotides are not added in the 5' end of the growing strand of DNA as replication runs in the 5' to 3' direction and the strands are antiparallel. Transfer RNA (tRNA) molecules carry amino acids to the ribosome during translation and are not involved in DNA replication (**Choice B**).\n\nThe sense strand of DNA has the same sequence of bases as the RNA strand that has been produced using the template strand (antisense strand) of DNA during transcription (**Choice C**)."},{"ref":"BI0473","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0473\n\nWhich of the following is/are characteristic(s) of Taq polymerase?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Requires a DNA primer\n- II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ Works well at temperatures above 98$\\degree$C\n- III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ Used in gel electrophoresis to fluorescent tag DNA molecules\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nTaq polymerase is used to produce multiple copies of DNA using the polymerase chain reaction (PCR). Taq polymerase requires primers, which are short sequences of single-stranded DNA nucleotides which are added at the start of PCR (**Statement I**).\n\nTaq polymerase is very heat-stable and works well at temperatures as high as 72$\\degree$C but activity significantly declines as temperatures approach 98$\\degree$C (**Statement II**).\n\nTaq polymerase is used in PCR but not in gel electrophoresis. Other molecules are used to add fluorescent tags to DNA in gel electrophoresis (**Statement III**).\\\n**Choice B** includes **Statement III**, so is not correct. **Choices C** and **D** include **Statements II** and **III**, so are not correct."},{"ref":"BI0477","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0477\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe diagram below shows the replication of DNA.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI0477.png\" alt=\"BI0477\">\n[Source: Adapted from Version 8.25 Textbook OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology. (2016, May). *File:0323 DNA Replication.jpg.* Wikimedia commons. Retrieved May 12, 2022, from https:// commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0323_DNA_Replication.jpg. Copyright under CC-BY 4.0]\n\n1. Explain how the characteristics of Taq polymerase make it suitable for use in the polymerase chain reaction (PCR).  [2 marks]\n\n2. Describe the possible consequences for a cell if DNA replication stopped. [2 marks]\n\n3. Outline the roles of helicase and DNA polymerase in DNA replication.      [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.    any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ extremely heat resistant **OR** heat-stable so does not denature at  high temperatures used to separate DNA strands in PCR     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ optimal activity around  70 - 75$\\degree$C allowing it to efficiently add nucleotides during the extension step               [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ does not need to be replenished after each denaturation cycle, making PCR\ncontinuous and efficient\n [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n    Taq polymerase is **thermostable**, so it **does not denature** at the **high temperatures** used to **separate DNA** strands during PCR.  ($\\checkmark$). It also has an **optimal** working **temperature** of around **70–75$\\degree$C**, allowing **efficient** DNA strand extension during the replication phase. ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.    any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell division **OR** mitosis **OR** meiosis would not occur [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell growth would stop because division is needed for growth  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ damaged DNA could not be repaired [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ eventual  loss of tissue maintenance **OR** tissue **OR** organ malfunction **OR** death [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n    If DNA replication stopped, the cell would be **unable** to **divide** because there would be no duplicated genetic material to pass on to daughter cell ($\\checkmark$). .As a result, **tissues** dependent on **cell renewal**, such as skin or blood, would eventually fails ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  helicase unwinds **OR** separates DNA **OR** double-stranded DNA **OR** breaks hydrogen bonds between DNA bases    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA polymerase adds free nucleotides  to form new strand **OR** ensures complementary base pairing using original DNA strand as template [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n    **Helicase unwinds** the double-stranded **DNA**  by **breaking** the **hydrogen bonds** between complementary bases ($\\checkmark$). **DNA polymerase** then **adds free nucleotides** to form a new strand, ensuring c**omplementary base pairing** using the original DNA strand as a template ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0504","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0504\n\nThe diagram shows a DNA profile of a child and the profiles of three potential pairs of parents. Which pair(s) could be the actual parents of the child?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI0504.png\" alt=\"BI0504\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Parents X only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Parents Y only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Parents Y and Z only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Parents X and Z only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA child inherits some of their short tandem repeats (STRs) from their father and some from their mother. So, the DNA profile of a child will contain some bands that align to bands on their father’s DNA profile and some bands that align to bands on their mother’s DNA profile. All bands in the child's profile must come either from the mother or from the father.\n\nThe image below shows that the bands from the DNA profile of the child align with the bands from the DNA profiles of Parents Y.\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI0504A.png\" alt=\"BI0504A\">\n\nOnly Parents Y could be the parents of the child.\nThe top band from the child’s profile is absent in both parents of pair X (**Choices A** and **D**) and Z (**Choices C** and **D**)."},{"ref":"BI0517","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0517\n\nA DNA profile was made using DNA from a crime scene. Locus TH01 was used to identify whether a suspect’s DNA matched the crime scene DNA. The suspect’s two alleles of the gene at Locus TH01 are shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI0517A.png\" alt=\"BI0517A\">\n\nThe alleles TH01-6 and TH01-4 were separated and visualised with gel electrophoresis. The resulting gel, with two bands of DNA, is shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI0517B.png\" alt=\"BI0517B\">\n\nWhat is the DNA in Band B and why is this DNA at this position?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ TH01-4; smaller fragments travel faster and farther in the gel\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ TH01-4; smaller fragments are more attracted to the negative pole\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ TH01-6; larger fragments travel faster and farther in the gel\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ TH01-6; larger fragments are more attracted to the positive pole","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn gel electrophoresis, DNA is loaded into the gel at the negative pole and then an electric current is applied. Since DNA is negatively charged, it migrates through the gel to the positive pole. Shorter strands of DNA can move more quickly and thus travel farther, than longer strands of DNA.\n\nAll DNA fragments are equally attracted to the positive electrode (**Choices B** and **D**). In the gel electrophoresis results, Band B is shown as being closer to the positive pole. So, the DNA that made up Band B travelled farther in the gel than the DNA that made up Band A, meaning the fragment of DNA in Band B is shorter than the fragment in Band A, not larger (**Choice C**).\n\nAccording to the image, TH01-6 has 6 TCAT repeats, while TH01-4 has 4 TCAT fragments. Therefore, TH01-4 is the shorter fragment of DNA. So, the DNA in Band B is TH01-4."},{"ref":"BI0773","marks":"10","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0773\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nBelow is an image showing the results from a paternity test.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI0773A.png\" alt=\"BI0773A\">\n\n1. State the two properties of DNA fragments that allow them to be separated on a gel. [1 mark]\n\n2. Outline the method used in DNA profiling in paternity testing to reach the stage shown in the diagram. [3 marks]\n\n3. Deduce, with a reason, who is most likely to be the father of:\n\n 1. Child A [1 mark]\n\n 2. Child B [1 mark]\n\n4. State one use other than paternity testing for DNA profiling. [1 mark]\n\n5. Draw a circle around the heaviest DNA band in father 4’s sample. [1 mark]\n\n6. Suggest why it is important to analyse the DNA of the mother, as well as the father in paternity testing. [1 mark]\n\n7. State one potential source of error in DNA profiling. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ charge **AND** size [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n DNA fragments are separated by their **charge** and **size** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA sample is obtained from child, alleged biological father and mother [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA is extracted from the sample **OR** separated from other cellular components [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sequences that vary between individuals **OR** VNTRs **OR** variable number of tandem repeats **OR** STRs **OR** short tandem repeats are selected [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA is amplified **OR** copied using PCR [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA is fragmented using restriction endonucleases **OR** enzymes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fragments are separated by charge and size **OR** using gel electrophoresis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ stain the DNA fragments with a dye to make visible [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the gel is observed under UV light to see the DNA bands [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n To create a DNA profile, a **DNA sample is obtained** from the child, alleged biological father and mother ($\\checkmark$), and analysed. **Sequences that vary** a lot between individuals such as **VNTRs** ($\\checkmark$) are selected and amplified using **PCR** ($\\checkmark$). The DNA is then split into **fragments** using **restriction endonucleases** ($\\checkmark$) and the fragments are separated by size and charge, using **gel electrophoresis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 1. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ *Answer must include a reason to gain a mark*\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ father 4 **BECAUSE** their 5th **OR** 6th fragment are unique to child A and them [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI0773ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0773ANSA\">\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ **Father 4** because their **5th and 6th fragments** from the top of the gel, are **unique** to them and Child A ($\\checkmark$). The remaining fragments for Child A either match to their mother’s fragments or are not unique to Child A, so cannot be used to distinguish between fathers.\n\n3.\n 2. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ *Answer must include a reason to gain a mark*\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ father 1 **BECAUSE** their 2nd **OR** 4th **OR** 9th fragment are unique to child B and them [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI0773ANSB.png\" alt=\"BI0773ANSB\">\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ **Father 1** because their **2nd and 4th fragments** from the top of the gel, are **unique** to them and Child B ($\\checkmark$). The remaining fragments for Child B either match to their mother’s fragments or are not unique to Child B, so cannot be used to distinguish between fathers.\n\n4. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ forensic testing [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to study genealogy **OR** anthropology **OR** population genetics [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to identify disaster victims **OR** identification of human remains [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ immigration and citizenship [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ disease diagnosis **OR** tailoring medical treatment [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n To identify **perpetrators** or **victims** at a **crime scene** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the band nearest to the top of the gel for father 4 should be circled [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI0773C.png\" alt=\"BI0773C\">\n\n6. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ half of the DNA fragments in offspring will be shared with the mother **AND** the rest must come from the father [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ helps to identify any potential errors **OR** contamination of samples by comparing child’s and mother’s DNA markers [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ some genetic markers are common in the general population, which can lead to false positives if child’s DNA is not compared to both mother and alleged father [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ father may have similar fragment pattern to another male relative [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n It is important to analyse maternal DNA as well as paternal DNA, in paternity testing as **half** of the DNA fragments in an offspring’s samples will be the **same as the mother** , which will show which **DNA fragments come from the biological father**($\\checkmark$).\n\n7. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ contamination from other sources of DNA [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ degradation of DNA OR fragmentation of DNA **OR** poor storage of DNA leading to damage [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ some genetic markers being more common in the population than expected giving false positives [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n One potential source of error in DNA profiling is **contamination** from other sources of DNA ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0814","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0814\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nData was collected on the protein composition of venom samples taken from the Malayan Krait (*B. candidus*) found in three localities; Malaysia, Indonesia and Thailand. All samples were carefully transported and preserved at temperatures below 20 :sup[o]C. The graph shows the number of different types of protein found in each venom sample.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI0814A.png\" alt=\"BI0814A\">\n[Source: Adapted from Rusmili, M., Othman, L., Abidin, SA., Yusof, FA., Ratanabanangkoon, K., Chanhome, L., Hodgson, W., Chaisakul, J. (2019). *Variations in neurotoxicity and proteome profile of Malayan krait (Bungarus candidus) venoms*. PLoS ONE 14(12): e0227122. Retrieved May 14, 2023, from https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0227122.Copyright-free]\n\n1.\n 1. Identify the independent and dependent variables in this investigation. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Venom samples were collected from different snakes, in different localities. Suggest two controlled variables, other than temperature, that would support the collection of comparable samples. [1 mark]\n\nBelow are the results from gel electrophoresis of one group of venom proteins.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI0814B.png\" alt=\"BI0814B\">\n\n2.\n 1. Identify the group of venom proteins shown. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Explain how proteins are separated using gel electrophoresis. [2 marks]\n\nDifferent mice were envenomed with samples from each of the three localities. It was observed that the venom with the lowest neurotoxicity was from the Malayan krait sampled in Malaysia.\n\n3. Suggest why it was important to transport venom samples at temperatures below 20 :sup[o]C. [2 marks]\n\n4. List two advantages of using mice as model organisms for humans [1 mark]\n\nThe venom samples were further analysed, and it was suggested that the Three Finger Toxins group (3FTx) of proteins contributed significantly to the neurotoxicity of the venom. This prompted a closer analysis of the different types of 3FTx proteins found in each venom sample. The data was presented in a graph.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI0814C.png\" alt=\"BI0814C\">\n[Source: Adapted from Rusmili, M., Othman, L., Abidin, SA., Yusof, FA., Ratanabanangkoon, K., Chanhome, L., Hodgson, W., Chaisakul, J. (2019).*Variations in neurotoxicity and proteome profile of Malayan krait (Bungarus candidus) venoms*. PLoS ONE 14(12): e0227122. Retrieved May 14, 2023, from https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0227122.Copyright-free]\n\n5. Based on the data, suggest which type of 3FTx protein is most likely responsible for differences in the levels of toxicity observed between the different venom samples. Justify your answer. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1. **BOTH** of the following (Students must indicate which is the independent and dependent variable):\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the locality of Malayan krait (independent variable) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ protein composition of venom samples (dependent variable) [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The independent variable is the **locality** of the Malayan krait (Malaysia, Thailand, or Indonesia), and the dependent variable is the **protein composition** of the venom sample ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ samples collected from snakes of similar sizes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ samples collected from snakes of similar ages [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ samples collected from snakes of the same sex [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ samples collected using the same equipment **OR** apparatus [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ samples stored using the same methods [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ samples collected at the same time of day [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ samples collected from snakes in a similar feeding condition **OR** samples not collected from snakes who have recently fed [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n In order to collect valid samples from snakes in different localities, you could collect samples from snakes of a **similar size** ($\\checkmark$) or **age** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ PI **OR** PI proteins [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **PI** proteins ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 2.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ gel electrophoresis separates proteins based on charge **OR** size **OR** molecular weight **OR** conformation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ proteins move toward the positive end of the gel [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ smaller proteins move further **OR** larger proteins move less [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Gel electrophoresis separates proteins based on their **size** ($\\checkmark$). Proteins with a smaller molecular weight will travel **further** than proteins with a heavier molecular weight ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ proteins can denature at high temperatures **OR** temperatures above 20 :sup[o]C may denature the proteins [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ denatured proteins may affect the neurotoxicity **OR** affect the potency of venom [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ venom components may include enzymes, so keeping them at a lower temperature could reduce the degradation of venom [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ prevention of bacterial growth **OR** decomposition [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Proteins **denature** ($\\checkmark$) at high temperatures, which could **impact the neurotoxicity** ($\\checkmark$) of the samples which need to be used in the experiments.\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fewer ethical considerations when using mice [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mice are easier to keep in a lab **OR** require less space **OR** fewer resources **OR** are more cost-effective [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mice reproduce quickly [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ share similar physiology to humans **OR** mice have a similar genome to humans [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mice can be genetically modified to study neurotoxins [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Mice are good model organisms to use for humans in research because there are **fewer ethical considerations** when using them; they also require **less space** ($\\checkmark$) and resources to keep them, so they are more cost-effective.\n\n5. **BOTH** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ non-conventional [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** they are the only type of 3FTx proteins that vary amongst the different localities **OR** because the other types of 3FTx proteins are the same amongst the three localities **OR** samples from Malaysia had the lowest neurotoxicity and the Malaysian samples had the lowest level of non-conventional proteins [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI0814ANS.png\" alt=\"BI0814ANS\">\n\n **Non-conventional proteins** ($\\checkmark$) are most likely the cause of the differences in toxicity because they are the **only type of 3FTx** proteins that vary between the **different localities** ($\\checkmark$). In the Malaysian samples with the lowest neurotoxicity, the levels of non-conventional proteins were also the **lowest between all three localities** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1150","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1150\n\nWhat is the role of Taq polymerase in the PCR process?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Copies DNA segments at a high temperature\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Used to separate the DNA double helix\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Used to cut DNA into fragments for use in electrophoresis\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Attaches DNA primers to target sequences","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe role of Taq polymerase is to produce copies of target DNA sequences. Taq polymerase is an enzyme that functions at 72⁰C during the annealing phase of PCR.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect, as no enzymes are required for this step. Instead, the DNA is heated to 95⁰C to split it into individual strands. **Choice C** is incorrect, as the role of restriction enzymes is to splice DNA, and is not a part of the PCR process. **Choice D** is incorrect, as DNA primers attach to the target region without the need for enzymes."},{"ref":"BI1165","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1165\n\nWhich enzyme involved in DNA replication is matched to its correct function?\n\n| | Enzyme | Function |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Helicase | Unwinds the DNA strand by breaking covalent bonds |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | DNA polymerase | Links the phosphate and sugars of adjacent nucleotides on the new strand |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Ligase | Prevents the DNA strand from becoming tangled  |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | DNA Primase | Sticks newly synthesised DNA fragments together |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDuring DNA replication, one role of DNA polymerase is to link phosphate and deoxyribose sugars of adjacent nucleotides on the newly synthesised DNA  strand; **Choice B**  is correct.\n\nHelicase unwinds the DNA strands by breaking the hydrogen bonds between paired nucleotides; **Choice A**  is incorrect. Ligase sticks newly synthesised DNA fragments together to form one continuous strand; **Choice C**  is incorrect. DNA primase adds a short length of RNA to the DNA template strand; **Choice D**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1186","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1186\n\nWhich of these statement(s) is/are correct concerning the Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR)?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | It creates RNA copies of DNA |\n    | II. | It uses a special DNA polymerase that can withstand high temperatures |\n    | III. | It requires helicase to work |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nPCR requires high temperatures to separate the strands of the DNA molecule. Therefore, it requires a heat resistant DNA polymerase (Taq polymerase) to work (**Statement II**). **Choice B**  is correct.\n\nDuring PCR, DNA is amplified to make many copies of DNA, not RNA, **Statement I**  is incorrect. The PCR reaction does not require DNA helicase to work, **Statement III**  is incorrect. **Choices A, C**  and **D**  are incorrect as they include **Statement I** and/or **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI1336","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1336\n\nThe image shows a DNA strand during replication. What is the position of the mismatched nucleotide, and which enzyme removes the mismatched base?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI1336%20a.png\" alt=\"BI1336 a\">\n\n[ copyright 2024]\n\n| | Position of mismatched base | Enzyme that removes mismatched base |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| P | DNA polymerase I |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Q | DNA polymerase I |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | P | DNA polymerase III |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Q | DNA polymerase III |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nIn a DNA strand, Cytosine (C) matches up with Guanine (G), and Adenine (A) matches up with Thymine (T). DNA is synthesised in the 5' to 3' direction, meaning that nucleotides are only added to the 3' end, so the top strand is the newly synthesised strand. C should not be matched with A (position P). DNA polymerase III is involved in proofreading by removing the mismatched base and replacing it with the correct one. **Choice C** is, therefore, correct.\n\n**Choices A** and **B** are incorrect as DNA polymerase I replaces the RNA primer at the start of the Okazaki fragment with a DNA strand during DNA replication. **Choice D** is incorrect as Q points to a base on the template strand."},{"ref":"BI1337","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1337\n\nDuring DNA replication, in which direction does DNA polymerase move along the template strand and in which direction is the new strand synthesised on the leading strand?\n\n| | Direction that DNA polymerase moves in | Direction that the new strand is synthesised in |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| 3' to 5' | 5' to 3' |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 5' to 3' | 5' to 3' |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$  | 5' to 3' | 3' to 5 |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 3' to 5' | 3' to 5' |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nDNA polymerase is an enzyme which attaches to the template strand to enable DNA replication. It requires a 3'-OH group to start the synthesis of a new DNA strand, and it adds the 5' end of a DNA nucleotide to the 3' end of a strand of nucleotides. This means it needs to move along the template strand in the 3' to 5' direction to synthesise the new strand in the 5' to 3' direction. **Choice A**  is correct.\n\n**Choices B and D** are incorrect as DNA strands in a double helix run antiparallel to each other, so DNA polymerase cannot move along the template strand in the same direction that the new strand is synthesised in. **Choice C**  is incorrect as the enzyme cannot add new nucleotides to the 5' end of the new DNA strand."},{"ref":"BI1338","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1338\n\nThe diagram shows a replication fork during DNA replication. Which letter represents the leading strand?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI1338.png\" alt=\"BI1338\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Porto, A. (2013, November 27). File: replication fork-es.svg. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved September 27, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Replication_fork-es.svg. Copyright under CC BY-SA 3.0]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nDNA replication occurs simultaneously on both template strands. The leading strand is synthesised continuously in the same direction as replication fork movement, while the lagging strand is synthesised discontinuously as Okazaki fragments.\n\nIn the diagram, **Choice C** represents an Okazaki fragment on the lagging strand, so it is not the correct answer. **Choice B** represents the continuously synthesised leading strand, so it is the correct answer.\n\n**Choices A** and **D** represent template strands, not newly synthesised DNA, so they are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1339","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1339\n\nWhich of the following is/are a role of DNA polymerases during DNA replication?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n\n  | | |\n\n  | :-- | :-- |\n\n  | I. | Creates one RNA primer on the leading strand  |\n\n  | II. | Synthesises the new DNA strand starting from the DNA primer |\n\n  | III. | Builds Okazaki fragments on the lagging strand |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only \n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only \n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only \n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\n**Statement I**  is false because DNA polymerases do not create RNA primers on the leading strand; this is the role of RNA primase. \n\n**Statement II** is false because, although DNA polymerases synthesise the new DNA strand, they start from an RNA primer, not a DNA primer. \n\n**Statement III** is true as DNA polymerase III adds nucleotides to RNA primers on the lagging strand, forming short stretches of DNA, called the Okazaki fragments. These are later joined by DNA ligase, but the synthesis of the fragments is indeed carried out by DNA polymerase III.\n\n \n\n**Choice C**  is correct as it only includes the one true **Statement III**.\n\n **Choices A, B** and **D**  are incorrect, as they include one or both of the false **Statements I** and **II**."},{"ref":"BI1340","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1340\n\nWhat is the role of DNA ligase in DNA replication?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Creates hydrogen bonds between the original and newly synthesised DNA strand\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Synthesises the new DNA strand\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Removes RNA primers\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sticks DNA fragments together","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nDNA polymerase III synthesises Okazaki fragments on the lagging strand during DNA replication. The role of DNA ligase in DNA replication is to connect these Okazaki fragments together to form a seamless strand, **Choice D** is correct.\n\nNo enzyme is involved in forming hydrogen bonds between the complementary bases on the original and newly synthesised DNA strands. **Choice A**  is incorrect. DNA ligase does not synthesise the new DNA strand; instead, this is the role of DNA polymerase I and III. **Choice B**  is incorrect. DNA ligase does not remove RNA primers from the newly synthesised strand. The removal of RNA primers is carried out by DNA polymerase I. **Choice C** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1360","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1360\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe image shows a replication fork that is formed during DNA replication.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI1360.png\" alt=\"BI1360\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Porto, A. (2013, November 27). File: replication fork-es.svg. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved September 27, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Replication_fork-es.svg. Copyright under CC BY-SA 3.0]\n\n1. State the DNA sequence of the newly synthesised strand in the 5' to 3' direction, using the leading strand as a template. Your answer should start after the RNA primer.     [1 mark]\n\n2. 1. Primers are needed for DNA replication. State the name of the enzyme that synthesises primers. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Explain why primers need to be removed from the newly synthesised DNA strand before replication can continue. [2 marks]\n\n3. Predict, with a reason, what would happen to the rate of DNA replication at a temperature of 65°C. [2 marks]\n\n4. 1.   Bases are occasionally mismatched during DNA replication. Predict what the potential result could be if mismatches are not repaired. [1 mark]\n\n  \n\n    2. Describe how a  mismatched base is replaced during proofreading. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.   \n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ ACG ATT GCG GCA TGA ATC CGA T    [1 mark]\n\n[1 mark]\n\n :u[Sample answer]:\\\n\n**ACG ATT GCG GCA TGA ATC CGA T**($\\checkmark$)\n\n2. 1. \n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ DNA primase **OR** primase    [1 mark]\n\n[1 mark]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\\\n\nThe enzyme that synthesises primers is **DNA primase**($\\checkmark$)\n\n2.  2. Any **two** of the following\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ the newly synthesised strand should only contain DNA [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ to ensure that exact copies of the original DNA sequence are created [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ DNA ligase **OR** ligase  is unable to join RNA to DNA [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ DNA polymerases need a 3'$-$OH group to add nucleotides to, RNA has a 2'$-$OH group [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ RNA bases are less stable and more prone to mutation [1 mark]\n\n[2 marks]\n\n3.  Any **two** of the following\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ slow down **OR** stop [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ enzymes involved in DNA replication would denature **OR** break down **OR** lose structure **OR** active sites would change [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ DNA would degrade **OR** denature [1 mark]\n\n[2 marks]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\\\n\nAt a temperature of 65$\\degree$C, the rate of DNA replication would **slow** down($\\checkmark$) because the **enzymes** involved in DNA replication would begin to **denature**($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.  1. Any **one** of the following\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ mutations [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ development of genetic diseases **OR** cancer [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ cellular dysfunction [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ translation errors such as the formation of no protein **OR** truncated proteins **OR** the wrong protein [1 mark]                            \n\n[1 mark]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\\\n\nIf a mismatch is not repaired, then this results in a point **mutation**($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.  2. Any **three** of the following\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ DNA polymerase III recognises the mismatched base [1 mark]    \n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ DNA polymerase III contains a nuclease [1 mark]    \n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ DNA polymerase III cleaves the sugar-phosphate bond between mismatched nucleotide and preceding one [1 mark]        \n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ at the 3' end of the mismatched strand [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ DNA polymerase III replaces the mismatched base with the correct base **OR** polymerisation continues [1 mark]                    \n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ polymerisation and proofreading are carried out by two different catalytic domains on the DNA polymerase III [1 mark]    \n\n[3 marks]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\\\n\nDNA polymerase III **recognizes** the **mismatched base** during replication ($\\checkmark$), and contains a **nuclease**($\\checkmark$)\n\nthat **cleaves** the **sugar-phosphate** bond($\\checkmark$)\n\n at the **3'** end of the strand($\\checkmark$)\n\n."},{"ref":"BI1361","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1361\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1.  Explain why Okazaki fragments are formed during DNA replication. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Compare and contrast DNA replication on the leading and lagging strands. [7 marks]\n\n3.  Outline the functions of the enzymes involved in prokaryotic DNA replication. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA polymerase can only work in the 5'-3' direction **OR** can only add nucleotides to the 3' end of the growing DNA strand            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA is made up of two complementary strands running in opposite directions **OR** antiparallel strands            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the lagging strand runs in the 5'-3' direction away from the replication fork **OR** DNA polymerase must move in the 5' to 3' direction away from the replication fork **OR** in the opposite direction to helicase            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ this results in discontinuous replication **OR** as the fork moves forward, DNA polymerase must fall off **AND** reattach on the newly exposed DNA strand            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ primase **OR** DNA primase creates many RNA primers on the lagging strand, so many small DNA fragments **OR** Okazaki fragments are formed            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA ligase joins the individual fragments together **OR** catalyses formation of sugar-phosphate bonds between fragments to form continuous lagging strand            [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\n\nOkazaki fragments are formed during DNA replication because **DNA polymerase can only work** in the **5' to 3'** direction away **from the replication fork** ($\\checkmark$); however, the **lagging** strand runs in the **5' to 3'** direction **from the replication fork** ($\\checkmark$).**Primase** synthesises many **RNA primers** on the lagging strand($\\checkmark$) because as the fork moves forward, DNA polymerase must **fall off and reattach** to the newly exposed DNA strand. **DNA ligase** then **joins** the individual **fragments** together ($\\checkmark$). \n\n2. any **seven** of the following (*answer must include at least **two** similarities and **two** differences to gain full marks*):\n\n***Similarities***\\\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** use complementary base pairing [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** require primers **OR** RNA primers to initiate DNA replication    [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** require enzymes **OR** DNA polymerase [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ replication occurs in the 5' to 3' direction on BOTH strands [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ Both have proofreading **OR** repair mechanisms [1 mark]\n\n***Differences***\\\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$\n\n Okazaki fragments are formed on the lagging strand **BUT** not on the leading strand [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$\n\n template for leading strand runs in the 3'$\\ce{ -> }$ 5' direction **WHEREAS** template for lagging strand runs in 5'$\\ce{ -> }$ 3' direction [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$\n\n DNA replication is continuous on the leading strand **BUT** discontinuous on the lagging strand [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$\n\n the leading strand only requires one RNA primer to initiate replication, **BUT** the lagging strand requires many [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$\n\n DNA ligase is required on the lagging strand to stick many fragments together, **BUT** not on the leading strand [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$\n\n replication occurs in opposite directions **OR** towards the replication fork for the lagging strand **AND** away from the replication fork for the lagging strand [1 mark]\n\n [7 marks]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\\\n\nBoth the leading and lagging strands use **complementary base pairing** ($\\checkmark$), require **primers** for the initiation of replication ($\\checkmark$), require **enzymes** ($\\checkmark$) such as DNA polymerase and replication occurs in the **5' to 3' direction** on both strands($\\checkmark$). However, the **leading strand** only requires **one RNA primer**, whilst the **lagging strand** requires **many** ($\\checkmark$).  **Okazaki fragments** are formed on the **lagging strand** but not on the leading strand ($\\checkmark$), so DNA replication is **continuous on the leading strand** but **discontinuous** on the **lagging** strand ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **five** of the following:\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$\n\n helicase unwinds the DNA strand **OR** separates the strands **OR** breaks the hydrogen bonds between the strands [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$\n\n DNA gyrase **OR** gyrase prevents supercoiling of the DNA strands **OR** helps overcome the strains as helix unwinds [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$\n\n DNA primase **OR** primase synthesises RNA primers[1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$\n\n DNA polymerase III forms the DNA strand starting at the RNA primer **OR** adds complementary nucleotides to the 3' end of the growing strand [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$\n\n DNA polymerase I replaces the RNA primer at the start of each Okazaki fragment with DNA nucleotides [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$\n\n ligase joins the DNA fragments **OR** Okazaki fragments on the lagging strand together **OR** catalyses the formation of sugar-phosphate bonds between Okazaki fragments **OR** DNA fragments [1 mark]\n\n [5 marks]\n\n :u[Sample answer]:\\\n\n Several enzymes are involved in DNA replication, including **helicase**, which **unwinds the DNA** strand ($\\checkmark$) to allow other enzymes to access the DNA and carry out replication. DNA **gyrase prevents supercoiling** of the DNA strands ($\\checkmark$). **Primase** creates **RNA primers** ($\\checkmark$), which **DNA polymerase III**  binds to in order to **start replication** ($\\checkmark$) and **DNA polymerase I replaces the RNA primer** at the start of each Okazaki fragment with a DNA strand ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1387","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1387\n\nThe following diagram shows the result of gel electrophoresis using samples taken from six individuals. What can be concluded based on these results?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI1387.png\" alt=\"BI1387\">\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n | | |\n | :-- | :-- |\n | I. | Subject 1 is more closely related to subject 3 than any of the other subjects.|\n | II. | Bands on the right indicate the largest molecules sampled. |\n | III. | Subject 2 and subject 6 may be related.|\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSubject 1 and subject 3 share two bands in common, more than any other pairing; **Statement I** is, therefore, true. Bands on the right have travelled the farthest from the origin and are, therefore, the smallest molecules, not the largest; **Statement II** is, therefore, false. Subject 2 and subject 6 have three bands in common and are, therefore, very likely to be related; **Statement III** is, therefore, true. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it is missing **Statement III**. **Choices B** and **D** are incorrect because they contain **Statement II**."},{"ref":"BI1396","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1396\n\nWhich of the following correctly describes the components needed in the polymerase chain reaction (PCR)?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | The enzyme Taq polymerase is needed because it functions at low temperatures. |\n    | II. | A supply of nucleotides is required to build new DNA sequences. |\n    | III. | Primers are needed to specify the DNA sequences to be copied. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nTaq polymerase is used in PCR because it continues to function efficiently even at high temperatures, not low temperatures as **Statement I** suggests. A large supply of nucleotides is necessary to build copies of the target DNA sequence as PCR can create millions or even billions of target sequences (**Statement II**). Primers are added to the reaction to allow Taq polymerase to initiate replication of the target DNA segment (**Statement III**). **Choice C** is, therefore, correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it does not include **Statement II**. **Choice B** is incorrect because it does not include **Statement III**. **Choice D** is incorrect because it includes **Statement I**."},{"ref":"BI1423","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1423\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nGel electrophoresis is a technique used to separate molecules such as nucleic acids. It involves loading the samples into wells in a supporting medium, often agarose gel, and applying an electric current. The diagram below shows the outcome of a gel electrophoresis run.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI1423.png\" alt=\"BI1423\">\n\n1.\n 1. Deduce in which direction DNA fragments would move through this gel. [1 mark]\n\n 2. State what causes the DNA to move in the direction given in (i) [1 mark]\n\n2. The DNA fragments are not visible immediately after separation. Outline how gene probes can be used to identify the DNA fragments. [2 marks]\n\n3. DNA profiling can be used to study genetic diversity and population dynamics in various species, aiding in conservation efforts. Describe one named ex-situ conservation technique used to help preserve biodiversity. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ towards the positive electrode **OR** from negative to positive electrode **OR** downwards [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n DNA would travel **towards the positively charged electrode** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n 2.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA is negatively charged **OR** phosphate group of DNA is negatively charged [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **phosphate group** present in nucleotides gives DNA a net **negative charge** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ short single-stranded DNA **OR** RNA with a complementary base sequence to the target DNA fragment [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ probe is radioactive **OR** stained with fluorescent dye [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ gel **OR** membrane is exposed to DNA probe, and if DNA fragment of interest present probe will bind to it **OR** they will hybridise [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ x-ray film exposes position of radiating probe [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ scanners **OR** imaging systems are used to detect fluorescence marked probe [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n A **short, radioactive** ($\\checkmark$), **single-stranded** section of DNA with a **complementary base sequence** to the **DNA fragment** of interest is used ($\\checkmark$). The gel with the DNA fragments is **exposed** to the **labelled probe** and If there are **DNA fragments** of interest on the membrane the **probes** will **bind** to them ($\\checkmark$). **X-ray film** is used to **capture** the **radiation** emitted by the radioactive **probe** ($\\checkmark$) and this indicates the presence and position of the DNA fragment.\n\n3. any **three** of the following (*name of type of conservation must be given to gain full marks*):\n\n ***Zoos OR Aquariums***\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ animal husbandry facilities to help battle extinction **OR** loss of biodiversity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ maintain detailed records **OR** pedigrees **OR** known studbooks to track genetic lineage used to choose breeding pairs to maintain genetic diversity **OR** minimise inbreeding [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ participation in collaborative species survival plans **OR** coordinated efforts to manage the breeding of specific species **OR** exchange animals between different institutions to introduce new genetic diversity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ captive breeding **OR** artificial insemination **OR** in vitro fertilisation allows offspring to be produced using two animals in different zoos **OR** increases biodiversity by using gametes from animals in other zoos [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ encouraging natural breeding to produce offspring [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ reintroduction programs **OR** release animals into wild [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$educational programs **OR** workshops **OR** exhibitions to educate the public about the importance of animal conservation [1 mark]\n\n ***Animal Tissue Banks*** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ store samples of animal genetic material **OR** DNA **OR** cells **OR** tissues from various species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ preserves genetic diversity for the conservation of endangered and threatened species **OR** maintains genetic traits that may be lost in the wild [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ germoplasm stores eggs **OR** ova **OR** sperm **OR** embryos [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ germoplasm collected from species in wild can be used for captive breeding programmes **OR** assisted reproductive technologies **OR** artificial insemination **OR** in vitro fertilisation in captivity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ tissues stored cryogenically [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ resource for species identification **OR** verification **OR** to resolve taxonomic uncertainties **OR** to differentiate between similar-looking species **OR** understanding of adaptation **OR** evolution [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ samples can be used for monitoring the health of wildlife populations **OR** for analysing tissues for diseases **OR** pathogens **OR** contaminants [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ somatic tissue can be used for cloning [1 mark]\n\n ***Botanical gardens*** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ conservation of plant biodiversity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ often maintain living collections of rare **OR** endangered plant species, helping safeguard their genetic diversity **OR** helping prevent extinction [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ scientific research into plant biology **OR** ecology **OR** taxonomy within controlled environment contributing to understanding of plant life [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ exchange of seeds or pollen between different gardens maintains biodiversity **OR** genetic variation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ educational programs **OR** workshops **OR** exhibitions to educate the public about the importance of plant conservation [1 mark]\n\n ***Seed Banks*** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ safely store variety of plant species' seeds, preserving genetic diversity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ seeds stored in cool **OR** dark **OR** dry conditions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ may include agricultural crop seeds, contributing to food security by providing genetic resources for crop improvement [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ seeds can be used for habitat restoration **OR** population reinforcement **OR** species reintroduction efforts. [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ researchers can study and understand the biology of various plant species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ serve as a biological insurance policy against catastrophic events **OR** natural disasters **OR** disease outbreaks, that can wipe out entire plant populations [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ educational programs **OR** workshops to educate the public about the importance of plant conservation [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Animal tissue banks** store **DNA, cells, and tissues** ($\\checkmark$), using **cryogenic preservation** ($\\checkmark$) methods. **Germplasm**, often composed of **ova** or **sperm** from **wild animals** ($\\checkmark$), is crucial for **captive breeding** programs, effectively **increasing genetic diversity** ($\\checkmark$) within captive populations. Stored tissue can be used for a variety of **research**, including investigating **evolutionary relationships** between species ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1631","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1631\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nUsing evidence collected from a crime scene, genetic profiles were amassed to aid in identifying the assailant (the person who committed the crime).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI1631%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1631 fig1\">\n\n1. Deduce, with a reason, who is most likely to have committed the crime. [1 mark]\n\n2. The genetic profiles from the crime scene were created using short tandem repeats (STRs). Explain why STRs are often used in genetic profiling. [2 marks]\n\n3. Explain why a control sample from someone who was not involved in the crime was included in the genetic profiles. [1 mark]\n\n4.\n\n 1. Forensic scientists were only able to recover a small volume of the suspect’s DNA from the victim’s clothing. State which technique was used to make many copies of the DNA to create the genetic profiles. [1 mark]\n\n 2. The technique you named in part (i) uses a specialised DNA polymerase, Taq polymerase, to make many copies of the DNA sample. State what property of Taq polymerase makes it suitable for this technique. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. any **one** of the following\\\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ suspect 1 **BECAUSE** the genetic profile from the victim’s clothing contains all of the fragments from both the victim and suspect 1 [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/1631.png\" alt=\"1631\">\n\n **Suspect 1** is most likely to have committed the crime because the genetic profile from the victim’s clothing contains **all of the fragments** from **suspect 1** and the **victim** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **two** of the following\\\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ STRs vary in the number of times the sequence is repeated **OR** vary in length [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ STRS are inherited from one generation to the next so can be used to infer relationships between individuals [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ STRs are distributed throughout the human genome, making them readily available for analysis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ STR regions are quite short so are easily amplified using polymerase chain reaction **OR** PCR techniques [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ combination of STRs is unique **OR** almost unique to each person [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the size of the STR determines how far it migrates **OR** its position on a genetic profile [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n STRs are used to create genetic profiles because they **vary** in the number of times the **sequence is repeated** in **individuals** ($\\checkmark$). E**veryone**, except for monozygotic twins, has a **unique combination of STRs, giving them a unique DNA profile** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **one** of the following\\\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to minimise the risk of false positives [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to ensure that any results that are observed are accurate and not due to any experimental errors [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to monitor for any potential contamination [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n A control sample was used in the genetic profiles to **minimise the risk of false positives** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.\n\n 1. the following\\\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ PCR **OR** polymerase chain reaction [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n **PCR**($\\checkmark$)\n\n 2. the following\\\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ enzyme is heat resistant **OR** capable of functioning at high temperatures without becoming denatured [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n Taq polymerase is **heat resistant**(√)."},{"ref":"BI2339","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2339\n\nWhich of these is responsible for the semi-conservative replication of DNA?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Action of DNA polymerase\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Complementary base pairing\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Action of DNA helicase\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrogen bonding","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because complementary base pairing means that adenine always pairs with thymine, and guanine always pairs with cytosine. This ensures that during DNA replication, each strand acts as a template for a new DNA strand, so the new helix has one strand of parent DNA and one strand of newly synthesised DNA, with the DNA code conserved.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because DNA polymerase is essential for forming the sugar-phosphate backbone of the new strand by linking nucleotides. However, it does not determine the sequence of bases; this is dictated by complementary base pairing.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because DNA helicase unwinds and separates the two strands of DNA, it does not have a role in accurate replication of the DNA base sequence on the parent strand.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because hydrogen bonds form between complementary base pairs and hold the double helix together, but hydrogen bonding does not have a direct role in determining the sequence of bases on the DNA strands."},{"ref":"BI2588","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2588\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe diagram below shows a DNA molecule at the replication fork. The dotted lines help to visualise the sugar-phosphate backbone as the two strands are separated.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI2588Q.png\" alt=\"BI2588Q\">\n\n1. Label the 5’ and 3’ ends of both parental strands. [1 mark]\n\n2. State the name of the enzyme responsible for breaking the hydrogen bonds between the two DNA strands. [1 mark]\n\n3. Describe the semiconservative nature of DNA replication which results in each daughter DNA molecule being identical to the original. [2 marks]\n\n4. An RNA primer has been added to each strand of DNA. Label the nitrogenous base in the RNA primer by writing the correct letter in the shaded rectangle. [1 mark]\n\n5. Draw the next nucleotides that will be incorporated into each strand by the enzyme DNA polymerase III. [2 marks]\n\n6. State the role of DNA primase in DNA replication. [1 mark]\n\n7. Compare and contrast DNA replication on the leading and lagging strands. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 5’ and 3’ are correctly written on each strand of DNA [1 mark]\n\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/AnsA.png\" alt=\"AnsA\">\n\n2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ helicase **OR** DNA helicase [1 mark]\n\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The enzyme that breaks the hydrogen bonds is **helicase** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ each newly synthesised DNA molecule consists of one original **OR** parental strand and one newly synthesised strand [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ original **OR** parental strands act as templates for new strands [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ base pairing ensures high accuracy in copying base sequences **OR** ensures new strand is complementary to template **OR** original **OR** parental strand [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen bonds form between the new and template strands **OR** parental strands, ensuring specificity in base pairing **OR** A-T and G-C [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n DNA replication is semiconservative, meaning each new DNA molecule consists of **one original (parental) strand** and **one newly synthesised strand** ($\\checkmark$). Base pairing ensures a high degree of **accuracy** in **copying base sequences** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ the letter U for uracil is correctly added to the diagram [1 mark]\n\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/AnsD.png\" alt=\"AnsD\">\n\n5. any **two** of the following *(only **one** hydrogen bond between strands required to gain full marks)*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA nucleotide with thymine base correctly added to the 3’ end of RNA primer on left-hand strand [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA nucleotide with guanine base correctly added to the 3’ end of RNA primer on right-hand strand [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI2588%20eMS.png\" alt=\"BI2588 eMS\">\n\n6.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ synthesises RNA primer **OR** provides a free 3' OH group for DNA polymerase III to extend the strand [1 mark]\n\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ DNA primase initiates replication by **synthesising** a short **RNA primer** that allows DNA polymerase to extend the new DNA strand ($\\checkmark$).\n\n7. any **two** of the following *(must include **one** similarity and **one** difference to achieve full marks):*\n\n ***Similarities***\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** leading and lagging strands have DNA nucleotides added in 5’ → 3’ direction **OR** have DNA nucleotides added to 3’ carbon of pentose sugar [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ on **BOTH** leading and lagging strands DNA polymerase III adds new DNA nucleotides [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** strands require RNA primers to initiate replication [1 mark]\n\n ***Differences***\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA is synthesised continuously on leading strand **WHEREAS** occurs discontinuously **OR** in Okazaki fragments on the lagging strand [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA primer is only needed once on leading strand **WHEREAS** primers are required repeatedly on lagging strand. [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ lagging strand requires DNA ligase to join fragments **WHEREAS** leading strand does not require DNA ligase [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ leading strand synthesis occurs toward the replication fork **WHEREAS** lagging strand synthesis occurs away from the replication fork [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n On both the leading and lagging strands, **DNA polymerase III** ($\\checkmark$) adds **nucleotides** in the **5’ to 3’ direction** ($\\checkmark$). However, DNA is synthesised **continuously** on the **leading strand**, whereas it is synthesised **discontinuously in Okazaki fragments** on the **lagging strand** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2717","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2717\n\nThe diagram below shows a DNA nucleotide bonded to an RNA primer. The dotted lines indicate possible locations for the addition of a new DNA nucleotide during replication.\n\nWhich dotted line correctly represents the site where the next nucleotide will be incorporated?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/eDIT2717.png\" alt=\"eDIT2717\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because during DNA replication nucleotides are added to the 3’ end of the growing strand from an RNA primer, which provides a hydroxyl group for DNA polymerase to extend the strand.\\\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it is part of the parent strand. **Choice B** is incorrect because a new nucleotide will not be added to a nitrogenous base. **Choice D** is incorrect because a new nucleotide will not attach to the 5’ end."},{"ref":"BI2718","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2718\n\nWhich of the following statements accurately describe(s) DNA replication on the lagging strand?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | DNA is synthesised continuously in the 5’ to 3’ direction|\n    | II. | Multiple RNA primers are required |\n    | III. | DNA polymerase III catalyses the synthesis of new DNA |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is false because on the lagging strand,  DNA is synthesised discontinuously in short segments called Okazaki fragments, not continuously.\\\n**Statement II** is true because multiple RNA primers are needed to initiate synthesis of each fragment.\\\n**Statement III** is true because DNA polymerase III catalyses the synthesis of new DNA on both strands.\n\n**Choice B** is, therefore, correct as only **Statements II** and **III** are true. **Choices A, C** and **D** are incorrect as they include the false **Statement I**."},{"ref":"BI2719","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2719\n\nThe diagram below shows DNA replication at a replication bubble. Identify the templates lagging strand(s).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/bidirectional%20DNA%20replication%20in%20replication%20bubble%20BR.png\" alt=\"bidirectional DNA replication in replication bubble BR\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and IV","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn the diagram, DNA replication is occurring in both directions from a replication origin, forming a replication bubble. Each fork has two template strands, and DNA is being synthesised on both.\nIn DNA replication, new DNA is always synthesised in the 5' → 3' direction, because DNA polymerase reads the template strand in the 3' → 5' direction. The leading strand is synthesised continuously towards the replication fork, whereas the lagging strand is synthesised discontinuously away from the fork in Okazaki fragments.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/bidirectional%20DNA%20replication%20explainer%20diagram.png\" alt=\"bidirectional DNA replication explainer diagram\">\n\nIf we apply this to the diagram provided: at the left-hand replication fork, the upper template strand (**I**) runs in the 5' → 3' direction, so polymerase synthesises the new strand right to left, which is towards the fork. This makes **I** a leading strand template. The lower template strand (**III**) runs 3' → 5', so polymerase synthesises the new DNA strand left to right, away from the fork using Okazaki fragments. This makes **III** a lagging strand template.\nAt the right-hand fork on the upper template (**II**), polymerase is still synthesising right to left, but now this means it is synthesising the new DNA away from the replication fork. This makes **II** a lagging strand template. On the lower template (**IV**), the new DNA strand is still being formed from left to right, but now this is towards the replication fork. This makes **IV** a leading strand template.\n\n**Choice C** is correct because both **II** and **III** label sections of DNA that are templates for lagging strand synthesis.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it includes only strand **II** and omits strand **III**, which is also lagging. **Choice B** is incorrect because it includes only strand **III** and omits strand **II**. **Choice D** is incorrect because strands **I** and **IV** are synthesised toward the replication fork and are therefore leading strands."},{"ref":"BI2720","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2720\n\nDuring DNA replication, which enzyme primarily proofreads and corrects mismatched bases before continuing elongation?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI2720.png\" alt=\"BI2720\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ DNA primase\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ DNA ligase\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ DNA polymerase I\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ DNA polymerase III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because DNA polymerase III can proofread and correct mismatched bases by removing errors immediately after adding nucleotides to the growing DNA strand during replication.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because DNA primase is an enzyme that synthesises short RNA primers to provide a starting point for DNA polymerase during replication. \\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because DNA ligase is an enzyme that seals nicks (gaps) in the sugar-phosphate backbone of the newly synthesised DNA strand. \\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because DNA polymerase I removes RNA primers and replaces them with DNA. While it also has exonuclease activity, DNA polymerase III is the primary enzyme responsible for correcting mismatched bases as they occur."},{"ref":"BI2721","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2721\n\nWhich letter indicates the type of bonds broken by the enzyme helicase?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI2721.png\" alt=\"BI2721\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because helicase breaks the hydrogen bonds between nitrogenous bases to separate the two strands of DNA.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the sugar-phosphate bond between a deoxyribose sugar and a phosphate group is not broken by helicase. **Choice B** is incorrect because the glycosidic bond between a deoxyribose sugar and a nitrogenous base is not broken by helicase. **Choice D** is incorrect for the same reason as **Choice B**."},{"ref":"BI2722","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2722\n\nWhich of the following processes require DNA replication?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | A skin cell dividing to replace damaged tissue |\n    | II. | A fertilised egg undergoing its first round of mitosis |\n    | III. | An *Escherichia coli* bacterium dividing in the gut |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because it describes mitosis. Before mitosis occurs, the cell must replicate its DNA so that both daughter cells receive a complete copy of the genetic material. \\\n**Statement II** is true because after fertilisation, the single-celled zygote must divide and grow into a multicellular organism. This requires DNA replication so that each new cell formed from mitosis gets an identical copy of the genome. \\\n**Statement III** is true; bacteria reproduce through binary fission, a process in which the bacterial chromosome is replicated and then distributed into two daughter cells.\\\n**Choice D** is, therefore, correct as **Statements I, II**, and **III** are true."},{"ref":"BI2723","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2723\n\nA scientist replicates a DNA strand in vitro with a high concentration of cytosine analogs that cannot hydrogen bond. What is the most likely result?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Only the lagging strand will replicate\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Guanine will fail to pair correctly, increasing error rate\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The sugar-phosphate backbone will be broken\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ DNA helicase will not be able to unwind the double helix","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCytosine normally forms three hydrogen bonds with guanine. If a cytosine analogue cannot hydrogen bond, guanine nucleotides will lack a proper complementary base. As a result, guanine in the newly synthesised strand cannot form stable hydrogen bonds with the cytosine analogue on the template strand. This reduces the stability of the DNA molecule and may disrupt accurate replication or cause it to stop entirely. **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because both leading and lagging strands require base pairing for replication. If cytosine analogues can’t pair, both strands will be affected, not just the lagging strand.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because the sugar-phosphate backbone is held together by covalent sugar-phosphate bonds between adjacent nucleotides and is not affected by the ability to hydrogen bond.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because helicase unwinds DNA by breaking hydrogen bonds between bases, but it acts before replication begins. The presence of cytosine analogues that fail to hydrogen bond affects base pairing during elongation, not the unwinding step."},{"ref":"BI2894","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2894\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA specific region of a DNA coding strand was investigated to determine the DNA sequence. PCR was used to amplify fragments of the template strand derived from this coding strand.\n\nThe electropherogram below shows the resulting DNA sequence of the DNA fragments.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI2894a.png\" alt=\"BI2894a\">\n\n1. Identify the DNA sequence of the coding strand based on the electropherogram. [1 mark]\n\n2. Explain why PCR is used before the sequencing begins. [2 marks]\n\n3. State the direction in which DNA replication occurs. [1 mark]\n\n The template DNA fragments were then run through gel electrophoresis, the results of which are shown below. The boxes at the top of the diagram represent the four bases.\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/DNA%20Replication/images/BI2894b.png\" alt=\"BI2894b\">\n\n4. The first box, located on the far left, has been identified as thymine. Based on this, identify the bases **X**, **Y** and **Z**. [1 mark]\n\n5. Distinguish between the nucleic acid fragments circled in the diagram [1 mark]\n\n6. Outline **one** reason why DNA sequencing is often preferred over protein sequencing in the study of evolutionary relationships between organisms. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. $\\ce{ -> }$ TAGTTCAAAA [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The DNA sequence of the coding strand is **TAGTTCAAAA** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ produce multiple copies of specific DNA fragments **OR** amplify DNA from small initial sample to produce enough material for sequencing [1 mark]\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to increase concentration of target DNA for improved reliability of sequencing [1 mark]\n $\\ce{ -> }$ rapid **OR** shorter time **OR** faster **OR** more efficient than other methods of DNA replication [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The polymerase chain reaction (PCR) is important before sequencing to **produce multiple copies** ($\\checkmark$) of DNA fragments in a **short period of time** ($\\checkmark$) ready to be sequenced.\n\n3. $\\ce{ -> }$ 5’ to 3’ [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The DNA replication occurs in the **5’ to 3’** direction ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. $\\ce{ -> }$ X: A **OR** adenine; Y: G **OR** guanine; Z: C **OR** cytosine [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **X** is **adenine**, **Y** is **guanine**, and **Z** is **cytosine** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. any **one** of the following:\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the top fragments consist of longer lengths of DNA than the bottom band **OR** the top fragments contain more nucleotides than the bottom fragments **OR** vice versa [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The difference between the nucleic acid fragments is that those in the **top band** are\n**longer in length** than those at the **bottom** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. any **one** of the following:\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA sequences include both coding and non-coding regions **WHEREAS** protein sequencing only reflects the expressed portion of genes [1 mark]\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA sequences have a higher mutation rate **OR** more potential mutation sites **THAN** protein sequences as a single amino acid can be coded by multiple codons [1 mark]\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA sequencing directly reflects genetic changes **OR** mutations over time **WHEREAS** protein sequencing may miss silent mutations that do not alter the amino acid sequence [1 mark]\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA sequencing allows rapid analysis of large genomes at a lower cost **COMPARED TO** protein sequencing, which can be more labour-intensive and expensive [1 mark]\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA sequences can be used to estimate time of divergence between species using the concept of molecular clocks **WHEREAS** more difficult to apply with protein sequences [1 mark]\n $\\ce{ -> }$ protein sequence can be determined from DNA sequence **HOWEVER** DNA sequence cannot be determined from protein sequence [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **DNA** sequences include both **coding and non-coding** regions whereas **protein** sequencing **only reflects** the **expressed** portion of genome ($\\checkmark$)."}]},{"name":"Gene Expression","questions":[{"ref":"BI0006","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0006\n\nWhich statement is **false** about the genome?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The genome is an organism’s entire set of genetic material.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ All the information carried by the genome is expressed in each cell.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The genome is a set of instructions to be followed by the cell.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Each skin cell contains a full copy of the genome.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is not true. Not all genes are expressed in each cell. Only the necessary genes are expressed based on the specialised function of the cell.\nAll other statements are true."},{"ref":"BI0586","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0586\n\nWhich of the following best defines the term “Proteome”?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The total number of protein molecules present in a cell, tissue, or organism\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$  The complete set of amino acids required to build proteins in a cell, tissue or organism, regardless of conditions\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ The complete set of genes present in the genome of a cell, tissue, or organism\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The complete set of proteins expressed by a cell, tissue, or organism at a particular time and under specific conditions","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because the proteome is the complete set of proteins expressed by a cell, tissue, or organism at a particular time and under specific conditions. It can vary between cell types and change in response to conditions, because different proteins are expressed at different times.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because this describes the total quantity of protein molecules, rather than the complete set of proteins expressed. The proteome concerns which proteins are present, not simply how many protein molecules there are.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because this describes amino acids, which are the building blocks of proteins, rather than proteins themselves. The proteome is the set of proteins expressed, not the set of amino acids required to make them.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because this describes the genome, which is the complete set of genetic material in a cell or organism. The proteome is the set of proteins expressed, not the set of genes."},{"ref":"BI0594","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0594\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe diagram below represents the structure of the protein haemoglobin.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Gene%20Expression/images/BI0594i.png\" alt=\"BI0594i\">\n\n1.\n 1. State the function of haemoglobin. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Describe the structural differences that distinguish one protein from another. [2 marks]\n\n2. Haemoglobin is part of an organism’s proteome. Define proteome. [1 mark]\n\n3. Describe how the structure of collagen or insulin is related to its function. [2 marks]\n\n4.\n 1. Explain the impact high temperatures can have on the structure and function of proteins such as haemoglobin. [3 marks]\n\n 2. State another abiotic factor which can impact the structure of a protein. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n 1.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ transport of oxygen (around the body) [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nHaemoglobin is a protein found in red blood cells which **transports oxygen** around the body ($\\checkmark$).\n\n 2. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ large number of amino acids **OR** 20 amino acids allows for many combinations of amino acids between different proteins/polypeptides [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ length of polypeptides varies [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ proteins are composed of different amino acids [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ multiple polypeptides can be linked to make different proteins [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nBecause there are **20 different amino acids**, there is a large number of **different combinations** of amino acids possible ($\\checkmark$). Polypeptides can also **vary greatly in length** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. $\\ce{ -> }$ complete set of proteins expressed by a cell **OR** tissue **OR** organism [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nThe proteome is the complete **set of proteins** that a cell or an organism can express ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **two** of the following (answers must contain named example and function for full marks):\n\n ***Collagen***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fibrous protein that provides structural support in connective tissues **OR** resists stretching **OR** supports tissue integrity [1 mark]\n $\\ce{ -> }$ three polypeptide chains wound into a triple helix, giving it high tensile strength [1 mark]\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycine every third residue allows chains to pack tightly [1 mark]\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen bonds between chains **OR** covalent cross-links between molecules increase stability and strength [1 mark]\n\n ***Insulin***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ globular protein hormone involved in the regulation of blood glucose [1 mark]\n $\\ce{ -> }$ two polypeptide chains **OR** α and β chains giving quaternary structure [1 mark]\n $\\ce{ -> }$ specific shape allows insulin to bind precisely to insulin receptors on target cell membranes [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nCollagen is a **fibrous protein** that provides **structural support** in **connective tissue** ($\\checkmark$). Its **three polypeptide chains** are wound into a **triple helix**, which is reinforced by **hydrogen bonds**, giving it **high tensile strength** ($\\checkmark$) and making it **resistant to stretching** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.\n 1. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ extreme temperatures can lead to denaturation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ denaturation is the change in the 3-dimensional shape **OR** tertiary structure **OR** secondary **OR** quaternary structure of a protein [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ high temperatures break hydrogen bonds [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ protein can no longer function [1 mark]\n\n [3 marks]\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nTemperatures which are too high can lead to a protein becoming **denatured** ($\\checkmark$), which causes a change in the **3-dimensional shape** of the protein ($\\checkmark$). If the shape of a protein is changed, it may no longer be able to carry out its **function** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n 2. $\\ce{ -> }$ pH [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ other factor that disrupts hydrogen bonding such as salts or ethanol [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n**pH** ($\\checkmark$) levels outside the optimum range can lead to a protein becoming denatured."},{"ref":"BI0600","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0600\n\nDNA methylation has an important function in gene expression. DNA methylation patterns can change in identical twins who are exposed to different environmental conditions.\n\nWhich of the following environmental factors would have an impact on DNA methylation in identical twins?\n\n-    I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Mutations of gene\n-    II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ Type of diet\n-    III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ Lifelong physical activity\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nEnvironmental factors can influence DNA methylation. Type of diet (**Statement II**) and lifelong physical activity (**Statement III**) are examples of environmental factors that can influence DNA methylation in identical twins. Different types of diet can either cause higher or lower rates of DNA methylation. Lifelong physical activity changes methylation patterns in genes related to exercise.\nGene mutation (**Statement I**) is not an environmental factor. It is an outcome or result of environmental factors that lead to gene mutation. For instance, exposure to an environmental factor such as UV radiation can lead to gene mutation."},{"ref":"BI0601","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0601\n\nNucleosomes play an important role in regulating transcription, which influences gene expression. The diagram below shows tightly packed nucleosomes and loosely packed nucleosomes.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Gene%20Expression/images/BI0601.png\" alt=\"BI0601\">\n[Source: Adapted from CNX OpenStax, (2016, May). *File:Figure 16 03 02.png*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved May 28, 2022, from https:// commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Figure_16_03_02.png. Copyright under CC-BY-4.0]\n\nWhich of the following statements is true about the nucleosome?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Transcription enzymes are excluded from loosely packed nucleosomes.\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Removal of an acetyl group from the histone tail causes the nucleosome to be less tightly packed.\n\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Gene expression is turned on when nucleosomes are tightly packed.\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Addition of a methyl group to the cytosine of the DNA inactivates the gene.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nChemical modifications to histones and DNA regulate transcription and hence gene expression. The addition of methyl groups to cytosine bases in DNA, that is tightly coiled around histone proteins, maintains the tight packing of the nucleosome (**Choice D**). This inactivates genes (**Choice C**). Genes are turned back on again when the methyl groups are removed, and the nucleosomes become more loosely packed.\n\nHistone acetylation (**not** acetyl group removal) results in less tightly packed DNA, which upregulates transcription (**Choice B**).\n\nWhen nucleosomes are tightly packed, there is less access for transcription enzymes to reach the DNA molecule (**Choice A**)."},{"ref":"BI0606","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0606\n\nGene regulation is important for cell differentiation and the development of an organism. For example, the developmental pattern of a common fruit fly (*Drosophila melanogaster*).\n\nWhich of the following play a direct role in regulating gene expression?\n\n-    I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Enhancer\n-    II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ Repressor\n-    III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ Epigenetic modifications\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$  II only\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nEnhancers and repressors bind to specific base sequences of DNA to regulate transcription and, therefore, gene expression. Epigenetic modifications are, notably DNA methylation and histone modification act as a layer of control, turning genes on or off in response to various internal and external signals, thereby determining which genes are expressed and to what extent.\n\n**Choices A**, **B** and **C** are incorrect as they do not include all three."},{"ref":"BI0755","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0755\n\nWhich statements about embryonic stem cells and specialised cells is/are **not** true?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Embryonic stem cells have higher levels of DNA methylation than differentiated cells |\n    | II. | Specialised cells are more differentiated than embryonic stem cells |\n    | III. | Most specialised cells and embryonic stem cells both contain the entire genome of an organism |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is not true because embryonic stem cells have relatively low levels of DNA methylation, which allows for greater plasticity and potential for differentiation into various cell types. In contrast, differentiated cells often have higher levels of DNA methylation, which contributes to their stable and specialized gene expression patterns. **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Statement II** is true, as specialised cells are more differentiated than embryonic stem cells as they are adapted to particular functions. **Statement III** is true, as the genome of an organism is the complete set of genes in an organism, and most cells in an organism contain the same set of genetic information. **Choices B, C** and **D**  are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0783","marks":"9","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0783\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe graphs show the results from a long-term study on 86 pairs of monozygotic twins, over 80 years.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Gene%20Expression/images/BI0783A.png\" alt=\"BI0783A\">\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Gene%20Expression/images/BI0783B.png\" alt=\"BI0783B\">\n\n1. Describe the relationship between the percentage similarity in DNA methylation pattern and age. [2 marks]\n\n2. Explain the relationship that you referred to in part (a). [1 mark]\n\n3. Explain the effect that DNA methylation most likely has on gene expression. [1 mark]\n\n4. Explain why monozygotic twins were used in this study. [2 marks]\n\n5. Suggest one limitation of conducting a study in humans over 80 years. [1 mark]\n\n6. Evaluate whether this study provides evidence that decreases in the percentage of DNA methylation is a predictor of an early death. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as age increases, the percentage similarity in the DNA methylation pattern decreases **OR** there is a negative correlation between age and percentage similarity in the DNA methylation pattern [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ between the ages of 20 - 40, the percentage similarity in DNA methylation pattern remains relatively stable **OR** the percentage similarity changed the most between ages 0 - 20 **OR** the greatest change was between 10 - 20 years [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n As **age increases**, the percentage similarity in the DNA methylation pattern **decreases** ($\\checkmark$), however between the ages of **20 - 40**, the percentage similarity remains **relatively stable** ($\\checkmark$), at approximately 82%.\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ differences in environment **OR** changes in lifestyle **OR** diet **OR** exposure to toxins cause differential expression of genes **OR** in the epigenome [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ errors in DNA replication **OR** damage to DNA over time [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Monozygotic twins have more differences in their patterns of methylation as they age, as the **differences**in their **environments** cause **changes** in their **gene expression** patterns ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ downregulates gene expression by inhibiting transcription **OR** preventing transcription factors from binding **OR** silences gene expression [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Direct DNA methylation will most likely **downregulate** gene expression by **inhibiting** gene **transcription** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allows for the control of genetic variation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ identical twins **OR** monozygotic twins are often raised in the same environment so helps isolate the effects of age [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ born with the same sequence of genes **OR** share the same genetic material **OR** develop from a single fertilised ovum **OR** develop from the same zygote that splits [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can be used to study how the environment and genes interact **OR** the epigenome [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Monozygotic twins were used in this study, as they are born with the **same alleles** ($\\checkmark$), allowing for the **control of genetic variation**, so studies conducted on them can be used to study how **genes and the environment interact** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ researchers cannot control the subject’s lifestyle **OR** difficulty in controlling environmental factors [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ subjects may die before the age of 80 [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ subjects may leave the study **OR** be difficult to keep track of [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ small sample size [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ethical considerations as older participants may not be capable of providing informed consent [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n One limitation of conducting a human study over 80 years is that the subjects may **die before the age of 80** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. Any **two** of the following:\n\n *Answer must include at least one positive and one negative statement to achieve full marks*\n\n **Yes because…**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as you age the degree of methylation decreases [1 mark]\n\n **No because…**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ correlation is not causation **OR** the graph does not indicate that hypomethylation is a predictor of death [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the sample size is too small [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Hypomethylation may be an indicator of an early death because **as you age** the degree of **methylation decreases** ($\\checkmark$), however, the graph **does not refer to death rate** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0981","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0981\n\nWhich of the following statements is / are correct regarding the epigenetic regulation of gene expression?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | The base that undergoes most frequent methylation is adenine. |\n    | II. | Gene transcription is inhibited by methylation of DNA |\n    | III. | Methylation of histones only causes activation of transcription |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWhen DNA is methylated at promoter regions, it leads to a closed chromatin structure and prevents transcription factors from binding to the promoter (**Statement II**).  This inhibits the initiation of gene transcription and results in reduced gene expression.\n\nThe most frequently methylated base in epigenetic regulation is cytosine, not adenine (**Statement I**).\n\nMethylation of histones does not just activate transcription (**Statement III**), it can both activate and repress gene transcription, depending on the specific histone residue being methylated and the context of other epigenetic modifications.\n\n**Choice A** is therefore the correct answer.\n**Choices B**, **C** and **D** are incorrect as they include **Statements I** or **III** or both."},{"ref":"BI0982","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0982\n\nWhich of the following correctly pairs changes in the rate mRNA is degraded in the cytoplasm with the effect on the rate polypeptides for the corresponding gene are synthesised?\n\n| | Rate mRNA is degraded | Rate polypeptides for this gene are synthesised\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | increases | decreases |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | increases | increases |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | decreases | decreases |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | decreases | unchanged |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because when there are fewer copies of a given strand of mRNA in the cytoplasm, ribosomes have fewer copies to bind to and thus the rate polypeptides are synthesised based on this strand will decrease, not increase (**Choice B**).\n\n**Choices C** and **D** are incorrect because if the rate mRNA is degraded decreases, there will be more copies of this strand of mRNA resulting in an increase in the rate ribosomes bind to these strands and synthesise polypeptides."},{"ref":"BI0983","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0983\n\nWhich are correct explanations of why the genome, transcriptome and proteome of monozygotic twins may differ?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | The genome of two monozygotic twins will differ only due to random mutations as each individual grows and develops from a zygote into an adult.|\n    | II. | The genome, transcriptome and proteome of two monozygotic twins are the same.|\n    | III. | The transcriptome and proteome of monozygotic twins differ mostly due to environmental factors which affect gene regulation. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is correct because monozygotic twins are genetically identical immediately after an embryo divides into two embryos.  As repeated rounds of cell division and DNA replication occur different mutations accumulate in each twin and so differences in the genome, transcriptome and proteome develop (**Statement II**).  **Statement III** is correct because environmental factors affect which genes are turned on or off and to what extent each gene is transcribed and translated into polypeptides (**Statement II**). Therefore Choice C is correct.\n\n**Choices A** and **D** are incorrect as they include **Statement II**. **Choice B** is incorrect as it does not include **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI0984","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0984\n\nLigers are the offspring of a male lion and a female tiger.  Tigons are the offspring of a female lion and a male tiger.  Ligers tend to be much larger and heavier than their parents, while this is not usually seen with tigons.  What is the best explanation for the large size difference between a liger in comparison to the parents?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Variation in the epigenetic tags removed from genes prior to zygote formation\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The Y chromosome suppresses the X chromosome\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Tigons are always female while ligers are always male\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The chromosome number is different between female and male lions","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because genes of sperm and ova in tigers and lions undergo varying rates of epigenetic tag removal.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect.  Although in some species sex is partially determined by the Y chromosome suppressing the X chromosome, this is not the reason for ligers being larger than their parents.  **Choice C** is incorrect because both tigons and ligons can be both male and female.  **Choice D** is incorrect because female and male lions have the same number of chromosomes."},{"ref":"BI0985","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0985\n\nThe rate mRNA is degraded in the cytoplasm is sometimes reduced in response to environmental stimuli.  How might this mechanism of producing more polypeptides for a given gene increase an individual's fitness?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Greater number of polypeptides increases fitness\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Polypeptide degradation decreases\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Rate of mRNA synthesis increases\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Quantity of ATP used per polypeptide synthesised decreases","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because if mRNA persists in the cytoplasm for a longer period of time, less mRNA needs to be synthesised per polypeptide synthesised.  mRNA synthesis requires ATP so a reduced need to synthesise mRNA conserves energy which may enhance the organism's fitness.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because a greater number of polypeptides does not necessarily increase fitness.  **Choice B** is incorrect because a reduced rate of mRNA degradation does not affect the rate polypeptides are degraded.  **Choice C** is incorrect because if mRNA persists in the cytoplasm for longer periods of time, less mRNA needs to be synthesised to replace degraded mRNA."},{"ref":"BI1042","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1042\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe table below shows estimates of the mean percentage similarity between monozygotic and dizygotic twins and also between brothers in terms of their genomes, transcriptomes and proteomes. Monozygotic twins result from the fertilisation of one egg by one sperm. The resulting embryo later divides into two embryos. Dizygotic twins result when two egg cells are fertilised by two different sperm cells.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Gene%20Expression/images/BI1042.png\" alt=\"BI1042\">\n\n1. Explain why it is important for different cells in multicellular organisms to have different proteomes. [2 marks]\n\n2. Describe how methylation can influence gene expression. [3 marks]\n\n3. Explain why the mean per cent similarity decreases from the most similarity between genomes to the least similarity between proteomes. [3 marks]\n\n4. With reference to the data in the table, discuss the similarities and differences in mean per cent similarity between the dizygotic twins and the brothers. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ different cells in multicellular organisms have different functions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ different proteins have different functions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ any valid example of a named cell **OR** protein performing a specific function, e.g. beta cells regulating blood glucose, muscle cells contracting [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ different cells are found in different areas of the organism and so need to adapt **OR** respond to the specific conditions **OR** signals present in their surroundings [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cells can optimise their energy usage and resource allocation, focusing on synthesising proteins that are necessary for their specialised functions rather than producing a complete set of proteins [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Different cells** in multicellular organisms have **different functions** ($\\checkmark$) and therefore need **different proteins** to carry out these functions ($\\checkmark$). For example, **beta cells** secrete **insulin** to **regulate blood sugar**($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ methylation of DNA represses transcription **OR** methylation of cytosine represses transcription **OR** methylation of DNA in promoter represses transcription [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA methylation interferes with the functioning of RNA polymerase **OR** prevents RNA polymerase from transcribing genes **OR** prevents RNA polymerase **OR** transcription factors from binding [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ methylation of histone proteins **OR** amino acids in histone proteins can cause transcription to be repressed **OR** activated [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ during gamete formation, specific genes on either the maternal or paternal allele are methylated, leading to differential expression of these genes in offspring [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ may silence tumour suppressor genes, promoting uncontrolled cell growth and cancer progression [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Methylation of cytosine** ($\\checkmark$) bases in the **promoter regions** ($\\checkmark$) of genes **represses transcription** ($\\checkmark$) by **interfering** with the functioning of **RNA polymerase** ($\\checkmark$). The **methylation of histone** proteins can either **repress or activate genes** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ genomes change very little over the course of an individual's life [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ genomes only change as a result of mutation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ transcriptomes change as a result of environmental factors [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ transcriptomes change as a result of epigenetic changes to DNA **OR** histone proteins [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ transcriptomes **OR** proteomes change as a result of hormones [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ proteomes change as a result of environmental factors **OR** changes to the transcriptome [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ transcriptomes **OR** proteomes change as a result of ageing [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ transcriptomes OR proteomes change as a result of disease [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ proteomes change as a result of post-transcriptional modifications to mRNA [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ proteomes change as a result of post-translational modifications to polypeptides **OR** proteins [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ proteomes change as a result of protein-protein interactions [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n All types of siblings are born with a given per cent similarity in their **genomes which changes only as a result of mutation** ($\\checkmark$), which amounts to **very little change** over the **course of an individual's life** ($\\checkmark$). **Transcriptomes and proteomes**, on the other hand, **change** more frequently in response to **environmental factors** ($\\checkmark$) and involve **epigenetic changes to DNA and histone proteins** ($\\checkmark$). **Proteomes** can undergo further **changes** as a result of **post-transcriptional modification to mRNA** ($\\checkmark$) and **post-translational modifications to polypeptides** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the genomes are both 50% similar because both brothers and dizygotic twins are the results of a sperm fertilising an egg which becomes a fetus [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the transcriptomes **OR** proteomes are more similar in dizygotic twins because they share the same environment **OR** prenatal environment **OR** because brothers do not share the same prenatal environment [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as dizygotic twins develop in the womb together, they may experience similar early developmental cues, which can impact gene regulation and expression during critical periods of growth and organ formation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the transcriptomes **OR** proteomes of both brothers and dizygotic twins are more different than their genomes because of environmental factors **OR** epigenetic changes, **OR** any other valid example of environmental effects [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **genomes** are both **50% the same** because both **brothers** and **dizygotic twins** are the result of a **sperm fertilising an egg** ($\\checkmark$) and because they both receive **50%** of their **DNA** from **each parent**. The **transcriptomes and proteomes** of the **dizygotic twins are more similar** compared to the brothers because they **share the same prenatal environment** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1172","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1172\n\nThe image shows cytosine and methylated cytosine. What effect does the direct methylation of cytosine in the promoter region of a gene have on gene expression?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Gene%20Expression/images/BI1172.png\" alt=\"BI1172\">\n[Source: Adapted from Walter, M. (2016, December 20). *File: DNA methylation.svg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved September 13, 2023, from Wikimedia commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: DNA_methylation.svg. Copyright under CC BY-SA 4.0]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Promotes gene expression\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Inhibits gene expression\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Has no effect on gene expression\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increases the expression of the gene twofold","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDirect methylation of cytosines in DNA represses transcription and inhibits gene expression; **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D**  are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1220","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1220\n\nWhich statement is **not** true about the genome?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The human genome is organised into chromosomes.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Each liver cell contains a full copy of the genome.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Organisms of the same species share most of their genome.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ All the information carried by the genome is expressed in each cell.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nNot all genes are expressed in each cell. Only the necessary genes are expressed based on the specialised function of the cell. **Choice D** is **not** a true statement, and so is the correct answer.\n\nThe genome is all the genetic information of an organism, including the genes and non-coding DNA. In humans, the genome is organised into linear chromosomes in the nucleus, and circular chromosomes in the mitochondria. **Choice A** is a true statement and so an incorrect answer. Organisms of the same species have most of their genome in common; however, variations give rise to some diversity between individuals. **Choice C** is a true statement and so an incorrect answer. Each cell in a multicellular organism typically contains a full copy of the genome, including liver cells, an exception is red blood cells as they do not have a nucleus. **Choice B** is a true statement and so an incorrect answer."},{"ref":"BI1418","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1418\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nGenetic variation is the root of phenotypic diversity between and within species.\n\n1. Phenotypic expression relies on the genome and proteome. Outline the differences between the genome and the proteome    [4 marks]\n\n2. While genetic variations can be beneficial, others can lead to detrimental health conditions, such as diabetes. Compare and contrast Type I and Type II diabetes.    [4 marks]\n\n3. Describe the approaches used to classify different species.    [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genome is the complete set of genetic material present in an organism **OR** includes all the genes, regulatory elements and non-coding regions of the genetic material of an organism **WHEREAS** proteome is the entire set of proteins produced **OR** expressed by a cell, tissue, or organism at a specific time under defined conditions           [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genome composed of DNA nucleotides **WHEREAS**  proteome composed of amino acids [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genome stores genetic information **OR** is a template for the synthesis of proteome **WHEREAS**  proteome carries out the functions of the organism **OR** is involved in cellular processes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genome relatively stable over time **WHEREAS** proteome is highly dynamic **OR** proteome constantly changing in response to cellular needs **OR** environment **OR** internal signals [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ size of genome is relatively constant within a species **WHEREAS** proteome can be much larger **OR** more diverse than the genome due to alternative splicing, post-translational modifications, and protein-protein interactions    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **genome** is the **complete set of genetic material** present in an organism, including **all** the **genes, regulatory elements** and **non-coding regions**, whereas the **proteome** is the **entire set** of **proteins** produced by a **cell, tissue,** or **organism** at a specific time under defined conditions ($\\checkmark$). The **genome** is made up of **nucleotides**, whereas the **proteome** is composed of **amino acids** ($\\checkmark$). Functionally, the **genome** acts as a source of genetic **instructions**, providing the information necessary for development and regulation, while the **proteome** actively **participates** in **cellular processes** ($\\checkmark$) based on these instructions. The **genome** is relatively **stable** with occasional genetic variations, whereas the **proteome** is highly **dynamic**, responding to environmental factors ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **four** of the following (**AT LEAST ONE** similarity and **AT LEAST ONE** difference must be given):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** Types of diabetes affect blood glucose concentration **OR** affect the body’s ability to control blood glucose concentration        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** types of diabetes cause health complications if blood glucose concentration is not controlled                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Type I is an autoimmune disease **WHEREAS** Type II is linked to genetics **OR** lifestyle **OR** ageing [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Type I diabetes is caused when the beta cells are destroyed by the immune system and do not secrete enough insulin **WHEREAS** Type II diabetes is caused when body cells become insensitive **OR** resistant to insulin    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Type I diabetes usually begins in childhood **WHEREAS** Type II diabetes usually begins in adulthood [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Type I diabetes cannot be controlled by lifestyle changes **WHEREAS** Type II diabetes can often be controlled by lifestyle changes            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Type I diabetes requires frequent measurement of blood glucose concentration **WHEREAS** Type II diabetes does not require frequent measurement of blood glucose concentration                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Type I diabetes requires insulin injections from start **WHEREAS** Type II diabetes is only treated with insulin if the disease progresses        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Type I tends to be diagnosed at early age **WHEREAS** Type II tends to be diagnosed in older people                                [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Both** Type I and Type II diabetes **disrupt** the **regulation** of **normal blood glucose concentration** ($\\checkmark$). **Type I diabetes** is an **autoimmune disease** where the **immune system destroys beta cells in the pancreas**, resulting in a **lack of insulin** production. The causes of **Type II diabetes** are **multifactorial**, including **genetics, lifestyle** and **ageing** ($\\checkmark$), and it manifests when body **cells** become **resistant to insulin** ($\\checkmark$). **Type I diabetes** requires **frequent measurement of blood glucose** concentration, whereas **Type II diabetes** does **not usually require** frequent measurements of blood glucose concentration ($\\checkmark$). **Type I** diabetes requires lifelong **insulin injections**, whereas **Type II** diabetes only requires **insulin** injections if the **condition progresses** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ classification is used to organise organisms into groups             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ binomial nomenclature **OR** binomial system used to name organisms **OR** name is genus and species      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows communication and understanding between scientists all around the world        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there are three domains of life                     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a species is the smallest unit of classification    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hierarchy is used to group species **OR** the taxa are arranged in order of decreasing group size: domain, kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus, species        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ morphological **OR** artificial classification is based on physical characteristics        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ morphology can lead to errors **OR** analogous structures can mislead classification  [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ biochemical classification analyses molecular components **OR** proteins **OR** DNA **OR** RNA  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ base sequences in nucleic acids **OR** amino acid sequences of proteins are analysed     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cladistics is based on shared characteristics traced to most recent common ancestor **OR** cladogram is a visual representation of how species are evolutionarily related, with a node representing a common ancestor        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ modern approach combines evidence **OR** uses all types of classification **OR** uses morphology, biochemistry and genetics      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ natural classification groups organisms based on evolutionary history    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ classification includes fossils        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms with a recent common ancestor are classified more closely together    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ species in the same genus share many characteristics          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms can be reclassified when new evidence is found      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ natural classification allows scientists to predict characteristics of species within a group  [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Classification is the process of **organising organisms** into appropriate categories or **groups** based on their **shared characteristics** ($\\checkmark$). Scientists use **binomial nomenclature** to assign **each organism** a unique two-part name made up of the **genus** and **species** ($\\checkmark$), facilitating **global scientific communication** ($\\checkmark$). Organisms are traditionally classified **hierarchically**, starting with the broadest category, the **domain**, then working downwards -  **kingdom, phylum, class, order, family genus, species** ($\\checkmark$) (which can then be split into sub-species). **Traditional, artificial**, classification has been based on **physical traits. However**, this **morphological** approach ($\\checkmark$) can be **misleading** due to **analogous structures** ($\\checkmark$). **Modern** classification integrates **morphological, biochemica**l, and **genetic** information, ($\\checkmark$). **Biochemical** classification examines the **molecular components** of organisms, including the **sequences** of **bases** of  **DNA** and **amino acids** of **proteins** ($\\checkmark$). **Cladistics** is based on **shared characteristics** traced to the **most recent common ancestor**, and these relationships can be represented visually on **cladograms** ($\\checkmark$). As **new data emerges, reclassification** may occur ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1583","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1583\n\nWhich of the following chromatin segments would result in a higher-than-normal rate of transcription with the addition of methyl groups to the DNA or acetyl groups to the histones, as shown in the diagrams?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Gene%20Expression/images/BI1583.png\" alt=\"BI1583\">\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A. | | $\\hspace{2cm}$ | |B.|\n| |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Gene%20Expression/images/BI1583A.png\" alt=\"BI1583A\"> | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Gene%20Expression/images/BI1583B.png\" alt=\"BI1583B\">|\n|C. | | | |D. |\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Gene%20Expression/images/BI1583C.png\" alt=\"BI1583C\"> | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Gene%20Expression/images/BI1583D.png\" alt=\"BI1583D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because acetylation involves the addition of acetyl groups to the histone proteins around which DNA is wound. This modification generally reduces the positive charge on histones, weakening their interaction with the negatively charged DNA. As a result, the chromatin becomes more open and accessible to transcription factors and RNA polymerase, enhancing transcription. Conversely, methylation of DNA reduces the binding of transcription factors, effectively silencing genes. Since this segment of chromatin shows only acetylation, transcription would be increased.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because DNA methylation and acetylation of histones have antagonistic effects, possibly causing no change in the rate of transcription. **Choice C** is incorrect because only DNA methylation is occurring, resulting in gene silencing. **Choice D** is incorrect because there is more DNA methylation than acetylation of histones, most likely leading to reduced transcription."},{"ref":"BI2287","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2287\n\nWhich of the following statements about gene expression in eukaryotic organisms is correct?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ A single gene always produces one protein.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Gene expression can be regulated at multiple stages.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ All genes are expressed in all cells.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Gene expression is not affected by environmental factors.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nGene expression is a complex process that can be regulated at various stages, including transcription, RNA processing, mRNA stability, translation, and post-translational modification of proteins. This flexibility allows cells to control protein production based on internal and external factors. **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because a single gene can produce multiple proteins through mechanisms such as alternative splicing, post-translational modifications, or the use of alternative promoters. These processes enable a single gene to produce a variety of protein isoforms depending on the cellular context.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because not all genes are expressed in all cells. The expression of genes is highly cell-specific and depends on the cell type, function, and environment. For example, liver cells express genes for detoxification enzymes, while neurons express genes for neurotransmitter receptors.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because environmental factors such as temperature, light, stress, diet, and exposure to pollutants can significantly impact gene expression. For example, environmental stress can activate stress response genes, and dietary components like lactose can regulate operons in bacteria."},{"ref":"BI2291","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2291\n\nA scientist observes that an mRNA with a short poly-A tail is degraded rapidly, but when the poly-A tail is artificially lengthened, the mRNA persists longer. Which of the following experimental observations would provide the best evidence that the poly-A tail regulates translation efficiency in addition to stability?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ mRNA with a longer poly-A tail is less affected by nuclease activity.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ mRNA with a longer poly-A tail produces more stable proteins.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ mRNA with a longer poly-A tail is transcribed more efficiently.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ mRNA with a longer poly-A tail binds ribosomes more frequently.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe poly-A tail not only enhances mRNA stability by protecting it from degradation but also plays a role in translation efficiency. Ribosome binding is a critical step in translation initiation, and the poly-A tail promotes ribosome recruitment through interactions with proteins. Observing increased ribosome binding in mRNA with a longer poly-A tail directly links the tail to enhanced translation efficiency. **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because while this observation explains the role of the poly-A tail in enhancing mRNA stability, it does not address translation efficiency. Stability and translation efficiency are separate functions, and the question specifically asks for evidence of the tail's role in translation.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because protein stability depends on factors like post-translational modifications and protein degradation pathways, not directly on the length of the poly-A tail. This observation would not directly link the poly-A tail to translation efficiency.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because transcription refers to the synthesis of mRNA from DNA and occurs in the nucleus. The poly-A tail is added post-transcriptionally and does not influence the efficiency of transcription itself."},{"ref":"BI2293","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2293\n\nThe diagram below shows methylated and unmethylated DNA.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Gene%20Expression/images/methylated%20and%20unmethylated%20DNA%20grey%20scale%20i.png\" alt=\"methylated and unmethylated DNA grey scale RVi\">\n[@ 2025. Created in https://BioRender.com]\n\nWhich statement about DNA methylation is correct?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Methylation enhances gene expression.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Methylation prevents transcription.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Methylation alters the DNA sequence.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Methylation increases protein synthesis.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDNA methylation typically occurs at cytosine residues near promoter regions. This modification represses transcription by blocking the binding of transcription factors or recruiting proteins that condense chromatin (e.g., methyl-CpG binding proteins). As a result, transcription is inhibited, and the gene is effectively silenced. **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because DNA methylation generally represses, rather than enhances, gene expression by preventing transcription factor binding and recruiting chromatin-condensing proteins.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because methylation does not change the nucleotide sequence of DNA. It is an epigenetic modification that adds a methyl group to cytosine bases without altering the underlying genetic code.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because methylation reduces or silences gene expression by repressing transcription, ultimately decreasing, not increasing, protein synthesis."},{"ref":"BI2294","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2294\n\nWhich of the following contribute(s) to the differences between the transcriptome and proteome?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | RNA processing |\n    | II. | Protein modification |\n    | III. | Gene expression patterns |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nmultiple different mRNA molecules from a single gene. These different mRNA molecules may result in the production of different proteins, contributing to differences between the transcriptome (the set of all mRNA molecules) and the proteome (the set of all proteins). **Statement I** is true.\n\nPost-translational modifications alter the structure and function of proteins without affecting the transcriptome. This is a key factor contributing to the diversity of the proteome compared to the transcriptome. **Statement II** is true.\n\nGene expression patterns dictate which genes are transcribed into mRNA and, subsequently, which proteins are produced. Differences in gene expression across cell types lead to variations in both the transcriptome and proteome. **Statement III** is true.\n\n**Choice D** is correct as it includes all the true **Statements** (**I, II** and **III**).\n\n**Choices A, B** and **C** are all incorrect as they are missing one or more of the true statements."},{"ref":"BI2296","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2296\n\nWhich of the following statements about the cell’s proteome is true?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | It is larger than the transcriptome due to post-translational modifications. |\n    | II. | It varies from the proteomes of other cell types.|\n    | III. | It includes all proteins a cell can potentially produce.|\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nPost-translational modifications (e.g., phosphorylation, glycosylation, and acetylation) create protein diversity that exceeds the number of mRNAs in the transcriptome. A single mRNA can give rise to multiple protein variants through these modifications, making the proteome larger than the transcriptome. **Statement I** is true.\n\nThe proteome differs between cell types because each cell expresses a unique subset of proteins based on its specific role. For example, muscle cells express contractile proteins like actin and myosin, while neurons express neurotransmitter-related proteins. **Statement II** is true.\n\nThe proteome refers to the actual set of proteins expressed in a cell at a given time, not the total potential protein repertoire encoded by the genome. The \"potential\" protein repertoire would encompass all proteins that could be produced by the genome, which is not the definition of the proteome. **Statement III** is false.\n\n**Choice C** is the correct answer as it includes only the true **Statements I** and **II**.\n\n**Choices A** and **B** are incorrect as they are missing one of the true statements. **Choice D** is incorrect as it includes the false **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI2298","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2298\n\nA researcher observes that a gene is silenced despite being unmethylated in its promoter. What might explain this observation?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Methylation of histones near the gene\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Activation of RNA polymerase\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Lack of RNA splicing\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Absence of introns","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nMethylation of histones, particularly at specific residues (e.g., H3K9 or H3K27), can lead to chromatin condensation, forming heterochromatin. This structure inhibits the binding of transcription factors and RNA polymerase to the promoter, silencing the gene even if the promoter itself is unmethylated. Histone methylation can repress gene expression without the need for DNA methylation in the promoter. **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because activation of RNA polymerase would lead to gene expression, not silencing. If the gene is silenced, RNA polymerase is likely unable to bind or initiate transcription due to chromatin structure or other regulatory mechanisms.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because splicing occurs after transcription and does not influence whether a gene is transcribed. A lack of RNA splicing would affect the maturation of the mRNA, not the silencing of the gene.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because, while introns can play regulatory roles, their absence does not inherently silence a gene. Many genes, such as bacterial genes, lack introns and are still actively expressed. The presence or absence of introns is unrelated to transcriptional regulation in this context."},{"ref":"BI2393","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2393\n\nWhich of the following describes the *lac* operon, as found in *Escherichia coli (E. coli*)?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Lactose interacts with a regulator protein, preventing it from binding with the promoter region of the gene.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The *lac* operon is activated in the presence or absence of lactose.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The *lac* operon operates by controlling the level of translation of mRNA.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The *lac* operon is also present in mammals to aid the digestion of lactose in milk.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because lactose interacts with the *lac* repressor protein, preventing it from binding to the operator region, located between the promoter and the structural genes for lactose-metabolising enzymes. If the operator is blocked, RNA is blocked from transcribing the gene. Once the operator is unblocked, RNA polymerase is free to transcribe the structural genes.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the *lac* operon is only activated when the bacterium is in the presence of lactose. This means that the bacterium does not waste cellular resources expressing a gene (for the metabolism of lactose) whose product would not be useful in the absence of lactose in the surroundings.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because the *lac* operon works by controlling the level of transcription, not translation.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the *lac* operon is found exclusively in prokaryotes, such as *E. coli*. Gene regulation in eukaryotes is far more complex and does not involve operons like the *lac* operon."},{"ref":"BI2448","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2448\n\nWhich row is correct for the mechanism by which genes affect the phenotype?\n\n| | Name of mechanism | Product of mechanism |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Gene expression | mRNA |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Translation | mRNA |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Transcription | Protein |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Gene expression | Protein |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is the correct answer because gene expression is the mechanism by which genes affect the phenotype. When a gene is expressed the sequence is transcribed onto mRNA and this mRNA is used by the ribosome to synthesise amino acids in the correct sequence to produce the protein which is the functional final product.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because mRNA is not the end product of gene expression. mRNA would be used by the ribosome to create a protein, which is the functional gene product that ultimately affects the genotype.\n\n**Choices B** and **C** are incorrect, as transcription (DNA to RNA) and translation (RNA to protein) are steps in the mechanism of gene expression."},{"ref":"BI2449","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2449\n\nIn an organism, which of the following regions has the most similar DNA sequence across all genes?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Poly-A tail\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Promoter\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Enhancer\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Silencer","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because promoters are DNA sequences near the start of a gene where RNA polymerase binds to initiate transcription. While there is some variation, promoters generally have similar sequences.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the poly-A tail is a long sequence of adenine bases that are added to mRNA after transcription, they are not found on genes. **Choice C** and **choice D** are incorrect because the sequences of enhancers and silencers are very variable which allows precise regulation of genes."},{"ref":"BI2450","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2450\n\nWhich of these enzymes has a role in regulating translation?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Nucleases\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Proteases\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Polymerases\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ligases","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because nucleases play a role in regulating translation by degrading mRNA. Once mRNA is degraded, the ribosome can no longer translate it into a protein, effectively stopping translation.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because proteases break down proteins into amino acids, they do not degrade mRNA. **Choice C** is incorrect because DNA and RNA polymerases assemble these molecules, they do not break down DNA or RNA. **Choice D** is incorrect because ligases join molecules together, for example, DNA ligase."},{"ref":"BI2451","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2451\n\nThe image shows the methylation of histone tails.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Gene%20Expression/images/BI2451.png\" alt=\"BI2451\">\n[Source: National Institute of Health (2005). *Epigenetic mechanisms.jpg*. Wikimedia. Retrieved January 18, 2025, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Epigenetic_mechanisms.jpg. Copyright under Public domain]\n\nWhich statement is true for the effect(s) of epigenetic changes?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Gene expression is always decreased\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Genotype is altered but phenotype is not\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Phenotype is altered but genotype is not\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Gene expression is always increased","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because epigenetic tags regulate gene expression, altering the phenotype without changing the DNA sequence.\n\n**Choice A** and **Choice D** are incorrect because epigenetic tags can either increase or decrease gene expression. **Choice B** is incorrect because epigenetic tags do not alter the genotype; they affect gene expression, which affects the phenotype."},{"ref":"BI2452","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2452\n\nWhich row is correct for genome, transcriptome, and proteome in a cell?\n\n| | Genome | Transcriptome | Proteome |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | The genes in the nucleus | The genes that are transcribed | The proteins that the genes code for |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | All of the genetic information | The genes that can be transcribed | The entire set of proteins produced |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | The full set of transcribed mRNAs | All of the genetic information | The entire set of proteins produced |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | All of the genetic information | The full set of transcribed mRNAs | The entire set of proteins produced |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because these are the correct definitions. The genome consists of all of a cell's genetic material, including genes in the nucleus, mitochondria, and (in plants) chloroplasts. The transcriptome refers to all of the mRNA molecules transcribed from DNA in a given cell at a specific time. Not all genes are transcribed, and the transcriptome varies between cell types. The proteome is the complete set of proteins synthesized in a cell, which depends on the transcriptome and post-translational modifications.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because the definitions for genome and transcriptome are incorrect. **Choice A** is incorrect because the genome includes genes in the mitochondria and chloroplasts. The transcriptome is not the genes that are transcribed, it is the strands of mRNA produced by transcription. The proteome is only the proteins that are produced by a cell, not all of the proteins that the genome has instructions for. **Choice B** is incorrect because the transcriptome is not the genes that can be transcribed, it is the mRNA strands that are produced by transcription."},{"ref":"BI2453","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2453\n\nWhich row is correct for the effect of methylation on transcription?\n\n| | Methylation of promoters | Methylation of amino acids in histones|\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Represses transcription | Represses or activates transcription |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Activates transcription | Represses transcription |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Represses or activates transcription | Represses or activates transcription |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Activates transcriptions | Activates transcription |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because when a methyl group attaches to promoters it reduces transcription. When a methyl group attaches to amino acids in histones it can repress or activate transcription.\n\n**Choices B, C**, and **D** are incorrect because at least one of the statements is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI2454","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2454\n\nWhich statement/s are true for epigenetic inheritance?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Epigenetic inheritance is not always permanent.|\n    | II. | Epigenetic tags are passed on to daughter cells during mitosis. |\n    | III. | Most epigenetic tags are not passed from gametes to zygotes. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because all three statements are true. Epigenetic tags can be removed after fertilisation, so they are not always permanent. Cells produced by mitosis can carry the pattern of epigenetic tags, but most of the epigenetic tags in gametes are removed before fertilisation.\n\n**Choices A, C**, and **D** are incorrect because they are all missing one or more true statements."},{"ref":"BI2455","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2455\n\nThe diagram shows methyl tags attached to histone tails.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Gene%20Expression/images/BI2455.png\" alt=\"BI2455\">\n\nWhich of the following best describes how air pollution affects histone methylation?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ It always increases methylation\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ It always decreases methylation\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ It can either increase or decrease methylation\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ It only causes acetylation, not methylation","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because air pollution can lead to either increased or decreased methylation depending on the type of pollutant, the specific histone modification and the tissue or cell type.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because while air pollution can increase methylation at certain histone sites, it does not do so universally. Some genes show decreased methylation in response to pollutants.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because numerous studies show increased methylation at certain histone marks or gene promoters due to air pollution.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because air pollution can influence both acetylation and methylation of histone, depending on the molecular pathways involved."},{"ref":"BI2456","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2456\n\nWhich statement explains the low numbers of epigenetic tags on the DNA in ova and sperm?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Epigenetic reprogramming during gametogenesis and early stages of zygote.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Epigenetic reprogramming of germ cells in testes and ova.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Epigenetic tags are removed from parent DNA before meiosis.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Epigenetic tags are not found on DNA in germ cells in the testes and ova.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because epigenetic reprogramming occurs during gametogenesis (spermatogenesis and oogenesis) and again in the early zygote, leading to the removal of most epigenetic tags from DNA.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because epigenetic epigenetic reprogramming happens during meiosis of the cells that are produced by mitosis of the germ cells, it does not happen in the germ cells. **Choice C** is incorrect because some of the epigenetic tags are passed to haploid cells by meiosis. **Choice D** is incorrect because epigenetic tags are found on the DNA of germ cells in the testes and ova."}]},{"name":"Homeostasis","questions":[{"ref":"BI0055","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0055\n\nThe graph shows the changes in blood pH levels of an individual before exercise, during exercise and after exercise.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Homeostasis/images/BI0055.png\" alt=\"BI0055\">\n\nWhich statement may explain the changes in blood pH shown in the graph?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The blood pH decreases due to the build-up of lactate.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The blood pH decreases due to muscle cells undergoing anaerobic respiration in the presence of oxygen.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The blood pH decreases due to the continued anaerobic respiration caused by oxygen debt.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The blood pH decreases due to the large yield of ATP produced during strenuous exercise.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because during strenuous exercise, muscle cells may not receive enough oxygen to meet energy demands through aerobic respiration alone. As a result, some muscle cells begin anaerobic respiration, producing lactate. Lactate accumulation lowers blood pH, explaining the decrease shown in the graph.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because anaerobic respiration occurs in the absence of oxygen, not in the presence of oxygen.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because oxygen debt develops after strenuous exercise due to insufficient oxygen availability, but it is not the direct cause of the decrease in blood pH. The decrease in pH is caused by lactate accumulation.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because anaerobic respiration produces only a small yield of ATP per glucose molecule. ATP production itself does not directly decrease blood pH."},{"ref":"BI0185","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0185\n\nWhich fluid acts as a coolant in the human body?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Tears\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Sweat\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Urine\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Saliva","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSweat (**Choice B**) is a coolant and helps to cool the body.\\\nTears (**Choice A**), urine (**Choice C**) and saliva (**Choice D**) do not lower or raise the body temperature."},{"ref":"BI0479","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0479\n\nWhich row is correct for insulin?\n\n| | Stimulated by: | Secreted by: |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| High blood glucose concentration | Beta cells |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | High blood glucose concentration | Alpha cells |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Low blood glucose concentration | Beta cells |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Low blood glucose concentration | Alpha cells |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because high  blood glucose concentration is detected by the beta cells in the pancreas and they respond by releasing insulin. **Choice B** is incorrect because alpha cells produce glucagon and not insulin. **Choice C** is incorrect because the release of insulin is triggered by high blood glucose concentration and not low blood glucose concentration. **Choice D** is incorrect because low blood glucose concentration does not cause the release of insulin and insulin is not secreted by alpha cells."},{"ref":"BI0480","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0480\n\nWhich row is correct for glucagon?\n\n| | Stimulated by: | Secreted by: |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| High blood glucose concentration | Beta cells |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | High blood glucose concentration | Alpha cells |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Low blood glucose concentration | Beta cells |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Low blood glucose concentration | Alpha cells |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because low blood glucose concentration is detected by the alpha cells in the pancreas and they respond by releasing glucagon. **Choice A** is incorrect because high blood concentration does not cause the secretion of glucagon and it is not secreted by beta cells. **Choice B** is incorrect because high blood concentration does not cause the secretion of glucagon, it causes the release of insulin. **Choice C** is incorrect because glucagon is not secreted by beta cells."},{"ref":"BI0529","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0529\n\nWhich letter indicates the part of the nephron that is impermeable to salts and plays a role in increasing filtrate solute concentration to 200 - 300% relative to normal blood solute concentration?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Homeostasis/images/BI0529.png\" alt=\"BI0529\">\n[Source: Adapted from Servier Medical Art. (n.d). *Nephron*. Smart servier medical art. Retrieved March 3, 2022, from https://smart.servier.com/smart_image/nephron-3/. Copyright under CC BY 3.0]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe descending limb of the loop of Henle is permeable to water but not salts. As the filtrate flows down the descending limb, it becomes progressively more concentrated as osmosis draws water out of the filtrate into the medulla. The result near the bottom of the descending limb is a filtrate whose solute concentration is between 200% and 300% that of the normal blood solute concentration. This position is labelled **B** in the diagram.\n\nIn the proximal convoluted tubule (**Choice A**), substances such as sodium are selectively reabsorbed from the filtrate into the blood. In the ascending limb of the loop of Henle (**Choice C**), sodium, but not water, is actively transported into the medulla. In the distal convoluted tubule (**Choice D**), the concentrations of individual solutes, such as sodium, potassium, and calcium, are adjusted by active transport."},{"ref":"BI0733","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0733\n\nWhich row shows a correct difference in the blood concentration of various substances between the renal artery and renal vein?\n\n| | Substance | Renal artery | Renal vein |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Oxygen | Low | High |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Carbon dioxide | Low | High |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Glucose | High | High |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Urea | Low | High |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because carbon dioxide concentration is higher in the renal vein due to aerobic respiration in the kidney.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because oxygen concentration is higher in the renal artery; it is used by kidney cells for aerobic cellular respiration.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because glucose concentration is lower in the renal vein as some of it is used for metabolic processes in kidney cells.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because urea concentration is higher in the renal artery; urea is filtered out in the nephrons."},{"ref":"BI0734","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0734\n\nWhich label corresponds to the medulla of the kidney?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Homeostasis/images/BI0734.png\" alt=\"BI0734\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because the medulla is the inner region of the kidney.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the outer region of the kidney is the cortex.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because it is the renal artery.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because it is the ureter."},{"ref":"BI0736","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0736\n\nThis diagram shows the basic structure of a nephron.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Homeostasis/images/BI0736.png\" alt=\"BI0736\">\n\nHow does the nephron differ between animals that have evolved in areas with low availability of water, for example, in the desert?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Larger surface area in glomerulus\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Higher density of capillaries in glomerulus\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Shorter proximal convoluted tubule\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Longer loop of Henle","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because animals that have evolved in environments where they need to conserve water have longer loops of Henle for increased reabsorption of water.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because a larger surface area in the glomerulus would lead to increased water concentration in the filtrate.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because a greater number of capillaries in the glomerulus would lead to more water being filtered out into the urine.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because a shorter proximal convoluted tubule would reduce the reabsorption of water and other substances."},{"ref":"BI0737","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0737\n\nWhich statement(s) is/are true for antidiuretic hormone (ADH)?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | ADH affects the permeability of the loop of Henle |\n    | II. | ADH production increases with low water intake |\n    | III. | ADH increases the permeability of the collecting duct |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is not true, ADH does not affect the loop of Henle.\n\n**Statements II** and **III** are both true. Low water intake stimulates ADH production. ADH increases the water reabsorption at the collecting duct, resulting in smaller volumes of more concentrated urine and so reducing water loss."},{"ref":"BI0738","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0738\n\nWhich substance is normally almost completely reabsorbed from the filtrate in the proximal tubule?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Urea\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Glucose\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Water\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sodium ions","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because, under normal conditions, almost all filtered glucose is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule and returned to the blood. Glucose enters the tubule cells by sodium–glucose cotransport and leaves them towards the blood by facilitated diffusion.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because only part of the filtered urea is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule; urea is not almost completely reabsorbed there.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because, although most filtered water is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule by osmosis, a substantial proportion remains in the filtrate and passes into the loop of Henle.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because only part of the filtered sodium ions is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule. Further sodium reabsorption occurs in later regions of the nephron."},{"ref":"BI0739","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0739\n\nWhich row correctly describes the effect of a decrease in the water concentration of the blood?\n\n| | ADH production | Permeability of collecting duct | Urine concentration |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Decrease | Increase | Increase|\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Decrease | Decrease | Increase |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Increase | Increase | Increase |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Increase | Decrease | Decrease |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because when the water concentration of the blood decreases, ADH production increases. ADH increases the permeability of the collecting duct so that more water is reabsorbed, and this increases the concentration of urine.\n\n**Choices A** and **B** are incorrect because when the water concentration of the blood decreases, ADH production increases. The increase in ADH production increases the permeability of the collecting duct.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because increased ADH production increases the permeability of the collecting duct and increases the concentration of urine."},{"ref":"BI0740","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0740\n\nWhich of these substances should not pass through the glomerulus and into Bowman’s capsule?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Amino acids\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ White blood cells\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Water\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Glucose","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because, in a healthy kidney, white blood cells are too large to pass from the glomerulus into the Bowman’s capsule. **Choices A, C** and **D** are incorrect because amino acids, water and glucose are small enough to be filtered from the capillaries in the glomerulus into the Bowman’s capsule."},{"ref":"BI0742","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0742\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline how the glomerulus and Bowman’s capsule are adapted for their function.     [4 marks]\n\n2. Explain the differences between the composition of blood in the renal artery and renal vein.         [4 marks]\n\n3. Describe the processes in the nephron that leads to the production of urine. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large surface area [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fenestrations **OR** large pores in the walls of the capillaries that allow ultrafiltration      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ podocytes allow fluid to pass through freely [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ basement membrane only allows molecules below a certain size to pass through    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ afferent arterioles are wider than efferent arterioles, so (hydrostatic) pressure is increased in the glomerulus    [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    The glomerulus and Bowman’s capsule are adapted for their function because they have a **large surface area** ($\\checkmark$). There are **fenestrations** ($\\checkmark$) in the walls of the capillaries that allow ultrafiltration, the **basement membrane** only allow molecules below a certain size to pass through ($\\checkmark$) and **podocytes** allow fluid to pass through freely ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following (difference and reason both need to be mentioned in order to get a marking point):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ blood in the renal artery has a higher concentration of oxygen due to aerobic respiration in the cells of the kidney    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (blood in the renal artery has a) higher concentration of glucose due to cell respiration in the kidney     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ blood in the renal artery has a higher concentration of urea because urea is filtered out of the blood by the nephron     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ blood in the renal vein has a higher concentration of carbon dioxide because it is a waste product of aerobic respiration in the cells of the kidney    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ blood in the renal vein has balanced optimum levels of water and ions compared to the renal artery because the ultra-filtration and selective reabsorption in the nephron results in the optimum concentrations     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the renal artery may contain toxins and undigestible pigments that are not found in the renal vein due to ultrafiltration in the nephron         [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Blood in the renal vein has lower concentrations of **oxygen** ($\\checkmark$) and **glucose** ($\\checkmark$) and a higher concentration of **carbon dioxide** due to **aerobic respiration** ($\\checkmark$) in the kidney cells. Blood in the renal artery has a higher concentration of **urea** because urea is **filtered** out ($\\checkmark$) of the blood in the glomerulus. Blood in the renal artery does not have **optimum water and ions concentrations** ($\\checkmark$). Those get balanced out by the ultrafiltration and consecutive re-absorption of both. The renal artery may contain **toxins** ($\\checkmark$) which are not found in the renal vein because they would be filtered out in the glomerulus and have no way of being re-absorbed.\n\n3. any **eight** of the following (answers could be accepted in the form of an annotated diagram):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ultrafiltration happens in Bowman’s capsule  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glomerulus has structural features for ultrafiltration **OR** glomerulus has fenestrations, podocytes and basal membrane to increase permeability **OR** substances are filtered from the blood due to the high pressure in the glomerulus caused by afferent arteriole being wider than the efferent [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water, salts, glucose, urea, water and mineral ions are filtered out of the blood [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ substances from the filtrate are reabsorbed by osmosis, simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion and active transport  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in the proximal convoluted tubule, all of the glucose is reabsorbed (into the blood) [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in the proximal convoluted tubule, most of the water, **OR** most of the salts, is, **OR** are, reabsorbed [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in the descending limb of the loop of Henle, water is reabsorbed  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in the ascending limb of the loop of Henle, sodium ions are reabsorbed by active transport **OR** are actively pumped out of the nephron **OR** glomerular filtrate     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the active transport of sodium ions in the ascending limb of the loop of Henle increases the solute concentration of the medulla, which increases water reabsorption in the descending limb and collecting duct  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in the distal convoluted tubule, mineral ions are reabsorbed into the blood, and blood pH and ion concentrations are balanced   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when blood water concentration falls, ADH is released from the pituitary gland **OR** when blood water potential decreases, osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus detect it and stimulate the release of ADH  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ADH results in more aquaporins in the plasma membrane of cells of the collecting duct wall, allowing more water to be reabsorbed         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a nephron consists of Bowman’s capsule and glomerulus, a proximal convoluted tubule, a loop of Henle, a distal convoluted tubule and a collecting duct (all have to be named and in the correct order for this marking point)     [1 mark]\n[8 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    **Ultrafiltration** happens in Bowman’s capsule ($\\checkmark$), and **reabsorption** of substances through osmosis, simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion, and active transport happens in the rest of the nephron ($\\checkmark$). The filtrate contains **water, salts, glucose, urea, water and mineral ions** ($\\checkmark$). In the **proximal convoluted tubule**, most water and salt ($\\checkmark$) are reabsorbed, as well as **all of the glucose** ($\\checkmark$). In the **descending limb** of the loop of Henle, water is reabsorbed ($\\checkmark$). In the **ascending limb** of the loop of Henle, sodium ions are reabsorbed by active transport ($\\checkmark$). The active transport of salts **increases the solute concentration of the medulla** ($\\checkmark$), which allows a higher volume of water to be reabsorbed in the descending limb and collecting duct. In the **distal convoluted tubule**, mineral ions are reabsorbed, and the pH and ion concentration of the blood is balanced ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0817","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0817\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nCreatine is a naturally occurring chemical produced in the body which supplies energy to muscles. It’s also a supplement used widely in the fitness industry to reduce recovery times and improve athletic performance.\n\nCreatine is broken down into creatinine which is toxic in high concentrations. A study was conducted on the effect of creatine supplementation on the creatinine concentration in the urine. Urine samples were collected from humans, and the creatinine concentration was measured.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Homeostasis/images/BI0817A.png\" alt=\"BI0817A\">\n[Source: Adapted from Yang, C-Y., Chen, F-A., Chen, C-F., Liu, W-S., Shih, C-J., Ou, S-M., et al. (2015) *Diagnostic Accuracy of Urine Protein/Creatinine Ratio Is Influenced by Urine Concentration*. PLoS ONE 10(9): e0137460. Retrieved May 29th, 2023 from https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0137460. Copyright free.]\n\nThe results from the study were used to make up corresponding creatine solutions to mimic the urine samples. These creatine solutions were then given to students to analyse. The specific gravity of each sample was measured using a hydrometer, a small graduated float used to compare the density of a liquid between two samples.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Homeostasis/images/BI0817B.png\" alt=\"BI0817B\">\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Homeostasis/images/BI0817C.png\" alt=\"BI0817C\">\n[Source: Adapted from Yang, C-Y., Chen, F-A., Chen, C-F., Liu, W-S., Shih, C-J., Ou, S-M., et al. (2015) *Diagnostic Accuracy of Urine Protein/Creatinine Ratio Is Influenced by Urine Concentration*. PLoS ONE 10(9): e0137460. Retrieved May 29th, 2023 from https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0137460. Copyright free]\n\n1.\n    1. Describe how urea enters the urine from the blood.  [2 marks]\n\n    2. The urine samples were collected from the subjects after the same intensity of exercise. Suggest how increased intensity of exercise could change creatinine concentration in the samples.      [2 marks]\n\n2. Suggest a variable that would need to be controlled during the student’s experiment. [1 mark]\n\n3. Suggest one advantage and one disadvantage of using model urine samples instead of human urine.         [2 marks]\n\n4. Calculate, as a percentage, how much greater the urine sample specific gravity with 10g of supplemental creatine intake was compared to that with 5g of supplemental intake. [1 mark]\n\n5. Describe **TWO** reasons why mice were used in this experiment and not humans.     [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ blood enters the kidney through the afferent arteriole  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ afferent arteriole is wider than efferent arteriole so pressure in glomerulus is high        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ high-pressure filtration **OR** ultrafiltration forces urea from blood **OR** plasma into Bowman’s capsule            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ urea is small **OR** water-soluble so passes freely through basement membrane into filtrate  [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Blood enters the nephron through the **afferent arteriole**, creating **high pressure** in the **glomerulus ** ($\\checkmark$). **High-pressure filtration** forces urea from the blood plasma into the **Bowman’s capsule** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    2. any **two** of the following:\n\n    ***increased creatinine concentration***:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased creatinine production **OR** increased breakdown of creatine in muscles due to more energy required for muscle activity    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ creatinine becomes more concentrated in nephron due to kidneys conserving water during exercise [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ exercise-induced muscle damage can increase creatine breakdown         [1 mark]\n\n     ***decreased creatinine concentration***:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reduced blood flow to the kidneys OR lower glomerular filtration rate during exercise  [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    During exercise, the **increased breakdown of creatine** in muscles to meet **higher energy demands** leads to **more creatinine** production, which can increase its concentration in the urine ($\\checkmark$). However, **reduced blood flow** to the **kidneys** during exercise **lowers the glomerular filtration rate**, which could **decrease** the **concentration** of creatinine in the urine ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the temperature of the artificial sample     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the same type of hydrometer                 [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ thorough mixing of samples (to prevent solute settlement)     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ time elapsed since sample was created and when hydrometer reading was taken     [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The the **type of hydrometer** used for obtaining the results would have to be the **same** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **one** of the following:\n\n    *Advantage*:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ artificial samples are more sanitary **OR** are safer for students to use [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ artificial samples can be recreated many times [1 mark]\n\n    Any **one** of the following:\n\n    *Weakness*:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ artificial samples only act as a model **OR** may not be exactly the same as\n    the urine sample **OR** are simplified versions of urine samples    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lack of interference factors                         [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The artificial samples are safer for students to use as they are **more sanitary** ($\\checkmark$) and have less risk of containing harmful bacteria or other pathogens. The artificial samples are acting as **models**, so they are more **simplified** ($\\checkmark$) versions of the urine samples, so they might not contain everything the urine sample has.\n\n4.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 66.7 (accept answers in the range of 66.6 - 66.7)     [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Homeostasis/images/BI0817ANSC.png\" alt=\"BI0817ANSC\">\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Percentage Increase = $\\left(\\dfrac{\\text{ final value-initial value}}{\\text{initial value}}\\right) $ X 100%\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Specific gravity for 5g supplement intake = 15\\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Specific gravity for 10g supplement intake =25\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 25 - 15 = 10\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 10/15 x 100%\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ = **66.7%** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n5. any **two** of the following\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ less ethical considerations     [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ require less space **OR** resources **OR** easier to keep in a lab **OR** cheaper [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ most model organisms have shorter lifespans **OR** life cycles **OR** grow quickly (so can be studied over generations)    [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ many model organisms have physiological similarities to humans [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ more control over the experimental environment                 [1 mark]\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Mice are advantageous because they require **less space** ($\\checkmark$) and resources. \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ They also grow more quickly, which means they can be studied over **many generations** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0991","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0991\n\nIdentify the line in the table below which gives the correct responses of the skin to a drop in environmental temperature.\n\n| | Sweat glands | Arterioles | Erector pili muscles\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ |Increase sweat production | Vasodilation | Relax |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ |Decrease sweat production | Vasoconstriction | Contract |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Increase sweat production | Vasoconstriction | Relax |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Decrease sweat production | Vasodilation | Contract|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWhen the environmental temperature drops, your body responds by trying to conserve heat. Reducing sweat production means there is less sweat to evaporate from the skin's surface, so less heat is lost. Vasoconstriction of the blood vessels reduces the volume of blood reaching the surface of the heat, so less heat is lost by radiation. Contraction of the erector pili muscles makes the hairs of the skin stand up, trapping an insulating layer of air next to the skin, again reducing heat loss by radiation and also by convection. **Choice B** is, therefore, the correct answer.\n\n**Choices A**, **C** and **D** are incorrect as they have one or more factors wrong."},{"ref":"BI0992","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0992\n\nWhich of the following best explains why insulin injections are necessary for individuals with type 1 diabetes?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Their cells are unable to respond to insulin\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Their liver is unable to store glucose\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Their immune system destroys β-cells in the pancreas\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Their immune system destroys α-cells in the pancreas","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nType 1 diabetes in an autoimmune disease. In type 1 diabetes, the immune system targets and destroys the β-cells in the pancreas, resulting in a severe deficiency of insulin. Without insulin, the body cannot properly regulate blood sugar levels, leading to hyperglycemia. Therefore **Choice C**  is correct.\n\nCells being are unable to respond to insulin (**Choice A**) describes a condition known as insulin resistance, which is more commonly associated with type 2 diabetes. In type 2 diabetes, cells become less responsive to the effects of insulin, leading to elevated blood sugar levels.\n\nThe liver plays a role in storing glucose as glycogen and releasing it when needed to maintain blood sugar levels. However, the primary issue in type 1 diabetes is the destruction of insulin-producing β-cells, leading to a lack of insulin, rather than a direct problem with liver glucose storage. **Choice B** is therefore not correct.\n\nα-cells in the pancreas produce glucagon, another important hormone involved in blood sugar regulation. However, the destruction of α-cells (**Choice D**) would primarily affect glucagon levels, not insulin production, and would not explain the need for insulin injections in type 1 diabetes."},{"ref":"BI0993","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0993\n\nWhich statements are true of feedback mechanisms in an organism?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Negative feedback acts to reverse a change |\n    | II. | An example of positive feedback is control of blood pH |\n    | III. | Only the nervous system is involved in the control of feedback mechanisms |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nNegative feedback is a regulatory mechanism in which the body responds to a change in order to return the homeostatic variable back to within its set points.  For example, if blood pH increases above the set point, negative feedback mechanisms will occur to bring blood pH back down. **Statement I** is therefore true and **Statement II** is therefore false. **Choices B** and **D** are not correct.\n\nWhile the nervous system plays a significant role in many feedback mechanisms, it is not the only system involved. The endocrine system, which releases hormones into the bloodstream also plays a crucial role in feedback control. **Statement III** is therefore false, meaning **Choice B** is incorrect, and **Choice A** is the correct response to the question."},{"ref":"BI0994","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0994\n\nIdentify the row in the table below which shows the correct information about insulin.\n\n| | Cell insulin secreted by | Released when… | Effect\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | α-cell  | Blood glucose levels increase | Increase in glucose uptake by body cells\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ |liver cells | Blood glucose levels fall |Increase in glucose uptake by liver cells only |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ |  β-cell | Blood glucose levels fall | Increase in glucose uptake by liver cells only\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ |  β-cell | Blood glucose levels increase | Increase in glucose uptake by body cells|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nInsulin is released by β-cells in the islets of Langerhans in the pancreas. The insulin is released into the blood and carried all around the body, so all body cells are exposed to it. When insulin binds to receptors on body cells it causes an increased uptake of glucose by these cells. Insulin has an added effect on liver and muscle cells as it stimulates them to take up glucose and store it as the complex storage carbohydrate, glycogen. **Choice D** is therefore correct.\n\nα-cells secrete glucagon, not insulin (**Choice A**). It is cells in the pancreas, not the liver (Choice B) that secrete insulin. Glucagon is secreted when blood glucose falls, and insulin brings about increased glucose uptake in most body cells, not just liver cells (**Choices B** and **C**)."},{"ref":"BI1184","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1184\n\nWhich row correctly describes the results due to a decrease in the blood solute concentration?\n\n| | ADH production | Permeability of collecting duct | Urine concentration |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Decreases | Decreases| Decreases |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Decreases | Increases | Decreases |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Increases | Increases | Increases |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Increases | Decreases | Decreases |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because when the blood solute concentration decreases, ADH production decreases. This means there are fewer aquaporins in the cell membranes of the cells lining the collecting duct, so less water is reabsorbed into the blood, and urine concentration decreases.\n\n**Choices C** and **D** are incorrect because when the solute concentration of the blood decreases, ADH production decreases.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because decreased ADH production decreases the permeability of the collecting duct."},{"ref":"BI1195","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1195\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nBelow is an image of a kangaroo rat.  Kangaroo rats are a group of species belonging to the genus *Dipodomys* and are well-adapted to dry, arid habitats.  They are generally nocturnal and are capable of producing urine approximately 14 times more concentrated than their blood.  These, and other adaptations, enable kangaroo rats to survive indefinitely without drinking water.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Homeostasis/images/BI1195.png\" alt=\"BI1195\">\n[Source: Adapted from Rex (2005, September 25). *Kangaroo rats (Dipodomys)*. Retrieved September 5, 2023, from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kangaroo_rat#. Copyright under public domain.]\n\n1. State which part of the nephron is responsible for ultrafiltration.     [1 mark]\n\n2. In proportion to their size, kangaroo rats have a loop of Henle that is much longer than other animals.  Explain how a longer loop of Henle adapts the kangaroo rat to desert environments.  [3 marks]\n\n3. Outline why, when organisms get too hot, the enzyme-controlled reactions in their cells start to fail.        [2 marks]\n\n4. Explain how natural selection may have led to kangaroo rats adapting to dry, arid conditions.      [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  :br\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glomerulus    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **glomerulus** ($\\checkmark$) is the part of the nephron where ultrafiltration takes place.\n\n2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ descending limb of loop of Henle is permeable to water but impermeable to sodium ions              [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ascending limb of loop of Henle is permeable to sodium ions but impermeable to water             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ascending limb pumps sodium ions into the medulla, by active transport, maintaining a high solute concentration in the medulla            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as filtrate flows down the descending loop of Henle, water is moves out by osmosis     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ longer loop of Henle will therefore increase the surface area of reabsorption, conserving water             [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **descending** loop of Henle is relatively **permeable to water** but **impermeable to sodium ions** ($\\checkmark$) whereas the **ascending** loop of Henle is **permeable to sodium ions** but **not** to **water** ($\\checkmark$). The **ascending loop** of Henle **pumps sodium ions** into the medulla by **active transport**, leading to a **high solute concentration** in the **medulla** ($\\checkmark$), so **water** moves out of the loop of Henle by osmosis ($\\checkmark$). The longer loops of Henle in desert rats will **enhance water reabsorption**  from the filtrate, concentrating urine further to **help survive** ($\\checkmark$) droughts.\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enzymes are globular proteins            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 3D structure held together by weak **OR** hydrogen bonds    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in temperature increases kinetic energy of molecules    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased kinetic energy **OR** vibration of the molecules can disrupt the weak bonds **OR** break the bonds, holding the 3D structure together        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ shape of the active site changes **OR** destroyed        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ substrate no longer fits **OR** binds to the active site so the reaction cannot take place    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enzyme is denatured **OR** denaturation of enzyme takes place    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Enzymes are **globular proteins** held together by **hydrogen bonds** ($\\checkmark$). An **increase** in **temperature increases** the **kinetic energy** ($\\checkmark$) of molecules. This **increased kinetic energy** can **disrupt** these **weak bonds** ($\\checkmark$), so the shape of the enzyme starts to break down. This may **change** the **shape** of the **active site** ($\\checkmark$), sometimes irreversibly, so the enzyme can no longer function and is **denatured** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ variety of traits **OR** genetic diversity found in the population of kangaroo rats    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some traits **OR** suitable example of trait may be more adapted to fit a drier climate    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ these individuals with the adaptive traits will be able to survive **OR** natural selection will allow the individual with the adaptive traits to survive    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ these individuals will pass their traits to their offspring     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ over time, more of the individuals in the population will be able to live in the dry, arid climate            [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    There is usually a variety of traits or **genetic diversity** in a **population** ($\\checkmark$), such as kangaroo rats. Some of these **traits** make the individuals **more adapted** to living in a **drier climate** ($\\checkmark$). **Natural selection** will allow the individuals with these adaptive traits to **survive** (survival of the fittest) ($\\checkmark$). These individuals will pass their traits to their offspring, and so **eventually, more of the population** will be able to **live** in the **dry, arid conditions** of the desert ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1237","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1237\n\nWhich is **not** a role of the kidneys?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ To regulate the concentration of ions and water in the blood\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ To make urine\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ To remove waste products from the blood\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ To make bile","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe kidney does not make bile, the liver does, **Choice D**  is correct.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **C**  are incorrect as they are all functions of the kidneys."},{"ref":"BI1271","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1271\n\nWhich set of statements is true regarding homeostasis?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | It involves the maintenance of internal conditions despite changes in the external environment  |\n    | II. | Homeostatic variables are kept constant  |\n    | III. | Includes the regulation of body temperature, blood pH, blood glucose concentration, and blood osmotic concentration  |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nHomeostasis is the maintenance of internal conditions despite changes in the external environment (**Statement I**). It includes homeostatic variables such as body temperature, blood pH, blood glucose concentration, and blood osmotic concentration (**Statement III**). It does not, however, require that all homeostatic variables remain constant (**Statement II**), instead allowing them to fluctuate within safe limits. Thus, **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as it does not include **Statement III**. **Choices C** and **D** are incorrect as they include **Statement II**."},{"ref":"BI1272","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1272\n\nWhich is **NOT** a correct example of a homeostatic response?\n\n| | Stimuli | Homeostatic Response |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Rise in body temperature | Sweating |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Rise in blood glucose levels | Release of insulin |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Fall in blood glucose levels | Release of glycogen |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Fall in body temperature | Shivering |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWhen blood glucose levels fall, the cells of the pancreas release the hormone glucagon (not glycogen).\n\nGlucagon promotes the breakdown of glycogen stored in the liver into glucose (glycogenolysis). This process, along with gluconeogenesis (the synthesis of glucose from non-carbohydrate sources), raises blood glucose levels. **Choice C** is, therefore, the correct answer."},{"ref":"BI1273","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1273\n\nWhich graph resembles the pattern observed in thermoregulators such as birds and mammals?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Homeostasis/images/BI1273.png\" alt=\"BI1273\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThermoregulators, or endotherms, maintain a relatively constant internal body temperature despite changes in environmental temperature. This includes birds and mammals. **Choice A** is correct because it shows a constant body temperature across a range of environmental conditions.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because it shows body temperature changing with environmental temperature, whereas endotherms maintain a relatively constant internal temperature across a range of conditions.\n\n**Choice C** is also incorrect because it shows body temperature decreasing as environmental temperature increases, which is not characteristic of either endotherms or ectotherms.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because it shows body temperature increasing linearly with environmental temperature, which is characteristic of ectotherms that rely on external heat sources."},{"ref":"BI1274","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1274\n\nWhen body temperature begins to fall, the change is detected by thermoreceptors in the skin. This causes the __I__ to stimulate thyroxin production in the __II__, resulting in a rise in basal metabolic rate. Which row in the table is correct for glands I and II?\n\n| | I | II |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Hypothalamus | Pituitary Gland |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Hypothalamus | Thyroid Gland |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Pancreas | Thyroid Gland |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Pancreas | Pituitary Gland |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWhen body temperature drops, the hypothalamus responds by releasing thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH). This stimulates the anterior pituitary to release thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH), which in turn acts on the thyroid gland, causing it to secrete thyroxin. Thyroxin increases the basal metabolic rate, generating heat and helping restore body temperature. **Choice B** is, therefore, the correct answer.\n\n**Choices A** and **D** are incorrect because although the pituitary gland releases TSH, it does not produce thyroxin - this is the role of the thyroid gland.\n**Choice C** is incorrect because the pancreas regulates blood glucose levels and plays no role in thermoregulation."},{"ref":"BI1275","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1275\n\nWhich is **not** a physiological human response to a decrease in temperature?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Vasodilation of blood vessels\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Hairs on the skin become erect\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sweat glands reduce sweat production\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Metabolic activity increases in the liver","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWhen exposed to low temperatures, the body reduces the volume of blood that reaches the extremities by vasoconstriction (not vasodilation), thereby reducing heat loss; **Choice A** is the correct answer.\n\n**Choices B, C**, and **D** are incorrect since these are all accurate physiological responses to a drop in temperature."},{"ref":"BI1276","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1276\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe graph below shows how the human body typically responds to changing blood glucose concentrations throughout the day.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Homeostasis/images/BI1276.png\" alt=\"BI1276\">\n[Source: Adapted from J. Suckale and M. Solimena. (2011). *Glucose-day-english.svg*. Retrieved October 10, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Glucose-day-english.svg. Copyright under CC BY-SA 3.0]\n\n1. State the hormone that is responsible for reducing blood glucose concentration.     [1 mark]\n\n2.\n    1. Suggest a reason why the blood glucose concentration in the graph rises sharply several times throughout the day.    [1 mark]\n\n    2. Explain why blood glucose concentration remains stable at night.     [3 marks]\n\n3.\n    1. List two examples of foods that could be considered sucrose-rich.    [2 marks]\n\n    2. Suggest, with a reason, one possible outcome for an adult with a diet that is consistently high in sucrose-rich foods.    [2 marks]\n\n4. State one other homeostatic variable that is controlled by negative feedback loops.\n[1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ insulin        [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The hormone responsible for reducing blood glucose concentration is **insulin** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    1.\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ blood glucose concentration rises several times due to the digestion of carbohydrates **OR** consumption of food **OR** meals  [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Rises in blood glucose concentration coincide with **meals consumed** ($\\checkmark$) during the day.\n\n2.\n    2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ blood glucose concentration is maintained through homeostasis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as blood glucose concentrations begin to drop, the pancreas, **OR** alpha cells, secrete glucagon         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glucagon promotes gluconeogenesis **OR** the breakdown of glycogen **OR** the hydrolysis of glycogen in the liver **OR** glycogenolysis        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glucose is released into the blood to maintain the blood glucose concentration                                            [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Blood glucose concentrations remain stable at night because **homeostasis** keeps glucose levels **balanced** ($\\checkmark$). As glucose concentrations begin to **decrease, pancreatic alpha cells release glucagon** ($\\checkmark$), which acts on the **liver** to promote the **breakdown** of stored **glycogen into glucose** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n    1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ table sugar         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ syrups **OR** maple syrup **OR** suitable example of syrup        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fruit **OR** any named fruit        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ desserts **OR** cake **OR** cookies **OR** any other named sweet dessert [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ processed food **OR** microwave meals **OR** snacks **OR** crackers **OR** any other suitable example    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sweetened beverages **OR** juice **OR** soda **OR** alcohol        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sauces **OR** salad dressing **OR** ketchup **OR** BBQ sauce **OR** any other named sauce    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ any other correctly named food high in sucrose        [1 mark]\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ (do not accept starchy foods such as grains, potatoes, bread, pasta)\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Apples** and **soda** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n    2. Any **one pair** of the following (must include both a suggested outcome and a reason to achieve full marks):\n\n    *Diabetes* \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ diabetes **OR** type II diabetes     [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** elevated glucose levels could lead to insulin resistance        [1 mark]\n\n    *Obesity* \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ obesity **OR** weight gain             [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** more food is ingested than needed **OR** imbalance between food intake and energy expenditure **OR** excess glucose is converted to fat **OR** stored as fat    [1 mark]\n\n    *Dental problems* \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ dental problems **OR** tooth decay             [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** the sugar is broken down by bacteria that harm the teeth **OR** acid produced by bacteria erodes tooth enamel **OR** demineralises the teeth     [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    An adult who consumes too much sucrose-rich food on a regular basis could develop **diabetes** ($\\checkmark$) because **elevated blood glucose levels** may lead to **insulin resistance** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ body temperature **OR** blood osmotic concentration **OR** blood pH **OR** other named example of a negative feedback-controlled variable    [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ **Body temperature** ($\\checkmark$) is another homeostatic variable controlled by negative feedback loops."},{"ref":"BI1277","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1277\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the concept of homeostasis.    [3 marks]\n\n2. Describe the physiological mechanisms of thermoregulation in humans and their effects on the body.    [5 marks]\n\n3. Compare and contrast type I and type II diabetes.    [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ homeostasis is the maintenance of the internal conditions **OR** internal environment of an organism        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ conditions inside the body are kept within a range of acceptable limits **OR** preset limits **OR** optimal range **OR** safe range                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ changes in the external environment **OR** conditions outside the body **OR** external conditions are not reflected                        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ receptors detect changes **OR** receive stimulus and pass to control centre **OR** CNS **OR** brain [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ control centre **OR** CNS **OR** brain sends information **OR** nerve impulses to effector **OR** muscle or gland         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use of negative feedback loops to return homeostatic variables to set point        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ named example, such as blood pH, body temperature, blood glucose concentration, or blood osmotic concentration        [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Homeostasis is the body’s way of **maintaining** stable **internal conditions** ($\\checkmark$). Homeostatic variables are **kept within an optimal range** ($\\checkmark$) through the use of **negative feedback loops**, which return internal conditions to the **set point** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following (*must include the mechanism **and** effect on the body to receive each mark*):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vasodilation **OR** relaxation of smooth muscle in arterioles supplying the skin **WHICH** increases blood flow near the skin surface so more heat is lost by radiation **OR** lowers body temperature **OR** cools the body    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vasoconstriction **OR** contraction of smooth muscle in arterioles supplying the skin **WHICH** diverts blood away from the skin surface so less heat is lost by radiation **OR** prevents heat loss **OR** keeps the body warm    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ shivering **WHICH** increases cellular respiration in muscles, releasing heat **OR** raises the body temperature **OR** warms the body    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sweating **WHICH** removes heat as the water in sweat evaporates **OR** lowers body temperature **OR** cools the body    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ arrector pili muscles relax **OR** hairs lie flat **WHICH** reduces the insulating layer of trapped air, increasing heat loss **OR** lowers body temperature **OR** cools the body    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ arrector pili muscles contract **OR** hairs stand erect **WHICH** traps a layer of air that insulates the skin **OR** prevents heat loss **OR** keeps the body warm    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ uncoupled respiration in brown adipose tissue **WHICH** releases energy as heat instead of producing ATP **OR** raises body temperature **OR** warms the body    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Shivering** and **sweating** are both used by humans to adjust the core body temperature. When humans **shiver**, rapid involuntary muscle contractions increase cellular respiration, releasing heat, which **raises** the **body temperature** slightly ($\\checkmark$). Conversely, **sweating** releases water onto the skin; as this water evaporates it removes heat from the body, lowering the **body temperature** ($\\checkmark$). Hairs on the skin are also involved in thermoregulation. When thermoreceptors detect a rise in body temperature, the arrector pili muscles relax, causing the hairs to lie flat. This reduces the layer of trapped air, increasing heat loss and cooling the body ($\\checkmark$). On the other hand, when body temperature falls, the arrector pili muscles contract, causing the hairs to stand erect. This traps a layer of insulating air next to the skin, reducing heat loss and keeping the body warm ($\\checkmark$). Blood vessels also respond: when body temperature rises, arterioles supplying the skin dilate (vasodilation), increasing blood flow near the skin surface so that more heat is lost by radiation ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following (must include at least **one** similarity and at least **one** difference to receive full marks):\n\n    *similarities:* \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** types are characterised by high blood sugar **OR** blood glucose concentrations **OR** hyperglycaemia    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** types can lead to excessive urination **OR** thirst (although more severe for type I)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glucose can be excreted in urine in **BOTH** types of diabetes when blood glucose levels exceed renal threshold    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ patients with type I **AND** type II must monitor and adjust their diets carefully    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** conditions, if untreated, can lead to serious health effects, such as renal failure **OR** heart attacks **OR** strokes **OR** blindness **OR** death    [1 mark]\n\n    *differences:* \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type I diabetes is rare **WHEREAS** type II diabetes is common (~90% of cases)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type I diabetes is also known as ‘early onset diabetes’ **OR** more common in young people **OR** people under the age of 20 **WHILE** type II diabetes is also known as ‘late onset diabetes’ **OR** historically associated with older adults but is now increasingly found in younger people    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type I is an autoimmune disorder **WHILE** type II is not    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type I patients produce very little **OR** no insulin **WHEREAS** type II patients may produce insulin but in insufficient amounts **OR** in a form the body cannot use effectively **OR** body does not respond to it    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type I is caused by the destruction of **OR** failure of beta (β) cells in the pancreas to produce insulin **WHEREAS** type II is caused by insulin resistance **OR** a failure of receptors to respond to insulin **OR** impaired insulin secretion    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type I must be treated by insulin injection **OR** insulin pumps **WHILE** type II can often be treated with diet **OR** weight management **OR** exercise **OR** may require medication **OR** insulin as the disease progresses    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type I can develop rapidly **OR** over days or weeks **WHEREAS** type II develops gradually **OR** over years    [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Both type I and type II diabetes are characterised by **high blood glucose levels** ($\\checkmark$) due to problems with insulin regulation, with **glucose** being **excreted** in the **urine** when blood **glucose levels exceed** the **renal threshold** ($\\checkmark$). Both sets of patients must modify their **diets** ($\\checkmark$) carefully to avoid long-term **health effects** such as **renal failure** and **stroke** ($\\checkmark$). Type I diabetes is considered an **autoimmune disorder**, while type II diabetes is not ($\\checkmark$). This is because type I diabetes is caused by the **destruction of pancreatic beta cells**, which produce **insulin**, whereas type II diabetes is caused by **insulin resistance**, where **cells do not respond properly** to insulin, even though it may still be produced ($\\checkmark$). Type I diabetes typically requires **lifelong insulin therapy**, whereas type II diabetes can often be **managed with lifestyle changes** such as **diet** and **exercise**, though **medication** may be necessary as the **condition progresses** ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, type I diabetes **develops rapidly**, often **over weeks**, while type II **progresses slowly** over **months or years** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1373","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1373\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nCongenital leptin deficiency is a genetic disorder caused by mutations in the leptin gene. A symptom of congenital leptin deficiency is extreme early-onset obesity. The diagram shows a pedigree chart for a family affected by the disease.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Homeostasis/images/BI1373.png\" alt=\"BI1373\">\n\n1.\n 1. State the mode of inheritance of congenital leptin deficiency. [2 marks]\n\n 2. Explain your reasoning for your answer to part (i). [1 mark]\n\n 3. Deduce the genotype of individual X. [1 mark]\n\n2. Leptin regulates obesity via a negative feedback mechanism. Define the term ‘negative feedback’. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n 1.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ autosomal [1 mark]\n $\\ce{ -> }$ recessive [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The mode of inheritance is **autosomal** ($\\checkmark$) **recessive** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ biological parents without the disease have offspring who have the disease **OR** biological parents in generation I **OR** generation III do not have the disease, but their biological offspring have the disease [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ heterozygous biological parents **OR** biological parents with only one recessive allele are able to have homozygous recessive biological children [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ a labelled punnett square may be accepted, e.g.\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Homeostasis/images/BI1373Ans.png\" alt=\"BI1373Ans\">\n\n [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Congenital leptin deficiency is autosomal recessive because **neither** the biological **male** parent nor the biological **female parent** in **generation 1** has the disease, but two of their biological **children do** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ heterozygous **OR** any upper **AND** lowercase letter combination [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n X is **heterozygous (Ll)** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ regulatory process in which a system’s response reverses any changes away from an initial set point **OR** system that returns homeostatic variables to the set point from values above or below the set point **OR** feedback that counteracts any deviation from equilibrium [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n A negative feedback mechanism is a regulatory process in which a system’s response **reverses** any **changes away from an initial set point** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1405","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1405\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nFabry disease is an inherited neurological disorder that can lead to damaged kidneys. Kidney transplantation is one possible treatment for patients with Fabry disease. The micrograph below shows some glomerular tissue from a patient with Fabry disease.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Homeostasis/images/BI1405.png\" alt=\"BI1405\">\n[Source: Adapted from Pr Marie-Claire Gubler. (2010, Nov 22). F\n*File: Morbus Fabry kidney biopsy 01.jpg*.\n Wikimedia commons. Retrieved January 18, 2024, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Morbus_Fabry_kidney_biopsy_01.jpg. Copyright under CC BY 2.0\n]\n\n1. State what type of solution the kidney would need to be stored in before it is transplanted into the patient.         [1 mark]\n\n2. Explain how the structure of the glomerulus and Bowman’s capsule are adapted for their functions.                [4 marks]\n\nUrea was discovered in human urine in the 18th century, and it was believed that it could only be produced by living organisms until, in 1828, Friedrich Wöhler artificially synthesised it from inorganic molecules. The Miller-Urey experiment, depicted below, extended the foundational principle established by Wohler, that organic molecules could be synthesised from inorganic molecules.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Homeostasis/images/urey%20miller%20experiment%20ii.png\" alt=\"urey miller experiment ii\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Carny. (2006, August 3). File: https:\n//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MUexperiment.png. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved March 6, 2024, from https:\n//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: MUexperiment.png. CC BY 2.5\n]\n\n3.\n    1. State what Miller and Urey were hoping to make using their apparatus.[1 mark]\n\n    2.  Identify what event on Earth is being simulated in vessel X. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. any **one** of the following\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ isotonic    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ same osmotic concentration **OR** same solute concentration **OR** same water concentration, as the cells of the kidney        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    It would need to be an **isotonic** solution ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **four** of the following (must include at least **one** adaptation for the glomerulus and **one** for the Bowman’s capsule)\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ afferent arteriole **OR** arteriole leading to glomerulus wider diameter than efferent arteriole **OR** arteriole leading away from it, increasing hydrostatic pressure **OR** blood pressure    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ capillaries of the glomerulus are under high blood pressure **OR** high hydrostatic pressure to allow ultrafiltration                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fenestrations **OR** gaps **OR** pores between cells of capillary walls, allow fluid **OR** plasma to escape [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ basement membrane prevents most plasma proteins **OR** larger molecules from being filtered out            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ podocytes **OR** filtration slits **OR** inner wall of Bowman’s capsule prevent larger molecules from being filtered out of the blood        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cup shape of Bowman’s capsule collects filtrate                [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **afferent arteriole** entering the glomerulus is wider than the **efferent arteriole** leaving it, thereby causing **high blood pressure** ($\\checkmark$)  in the **glomerulus**, which brings about **ultrafiltration** ($\\checkmark$). The high pressure forces **plasma out** through the **fenestrations** of the **capillary walls** ($\\checkmark$). Two layers of filters **prevent larger molecules** from entering the **filtrate**. These are the **basement membrane** ($\\checkmark$), which is found around the outside of the capillary walls, and the **filtration slits** between the **podocytes** of the Bowman’s capsule’s wall ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n    1.\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ amino acids    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Miller and Urey were trying to make **amino acids** ($\\checkmark$) from inorganic molecules.\n\n    2.\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lightning    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Miller and Urey were  trying to simulate **lightning storms** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1490","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1490\n\nThe graph shows blood hormone levels before and after a person consumes a meal. Which line represents insulin?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Homeostasis/images/BI1490i.png\" alt=\"BI1490i\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nAfter a meal, beta cells in the pancreas synthesise and secrete insulin so insulin levels quickly rise. Insulin triggers the uptake of glucose by the liver and muscles and then insulin levels return to a baseline after blood glucose levels drop. **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as although insulin levels rise immediately after a meal, they do not remain high for many hours after. **Choice B** is incorrect as insulin levels do not fall after a meal. **Choice C** is incorrect as it rises and falls irrespective of meal consumption."},{"ref":"BI1595","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1595\n\nAntidiuretic hormone (ADH) helps regulate the amount of water in the body. In normal adults, the brain produces a steady supply of ADH at night, however, many children are deep sleepers and do not produce as much ADH as a result.\n\nWhat is a possible consequence of reduced ADH production in children?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increased urine production\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Decreased thirst\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ High blood pressure\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Low blood sodium levels","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because ADH increases water reabsorption in the collecting ducts, reducing the quantity of urine produced. Children who secrete less ADH would produce more urine as a result.\n\n**Choices B, C,** and **D** are incorrect because excessive thirst (not decreased thirst), low blood pressure (not high blood pressure), and high blood sodium (not low blood sodium) are all symptoms of low ADH production."},{"ref":"BI2346","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2346\n\nWhich row is correct for uncoupled respiration in brown adipose tissue?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ ATP is produced, used to generate heat, and is more common in adulthood.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ ATP is produced, used to generate heat, and is more common in childhood.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ No ATP is produced, and is more common in adulthood.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ No ATP is produced, and is more common in childhood.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because in uncoupled respiration, ATP is not produced by the electron transport chain. Instead, the energy released from the electron transport chain is entirely converted into heat. This process is vital for thermoregulation, especially in children who are more prone to heat loss due to their higher surface-area-to-volume ratio. Brown adipose tissue is more abundant in childhood to generate heat and prevent hypothermia.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because ATP is not produced by uncoupled respiration in brown adipose tissue, and there is a higher percentage of brown adipose tissue in childhood.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because ATP is not produced by uncoupled respiration in brown adipose tissue.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because there is a higher percentage of brown adipose tissue in childhood."},{"ref":"BI2396","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2396\n\nA typical adult male has 6 $\\ce{dm^3}$ of blood. The blood passes through the kidneys 300 times per day. The volume of urine produced is 3 $\\ce{dm^3}$.\nWhat proportion of the kidneys’ input is excreted as urine?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 50%\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 1.7%\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 1%\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 0.17%","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because 6 $\\ce{dm^3}$ of blood passes through the kidneys 300 times so the kidneys’ total input is 6 $\\times$ 300 = 1800 $\\ce{dm^3}$. \\\nThe proportion of the kidneys’ input that is excreted as urine, as a percentage, is:\n3dm3 of urine output as a proportion of 1800 $\\ce{dm^3}$ total input = $\\dfrac{3}{1800} \\times 100$% = $1.7 \\times 10^{-3} \\times 100$% = 0.17%\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it calculates urine output as a proportion of the volume of blood in a person’s body, which is not what the question requires.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because it is too high by a factor of 10.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because it is a miscalculation."},{"ref":"BI2665","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2665\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe diagram below is a simple outline of the nephron.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Homeostasis/images/BI2665.png\" alt=\"BI2665\">\n\n1. Name the structures labelled I, II, III and IV in the diagram. [4 marks]\n\n2. State the functions of structures I and II from part (a). [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ I – Bowman’s capsule [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ II – Descending limb of the loop of Henle [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ III – Ascending limb of the loop of Henle [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ IV – Distal convoluted tubule [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ I (Bowman’s capsule) is the location of ultrafiltration **OR** small substances are filtered from the blood into the Bowman’s capsule [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in II (the descending limb of the loop of Henle), water is reabsorbed, **OR** the walls of the descending limb are permeable to water but not to sodium ions [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n :u[Sample answer]:\n\n The Bowman’s capsule (I) is the location of **ultrafiltration** ($\\checkmark$), small substances are filtered here from the blood into the nephron. **Water is reabsorbed** ($\\checkmark$) in the descending limb of the loop of Henle (II)."},{"ref":"BI2748","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2748\n\nWhich statement is correct for excretion or osmoregulation?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Osmoregulation is the control of the level of metabolic wastes in the blood.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Excretion removes metabolic wastes from the blood.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Excretion controls the water and salt concentration of the blood.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Osmoregulation removes metabolic wastes from the blood.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because excretion is the process that removes metabolic wastes such as carbon dioxide, urea, and excess salts from the blood. These waste products are produced by metabolic processes and must be eliminated to maintain homeostasis.\\\n**Choice A** is incorrect because osmoregulation refers to the regulation of water and salt concentration in the blood; it is not the removal of metabolic wastes from the blood.\\\n**Choices C** and **D** are incorrect because the definitions are with the wrong terms. Excretion is the removal of metabolic wastes from blood, and osmoregulation is the regulation of the water and salt concentration of the blood."},{"ref":"BI2750","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2750\n\nThe concentration of proteins in blood plasma is approximately 750 g $\\ce{dm^{-3}}$. Which row is correct for protein concentrations under normal body conditions?\n\n| | Concentration in glomerular filtrate / $\\mathbf{g\\ dm^{-3}}$ |\n| --- | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 40 |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 700 |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 350 |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 0 |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because, under normal physiological conditions, proteins are not found in the glomerular filtrate because, due to their large size and negative charge, they cannot pass through the basement membrane. The filtration barrier is selectively permeable, allowing water and small solutes through but retaining plasma proteins in the blood.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **C** are incorrect because they suggest the presence of proteins in the filtrate. However, under normal conditions, proteins do not pass into Bowman’s capsule, so their concentration in the filtrate is effectively 0 g $\\ce{dm^{-3}}$."},{"ref":"BI2752","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2752\n\nWhich row could be correct for the labelled parts of the nephron?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Homeostasis/images/BI2752.png\" alt=\"BI2752\">\n\n| $\\hspace{2em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Osmolarity / mOsmol $\\mathbf{L^{-1}}$ $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n| --- | :-: |\n\n| | $\\hspace{1em}$ W $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ X $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Y $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Z $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 1200 | 300 | 400 | 1000 |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 300 | 400 | 1000 | 1200 |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 400 | 1000 | 1200 | 300 |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 1000 | 1200 | 300 | 400 |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct. **W** is the medulla. Active transport in the ascending limb of the loop of Henle moves sodium ions into the medulla and water does not move by osmosis from the ascending limb because the walls of this part of the nephron are impermeable to water. This increases the solute concentration of the medulla up to 1200 mOsmol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$.\n\n**X** is the proximal convoluted tubule. Reabsorption of water and solutes produces an isotonic solution of approximately 300 mOsmol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$.\n\n**Y** is the ascending limb of the loop of Henle. Active transport of sodium ions after reabsorption of water in the descending limb reduces the solute concentration in this part of the nephron.\n\n**Z** is the collecting duct. Antidiuretic hormone (ADH) increases water reabsorption from the collecting duct which increases the osmolarity in this part of the nephron.\n\n**Choices B, C** and **D** are incorrect because the osmolarity for at least one of the labelled parts is outside the normal range."},{"ref":"BI2754","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2754\n\nWhich is the best description of the effect of antidiuretic hormone?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increased volume of urine production.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Movement of aquaporins from vesicles to membranes in the collecting duct.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increased active transport of sodium ions in the loop of Henle.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increased reabsorption of water in the descending limb of the loop of Henle.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because antidiuretic hormone increases the permeability to water of the distal convoluted tubule and collecting ducts by causing aquaporins to move from vesicles to cell membranes. Aquaporins are membrane channel proteins that facilitate the movement of water by osmosis.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because antidiuretic hormone increases water reabsorption in the distal convoluted tubule and collecting duct so the volume of urine is reduced.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because antidiuretic hormone causes increased reabsorption of water, not increased active transport of sodium ions.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because antidiuretic hormone does not affect the permeability of the loop of Henle."},{"ref":"BI2756","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2756\n\nWhich row is correct for the effect of the three conditions on blood flow to the kidney?\n\n| | Vigorous exercise | Wakeful rest | Sleep |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-:\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Increased | High | Increased |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Increased | Low | Reduced |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Reduced | High | Reduced |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Reduced | Low | Reduced |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because blood supply to the kidneys is reduced during both vigorous physical exercise and sleep. During exercise, blood flow to the muscles increases to bring oxygen and glucose for aerobic respiration to facilitate muscle contraction, which decreases blood flow to the kidneys. During sleep, arterioles contract and reduce blood flow to the glomeruli.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because during vigorous exercise and sleep, blood flow to the kidney is reduced, not increased.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because during vigorous exercise, blood flow to the kidneys is reduced, and blood flow to the kidneys during wakeful rest is high.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because during wakeful rest, blood flow to the kidneys is high, not low."},{"ref":"BI2758","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2758\n\nWhat happens to organ blood flow during prolonged vigorous physical activity?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Decreases slightly to the kidneys |\n    | II. | Increases to skeletal muscle |\n    | III. | Decreases to the brain |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDuring prolonged vigorous exercise, vasoconstriction of the renal arterioles occurs, reducing blood flow to the kidneys. This helps divert blood to more metabolically active organs, such as skeletal muscle. **Statement I** is true.\n\nSkeletal muscle blood flow increases greatly during vigorous activity to supply more oxygen and nutrients for aerobic respiration and to remove waste products like carbon dioxide. Muscles may receive up to 80–85% of cardiac output during peak exertion. **Statement II** is true.\n\nBrain blood flow is tightly autoregulated, meaning that despite changes in cardiac output or systemic vasoconstriction, cerebral perfusion remains relatively constant. It does not decrease during exercise; in fact, it may increase slightly due to elevated $\\ce{CO_2}$ levels or local metabolic activity. **Statement III** is false.\n\n**Choice C** is the correct answer to the question because it only includes the two true **Statements I** and **II**.\\\n**Choice A** is incorrect as it is missing the true **Statement II**.\\\n**Choices B** and **D** are incorrect because they include the false **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI2759","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2759\n\nWhich of the following are true of homeostatic mechanisms in humans?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | They maintain body temperature at a constant 37.0$\\degree$C |\n    | II. | They maintain blood glucose concentration within a preset range |\n    | III. | They maintain osmotic concentration of human blood plasma at a constant 296 mOsmol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nHomeostasis is the maintenance of a stable internal environment within narrow limits, despite changes in the external environment.\n\nHuman body temperature fluctuates within a narrow range (typically ~36.1$\\degree$C to 37.2$\\degree$C) due to circadian rhythm, activity, and hormonal cycles. **Statement I** is false.\n\nInsulin and glucagon regulate blood glucose within a homeostatic preset range of approximately 4–8 mmol  $\\ce{L^{-1}}$. **Statement II** is true.\n\nPlasma osmolarity is not maintained at exactly 296 mOsmol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$; homeostatic mechanisms regulate it within a typical range of 280–296 mOsmol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$. Therefore, **Statement III** is false.\n\n**Choice A** is therefore the correct answer, as it only includes the one true **Statement II**.\n\n**Choices B, C** and **D** are incorrect as they include one or more false statement."},{"ref":"BI2760","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2760\n\nThe following table shows hormone levels and responses:\n\n|Blood Glucose| Hormone Released | Body Response |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| High | Insulin | Glucose uptake by cells |\n| Low | Glucagon | Glycogen breakdown |\n\nWhich process is an example of negative feedback?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Neither process\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Both processes\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Only insulin release\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Only glucagon release","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nNegative feedback is a regulatory mechanism in which a change in a variable triggers a response that counteracts the original change, returning the system toward its set point.\n\nWhen blood glucose concentration increases, the pancreas releases insulin. Insulin promotes glucose uptake by body cells and stimulates the conversion of glucose to glycogen in the liver and muscles. This reduces blood glucose levels, bringing them back to normal. This reversal of the initial rise in glucose clearly demonstrates negative feedback.\n\nWhen blood glucose concentration decreases, the pancreas releases glucagon. Glucagon stimulates the breakdown of glycogen into glucose in the liver, raising blood glucose levels. This also restores glucose concentration to its normal range and is, therefore, another example of negative feedback.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **Choice B**.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect because both processes exhibit negative feedback mechanisms. **Choice C** is incorrect because it ignores the role of glucagon. **Choice D** is incorrect because it ignores insulin's role."}]},{"name":"Inheritance","questions":[{"ref":"BI0075","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0075\n\nWhich of these statements is a correct definition of a gene?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ A gene is the length of a chromosome\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ A gene is a length of DNA that is wrapped around histones\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ A gene is a heritable factor made of DNA that influences a specific characteristic\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Genes control all characteristics of an organism","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is a correct definition of a gene. Chromosomes are made of DNA and protein (**Choice A**), while genes are only made of DNA. When DNA is at various levels of supercoiling, it is wrapped around histone proteins.  However, the presence of histones is not part of the definition of a gene (**Choice B**). Genes control some characteristics, but some characteristics are also affected by the environment (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0076","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0076\n\nThe diagram shows a pair of homologous chromosomes.\n\nHow many loci are shown?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI0076.png\" alt=\"BI0076\">\n[ 2022. Created with BioRender.com]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 1\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 2\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 4\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 8","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA locus is the location of a gene, regardless of the allele. Homologous chromosomes have genes for the same characteristics, therefore, also the same loci. The diagram shows alleles for four genes because they are in the same location. The location of four genes is shown (**Choice C**).\n\nThere is one pair of homologous chromosomes (**Choice A**). There are two chromosomes (**Choice B**). There are eight alleles shown (**Choice D**) but only 4 loci."},{"ref":"BI0077","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0077\n\nWhich row shows the correct annotation of the alleles for blood groups?\n\n| | | | |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | I$^A$ | I$^B$ | i |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Blood group B | Blood group A | Blood group O |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | I$^A$ I$^B$ | I$^B$ I$^B$ | I$^A$ i |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | A$^+$ | B$^+$ | O$^-$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAlleles are versions of a gene. **Choice A** shows 3 different alleles. **Choice B** shows three blood groups (phenotypes). **Choice C** shows three genotypes, and **Choice D** shows 3 blood groups (phenotypes)."},{"ref":"BI0078","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0078\n\nWhich of these statements are true for alleles?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | Alleles are versions of a gene |\n  | II. | Alleles differ by one or more base |\n  | III. | Alleles affect the same characteristic |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAlleles are versions of a gene. Each allele represents a different form or variant of the same gene; **Statement I** is true.\n\nAlleles differ by one or more bases. Variations between alleles are due to differences in the nucleotide sequence of the DNA; **Statement II** is true.\n\nAlleles affect the same characteristics. Since they are different forms of the same gene, they contribute to variations in the expression of a particular characteristic e.g. I:sup[A], I:sup[B], and  i  are versions of the gene for blood groups; **Statement III** is true.\n\n**Choice C** is therefore the correct answer to the question.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **D** are not correct as they are all missing one or more true statements.\n."},{"ref":"BI0082","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0082\n\nMendel studied eight characteristics of pea plants, including flower colour. If the pea plants Mendel was studying can only be white or purple and follow the rules of Mendelian genetics, which of these statements was correct?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Pea plants show co-dominance for flower colour\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Flower colour is an example of polygenic inheritance\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Flower colour is controlled by genes at two loci\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Pea plants have two alleles for flower colour","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nTwo different colours show that there are two alleles for this characteristic (**Choice D**). Polygenic inheritance results in continuous variation (**Choice B**).  Two distinct colours are an example of discontinuous variation. If there was co-dominance, there would be three possible flower colour variations (**Choice A**). If there were genes at two loci that controlled flower colour, then the trait would be a polygenic trait and show continuous variation (**Choice C**)."},{"ref":"BI0085","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0085\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe majority of the genes in the human genome have already been mapped, meaning the exact locations of the genes on chromosomes are known. The diagram below shows one representation of a homologous pair of chromosomes.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/Picture%201.png\" alt=\"Picture 1\">\n\n1. Define locus. [1 mark]\n\n2. Identify the number of loci shown in the diagram. [1 mark]\n\n3. Distinguish between gene, allele, and locus. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ locus is the specific location of a gene. [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ :u[Sample answer]:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The locus of a gene is its **specific location** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ there are 3 loci shown in the diagram. [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ :u[Sample answer]:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ This diagram has **three loci** ($\\checkmark$) because it shows the location of alleles for three different genes.\n\n3. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ a gene is a heritable factor consisting of DNA that controls a specific characteristic **WHEREAS** an allele is a version of a gene [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the gene for blood type codes for a protein on the surface of red blood cells **WHEREAS** alleles of this gene code for different types of proteins associated with blood groups (or any other valid examples) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ a locus is the specific location for a gene **WHILE** genes are sequences of base pairs coding for proteins [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ a locus is the specific location of a gene **WHILE** alleles are different versions of a gene **OR** genes code for proteins [1 mark]\n\n **Accept any other valid examples instead of definitions**\n[3 marks]\n\n :u[Sample answer]:\n\n A gene is a **heritable factor** made of DNA that controls a specific characteristic while alleles are **different versions** of a gene ($\\checkmark$). As an example of differences between the gene and allele, we can discuss red blood cells. The **gene for blood type codes for proteins** on red blood cells while **alleles** of this gene code for **different versions of this protein** on red blood cells ($\\checkmark$) . A **locus is the specific location** of a gene on a chromosome while **genes code for proteins** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0192","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0192\n\nWhich row correctly describes diploid and haploid cells in humans?\n\n| | Haploid cell  | Diploid cell |\n| :-- | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| Chromosomes **not** in pairs; have **two** sex chromosomes | Chromosomes **not** in pairs; have **one** sex chromosome |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Chromosomes **not** in pairs; have **one** sex chromosome | Chromosomes in pairs; have **two** sex chromosomes |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Chromosomes in pairs; have **two** sex chromosomes  | Chromosomes **not** in pairs; have **one** sex chromosome |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Chromosomes in pairs; have **one** sex chromosome | Chromosomes in pairs; have **two** sex chromosomes |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because haploid cells have one chromosome from each homologous pair, diploid cells have pairs of chromosomes, haploid cells have one sex chromosome, and diploid cells have two sex chromosomes. **Choice A** is incorrect because diploid cells have pairs of chromosomes, haploid cells have one sex chromosome, and diploid cells have two sex chromosomes. **Choice C** is incorrect because haploid cells do not have pairs of chromosomes, and diploid cells have two sex chromosomes. **Choice D** is incorrect because haploid cells do not have pairs of chromosomes."},{"ref":"BI0194","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0194\n\nWhich diagram shows the sex chromosomes of a somatic cell in a human male?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |   |   |B.|\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI0194_A.png\" alt=\"BI0194_A\">|   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI0194_B.png\" alt=\"BI0194_B\">|\n|C. |   |   |  |D. |\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI0194_C.png\" alt=\"BI0194_C\"> |   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI0194_D.png\" alt=\"BI0194_D\">|\n\n[Source: Adapted from Nami-ja. (5th March 2009). *File:Human chromosomesXXY02.png*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved 18th May 2022 from https:// commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Human_chromosomesXXY02.png. Copyright-free]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because human biological males have one X chromosome and one Y chromosome.\n\n **Choice A** shows the sex chromosomes of a biological female. **Choice C** only shows one X chromosome, and **Choice D** only shows the Y chromosome, both of which are incorrect as biological males have an X and a Y chromosome."},{"ref":"BI0196","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0196\n\nNeurons of the fruit fly (*Drosophila melanogaster)* have 8 chromosomes. How many chromosomes are in a fertilised oocyte of a fruit fly?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$8\n\n- B.    $\\hspace{1em}$4\n- C.    $\\hspace{1em}$2\n- D.    $\\hspace{1em}$16","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAt fertilisation, two gametes, which are haploid, fuse together to produce a diploid cell. Gametes of a fruit fly have a haploid number of 4, so fertilisation produces a cell with 8 chromosomes (**Choice A**).\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because 4 is the haploid number. **Choice C** is incorrect because fruit fly somatic cells have 8 chromosomes and fertilised egg cells have 8 chromosomes. **Choice D** is incorrect because 16 is twice the diploid number of fruit flies."},{"ref":"BI0214","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0214\n\nTwo individuals, heterozygous for a specific trait, were crossed in an experiment. Which row shows the expected phenotypic ratios of the offspring resulting from this cross?\n\n| | Offspring phenotypes | Ratio |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| All dominant | 1 |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Dominant: Recessive | 1:1 |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Dominant: Recessive | 3:1 |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Dominant: Recessive | 1:3 |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nExample:\n\n- Parent 1 Genotype - Rr (heterozygous)\n- Parent 2 Genotype - Rr (heterozygous)\n- Gametes - R & r\n\n| | R | r |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{.5em}$R$\\hspace{.5em}$| $\\hspace{.5em}$RR$\\hspace{.5em}$ | $\\hspace{.5em}$Rr$\\hspace{.5em}$ |\n|$\\hspace{.5em}$r$\\hspace{.5em}$ | $\\hspace{.5em}$Rr$\\hspace{.5em}$ | $\\hspace{.5em}$rr$\\hspace{.5em}$ |\n\nThe expected phenotypic ratio would be 3:1. Three individuals would display the dominant trait, as 3 of the possible combinations of alleles contain the dominant allele. One individual would display the recessive trait, as only one of the possible combinations of alleles contains two copies of the recessive trait."},{"ref":"BI0215","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0215\n\nThere are two alleles for flower colour in snapdragons. However, the result of a cross between two heterozygous plants yielded offspring with three different observed phenotypes, red, white and pink flowers. Which is the most likely explanation for this observation?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ One allele is dominant to the other.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    There is codominance between alleles.\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    There is incomplete dominance between alleles.\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    The alleles are sex-linked.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIf there are two alleles for a gene, but there are three possible phenotypes, this could suggest incomplete dominance (**Choice C**). This is when the dominant allele doesn’t completely suppress the effects of the recessive allele. **Choice A** is incorrect because if one allele was completely dominant over the other, then there would only be two possible phenotypes. **Choice B** is incorrect because if the alleles were codominant, then we would see both expressed in the phenotype. In this case, some petals would be white and others red, instead of the flower being pink. **Choice D** is incorrect because sex-linkage does not create an additional phenotype."},{"ref":"BI0216","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0216\n\nMendel observed that the seed shape in individual pea plants was either round or wrinkled. The results of an experimental cross yielded 315 offspring with round seeds and 105 offspring with wrinkled seeds.\n\nWhich row in the table below states the most likely genotypes of the parents?\n\n| | $\\hspace{1em}$ Parent I $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Parent II $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| RR | rr |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Rr | rr |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Rr | Rr |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Rr | RR |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n315:105 simplified is 3:1.\nIn a monohybrid cross, 3:1 is the expected phenotypic ratio of offspring from two heterozygous parents (**Choice C**).\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect, because a cross between a homozygous dominant parent (RR) and a homozygous recessive parent (rr) would result in all offspring having smooth seeds.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect, because a cross between a heterozygous parent (Rr) and homozygous recessive parent (rr) would produce the phenotypic ratio of 1:1 among offspring.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect, because a cross between a heterozygous parent (Rr) and a homozygous dominant parent (RR) would result in all offspring having round seeds."},{"ref":"BI0217","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0217\n\nMendel observed that there are two possible variations of flower colour in pea plants, purple and white. After a genetic cross, the ratio of plants with purple flowers to white flowers is 1:1. What were the genotypes of the parents?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Both parents are heterozygous.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ One parent is heterozygous and the other is homozygous recessive.\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Both parents are homozygous recessive.\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ One parent is heterozygous and the other is homozygous dominant.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct, because for a 1:1 ratio in the offspring, one parent must be heterozygous (Rr) so that half of the offspring receive the dominant allele and half receive the recessive allele from that parent, and one parent must be homozygous recessive (rr).\n\n| | R | r |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$r$\\hspace{1em}$| $\\hspace{1em}$ Rr $\\hspace{1em}$  |  $\\hspace{1em}$ rr $\\hspace{1em}$  |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$r$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Rr $\\hspace{1em}$  | $\\hspace{1em}$  rr $\\hspace{1em}$  |\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect, because the ratio of offspring in two heterozygous parents would be 3:1. **Choice C** is incorrect, because all of the offspring from two homozygous recessive parents will have the same (recessive) characteristic. **Choice D** is incorrect, because with one heterozygous parent and one homozygous dominant parent, the offspring would all have the same (dominant) characteristic."},{"ref":"BI0218","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0218\n\nSickle cell anaemia is caused by a recessive allele (Hb$^{S}$). The dominant allele is Hb$^{A}$. This image shows the inheritance of sickle cell anaemia in a family.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI0218i.png\" alt=\"BI0218i\">\n\nWhat are the genotypes for individuals 1 and 7?\n\n| | Individual 1 | Individual 7 |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| Hb$^{A}$ Hb$^{S}$| Hb$^{S}$ Hb$^{S}$ |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Hb$^{A}$ Hb$^{S}$| Hb$^{A}$ Hb$^{S}$ |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Hb$^{A}$ Hb$^{A}$| Hb$^{A}$ Hb$^{S}$|\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Hb$^{A}$ Hb$^{A}$ | Hb$^{S}$ Hb$^{S}$|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct, because individual 1 (and 2) must be heterozygous because they are both unaffected but have affected biological children. Individual 7 must be homozygous recessive (Hb$^{S}$ Hb$^{S}$) because they are affected.\n**Choices B** and **C** are incorrect, because individual 7 is affected and thus must be homozygous recessive.\n**Choice D** is incorrect, because if individual 1 were homozygous dominant (Hb$^{A}$ Hb$^{A}$), none of their offspring would be affected."},{"ref":"BI0219","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0219\n\nWhich of these statements about genetic disorders is/are correct?\n\n- I.    $\\hspace{1em}$ The majority of genetic disorders are caused by recessive alleles.\n- II. $\\hspace{.7em}$    Genetic disorders can be sex-linked.\n- III. $\\hspace{.4em}$    Offspring of two parents with the same genetic disease will always be\naffected.\n\n- A.    $\\hspace{1em}$All three\n\n- B.    $\\hspace{1em}$II only\n- C.    $\\hspace{1em}$I and III\n- D.     $\\hspace{1em}$I and II","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because **Statements I** and **II** are both true for genetic disorders. Dominant alleles cause some genetic disorders, but most are caused by recessive ones (**Statement I**) and some genetic disorders, such as haemophilia, are sex-linked (**Statement II**).\n\nIf a parent has a genetic disorder that is caused by a dominant allele, they may be heterozygous, so two heterozygous individuals can have a child that is homozygous dominant for the characteristic and they will not have the disease, therefore **Statement III** is not true.\n\n**Choices A** and **C** include **Statement III** and so are incorrect. **Choice B** is incorrect as it is missing **Statement I**"},{"ref":"BI0220","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0220\n\nA biological man and a biological woman have a biological daughter and son. Their biological daughter has normal vision, but their biological son is colourblind. Which one of these genotypes could be correct for the biological mother?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ X $^{N}$ Y\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ X $^{n}$ Y\n\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ X $^{N}$ X $^{n}$\n\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ X $^{N}$ X $^{N}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because the biological mother passes on the X chromosome to a biological son and the biological father contributes only with Y. For the biological son to be colourblind, he must have an allele for it on the X chromosome. Therefore the biological mother must have at least one recessive allele (n).\n\n**Choices A** and **B** are incorrect because individuals with X and Y are biologically male.\n**Choice D** is incorrect because, in this case, no biological sons would have the condition."},{"ref":"BI0222","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0222\n\nIn some animals, the agouti allele causes individual hairs to have a banded pattern. The non-agouti allele causes hairs to be a single colour. Individuals who are heterozygous have hairs with a banded pattern.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BIO222.png\" alt=\"BIO222\">\n\nWhich combination of parent genotypes would allow for production of offspring that do **not** have the agouti characteristic?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ One heterozygous parent and one parent who is homozygous for the agouti allele.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Two parents who are both heterozygous.\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ One parent who is homozygous for the agouti allele, and one parent who is homozygous for the non-agouti allele.\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Two parents who are homozygous for the agouti allele.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe question states that heterozygous individuals have the banded pattern on their hairs, which means that the agouti trait is dominant. Two heterozygous parents will produce offspring in the likely phenotypic ratio of 3 agouti:1:non-agouti (**Choice B**).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/B.jpg\" alt=\"B\">\n\nThe offspring of a heterozygous individual and a homozygous agouti individual will all receive at least one dominant allele, so they would all be agouti (**Choice A**).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/Choice%20A.jpg\" alt=\"Choice A\">\n\nThe offspring of one parent who is homozygous for the agouti allele, and one parent who is homozygous for the non-agouti allele (**Choice C**) would all be heterozygous and so banded.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/C.jpg\" alt=\"C\">\n\nThe offspring of two parents who are homozygous for the agouti allele will all be the same as their parents. (**Choice D**).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/D.jpg\" alt=\"D\">"},{"ref":"BI0223","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0223\n\nWhat best describes the inheritance of the condition shown in the pedigree chart below?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI0223QB.png\" alt=\"BI0223QB\">\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Sex-linked trait\n- II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ Dominant trait\n- III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ Recessive trait\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because it is an autosomal recessive trait. We know it is a recessive trait (**Statement III**) because the unaffected biological parents (individuals 5 and 6) have affected offspring (individual 8). That means both biological parents are carriers of the recessive allele.\n\nIt is not caused by a dominant allele (**Statement II**) because an affected child (individual 8) has unaffected biological parents. It is not a sex-linked trait (**Statement I**) because an unaffected biological mother (individual 6) and unaffected biological father (individual 5) have an affected daughter (individual 8). If this was a sex-linked condition, in order for her to be affected, she would have received the trait on both copies of the X chromosomes she inherited from her biological parents (if recessive sex-linked) or at least from one biological parent (if dominant sex-linked, but then at least one of the biological parents should have had the condition)."},{"ref":"BI0224","marks":"11","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0224\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\n1. Sickle cell anaemia is caused by the allele Hb$^S$. The normal allele is Hb$^A$.\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI0224.png\" alt=\"BI0224\">\n\n 1. Identify the genotype of individuals 1 and 2. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Predict the probability of individuals 5 and 6 having another child that is unaffected. Justify your answer. [2 marks]\n\n 3. If individual 7 had a child with an affected biolgical female, identify the possible genotypes of the child. Justify your answer. [2 marks]\n\n2. Deduce, **with evidence**, the pattern of inheritance of sickle cell anaemia that is shown in this pedigree chart. [3 marks]\n\n3. Distinguish between sex-linked inheritance and autosomal inheritance in humans. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Hb$^A$ Hb$^S$ **AND** Hb$^A$ Hb$^S$ [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n The genotypes of individuals 1 and 2 must be **Hb$^A$ Hb$^S$** ($\\checkmark$) (heterozygous) because they are unaffected, but have affected children.\n\n 2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 50% **OR** 1:1 **OR** ½ [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ individual 8 is affected, so the unaffected parent must be heterozygous **OR** Punnett square showing the outcomes between heterozygous and homozygous recessive individuals [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n The chances are **50%** ($\\checkmark$). The **unaffected biological father** had an **affected biological daughter**, so he must be **heterozygous** ($\\checkmark$). Hb$^A$ Hb$^S$ and Hb$^S$ Hb$^S$ parents have one out of two chances to have an affected child.\n\n 3. Any **two** of the following (annotated Punnett square is acceptable for full marks):\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ possible genotypes are Hb$^A$ Hb$^S$ and Hb$^S$ Hb$^S$ **OR** offspring can be only heterozygous or homozygous recessive [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ individual 7 is heterozygous **OR** individual 7 can pass on either allele, Hb$^A$ or Hb$^S$ to their offspring [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ affected biological females will pass the recessive allele to all offspring **OR** biological mother would be Hb$^S$ Hb$^S$ genotype [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\nThe offspring could be **Hb$^A$ Hb$^S$ or Hb$^S$ Hb$^S$** ($\\checkmark$) because individual 7 has an affected biological mother, but isn’t affected themselves, so they must be **heterozygous** ($\\checkmark$) for sickle cell anaemia. If they have a child with an affected biological female, the child would receive the **recessive allele** from the biological mother ($\\checkmark$) but could receive the dominant or recessive allele from the biological father.\n\n2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sickle cell anaemia is recessive [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** two unaffected **OR** heterozygous **OR** carrier parents had an affected child [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sickle cell anaemia is controlled by a gene on an autosome **OR** is an autosomal recessive trait **OR** is not a sex-linked trait [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** an affected bioloical mother can have an unaffected biological son [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n This pedigree chart shows that **sickle cell anaemia is a recessive trait** ($\\checkmark$). Evidence for this is that **individuals 1 and 2 are unaffected but can have an affected child** ($\\checkmark$)\n. This pedigree chart also shows that this is **not a sex-linked trait** ($\\checkmark$)\n. Evidence for this is that **an affected biological mother can have an unaffected biological son** ($\\checkmark$); if it was a sex-linked trait, all of the biological sons of an affected biological female would be affected.\n\n3. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sex-linked genes are usually found on the X chromosome from the 23rd, sex chromosome, pair **WHILE** autosomal genes are found on chromosomes 1-22 [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ inheritance is dependent on the biological sex of the offspring in sex-linked BUT not in non-sex linked [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ biological males receive one allele for sex-linked characteristics, and biological females receive two alleles for sex-linked characteristics (when carried on the X chromosome), **BUT** biological males and females both receive two alleles for characteristics controlled by genes on autosomes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ biological males have a higher chance of having a sex-linked recessive characteristic than biological females, **WHILE** there is no difference in the inheritance of autosomal characteristics between biological males and females [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ if a biological female is affected by a recessive sex-linked trait, all of her biological sons will be affected, **BUT** for autosomal traits, some biological males of an affected biological mother may not be affected [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in sex-linked inheritance, biological females can be carriers for a trait, and biological males cannot be carriers, **WHILE** in autosomal inheritance, both biological sexes can be carriers [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n Sex-linked inheritance refers to the inheritance of **genes typically located on the X chromosome** of the 23rd pair, whereas autosomal inheritance is the inheritance of genes found on **chromosomes 1-22** ($\\checkmark$)\n. **Biological males inherit a single allele** for X-linked characteristics from their biological mother, but **biological males and females inherit two alleles** ($\\checkmark$)\n for characteristics controlled by genes on autosomes. **Biological males** have a **higher chance of sex-linked recessive** characteristics\nthan biological females, but there is **no difference** in the inheritance of **autosomal characteristics** between **biological males and females** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0501","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0501\n\nA biological female with blood type A had a child with blood type O. What are the possible genotypes of the biological father?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | ii |\n    | II. | I:sup[A]i |\n    | III. | I:sup[B]i |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe human blood typing system includes three alleles:\n- I:sup[A]\n- I:sup[B]\n- i\n\nI:sup[A] codes for blood type A, and I:sup[B] codes for blood type B. These alleles are codominant to each other (such that the genotype I:sup[A]I:sup[B] results in blood type AB), and dominant to the allele i (such that the genotype  I:sup[A]i results in blood type A and the genotype  I:sup[B]i results in blood type B). Only the genotype ii  codes for blood type O.\n\nThe genotype of the child must be ii. This child must have received one i allele from their mother (meaning the genotype of the mother must be I:sup[A]i) and one i allele from their father. So, the father could be of any genotype that contains at least one i allele. Therefore, the genotype of the father could be any of ii, I:sup[A]i, or I:sup[B]i."},{"ref":"BI0502","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0502\n\nWhat suggests that the inheritance of the disorder shown in the pedigree chart is X-linked recessive and not autosomal recessive?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI0502QB.png\" alt=\"BI0502QB\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Two unaffected individuals can have affected offspring.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ An unaffected biological female can produce unaffected biological sons.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Biological males are more likely to be affected than biological females.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Both biological males and biological females can be affected.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn the pedigree, only biological males are shown to inherit the disorder. The single biological female with the disorder is a mate of a biological male in generation II and not an offspring of the original parents. So, according to the pedigree, biological males are more likely than biological females to be affected, which suggests that the trait is X-linkage recessive.\n\nTwo unaffected individuals can have affected offspring (**Choice A**), an unaffected biological female can produce unaffected biological sons (**Choice B**), and both biological males and biological females can be affected (**Choice D**) when a trait is transmitted through either an X-linked recessive or autosomal recessive pattern of inheritance."},{"ref":"BI0543","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0543\n\nA biological male with haemophilia and a biological female without haemophilia have a daughter without haemophilia. What are the possible genotypes of the daughter?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | X:sup[H]X:sup[H] |\n    | II. | X:sup[H]X:sup[h] |\n    | III. | X:sup[h]X:sup[h] |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nHaemophilia is a sex-linked recessive disorder carried by a recessive allele, X:sup[h], on the X chromosome. Since biological males only receive one copy of the X chromosome, a biological male with haemophilia must carry the recessive X:sup[h] allele. A biological female without haemophilia could have the genotypes X:sup[H]X:sup[H], meaning she does not carry any copies of the recessive allele, or X:sup[H]X:sup[h], meaning she carries one dominant and one recessive allele. However, the biological daughter must inherit her biological father’s recessive allele, so the biological daughter will have one X:sup[h] allele (**Statement II** and **Statement III**). Since the biological daughter does not have haemophilia, she must have inherited the X:sup[H] allele from her biological mother (**Statement II**). Therefore, the only possible genotype for the biological daughter is X:sup[H]X:sup[h] (**Statement II**). **Choice B** is the correct answer to the question.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are all incorrect as they include **Statement I** and/or **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI0692","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0692\n\nThe graph shows the frequency of birth weight of children born in London in 1991.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI0692QB.png\" alt=\"BI0692QB\">\n\nWhat type of inheritance is indicated by this distribution?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Sex-linked\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Polygenic\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Monogenic\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Autosomal recessive","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nPolygenic inheritance (inheritance caused by more than one gene) can show the pattern of normal distribution in the graph. **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choices A**, **C** and **D** are incorrect as they do not show a pattern of normal distribution."},{"ref":"BI0693","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0693\n\nWhich is the correct definition of linked genes?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Genes that are found on the same chromosome\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Genes that are found on different chromosomes\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Genes that are recombined during crossing over\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Alternative forms of the same gene found on homologous chromosomes","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is incorrect as it describes unlinked genes. **Choice C** is incorrect as all genes can be recombined during crossing over.  However, the frequency of recombination due to crossing over is much lower in linked genes, depending on how close they are. **Choice D** is incorrect as it describes alleles."},{"ref":"BI0694","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0694\n\nWhich is the correct definition of a gene locus?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The DNA sequence of a gene\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ A variant of the same gene\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ A gene with the same sequence as another gene\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The position of a gene on a chromosome","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA gene locus (plural: loci) refers to the specific, fixed position of a gene on a chromosome. It indicates where a gene is located within the genome. **Choice D** is correct\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because this describes the genetic code or nucleotide sequence of a gene, not its physical position. The sequence and the locus are related but not the same. The locus tells you where the gene is found, not what it codes for.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because this defines an allele, not a locus. Alleles are different versions of the same gene that occupy the same locus on homologous chromosomes.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because the locus refers to the location, not sequence similarity, of a gene."},{"ref":"BI0696","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0696\n\nThe diagram shows a karyogram of a brown male rat (*Rattus norvegicus*). Which arrow is pointing to a pair of autosomes?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI0696.png\" alt=\"BI0696\">\n[Source: Adapted from Hamanaka , S., Yamaguchi , T., Kobayashi, T., Kato-Itoh, M., Yamazaki, S., Sato, H., Umino, A., Wakiyama, Y., Arai, M., Sanbo, M., Hirabayashi, M., & Nakauchi, H. (2011, July 15). *File: Karyogram of normal rat.png - wikimedia commons*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved July 31, 2022, from https ://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Karyogram_of_normal_rat.png Copyright under CC BY 2.5]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAutosomes are chromosomes that are not the sex chromosomes. **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as it is pointing to one autosome, not a pair. **Choice C** is incorrect as it is pointing to two pairs of autosomes. **Choice D** is incorrect as it is pointing to the sex chromosomes."},{"ref":"BI0697","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0697\n\nWhich of these traits is an example of continuous variation?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Blood type\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Eye colour\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Adult height\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Colour blindness","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nContinuous variation is when a particular characteristic shows a large range of phenotypes. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choices A**, **B** and **D** are incorrect as they are all examples of discrete/discontinuous variation, which is when a particular characteristic falls into distinct categories."},{"ref":"BI0698","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0698\n\nThe diagram below shows a cross between a male cat that is homozygous for brown, long hair and a female cat that has black, short hair.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI0698.png\" alt=\"BI0698\">\n\nWhich of the offspring has a recombinant genotype?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A. | | | |B.|\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI0698-A-.png\" alt=\"BI0698-A-\">| | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI0698-B-.png\" alt=\"BI0698-B-\">|\n|C. | | | |D. |\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI0698-C-.png\" alt=\"BI0698-C-\"> | | | | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI0698-D-.png\" alt=\"BI0698-D-\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI0698MS.png\" alt=\"BI0698MS\">\n\nBbll is a recombinant as it is a mixture of the parental genotypes. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choices A** and **D** are incorrect because they are the parental genotypes, not recombinants. **Choice B** is incorrect because it is impossible to have offspring with BB, as only one parent has a B in their genotype."},{"ref":"BI0699","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0699\n\nA dihybrid cross was carried out between two sunflower plants (*Helianthus annuus*)  to see if the genes for seed number and seed shape are linked. The critical value for the chi-squared test at the 0.05 significance level was 7.82. The calculated value for the chi-squared test was 10.05; what does this indicate?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The null hypothesis can be rejected and the genes are linked\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The null hypothesis can be accepted and the genes are unlinked\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ The alternative hypothesis can be rejected and the genes are linked\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The alternative hypothesis can be accepted and the genes are unlinked","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIf the calculated value (10.05) is above the critical value (7.82), then the null hypothesis is rejected and we can conclude that the traits are linked at the 0.05 significance level. **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect as the calculated critical value is above 7.82, so the null hypothesis should be rejected. **Choice C** is incorrect as although the genes are linked the alternative hypothesis should be accepted. **Choice D** is incorrect because if the alternative hypothesis is accepted then the genes are linked."},{"ref":"BI0700","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0700\n\nWhich keyword is matched to the correct definition?\n\n| | Keyword | Definition |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| Autosomes | Chromosomes that determine biological sex |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Linked genes | Genes that are found on homologous chromosomes |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Gene locus | The sequence of genes on a chromosome |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Segregation | Separation of alleles of genes that occurs during meiosis |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is incorrect as autosomes are chromosomes that are not the sex chromosomes and so do not determine biological sex. **Choice B** is incorrect as linked genes are genes that are found on the same chromosome. **Choice C** is incorrect as a gene locus is the position of a gene on a chromosome."},{"ref":"BI0701","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0701\n\nIn dandelions (*Taraxacum officinale*), the allele for diamond-shaped seeds (D) is dominant over oval-shaped seeds (d) and the allele for long seeds (L) is dominant over short seeds (l).\n\nIn a genetic cross between a dandelion that is heterozygous for both traits and a dandelion that is homozygous recessive for both traits, what would be the expected proportion of long seeds?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2 out of 16\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 4 out of 16\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 5 out of 16\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 8 out of 16","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choices A, B**  and **C**  are incorrect as when the cross is carried out there is a 50% chance of producing long seeds. In a punnett square, there are 4 possible genotypes; DdLl (diamond oval seeds), Ddll (diamond short seeds), ddLl (oval long seeds) and ddll (oval short seeds), each with a 4 out of 16 probability of being produced.  Therefore, there is an 8 out of 16 chance of producing long seeds. **Choice D is correct.**"},{"ref":"BI0702","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0702\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nIn Mongolian horses (*Equus przewalskii*), fur colour is determined by a number of genes. Brown colour (B) is dominant to sandy colour (b) and an ombre (two tone) colour (P) is dominant to one tone (p).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI0702.png\" alt=\"BI0702\">\n[Adapted from: Source Ksuryawanshi. (2015, October 15). *Category: Mongolian horse*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved July 31, 2022, from https ://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Mongolian_horses.jpg Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n1.    A homozygous sandy ombre male horse is crossed with a heterozygous horse for both traits. Draw a Punnett square to show the potential results of this dihybrid cross. [3 marks]\n\n2.    Identify the possible genotype recombinations, after fertilisation, from the cross in part (a).  [1 mark]\n\n3.    In pigs, the curly tail is dominant to the straight tail, and pink skin is dominant to black skin. On a pig farm, the resulting phenotypes from double-heteroygous genotype breeding were recorded. The chi-square value for the phenotypes of the offspring was calculated and the result was 340.6.\\\nThe table below shows the degrees of freedom (D.f.) and critical values at a significance level (p) of 0.05 (5%) for a chi-squared test.\\\n Discuss whether the genes for skin colour and tail shape in pigs are linked or unlinked. [3 marks]\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.f. $\\hspace{1.5em}$|$\\hspace{1.5em}$p=0.1 $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$ p=0.05  $\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ p=0.01 $\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| 1 | 2.706 | 3.841 | 6.635\n| 2 | 4.605 | 5.991 | 9.210\n| 3 | 6.251 | 7.815 | 11.345\n| 4 | 7.779 | 9.488 | 13.277\n| 5 | 9.236 | 11.070 | 15.086","markScheme":"1.    Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (correct alleles for gametes in both parents) bP for all male gametes and BP, Bp, bP, bp for female gametes     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (correct genotypes of offspring) BbPP, BbPp, bbPP, bbPp     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (correctly identified phenotypes of offspring) Brown ombre, Sandy ombre [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (correct probabilities for each phenotype **OR** genotype) 1:1,  **OR** 50%:50%, for Brown ombre : Sandy ombre **OR** 1:1:1:1,  **OR** 25%:25%:25%:25%, for BbPP : BbPp : bbPP : bbPp  [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n    Parents:     $\\hspace{3em}$ bbPP (male) $\\hspace{3em}$    x $\\hspace{3em}$ BbPp (female)\n\n    Gametes:   $\\hspace{3em}$ bP, bP, bP, bP $\\hspace{1.5em}$ x    $\\hspace{1.5em}$    BP, Bp, bP, bp   $\\hspace{3em}$  ($\\checkmark$)\n\n    Punnet square:\n\n|  | BP | Bp | bP | bp |\n| :-: | :-: | :-:| :-: | :-: |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$ **bP** $\\hspace{1.5em}$|$\\hspace{1.5em}$ BbPP$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$BbPp$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$bbPP$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\hspace{1.5em}$bbPp$\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n\n$\\hspace{1.5em}$ Offspring:\n\n| Genotypes of the offspring | $\\hspace{1.5em}$ BbPP$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$BbPp$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$bbPP$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\hspace{1.5em}$bbPp$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | ($\\checkmark$)|\n| :-: | :-: | :-:| :-: | :-: | :-:|\n|Probability of genotypes|$\\hspace{1.5em}$ 25%$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$25%$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$25%$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\hspace{1.5em}$25%$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | ($\\checkmark$)|\n\n| Phenotypes of the offspring | $\\hspace{1.5em}$ Brown ombre$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$ Sandy ombre $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | ($\\checkmark$)|\n| :-: | :-: | :-:| :-: |\n|Probability of phenotypes|$\\hspace{1.5em}$ 50%$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$50%$\\hspace{1.5em}$  | ($\\checkmark$)|\n\n2.    **Both** answers needed for the mark\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ bbPp **AND** BbPP     [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n3.    Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genes are linked [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the calculated chi square value is higher than the critical value    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the degrees of freedom is calculated by subtracting one from the number of categories [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ three degrees of freedom [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the null hypothesis should be rejected **OR** the alternative hypothesis should be accepted [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nThere are four possible offspring phenotypes in the double heterozygous genetic cross. To calculate the degrees of freedom, we need **to subtract 1 from 4** ($\\checkmark$); therefore, there are 3 degrees of freedom in this cross. The genes are **linked** ($\\checkmark$), as the calculated chi-square value is **higher than the critical value** ($\\checkmark$) of 7.815 (p<0.05) in the p=0.05 column, at **three degrees of freedom** ($\\checkmark$). The **null hypothesis** assuming that there is no link between the genes,  is therefore **rejected** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0703","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0703\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\n1.     Distinguish between linked and unlinked genes. [2 marks]\n\n2.    Coat colour is a polygenic trait. State the meaning of *polygenic*. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.    Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  linked genes are always found on the same chromosome **WHEREAS** unlinked genes are usually found on different chromosomes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  linked genes are close together on the chromosome **WHEREAS** if unlinked genes are on the same chromosome they are far apart [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  linked genes do not segregate independently during meiosis **BUT** unlinked genes do [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  crossing over can cause linked genes to segregate independently, **BUT** unlinked genes always independently segregate. [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  linked genes are usaully inherited together **WHEREAS** unlinked genes are rarely inherited together [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n**Linked genes** are found on the **same chromosome** whereas **unlinked genes** are usually found on **different chromosomes** ($\\checkmark$).  This means that during meiosis **linked genes do not segregate independently but unlinked genes do** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.    $\\ce{ -> }$   traits that are genetically determined by two or more genes [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nA **polygenic** trait is one that is genetically determined by **two or more genes** ($\\checkmark$)\n."},{"ref":"BI0793","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0793\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe maps show the incidence of sickle cell anaemia and the spread of malaria across the continent of Africa.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI0793A.png\" alt=\"BI0793A\">\n[Source: Adapted from Allison, A. (2009, December 10). *File: Malaria versus sickle-cell trait distributions.png*. Retrieved May 3, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malaria_versus_sickle-cell_trait_distributions.png. Copyright free]\n\nThe graph shows the average density of malarial parasites present in the blood of Hb AA (not affected by sickle cell anaemia) and Hb AS (carriers of sickle cell anaemia) people in Sub-Saharan Africa.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BIO793B.png\" alt=\"BIO793B\">\n[Source: Adapted from Luzzatto, L. (2012, October 3). *Sickle cell anaemia and malaria*. Mediterranean journal of hematology and infectious diseases. Retrieved May 4, 2023, from https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3499995/ Copyright under CC BY 2.0]\n\n1. Compare and contrast between the distribution of sickle cell trait and malaria in Africa.    [2 marks]\n\n2.\n    1. State the mode of inheritance of sickle cell anaemia.    [1 mark]\n\n    2. Using a genetic diagram, determine the probability of a child having sickle cell anaemia, if both their parents are carriers of the disease.        [2 marks]\n\n3. Blood samples were taken from children to create the graph. Suggest one difference between the appearance of blood cells in Hb AA and Hb AS samples.    [1 mark]\n\n4. State at which age there is the largest difference between the number of parasites found in the blood of Hb AA and Hb AS samples.    [1 mark]\n\n5. Suggest one difficulty of collecting sufficient data for this study. [1 mark]\n\n6. Using your own knowledge and the data provided, discuss whether carrying the sickle cell allele offers protection from malaria.    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    **Similarities**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is an overlap in the distribution of malaria and distribution of people with the sickle cell trait in the middle of Africa **OR** Sub-Saharan Africa        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is an overlap in a small region of East Africa, in the distribution of malaria and distribution of people with the sickle cell trait in the middle of Africa **OR** Sub-Saharan Africa [1 mark]\n\n    **Differences**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ malaria is present in Northern Africa **BUT** there is less than 1% **OR** a very low percentage of people with the sickle cell trait in Northern Africa    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ malaria is present in Northern Africa **BUT** there is less than 1% **OR** a very low percentage of people with the sickle cell trait in Northern Africa    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is a much wider distribution of malaria across the continent **COMPARED** to the distribution of the sickle cell trait **OR** sickle cell trait is mainly found in western and central Africa **WHEREAS** malaria is distributed as a band across the central third of Africa and at the Northern tip                             [1 mark]\n\n    *Answer must include at least one difference and one similarity to gain full marks*\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    There is an **overlap** in the **distribution of malaria and distribution of people with the sickle cell trait** ($\\checkmark$) across Sub-Saharan Africa  However across the whole continent, there is a **much wider distribution of malaria** across the continent **compared** to the distribution of the sickle cell trait ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ autosomal recessive    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The mode of inheritance for the sickle cell trait is **autosomal recessive** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    2.  Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ alleles must be written correctly for both parents; Hb:sup[A] Hb:sup[S] **OR** AS    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ correct genotypes for all four potential offspring in a punnett square; Hb:sup[A] Hb:sup[A], Hb:sup[S] Hb:sup[A], Hb:sup[S] Hb:sup[A], Hb:sup[S]Hb:sup[S] OR AA, SA, SA, SS        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  25 % **OR** 0.25  **OR** ¼     [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI0793ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0793ANSA\">\n\n    There is a **25 %** ($\\checkmark$)  chance of having offspring who have sickle cell anaemia.\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Hb AS blood samples would have a mixture of sickle cell shaped red blood cells and normal **OR** biconcave red blood cells **WHEREAS** HbAA blood samples would have only normal **OR** biconcave shaped red blood cells    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    One difference between the blood samples is that Hb AS blood samples would have a **mixture** of **sickle cell shaped** red blood cells and **normal shaped** red blood cells whereas HbAA blood samples would have **only normal shaped red blood cells** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **one** of the following\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 3 years old        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI0793ANSB.png\" alt=\"BI0793ANSB\">\n\n    The age at which there is the largest difference between the number of parasites found in the Hb AA and Hb AS samples is **3 years old** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ have to collect data from a large continent    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ children cannot always give consent, so consent has to be sought from parents    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ limited access to healthcare facilities to collect blood samples [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ collecting and analysing blood samples for malarial parasites can be time consuming **OR** costly    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cultural barriers **OR** collecting blood samples may be viewed with suspicion **OR** reluctance in some communities    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    One difficulty in collecting sufficient data for this study is **collecting data from a large continent** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    **Yes because…**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the presence of the sickle cell allele in the population overlaps with the prevalence of malaria, due to selective pressures    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ from ages 3 - 9 children carrying the sickle cell anaemia trait have a lower density of malarial parasites in their blood compared to those that don’t carry the trait [1 mark]\n\n    **No because…**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is incomplete overlap between the prevalence of malaria and carrying the sickle cell anaemia trait [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the study comparing malarial parasite density between Hb AA and Hb AS children, only focuses on the first ten years of life     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at ten years old there is no difference in malarial parasite density between the Hb AA and Hb AS children    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at two years old Hb AS children have a slightly higher malarial parasite density than Hb AA children [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Having the sickle cell anaemia trait may offer some protection from malaria, as the **sickle cell trait overlaps with the persistent prevalence of malaria** due to selective pressures ($\\checkmark$), however the study comparing parasite density in HbAA and HbAS children, shows that at **ten years old** there is **no difference** in malarial parasite density between the Hb AA and Hb AS children ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0811","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0811\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nIn guinea pigs (*Cavia porcellus*), long hair (L) is dominant to short hair (l), and curly hair (C) is dominant to straight hair (c). A test cross was carried out between male heterozygous long, curly-haired guinea pigs and female short, straight-haired guinea pigs and a chi-squared test (χ2) was carried out on the offspring.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI0811A.png\" alt=\"BI0811A\">\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI0811B.png\" alt=\"BI0811B\">\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI0811C.png\" alt=\"BI0811C\">\n\n1.\n 1. Using a genetic diagram, state the expected ratio of the cross between the two parents. [3 marks]\n\n 2. Identify the recombinants. [1 mark]\n\n2.\n 1. Calculate the ratio of the observed phenotypes. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Suggest a reason for the difference between the expected and observed ratios. [1 mark]\n\n3.\n 1. State the null hypothesis in this study. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Deduce with reasons whether the null hypothesis should be accepted. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ parental gamete genotypes identified in the punnett square; male gametes: LC, Lc, lC, lc **AND** female gametes: lc [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ offspring genotypes identified as LlCc, Llcc, llCc, llcc [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 1:1:1:1 [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Father´s genotype = LlCc\n\n Mother’s genotype = llcc\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI0811ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0811ANSA\">\n\n There is a **1:1:1:1** ratio ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Llcc **AND** llCc [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ long straight **AND** short curly [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The recombinants are **Llcc** and **llCc** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 4:2:1:3 **OR** 0.4: 0.2: 0.1 : 0.3 **OR** 0.368: 0.194: 0.110: 0.328 **OR** 0.37: 0.19: 0.11: 0.33 [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n 184 + 97 + 55 + 164 = 500\n\n Curly long hair = 184 ÷ 500 = 0.368\n\n Curly short hair = 97 ÷ 500 = 0.194\n\n Straight long hair = 55 ÷ 500 = 0.110\n\n Straight curly hair = 164 ÷ 500 = 0.328\n\n 0.368 : 0.194 : 0.110 : 0.328\n\n **0.4 : 0.2 : 0.1 : 0.3** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ genes located on the same chromosomes **OR** genes linked [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ frequent crossing over, during meiosis **OR** prophase 1 **OR** production of gametes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ multiple genes **OR** gene interactions, may influence expression of trait [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ environmental factors influencing gene expression [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The expected and observed phenotypic ratios may be different due to the two **genes being linked** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ there is no significant difference between the observed and expected frequencies of offspring phenotypes **OR** the observed offspring ratios are consistent with the expected ratios based on Mendelian genetics [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the genes are unlinked [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The null hypothesis is that there is **no significant difference** ($\\checkmark$) between the observed offspring ratios, and the expected ratios that are based on Mendelian genetics.\n\n3.\n 2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ null hypothesis should be rejected [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ expected ratio differs significantly from the observed ratio [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at three degrees of freedom **AND** the 0.05 significance level [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chi squared value **OR** 95.6 is higher than the critical value **OR** 7.815 [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n At a significance level of **0.05** and **three degrees of freedom** ($\\checkmark$), the calculated chi-squared value **exceeds the critical value** ($\\checkmark$). This indicates a **significant difference** between the observed and expected ratios ($\\checkmark$) and so the **null hypothesis should be rejected** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1066","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1066\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Explain how parents with blood type A and blood type B can have a child with blood type O.    [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline how polygenic traits can show a normal distribution.        [4 marks]\n\n3. Explain the role of inheritance in genetic diseases.        [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following (accept any marking point clearly shown in a genetic diagram):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the allele for blood group A **OR** allele I:sup[A], is dominant over the allele for blood group O **OR** allele i     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the allele for blood group B **OR** allele I:sup[B], is dominant over the allele for blood group O **OR** allele i      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ parents with blood group A or blood group B could be heterozygous **OR** I:sup[A]i and I:sup[B]i      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a parent who is heterozygous for blood group A can pass on the allele for blood group O **OR** allele i        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a parent who is heterozygous for blood group B can pass on the allele for blood group O **OR** allele i        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ offspring that receive the allele for blood type O **OR** i allele from both parents will be blood group O    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The alleles for blood type A and blood type B are **dominant** ($\\checkmark$) to the allele for blood type O. Parents with blood type A or B could be **heterozygous** ($\\checkmark$) and possess the recessive allele for blood type O. If the offspring **inherits the recessive allele** ($\\checkmark$) from both parents, then they will have blood type O.\n\n   <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polygenic traits are caused by two or more genes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the greater the number of genes that are responsible for a trait, the greater the number of phenotypes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polygenic traits often show continuous variation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ which is a continuous range of variation rather than distinct categories    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is a large range of phenotypes, where the majority of organisms are in the middle of the normal distribution curve            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polygenic traits are often influenced by the environment    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Polygenic traits are caused by **two or more genes** ($\\checkmark$). The **higher the number of genes** ($\\checkmark$) that are responsible for a trait, the **higher the number of possible phenotypes** ($\\checkmark$) which means that the traits show **continuous variation** ($\\checkmark$). Continuous variation is when a phenotype shows a **continuous range of variation** ($\\checkmark$), rather than having distinct categories and usually shows a normal distribution, where the majority of organisms are in the **middle of the normal distribution curve** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic diseases are controlled by genes **OR** alleles        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ alleles are passed from parents to offspring **OR** gametes have one allele for each characteristic from each parent        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at fertilization the alleles from both parents come together **OR** the combination of alleles determines the characteristic in the offspring    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ diseases caused by dominant alleles only need the allele from one parent to cause the disease in the offspring        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Huntington’s is an autosomal dominant condition **OR** any other valid autosomal dominant condition example            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ diseases caused by recessive alleles need the allele from both parents to cause the disease in the offspring        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phenylketonuria is an autosomal recessive condition **OR** any other valid autosomal recessive condition example        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phenylketonuria caused by mutation of gene that codes for enzyme needed to convert phenylalanine to tyrosine **OR** other suitable example        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (most) sex-linked characteristics are controlled by alleles found on the X chromosome            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ haemophilia is a sex-linked recessive condition **OR** any other valid sex-linked recessive condition example            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sex-linked genetic diseases are more common in males than females     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if a mother is affected by a sex-linked characteristic all of her sons will have the condition        [1 mark]\n    [9 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Genetic diseases are **controlled by alleles** ($\\checkmark$), inherited from biological parents. During meiosis, gametes are produced, which contain **one allele for each characteristic** ($\\checkmark$), and these come together during **fertilization**. The **combination** of alleles passed to offspring can determine whether the offspring is affected by a genetic disease or not ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    Some genetic diseases, such as **Huntington’s disease** ($\\checkmark$), are caused by **dominant alleles** ($\\checkmark$), so one copy of the allele needs to be inherited for the disease to be expressed and therefore affected by the disease. Other genetic diseases, such as **cystic fibrosis**, are caused by **recessive alleles** ($\\checkmark$), which means two copies need to be inherited, one from each biological parent, in order to be expressed and affected with the disease.\n\n    Sex-linked genetic disorders, such as **haemophilia** ($\\checkmark$), are mostly controlled by genes found on the **X chromosome** ($\\checkmark$). If a male inherits an allele that codes for a genetic disorder from his biological mother, he will be affected; however, if a female inherits the same allele, she won’t always be affected, depending on the allele she inherited on the X chromosome from her biological father. It’s because of this that males have a **higher chance** ($\\checkmark$) of being affected by sex-linked genetic diseases."},{"ref":"BI1147","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1147\n\nWhat can be concluded from the following karyogram?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/karyogram%20XYY.png\" alt=\"karyogram XYY\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ There was non-disjunction during meiosis in the mother.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ There was non-disjunction during meiosis in the father.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ There is one missing chromosome.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ There are two extra chromosomes.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe primary mechanism for the development of the XYY syndrome is a non-disjunction event during the sperm production process. Non-disjunction is when chromosomes fail to separate properly during nuclear division. In the case of XYY syndrome, during meiosis in the male, the two Y sister chromatids fail to separate properly during meiosis II. This results in a sperm cell ending up with two Y chromosomes. When this sperm (carrying YY) fertilises a normal egg with one X chromosome, the resulting zygote will have one X and two Y chromosomes (XYY). **Choice B** is correct.\n\nThe biological mother can only contribute X chromosomes. Non-disjunction in the mother could lead to other chromosomal abnormalities, such as Triple X syndrome (XXX)), but not to an increase in the number of Y chromosomes. **Choice A** is incorrect.\n\nMissing chromosomes typically refer to conditions where a chromosome or a part of a chromosome is missing, such as Turner syndrome (XO), where an individual has only one X chromosome and no second sex chromosome. This individual has 48 chromosomes, not 45 so **Choice C** is incorrect.\n\nWhile having 2 Y chromosomes in addition to an X chromosome does involve one extra chromosome, it does NOT involve two extra. **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1149","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1149\n\nWhat type of inheritance is shown in this pedigree chart?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI1149FCM.png\" alt=\"BI1149FCM\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ X-linked dominant\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ X-linked recessive\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Autosomal dominant\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Autosomal recessive","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAutosomal recessive conditions are the only ones that allow for male carriers.\n\n**Choices A** and **C** are incorrect because dominant genetic disorders cannot result in male or female carriers. **Choice B** is incorrect because males cannot be carriers for X-linked conditions, which is another reason why **Choice A** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1180","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1180\n\nWhat is the correct reason for variation in skin colour between humans?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Monogenic inheritance and exposure to UV rays\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Nutrition and monogenic inheritance\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sex-linked inheritance\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Polygenic inheritance and exposure to UV rays","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nTraits that are inherited polygenically are caused by more than one gene and are usually influenced by the environment. Skin colour is influenced both by a mixture of genes and UV rays; **Choice D**  is correct.\n\nSkin colour is a result of polygenic, not monogenic, inheritance; **Choices A** and **B** are incorrect. The gene for skin colour is not found on the sex chromosomes, skin colour is not sex-linked; **Choice C**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1190","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1190\n\nA biological male with red-green colourblindness and a biological female without red-green colourblindness have a biologically female child without red-green colourblindness. What are the possible genotypes of this child?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | X:sup[B]X:sup[B] |\n    | II. | X:sup[b]X:sup[b] |\n    | III. | X:sup[B]X:sup[b] |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nRed-green colour blindness is a sex-linked recessive disorder carried on the X chromosome, X:sup[b]. Since biological males only receive one copy of the X chromosome, a biological male with red-green colour blindness must have the recessive X:sup[b] allele. A biological female without red-green colour blindness could be either homozygous dominant (X:sup[B]X:sup[B]) or heterozygous (X:sup[B]X:sup[b]). The biologically female child must inherit the biological father’s recessive allele, so will have one X:sup[b] allele (**Statement II** and **Statement III**). Since the child does not have red-green colour blindness, they must have inherited the dominant, X:sup[B], allele from the biological mother (**Statement II**). Therefore, the only possible genotype for the child is X:sup[B]X:sup[b] (**Choice C**)."},{"ref":"BI1230","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1230\n\nCystic fibrosis is caused by a recessive allele. Which individuals in generation II must be heterozygous for the trait?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI1230.png\" alt=\"BI1230\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 5 and 3\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 3 and 4\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 9 and 5\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 6 and 3","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nTo be heterozygous for a trait, a person must have one dominant and one recessive allele (Cc). Both persons 1 and 2 must be heterozygous in order for them to not have cystic fibrosis and have offspring (individual 6) with the disease (cc). In generation 2, all of the offspring of persons 1 and 2 could be heterozygous except person 6, who has cystic fibrosis (cc). However, only persons 3 and 4 must be heterozygous for the trait, as they do not have the disease and one of their offspring, person 10 has cystic fibrosis. **Choice B** is correct.\n\nWithout more information, it is difficult to say whether person 5 is heterozygous (Cc) or homozygous unaffected (CC), they have a 50% chance of being either; **Choice A** is incorrect. Person 9 is in generation 3; **Choice C** is incorrect. Person 6 is homozygous for the cystic fibrosis trait (cc); **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1244","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1244\n\nA set of identical (monozygotic) twins separated at birth are raised by different adoptive parents. As adults, their heights differ significantly. Which of the following could explain this observation?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mutation\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Natural selection\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Phenotypic plasticity\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Polygenic inheritance","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nPhenotypic plasticity is the capacity for traits to vary as a result of environmental differences and gene expression. Environmental factors such as diet could explain the variation in the adult height of the twins.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because identical twins only differ by a very small number of mutations which are unlikely to affect their height. **Choice B** is incorrect because natural selection acts over generations, not within an organism's lifetime. **Choice D** refers to traits that are influenced by multiple genes, of which height is an example, however this cannot explain the differences observed here as identical twins would have the same genotype."},{"ref":"BI1245","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1245\n\nA single-nucleotide polymorphism (SNP) at a particular location on a gene results in the production of a new allele. How many genotypes would be possible for this gene?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI1245.png\" alt=\"BI1245\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 2\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 3\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 4\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 6","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA gene with two alleles (A and B, for example) would have three possible genotypes (AA, BB, and AB).\n\n**Choices A** and **C** are not possible for a gene with two alleles. **Choice D** could only be correct for a gene with three alleles (A, B, and C, for example), which would have six possible genotypes (AA, BB, CC, AB, AC, BC)."},{"ref":"BI1246","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1246\n\nA man is homozygous for blood type B. His wife has blood type AB. What would be the possible blood types for their children?\n\n| | Type A | Type B | Type AB |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 25% | 75% | 0% |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 50% | 50% | 0% |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 25% | 50% | 25% |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 0% | 50% | 50% |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe man has the genotype I:sup[B]I:sup[B] while his wife has the genotype I:sup[A]I:sup[B]. Their children have to inherit the IB allele from the father, but the mother has a 50% chance of giving either one of her alleles to the children. This would give them a 50% chance of being type B and a 50% chance of being type AB.\n\n**Choices A, B**, and **C** represent incorrect or impossible phenotype frequencies."},{"ref":"BI1247","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1247\n\nIn domesticated horses (*Equus caballus*), coat colour is affected by a gene that has a cream (CR) allele. A chestnut horse with no cream allele has a reddish-brown coat. A horse with one copy of the cream allele is a palomino, with a golden coat and a pale mane and tail. A horse with two copies of the cream allele is a cremello, with a cream or off-white coat.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI1247.png\" alt=\"BI1247\">\n\nWhat can be deduced about the inheritance of coat colour controlled by this gene?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ It exhibits complete dominance.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ It exhibits incomplete dominance.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ It exhibits codominance.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ It does not follow known inheritance patterns.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA gene exhibiting incomplete dominance displays an intermediate phenotype, as seen here. The cream allele and non-cream alleles are both partially expressed in the heterozygous individual, resulting in a palomino horse.\n\n**Choice A** cannot be correct because if the gene exhibited complete dominance then horses with one or two copies of the cream allele would display the same phenotype (cremello and palomino would be indistinguishable). **Choice C** is incorrect because with codominance both alleles are fully expressed leading to a combination effect (some chestnut and some cream hair, for example) instead of an intermediate phenotype. **Choice D** is incorrect because this example in the question shows incomplete dominance and this is a well-understood inheritance pattern."},{"ref":"BI1248","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1248\n\nThe following graphs show the weights of piglets born from 27 litters in Utrecht, the Netherlands. Weights were compared based on birth order at various life stages.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI1248.png\" alt=\"BI1248\">\n[Source: Slegers, Y., Oolbekkink, Y., Roelofs, S., Van der Staay, F. J., & Nordquist, R. (2021). *Effects of Birth Order on Performance and Affective State of Pigs*. Frontiers in Animal Science, 2(July), 1-13. https://doi.org/10.3389/fanim.2021.669692. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\nWhich of the following is **NOT** true regarding the data?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ No pig weighed less than 80kg at the end of the study.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The median weight of last-born pigs was higher than first-born pigs at all life stages.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Outliers were recorded for each group of pigs in the study.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ At the time of weaning, last-born pigs had weights between 6 and 10kg.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAt the time of weaning, last-born pigs had weights of between 6 and 10kg in the 2nd and 3rd quartiles only. If you include all pigs, their weights fall between 4 and 12kg instead, making **Choice D** a false statement.\n\n**Choices A, B**, and **C** are all true based on the data and are therefore incorrect responses."},{"ref":"BI1401","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1401\n\nCorn snakes (*Pantherophis guttatus*) are popular pets. In this species, separate genes determine red and black colouration. A dihybrid cross is performed and the results are found in the table below. What can be concluded from this data?\n\n| Phenotype | Number of offspring |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| No pigment | 0 |\n| Red pigment only | 5 |\n| Black pigment only | 3 |\n| Red and black pigment  | 12 |\n|  | N = 20 |\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | The alleles for red and black pigment are dominant. |\n    | II. | One parent was black and one parent was red.|\n    | III. | The phenotypic ratios suggest that gene linkage is present. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe question tells us that different genes control black and red pigments, and the data indicates that an individual with both black and red colouration is much more common than those that are just red, just black, or lacking pigmentation altogether. This suggests that the alleles for black and red colouration are dominant, or co-dominant, so **Statement I** is true. Since a dihybrid cross was performed and the red colouration is carried on a separate gene to the black colouration, both parents must be red and black. **Statement II** is false.\n\n|  |  |\n| :-- | :-- |\n| R - allele for red colouration | r - allele for no colouration |\n| B - allele for black colouration | b - allele for no colouration |\n\nDihybrid cross (heterozygous parents): RrBb $\\times$ RrBb\n\n| | RB | Rb | rB | rb |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| **RB** | RRBB | RRBb | RrBB | RrBb |\n| **Rb** | RRBb | RRbb | RrBb | Rrbb |\n| **rB**| RrBB | RrBb | rrBB | rrBb |\n| **rb** | RrBb | Rrbb | rrBb |rrbb |\n\nPhenotypic outcomes:\\\n9 red and black pigment (RRBB, RrBb, RRBb, RrBB)\\\n3 red pigment only (RRbb, Rrbb)\\\n3 black pigment only (rrBB, rrBb)\\\n1 no pigment (rrbb)\n\nFor a non-linked dihybrid cross, the typical phenotypic ratios would be 9:3:3:1, however, the sample size given in this question is far too small to indicate whether or not gene linkage exists for these traits. **Statement III** would also be false. Therefore **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choices B, C**, and **D** are incorrect because they include one or more untrue statements."},{"ref":"BI1591","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1591\n\nIn chickens (*Gallus gallus domesticus*), the comb is a red crest at the top of the head. Comb type is primarily controlled by two genes, both exhibiting simple dominance. A flock of 144 chickens was produced from a single breeding pair heterozygous for both comb genes. A chi-square test was carried out to determine whether gene linkage between comb genes exists. The results are summarised in the table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/BI1591i.png\" alt=\"BI1591i\">\n\nWhat can be concluded based on the results of the chi-square test at a probability of 0.05?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Accept alternative hypothesis, gene linkage is significant.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Accept null hypothesis, gene linkage is significant.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Accept alternative hypothesis, gene linkage is not significant.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Accept null hypothesis, gene linkage is not significant.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because the chi-square value of 8.33 exceeds the critical value for a 0.05 significance level with three degrees of freedom (7.82). This indicates that there is a 95% chance that the difference between the observed and expected values is not due to chance, meaning we must accept the alternative hypothesis, which suggests that there is evidence of gene linkage.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because we would reject the null hypothesis, which is that the difference between the observed and expected values are simply due to chance. **Choice C** is incorrect because accepting the alternative hypothesis suggests that gene linkage is occurring. **Choice D** is incorrect because we would reject the null hypothesis based on the chi-square value obtained, suggesting significant gene linkage"},{"ref":"BI2345","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2345\n\nBox-and-whisker plots can be used to represent continuous data. The box-and-whisker plots below show data for heights of two groups of people.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/box%20and%20whisker%20plots%20heights%20BI2345.png\" alt=\"box and whisker plots heights BI2345RV\">\n\nWhich statement is correct for one of these box-and-whisker plots?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mean has the same value as the median and the lower quartile is 124 cm.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mean has a lower value than the median and the lower quartile is 128 cm.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mean has a higher value than the median and the upper quartile is 133 cm.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mean has a lower value than the median and the interquartile range is the larger of the two plots.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because ‘X’ represents the mean, while the line inside the box represents the median. In Plot I, the mean (X) is higher than the median (line inside the box). Additionally, the right side of the box corresponds to the upper quartile, which is 133 cm.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the mean and median are not equal in either Plot I or Plot II.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because Plot II has a mean which is lower than the median but the lower quartile is not 128 cm.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the mean has a lower value than the median in Plot II but the interquartile range is smaller than in Plot I."},{"ref":"BI2414","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2414\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nAn ordered tetrad is a group of four haploid fungal spores contained within an ascus (a sac). These spores are arranged in a straight line, reflecting the exact order in which they were produced during meiosis.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/EditA.png\" alt=\"EditA\">\n[Source *File: Peziza nivalis (Port-neuf, Québec) 4.jpg*. (2022, April 8). Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 03:33, January 15, 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=File: Peziza_nivalis_ (Port-neuf,_Qu%C3%A9bec)_4.jpg&oldid=647612529.]\n\nThe diagram below illustrates the process of crossing over and its effects on the arrangement of alleles within an ascus of a fungus. Scientists use biochemical markers of specific sequences or chromosomes to visualise the differences between spores.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Inheritance/images/EditB.png\" alt=\"EditB\">\n\n1. Explain how the arrangement of spores in the ascus without crossing over reflects the segregation of alleles during meiosis. [1 mark]\n\n2. Describe how the recombination event alters the spore arrangement by contrasting the diagrams. [1 mark]\n\n3. In a study of meiosis in a fungal species, the following percentages of tetrad types were observed: 65% no crossing over, and 35% crossing over. Predict how these percentages would change if the gene locus was closer to the centromere. [1 mark]\n\n4. Describe a method to determine the relative positions of three genes on a chromosome using this type of analysis and only testing one gene at a time. [2 marks]\n\n5. Describe the key events in meiosis I and meiosis II that lead to the spore arrangements shown in the diagram. [3 marks]\n\n6. Explain how a mutation affecting the recombination machinery in fungi might alter the ratio of the \"crossing over\" to \"no crossing over\" patterns in asci **AND** predict the consequences for genetic diversity. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous chromosomes separate during meiosis I into separate nuclei [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the alleles are distributed into separate cells according to the law of segregation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ each haploid spore receives only one allele from each homologous pair [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n According to the law of segregation, only **one of the two copies of an allele is passed onto each daughter nucleus in meiosis** ($\\checkmark$). Each spore has only one allele.\n\n2. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ crossing over in prophase I results in a new allele arrangement [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the diagram on the right shows a different order of spores, resulting from a recombination [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ recombination between homologous chromosomes occurred during meiosis I in the diagram on the right [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ no recombination on the left diagram so the order does not change [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ genes near the centromere are physically constrained, limiting crossing-over events near the centromere [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n In the diagram on the right, the spores show a **different allele order** due to **recombination during meiosis I** ($\\checkmark$). On the **left, no recombination occurred**, and the spores retained the **original parental allele order** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ genes closer to the centromere would have lower recombination frequencies [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the percentage of no crossing over would increase **OR** the percentage of crossing over would decrease [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n If the gene were closer to the centromere, the **recombination frequency** would **decrease**, and the percentage of **crossing over** would also **decrease** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cross strains that differ at only one locus of interest each time [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ compare the recombination frequencies for each gene by measuring the proportion of asci showing crossing over versus no crossing over [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ genes with higher crossover frequency are farther from the centromere [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ compare the percentages to determine the relative positions of the 3 genes to each other and the centromere [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ results could be misleading if one of the genes is on the other side of the centromere [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The relative positions of the genes on the chromosome could be determined by doing one ascus analysis at a time. By **measuring the proportion of asci showing crossing over for each gene** ($\\checkmark$), the frequencies of crossing over between each of the genes and the centromere can be determined, and then the **genes can be put in relative order based on those frequencies** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. any **three** of the following *(must include at least **one** statement for meiosis I and **one** for meiosis II)*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous chromosomes pair **OR** synapsis occurs in prophase I **OR** meiosis I [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous chromosomes cross over in prophase I **OR** meiosis I [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous chromosomes separate during anaphase I **OR** meiosis I [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at the end of meiosis I the alleles **OR** homologous chromosomes are in two different cells **OR** spores [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sister chromatids separate during anaphase II **OR** meiosis II [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at the end of meiosis II four haploid cells **OR** spores are produced [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the 4 haploid cells **OR** spores contain unique allele combinations [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n During **meiosis I, homologous pairs undergo crossing over** ($\\checkmark$) and are separated into different cells. During **meiosis II, sister chromatids separate** into **different cells** ($\\checkmark$), resulting in **four haploid cells** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ disrupt **OR** reduce frequency of crossing over in meiosis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ proportion of asci with crossing-over would decrease **OR** proportion of asci with no cross-over patterns would increase [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fewer new combinations of alleles would be produced **OR** genetic diversity would decrease [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ no recombination could occur, leading to no independent assortment of alleles [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ without recombination offspring predominantly inherit only complete **OR** unchanged chromosomes from each parent [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n If the recombination machinery in fungi were mutated, the **number** of asci with no **crossing-over would increase** ($\\checkmark$). This would **decrease genetic diversity** because there would be **fewer new combinations** of **alleles** ($\\checkmark$)."}]},{"name":"Mutations & Gene Editing","questions":[{"ref":"BI0079","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0079\n\nWhich of these does **not** cause mutations?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Nitrosamines in tobacco\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Errors by DNA polymerase\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Telophase\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Radioactive isotopes","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nTelophase is a phase of cell division. During telophase, the chromosomes that were separated during anaphase are enclosed in two new nuclear envelopes. Telophase is involved in the physical separation and distribution of chromosomes into daughter nuclei but does not directly interact with or alter the DNA sequence and so does not lead to mutations. This makes **Choice C** the correct answer.\nNitrosamines are chemicals found in tobacco smoke that are highly carcinogenic. They can cause mutations in the DNA of cells by altering the structure of DNA bases, leading to errors during DNA replication. Therefore, nitrosamines do cause mutations and are linked to the development of various types of cancer, especially lung cancer. **Choice A** is therefore not the correct answer to this question.\n\nDNA polymerases are enzymes responsible for copying the DNA molecule during cell division. If they make errors it can result in mutations if not corrected by the cell's repair mechanisms. **Choice B** is therefore not the correct answer to this question.\n\nRadioactive isotopes emit radiation as they decay, which can be in the form of alpha particles, beta particles, or gamma rays. This radiation can damage DNA, causing various types of mutations. **Choice D** is therefore not the correct answer to this question."},{"ref":"BI0080","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0080\n\nShown below is the single base substitution mutation that causes sickle cell anaemia in individuals who are homozygous for the allele. Which of the following is a result of this mutation?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Mutations%20%26%20Gene%20Editing/images/Image%2006-02-2023%20at%2012.31%20PM.jpeg\" alt=\"Image 06-02-2023 at 12.31 PM\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Translation of one incorrect amino acid that affects the function of haemoglobin\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Production of haemoglobin that cannot bind to oxygen\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Translation of one incorrect amino acid that does not affect the function of haemoglobin\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Translation of two incorrect amino acids","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe haemoglobin that is produced by the sickle cell allele causes cells to become sickle-shaped in homozygous individuals (**Choice A**). **Choice B** is incorrect because the haemoglobin that is produced can still bind to and transport oxygen. This single base substitution translates one incorrect amino acid that causes haemoglobin to polymerize in blood vessels with low oxygen concentrations (**Choice C**). This single base substitution causes the translation of one incorrect amino acid, not two. (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0083","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0083\n\nThe diagram shows normal red blood cells on the left, and sickle-shaped red blood cells, caused by abnormal haemoglobin, on the right.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Mutations%20%26%20Gene%20Editing/images/Picture%201.png\" alt=\"BI0083\">\n\nWhich of the following is the reason for the sickle-shaped red blood cells?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Physical cellular damage\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Being placed in a hypertonic solution\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ A base substitution mutation\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ An error in mitosis","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSickle-shaped red blood cells result from a mutation in the HBB gene, which encodes the beta-globin subunit of haemoglobin. This mutation is specifically a point mutation (base substitution), where adenine (A) is replaced with thymine (T) in the DNA sequence. This single change alters the codon, leading to the substitution of valine instead of glutamic acid in the haemoglobin protein. **Choice C** is the correct answer.\n\nWhile sickled red blood cells are fragile and prone to rupture, the cause of their abnormal shape is not physical damage. Instead, it originates from a genetic mutation affecting haemoglobin structure. **Choice A** is incorrect.\n\nIf red blood cells were placed in a hypertonic solution, they would shrink due to osmotic water loss, but they wouldn’t take on the characteristic sickle shape. **Choice B** is incorrect.\n\nMitosis errors can cause chromosomal mutations, leading to abnormal cell division, but they do not directly result in sickle cell formation. **Choice D** is incorrect.."},{"ref":"BI0084","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0084\n\nThis table is a short section of the output from a DNA sequence database comparing the cytochrome C gene sequence in two species. What does it show?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Mutations%20%26%20Gene%20Editing/images/Picture%201.png\" alt=\"Picture 1\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ These sequences are for two different genes\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ There are two differences in the base sequence between the two species\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ There are three differences in the base sequence between the two species\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ There are four differences in the base sequence between the two species","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because the base sequences differ at two positions:\n\n- Position 3: A in the top sequence vs T in the bottom\n- Position 6: T in the top sequence vs A in the bottom\n\nThis indicates two nucleotide differences between the two sequences.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the question specifies these are from the same region of the cytochrome C gene. These are homologous sequences; the high degree of similarity (8 out of 10 bases are identical) confirms they are variations of the same gene in different species, likely resulting from a relatively recent common ancestor.\n\n.**Choice C** is incorrect because the sequences differ at two positions, not three.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the sequences differ at two positions, not four."},{"ref":"BI0086","marks":"5","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0086\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nDifferent species of living organisms have different numbers of genes characteristic to them. The table shows the estimated number of genes for five species.\n\n| Species | Estimated number of genes |\n| :-- | :-- |\n| *Escherichia coli* (bacteria species) | 4,000 |\n| *Drosophila melanogaster* (fruit fly) | 13,000 |\n| *Canis lupis familiaris* (dog) | 20,000 |\n| *Homo sapiens* (humans) | 30,000 |\n| *Oryza sativa* (rice) | 40,000 |\n\n1. Using the information in the table deduce the estimated number of loci in a human genome. [1 mark]\n\n2. Explain how a mutation can affect a characteristic. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ Estimated number of loci in a human is 30,000 [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ :u[Sample answer]:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Each gene has a specific location (locus), therefore for an estimated 30,000 genes, the **estimated number of** **loci in a human is 30,000** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a mutation is a change in the base sequence of a gene [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the base sequence determines the sequence of amino acids in a protein [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if the base sequence changes, it might cause a change in the sequence of amino acids [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a change in the sequence of amino acids produces a protein that has different properties (shape and function)  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ proteins may not function correctly **OR** could cause harm **OR** some mutations cause cancer    [1 mark]\n\n    **Example (or any other valid example, one mark for cause and one mark for effect):**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the allele for sickle cell anaemia is caused by a single base substitution mutation from codon GAG to GTG    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ causes valine **OR** Val to be translated in place of glutamic acid **OR** Glu which causes haemoglobin to polymerize. [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    A **mutation is a change in the base sequence of a gene** ($\\checkmark$). The sequence of bases **determines the sequence of amino acids in the protein** ($\\checkmark$) that is produced by a gene. If the altered sequence of bases changes the sequence of amino acids, it will **produce a protein that has different properties, e.g.** changing **the shape and function of the protein** ($\\checkmark$). **Some mutations cause cancer** ($\\checkmark$) by affecting the rate of cell division. The sickle-cell allele is caused by **a single base substitution mutation** ($\\checkmark$). This single base substitution changes one amino acid in haemoglobin; valine **is translated in place of glutamic acid** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0239","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0239\n\nWhat three processes can increase variation in a population?\n\n| | 1 | 2 | 3 |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| Mutation | Mitosis | Meiosis |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Meiosis | Asexual reproduction | Sexual reproduction |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Sexual reproduction | Mutation | Meiosis |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Binary fission | Asexual reproduction | Mutation |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSexual reproduction results in variation as it is the fusion of genetically distinct gametes from two genetically different individuals. Mutation results in variation as it produces new alleles. Meiosis results in variation as it produces new combinations of alleles. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choices A**, **B** and **D** are incorrect as they all include either mitosis, asexual reproduction or binary fission, which all produce genetically identical cells."},{"ref":"BI0245","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0245\n\nIn species that do **not** carry out sexual reproduction, what is a possible cause of variation?\n\n- A.    $\\hspace{1em}$ Meiosis\n\n- B.    $\\hspace{1em}$ Mitosis\n- C.    $\\hspace{1em}$ Mutation\n- D.    $\\hspace{1em}$ Growth","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nMutation (**Choice C**)  is correct as random changes to the DNA sequence can result in variation. **Choice A** is incorrect as meiosis does not occur in organisms that do not carry out sexual reproduction. **Choice B** is incorrect as mitosis produces genetically identical cells. **Choice D** is incorrect as growth does not result in genetic variation."},{"ref":"BI0550","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0550\n\nA population of plants with no resistance to herbivores may develop into a population of plants that secrete a compound toxic to herbivores. How could this change occur?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The gene for the toxic compound was inherited from an ancestor of the plant population.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ A mutation during DNA replication, prior to meiosis, resulted in a DNA sequence that codes for the compound.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The enzyme needed to produce the compound was transferred by a plant from another population.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Individuals in the plant population mutated in response to the pressure from herbivores.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe appearance of new traits in a population can only occur if the genetic sequence for the trait is introduced to the population. Novel genetic sequences can result from several processes, one of which is through random mutations that occur during DNA replication. If the mutated DNA sequence codes for a functional protein product, it can produce a new trait in the offspring. If the trait is beneficial, the offspring will be more likely to survive and reproduce than other individuals in the population, and so the gene sequence for the trait will increase in frequency in the population.\n\nIn the case of the plant population, the ability to secrete a toxic compound could initially occur if a mutation during DNA replication resulted in a DNA sequence that codes for the compound (**Choice B**). Since this trait would likely be beneficial, as it protects against herbivores, it would likely increase in frequency over time until the entire population expresses the trait.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because if the gene had been inherited from an ancestor of the population, the population would have always had the ability to secrete the toxic compound, rather than having no resistance to herbivores.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because enzymes (proteins) cannot be transmitted between generations, so the receipt of an enzyme by one individual would have no effect on the ability of future generations to produce the toxic compound.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because mutations are random, not directional. An organism cannot mutate its DNA in response to an environmental pressure. However, random mutations do sometimes occur which result in a selective advantage when selection pressures change."},{"ref":"BI0881","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0881\n\nThe DNA sequence shown has been taken from exon 1 of DNA within the human insulin gene. Which sequence corresponds to a deletion mutation in the sequence?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Mutations%20%26%20Gene%20Editing/images/BI0881A.png\" alt=\"BI0881A\">\n[Source: Adapted from NCBI]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ TACAAGGACAAGCTGCAT\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ TACAAGAGACAAGCTGCA\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ TACAAGGAAAGCTGCATC\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ TACAAGGACAAGCGGCAT","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C**  is correct, as it shows a deletion mutation where the 9th nucleotide (C) from the original sequence has been deleted. This causes a frameshift, altering all downstream codons.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it is identical to the original sequence, so no mutation has occurred.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because it represents an insertion mutation where an additional adenine has been inserted at the 7th position, shifting the reading frame.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because it shows a base substitution where a G has replaced the T at the 14th position, altering one codon but not shifting the frame."},{"ref":"BI0882","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0882\n\nWhich of the following statement(s) are possible consequences to a polypeptide if a base substitution mutation occurs?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | A shorter polypeptide chain is translated |\n    | II. | An altered polypeptide chain is translated |\n    | III. | There is no change to the polypeptide sequence |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA base substitution is when one nucleotide randomly replaces another in a sequence causing a single nucleotide polymorphism. This can result in a shorter polypeptide being translated (**Statement I**) due to the base change causing a stop codon to appear earlier in the DNA/mRNA sequence. It can also cause an altered polypeptide chain to be translated (**Statement II**) if the base substitution causes a different amino acid to be translated. It can also have no effect on the polypeptide that is translated (**Statement III**), as the genetic code is degenerate, meaning many codons code for the same amino acid. **Choice D** is therefore correct.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **C** are incorrect, as they do not include all three statements."},{"ref":"BI0883","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0883\n\nWhat is a possible cause of a genetic mutation?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ An organism’s need to adapt to the environment\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Random errors during DNA replication\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Physical damage to body tissue\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Random errors during transcription","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B**  is correct, as mutations are random changes to the DNA sequence and many of them occur during DNA replication.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect as mutations are random and not controlled by organisms. **Choice C**  is incorrect as physical damage does not affect the genome. **Choice D**  is incorrect as errors during transcription will affect mRNA not DNA (the genome)."},{"ref":"BI0884","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0884\n\nWhich statement about mutations in germ and/or somatic cells is correct?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mutations in somatic cells can be inherited by offspring\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mutations in germ cells cannot increase the likelihood of developing cancer\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mutations in both somatic and germ cells can be inherited by offspring\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mutations in somatic cells can be acquired during a lifetime","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSomatic cells are all the cells in the body other than the gametes, which are germ cells.  Therefore mutations in somatic cells can be acquired during a lifetime during DNA replication or via exposure to mutagens. **Choice D**  is correct.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect because germ cells are involved in fertilisation not somatic cells, so mutations in somatic cells cannot be inherited by offspring. **Choice B**  is incorrect as mutations in genes such as oncogenes in germ cells can increase the likelihood of the offspring developing cancer. **Choice C**  is incorrect as mutations in somatic cells cannot be inherited by offspring."},{"ref":"BI1096","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1096\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Describe how a mutation may be a silent mutation. [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline five causes of gene mutations.    [5 marks]\n\n3. Compare and contrast somatic and germ cell mutations including their biological implications [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ silent mutation doesn't alter the amino acid sequence of the polypeptide chain encoded by the gene [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ brought about by a point mutation [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ occurs because of degenerate OR redundant genetic code  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ multiple codons can code for the same amino acid [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ change in nucleotide may not affect amino acid it codes for [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ protein's structure OR function may remain unchanged [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    A **silent mutation** is a type of point mutation that **doesn't alter** the **amino acid sequence** of the protein encoded by the gene ($\\checkmark$). This occurs because of the **degenerate genetic code** ($\\checkmark$), where **multiple codons** can code for the **same amino acid** ($\\checkmark$). Therefore, a **change** in the third position of a codon can result in a different **nucleotide without changing** the **amino acid** it codes for ($\\checkmark$). As a result, the **protein's structure** and function remain **unchanged** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ during DNA replication, mistakes can occur [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA polymerase may insert the wrong nucleotide, **OR** DNA polymerase may slip during replication, resulting in the addition or deletion of nucleotides [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ errors in the cellular machinery responsible for correcting DNA mismatches during replication can result in mutations [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ spontaneous loss of amino groups **OR** deamination from bases can lead to the conversion of one base to another [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reactive oxygen species generated during normal cellular processes can damage DNA bases [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chemical mutagens **OR** suitable example can directly alter DNA bases [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ radiation **OR** ionising radiation **OR** X-rays **OR** gamma rays **OR** ultraviolet **OR** UV radiation can damage DNA [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ biological agents **OR** some viruses can integrate their genetic material into the host DNA, potentially disrupting or altering host genes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ exposures to environmental toxins **OR** pollutants **OR** industrial chemicals **OR** carcinogens [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transposons **OR**  \"jumping genes,\" **OR** DNA sequences that can move around within the genome can disrupt genes  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mechanical **OR** physical damage to DNA **OR** breaks **OR** lesions caused by mechanical forces can lead to mutations if not repaired correctly [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ errors in the cellular machinery responsible for correcting DNA mismatches during replication can result in mutations [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Causes of mutations can be split into spontaneous and induced mutations. An example of a spontaneous mutation is an **error during DNA replication**, such as slipping of DNA polymerase leading to the **insertion or deletion** of nucleotides ($\\checkmark$). Then, there can be **errors** in the cellular machinery responsible for **correcting DNA mismatches** during replication, which can also result in mutations ($\\checkmark$).  **Deamination of bases** can occur, which leads to the change of one base into another ($\\checkmark$). On the other hand, induced mutations result from external factors, including exposure to **chemical mutagens** like ethidium bromide ($\\checkmark$) and **radiation** sources such as **ionising** or **UV** radiation ($\\checkmark$), which can damage DNA directly. Additionally, **biological agents** like certain viruses can integrate their genetic material into host DNA, potentially disrupting genes ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    **similarities** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** arise from changes in the DNA sequence **OR** involve substitutions **OR** insertions **OR** deletions **OR** rearrangements of nucleotides [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can lead to changes in the encoded proteins or RNA molecules [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can have biological effects [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can be influenced by environmental factors [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have DNA repair mechanisms that attempt to correct mutations [1 mark]\n\n    **differences** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ somatic cell mutation occurs in body cells **WHEREAS** germ cell mutation occurs in reproductive organs [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mutations in somatic cells are not passed on to offspring **OR** do not affect the genetic makeup of future generations **OR** mutations in somatic cells can lead to health issues **OR** diseases in the affected individual **WHEREAS** mutations in germ cells can be inherited by offspring **OR** can be passed onto next generation **OR** mutations in germ cells directly affect offspring **OR** next generation [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ somatic mutations are relatively common **OR** can occur throughout an individual's life **OR** readily exposed to mutagens **WHEREAS** germ cell mutations are relatively rare in comparison **OR** are isolated in the reproductive organs **OR** are not as exposed to the same mutagenic factors as somatic cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ somatic cells have more DNA repair mechanisms **OR** can correct more types of mutations **OR** trigger cell death to prevent the spread of mutated cells **WHEREAS** germ cells have fewer DNA repair mechanisms [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ somatic mutations primarily impact the health **OR** functioning of the individual in which they occur **OR** are not directly related to evolutionary changes **WHEREAS** germ cell mutations can contribute to genetic diversity within a population **OR** can drive evolutionary changes  [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Somatic** cell mutations can occur in most of the **cells of the body**, whereas **germ** cell mutation can only occur in **reproductive organs** ($\\checkmark$). **Both** somatic and germ cell mutations come about by **changes in the base sequences** of the DNA ($\\checkmark$), which can lead to **changes** in the **resulting proteins** ($\\checkmark$) and therefore can have **biological effects** in the organisms ($\\checkmark$). However, **mutations in somatic** cells are **not passed on to offspring**, they primarily **impact** the **health** of the **individual** in which they occur, whereas mutations in **germ** cells can be **inherited by offspring** ($\\checkmark$) and so can **drive evolutionary changes** ($\\checkmark$). Mutations in **somatic** cells are **more common** than those in germ cells, as they are more readily exposed to **environmental pollutions**, whereas **germ** cells are relatively **isolated** in the reproductive organs ($\\checkmark$); and when mutations do occur **somatic cells** have **more DNA repair mechanisms** than germ cells ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1146","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1146\n\nThe sequence of nucleotides in a section of DNA is as follows:\n\nATGGCTACCTGT\n\nWhich of the following mutations would represent a substitution?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ ATGGCTACTGT\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ ATGGCCTACCTGT\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ ATGGCACCTGT\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ ATGGCCACCTGT","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe sequence for **Choice D** contains the same number of bases as the original, but a cytosine has replaced thymine at position six, making this a substitution.\n\n**Choices A** and **C** cannot be correct since they have each lost one base, and so represent deletions. **Choice B** cannot be correct since it has gained one base, and so represents an insertion."},{"ref":"BI1214","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1214\n\nWhich statement does **not** explain why fruit flies (*Drosophila melanogaster*)  are used as model organisms for humans in gene knockout studies?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Mutations%20%26%20Gene%20Editing/images/BI1214.png\" alt=\"BI1214\">\n[Source: Adapted from University, O. S. (2010, March 1). *Drosophila melanogaster*. Flickr. Retrieved 04 September 2023 from https://www.flickr.com/photos/oregonstateuniversity/4399088160. Copyright by CC 2.0]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Fruit flies share a high number of homologous genes with humans\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Fruit flies have a short life cycle\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Fruit flies are easy and inexpensive to culture in research laboratories\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Fruit flies have a different respiratory and cardiovascular system to humans","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D**  is correct, as fruit flies do have a different respiratory and cardiovascular system to humans so it is difficult to use them as a model when studying those systems.\n\nHomologous genes are genes from the same genetic ancestor that have the same function in different organisms. Fruit flies and humans having a high number of shared homologous genes means that gene function can be studied in fruit flies as a model for human gene function; **Choice A**  is incorrect. Fruit flies having a short life cycle, means that the effect of gene knockouts can be studied relatively rapidly; **Choice B**  is incorrect. Fruit flies being easy and inexpensive to culture in research laboratories means that many can be bred and used in knockout studies; **Choice C**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1215","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1215\n\nWhich of the following does CRISPR-Cas9 have a **direct** effect on?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Genome\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Transcriptome\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Proteome\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Epigenome","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe genome is the complete set of genes present in an organism. **Choice A** is correct, as CRISPR-Cas9 can be used to directly edit genes by cutting out or replacing DNA sequences.\n\nThe transcriptome is the complete set of RNA molecules transcribed from the genome in a particular cell or tissue at a given moment. CRISPR-Cas9 does not directly target RNA so **Choice B** is incorrect. The proteome is all of the proteins expressed in an organism, CRISPR-Cas9 does not directly target proteins; **Choice C** is incorrect. The epigenome is all of the modifications on top of DNA that regulate the expression of genes, CRISPR-Cas9 does not directly target the epigenome; **Choice D**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1216","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1216\n\nWhich row correctly shows a potential advantage and disadvantage of the widespread use of CRISPR-Cas9 technology?\n\n| | Advantage | Disadvantage |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Stop the translation of defective proteins | Study the function of genes |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Quickly create animal models | Off-target mutations |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Loss of diversity | Unpredictable effects on future generations |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Can be used to target and destroy cancer cells | Treat genetic diseases |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCRISPR-Cas9 can be used to quickly knockout genes in animal embryos, which can be used to rapidly create animal models. However, using CRISPR-Cas9 technology can result in off-target mutations as molecular pathways are complex and knock-on effects cannot always be predicted, or Cas9 may act on the wrong target site leading to unintended consequences. **Choice B**  is correct.\n\nStudying the function of genes is an advantage of CRISPR-Cas9 technology, not a disadvantage; **Choice A**  is incorrect. Loss of diversity can occur if CRISPR-Cas9 is used for non-therapeutic reasons or aesthetic reasons, so the loss of diversity is a disadvantage of the technology; **Choice C** is incorrect. CRISPR-Cas9 can be used to treat genetic diseases by repairing mutations in cells or destroying faulty genes. This is an advantage of CRISPR-Cas9 technology so **Choice D**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1217","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1217\n\nWhich is a possible feature of a conserved sequence?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Does not have a similar sequence in multiple organisms\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ High mutation rate\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Any changes to the sequence are likely to have no effect on the organism\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Non-coding sequence that regulates the expression of other genes","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA conserved sequence is a DNA sequence that has remained relatively unchanged over long periods of evolution, often because the sequence has an important function for life. Conserved sequences can be coding or non-coding, and when they are non-coding often have a regulatory role in gene expression; **Choice D** is correct.\n\nConserved sequences have a similar sequence in multiple organisms; **Choice A** is incorrect. Conserved sequences have slower rates of mutation compared to the background mutation rate; **Choice B** is incorrect. Conserved sequences are maintained by natural selection, due to their functional role, therefore, any changes to their sequence are not conducive to life; **Choice C** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1218","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1218\n\nWhat feature do knockout organisms have in common?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ They are all mammals\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ They have one non-functional gene\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ They express the same genes as humans\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ They have the same phenotype as non-knockout organisms","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nGene knockout is a technique that produces a genetically engineered organism with one non-functional gene; **Choice B**  is correct.\n\nKnockout organisms are not all mammals; **Choice A** is incorrect. Not all knockout organisms express the same genes as humans; **Choice C** is incorrect. Knockout organisms sometimes have the same phenotype as non-knockout organisms, but not always; **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1239","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1239\n\nWhat is the purpose of gene knockout techniques?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Overexpress a gene to study its function\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Make a gene non-functional to study its function\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Lower the expression of a gene to study its function\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sequence genes to understand how they work","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct, as the purpose of gene knockout techniques is to make genes inoperative so that their function can be studied. **Choice A**  is incorrect, as gene knockout techniques do not increase the expression of their target genes. **Choice C** is incorrect as gene knockout techniques aim to completely stop the expression of a gene, not just lower it. **Choice D** is incorrect, as gene knockout techniques are not used to sequence genes."},{"ref":"BI1240","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1240\n\nWhat is the role of the Cas9 enzyme in the CRISPR-Cas9 gene editing technique?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Acts as an endonuclease that cuts target DNA at a specific location\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ To cut target mRNA at a specific location\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Acts as an RNA guide for the CRISPR enzyme\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Prevents the wrong section of DNA from being cut","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct as Cas9 is an enzyme (endonuclease) that precisely cuts the double stranded DNA at a specific location. **Choice B** is incorrect as Cas9 cuts DNA, not mRNA. **Choice C**  is incorrect, as Cas9 itself is an enzyme and not an RNA molecule. Cas9 acts in conjunction with a guide RNA molecule, which is used to direct Cas9 to the specific DNA sequence that needs to be edited. **Choice D** is incorrect as Cas9 does not prevent the wrong section of DNA from being cut, and is instead guided to the correct sequence by an RNA guide. The specificity of the editing process relies on the guide RNA directing the Cas9 to the correct DNA sequence."},{"ref":"BI1241","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1241\n\nWhich of the following are hypotheses for the presence of highly conserved sequences in genes?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | The sequences are important for the survival of the organisms |\n    | II. | The sequences have a lower rate of mutation |\n    | III. | The sequences only code for proteins |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nHighly conserved sequences are DNA sequences that have remained relatively unchanged far up the phylogenetic tree, indicating that they have been maintained by natural selection. **Choice B** is correct as these sequences are often important for an organism's survival (Statement I) and may be involved in processes such as cell stability, reproduction or metabolism. These sequences have been shown to have a lower rate of mutation (Statement II) as compared to other sequences, which allows them to remain relatively stable over time.\n\n**Choices C**  and **D**  are incorrect as highly conserved sequences can be both coding and non-coding (Statement III). **Choice A**  is incorrect as it only includes statement I."},{"ref":"BI1242","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1242\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nMyostatin is a protein with the function of limiting skeletal muscle growth in cattle, sheep, dogs, humans and mice. Research has been carried out to investigate what happens when the myostatin gene is removed, or knocked out, in mice. The photographs below show the results of the research. On the right is a mouse with the gene removed, known as a myostatin knockout mouse (Mstn:sup[−/−]). On the left is a mouse which still has the myostatin gene, known as a myostatin wild type (WT).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Mutations%20%26%20Gene%20Editing/images/BI1242.png\" alt=\"BI1242\">\n[Source: Adapted from Lee, S.-J. (2007, August 29). *File: Muscle Mouse 01.jpg* - Wikimedia Commons. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved 28 July 2023 https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Muscle_Mouse_01.jpg Copyright free]\n\n1. State the phenotypic difference between the wild type and myostatin knockout mouse. [1 mark]\n\n2. Suggest one advantage of using mice in this experiment instead of humans. [1 mark]\n\n3. State one purpose for making knockout mice.    [1 mark]\n\n4. Having seen the effect of knocking out the myostatin gene in mice, researchers hypothesised that knocking out this gene in humans could result in the restoration of muscle in humans with degenerative muscle diseases. Discuss to what extent you agree with this.        [4 marks]\n\n5. The Myostatin knockout mouse has been added to an open-access knockout library. Suggest an advantage of this.[1 mark]\n\n6.\n    1. Myostatin is regulated by a repressor protein. Predict, with a reason, what would happen to the expression of myostatin if the repressor protein was removed rather than the myostatin gene.        [1 mark]\n\n    2. Predict the phenotype of the mouse you referred to in part (i).     [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ knockout mouse has more muscle mass **OR** is more muscular than the wild type **OR** the wild type mouse has less muscle mass **OR** is less muscular than the knockout mouse        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The knockout mouse has **more muscle mass** ($\\checkmark$) than the wild type.\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ less ethical considerations  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ easier to control as kept in a lab     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ require less space         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ less resources required **OR** more economical    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ shorter life span **OR**  reproduce quickly so effects can be observed over several generations in a relatively short period         [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Using mice in experiments has significantly **less ethical considerations** than using human subjects ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ study the function of genes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ study biological pathways     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ study intolerances    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ study the mechanisms of certain drugs **OR** drug development        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ disease research    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ study of embryo development     [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Knockout mice are made to study **gene function** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **four** of the following: \\\n*Answer must include one point from agree and one point from disagree to gain full marks*\n\n    **Agree because…** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mice provide a good model for human disease    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myostatin has the same role in humans and mice    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ could lead to improved muscle function in individuals with degenerative muscle disease, or improved quality of life        [1 mark]\n\n    **Disagree because…** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myostatin may not work in the same way in humans as in mice, **OR** loss of expression of  myostatin may have unexpected off-target effects     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ do not know how many mice were used in the study as only two shown in the photograph **OR** need more results **OR** more mice need testing as only one knockout mouse shown [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myostatin may not be involved in the progression of degenerative muscle diseases **OR** myostatin knockout mice do not have degenerative muscle diseases    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the phenotype of knockout myostatin mice is not enough evidence to begin human trials    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gene editing techniques raise ethical dilemmas in humans    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ could lead to uncontrolled muscle growth which could have damaging effects **OR** could damage heart **OR** metabolism            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ effectiveness could vary between individuals        [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Knocking out myostatin in humans with degenerative muscle diseases would not necessarily cure degenerative muscle diseases as the loss of expression of myostatin may have **unexpected off-target effects** ($\\checkmark$) and myostatin may **not** be involved in the **progression of degenerative muscle diseases** ($\\checkmark$). Also, the phenotype of knockout  myostatin mice is **not enough evidence** to begin **human trials** ($\\checkmark$). However, myostatin plays the **same role in humans** and mice so it may help ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ other scientists can access the mice for further study **OR** to support each other's findings **OR** allows for collaboration        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enables experiments to be reproduced consistently        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    An advantage of adding the myostatin knockout mice to an open access library is that other scientists can access the **same mice for further study** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6.\n    1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myostatin would be overexpressed **OR** show higher levels of expression **BECAUSE** the repressor would normally downregulate its expression    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    If the repressor of myostatin expression was knocked down, then the myostatin gene would be **overexpressed** because the **normal role of the repressor is to downregulate** the expression of myostatin ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6.\n    2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ low skeletal muscle mass **OR** lower muscle mass than the wild type mouse    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The mouse would have a very **low skeletal muscle mass** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1243","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1243\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Suggest reasons why some DNA sequences are highly conserved.        [3 marks]\n\n2. Describe how CRISPR-Cas9 can be used to remove a gene from a section of DNA.        [5 marks]\n\n3. Discuss the economic, social and ethical issues surrounding the use of CRISPR-Cas9 technology to edit genes in humans.        [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they have slower rates of mutation than other sequences        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they regulate genes that are vital for life **OR** they are DNA sequences that control gene expression **OR** promoters OR enhancers **OR** they are binding sites **OR** transcription factor binding sites [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they code for proteins that are vital for life **OR** they code for proteins with crucial functions **OR** required for basic metabolism **OR** reproduction **OR** embryonic development  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they code for structural and functional RNA    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they provide structural stability      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ required for immune system recognition        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mitochondrial DNA **OR** encodes essential components for respiration in mitochondria     [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The hypotheses that account for highly conserved sequences in genes, are that they have **slower** rates of **mutation** than other sequences ($\\checkmark$), they **regulate genes** or code for transcription factors that are **vital for life** ($\\checkmark$), or they **code for genes** that are functionally **important** ($\\checkmark$), such as those involved in cell structure or metabolism.\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA guide **OR** gRNA sequence made that is complementary to a specific DNA sequence **OR** DNA strand with target gene is used as a template to make guide RNA **OR** gRNA **OR** guide RNA **OR** gRNA produced to identify gene of interest **OR** that matches gene of interest        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA guide **OR** gRNA and Cas9 enzyme bind together **OR** Cas9 enzyme is attached to guide RNA **OR** gRNA        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ guide RNA **OR** gRNA and Cas9 injected **OR** delivered into the cell        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA guide **OR** gRNA sequence binds to **OR** identifies target DNA sequence **OR** gene of interest to be cut    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ PAM sequence **OR** protospacer adjacent motif assists Cas9 to attach to DNA        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Cas9 cuts both strands of DNA **OR** cuts out gene of interest **OR** mutated DNA **OR** DNA sequence    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell’s own DNA repair machinery silences gene of interest **OR** targeted gene        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ removed **OR** mutated gene can be replaced by correct gene        [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    CRISPR-Cas9 can be used to remove genes from cells by researchers making a **RNA guide**  that is **complementary** to the specific target DNA sequence ($\\checkmark$). The **RNA guide binds** to the **Cas9 enzyme** ($\\checkmark$), as it will **direct** the enzyme to the correct DNA sequence to be cleaved. The  **RNA guide binds** to the **target DNA** sequence ($\\checkmark$) and **Cas9 cuts both strands** of the DNA ($\\checkmark$). Finally, the cell’s own **DNA repair machinery** will **silence the targeted gene** ($\\checkmark$) and repair the strand.\n\n3. any **eight** of the following (at least **one** from each area):\n\n    **Ethical** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ any changes to genes in embryos **OR** gametes will be inherited by offspring    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ has only been used in a few clinical trials    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ humans are ‘playing God’ [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ could lead to unexpected mutations **OR** off-target effects that radically change humans **OR** potential consequences may not be seen until later generations        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ could lead to ‘designer babies’ **OR** designing aesthetically pleasing humans    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ensuring informed consent from individuals or parents is challenging        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there must be international governance **OR** coordination to address ethical standards and safe usage    [1 mark]\n\n    **Economic** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cures for diseases may be too expensive for poorer countries **OR** may strain healthcare budgets        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ could save governments money in the long term as diseases could be eliminated        [1 mark]\n\n    **Social** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ has the potential to eliminate diseases from the human population        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can be used to target molecular cancer pathways which is less stressful to the body than current cancer treatments        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ could introduce genes that improve the human race e.g. genes that boost immunity [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is some public mistrust of genetically modified organisms        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ availability and affordability could be limited to certain socio-economic groups **OR** exacerbate existing social disparities        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ could create unrealistic expectations about genetic enhancement **OR** discrimination against people with genetic diseases        [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    CRISPR-Cas9 technology can be used to edit genes, however, there are many issues surrounding its use in humans. Some of the ethical issues include that any changes to genes in **embryos** will be **inherited by offspring** ($\\checkmark$) who have no say over these changes. As humans are genetically complex and CRISPR has only been used in a **few clinical trials** ($\\checkmark$),  there may be **off target effects** ($\\checkmark$) introduced to people which may pose a risk to the population. Economic issues include that the technology may be too **expensive for poorer countries** ($\\checkmark$) but could also **save** governments or charities a **large amount of money** in the long term ($\\checkmark$), as **diseases could be eliminated** from the population ($\\checkmark$). Socially, there is already some **public mistrust** of GMOs ($\\checkmark$) so people may either be reluctant to receive treatments or people with treatments may be treated differently. CRISPR could be used to introduce genes into the human population that **‘improve’ the human race** ($\\checkmark$) for example, genes that protect people from catching flu. A positive outcome is if it could be used to **target pathways involved in cancer progression** ($\\checkmark$), which may be a less stressful treatment to the body than current cancer treatments such as chemotherapy."},{"ref":"BI1343","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1343\n\nWhich type of mutation can result in a frameshift?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Deletion of a base from a non-coding sequence\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Substitution of one base for another in a coding sequence\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The wrong amino acid being incorporated into a growing polypeptide chain\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Insertion of four bases into a coding sequence","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA frameshift mutation occurs when nucleotides are inserted into or deleted from a coding sequence of DNA in a number that is not a multiple of three. Since the genetic code is read in triplets (codons), any insertion or deletion that is not a multiple of three causes a shift in the reading frame, changing the way the entire sequence is read. This can lead to the production of a completely different amino acid sequence, potentially resulting in a nonfunctional or malfunctioning protein. Inserting four bases into a coding sequence will, therefore, alter the sequence of amino acids. **Choice D**  is correct.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect, as the deletion is in the non-coding sequence, so it will not be transcribed or translated and cannot cause a frameshift mutation. **Choice B**  is incorrect, as a substitution of one base can cause a change in a single amino acid, but it will not cause the whole sequence to shift. **Choice C**  is incorrect, as a frameshift mutation occurs in the DNA sequence, not the polypeptide chain."},{"ref":"BI1344","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1344\n\nWhich statement explains why some mutations have a neutral effect on the proteome?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Only four bases are found in DNA\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The genetic code is degenerate\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mutations are random\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Some mutations are dominant, while others are recessive","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThere are 64 possible codons in the genetic code, but only 20 amino acids. This means that multiple codons can code for the same amino acid, so the code is degenerate. As a result, a mutation that changes one nucleotide in a codon may still code for the same amino acid. Such mutations do not alter the amino acid sequence of the protein. As a result, they have a neutral effect on the proteome. **Choice B**  is correct.\n\nAlthough only four nucleotide bases are found in DNA (**Choice A**) and mutations are random (**Choice C**), these are not reasons that some mutations are neutral, **Choice A**  and **C**  are incorrect. Mutations are not dominant or recessive, alleles are; **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1345","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1345\n\nWhich is **not** a mutagen?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ultraviolet light\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Carbon monoxide\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Viruses\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Chemicals found in cigarettes","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nMutagens are factors capable of causing a random change to DNA (a mutation). Carbon monoxide is a gas with a high affinity for haemoglobin, but it does not cause changes to DNA. **Choice B** is correct.\n\nUltraviolet light, viruses, and chemicals found in cigarettes can cause changes to DNA, so they are all mutagens. **Choices A, C** and **D** are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1346","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1346\n\nWhat type of mutation is being shown in the diagram?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Mutations%20%26%20Gene%20Editing/images/1346i.png\" alt=\"1346i\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Substitution\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Deletion\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Insertion\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Frame shift","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because the diagram shows thymine being replaced by guanine. This is a base substitution, which does not change the length of the gene.\n\nA deletion removes one or more nucleotides from a gene, and an insertion adds one or more nucleotides. Both change the number of bases in the gene, which the diagram doesn't show. **Choices B** and **C** are therefore incorrect.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because a frameshift happens when an insertion or deletion of a number of bases that isn't a multiple of three moves the reading frame, so every codon after that point changes. A substitution changes only one codon and leaves the reading frame unchanged."},{"ref":"BI1347","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1347\n\nWhich statement(s) explain why mutations are necessary for natural selection?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Mutations introduce variation into a population |\n    | II. | Mutations always introduce beneficial adaptations to a population |\n    | III. | Mutations are always heritable |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nMutations are random changes to the DNA base sequence that can result in variation within a population (**Statement I**). Mutations create new alleles (versions of a gene), some of which may be advantageous, while others may be neutral or harmful. If the new alleles lead to some individuals having a selective advantage, they have a better chance of survival. **Choice A**  is correct.\n\nMutations can have beneficial, neutral or harmful effects; **Statement II**  is incorrect. Mutations are only heritable if they are in germ cells (cells that develop into gametes); **Statement III**  is incorrect. **Choices B, C**  and **D**  are incorrect as they all include **Statements II** and **III**."},{"ref":"BI1348","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1348\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nHuntington’s disease is an inherited neurodegenerative disease. It is caused by a dominant allele resulting from multiple insertion mutations in the HTT gene on chromosome 4. The images below show a neuron and brain from an individual affected by Huntington’s disease compared to a healthy individual.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Mutations%20%26%20Gene%20Editing/images/BI1348.png\" alt=\"BI1348\">\n\n1. Distinguish between the phenotypes of the neuron and cross-section of the brain\n of the healthy individual and the individual with Huntington’s disease. [2 marks]\n\n2. Deduce the genotype of the healthy individual. [1 mark]\n\n3.\n 1. Explain how an insertion mutation can result in a frameshift. [2 marks]\n\n 2. The insertion mutation in Huntington’s disease results in the translation of a longer polypeptide chain. Suggest how this occurs. [1 mark]\n\n4. Deduce whether Huntington’s disease is caused by a germline or somatic cell mutation.[1 mark]\n\n5. Describe, using a Punnet square, how two biological parents with Huntington’s disease could have an unaffected child, and state the probability of this occurring. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following: \\\n*Answer must include **one** statement about the neuron and **one** about the brain to achieve full marks*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the neuron of the healthy individual has more dendrites **THAN** the neuron of the individual with Huntington’s disease **OR** the neuron of the individual with Huntington’s disease has fewer dendrites **THAN** the neuron of the healthy individual [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the gaps in the centre of the brain are smaller in the healthy individual **THAN** the in the centre of the brain with Huntington’s disease **OR** the ventricles of the individual with Huntington’s disease are larger **THAN** the healthy brain [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ there is less folding of the cerebral cortex in the brain affected with Huntington’s disease **COMPARED** to the brain of the healthy individual **OR** there is more folding of the cerebral cortex of the brain of the healthy individual **COMPARED** to the brain affected by Huntington’s disease [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ there is a gap between the hemispheres **OR** left and right side of the brain affected by Huntington’s disease WHEREAS there is no gap between the hemispheres **OR** left and right side of the brain of the healthy individual [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ loss of brain volume **OR** greater atrophy in brain affected by Huntingdon’s disease **THAN** the brain of the healthy individual [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ outermost layer of the brain is more jagged **OR** irregular **THAN** the brain of the healthy individual [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The neuron of the **healthy** individual has **more dendrites** than the neuron of the individual with Huntington’s disease ($\\checkmark$). The **brain** of the individual with **Huntington’s** disease has undergone a **greater** degree of **atrophy** than the healthy brain ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hh **OR** homozygous recessive [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Homozygous recessive** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ three bases **OR** codons code for an amino acid **OR** bases are read in multiples of three [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ therefore an insertion of bases that are not multiples of three **OR** in the middle of a codon, will shift the whole sequence along **OR** shift the normal reading frame [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ different groups of bases in codons **OR** different amino acids are translated **OR** stop codons are inserted in the wrong place [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n An insertion mutation can result in a frameshift, because **three bases** code for an **amino acid** ($\\checkmark$). Therefore, an insertion of bases that are **not multiples of three** will **shift** the whole sequence along ($\\checkmark$), resulting in **different amino acids** being inserted into the polypeptide chain ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ insertion mutation results in a frameshift which alters the sequence of the first STOP codon [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ a new STOP codon is formed further along the mRNA sequence [1 mark]\n\n **OR**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ multiple insertions **OR** expansion of repeat sequence in the coding region of a gene could result in the translation of a longer polypeptide [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The insertion mutation could result in a **frameshift** which **alters** the **sequence** of the first **STOP codon** ($\\checkmark$). A new STOP codon is formed **further along** the mRNA sequence ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. The following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ germline mutation because it is heritable **OR** can be passed onto offspring [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Huntington’s disease is caused by a **germline** mutation because it is a **heritable** disease ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. Any **three** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ alleles written correctly for both biological parents; Hh for both parents [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ correct genotypes for all four potential offspring in a punnett square; HH, Hh, Hh, hh [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hh labelled to indicate unaffected child **OR** unaffected child identified as homozygous recessive [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 25% **OR** ¼ chance of having an unaffected child [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Mutations%20%26%20Gene%20Editing/images/Huntingdons%20punnett%20square%20b.png\" alt=\"Huntingdons punnett square b\">\n\n **25% chance** of **homozygous recessive**, child who therefore **does not have Huntingdon’s disease** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1443","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1443\n\nWhich of the following can result in mutations in DNA?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Single base substitution\n- II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ Infection by a retrovirus\n- III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ Errors during DNA replication by DNA polymerase III\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA single base substitution is not a cause of mutations but a type of mutation itself. A single base substitution occurs when one nucleotide in the DNA sequence is replaced with another. This can be caused by errors in DNA replication (such as those made by DNA polymerases), chemical damage to DNA, or exposure to radiation.  **Statement I** is not true.\n\nRetroviruses, such as HIV, integrate their genetic material into the host cell's DNA. This integration process can disrupt the host genome, potentially inactivating genes or causing mutations. Additionally, the process of reverse transcription (converting the retrovirus's RNA into DNA for integration) is prone to errors, which can introduce mutations into the viral DNA that is then integrated into the host genome. **Statement II** is true.\n\nErrors made by DNA Polymerase III during DNA replication can cause mutations. One of the roles of DNA Polymerase III in prokaryotes is to proofread the newly formed strands and to correct any errors. However, if it incorporates a wrong nucleotide and the error escapes correction by its proofreading activity, this can lead to a mutation. **Statement III** is true.\n\n**Choice C** is therefore the correct answer to this question. **Choices A, B** and **D** are all incorrect as they include **Statement I**."},{"ref":"BI1533","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1533\n\nWhich statement(s) explain how CRISPR technology can potentially be used in cancer treatments?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1.6em}$ Can be used to remove tumour-suppressor genes\n\n- II. $\\hspace{1.3em}$ Can be used to target mutated genes that cause uncontrolled cell proliferation\n\n- III. $\\hspace{1em}$ Can be used to prevent the expression of oncogenes\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation:}}$\\\n\nCRISPR technology can be used to edit genes. It can be used to target and repress, or cut out mutated genes that cause uncontrolled cell proliferation resulting in tumour growth (**Statement II**). CRISPR can also be used to stop the expression of oncogenes (**Statement III**). Oncogenes are mutated genes that have the potential to cause cancer. By preventing their expression, the risk of developing cancer can be reduced; **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Statement I**  is incorrect as the role of tumour suppressor genes is to inhibit cell proliferation and the development of tumours. Therefore, by removing tumour suppressor genes, cancerous tumours are more likely to develop. **Choices A, C** and **D** are incorrect as they all include **Statement I**."},{"ref":"BI1534","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1534\n\n**Maximum mark: 1**\n\nWhich is **not** a risk of using CRISPR technology in humans?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ CRISPR-Cas9 may be recognised as foreign causing an adverse immune reaction\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The incorrect DNA sequence may be removed by the Cas-9 enzyme\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Whole cells may be removed from the body, resulting in tissue breakdown\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Off-target effects may affect other functional genes","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation:}}$\\\n\nCRISPR technology targets DNA sequences, not whole cells; **Choice C** is correct.\n\nCRISPR-Cas9 may be recognised as foreign causing an adverse immune reaction; this is a risk of using CRISPR technology in humans, and therefore **Choice A**  is not the correct answer to this question. If the incorrect DNA sequence is removed by the Cas-9 enzyme, this may cause unanticipated effects; this is a risk of using CRISPR technology in humans, and therefore **Choice B**  is not the correct answer to this question. Off-target effects may affect the expression of other essential, functioning genes; this is a risk of using CRISPR technology in humans, and therefore **Choice D**  is not the correct answer to this question."},{"ref":"BI1569","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1569\n\nWhich of the following statements regarding sickle cell anaemia is/are true?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. |  It is caused by a deletion mutation that removes glutamic acid from the amino acid sequence. |\n    | II. | It affects the structure of membrane proteins on the surface of the red blood cell. |\n    | III. | It causes haemoglobin to form tight bundles under low oxygen conditions.  |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because sickle cell disease is caused by a base substitution that changes glutamic acid to valine. This results in a change to the haemoglobin molecule that causes it to form tight chains or bundles under low oxygen conditions (**Statement III**), leading to the formation of sickle-shaped red blood cells.\n\n**Statement I** is false because sickle cell anaemia is caused by a base substitution, not a deletion. **Statement II** is false because the HbS allele, which causes sickle cell anaemia, does not affect membrane proteins. **Choices A, C**, and **D** are incorrect because they contain one or more false statements."},{"ref":"BI1606","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1606\n\nWhich of the following is why mutations are more likely to occur in regions with more CpG (cytosine next to guanine) sequences?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Both C and G pair with bases on the opposite strand with two hydrogen bonds, so the bonds can easily be broken.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ CpG regions are often acetylated, which is an easy epigenetic tag to remove.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The phosphate between C and G is very susceptible to environmental mutations.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ CpG regions form three hydrogen bonds between complementary bases, making the DNA more tightly bound and less accessible to proofreading.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nEach C–G base pair is held by three hydrogen bonds, compared with two in A–T pairs.\n\nRegions rich in C–G pairs are therefore more stable and tightly bound, making them less accessible to repair enzymes during proofreading. This can increase the chance that any replication errors remain uncorrecte\n\n; **Choice D**  is correct.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect as as both C and G pair with bases on the opposite strand with three hydrogen bonds, rather than two. This means they are more stable, not easier to break.\n\n**Choice B**  is incorrect because acetylation is an epigenetic modification of histone proteins, not of DNA bases. CpG regions are commonly methylated, not acetylated. Acetylation affects DNA packaging and transcription, not mutation rate, so this statement is unrelated to CpG mutation frequency. \n\n**Choice C**  is incorrect, as the sugar-phosphate bond linking nucleotides in DNA is chemically stable and not typically affected by environmental mutagens."},{"ref":"BI1607","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1607\n\nAt which stage of the cell cycle are mutations most likely to happen?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ S phase\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Anaphase I\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Prophase\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Metaphase II","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nMutations are more likely to occur during DNA replication in both somatic cells and gametes. However, only mutations in gametes can be inherited and potentially have consequences for future generations. DNA replication occurs during the S phase of interphase, making this the stage where mutations are most likely to occur; **Choice A**  is correct.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect as homologous chromosomes are pulled apart during anaphase I, but individual DNA bases are unaffected. Meiosis is also not usually considered part of the cell cycle as it is a one-way reduction division that produces gametes. **Choice C**  is incorrect as during prophase of mitosis, the chromosomes condense, but individual DNA bases are unaffected.  **Choice D**  is incorrect as, firstly, metaphase II is a stage of meiosis. During metaphase II, chromosomes align along the equator of the cell, but again, the individual DNA bases are unaffected."},{"ref":"BI2341","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2341\n\nThe diagram shows a section of one DNA strand before and after a mutation. Which term(s) describe this mutation?\n\n| $\\ce{Before mutation}:$ | $\\ce{GTC GAG TCT AGC GCT ATC GCT}$|\n| :-- | :-: |\n| After mutation : | GTC GAG TCT AAG CGC TAT CGC T |\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n | | |\n | :-- | :-- |\n | I. | Frameshift |\n | II. | Deletion |\n | III. | Insertion|\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n| $\\ce{Before mutation}:$ | $\\ce{GTC GAG TCT AGC GCT ATC GCT}$|\n| :-- | :-: |\n| After mutation : | GTC GAG TCT A**A**G CGC TAT CGC T |\n\n**Choice C** is correct because adding one base to this sequence is an insertion (**Statement III**). This insertion causes the genetic code to not be divisible by three, so the reading frame of the strand is shifted (**Statement I**).\n\nDeletion removes one or more bases from the DNA sequence; **Statement II** is false.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it is missing one true statement. **Choices B** and **D** are incorrect because **Statement II** is not true."},{"ref":"BI2442","marks":"4","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2442\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nThe graph below represents the allele frequency of different mutations in a population over time.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Mutations%20%26%20Gene%20Editing/images/allele%20frequ%20graph%20BI2442.png\" alt=\"allele frequ graph BI2442\">\n\n1. Deduce which of the alleles from the figure represents…\n\n 1. A dominant mutation that is beneficial and selectively favoured. [1 mark]\n\n 2. A recessive mutation that is detrimental and selected against. [1 mark]\n\n2. Explain why commercial genetic testing for humans can be both beneficial and potentially harmful. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1.\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ A [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The dominant allele with the beneficial mutation would be **A** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n 2.\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ C [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The recessive allele with the detrimental mutation would be **C** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **two** of the following *(must include **one** advantage and **one** disadvantage)*:\n\n *Advantages*\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can identify individuals who are genetically predisposed to certain health conditions **OR** diseases **OR** cancer **OR** heart disease **OR** genetic disorders **OR** cystic fibrosis **OR** Huntington's disease etc. **AND** early detection can lead to preventative measures (such as lifestyle changes, early monitoring, or targeted interventions) to reduce the risk **OR** delay the onset of disease [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can help doctors provide personalised medicine **OR** tailor medical treatments based on an individual's genetic makeup **WHICH** can improve the effectiveness of treatments **OR** reduce the likelihood of adverse reactions to medications [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can help individuals with family planning **BY** assessing the risk of passing on inherited conditions to their children [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can offer insights into ancestry **WHICH** helps individuals understand their genetic heritage **OR** the origins of specific genetic traits **OR** link individuals to distant populations or regions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can help individuals make informed decisions about their health and lifestyle **WHICH MEANS** people may take proactive steps to modify behaviours (such as increasing exercise, improving diet, or reducing risk factors) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can offer the opportunity to track the progression of certain genetic conditions over time **OR** provide insights into how genes are influencing an individual's health **WHICH** can be crucial for planning medical treatments or interventions [1 mark]\n\n *Disadvantages*\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can raise ethical questions regarding privacy **OR** the potential for genetic discrimination **OR** the psychological impact of knowing one's genetic risks [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ without expert interpretation, the results from genetic tests can sometimes lead to misunderstanding **OR** fear **OR** worry [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ possible false positives **OR** false negatives **OR** may give inaccurate results, leading to unnecessary fear **OR** anxiety, **OR** false reassurance [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ testing may reveal unknown information about family members' genetics **OR** undisclosed paternity **OR** unknown sibling, leading to unintended emotional **OR** familial consequences [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ may lead to unnecessary medical procedures **OR** treatments based on perceived risks rather than actual symptoms [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Commercial genetic testing can help couples with **family planning** to assess their risk of passing on **inherited conditions** to their children ($\\checkmark$), however knowing this could have a negative **psychological impact** on the couple, which could affect their mental health and relationship ($\\checkmark$)."}]},{"name":"Natural Selection","questions":[{"ref":"BI0231","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0231\n\nThe peppered moth (*Biston betularia*) is a classic example of natural selection. During the Industrial Revolution in Britain, soot from factories darkened tree bark and reduced the amount of pale lichen. Over time, the frequency of the melanic (dark) form increased relative to the light form.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Natural%20Selection/images/BI0231i.png\" alt=\"BI0231i\">\n\n[Source: Sale, B.(2010, July 8). File:(1931) Peppered Moth (*Biston betularia*) - f.carbonaria (4777708667).jpg. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved October 30, 2022, from https:// commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(1931)_Peppered_Moth_(Biston_betularia)_-_f.carbonaria_(4777708667).jpg. CC-BY-2.0.]\n\nWhich process best explains the increase in the frequency of the melanic form?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Increased mutation rate\n- II. $\\hspace{.7em}$    Natural selection\n- III. $\\hspace{.5em}$    Sexual selection\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$    I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    II only\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    I and II only\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$     I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is false because mutation only creates new alleles and does not make one form become common on its own; the rise in melanic moths happened because the melanic allele was favoured and selected for, not because mutations suddenly started happening more often.\n\n**Statement II** is true because natural selection increased the frequency of the melanic allele. In polluted environments, dark moths were better camouflaged against soot-darkened bark, so they were less likely to be eaten and more likely to survive and reproduce, passing the melanic allele to their offspring. Increased mutation rate is not responsible because mutation mainly creates new variation and does not direct which phenotype becomes common; the change in frequency occurred because selection favoured the dark form, and sexual selection is unrelated because the change was driven by predation, not mate choice.\n\n**Statement III** is false because sexual selection involves differences in mating success due to mate choice or competition, whereas industrial melanism was caused by selective predation (birds eating the more visible moths), not a preference for dark moths as mates.\n\n**Choice B** is therefore the correct answer to the question as it only includes the one true **Statament II**.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are all incorrect as they include one or more of the false **Statements I** and **III**."},{"ref":"BI0234","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0234\n\nWhich of the following rows correctly identifies a similarity and a difference between natural selection and selective breeding in the context of genetics and evolution?\n\n| | Similarity | Difference |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| Both processes rely on specific trait selection | Natural selection is driven by environmental pressures |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Both processes enhance genetic diversity | Selective breeding occurs primarily in wild populations |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Both processes rely on specific trait selection | Selective breeding increases genetic variation |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Both processes enhance genetic diversity | Natural selection results in rapid change in phenotypes |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because in both selective breeding and natural selection, specific traits are selected for by either the environmental pressures (natural selection) or humans (selective breeding). **Choice B** is incorrect because selective breeding typically reduces rather than increases genetic diversity, and it is controlled by man, so occurs primarily in domestic situations. **Choice C** is incorrect as although both do rely on specific trait selection, selective breeding reduces genetic variation.  **Choice D** is incorrect as selective breeding decreases genetic diversity, and the pace of natural selection varies depending on environmental factors."},{"ref":"BI0238","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0238\n\nWhich is **not** an example of natural selection?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Elephants and Hippopotami have similar body types.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    Peacocks with bright feathers are more likely to breed.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    Giraffes with longer necks are less likely to starve as they can reach leaves higher up in trees.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    Zebras that are born with white fur are more likely to be killed by predators.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nElephants and hippopotami having similar body types is not an example of adapting and surviving better in their environments. **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choices B** and **C** are all examples of organisms that survive better in their environments, they represent a selective advantage, as they have favourable adaptations. **Choice D** is an example of how mutation (albinism) can be selected against, through predation."},{"ref":"BI0241","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0241\n\nWhich statement(s) about natural selection is/are true?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Learned behaviour changes over time.\n- II. $\\hspace{.7em}$    Individuals that are better adapted are more likely to survive.\n- III. $\\hspace{.4em}$    It decreases the frequency of characteristics that make individuals better adapted.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$    I and II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    II only\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    II and III only\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is false because learned behaviours are acquired during an individual’s lifetime and are not encoded in the base sequence of genes, so they are not heritable and do not cause evolutionary change by natural selection.\n\n**Statement II** is true because individuals with traits that increase fitness are more likely to survive and reproduce (differences in adaptation, survival and reproduction are the basis of natural selection).\n\n**Statement III** is false because natural selection tends to increase, not decrease, the frequency of advantageous heritable characteristics (those linked to higher survival/reproductive success).\n\n**Choice B** is, therefore, the correct answer, as it provides only the one true **Statement II**\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are incorrect as they include one or more of the false **Statements I** and **Statement III**"},{"ref":"BI0242","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0242\n\nThe graph shows the number of female African elephants (*Loxodonta africana*) born without tusks in Gorongosa National Park. How does natural selection explain the trend shown in the graph?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Natural%20Selection/images/female%20elephants%20born%20tuskless%20graph.png\" alt=\"female elephants born tuskless graph\">\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Elephants broke their tusks while fighting, and their offspring were born without tusks.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Poachers hunted elephants with tusks, resulting in an increased survival rate for elephants without tusks.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Elephants born without tusks are better fighters and so survived better.\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mutations resulting in the absence of tusk formation began to occur more frequently.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because poaching removed tusked elephants from the breeding population, so females that were genetically tuskless had higher survival and reproductive success. Because tusklessness is heritable, the allele(s) for tusklessness were passed on more often, increasing the proportion born without tusks over generations, which is an example of natural selection in response to a selection pressure.\n\n **Choice A** is incorrect because broken tusks are an acquired change and are not inherited.\n\n **Choice C** is incorrect because the data are explained by poaching-driven selection rather than fighting ability.\n\n **Choice D** is incorrect because mutation is the source of variation, but the trend is best explained by selection changing frequencies, not a sudden rise in mutation rate."},{"ref":"BI0244","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0244\n\nBroad-leaved plants are often found near the ground in tropical rainforests. Which characteristic of the leaf is an example of a useful adaptation?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Natural%20Selection/images/BI0244.png\" alt=\"BI0244\">\n[Source: ysen. (2020, March 5). *Broad-leaved flat pea - free photo on Pixabay*. Pixabay. Retrieved May 26, 2022, from https://pixabay.com/photos/broad-leaved-green-leaf-4900105/.  Copyright free]\n\n- A.    $\\hspace{1em}$ Leaves are covered in a thin layer of wax, which allows for the absorption of more water.\n\n- B.    $\\hspace{1em}$ Leaves have a large surface area, allowing them to float on water.\n\n- C.    $\\hspace{1em}$ Leaves have curled edges to capture more rainwater.\n- D.    $\\hspace{1em}$ Leaves have a large surface area to absorb more sunlight.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because broad leaves provide a large surface area, increasing light capture in the shaded rainforest understorey.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the waxy cuticle mainly reduces water loss and protects the leaf; water uptake occurs primarily via roots, not leaf absorption.\n\n **Choice B** is incorrect because floating is irrelevant for ground plants in rainforests.\n\n  **Choice C** is incorrect because curled edges are not a typical adaptation for understorey plants and would not primarily increase light absorption."},{"ref":"BI0246","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0246\n\nAs pollution levels rise, the trees that the peppered moth (*Biston betularia*) lives on will become darker.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Natural%20Selection/images/BI0246.png\" alt=\"BI0246\">\n[Source: Phillips, A. (2010, June 21). *Peppered moth*. Flickr. Retrieved May 26, 2022, from https: //www .flickr.com/photos/wildhastings/4720082589. Copyright free]\n\nWhat will happen to the frequency of the melanic (darker) form in the population?\n\n- A.    $\\hspace{1em}$ Decreases\n\n- B.    $\\hspace{1em}$ Increases\n- C.    $\\hspace{1em}$ Remains constant\n- D.    $\\hspace{1em}$ Fluctuates randomly","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because darker bark creates a selection pressure where melanic moths are less visible to predators, so they survive and reproduce more. Because the trait is heritable, the melanic allele becomes more common, increasing the frequency of the melanic form over generations.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because when tree bark becomes darker, melanic moths are better camouflaged and are less likely to be eaten by birds, so their survival and reproduction increase rather than decrease.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because if survival differs between forms (melanic is better camouflaged on dark bark), then reproductive success differs too, so allele/phenotype frequencies change over time rather than staying constant.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because random fluctuation (genetic drift) is not the main expectation here because there is a directional selection pressure (predation based on visibility) that consistently favours the melanic form as pollution darkens trees."},{"ref":"BI0247","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0247\n\nWhich bird’s beak is best adapted for sucking nectar from flowers?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Natural%20Selection/images/BI0247.png\" alt=\"BI0247\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Shyamal, L. (2007, June). *birdbeaksa.svg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved May 26, 2022, from https ://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: BirdBeaksA.svg. Copyright free]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct, as the beak is curved and thin, adapted to suck nectar from a plant. **Choice A**  is incorrect as the beak is wide and the lower part protrudes; it is adapted for water surface skimming. **Choice B** is incorrect as the beak is thick and adapted to eating fruits and cracking the nuts. **Choice D** is incorrect, as its beak is too short and stunted and adapted to tear flesh."},{"ref":"BI0248","marks":"11","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0248\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nDarwin’s finches are a group of approximately 18 finches which share a common ancestor and are found on the Galapagos islands. Four of these finches are shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Natural%20Selection/images/BI0248.png\" alt=\"BI0248\">\n[Source: Adapted from Rex, K. (2019, February 21). File: Darwin's finches.png - Wikimedia Commons. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 30, 2022, from https ://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Darwin%27s_finches.png. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n1.\n    1. Explain the differences in beak shape and size shown in the four individuals A, B, C, D. [4 marks]\n\n    2. State two possible causes of genetic variation between finches found on the **same** island. [2 marks]\n\n    3. Discuss the hypothesis that the finches found on different islands are different species. [2 marks]\n\n2. Species tend to produce more offspring than their environment can support. Explain how this contributes to natural selection. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. Any **four** of the following:\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ beak variation is due to natural selection [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ adaptive radiation [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ finches with different beaks have different food sources **OR** occupy different niches    [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ some individuals, **OR** finches, in a population are born with a random genetic mutation that affects beak size **OR** shape [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ finches with beaks better adapted to getting the food source are more likely to survive    [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ surviving individuals reproduce **OR** pass the trait onto their offspring [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ repeating selection over many generations leads to changes in beak size **OR** shape on the individual islands [1 mark]\n\n        [4 marks]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n        Differences in the beak shapes developed through the process of **natural $\\hspace{1.5em}$ selection** ($\\checkmark$). Natural selection is selecting the best-fitted traits over generations in a species. Populations of finches on different islands have **different food sources** ($\\checkmark$). Some finches in a population are born with a **random genetic mutation** that affects their beak shape ($\\checkmark$); if the beak shape makes them better adapted to the food on the island, then the birds are **more likely to survive** ($\\checkmark$). The finches that survive breed and **pass the mutation onto their offspring** ($\\checkmark$); therefore, the more advantageous beak shape gets enriched in the population, out-competing other beak shapes.\n\n    2. Any **two**  of the following:\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ crossing over [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ mutation [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ random fertilisation [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ random orientation of chromosomes in meiosis [1 mark]\n\n        [2 marks]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n        Genetic variation could be caused by **mutations** ($\\checkmark$) or through **random fertilisation** ($\\checkmark$)in sexual reproduction.\n\n    3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ finches on the different islands look similar, so potentially could be the same species [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ to test the hypothesis, finches from different islands need to be bred together [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ members of the same species can breed to produce fertile offspring **OR** if finches cannot produce fertile offspring, then they are different species [1 mark]\n\n        [2 marks]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nFinches found on different islands are **similar in looks**  ($\\checkmark$) and behaviour, which could suggest that the hypothesis is correct. However, it can be tested to be sure. Organisms are considered the same species if they can interbreed and produce fertile offspring. To test this, we would have to **introduce males and females from different islands** and see if they **would breed** ($\\checkmark$) **and produce fertile** offspring ($\\checkmark$). So far, on the Galapagos islands, there are reported 13 different species of finches.\n\n2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ variation exists within a population [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ competition for resources due to more individuals than the environment can support    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ best adapted more likely to survive **OR** survival of the fittest [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ surviving organisms pass on characteristics to their offspring     [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    Species having more offspring than can be supported by the environment will lead to **competition for resources**, such as food or living spaces ($\\checkmark$). All offspring produced will have a **variation in traits** ($\\checkmark$)due to the mutations, sexual reproduction and meiosis. The individuals better adapted to the given environment will survive and pass on their **genes to the next generation** ($\\checkmark$). Natural selection refers to the mechanism of evolution in which each succeeding generation has better adaptation to the environment than the previous one due to the **“survival of the fittest”**."},{"ref":"BI0508","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0508\n\nIn a population of finches, the frequency of finches with large beaks has increased relative to the frequency of finches with small beaks. What could have been a cause for this?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ A decrease in the competition for food\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ A change in the availability of seeds\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Selection in favour of individuals with small beaks\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ An increase in the heritability of beak size","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIf the frequency of large beaks has increased relative to the frequency of small beaks, the population has likely undergone natural selection. In natural selection, a particular pressure in the environment promotes the survival and reproduction of organisms with a trait that makes them best able to contend with that environmental pressure. In the case of the finches, an increase in the frequency of large beaks could result from a change in the availability of seeds. For example, an increase in the availability of large seeds would select for finches that have the beaks that are best suited to cracking those seeds (i.e., large beaks). As large-beaked finches survived and reproduced at a greater rate than small-beaked finches, the frequency of finches with large beaks would increase.\n\nA decrease in the competition for food (**Choice A**) would reduce the selection pressure on beak size, and so would likely not result in a change in the relative frequency of large and small beaks. Selection in favour of individuals with small beaks (**Choice C**) would increase the frequency of finches with small beaks relative to the frequency of finches with large beaks. The heritability of beak size (**Choice D**) cannot change. The heritability of a particular trait is determined by the genes underlying the trait, and so is a fixed factor."},{"ref":"BI0705","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0705\n\nThe graph shows the relationship between the neck length of adult Masai giraffes (*G. tippelskirchi*) and their mean lifespan. What type of selection is shown by the graph?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Natural%20Selection/images/BI0705QB.png\" alt=\"BI0705QB\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Disruptive selection\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Directional selection\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Stabilising selection\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ No selection","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn directional selection, the population favours one extreme, as they are better adapted to the environment. Giraffes with longer necks have longer lifespans. **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect, as disruptive selection would result in the extremes being favoured, i.e. giraffes with shorter and longer necks would have longer lifespans. **Choice C** is incorrect as stabilising selection would result in the intermediate neck lengths having the longest lifespans. **Choice D** is incorrect as long-necked giraffes are being ‘selected’ for."},{"ref":"BI0707","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0707\n\nWhat is the correct definition of a gene pool?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ All the genes and alleles, in an interbreeding population.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ All the genes and proteins, in an interbreeding population.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ All the phenotypes and genotypes, in an interbreeding population.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ All the species present, in an interbreeding population.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choices B, C** and **D**  are incorrect, as the gene pool consists of all the genes and alleles in an interbreeding population."},{"ref":"BI0708","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0708\n\nIn the Indian ocean, sand coloured oysters blend into rocks in the sand bar and dark coloured oysters blend better into the deepest parts of the ocean. What type of selection is this an example of?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Directional selection\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Stabilising selection\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Disruptive selection\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Artificial selection","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIt is disruptive selection, as any intermediate varieties of oysters have been selected against. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as directional selection would mean that the population moves towards one extreme, i.e. either dark or light coloured oysters. **Choice B**  is incorrect as stabilising selection promotes intermediate varieties and gets rid of extreme varieties. **Choice D** is incorrect as artificial selection is controlled by humans."},{"ref":"BI0709","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0709\n\nWhich are examples of directional selection?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Human babies with an average birth weight are more likely to survive than those that are below or above average |\n    | II. | Black peppered moths are more likely to survive than lighter moths in industrial areas as they can camouflage better |\n    | III. | Larger salmons are fished, resulting in the better survival of smaller salmons |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nBoth **Statements II** and **III**, describe directional selection, when one extreme in a population is selected for. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Statement I** is incorrect as it describes stabilising selection, where the intermediate individuals within a population are selected for (**Choices A** and **D**). **Choice B**  is incorrect because it only describes **Statement II**."},{"ref":"BI0710","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0710\n\nWhich statement is true of punctuated equilibrium?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ It doesn’t account for intermediate evolutionary forms.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ It involves changes happening gradually over time.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ It is not supported by the fossil record.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ It has periods of rapid evolutionary changes.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe punctuated equilibrium is a theory that evolutionary development has long periods of stasis punctuated by short periods of rapid change (**Choice D**). **Choice A** is incorrect as punctuated equilibrium explains that long periods of stability are interrupted by periods of rapid evolution and so accounts for intermediate forms. **Choice B** is incorrect, as punctuated equilibrium causes evolution to happen rapidly. **Choice C** is incorrect, as punctuated equilibrium is supported by the fossil record."},{"ref":"BI0711","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0711\n\nWhich would **not** cause changes to the allele frequency within a population?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mutations\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Reproductive isolation\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Variable protein expression\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Natural selection","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choices A, B** and **D**  are all incorrect as they all cause changes to allele frequency."},{"ref":"BI0713","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0713\n\nEvolutionary fitness is a term used to describe adaptations.  Which of the following best describes the term evolutionary fitness?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ How effectively a phenotype attracts mates\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The probability that a phenotype will be found in the next generation\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ How well a phenotype is suited to an environment\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The underlying genotype is susceptible to mutations","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe evolutionary fitness of a phenotype is a measure for how many offspring will inherit the phenotype from their parents. (**Choice B**)\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because although the attractiveness of a phenotype improves the chances of mating, this by itself does not guarantee that offspring will inherit the trait.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because although the suitability of the phenotype to the environment plays an important role in the survival of the individual, this by itself does not guarantee that the offspring will inherit the trait.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because mutations in the genotype would change the phenotype and it would be a new trait. Fitness of a phenotype is the frequency of how much we see this trait in the offspring unchanged."},{"ref":"BI0716","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0716\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n1. Outline the possible causes of changes to a gene pool.         [3 marks]\n\n2. Suggest **one** possible cause of the gene pool getting smaller.  [1 mark]\n\n3. Explain how a small gene pool could have a detrimental effect on a population.  [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ a gene pool is all the genes **OR** alleles in an interbreeding population     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ changes in allele frequency      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ reproductive isolation **OR** temporal isolation **OR** behavioural isolation **OR** geographical isolation  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ natural selection **OR** evolution     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ accumulation of mutations      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ crossing over during meiosis     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ mixing with individuals from another population     [1 mark]\n[3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    A gene pool is all the **genes in an interbreeding population** ($\\checkmark$). Changes occur in a gene pool when the **allele frequency changes** ($\\checkmark$), and this can be caused by **reproductive isolation** ($\\checkmark$), **natural selection** ($\\checkmark$) or **crossing over during meiosis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ **MAY** be due to organisms with a specific allele which died before having offspring     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ **MAY** be due to inbreeding         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ **MIGHT** be due to a small population size         [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Inbreeding** ($\\checkmark$) can lead to the gene pool getting smaller as no new alleles are coming in, and many homozygous recessive genotypes can **reduce survival rate** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ small gene pool has a limited variety of traits in the population     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ reduces the ability of a species to respond to environmental changes **OR** reduces disease resistance [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ increased risk of extinction **OR** reduced population growth rate         [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    A small gene pool has a **limited variety of gene alleles** in the population ($\\checkmark$). This can **reduce the ability** of a species to **adapt to environmental changes** ($\\checkmark$) and so **increases** the **risk** of the species **becoming extinct** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1044","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1044\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nSickle cell anaemia is a trait that is inherited in a codominant manner. The graph shows the proportions of people who are homozygous affected (Hb$^S$Hb$^S$), homozygous unaffected (Hb$^A$Hb$^A$) and heterozygous (Hb$^A$Hb$^S$) for the trait over 50 years in Nigeria, West Africa. Malaria, which is a parasitic disease that infects red blood cells, is prevalent in Nigeria.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Natural%20Selection/images/BI1044.png\" alt=\"BI1044\">\n\n1. Using the graph, explain whether the sickle cell trait is likely to be in Hardy Weinberg equilibrium. [1 mark]\n\n2.\n 1. Describe the trends observed in the graph. [3 marks]\n\n 2. Suggest a reason for the trend in heterozygotes found in this malaria infected region of Africa. [1 mark]\n\n3. The frequency of the recessive allele in the population was found to be 0.62, calculate the frequency of the dominant allele in the population. [1 mark]\n\n4.\n 1. Assuming that this population is in Hardy Weinberg equilibrium, predict what would happen to the frequency of recessive alleles in the population over 50 more years. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Outline a reason for your answer. [1 mark]\n\n5. Explain why, under ‘real world’ conditions, it is difficult for organisms to be in Hardy Weinberg equilibrium. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ unlikely **BECAUSE** the frequency of allele pairs is not constant indicating that evolution might be happening [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ unlikely **BECAUSE** the error bars do not overlap for each allele pair, indicating that there is a significant difference within allele pair frequencies, indicating that evolution might be happening [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n It is **unlikely** that the sickle cell trait is in Hardy Weinberg equilibrium because the **frequency of allele pairs** is **not constant** over the 50 years, indicating that **evolution** might be happening ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ percentage of people who are heterozygous for the sickle cell trait **OR** Hb$^A$Hb$^S$ increases overall between 1950 and 2000 [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ percentage of people who are heterozygous for the sickle cell trait **OR** Hb$^A$Hb$^S$ remains higher than people that are homozygous **OR** Hb$^S$Hb$^S$ **OR** Hb$^A$Hb$^A$ [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ from 1985 percentage of people who are Hb$^S$Hb$^S$ and percentage of people who are Hb$^A$Hb$^A$ **OR** only homozygotes is similar **OR** is approximately 21-24% [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ between 1950 and 1985, the percentage of people who are Hb$^S$Hb$^S$ decreases **AND** then increases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ between 1950 and 1985, the percentage of people who are Hb$^A$Hb$^A$ increases **AND** then decreases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the percentage of the population that are homozygous **OR** Hb$^S$Hb$^S$ **OR** Hb$^A$Hb$^A$ is lower in 2000, than in 1950 [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ between 1975 and 1985, there is a decrease in the percentage of the population who are heterozygous **OR** Hb$^A$Hb$^S$ **OR** the percentage of the population who are heterozygous **OR** Hb$^A$Hb$^S$ dips in 1965 and 1985 [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The graph shows that over the course of 50 years, the percentage of people who are **heterozygous** for the sickle cell trait **increases** ($\\checkmark$) and **remains higher than** the percentage of people who are **homozygous** (Hb$^S$Hb$^S$ or Hb$^A$Hb$^A$)($\\checkmark$). It also shows that **from 1985**, the percentage of people who are **Hb$^S$Hb$^S$** and the percentage of people who are **Hb$^A$Hb$^A$** is **similar** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ people who are heterozygous **OR** carriers of the sickle cell trait have increased resistance to malaria as they have a mix of normal and sickle shaped red blood cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ stabilising selection is acting on the population as being heterozygous confers resistance to malaria with a lower risk of anaemia than people affected by sickle cell anaemia [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The percentage of heterozygotes in the population remains relatively high, as **carriers** of the sickle cell trait have **increased resistance to malaria** as they have a **mix of normal and sickle cell** shaped **red blood cells** ($\\checkmark$), but also have a lower risk of anaemia compared to people who are homozygous for the sickle cell trait.\n\n3. of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 0.38 [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Frequency of the dominant allele (p) + frequency of recessive allele (q) = 1 \\\n p + 0.62 = 1 \\\n p = 1 - 0.62 \\\n p = **0.38** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4.\n 1. of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ would remain constant **OR** at 0.38 [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The frequency of recessive alleles would remain at **0.38** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.\n 2. of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic equilibrium is maintained **OR** allele frequency does not change [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The frequency of recessive alleles would remain constant as when a population meets the conditions for Hardy Weinberg, the **allele frequency does not change** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mutations randomly occur in nature [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms often choose their mates based on them having beneficial traits [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ genes can flow in and out of most populations **OR** organisms can migrate in and out of populations [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ populations of organisms are finite [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms are under the influence of natural selection **OR** most organisms are constantly evolving [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms must be diploid [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms must reproduce sexually [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the gene studied cannot be sex-linked [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Under ‘real world’ **conditions**, it is difficult for organisms to be in Hardy Weinberg equilibrium, as different conditions need to be met that cannot easily be controlled in nature, for example **mutations randomly occur** in nature ($\\checkmark$) and cannot be controlled and **mating isn’t always random**, as organisms often choose their mates based on them having **beneficial traits** ($\\checkmark$), such as good immunity."},{"ref":"BI1125","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1125\n\nIn a population of beetles that meets the conditions for Hardy Weinberg equilibrium, brown shells are recessive to black shells. If the frequency of homozygous brown beetles is 0.16, what is the frequency of dominant alleles in the population?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 0.4\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 0.84\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 0.6\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 0.16","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe Hardy-Weinberg equation is p:sup[2] + 2pq + q:sup[2] = 1, where q2 equals the frequency of homozygous recessive individuals in the population. Therefore, q:sup[2] = 0.16, the square root of  q:sup[2]  is 0.4. The general formula to represent the frequency of dominant and recessive alleles in a population is p + q = 1, where p represents the frequency of dominant alleles, and q represents the frequency of recessive alleles.  Therefore, p + 0.4 = 1, and p = 1 - 0.4 = 0.6. **Choice C**  is correct.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **D**  are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1126","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1126\n\nWhich statement is correct if a population of Scottish wildcats (*Felis silvestris silvestris*) is **not** in Hardy Weinberg equilibrium (HW)?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | There are a few hundred wildcats in Scotland  |\n    | II. | Scottish wildcats with darker fur are more likely to survive as they can camouflage themselves and hide from humans  |\n    | III. | Scottish wildcats are only found in Scotland, and they only breed with each other |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA major assumption that has to be met for a population to be in Hardy Weinberg equilibrium is an infinite or very large population (**Statement I**), however, there are only a few hundred wildcats in Scotland. Another assumption that has to be met is that no natural selection is occurring, but wildcats with darker fur have a better chance of survival (**Statement II**), therefore, they are under selection pressures. **Choice C**  is correct.\n\nFor the Scottish wildcats to be in Hardy Weinberg equilibrium, there must be no gene flow, which means that there is no movement of Scottish wildcats out of the population (**Statement III**). **Choice D**  is incorrect. **Choices A** and **B** are incorrect as they only include one of the true statements, not both."},{"ref":"BI1127","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1127\n\nPrader-Willi syndrome is a rare autosomal dominant disease which causes excessive, uncontrolled eating in adulthood. Assuming that the Prader-Willi trait is in Hardy Weinberg equilibrium, if the frequency of the dominant allele in the general population is 1 in 10000, what is the frequency of the recessive allele?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 0.9999\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 0.0009\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 0.99999999\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 0.9","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe formula to represent the frequency of dominant and recessive alleles in the population is p + q =1, where p represents the frequency of dominant alleles, and q represents the frequency of recessive alleles. Therefore, p = 1 in 10000, which is equivalent to 0.0001, so 0.0001 + q = 1, q = 1 - 0.0001, q = 0.9999, **Choice A**  is correct.\n\n**Choices B, C** and **D** are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1128","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1128\n\nIn a population of 100 daisies, where white petals (W) are dominant to purple petals (w), 75 daisies are white, and 25 daisies are purple. What is the frequency of recessive alleles in the population?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 0.75\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 0.25\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 0.50\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 0.06","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDaisies with white petals have the genotype of either WW or Ww, and have a frequency of 75 out of 100, which is the equivalent of 0.75. Daisies with purple petals have the genotype of ww, and have a frequency of 25 out of 100, which is the equivalent of 0.25. Using the Hardy-Weinberg formula, the frequency of homozygous recessive flowers is represented by q:sup[2]. q:sup[2] = 0.25, therefore q = 0.5 (the square root of 0.25). **Choice C**  is correct.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **D**  are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1129","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1129\n\nIn the Hardy Weinberg formula, p:sup[2] + 2pq + q:sup[2] =1 , what do p:sup[2], 2pq and q:sup[2] represent?\n\n| | p:sup[2] | 2pq |  q:sup[2] |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Frequency of dominant alleles | Frequency of heterozygous individuals | Frequency of recessive alleles |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Frequency of homozygous dominant individuals | Frequency of heterozygous individuals | Frequency of homozygous recessive individuals |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Frequency of recessive alleles | Frequency of homozygous dominant individuals | Frequency of heterozygous individuals |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Frequency of homozygous recessive individuals | Frequency of heterozygous individuals | Frequency of homozygous dominant individuals |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn the Hardy-Weinberg equation, p:sup[2] represents the frequency of homozygous dominant individuals, 2pq represents the frequency of heterozygous individuals, and q:sup[2] represents the frequency of homozygous recessive individuals. **Choice B**  is correct.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D**  are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1181","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1181\n\nThe graph shows the relationship between the mean number of Forrest mice (*Leggadina forresti*) in the Tasmanian desert and fur colour. What type of natural selection is demonstrated on this graph?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Natural%20Selection/images/BI1181.png\" alt=\"BI1181\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sexual selection\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Stabilising selection\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Directional selection\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Disruptive selection","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn stabilising selection, selection pressures act to remove extreme varieties. There are a larger number of mice with intermediate colours of fur, compared to the extremes. **Choice B** is correct.\n\nIn sexual selection, selection pressures act to make some mates more appealing to other members of the same species e.g. male mice with the darkest fur attract more mates; **Choice A** is incorrect. Directional selection describes natural selection where one extreme trait is favoured over others e.g.there are more darker mice in the population than any other fur colour; **Choice C** is incorrect. Disruptive selection describes when selection pressures act to remove intermediate varieties e.g. there would be a higher number of light and dark furred mice compared to the intermediate mice; **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1206","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1206\n\nWhich factor is **not** a density-independent factor that affects the survival of organisms within a population?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Temperature\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Presence of predators\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Extreme weather conditions\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Rainfall levels","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDensity-independent factors can affect population growth but are not influenced by the population size. The presence of predators is a density-dependent factor as its impact on a population’s survival and growth becomes more significant as the population’s density increases. **Choice B**  is correct.\n\nTemperature (**Choice A**), extreme weather conditions (**Choice C**) and rainfall levels (**Choice D**) are all density independent factors, as they affect the survival of organisms within a population regardless of the population’s size or density. **Choices A, C** and **D** are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1207","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1207\n\nThe graph shows the changes in the population of pale (primarily white) and melanistic (mostly black) peppered moths (*Biston betularia*) before and after the industrial revolution. What selection pressure was the likely cause of the changes seen in the graph below?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Natural%20Selection/images/BI1207QB.png\" alt=\"BI1207QB\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increased atmospheric carbon dioxide concentration\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increased rates of pollution\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increased number of predators of black peppered moths\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Differences in diet of the peppered moths","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIncreased rates of pollution (particularly soot) during the Industrial Revolution caused white lichen and mosses on trees to turn black. This meant that light-coloured peppered moths were unable to camouflage and were at a higher risk of predation. This allowed black peppered moths to become the dominant species as they were better camouflaged; **Choice B** is correct.\n\nIncreased carbon dioxide concentration is not a likely cause for the increase in the number of black peppered moths. Increased carbon dioxide concentration primarily affects global climate and is not directly linked to the colouration of the moths; **Choice A** is incorrect. The increase in predators would not explain the rise in the black peppered moths. The rise in number of black peppered moths was due to the increased success of predators catching the lighter-coloured moths against the darkened background; **Choice C** is incorrect. While a change in diet may influence various aspects of the organism’s biology, it is not the reason for the increase in the numbers of black peppered moth after the Industrial Revolution; **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1208","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1208\n\nWhich statement(s) indicate how genetic variation contributes to natural selection?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Offspring with unfavourable characteristics are more likely to survive  |\n    | II. | Offspring that look different from others in their species are more likely to survive and breed |\n    | III. | Offspring with favourable alleles are able to survive and reproduce |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nGenetic variation (differences within species caused by differences in gene expression or mutations) can influence natural selection, as offspring with favourable alleles or characteristics for survival, such as increased speed, camouflage or disease resistance, are more likely to survive, reproduce and pass on their alleles to the next generation (**Statement III**), **Choice C**  is correct.\n\n**Statement I**  is incorrect, as offspring with unfavourable characteristics are less likely to survive. **Statement II**  is incorrect, as offspring that look different from other species are only more likely to survive if those differences are favourable for survival, such as better camouflage from predators. **Choice A** is incorrect, as it includes **Statement I**. **Choices B** and **D**  are incorrect, as they include **Statements I** and **II**."},{"ref":"BI1210","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1210\n\nWhich term related to natural selection is matched correctly to its definition?\n\n| | Term | Definition |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Fitness | An organism's ability to pass its genetic material to its offspring |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Niche | The interactions between different species in an area |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Species | A group of organisms in an area that can interbreed |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Differential mortality | Differences in lifespan due to differences in gene expression |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAn organism well adapted for survival in its environment is said to be fit for its environment. If an organism has high fitness, it is more likely to survive, reproduce and pass on its genes to its offspring, passing on the beneficial characteristics; **Choice A**  is correct.\n\nA niche is an area where individuals of the same species live; **Choice B** is incorrect. A species is a group of organisms that can interbreed to produce fertile offspring. **Choice C**  is incorrect as it is missing the last part of the definition.  Differential mortality refers to organisms that are less well adapted to their environment not surviving, whereas organisms that are better adapted are more likely to survive, breed and pass on their alleles to the next generation; **Choice D**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1280","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1280\n\nThe photograph shows one species of bird of paradise, a family of birds renowned for their bright colours.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Natural%20Selection/images/bird%20of%20paradise.png\" alt=\"bird of paradise\">\n[Source: Ltshears. *Bird of Paradise 001.jpg*. Wikimedia. Retrieved, September 28th 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Bird_Of_Paradise_001.jpg. Copyright under Public Domain]\n\nWhat type of selection pressure likely acts on the colours of the feathers of a bird of paradise?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Abiotic factor\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Overproduction of offspring\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sexual selection\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Interspecific competition","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because the feather colour of birds of paradise is a trait that is sexually selected because it affects mating success. **Choice A** is incorrect because abiotic factors are unlikely to affect feather colour. **Choice B** is incorrect because overproduction of offspring does not affect the feathers of birds of paradise. **Choice D** is incorrect because the feathers of birds of paradise have a role in mating rituals and will not be affected by interspecific competition, which is competition between different species."},{"ref":"BI1281","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1281\n\nWhich row is correct for inherited and acquired traits?\n\n| | Inherited traits | Acquired traits |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Not encoded in genes | Encoded in genes |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Encoded in genes | Encoded in genes |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Encoded in genes | Not encoded in genes |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Not encoded in genes | Not encoded in genes |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because inherited characteristics are encoded in genes, and acquired traits are not encoded in genes; they are affected by environmental factors. **Choice A** and **Choice D** are incorrect because inherited traits are encoded in genes. **Choice B** is incorrect because acquired traits are not encoded in genes."},{"ref":"BI1282","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1282\n\nWhich of these are factors affecting evolution by natural selection?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Competition |\n    | II. | Mutation |\n    | III. | Overproduction of offspring |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true as individuals within a population often compete with each other for resources, and those with advantageous traits are more likely to survive and reproduce. **Statement II** is true, as mutations that provide a selective advantage to a particular environment can become more common in future generations through natural selection.\n\n**Statement III** is true as it leads to competition among offspring for limited resources, and the ones with traits that make them better suited to the environment are more likely to survive and pass on their genes to the next generation **Choice D** is therefore correct.\n\n**Choice A, Choice B** and **Choice C** are incorrect because they do not include all three statements."},{"ref":"BI1283","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1283\n\nWhich of these is not an abiotic selection pressure?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Competing for mates\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ High temperatures\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Oxygen availability\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Pollution","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAbiotic factors are non-living factor, and competing for mates (**Choice A**) is a living, or biotic factor. **Choice B, choice C** and **choice D** are incorrect because they are all non-living (abiotic) factors."},{"ref":"BI1284","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1284\n\nWhich of these is not a consequence of natural selection?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Evolution\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Changes in biodiversity\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Changes in allele frequencies\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Environmental change","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because environmental change contributes to evolution by natural selection, it is not a consequence.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because natural selection leads to evolution. **Choice B** is incorrect because natural selection can increase biodiversity by the evolution of new species (e.g. adaptive radiation) or decrease biodiversity by the reduction of species (e.g. extinction). **Choice C** is incorrect because natural selection favours reproduction of individuals with suitable adaptations, which leads to changes in allele frequencies in populations."},{"ref":"BI1312","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1312\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nEvolution by natural selection was first proposed by Charles Darwin based on his observations of traits in species.\n\n1. Outline the requirements for evolution by natural selection.    [4 marks]\n\n2. Distinguish between inherited and acquired characteristics.    [3 marks]\n\n3. Describe the selection pressures acting on guppies using John Endler’s experiments as an example.            [9 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ overproduction of offspring        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ variation in populations        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ variations must be inheritable **OR** genetic for natural selection    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is a struggle for survival **OR** competition for resources    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ individuals that are best adapted to survive and reproduce **OR** fittest for the environment survive and reproduce     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ over long periods of time **OR** many generations, natural selection leads to genetic change in population    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ natural selection over long periods of time leads to evolution    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Darwin observed **variations** ($\\checkmark$) within populations and that there is an **overproduction of offspring** ($\\checkmark$). Darwin also observed that population sizes tended to remain constant over time, which led him to conclude that there is a **struggle for survival** ($\\checkmark$). Individuals **best adapted to the environment survive, reproduce** ($\\checkmark$), and pass on their genes. This **leads to evolution over long periods of time** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ inherited characteristics are controlled by genes **OR** encoded in DNA, **BUT** acquired characteristics are not controlled by genes **OR** encoded in DNA    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ inherited characteristics are not affected by the environment, **BUT** acquired characteristics are affected by the environment    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ inherited characteristics cannot be learned, **BUT** acquired characteristics can be learned                        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ inherited characteristics can be passed onto the next generation **OR** offspring, **BUT** acquired characteristics cannot                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ an example of an inherited characteristic is blood group **OR** haemoglobin **OR** any other relevant example **OR** an example of an acquired characteristic is language **OR** dyed hair colour **OR** any other relevant example            [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Inherited characteristics are controlled by genes**, but acquired characteristics are not controlled by genes  ($\\checkmark$). **Inherited** characteristics are **not affected** by the **environment**, but **acquired** characteristics **are affected by** the **environment** ($\\checkmark$). **Inherited** characteristics are **not learned**, but **acquired** characteristics **can be learned**  ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **nine** of the following:\\\n    Statements for **both** sexual selection **and** natural selection are required.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sexual selection affects mating and reproduction success, often at the cost of survival        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ natural selection is the process where individuals that are best adapted to their environment are most likely to survive and reproduce                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ male guppies express genes for bright colours                 [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Endler hypothesised that guppies in habitats protected from predators would show brighter colours **OR** brighter spots than those in habitats where they are exposed to predators  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Endler observed guppies in wild **AND** in man-made **OR** artificial ponds     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ independent variable was the presence of predators **OR** cichlids        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ guppies from the wild with varying colours **OR** spots were introduced to tanks OR guppies introduced to tanks and allowed to breed            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ one set of guppies was kept in ponds with predators **OR** cichlids present, **AND** one set with non-predatory **OR** weak-predatory fish present **OR** no predators        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ponds had different sized gravel, **OR** some ponds had fine gravel, **AND** some had course gravel        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in ponds with predators **OR** cichlids, guppies had duller colours **OR** duller spots        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in ponds with coarser gravel, guppies had larger spots **OR** in ponds with finer gravel, guppies had smaller spots     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ more brightly coloured males **OR** those with brighter spots **OR** the more conspicuous males **OR** those that have spots that do not match the gravel size tend to be eaten, so less likely to mate and pass on their alleles to the next generation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ this is predatory selection pressure and leads to a decrease in frequency of alleles for bright colours **OR** spots in the population over time     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in ponds with no predators **OR** non-predatory fish, guppies had brighter colours **OR** brighter spots                     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ brighter coloured guppies **OR** those with brighter spots were more likely to mate and pass on their alleles to the next generation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ this is sexual selection and leads to an increase in frequency of alleles for bright colours **OR** spots in the population over time    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ these experiments show the evolution of guppy colouration involves a dynamic interplay between sexual and natural selection     [1 mark]\n    [9 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Endler hypothesised that **male guppies** would have **brighter colours** in areas where they were **protected from predators** compared to where predators were present ($\\checkmark$). He observed guppies in **natural** and **artificial ponds** ($\\checkmark$). **One set** of male guppies was kept in ponds with **predators**, and **one set** was kept in ponds **without predators** ($\\checkmark$). In the ponds where **predators** were **present**, the **male guppies** were **duller** in colour ($\\checkmark$), which means the males were more **camouflaged** and less likely to be eaten, and so **more likely to survive** to reproductive age and pass on their **alleles** to the **next generation** ($\\checkmark$).  In ponds where there were **no**, or few, **predators**, male guppies were more **brightly coloured** ($\\checkmark$), suggesting **female guppies** were **more attracted** to brightly coloured males, so they were **more likely** to **mate** and **pass on** their **alleles** to the next generation ($\\checkmark$).  Over time, this increases the **frequency** of **alleles** for **bright colours** in these populations ($\\checkmark$). These experiments provide **evidence** that the **evolution** of male guppie colouring is influenced by **natural** and **sexual selection** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1402","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1402\n\nMale peregrine falcons (*Falco peregrinus*) try to interest females with aerial manoeuvres and dives. If females prefer males that are faster and more athletic, what is this an example of?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Artificial selection\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Directional selection\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Stabilising selection\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Disruptive selection","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAlthough this question definitely describes sexual selection, it is also directional selection because only faster males are chosen. This would likely lead to an increase in the average speed of falcons over time as offspring inherit this trait. **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because artificial selection involves humans selecting desired traits when breeding organisms. **Choice C** is incorrect because stabilising selection tends to favour individuals with average characteristics rather than those at the extremes (in this case, speed and athletic ability). Disruptive selection involves selection pressures that favour both extremes. Since only faster birds are favoured, **Choice D** cannot be correct."},{"ref":"BI1485","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1485\n\nThe image shows the Galapagos marine iguana (*Amblyrhynchus cristatus*) and the Galapagos land iguana (*Conolophus subcristatus*). Marine iguanas have flatter tails and are thought to have diverged from land iguanas 8$-$10 million years ago. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Natural%20Selection/images/BI1485%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1485 fig1\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Matzinger, S. (2014, February 19). File: galapagos iguana.jpg - wikimedia commons. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved February 12 , 2024, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Galapagos_iguana.jpg. Copyright free. Adapted from YYC, R. (2009, June 18). File: Amblyrhynchus Cristatus (3838137696).JPG - wikimedia commons. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved February 12 , 2024, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amblyrhynchus_cristatus_(3838137696).jpg. Copyright free]\n\nWhat could explain the difference in tail morphology?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Random mutations in the marine iguana’s DNA\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Differences in diet\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ The need for the marine iguana to swim for food\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The marine iguana smashing its tail on rocks while swimming","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nEvolution by natural selection acts on variations that arise due to random mutations in DNA. If organisms develop mutations that are beneficial to their survival, then they are more likely to survive and breed; **Choice A**  is correct.\n\nDiet may affect health, but it does not change inherited traits like the tail structure of marine iguanas; **Choice B**  is incorrect. \n\nThe need for the marine iguana to swim for food describes the selective pressure. However, \"need\" does not cause genetic change. Traits do not arise because they are needed - they arise through mutation and are selected if advantageous; **Choice C**  is incorrect. \n\nFlattening a tail by smashing it on rocks is an acquired characteristic, which is not inherited; **Choice D**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1592","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1592\n\nHLA (human leukocyte antigen) genes help the immune system differentiate between pathogens and the body’s own cells. The HLA A*02:01 allele has been recorded in over 300 populations and may be associated with an improved ability to clear the body of hepatitis C infections. The map shows the incidence of this allele across South and Central America.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Natural%20Selection/images/BI1592i.png\" alt=\"BI1592i\">\n\nWhich of the following statements are supported by the data?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n | | |\n | :-- | :-- |\n | I. | This allele may have originated in Peru. |\n | II. | A population from South America may have settled in Mexico. |\n | III. | Some Argentinians may be more resistant than Brazilians to hepatitis C infection. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because Peru has the largest frequency (0.5) for this allele, suggesting that it may have originated in this population (**Statement I**), although it doesn’t exclude the possibility that it may have originated elsewhere. Mexico has a higher allele frequency than nearby countries to the south. This suggests that a population from further south may have migrated to and settled in Mexico at some point (**Statement II**), although other explanations are possible. Lastly, Brazil has a lower allele frequency than Argentina, which suggests that they may be more susceptible to hepatitis C infection (**Statement III**).\n\n**Choices A, B,** and **C** are incorrect as they are missing one or more statements."},{"ref":"BI2322","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2322\n\nJohn Endler studied sexual selection and natural selection pressures using guppies (*Poecilia reticulata*). Which row contains his observations for colourful males?\n\n| | Mating success | Survival in shallow water |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Low | Low |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Low | High |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | High | High |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | High | Low |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because colourful male guppies have a reproductive advantage due to sexual selection. Females preferentially select colourful males for reproduction, giving them high mating success. However, in shallow water, colourful males are more visible to predators, resulting in low survival due to increased predation.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because colourful males have high, not low, mating success.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because colourful males have low survival in shallow water, not high.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because colourful males do not have high survival in shallow water."},{"ref":"BI2395","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2395\n\nWhich of the following characteristics would not be subject to selective breeding by farmers?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Size of wheat kernels\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Muscle bulk in beef cattle\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Longevity of sheep\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Wool yield from alpacas","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because longevity, whilst a desirable characteristic (because a longer-living animal will, in theory, produce more wool in its lifetime) cannot be selected for. Selective breeding involves a farmer deciding which animals can be allowed to mate and which cannot, thereby concentrating the gene pool in favour of desirable characteristics. The only way a farmer will know of the longevity of an animal is to let it live its full life, at which point it will likely be too old to reproduce.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because a large wheat kernel means more endosperm, which is the ‘cash crop’ as it contains the starch-containing flour that will be milled from the crop.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because muscle mass is important in beef herds; more muscle means more saleable meat per head of cattle. For this reason, a farmer may choose only the largest and most muscle-bound bulls and cows to reproduce.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because alpaca wool is a valuable crop, and the animals that produce the greatest yield (mass of saleable wool per animal) will be selected for by a farmer."},{"ref":"BI2595","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2595\n\nSickle cell anaemia is a recessively inherited disease. People who are affected by sickle cell anaemia (ss) have sickle-shaped red blood cells that can occlude blood vessels. The table shows the frequency of the sickle cell anaemia genotype in a local population in Nigeria. What is the frequency of homozygous dominant individuals?\n\n| Genotypes $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{SS and Ss}$ $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ss}$ $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n| :-- | :-: | :-: |\n| **Frequency** $\\hspace{1em}$ | 0.75 | 0.25 |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 0.5\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 0.75\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 0.25\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 0.05","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe Hardy-Weinberg formula is used to calculate genotype frequencies in a population and is summarised by: $\\ce{p^2 + 2pq + q^2 = 1}$. Where $\\ce{p^2}$ equals the frequency of dominant homozygous individuals, 2pq equals the frequency of heterozygous individuals, and $\\ce{q^2}$ equals the frequency of homozygous recessive individuals. The general formula to represent the frequency of dominant and recessive alleles is p + q = 1, where p equals the frequency of dominant alleles and q equals the frequency of recessive alleles.\n\nTherefore $\\ce{q^2 = 0.25, so q = \\sqrt{0.25} = 0.5}$\\\np = 1 - 0.5 = 0.5\\\nThe frequency of dominant homozygous individuals = $\\ce{p^2 = 0.5^2 = 0.25}$\n\n**Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **D** are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI2597","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2597\n\nThe frequency of a recessive phenotype in a population of dung beetles (*Onthophagus australis*) is higher than predicted by the Hardy-Weinberg equation. Which is the most likely reason?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Natural selection favours individuals with the recessive phenotype\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The frequency of dominant alleles is decreasing due to selection against heterozygotes\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Dung beetles with a dominant phenotype have a higher chance of survival\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Heterozygotes have a higher reproductive success rate than homozygotes","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIf natural selection favours individuals with the recessive phenotype, then these individuals are more likely to survive and reproduce. This will result in a higher-than-predicted frequency of the recessive phenotype; **Choice A**  is correct.\n\nHeterozygotes will express both the dominant and recessive allele; therefore, if heterozygotes are being selected against, then the frequency of both dominant and recessive alleles will be affected. However, this alone does not explain why the frequency of recessive phenotypes is affected; **Choice B** is incorrect. If dung beetles with a dominant phenotype have a higher chance of survival, then the frequency of the dominant phenotype would be higher than expected rather than the recessive phenotype; **Choice C**  is incorrect. If heterozygotes have a higher reproductive success rate than homozygotes, then this would maintain the frequency of both alleles in the population, preventing either allele from having a higher-than-expected frequency; **Choice D**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI2599","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2599\n\nWhich of the statements explains why artificially selected characteristics can sometimes cause problems for an organism?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Artificial selection increases mutation rates, resulting in a higher number of genetic disorders.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Desirable traits selected by humans do not always improve the survival of organisms in a natural environment.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Artificial selection makes organisms more resistant to environmental changes.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Artificial selection results in higher genetic diversity so populations are more vulnerable to disease.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nArtificial selection involves humans selectively breeding animal or plant organisms over many generations so that their offspring have desired traits.  However, desirable traits selected by humans do not always improve the survival of organisms in a natural environment. For example, the selective breeding of dogs for aesthetic reasons, such as short snouts, may have the unintended consequences of dogs having breathing difficulties; **Choice B**  is correct.\n\nMutations occur randomly in DNA, they are not due to artificial selection. **Choice A**  is incorrect. \\\nArtificial selection results in certain alleles becoming more prominent in populations, which reduces genetic diversity. A reduction in genetic diversity makes organisms less able to adapt to environmental changes; **Choice C**  is incorrect. \\\nArtificial selection results in lower genetic diversity; **Choice D**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI2746","marks":"11","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2746\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nGreen skin colour in *Rana temporaria* (European common frog) is controlled by a dominant allele **G**, while brown skin is caused by a recessive allele **g**. Scientists set up an investigation in one population of *Rana temporaria* to see how allele and genotype frequencies in the gene pool changed over time in response to environmental factors. \\\nThe Hardy-Weinberg equation (shown below) was used to calculate the expected allele and genotype frequencies.\n\n$\\ce{p^2 + 2pq + q^2}$ = 1\n\n1. Define the term gene pool.        [1 mark]\n\n2. The scientists observed that 10% of the frogs had brown skin.\n\n    1. Assuming the population is in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium, calculate the frequency of the recessive allele                 [1 mark]\n\n    2. Use the equation provided to calculate the expected frequency of heterozygotes.                [2 marks]\n\n    The actual observed frequency of heterozygous frogs in the population was 0.38.\n\n    3. By comparing the observed and expected heterozygote frequencies, deduce, with a reason, whether this population is in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium.    [1 mark]\n\n3. Identify **one** condition required for a population to remain in Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium. [1 mark]\n\nIn a different region, the scientists observed that brown frogs have a higher survival rate than green frogs.\n\n4. Explain how this would lead to evolutionary change in the gene pool of that population.               [3 marks]\n\n5. Distinguish between artificial selection and natural selection.       [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ complete set of genes and their different alleles in a population         [1 mark]\n\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The gene pool is the **total set of genes** and their different **alleles** in a **population** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. 1.\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 0.316 **OR** 0.32            [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{q = \\sqrt{(gg)} = \\sqrt{(0.10)} =}$ **0.316**  ($\\checkmark$)\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ The frequency of the recessive allele is **0.32** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n    2. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ p = 0.684 **OR** 2pq = 2 $\\times$ 0.684 $\\times$ 0.316                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 0.432 **OR** 0.43        [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ p = 1 – q\\\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ = 1 – 0.316 \\\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ = **0.684** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ 2pq = 2 $\\times$ 0.684 $\\times$ 0.316 = **0.432** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n    3.\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ no **BECAUSE** observed heterozygous frequency is lower than expected         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ yes **BECAUSE** the difference between observed frequency and expected is very small    [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ Since the **observed frequency** is **lower than expected**, this suggests the population is **not in** Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium ($\\checkmark$)\n\n3. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ no mutation         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ no migration **OR** gene flow        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ no natural selection         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ random mating                 [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large population size         [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    There must be **random mating** in the population ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ brown frogs have the recessive genotype (gg)        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ brown frogs survive and reproduce more than green frogs        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ g **OR** recessive allele becomes more frequent in the gene pool over generations     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ natural selection acts on heritable variation in the population     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ population undergoes directional selection favouring recessive **OR** g allele         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ shift in allele frequencies in gene pool over time         [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Brown frogs have the **recessive genotype** (gg) ($\\checkmark$). They **survive** and **reproduce** more, so the **g allele** becomes **more common** in the population ($\\checkmark$). Over generations, **natural selection increases** the **frequency** of the **recessive allele**, leading to a **shift** in **allele frequencies** favouring brown frogs ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. any **two** of the following *(must include **one** statement for artificial selection and **one** statement for natural selection to gain full marks)*\n\n    ***Artificial selection***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ driven by human choice of which individuals reproduce        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ aims to increase traits desirable to humans                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ usually results in reduced genetic variation            [1 mark]\n\n    ***Natural selection***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ driven by environmental pressures affecting survival and reproduction            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increases the frequency of traits that improve survival or reproductive success in the wild                                 [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ maintains **OR** shifts genetic variation based on fitness             [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Artificial selection is when **humans choose** individuals with a **desired trait** to **breed** ($\\checkmark$). **Natural selection** happens when the **environmental pressures favour** traits that increase **survival** or **reproduction** ($\\checkmark$)."}]},{"name":"Protein Synthesis","questions":[{"ref":"BI0046","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0046\n\nWhich nucleic acid carries genetic material coding for proteins?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ribosomal RNA\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ mRNA\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ ATP\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ tRNA","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nmRNA, also known as messenger RNA, carries the genetic information needed for protein synthesis (**Choice B**).\n\nRibosomal RNA is found in ribosomes. This RNA contributes to the structure of the ribosome and does not code for proteins (**Choice A**).\n\nATP (**Choice C**) is a molecule made out of a nucleotide (adenosine tri-phosphate), while nucleic acid is made out of many nucleotides.\n\ntRNA (**Choice D**), also known as transfer RNA, brings amino acids to the ribosomes and does not code for proteins."},{"ref":"BI0327","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0327\n\nRibosomes have three binding sites. Which statements are correct for the functions of these sites?\n\n| | A site | P site | E site |\n\n| :- | :- | :- | :- |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ A. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Amino acid carried by the tRNA is bound to the growing polypeptide chain | Empty tRNA is released | tRNA carrying an amino acid binds to the corresponding codon |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ B. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Empty tRNA is released  | Amino acid carried by the tRNA is bound to the growing polypeptide chain | tRNA carrying an amino acid binds to the corresponding codon |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ C. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | tRNA carrying an amino acid binds to the corresponding codon | Amino acid carried by the tRNA is bound to the growing polypeptide chain | Empty tRNA is released  |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ D. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Amino acid carried by the tRNA is bound to the growing polypeptide chain | tRNA carrying an amino acid binds to the corresponding codon | Empty tRNA is released  |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nThe A site (aminoacyl or acceptor site) is where the incoming tRNA, carrying an amino acid, binds to the codon on the mRNA through complementary base pairing. The P site (peptidyl site) holds the tRNA with the growing polypeptide chain and is where the new amino acid is added through the formation of a peptide bond. The E site (exit site) is where the uncharged (\"empty\") tRNA is released from the ribosome after its amino acid has been transferred. **Choice C** is therefore the correct answer.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect because peptide bond formation and chain elongation occur at the P site, not the A site.\n\n**Choice B**  is incorrect because the E site, not the A site, is responsible for releasing the empty tRNA.\n\n**Choice D**  is incorrect for the same reason as A — peptide bond formation does not occur at the A site."},{"ref":"BI0332","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0332\n\nWhich statements about translation are **not** true?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n\n  | | |\n\n  | :-- | :-- |\n\n  | I. | Mature mRNA binds to the large subunit of the ribosome before the small subunit. |\n\n  | II. | The A site is the first site that the initiation tRNA binds to. |\n\n  | III. | The polypeptide chain is released when release factors bind to the E site. |\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\n**Choices A, B** and **C**  are incorrect, as all of the statements about translation are false. **Statement I** is incorrect as mature mRNA binds to the small subunit first, specifically at the mRNA's ribosome-binding site. Then, the large subunit joins, forming the complete ribosome-mRNA complex. **Statement II** is incorrect as the initiator tRNA binds to the P site first. **Statement III** is incorrect as the polypeptide chain is released when the release factors bind to the A site. These release factors promote the hydrolysis of the bond between the polypeptide chain and the tRNA at the P site, resulting in the release of the completed polypeptide chain from the ribosome. The E site (exit site) is where the deacylated tRNA exits the ribosome after releasing its amino acid."},{"ref":"BI0333","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0333\n\nWhat is the correct function of the tRNA-activating enzymes?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Matches mRNA codons to tRNA anticodons \n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Adds a specific amino acid to the 5' end of the correct tRNA\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Transfers tRNAs from the A site to the P site within the ribosome\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Adds a specific amino acid to the 3' end of the correct tRNA","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\ntRNA-activating enzymes add specific amino acids to the 3’ end of their corresponding tRNAs using energy from ATP. **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because matching codons to anticodons occurs through complementary base pairing during translation, not via tRNA-activating enzymes.\n\n **Choice B**  Is incorrect because the amino acid is added to the 3' end of the tRNA, not the 5' end.\n\n \n\n  **Choice C**  is incorrect because tRNA movement within the ribosome (from A to P site) is facilitated by the ribosome itself, not by tRNA-activating enzymes."},{"ref":"BI0335","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0335\n\nThe diagram shows a tRNA molecule. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/335.png\" alt=\"335\">\n\n[Adapted from Vossman. (2005, November 7). *Category: TRNA*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved June 1, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category: TRNA. Copyright free]\n\nWhich of the following would bind to the position labelled R?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Anticodon\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Codon\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ rRNA\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ An amino acid","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nThe position labelled R on the tRNA molecule represents the anticodon. The anticodon is a sequence of three nucleotide bases on the tRNA that binds to the complementary codon on the mRNA strand during translation. This specific base pairing ensures that the correct amino acid, carried by the tRNA, is added to the growing polypeptide chain. **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the anticodon is the region labelled R on the diagram of the tRNAs, and this binds to the codon on the mRNA. \n\n **Choice C** is incorrect because rRNA is part of the ribosomal structure and does not bind to the anticodon.\n\n **Choice D** is incorrect because the amino acid binds to the 3′ end of the tRNA, not to the anticodon region."},{"ref":"BI0336","marks":"11","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0336\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nThe diagram below represents the process of translation.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n[Adapted from Miller, C. L. (2020, December 30). *ribosome mrna translation pt.svg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved June 1, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Multiple_Ribosomes_Translation_Protein_Synthesis.png. Copyright free]\n\n1. \n\n    1. Identify structures Q and R on the diagram. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Outline the roles of the binding sites within structure R. [3 marks]\n\n    3. State the name of the entire structure which has formed in the diagram. [1 mark]\n\n2. Describe how the named structure a(iii) works. [3 marks]\n\n3. Describe the secondary structure of a protein. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. \n\n    1. $\\hspace{1em}$ Any **two** of the following:\n\n        $\\ce{->}$ Q is the polypeptide chain **OR** chain of amino acids [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{->}$ R is a ribosome [1 mark]\n\n        [2 marks]\n\n    2. Any **three** of the following (an annotated diagram is also acceptable):\n\n        $\\ce{->}$ three binding sites, A, P and E (in the large subunit of the ribosome) [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{->}$ one binding site for mRNA in the small subunit [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{->}$ A site is the binding site for the tRNA carrying the the next amino acid [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{->}$ P site is where initiator tRNA binds [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{->}$ P site is where the amino acid from tRNA is bound to the growing polypeptide [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{->}$ E site is for the tRNA that is exiting the ribosome [1 mark]\n\n        [3 marks]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n        Ribosomes have three binding sites — **A, P and E** ($\\checkmark$), on the large subunit of the ribosome and **mRNA binding site**  on the small subunit ($\\checkmark$). The **A site** is where the **tRNA** ($\\checkmark$) with the amino acid attached enters the ribosome. The **P site** is where the amino acid from the tRNA is bound by the peptide bond to the growing **polypeptide chain** ($\\checkmark$) ,  and the **E site** is where the empty tRNA **exits** ($\\checkmark$) the ribosome.\n\n    3. $\\hspace{1em}$\n\n        $\\ce{->}$ polysome **OR** polyribosome  [1 mark]\n\n        [1 mark]\n\n2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ ribosomes are the site of protein synthesis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ a polysome is multiple ribosomes bound to the same mRNA molecule **OR** Multiple ribosomes bind to the same mRNA molecule [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ many copies of the same polypeptide can be made at the same time [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ ribosomes move from the 5' end of mRNA to the 3'-end [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Ribosomes are the site of **protein synthesis** ($\\checkmark$). A polysome is **multiple ribosomes** bound to the same mRNA molecule ($\\checkmark$); therefore, many **copies of the same polypeptide** ($\\checkmark$) chain can be made at the same time. Ribosomes assemble around the start codon at the **5' end** ($\\checkmark$) of the mRNA and then synthesise polypeptide while moving towards the 3' end. \n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ due to to hydrogen bonding [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ hydrogen bonds form between carboxyl and amino groups, **OR** C=O and N-H groups, of different amino acids **OR** between amino acids within the same polypeptide chain [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ results in the formation of alpha helices **OR** beta pleated sheets [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ bonding happens only between groups involved in the peptide bonds [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Secondary structures are formed by folding within the same polypeptide chain. This is caused by the **hydrogen bond** ($\\checkmark$) formation between **carboxyl**  (C=O) and **amino** ($\\checkmark$) (N-H) groups of peptide bonds on different amino acids. This can form secondary structures called **alpha helices** ($\\checkmark$) and beta-pleated sheets."},{"ref":"BI0470","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0470\n\nThe diagram shows a molecule that plays an important role in protein synthesis.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/BI0470.png\" alt=\"BI0470\">\n[Source: Adapted from CNX OpenStax (2016, November). *File: OSC Microbio 11 04 tRNA.jpg.* Wikimedia commons. Retrieved May 13, 2022, from https:// commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:OSC_Microbio_11_04_tRNA.jpg. Copyright under CC-BY-4.0]\n\nWhich is found at X?\n\n-    I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Uncoiled DNA\n-    II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ Amino acid binding site\n-    III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ RNA nucleotides\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe diagram shows a tRNA molecule. It is made of RNA nucleotides (**Statement III**). The region labelled X represents the binding site for an amino acid (**Statement II**). This means that both **Statements II and III** are true of X, so **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** only gives **Statement II** so is not correct, as **Statement III** also applies to X.  DNA contains genetic information that is transferred to mRNA for protein synthesis. However, this is not a DNA molecule, therefore **Statement I** does not apply to X and makes **Choices B and D** incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0471","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0471\n\nThe diagram below shows the genetic code.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/BI0471.png\" alt=\"BI0471\">\n[Source: Greenwood, S. (2018, August). *File: Genetic Code.png*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved May 14, 2022, from https ://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Genetic_Code.png. Copyright under CC-BY-SA 4.0]\n\nWhich of the following features can be deduced from the genetic code?\n\n-    I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Codons consisting of different bases can code for the same amino acid\n-    II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ Codon AUG does not code for an amino acid\n-    III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ mRNA strands coding for a protein will always contain codons that do not code for amino acids\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe genetic code shows sequences of three RNA bases, each of which correspond to one amino acid to be added to a polypeptide. A sequence of three bases is known as a codon and different codons can code for the same amino acid (**Statement I**).\n\nThere are three $\\text{\\textquoteleft}$STOP$\\text{\\textquoteright}$ codons, UAA, UAG and UGA which do not code for an amino acid but code for the end of translation.  Therefore they are always included in mRNA sequences (**Statement III**).\n\nCodon AUG is a $\\text{\\textquoteleft}$START$\\text{\\textquoteright}$ codon which also codes for the amino acid methionine (**Statement II**). **Choices A, C** and **D** are all incorrect as they include **Statement II**."},{"ref":"BI0472","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0472\n\nThe diagram below shows the genetic code table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/BI0472.png\" alt=\"BI0472\">\n[Source: Greenwood, S. (2018, August). *File: Genetic Code.png*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved May 14, 2022, from https:// commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Genetic_Code.png. Copyright under CC-BY-SA 4.0]\n\nWhich of the following shows an mRNA sequence that can be converted into a polypeptide?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 5$\\text{\\textquoteright}$ - AUG GCC AAA AGG CCC AGU UAG - 3$\\text{\\textquoteright}$\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 5$\\text{\\textquoteright}$ - UAG CCG AAA AGG CCC AGU AUG - 3$\\text{\\textquoteright}$\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 5$\\text{\\textquoteright}$ - AAA CGG ATG ACC CTC AGT TAC - 3$\\text{\\textquoteright}$\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 5$\\text{\\textquoteright}$ - TAC AGT CTC ACC ATG CGG AAA - 3$\\text{\\textquoteright}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA mRNA strand contains four nitrogenous bases: adenine (A), cytosine (C), guanine (G) and uracil (U). It does not contain thymine (T) (**Choice C** and **Choice D**).\n\nMost mRNA strands which code for polypeptides begin with the $\\text{\\textquoteleft}$START$\\text{\\textquoteright}$ codon AUG and end with a `STOP$\\text{\\textquoteright}$ codon, UAG, UAA or UGA (**Choice A**). **Choice B** has a $\\text{\\textquoteleft}$STOP$\\text{\\textquoteright}$ codon at the beginning which would terminate translation before it begins."},{"ref":"BI0474","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0474\n\nWhat does the phrase $\\text{\\textquoteleft}$the genetic code is universal$\\text{\\textquoteright}$ mean?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Adenine always pairs with thymine, and guanine always pairs with cytosine\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Almost all living organisms use the same genetic code\n\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Different codons cannot code for the same amino acid\n\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Proteomes are always larger than the genomes","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nUniversality of the genetic code means that all organisms with very few exceptions use the same genetic code (**Choice B**). This is why gene transfer between species is possible. An example of the universality of genetic code is the production of human insulin in bacterial cells.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it is a complementarity rule and is not related to the $\\text{\\textquoteleft}$universal genetic code$\\text{\\textquoteright}$ concept.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because several codons may code for the same amino acid.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the size of the genome or proteome is not related to the $\\text{\\textquoteleft}$universal genetic code$\\text{\\textquoteright}$ concept."},{"ref":"BI0475","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0475\n\nRibosomes are reused many times for protein synthesis. What happens to a ribosome after completing translation of an mRNA molecule?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ It twists and folds proteins into a tertiary structure suited to perform a specific function\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ It binds to another amino acid\n\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ The large subunit and the small subunit of the ribosome separate to be reused again later\n\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ It is destroyed by a lysosome","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAfter a ribosome completes translation of an mRNA molecule, the large and small subunits separate. These subunits can then be reused to initiate translation of another mRNA. This recycling of ribosomes makes protein synthesis efficient and continuous in the cell. **Choice C** is the correct answer to the question.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because ribosomes synthesise the primary structure (the polypeptide chain), but folding into a tertiary structure happens after translation and is not carried out by the ribosome.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because it is tRNA, not the ribosome, that binds amino acids. Also, binding amino acids happens during translation, not after it’s completed.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because ribosomes are not broken down after translation. They are reused repeatedly, and only damaged or non-functional ones may be degraded."},{"ref":"BI0476","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0476\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nThe diagram below shows the genetic code.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/BI0472.png\" alt=\"BI0472\">\n[Source: Greenwood, S. (2018, August). *File: Genetic Code.png*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved May 14, 2022, from https:// commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Genetic_Code.png. Copyright under CC-BY-SA 4.0]\n\n1. Identify the amino acid sequence of a peptide produced from the following mRNA codon sequence using the genetic code. [1 mark]\n\n5$\\text{\\textquoteright}$ $\\text{\\textendash}$ AUG GGG AAG GGU CGA AGC UAA $\\text{\\textendash}$ 3$\\text{\\textquoteright}$\n\n2. Codons need to consist of a sequence of three bases to code for one amino acid. State **one** reason why codons need to consist of three bases. [1 mark]\n\n3. Calculate the number of nucleotides present in the mRNA strand if the polypeptide synthesised has a length of 120 amino acids. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.      $\\ce{ -> }$ Met  Gly  Lys   Gly   Arg   Ser [1 mark]\n\n[1 mark]\n\n2.    Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there are only four different nitrogenous bases, **WHILE** there are twenty amino acids [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ one base cannot code for one amino acid    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ two base codons could only code for sixteen (4$^2$) amino acids **OR** two base codons are too few to code for twenty amino acids    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ three base codons allow for (4$^3$) sixty-four possible codons **OR** combinations [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\nCodons need to consist of a sequence of three bases because there are **twenty types of amino acids but only four types of nucleotides** ($\\checkmark$). Thus, one base cannot code for one amino acid; in this case, there would be only four possible codons. **Two nucleotide codons would lead to sixteen codons** ($\\checkmark$), but **three nucleotide codons lead to 64** different codons ($\\checkmark$), which is enough to code for all twenty amino acids.\n\n3.    Show working:\n$\\ce{ -> }$ 363 (nucleotides) [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\none amino acid is coded by three nucleotides\\\n120 amino acids $\\times$ 3 = 360 nucleotides\\\n 360 + 3 nucleotides (stop codon) = **363** nucleotides ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"BI0500","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0500\n\nA non-coding (antisense) strand of DNA consists of the following nucleotides:\n\nGGCACATTAG\n\nWhich of the following represents the RNA transcript produced by this DNA strand?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ GGCACAUUAG\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ CCGUGUAAUC\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ GGCACATTAG\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ CCGTGTAATC","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe non-coding, or antisense strand, is the strand that serves as a template for RNA synthesis. RNA is formed from the antisense strand through complementary base pairing:\n\n- The base A (adenine) in DNA binds with the base U (uracil) in RNA.\n- The base T (thymine) in DNA binds with the base A (adenine) in RNA.\n- The base C (cytosine in DNA binds with the base G (guanine) in RNA.\n- The base G (guanine) in DNA binds with the base C (cytosine) in RNA.\n\nSo, the RNA transcript produced by complementary base pairing with the DNA strand GGCACATTAG is CCGUGUAAUC.\n\nThe base thymine (T) is not found in RNA (**Choice C** and **Choice D**). **Choice A** does not show complementary base pairing, which is required for the production of mRNA."},{"ref":"BI0518","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0518\n\nThe diagram shows how RNA is transcribed from DNA. What structures are indicated by the labels X, Y, and Z?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/BI0518.png\" alt=\"BI0518\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from National Human Genome Research Institute. (n.d.). *Transcription*. Retrieved March 18, 2022, from https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Transcription.  Copyright under Public Domain]\n\n| | X | Y | Z |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 5’ end of DNA | 3’ end of RNA | RNA polymerase |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 5’ end of DNA | 3’ end of RNA | DNA ligase |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 3’ end of DNA | 5’ end of RNA | RNA polymerase |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 3’ end of RNA | 5’ end of DNA | DNA polymerase |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nTranscription proceeds in the 5’ to 3’ direction. During transcription, RNA polymerase adds the 5’ end of an RNA nucleotide to the 3’ end of the growing RNA transcript.\n\nIn the image, Y marks the start of the RNA transcript. Since RNA is transcribed in the 5’ to 3’ direction, the start of the transcript is the 5’ end. Therefore, Y represents the 5’ end of RNA, not the 3’ end (**Choices A** and **B**).\n\nX marks the start of the DNA template strand. The RNA transcript is synthesised antiparallel to the DNA. So, the transcription starts at the 3’ end of DNA. Therefore, X represents the 3’ end of DNA.\n\nZ marks the enzyme synthesising the new RNA transcript. RNA is produced by RNA polymerase. Therefore, Y represents RNA polymerase, not DNA polymerase (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0542","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0542\n\nThe table gives the genetic code.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/BI0542.png\" alt=\"BI0542\">\n[Source: CNX OpenStax. (2016, November 11). *File: OSC Microbio 11 04 GenCode.jpg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved January 4, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: OSC_Microbio_11_04_GenCode.jpg.  Copyright under CC-BY 4.0]\n\nWhich polypeptide is produced by the mRNA sequence AUG GCU AAA UAG CGA?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Met-Ala-Lys\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Met-Ala-Lys-Tyr-Arg\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Met-Arg-Asn\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Met-Arg-Asn-Cys-Arg","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAccording to the table:\n- AUG codes for Met\n- GCU codes for Ala\n- AAA codes for Lys\n- UAG is a stop codon\n- CGA codes for Arg\n\nWhen translational machinery encounters a stop codon, the ribosome dissociates from the mRNA and translation ends. So, the translational machinery will produce a polypeptide containing Met, Ala, and Lys, but will stop translation when it reaches the UAG codon. Therefore, the polypeptide produced by translation of this mRNA sequence will only contain Met, Ala, and Lys, in that order. **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect, as translation cannot continue beyond Lys, due to the stop codon. **Choice C** and **Choice D** are incorrect as GCU codes for Ala, not Arg and AAA codes for Lys, not Asn."},{"ref":"BI0545","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0545\n\nWhy can a eukaryote produce a larger number of mRNA transcripts than the number of genes in its genome?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Some mRNA transcripts are double-stranded.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ There are genes that produce multiple different mRNA transcripts.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Not all mRNA transcripts are produced by genes.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Some mRNA transcripts are produced by other mRNA transcripts.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nMessenger RNA transcripts are produced as a result of transcription. During the transcription of RNA from DNA in eukaryotes, genes are transcribed to produce an RNA molecule that contains introns (non-coding regions) and exons (coding regions) of the gene. During post-transcriptional RNA processing, the introns are removed, and exons are spliced together to produce the mRNA transcript. However, the exons that make up the final transcript are not always the same: some genes can undergo alternative splicing, in which different combinations of exons from the gene are spliced to create mRNA. So, multiple mRNA transcripts can be produced by a single gene, which results in the number of possible RNA transcripts exceeding the number of genes in the genome.\n\nmRNA is never double-stranded (**Choice A**).  All mRNA transcripts are produced by genes (**Choice C**).  One mRNA transcript cannot code for another mRNA transcript (**Choice D**).  mRNA is used in translation."},{"ref":"BI0582","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0582\n\nWhich of the following statements is **false** regarding translation?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Translation needs mRNA, which carries genetic information in the form of codons.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The process of translation starts with the start codon AUG.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Translation takes place in the nucleus.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ tRNAs contain anticodons which are complementary to the mRNA’s codons.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A, B**, and **D** – These statements are true.\n\n**Choice C** -This statement is false. In eukaryotic cells, translation takes place in the cytoplasm. Prokaryotic cells do not have a nucleus."},{"ref":"BI0599","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0599\n\nWhich of the following shows the correct sequence of mRNA synthesis?\n\n-    I. $\\hspace{1em}$ RNA polymerase matches free nucleotides to the DNA strand.\n-    II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ Transcription continues until it reaches a terminator sequence.\n-    III. $\\hspace{.4em}$  RNA polymerase binds to the promoter region.\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$  III, I, II\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$  I, II, III\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$  II, I, III\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$  II, III, I","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nRNA polymerase binds to the promoter region of the DNA strand (**Statement III**). RNA polymerase then matches free nucleotides to the exposed template strand of DNA and continues adding complementary nucleotides to the growing mRNA strand (**Statement I**). Transcription continues until RNA polymerase reaches a terminator sequence, which signals the end of transcription, and the new mRNA strand is released (**Statement II**).\n\n**Choices B**, **C** and **D** are incorrect as they are not showing the correct sequence."},{"ref":"BI0604","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0604\n\nThe diagram below shows pre-mRNA and three mature mRNA transcripts where the introns have been removed.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/604.png\" alt=\"604\">\n\nWhat is an advantage of the above process?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The rate of transcription increases.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Different polypeptides can be produced from one pre-mRNA transcript.\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Translation occurs immediately after transcription.\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Prokaryotes can produce a variety of proteins from one section of DNA.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe above process shows the splicing of mRNA in which introns are removed and exons are joined together in different arrangements. The advantage of mRNA splicing is that the number of different polypeptides that can be produced by one segment of DNA is increased. mRNA splicing only occurs in eukaryotes.\n\nSplicing does not increase the rate of transcription as it occurs after transcription is complete (**Choice A**).\n\nIn eukaryotes, translation takes place after post-transcriptional modifications are complete which requires several steps, so translation does not occur immediately after transcription (**Choice C**).\n\nSplicing of mRNA does not take place in prokaryotes because there are no introns in prokaryotic DNA (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0607","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0607\n\nWhat happens when RNA polymerase binds to DNA at the promoter region?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Introns are removed and exons are joined together.\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Synthesis of a new strand of DNA begins.\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ RNA polymerase detaches from the promoter region.\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ RNA nucleotides are added to the 3’ end of the elongating RNA strand.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAfter RNA polymerase binds to DNA at the promoter region (a process known as initiation), elongation takes place. During elongation, the 5\\' end of the free RNA nucleotide binds to the 3\\' OH end of the elongating RNA strand  (**Choice D**). \\\nAfter the elongation, termination takes place, during which RNA polymerase detaches from the promoter region (**Choice C**). \\\nRemoval of introns and the joining of exons (**Choice A**) is a step in the post-transcriptional modifications of mRNA, which takes place after termination is over. \\\nSynthesis of a new DNA strand is part of DNA replication and not transcription (**Choice B**)."},{"ref":"BI0611","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0611\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Distinguish between eukaryotic and prokaryotic transcription. [3 marks]\n\n2. Describe the roles of non-coding DNA sequences.  [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe the role of DNA-binding proteins in gene expression. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1.    any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in prokaryotic cells transcription and translation of a gene can happen simultaneously, **WHEREAS** in eukaryotes they occur separately [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in eukaryotic cells there are post-transcriptional modifications, **OR** addition of 5\\' cap and poly-A tail, **OR** RNA splicing, **OR** removal of introns and joining of exons, **BUT** in prokaryotes, there are no post-transcriptional modifications [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in eukaryotic cells transcription takes place in the nucleus, **BUT** in prokaryotes, transcription takes place in the cytoplasm [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in eukaryotic cells, mature mRNA is produced from pre-mRNA **WHEREAS** in prokaryotes, mRNA is formed immediately through the transcription of the DNA strand [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nA difference between transcription in eukaryotes and prokaryotes is that transcription takes place in the **nucleus** for eukaryotes, whereas, in prokaryotes, transcription takes place in the **cytoplasm** ($\\checkmark$). **Prokaryotic** cells can also transcribe and translate a gene at the **same time**, whereas in **eukaryotes**, these two processes are **separated in time and place** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    Another difference is that in eukaryotes, there are **post-transcriptional modifications** such as RNA splicing, where introns are removed and exons are joined, while in prokaryotes, there are **no post-transcriptional modifications** ($\\checkmark$) due to the absence of introns.\n\n2.    any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ do not code for polypeptides [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA polymerase binds to promoters to initiate transcription **OR** promoters are where transcription is initiated     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ proteins **OR** activators bind to enhancers to increase transcription [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ proteins, **OR** repressors bind to silencers to prevent transcription [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ promoter-proximal elements regulate transcription [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ introns allow for alternative mRNA splicing [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ telomeres protect the ends of chromosomes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some DNA sequences code for tRNA and rRNA [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some functions are still unknown **OR** accept any other valid example of non-coding DNA and its function [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nNon-coding DNA sequences **do not code for polypeptides** ($\\checkmark$). For example the promoter is a section of DNA **where RNA polymerase binds** to initiate transcription ($\\checkmark$). Enhancers **increase transcription** ($\\checkmark$) when proteins bind to them and silencers **prevent transcription** ($\\checkmark$) when proteins bind to them. There are also genes that **code for tRNA and rRNA** ($\\checkmark$) as the final product.\n\n3.    any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transcription factors are proteins that bind to DNA sequences to regulate transcription [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ activator proteins bind to enhancer sequences [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  (this) increases the rate of transcription    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ repressor proteins bind to silencer sequences [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  (this) decreases the rate of transcription [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ proteins bind to promoter-proximal elements [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  (this is) to modulate **OR** regulate the transcription of genes     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA polymerase binds to the promoter [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  (this) initiates  transcription    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ histones bind to DNA in order to pack the DNA more tightly [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (this is)  to prevent gene expression [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nMany proteins bind to DNA sequences to regulate gene expression. For example, **activator proteins** bind to enhancers ($\\checkmark$) which are non-coding sections of DNA close to the promoter.  This **increases** the rate of **transcription** ($\\checkmark$). Regulatory proteins also bind to **promoter-proximal elements** ($\\checkmark$). The binding of proteins to this region **regulates transcription** ($\\checkmark$) of the gene.  **Repressor proteins** bind to the **silencer** ($\\checkmark$) and when this happens, there is a **decrease in the rate of transcription** ($\\checkmark$). Finally, **RNA polymerase** binds to the **promoter** ($\\checkmark$) to **initiate transcription** ($\\checkmark$) of the gene."},{"ref":"BI0879","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0879\n\nA single base substitution mutation occurs and a stop codon is not produced.  Which of the following is **not** a possible consequence of this type of mutation?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The quaternary structure of the protein this gene codes for is altered.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ No amino acids in the polypeptide this gene codes for are changed.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Two amino acids in the polypeptide this gene codes for are changed.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ DNA repair mechanisms are activated, producing a stop codon.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because single base substitution mutations only affect one codon and therefore can only change one amino acid, not two.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because a single base substitution mutation can sometimes change the amino acid sequence of a polypeptide which can then alter the quaternary structure of the protein. **Choice B** is incorrect because sometimes single base substitution mutations do not change the amino acid sequence even though a codon is changed.  This is possible because the genetic code is degenerate. **Choice D** is incorrect because it is possible for DNA repair mechanisms to repair the DNA incorrectly which could result in an alteration of the base sequence, producing a stop codon.  It is also possible the mutation occurred within a stop codon and that the DNA repair mechanisms correctly repaired the DNA, restoring the stop codon that mutated."},{"ref":"BI0896","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0896\n\nWhich function and process are correctly matched in the table below?\n\n| | Process | Function |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Replication | Addition of the 5’ cap and poly-A tail to the 3’ end |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Transcription | RNA polymerase adds nucleotides to the 3’ end of the growing mRNA strand |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Translation | DNA polymerase adds nucleotides to the 3’ end of the primer |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Splicing | Ribosome moves along the mRNA from the 5’ end to the 3’ end |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because RNA polymerase adds nucleotides to the 3’ end of the growing mRNA strand in transcription.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the addition of the 5’ cap and poly-A tail takes place in splicing. **Choice C** is incorrect because the addition of nucleotides by DNA polymerase takes place during DNA replication. **Choice D** is incorrect because the movement of ribosomes along the mRNA takes place during translation."},{"ref":"BI1001","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1001\n\nWhich of the following accurately describes a key difference in transcription between prokaryotes and eukaryotes?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Prokaryotes have RNA as a template for transcription, while eukaryotes have DNA as a template.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ In prokaryotes, transcription occurs in the nucleus, while in eukaryotes, it occurs in the cytoplasm.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Prokaryotic mRNA does not undergo splicing, while eukaryotic mRNA does.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Prokaryotic tmRNA undergo splicing, while eukaryotic mRNA does not.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn prokaryotes, the mRNA produced after transcription is ready for translation and does not undergo splicing. In contrast, eukaryotic pre-mRNA undergoes splicing to remove introns and join exons, producing mature mRNA. **Choice C** is, therefore, correct.\n\nBoth prokaryotes and eukaryotes use DNA as a template for transcription. **Choice A** is, therefore, incorrect.  In prokaryotes, transcription occurs in the cytoplasm, while in eukaryotes, it occurs in the nucleus. **Choice B** is, therefore, incorrect. Splicing of mRNA occurs in eukaryotes, not prokaryotes. **Choice D** is, therefore, incorrect"},{"ref":"BI1115","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1115\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Describe the characteristics of the genetic code. [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline, with examples, the importance of complementary base pairing. [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain the roles of  RNA and ribosomes in the formation of a polypeptide chain once mRNA has exited the nucleus. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1.    any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic code consists of sequence of three nucleotides **OR** three bases **OR** triplet code **OR** codons on mRNA [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 64 **OR** 4$^3$ possible combinations [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ each of the 20 amino acids is coded for by at least one codon [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  more codons exist than there are amino acids to be coded for **OR** most amino acids coded for by more than one codon     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ degenerate code [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ code is conservative **OR** the first two bases of a codon are often more critical than the third in determining the amino acid specified **OR** wobble effect allows some flexibility in third base [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ universal **OR** nearly universal across all organisms [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ non-overlapping **OR** codons are read sequentially without overlapping [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ specific codons start **OR** stop protein synthesis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ directional **OR** codons read in a specific direction [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The genetic code consists of **sequences of three nucleotides** ($\\checkmark$), known as **codons** which make up mRNA. As there are **64** different **codons** ($\\checkmark$) most of the **20 amino acids** are coded for by **more than one** ($\\checkmark$); this is known as a **degenerate code** ($\\checkmark$). The code includes **specific start** and **stop** codons ($\\checkmark$) that initiate and terminate translation respectively. Additionally, the genetic code is **nearly universal** applying across **almost all living organisms** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.    any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  allows for semi-conservative replication [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in DNA replication, each parent DNA strand acts as a template for a new one so new DNA molecules produced are identical to the parent DNA strands [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adenine pairs with thymine **OR** uracil and cytosine pairs with guanine **OR** A pairs with T **OR** U  and C with G [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  to provide structural stability to the DNA molecule    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ complementary bases pair up through hydrogen bonds **OR** A forms two hydrogen bonds with T, and C forms three hydrogen bonds with G  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to synthesize complementary RNA from a DNA template [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  in transcription **OR** formation of tRNA OR formation of rRNA **OR** formation of mRNA, complementary base pairing between free RNA nucleotides and the DNA strand forms RNA    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ U OR uracil is found in RNA instead of  T **OR** thymine [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enables synthesis of polypeptides OR proteins from mRNA [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ensures accuracy **OR** less or no mistakes **OR** errors [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  in translation, mRNA codons are complementary to anticodons of tRNA, which carry amino acids **OR** enabling correct amino acids to be brought into place    [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n    Complementary base pairing between nitrogenous bases of nucleotides occurs between **cytosine and guanine**, and between **adenine and thymine** (in DNA) or **uracil** (in RNA) ($\\checkmark$). This allows for the **semi-conservative replication** of  DNA ($\\checkmark$). Here **parental strands** of DNA act as **templates** for the new strands to be formed, so that new DNA molecules are **identical to the parental** ones ($\\checkmark$). It is also important for the **synthesis of RNA** and **proteins** ($\\checkmark$). This can be seen in **transcription**, where bases of the **DNA** molecule serve as a template for the complementary pairing of bases in **RNA** ($\\checkmark$) and in **translation** where **mRNA codons pair** up with the **anticodons** found on tRNA ($\\checkmark$) molecules which carry the amino acids.\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA **OR** messenger RNA serves as a template for the synthesis of polypeptides **OR** for translation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA **OR** messenger RNA carries the code from the DNA in the nucleus to the ribosomes in the cytoplasm [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rRNA **OR** ribosomal RNA, along with ribosomal proteins, forms ribosomes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tRNA **OR** transfer RNA carries the correct amino acid to the correct codon on the mRNA\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA binds with  small subunit of ribosome [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ribosome binds at 5’ end of mRNA [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tRNA carrying correct amino acid, binds to large subunit [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ anticodon to codon on mRNA **OR** through complementary base pairing **OR** through G to C and U to A [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ second, tRNA brings in next amino acids and attaches to large subunit [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ peptide bond forms between adjacent amino acids **OR** condensation reactions form bond between the amino acids **OR** enzyme catalyses formation of covalent bond between amino acids [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ first tRNA then released to collect its next amino acids    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ribosome moves along mRNA to help bring in next tRNA     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ process repeats until stop codon **OR** until polypeptide complete     [1 mark]\n\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n   The **mRNA** carries the **genetic code** for the formation of the polypeptide from the DNA **into the cytoplasm** ($\\checkmark$) to act as a **template for transcription** ($\\checkmark$). The **mRNA binds** with the **small unit** of the **ribosome** ($\\checkmark$). The **first tRNA**, carrying the corresponding amino acid, then **binds** to the **large subunit** of the ribosome ($\\checkmark$), bringing the **correct amino acid** into the correct place along the messenger RNA, due to **complementary base pairing** between the **anticodon** on the tRNA and **codon** ($\\checkmark$)on the mRNA. A **second tRNA**, also **binds** to the **large subunit** of the ribosome and brings in the **next amino acid** ($\\checkmark$). A strong **covalent peptide bond** forms between the **two amino acids** ($\\checkmark$), and the first tRNA is released to collect its next amino acid. This **process continues until** a **stop codon** on the mRNA is reached ($\\checkmark$), and then the polypeptide is released."},{"ref":"BI1173","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1173\n\nWhat is the role of the A-site in the ribosome?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ It is where the initiator tRNA binds to the start codon\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ It holds the tRNA with the growing polypeptide chain whilst a peptide bond forms between adjacent amino acids\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ To allow complementary base pairing between codons and anticodons, so that the correct amino acid is incorporated into the polypeptide\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ It is where the discharged tRNA exits from the ribosome","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThere are three sites on the ribosome which are important in translation. The role of the A-site is to allow complementary base pairing of incoming tRNA molecules (anticodons) and mRNA (codons) so that the correct amino acid is incorporated into the growing polypeptide. **Choice C**  is correct.\n\nThe site where the initiator tRNA binds to the start codon on the mRNA is the P site; **Choice A**  is incorrect. Whilst the A site does hold the incoming tRNA while a peptide bond is formed between the amino acid on this tRNA and the growing polypeptide chain, the growing polypeptide chain is on the P-site tRNA; **Choice B**  is incorrect. The site where the discharged tRNA exits from the ribosome is the E site; **Choice D**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1249","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1249\n\nWhich is **not** a reason why some proteins are broken down by proteolysis?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ They have become damaged\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ They are misfolded\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ They have become too large\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ They are no longer needed by the cell","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nProteins must be continuously broken down in the cell to provide a constant supply of amino acids for protein synthesis. This is accomplished through proteolysis. The main reasons proteins must be broken down are when they become damaged, misfolded, or are no longer needed by the cell. Proteins growing too large is not why a protein needs to be degraded, so **Choice C** is the correct option.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect, as proteins can sometimes become damaged due to various factors and are then targeted for proteolysis to prevent them from interfering with regular cell functions. **Choice B** is incorrect as misfolded proteins have a modified 3-D shape, which may disrupt normal function, potentially leading to cellular dysfunction and disease. **Choice D** is incorrect since proteins that are no longer required by the cell are often targeted for proteolysis, allowing their amino acids to be recycled."},{"ref":"BI1251","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1251\n\nWhat mRNA modifications must take place immediately following transcription in eukaryotes?\n\n| | Modification I | Modification II | Modification III |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Removal of introns | Splicing of introns | Addition of 3' caps and 5' poly-A tails |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Removal of exons | Splicing of introns | Addition of 5' caps and 3' poly-A tails |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Removal of introns | Splicing of exons | Addition of 5' caps and 3' poly-A tails |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Removal of exons | Splicing of exons | Addition of 3' caps and 5' poly-A tails  |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIntrons are non-coding segments between exons within a gene, that are transcribed but then removed during RNA processing, to make the mature mRNA. Exons are coding regions of a gene that contain the genetic information necessary to be transcribed into mRNA and translated into functional proteins. Once introns have been removed from the immature mRNA,  the exons are spliced back together to form mature mRNA.  As introns are removed, and exons are spliced back together during the modification of immature mRNA, **Choices B** and **D** are incorrect.\n\n5’ caps (not 3’ caps) are modified nucleotides added to the 5’ end of eukaryotic mRNA, and polyA-tails are added to the 3’ end (not the 5’ end) during transcription. Their purpose is to stabilise the mRNA, aid its transport out of the nucleus and help facilitate the initiation of translation. Therefore **Choice A** is incorrect, and **Choice C** is correct."},{"ref":"BI1252","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1252\n\nThe image below shows part of the transcription process. What can be concluded from the information in the diagram?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/BI1252.png\" alt=\"BI1252\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Different proteins can be produced from the same gene.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Exons are not involved in gene expression.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The DNA sequence is unrelated to the final mRNA sequence.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The removal of introns causes exons to arrange themselves in a random order.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe diagram shows alternative splicing, where exons can be combined in different orders to create different proteins from the same gene. This makes **Choice A** correct.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect, as exons do have a role in gene expression. **Choice C** is incorrect as the DNA sequence still determines the mRNA sequence, even though the order of nucleotides may be different depending on the exons included. **Choice D** is incorrect since the process of alternative splicing is not random and has little to do with the introns themselves. Furthermore, this cannot be concluded from the diagram."},{"ref":"BI1253","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1253\n\nThe following graph shows the number of genes suspected to exist in humans based on estimates collected between 1964-2007. The earliest estimate was calculated by comparing the average amino acid length of proteins and the molecular weight of chromosomes in the human body.\n\nWhy were initial gene estimates much higher than those collected more recently?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/BI1253.png\" alt=\"BI1253\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The structure of DNA had not yet been discovered.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The presence of introns and other non-coding regions of DNA had not yet been discovered.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The structure of proteins had not yet been discovered.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The human genome project had not yet begun.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIntrons were not discovered until 1977, more than 10 years after the initial gene estimate calculated in 1964. Friedrich Vogel made this estimate assuming that all DNA is involved in polypeptide production, which we now know to be false. Thus, **Choice B** is the correct response.\n\n**Choice A** is not correct since Watson and Crick described the structure of DNA in the 1950s. Similarly, **Choice C** is not correct because the earliest protein structures were also discovered in the 1950s. Furthermore, Vogel could not have made his 1964 gene estimate without knowing these two factors, as described in the question. Although it is true that the human genome project occurred much later, it had more to do with sequencing the genome than identifying the total number of genes, and gene estimates had already dropped considerably by this point anyway, so **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1363","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1363\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nA study was conducted on germinating soybeans (*Glycine max*)  to observe the effects of cold stress conditions (10°C) and osmotic stress conditions on the germination rate. Researchers were also interested in the effects of osmotic stress and cold stress conditions on the concentration of polysomes in the germinating soybeans. The control soybeans were grown at 25 °C, in sterilised water.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/BI1363a.png\" alt=\"BI1363a\">\n[Source: Adapted from Swigonska, S., Badowiec, A., Mostek, A., Krol, A., & Weidner, S. (2013, December 6). *Formation and stability of polysomes and polysomal populations in roots of germinating seeds of soybean (glycine Max L.) under cold, osmotic and combined cold and osmotic stress conditions - Acta Physiologiae plantarum*. SpringerLink. Retrieved June 18, 2023, from  https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11738-013-1443-2 Copyright under CC BY]\n\n1. Identify the independent variable in this study.    [1 mark]\n\n2. Describe the effect of cold and osmotic stress on germination.        [2 marks]\n\nThe mean concentrations of polysomes in the soybean seeds were measured at various times during their germination, in the different stress conditions. The results are shown in the graph below. Polysomes are clusters of ribosomes which simultaneously translate a single mRNA molecule to synthesise multiple polypeptide chains.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/BI1363.png\" alt=\"BI1363\">\n[Source: Adapted from Swigonska, S., Badowiec, A., Mostek, A., Krol, A., & Weidner, S. (2013, December 6). *Formation and stability of polysomes and polysomal populations in roots of germinating seeds of soybean (glycine Max L.) under cold, osmotic and combined cold and osmotic stress conditions - Acta Physiologiae plantarum*. SpringerLink. Retrieved June 18, 2023, from  https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11738-013-1443-2 Copyright under CC BY]\n\n3. State the stress condition that has the greatest effect on polysome formation compared to the controls.    [1 mark]\n\n4. Calculate the difference in polysome concentration between the control and the soybean seeds under osmotic stress at 24 hours.    [1 mark]\n\n5. Explain how an increase in polysome formation could affect germination.    [2 marks]\n\n6. Suggest a method that could be used to put the soybean seeds under osmotic stress.    [1 mark]\n\n7. Explain why it is important that the soybeans under osmotic stress are grown at 25°C.        [1 mark]\n\n8. Predict with a reason what would happen to the average concentration of polysomes in soybeans if they were put under heat stress conditions of 50°C.        [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ the stress conditions that the soybeans are subject to     [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The independent variable is the **stress conditions** ($\\checkmark$) that the soybeans are subject to.\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ both cold stress **AND** osmotic stress caused the root to lengthen more slowly        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at 72 hours, both cold **AND** osmotic stress resulted in a similar root length        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ overall osmotic stress decreased root length more than cold stress **OR** osmotic stress decreased root length more than cold stress except at 72 hours    [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nBoth **cold and osmotic** stress **decreased lengthening** of the root ($\\checkmark$) compared to the control. However, overall **osmotic stress decreased root length more** than cold stress ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ cold stress    [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ **Cold stress** ($\\checkmark$) has the greatest effect on polysome formation compared to the control.\n\n4. Units **MUST** be included to gain the mark\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 1.7 mg g:sup[-1] OR 1.7 mg per g    [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/BI1363b.png\" alt=\"BI1363b\">\n[Source: Adapted from Swigonska, S., Badowiec, A., Mostek, A., Krol, A., & Weidner, S. (2013, December 6). *Formation and stability of polysomes and polysomal populations in roots of germinating seeds of soybean (glycine Max L.) under cold, osmotic and combined cold and osmotic stress conditions - Acta Physiologiae plantarum*. SpringerLink. Retrieved June 18, 2023, from  https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11738-013-1443-2 Copyright under CC BY]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 4.6 mg g:sup[-1] - 2.9 mg g:sup[-1] = 1.7 mg g:sup[-1] ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increases in polysome concentration increase the rate that proteins are translated **OR** increases translation efficiency        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ proteins are needed to stimulate **OR** control germination        [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    An increase in polysome formation could affect germination because as the concentration of **polysomes increases** the rate **proteins** are **translated** also increases ($\\checkmark$).  Proteins are likely needed to **stimulate germination** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ place soybeans in a solvent with a much higher solute concentration than the internal concentration of the soybeans **OR** in a strong salt solution **OR** in strong saline solution    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ place soybeans in less water than the control soybeans, in an environment with low humidity    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ place soybeans in a highly concentrated glucose solution    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    One method that could be used to place the soybeans under osmotic stress would be to place the soybeans in a **solvent with a much higher solute concentration** than the **internal** concentration of the soybeans ($\\checkmark$).\n\n7. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ so that any differences that are observed are only due to osmotic stress [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ helps to minimise the impact of confounding variables    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to allow for a fair comparison with the control        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    It is important that the soybeans under osmotic stress are kept at 25°C so that any **differences** that are observed are **only due to osmotic stress** ($\\checkmark$) and not temperature.\n\n8. Any **one** of the following *(answer must include a correct statement and a reason to gain the mark)*:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ concentration of polysomes would decrease **BECAUSE** ribosomes contain proteins **OR** RNA  which would be denatured        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ concentration of polysomes would decrease **BECAUSE** of denaturation of enzymes involved in translation, which therefore reduces the production of ribosomes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ concentration of polysomes would decrease **BECAUSE** there will be reduced mRNA stability resulting in less mRNA templates for protein synthesis, so formation of polysomes may be limited    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The average concentration of polysomes would **decrease because** ribosomes are partly made up of **protein**, which would **denature** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1383","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1383\n\nWhat tRNA anticodon would pair with an mRNA codon transcribed from a DNA sense strand with the sequence ACT?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ UGA\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ ACT\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ ACU\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ TGA","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe DNA sense strand is not used to transcribe mRNA. The antisense, or template strand, is used instead. Therefore, a sense strand with a sequence of ACT would have a corresponding antisense strand of TGA. The complementary pairing created during transcription would then be ACU since uracil (U) replaces thymine (T) in RNA. This is the mRNA codon, which would then pair with the tRNA anticodon UGA. **Choice A** is correct.\n\nDNA sense strand = ACT\\\nDNA antisense strand = TGA\\\nmRNA codon = ACU\\\ntRNA anticodon = UGA\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because ACT is the sequence for the sense strand. **Choice C** cannot be correct because this is the mRNA codon sequence as opposed to the tRNA anticodon sequence. **Choice D** cannot be correct as it represents the DNA antisense strand. Additionally, RNA does not contain thymine so **Choices B** and **D** are not possible."},{"ref":"BI1478","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1478\n\nWhich mRNA sequence codes for the polypeptide Ser-Ala-Pro-Asn-Ile-Phe?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/1478.png\" alt=\"1478\">\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ UCAGCUCCUAACAUCUUU\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ UCGGCUCCAAAUAUGUUU\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ UCCGAUCCGAAUAUUUUU\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ UCGGCUCCCAAGAUCUUU","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nThe amino acid code is read from the 5’ to 3’ direction. Using the diagram, UCA codes for Ser, GCU codes for Ala, CCU codes for Pro, AAC codes for Asn, AUC codes for Ile and UUU codes for Phe. **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect as the fifth mRNA codon (AUG) does not code for Ile. **Choice C** is incorrect as the second mRNA codon (GAU) does not code for Ala. **Choice D** is incorrect as the fourth mRNA codon (AAG) does not code for Asn"},{"ref":"BI1515","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1515\n\nThe image shows a mature mRNA sequence. What would be the likely result if the poly-A tail was missing?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/BI1515%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1515 fig1\">\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The 5’ cap would not be added\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The mRNA sequence would not be translated\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Splicing of introns would not occur\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Exonucleases would degrade the mRNA sequence","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nThe poly-A tail is a long sequence of adenines added to the 3’ end of mature mRNA by the enzyme poly-A polymerase. Adding the poly-A tail increases the stability of the mRNA and protects the mRNA from degradation by exonucleases. **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the addition of the 5’ cap to mRNA by the enzyme guanylyl transferase is independent of the polyadenylation of the 3’ end. The 5' cap, which is a modified guanine nucleotide, is added to the 5' end of the mRNA precursor during the initial phases of transcription and before the mRNA is fully processed and exported to the cytoplasm. This process is not affected by the presence or absence of a poly-A tail. **Choice B** is incorrect because while the lack of a poly-A tail can lead to increased mRNA degradation and, thus, reduced translation, the actual process of translation itself does not directly require a poly(A) tail. If an mRNA lacking a poly(A) tail managed to evade degradation and reach a ribosome, it could still theoretically be translated. **Choice C** is incorrect because splicing is carried out by the spliceosome before the polyadenylation of the mRNA."},{"ref":"BI1516","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1516\n\nWhich diagram shows a mature mRNA immediately prior to attachment to a ribosome?\n\n- A. <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/BI1516%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1516 fig1\">\n\n- B. <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/BI1516%20fig2.png\" alt=\"BI1516 fig2\">\n\n- C. <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/BI1516%20fig3.png\" alt=\"BI1516 fig3\">\n\n- D. <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/BI1516%20fig4.png\" alt=\"BI1516 fig4\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nImmediately before a mature mRNA is attached to a ribosome, the spliceosome complex will have spliced out the introns, leaving only exons. A cap will be added to the 5’ end and a poly-A tail to the 3’ end. **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as it contains both exons and introns. **Choice B** is incorrect as it is missing a 5’ cap. **Choice C** is incorrect as the poly-A tail is on the 5’ end, and the cap is on the 3’ end."},{"ref":"BI1517","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1517\n\nWhich of the following would be possible reasons for polypeptides being modified after translation?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1.6em}$ So that polypeptides are only functional at specific sites \n\n- II. $\\hspace{1.3em}$ To stabilise the mRNA sequence\n\n- III. $\\hspace{1em}$ To mark them for degradation\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III \n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nModifications of polypeptides after translation, such as phosphorylation, methylation, acetylation, ubiquitination, and more, can regulate the activity of proteins, control their localisation within the cell, and determine their interactions with other molecules. These modifications ensure that proteins are active only in specific cellular contexts or locations, thereby facilitating precise control over cellular processes; **Statement I** is therefore true. The attachment of ubiquitin proteins to a lysine residue on a target protein often serves as a signal for the protein to be degraded by the proteasome, a protein complex responsible for degrading and recycling unneeded or damaged proteins within the cell. This process is crucial for regulating protein levels and removing malfunctioning proteins, thus maintaining cellular health and function; **Statement III** is also true. This means that **Choice C**  is correct.\n\nModifications such as adding 3’ poly-A tails and 5’ caps do function to stabilise mRNA sequences, but these are post-transcriptional modifications, not post-translational modifications. Therefore, **Statement II** is incorrect. **Choices A, B** and **D**  are incorrect as they include **Statement II**."},{"ref":"BI1518","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1518\n\nInsulin is a protein that undergoes a two-step post-translational modification before it can function. The micrograph shows a pancreatic cell.  In which part of the cell would the post-translational modification of insulin take place?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/BI1518%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1518 fig1\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Deernick, T. (n.d.). Pancreatic cells, TEM - stock image - C037/6460 - science photo library. Science Photo Library. Retrieved April  10, 2024 from URL https://www.sciencephoto.com/media/891878/view/pancreatic-cells-tem. Copyright free]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nInsulin is synthesised as pre-proinsulin by the ribosomes. When it enters the lumen of the rough endoplasmic reticulum, it undergoes initial processing to remove the signal peptide, forming proinsulin. Proinsulin then moves to the Golgi apparatus, where it is further processed into mature insulin by cleaving the proinsulin to remove the C$-$peptide, resulting in the formation of the active insulin molecule, which consists of an A and B chain linked by disulfide bonds. A indicates the Golgi apparatus; **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect as it indicates a mitochondrion. **Choice C**  is incorrect as it indicates the nucleus. **Choice D**  is incorrect as it indicates a vesicle."},{"ref":"BI1519","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1519\n\nThe diagram illustrates a process involving ubiquitin and the proteasome system.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/BI1519.png\" alt=\"BI1519\">\n\nFrom the diagram and your own knowledge, deduce which of the following is the role of ubiquitin.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ To hydrolyse proteins into short peptides\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ To mark proteins for degradation\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ To create a pool of amino acids that have been recycled from degraded proteins\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ To act as a signal to the proteasome, preventing protein degradation.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nUbiquitin is a short-chained regulatory protein that attaches to proteins so that the proteasome recognises them. The proteasome breaks down proteins into peptides so that they can be further degraded into amino acids; **Choice B** is correct.\n\nUbiquitin does not hydrolyse proteins, the proteasome does; **Choice A** is incorrect. Ubiquitin does not create a pool of amino acids that have been recycled from degraded proteins; **Choice C** is incorrect. Ubiquitin acts as a signal to the proteosome to promote degradation of the tagged protein, not prevent it; **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1520","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1520\n\nWhat is needed to sustain a functional proteome?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1.6em}$ Breakdown of proteins\n\n- II. $\\hspace{1.3em}$ Synthesis of proteins\n\n- III. $\\hspace{1em}$ Recycling of amino acids\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nA proteome is the complete set of proteins that can be expressed by a cell, tissue or organism. To maintain a functional proteome, there must be constant, regulated, breakdown of proteins (**Statement I**). This is crucial for removing damaged, misfolded, or unnecessary proteins from the cell to help maintain cellular health. Constant synthesis of proteins (**Statement II**) is driven by the genetic information encoded in DNA and mediated by RNA. Protein synthesis is fundamental for cell growth, repair, and the maintenance of cellular functions. The constant recycling of amino acids (**Statement III**) is important for efficient cellular operation, allowing cells to reuse them to synthesise new proteins or for other metabolic functions. **Choice D**  is correct.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **C**  are incorrect as they do not include all three statements."},{"ref":"BI1566","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1566\n\nWhat would the translated amino acid sequence be for a DNA sense strand with base sequence TTGTTAGAAAACCCG?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/BI1566.png\" alt=\"BI1566\">\n[Source: Mouagip, (2009, February 18). *File: Aminoacids table.svg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved April 24, 2024, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Aminoacids_table.svg. Public domain]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Asn-Asn-Leu-Leu-Gly\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Leu-Ile-Glu-Asn-Pro\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Asn-Asp-Leu-Leu-Gly\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Leu-Leu-Glu-Asn-Pro","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because the mRNA strand is transcribed from the DNA antisense strand. This means that the mRNA sequence matches the DNA sense strand given, except that thymine (T) is replaced by uracil (U). The mRNA sequence would therefore be UUG-UUA-GAA-AAC-CCG. When translated to amino acids, this yields Leu-Leu-Glu-Asn-Pro.\n\n**Choices A, B,** and **C** are incorrect as they contain one or more incorrect amino acids."},{"ref":"BI1813","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1813\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nBelow is a diagram showing gene expression.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/BI1813.png\" alt=\"BI1813\">\n\n1. Name the processes labelled I and II. [1 mark]\n\n2. Describe the role of RNA polymerase in the process labelled ‘I’. [2 marks]\n\n3. Explain why the DNA template strand remains stable during transcription. [2 marks]\n\nThe table below shows mRNA codons for amino acids.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/BI1813b.png\" alt=\"BI1813b\">\n\n4. Determine the amino acid sequence coded for by this DNA template sequence. [2 marks]\n\nCGT GGA CTT ACG CCG\n\n5. Explain how the deletion of the sixth base (A) in the sequence shown in (b) would have a different effect on the primary structure of the polypeptide chain to if there was a substitution of the sixth base with guanine (G). [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ I is transcription **AND** II is translation [1 mark]\n\n[1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Process **I is transcription** and process **II is translation**($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ forms mRNA from the DNA template **OR** non-coding DNA strand [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ aligns RNA nucleotides with complementary DNA bases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleotides held in place by hydrogen bonding [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ links 5’ end of nucleotide to 3’ end of growing RNA molecule **OR** synthesises RNA in the 5' to 3' direction [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ catalyses formation of sugar-phosphate bonds between RNA nucleotides [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ continues elongation of the RNA strand until it reaches a terminator sequence **OR** stop codon [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n RNA polymerase **synthesises mRNA** from the **non-coding DNA strand** ($\\checkmark$) by aligning **complementary RNA nucleotides** ($\\checkmark$) along the DNA strand and **catalysing** the formation of **sugar-phosphate bonds** between them ($\\checkmark$). In this way, mRNA is **synthesised** in the **5' to 3' direction** ($\\checkmark$) until a **terminator sequence** is reached ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ only a small portion of DNA is unwound at a time [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA template strand is used for transcription without changes to base sequence **OR** coding strand is not used in transcription [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ rest of the DNA remains in its double-helix structure, stabilised by hydrogen bonds [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA quickly rewinds back into its double-helix form [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ double-helix structure itself provides stability through strong covalent bonds in the sugar-phosphate backbone [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n During transcription, only a **small portion** of the **DNA** is **unwound** at a time reducing the exposure to potential damage ($\\checkmark$. The rest of the molecule remains in its **double-helix structure stabilised** by **hydrogen bonds** ($\\checkmark$ between complementary base pairs.\n\n4.\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ GCA - CCU - GAA - UGC - GGC [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ala **OR** alanine - pro **OR** proline - glu **OR** glutamic acid - cys **OR** cysteine - gly **OR** glycine (*full marks awarded if correct sequence of amino acids achieved without showing steps*) [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/BI1813c.png\" alt=\"BI1813c\">\n\n **Alanine - Proline - Glutamic Acid - Cysteine - Glycine** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ deletion causes a frameshift mutation **OR** alters the reading frame from the point of mutation onwards [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ deletion results in a different sequence of amino acids **OR** different primary structure **OR** would be Alanine - Proline - Asparagine - Alanine **OR** ala - pro - asp - ala - [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ substitution results in a silent mutation **OR** both GAA and GAG code for the same amino acid **OR** glutamic acid [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ no change in amino acid sequence **OR** primary structure **OR** would remain alanine - proline - glutamic acid - cysteine - glycine **OR** ala - pro - glu - cys - glyc [1 mark]\n\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Deletion** of the sixth base causes a **frameshift mutation**, altering the **reading frame** ($\\checkmark$) and resulting in a **different sequence** of **amino acids**, or **primary structure** ($\\checkmark$). In contrast, the **substitution** of the sixth base with G results in a **silent mutation** because both **GAA** and **GAG** code for the same amino acid, **glutamic acid** ($\\checkmark$), so the **primary structure** remains the **same** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2340","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2340\n\nWhich statement best describes the assembly of amino acid chains by ribosomes?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ribosomes translate DNA codons to an amino acid chain by condensation.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ribosomes move along tRNA joining amino acids, forming peptide bonds.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ribosomes move along mRNA joining amino acids, forming peptide bonds.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ribosomes move along tRNA joining amino acids, using condensation reactions.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because ribosomes move along the sequence of codons on mRNA, reading the genetic information to guide the assembly of an amino acid chain. Transfer RNA (tRNA) molecules bring specific amino acids to the ribosome, and these amino acids are joined to the growing chain by peptide bonds.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because ribosomes translate RNA codons (on mRNA), not DNA codons. DNA is transcribed into mRNA before translation.\n\n**Choices B** and **D** are incorrect because ribosomes move along mRNA, not tRNA. tRNA delivers amino acids to the ribosome but is not the template for translation."},{"ref":"BI2394","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2394\n\nWhich of the following allows a eukaryotic gene to code for multiple polypeptides?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Accurate A-T and C-G base pairing\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The presence of introns\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The degenerate code\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Alternative splicing","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because alternative splicing allows transcribed exons to be spliced together in different orders, resulting in multiple polypeptides. These polypeptides can have a range of effects on the organism, so is a selective advantage to that organism. This is one way that a small genome can result in a large proteome.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because accurate base pairing allows for stable DNA structure with relatively few mutations, but does not increase the number of polypeptides that any particular gene can code for.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because introns are removed before splicing takes place; splicing is when different mRNA sequences can be translated into multiple polypeptides.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because the degenerate code applies in the translation of mRNA, in which multiple codons can code for the same amino acid."},{"ref":"BI2636","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2636\n\nWhich explains how alternative splicing affects gene expression?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increases the number of genes in a genome\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Alters the position of the promoter region of a gene\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increases the degeneracy of the genetic code\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Allows different combinations of exons to be included in mRNA","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAlternative splicing is a eukaryotic post-transcriptional process where a single gene can produce multiple mRNA variants due to exons being spliced together in different combinations. The different combination of exons results in the synthesis of different polypeptides from the same gene; **Choice D** is correct.\n\nAlternative splicing does not change the number of genes, it affects how mRNA is processed to produce different proteins from the same gene; **Choice A** is incorrect.\nAlternative splicing is a post-transcriptional process. The promoter region of a gene is a DNA sequence upstream of a gene that acts as a binding site for transcription factors and RNA polymerase to bind to initiate transcription, transcription and alternative splicing are not directly linked; **Choice B** is incorrect. \\\nThe genetic code is classed as degenerate as multiple codons can code for the same amino acid, this affects translation, not post-transcriptional processes; **Choice C** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI2638","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2638\n\nWhat would be the most likely result if the A site in the ribosome was non-functional?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The wrong amino acids would be incorporated into the polypeptide chain\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Only the start codon would be translated\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The rate of translation would be slower\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Translation would not be able to be initiated","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDuring the initiation of translation, the initiator tRNA, which carries the amino acid methionine, binds to the P site of the ribosome. The next tRNA with the complementary anticodon to the next codon binds in the A site, which allows the elongation phase to start. If the A site is non-functional, then no new tRNAs can bind after the initiator tRNA, therefore only the start codon can be translated; **Choice B** is correct.\n\nIf the A site is non-functional, then no new tRNAs can be incorporated into the polypeptide chain after the initiator tRNA; **Choice A** is incorrect.\n\nIf the A site is non-functional, translation would not just slow down, it would stop completely; **Choice C** is incorrect.\n\nThe P site, not the A site, is necessary for the initiation of translation; **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI2641","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2641\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe image shows a eukaryotic pre-mRNA strand and a mature mRNA strand.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/BI2641i.png\" alt=\"BI2641i\">\n\n1.\n 1. State the name of the protein complex that removes introns from pre-mRNA. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Describe the potential consequences to protein structure or function if the complex you named in part (a)(i) is faulty. [2 marks]\n\n2.\n 1. The diagram shows a 5’ cap on the mature mRNA; there is another post-transcriptional modification missing from the diagram. Draw the missing post-transcriptional modification by completing it on the diagram. [1 mark]\n\n 2. State the function of the modification you added in part (b)(i). [1 mark]\n\n3. Explain why prokaryotic mRNA undergoes less post-transcriptional modification than eukaryotic mRNA. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1.\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ spliceosome [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Spliceosome** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n 2. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ if introns are not spliced out **OR** are retained, this may lead to the incorrect amino acid sequence being translated **OR** truncated proteins due to the introduction of premature stop codons **OR** the translation of nonfunctional proteins [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ if exons are removed rather than introns, this may lead to the translation of a truncated polypeptide **OR** a nonfunctional protein **OR** prevent the proper folding of the protein [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ if a splicing error changes the reading frame of the mRNA, this may lead to incorrect protein being translated **OR** the translation of a nonfunctional **OR** detrimental protein **OR** the translation of a truncated protein due to the introduction of a premature stop codon [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ if splicing errors affect integral regions of the protein, then it may lose its ability to perform its function **OR** become misfolded [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ a new allele may be created that confers a selective advantage to the organism [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n If the spliceosome is faulty, then **introns may not be spliced out**, which can lead to the **incorrect amino acid sequence** being **translated** from mRNA ($\\checkmark$). If **exons are removed** from the mRNA, this could result in the translation of a **truncated or non-functional protein** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1.\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ diagram should show a poly-A tail **OR** a sequence of As, after the 3rd exon on the 3’ end of the mature mRNA [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/AnsBi.png\" alt=\"AnsBi\">\n ($\\checkmark$)\n\n 2. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to protect the mRNA from enzymatic degradation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to aid in the transport of mature mRNA from the nucleus to the cytoplasm [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to act as a signal for the end of transcription, so that translation may begin [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The purpose of the poly-A tail is to **protect** the mRNA from **enzymatic degradation** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in prokaryotes, transcription and translation occur simultaneously in the cytoplasm, **THEREFORE** protective post-transcriptional modifications **OR** the addition of a poly-A tail **OR** a 5’ cap are unnecessary [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ most prokaryotic genes do not usually contain introns **THEREFORE** no need for splicing [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic mRNA is often degraded quickly after translation and therefore has a shorter lifespan than eukaryotic mRNA **THEREFORE** post-transcriptional modifications that increase stability are unnecessary [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the 5’ cap and poly-A tail aid in the export of eukaryotic mRNA from the nucleus **HOWEVER** prokaryotes do not have nuclei [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Because **transcription and translation occur simultaneously** in the cytoplasm, so **protective** post-transcriptional modifications are **unnecessary** ($\\checkmark$). Also, prokaryotic genes do **not** usually **contain introns**, therefore there is **no need for splicing** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2642","marks":"3","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2642\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nThe image shows two mRNA transcripts of the cell death regulator, Bcl-extra (Bcl-x). When the longer mRNA transcript (Bcl-xL) is expressed, it promotes cell survival. However, when the shorter transcript (Bcl-xS) is expressed, it promotes cell death.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/BI2642.png\" alt=\"BI2642\">\n[Source: Adapted from Dou, Z., Zhao, D., Chen, X., Xu, C., Jin, X., Zhang, X., Wang, Y., Xie, X., Li, Q., Di, C., & Zhang, H. (2021, June 12). *Aberrant Bcl-X splicing in cancer: From molecular mechanism to Therapeutic Modulation - Journal of Experimental & Clinical Cancer Research*. BioMed Central. Retrieved 5 February 2025 from https://jeccr.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13046-021-02001-w. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\nDiscuss the disadvantages and advantages of the alternative splicing of Bcl-x.    [3 marks]","markScheme":"any **three** of the following:\\\n*Answer must include at least **one** advantage and **one** disadvantage to gain full marks*\n\n**Advantages of alternative splicing of Bcl-x**\\\n$\\ce{ -> }$ allows cells to quickly adapt, maintaining tissue homeostasis **OR** the right balance of cell death and cell division **OR** proliferation e.g. cell death can be triggered in damaged cells or functional cells can be kept alive        [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ Bcl-xS expression can prevent uncontrolled cell division **OR** remove damaged **OR** mutated cells, lowering the risk of cancer        [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ a single gene can produce both forms of Bcl-x, saving genetic space     [1 mark]\n\n**Disadvantages of alternative splicing of Bcl-x**\\\n$\\ce{ -> }$ dysregulation **OR** loss of regulation of splicing can lead to errors in splicing    [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ which may hinder cell death and contribute to cancer development    [1 mark]\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nThe advantages of the alternative splicing of Bcl-x are that it allows cells to **quickly adapt** maintaining **tissue homeostasis** e.g. cell death can be triggered in damaged cells ($\\checkmark$). And a **single gene** can produce **both forms of Bcl-x**, which saves genetic **space** ($\\checkmark$). A disadvantage of the alternative splicing of Bcl-x is that if it is **dysregulated**, this can lead to errors in splicing ($\\checkmark$) which may **prevent cell death** and contribute to **cancer** development ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2814","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2814\n\nA mRNA molecule has the following codon.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/BI2814%2C%20BI2815%20D1.2%20MCQ%20Julia.jpeg.JPEG\" alt=\"BI2814, BI2815 D1.2 MCQ Julia.jpeg\">\n\nWhat is the corresponding DNA code on the sense strand?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ TGA\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ ACU\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ ACT\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ AGT","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe mRNA codon UGA is transcribed from the DNA template strand (also called the antisense strand). Complementary base pairing means that mRNA to DNA:\n\nU (in mRNA) pairs with A (in DNA template)\\\nG (in mRNA) pairs with C (in DNA template)\\\nA (in mRNA) pairs with T (in DNA template)\n\nSo, mRNA UGA corresponds to the DNA template strand bases ACT. Therefore, the sense strand (same as mRNA but with T instead of U) is TGA. **Choice A** is the correct answer.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Protein%20Synthesis/images/BI2814%2C%20BI2815%20D1.2%20MCQ%20Julia.jpeg.JPEG\" alt=\"BI2814, BI2815 D1.2 MCQ Julia.jpeg\">"},{"ref":"BI2815","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2815\n\nWhat is/are the role(s) of RNA polymerase during transcription?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Unwinding the DNA to expose the template strand |\n    | II. | Adding a short RNA primer to start transcription |\n    | III. | Synthesising RNA by adding ribonucleotides to the 5' end |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true. During transcription, RNA polymerase unwinds the DNA double helix as it moves along the gene.\\\n**Statement II** is false. RNA polymerase does not need a primer to initiate transcription, unlike DNA polymerase during DNA replication.\\\n**Statement III** is false. RNA polymerase adds ribonucleotides to the 3' end of the growing RNA strand, not the 5' end. RNA synthesis always proceeds in the 5' $\\rarr$ 3' direction, meaning new nucleotides are attached to the 3' end.\n\n**Choice A** is, therefore,  the correct answer because it only includes the true **Statement I**.\\\n**Choices B, C** and **D** are incorrect as they include one or more of the false **Statements II** and **III**."},{"ref":"BI2968","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2968\n\nHow is complementary base pairing essential for accurate transcription of genetic information?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ It ensures both DNA strands are transcribed at the same time.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ It minimises mutations by preventing mismatched bases during replication\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ It strengthens the hydrogen bonds between DNA strands during transcription.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ It allows RNA nucleotides to form complementary pairs with the DNA template strand.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because during transcription, complementary base pairing ensures that RNA nucleotides (A, U, C, G) are matched correctly with the DNA template strand (T, A, G, C), producing an accurate mRNA copy of the gene.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because only one DNA strand (the template strand or antisense strand) is used for transcription; the other strand is non-coding. Complementary base pairing does not involve both strands being transcribed.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because this refers to DNA replication, not transcription. While complementary base pairing does contribute to replication accuracy, transcription errors affect only RNA and do not cause mutations in DNA.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because hydrogen bonds between DNA strands are broken, not strengthened, during transcription, so RNA polymerase can access the template strand; complementary base pairing occurs between RNA and DNA bases, not between the two DNA strands."}]},{"name":"Reproduction","questions":[{"ref":"BI0483","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0483\n\nWhich of these is **not** an effect of testosterone?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Development of testes\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Development of embryonic gonads into testes\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Sperm production\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Development of secondary male characteristics","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because a protein called testis determining factor (TDF) stimulates the development of embryonic gonads as testes. Testosterone causes the development of the testes (**Choice A**), stimulates sperm production (**Choice C**) and stimulates the development of secondary male characteristics (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0515","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0515\n\nWhich hormone is released by the ovary and stimulates the release of the hormone that triggers ovulation?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ FSH\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Oestradiol\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ LH\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Progesterone","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDuring the menstrual cycle, one follicle in the ovaries becomes the dominant follicle. This follicle releases the hormone oestradiol. As the follicle continues to develop, it secretes more oestradiol, which leads to an increase in its concentration in the bloodstream. The high levels of oestradiol stimulate the pituitary gland to release luteinising hormone (LH). The surge in LH is responsible for triggering ovulation, the release of the egg from the ovary. **Choice B** is the correct answer.\n\n**Choice A** s incorrect because follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) is released by the pituitary gland and stimulates the development of ovarian follicles, but it does not trigger ovulation.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because luteinising hormone (LH) is the hormone released by the pituitary that triggers ovulation, but it is stimulated by the increased levels of oestradiol.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because progesterone is produced after ovulation by the corpus luteum and plays a role in preparing the uterus for pregnancy. It inhibits the release of LH and FSH to prevent further ovulation after the egg has been released."},{"ref":"BI0523","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0523\n\nWhich seed structures are represented by the labels X, Y, and Z?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/BI0523ii.png\" alt=\"BI0523ii\">\n\n| | X | Y | Z |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Plumule (embryonic shoot) | Radicle (embryonic root) | Testa (seed coat) |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Radicle (embryonic root) | Plumule (embryonic shoot) | Cotyledon (first leaves)|\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Testa (seed coat) | Plumule (embryonic shoot) | Radicle (embryonic root) |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Testa (seed coat) | Radicle (embryonic root) | Cotyledon (first leaves)|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe radicle is the first part of a seedling to emerge from the seed during germination. The radicle will become the root of the plant. The radicle is labelled by X in the diagram. The plumule is the seedling stem and will give rise to the leaves. The plumule is labelled by Y in the diagram. The cotyledon is the seed leaf within the embryo of a seed. Cotyledons supply the nutrition a plant embryo needs to germinate. They make up a significant portion of the seed. The cotyledon is labelled by Z in the diagram. **Choice B** is, therefore, the correct answer.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/BI0523ms.png\" alt=\"BI0523ms\">"},{"ref":"BI0530","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0530\n\nThe diagram shows the transfer of a substance from maternal blood to foetal blood through the placenta. Which substance is being transferred?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/BI0530i.png\" alt=\"BI0530i\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Palmeira, P., Quinello, C., Silveira-Lessa, A.L., Zago, C.A., & Carneiro-Sampaio, M.. (2012). *IgG placental transfer in healthy and pathological pregnancies*. Clin Dev Immunol. 2012;2012:985646. Retrieved March 10, 2022, from https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22235228/. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Water\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Antibodies\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Glucose\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Electrolytes","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe diagram shows the transfer of a substance across the placenta by endocytosis. The substance from the maternal blood is transported into cells of the placenta through the invagination of the cell membrane and subsequent formation of a vesicle (endosome), which then empties its contents into the foetal blood. Of the answer choices, only antibodies require transport via endocytosis to reach foetal blood.\n\nWater (**Choice A**) crosses the placenta through osmosis. Glucose (**Choice C**) crosses the placenta by facilitated diffusion through glucose transporters. Electrolytes (**Choice D**) are transported across the placenta by facilitated diffusion or active transport via carrier proteins."},{"ref":"BI0558","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0558\n\nThe diagram shows a frontal view of the female reproductive organs.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/BI0558i.png\" alt=\"BI0558i\">\n\nWhat structures are labeled by the numbers I, II, and III?\n\n| | I | II | III|\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | ovary | uterus | cervix |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | fallopian tube | uterus | vagina|\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | ovary | cervix | vagina |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | fallopian tube | cervix | cervix |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe structure labelled I is the fallopian tube. It transports the egg from the ovary to the uterus and is the site of fertilisation.\n\nThe structure labelled II is the uterus. It is where the fertilised egg implants and the embryo develops during pregnancy.\n\nThe structure labelled III is the vagina. It serves as the passage through which sperm enters the female reproductive system and also serves as the birth canal during delivery.\n\n**Choice B** is therefore the correct answer.\n\nThe cervix, which is located between the vagina and the uterus, is not labelled in this diagram. Therefore, answer **Choices A, C**, and **D** are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0659","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0659\n\nHome pregnancy tests test for hCG in urine. Which of these binds to hCG?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Monoclonal antibodies\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Hybridoma cells\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Progesterone\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Oxytocin","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because monoclonal antibodies in home pregnancy tests bind with hCG. **Choice B** is incorrect because hybridoma cells produce the monoclonal antibodies used in home pregnancy tests. **Choices C** and **D** are incorrect because the monoclonal antibodies in home pregnancy tests do not bind with progesterone or oxytocin."},{"ref":"BI0679","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0679\n\nWhich structure in a seed contains a food store?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Radicle\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Plumule\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Testa\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Cotyledon","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because food is stored in the cotyledon(s). **Choice A** is incorrect because the function of the radicle is to develop into the root of the seedling. **Choice B** is incorrect because the function of the plumule is to develop into the shoot of the seedling. **Choice C** is incorrect because the testa is the outer covering of the seed."},{"ref":"BI0681","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0681\n\nWhich statement is the correct definition of pollination?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The fusion of a male and female gamete\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The transfer of pollen from an anther to a stigma\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ The transfer of seeds away from the parent plant\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The transfer of pollen by a pollinator","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because pollination is the transfer of pollen from an anther to a stigma. **Choice A** is incorrect because the fusion of a male and female gamete is fertilization. **Choice C** is incorrect because the transfer of seeds away from the parent plant is dispersal. **Choice D** is incorrect because pollination can occur without a pollinator.  Some plants are wind-pollinated."},{"ref":"BI0682","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0682\n\nWhich statement is the correct definition for seed dispersal?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$  The fusion of a male and female gamete\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$  The transfer of pollen from an anther to a stigma\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$  The transfer of seeds away from the parent plant\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$  The transfer of pollen by a pollinator","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because the transfer of seeds away from the parent plant is dispersal. **Choice A** is incorrect because the fusion of a male and female gamete is fertilization. **Choice B** is incorrect because the transfer of pollen from an anther to a stigma is pollination. **Choice D** is incorrect because pollen transfer involves the movement of pollen, not seeds."},{"ref":"BI0683","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0683\n\nFlowers produce male gametes, known as pollen, and female gametes, called ova. The diagram below shows an animal-pollinated flower.\n\nWhich row correctly matches the gametes with the structure that produces them?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/BI0683.png\" alt=\"BI0683\">\n\n| | Pollen | Ovum |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Anther | Ovule |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Anther | Stigma |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Filament | Ovule |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Filament | Style |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because pollen is produced by the anther and the ovum (plural: ova) is produced by the ovule, found inside the ovary. **Choice B** is incorrect because the stigma is where pollen lands. **Choice C** is incorrect because the filament supports the anther and does not produce pollen. **Choice D** is incorrect because the filament supports the anther and the style is the part of the flower that the pollen passes through."},{"ref":"BI0684","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0684\n\nWhich row correctly describes the structure of insect-pollinated flowers?\n\n| | Petal | Stigma and anther | Nectary |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| Green | Outside flower | Absent |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Green | Inside flower | Absent |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Colourful | Inside flower | Present |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Colourful | Outside flower | Present |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because insect pollinated flowers have colourful petals with the stigma and anther inside the flower and have a nectary. **Choice A** is incorrect because insect-pollinated flowers have colourful petals with the stigma and anther inside the flower and have a nectary. **Choice B** is incorrect because insect-pollinated flowers have colourful flowers and have a nectary. **Choice D** is incorrect because insect-pollinated flowers have the stigma and anther inside the flower."},{"ref":"BI0687","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0687\n\nWhich of these is **not** part of plant reproduction?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Pollination\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Fertilisation\n\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Seed dispersal\n\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Transpiration","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because transpiration is the loss of water from the stomata of a plant, it is not a stage of plant reproduction. **Choices A**, **B** and **C** are incorrect because pollination, fertilisation and seed dispersal are all stages of plant reproduction."},{"ref":"BI0688","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0688\n\nIn which location does fertilisation take place?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/Untitled%204.png\" alt=\"Untitled 4\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because fertilisation takes place in the ovule. **Choice A** is incorrect because although pollen lands on the stigma, fertilisation occurs in the ovule. **Choice C** is incorrect because the anther produces pollen. **Choice D** is incorrect because the nucleus of the pollen just travels through the style in order to arrive at the ovule, where fertilisation takes place."},{"ref":"BI0689","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0689\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe diagram below shows an animal-pollinated flower.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/BI0689.png\" alt=\"BI0689\">\n\n1. Identify the processes that occur in locations I and II. [2 marks]\n\n2. Sexual reproduction in flowers produces seeds. Outline the process of germination in seeds. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ location I: meiosis or pollen formation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ location II: meiosis or fertilisation [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n**Meiosis** occurs at location I ($\\checkmark$). **Fertilisation** happens at location II ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following features clearly drawn with a label line in contact with each part:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ imbibition occurs **OR** water is absorbed by the seed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ seed coat **OR** testa ruptures due to swelling of seed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ metabolic activity is reactivated [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ water uptake brings about gibberellin production [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ gibberellins stimulate production of enzymes **OR** amylase [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ amylase hydrolyses **OR** digests stored starch **OR** enzymes digest food stores [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ soluble products **OR** glucose **OR** maltose **OR** sugars **OR** amino acids transported to embryo [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ radicle **OR** embryonic root emerges before plumule **OR** plumule **OR** embryonic shoot grows after the radicle [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mitosis occurs in meristematic regions of the embryo for growth [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ energy for growth comes from aerobic respiration of the mobilised food reserves [1 mark]\n\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n **Water uptake** by the seed (**imbibition**) initiates germination and **reactivates metabolic processes** ($\\checkmark$). **Gibberellins** produced in the embryo **stimulate** the **enzyme synthesis**, including **amylase** ($\\checkmark$). Amylase **breaks down starch** into **sugars** ($\\checkmark$), which are used in **aerobic respiration** to release **energy** for **growth** ($\\checkmark$). The **radicle grows first**, forming the root, followed by the **plumule**, which develops into the shoot ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0690","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0690\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\n1.    List the two types of gametes in flowering plants.    [2 marks]\n\n2.    Outline the role of pollinators in plant reproduction.    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.    Both of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pollen (male gamete) [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ovum **OR** ova (female gamete) [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n**Pollen** ($\\checkmark$) and **ova** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.    Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  to transfer the male gamete to the female part of a flower [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  collect pollen from the anther of insect-pollinated flowers [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  bring pollen to the stigma of the flower [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  for fertilisation [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nPollinators are needed for **fertilisation** ($\\checkmark$) in animal-pollinated flowers, as pollinators transfer the **male gamete** to the **female part** of the flower ($\\checkmark$). Pollinators collect pollen from the **anther** ($\\checkmark$) and transfer it to the **stigma** ($\\checkmark$) of insect-pollinated flowers."},{"ref":"BI0718","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0718\n\nWhat processes are common to both oogenesis and spermatogenesis?\n\n| | Type of cell division | Change in cell size | Metabolic process |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Mitosis | Increase in size | Differentiation |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Meiosis | Decrease in size | Apoptosis |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Binary fission | No change | Generation of a polar body |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Budding | Increase in size | Fertilization |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nBoth oogenesis and spermatogenesis involve mitosis, increase in cell size (cell growth), two meiotic divisions and differentiation. **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect as it includes both apoptosis (cell death) and decrease in size, which does not occur in either process. **Choice C** is incorrect as binary fission occurs in prokaryotic cells and the generation of a polar body only occurs in oogenesis. **Choice D** is incorrect as budding only occurs in fungi, and fertilization (when mature sperm and egg cells fuse) occurs after both oogenesis and spermatogenesis."},{"ref":"BI0719","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0719\n\nThe micrograph shows spermatogenesis inside a mammalian seminiferous tubule. Which arrow indicates a spermatocyte?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/BI0719.png\" alt=\"BI0719\">\n[Source: Adapted from Thomas, D. (2019, October 23). *RED07230 Mammalian spermatogenesis*. Flickr. Retrieved August 2, 2022, from https://www.flickr.com/photos/davidjthomas/48948937222/in/photostream/ Copyright free]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nArrow C is pointing to a round cell with a large nucleus, that is closer to the middle of the tubule than the basement membrane. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as it is pointing to the space between the cells (interstitial cells). **Choice B** is incorrect as it is pointing to spermatids, as indicated by the presence of tails and smaller nuclei. **Choice D** is incorrect as it is pointing to the basement membrane, the membrane surrounding the seminiferous tubules."},{"ref":"BI0720","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0720\n\nThe micrograph shows a sperm cell. Which part contains enzymes to break down the zona pellucida of the egg cell?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/BI0720.png\" alt=\"BI0720\">\n[Adapted from: Source Bobjgalindo. (2005, March 15). *File: sperm stained.JPG - Wikimedia Commons*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved August 2, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Sperm_stained.JPG   Copyright under CC BY 4.0]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nArrow A is pointing to the acrosome, which is found at the front of the sperm head. The acrosome contains enzymes that break down the zona pellucida around the egg. **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choice B** is pointing to the mid-piece, the site of the mitochondria which produce ATP to power the movement of the cell. **Choice C** is pointing to the nucleus, which contains the genetic information. **Choice D** is pointing to the tail of the sperm cell which propells the cell forward."},{"ref":"BI0721","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0721\n\nThe micrograph shows an oocyte after the first meiotic division. This results in the formation of one large cell and one small cell.  What is the fate of the large cell?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/BI0721.png\" alt=\"BI0721\">\n\n[Adapted from: Source Lillie, F. R., Moore, C. R., & Redfield, A. C. (2015, August 7). *Image from page 254 of \"The biological bulletin\"*. Flickr. Retrieved August 2, 2022, from https://www.flickr.com/photos/internetarchivebookimages/20385682781 Copyright free]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ It degenerates and dies.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ It undergoes mitosis.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ It secretes the hormone human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG).\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ It becomes a gamete.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe small cell is the polar body and the large cell is the primary oocyte which will divide by meiosis again and become the gamete. **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as the small cell (polar body) degenerates and dies, not the large cell. **Choice B** is incorrect as the large cell divides by meiosis, not mitosis. **Choice C** is incorrect, as hCG is a hormone that is secreted by the fertilized embryo."},{"ref":"BI0723","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0723\n\nWhich is the correct order for the stages in spermatogenesis?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Meiosis → Mitosis →  Cell growth → Differentiation\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mitosis → Differentiation → Cell growth → Meiosis\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mitosis → Cell growth → Meiosis→ Differentiation\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cell growth → Meiosis → Mitosis →  Differentiation","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDuring spermatogenesis, firstly the germinal epithelium cells divide via mitosis, then these cells grow larger to produce primary spermatocytes, then they carry out two rounds of meiosis to produce secondary spermatocytes and spermatids, respectively. Finally, the spermatids differentiate into mature sperm cells. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **D** are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0724","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0724\n\nWhat is the purpose of the cortical reaction?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ To allow the fertilised egg to implant properly.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ To prevent polyspermy.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ To allow fertilisation to take place.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ To trigger oogenesis.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDuring the cortical reaction, vesicles (cortical granules) near the membrane of the egg release enzymes that make the zona pellucida hard and prevent more than one sperm (polyspermy) from fertilising the egg. **Choice B**  is correct.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D** do not describe the purpose of the cortical reactions. **Choices A, C** and **D** are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0725","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0725\n\nWhich of the following is an advantage of external fertilization?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Predators will consume gametes.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Extreme changes in temperature will affect gametes.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Greater genetic variation in the offspring.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Lower chance of gametes coming in contact for fertilisation.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nExternal fertilisation often bypasses the selection of the mates. This, and a greater variety of the gametes (because of the increased number), leads to the greater genetic variation of the offspring as compared to offspring of organisms following the internal fertilisation strategy. **Choice C**  is correct.\n\n**Choices A, B** and  **D** are all disadvantages of external fertilisation. **Choices A, B** and  **D** are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0726","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0726\n\nWhich two hormones are involved in a positive feedback loop to promote muscle contractions during childbirth?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Oxytocin and estrogen\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Oestradiol and progesterone\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Human chorionic gonadotropin and oxytocin\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Progesterone and oxytocin","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDuring the last third of pregnancy, progesterone levels fall, resulting in the rise of oxytocin levels. There is also a rise in oestradiol levels which increases the number of oxytocin receptors on the muscle cells in the uterine wall. When oxytocin binds to these receptors, the muscle contracts, and the contraction results in the secretion of more oxytocin. This is positive feedback. **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choices B** and **D** are incorrect, as high progesterone levels prevent uterine contractions. **Choice C**  is incorrect because human chorionic gonadotropin stimulates the ovary to produce progesterone during early pregnancy and is at lower levels during later pregnancy."},{"ref":"BI0727","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0727\n\nThe graph shows the level of hormones in a woman's body during pregnancy. Which line on the graph represents the level of human chorionic gonadotropin?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/BI0727.png\" alt=\"BI0727\">\n[Adapted from: Source Openstax college. (2013, June 19). *File:2919 hormones initiating Labor-02.jpg - Wikimedia Commons. Wikimedia commons*. Retrieved August 2, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2919_Hormones_Initiating_Labor-02.jpg Copyright under CC BY 3.0]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDuring pregnancy, the levels of human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) increase to maintain the corpus luteum in the ovary, so that estrogen and progesterone are secreted to maintain the uterus lining and prevent ovulation. Once the placenta takes over the support of the embryo, hCG levels fall. **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as it represents estrogen which rises towards the end of pregnancy to promote contractions. **Choice B** is incorrect as it represents progesterone which decreases towards the end of pregnancy to promote contractions. **Choice C** is incorrect, as the hormones involved in pregnancy all fluctuate and do not remain stable."},{"ref":"BI0728","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0728\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nBelow is a diagram of the internal reproductive structure of a woman during pregnancy.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/BI0728.png\" alt=\"BI0728\">\n[Adapted from Source Unknown. (2005, March 15). *The anatomy of the human gravid uterus: Foetus in utero*. Look and Learn History Picture Library. Retrieved August 2, 2022, from https://www.lookandlearn.com/history-images/YW004302L/The-anatomy-of-the-human-gravid-uterus-foetus-in-utero. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n1.\n    1. Label the placenta using the letter **P**.         [1 mark]\n\n    2. Explain how the structure of the placenta is related to its function. [3 marks]\n\n    3. Suggest an outcome if the placenta does not produce enough of the correct hormones during pregnancy.      [1 mark]\n\n2. Outline the role of progesterone in pregnancy.      [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ an arrow labelled with the letter P, pointing to the placenta     [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/bi0728c.png\" alt=\"bi0728c\">\n\n1.\n    2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ villi to provide a large surface area for the exchange of material between mother and child **OR** for exchange of named substances between mother and foetus [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ large surface area for exchange of named substances between mother and foetus (direction must be stated) [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ it is an endocrine gland that releases pregnancy hormones, **OR** it releases hCG, estrogen **AND** progesterone, to maintain the pregnancy     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ has a large supply of blood vessels **OR** capillaries, to allow for the rapid exchange of materials between mother and child **OR** to maintain a steep diffusion gradient [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ the placenta barrier, **OR** membrane, between mother's and foetus' blood is thin to allow for rapid diffusion [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ mothers and foetus' blood do not mix **OR** mothers and foetus' blood are separated by placental barrier **OR** membrane, to prevent immunological response if mothers and foetus' blood groups are different     [1 mark]\n[3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The placenta is the primary physical connection between mother and child; it has **villi that provide a large surface area**  for the **exchange of material** ($\\checkmark$) such as **oxygen and nutrients** from the mother to the fetus ($\\checkmark$). It also releases **oestrogen and progesterone** to **maintain the pregnancy** and prevent premature contractions ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ miscarriage [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$  premature labour due to lack of progesterone         [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    If the placenta does not produce enough of the correct hormones during pregnancy, this can result in **miscarriage** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ maintains the endometrium     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ to allow for successful implantation of the embryo [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ inhibits the secretion of the hormone oxytocin [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ which prevents early contractions [1 mark]\n[3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Progesterone **maintains the endometrial lining** ($\\checkmark$) in the uterus to allow for the successful **implantation of the embryo** ($\\checkmark$).  It also **inhibits the secretion of the hormone oxytocin** ($\\checkmark$), thereby **preventing early contractions** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0729","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0729\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nDescribe how the egg cell protects itself from polyspermy. [3 marks]","markScheme":"Any **three** of the following:\n\n$\\ce{->}$ polyspermy is when more than one sperm fertilizes an egg     [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{->}$ when the sperm fuses with the egg, the egg becomes activated [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{->}$ cortical granules **OR** vesicles near the egg membrane release enzymes     [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{->}$ enzymes digest binding proteins  [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{->}$ zona pellucida hardens [1 mark]\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nPolyspermy is when **more than one sperm fertilises an egg** ($\\checkmark$). When the sperm fuses with the egg, the **egg becomes activated** ($\\checkmark$), so that **cortical granules release enzymes** ($\\checkmark$) which **digest binding proteins** ($\\checkmark$) and also result in the **hardening of the zona pellucida** ($\\checkmark$), which prevents any more sperm from fusing with the egg."},{"ref":"BI0730","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0730\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the adaptations of sperm cells.      [4 marks]\n\n2. Describe how positive feedback results in contractions during pregnancy.         [4 marks]\n\n3. Discuss the importance of cell division in pregnancy. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a flagellum **OR** tail enables the sperm cell to swim towards the egg [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ numerous mitochondria in the midpiece produce ATP to provide energy for movement [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ an acrosome contains enzymes that help the sperm penetrate the outer layers surrounding the egg [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a haploid nucleus contains one set of chromosomes, allowing the diploid chromosome number to be restored at fertilisation [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a streamlined shape **OR** reduced cytoplasm reduces resistance during movement through fluid [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ receptors on the sperm cell membrane enable recognition of **OR** binding to the egg [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Sperm cells have **a flagellum that enables them to swim towards the egg** ($\\checkmark$). They contain **numerous mitochondria in the midpiece that produce ATP to provide energy for movement** ($\\checkmark$). **The acrosome contains enzymes that help the sperm penetrate the outer layers surrounding the egg** ($\\checkmark$). Sperm cells also have **a haploid nucleus containing one set of chromosomes, allowing the diploid chromosome number to be restored at fertilisation** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ positive feedback acts to increase an effect    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxytocin stimulates contractions in the uterus **OR** myometrium     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the contractions are detected by stretch receptors  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ signal to the pituitary gland to release more oxytocin    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ more oxytocin makes the contractions more vigorous and frequent     [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    **Positive feedback acts to increase an effect** ($\\checkmark$). Towards the end of pregnancy, levels of progesterone fall and levels of oxytocin increase. **Oxytocin stimulates contractions in the myometrium** ($\\checkmark$), which is detected by **stretch receptors** ($\\checkmark$), this sends a signal to the **pituitary gland to release more oxytocin** ($\\checkmark$), which makes the **contractions even more vigorous** and frequent ($\\checkmark$), causing even more oxytocin secretion, allowing the baby to be born more rapidly.\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ occurs during oogenesis in the ovaries **OR** spermatogenesis in the testes       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mitosis occurs first, allowing spermatocytes **OR** oocytes to be made       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ precursor to two rounds of meiosis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allowing mature gametes to be made  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ without mature gametes, there would be no fertilisation **OR** continuation of life [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ post fertilisation, the zygote divides many times via mitosis   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ forming pluripotent cells with high differentiation potential  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if extracted at this point, they can be used for stem cell treatments or research     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if they continue to divide via mitosis, a blastocyst will eventually form [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ outer layer of blastocyst implants into the endometrium [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ outer layer of blastocyst will eventually differentiate into the placenta, which allows for exchange of materials between mother and foetus [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allowing the foetus to grow and develop     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ inner part of blastocyst will eventually differentiate into the foetus     [1 mark]\n[8 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Cell division occurs during both **oogenesis and spermatogenesis** ($\\checkmark$), in the ovaries and testes, respectively. **Mitosis occurs first** ($\\checkmark$), allowing spermatocytes or oocytes to be made, next the cells undergo **two rounds of meiosis** ($\\checkmark$), which allows **mature egg and sperm cells to be made** ($\\checkmark$). Without the mature gametes, **there would be no fertilisation** ($\\checkmark$). **Post fertilisation**, the zygote (first cell formed after fertilisation) **divides many times via mitosis** ($\\checkmark$) forming identical **pluripotent cells** with the ability to differentiate into most cell types ($\\checkmark$), if these cells are extracted at this point, they can be used for **stem cell treatments** ($\\checkmark$) such as treating Stargardt disease. If the cells are not extracted, at this point they will continue to divide via **mitosis**, until a **blastocyst** ($\\checkmark$) (a hollow ball of cells)  is formed. The **outer layer** of the blastocyst will **implant into the endometrium** ($\\checkmark$) and will eventually **differentiate into the placenta** which allows for **exchange of materials** ($\\checkmark$) such as nutrients, waste and oxygen between the mother and child, **sustaining the foetus’s life** ($\\checkmark$). The **inner part of the blastocyst will differentiate into the foetus** ($\\checkmark$) which will eventually develop into a child."},{"ref":"BI0802","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0802\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/BI0802A.png\" alt=\"BI0802A\">\n\nThe following results were obtained when a researcher investigated the effect of temperature and pH on the germination of black-eyed peas. He placed the seeds in petri dishes with some cotton wool spread out evenly at the bottom. He then added water every day and monitored the growth by counting the seeds that had germinated.\n\nThe results below on the left show the cumulative percentage of seeds that germinated over a 28 day period. Different temperatures (5, 10, 15, 20 and 25 :sup[o]C) were investigated by placing the petri dishes in ovens at the appropriate temperature.\nAgain, pH was investigated by adding water (with pH adjusted to 2, 3.5, 5, 7, 9, 11 and 12.5) as shown in the results.\n\nThe results on the right show the final percentage of seeds that germinated at the different temperatures and pH.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/BI0802B.png\" alt=\"BI0802B\">\n[Source:Adapted from Cherrate, Mustapha, et al.( Mar,2023) *“Effects of Temperature, Ph, and Salinity on Seed Germination of Acinos Alpinus Subsp*. Meridionalis and FTIR Analysis of Molecular Composition Changes.” MDPI, Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute, Retrieved May 5, 2023, from https://www.mdpi.com/2071-1050/15/6/4793, Copyright under CC0 4.0]\n\nThe percentage germination rate was calculated as follows, for the days the experiment was monitored.\n\nGermination rate = (number of seeds that germinated / total number of seeds) x 100 %\n\nA student wanted to set up an experiment to check the effect of salinity on germination and plant growth, using the data from above.\n\n1. Based on the results shown,\n 1. State the optimum temperature for germination of black-eyed peas. [1 mark]\n\n 2. State the optimum pH for growth of black-eyed peas. [1 mark]\n\n2. As an extension of the studies shown above, state what the student would\nneed to replace the pH solutions with if they wanted to investigate the effect of salinity on the germination of peas? [1 mark]\n\n3. Other than pH and temperature, identify two controlled variables for setting up the experiment [2 marks]\n\n4. Other than germination rate, state two other dependent variables that could be monitored to see the effect of salinity on the growth of black-eyed peas. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 15 :sup[o]C [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The optimum temperature for the germination of black eyed peas is **15 :sup[o]C** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ pH7 [1 mark]\n\n (6.75 to 7.25 acceptable)\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The optimum pH for the germination of black eyed peas is **pH 7** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ by making equal volumes of aqueous solutions containing different masses of salt (NaCl) and adding this to the different petri dishes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ adding water to the control experiment, and adding at least five different concentrations of saline solutions to subsequent petri dishes [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The student can set up different petri dishes with the same numbers of black eyed peas. The student can then add water to the control plate, and **five different concentrations of saline solutions** ($\\checkmark$) to the other petri dishes.\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ adding equal volumes of water and salt solutions (regularly) to all the petri dishes on a daily basis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ counting the germinated seeds for all the petri dishes at the same time of day [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ all the petri dishes are so placed that they receive the same light intensity on a daily basis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ number of seeds placed in the petri dishes **OR** placed same distance apart [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sizes of the petri dishes are the same [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ same mass of cotton wool [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ peas from the same source **OR** stored in similar conditions **OR** same age [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ accept any other valid control [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Other than pH and temperature, controlled variables are **the number of seeds** ($\\checkmark$) placed in the petri dishes, and making sure **the germinated seeds are counted for all the petri dishes at the same time** ($\\checkmark$) on a daily basis.\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fresh **OR** dry weight (of plants at end of experiment) [1]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ shoot length [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ root length [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ shoot to root ratio [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ number of root hairs [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ shoot **OR** root, width [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Other than germination rate, two dependent variables that could be measured are **shoot length** ($\\checkmark$) and **root length** ($\\checkmark$) at the end of the experiment."},{"ref":"BI0803","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0803\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nTo understand the effects of abiotic stress, investigations were carried out using barley seeds. The researchers wanted to select strains tolerant to drought. Petri dishes were set up with some cotton wool spread out evenly at the bottom and the barley seeds placed on top, as shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/BI0803.png\" alt=\"BI0803\">\n\nTo simulate the effects of drought, polyethylene glycol (PEG) was added at a concentration of 20% to experimental petri dishes while distilled water (without PEG) was added to control petri dishes.\n\nGermination percentage was calculated as follows:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/BI0803A.png\" alt=\"BI0803A\">\n\nwhere ‘n’ is number of seeds germinated on the final day and ‘N’ is the total number of seeds.\n\nGermination pace is expressed as GP\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/BI0803B.png\" alt=\"BI0803B\">\n\nWhere **N** is the total number of germinated seeds at the end of the experiment and n is the number of germinated seeds on day **g** (1, 2, 3..).\n\nThe results obtained are shown below:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/BI0803C.png\" alt=\"BI0803C\">\n[Source: Adapted from Thabet, Samar G., et al.(Nov, 2018) *Genetic Basis of Drought Tolerance during Seed Germination in Barley*.PLOS ONE, PLoS ONE 13(11):e0206682.Retrieved May 5, 2023, from https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0206682 Copyright under CC0 4.0]\n\n1. Based on the results shown, explain how a drought is likely to affect a crop such as barley. [2 marks]\n\n2. State another abiotic factor, apart from water, that is required for germination of seeds. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the germination rate of the barley crop will decrease overall [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the percentage of seeds that germinate is reduced to approximately 80% [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the pace at which the seeds germinate will decrease [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ delay in the time taken by the seeds to germinate will delay growth of the crop [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **germination rate will decrease** ($\\checkmark$), meaning fewer seeds will germinate than when there is no drought and there will be a **delay in the time taken** ($\\checkmark$) for the seeds to germinate during a drought period.\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ suitable temperature **OR** warmth [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ suitable pH [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Another abiotic factor required for seed germination is **oxygen** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1061","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1061\n\nWhich statement(s) is/are correct for the hormones of the menstrual cycle?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Progesterone inhibits FSH. |\n    | II. | There is positive feedback between oestradiol and FSH. |\n    | III. | There is positive feedback between progesterone and LH. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nProgesterone inhibits the secretion of FSH (follicle-stimulating hormone) by negative feedback. This mechanism prevents the development of additional follicles during the luteal phase when progesterone levels are high; **Statement I** is true. During the follicular phase, moderate levels of oestradiol inhibit FSH secretion via negative feedback. However, when oestradiol levels peak (just before ovulation), they stimulate a surge in FSH secretion via positive feedback, which helps trigger ovulation; **Statement II** is true.\n\n**Choice C** is therefore correct because **Statements I** and **II** are true.\n\nProgesterone does not have positive feedback on LH (luteinizing hormone). Instead, high levels of progesterone inhibit LH secretion through negative feedback. This inhibition occurs during the luteal phase to prevent a second ovulation and maintain the uterine lining; **Statement III** is not true.\n\n**Choices A** and **B** are not correct as they are missing one true statement.\n**Choice D** is not correct as it includes the untrue **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI1062","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1062\n\nWhich row shows the correct functions for follicle stimulating hormone (FSH), luteinizing hormone (LH) and progesterone?\n\n| | FSH | LH | Progesterone |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Stimulates development of follicles | Stimulates ovulation | Maintains endometrium |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Stimulates development of follicles | Stimulates ovulation | Repairs endometrium |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Stimulates the release of progesterone | Stimulates release of oestradiol | Maintains endometrium |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Stimulates release of egg | Stimulates release of oestradiol | Repairs endometrium |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because FSH stimulates the development of follicles, LH stimulates ovulation and progesterone maintains the endometrium. **Choice B** is incorrect because progesterone does not cause the repair of the endometrium. **Choice C** is incorrect because FSH does not stimulate the production of progesterone and LH does not stimulate the release of oestradiol. **Choice D** is incorrect because FSH does not stimulate the release of an egg, LH does not stimulate the release of oestradiol, and progesterone does not repair the endometrium."},{"ref":"BI1063","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1063\n\nWhich pair of hormones are produced by the gland labelled X?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/BI1063QB.png\" alt=\"BI1063QB\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ FSH and oestradiol\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ FSH and progesterone\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Oestradiol and progesterone\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ FSH and LH","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because gland X is the pituitary gland and FSH and LH are produced by the anterior pituitary gland whereas progesterone and oestradiol are produced by the ovary (**Choice A, B,** and **C**)."},{"ref":"BI1064","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1064\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline how the sperm is adapted for its role of fertilising the egg. [4 marks]\n\n2. Describe what happens when a sperm reaches the egg in the oviduct to when fertilisation is complete.    [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain the role of hormones in the control of the ovarian and uterine cycles.     [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ haploid nucleus **OR**  nucleus contains half of the genetic material to fuse with nucleus of egg during fertilisation [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ acrosome contains enzymes that help penetrate the egg        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ midpiece packed with mitochondria **OR** lots of mitochondria to provide energy for movement [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tail **OR** flagellum propels the sperm towards the egg    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ streamlined shape **OR** very small to reduce resistance as it swims     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chemotaxis **OR** can detect chemical signals released by the egg for navigation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ short lifespan ensures only most viable sperm have a chance to fertilise egg    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The sperm is very **small** and **streamlined** to **reduce** the **resistance** as it swims ($\\checkmark$) through fluids. It has a **flagellum** to **propel** itself forward ($\\checkmark$) and **many mitochondria** in the midpiece to provide the **energy**  ($\\checkmark$) for swimming movements. The **acrosome** contains digestive **enzymes** to help the sperm **penetrate** the protective outer **layers of the egg** ($\\checkmark$) and a **haploid nucleus** ($\\checkmark$) to provide half the genetic material during fertilisation.\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enzymes in sperm head help penetrate plasma membrane **OR** zona pellucida of egg    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sperm plasma membrane fuses with egg plasma membrane    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles released from egg break down sperm flagellum **OR** mid-piece    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sperm haploid nucleus enters egg    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ haploid nucleus of sperm forms pronucleus    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA in pronucleus of egg and pronucleus of sperm **OR** pronuclei replicates    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pronuclei fuse **OR** nucleus of the sperm fuses with the nucleus of the egg    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ forms diploid zygote **OR** zygote contains complete set of chromosomes     [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    When the sperm reaches the egg, it releases **enzymes** from the **acrosome** to help **penetrate** the egg’s cell **membrane** ($\\checkmark$). The plasma **membrane** of the head of the **sperm fuses** with the plasma **membrane** of the **egg** ($\\checkmark$), and the **haploid nucleus enters** the egg’s cytoplasm ($\\checkmark$). **Vesicles** released from the egg **destroy** the sperm’s **flagellum** and **midpiece** ($\\checkmark$). The sperm’s nucleus **forms** a **pronucleus** ($\\checkmark$). The **DNA** in the pronucleus of sperm and pronucleus of egg **replicates** ready for mitosis ($\\checkmark$), and then they **fuse** to form a **diploid zygote** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hypothalamus secretes Gonadotropin-Releasing Hormone (GnRH)        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ GnRH stimulates the anterior pituitary gland to release gonadotropin hormones    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gonadotropin hormones are follicle-stimulating hormone **OR** FSH and luteinising hormone **OR** LH [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ LH  **OR** luteinising hormone triggers ovulation [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ovarian cycle involves follicular phase **OR** development of follicles, ovulation, **AND** luteal phase **OR** corpus luteum    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ uterine cycle controls preparation and maintenance of uterine lining **OR** endometrium  ready for fertilised egg        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ FSH **OR** follicle-stimulating hormone stimulates follicular phase **OR** development of follicles\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ follicles produce oestradiol as they grow    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oestradiol promotes the thickening **OR** increases blood vessel density of the uterine lining **OR** endometrium     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ positive feedback loop as oestradiol increases FSH and LH levels        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in levels of FSH and LH results in development of Graafian follicle     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ surge in LH (and FSH) leads to ovulation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oestradiol inhibits further release of FSH     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ inhibiting FSH is a negative feedback loop **OR** prevents excessive follicular development    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ remaining follicular cells transform into the corpus luteum        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ corpus luteum produces progesterone (and oestradiol) to maintain the uterine lining    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ high levels of progesterone and oestradiol prevent production of GnRH **OR** has negative feedback effect on GnRH **OR** hypothalamus    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if pregnancy doesn't occur, corpus luteum degenerates, therefore decreasing progesterone and oestradiol levels, triggering menstruation        [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **gonadotrophin** hormones, **luteinising hormone** (LH) and **follicle-stimulating hormone** (FSH) ($\\checkmark$) are released from the **pituitary** ($\\checkmark$) when **GnRH** levels rise ($\\checkmark$). **FSH** triggers the **development** of the **follicles** ($\\checkmark$), and as they develop, they **release oestradiol** ($\\checkmark$). The **rising** levels of **oestradiol** cause a further **increase** in **FSH** and **LH**, so this is an example of a **positive feedback** ($\\checkmark$) loop. A **surge in LH**, and to a lesser extent, **FSH**, brings about **ovulation** ($\\checkmark$). **Oestradiol** now **inhibits** further release of **FSH** ($\\checkmark$) to prevent more follicles from developing, an example of a **negative feedback** loop ($\\checkmark$). The remaining cells of the ruptured Graafian follicle now develop in the **corpus luteum**, an endocrine gland in its own right. This **secretes progesterone** and **oestradiol** to **maintain** the thickened **endometrium** ($\\checkmark$), brought about by the **increase** in **oestradiol** before ovulation ($\\checkmark$). If pregnancy does not occur, the **corpus luteum breaks down, progesterone** and **oestradiol levels drop**, and **menstruation** begins ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1093","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1093\n\nWhich hormone is released by an embryo early in pregnancy in order to stimulate the ovary to secrete progesterone?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ LH\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Oestradiol\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ hCG\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ FSH","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nEarly in pregnancy, a human embryo secretes the hormone hCG, human chorionic gonadotrophin. This hormone maintains the corpus luteum in the ovary so that it secretes progesterone, which helps prepare the lining of the uterus for the implantation of the embryo.\n\nLH (**Choice A**) and FSH (**Choice D**) are released by the pituitary gland, not the embryo. Oestradiol (**Choice B**) is released by the ovaries, not the embryo."},{"ref":"BI1094","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1094\n\nWhich is **not** a role of progesterone during pregnancy?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Stimulates the corpus luteum to produce hCG and oestradiol  |\n    | II. | Inhibits the secretion of oxytocin  |\n    | III. | Prepares the uterus lining for implantation |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is **not** a role of progesterone. Progesterone is produced by the corpus luteum; hCG is produced by the blastocyst after implantation and this hCG  maintains the corpus luteum at the beginning of pregnancy. **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Statements II** and **III** are roles of progesterone. **Choices A, C** and **D** are therefore incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1095","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1095\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Describe the mutualistic relationship between insect-pollinated plants and pollinators.[4 marks]\n\n2. Outline the self-incompatibility mechanisms that some plants have to prevent fertilisation resulting from self-pollination.    [4 marks]\n\n3. Explain how insect-pollinated flowers are adapted to their function.    [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following (*must include at least **one** statement for insect-pollinated plants and at least **one** for pollinators*)\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ both benefit from the relationship        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ insect-pollinated plants need pollinators to transfer pollen from the anther to the stigma **OR** from the male reproductive structures to the female reproductive structures    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  insect-pollinated plants need pollinators for fertilisation **OR** seed production **OR** to reproduce        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  pollinators increase genetic variation of plant species through cross-polllination    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  pollinators obtain food from the nectar and pollen of flowers [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  nectar provides carbohydrate **OR** is an energy source        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  pollen provides proteins and other nutrients [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The relationship between insect-pollinated plants and pollinators is a mutualistic relationship because **both benefit** ($\\checkmark$). Pollinators obtain **food** from the **nectar** and **pollen** ($\\checkmark$) of flowers. Pollinators are necessary for insect-pollinated plants **to reproduce** ($\\checkmark$). Pollinators **transfer pollen** from the **anther** to the **stigma** ($\\checkmark$). Pollinators **increase** the **genetic variation** of the plant species through **cross-pollination** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ S-gene system **OR** genetic mechanisms control self-incompatibility in many flowering plants\n [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ S-locus contains many different alleles across a population [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ each plant expresses specific S-allele **OR** S-alleles **OR** have specific alleles in BOTH pollen and stigma **OR** style **OR** carpel [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when pollen lands on a stigma, the plant compares the alleles **OR** S-allele of the pollen with the allele **OR** S-allele of stigma **OR** style **OR** carpel [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if  alleles **OR** S-alleles match **OR** are the same, the pollen is recognised as \"self\"  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gametophytic self-incompatibility **OR** GSI **OR** pollen tube growth inhibited within the style **OR** pollen tube is stopped during its growth towards ovule **OR** pollen tube forms but growth of it is arrested **OR** it does not grow down style **OR** carpel [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sporophytic self-incompatibility **OR** SSI **OR** pollen germination blocked [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ recognition of self-pollen activates biochemical pathways **OR** degradation of pollen RNA **OR** inhibition of pollen tube enzymes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ male gamete **OR** pollen nucleus breaks down before entering ovule **OR** ovary **OR** fusing with ovum [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ embryo formed but breaks down before seed formed [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Plants can prevent self-pollination through genetic mechanisms. If the pollen is recognised as ‘self’ by the plant the **germination** of the **pollen grain** to form a **pollen tube** can be **halted** ($\\checkmark$). If the **pollen tube** does manage to germinate it is **prevented** from **growing down to the ovary** ($\\checkmark$). The **pollen nucleus** may also **break down**, ($\\checkmark$) preventing fertilisation if it reaches the ovary. If fertilisation is successful the **embryo** may be **prevented from developing** ($\\checkmark$) into a seed.\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ flowers are used for sexual reproduction    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ insect-pollinated flowers have coloured petals to attract pollinators    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ insect-pollinated flowers have nectar, to attract **OR** reward, pollinators    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ anthers produce pollen that is consumed by pollinators     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the nectary is inside the flower so that pollinators touch the anthers and stigma when feeding            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pollen grains have spines which stick to insects **OR** pollinator            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the ovule develops into the seed, which is used for dispersal            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the ovary develops into the fruit, which is used for dispersal **OR** attracts animals to eat it for seed dispersal        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a large amount of pollen is produced to increase the likelihood of pollination    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some species of flowers are structured so that only one species can pollinate them        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ flowers attract insects using smell **OR** gases         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sepal protects bud **OR** developing flower             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ filament holds anther in best position to touch pollinators [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ top of stigma is sticky so holds pollen in place for germination    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ flowering occurs at same time **OR** after certain night length for the same species to increase chance of cross-pollination        [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Flowers are adapted to their function in several ways. The structure and shape of flowers are adapted for reproduction. **Coloured petals** ($\\checkmark$) and the **nectary** attract pollinators ($\\checkmark$). When pollinators **feed** on nectar and **pollen** on one plant ($\\checkmark$), some of the **pollen** from the anther **sticks** to the insect due to **spikes** on the pollen ($\\checkmark$).  Then, when the same pollinator **feeds** on another plant's nectar or pollen, some of the pollen lands on the **stigma** ($\\checkmark$) because the nectary is inside the flower. This is known as pollination and is used for **sexual reproduction** ($\\checkmark$) in flowering plants. Both the **seed**, which develops **from the ovule** ($\\checkmark$), and the **fruit**, which develops from the **ovary**, are used for seed dispersal ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1123","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1123\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nBelow is a graph showing the gestation periods and body masses of four different animals.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n1. Describe the relationship between body mass and the gestation period. [1 mark]\n\n2. Using the data, deduce, with a reason, whether a rabbit would have a more similar gestational period to a hamster or an elephant. [1 mark]\n\n3. State one method, other than body mass, to measure body size of animals. [1 mark]\n\n4. The foetus of a mammal that develops a placenta can remain in the uterus to a later stage of development than in mammals that do not. State one substance that passes from the biological mother to the foetus that helps their development. [1 mark]\n\n5. Humans have a longer gestation period than organisms with similar body mass. State the term used to indicate extreme data points that stand out and do not fit the pattern of a graph. [1 mark]\n\n6. Determine the gestation period for giraffes from the graph. [1 mark]\n\n7. Explain the roles of the main hormones that, when secreted, help bring about childbirth in humans. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ the smaller **OR** lower the body mass, the shorter the gestation period **OR** the larger **OR** higher the body mass, the longer the gestation period [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The **smaller the body mass**, the **shorter the gestation period** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ hamster; as closer in size to hamster than elephant [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The rabbit would have a gestational period that is more similar to that of the **hamster** as it is **much closer in size** to the hamster than the elephant. \n ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ body height **OR** length **OR** width **OR** girth **OR** circumference **OR** linear measurements **OR** limb length **OR** head width **OR** skull size **OR** surface area **OR** wing span [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ One other way of measuring the growth in body size is by measuring the animal’s **body length** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nutrients **OR** amino acids **OR** carbohydrates **OR** lipids **OR** vitamins **OR** minerals **OR** other suitable named nutrient [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ antibodies [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hormones **OR** porgesterone **OR** oestrogen **OR** hCG [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n One substance that passes from mother to foetus is **oxygen** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ outlier **OR** anomaly **OR** deviation **OR** extreme value **OR** aberration **OR** outlying data [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ A human’s gestation period and body mass data would be an **outlier** ($\\checkmark$) on the graph.\n\n6.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ any value in between 410 - 430 days [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The giraffe gestation period is **420 days** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n7. Any **four** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ oxytocin stimulates **OR** induces **OR** regulates **OR** causes more frequent **OR** more vigorous uterine contractions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ prostaglandins soften cervix **OR** prepare cervix for labour **OR** help cause dilation of cervix [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ prostaglandins help initiate uterine contractions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ relaxin relaxes the ligaments in the pelvis **OR** helps soften **OR** widen cervix [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ oestrogen enhances sensitivity of uterine muscle cells to oxytocin **OR** increases number of oxytocin receptors in uterus wall [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as contractions become more frequent **OR** intense, they help dilate the cervix **OR** aid descent of baby [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ positive feedback loop continues until baby born [1 mark]\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Oxytocin** ($\\checkmark$) and **prostaglandins** stimulate **contractions** of the **uterine wall** ($\\checkmark$). **Prostaglandins** also help **soften** the **cervix** allowing it to **dilate** ($\\checkmark$). **Oestrogen** enhances the **sensitivity** of the **uterine muscle** cells to **oxytocin** ($\\checkmark$). As **contractions** become more **intense**, they help **dilate** the **cervix** and aid the **descent** of the **baby** through the birth canal ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1170","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1170\n\nWhich row correctly shows a hormone involved in the menstrual cycle matched to where it is secreted from and its function?\n\n| | Hormone | Secreted from… | Function |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Oestradiol (oestrogen) | Ovaries | Stimulates the build-up of the endometrium |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Progesterone | Pituitary gland | Maintains the endometrial lining |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | FSH | Hypothalamus | Stimulates the maturation of egg cells in the follicles |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | LH | Pituitary gland | Stimulates an increase in the number of FSH receptors |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nOestradiol, or oestrogen, is secreted from the ovaries and stimulates the build-up of the endometrial lining; **Choice A**  is correct.\n\nAlthough progesterone maintains the endometrium lining, it is secreted from the ovaries and corpus luteum; **Choice B**  is incorrect. Although FSH does stimulate the maturation of egg cells in the follicles, it is secreted from the pituitary gland; **Choice C**  is incorrect. Although LH is secreted from the pituitary gland, it does not stimulate an increase in the number of FSH receptors, instead, it stimulates ovulation and promotes development of the corpus luteum; **Choice D**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1178","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1178\n\nWhat feature is **not** likely to  be found in an insect-pollinated flower?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Large, colourful petals\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Feathery stigma dangling outside the flower\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Strong scent\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Nectary present inside flower","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nFlowers can be insect or wind-pollinated. Flowers that are insect-pollinated have a sticky stigma inside the flower. As the insect enters the flower, some of the pollen carried on the insect sticks to this stigma. In wind-pollinated flowers, a feathery stigma hangs out of the flower to catch pollen carried in the air. **Choice B**  is correct.\n\nInsect-pollinated flowers often have large, colourful petals, a strong scent and nectar produced in nectaries, to attract insects; **Choices A, C** and **D** are true of insect-pollinated flowers, and so are not  correct answers."},{"ref":"BI1185","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1185\n\nWhich line represents the levels of progesterone during pregnancy and childbirth?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/BI1185QB.png\" alt=\"BI1185QB\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe role of high progesterone levels is to maintain the uterus lining in preparation and during pregnancy. It is produced by the corpus luteum until approximately the 9th week of pregnancy when the placenta takes over and the corpus luteum degrades. Immediately before birth, progesterone levels decline sharply allowing contractions to start. **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as progesterone levels do not remain constant during pregnancy and childbirth. **Choice B** is incorrect as progesterone levels do not start at a high level at the beginning of pregnancy and then decrease throughout pregnancy as this would lead to the breakdown of the endometrium. **Choice C** is incorrect, as progesterone levels do not drop halfway through pregnancy."},{"ref":"BI1254","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1254\n\nWhy is hormone replacement therapy (HRT) used as a treatment in biological females?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ It regulates the menstrual cycle\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ It stimulates ovulation\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ It increases fertility\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ It reduces the symptoms of menopause","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDuring menopause, the production of progesterone and oestradiol is reduced by the ovaries. This is thought to cause many of the symptoms associated with menopause, including temperature changes and difficulty focusing. HRT is used to replace biologically female hormones in order to reduce these symptoms.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect, as menstruation stops during menopause. **Choice B** is incorrect since the hormone responsible for ovulation is luteinising hormone (LH), which is no longer effective during menopause. **Choice C** is incorrect since fertility treatment typically relies on FSH and LH, while HRT uses progesterone and oestradiol."},{"ref":"BI1255","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1255\n\nWhich hormones are administered in hormone replacement therapy (HRT)?\n\n| | Hormone 1 | Hormone 2 |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Oestradiol | Progesterone |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Follicle Stimulating Hormone | Luteinising Hormone |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Oestradiol | Luteinising Hormone |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Follicle Stimulating Hormone | Progesterone |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDuring menopause, the production of progesterone and oestradiol is reduced by the ovaries. HRT is used to replace these hormones.\n\n**Choices B, C**, and **D** are incorrect as follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and luteinising hormone (LH) no longer cause a response from the ovaries, so replacing them would have no effect."},{"ref":"BI1256","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1256\n\nThe graph shows the relative risks of various health outcomes as a result of hormone replacement therapy (HRT) collected from a meta-analysis of 38908 postmenopausal biological females. The researchers compared the risks of combined or oestrogen only HRT to a control group that received a placebo. Oestradiol is a type of oestrogen.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/BI1256QBi.png\" alt=\"BI1256QBi\">\n\nWhat conclusion could be drawn from the results?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Patients receiving oestrogen-only HRT have a reduced risk of death from all causes.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Patients receiving oestrogen-only HRT have a reduced risk for all health outcomes.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The risk of stroke is significantly lower for patients not receiving HRT.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The risk of coronary events is higher for patients receiving HRT.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe risk of stroke is elevated for both the combined therapy and oestrogen-only HRT groups. This means that the placebo group had a lower risk of stroke than either group receiving HRT.\n\nAlthough the risk of cardiac death appears to be reduced for oestrogen-only HRT patients, the risk of total deaths has not been reduced, so **Choice A** cannot be correct. **Choice B** is incorrect as oestrogen-only HRT led to a slightly increased risk for total deaths, and stroke in particular. **Choice D** is incorrect since the data shows that oestrogen-only HRT may reduce the risk of coronary events while combined therapy HRT may increase it."},{"ref":"BI1257","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1257\n\nEarly epidemiological studies indicated that patients receiving hormone replacement therapy (HRT) had a reduced risk of coronary heart disease (CHD). Later studies conducted using randomised trials showed the opposite effect.\n\nWhat could be deduced from these findings?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ There is a strong correlation between HRT and CHD.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ There is a strong causal link between HRT and CHD.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ There may be a correlation between HRT and CHD, but a causal link cannot be implied.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ There may be a causal link between HRT and CHD, but a correlation cannot be implied.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe information provided indicates that there might be some correlation between HRT and CHD, but the contradictory findings between early epidemiological studies and later randomised trials emphasise that causation cannot be assumed without further investigation or evidence. Later research showed that women’s socioeconomic status was the likely cause of the reduced risk of CHD, not HRT.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect, as a strong correlation should generally produce consistent results instead of the mixed results observed here. **Choice B** is incorrect as a cause cannot be determined based on this data alone. **Choice D** cannot be correct, as a causal link should always result in a correlation of some kind."},{"ref":"BI1292","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1292\n\nWhich statement(s) is a method of promoting cross-pollination?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | A bat brushing against the anther of a plant as it consumes nectar |\n    | II. | The wind carrying pollen across a field of flowers |\n    | III. | Floating pollen between aquatic plants |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCross-pollination is when pollen is transferred from the anther of one flower to the stigma of another flower of the same species. Cross-pollination can occur when animals such as bats rub against the anther of one plant and fly to another plant of the same species (**Statement I**). Cross-pollination can occur when wind carries pollen between one plant and lands on the stigma of another plant of the same species (**Statement II**).  Cross-pollination can occur when pollen floats between aquatic plants and lands on the stigma of aquatic plants of the same species (**Statement III**). **Choice D**  is correct.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **C** are incorrect as they do not include all three statements."},{"ref":"BI1293","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1293\n\nThe image shows a butterfly bush being pollinated by a bee and butterfly. What is not a factor that attracts pollinators to flowers?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/BI1293x.png\" alt=\"BI1293x\">\n\n[@ 2025]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Colour of petals\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Scent of flower\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Using fertilisers\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Presence of nectar","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nFertiliser around a plant can mask the scent of a flower so that pollinators are less attracted or may be harmful to pollinators; **Choice C** is correct.\n\nPollinators such as bees are attracted to flowers because of their brightly coloured petals, attractive scents emanating from flowers or the presence of nectar which they feed on. **Choices A, B** and **D** are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1294","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1294\n\nThe table shows possible advantages and disadvantages of sexual reproduction via cross-pollination in flowering plants, compared to asexual reproduction. Which row of the table is correct?\n\n| | Advantage of cross-pollination | Disadvantage of cross-pollination |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Reduces variation within a species | Limited ability to adapt to environment |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Promotes variation within a species | More pollen required |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ |Reduces variation within a species | More pollen required |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Promotes variation within a species | Limited ability to adapt to environment |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCross-pollination is when pollen grains, the male gametes, are transferred from the anther of one flower to the stigma of another flower of the same species. When the pollen fertilises the ovules of another plant, it combines genetic material from two different individuals. This means that it promotes variation within a species so that plants are more likely to adapt to change in their environment, a great advantage. A disadvantage of cross-pollination is that, because it is brought about by animals or wind, there is a high risk that the pollen picked up from one flower, will not reach the stigma of another flower of the same species.  Therefore, plants need to produce a lot of pollen to increase their chances of cross-pollination. **Choice B**  is correct.\n\nCross-pollination is more likely to result in genetic diversity, which promotes variation and would increase the chances of plants being able to adapt to the environment; **Choice A** is incorrect. Although more pollen is required for cross-pollination, cross-pollination promotes variation within a species; **Choice C**  is incorrect. Although cross-pollination does promote variation within a species, this increases the chances of plants to adapt to changes in the environment; **Choice D**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1295","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1295\n\nThe image shows the reproductive system of a flower. What method is this plant using to prevent self-pollination?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/BI1295.png\" alt=\"BI1295\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The anther and stigma mature at different times\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ It only contains female reproductive organs\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The insects have to brush past the stigma to reach the anthers\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ It only contains male reproductive organs","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSelf-pollination is when pollen is transferred from the anther to the stigma of the same flower. The diagram shows anthers that have already matured and released their pollen. This temporal separation ensures that the flower is unlikely to self-pollinate because its own pollen is no longer available when the stigma is ready to receive pollen from a flower of the same species. **Choice A** is correct.\n\nThe stigma, style and ovary that make up the female reproductive organs and the anther and filament that make up the male reproductive organs are clearly visible. **Choices B** and **D** are incorrect. If the insects have to brush past the stigma to reach the anthers, then the insects may deposit the pollen from the anthers on the stigma as they leave the flower, which could allow self-pollination, not prevent it; **Choice C** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1296","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1296\n\nTo prevent self-pollination, plants have self-incompatibility mechanisms. Which statement about self-incompatibility mechanisms is **incorrect**?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ They are only activated in response to changes in the environment\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ They involve the carpel recognising and rejecting pollen produced by the anthers of the same flower\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ They are genetically controlled\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ They inhibit the growth of the pollen tube if the pollen has been produced in the same flower as the style","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSelf-incompatibility mechanisms are not only activated in response to environmental changes, they are genetically controlled and activated throughout the plant's lifetime, **Choice A**  is correct.\n\nSelf-incompatibility involves the carpel recognising and rejecting pollen produced by the same flower (**Choice B**), and this involves genetic control (**Choice C**). One mechanism used to reject the pollen produced by the same flower is to inhibit the growth of the pollen tube (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI1399","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1399\n\nCarrot (*Daucus carota*) seeds were collected over a number of seasons and stored in a cool, dark place. Seeds of each age were then grown and from the results, the probability of germination was calculated based on seed age, as seen in the graph below. What can be concluded based on the results of this experiment?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/1399i.png\" alt=\"1399i\">\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n | | |\n | :-- | :-- |\n | I. | Seeds from the most recent season were the most likely to germinate. |\n | II. | The germination rate for all seeds was highest in week 1. |\n | III. | It can be estimated that 60% of seeds from a 2020 pack would germinate by week 5. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe graph indicates that by week 10, more than 90% of the 2022 season carrot seeds will likely have germinated. This is considerably higher than either the 2021 or 2020 season seeds, so **Statement I** is true. During week 1, the graph indicates that it is likely that none of the seeds will have germinated, so **Statement II** would be false. By week 5, it can be estimated that 40% of the 2020 season seeds had germinated, not 60% as **Statement III** indicates. Therefore Choice A is correct.\n\n**Choices B, C**, and **D** are incorrect as they all include one or more untrue statements."},{"ref":"BI1406","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1406\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe diagram shows how a pregnancy test works to detect hCG in urine.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/1406%20new%20D3.1.png\" alt=\"1406 new D3.1\">\n\n1. Monoclonal antibodies are used in pregnancy tests. Describe what a monoclonal antibody is. [2 marks]\n\n2. Outline the purpose of the second coloured line in the pregnancy test. [2 marks]\n\n3. Explain the role of hCG in pregnancy. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ antibodies produced by a single clone **OR** one particular type of B-lymphocytes **OR** plasma B cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ specific to one antigen **OR** binds to only one particular antigen [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ produced under laboratory conditions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ produced using hybridoma cells **OR** by recombinant DNA technology [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Monoclonal antibodies are antibodies that bind to only **one specific antigen** ($\\checkmark$). They are produced by a single **clone** of **hybrididoma** ($\\checkmark$) **plasma B cells** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ acts as control line [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to confirm that the test is working correctly **OR** to confirm urine has been absorbed properly **OR** to show test is valid [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ contains immobilised antibodies specific to the mobile antibodies **OR** produce colour regardless of hCG presence [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The second coloured line acts as a **control** ($\\checkmark$), ensuring the **test is working correctly** by confirming that **urine** has been **absorbed** and the test is **valid** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ maintains the corpus luteum **OR** stimulates corpus luteum to continue producing progesterone **OR** oestradiol **OR** oestrogen [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ thereby supporting early pregnancy **OR** maintenance of uterine lining **OR** endometrium [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ involved in foetal development **OR** plays a role in placental development [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n hCG maintains the **corpus luteum** as an **endocrine gland** ($\\checkmark$)that secretes **progesterone** to **maintain** the **uterine lining** during pregnancy($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1582","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1582\n\nThe diagram shows the male reproductive system. Which structure is responsible for secreting alkaline fluid and nutrients to support the sperm?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/BI1582.png\" alt=\"BI1582\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because it points to the seminal vesicles, which produce alkaline fluid to offset the acidic environment of the male and female reproductive systems. They also secrete nutrients such as fructose, which provides energy to the sperm.\n\n**Choice B** is the urethra, a duct not involved in secretion. **Choice C** is the epididymis, which is involved in sperm storage and maturation. **Choice D** is the testes, which produces sperm and reproductive hormones."},{"ref":"BI1590","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1590\n\nThe diagram shows a half-view of an almond flower (*Prunus dulcis*).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/BI1590.png\" alt=\"BI1590\">\n\nWhat feature indicates that this species is adapted for animal pollination?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Large petals\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Many stamens\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Presence of a central stigma\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Stigma longer than stamen","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because large petals are a characteristic feature of animal-pollinated flowers. They attract pollinators with their size and colour and provide a surface for smaller pollinators to land on.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because both wind and animal-pollinated flowers typically have many stamens to increase pollen production. **Choice C** is incorrect because a central stigma is common for most types of flowers, regardless of whether they are wind or animal-pollinated. **Choice D** is incorrect because having a longer stigma than the stamen does not indicate what type of pollination a flower requires. Instead, it promotes cross-pollination by reducing self-pollination."},{"ref":"BI1602","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1602\n\nWhich of the following statements is/are true?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Gonadotrophin-releasing hormone (GnRH) triggers the release of follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and luteinising hormone (LH) in biological females but not biological males.  |\n    | II. | Development of both ova and sperm involves the secretion of follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH). |\n    | III. | Primary oocytes and spermatocytes follow a similar pathway through meiosis. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nFSH stimulates the growth and maturation of follicles in the ovaries, which contain developing ova, producing mature eggs capable of being fertilised. FSH also stimulates the development of Sertoli cells. These cells play a vital role in supporting the developing sperm cells, contributing to the production of functional sperm cells. **Statement II** is the only true statement therefore **Choice B** is correct.\n\nGnRH triggers the release of FSH and LH in both biological females and males. In biological females, FSH and LH play essential roles in regulating the menstrual cycle, follicle development, ovulation, and hormone production. In males, FSH and LH regulate spermatogenesis and testosterone production. **Statement I** is, therefore, not true.\n\nWhile both primary oocytes and spermatocytes undergo meiosis, their pathways through meiosis differ significantly. In females, primary oocytes undergo meiosis I during foetal development but arrest in prophase I until puberty. A primary oocyte completes meiosis I upon ovulation to form a secondary oocyte and a polar body. Meiosis II only occurs after fertilisation. In males, spermatocytes undergo meiosis I and meiosis II after puberty to produce four haploid sperm cells that may then fertilise an ovum. **Statement III** is, therefore, not true.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are incorrect answers to the question as they include **Statement I** and/or **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI1688","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1688\n\nAntibodies pass across the placenta from mother to baby. What type of immunity is this an example of?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Natural active\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Natural passive\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Artificial active\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Artificial passive","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nPassive immunity occurs when antibodies are transferred from one individual to another, rather than being produced by the recipient’s immune system. Natural immunity refers to immunity acquired without medical intervention. Since the antibodies are naturally transferred from mother to baby via the placenta, this classifies as passive natural immunity.   **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because active immunity requires the body to produce its own antibodies after exposure to a pathogen. In this case, the baby receives pre-made antibodies from the mother, making it passive, not active, immunity.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because artificial immunity involves medical interventions, such as vaccines, which stimulate the immune system to produce its own antibodies. Since no vaccine or medical treatment is involved, this is not artificial active immunity.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because, although passive immunity is correct, artificial passive immunity involves medical treatment (e.g., antibody injections). Since the antibodies were transferred naturally across the placenta, this is natural, not artificial, passive immunity."},{"ref":"BI2344","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2344\n\nSelf-incompatibility mechanisms in flowering plants prevent self-pollination. Which of these are advantage(s) of self-incompatibility mechanisms?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Increased genetic diversity |\n    | II. | Increased vigour |\n    | III. | Increased number of offspring |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCross-pollination introduces genetic variation, reducing the likelihood of harmful genetic combinations and increasing adaptability to environmental changes. **Statement I** is true.\n\nCross-pollination produces genetically different offspring, some of which will have characteristics that are better adapted to the environment than the parents and have more vigour. **Statement II** is true.\n\nSelf-incompatibility mechanisms prevent fertilisation by pollen from the same plant, but fertilisation is still possible by gametes from other plants, so the number of offspring is not directly affected by these mechanisms, **Statement III** is  false.\n\n**Choice B** is correct because it includes only the two true **Statements I** and **II**.\n\n**Choices A** and **C** are incorrect as they are missing one true statement. **Choice D** is incorrect because it includes the flase **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI2392","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2392\n\nWhich row of the table contains **three** correct statements about the transport of substances across the mammalian placenta?\n\n| | Simple diffusion | Facilitated diffusion | Active transport |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | carbon dioxide | mineral ions | fatty acids |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | oxygen | glucose| amino acids |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | water | amino acids | mineral ions |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | antibodies | amino acids | glucose |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nOxygen and carbon dioxide cross maternal and placental membranes by simple diffusion. \\\nWater crosses maternal and placental membranes by osmosis. \\\nFatty acids cross maternal and placental membranes by simple diffusion and facilitated diffusion (depending on the fatty acid’s chemical composition).\\\nGlucose and antibodies (immunoglobulin G) cross maternal and placental membranes by facilitated diffusion.\\\nMineral ions and amino acids cross maternal and placental membranes by active transport.\n\n**Choice B** is correct because it contains three correct statements.\\\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it only contains one correct statement (carbon dioxide).\\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because it only contains one correct statement (mineral ions).\\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because it contains three incorrect statements.\n\nThe table is shown below with the correct statements **in bold** and the incorrect statements ~struck out~.\n\n| | Simple diffusion | Facilitated diffusion | Active transport |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | **carbon dioxide** | ~mineral ions~ | ~fatty acids~ |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | **oxygen** | **glucose**| **amino acids** |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | ~water~ | ~amino acids~ | **mineral ions** |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | ~antibodies~ | ~amino acids~ | ~glucose~ |"},{"ref":"BI2733","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2733\n\nWhat is the correct chronological order of the events in spermatogenesis?\n\n1\\. Growth into primary spermatocytes\\\n2\\. Meiosis I produces secondary spermatocytes\\\n3\\. Meiosis II produces spermatids\\\n4\\. Mitosis of spermatogonia\\\n5\\. Differentiation into spermatozoa\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 1 $\\rarr$ 4 $\\rarr$ 2 $\\rarr$ 3 $\\rarr$ 5\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 4 $\\rarr$ 2 $\\rarr$ 1 $\\rarr$ 3 $\\rarr$ 5\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 4 $\\rarr$ 1 $\\rarr$ 2 $\\rarr$ 3 $\\rarr$ 5\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 4 $\\rarr$ 1 $\\rarr$ 2 $\\rarr$ 5 $\\rarr$ 3","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because spermatogenesis occurs in the following sequence: 1. Spermatogonia undergo mitotic divisions. 2. Some spermatogonia grow and differentiate into primary spermatocytes. 3. Primary spermatocytes undergo meiosis I, producing secondary spermatocytes. 4. Secondary spermatocytes undergo meiosis II, forming spermatids. 5. Spermatids mature through a process called spermiogenesis, where they develop into spermatozoa.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the growth into primary spermatocytes happens after mitosis of spermatogonia, so placing 1 before 4 is incorrect.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because meiosis I cannot happen before primary spermatocytes are formed. The primary spermatocytes must first grow before they can enter meiosis.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because differentiation into spermatozoa must happen after meiosis II, not before. This choice incorrectly places differentiation before the final step of meiosis."},{"ref":"BI2736","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2736\n\nThe figure below shows a home pregnancy test strip.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Reproduction/images/BI2736.png\" alt=\"BI2736\">\n\nWhich of the following is correct regarding the result of the test?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ It is positive because the hCG in the urine is bound to mobile antibodies, allowing them to be captured at the test line.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ It is negative because the presence of a test line only confirms that antibodies are functional, not that hCG is present.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ It is negative because hCG must bind to the control line for a positive result.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ It is positive because the mobile antibodies always bind to the test line, regardless of hCG presence.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because hCG in the urine binds to mobile monoclonal antibodies, which carry a colour-changing enzyme. As these antibody–hCG complexes move along the strip, they are captured by immobilised antibodies at the test line, producing a visible line that indicates a positive result.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the test line only appears if hCG is present, not just because antibodies are functional.\\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because the control line binds excess mobile antibodies but does not detect hCG presence. This is because it contains immobilised antibodies that bind to mobile antibodies regardless of hCG presence.\\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because mobile antibodies only bind at the test line if hCG is present. Without hCG, they pass by and only bind at the control line."},{"ref":"BI2738","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2738\n\nWhich of the following are functions of the placenta?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Oxygen and nutrient exchange |\n    | II. | Removal of foetal waste products |\n    | III. | Secretion of hormones to maintain pregnancy |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because the placenta enables the exchange of oxygen and nutrients between maternal and foetal blood. Oxygen and essential molecules like glucose and amino acids diffuse from the mother’s blood to the foetus, supporting growth and development.\n\n**Statement II** is true because the placenta also facilitates the removal of foetal waste products, such as carbon dioxide and urea, by transferring them into the maternal bloodstream for excretion through the mother's kidneys and lungs.\n\n**Statement III** is true because the placenta secretes essential hormones, including human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG), progesterone, and oestrogen, which help maintain pregnancy by preventing uterine contractions and supporting the endometrium.\n\n**Choice D** is correct because it includes all three true statements\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it omits the true **Statement III**.    \\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because it omits the true **Statement II**.  \\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because it omits the true **Statement I**."},{"ref":"BI2740","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2740\n\nWhich of the following statements about the relationship between hormone replacement therapy (HRT) and coronary heart disease (CHD) are correct?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Early observational studies indicated that HRT resulted in a lower CHD risk |\n    | II. | Randomised controlled trials later found a slight increase in CHD risk with HRT use |\n    | III. | Women who take HRT under the age of 50 have a higher risk of CHD. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because early observational studies did show an apparent association between HRT use and reduced CHD risk. However, this association was later challenged, as the observed benefit was likely due to confounding factors like socio-economic status and health behaviours.\n\n**Statement II** is true because randomised controlled trials later found that HRT was associated with a slight increase in CHD risk, especially when initiated after age 60.\n\n**Statement III** is false because this contradicts current evidence. In fact, younger postmenopausal women (< 60 years or within 10 years of menopause) typically show no increased CHD risk and may even see some cardiovascular benefit when HRT is initiated early.\n\n**Choice A** is correct because it includes the two true **Statements I** and **II** and not the false one.\n\n**Choices B, C** and **D** are incorrect because they all include the false **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI2741","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2741\n\nWhat is the correct chronological order of the events in oogenesis?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Growth of primary oocyte|\n    | II. | Mitosis of oogonia |\n    | III. | Formation of secondary oocyte and polar body |\n    | IV. | Migration of primordial germ cells and differentiation into oogonia |\n    | V. | Completion of meiosis II after fertilisation |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ IV $\\rarr$ II $\\rarr$ I $\\rarr$ III $\\rarr$ V\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II $\\rarr$ IV $\\rarr$ I $\\rarr$ V $\\rarr$ III\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ IV $\\rarr$ I $\\rarr$ II $\\rarr$ III $\\rarr$ V\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ II $\\rarr$ IV $\\rarr$ I $\\rarr$ III $\\rarr$ V","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because it reflects the correct chronological order of events in oogenesis:\n\n-   1\\. Migration of primordial germ cells and differentiation into oogonia (**Statement IV**): During early embryonic development, primordial germ cells migrate to the developing ovaries and differentiate into oogonia.\n\n-   2\\. Mitosis of oogonia (**Statement II**): The oogonia, which are diploid, undergo mitotic divisions to increase their number.\n\n-   3\\. Growth of primary oocyte (**Statement I**): Some oogonia enlarge and enter meiosis I, pausing in prophase I — they are now called primary oocytes.\n\n-   4\\. Formation of secondary oocyte and polar body (**Statement III**): At puberty, during each menstrual cycle, one primary oocyte completes meiosis I, producing a secondary oocyte and a polar body.\n\n-   5\\. Completion of meiosis II after fertilisation (**Statement V**): The secondary oocyte completes meiosis II only if fertilisation occurs, producing a mature ovum and a second polar body.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because it places mitosis before the initial differentiation of germ cells into oogonia.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because it places the growth of the oocyte before mitosis, which is biologically out of order.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because it places mitosis before differentiation, which is inconsistent with embryonic development."},{"ref":"BI2742","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2742\n\nWhich of the following best explains why only one sperm typically fertilises the human egg?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The acrosome reaction modifies the sperm’s plasma membrane, allowing it to bind to the zona pellucida\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sperm binding to  receptors blocks other sperm from recognising the zona pellucida\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The cortical reaction alters the zona pellucida to prevent additional sperm fusion after the first has entered\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Completion of meiosis II ensures only one sperm nucleus is capable of fertilising the ovum at any time","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWhen the first sperm fuses with the egg membrane, the cortical granules inside the egg release their contents into the space around the egg. This causes biochemical changes that harden the zona pellucida and inactivate sperm-binding receptors, a process called the zona reaction. This mechanism prevents polyspermy, which would lead to an inviable zygote. **Choice C**  is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the acrosome reaction allows sperm to penetrate the zona pellucida, but multiple sperm can undergo this reaction simultaneously. It is the cortical reaction, not the acrosome reaction, that enforces the one-sperm rule.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because binding to receptors initiates the acrosome reaction, but does not block other sperm. Only after one sperm fuses with the egg does the zona become impenetrable.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because completion of meiosis II in the oocyte occurs after sperm entry, and has no role in limiting sperm fusion. It ensures the oocyte becomes haploid before the male and female nuclei fuse, but does not prevent polyspermy."},{"ref":"BI2889","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2889\n\nWhich of the following statements best describes the relationship between sexual and asexual reproduction in terms of adaptation?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Asexual reproduction leads to offspring that are adapted to an existing environment. |\n    | II. | Sexual reproduction results in offspring with new gene combinations, which may provide an advantage in adapting to a changed environment. |\n    | III. | Asexual reproduction results in genetic diversity that allows for rapid adaptation to environmental changes. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because asexual reproduction generates genetically identical offspring, which are typically well-adapted to the existing environment. This is advantageous in stable environments but limits the potential for adaptation to new challenges.\n\n**Statement II** is true because sexual reproduction introduces genetic diversity, resulting in offspring with new gene combinations. This genetic variation provides a significant advantage for adapting to environmental changes, which may be beneficial in fluctuating conditions.\n\n**Statement III** is false because asexual reproduction produces offspring that are genetically identical to the parent, meaning there is little or no genetic diversity. As a result, asexual reproduction does not facilitate rapid adaptation to changing environments.\n\n**Choice A** is, therefore, the correct answer as it only includes the two true **Statements I** and **II**.\n\n**Choices B, C**, and **D** are all incorrect as they include the false **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI2890","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2890\n\nWhich of the following statements is true about meiosis and genetic diversity?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Meiosis produces genetically identical haploid gametes. |\n    | II. | Crossing over during meiosis creates new combinations of alleles. |\n    | III. | Independent assortment leads to genetic variation in offspring.|\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is false because meiosis produces genetically diverse gametes, not identical ones. This is due to the processes of crossing over and independent assortment during meiosis, both of which contribute to genetic variation.\n\n**Statement II** is true because during prophase I of meiosis, crossing over occurs between homologous chromosomes. This process results in the exchange of genetic material, creating new combinations of alleles and increasing genetic diversity.\n\n**Statement III** is true because independent assortment of chromosomes during metaphase I of meiosis leads to the random distribution of maternal and paternal chromosomes into gametes. This process results in different combinations of chromosomes in the offspring, further contributing to genetic variation.\n\n**Choice B** is correct because it only includes the true **Statements II** and **III**.\\\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are all incorrect as they include the false **Statement I**."},{"ref":"BI2891","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2891\n\nWhich of the following is the primary biological reason for the difference in the number of gametes produced by males and females?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Females produce gametes only when fertilisation is imminent.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Males need to conserve energy by producing fewer gametes.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Females need to invest more in each gamete to provide nutrients for early development.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Males need to ensure they produce genetically identical offspring.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because female gametes (eggs) are large and contain significant cytoplasm, which provides the nutrients needed for the early development of the embryo after fertilisation. This requires more energy investment, so females produce fewer eggs over their lifetime.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because males produce large numbers of small, mobile gametes (sperm) to increase the chances of fertilisation, not to wait for fertilisation to be imminent.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because females do not only produce eggs when fertilisation is imminent. They produce eggs periodically throughout their reproductive life, with the timing of release being controlled by hormonal cycles.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because males do not produce gametes to create genetically identical offspring. Instead, they produce large numbers of genetically diverse gametes to maximise the chance of successful fertilisation and increase genetic variation in offspring."},{"ref":"BI2892","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2892\n\nWhich of the following best describes the involvement of both sperm and egg chromosomes in fertilisation?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Both sperm and egg chromosomes undergo mitosis immediately after the sperm enters the egg. |\n    | II. | The sperm chromosomes fuse with the egg chromosomes to restore the diploid chromosome number. |\n    | III. | The sperm tail and mitochondria provide energy for the fusion of chromosomes. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is false because mitosis occurs after fertilisation, not immediately after the sperm enters the egg. The sperm and egg chromosomes first fuse to restore the diploid chromosome number, and mitosis begins later for cell division.\n\n**Statement II** is  true because after the sperm nucleus enters the egg, the chromosomes from both the sperm and egg combine. This fusion of genetic material forms a diploid zygote, which contains a complete set of chromosomes (half from each parent).\n\n**Statement III** is false because the sperm’s mitochondria and tail are discarded during fertilisation. The tail is used for mobility, but it does not contribute to the embryo, and the mitochondria are typically not involved in the fusion process.\n\n**Choice B** is correct because includes the only true **Statement II**.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are all incorrect because they include one or more of the false **Statements I** and **III**."},{"ref":"BI2893","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2893\n\nWhich of the following is typically suspended during IVF treatment to ensure successful egg retrieval?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Luteal phase hormone production\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Production of oestradiol and progesterone\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ovulation of eggs without stimulation\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Hormone production from the hypothalamus and pituitary gland","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because during IVF treatment, the natural hormone production by the hypothalamus and pituitary gland is suppressed using drugs like GnRH agonists or GnRH antagonists. These drugs inhibit the secretion of gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH), which in turn reduces the production of luteinising hormone (LH) and follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) from the pituitary gland. This suppression prevents premature ovulation and allows for precise control over the timing of egg.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the luteal phase is not suspended during IVF. In fact, it is artificially induced after egg retrieval and embryo transfer by administering progesterone to support the uterine lining and prepare it for potential implantation.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because oestradiol and progesterone levels are regulated via external hormone supplementation during IVF. These hormones are used to stimulate the ovaries for egg maturation and to maintain the uterine lining, but they are not suppressed.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because ovulation is not left to occur naturally. In IVF, ovulation is carefully triggered using hormones like hCG after the eggs are matured through the administration of FSH and LH. This ensures the eggs are retrieved at the correct time."}]},{"name":"Stability & Change","questions":[{"ref":"BI0107","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0107\n\nA student set up an aquatic mesocosm using a commercially available seawater-filled glass orb, which included algae, shrimp, some corals and gravel. The student placed it near a window to provide a light source. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | The algae are autotrophs, produce carbon compounds and provide oxygen for organisms in the mesocosm |\n  | II. | Saprotrophs present in the water and gravel help to recycle nutrients found in dead organisms |\n  | III. | Herbivores usually present in a natural ecosystem can be excluded in the mesocosm |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$  I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$  I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$  II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$  I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAll the statements are true. A mesocosm is a stable, small representation of an ecosystem and can be used to study how one factor in an ecosystem affects other factors in an ecosystem. In all ecosystems, energy is lost and autotrophs such as algae or green plants generate the energy needed for the ecosystem. Saprotrophs are necessary for nutrient cycling, which is why no external addition of nutrients is generally required. Herbivores are not necessary for mesocosms to be stable as autotroph abundance is also regulated by the availability of resources such as nutrients and light. The only choice that includes all three statements is **Choice C**. All other choices (**A, B and C**) are therefore incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0128","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0128\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Stability%20%26%20Change/images/BI0128.png\" alt=\"BI0128\">\n\nThe diagram above shows a simplified diagram of the movement of carbon in the ocean. Which of the following rows correctly identifies processes involved in the carbon cycle?\n\n| | I | II | III | IV |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A. $\\hspace{1em}$ | Respiration | Eaten by | Decomposition | Photosynthesis |\n| B. $\\hspace{1em}$ | Decomposition | Photosynthesis | Respiration | Eaten by |\n| C. $\\hspace{1em}$ | Photosynthesis | Respiration | Decomposition | Eaten by |\n| D. $\\hspace{1em}$ | Photosynthesis | Respiration | Eaten by | Decomposition |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nProcess I is photosynthesis and shows the capture of energy by phytoplankton from the Sun. Process II is respiration where carbon dioxide is released into the atmosphere. Process III shows the movement of carbon when organisms are eaten by members of the next trophic level, and process IV is the decomposition of dead organisms by bacteria which eventually causes a release of carbon dioxide.\n\nAll other choices (**Choice A, Choice B and Choice C**) do not identify the processes correctly."},{"ref":"BI0135","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0135\n\n**Maximum mark: 2**\n\nThe diagram below shows a simplified scheme of the movement of carbon in the ocean.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Stability%20%26%20Change/images/BI0135.png\" alt=\"BI0135\">\n\nIdentify the processes marked I and II. [2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\ce{ -> }$ Process **I** is photosynthesis [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ Process **II** is consumed by **OR** eaten by **OR** predated by [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1.5em}$:u[Sample answer]:\n\n$\\hspace{1.5em}$Process I is **photosynthesis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n$\\hspace{1.5em}$Process II is zooplankton being **consumed by** secondary consumers ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0136","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0136\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\n1. Compare and contrast forests and marine phytoplankton in their role in the carbon cycle. [2 marks]\n\n2. Describe how aquatic autotrophs absorb carbon dioxide from the surroundings.  [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **both** are autotrophs **OR  both** carry out photosynthesis     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ trees in forests absorb carbon dioxide from the air **WHILE**  marine phytoplankton absorbs carbon dioxide dissolved in water [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ancient trees were compressed beneath the soil and formed coal **WHILE** phytoplankton were compressed beneath sediment and formed oil and natural gas [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Both marine phytoplankton and trees carry out **photosynthesis** ($\\checkmark$).  However, the trees in the forests take in carbon dioxide from the **air** while marine phytoplankton take in **dissolved carbon dioxide** from the water ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide in water is absorbed by aquatic autotrophs by diffusion  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide dissolves in water to form carbonic acid     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbonic acid can dissociate to form hydrogen and hydrogen carbonate ions  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ aquatic autotrophs use both dissolved carbon dioxide and hydrogen carbonate ions for photosynthesis [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    In water; **carbon dioxide forms carbonic acid** ($\\checkmark$) which can **dissociate** to form hydrogen and **hydrogen carbonate ions** ($\\checkmark$). The aquatic **autotrophs** can use both dissolved **carbon dioxide** and  **hydrogen carbonate ions** for **photosynthesis** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0784","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0784\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nDissolved CO:sub[2] is increasing in standing waters of lakes and ponds. Research has shown this will impact the nutritional quality of phytoplankton (primary producers) and may further affect zooplankton (consumers) that feed on the phytoplankton.\n\nMesocosms were used to monitor three species of zooplankton, the water flea (*Daphnia magna*), the seed shrimp (*Heterocypris incongruens*) and the rotifer (*Brachionus calyciflorus*) under three different conditions of CO:sub[2] in ponds and shallow lakes:\n\n- Treatment C - present day levels of CO:sub[2], (control)\n- Treatment 1 - moderate increase of CO:sub[2] (possible future levels)\n- Treatment 2 - high increase of CO:sub[2]  (more extreme future levels)\n\nGlass jars filled with filtered pond water were injected with both carbon dioxide and air under pressure, and the representative species of zooplankton, water flea, seed shrimp or rotifer were added to the jars. They were monitored for survival, growth rate and body size over the next several days.\n\nThe image below shows the three species of zooplankton that were studied in the mesocosms.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Stability%20%26%20Change/images/BI0784%20a.png\" alt=\"BI0784 a\">\n[Adapted from Ramaekers, L., Pinceel, T., Brendonck, L., & Vanschoenwinkel, B. (2022, April 12). *Direct effects of elevated dissolved CO2 can alter the life history of Freshwater Zooplankton*. Nature News. Retrieved March 22, 2023, from https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-10094-2, Copyright under CC-BY 4.0]\n\nThe survival probability of the three species of zooplankton were monitored over several days in the mesocosms and the results are shown in the graphs below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Stability%20%26%20Change/images/BI0784b.png\" alt=\"BI0784b\">\n[Adapted from Ramaekers, L., Pinceel, T., Brendonck, L., & Vanschoenwinkel, B. (2022, April 12). Direct effects of elevated dissolved CO2 can alter the life history of Freshwater Zooplankton. Nature News. Retrieved March 22, 2023, from https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-10094-2,Copyright under CC-BY 4.0]\n\n1. Giving a reason, predict which zooplankton is the least resilient to the effects of increased carbon dioxide.    [1 mark]\n\n2. Describe how using mesocosms for research is both an advantage and a disadvantage in predicting the effects of increasing carbon dioxide in the atmosphere.     [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the water flea, because it could not survive treatment T2 or extreme future levels of CO:sub[2] and therefore was the most sensitive  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ both seed shrimp and rotifer could survive treatment T2 for more days and therefore the    water flea is the most sensitive to elevated levels of CO:sub[2]  [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Unlike the seed shrimp and rotifer, **the water flea could not survive treatment T2** ($\\checkmark$)  after the first two days and is therefore the most sensitive to future elevated levels of CO:sub[2].\n\n2. Any **one** from each section\n\n    **Advantages:**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ with a closed system it is easier to increase levels of CO:sub[2]     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mesocosms can be monitored to see the effects of elevated CO2    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mesocosms will yield results in a short period of time     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mesocosms allow small scale experiments to be set up    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reproducibility - can do repeats more easily to collect more data    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ costs - can be cheaper to set up [1 mark]\n\n    **Disadvantages:**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it is not possible to simulate all that happens in the environment     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it is unethical to add larger organisms to a mesocosm    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it can be difficult to set up mesocosms and monitor the changes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ often short term experiments do not capture the long term effects of changes in carbon dioxide [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    An advantage is that it is **easier to increase levels of CO:sub[2] in a closed system** ($\\checkmark$) like a mesocosm to study the effects. A disadvantage is that it is **not possible to simulate** ($\\checkmark$) exactly all that happens in the environment."},{"ref":"BI0933","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0933\n\nEcological reserves in adjoining areas are connected by passageways or small strips of land to enable animals to cross between the regions. Which of the following would describe these connections?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Biological conduits\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Edge effects\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Landscaping\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Wildlife corridors","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWildlife corridors are strips of habitat that connect fragmented areas, allowing animals to move between different regions. They facilitate the movement of species, support gene flow, provide access to food and resources, and contribute to overall ecosystem health. Wildlife corridors help mitigate the negative effects of habitat fragmentation and promote biodiversity conservation. **Choice D** is therefore correct.\n\nBiological conduits (**Choice A**) is not a term commonly used in biology. When it is used, it refers to a natural or synthetic structure that facilitates the transport of biological fluids, cells, or tissues within an organism or between organisms,e.g. blood vessels and catheters.\n\nEdge effects (**Choice B**) are changes in ecological conditions that occur at the boundaries, or edges, between different ecosystems. While edge effects can be relevant to the areas surrounding wildlife corridors, they do not specifically describe the connections themselves.\n\nLandscaping is designing outdoor spaces with plants and other structures (**Choice C**)."},{"ref":"BI0934","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0934\n\nWhat are the effects of plastic pollution in marine waters?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Temporary formation of microplastics due to sunlight, wind and water action. |\n    | II. | Sea turtles consume plastic because of its transparency and resemblance to jellyfish. |\n    | III. | Plastic nets entrap fish, sea turtles and mammals. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSea turtles often mistake plastic bags and other transparent plastics for jellyfish, a primary food source. Consuming plastic can cause blockages in their digestive system, leading to malnutrition, injury, or death; **Statement II** is true.\n\nDiscarded plastic fishing nets, known as \"ghost nets,\" pose a serious threat to marine life. Fish, sea turtles, and marine mammals can become entangled in these nets, leading to injury, suffocation, or drowning; **Statement III** is true.\n\n**Statement I**: the temporary formation of microplastics due to sunlight, wind, and water action is false because the formation of microplastics is not temporary. Microplastics are formed when larger plastic debris is broken down by sunlight (UV radiation), wind, and water action. However, once microplastics are formed, they persist in the environment for long periods of time and are not easily degraded. Their presence is a long-term environmental concern, not a temporary one.\n\n**Statements II** and **III** are the only true statements, making **Choice B** the correct answer.\n\n**Choice D** is not the correct answer as it is missing the true **Statement II**. **Choices A** and **C** are not correct as they include the false **Statement I**."},{"ref":"BI1019","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1019\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nMuch of plastic pollution is non-biodegradable, which means it persists in the environment for long periods of time. Macroplastic pollution is defined as plastic debris that is larger than 1 mm and microplastic pollution less than 1mm.\n\nOn a field trip to a local beach in a temperate climate, students were asked to collect samples of sand and measure the number of pieces of plastic debris present. To do this, 5g samples of sand were collected and placed in a beaker. 50 ml of a concentrated salt solution was then added to the samples, which caused plastic debris to float to the surface. Plastic debris was removed from the samples, measured, and categorised, and the results were recorded in a table. The experiment was repeated in another school in a tropical climate, and the results were compiled.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Stability%20%26%20Change/images/BI1019%20table.png\" alt=\"BI1019 table\">\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Stability%20%26%20Change/images/BI1019a.png\" alt=\"BI1019a\">\n\n1.\n 1. Identify one possible controlled variable not already mentioned in the question. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Suggest the importance of having a standard classification for plastic debris in this investigation. [1 mark]\n\n2.\n 1. Calculate how many times greater the mean number of microplastic debris sampled was in the tropical climate compared to the temperate climate. [1 mark]\n\n 2. State which uncontrolled variable is most likely responsible for the higher numbers of microplastics sampled compared to macroplastics in the tropical climate. Give a reason for your answer. [2 marks]\n\n3. Students in both climates noted the varying sand particle size of the sand samples taken at each beach.\n\n 1. Describe a limitation to the methodology based on this information. [2 marks]\n\n 2. Suggest a method to reduce the variability in particle size. [1 mark]\n\n4. Evaluate the use of microplastic and macroplastic categories in this investigation. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ concentration of salt solution used to float the plastic debris [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ size **OR** diameter of beaker used to collect the sample [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ time allowed for debris to float before collection [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sand collected from same tide line **OR** distance up the shore [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ depth sand sample taken from [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ any other suitable collection method **OR** consistent sampling technique [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n A controlled variable would be the **concentration of the salt solution**, used to float the plastic debris ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ standard classification ensures that all researchers categorise plastic debris in the same way, which allows for uniform data collection **OR** consistency across data collection [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ reduces errors in identifying **OR** categorising plastic debris, leading to more accurate results [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ensures data can be easily compared to other studies on plastic pollution **OR** makes data comparable [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ enables trends to be analysed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allows for clearer communication of results **OR** allows understanding of results without confusion [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allows for replication of experiment **OR** repeatability [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ helps maintain the validity of the investigation **OR** reduces the risk of biased or subjective classification [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Having a standard size for the different categories of plastic supports the collection of **valid results** from students in the different climates sampled ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1.\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 2.18 (accept in the range of 2.18 - 2.2) [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Stability%20%26%20Change/images/BI1019msb.png\" alt=\"BI1019msb\">\n\n Calculation\n\n $\\dfrac{\\text{mean number in tropical climate}} {\\text{mean number in temperate climate}}$\n\n $\\dfrac{12} {5.5}$\n\n = **2.18** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2.\n 2. any **two** of the following (*answer must include cause with corresponding reason for full marks*)\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ light **OR** sunlight **OR** UV rays [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** it causes the breakdown of macroplastic into microplastic **OR** degrades plastic [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ greater abundance of diverse marine and terrestrial species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **WHICH** results in the fragmentation and breakdown of larger plastics through physical and biological processes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** higher temperatures could increase thermal degradation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ wave action **OR** ocean currents [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** stronger wave action **OR** ocean currents can break down macroplastics into smaller microplastic fragments more quickly [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Sunlight** ($\\checkmark$) is most likely responsible for the higher levels of microplastic in the tropical climate because there would be more sunlight, and light can **break down macroplastic into microplastic** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 1. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ different sand particle sizes may affect the separation of plastic debris [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the varying sand particle size between the different samples is an uncontrolled variable, meaning that extraneous factors could affect the results [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ different sand particle sizes introduces inconsistencies in the data making it difficult to compare the results accurately between samples [1 mark]\n\n **and** any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ finer sand may trap microplastics more effectively, leading to higher recorded numbers [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ coarser sand may allow microplastics to escape detection leading to underrepresentation of plastic debris [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **varying sand particle size** between the different samples is an **uncontrolled variable** that affects the methodology. **Finer sand** may **trap more microplastics**, leading to higher counts ($\\checkmark$), while **coarser sand** may allow **microplastics** to **escape detection** ($\\checkmark$). This introduces **inconsistencies** in the data and makes it **difficult to compare** the **results accurately** ($\\checkmark$) between samples collected at different beaches.\n\n3.\n 2.\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ use a filter **OR** sieve **OR** strainer to remove larger sand grains only before weighing the sample [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Students could use a **sieve** to remove some of the larger sand particles, but not the plastic debris ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **two** of the following (*must include at least **one** strength and **one** weakness to gain full marks*):\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ **Strengths** \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ categories help increase the validity of results **OR** allow different students to record valid results [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ having only two categories increases the rate at which plastic debris can be counted and sorted [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ allows a systematic approach **OR** provides clear **OR** consistent criteria **OR** allows the collection of accurate results [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ **Weaknesses** \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ the macroplastic category has a much larger range than the microplastic category [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ some microplastic debris may be hard, **OR** too small, to count [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ not all microplastic can be seen by the eye [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Having two categories helps to **increase the rate** at which plastic can be **sorted** or organised ($\\checkmark$). However, having only two categories may make it difficult to record all plastic debris, as some plastic, such as nano plastic, may be **too small** to see with only the **naked eye** ($\\checkmark$) and may need specialist equipment like a microscope or magnifying glass, otherwise it's not counted."},{"ref":"BI1097","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1097\n\nHummingbirds are the chief pollinators for a wide variety of flowering and fruit plants and are considered essential keystone species. Which of the following statements describe(s) a keystone species?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | The species, if removed, would drastically change the ecosystem |\n    | II. | The species whose influence is out of proportion to its abundance or biomass |\n    | III. | The keystone species is a dominant species, one that has a significant effect |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe keystone species, by definition, is a species within a community with a disproportionate role to its abundance or biomass. It has a unique role in maintaining the balance and structure of the ecosystem. If the species were removed, it would have severe ramifications for the rest of the community.\n\n[The analogy is the keystone in the architecture of an arch, if the stone is removed, the arch would collapse. Though small in size, this stone plays a key structural role in maintaining the arch.]\n\nAn example of a keystone species is the beaver, a small animal that builds a dam, flooding a river valley, allowing for fish, other aquatic animals and vegetation to flourish in the resultant water body that is formed. If the beaver were removed, the entire ecosystem would collapse.\n\nA keystone species is not a dominant species, one that has a significant influence on the community because of its abundance or high biomass, it can be relatively rare compared to the other species in the ecosystem. Therefore, **Choice D** is incorrect.\n**Choices A** and **B** do not include both the correct statements."},{"ref":"BI1098","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1098\n\nTraditional farming methods often come at a high environmental cost, and are not sustainable in the long term. Which of the following factors is correctly paired with the direct effect on the environment?\n\n| | Factors | Effect\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Soil erosion | Leaching of nutrients |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Leaching of nutrients | Increases availability of nutrients for crops |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Application of agrochemicals | Pollution of water |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Use of fungicide | Decreases availability of nutrients for crops |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nApplication of agrochemicals (**Choice C**) is the addition of a chemical used in agriculture, such as fertilisers, herbicides and other pesticides, growth regulators, and soil enhancers. While they increase crop yields and productivity, they can dissolve in water and leach into nearby water bodies, leading to **water pollution**. This pollution can harm aquatic ecosystems, degrade water quality, and contribute to issues like eutrophication, harming biodiversity and long-term agricultural sustainability.\n\nSoil erosion (**Choice A**) is the removal of soil due to the forces of wind or water, and it can affect the sustainability of agriculture. Once the topsoil layers are removed, **soil fertility is reduced**, and it can take a long time to be restored. Soil erosion may indirectly affect the leaching of nutrients, but it is not a direct effect.\n\nLeaching of nutrients (**Choice B**) is the process by which water-soluble nutrients are washed out of the soil, typically by rainfall or irrigation. This results in a loss of essential nutrients, **decreasing** the availability of nutrients (not increasing) for crops, and can negatively affect plant growth.\n\n**Choice D**, the use of fungicides, is the use of an agrochemical to prevent fungal diseases from crops. Fungicides are water-soluble and lead to pollution. They are not directly related to nutrient availability to crops."},{"ref":"BI1099","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1099\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Logging trees from forests and fishing from marine waters are two forms of income derived from natural resources. Outline the factors that need to be addressed to make both forms of income sustainable.    [5 marks]\n\n2. Describe how choosing to consume locally grown foods can reduce the environmental impact of agriculture, making it more sustainable.    [3 marks]\n\n3. Discuss the causes and impacts of eutrophication on water bodies due to agricultural practices.         [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **five** of the following:(*answer must includes at least **one** statement from each section*)\n\n    ***Harvesting trees OR timber as natural income*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sustainability of harvesting trees can be monitored by surveying soil characteristics of logged and unlogged sites in the forest     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ selective logging practices that only target mature trees **OR** target overpopulated trees **OR** ensure rate of logging does not exceed growth rate    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ promote reforestation **OR** afforestation **OR** replacing **OR** replanting trees [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ protect **OR** conserve biodiversity within forest        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ leave enough trees to produce fruit **OR** seeds for next season    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ monitor **OR** collect data to compare volume of trees removed with volume of growth    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ encourage certification programmes like the Forest Stewardship Council [1 mark]\n\n    ***Harvesting fish from marine waters as natural income*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sustainability fishing can be achieved by catching fish that do not diminish the fish stock    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the population size of the fish can be monitored     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the number of juveniles in the population of fish can be monitored, **OR** population of fish can be monitored to see if it has more younger or more older fish           [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ more younger fish in the population indicates a growing population, so more sustainable **OR** more older fish in the population indicates a declining population, so not sustainable    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the increase in number of fish annually will provide an estimate of what can be caught        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ maximum sustainable yield (MSY) is the amount of harvest **OR** the maximum average catch without reducing the potential for growth of the fish in the future    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ harvesting at lower than the MSY or at the rate of the MSY is favourable [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use fishing techniques **OR** equipment that minimise bycatch **OR** non-target species    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ designate marine protected areas **OR** breeding grounds    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enforce regulations to prevent overfishing **OR** illegal fishing practices    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ promote international cooperation in managing shared marine resources    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The sustainability of harvesting trees can be achieved by **replanting trees** ($\\checkmark$) that are cut down. The **volume** or mass of **timber** can be **monitored** and replaced by **planting saplings** that will **replace** the timber when **fully grown** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    Fishing practices need to be carried out so that by catching fish, the **original stock** is **not diminished** ($\\checkmark$). **Maximum sustainable yield** is the amount of harvest or catch that **does not decrease** the **potential** for **growth** ($\\checkmark$) in the future. **Harvesting fish** at a rate **lower than the maximum sustainable yield** ($\\checkmark$) will make fishing sustainable.\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ agricultural crops are usually grown in rural areas and transported to the cities        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transportation uses fossil fuels, which release carbon dioxide    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ release of carbon dioxide causes global warming     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ release of carbon dioxide increases the carbon footprint of crops    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ consumption of locally grown crops reduces carbon footprint     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ demand for unseasonal foods increases carbon footprint    **OR** consumption of seasonal foods will reduce carbon footprint      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reducing carbon footprint will make crops more sustainable        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ locally grown produce minimises need for packaging, as it is not being transported long distances and will therefore reduce waste        [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Transportation** of agricultural crops from rural areas to cities **uses fossil fuels** ($\\checkmark$). This causes the release of carbon dioxide, a greenhouse gas, **increasing** the **carbon footprint** ($\\checkmark$) of crops. **Locally grown produce** has a **smaller carbon footprint**, and because it is not being transported long distances, it needs **minimal packaging**, so there is **less waste** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    Also, eating foods grown **seasonally will reduce the carbon footprint** ($\\checkmark$) because some unseasonal foods, like strawberries, have to be brought in from where they are grown. In the long run, this is not sustainable.\n\n3. any **eight** of the following (at least one from each section):\n\n    ***Causes*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ excessive fertiliser used **OR** applying fertiliser before heavy rainfall        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ excess nutrient runoff from fertilisers     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ improper management of manure can lead to its runoff into water bodies        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nitrogen **OR** phosphates carried in runoff    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ erosion from agricultural fields can carry sediment into water bodies, which carries nutrients        [1 mark]\n\n    ***Impacts*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ excess nutrients to the water bodies will cause algae to grow rapidly **OR** algal blooms    [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ growth of algae will block sunlight to underwater plants, causing them to die    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plant and algae die under surface due to lack of light        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in nutrients **OR** dead organic matter leads to more algal growth    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in dead organic matter decomposed    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ aerobic bacteria are decomposers [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ greater oxygen demand **OR** biochemical oxygen demand **OR** BOD    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen-depleted water can lead to fish **OR** other aquatic organisms dying    [1 mark]\n\n    ***As eutrophication takes place, leads to further effects*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in net productivity    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ decrease in biodiversity **OR** diversity of organisms    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ shorter food chains    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ toxic cyanobacteria can take over    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ changes in fish communities        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some types of algae that thrive in eutrophic conditions can produce toxins harmful to humans and wildlife, which can enter the food chain and pose health risks    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ economic impacts on tourism **OR**, fisheries **OR** water treatment [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The runoff from using **fertilisers** ($\\checkmark$) in agricultural fields can contaminate local water bodies, leading to an **increase** in the **growth of algae** ($\\checkmark$) due to added nutrients. The algal growth will **block light** from penetrating the water bodies, so the **underwater plants** die and decompose ($\\checkmark$). The death of underwater plants will cause an **increase** in **dead organic matter** ($\\checkmark$), promoting the **growth** of **bacteria** ($\\checkmark$). These aerobic bacteria will cause a **decrease** in **dissolved oxygen** ($\\checkmark$) in the water as they respire and may lead to the **death** of the **fish** and other **organisms** ($\\checkmark$) in the water.\n\n    This can further lead to a **decrease in biodiversity** ($\\checkmark$) and the **growth of toxic algae** or bacteria ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1100","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1100\n\nWhich of the following is an example of ecological succession?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ A forest is cleared for farmland, and eventually, various vegetables grow in place of the forest.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ A glacier melts, and barren rock is exposed, which is eventually covered by small autotrophs.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Eventually, one tree species and an herbivorous bird species outnumber other organisms.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ A population of large herbivores migrates after consuming most of the grass in an area.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** starts with a barren, lifeless environment, the bare rock, and progresses as pioneer species like autotrophs (such as algae or lichens) colonise the area. The death and decay of these organisms, as well as other factors, leads to soil development.  Plants may begin to grow, and animal species eventually immigrate to the area. **Choice B** is, therefore, correct.\n\nAlthough a forest being cleared by humans is an example of a disturbance caused by a biotic factor (humans), the growth of agricultural plants is a controlled and managed process, not ecological succession. **Choice A** is, therefore, incorrect. **Choice C** is a partial description of a climax community, which tends to be dominated by fewer species as a result of competition, leading to a decrease in biodiversity.  These few species utilise a significant proportion of the habitat's resources. **Choice D** is incorrect as it is an example of migration.  Some species migrate from area to area, consuming significant proportions of the available food and then moving on to a new location in search of more food."},{"ref":"BI1101","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1101\n\nWhich of the following are the **same** for the processes of primary and secondary succession?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | The presence of pioneer species  |\n    | II. | A varying rate of succession |\n    | III. | The starting point of succession   |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nBoth primary and secondary succession involve pioneer species (**Statement I**). Pioneer species are the first organisms to colonise an area during succession, regardless of whether it's primary or secondary succession.\n\nThe rate of succession can vary in both primary and secondary succession (**Statement II**). The rate can depend on various factors, including the environmental conditions and the specific organisms involved. **Choice B** is therefore, correct, and **Choice A** is incorrect as it only includes one of the true statements.\n\nPrimary succession begins in barren, lifeless environments, for example, when glaciers expose bare rock, whereas secondary succession occurs in areas with existing communities that have been disturbed, such as after forest fires. **Statement III** is, not true, making **Choices C** and **D** incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1102","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1102\n\nWhich of the following is a characteristic of a climax community?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ High species turnover\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Constant change in species composition\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Stable and persistent community\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Low biodiversity","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA climax community is characterised by its stability and persistence over time, making **Choice C** correct.\n\nA climax community has low species turnover, therefore, **Choice A** is incorrect. In a climax community, the species composition remains relatively stable over time, with only minor changes in response to long-term environmental conditions. High species turnover is more typical of disturbed or transitional communities.\n\nWhile there may be some minor fluctuations in the presence of species, a climax community generally maintains a relatively consistent species composition, therefor, **Choice B** is incorrect.\n\nA low biodiversity, **Choice D**, is more characteristic of disturbed or simplified ecosystems. Climax communities represent a mature and diverse ecosystem with a wide variety of species that have adapted to the prevailing environmental conditions."},{"ref":"BI1111","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1111\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the primary sources of variation in a species.     [4 marks]\n\n2. Describe the possible outcomes of a population producing more offspring than available resources can support.         [5 marks]\n\n3. John Endler carried out a series of experiments in the 1970s on the selection pressures that influenced the colour of guppy fish. State the independent variable in Endler’s experiments and explain the selective pressures exerted on male guppies. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mutations result in changes to the base sequence of DNA        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mutations can be neutral, advantageous or detrimental          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ recombination of genes during sexual reproduction        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ random orientation of chromosomes in metaphase 1 of meiosis        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ crossing over during meiosis [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Genetic variation within a species arises primarily from several sources. First, **mutations** are spontaneous **changes in the DNA sequence**,($\\checkmark$) which can result in new alleles or gene variants. **Recombination of chromosomes** during **sexual reproduction** ($\\checkmark$) results in unique combinations of alleles in offspring. Then, in meiosis, **crossing over** ($\\checkmark$) can occur during prophase 1, and **random orientation of chromosomes** ($\\checkmark$) does occur in metaphase 1 of meiosis.\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in population increases genetic diversity **OR** variation **OR** increases gene pool        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ individuals with favourable characteristics OR advantageous traits **OR** better adapted, survive and reproduce     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ species may become better adapted over time    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in population causes an increase in competition for resources     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ depletion in essential resources lowers overall fitness of population **OR** reduces reproductive success **OR** struggle for survival **OR** increases mortality rates **OR** increased suffering of individuals less adapted **OR** with unfavourable characteristics  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ higher population densities increase disease transmission **OR** environmental degradation [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ emigration reduces gene pool **OR** changes population dynamics    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Overproducing offspring in an environment with limited resources can have the advantageous effect of **increasing** the **genetic diversity** ($\\checkmark$) of the population. This increased diversity can lead to more individuals with **favourable characteristics** surviving to reproductive age ($\\checkmark$). This could eventually lead to a **better adapted population** ($\\checkmark$) over the generations.  However, this population rise also has disadvantages, as there is **greater competition for resources** ($\\checkmark$), and if **essential resources** are **depleted** too much, the **death rate may increase** ($\\checkmark$). There is also an **increased risk** of the spread of **disease** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ independent variable is presence or absence of predators    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ predation pressure from visual predators **OR** birds **OR** larger fish     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ brighter colours make guppies more vulnerable to predation **OR** more conspicuous [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sexual selection pressure [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ female guppies prefer males with vibrant colours **OR** that are more colourful     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ female guppies prefer males with more elaborate courtship displays    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in low-predation environments **OR** areas with fewer predators, guppies have brighter colours **OR** in high-predation areas, males have duller **OR** less bright colours    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ environmental conditions can influence health **OR** size of male guppies, indirectly affecting attractiveness to females    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ male-male competition can affect size **OR** aggressiveness of male guppies    [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **independent variable** in Endler’s experiments is the presence or absence of **predators** ($\\checkmark$). **Predation pressure** from visual predators ($\\checkmark$) poses a shared risk to males with bright colours, leading to **duller colours** in **high predation** environments ($\\checkmark$) to reduce detection. **Sexual selection** ($\\checkmark$), driven by female mate choice, favours **more colourful traits** ($\\checkmark$) and more **elaborate courtship displays** ($\\checkmark$) if they are to reproduce successfully. **Environmental conditions**, such as **water temperature**, also affect the **health** and **size** of males ($\\checkmark$) and indirectly **influence their attractiveness** to females. Additionally, selection pressures stemming from **male-male competition** for access to females can result in variations in traits like **size or weaponry** ($\\checkmark$), particularly in areas with high population density or resource scarcity."},{"ref":"BI1457","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1457\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe Food Conversion Ratio (FCR) is a measure commonly used in livestock production to assess the efficiency at which animals convert feed into the desired output, such as weight gain. Below is a graph showing the mean food conversion ratios of four different farmed animals.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Stability%20%26%20Change/images/BI1457%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1457 fig1\">\n\n1. Deduce, with a reason, which animal is most sustainable to farm. [1 mark]\n\n2. Suggest why there is such a large difference between the food conversion ratio of cattle and fish. [2 marks]\n\nArtificial fertilisers are often used in agriculture and can be washed into watercourses, leading to eutrophication.\n\n3. Describe the process of eutrophication. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. The following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ crickets **BECAUSE** they have the highest food-to-body mass conversion ratio **OR** they have the lowest food-conversion ratio [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n **Crickets** as they have they are the **most efficient** at **converting food to body mass** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cattle have a higher respiration **OR** metabolic rate than fish [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cattle are endothermic, so need to expend a significant portion of their ingested energy to maintain a constant body temperature, whereas fish are ectothermic temperature of their bodies adjusts to match the water in which they live [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ digestion in cattle is less energy-efficient compared to the digestion processes in fish [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cattle spend energy on locomotion and supporting their body weight against gravity, whereas fish benefit from buoyancy, which reduces the energy needed for supporting their body weight and movement [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ growth rate of cattle is slower than that of fish [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n **Cattle**, being **endothermic**, expend **significant energy** to maintain a **constant body temperature**, contrasting with **ectothermic fish** whose body temperature adapts to their aquatic environment, leading to **lower metabolic demands** ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, **cattle** need to support their **body weight** against **gravity**, and their locomotion on land consumes **more energy** than **fish**, which are supported by **buoyancy** in water, **reducing** the **energy required** for **movement** ($\\checkmark$) and contributing to their lower food conversion ratio. This results in **cattle** having a notably **higher respiration** rate than fish ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **four** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nitrates **OR** phosphates enter water course [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ excess nutrients to the water bodies will cause algae to grow rapidly **OR** algal blooms [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ growth of algae will block sunlight to underwater plants, causing them to die [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in nutrients **OR** dead organic matter leads to more algal growth [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in decomposing dead organic matter [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ aerobic bacteria are decomposers [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ greater oxygen demand **OR** biochemical oxygen demand **OR** BOD [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen-depleted water can lead to fish **OR** other aquatic organisms dying [1 mark]\n\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n The runoff from using **fertilisers** in agricultural fields can contaminate local water bodies, leading to an **increase** in the **growth of algae** ($\\checkmark$) due to added nutrients. The algal growth will **block light** from penetrating the water bodies, so the underwater plants die and decompose ($\\checkmark$). The death of **underwater plants** will cause an **increase** in dead organic matter($\\checkmark$), promoting the **growth** of **bacteria** ($\\checkmark$). These aerobic bacteria will cause a **decrease** in **dissolved oxygen** ($\\checkmark$) in the water as they respire and may lead to the **death** of the **fish** and other aquatic **organisms** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1492","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1492\n\nThe diagram shows an example of ecological succession. Which stage represents a pioneer community?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Stability%20%26%20Change/images/BI1492FC.png\" alt=\"BI1492FC\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Joshfn. (2014, May 19). File: AP Biology - primary succession drawing.svg. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved November 28 , 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: AP_Biology_-_Primary_Succession_Drawing.svg.  Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Bare rocks\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Mosses and lichens\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Annual weeds, mosses and lichens\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Beech and maple broadleaf forest","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nThe pioneer community is the first stage of ecological succession and is characterised by species that are hardy, can colonise new areas quickly, and often can endure harsh environmental conditions. These characteristics enable them to establish a foothold in an area where other organisms cannot yet survive. In the diagram, these are the mosses and lichens. By breaking down rock into soil and retaining moisture, they help create conditions that allow other species to subsequently colonise the area; **Choice B**  is correct.\n\nBare rocks are the initial condition of an area before colonisation by a biological community, not a stage of the biological community itself. So, they are not a pioneer community but rather the substrate on which the pioneer community will establish; **Choice A**  is incorrect. Although lichens and mosses represent the pioneer community, annual weeds represent the next stage of succession; **Choice C**  is incorrect. Beech and maple broadleaf forests represent the climax community, as they represent the end point of ecological succession; **Choice D**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1493","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1493\n\nWhich process(es) can trigger succession within a community?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1.6em}$ New plants colonising an arid area\n\n- II. $\\hspace{1.3em}$ Recolonisation of an area by plants after a forest fire\n\n- III. $\\hspace{1em}$ Changes in rainfall patterns\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nSuccession is the progression of species that gradually replace one another over time within a community. It can be triggered by changes in both abiotic and biotic factors. New plants colonising an arid area (**Statement I**) would be an example of primary succession if the area was previously completely lifeless and soilless, such as bare rock or sand dunes, and plants begin to colonise it for the first time. The recolonisation of an area by plants after a forest fire (**Statement II**) is a classic example of secondary succession. After a forest fire, the existing community is destroyed or heavily damaged, but the soil remains, allowing plants and animals to recolonise the area. This process starts with pioneer species and gradually leads to the re-establishment of a forest ecosystem. Changes in rainfall patterns (**Statement III**) can directly trigger primary succession by altering previously uninhabitable areas, for example creating new ponds or wetlands or it can initiate secondary succession by changing the conditions of existing ecosystems so drastically that the current community composition cannot be maintained, leading to a significant shift in the species makeup over time. As all three statements are true, **Choice D** is the correct answer to the question.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **C** are incorrect, as they do not include all three statements."},{"ref":"BI1494","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1494\n\nThe coast of California is composed of a climax community of shrub-sized vegetation. Every 10-15 years the area is destroyed by naturally occurring forest fires, and it takes several years for the climax community to be restored. What is this an example of?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Stability%20%26%20Change/images/BI1493%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1493 fig1\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Region, U. P. S. (2011, August 11). Coastal sage scrub and riparian habitat at San Diego National Wildlife Refuge. Flickr.  Retrieved November 28, 2023, from https://www.flickr.com/photos/usfws_pacificsw/6033289259. Copyright under CC BY 2.0]\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Cyclical succession\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Reforestation\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Arrested succession\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Ecosystem restoration","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nCyclical succession refers to a repeated ecological sequence of events, where a community undergoes a series of changes following a disturbance (like forest fires), eventually returning to a similar state as before the disturbance. The Californian coastline community goes through a similar cycle of succession every 10-15 years. **Choice A**  is correct.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect as reforestation is the replanting of trees in an area that was formerly forested. \n\nArrested succession is when an ecological succession is halted at an early or mid-successional stage due to persistent environmental conditions or disturbances that prevent the ecosystem from developing into a climax community. The California coast scenario describes a system that does reach a climax community before being reset by fire, so it does not fit the definition of arrested succession; **Choice C**  is therefore incorrect.\n\nEcosystem restoration involves active human intervention to restore degraded, damaged, or destroyed ecosystems and habitats to their original state. While the cyclical process occurring on the California coast describes a natural process of ecosystem recovery following fires, it does not indicate that human intervention is involved in the restoration process; **Choice D**  is therefore incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1495","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1495\n\nEcologists studied plant communities on land exposed by a retreating glacier. They sampled sites that had been ice-free for different lengths of time, from 10 to 200 years. Which trend would be expected as the time since exposure increases?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Nitrogen-fixing pioneer species become more abundant as soil nitrogen content rises\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Soil pH increases as organic matter accumulates in the soil\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Total respiration by the community decreases as the community approaches the climax stage\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ A greater proportion of the ecosystem's nutrients is held in living biomass and recycled through decomposition","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nAs primary succession progresses, the community's biomass increases. Larger, longer-lived plants such as shrubs and trees build up nutrients in their tissues. When leaf litter and dead organisms fall, decomposers break them down and return the nutrients to the soil, where plants take them up again. So more of the ecosystem's nutrients are held in living biomass, and nutrient cycling increases; **Choice D** is correct.\n\nNitrogen-fixing pioneer species, such as legumes and alder, grow well early on because the soil has very little nitrogen. As they add nitrogen to the soil, they make conditions suitable for other species. These later species then outcompete the pioneers, so nitrogen-fixers become less abundant over time. This is an example of facilitation; **Choice A** is incorrect.\n\nPlant litter and humus build up as succession goes on. Their decomposition releases organic acids and carbon dioxide, so soil pH usually decreases over time; **Choice B** is incorrect.\n\nThe community's biomass increases during succession, so the total amount of respiration also increases. Near the climax stage, community respiration approaches gross primary production; **Choice C** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1496","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1496\n\nWhich statement concerning ecological succession is correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ A climax community is always reached\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Human activity does not affect ecological succession\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Communities become less complex as succession progresses\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Succession can occur in areas which are devoid of vegetation and soil","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nPrimary succession occurs in lifeless areas where there is no soil present, such as on bare rock exposed after a landslide, lava flows, areas left after a glacier retreats, or on artificial structures like buildings. Pioneer species such as lichens and certain types of algae are the first to colonise these areas, eventually breaking down rock into soil and allowing plants that require soil to take root, leading to a more complex ecosystem over time. **Choice D**  is correct.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect as a climax community may not be reached if cyclical succession is taking place or succession is arrested. For example, if land that is going through succession is changed into farmland, the climax community would not be reached. **Choice B**  is incorrect because humans can affect ecological succession such as through deforestation. **Choice C**  is incorrect because complexity generally increases as succession progresses."},{"ref":"BI1497","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1497\n\nWhich statement explains why climax communities are more stable than pioneer communities?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Climax communities are often less complex than pioneer communities\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Climax communities often have deeper and more nutrient-rich soils than pioneer communities\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Climax communities are often less species-rich than pioneer communities\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Climax communities provide less energy to support consumers","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation:}}$\\\n\nClimax communities are the last stage of ecological succession and are in equilibrium with natural environmental conditions. Pioneer communities are the first stage of ecological succession and are populated by hardy species that can withstand difficult environmental conditions. Climax communities often have deeper and more nutrient-rich soils than pioneer communities which means that they can support a range of organisms for a long period of time. **Choice B**  is correct.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect as climax communities are often more complex than pioneer communities as they contain a larger range of organisms than pioneer communities. **Choice C**  is incorrect as climax communities are often more species-rich than pioneer communities. Climax communities, with their greater plant biomass and diversity, generally have a higher primary productivity, which translates into more energy available to support a wider array of consumers. The complex food webs in climax communities efficiently recycle nutrients and energy, supporting larger populations of consumers than is possible in less developed communities. **Choice D**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1498","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1498\n\nWhich process would **not** lead to arrested ecological succession?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Adding organic matter and fertilisers to the soil \n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Hunting species to extinction \n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Grazing by livestock\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Climate change resulting in increased drought","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nAdding organic matter and fertilisers to the soil is likely to enhance soil quality and fertility, potentially accelerating succession by improving conditions for the growth of a wider range of plant species. This intervention can increase the rate at which a disturbed or degraded area recovers by providing the essential nutrients needed for plant growth, leading to quicker establishment of a stable ecosystem. In contrast, arrested succession is characterised by a halt or significant slowdown in the natural progression towards a more complex ecosystem, often due to persistent adverse conditions or disturbances.\\\n\n**Choice A is correct**.\n\n**Choice B**  is incorrect as hunting species can remove key species from an ecosystem, potentially halting succession if those species play critical roles in ecological processes such as seed dispersal, pollination, or predation that maintain the balance of the ecosystem.\\\n\n**Choice C**  is incorrect as grazing by livestock lowers the chances of a community reaching a climax as native vegetation may be destroyed, which reduces the biodiversity in the community.\\\n\n**Choice D**  is incorrect as drought will lower the volume of water that an area receives which may prevent a community from reaching a climax."},{"ref":"BI1499","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1499\n\nWhich set of statements is correct about gross primary productivity (GPP) and net primary productivity (NPP) in the early, middle and late ages of ecological succession?\n\n| | Early stages | Middle stages | Late stages |\n\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | GPP is low, and NPP makes up a high percentage of GPP | GPP and NPP decrease | GPP begins to increase, and NPP remains low |\n\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | GPP is low, and NPP makes up a high percentage of GPP | GPP increases, and NPP reaches a peak and falls | GPP remains high, but NPP decreases |\n\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | GPP is high, and NPP makes up a high proportion of GPP | GPP and NPP decrease | Both GPP and NPP continue to decrease |\n\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | GPP is high, but NPP makes up a low proportion of GPP | GPP decreases, but NPP makes up a higher proportion of GPP | Both GPP and NPP decrease to zero |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nGross primary productivity (GPP) is the total amount of energy that producers convert to organic matter (biomass) per unit area per unit time. Net Primary Productivity (NPP) is the amount of energy that remains available for the growth of the producer and for consumption by herbivores and decomposers after subtracting the energy lost from the producer during respiration. NPP = GPP - respiration by producer. Early succession ecosystems tend to have low GPP because plant biomass is initially low. However, because the biomass is low, the respiration rates are also low, meaning a relatively high proportion of the GPP is available as NPP. During the middle stages of succession, producer biomass increases, leading to an increase in both GPP and NPP. The ecosystem becomes more productive as producers synthesise more organic matter through the processes of photosynthesis or chemosynthesis. NPP reaches its peak during these stages because while GPP increases due to higher producer biomass, the energy used in respiration by producers also increases but not to the extent that it diminishes the gains in GPP. In the late stages of succession, the ecosystem stabilises, and while GPP remains high due to the substantial biomass of mature producers, NPP may decrease from its peak. This decrease is because a significant portion of GPP is used for respiration by these mature and often larger producers present in a climax community. **Choice B**  is correct.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect as GPP does not decrease in the middle stage of succession as the biomass in the community increases. **Choice C**  is incorrect as GPP is not high during the early stage of succession, as the biomass in the community is low due to a low number of producers. Also, GPP does not decrease in the middle and late stages of succession, as the biomass in the community increases, which increases the GPP. **Choice D**  is incorrect because the GPP is not high in the early stages of succession and GPP and NPP do not fall to zero in the late stages of succession if a climax community is formed."},{"ref":"BI1500","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1500\n\nWhich statement best reflects the role of keystone species in succession?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Keystone species typically prevent succession by dominating the ecosystem.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Keystone species only appear in climax communities and maintain the community structure.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Keystone species can influence the successional pathway by creating or modifying habitats.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Keystone species have minimal impact on succession as they are not involved in ecosystem engineering","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nKeystone species have a disproportionate effect on their environment relative to their abundance. They can modify the habitat in ways that affect which species are present during succession. For example, beavers are considered a keystone species because their dam-building activities create wetland habitats that support a diverse array of other species. This, in turn, can alter the trajectory of succession in the area. **Choice C** is therefore the correct answer. \n\nKeystone species do not typically prevent succession by dominating the ecosystem. In fact, they often facilitate succession by creating conditions that allow other species to thrive. **Choice A** is therefore incorrect. \n\nKeystone species are not exclusive to climax communities. They can be found in various stages of succession and can influence the community structure throughout the process, for example beavers creating new habitats that support an array of plants and animals. **Choice B** is therefore incorrect.\n\nKeystone species do have a significant impact on succession as they often play roles in ecosystem engineering, which involves modifying the physical environment in ways that affect other organisms. This can include activities like building nests, digging burrows, or altering the availability of resources such as food or shelter. **Choice D** is therefore incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1501","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1501\n\nWhich one of the following examples of disturbance is most likely to initiate secondary succession rather than primary succession?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The retreat of a glacier, exposing bare rock. \n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ A landslide that removes all vegetation and soil in an area. \n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ A forest fire that burns down all the trees but leaves the soil intact. \n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The drying of a salt lake, resulting in a newly exposed land surface.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nSecondary succession occurs in areas where there is already soil present, and some form of previous vegetation existed before the disturbance. The disturbance event, such as a fire, clears away the existing vegetation but doesn't destroy the soil, allowing for the relatively rapid regeneration of plant life from seeds, roots, or other surviving plant parts. **Choice C** is therefore the correct answer. \n\nThe retreat of a glacier, exposing bare rock, would typically initiate primary succession. In this scenario, organisms would colonise the newly exposed rock and gradually break it down to form soil, eventually leading to the establishment of a community of plants and other organisms. **Choice A** is incorrect.\n\nA landslide that removes all vegetation and soil in an area would also typically initiate primary succession. Similar to the retreat of a glacier, the area would start with bare rock or sterile soil, and the process of soil formation and colonisation by pioneer species would begin. **Choice B** is incorrect.\n\nThe drying of a salt lake can result in saline or sodic soils which may present inhospitable conditions for many plant species, indicating that primary succession, starting from sterile soil, is more likely. Secondary succession is possible if there are enough nutrients in the soil, however, this disturbance is not as likely to cause secondary succession as forest fires. **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1598","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1598\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe table below shows the biomagnification of DDT in an aquatic food chain.\n\n|  | DDT concentration (ppm) |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| Water | 0.05 |\n| Zooplankton | 0.10 |\n| Small fish | 0.50 |\n| Large fish | 2.00 |\n| Birds eating fish | 25.00 |\n\n1. Describe the change in the concentration of DDT as the trophic level increases.    [2 marks]\n\n2. Distinguish between biomagnification and bioaccumulation. [1 mark]\n\n3.\n    1. Outline the use of DDT, with a reason why it was so popular.    [2 marks]\n\n    2. State a property of DDT that makes it accumulate in biological tissues.    [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the concentration of a DDT increases with each ascending trophic level in the food chain  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the increase in concentration between water and zooplankton **OR** producers is two-fold   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ between zooplankton and small fish increase is five-fold                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase is four-fold between small fish and larger fish            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increases exponentially at higher trophic levels **OR** largest increase **OR** twelve-fold increase from the large fish to sea birds    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **DTT concentration increases** fivefold as we move **up the trophic levels** from **zooplankton to small fish** ($\\checkmark$). The greatest **increase is twelvefold from large fish to sea birds** ($\\checkmark$), showing it is the most concentrated in the top predators.\n\n2. the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ biomagnification is the process by which chemicals become more concentrated in the tissues of living organisms as they move up the trophic levels **WHEREAS** bioaccumulation is the build-up of non-degradable substances in the body of individual living organisms over time **OR** accumulation of a substance in an organism’s tissues because the organism absorbs the substance faster than it can eliminate it     [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Biomagnification** is the process by which **chemicals become more concentrated** in the tissues of **living organisms** as they **move up the trophic levels**, whereas **bioaccumulation** is the **build-up** of **non-degradable substances** in the bodies of individual living organisms **over time** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n    1. any **two** of the following (must include **one** use and **one** reason to gain full marks):\n\n    **Use:** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DDT was used to control pest outbreaks in cropland **OR** to control mosquitoes **OR** to help control incidence of malaria **OR** other suitable insect          [1 mark]\n\n    **Reason:** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DDT is effective at low concentrations in preventing outbreaks of pests        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ initially thought to be of low toxicity to mammals             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DDT has a broad-spectrum effect as an insecticide        [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    DDT is a synthetic pesticide that was widely used to **control insect populations**, particularly **mosquitoes** that **transmit malaria**, as well as **agricultural pests** ($\\checkmark$). Its use became popular because it is **effective** at **low concentrations** ($\\checkmark$) and against a **wide variety of insects** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n    2. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DDT is persistent **OR** chemically stable **OR** resistant to degradation so has long-lasting effects on the environment.    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DDT is fat-soluble **OR** is retained in fatty tissues without being readily excreted    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    DDT accumulates in biological tissues because it is a **fat-soluble chemical** ($\\checkmark$) and tends to be retained in fatty tissues."},{"ref":"BI1763","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1763\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nA study was conducted to investigate the relationship between water temperature and phytoplankton density in a bay along the southwest shoreline of Wales, U.K. This study was motivated by concerns about the potential effects of climate change on marine ecosystems, particularly how rising water temperatures could affect phytoplankton populations in the sea. Phytoplankton, which are the primary producers in marine food webs, play a crucial role in carbon cycling and are sensitive to changes in environmental conditions.\nEach month the chlorophyll concentration of seawater samples was measured, using a spectrophotometer. The concentration of chlorophyll is an indicator of phytoplankton density.\nIn parallel with the in situ measurements, mesocosm experiments, like the one shown below, were conducted in the laboratory using water samples from the bay over the same time period.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Stability%20%26%20Change/images/BI1763.png\" alt=\"BI1763\">\n\nDiscuss the advantages and limitations of carrying out these laboratory mesocosm experiments compared to the monthly measurements of chlorophyll concentration in situ. [3 marks]","markScheme":"any **three** of the following *(answer must include at least one advantage and one limitation to gain full marks):*\n\n***Advantages:***\\\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ ease of collection of continuous data **OR** frequent monitoring of phytoplankton growth **OR** chlorophyll concentration [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ precise manipulation of water temperature in mesocosm compared to fluctuating temperatures of seawater in situ [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ controlling flow of substances into **OR** out of mesocosms reduces risk of external contamination **OR** helps ensure stable **OR** consistent experimental conditions **OR** better control of environmental variables **OR** named environmental variable **OR** minimises influence of external factors **OR** pollution **OR** weather variations **OR** any other environmental disturbances [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ enhances reliability **OR** repeatability of the study **OR** conditions can be replicated across multiple trials [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ facilitates prolonged observation **OR** monitoring of biological responses over time **OR** can be maintained for extended periods under constant conditions **OR** allows for long-term studies [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ reduce the potential for ecological damage that could arise from in situ experiments **OR** minimises environmental impact **OR** ethical concerns associated with manipulating natural habitats [1 mark]\n\n***Limitations:***\\\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ may not fully replicate the complexity **OR** variability of natural conditions in the bay **OR** might not be directly applicable to natural settings [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ may not capture the full scale **OR** range of natural ecosystem interactions **OR** limited size can restrict the range of species **OR** interactions studied [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ can be costly **OR** resource-intensive **OR** can have higher costs **OR** more logistical challenges **OR** need specialised equipment compared to in situ studies [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ smaller scale of mesocosms compared to the actual bay can influence the behaviour **OR** growth patterns of phytoplankton [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ ethical concerns regarding welfare of organisms in mesocosm [1 mark]\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nMesocosm experiments offer the **advantage** of **controlled environmental conditions**, allowing for **precise manipulation** of **variables** like **temperature** ($\\checkmark$), and they **minimise environmental impact** by **avoiding direct interference** with natural ecosystems ($\\checkmark$). However, they may **not fully replicate** the complexity of **natural environments**, potentially leading to results that **lack ecological validity** ($\\checkmark$), and they can be **resource-intensive**, requiring **specialised equipment**($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2347","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2347\n\nWhich statement is correct for how alien plants have been managed in the rewilding of Hinewai Reserve in New Zealand?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Some are tolerated to provide shelter for young trees, which then outcompete the alien plants for light\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Tolerated to provide food for native herbivorous species\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ All are systematically removed to prevent competition with native plants\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ All are systematically removed and replaced with seedlings for native plants","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because, in the rewilding of Hinewai Reserve, some alien plant species are tolerated as they provide shelter for young seedlings of native species. Over time, once the native trees establish themselves, they outcompete the alien plants for light, leading to the eventual decline of these alien species. However, highly invasive species that threaten native ecosystems are not tolerated and are managed accordingly.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because alien plant species are not tolerated to provide food for native herbivorous species. Instead, their role is primarily to protect native saplings during early growth stages.\n\n**Choices C** and **D** are incorrect because alien plants are not systematically removed. Only highly invasive species are actively managed, while others are allowed to coexist temporarily to support the regeneration of native plants."},{"ref":"BI2391","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2391\n\nWhich of the following events could lead to the process of ecological succession?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | A nuclear accident that destroys plant and animal life within a 20 km radius. |\n    | II. | The dumping of spoil from shale oil mining in large heaps that resemble hills. |\n    | III. | Any human intervention on an ecosystem, followed by human abandonment.  |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because a nuclear accident, such as the Chornobyl (Ukraine) disaster of 1986, caused the abandonment of many towns, including nearby Pripyat. There are many examples where Pripyat’s once urban landscape has become rewilded as succession has taken place. For example, mature trees now stand around where the town’s sports centre is located.\n\n**Statement II** is true because this has occurred in several locations. For example, near Edinburgh (Scotland), where the remnants of shale oil mining were dumped into large hills or ‘bings’ in the 19th and 20th centuries. The tallest of these is 95 metres. Initially a barren landscape, succession on these bings has been so successful that they are now a protected nature reserve, with their own extensive and well-established food webs including some otherwise rare plant and animal species.\n\n**Statement III** is true because any human activity that disrupts a climax community will create the conditions for succession to take place. Even the simple act of felling a tree in mature woodland will allow sunlight to reach the forest floor, favouring species of ground-living plants, and in turn, the consumers that feed on them directly and indirectly. Humans must leave the area undisturbed for succession to continue and for a climax community to become re-established.\n\n**Choice D** is correct because it contains the three true statements (**I, II** and **III**).\n\n**Choices A, B** and **C** are incorrect because they all omit one or more of the true statements."},{"ref":"BI2707","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2707\n\nThe Amazon rainforest is a long-standing ecosystem, however, logging has caused instability. Which factors contributing to ecosystem stability are disrupted by logging?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Recycling of nutrients |\n    | II. | Genetic diversity |\n    | III. | Climatic variables remaining within tolerance levels |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because logging can disrupt the nutrient cycle by removing trees that would otherwise decompose and enrich the soil. \\\n**Statement II** is true because logging can reduce genetic diversity by removing certain species or age classes of trees, which may impact the overall genetic pool of plants and animals that rely on these species. \\\n**Statement III** is true because the Amazon rainforest helps to regulate atmospheric moisture (cloud formation, rainfall) through transpiration. However, deforestation disrupts this process, leading to reduced rainfall and increased temperatures.\n\n**Choice D** is, therefore, correct because all three statements are true."},{"ref":"BI2708","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2708\n\nWhy are sea otters and African elephants considered keystone species?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\" valign=\"top\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. $\\hspace{0.8em}$ | Their activities, such as eating urchins and clearing trees, disproportionately shape the structure of the ecosystem, supporting other species |\n    | II. $\\hspace{0.4em}$ | If the species were to disappear from the ecosystem, no other species would be able to perform the same ecological functions or roles|\n    | III. | They are apex predators that exert top-down control of populations in their communities |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because keystone species have a disproportionate impact on community structure. **Statement II** is true because the ecological roles of keystone species are often unique, and their removal can lead to significant cascading effects in the ecosystem.\\\n**Statement III** is false because while some keystone species may be predators, being an apex predator is not a requirement for keystone status. Sea otters are mid-level predators, and African elephants are herbivores — neither is an apex predator.\\\n**Choice C** is, therefore, correct because **Statements I** and **II** are true. \\\n**Choices A** and **B** are incorrect as they are missing one of the true statements.\\\n**Choice D** is incorrect as it includes the false **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI2709","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2709\n\nWhy are wolves considered a keystone species in Yellowstone National Park?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ They are the most abundant animal in their region, and their population density determines ecosystem health.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ They compete with other species for food, creating a more balanced community structure.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Their presence is key to maintaining biodiversity within their ecosystem.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ They physically modify the park’s landscape to produce and maintain habitats for other species.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because keystone species help maintain biodiversity by regulating populations and interactions within the ecosystem, which prevents imbalances that could lead to species loss. \\\n**Choice A** is incorrect. Their abundance or population density does not define keystone species.\\\n**Choice B** is incorrect. While keystone species can affect competition, their role is not defined solely by competition. \\\n**Choice D** is incorrect. Some keystone species may modify habitats (e.g., beavers building dams), but physical landscape modification is not a required characteristic of a keystone species."},{"ref":"BI2710","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2710\n\nData about the biochemical oxygen demand (BOD) and fish biomass (kg) were collected from a lake over a period of 12 months. The graph below shows the results:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Stability%20%26%20Change/images/BI2710.png\" alt=\"BI2710\">\n\nWhich of the following processes is most likely responsible for this change in the lake's ecosystem?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Acidification due to increased industrial activity\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Eutrophication caused by nutrient overload\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Water temperature rise due to climate change\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Overfishing and depletion of fish resources","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct. Eutrophication occurs when excess nutrients lead to algal blooms. When these algae die and decompose, oxygen is depleted, increasing BOD and causing fish populations to decline due to hypoxia. \\\n**Choice A** is incorrect. Acidification from industrial activity could lower pH and impact aquatic life, but it does not directly explain the observed increase in biochemical oxygen demand (BOD) and decline in fish biomass. \\\n**Choice C** is incorrect. Rising water temperatures can stress aquatic life, but they do not directly cause a significant increase in BOD or nutrient-driven oxygen depletion. \\\n**Choice D** is incorrect. Overfishing reduces fish populations, but it does not cause an increase in BOD."},{"ref":"BI2711","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2711\n\nThe diagram shows the response of a chaparral ecosystem to periodic wildfires. What type of ecological succession does this illustrate?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Stability%20%26%20Change/images/eDIT2711.png\" alt=\"eDIT2711\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Primary succession\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Secondary succession\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Arrested succession\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cyclical succession","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because cyclical succession describes the recovery and renewal of ecosystems that rely on regular natural disturbances. Chaparral vegetation is specifically adapted to survive and regenerate after fires, which are essential to its long-term stability. \\\n**Choice A** is incorrect. Primary succession occurs in areas where no previous ecosystem existed, such as after a volcanic eruption or the retreat of a glacier. Since chaparral ecosystems recover after fire rather than forming from barren land, this is not primary succession.\\\n**Choice B** is incorrect. Secondary succession occurs after a disturbance removes vegetation but leaves the soil intact. While fire initiates regrowth, chaparral ecosystems are specifically adapted to recurring fire cycles, making this a better example of cyclical succession. \\\n**Choice C** is incorrect. Arrested succession occurs when environmental conditions prevent the further progression of succession, often due to factors such as poor soil quality or human intervention. Chaparral ecosystems continue to regenerate, so this does not apply."},{"ref":"BI2712","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2712\n\nThe following data shows the percentage cover of annual weeds, grasses, and shrubs in an area over ten years. Which human activity best explains why this ecosystem has not progressed beyond the grass-shrub stage of succession over the past six years?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Stability%20%26%20Change/images/BI2712.png\" alt=\"BI2712\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increased levels of rainfall in the region\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Intensive livestock grazing and wetland drainage for agriculture\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Loss of permafrost due to global warming\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Pollution due to agrochemicals","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct. Intensive livestock grazing and wetland drainage for agriculture prevent succession by physically removing or suppressing tree seedlings. **Choice A** is incorrect. Increased levels of rainfall may actually promote growth and accelerate succession rather than prevent it. **Choice C** is incorrect. Loss of permafrost would not cause halted succession in a grassland/shrubland setting. **Choice D** is incorrect. Agrochemicals could harm plant life but wouldn't necessarily stabilise the ecosystem in a specific stage of succession."},{"ref":"BI2713","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2713\n\nIn which of the following environments is arrested succession most likely to occur?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ An area that has experienced a volcanic eruption and is now devoid of soil\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ A grassland with heavy grazing by herbivores, such as cattle\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ A wetland area that experiences seasonal flooding, allowing regular plant turnover\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ A forest recovering from a fire, progressing to a mature climax community","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because heavy grazing removes young woody plants, preventing the community from developing beyond the grassland stage. This halts the progression of succession, a phenomenon known as arrested succession. \\\n**Choice A** is incorrect. This is an example of primary succession, which starts from bare rock but is not necessarily arrested unless another disturbance occurs. \\\n**Choice C** is incorrect. This causes cyclic succession, where plant communities shift but are not permanently halted. \\\n**Choice D** is incorrect. This represents secondary succession, where the ecosystem gradually returns to a climax community rather than being stopped in an earlier stage."},{"ref":"BI2762","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2762\n\nWhich of the following is most likely to be a pioneer species after a glacier retreats, exposing bare rock?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Pincushion moss\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Buffalo grass\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Oak tree\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Prickly rose bush","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because moss is a common pioneer species that can grow on bare rock, sand, or other hard surfaces where soil has not yet developed. This makes them ideal for primary succession, such as after a glacier retreat.\n\n**Choices B, C** and **D** are incorrect because unlike mosses and lichens, grasses, trees and bushes require soil to anchor their roots and absorb nutrients. In primary succession (e.g., after a volcanic eruption or glacier retreat), there is no soil initially, making it impossible for them to establish themselves."},{"ref":"BI2764","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2764\n\nThe graph below shows the change in total ecosystem biomass over time during primary succession on a newly formed volcanic island.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Stability%20%26%20Change/images/BI2764.png\" alt=\"BI2764\">\n\nWhich of the following best explains the pattern shown in the graph?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Pioneer species dominate early on, but biomass declines as conditions become less favourable.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Rapid colonisation by herbivores boosts biomass early, but plant growth later slows due to overgrazing.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Biomass increases quickly as simple organisms establish, then levels off as the ecosystem reaches a stable climax community.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Initial volcanic activity provides organic compounds for early growth, but the eventual cooling of lava reduces productivity.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because this accurately reflects primary succession, where pioneer species such as lichens and mosses colonise bare rock, contributing to soil formation. Over time, more complex plant species establish and animal species migrate, increasing biomass. As the community matures and reaches a climax community, the rate of increase slows, leading to a plateau in total biomass.\\\n**Choice A** is incorrect because while pioneer species do dominate early, conditions generally become more favourable over time due to improved soil and nutrient availability. Biomass doesn't decline — it increases as more species establish.\\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because herbivores do not initiate primary succession, and early biomass increase is driven primarily by plants, not animals. Overgrazing may affect succession in some ecosystems, but it's not a typical explanation for the plateau in biomass during primary succession.\\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because, despite the high mineral ion content of newly-formed volcanic rock, there is a lack of suitable complex organic compounds (e.g. amino acids) that contain those minerals. Only succession can allow those organic compounds to be built up and to be recycled over successive generations. The graph does not indicate a reduction in productivity."},{"ref":"BI2766","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2766\n\nWhich of the following statements about cyclical succession is/are true?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Cyclical succession occurs when disturbances such as fire or grazing periodically reset an ecosystem to an earlier stage.|\n    | II. | Unlike primary succession, cyclical succession does not involve pioneer species recolonising an area. |\n    | III. | Some ecosystems rely on cyclical succession to maintain biodiversity. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because cyclical succession is driven by periodic disturbances like fire, grazing, or storms, which reset the community to an earlier stage. For example, in heathlands, fires remove older vegetation and allow heather to regrow.\\\n**Statement II** is false because cyclical succession can involve pioneer species recolonising an area after a disturbance. For example, after a fire, early-successional species (e.g., heather seedlings) recolonise the land first before it transitions to later stages of succession. \\\n**Statement III** is true because some ecosystems depend on cyclical succession to maintain biodiversity. Without periodic disturbances, dominant species may outcompete others, reducing overall diversity. Heathlands, for example, require periodic fires to maintain habitat diversity.\n\n**Choice B** is the correct answer as it only includes the two true **Statements I** and **III**.\\\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are incorrect as they include the false **Statement II**."},{"ref":"BI2767","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2767\n\nIn a temperate forest, periodic wildfires occur every 20–30 years, destroying most mature trees. After each fire, grasses and shrubs dominate before trees gradually return. Which of the following best describes this pattern?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Primary succession, since fire resets the ecosystem to its original state.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Linear succession, ending in a permanent climax community.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cyclical succession, where disturbances restart succession repeatedly.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Zonal succession, with predictable changes due to climate differences.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because ​​the ecosystem undergoes repeated disturbance (wildfire) and then goes through the same successional stages again: grasses $\\rarr$ shrubs $\\rarr$ trees. It doesn't move toward a single, fixed climax community — instead, it cycles back to earlier stages repeatedly. This is a textbook example of cyclical succession, where recurrent events (like fire) reset the community.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because primary succession occurs on newly formed or barren land where no soil exists — e.g., lava flows, glacial retreat. After a forest fire, soil remains, and the ecosystem recovers through secondary, not primary, succession. Also, primary succession isn’t cyclical — it’s a progression from bare substrate to climax, not a repeating loop.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because linear succession suggests a one-directional path toward a stable climax. In this case, the climax community (mature trees) is regularly disrupted, so the system doesn't stabilise permanently. The repeated resetting by fire means the successional path is not linear, but repeating. The term linear succession is rarely used, and the IB syllabus focuses on the use of primary, secondary and cyclical succession.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because zonal succession refers to regional patterns in vegetation due to climate zones (e.g., tundra $\\rarr$ boreal forest $\\rarr$ temperate forest). It’s about geographical variation, not repeating patterns within the same ecosystem. The succession described in the question is caused by local disturbances, not zonal climate variation. This term is also rarely used."},{"ref":"BI2769","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2769\n\nThe table shows data collected from an investigation into the effects of grazing on vegetation types over time:\n\n| Year | Undisturbed site | Grazed site |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| 0 | Grass | Grass |\n| 10 | Shrubs | Grass |\n| 20 | Young trees | Grass |\n| 30 | Mature trees | Grass |\n\nWhich conclusion is most likely to be formed from the data?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The grazed site reached its climax community earlier than the undisturbed site.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Succession has been delayed at the grazed site due to lower nutrient availability.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Grazing has limited species diversity, but not the rate of succession.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The grazed site shows arrested succession, preventing progression beyond early stages.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because it reflects that succession is not progressing in the grazed site — it remains grass-only.\\\n**Choice A** is incorrect because a grass-only system in this context isn’t the natural climax community; the climax would be a forest.\\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because while overgrazing can affect nutrients, the main factor shown here is continued grazing pressure, not soil fertility. Plus, nutrients often increase in grazed systems from dung/urine, and we have no data on the nutrient availability. \\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because the site hasn’t progressed beyond grass in 30 years, showing that succession is clearly being impacted."}]},{"name":"Unassigned","questions":[{"ref":"BI0100","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0100\n\nRain forests are known to have tall trees and a large biomass with high productivity, despite infertile soils. The growing conditions have been known to nurture the presence of many interdependent organisms.  These species also interact with the physical environment they inhabit.\n\nWhich of the following is described here?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$  A habitat\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$  An ecosystem\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$  A population\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$  A community","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAn ecosystem refers to an area where organisms interact with each other and with the physical environment.\n\nA habitat (**Choice A**) refers to the environment in which a species normally lives. A population (**Choice C**) is a group of organisms of the same species living in the same area at the same time which can interbreed. A community (**Choice D**) is a group of populations living and interacting with each other in the same habitat."},{"ref":"BI0110","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0110\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA student wants to set up an aquatic mesocosm using seawater to simulate conditions in the food web (shown in the image below) and test its stability. He used seawater and sand from the area to set up the system and added some small organisms to the mesocosm.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BI0110fw.png\" alt=\"BI0110fw\">\n[Source: Adapted from Cmglee. (2003, October). *File: Arctic Food Web.svg - Wikimedia Commons*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved April 14, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Arctic_food_web.svg. Copyright under CC BY-SA 3.0]\n\n1. In the food web shown, identify an autotroph. [1 mark]\n\n2. State a reason why phytoplankton shown in the food web would be a good addition to the mesocosm. [1 mark]\n\n3. Discuss how saprotrophs perform a necessary function for the mesocosm. [3 marks]\n\n4. Describe the disadvantages of using mesocosms in scientific investigations. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ phytoplankton [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phytoplankton are autotrophs **OR** can add energy to the mesocosm through photosynthesis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to provide energy continuously to the closed system [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    The phytoplankton **are autotrophs** ($\\checkmark$). Autotrophs are necessary for all ecosystems because they produce organic compounds through photosynthesis.\n\n3. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ saprotrophs secrete enzymes into the environment [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ saprotrophs break down food outside their bodies into simpler substances [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ saprotrophs are found in the water **OR**  sand [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ saprotrophs recycle nutrients in ecosystems [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ saprotrophs sometimes remove toxic compounds [1 mark]\n[3 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Saprotrophs **recycle nutrients in ecosystems** ($\\checkmark$) by **secreting enzymes into the environment** ($\\checkmark$) which **break down organic material into simpler substances** ($\\checkmark$) which they then absorb and use for food.  Saprotrophs can also **break down toxic compounds** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large organisms may not fit in the mesocosms **OR** sometimes not all organisms from the ecosystem can be added to the mesocosm **OR** mesocosms are sometimes limited in size[1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mesocosms are limited in their capacity to simulate natural conditions [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ small organisms added from the field to a mesocosm may be stressed [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mesocosms need to be monitored regularly [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Mesocosms are experiments set up in laboratories that are **limited in their physical space** ($\\checkmark$). Only small organisms can be used in the experiments and the organisms **can be stressed** once inside a mesocosm ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0111","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0111\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast how detritivores and saprotrophs obtain nutrition. [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline how a quadrat can be used to estimate the population of poppies in a field. [5 marks]\n\n3. Energy and nutrients move through ecosystems differently.  Describe how energy and nutrients move through terrestrial ecosystems. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following (must include at least one similarity and one difference to gain full marks):\n\n    ***Similarities***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** obtain energy/nutrients exclusively from dead organic matter/detritus/dead leaves/feathers/animal carcasses [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are heterotrophic [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ detritivores ingest dead organic matter **WHEREAS** saprotrophs secrete enzymes that digest dead organic matter outside their body [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ saprotrophs absorb soluble/digested nutrients externally across their cell membranes/walls, **WHEREAS** detritivores absorb digested nutrients internally across a gut/digestive system wall [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ example of detritivore e.g. earthworms **AND** example of saprotrophs e.g. fungi/bacteria [1 mark]\n[3 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Both detritivores and saprotrophs feed on **dead organic matter** ($\\checkmark$) such as leaves. **Detritivores** such as **earthworms**, which are found in soil, **ingest dead organic matter**, and digestion occurs in their digestive system. **Saprotrophs such as fungi and bacteria** ($\\checkmark$) **secrete enzymes** into the environment, **digest** the organic matter **outside of themselves** and then **absorb** some of the **resulting products** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ drawing shown of a field roughly divided to show where quadrats can be placed to sample the number of poppies [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ description of quadrat as a square with known dimensions [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ random **OR** systematic placing of quadrats in the field to measure poppy abundance [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ abundance of poppies in each quadrat recorded [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ repeated sampling [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ average (mean) number of poppies in quadrats is calculated [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ extrapolate to estimate the size of the population in the field [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ quadrats are easy to carry to the study site [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ population of poppies in the field using quadrat sampling is only an estimate [1 mark]\n[5 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/Picture%201.png\" alt=\"Picture 1\">\n\n    **(Quadrats placed randomly used to estimate numbers of poppies in the area shown)**\n\n    Quadrats, which **have a known dimension** ($\\checkmark$), can be used in fields  to estimate the abundance of sessile plants and animals. They are placed **randomly or in a systematic pattern** ($\\checkmark$), and the **number of poppies** in **each quadrat** is recorded ($\\checkmark$). This process is **repeated to obtain a representative sample** ($\\checkmark$) and the **mean abundance of poppies per quadrat is calculated** ($\\checkmark$).  The mean abundance of poppies per quadrat is used to **extrapolate an estimate of the population** ($\\checkmark$) of poppies in the field.\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy is not recycled in ecosystems [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light energy is converted to chemical energy by autotrophs through photosynthesis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chemical energy moves from autotrophs to heterotrophs through feeding [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when energy is transferred between trophic levels most of the energy is lost [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy is lost between trophic levels as heat **OR** excreted waste products **OR** indigestible parts of a plants or animals such as hair [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plants absorb nutrients and water from the soil [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nutrients move from one trophic level to the next through feeding [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ saprotrophs are decomposers, break down dead organic matter and release nutrients into the environment **OR** detritivores consume dead organic matter and release nutrients into the environment [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ saprotrophs secrete enzymes into the environment and digest their food externally **OR** detritivores ingest their food and break it down internally [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plants absorb nutrients released through decomposition [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nutrients are recycled in ecosystems [1 mark]\n[8 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Plants **are autotrophs** and **make organic compounds via photosynthesis using light energy** ($\\checkmark$).  **Primary consumers feed on autotrophs** and **energy flows** ($\\checkmark$) from primary to secondary to tertiary consumers. All organisms that feed on others are known as **heterotrophs**. **As energy is transferred** from one trophic level to the next, energy is **lost as heat** ($\\checkmark$) and **cannot be reused** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    **Saprotrophs are decomposers** and break **down dead organic matter** in the soil by **secreting enzymes** ($\\checkmark$).  This process **releases nutrients into the environment** ($\\checkmark$) and **plants absorb and use them** ($\\checkmark$). In this manner **nutrients are recycled** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0151","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0151\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare the concentration and potency of carbon dioxide and methane as greenhouse gases in the atmosphere.  [3 marks]\n\n2. Explain the reasoning of climate scientists for identifying human activity as the main cause of climate change. [5 marks]\n\n3. Discuss the consequences of global warming with reference to the climate, marine organisms, and changes to coastal regions and Arctic ecosystems. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide concentrations are higher **THAN** those of methane   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ methane is a more potent greenhouse gas **THAN** carbon dioxide (20:1)  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide stays in the atmosphere for a longer time **THAN** methane [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ methane is converted to carbon dioxide and water in the atmosphere   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide is the main greenhouse gas  [1 mark]\n[3 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    **The levels of carbon dioxide are more than methane** in the atmosphere ($\\checkmark$). **Carbon dioxide persists for a longer time than methane** ($\\checkmark$). **Methane is converted to carbon dioxide and water in the atmosphere** after a while ($\\checkmark$). The potency or **warming potential of methane is more than that of carbon dioxide**, almost 20 times more ($\\checkmark$). It can trap more heat energy.\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ scientists have calculated that Earth would be much cooler without the added greenhouse gases (GHG) [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ global temperatures are influenced by GHG concentrations  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the burning of fossil fuels has added carbon dioxide to the atmosphere [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ global fluctuations **OR** increase and decrease of carbon dioxide and temperature are strongly correlated [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ for hundreds of years, CO$_2$ concentrations were between 200 and 300 parts per million [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ currently CO$_2$ concentrations have risen to almost 400 parts per million [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ human activity is causing the increase **OR** release of GHG in the atmosphere [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the GHGs carbon dioxide, methane, halocarbons, and nitrogen oxides are associated with global warming [1 mark]\n[5 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Scientists have calculated **that the Earth would be much cooler without the effects of warming** caused by greenhouse gases ($\\checkmark$). **Global temperatures are influenced by the concentration of greenhouse gases** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    Starting about 200 years ago which was the start of the industrial age, carbon dioxide levels on Earth have been steadily increasing. This shows that **burning fossil fuels has added extra carbon dioxide to the atmosphere** and this will continue unless alternative technologies are established ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    Ice core data has shown that **carbon dioxide levels and temperature are strongly correlated** in the past ($\\checkmark$). Therefore, **human activities which release carbon dioxide and other greenhouse gases** are clearly responsible for the rising temperatures associated with climate change ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Maximum of **two** marks from each category\n\n    Climate (any **two** of the following):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ consequences will vary from region to region   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some colder countries will have warmer temperatures and may benefit through increased agricultural production   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased global temperatures cause heat waves **OR** typhoons **OR** hurricanes **OR** change of ocean currents     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased ice melting may disrupt ocean currents [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in frequency or severity of droughts **OR** desertification in some areas  [1 mark]\n\n    Marine organisms (any **two** of the following):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ coral reefs will undergo bleaching  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ loss of biodiversity in the oceans  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ higher temperatures will lead to ocean acidification [1 mark]\n\n    Change to coastal regions (any **two** of the following):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ coastal flooding **OR** coastal cities could be submerged [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some islands may disappear completely     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ existing coastal ecosystems may be lost    [1 mark]\n\n    Arctic ecosystems (any **two** of the following):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Arctic **OR** Antarctic ice could melt **OR** melting of glaciers   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ loss of habitat for animals (like polar bears)  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ permafrost melting **OR** permafrost melting will result in the release of methane  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the latitude and altitude of biomes may shift [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased spread of tropical diseases  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ other correct consequences can be accepted   [1 mark]\n[8 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    The **consequences of global warming will vary from region to region** ($\\checkmark$). Though mostly detrimental, some countries will have **warmer temperatures and may benefit from increased agricultural production** ($\\checkmark$). Increased temperatures are occurring in most places and in some places, **increased rainfall is causing flooding**.\n\n    **Coral reefs will undergo bleaching** ($\\checkmark$) due to **ocean acidification** ($\\checkmark$). There will be a **loss of biodiversity in oceans** as well ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    **Coastal cities will be submerged** ($\\checkmark$) when sea levels rise due to the melting of ice sheets and increased rainfall.  Some **islands may be completely submerged** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    Global warming will lead to the **loss of habitat for animals like polar bears** ($\\checkmark$) in the Arctic and penguins in the **Antarctic due to large areas of ice melting** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    Overall, scientists foresee an increase in catastrophic weather events for humans everywhere as temperatures continue to rise globally."},{"ref":"BI0197","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0197\n\nThe following table shows the diploid chromosome number of a range of species.\n\n| Organism | Diploid chromosome number\n| :-: | :-: |\n| Yellow fever mosquito \\(*Aedes egypti*) | 6 |\n| Fruit fly (*Drosophila melanogaster*) | 8 |\n| Spinach (*Spinacia oleracea*)  | 12 |\n| Carrot (*Daucus carota*) | 18 |\n| Rice (*Oryza sativa*) | 24 |\n| Giraffe (*Giraffa camelopardalis*) | 30 |\n| Giant panda (*Ailuropoda melanoleuca*)| 42 |\n\nWhich of these statements is correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The giant panda has more genes than the other species\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The yellow fever mosquito has the fewest genes from these species\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ The gametes of carrots have 18 chromosomes\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The chromosome number is a characteristic of a species","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAll species have a characteristic number of chromosomes (**Choice D**).\n\nGene number is not determined by chromosome number because chromosomes are not all the same size and do not hold the same number of genes (**Choice A** and **Choice B**). **Choice C** is incorrect because gametes have a haploid number of chromosomes which means gametes of carrot plants have 9 chromosomes."},{"ref":"BI0213","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0213\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the events which would lead to an individual being born with Down syndrome, which is characterised by the presence of three copies of chromosome 21. [4 marks]\n\n2. Explain how meiosis increases variation.    [3 marks]\n\n3. Outline the stages of meiosis. [9 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ non-disjunction as the cause of trisomy **OR** having three copies of a chromosome **OR** an extra chromosome [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous chromosomes do not separate properly during cell division     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ both chromosomes from a homologous pair move to the same pole **OR** end of the cell [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some gametes receive an extra chromosome    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the chances of non-disjunction increase with the mother’s age **OR** there is a positive correlation between the mother’s age and non-disjunction [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    **Nondisjunction** ($\\checkmark$) is the reason individuals are born with an **extra chromosome**  which is the cause of the condition down syndrome. Nondisjunction can happen when the chromosomes **do not separate properly** during the meiosis. ($\\checkmark$) For example, **homologous pair moves to the same end of the cell** ($\\checkmark$). The likelihood of non-disjunction increases for individuals with **older mothers** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.    any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ random orientation of **homologous** pairs in metaphase I [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ independent assortment increases the number of potential combinations of alleles [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ crossing over during prophase I **OR** exchange of sections of DNA between non-identical sister chromatids leads to new combinations of alleles **OR** recombinant chromatids     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reduction division **OR** production of haploid cells allowing for fertilisation   [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nMeiosis increases genetic variation through three primary mechanisms: **crossing over during prophase I**, where **non-identical chromatids** of homologous pairs exchange segments of genetic material ($\\checkmark$); **independent assortment during metaphase I**, leading to a multitude of possible genetic combinations ($\\checkmark$); and the formation of unique **haploid gametes** allowing for **fertilisation** during sexual reproduction ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.    any **nine** of the following (accept marking points clearly shown in an annotated diagram):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ meiosis has eight stages, prophase I, metaphase I, anaphase I and telophase I, and prophase II, metaphase II, anaphase II and telophase II    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ meiosis I is called a reduction division; it reduces the number of chromosomes by half **OR** produces haploid cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ meiosis II separates sister chromatids to produce cells with a single copy of one chromosome from each homologous pair [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ meiosis produces four unique haploid nuclei [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in $\\underline{\\textrm{Prophase I}}$ nuclear membrane breaks down and condensed chromosomes are clearly visible [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in $\\underline{\\textrm{Metaphase I}}$, homologous chromosomes line up on the equator [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in $\\underline{\\textrm{Anaphase I}}$, spindle fibres contract and separate the chromosomes of each homologous pair **OR** homologous chromosomes are pulled to opposite poles [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in $\\underline{\\textrm{Telophase I}}$ two haploid nuclei are formed    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in $\\underline{\\textrm{Prophase II}}$, chromosomes condense, and the nuclear membrane breaks down [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in $\\underline{\\textrm{Metaphase II}}$, chromosomes line up on the equator **OR** each chromosome is attached to both poles (at the centromere)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in $\\underline{\\textrm{Anaphase II}}$, spindle fibres contract and sister chromatids are separated and move to opposite poles [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in $\\underline{\\textrm{Telophase II}}$ two haploid nuclei are formed from each cell, 4 haploid cells in total     [1 mark]\n\n    [9 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    In meiosis, there are **two cycles of prophase, metaphase, anaphase and telophase** (meiosis I and meiosis II). Meiosis I separates homologous chromosomes and produces 2 haploid nuclei; this is called a **reduction division.** ($\\checkmark$) Meiosis II separates **sister chromatids** ($\\checkmark$) and produces **four haploid nuclei** ($\\checkmark$) in total.\n\n    **Meiosis I**\n\n    In **prophase I**, chromosomes are made of sister chromatids and are **visible** (e.g. 46 chromosomes with two strands in human cells). **The nuclear membrane breaks down** ($\\checkmark$). In **metaphase I**, **homologous chromosomes line up on the equator** ($\\checkmark$). The centromere of each chromosome is attached to one pole. In **anaphase I** spindle fibres contract and **homologous chromosomes are pulled to opposite poles** ($\\checkmark$). In **telophase I, two haploid nuclei form** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    **Meiosis II**\n\n    In **prophase II** the **nuclear membrane breaks down, and chromosomes condense** (haploid number, e.g. 23 in a human cell) ($\\checkmark$). In **metaphase II, chromosomes are lined up on the equator** ($\\checkmark$), and each centromere is attached to both poles. In **anaphase II, spindle fibres contract** and **sister chromatids are separated** and are pulled to opposite poles ($\\checkmark$). **In telophase II**, two haploid nuclei form from each cell containing haploid number of chromosomes ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0225","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0225\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n1. Describe how parents with blood type A and blood type B can have a child with blood type O.     [4 marks]\n\n2.    Outline, using a named example, how polygenic inheritance results in continuous variation [4 marks]\n\n3.    Explain the role of inheritance in genetic diseases.    [8 marks]","markScheme":"1.    any **four** of the following (accept any marking point clearly shown in a genetic diagram)\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the allele for blood group A **OR** allele I$^A$, is dominant over the allele for blood group O **OR** allele i  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the allele for blood group B **OR** allele I$^B$, is dominant over the allele for blood group O **OR** allele i  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ parents with blood group A or blood group B could be heterozygous **OR** I$^A$i and I$^B$i [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a parent who is heterozygous for blood group A can pass on the allele for blood group O **OR** allele i    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a parent who is heterozygous for blood group B can pass on the allele for blood group O **OR** allele i     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ offspring that receive the allele for blood type O **OR** i allele from both parents will be blood group O    [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    The alleles for blood type A and blood type B are **dominant** to the allele for blood type O ($\\checkmark$). Parents with blood type A or B could be **heterozygous** ($\\checkmark$) and possess the recessive allele for blood type O ($\\checkmark$). If the offspring **inherits the recessive allele** ($\\checkmark$) from both parents, then they will have blood type O.\n\n    Parents:     $\\hspace{2.5em}$      **I$^A$ i**  $\\hspace{3em}$ x   $\\hspace{2.5em}$  **I$^B$ i**\n\n    Gametes:        $\\hspace{1.5em}$     **I$^A$ and i**  $\\hspace{1.5em}$ x   $\\hspace{1.5em}$  **I$^B$ and i**\n\n    Punnett square:\n\n    | | I$^B$ | i |\n    | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n    | **I$^A$**|  $\\hspace{1.5em}$ I$^A$ I$^B$ $\\hspace{1.5em}$  |  $\\hspace{1.5em}$ I$^A$ i  $\\hspace{1.5em}$  |\n    | **i** |  $\\hspace{1.5em}$ I$^B$ i $\\hspace{1.5em}$  |  $\\hspace{1.5em}$ i i $\\hspace{1.5em}$  |\n\n    Genotypes of offspring: $\\hspace{1.5em}$ $\\hspace{1.5em}$ **I$^A$ I$^B$ $\\hspace{1.5em}$ :  $\\hspace{1.5em}$ I$^A$ i  $\\hspace{1.5em}$ : $\\hspace{1.5em}$ I$^B$ i $\\hspace{1.5em}$ : $\\hspace{1.5em}$ i i $\\hspace{1.5em}$**\n\n    $\\hspace{14em}$ **1 $\\hspace{2.8em}$ : $\\hspace{2em}$ 1 $\\hspace{2.2em}$: $\\hspace{2.5em}$1$\\hspace{2.1em}$ :$\\hspace{1.8em}$ 1**\n\n    Phenotypes of offspring:  $\\hspace{1.5em}$ **blood type AB : blood type A : blood type B : blood type O**\n\n    $\\hspace{15.5em}$ **1 $\\hspace{3em}$ : $\\hspace{3em}$ 1 $\\hspace{2.5em}$: $\\hspace{2.5em}$1$\\hspace{3.2em}$ :$\\hspace{3.5em}$ 1**\n\n2.  any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ human skin colour OR any other suitable example                [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at least three OR several genes control phenotype                [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ each gene has two or more alleles contributing to the phenotype            [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ no single allele is solely responsible for the phenotype                [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ many possible allele combinations                         [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ additive OR cumulative effect of alleles results in continuous range of phenotypes    [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phenotype shows a bell-shaped OR normal distribution in the population        [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $ \\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    **Human skin colour** ($\\checkmark$) is controlled by **several genes**  ($\\checkmark$).  **Each allele** has a small **dditive effect** on the amount of melanin produced.  The **many possible combinations** of **alleles** ($\\checkmark$) result in a **continuous range**  ($\\checkmark$) of skin colours.\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic diseases can be caused by inherited mutant alleles [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ alleles are passed to offspring via haploid gametes **OR** sperm and eggs [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gametes carry one allele per gene [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fertilisation restores diploidy **OR** combines parental alleles [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allele combination **OR** genotype influences whether genetic disease is expressed [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ autosomal dominant disease require one disease allele to be expressed [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ huntington’s disease **OR** any other valid autosomal dominant example, is an autosomal dominant condition [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ autosomal recessive disease requires two disease alleles to be expressed [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ heterozygotes can be carriers of autosomal recessive diseases [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phenylketonuria (PKU) **OR**  any other valid autosomal recessive example, is an autosomal recessive condition [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ many sex-linked genes are carried on the X chromosome [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ X-linked recessive diseases are more common in biological males [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ haemophilia **OR**  any other valid X-linked recessive example, is an X-linked recessive condition [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ affected X-linked recessive biological mother will pass diesease to all biological sons [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carrier X-linked recessive biological mother will pass disease to 50% of biological sons **OR** 50% biological daughters, if father unaffected [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    Genetic diseases can be caused by **inheriting mutant alleles** of genes ($\\checkmark$). Alleles are passed from parents to **offspring in haploid gametes** ($\\checkmark$), and **fertilisation** combines **one allele** from **each parent** to form the offspring’s genotype ($\\checkmark$). If a disease allele is **dominant**, only **one copy** is needed for the condition to be **expressed** ($\\checkmark$), for example, **Huntington’s disease** ($\\checkmark$). If a disease allele is **recessive, two copies** are needed for the condition to be **expressed** ($\\checkmark$), and people with **one recessive** allele are **carriers** who can pass it on ($\\checkmark$). Some genetic diseases are **sex-linked** because the gene is on the **X chromosome**, so X-linked recessive diseases are **more common** in biological **males** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0236","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0236\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe diagram represents the evolution of features in the forefoot of horses from the oldest ancestor (a) to modern day horses (e).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BI0236.png\" alt=\"BI0236\">\n[Source: Heard, J.M. (2015, August 8). *Image from page 65 of \"breeding, training, management and diseases of the horse and other domestic animals*\" (1893). Flickr. Retrieved June 7, 2022, from https://www.flickr.com/photos/internetarchivebookimages/20385840566/. CC0 1.0]\n\n1.\n    1. Distinguish between the earliest and most recent forefoot. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Explain the mechanism through which the horse forefoot has changed over time. [4 marks]\n\n    3. Suggest what would happen if ancestral horses were brought back and introduced into the wild today. Justify your answer. [1 mark]\n\n2. State the definition of evolution. [1 mark]\n\n3. Different horse breeds exist in varying geographical locations. Suggest **one** possible way to show that these horses living in different geographical locations were from the same species. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  any **two** of the following:\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ more recent forefoot is longer **THAN** the older forefoot (or vice versa) [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ more recent forefoot is more narrow **THAN** the older forefoot (or vice versa) [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ more recent forefoot has **FEWER** toes **OR** only one toe **COMPARED** to the older forefoot **OR** more recent foreleg has **FEWER** individual **OR** fused bones **COMPARED** to the older forelegs [1 mark]\n\n        [2 marks]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    The more recent forefoot is **longer than the earlier forefoot** ($\\checkmark$) and has fewer **toes** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    2. any **four** of the following:\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ heritable variation in toe number OR forefoot length existed in the population    [1 mark]\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ random mutation produced individuals with fewer toes OR more weight on the central toe OR longer forefoot                                    [1 mark]\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ environmental selection pressure meant this phenotype gave a fitness advantage OR better survival OR greater reproductive success                        [1 mark]\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ individuals with the advantageous trait survived and reproduced more than others    [1 mark]\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ trait was inherited by offspring                            [1 mark]\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ over many generations, the frequency of the advantageous allele increased in the population                                        [1 mark]\n\n        [4 marks]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    **Random mutations** and recombination produced individuals with **variation** in forefoot structure, including **reduced toe number**  and a **longer central toe** ($\\checkmark$). In **changing environments**, individuals with these traits had a **fitness advantage** ($\\checkmark$), and their **offspring inherited the alleles** ($\\checkmark$). Over **many generations, allele frequencies shifted** ($\\checkmark$), and the reduced-toe forefoot became common.\n\n    3. **one** of the following:\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ wouldn\\'t survive **OR** would die **BECAUSE** they are less adapted to the environment **OR** would be outcompeted **OR** would be selected against **OR** other reasonable justification [1 mark]\n        [1 mark]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    Ancestral horses **would not survive**  ($\\checkmark$) in the wild because they are not as well **adapted to the environment** ($\\checkmark$) as modern horses.\n\n2. $\\ce{ -> }$ heritable characteristics of a species change over time [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    Evolution is when the **heritable characteristics** ($\\checkmark$) of a species change over time.\n\n3. any **two** of the following (*must be from the same pair of statements to gain full marks*):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cross-breed the horses [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if produce fertile offspring they are same species [1 mark]\n\n    **OR**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ analyse horses' DNA [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ look for very similar base sequences **OR** same barcode [1 mark]\n\n    **OR**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ compare chromosome number **OR** karyotypes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ same chromosome number supports being the same species [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    **Cross-breed** the **horses** from different locations ($\\checkmark$). If they **produce fertile offspring** then they are of the same species ($\\checkmark$), ."},{"ref":"BI0249","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0249\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n1. Describe how mutations can result in natural selection. [4 marks]\n\n2. Discuss the advantages and disadvantages of a population producing more offspring than the environment can support. [4 marks]\n\n3. Explain how the misuse of antibiotics can lead to the development of bacterial resistance. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1.    any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mutations are a random change in the DNA sequence [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ natural selection as the mechanism by which organisms better adapted survive and pass on their traits **OR** characteristics **OR** genes to their offspring     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase variation **OR** diversity within a population **OR** species  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mutations can be neutral, advantageous or detrimental    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ individuals with advantageous mutations are better adapted for the environment  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ individuals with advantageous mutations, survive and reproduce **OR** they pass these mutations onto their offspring [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    Mutations are **random changes in the DNA sequence** ($\\checkmark$)\n that can lead to an increase in the **variation** within a species ($\\checkmark$). Individuals with advantageous mutations that make them **better adapted** to their environment ($\\checkmark$) **survive and reproduce**, so they are able to pass these advantageous mutations onto their **offspring** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.    any **four** of the following (advantages and disadvantages must be present for full marks):\n\n    **Advantages:**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increases genetic diversity  **OR** variation [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ individuals with favourable characteristics **OR** advantageous traits **OR** better adapted, survive and reproduce    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ species becomes better adapted (to the environment) over time [1 mark]\n\n    **Disadvantages:**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increases competition for resources    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ higher population densities increase disease transmission **OR** environmental degradation [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ struggle for survival **OR** suffering of individuals less adapted **OR** with unfavourable characteristics    [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    Producing more offspring than the environment can support can be advantageous because it can **increase the genetic diversity** ($\\checkmark$) within a population\nand increasing diversity allows individuals with more **favourable characteristics** to survive ($\\checkmark$)\n, which leads to the population becoming **better adapted** to the environment over time ($\\checkmark$)\n. However, this can also be a disadvantage because if there are a large number of individuals within a population it could increase the **spread of disease** as the population is more dense ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.    any **eight** of the following (maximum of 7 marks if no example of misuse is stated):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibiotics as a selective pressure [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some bacteria are resistant to some antibiotics **OR** possess a mutation **OR** gene for antibiotic resistance    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ bacteria (with antibiotic resistance) are not killed by antibiotics **OR** antibiotics are ineffective [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transfers of the gene for antibiotic resistance to another bacterium through conjugation **OR** using plasmids     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ resistance gene can pass to next generation **OR** offspring, through cell division **OR** binary fission [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ resistant bacteria have no competition and proliferate in the absence of other bacteria **OR** proportion of resistance bacteria in a population increase [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ MRSA (methicillin resistant *Staphylococcus aureus*) is an example of antibiotic resistant bacteria **OR** any other valid example [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ example of misuses such as over prescription of antibiotics **OR** failure to complete the full course    [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    **Overprescribing antibiotics** to patients who don’t need them can lead to the development of bacterial resistance ($\\checkmark$). This is an issue which is becoming a global health concern.\n\n    Some bacteria may **possess a mutation** which confers resistance to an antibiotic ($\\checkmark$). If this antibiotic is used to treat a bacterial infection, then it acts as a **selective pressure** ($\\checkmark$), which results in the death of bacteria that do not possess resistance, but the **survival** of those that do ($\\checkmark$). Surviving bacteria are able to **pass on the gene for resistance** to the next generation through **binary fission** ($\\checkmark$) or to other bacteria through **plasmids** ($\\checkmark$). This would result in the **increase in the proportion** of resistant bacteria in a population ($\\checkmark$), which means that another antibiotic would have to be used to help treat the infection. Some bacteria, such as **MRSA**, can have multiple resistances, ($\\checkmark$) which means that a combination of antibiotics must be used."},{"ref":"BI0330","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0330\n\nWhat is the main role of the ribosomes labelled in the micrograph?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/rough%20endoplasmic%20reticulum%20RER%20rough%20ER.png\" alt=\"rough endoplasmic reticulum RER rough ER\">\n\n[Adapted from OpenStax. (2016, May 18). *File:0313 endoplasmic reticulum B en.png* - Wikimedia Commons. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 1, 2022, from https ://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0313_Endoplasmic_Reticulum_b_en.png. Copyright free]\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Synthesise enzymes to be used within the cytoplasm\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Transport mRNA from the nucleus to the cytoplasm\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Destroy proteins that the cell no longer needs\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Synthesise proteins for use in lysosomes","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nBound ribosomes synthesise proteins for secretion outside of the cell, membrane-associated proteins or proteins used in lysosomes (**Choice D**).\n\nWhile ribosomes do synthesize enzymes, the ribosomes attached to the rough ER specifically target the proteins they synthesize for secretion or for use in membrane-bound organelles (like lysosomes) or the cell membrane, rather than for immediate use within the cytoplasm. Proteins meant to function within the cytoplasm are typically synthesized by free ribosomes that are not attached to the rough ER. **Choice A**  is incorrect. \\\n\n**Choice B**  is incorrect as bound ribosomes do not transport mRNA from the nucleus to the cytoplasm. \\\n\n**Choice C**  is incorrect as ribosomes do not destroy proteins."},{"ref":"BI0337","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0337\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast the structure and function of free and bound ribosomes. [3 marks]\n\n2. Describe the steps in the initiation of translation. [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain how the bonds between amino acids contribute to the structure of proteins. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following (answer can be in a table format):\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ **BOTH** free and bound ribosomes synthesise proteins [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ **BOTH** free and bound ribosomes consist of small and big subunits, **OR** both have A P E and mRNA binding sites [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ free ribosomes are found in the cytoplasm **WHILE** bound ribosomes are attached to the endoplasmic reticulum **OR** ER [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ free ribosomes synthesise proteins for use within the cell unattached to any structures, **BUT** bound ribosomes synthesise proteins for secretion **OR** use in the lysosomes **OR** membrane-bound proteins [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Both free and bound ribosomes **synthesise proteins** ($\\checkmark$). However, free ribosomes are found floating in the **cytoplasm** , and bound ribosomes are attached to the **endoplasmic reticulum** ($\\checkmark$). Free ribosomes synthesise proteins for use **within the cell** , but bound ribosomes synthesise proteins for **secretion** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ mRNA molecule binds to the small ribosomal subunit [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ initiator tRNA (carrying methionine) binds to the start codon [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ mRNA/tRNA **OR** codon/anticodon binding is facilitated by complementary base pairing [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ large ribosomal subunit binds to the initiator tRNA at the P site [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ amino acid carrying tRNA attaches to the next codon at the A site [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ peptide bond is formed between amino acids in the P and A site [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ ribosome moves a codon space on the mRNA, so that tRNA at start codon is now at E site [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Translation occurs in the cytoplasm. During the initiation stage, the mRNA molecule binds to the **small ribosomal subunit** ($\\checkmark$). Next, the **initiator tRNA** binds to the start codon ($\\checkmark$) on the mRNA, and finally, the **large ribosomal subunit** binds to the tRNA through the **P site** ($\\checkmark$) in the ribosome and then the next codon signals another tRNA to bind in the **A site** ($\\checkmark$), using complementary base pairing.  A **peptide bond** ($\\checkmark$) is formed between the amino acids carried by tRNAs in the P and A sites.\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ primary structure of a protein is the sequence of amino acids [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ peptide bonds link amino acids in the polypeptide chain [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ secondary structure is the formation of alpha helices **OR** beta pleated sheets within the same polypeptide [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ (secondary structure is) the result of hydrogen bonding **OR** hydrogen bonds form between the carboxyl and amino group **OR** C=O and N-H [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ tertiary structure is caused by interactions between R groups of amino acids [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ (in tertiary structure) positively charged R groups interact with negative charged R groups [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ (in tertiary structure) polar R groups will form hydrogen bonds with other polar R groups [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ (in tertiary structure) disulphide, **OR** S-S, bridges can form between cysteines in R groups [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ (tertiary structure) gives the polypeptide its 3D shape [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ quaternary structure is formed in proteins with more than one polypeptide chain [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ (quaternary structure is) formed by the interactions between R groups on different polypeptide chains [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ (quaternary structure is) supported by S-S bridges, **OR** any other examples of R group bounds in the previous marking statements  [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    The primary structure of a protein is the **sequence** of amino acids ($\\checkmark$), which are linked together with **peptide bonds** ($\\checkmark$). The secondary structure of a protein is the formation of **alpha-helices** ($\\checkmark$), which are stabilised by **hydrogen bonding** between the carboxyl and amino groups ($\\checkmark$) from peptide bonds between different amino acids in the same chain. The tertiary structure is caused by interactions between **R groups** ($\\checkmark$), which gives the polypeptide its **3D shape** ($\\checkmark$). Some of the bonds formed between R groups include **polar R groups**  forming hydrogen bonds with other polar R groups ($\\checkmark$) and cysteines in R groups, forming **disulphide bridges** ($\\checkmark$). The quaternary structure of proteins describes proteins with **more than one** polypeptide chain ($\\checkmark$) in their structure. The quaternary structure is supported by the same type of R-R group interactions as the tertiary level, such as **disulphide bridges** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0376","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0376\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Draw a labelled diagram to represent the process of crossing over.     [4 marks]\n\n2. Compare and contrast random orientation and crossing over. [4 marks]\n\n3. Outline the stages of meiosis. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following are correctly shown in a labelled diagram:\n\n    **Labels**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous chromosome pair   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ synapsis (chromosomes attached together) **OR** tetrad      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chiasmata (between non-sister chromatids)      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ recombinant chromatids    [1 mark]\n\n    **Drawing**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ diagram is shown as at least three stages –  aligned chromosome pair, chiasmata between homologous chromosomes, resulting in two chromosomes with swapped material       [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BI0376.png\" alt=\"BI0376\">\n\n2. any **four** of the following (answer can be in a table format):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** occur during meiosis I    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** promote variation      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are random processes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Crossing over occurs during prophase I **WHEREAS** random orientation occurs during metaphase I [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ During crossing over, there is an exchange of genetic information **OR** alleles **WHEREAS** during random orientation pairs of homologous chromosomes align randomly along the equator         [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Both random orientation and crossing over occur during **meiosis I** ($\\checkmark$), and are **random processes** ($\\checkmark$) that promote **variation** ($\\checkmark$), as they encourage the formation of genetically distinct gametes at the end of meiosis. However, crossing over occurs during **prophase I** and random orientation occurs during **metaphase I** ($\\checkmark$). During crossing over there is an **exchange** of genetic information between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes, whereas during random orientation homologous chromosomes **independently and randomly align** on either side of the equator ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 4 stages in meiosis I and meiosis II; prophase, metaphase, anaphase, telophase   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prophase I: homologous chromosomes condense **OR** become visible       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prophase I: crossing over occurs between homologous chromosomes **OR** non-sister chromatids              [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ metaphase I: homologous chromosomes align on the equator     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ metaphase I:  random orientation **OR** random alignment of homologous chromosomes     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ anaphase I: spindle fibres attach to the centromeres of each chromosome        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ anaphase I: each chromosome in a pair **OR** each pair of sister chromatids, is pulled to either side of the cell      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ telophase I: cytokinesis occurs **OR** two nuclear envelopes form **OR** cells go from diploid to haploid      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prophase II: chromosomes condense and become visible [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ metaphase II: chromosomes **OR** pairs of sister chromatids, align on the equator of the haploid cells     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ anaphase II: spindle fibres attach to the centromeres of each chromosome     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ anaphase II: sister chromatids are pulled apart (now chromosomes in own right)         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ anaphase II: chromosomes are pulled to either pole of the cell         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ telophase II: cytokinesis occurs **OR**  four nuclear envelopes are formed         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ telophase II: four haploid cells are formed         [1 mark]\n[8 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Meiosis is a form of cell division that results in the production of four haploid gametes. It is split into meiosis I and II, which both consist of four stages; **prophase, metaphase, anaphase and telophase** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    **Meiosis I**\n\n    In prophase I, homologous chromosomes **condense** ($\\checkmark$) and become visible, they form tetrads and **crossing over** ($\\checkmark$) can occur between non-sister chromatids. In metaphase I, the homologous chromosomes align on the **equator** ($\\checkmark$) and are subject to **random orientation** ($\\checkmark$). In anaphase I, the chromosomes, each composed of a pair of sister chromatids, are pulled to **opposite sides** ($\\checkmark$) of the cell and then in telophase I the nuclear membrane reforms and the cytoplasm divides during **cytokinesis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    **Meiosis II**\n\n    In prophase II, chromosomes **condense** ($\\checkmark$) and become visible, they then align at the **equator** ($\\checkmark$) during metaphase II. In anaphase II the **sister chromatids are pulled apart** ($\\checkmark$) and now, as chromsomes in their own right, move to **opposite sides** of the cell ($\\checkmark$) and finally in telophase, the nuclear membrane **reforms** in each of the haploid cells ($\\checkmark$), resulting in the production of four **haploid gametes** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0435","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0435\n\nWhich of the following contain(s) covalent bonds between monomers?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n\n  | | |\n\n  | :-- | :-- |\n\n  | I. | Dipeptides |\n\n  | II. | Amino acids |\n\n  | III. | Triglycerides |\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nDipeptides (**molecule I**) contain covalent bonds between monomers. The condensation reaction between two amino acid molecules results in the formation of a peptide bond, which is a type of covalent bond. Since amino acids act as monomers in protein structures, this bond qualifies as a covalent bond between monomers.\n\nAmino acids (**molecule II**) themselves do not contain covalent bonds between monomers. Each amino acid is a single monomer, composed of covalently bonded atoms within its structure. However, it lacks a linkage to another monomer, meaning it does not satisfy the condition of having covalent bonds between monomers.\n\nTriglycerides (**molecule III**) are formed when one glycerol molecule reacts with three fatty acid molecules, creating ester bonds. Although ester bonds are covalent, glycerol and fatty acids are not identical monomers. Since polymers consist of repeating monomer units, triglycerides do not qualify as polymers, meaning the bonds in their structure do not count as covalent bonds between monomers.\n\nOnly dipeptides contain covalent bonds between monomers, so the correct answer to this question is **Choice A**.\n\n**Choices B, C** and **D** are incorrect because they include incorrect molecules."},{"ref":"BI0490","marks":"4","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0490\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nHormones can be used as medical treatments in some situations.\n\n1. Name the hormone that can be used to treat jet lag and state its effect on the body. [2 marks]\n\n2. Outline two uses of hormones in IVF. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following (*the name must be given*):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ melatonin is the hormone that can be used to treat jet lag [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ melatonin regulates the sleep-wake cycle **OR** melatonin regulates circadian rhythms     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ melatonin concentration increases in the evening [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ melatonin promotes sleepiness [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ changing time zones affects the release of melatonin so the sleep-wake cycle is affected     [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n**Melatonin** is the hormone used to treat jet lag ($\\checkmark$). It is produced by the pineal gland and **affects the circadian rhythm** of the body ($\\checkmark$). The **concentration of melatonin increases** in the **evening** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ daily FSH injections are given to increase the development of follicles **OR** daily LH injections are given daily to increase the development of follicles **OR** daily FSH and LH injections are given to increase the development of follicles [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ injection of hCG **OR** LH is given which stimulates the follicles to mature before ova are collected [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oestrogen used to prepare endometrium for implantation **OR** to prepare for embryo transfer [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ after embryos are placed in the uterus a progesterone pessary **OR** tablet is placed in the vagina to maintain the endometrium to allow implantation [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    IVF uses hormones in a number of ways. After a nasal spray is used to stop the release of FSH and LH from the pituitary gland a woman is given **daily FSH and LH injections** for approximately ten days to stimulate the **development of follicles** ($\\checkmark$). An **injection of hCG** is then given to **stimulate the follicles to mature** ($\\checkmark$). After embryos are placed in the uterus **a progesterone pessary** is placed in the vagina to **maintain the endometrium to allow implantation** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0665","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0665\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the importance of international cooperation in fighting infectious diseases. [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline the role of monoclonal antibodies in home pregnancy tests. [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain the specific immune response to a pathogen in terms of antibodies and lymphocytes.    [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ infectious diseases can cross borders [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if a disease is found in one population it can spread to other populations [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ herd immunity reduces rate of transmission [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ knowledge needs to be shared internationally so that all countries can take measures to prevent disease transmission **OR** so that countries know the best ways to prevent disease transmission [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ resources such as vaccinations and other medications need to be shared between richer and poorer countries  [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nInternational cooperation is needed to fight infectious diseases because diseases **cross borders** ($\\checkmark$). **Knowledge** ($\\checkmark$) and **vaccines** **need to be shared**  ($\\checkmark$) between richer and poorer countries so that all countries can **effectively reduce rates of transmission**.\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ home pregnancy tests contain monoclonal antibodies to hCG [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ home pregnancy tests have a test site and control site    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the urine of a pregnant woman contains hCG [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if hCG is present, it binds to monoclonal antibodies [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the monoclonal antibodies to hCG have a dye attached    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if there are antibodies with hCG attached, they will bind at the test site producing a line     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ monoclonal antibodies with dye attached bind to the control site regardless of the presence of hCG [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a line at the control site shows that the test is valid [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nHome pregnancy tests contain monoclonal antibodies for the hormone **hCG** ($\\checkmark$), which is found in the **urine of pregnant women** ($\\checkmark$). If hCG is present it **binds to monoclonal antibodies** ($\\checkmark$) near the start of the strip. The antibodies move along the strip with the urine. If there are antibodies with hCG attached, they bind to the **test site producing a line** ($\\checkmark$), which indicates a positive result. The test is considered to be valid if there is a line appearing at the **control site** ($\\checkmark$), regardless of the pregnancy status.\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ B lymphocytes and T lymphocytes are **both** part of the specific immune response [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ B lymphocytes and T lymphocytes **both** react to antigens [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ B lymphocytes and T lymphocytes **both** produce memory cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ B lymphocytes produce antibodies that are specific to one antigen [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ helper T cells activate B lymphocytes **OR** activated helper T cells activate B lymphocytes     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ B lymphocytes divide, producing plasma B cells     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibodies are produced by plasma B cells  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibodies bind to antigens on pathogens [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibodies cause agglutination **OR** antibodies cause pathogens to stick together [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibodies help phagocytes bind to and engulf pathogens [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibodies prevent pathogens from attaching to host cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibodies cause the destruction of pathogens [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ memory cells give long-term immunity [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n**B lymphocytes and T lymphocytes** are both part of the specific immune response ($\\checkmark$) and **both react to antigens** ($\\checkmark$). B lymphocytes and T lymphocytes **both produce memory cells** ($\\checkmark$). **Helper T cells** activate **B lymphocytes** ($\\checkmark$) which divide, producing memory B cells and **plasma B cells** ($\\checkmark$). Plasma B cells produce **antibodies** ($\\checkmark$). **Antibodies bind to antigens** ($\\checkmark$). Antibodies help **phagocytes** bind to and **engulf pathogens** ($\\checkmark$), **prevent pathogens from attaching to host cells** ($\\checkmark$) and cause **agglutination of pathogens** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0685","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0685\n\nWhich parts of insect-pollinated flowers are adaptations for mutualistic relationships with pollinators?\n\n-    I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Nectary\n-    II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ Coloured petals\n-    III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ Stigma\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because insect-pollinated flowers have coloured petals (**Statement II**) to attract pollinators and a nectary (**Statement I**) to reward them, whereas all flowers have a stigma (**Statement III**). **Choice A** is incorrect because both statements I and II are correct. **Choices C** and **D** are incorrect because the stigma is not adapted for a mutualistic relationship with insects.  Only the flower benefits from the presence of a stigma."},{"ref":"BI0704","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0704\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline how polygenic traits can show a normal distribution. [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline the differences and similarities between homologous chromosomes.     [4 marks]\n\n3. Explain why expected and observed Mendelian ratios may differ. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polygenic traits are caused by two or more genes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the greater the number of genes that are responsible for a trait, the greater the number of phenotypes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polygenic traits often show continuous variation [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ which is a continuous range of variation rather than distinct categories [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is a large range of phenotypes, where the majority of organisms are in the middle of the normal distribution curve [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polygenic traits are often influenced by the environment [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nPolygenic traits are caused by **two or more genes** ($\\checkmark$). The **higher the number of genes** that are responsible for a trait, the **higher the number of possible phenotypes** ($\\checkmark$) which means that the traits show **continuous variation** ($\\checkmark$). Continuous variation is when a phenotype shows a **continuous range of variation**, rather than having distinct categories ($\\checkmark$) and usually shows a normal distribution, where the majority of organisms are in the **middle of the normal distribution curve** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following (at least **ONE** marking statement should come from the similarities and **ONE** from the differences):\n\n    *Similarities:*\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ length [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ centromeres are  in the same position [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sequence of genes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gene loci **OR** position of genes [1 mark]\n\n    *Differences:*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ alleles [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ one chromosome is inherited maternally and one chromosome is inherited paternally [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nHomologous chromosomes are pairs of chromosomes with the same genes, but **different alleles** ($\\checkmark$). In a pair, one chromosome is inherited **paternally** and one is inherited **maternally** ($\\checkmark$). Homologous chromosomes are similar in that they are the **same length** ($\\checkmark$), have their **centromeres in the same place** ($\\checkmark$) and have the **same sequence** ($\\checkmark$) and **loci** of genes ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mendelian ratios are the proportions of genotypes that would be expected from a punnett square     [1 mark]\n\n    *Observed and expected ratio may be different due to:* \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mutations [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genes are not inherited as expected [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ interactions between genes and the environment     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ environment affects some phenotypes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ autosomal linkage **OR** when genes are found on the same autosome [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sex linkage **OR** when genes are found on the sex chromosomes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ so do not segregate independently during meiosis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when linked genes are far apart on the same chromosome, crossing over can occur making them seem unlinked  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms inheriting diseases that are fatal to embryos disrupts phenotypic ratios    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polygenic inheritance **OR** inheritance caused by two or more genes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when scientists incorrectly assume that a trait is determined by one gene     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if inheritance is monogenic, but two different genes result in the same phenotype     [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nMendelian ratios are the **proportions of genotypes or phenotypes that are expected from a given cross** ($\\checkmark$).  However, observed ratios are not always the same as expected ratios.  This can be due to a number of factors including random **mutations** ($\\checkmark$), and **interactions between genes and the environment** ($\\checkmark$);  the **environment** sometimes alters a **phenotype** ($\\checkmark$). Observed and expected values can also be affected by **autosomal gene linkage** ($\\checkmark$) which is when genes are found on the same autosome.  Autosomally linked genes **do not segregate independently during meiosis** ($\\checkmark$). **Linked genes** that are found **far apart** from each other ont he same chromosome are more likely to **become unlinked** due to **crossing over** ($\\checkmark$). Observed and expected values can also differ if organisms inherit diseases that are **fatal to embryos, which changes phenotypic ratios** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0764","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0764\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nThe mitotic index is an indirect way of measuring plant growth. A study was conducted on white onions (*Allium cepa*), under controlled conditions in a lab, to investigate the effect of soil pH on the mitotic index of the roots.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BI0764A.png\" alt=\"BI0764A\">\n\n1. Describe the trends shown in the graph. [2 marks]\n\n2. Outline how the mitotic index is calculated. [1 mark]\n\n3. Discuss whether the data support the hypothesis that onion plants should be grown in soil with a pH of 5.5. [2 marks]\n\n4. Suggest how a pH of 9 affects the process of mitosis. [2 marks]\n\n5.\n 1. Explain why measuring the mitotic index in the root is an indirect way of measuring the overall growth rate of the onion plant. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Suggest another method, apart from measuring mitotic index, that could be used to measure plant growth. [1 mark]\n\n6. State one of the variables that must be controlled to make this experiment valid. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ from pH 3 to pH 5.5, the mitotic index increases **AND** when the pH is higher than 5.5 the mitotic index decreases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ pH 5.5 shows the highest mitotic index [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the steepest fall in mitotic index is between pH 7 and 7.5 [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The graph shows that from **pH 3 to 5.5, the mitotic index increases** and when the **pH is higher than 5.5, the mitotic index decreases** ($\\checkmark$). At pH **5.5**, the onion root has the highest **mitotic index** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ number of cells in mitosis **OR** prophase, metaphase, anaphase and telophase divided by the total number of cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Mitotic index = $\\left(\\dfrac{\\text{Number of cells in mitosis}}{\\text{Total number of cells}}\\right) $ **OR**\n Mitotic index = $\\left(\\dfrac{\\text{Number of cells in mitosis}}{\\text{Total number of cells}}\\right) $X100%\n[1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The mitotic index can be calculated by counting the **number of cells** undergoing **mitosis** and **dividing** that by the **total number of cells** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n *Answer must include at least one positive and one negative statement to achieve full marks*\n\n *Reasons it supports hypothesis:*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 5.5 is the pH with the highest mean mitotic index **OR** results indicate that root growth rate is highest at pH 5.5 [1 mark]\n\n *Reasons it does not support hypothesis:*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the error bars for pH 5.5 overlap with the error bars for pH 5 and 6 [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mitotic index is an indirect method of measuring growth **OR** in this study only the rate of growth of the root was investigated [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in the study, the pH increases in increments of 0.5, however the optimum pH for growth in roots could be either side of 5.5 [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ there is no data showing an increase in dry mass [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The data supports the hypothesis that onion plants should be grown at a **pH of 5.5**, as at pH 5.5 the onion root has the **highest mitotic index** ($\\checkmark$), however, the **error bars for pH overlap** with the error bars for **pH 5 and 6** ($\\checkmark$) suggesting that there is no significant difference between the mitotic index at those pHs.\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ enzymes **OR** cyclin-dependent kinases control the progression of cells through mitosis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at pH 9, enzymes may begin to denature **OR** the active site changes shape irreversibly [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at pH 9, the formation of the mitotic spindle may be adversely affected **OR** any other named process involved in mitosis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ so less cells will be actively undergoing mitosis [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The mitotic index at pH 9 is relatively lower, as progression through the cell cycle is controlled by the activity of **cyclin dependent kinases** ($\\checkmark$). Cyclin dependent kinases are enzymes so may begin to **denature** at pH 9 ($\\checkmark$), so **less cells** will undergo **mitosis** ($\\checkmark$), reducing the mitotic index.\n\n5.\n 1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ does not indicate that the onion plant is growing **OR** the growth rate of the onion plant is not directly proportional to the growth rate of the root **OR** does not indicate the dry mass of the plants [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ growth does not only rely on producing more cells but also an increase in the size of cells [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The mitotic index of the root is an indirect way of measuring the growth rate of the onion plant as the growth rate of the onion plant is **not directly proportional to the growth rate of the root** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ measuring the length of the shoot [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ measuring the length of the root [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ counting the number of leaves [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ measuring the size of the leaves [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ measuring stem diameter [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ measuring dry mass [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ An alternative way of measuring plant growth is by **measuring the length of the shoot** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ light intensity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide concentration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ volume of water [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature of soil OR air [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ concentration of nutrients [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ time that onion plant is grown in soil before mitotic index is measured [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ distance between onion plants in the soil [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ type of soil [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ age of onion plant [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ humidity [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n One variable that must be controlled in this experiment is the **temperature of the soil** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0768","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0768\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe graph shows the results of an experiment on osmosis in potato tissue (*Solanum tuberosum*), cactus tissue (*Carnegiea gigantea*) and sunflower seeds (*Helianthus annuus*). Similar sized pieces of tissue were immersed in different solutions of sucrose for 30 hours.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BI0768A.png\" alt=\"BI0768A\">\n\n1. Estimate the concentration of sucrose that is isotonic to the interior of the potato tissue. [1 mark]\n\n2. Using the graph deduce, with a reason, the tissue that has the highest solute concentration. [1 mark]\n\n3. Suggest one reason for the difference in internal solute concentration between the sunflower seed and cacti tissue. [1 mark]\n\n4. The epidermis of the potato and cactus and the testa of the sunflowers seeds were removed. Suggest a reason for this. [1 mark]\n\n5. Predict what the appearance of the cacti tissue would be like at 0.5 mol dm-3 sucrose solution. [1 mark]\n\n6.\n 1. State how percentage change in mass is calculated. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Explain why percentage change in mass is calculated rather than change in mass. [2 marks]\n\n7. Explain why temperature has to be controlled in this study. [1 mark]\n\n8. State one way in which this experiment could be made more reliable. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 0.4 mol dm:sup[-3] [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BI0768ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0768ANSA\">\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The concentration of sucrose in the potato tissue is **0.4 mol dm:sup[-3]** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n ***Answer must include a reason to gain a mark***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sunflower seed **BECAUSE** it gained the most mass **OR** it still gains mass at the most concentrated solution whereas the other tissues are losing mass at higher sucrose concentrations **OR** the sunflower seed is the only tissue to gain mass at all sucrose concentrations [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The tissue with the highest solute concentration is the **sunflower seed**, because it **gained the most mass** via osmosis ($\\checkmark$), as it has a higher solute concentration compared to the solution that it was immersed in.\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sunflower seed is a storage organ for carbohydrates and other molecules **WHEREAS** the cacti tissue is a storage organ for water [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sunflower seed acts as a sink for sucrose **WHEREAS** the cacti tissue contains a higher concentration of water [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sunflower plants may prioritise synthesis and accumulation of various solutes **OR** carbohydrates **OR** proteins, **WHEREAS** cacti prioritise water conservation [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The sunflower seed has a higher concentration of solutes, because it is a **storage organ for carbohydrates, proteins** and other molecules, which can be used for plant growth, whereas the cacti tissue is a **storage organ for water** ($\\checkmark$), which cacti need to grow in dry areas.\n\n4. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the rate of osmosis **OR** movement of water may be different through the testa or epidermis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to eliminate potential barriers to water molecules [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ removing testa and epidermis from all tissue samples minimises confounding variables **OR** makes the results more valid [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The rate of osmosis through the epidermis or testa may **vary from the rate of osmosis** ($\\checkmark$) in underlying tissue. Removing the epidermis or testa from all samples makes the results **more valid** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ shrivelled **OR** smaller in size **OR** wilted **OR** dehydrated [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n At 0.5 mol dm:sup[-3], the cacti tissue would appear **shrivelled** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6.\n 1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ((final mass - initial mass) ÷ initial mass) x 100% [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Percentage change in mass = **((final mass - initial mass) ÷ initial mass) x 100%** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6.\n 2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it provides a standardised measure by expressing the change in proportion to the starting mass [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the pieces of tissue may not have the same mass at the start of the experiment [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allows for comparison between the changes in mass of samples of different initial masses [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ percentage change in mass is less sensitive to small variations in the mass measurement (as it calculated as a ratio of two values) [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Percentage change in mass is used rather than mass in this experiment, as the pieces of tissue may **not** be the **same mass at the beginning** ($\\checkmark$) of the experiment, so it allows for accurate **comparison** ($\\checkmark$) in the changes in mass.\n\n7. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the higher the temperature, the faster the rate of osmosis **OR** the lower the temperature, the slower the rate of osmosis **OR** the higher the temperature, the higher the kinetic energy of the water molecules, so they move in or out of the cells at a faster rate [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature affects the structure of the plasma membrane, which can alter the permeability of the membrane, thus altering the rate of osmosis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature affects the rate of metabolic reactions, which can alter the concentration of solutes inside the cells, changing the water potential [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allows for accurate comparison of results [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Temperature has to be controlled in this study because the **higher** the **temperature**, the **faster** the rate of **osmosis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n8.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ do repeats **OR** record at least three sets of results for each tissue sample [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ To make the results more reliable **repeats** ($\\checkmark$) need to be carried out to make sure that there are at least three results for each type of tissue at each sucrose concentration."},{"ref":"BI0775","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0775\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n1\\.\nA student had a small pond in the backyard and wanted to add some fish. The fish would be predators of the pond and feed on the zooplankton (prey) growing in the pond. Zooplankton are small floating or weakly swimming organisms that are dependent on the phytoplankton or algae for their supply of nutrition.\n\nShe wanted to first determine whether Fish A or Fish B would be more suitable, or if both fish could be added to the pond. As a trial, she set up four mesocosms using water from the pond. She monitored the mesocosms and then added the fish to three of the mesocosms as shown. She took samples of water from the different mesocosms and tested them for chlorophyll content at a research lab nearby.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BI0775.png\" alt=\"BI0775\">\n\n1. Outline why the mesocosms used would be considered a ‘closed system’. [1 mark]\n\n2. Describe why adding mud or soil to the mesocosms would be advantageous. [1 mark]\n\n3. Outline the reason for including ‘Mesocosm A’ with the experiment. [1 mark]\n\n4. Explain the reason for monitoring chlorophyll content to analyse the effects of the fish as a predator. [1 mark]\n\n5. State another variable that could be monitored to see the effect of adding fish to the mesocosms. [1 mark]\n\nThe following table shows the results obtained for chlorophyll (μg dm:sup[-3]) with the different additions of fish.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BI0775B.png\" alt=\"BI0775B\">\n[Source: Adapted from Froneman, P. W. (2022, July 22). *Predator diversity does not contribute to increased prey risk: Evidence from a mesocosm study*. MDPI. Retrieved March 23, 2023, from https://www.mdpi.com/1424-2818/14/8/584, Copyright under CC-BY 4.0]\n\n6. From the results obtained, suggest why the student decided to add both fish A and fish B to the pond. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ energy can enter and leave the mesocosm system,(even if mesocosms are covered) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ matter **OR** organisms, cannot enter or leave the mesocosm system, mesocosms are covered [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The mesocosms are a ‘closed system’ because they are covered, and though energy can enter and leave the system, the **matter is not added or removed from the system** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mud will contain saprotrophs **OR** detritus [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mud will aid decomposition and cycling of nutrients in the mesocosm [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mud provides nutrients for algae [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mud increases habitat complexity so more surfaces for microorganisms to attach [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ more realistic ecosystem- more closely mimics the pond ecosystem [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n If mud is added to the mesocosms, this would also add decomposed material and aid in the **decomposition and cycling of nutrients** ($\\checkmark$) by bacteria and other organisms.\n\n3. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ control for the experiment [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ obtain results without fish to compare results when fish are added [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Mesocosm A serves as a **control for the experiment** ($\\checkmark$) and shows the values for chlorophyll when no fish are added.\n\n4. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chlorophyll concentrations will indicate how much (green) algae is present in the system [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the zooplankton eat the algae, so it is an indirect measure of zooplankton levels [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the more the algae, the lesser the levels of zooplankton [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ It is possible **OR** easier to obtain algae levels by measuring chlorophyll concentrations [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n It is possible to **estimate algae levels** ($\\checkmark$) in the system by measuring chlorophyll concentrations. As the fish eat the zooplankton, algal levels might increase, so it is an indirect measure of the zooplankton present.\n\n5. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the zooplankton levels could be monitored [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the zooplankton could be counted directly (using microscopy and counting of the organisms) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ diversity of zooplankton could be measured [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ water quality **OR** dissolved oxygen levels **OR** pH **OR** nutrient levels **OR** turbidity **OR** nitrate levels **OR** phosphate levels [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **zooplankton populations** ($\\checkmark$) could be monitored directly by counting them.\n\n6. Any **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the prey **OR** zooplankton populations were maintained in the presence of the predator **OR** fish [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ both fish A and fish B did not decrease the zooplankton population significantly when both were added together [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the fish A and fish B added together did not interfere with the stability of the system [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ adding fish A and B together did not significantly affect primary productivity **OR** chlorophyll levels [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **zooplankton populations were maintained** ($\\checkmark$) when fish A and fish B were added together. The addition of **both fish did not decrease** ($\\checkmark$) the zooplankton population. Both fish could survive together in the mesocosms."},{"ref":"BI0778","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0778\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nMesocosms were used to study the response of freshwater snails and algae to warming in two different systems, one with fish present and one without fish. Each mesocosm had the following added to it: two different species of snails *Bellamya sp.* and *Radix sp.*, and the same (one) species of phytoplankton. The snails feed on phytoplankton and are in turn preyed upon by the fish.\n\nThe following diagrams show the mesocosms used for the study. **C** is the control without fish at environmental temperature and **F** is the control with fish at environmental temperature. **W** is the mesocosm at increased (+4.5 °C) temperature without fish, and **WF** is the mesocosm at increased temperature with fish. The temperature increase of +4.5 °C was chosen to simulate the possible environmental warming that could happen in this region by the end of the century.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BI0778ai.png\" alt=\"BI0778ai\">\n\n1. State two other variables, other than those given, that need to be controlled in the mesocosms. [2 marks]\n\nThe following graphs show the fluctuations in the density of the two snails against the period of time (x axis) the study took place, along with the treatments.\n\n(**C** - control, **W** - warming , **F** - with fish , **WF** - warming with fish)\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BI077B.png\" alt=\"BI077B\">\n[Source: adapted from Cheng, H., Feng, M., Zhang, P., Zhang, H., Wang, H., Xu, J., & Zhang, M. (2022, December 30). *Effects of warming on aquatic snails and periphyton in freshwater ecosystems with and without predation by common carp*. MDPI. Retrieved January 10, 2023, from https://www.mdpi.com/2073-4441/15/1/153, Copyright under CC-BY 4.0]\n\n2. Draw a line in graph A to show the **peak** density value for both the control (C) and warming (W) for the snail *Bellamya sp.*, as shown in graph B for the snail *Radix sp.* [1 mark]\n\n3. Compare and contrast the effects of **warming** on each species of snails in the mesocosms (W and WF). [2 marks]\n\n4. State which is the preferred snail for consumption by the fish. [1 mark]\n\n5. Predict the effect of warming in the presence of fish on the biodiversity (of snails) in open ecosystems. [1 mark]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BI0778C.png\" alt=\"BI0778C\">\n[Source: adapted from Cheng, H., Feng, M., Zhang, P., Zhang, H., Wang, H., Xu, J., & Zhang, M. (2022, December 30). *Effects of warming on aquatic snails and periphyton in freshwater ecosystems with and without predation by common carp*. MDPI. Retrieved January 10, 2023, from https://www.mdpi.com/2073-4441/15/1/153, Copyright under CC-BY 4.0 ]\n\n6. A study was carried out on variation in periphyton density in the four different mesocosms, the results of which are shown in the graph above. Periphyton is a mixture of autotrophic microorganisms, heterotrophic microorganisms and detritus. Suggest a reason for the increase in periphyton density shown in the warmer mesocosm containing fish. [1 mark]\n\n7. Mesocosms have been used to simulate conditions for climate change and other studies. Outline an advantage and a disadvantage of using mesocosms to carry out these investigations. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ number/ biomass of snails added initially [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ biomass of phytoplankton added initially [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mass of water /dimensions of tanks used [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ light intensity/temperature [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ time span of experiment monitored [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ pH of **OR** dissolved oxygen **OR** nutrient levels in water [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Two variables that need to be controlled are the number **of the two different snails added initially** ($\\checkmark$) and the **time span** ($\\checkmark$) of the experiment.\n\n2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ line drawn vertically through 200 days (allow range of 190-200 days) [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BI0778ANS%20B.png\" alt=\"BI0778ANS B\">\n\n3. Any **two** of the following (at least **one** similarity and **one** difference):\n\n **similarities**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** snails decrease in density with warming of the environment, W, as compared to the control, C [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ In **BOTH** *Bellamya* and *Radix* there is virtually no difference between warmed mesocosms, W, and controls, C, by the end of the study time [1 mark]\n\n **differences**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ for *Bellamya*, the peak day/time for C and W is equal **WHEREAS** for *Radix* the peak day/time for C and W is different **OR** For *Bellamya*, the peak day/time for C and W is at the same time **WHEREAS** for *Radix* the peak day/time for C is earlier than W [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in W density of *Radix* was approximately half that of C around day 150 **WHEREAS**, after day 100, although density of *Bellamya* was always lower in W than in C it was never as low as half of C [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in the first 200 days, *Bellamya* density is greater in F than WF, **WHEREAS** in the first 200 days *Radix* sp. density is similar for F and WF [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in *Bellamya* there are snails present at end of experiment for W and C, **WHEREAS** in *Radix*, there are likely no snails present for W and C [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in *Bellamya* the density of snails remains relatively constant for W and C, **WHEREAS** in *Radix*, the density of snails increases and drops for W and C [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Both snails decrease in density** ($\\checkmark$) with warming in the mesocosms without fish, W.\n\n In ***Radix*** snails, the population density is **0 in warmed** mesocosms, W, and **controls**, C, by day **240** whereas in ***Bellamya*** **snails**, the population density is **similar** in **W and C** by day **300** and there are still snails present ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ *Radix* snail [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The preferred snail for consumption by the fish is the ***Radix*** **snail** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ since the *Radix* snail is preferred by the fish, overtime *Radix* snail will decrease and *Bellamya* will increase in numbers [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ *Bellamya* snail will out compete *Radix* snail, and become the dominant snail the ecosystem [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ biodiversity of snails will decrease over time [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n ***Bellamya*** **snails will out compete** ***Radix*** **snails** ($\\checkmark$) in the presence of fish, over time, in the ecosystems.\n\n6. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in the presence of fish, the snails are eaten **OR** are fewer in number, and therefore there is an increase of periphyton [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n In the presence of fish, **snails are eaten** ($\\checkmark$) and therefore there is an increase in periphyton as less is eaten by snails, (showing a top down control of the ecosystem).\n\n7. Any **one** of the following:\n\n **advantages**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ easier to simulate (ecological) conditions for research in a small (and defined) environment **OR** in a laboratory setting [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ different conditions can be set for varying periods as needed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ particularly useful to make predictions for effects of climate change [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can be made sustainable for long periods of time with only inputs of energy, nutrients being recycled **OR** easy to collect data for a continuous period of time [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can be more realistic representation of an ecosystem than experiments in lab [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can easily be replicated **OR** reproducible [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can be more ethical than manipulating the natural ecosystem of an organism [1 mark]\n\n and any **one** of the following :\n\n **disadvantages**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ may not reproduce conditions as in the wild for ecological studies **OR** usually smaller than natural ecosystems which limits findings [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ difficult to work with large animals since it is not considered ethical [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ there are limitations, as setting up mesocosms may be complex [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ usually limited duration so may not be useful for investigating long-term effects [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can be expensive to set up and maintain [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n An advantage of using a mesocosm is that it is easier **to simulate conditions** ($\\checkmark$) for research. A disadvantage is that mesocosms **may not reproduce all the conditions** ($\\checkmark$) that occur in natural open ecosystems."},{"ref":"BI0782","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0782\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe graph shows the mean blood glucose levels in a study of 300 people, 10 minutes after eating a meal and 3 hours after eating a meal. Of the 300 people studied, 100 people were non-diabetic, 100 people had untreated Type 1 diabetes and 100 people had untreated Type 2 diabetes.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BI0782A.png\" alt=\"BI0782A\">\n\n1. Compare and contrast the blood glucose levels of people without diabetes and those with diabetes. [2 marks]\n\n2. Suggest one variable, other than those already mentioned in the question, that needs to be controlled to make this study valid. [1 mark]\n\n3. Predict how the mean blood glucose concentration after 3 hours would change if the Type 1 diabetics were injected with insulin 15 minutes after eating. [1 mark]\n\n4.\n 1. Explain the effect of insulin on blood glucose levels. [2 marks]\n\n 2. State the name of the cells that make and secrete insulin. [1 mark]\n\n5. Calculate the percentage change between the blood glucose concentration of people with Type 2 diabetes 10 minutes after eating a meal and 3 hours after eating a meal. [1 mark]\n\n6. Explain why Type 2 diabetics have a lower blood glucose concentration 3 hours after eating a meal, compared to Type 1 diabetics. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following\n\n **Similarities**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** diabetics and non diabetics have a higher blood glucose concentration 10 minutes after eating compared to 3 hours after the meal [1 mark]\n\n **Differences**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ People with diabetes have a higher blood glucose concentration 3 hours after a meal **COMPARED TO** people without diabetes. [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ People with diabetes have a higher blood glucose concentration, 10 minutes after a meal **COMPARED TO** people without diabetes. [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Both** diabetics and non diabetics have a **higher blood glucose concentration 10 minutes after eating** compared to 3 hours after the meal ($\\checkmark$). However, people with diabetes have a **higher blood glucose concentration 3 hours after a meal compared** to people without diabetes ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ biological sex [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ age [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ type of meal eaten [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mass of food eaten during meal [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mass of glucose in meal eaten [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ body mass of individuals taking part in study [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ activities conducted by participants [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ period of time since last meal [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ same volume of water consumed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ time of day study conducted [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ same hours of sleep before study [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ no medication or type of medication taken [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Mass of glucose** in meal eaten ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it would be lower than 180 mg dL:sup[-1] **OR** it would be similar to that of the non-diabetics [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n It would be **lower** than 180 mg L:sup[-1] ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.\n 1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ insulin lowers blood glucose levels [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ stimulates uptake of glucose by cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ activates enzymes involved in the conversion of glucose to glycogen [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glucose is converted into glycogen **OR** glycogenesis, in the liver **OR** skeletal muscle [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ causes an increase in respiration rate, increasing glucose breakdown [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increases conversion of glucose to lipids for storage in adipose tissue [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Insulin **lowers** blood glucose levels ($\\checkmark$) by stimulating the **uptake of glucose** by cells ($\\checkmark$). Insulin also **activates enzymes** involved in the conversion of **glucose to glycogen** in the **liver** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ beta (β) cells [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Beta cells** ($\\checkmark$) in the pancreas make and secrete insulin.\n\n5. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ((200 - 130) ÷ 200) x 100 % = 35 % [1 mark]\n *Units are required to gain the mark* \\\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BI0782ANS%20A.png\" alt=\"BI0782ANS A\">\n\n (200 - 130) ÷ 200 = 0.35 x 100% = **35 %** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n6. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Type 1 diabetes is caused by an inability to produce insulin **OR** in Type 1 diabetes there is destruction of ꞵ cells that produce insulin **OR** with Type 2 diabetes there is reduced ability of the body to respond or process insulin **OR** with Type 2 diabetes there is a deficiency of insulin receptors **OR** glucose transporters [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ with Type 1 diabetes little glucose is taken up and stored as glycogen in liver **OR** muscle cells **OR** in Type 2 diabetics some glucose can still be taken up and stored as glycogen in liver **OR** muscle cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ with Type 1 diabetes little glucose is taken up and stored as fat in adipose cells **OR** with Type 2 diabetes some glucose is still stored as fat in adipose cells [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The Type 1 diabetics have a higher blood glucose level after three hours compared to the Type 2 diabetics, as Type 1 diabetes is caused by an **inability to produce insulin** and Type 2 diabetes is caused by a **reduced ability of the body to respond to insulin** ($\\checkmark$) effectively. **Therefore Type 1 diabetics produce much less insulin** ($\\checkmark$) and so convert much less glucose into glycogen."},{"ref":"BI0794","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0794\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe diagram shows how the effect of growing bacteria with different antibiotics can be investigated. A study was conducted to test the degree of antibiotic resistance that bacteria from a hospital door handle had developed. Measurements of the zone of inhibition were taken every 3 days, for 15 days.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BI0793A.png\" alt=\"BI0793A\">\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BI0794B.png\" alt=\"BI0794B\">\n\n1. State which antibiotic is the least effective at preventing bacterial growth. [1 mark]\n\n2. State two variables that must be controlled in this experiment, other than those already given. [2 marks]\n\n3.\n 1. Identify the anomalous result from the study. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Suggest how the anomalous result may have arisen. [1 mark]\n\n4. Explain how natural selection can lead to antibiotic resistance in bacteria. [2 marks]\n\n5. The researchers suggested that vancomycin could be used more extensively to treat bacterial infections. Discuss to what extent you agree with this. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ amoxicillin [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The least effective antibiotic is **amoxicillin** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature that bacteria are incubated at [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the type of agar [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the density of bacteria plated [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the concentration of antibiotic [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the humidity that the bacteria are incubated at [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the concentration of oxygen supplied to the bacteria [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the size of the petri dish [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sterilisation of equipment **AND** area that bacteria are studied in [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ size of the disc soaked in antibiotic [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Two variables that must be controlled in this experiment are the **temperature** ($\\checkmark$) that the petri dishes are stored in and the **type of agar** ($\\checkmark$) that the bacteria are grown on.\n\n3.\n 1. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 21.9 mm:sup[2] **OR** cefaclor on day 12 [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The anomalous result is **21.9** mm:sup[2] ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 2. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ not measuring the diameter **OR** radius accurately [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ not calculating the area of the zone of inhibition accurately [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ contamination [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ natural variability in bacterial growth [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The anomalous result may have arisen from the researcher **not measuring the diameter accurately** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ bacterium within a population develops a random mutation that confers resistance [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mutation spreads through population through transfer of plasmid **OR** horizontal transfer **OR** reproduction **OR** conjugation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ bacteria without the mutation **OR** that are susceptible to antibiotics are killed by antibiotics, reducing competition for those with the resistance gene [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ bacteria with the mutation survive and breed [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n A bacterium within a population develops a **random mutation** which confers resistance ($\\checkmark$) to antibiotics, and this mutation can **spread** throughout the population through **horizontal transfer** ($\\checkmark$), so that more bacteria are resistant to antibiotics. The bacteria with resistance are more likely to **survive** ($\\checkmark$) when treated with antibiotics, and so can go on to **reproduce** so that the population of antibiotic-resistant bacteria increases.\n\n5. Any **three** of the following:\n\n **Agree because…**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it had the largest zone of inhibition over the whole study [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the zone of inhibition didn’t change drastically in size over the 15 days [1 mark]\n\n **Disagree because…**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ if it is overused, it could lead to more resistance to vancomycin in a range of bacteria [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ only bacteria from door handles were tested [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the study does not include repeats [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the study only used a limited number of antibiotics [1 mark]\n\n *Answer must include at least one point from agree and one point from disagree to achieve full marks*\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The evidence from the study supports the suggestion of the researchers that vancomycin could be used more extensively to treat bacterial infections as it had a **larger zone of inhibition** compared to the rest of the antibiotics ($\\checkmark$) across the whole study. However, it shouldn’t be used to treat bacterial infections as **only bacteria from door handles** were tested ($\\checkmark$) and other locations in the hospital may have bacteria that are resistant to vancomycin. Also, its overuse could result in bacteria eventually **developing resistance** to it ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0818","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0818\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nDuring a field day, students were asked to investigate the effect of soil salinity on the distribution of plant species. Students designed an experiment that measured the number of plant species found up the shore at varying distances from the high tide line. Three groups of students investigated three sites on the same shore. The distance from the high tide line was taken as an indication of the salinity levels. The results are shown in the table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BI0818A.png\" alt=\"BI0818A\">\n\n1.\n    1. Identify the independent variable. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Outline the steps students could take, and the equipment they could use, to collect their results.                                [3 marks]\n\n2.\n    1. Suggest a reason for the discrepancy in the number of species sampled in areas 1, 2, and 3.                                     [1 mark]\n\n    2. Describe a way the students could reduce the impact of discrepancies on their collected data.                             [1 mark]\n\n3. The different species of plants found in each area were recorded in a table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BI0818B.png\" alt=\"BI0818B\">\n\nCalculate how many times greater the number of plant species found in Area 1 was compared to Area 2.                                 [1 mark]\n\n4. Evaluate the students’ investigation into salinity's effect on plant species distribution.                                 [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. (units are required for the mark):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ distance from the high tide line            [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **distance from the high tide line** ($\\checkmark$) is the independent variable.\n\n1.\n    2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use a measuring tape to measure 15m from the high tide line towards inland **OR** up shore                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use a belt transect (to mark out the area of investigation)    **OR**  transect line **OR** quadrats placed next to a measuring tape    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use a quadrat to sample plants every 3m **OR** at regular distances along the transect     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the distance between the transect and the quadrat should be kept constant [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ count the number of different plant species found within each quadrat     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use a clipboard **OR** notepad **OR** device for recording data   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use a dichotomous key **OR** plant identification guide **OR** expert to identify plant species [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    To carry out their investigation, students could use a measuring tape to **measure 15 m** up the shore away **from the high tide line** ($\\checkmark$). Place a **quadrat every 3 m** along the tape to **form a belt transect** ($\\checkmark$). Record the **number** of different **plant species** found in **each quadrat** ($\\checkmark$) in a results table.\n\n2.\n    1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some areas may have different soil **OR** soil structure **OR** more fertile soil **OR** more suitable soil pH **OR** salinity **OR** depth of soil **OR** mineral availability            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ human disturbances (footpaths, for example)        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light intensity                                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water availability                                 [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plant density                                     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ herbivory                                         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ accept any other valid description of habitat variation **OR** disturbance     [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    There may be discrepancies in the results because the areas sampled might have different soil types, with some being more **fertile** than others ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    2.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase the sampling frequency OR more quadrats OR more transects    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ decrease the distance between quadrats, so more taken within 15m [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase expertise **OR** use better resources to identify species [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ avoid using sites that clearly had foot trails                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ control more variables **OR** use areas with same angle of slope (accept any suitable example) [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Students could reduce the impact of discrepancies by increasing sampling frequency by adding more quadrats ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 1.29 **OR** 1.286            [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BI0818ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0818ANSA\">\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Area 1 - No. of plant species  = 9\\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Area 2 - No. of plant species = 7\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 9/7 = **1.29** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4. Any **three** of the following (must include strengths and weaknesses):\n\n    **Strengths**\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ students sampled three areas **OR** more than one area, **WHICH MEANS** they can calculate a mean from their results **OR** increases the reliability of their results        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ all areas sampled were at the same beach, **WHICH** decreases the effects of confounding variables between trials        [1 mark]\n\n    **Limitations**\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the salinity of the soil wasn’t measured, **OR** distance from the high tide line was used as an indirect measurement, **WHICH** means the research question is not addressed, **OR** a conclusion cannot be reached        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large discrepancies in students' results **MEANS** that the data isn’t reliable    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ more areas should be sampled to improve reliability    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lack of controlled variables, so students should identify variables such as soil moisture **OR** angle of slope **OR** soil pH **OR** light intensity, **OR** other valid controlled variable (example must be given to award mark)  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ all areas sampled were at the same beach, **WHICH** means the results may not be valid for other beaches            [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The students sampled **more than three** areas so a mean could be taken, and this increases the **reliability** ($\\checkmark$) of the results. However, there were **large discrepancies** between student results ($\\checkmark$), so **more areas need to be sampled** ($\\checkmark$). Also, the **salinity** of the soil was **not measured**; instead, they relied on **indirect measurements**, which means a conclusion can only be supported to a limited degree ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0871","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0871\n\nThe diagram shows prophase, an early stage of mitosis.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BIO871ii.png\" alt=\"BIO871ii\">\n\nWhat is the fate of the nuclear membrane immediately after prophase is complete?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Forms pores to allow movement of molecules in and out of the nucleus\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Reassembles around the sister chromatids\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Breaks down into small vesicles\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Reforms at the poles of the cell","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because immediately after prophase is prometaphase, during which the nuclear membrane breaks down into small vesicles.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because, during prophase, the nuclear membrane already has existing pores. **Choice B** is incorrect because the nuclear membrane only reassembles during telophase, which is the last stage of mitosis, and not immediately after prophase. **Choice D** is incorrect because the mitotic spindle forms between the poles of the cell immediately after prophase."},{"ref":"BI1056","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1056\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline how gel electrophoresis separates molecules and suggest a use for this separation technique. [3 marks]\n\n2. Explain how compartmentalisation is advantageous to eukaryotic cells. [5 marks]\n\n3. In eukaryotic cells, transcription occurs in the nucleus and translation occurs in the cytoplasm. Explain the benefits to the cell of this physical separation.     [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ separates molecules by passing them through a gel using an electrical charge    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ separates molecules by size **OR** charge, **OR** smaller **OR** shorter molecules move further than larger **OR** longer ones                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ separating DNA **OR** protein **OR** RNA,  **OR** DNA profiling **OR** DNA fingerprinting     [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Gel electrophoresis separates molecules by **size or charge** ($\\checkmark$). The molecules are placed into wells in the gel, and an **electrical charge** is applied ($\\checkmark$). This process can be used to **separate DNA fragments**, in DNA profiling ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows for division of labour    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enzymes and substrates involved in the same processes can be in close proximity to each other          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$   reactions can happen faster **OR** more efficiently        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ optimal pH can be maintained in areas of the cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ substances that can cause damage to the cell can be safely stored **OR** reduces risk of damage from digestive **OR** hydrolytic enzymes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organelles can be moved around the cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ post-transcriptional modifications such as splicing can take place    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lower chance of non-specific interactions    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Eukaryotes having a compartmentalised structure means that enzymes and substrates involved in the same processes can be in close **proximity** ($\\checkmark$) to each other. **Optimal pH** ($\\checkmark$) can be maintained in certain areas of the cell so reactions can happen **faster** or more efficiently ($\\checkmark$) . It also means that substances that can damage the cell such as digestive enzymes can be **safely stored** ($\\checkmark$) , and organelles such as ribosomes can **move** around the cell ($\\checkmark$). It also allows important processes to occur such as **splicing** of pre-mRNA ($\\checkmark$), which increases the variety of polypeptides that can be made.\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleus provides a controlled environment for transcription        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA protected within nucleus    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transcription factors **OR** regulatory proteins can enter nucleus     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ this separation allows for precise control over when and where genes are transcribed    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ribosomes are located in the cytoplasm        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ newly transcribed RNA can be processed and modified in the nucleus before it is transported to the cytoplasm (for translation)         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ this spatial separation prevents potential conflicts between transcription and translation machinery    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ modifications of mRNA in nucleus are essential for the maturation and stability of the RNA before it is transported to the cytoplasm     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ribosomes can simultaneously synthesise multiple polypeptides from a single mRNA molecule, increasing efficiency             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA molecules that are not correctly processed **OR** modified in the nucleus can be prevented from entering the cytoplasm for translation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ helps maintain the accuracy of gene expression        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ regulatory proteins **OR** other factors can interact with mRNA molecules in the cytoplasm to influence their translation        [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The DNA is **protected inside the nucleus** ($\\checkmark$) and the nucleus provides a **controlled environment** for transcription ($\\checkmark$) to occur. **Transcription factors** can **enter** the nucleus ($\\checkmark$) but DNA cannot leave, so this provides **precise control** over where and when the genes are **transcribed** ($\\checkmark$). The **mRNA** is **modified** ($\\checkmark$) before leaving the nucleus, and this is essential for its **stability** ($\\checkmark$) and use in translation. **Ribosomes** are located in the **cytoplasm** ($\\checkmark$) and this **spatial separation prevents conflict** ($\\checkmark$) between transcription and translation machinery. Cytoplasmic ribosomes can **simultaneously synthesise multiple polypeptide** chains ($\\checkmark$) from one mRNA strand, **increasing efficiency** ($\\checkmark$). mRNA that has **not been properly processed** can be **prevented from leaving** the nucleus ($\\checkmark$) **preventing incorrect** proteins from being formed by **translation** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1162","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1162\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast anaerobic and aerobic respiration in animal cells, in terms of substrates, products and yield of ATP.     [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline the process of negative feedback, referring to either temperature regulation or control of blood glucose concentration, in humans as an example.    [4 marks]\n\n3. Define an ecosystem, and explain how the flow of energy and recycling of nutrients make a terrestrial ecosystem sustainable. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:( at least one from each section)\n\n    ***Similarities*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** use glucose as a substrate for energy release     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in **BOTH**, pyruvate is the end product of glycolysis    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** processes release energy **OR** produce ATP    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in aerobic respiration, oxygen is required **WHEREAS**  anaerobic respiration occurs in the absence of oxygen        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ aerobic respiration can use substrates other than glucose **OR** can use fatty acids **OR** amino acids **WHEREAS** in anaerobic respiration only substrate is glucose\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ end products of aerobic respiration are carbon dioxide and water, **WHEREAS** the end product of anaerobic respiration is lactic acid         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ aerobic respiration is more efficient at generating ATP **OR** generates more molecules of ATP per glucose molecule **THAN** anaerobic respiration    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Aerobic** and anaerobic respiration use **glucose** as the **substrate** ($\\checkmark$) to **release energy** in the form of **ATP** ($\\checkmark$).  In **aerobic** respiration, **oxygen** is required, whereas **anaerobic respiration** occurs in the **absence of oxygen** ($\\checkmark$). The **end products of aerobic respiration** in animal cells are **carbon dioxide and water**, whereas the end product of **anaerobic** respiration is **lactic acid** ($\\checkmark$). **Aerobic respiration** produces many **more** molecules of **ATP per glucose molecule** than anaerobic respiration ($\\checkmark$) (approximately 15 times more).\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ negative feedback is involved in maintaining an equilibrium **OR** involved in homeostasis, since it brings the value back to the original    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when there is a change from a set value **OR** optimal level, brings about change to return to set value **OR** reduce changes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ involves receptors detecting change and sending information to control centre **OR** brain    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ control centre **OR** brain sends information to effector **OR** muscle **OR** gland to carry out response [1 mark]\n\n    Any **one** of the following examples (max two marks):\n\n    ***Blood glucose*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in blood glucose detected by the pancreatic **OR** β-cells cells which release insulin, **OR** fall in blood glucose detected by the pancreatic **OR** α-cells which release of glucagon      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ insulin causes glucose to be converted to **OR** stored as glycogen in liver **OR** muscle, so decreasing blood glucose, **OR** glucagon causes glycogen to be broken down to glucose **OR** amino acids to be converted to glucose in liver, so increasing blood glucose [1 mark]\n\n    ***Body temperature*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase **OR** decrease in body temperature signals to hypothalamus in the brain      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (increase in temperature) leads to vasodilation **OR** arterioles supplying blood to the skin dilate **OR** sweat glands increase sweat production (do not allow hairs lie flat as not significant in humans) and so cooling **OR** decrease in body temperature, **OR** (decrease in body temperature) leads to vasoconstriction **OR** arterioles supplying blood to the skin constrict **OR** sweat glands decrease  sweat production (do not allow hairs stand up as not significant in humans) **OR** shivering, to help raise body temperature    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    When there is a change from the normal, **negative feedback** mechanisms are a form of **control** that brings the value back to **within a normal range** ($\\checkmark$). The **change** is **detected by receptors**, which send **signals** to the **brain** ($\\checkmark$) and then signals from the brain to the **effector** to bring about the **response** ($\\checkmark$).  In the control of blood glucose concentration, an **increase** in **blood glucose** will be detected by the **β-cells** and lead to the **release of insulin** ($\\checkmark$), causing **glucose** to be **converted** to and stored as **glycogen** in **liver** and skeletal **muscle** cells. This then restores the **blood glucose** concentration to **within normal levels** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ an ecosystem is a community interacting with its physical environment     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy from the Sun is used for photosynthesis **OR** light energy is converted to chemical energy by autotrophs **OR** plants        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ this initial capture of energy then moves through the trophic levels **OR** from producers to consumers in a food chain            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ only 5 to 10% of the energy is transferred from one trophic level to the next    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy transfer from one level to the next trophic level is not 100% efficient    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy is lost as heat  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy flow is depicted in a pyramid shape and shows the loss of energy   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms absorb nutrients from food that they consume        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ decomposers break down organic matter after death, releasing nutrients    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (saprophytic) bacteria and fungi are decomposers and help release nutrients back to the soil             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nutrients in soil are in simpler compounds and are absorbed by plants    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ recycling of nutrients in ecosystems takes place    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy needs to be supplied continuously for initial capture by plants    [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    An **ecosystem** is a **community** of living organisms interacting with its **physical environment** ($\\checkmark$). **Energy** from the Sun is used by plants for **photosynthesis** ($\\checkmark$) to make organic compounds, and this **energy moves** from **one trophic level** to the **next** ($\\checkmark$) when organisms consume plants or other organisms. This **transfer** of energy is not efficient, only about **5 to 10%** is transferred ($\\checkmark$) to the next level, much is **lost as heat** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    **Organisms** absorb the **nutrients** from the food they consume ($\\checkmark$). After their death, **decomposers break down** the **organic matter** ($\\checkmark$), releasing the nutrients. The **nutrients** are in **simpler compounds** and can be **absorbed by plants** ($\\checkmark$) from the soil to be used to make further organic compounds in plants."},{"ref":"BI1209","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1209\n\nWhich statement is an example of intraspecific competition that could lead to evolution by natural selection?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sharks and dolphins eat the same types of fish\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Parasitic vines growing on tall kapok trees in the rainforest\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Roses and weeds grow in the same areas and compete for nutrients\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Male zebras fight over territory, and female partners","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIntraspecific competition is competition between organisms of the same species. Male zebras are from the same species and compete with each other over territory and mates; **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choices A** and **C** are incorrect as they describe interspecific competition, which is competition between organisms from different species. The growth of vines on tall kapok trees represents a parasitic relationship in which the vines derive nutrients and water from the kapok trees. This interaction does not involve intraspecific competition; **Choice B**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1221","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1221\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nDNA and RNA are nucleic acids that are essential to all living organisms. ATP is a type of nucleotide.\n\n1. Distinguish between ATP and the monomers of DNA in humans. [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline the roles of the different types of RNA involved in translation.     [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe the structure of the DNA double helix.  [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP contains three phosphate groups **WHEREAS** each DNA nucleotide contains only one phosphate group         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP has adenine for a nitrogenous base **WHEREAS** a DNA nucleotide can contain adenine, cytosine, guanine or thymine    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a component of ATP is ribose **WHEREAS** DNA nucleotides have deoxyribose instead        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP stores energy **OR** provides energy, **WHEREAS** DNA carries **OR** transmits genetic information                          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP can perform its function as a single monomer **WHEREAS** DNA can only perform its function, **OR** carry genetic material as a polymer            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP is found throughout the cell **OR** primarily in the cytoplasm **OR** mitochondria **WHEREAS** DNA is found primarily in the nucleus **OR** only found in the nucleus AND mitochondria            [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Both ATP and DNA molecules are nucleic acids which contain a phosphate group, a sugar and a base. The structural difference between a molecule of ATP and a molecule of DNA is that one ATP molecule contains **three phosphate groups** whereas one DNA nucleotide contains **one phosphate group** ($\\checkmark$). In addition, the nitrogenous base in ATP is **adenine** whereas there are four possible nitrogenous bases in one nucleotide of DNA which are **adenine, cytosine, guanine and thymine** ($\\checkmark$). The functional difference is that ATP **stores energy**, whereas DNA **carries genetic material** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following (there must be at least one statement for each of mRNA, tRNA and rRNA to achieve maximum marks):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA carries the genetic information from DNA to the ribosome            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA serves as a template for protein synthesis        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ codons of mRNA  specify positioning of  amino acids along polypeptide **OR** codons guide the assembly of amino acids into a polypeptide         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tRNA molecules carry amino acids to mRNA **OR** ribosome    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ anticodons on tRNA complementary to codons on mRNA allow amino acids to be positioned correctly along the polypeptide **OR** anticodons bind to codons to bring corresponding amino acid to the ribosome             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rRNA provides the ribosome's structural framework **OR** forms ribosomes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rRNA catalyses the formation of peptide bonds between adjacent amino acids     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rRNA facilitates binding of mRNA and tRNA        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA, tRNA, and rRNA orchestrate protein synthesis during translation    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Together, mRNA, tRNA, and rRNA** orchestrate the process of **protein synthesis** ($\\checkmark$) during translation. **mRNA carries** the genetic **information** from **DNA** to the **ribosome**  ($\\checkmark$). **Codons** on the mRNA **specify the positioning** of **amino acids** along the **polypeptide** chain  ($\\checkmark$). Each **tRNA** molecule has a specific **anticodon** sequence that is **complementary** to a **codon** on the mRNA  ($\\checkmark$). This base-pairing specificity allows tRNA to bring the **corresponding amino acid** to the **correct position** to form the polypeptide  ($\\checkmark$). The rRNA **catalyses** the formation of **peptide bonds** between adjacent amino acids  ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following: \\\n    *(appropriately labelled and correctly drawn DNA segment can be used in the answer)*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ each strand of DNA is a polymer of nucleotides         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ each strand has a sugar-phosphate backbone            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ four nitrogenous bases A **OR** adenine **AND** T **OR** thymine **AND** C **OR** cytosine **AND** G **OR** guanine              [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ two strands are anti-parallel **OR** parallel but in opposite directions **OR** one strand is in the 5’ to 3’ direction and the other strand is in the 3’ to 5’ direction    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ strands are twisted **OR** right-handed and turn into a double helix with around 10 base pairs per turn          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleotides consist of one phosphate group, one deoxyribose sugar and one nitrogenous base                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phosphate group is bound to the 5’ carbon of the sugar **OR** deoxyribose **OR** pentose                           [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phosphate group is bound to the 3’ carbon OH group of deoxyribose (of the next nucleotide)                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ covalent **OR** sugar-phosphate bond between phosphate and sugar        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nitrogenous base **OR** base is bound to the 1’ carbon of the sugar **OR** deoxyribose **OR** pentose sugar             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ covalent bond between nitrogenous base and deoxyribose **OR** sugar **OR** pentose sugar                     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ two strands are held together by hydrogen bonds between nitrogen bases of two nucleotides **OR** 3 hydrogen bonds between guanine and cytosine **OR** 2 hydrogen bonds between adenine and thymine    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ complementary base pairing between nitrogenous bases    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ A pairs up with T AND C pairs up with G        [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    DNA is a nucleic acid, also known as a **polymer of nucleotides** ($\\checkmark$) . Many nucleotides are joined together to form it. **One nucleotide** consists of three parts; a **phosphate group, deoxyribose sugar and a nitrogenous base** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    One end of the **phosphate group** is bonded to the **5’ carbon** of the **deoxyribose sugar** ($\\checkmark$). On the other side of the nucleotide, **the phosphate group is bonded to the 3’ carbon sugar** ($\\checkmark$) of another nucleotide. Both bonds are covalent **sugar-phosphate bonds** ($\\checkmark$). Repeated alternating sugar-phosphate bonds form the **sugar-phosphate backbone** ($\\checkmark$).  The **1’ carbon of the sugar is covalently bonded to the nitrogenous base** ($\\checkmark$) also through a condensation reaction to make up one complete nucleotide.\n\n    **DNA is a double helix** ($\\checkmark$) in which nitrogenous bases form the centre of the structure, and **both strands are held together by hydrogen bonds** ($\\checkmark$) between the complementary bases from each strand. **A** pairs up **with T**, and **C** always pairs up **with G** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1291","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1291\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe pale and dark forms of the peppered moth (Biston betularia) are a classic example of natural selection. The graph shows the changes in the population of pale (mostly white) and dark (mostly black) peppered moths (*Biston betularia*) during and post the industrial revolution.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BI1291.png\" alt=\"BI1291\">\n\nThe image shows the phenotype of pale and dark moths on white tree bark.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BI1291b.png\" alt=\"BI1291b\">\n[Source: Adapted from Bedin, T. (2020, May 26). *Tilpasning og evolusjon - natural science (PB) - NDLA. ndla.no*. Retrieved October 02, 2023, from https://ndla.no/en/subject:1:bb834c76-d1e4-46c4-8c0a-8f978bd2c956/topic:6:f604b380-5cdb-4227-885e-bc9b57cb3237/topic:3:aa019f6a-a569-4491-b0d2-c8d2df21733f/resource:09fc6c1a-eaa1-4901-b2ae-ee5e7ce1900c. Copyright free]\n\n1. Distinguish between the changes in the numbers of pale and dark moths shown in the graph. [2 marks]\n\n2.\n 1. Outline how the trends in the graph can be explained by natural selection. [3 marks]\n\n 2. Suggest a technique a group of students could use to compare the population sizes of peppered moths between a deciduous (broadleaved) and a coniferous (evergreen) wood. [1 mark]\n\n3. Calculate the percentage increase in dark-coloured moths between 1850 and 1900. [1 mark]\n\n4. Draw a line on the graph, predicting how the number of pale moths would have changed, if the industrial revolution did not happen. [1 mark]\n\n5. In addition to collecting data such as that shown in the graph, scientists rely on various forms of evidence to explore the concept of evolution. State two other forms of evidence that supports the theory of evolution. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ between 1800 and 1850 **OR** around the middle of the Industrial Revolution, the number of pale moths remained high **OR** between 95 and 100% **WHEREAS** the number of dark moths remained low **OR** between 0 and 5% [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ from 1850, the number of pale moths began to rapidly decrease **WHEREAS** the number of dark moths rapidly increased [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ around 1870 **OR** just before 1875, the number of dark moths surpasses the number of pale moths [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ from 1900, the number of pale moths was low **OR** less than 5% **WHEREAS** the number of dark moths was high **OR** around 95% [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Between **1800 and 1850**, the number of **pale** moths was between **95 and 100%**, whereas the number of **dark** moths was between **0 and 5%** ($\\checkmark$). However, **from 1850** the number of **pale** moths **decreased** rapidly, whereas the number of **dark** moths **increased** rapidly ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1. Any **three** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ natural selection explains how individuals with advantageous traits are more likely to survive to reproduce [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ before the Industrial Revolution, lichens **OR** mosses **OR** bark on trees **OR** brickwork were white, so the pale moths were able to camouflage from predators [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increased rates of pollution **OR** soot during the Industrial Revolution caused white lichen and mosses on lichen **OR** moss **OR** bark on trees **OR** brickwork to turn black [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ pale moths were more visible to predators, so their numbers decreased [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ dark moths were able to camouflage from predators, so were better able to survive and breed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the dark moths gained a selective advantage over the pale moths **OR** were selected for **OR** are under directional selection **OR** pale moths faced intense selection pressure due to predation [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Before the Industrial Revolution, **lichen and mosses** on trees were **white**, so the pale moths were able to **camouflage from predators** ($\\checkmark$). However, **post-Industrial Revolution**, there were increased rates of **pollution** which caused trees to turn **black** ($\\checkmark$). This meant that the dark moths were able to **camouflage** from predators, so were better able to **survive and breed** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 2.\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ capture-mark-release-recapture [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n One technique that students could use is the **mark-release-recapture** of **peppered** moths ($\\checkmark$) in coniferous and deciduous woods.\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 1800 % [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BI1291c.png\" alt=\"BI1291c\">\n\n Percentage increase = ((final value - initial value) ÷ initial value) $\\times$ 100% \\\n Percentage increase = ((95 - 5) ÷ 5 ) $\\times$ 100% \\\n Percentage increase = **1800%** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ line should continue the same trend from before 1850 to 1900 [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BI1291d.png\" alt=\"BI1291d\">\n\n5. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA sequence **OR** RNA sequencing [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ amino acid sequence of proteins [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ selective breeding **OR** artificial selection [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous structures **OR** comparative anatomy **OR** vestigial structures [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ embryonic development [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the fossil record [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ geographic distribution of species [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **fossil record** ($\\checkmark$) and **DNA sequencing** ($\\checkmark$) provide evidence for the theory of evolution."},{"ref":"BI1319","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1319\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast transcription and translation between eukaryotes and prokaryotes.[5 marks]\n\n2. Describe how vesicles link translation and exocytosis.    [3 marks]\n\n3. Explain how errors in transcription may result in altered enzyme function.    [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **five** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** similarity and **one** difference to achieve full marks*):\n\n    ***Similarities*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ribosomes are the site of translation in **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ translation occurs in the cytoplasm  in **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA is synthesised in the 5’ to 3’ direction during transcription in  **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the mRNA code is read in the 5’ to 3’ direction in  **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** prokaryotes and eukaryotes have promoter regions **OR** sequences which enzymes **OR** transcription factors can bind to initiate **OR** enhance **OR** repress transcription    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA polymerase is involved in transcription in **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transcription involves the use of a DNA template to synthesise RNA in **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ translation involves the use of an RNA template **OR** mRNA to synthesise protein in **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the base pairing rules for DNA and RNA are the same **OR** Adenine (A) pairs with Thymine (T) in DNA **OR** Uracil (U) in RNA, while Cytosine (C) pairs with Guanine (G)  in **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there are specific stop signals that mark the end of transcription and translation in **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** prokaryotes and eukaryotes use transfer RNA (tRNA) molecules in translation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the same codons typically code for the same amino acids in **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes            [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transcription and translation are coupled **OR** occur simultaneously in prokaryotes **WHEREAS** in eukaryotes transcription occurs before translation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in eukaryotes transcription occurs in the nucleus **AND** translation occurs in the cytoplasm **WHEREAS** in prokaryotes **BOTH** processes occur in the cytoplasm    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleosomes are present in eukaryotic DNA and help to regulate transcription **BUT** are absent from prokaryotic DNA    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$eukaryotic mRNA undergoes post-transcriptional modification **OR** splicing **OR** addition of a poly-A tail **OR** addition of a 3’ cap **WHEREAS** prokaryotic DNA does not **OR** eukaryotes have introns **WHEREAS** prokaryotes do not        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 70S ribosomes carry out translation in prokaryotes **WHEREAS** 80S ribosomes carry out translation in eukaryotes                    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Ribosomes** are the site of **translation** in both eukaryotes and prokaryotes ($\\checkmark$), however, **70S ribosomes** carry out translation in prokaryotes, and **80S ribosomes** carry out translation in eukaryotes ($\\checkmark$). During transcription, **mRNA is synthesised** in the **5’ to 3’** direction in both eukaryotes and prokaryotes ($\\checkmark$), and both involve **RNA polymerase** ($\\checkmark$). However, in eukaryotes, the mRNA undergoes **post-transcriptional modification**, whereas this does not occur in prokaryotes ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polypeptides are translated by ribosomes on the rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER) **OR** after translation, polypeptide chains are folded into proteins **OR** modified inside RER    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles containing polypeptides bud off the RER **AND** are transported to the Golgi apparatus    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polypeptides are processed into their final form in the Golgi apparatus    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles bud off the Golgi apparatus        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles fuse with the plasma membrane of the cell     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ releasing proteins in the process of exocytosis    [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Exocytosis is the process by which **vesicles** (fluid-filled membrane sacs) within cells move towards the **plasma membrane** of the cell and **fuse** with it ($\\checkmark$), which allows them to **release their contents** ($\\checkmark$). This process can be linked to translation, as polypeptides are **translated** by ribosomes on the **RER** ($\\checkmark$), and then transferred in **vesicles** to the **Golgi** apparatus where they are **processed**  ($\\checkmark$) and then are transferred via vesicles to where they are needed outside the cell, via **exocytosis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a substitution mutation **OR** the incorrect RNA nucleotide is incorporated into the growing mRNA chain            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ an extra RNA nucleotide **OR** base is incorporated **OR** inserted **OR** removed **OR** deleted from the growing mRNA chain    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ may result in incorrect splicing of pre-mRNA     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ may result in translation **OR** production of a longer **OR** shorter polypeptide chain    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sequence of amino acids in the polypeptide chain can only be altered if the mutation occurs in the coding region of the gene **OR** exon    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic code dictates the correspondence between codons and specific amino acids [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ may result in the wrong amino acid being incorporated into the polypeptide chain **OR** wrong primary structure during translation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ leads to incorrect folding of the tertiary structure of the polypeptide chain        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as the incorrect amino acid may have an R-group that interacts differently with the rest of the chain            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the incorrect polypeptide chain is incorporated into the tertiary **OR** quaternary structure of the globular enzyme structure        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ results in an altered active site shape **OR** altered allosteric sites **OR** loss of active site [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ therefore substrate is unable to bind **OR** loss of catalytic activity        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ results in potential loss of stability in enzyme structure **OR** enzyme is more easily affected by changes in the environment **OR** enzyme denatures more easily        [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Transcription is the process by which an RNA copy of DNA is made. Errors in transcription can result in the **incorrect RNA** ($\\checkmark$) nucleotide, or an **extra RNA** nucleotide being incorporated into the growing mRNA chain ($\\checkmark$). This may result in **incorrect splicing** of the **pre-mRNA** molecule ($\\checkmark$) before it leaves the nucleus, which can result in the translation of a **truncated polypeptide** chain ($\\checkmark$) or the **wrong amino acid** being incorporated into the polypeptide chain ($\\checkmark$). This can result in the incorrect polypeptide chain being incorporated into the **tertiary structure** of the **globular** enzyme structure ($\\checkmark$) , which may result in an **altered active site** ($\\checkmark$), thereby **preventing** a **substrate** from successfully binding ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1372","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1372\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Describe centrifugation and how developments in this technique allowed scientists to improve their understanding of the components of cells.  [4 marks]\n\n2. Distinguish between transcription and translation in prokaryotic and eukaryotic organisms.    [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain the advantages of cell compartmentalisation in eukaryotes.  [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ centrifugation allows the separation of molecular components based on differences in their relative densities **OR** sizes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a centrifuge **OR** ultracentrifuge is a device that spins at high speeds to separate substances in a suspension using centrifugal force    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows the separation of individual organelles            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell contents are homogenised **OR** broken up **OR** plasma membrane is broken to release organelles into the solution                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ substances that are less dense stay at the top **OR** form the supernatant, **WHILE** substances that are more dense settle at the bottom **OR** form the pellet    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ using cell fractionation, a multi-step process that involves successively higher speeds of centrifugation to collect progressively smaller cell components **OR** denser cell components can be separated at lower speeds    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows biologists to study **OR** determine the roles of individual organelles **OR** molecules **OR** specific chemical pathways in a controlled environment without the presence of confounding substances **OR** separate from the other reactions that occur in the cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ used in the famous experiment by Meselson and Stahl to discover the semi-conservative nature of DNA replication **OR** discovery of translation of mRNA **OR** any other example of an experiment or discovery involving centrifugation            [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Centrifugation** is a process used to **separate cell parts based on their relative densities** ($\\checkmark$). An **ultracentrifuge** is a device that **rotates at high speeds** to **separate cell components** using **centrifugal force** ($\\checkmark$). This allows scientists to **isolate** individual **organelles for experimentation** ($\\checkmark$). **Dense organelles settle to the bottom** of the sample following centrifugation, while **less dense organelles stay at the top** ($\\checkmark$). By **successively separating** the **supernatant** and **pellets** formed during centrifugation, many organelles can be isolated in a process known as **cell fractionation** ($\\checkmark$). These techniques have allowed scientists to make many **discoveries** regarding the nature of various organelles and cell processes, such as the **semi-conservative nature of DNA** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in eukaryotes, the genetic material **OR** DNA is separated from the cytoplasm by the nuclear membrane **OR** envelope **WHEREAS**, in prokaryotes, the genetic material is found in the cytoplasm [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in eukaryotes, transcription takes place in the nucleus, and translation occurs in the cytoplasm **WHEREAS** in prokaryotes, both processes take place in the cytoplasm    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transcription and translation are discontinuous **OR** completed separately in eukaryotes **WHEREAS** transcription and translation are continuous **OR** completed simultaneously in prokaryotes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ post-transcriptional modification occurs in eukaryotes **WHEREAS** no post-transcriptional modification **OR** minimal RNA modification occurs in prokaryotes     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ eukaryotic genes contain non-coding segments called introns **WHILE** prokaryotic genes do not **OR** eukaryotes have introns and exons **WHILE** prokaryotes do not [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in eukaryotes, introns are removed to produce mature mRNA **OR** RNA splicing occurs  **WHEREAS**, in prokaryotes, this process does not occur         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ movement of mRNA through nuclear pores into cytoplasm is controlled in eukaryotes, which helps regulate gene expression  **WHEREAS**, in prokaryotes, this form of gene expression regulation cannot happen    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    In **eukaryotic** organisms, the **DNA** is **separated from** the **cytoplasm** by the **nuclear membrane**, whereas the genetic material of **prokaryotes** is **within the cytoplasm** ($\\checkmark$).  In eukaryotes, **transcription** occurs **within the nucleus**, while **translation** occurs **in the cytoplasm**. In prokaryotes, however, **both processes** occur in the **cytoplasm** as these organisms do not have a nucleus ($\\checkmark$). Due to this lack of separation, both **transcription and translation happen simultaneously** in prokaryotes, while in eukaryotes, **transcription** must be **completed first** before **translation** can begin ($\\checkmark$). This allows **post-transcriptional modification** to take place in eukaryotes ($\\checkmark$), whereby the **immature mRNA is edited** through the process of **RNA splicing**, which **removes introns** ($\\checkmark$), sections of a gene that **do not code for proteins**. This **post-transcriptional modification** does not occur in **prokaryotes** as they **do not have introns** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows organelles to have specialised functions                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ such as the enzymatic digestion of substances in lysosomes **OR** the ingestion of foreign particles into phagocytic vacuoles **OR** any other named example of a compartmentalised organelle and its specialised function                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows metabolic processes to be isolated from each other        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ improves efficiency **OR** prevents incompatible reactions from occurring in the same part of the cell **OR** allows different cellular processes to occur simultaneously without interfering with each other **OR** ensures that ATP production is focused and efficient, preventing wasteful reactions in other parts of the cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows the maintenance of concentration gradients **OR** internal conditions that differ from that of the cytoplasm                        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ by controlling the distribution of substances such as enzymes and metabolites **OR** the products of metabolism **OR** nutrients    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ provides increased surface area for reactions                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ such as those that occur in the inner membrane of the mitochondria **OR** electron transport chain **OR** chemiosmosis **OR** other named example of a process within the cell that requires a high surface area    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows nutrients **OR** wastes to be stored separately from other cell components    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ such as starch **OR** cell sap in the vacuoles of plant cells             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows cellular communication **OR** allows precise control of cellular responses    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ using signaling molecules that are released from one compartment and target specific receptors in another            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ protects cellular components from potentially damaging substances **OR** isolates toxic substances, reducing cytotoxicity **OR** risk to the rest of the cell            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ provides an additional layer of protection for important cell structures        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ such as the DNA within the nucleus **OR** any other named example        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows transport **OR** sorting **OR** modification of molecules        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ including the transport of substances in vesicles from the rough endoplasmic reticulum to the Golgi apparatus                            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows the ability to respond to external stimuli using modification of the number **OR** structure of specific organelles **OR** cell compartments    [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Cell **compartmentalisation** uses **membranes** to separate cell components into **specialised organelles** ($\\checkmark$). This allows each organelle to take on unique roles, such as **absorbing foreign particles**, as seen in **phagocytic vacuoles** ($\\checkmark$). Various **cell processes** can be **completed separately** ($\\checkmark$), making them more **efficient** ($\\checkmark$). Compartmentalisation also allows the cell to **control** the production and **distribution** of substances such as **enzymes** ($\\checkmark$), directing them to areas where they are needed. Furthermore, compartmentalisation provides organelles such as the **mitochondria** with vastly **increased surface area** ($\\checkmark$) so that **metabolic reactions** can occur, allowing it to produce much more ATP than would otherwise be possible ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1375","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1375\n\nThe image shows a cladogram for several species of hamster.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BI1375.png\" alt=\"BI1375\">\n[Source: Adapted from Michaplot. (2012, January 4). *File: PhodopusCladogram.jpg*. Wikipedia. Retrieved December 04, 2023 from URL  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:PhodopusCladogram.jpg. Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n1. State what is represented by the letter **X**. [1 mark]\n\n2. Other than their positions on the cladogram, explain what information indicates that *Phodopus campbelli* and *Phodopus sungorus* are closely related.    [1 mark]\n\n3. Modern hamsters have small, furry ears. The common ancestor of the hamsters in the cladogram has been hypothesised to have large, furry ears. Suggest how natural selection explains these changes.        [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  node    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  the last common ancestor of *Phodopus campbelli* **AND** *Phodopus sungorus*        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Letter X represents a **node** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they are from the same genus **OR** in the *Phodopus* genus        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    They are in the same **genus** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ natural selection is the process by which species change over time in response to changes in the environment **OR** competition between organisms in order for species to survive [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ random mutation resulted in new allele for smaller ears        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increases genetic variation **OR** diversity within the  population        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mutation **OR** new allele **OR** allele for smaller ears proved beneficial **OR** led to greater chance of survival        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased reproductive success    **OR** more chance of having offspring **OR** having more offspring        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the offspring inherited the allele for smaller ears **OR** beneficial alleles passed onto next generation            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ over many generations the allele for shorter ears increased in frequency in the population **OR** small-eared hamsters increased in number in the population [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n   Many years ago, **random mutations** occurred in some hamsters that gave them **new alleles** for smaller ears ($\\checkmark$), the allele for smaller ears gave those hamsters a **survival advantage** ($\\checkmark$). These hamsters were **more likely** to have **offspring** ($\\checkmark$), and the **beneficial allele** for small ears was **passed onto** the **offspring** ($\\checkmark$). Over **many generations**, there was an **increase** in the number of hamsters with **smaller ears** and a **decrease** in the number of hamsters with **larger ears** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1425","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1425\n\nWhich of the following proteins is a hormone?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$  Haemoglobin\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$  Collagen\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$  Glucagon\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$  Amylase","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nGlucagon is a hormone involved in maintaining blood glucose levels; **Choice C** is, therefore, the correct answer.\n\nHaemoglobin is a protein responsible for the transport of oxygen and carbon dioxide in red blood cells; **Choice A** is therefore not correct. Collagen is a structural protein, and amylase is an enzyme that catalyses the hydrolysis of starch; **Choices B** and **D** are, therefore, also not correct."},{"ref":"BI1578","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1578\n\nEastern Egg Rock Island, Maine is home to a population of Atlantic puffins (*Fratercula arctica*). The colony was reintroduced to the island in 1973 by translocating six chicks from Newfoundland. The Audubon society has recorded the number of breeding pairs for more than fifty years, some of the results are provided in the table.\n\n| Year | Number of Breeding Pairs |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| 1981 | 4 |\n| 2001 | 37 |\n| 2005 | 71 |\n| 2013 | 111|\n| 2015 | 150 |\n| 2017 | 172 |\n| 2019 | 188 |\n| 2021 | 180 |\n\nWhich of the following statements does the data support?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Puffins exhibit sexual selection.|\n    | II. | The puffin population exhibited sigmoid growth following reintroduction.|\n    | III. | The puffin population has stabilised near the carrying capacity of the environment. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because the puffin population has risen sharply before becoming stable in recent years, characteristic of sigmoid growth (**Statement II**). The recent slight decline in the number of breeding pairs suggests that the population has begun to stabilise near the environment’s carrying capacity, leading to the death of some puffins and an overall plateau in population growth (**Statement III**).\n\n**Statement I** is likely to be true, but the data does not describe sexual selection taking place in the puffin population. **Choice A** is incorrect because it is missing **Statement II**. **Choices B** and **D** are incorrect because they include **Statement I**."},{"ref":"BI1630","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1630\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe karyogram shows a karyotype of a healthy human foetus.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BI1630%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1630 fig1\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from National Cancer Institute (2013, January 21). File: Karyotype (normal). jpg. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved April 28, 2024. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karyotype_(normal).jpg. Copyright free]\n\n1.  Deduce, with a reason, the biological sex of the foetus.    [1 mark]\n\n2.  The biological father of the foetus has haemophilia, and the biological mother is a carrier of the disease. Using a Punnett square, calculate the probability that the foetus has haemophilia.    [3 marks]\n\n3.  Haemophilia occurs in approximately 1 in 6000 biological males and 1 in 100,000 biological females. Suggest a reason for this difference.    [1 mark]\n\n4.  Much research has been conducted into the use of stem cells to treat genetic diseases. Outline the advantages and disadvantages of the use of adult stem cells over embryonic ones in stem cell therapy.    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  any **one** of the following\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ biologically male **BECAUSE** they have a Y chromosome **OR** they have 22 pairs of homologous chromosomes **AND** 1 pair of non-homologous sex chromosomes        [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n    The foetus is biologically **male** because they have a **Y chromosome** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.  any **three** of the following\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ parental genotypes identified in the Punnett square; biological father: $\\ce{X^hY}$ **AND** biological mother: $\\ce{X^HX^h}$    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ offspring genotypes identified as $\\ce{X^HY, X^hY}$    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 0.50 **OR** 50% **OR** $\\frac{1}{2}$    [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/1630.png\" alt=\"1630\">\n\n    The biological sex of the foetus is male, so it must inherit the Y chromosome from its biological father. The probability of a biologically male foetus having haemophilia is **50 %** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.  any **one** of the following\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it is a sex-linked recessive disease, **OR** the gene is carried on the X chromosome **AND** biological males only have one X chromosome, so if they have the recessive allele, they will have the disease, whereas biological females with one copy would be carriers    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ for a biological female to have haemophilia, they would need to have two copies of the recessive allele, whereas biological males only need one copy of the recessive allele to have haemophilia    [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n    Haemophilia is more common in biological males because it is a **sex-linked disease** on the **X chromosome**. **Biological males** have only **one X chromosome**, so inheriting the **recessive allele results** in the **disease**, while **biological females** with **two X chromosomes** can be **carriers without symptoms** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.  any **three** of the following (*at least* ***one*** *advantage and* ***one*** *disadvantage must be given to gain full marks*)\\\n\n    ***advantages:***\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adult stem cells can be obtained from the individual needing treatment, reducing the risk of immune rejection **WHILE** embryonic stem cells may be rejected by the patient’s immune system [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ harvesting adult stem cells is generally less controversial **OR** has fewer ethical implications compared to embryonic stem cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adult stem cells have a lower risk of producing tumours compared to embryonic stem cells  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adult stem cell therapies generally face fewer regulatory hurdles and greater public acceptance compared to therapies involving embryonic stem cells    [1 mark]\n\n    ***disadvantages:***\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adult stem cells are multipotent **OR** are often restricted in the number of cell types they can produce **WHILE** embryonic stem cells are pluripotent **OR** can differentiate into any bpdy cell type [1 mark]    \n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adult stem cells are limited in number within adult tissues **OR** can be challenging to obtain **OR** culture **WHILE** embryonic stem cells are more numerous OR easier to harvest    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adult stem cells have less potential for long-term proliferation **OR** durability in culture **COMPARED TO** embryonic stem cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adult stem cells are derived from fully developed tissues so typically more differentiated, **WHEREAS** embryonic stem cells are derived from a very early developmental stage so less differentiated    [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n    One **advantage** of using adult stem cells in therapy is that they can be obtained from the patient, **reducing the risk of immune rejection** ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, harvesting adult stem cells is **less controversial** and has **fewer ethical implications** ($\\checkmark$) compared to embryonic stem cells. However, adult stem cells are **multipotent** and **limited** in the **types of cells**($\\checkmark$)  they can produce, unlike pluripotent embryonic stem cells. Moreover, adult stem cells are **less numerous** and **harder to harvest** ($\\checkmark$) than embryonic stem cells."},{"ref":"BI1638","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1638\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline how some flowers are adapted to increase their chance of pollination by insects. [4 marks]\n\n2. Describe how the structure of the placenta is adapted for its role in the development of the foetus. [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain the process of IVF (in vitro fertilisation) in humans.     [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ coloured petals to attract insects     towards them **OR** large petals for insects to land on     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ insect-pollinated flowers have nectar that provides nutrients **OR** carbohydrates **OR** sugars for insects    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ anthers produce pollen that is consumed by **OR** provides proteins for insects        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nectary is inside the flower so that insects touch the anthers and stigma when feeding    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pollen grains have spines which stick to insects    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ smell **OR** scent to attract insects        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ filament holds anther in best position to touch insect body    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ top of stigma is sticky so holds pollen in place when insect brushes it        [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Coloured petals** ($\\checkmark$) and the **scent** attract insects to the flower ($\\checkmark$). When insects **feed** on nectar and **pollen** on one plant, some of the **pollen** from the anther **sticks** to the insect due to **spikes** on the pollen ($\\checkmark$).  Then, when the same insect **feeds** on another plant's nectar or pollen, some of the pollen lands on the sticky **stigma** ($\\checkmark$) because the nectary is inside the flower. **Nectar provides carbohydrates** ($\\checkmark$) to the insect, and **pollen provides protein** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chorionic villi **OR** large surface area for exchange of materials        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ thin exchange membrane facilitates efficient diffusion of gases **OR** nutrients    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rich blood supply **OR** maternal blood pools around villi for efficient nutrient **OR** gas exchange [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ intervillous spaces allow maternal blood to flow freely for exchange of substances    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ foetal capillaries close to maternal blood for effective exchange of substances        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ selective permeability protects foetus by allowing essential nutrients **OR** gases **OR** substances through **WHILE** blocking harmful substances    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hormone production **OR** progesterone **OR** hCG secretion to maintain uterine lining **OR** support pregnancy                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ active transport of nutrients **OR** glucose **OR** amino acids    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ immune protection due to transfer of maternal antibodies to foetus        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ removal of foetal waste products **OR** carbon dioxide **OR** urea    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The placenta is adapted for foetal development with **chorionic villi**, providing a **large surface area** for **material exchange** ($\\checkmark$). It has a **thin exchange membrane** that facilitates **efficient diffusion** of **gases** and **nutrients** ($\\checkmark$). A **rich blood supply** surrounds the **villi**, and **intervillous spaces** allow **maternal blood** to **flow freely** ($\\checkmark$) for effective nutrient and gas exchange. **Foetal capillaries** are **close to maternal blood** ($\\checkmark$), enhancing **substance exchange**. The placenta's **selective permeability** permits essential nutrients while **blocking harmful substances** ($\\checkmark$). It produces **hormones** like **progesterone** and **hCG** to **maintain** the **uterine lining** ($\\checkmark$) and support pregnancy.\n\n3. Any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fertilisation outside the body **OR** in the lab        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ drug used to arrest normal menstrual cycle        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ high levels of FSH injected to stimulate maturation of multiple ovules **OR** follicles **OR** superovulation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) agonists are injected to prevent premature ovulation **OR** control the timing of ovulation        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hCG injected to complete maturation of follicles    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ova **OR** eggs harvested **OR** removed from ovaries    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sperm sample collected **OR** concentrated for fertilisation            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ova **OR** eggs mixed with sperm  **OR** ova **OR** eggs fertilised with selected sperm        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fertilised ova **OR** eggs **OR** zygotes **OR** embryos cultured in lab for several days        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ progesterone injected before the fertilised egg is implanted to improve the chances of successful implantation                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ several embryos selected for implantation into uterus        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pregnancy test used to check if pregnancy has occurred **OR** to check if embryo implanted                        [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    In vitro fertilisation (IVF) involves **fertilisation outside the body** ($\\checkmark$) in a laboratory. First, **drugs** are used to **suppress** the **menstrual cycle**  ($\\checkmark$), followed by injection of high levels of **FSH** injections to **stimulate** multiple **ova to mature** ($\\checkmark$). **Gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) agonists** are injected to **prevent premature ovulation** ($\\checkmark$), and **hCG** is injected to complete the **maturation of follicles** ($\\checkmark$). The **mature eggs** are **retrieved from** the **ovaries**  ($\\checkmark$) and **mixed** with **sperm** in the **lab** ($\\checkmark$). Selected **embryos** are then **implanted** into the **uterus**  ($\\checkmark$), and a **pregnancy test** is performed to confirm **implantation** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1684","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1684\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe Eurasian beaver (*Castor fiber*) has been described as a species that promotes biodiversity. Beavers are herbivores that feed on freshwater plants and tree bark. They use their sharp front teeth to fell trees and use the logs and branches to build dams that create pools in which they can build their lodge. A beaver lodge is a construction in which they nest and raise their young, providing protection from predators.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BI1684%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1684 fig1\">\n\n[Source: Jerzy Strzelecki, retrieved July 2024 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Castor_fiber_07(js),_Narew_River,\n\n_Poland.jpg, copyright under CC BY-SA 3.0]\n\n1. Suggest ways in which the reintroduction of beavers can increase biodiversity. [4 marks]\n\nThe effect of introducing beavers on the species diversity of birds and bats was studied in four areas: on river banks, one without any beaver activity and one where beavers are present, and then ponds, again, one without beaver activity and one where beavers were present. The graph below shows the calculated Simpson’s reciprocal index of Diversity for each area.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BI1684%20fig2.png\" alt=\"BI1684 fig2\">\n\n2. State two necessary measurements to calculate Simpson’s reciprocal index of diversity. [1 mark]\n\n3. Calculate the percentage increase in the species diversity of bats in the pond after the introduction of the beaver. [2 marks]\n\nBeavers became extinct in the UK over 400 years ago. Reintroduction programmes are now being carried out in various regions of the UK.\n\n4. Suggest **three** anthropogenic activities that could have led to the extinction of the beavers in the UK. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ many different habitats **OR** niches are created by beaver activity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ dams create pools that slow the run-off of rivers, benefiting plant rooting [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ dams generate wetlands that harbour insect **OR** invertebrate species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ this promotes a food chain with consumers such as birds [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ pools are habitats for aquatic plants **OR** fish [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ felling trees allows sunlight onto the forest floor, benefiting ground-level plants and shrubs [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fallen trees are a source of food for microorganisms **OR** detritivores [1 mark]\n\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n The activity of beavers **creates many habitats** for other organisms ($\\checkmark$), with an overall increase in biodiversity. For example, the felling of trees opens up patches of ground to sunlight, **promoting ground-level vegetation** ($\\checkmark$). The pools created by beaver dams **attract insects** ($\\checkmark$) and the consumers (such as birds) that **feed on those insects** ($\\checkmark$). Dams slow the run-off of rainwater which has a **beneficial effect on plant roots** which are not washed away by fast-flowing water ($\\checkmark$). The pools also attract **fish species and aquatic plants** that can oxygenate the pond water ($\\checkmark$); beavers do not eat fish, so there is no harm to the fish from them.\n\n2. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ total number of different species in the area **OR** species richness **AND** total number of organisms of each species **OR** species evenness [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n Two measurements needed to calculate Simpson’s Diversity Index are the **total number of species in the area** and the **total number of organisms of each species**($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ percentage increase = {(final volume - initial volume)/initial volume} $\\times$ 100\\% [1 mark]\n\n **OR** {(0.81-0.51) / 0.51} $\\times$ 100\\%\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 58.8\\% (allow numbers between 52 and 64%) [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n Percentage increase = **{(0.81$-$0.51) / 0.51} x 100\\%** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n = **58.8** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hunting for fur **OR** castoreum [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ habitat destruction **OR** the draining of wetlands **OR** other hydrological changes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ deforestation **OR** logging **OR** increase in agricultural land [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ water pollution [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fragmentation of landscape [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ urbanisation **OR** industrial development **OR** road construction [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ introduction of alien species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ trade **OR** travel leading to introduction of disease [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ use of pesticides **OR** herbicides **OR** agricultural chemicals [1 mark]\n\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n The extinction of beavers in the UK may have been caused by **overhunting** for their valuable **fur** and **castoreum** ($\\checkmark$), combined with **extensive habitat destruction** due to the **draining of wetlands** ($\\checkmark$) and **deforestation** for **agricultural expansion** ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the **introduction of non-native species** ($\\checkmark$) and **diseases**, through international **travel**, ($\\checkmark$) further contributed to their decline."},{"ref":"BI2446","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2446\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nMonozygotic twin studies have been used to investigate the effect of environmental factors on epigenetic tags.\n\nThe table below summarises data from three twin studies.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/BI2446.png\" alt=\"BI2446\">\n\n1. Define the term epigenesis. [1 mark]\n\n2. Explain why monozygotic twins are suitable to investigate the effect of the environment on methylation patterns. [3 marks]\n\n3. Describe how methylation affects gene expression. [2 marks]\n\n4. Evaluate the validity of the following statement:\n“Stress has the most significant impact on methylation in humans.” [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ development of differentiation patterns in cells of multicellular organisms [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Epigenesis is the process by which **cells** in a **multicellular organism** develop **different functions** through controlled **gene expression**, influenced by **epigenetic modifications** without changing the DNA sequence ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ monozygotic twins share 100% of their DNA [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allows comparison of identical DNA **OR** DNA is a control variable [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ environmental factors are the independent factors [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ differences between the methylation patterns are the dependent variable **OR** can be attributed to the environment [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ methylation differences can be quantified **OR** extent of DNA methylation at specific genes can be measured [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Monozygotic twins have **identical DNA** ($\\checkmark$), making genetic differences **a controlled variable**, and **environmental factors** the **independent variable** ($\\checkmark$). This means that differences between the methylation patterns of the twins can be **attributed to the environment** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ methyl tags attach to cytosine in gene promoters [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ methylation of promoters reduces transcription [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ methyl tags attach to amino acids in histones [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ methylation of histones can repress **OR** activate gene expression [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Methyl tags **attach** to **cytosine** in **promoters** ($\\checkmark$) and **amino acids** in **histones** ($\\checkmark$). Methylation of promoters **reduces transcription** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **four** of the following:\n\n ***Results could be valid because:***\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ difference in methylation between the low- and high-stress environment is highest compared to the other variables **OR** the difference in methylation between the low and high-stress environment is 25%, but it is only 10% **AND** 15% for exercise and pollution respectively [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ experiments used monozygotic twins wo share one hundred percent of their DNA **OR** are suitable to be used as a control [1 mark]\n\n ***Results may not be valid because:***\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ no test for significance of results **OR** statistical analysis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ small data set means there is limited confidence in results **OR** results may not be generalisable **OR** fit the wider population [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ only three environmental factors were investigated **OR** other factors not investigated may have more of an impact [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ uncontrolled variables may have influenced results **OR** it is difficult to control all the environmental variables to ensure the validity of results [1 mark]\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n There is a **greater difference** in **% methylation** between the **high-stress** and **low-stress** environmental conditions compared to the other variables tested ($\\checkmark$). The data could be valid because **monozygotic twins share 100% of their DNA** so they are a suitable control ($\\checkmark$). The results may not be valid because there is **no statistical analysis** ($\\checkmark$) and **uncontrolled variables may have influenced the data** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2623","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2623\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nHospital doctors compared the mitotic index of some tumour cells with that of some non-tumour cells retrieved from a patient. They prepared a light microscope slide for each specimen. The doctors diagnosed the patient’s tumour as melanoma, a skin cancer characterised by abnormally shaped, sometimes coloured moles on the skin surface.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/2623%20Edit.png\" alt=\"2623 Edit\">\n\n1. Calculate the mitotic index for each sample.        [2 marks]\n\n2. Describe **three** other requirements of the slide and microscope setup that enable the mitotic index to be calculated by light microscopy.         [3 marks]\n\n3. Melanomas have a high probability of giving rise to secondary tumours. State the name of the process by which secondary tumours form.    [1 mark]\n\n4. Suggest how the doctors could have improved the accuracy and reliability of their determination of the mitotic index.   [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ tumour sample, mitotic index 9.41 **OR** 9.4%         [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ non-tumour sample, 2.17 **OR** 2.2%        [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ *ignore omission of %*\\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ *do not credit decimal values e.g. 0.91, 0.02*\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Mitotic index formula = $(\\dfrac{\\ce{number of cells in the active stages of mitosis}}{\\ce{total number of cells observed}}) \\times 100$%\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ For the tumour sample, mitotic index = $\\dfrac{8}{85} \\times 100$% = **9.4%** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ For the non-tumour sample, mitotic index = $\\dfrac{2}{92} \\times 100$% = **2.2%** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sample must be one cell thick to allow enough light to pass through the sample            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sample must be stained    to colour the cell nuclei **OR** to provide visual contrast            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cells must be fixed **OR** dead **OR** no longer actively dividing so that no further cell division takes place **OR** to prevent the mitotic index from fluctuating            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ high magnification (200$\\times$ to 1000$\\times$) to allow enough detail to be observed within cell nuclei **OR** to be able to see condensed chromosomes of cells undergoing mitosis        [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The sample must be **dead** so that no cells are still actively dividing; this **removes the risk** of the mitotic index **fluctuating** during the study ($\\checkmark$). Tissue samples must be **one cell thick** so that **enough light** can pass through the sample to give an adequate visual picture ($\\checkmark$). The sample should be **stained** appropriately to provide **visual contrast** so that chromosomes can be observed ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ metastasis [1 mark]\n\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ This is called **metastasis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **four** of the following:\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ examined additional fields of view **OR** calculated the mitotic index from different areas of the same tissue samples [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ taken additional biopsies **OR** tissue samples from different regions **AND** analysed them microscopically [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ counted a larger number of cells **OR** increased the sample size [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ identified anomalous results **AND** investigated them [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ calculated mean mitotic indices from replicate measurements [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ used higher-resolution imaging **OR** microscopy to identify cells undergoing mitosis more accurately [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ carried out an appropriate statistical test to determine whether the difference between tumour and non-tumour samples was statistically significant [1 mark]\n\n   [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The doctors could have examined **additional fields of view** from each tissue sample ($\\checkmark$). They could have taken **additional biopsies from different regions** of the tumour and comparable non-tumour tissue ($\\checkmark$). They could have **counted a larger number of cells** ($\\checkmark$) and **calculated mean mitotic indices** from **replicate measurements** ($\\checkmark$). They could also have used **higher-resolution microscopy** to identify cells undergoing mitosis more accurately ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2772","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2772\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nJohn Endler experimented on guppies, investigating sexual and natural selection. In one experiment, he raised guppies for six months in tanks with no predators, then transferred them to three different pools. In one pool, no predators were added, in another, a strong predator was added, and in a third, a weak predator was added. Six months later, he collected data on the number and colour of spots on the guppies.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/2772.png\" alt=\"2772\">\n\n1. Determine which colour showed the greatest decrease in mean number of spots when predators were added. [1 mark]\n\n2. Using the data and your own knowledge, discuss the hypothesis that having spots makes individuals more fit. [3 marks]\n\n3. Suggest with reasons what selection pressures may have led to variation in spot colour in guppies. [2 marks]\n\nA fourth group of guppies was transferred from a pool back into the wild, and the mean number of spots was determined intermittently over 7 months.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Unassigned/images/2772b.png\" alt=\"2772b\">\n\n4. Describe how the mean number of spots changed after the guppies were released into the wild. [2 marks]\n\n5. Suggest reasons for the trend seen in the mean number of spots between 5 and 7 months. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ blue [1 mark]\n\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The mean number of **blue** spots decreased the most when predators were added ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in guppies females tend to mate with males that have more spots [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ males with more spots are more reproductively successful [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mean number of red spots did not show a large decrease under predation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ having red spots in the presence of predators is adaptive because females may prefer red spots [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ spots remained for all colours when predators were added suggesting sexual selection may favour having spots even in the presence of predators [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ predators may not always be present in the wild, so selection pressure from predation may vary [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ having blue spots decreases fitness in the presence of predators, **OR** having red spots slightly decreases fitness in the presence of predators [1 mark]\n\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Having spots** makes males more **reproductively fit** ($\\checkmark$) because **females tend to mate** with males with **more spots** ($\\checkmark$). On the other hand, **having coloured spots** makes males **more visible to predators**, which would make them **less fit** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n ***Sexual selection:***\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ different females may prefer different spot colours [1 mark]\n\n ***Natural selection:***\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ different colours may be more **OR** less visible to different predators [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ different environments **OR** backgrounds **OR** rock colours may influence which colours provide effective camouflage [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n If **females prefer different** spot **colours**, then **sexual selection** may sometimes select for a new colour of spot ($\\checkmark$). Some spot colours may be **less visible** to different **predators**, so **natural selection** may select for different colours depending on what predator is present ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mean number of spots decreased until month 5 [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ between months 5 and 7, the mean number of spots stayed the same [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ during the first month mean number of spots did not change [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The mean number of **spots decreased** for the **first 5 months** ($\\checkmark$) then **stayed the same** between **months 5 and 7** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ individuals with one spot may have higher fitness than those with no spots or many spots [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ having one spot does not increase guppy visibility to predators much [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ although one spot may increase visibility of guppies to predators, the benefit of finding a mate outweighs the risk [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ individuals with alleles for more spots may have been removed by predation, reducing the frequency of those alleles in the gene pool [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ natural selection has acted on the population over time, favouring guppies with intermediate spotting [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Having **one spot** makes individual guppies **more fit** than having no spots or many spots ($\\checkmark$). Over time, individuals with **more spots** may have been **eaten**, so the **gene pool** now mostly includes **alleles** for **fewer spots** ($\\checkmark$). This means the **population trends** toward **intermediate spotting** because it's the **best trade-off** between **avoiding predators** and **attracting mates** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2917","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI2917\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Describe the process of epigenetic inheritance.      [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline    the epigenetic origins of phenotypic differences in lion-tiger hybrids.     [6 marks]\n\n3. Explain how transcription in eukaryotic cells is regulated by proteins that bind to specific DNA sequences.        [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ epigenetic inheritance involves passing on changes in gene expression without altering the DNA sequence            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can result in phenotypic differences being passed to daughter cells **OR** offspring        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA methylation is a chemical tag added to DNA            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ histone modification alters chromatin structure [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can lead to phenotypic differences between genetically identical cells or organisms [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA methylation can prevent transcription factor binding, blocking RNA polymerase from initiating transcription       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ histone acetylation can loosen chromatin, increasing gene expression        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ histone methylation can tighten **OR** loosen chromatin, affecting transcription    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ epigenetic tags **OR** DNA methylation **OR** histone modification can remain in place during mitosis or meiosis     [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Epigenetic inheritance occurs when **changes** in **gene expression** are passed to **daughter cells** or **offspring without altering** the **DNA nucleotide sequence** ($\\checkmark$). Chemical modifications such as **DNA methylation** can **prevent transcription factors** from **binding** to **promoters** ($\\checkmark$), while **histone modification**, such as **methylation**, alters **chromatin structure**, affecting whether **genes** are **accessible** for **transcription** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **six** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ligers are the offspring of a male lion and a female tiger **AND** tigons are the hybrid offspring of a female lion and a male tiger    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ IGF2 is a growth-promoting gene involved in cell division **OR** development     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ IGF2 is expressed differently in the gametes **OR** sex cells of male and female lions due to epigenetic imprinting        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the IGF2 allele in a lion’s sperm is active and promotes rapid foetal growth     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the IGF2 allele in a lioness’ egg is silenced via imprinting **OR** DNA methylation **OR** epigenetic tags        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in tigers, both maternal and paternal IGF2 alleles are active **OR** expressed **OR** there is no imprinting on **OR** silencing of the tiger maternal IGF2 gene         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ligers are larger than both parents because both IGF2 alleles are active, leading to excessive growth        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tigons are smaller **OR** normal size because the maternal IGF2 allele from the lioness is silenced **OR** only the paternal IGF2 allele from the tiger is expressed     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phenotypic differences arise from mismatched epigenetic imprinting systems between lions and tigers        [1 mark]\n\n    [6 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    When lions and tigers interbreed, the resulting hybrids, **ligers** from **male lions** and **female tigers** and **tigons** from **male tigers** and **female lions** ($\\checkmark$), show distinct phenotypic differences. These differences arise from **epigenetic imprinting** of the **IGF2 gene**, which **promotes growth** ($\\checkmark$). In **lions**, the **maternal IGF2 allele** is **silenced** by **DNA methylation**, so only the **paternal allele** is **expressed** ($\\checkmark$). In tigers, **both parental IGF2 alleles** are **active** ($\\checkmark$). As a result, **ligers** inherit **two active IGF2 alleles**, one from the lion and one from the tigress, leading to **excessive growth** ($\\checkmark$). **Tigons** are the **same size**, or slightly **smaller**, than their parents because **only** the **paternal IGF2 allele** is **expressed** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    |Feature | Lion | Tiger | Liger | Tigon  |\n    | :-- | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n    | Parental combination | — | — | **Male lion** $\\times$ **Female tiger** | **Female lion** $\\times$ **Male tiger**   ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | Maternal IGF2  | **Silenced via methylation** ($\\checkmark$) | **Active** | Active (from tigress) | Silenced (from lioness) |\n    | Paternal IGF2  | Active | **Active** ($\\checkmark$) | Active (from lion) | Active (from tiger) |\n    | Total IGF2 expression | One active allele | Two active alleles | **Two active alleles** |  **One active allele** |\n    | Phenotypic outcome | Normal growth | Normal growth | **Excessive growth (larger)** |  **Normal or smaller size** |\n    | Cause of hybrid difference | — | — | **No silencing of maternal IGF2** ($\\checkmark$) | **Silencing of maternal IGF2** ($\\checkmark$) |\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA polymerase II and general transcription factors are required to initiate transcription    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA polymerase II reads the DNA template to build a strand of mRNA    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ general transcription factors help RNA polymerase II bind correctly to the promoter [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a promoter is a specific DNA sequence located near the start of a gene    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ promoter indicates where RNA polymerase should bind **AND** where transcription begins [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enhancers are DNA sequences that increase transcription of a gene    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enhancers can be located far upstream  **OR**  downstream from the gene to be transcribed    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ activator proteins bind to enhancers to increase transcription of the gene [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ external signals **OR** hormones **OR** growth factors **OR** stress activate activator transcription factors         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA looping brings enhancer-bound activators close to promoter-bound factors to initiate transcription            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ repressor factors bind to silencers to inhibit transcription        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ co-activators **OR** mediator proteins facilitate communication between transcription factors and RNA polymerase     [1 mark]\n\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Transcription in eukaryotic cells is tightly regulated by proteins that bind to specific DNA sequences. **RNA polymerase II** and **general transcription factors** are essential for **initiating transcription** ($\\checkmark$). General **transcription factors** help **RNA polymerase bind** to the **promoter** ($\\checkmark$), a **DNA sequence** near the **start** of a **gene** that marks **where transcription begins** ($\\checkmark$). **Enhancers** are regulatory DNA elements that **increase transcription rates** ($\\checkmark$), even when located **far upstream** or **downstream** from the **gene** ($\\checkmark$). **Activator proteins bind** to **enhancers** and are **activated** by **external signals** such as **hormones** or **stress** ($\\checkmark$). **DNA looping** brings **enhancer-bound activators** into proximity with **promoter-bound transcription factors**, facilitating the **assembly** of the **transcription initiation complex** ($\\checkmark$)."}]},{"name":"Water Potential","questions":[{"ref":"BI0405","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0405\n\nThe image below shows red blood cells placed in three different solutions as seen under the light microscope. Which type of solution were the cells shown in micrograph A most likely placed in?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/BI0405.png\" alt=\"BI0405\">\n[Source: Adapted from Zephyris. (2012). *Human Erythrocytes Osmotic Pressure Phase Contrast Plain* [Micrograph]. Retrieved April 21, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=18401754. Copyright under CC BY-SA 3.0]\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ A hypertonic solution\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ A hypotonic solution\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ An isotonic solution\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Distilled water","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nRed blood cells placed in a hypertonic solution shrivel or become crenated, as shown in micrograph A.  Plant cells undergo plasmolysis.\n\nRed blood cells placed in a hypotonic solution (**Choice B**) or distilled water (**Choice D**) would swell and eventually burst, as shown in micrograph C. Cells placed in an isotonic solution (**Choice C**) would show no change in appearance, as shown in micrograph B."},{"ref":"BI0408","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0408\n\nThe graph below shows the change in mass of a potato slice (%) at different concentrations of sucrose solution ($mol$ $dm^{-3}$).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/BI0408QB.png\" alt=\"BI0408QB\">\n\nWhich of the concentrations is the closest estimate of the molarity of the potato cells?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.01 $mol$ $dm^{-3}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.22 $mol$ $dm^{-3}$\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.38 $mol$ $dm^{-3}$\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.57 $mol$ $dm^{-3}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe trendline crossing the horizontal axis at a 0.0% change in mass indicates that the potato cells are in an isotonic solution with no net movement of water by osmosis. Here, the concentration of the sucrose solution would be equal to the molarity of the potato cells.\n\nThe changes in mass at sucrose concentrations of 0.01 $mol$ $dm^{-3}$ (**Choice A**) and 0.22 $mol$ $dm^{-3}$ (**Choice B**) is positive, and the change is negative at 0.57 $mol$ $dm^{-3}$ (**Choice D**) thus indicating that the concentrations of the sucrose solutions were not equal to the molarity of the potato cells."},{"ref":"BI0409","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0409\n\nWhich statement best describes the osmotic concentration of a solution used to bathe organs for use in organ transplant procedures?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The solution must have an osmotic concentration higher than that of the organ to be transplanted.\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The solution must have an osmotic concentration lower than that of the organ to be transplanted.\n\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ The solution must be isotonic compared to the cells of the organ to be transplanted.\n\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The solution must be mixed with cells from the organ to be transplanted.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe solution used to bathe organs to be used in a transplant should have an osmotic concentration equal to that of the organ to be transplanted and so the solution is isotonic compared to the organ’s tissues.\n\nA solution with an osmotic concentration higher than that of the organ to be transplanted (**Choice A**) would cause crenation of tissue cells, while a solution with an osmotic concentration lower than that of the organ to be transplanted (**Choice B**) would cause lysis of organ cells. There are no described protocols that indicate that the solution should be mixed with cells from the organ to be transplanted (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0410","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0410\n\nWhich of the following surface area to volume ratios would result in the highest rate of diffusion for a cell?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The higher the surface area to volume ratio, the higher the rate of diffusion\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The lower the surface area to volume ratio, the higher the rate of diffusion\n\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ The higher the volume to surface area ratio, the higher the rate of diffusion\n\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The lower the volume to surface area ratio, the lower the rate of diffusion","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nHaving a high surface area to volume ratio results in increased rates of diffusion across the cell membrane as a greater surface area of the plasma membrane is available for the exchange of materials compared to the cell’s volume.\n\nLower surface area to volume ratios (**Choice B**) and higher volume to surface area ratios (**Choice C**) result in lower rates of diffusion. A lower volume to surface area ratio would result in an increased rate of diffusion, not a decreased rate (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0411","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0411\n\nAn artificial cell is placed in a beaker of sucrose solution, as shown below. In which direction will there be a net movement of water?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/0411i.png\" alt=\"0411i\">\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Water molecules will diffuse out of the cell.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Water molecules will diffuse into the cell.\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Water molecules will diffuse equally into and out of the cell.\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Water molecules will not move across the membrane.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe sucrose concentration inside the cell is 0.25 mol dm:sup[-3], making it hypotonic (lower solute concentration, higher water potential). The sucrose concentration of the solution surrounding the cell is 0.35 mol dmdm:sup[-3], making it hypertonic (higher solute concentration, lower water potential). Water molecules will move from the hypotonic region (inside the cell) to the hypertonic region (outside the cell) by osmosis. **Choice A** is correct.\n\nWater molecules diffusing into the cell (**Choice B**) would occur if the inside of the cell had a higher solute concentration than the outside, which is not the case here.\n\nWater molecules diffusing equally into and out of the cell (**ChoiceC**) would happen if the solute concentrations inside and outside the cell were equal, leading to no net movement of water. However, the solute concentrations are not the same in this scenario.\n\nWater molecules will always diffuse across selectively permeable membranes, even in cases of dynamic equilibrium. When solute concentrations on each side are the same, water molecules move, but there is no overall net movement. **Choice D** is, therefore, not possible."},{"ref":"BI0413","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0413\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nMembrane transport is important for allowing particles to move across the cell membrane and respond to changes in the chemical composition of the extracellular fluid.\n\n1. Outline the effects of placing a liver cell in a hypotonic solution.    [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline how the fluidity of membranes allows materials to be taken into cells or to be released from cells.  [6 marks]\n\n3. Compare and contrast four types of membrane transport. Include an example of each. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the liver cell swells **OR** enlarges **OR** expands **OR** increase in volume [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the liver cell ruptures **OR** burst **OR** lyses    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water diffuses into the liver cell **OR** water moves from the medium of lower solute concentration  to a medium where there is higher solute concentration  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ osmotic pressure favors the movement of water into the cell **OR** water will diffuse from a medium of lower osmotic pressure to higher osmotic pressure [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    Once a liver cell is placed in a hypotonic solution, **water** will diffuse from a **medium of lower solute concentration to a region of higher solute concentration** ($\\checkmark$) by osmosis. This results in **an influx of water into the liver cell** that **causes the cell to swell**($\\checkmark$) and eventually **rupture** ($\\checkmark$) depending on how steep the concentration gradient is.\n\n2. any **six** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the phospholipid composition of the cell membrane allows it to be in a fluid state [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the fluidity of the membrane allows membranes to change shape **OR** move [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the fluidity of the membrane allows the membrane to interact **OR** fuse with vesicles **OR** vesicles pinch off from  the membrane  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles are composed of phospholipids [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ during exocytosis, material to be exported by the cell is packaged in membrane-bound vesicles that fuse with the plasma membrane [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ during exocytosis, vesicles release their contents to the cell exterior and the membrane flattens out again [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ during endocytosis, materials to be imported into the cell is packaged in membrane-bound vesicles that pinch off  the plasma membrane and are transported to the cell interior     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ proteins in the membrane form vesicles using ATP [1 mark]\n\n    [6 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n    The cell membrane is a **phospholipid bilayer that is fluid** ($\\checkmark$). This allows **membranes to change shape** ($\\checkmark$) and to **form vesicles that pinch off of it or receive vesicles that fuse with it.** ($\\checkmark$)  These vesicles are involved in membrane transport known as endocytosis and exocytosis. **Exocytosis** is an active form of transport in which cells export material to the cell membrane or to the extracellular fluid via vesicles. These vesicles are transported to the cell surface where they **fuse with the plasma membrane and release their contents** to the extracellular fluid ($\\checkmark$). Vesicles fusing with the cell membrane help rebuild the cell membrane as they too are composed of **phospholipids**. ($\\checkmark$) In **endocytosis**, material entering the cell is engulfed by the cell membrane which then pinches inwards to form a vesicle surrounding the molecules. These vesicles are then **transported to the interior of the cell** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **ALL** four types of membrane transport move substances across a partially permeable membrane [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion **AND** osmosis are passive **OR** do not require ATP **WHILE** active transport uses energy **OR** requires ATP [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen diffuses across membranes by simple diffusion **OR** accept other valid examples [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the sodium potassium pump is an example of active transport and hydrolyzes ATP **OR** accept other valid examples [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ simple diffusion does not require membrane proteins to occur **WHILE** facilitated diffusion, osmosis, **AND** active transport all occur via membrane proteins **OR** facilitated diffusion requires protein channels or carrier proteins, osmosis sometimes uses aquaporins, **AND** active transport requires protein pumps    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the transport of chloride ions through channel proteins is an example of facilitated diffusion **OR** accept other valid examples [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the movement of water across a semipermeable membrane through aquaporins is an example of osmosis **OR** accept other valid examples [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion **AND** osmosis allow the diffusion of molecules down their concentration gradients **WHILE** active transport transports substances against their concentration gradient [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion **AND** osmosis allow the system to reach equilibrium **WHILE** active transport drives the system away from equilibrium [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ simple diffusion occurs with small polar particles **OR** hydrophobic molecules **OR** non-polar particles, **WHILE** facilitated diffusion occurs with larger or charged molecules, **WHEREAS** osmosis occurs with water molecules only **WHILE** active transport can transport any molecules [1 mark]\n\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    | Simple Diffusion | Facilitated Diffusion | Osmosis | Active Transport | $\\hspace{1cm}$ |\n    | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n    | passive | passive | passive | active | ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | does not require ATP hydrolysis | does not require ATP hydrolysis | does not require ATP hydrolysis | requires ATP hydrolysis | |\n    | does not require membrane proteins | requires membrane proteins | requires membrane proteins | requires membrane proteins | ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | transports substances down their concentration gradient | transports substances down their concentration gradient | transports water down its concentration gradient | transports substances up their concentration gradient | ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | applies to small polar, or small nonpolar, or hydrophobic molecules | applies to larger or charged molecules | applies to water molecules only | applies to any molecules  | ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | system eventually reaches equilibrium | system eventually reaches equilibrium | system eventually reaches equilibrium | does not allow the system to reach equilibrium | ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | Example: diffusion of **O$_2$** | Example: Diffusion of **Cl$^{-}$ ions** through **Cl$^{-}$ channels** | Example: diffusion of water through **aquaporins** in the kidneys| Example: pumping of **Na$^{+}$ and K$^{+}$ ions** through the **Na$^{+}$/K$^{+}$ pump** | ($\\checkmark$) |"},{"ref":"BI0765","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0765\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nIn an investigation into the effect of solute concentration on diffusion rate, starch solutions, of varying concentrations, were placed inside starch permeable membrane balloons. Each balloon was put into a beaker of distilled water and at 3 minute intervals, samples were taken from the solution surrounding the membrane balloons in each beaker, placed on spotting tiles and tested for the presence of starch. The time taken for a positive test to show was recorded for each trial.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/BI0765A.png\" alt=\"BI0765A\">\n\nThe results are shown in the graph.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/BI0765COR.png\" alt=\"BI0765COR\">\n\n1.\n 1. Identify the dependent and independent variable in this investigation. [2 marks]\n\n 2. State one variable, other than time of testing, that should be kept constant in this investigation to ensure the results are valid. [1 mark]\n\n2. Describe a positive test for starch. [1 mark]\n\n3. Explain why the method used in this study would not be appropriate for an investigation into the effects of solute concentration on active transport. [2 marks]\n\n4.\n 1. Samples taken from the 5% and 10% starch solution both tested positive for starch after 3 minutes. Suggest a way in which the experiment could be improved to provide a more accurate result. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Predict the time taken for a positive starch test for a starch concentration of 3%. [1 mark]\n\n5. Suggest modifications to the methodology that would make it suitable to investigate the effect of glucose concentration on the rate of diffusion. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ independent variable is starch concentration **OR** solute concentration [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ dependent variable is time taken for a positive starch test **OR** diffusion rate [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The independent variable in this investigation is the **starch concentration** ($\\checkmark$) and the dependent variable is the **time taken for a positive starch test** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2. Any **one** of following:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ volume of distilled water [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ volume of starch solution [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ size of beaker [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ surface area of membrane balloon **OR** size and shape of membrane balloon [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ In the experiment, **the volume of distilled water** ($\\checkmark$) used should be kept constant.\n\n2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ orange **OR** brown to blue **OR** black [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ A positive test for starch would result in the iodine changing colour from **brown to black** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ active transport requires energy **OR** proteins [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ membrane balloon is non-living **OR** artificial **OR** lacks requirements for active transport [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ starch does not move by active transport **OR** starch only moves actively by exocytosis **OR** endocytosis (which the membrane balloon cannot replicate) [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Active transport **requires energy** ($\\checkmark$), but this methodology, which uses an **artificial** membrane balloon, does **not have** a source of energy ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.\n 1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ decrease time between samples **OR** sample at shorter time intervals **OR** increase the frequency of samples\n[1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The accuracy of the experiment could be improved by **increasing the frequency** ($\\checkmark$) of samples, for example, every one minute, instead of every three minutes.\n\n4.\n 2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 6 minutes [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/BI0765ANSACHG1.png\" alt=\"BI0765ANSACHG1\">\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ A positive test for starch, at 3%, would take approximately **6 minutes** ($\\checkmark$) based on the data in the graph.\n\n5. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ dialysis tubing **OR** visking tubing (instead of starch permeable membrane balloon) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ put different concentrations of glucose solution in dialysis **OR** visking tubing [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Benedict’s reagent (instead of iodine solution) **OR** glucose test strips [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ heat samples to above 70°C for positive (glucose) test with Benedicts [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ enzyme **OR** amylase to break down starch OR different concentrations of glucose in tubing at start [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ same concentration **OR** same volume, of enzyme OR same volume of starting glucose concentrations [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n To modify the methodology, we could use **dialysis tubing** ($\\checkmark$) instead of the starch permeable membrane balloon. We would also need to use **Benedict’s reagent** ($\\checkmark$) instead of iodine to test for glucose."},{"ref":"BI0973","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0973\n\nWhich is **not** a correct definition or equation for water potential?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The sum of the solute and pressure potential of a solvent\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ ψ${_w}$ = ψ${_s}$ + ψ${_p}$\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The direction that water will move through a semipermeable membrane\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The potential energy of water per unit volume","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWater potential refers to the energy stored in water, not the direction that water will move. **Choice C** is not true of water potential, and so is the correct choice.\n\n**Choice B**  represents the correct equation for water potential, where ψ${_w}$ represents water potential, ψ${_s}$ represents solute potential, and ψ${_p}$  represents pressure potential. **Choice A** is true of water potential and is the same as the equation in **Choice B** but in words rather than symbols. **Choice D**  is a true definition of water potential. **Choices A**, **B** and **D** are, therefore all incorrect answers to the question."},{"ref":"BI0974","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0974\n\nThe diagram shows a piece of potato tissue that has been placed in distilled water. Which is the correct reason for the movement of water from the distilled water into the potato cells?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/beaker%20and%20potato%20tissue.png\" alt=\"beaker and potato tissue\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The distilled water has a lower water potential than the cytoplasm of the potato cells\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The cytoplasm of the potato cells has a lower solute potential than the distilled water\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The cytoplasm of the potato cells has a higher water potential than the distilled water\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The distilled water has a lower solute potential than the cytoplasm of the potato cells","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDistilled water has a solute potential of zero, and the cytoplasm of the potato cells has a negative solute potential due to the presence of solute. Water potential is dependent on solute potential and pressure potential, and the lower solute potential of the cytoplasm will make the water potential inside the potato cells lower, or more negative. This means water will move by osmosis from an area of higher, or less negative, water potential (distilled water) to an area of lower, or more negative, water potential (cytoplasm). **Choice B** is therefore correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as the distilled water has a higher water potential than the cytoplasm of the potato cells, due to the absence of solutes. **Choice C** is incorrect as the cytoplasm of the potato cells has a lower water potential than the distilled water due to the presence of solutes. **Choice D** is incorrect as the distilled water has a higher solute potential than the cytoplasm of the potato cells."},{"ref":"BI0975","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0975\n\nWhich is the most likely solute potential  (ψ${_s}$) of  50 cm:sup[3] distilled water if 10g of salt is added to it?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ -0.2 kPa\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 0.8 kPa\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 0 kPa\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 0.1 kPa","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDistilled water has a solute potential of 0 kPa, and with increasing solute concentration the solute potential decreases. -0.2 kPa is below 0 kPa, **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choice C**  is incorrect as 0 kPa is the solute potential of distilled water. **Choices B** and **D**  are incorrect, as solute potentials cannot be above zero."},{"ref":"BI0976","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0976\n\nThe diagram shows the setup for an osmosis experiment. The potato tissue was placed in a solution of unknown concentration for 3 days. After 3 days, the potato had lost mass. Which type of solution was the potato placed in, and what was the effect on the pressure potential?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/beaker%20and%20potato%20tissue.png\" alt=\"beaker and potato tissue\">\n\n| | Concentration of solution | Effect on pressure potential of tissue |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Hypertonic | Decrease |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Hypotonic | Increase |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Hypertonic| Increase |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Hypotonic | Decrease |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe term hypertonic means that the unknown solution has a higher concentration of solutes than the potato tissue, indicating that the solution has a lower, or more negative, water potential than inside the potato cells. This will cause water to move out of the potato cells via osmosis, decreasing the pressure inside the cells and thereby decreasing the pressure potential. **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choices B** and **D** are incorrect as the solution must be hypertonic for water to leave the potato cells and cause the loss in mass. **Choice C** is incorrect, as when the potato tissue loses mass, the pressure inside the potato cells will decrease, decreasing the pressure potential inside the cells."},{"ref":"BI0977","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0977\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe micrograph shows onion epidermal cells.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/BI0977A.png\" alt=\"BI0977A\">\n[Source: Adapted from kaibara87. (2012, April 26). *File: Onion Cells.jpg.* Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved August 05, 2023, from URL https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Onion_Cells.jpg. Copyright under CC BY 2.0]\n\n1.\n    1. Draw a diagram of one onion cell after it has been placed in a highly hypertonic solution, labelling the position of the cell membrane, cell wall and vacuole.        [2 marks]\n\n    2. State a definition for the term ‘hypertonic solution’.[1 mark]\n\n    3. Explain the reasons for the changes to the onion cell in your answer to part (i), with reference to water potential.        [4 marks]\n\n    4. The water potential of the onion cell in the micrograph is -700kPa, and the pressure potential is 900 kPa. Calculate the solute potential of the cell using the equation below.\n\n    ψw = ψs + ψp\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n2. Explain why the calculated value for water potential is always relative to pure water at atmospheric pressure and a temperature of 20 °C.        [1 mark]\n\n3. Predict, with a reason, how the solute potential of the cytoplasm of an onion cell would be affected if it was placed in a hypotonic solution.        [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a clear diagram showing an onion cell where the cell membrane has pulled away from the cell wall             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ correctly labelled cell wall, cell membrane and vacuole            [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/bi977ansa.png\" alt=\"bi977ansa\">\n\n1.\n    2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ an external solution with a higher concentration of solute **OR** lower concentration of water molecules compared to the cytoplasm of a cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ an external solution with a lower **OR** more negative, solute potential compared to the cytoplasm of a cell        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    A hypertonic solution is an external solution with a **higher concentration of solute** compared to the cytoplasm of a cell ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    3. Any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cytoplasm of onion cell has a higher **OR** less negative, water potential than the external solution    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ causes water to leave the onion cell via osmosis **OR** water moves from an area of higher **OR** less negative, water potential to an area of lower **OR** more negative, water potential via osmosis        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ causes the cytoplasm to lose water so that the cell membrane pulls away from the cell wall                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ causes the vacuole to lose water, becoming smaller        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water potential is a combination of pressure potential **AND** solute potential    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as water leaves the onion cell, the solute potential decreases **OR** becomes more negative, **AND** the pressure potential decreases     [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The cytoplasm of the onion cell has a **higher, or less negative, water potential** than the hypertonic solution ($\\checkmark$). This results in the water molecules moving out of the onion cell via **osmosis** ($\\checkmark$), causing the **cytoplasm and vacuole to lose water** ($\\checkmark$). As a result, the cell **membrane pulls away** from the cell wall, and the **vacuole becomes smaller** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    4. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ -1600 kPa        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Water potential = Solute potential + Pressure potential \\\n    Solute potential = Water potential - pressure potential \\\n    Solute potential = -700 kPa - 900 kPa \\\n    Solute potential = **-1600 kPa** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2.  Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it is extremely difficult to measure the absolute potential energy of water as it’s difficult to measure intermolecular energy **OR** potential energy of water molecules    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pure water at atmospheric pressure at 20 °C has a pressure potential of zero, so water potential is equal to solute potential, so it is a good reference point    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ using pure water at atmospheric pressure at a specific temperature provides a standard against which other solutions can be compared [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ using a standard temperature like 20 °C reflects conditions that are often relevant to living organisms, providing a practical point of reference for studying water potential in biological systems [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use of pure water at standard conditions simplifies the mathematical calculations and theoretical models used to determine water potential [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The calculated value for water potential is always relative to pure water at atmospheric pressure at 20°C because it is **difficult to measure the potential energy** contained in water molecules ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase **BECAUSE** the hypotonic solution has a higher, or less negative, water potential **OR** higher water concentration **OR** lower solute concentration than inside the cell, so water would move into the cell        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The solute potential of the onion cell would **increase** because the hypotonic solution has a higher, or less negative,  water potential than the cytoplasm of the cell, so **water** would **move into** the cell via **osmosis**, **diluting** the contents of the cell ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0986","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0986\n\nTwo patients were given solutions intravenously as part of a medical treatment.  One patient had a generally negative health outcome while the other had a positive health outcome.  Blood samples were taken from both patients and then viewed through a light microscope.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/BI0986.png\" alt=\"BI0986\">\n[Source: Adapted from Zephyris (2012, February 16). Tonicity-Tonicity.  Wiki.  Retrieved July 24th, 2023, from https:// en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File: Human_Erythrocytes_OsmoticPressure_PhaseContrast_Plain.svg. CC 3.0]\n\nWhich correctly identifies the health outcomes for the two patients and the likely reasons for these outcomes?\n\n| | Patient A | Patient A | Patient B | Patient B\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-:\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Negative health outcome | Solution was hypotonic | Survived | Solution was isotonic |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Negative health outcome | Solution was hypertonic | Survived | Solution was isotonic|\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Survived | Solution was hypertonic| Negative health outcome | Solution was hypotonic |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Survived | Solution was isotonic | Negative health outcome| Solution was hypertonic |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because when red blood cells are exposed to hypertonic solutions, water diffuses out of the cells causing the cells to become crenated (shrivelled) which can lead to negative effects on a person's health.  Patient B survived because red blood cells typically function normally in isotonic solutions.\n\n**Choices A** and **C** are incorrect because in hypotonic solutions, red blood cells may lyse whereas the red blood cells of patient A are crenated and the those of patient B have retained their original shape.  **Choice D** is incorrect because the red blood cells of patient A are crenated and in an isotonic solution they would not be crenated.  In patient B the red blood cells are not crenated which would be expected if they were placed in an isotonic solution."},{"ref":"BI0987","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0987\n\nAn organ donor's heart is removed to be transplanted and is stored in an isotonic solution prior to transplant.  Which of the following may result if the heart is not stored in a solution that is isotonic?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Water will diffuse into the cardiac muscles.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ There will be no net movement of water into heart tissues.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The heart will dry out.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The recipient’s immune system may reject the heart.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because if the heart were placed in a hypotonic solution (as opposed to isotonic) water would diffuse into the cardiac muscles.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because there would be no net movement into the tissues of the heart in an isotonic solution, not a hypotonic or hypertonic solution.  **Choice C** is incorrect because even if it is placed in a solution other than an isotonic solution, it cannot dry out as it is still bathed in a watery solution.  **Choice D** is incorrect as the tonicity of the solution the heart is stored in has no effect on whether the recipient’s immune system identifies the heart as foreign which would lead to rejection of the heart by the immune system."},{"ref":"BI0988","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0988\n\nA solution of NaCl that is isotonic to the tissues of a donor's heart is used to store the heart prior to transplanting the heart into another person.  How might this affect the ability of myocytes, making up the heart tissue, to contract?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ There will be no effect on myocytes ability to contract.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sodium ions will diffuse out of the sarcoplasmic reticulum.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Binding sites on actin will be exposed less frequently.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Intercalated discs will lose their structural stability, reducing the heart rate.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because if the solution in which the heart is stored lacks Ca:sup[2+], there is a likelihood that  Ca:sup[2+]  will diffuse out of the myocytes. This diffusion of Ca:sup[2+] from the myocytes can lead to a decrease in the overall Ca:sup[2+] concentration within the heart. This reduced Ca:sup[2+] concentration may result in an insufficient amount available to effectively bind to troponin, and so tropomyosin will not be moved to expose the binding sites on actin filaments.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because myocyte contractions will be affected by Ca:sup[2+] diffusing out of the myocytes.  **Choice B** is incorrect because sodium ions are not present in the sarcoplasmic reticulum to any significant extent and although they do play a role in action potentials in myocytes, they are not involved in myocyte contractions.  **Choice D** is incorrect because the structure of intercalated discs is unlikely to be affected by ion concentrations."},{"ref":"BI0989","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0989\n\nWhat is the likely effect on oxygen transport in a human if a hypotonic solution is given intravenously?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Oxygen transport by red blood cells will increase.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Oxygen transport in blood plasma will increase.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Oxygen transport in blood plasma will decrease.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Oxygen transport by red blood cells will decrease.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because a hypotonic solution can cause red blood cells to swell and lyse, reducing the number of cells available to transport oxygen.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because red blood cell lysis decreases, not increases, oxygen transport.\n\n**Choices B** and **C** are incorrect  because adding a hypotonic solution does not significantly change the amount of oxygen dissolved in blood plasma."},{"ref":"BI1065","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1065\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the stages of solvation when water acts as the solvent.        [5 marks]\n\n2. Describe what you would see down a microscope if an onion cell is first put into pure water and then into a strong salt solution.    [4 marks]\n\n3. Explain the movement of water across the cell surface membrane when an animal cell is placed in different solute concentrations.    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ separation of solute particles **OR** molecules **OR** ions [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ breaking of intermolecular forces OR ionic OR hydrogen bonds holding solute particles together [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ disruption of hydrogen bonds between water molecules to accommodate solute particles [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ attractive forces **OR** hydrogen bonds between solute and water molecules form  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water molecules surround solute particles **OR** \"hydration shell\" surrounds each solute particle, preventing  re-aggregating [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ over time, a dynamic equilibrium is established between solute particles leaving the solid and water molecules joining the hydration shells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at maximum solubility of the solute in the water, solution is saturated [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Solvation is the interaction of water molecules with **solute particles**. First, the **solute particles** must **separate** ($\\checkmark$), which often involves the **breaking of intermolecular bonds** ($\\checkmark$)  holding the solute particles together. Some **hydrogen bonds** between **water molecules** also **break**  ($\\checkmark$) . The **water molecules** then form **hydrogen bonds** or electrostatic attractions with the **solute** ($\\checkmark$). These **water molecules** surround the **solute** particles, forming **hydration shells** that **prevent** them from **re-joining** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following (*must include at least **one** from pure water and **one** from strong salt solution to gain full marks*):\n\n    ***pure water:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water moves into the cell, **AND** the cell contents swell  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell membrane pushes against the cell wall [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the cell has become turgid, **OR** cell exerts turgor pressure [1 mark]\n\n    ***strong salt solution:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water moves out of the cell, **AND** the cell contents begin to shrink **OR** reduce in volume [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell membrane pulls away from cell wall [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plasmolysis occurs [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    When a plant cell is put into **pure water** and is observed under a microscope, the **cell contents** can be seen to **swell** ($\\checkmark$), and the **cell membrane pushes** out against the **cell wall** ($\\checkmark$), exerting **turgor pressure** ($\\checkmark$). If the plant cell is then put into a strong salt solution, the **cell contents shrink** ($\\checkmark$), the **cell membrane pulls away** from the **cell wall** ($\\checkmark$), and the cell is said to be **plasmolysed** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following (*must include at least **one** of the first 3 to gain full marks*):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water moves across the membrane by osmosis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water molecules move by diffusion    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ membrane is selectively permeable    **OR** membrane is impermeable to some solutes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water moves through phospholipid bilayer **OR** membrane, which is not a barrier to water    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ net movement of water until equilibrium is reached **OR** until solute concentrations are the same    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water diffuses **OR** moves towards area of higher solute concentration    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ passive transport **OR** no ATP needed    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water moves into cell if placed in hypotonic solution    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell swells, **OR** lysis occurs **OR** cell bursts in hypotonic solution    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water moves out of cell if placed in hypertonic solution    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell shrivels, **OR** crenation occurs in hypertonic solution    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ no net movement of water if cell is placed in isotonic solution     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell maintains normal shape in isotonic solutions                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rate of diffusion **OR** osmosis **OR** water movement is increased by water channels **OR** specific proteins in membranes **OR** aquaporins    [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Water diffuses** ($\\checkmark$) directly **across** the membrane **phospholipid bilayer** ($\\checkmark$) in a process called **osmosis** ($\\checkmark$).  When there is a difference in solute concentration, **water** will **move** to areas of **higher solute** concentration ($\\checkmark$). **Water moves into** an animal **cell** if placed in a **hypotonic solution** ($\\checkmark$), which may lead to **lysis** ($\\checkmark$) of the cell. **Water moves out** of a cell if placed in a **hypertonic solution** ($\\checkmark$), which may lead to **crenation** ($\\checkmark$). There is **no net movement** of water if the cell is placed in an **isotonic solution** ($\\checkmark$), and so the cell will maintain its **normal shape** ($\\checkmark$). The movement of water is a type of **passive transport** ($\\checkmark$) and does not involve ATP."},{"ref":"BI1090","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1090\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nBelow is a diagram showing the response of red blood cells when placed in different solute concentrations.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/BI1090.png\" alt=\"BI1090\">\n[Source: Adapted from Zephyris (2012, February 16). *Tonicity-Tonicity*.  Wiki.  Retrieved July 24th, 2023, from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File: Human_Erythrocytes_OsmoticPressure_PhaseContrast_Plain.svg. CC 3.0.]\n\n1. State what is meant by the term isotonic solution.    [1 mark]\n\n2. Identify which concentrations were hypotonic and which were hypertonic to the red blood cells.    [2 marks]\n\n3. If red blood cells were placed in a hypotonic solution, explain the likely changes in ion concentration in the cytoplasm of the cells.    [2 marks]\n\n4. Describe the process of how a solid granule of NaCl becomes a solute when placed in water.    [2 marks]\n\n5. Compare and contrast how animal and plant cells are affected by being placed in hypotonic solutions and hypertonic solutions.     [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ solute concentration **OR** water potential in the cytoplasm **OR** cytosol **OR** cell **OR** across the cell membrane is the same as its environment **OR** solution    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    An isotonic solution is a solution that has the **same** tonicity as the **cytoplasm** of a cell ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. **BOTH** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ concentration A was hypertonic to the red blood cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ concentration C was hypotonic to the red blood cells [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Concentration **A** was **hypertonic** to the red blood cells ($\\checkmark$) and concentration **C** was **hypotonic** to the red blood cells ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ion concentrations would decrease        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water would diffuse into the cells down its concentration gradients **OR** from higher water potential to lower water potential **OR** by osmosis **OR** water molecules diffuse into cell from a lower to higher solute concentration    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased water concentration in red blood cells OR decreased ion concentration        [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The ion concentration would **decrease** ($\\checkmark$) as a result of water **diffusing** into the cell by **osmosis** ($\\checkmark$), which would **increase** the **percent** composition of **water** in the cytosol ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen bonds form between water molecules and Na+ and Cl- OR NaCl [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$slightly positive hydrogen atoms are attracted to the negatively charged chloride ions OR slightly negative oxygen atoms are attracted to the positively charged sodium ions [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water surrounding Na+ and Cl- ions weakens ionic bonds holding the NaCl crystal together [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Na+ and Cl- ions separate and a shell of water molecules surround each ion [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrated sodium and chloride ions are now dispersed throughout the water OR move independently of each other [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water surrounds the Na+ and Cl- ions [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water prevents ionic bonds from reforming [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    When NaCl is placed in water, polar **water molecules** form **hydrogen bonds** with the **Na⁺** and **Cl⁻** ions ($\\checkmark$), surrounding each ion to form **hydration shells** (:span), which **prevent** the **ionic bonds between Na⁺ and Cl⁻** from **reforming** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. Any **three** of the following (students must include **one** similarity and **one** difference for full marks):\n\n    **In hypotonic solutions…**\n\n    **Similarity** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in **BOTH** plant and animal cells, water diffuses into the cells **OR** moves in by osmosis    [1 mark]\n\n    **Difference** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plant cells become turgid **WHEREAS** animal cells may lyse     [1 mark]\n\n    **In hypertonic solutions…**\n\n    **Similarity** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in **BOTH** plant and animal cells, water diffuses out of the cells **OR** moves out by osmosis    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** plant and animal cells may die in hypertonic solutions [1 mark]\n\n    **Differences** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in animal cells, the cell will shrivel **OR** become crenated **WHEREAS** in plant cells the cell will become plasmolysed **OR** flaccid    [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    In hypotonic solutions, water **diffuses into** plant and animal cells ($\\checkmark$) and in hypertonic solutions water **diffuses out** of plant and animal cells ($\\checkmark$).  However, **animal cells** may **lyse in hypotonic solutions**, whereas **plant cells** become **turgid** ($\\checkmark$).  Finally, **plant cells** may become **plasmolysed in hypertonic solutions**, whereas **animal cells** may become **crenated** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1091","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1091\n\nBelow is a diagram showing three red blood cells that have been placed in solutions of different solute concentrations.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/red%20blood%20cells%20hypotonic%20hypertonic%20isotonic.png\" alt=\"red blood cells hypotonic hypertonic isotonic\">\n\nWhich row correctly matches each cell with the type of solution that it has been immersed in?\n\n| | I | II | III\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Isotonic | Hypertonic | Hypotonic |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Hypertonic | Hypotonic | Isotonic |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Hypotonic | Isotonic | Hypertonic |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Isotonic | Hypotonic | Hypertonic|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Cell I** is the normal shape for a red blood cell, so there has been no net movement of water into or out of the cell. **Cell II** is swollen due to a net movement of water into the cell. **Cell II** has therefore been placed in a hypotonic solution, which is a solution with a lower solute concentration than inside the cell, and so water has moved into the cell by osmosis. **Cell III** is crenated due to a net movement of water out of the cell. **Cell III** has therefore been placed in a hypertonic solution, which is a solution with a higher solute concentration than inside the cell, and so water has moved out of the cell by osmosis. **Choice D** correctly matches the cells with the solution they have been put into.\n\n**Cell II** has been put into a hypotonic solution, and **Cell III** has been put into a hypertonic solution, therefore, **Choice A** is incorrect. All cells are incorrectly matched with solutions in **Choice B**. **Cell I** has been put into an isotonic solution, and **Cell II** has been put into a hypertonic solution, therefore, **Choice C** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1163","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1163\n\nThe image below shows red blood cells placed in an isotonic solution and a solution of unknown solute concentration. Which type of solution is that of unknown concentration, and what effect does this have on the volume of the cells?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/BI1163.png\" alt=\"BI1163\">\n[Source: Adapted from Zephyris. (2012). *Human Erythrocytes Osmotic Pressure Phase Contrast Plain [Micrograph]*. Retrieved April 21, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=18401754. Copyright under CC BY-SA 3.0]\n\n| | Type of solution | Effect on the volume of red blood cell |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Hypotonic | Increase |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Hypotonic | Decrease |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Hypertonic | Increase |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Hypertonic | Decrease |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA hypotonic solution has a low concentration of solute and a high concentration of water compared to the inside of the cell. A red blood cell placed in a hypotonic solution will increase in volume, as water will move into the cell via osmosis, from an area of higher water concentration/lower solute concentration to an area of lower water potential concentration/higher solute concentration across the cell surface membrane. **Choice A**  is correct, and **Choice B** is incorrect.\n\nA hypertonic solution has a higher concentration of solute and a lower concentration of water compared to the cell. A red blood cell placed in a hypertonic solution would shrivel or become crenated. **Choice C** is incorrect. Although the cell is in a hypotonic solution, this caused it to increase in volume, not decrease; **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1328","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1328\n\nWhich of the following factors is essential for calculating water potential in a solution?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Surface area of the container\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Rate of transpiration in the plant\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Solute concentration in the solution\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ pH level of the surrounding environment","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nSolute concentration is a crucial factor in determining water potential. A higher solute concentration results in a more negative solute potential, contributing to a lower water potential. **Choice C** is therefore correct.\n\nWhile surface area can affect the rate of evaporation or condensation it does not directly affect water potential. **Choice A** is therefore incorrect.  Transpiration affects water potential gradients, but it is not used in the calculation of water potential. **Choice B** is therefore not correct.  pH level can influence various biochemical reactions and cellular processes, but it does not directly impact water potential. **Choice D** is therefore incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1329","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1329\n\nThe diagram represents two solutions with different water potentials separated by a selectively permeable membrane.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/Water%20and%20Sugar%20Molecule%20Distribution.png\" alt=\"Water and Sugar Molecule Distribution\">\n\nSelect the row with the correct direction for the net movement of water molecules and the correct reason for the movement.\n\n| | Direction of Movement | Reason of Movement |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| Left to Right | Lower water potential on left of membrane |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Left to Right | Higher water potential on left of membrane |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Right to Left | Lower water potential on left of membrane |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Right to Left | Higher water potential on left of membrane |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nThe solution on the left has a higher water potential, or lower solute potential, than the right-hand side. This is because even though both sides have the same number of water molecules, there are fewer glucose molecules on the left-hand side. The net movement of water molecules will be from the higher water potential (or lower solute potential), on the left to the lower water potential (or higher solute potential) on the right; **Choice B** is, therefore, correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the solution on the left does have a higher water potential, but water will move from left to right. **Choice C** is incorrect because water molecules will move from left to right. **Choice D** is incorrect because water molecules will move from left to right because there is a higher water potential on the left."},{"ref":"BI1330","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1330\n\nThe diagram shows onion tissue in a hypertonic solution.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/BI1330.jpg\" alt=\"BI1330\">\n\nWhich row correctly describes the initial net movement of water and the resulting condition of the onion cells?\n\n| | Net movement of water | Condition of onion cells |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| Out of onion cells | Turgid |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Out of onion cells | Plasmolysed |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Into onion cells | Lysed |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Into onion cells | Plasmolysed |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\n**Choice B** is correct because the surrounding hypertonic solution initially has a lower water potential than the onion cells, so there is a net movement of water out of the cells by osmosis. As water is lost, the vacuoles shrink, and the cell contents decrease in volume, causing the cell-surface membranes to pull away from the cell walls. This condition is called plasmolysis.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because, although water moves out of the cells, this reduces turgor pressure rather than making the cells turgid. Turgidity results from water entering plant cells and the cell contents pressing against the cell walls.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because the net movement of water is out of the cells, not into them. Furthermore, the rigid cell walls normally prevent onion cells from bursting when water enters them in a hypotonic solution.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because plasmolysis results from a net movement of water out of the cells, not into them."},{"ref":"BI1331","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1331\n\nWhich equation is the correct equation for the water potential of cells with cell walls?\n\n- A.$\\Psi_w$ = $\\Psi_w$ + $\\Psi_p$\n\n- B.$\\Psi_w$ = $\\Psi_s$ $-$ $\\Psi_p$\n\n- C.$\\Psi_w$ = $\\Psi_s$ + $\\Psi_p$\n\n- D.$\\Psi_w$ = $\\Psi_p$ $-$ $\\Psi_s$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\n**Choice C** is correct because water potential is equal to solute potential plus pressure potential. **Choice A** is incorrect because water potential is not equal to water potential plus pressure potential. **Choice B** and **Choice D** are incorrect because water potential is calculated by adding solute and pressure potential, not subtracting."},{"ref":"BI1332","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1332\n\nAt which location in a plant would water potential be highest?\n\n- A. Photosynthesising leaf\n\n- B. Growing root\n\n- C. Root hair cells\n\n- D. Phloem","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\n**Choice C** is correct because root hair cells absorb mineral ions mainly by active transport. The mineral ions decrease the water potential inside the cells, therefore water enters the root hair cells, increasing water potential.  \n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because a photosynthesising leaf has a high concentration of sugars, so the water potential is low. Also, the leaves are losing water through their stomata, leading to a lower water potential. **Choice B** is incorrect because sugars are transported to meristems (locations of plant growth), so the water potential would be low. **Choice D** is incorrect because phloem transports sugars, so water potential is low."},{"ref":"BI1333","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1333\n\nWhich factor plays a crucial role in determining the pressure potential component of water potential in plants?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Solute concentration in the soil\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Turgor pressure within the cells\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Atmospheric pressure\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Surface area of the leaves","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nTurgor pressure, the pressure exerted by the cell contents against the cell wall, is a key component of pressure potential within plant cells. **Choice B** is, therefore, correct.\n\nWhile solute concentration in the soil (**Choice A**) influences water uptake by plant roots, it primarily affects solute potential rather than pressure potential. The solute concentration gradient between the soil and root cells affects the movement of water into the roots but does not directly contribute to pressure potential within the cells.\nPressure potential relates to the pressure exerted by the cell contents against the cell wall due to water uptake, not to atmospheric pressure; **Choice C** is incorrect.\nThe surface area of leaves affects factors like gas exchange and transpiration but does not directly contribute to pressure potential within cells. **Choice D** is, therefore, incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1334","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1334\n\nWhich environmental condition generally increases water potential in the cells of plant leaves?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ High humidity\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ High temperature\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ High atmospheric pressure\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ High light intensity","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nHigh humidity reduces the water potential gradient between the inside of the leaf and its surrounding environment, thereby decreasing the rate of water loss via evaporation. This allows water to accumulate in the plant's cells, thus increasing their water potential. **Choice A** is correct.\n\nHigh temperatures are likely to lead to greater loss of water from the leaves, and so reduce water potential; **Choice B** is therefore incorrect. High atmospheric pressure does not significantly influence water potential within plant cells; **Choice C** is therefore incorrect. High light intensity is likely to increase water loss from the leaves, and so decrease the water potential inside the cells of the leaf; **Choice D** is therefore incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1335","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1335\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Describe how the structure of water molecules gives water its solvent properties. [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline the importance of isotonic solutions in medical applications. [5 marks]\n\n3. A turgid plant cell is placed in a solution with a higher solute concentration than its cell contents.\n Explain the changes in the solute potential, pressure potential and water potential of the plant cell.\n\n*(Assume constant temperature, no movement of solutes across the cell membrane, and atmospheric pressure in the surrounding solution.)* [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ electrons are shared unequally in the covalent bonds between oxygen and hydrogen [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ water molecules are polar **OR** oxygen has a partial negative charge **AND** hydrogen has a partial positive charge [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ positively charged ions are attracted to the oxygen atoms of water molecules [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ negatively charged ions are attracted to the hydrogen atoms of water molecules [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen bonds form between water molecules and polar solute molecules [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ water molecules surround solute particles, allowing them to disperse through the water [1 mark]\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\nWater molecules are **polar**, with a **partial negative charge** on **oxygen** and **partial positive charges** on **hydrogen** ($\\checkmark$). **Positively charged ions** are **attracted** to the **oxygen atoms** of water molecules ($\\checkmark$), whereas **negatively charged ions** are **attracted** to the **hydrogen atoms** ($\\checkmark$). Water molecules surround **solute molecules**, allowing them to **disperse** through aqueous solutions ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ isotonic solutions have the same effective solute concentration as the cell contents [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ no net movement of water into or out of cells [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ used in intravenous fluids [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ used to bathe organs before transplantation [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ animal cells lack a cell wall to prevent bursting [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ prevent cells from swelling and bursting/lysis [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ prevent cells from shrinking/crenation [1 mark]\n\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n**Isotonic** solutions have the **same solute concentration** as the **cells** ($\\checkmark$) and so are used in **intravenous fluids** ($\\checkmark$) and to **bathe organs** before **transplantation** ($\\checkmark$). They **prevent cells** from **swelling** and **bursting** ($\\checkmark$) or **shrinking** and becoming **crenated** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** statement about the change in each of: solute potential, pressure potential **AND** water potential to gain full marks*):\n\n***Water movement***\\\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ the surrounding solution initially has a lower water potential than the cell contents [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ water moves out of the cell by osmosis [1 mark]\n\n***Solute potential***\\\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ the solute concentration within the cell increases [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ solute potential decreases **OR** becomes more negative [1 mark]\n\n***Pressure potential***\\\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ the cell contents exert less pressure against the cell wall **OR** the cell wall exerts less pressure on the cell contents [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ pressure potential decreases [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ the vacuole/cytoplasm shrinks **OR** the volume of the protoplast decreases [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ pressure potential may fall to zero **OR** the cell becomes flaccid/plasmolysed (if the solution is sufficiently concentrated) [1 mark]\n\n***Water potential and equilibrium***\\\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ water potential is equal to solute potential plus pressure potential **OR** $\\psi_w = \\psi_s + \\psi_p$ [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ the water potential of the cell decreases [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ net movement continues until the water potential of the cell equals that of the surrounding solution [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ at equilibrium, water continues to move in both directions at equal rates [1 mark]\n\n[7 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\nWater moves **out** of the cell by **osmosis** ($\\checkmark$). This **increases** the **solute concentration within** the **cell** ($\\checkmark$), making its **solute potential more negative** ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAs **water** is **lost from** the **vacuole**, **pressure potential decreases** ($\\checkmark$). As $\\psi_w = \\psi_s + \\psi_p$, the decreases in solute potential and pressure potential **lower the water potential** of the cell ($\\checkmark$).\n\n**Net water movement continues until** the **water potential** of the **cell equals** that of the surrounding **solution** ($\\checkmark$). Once **equilibrium** has been reached, **net movement** of water in **each direction** is **equal** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1404","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1404\n\nFour shell-less eggs were placed into 4 different solutions for 24 hours, after the mass of each was recorded. After 24 hours the eggs were removed from the solution, dried, and their final mass was recorded. The ratio of the final mass to the initial mass was then calculated. The results are shown below.\n\n| Egg | Ratio of final mass to initial mass |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| A | 1.31:1 |\n| B | 0.85:1 |\n| C | 1.14:1 |\n| D | 0.92:1 |\n\nWhich egg was placed in the solution nearest to being isotonic?","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe solution nearest to being isotonic is the one which is closest to the solute concentration inside the egg. This solution would result in the least net osmotic movement of water across the egg membrane and, therefore, the least change in mass. The egg with the ratio of final mass to initial mass which is closest to 1:1 is **Choice D**, with a ratio of 0.92:1.\n\n**Choice A** is the egg that gained the most mass, indicating it was placed in the most hypotonic solution. **Choice C** was placed into a weaker hypotonic solution, as it gained less mass. Egg **Choice B**, lost the most mass and so was placed in the most hypertonic solution."},{"ref":"BI1473","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1473\n\nDifferent plant tissues were placed in solutions of different sucrose concentrations, and percentage change in mass was recorded. What is the concentration of sucrose in the potato tissue?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/BI1473.png\" alt=\"BI1473\">\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.33 $\\mu$M\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 5.8 $\\mu$M\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.53 $\\mu$M\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.00 $\\mu$M","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nIsotonic solutions have the same concentration of sucrose as the plant tissue. When tissue is placed in an isotonic solution, the net movement of solvent is zero, therefore the mass of the potato would not change. The sucrose concentration at which the percentage change in mass is 0% is approximately 0.53 $\\mu$M. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **D** are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI2003","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2003\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nAn investigation was carried out to estimate the osmolarity of potato tissue. Potato tissue samples were cut into cylinders of equal dimensions, and the mass of each was recorded. The cylinders were then submerged in sucrose solutions of varying concentrations for one hour, and their final mass was recorded.\n\nFive cylinders were used at each concentration. The results are shown in the table below.\n\n| Molarity / M | Mean change in mass / g | Mean percentage change in mass / % | Standard deviation of percentage change in mass / % |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| 0.0 | 0.54 | 4.50 | 0.8 |\n| 0.2 | 0.38 | 3.20 | 0.4 |\n| 0.4 | 0.05 | 0.40 | 1.8 |\n| 0.6 | -0.96 | -8.00 | 1.4 |\n| 0.8 | -1.14 | -9.50 | 2.3 |\n\n1. Explain why the results are shown as percentage change in mass rather than change in mass. [1 mark]\n\n2. State, **two** variables, not described in the method above, that must be controlled in this investigation. Provide a reason for why each variable needs to be controlled. [4 marks]\n\n3. Use the standard deviation values to comment on the consistency of the results from this investigation. [1 mark]\n\nIn a similar investigation, a graph was plotted using data from five trials at each concentration. The error bars shown on the graph represent ±1 standard deviation.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/BI2003newi.png\" alt=\"BI2003newi\">\n\n4. Using the line of best fit, estimate, with a reason, the osmolarity of the potato tissue. [2 marks]\n\n5. Discuss the variability of the data shown in the graph. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it standardises the data, allowing for direct comparison between samples of different starting masses [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it normalises the data, making it comparable regardless of variations in initial mass [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Percentage change in mass** is used because it **standardises** the results, **allowing a direct comparison** between the samples even if their **starting masses** are **different** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following *(maximum. **two** marks for variables to be controlled and maximum **two** marks for appropriate reason matched to variable)*:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** it affects kinetic energy of molecules , influencing the rate of osmosis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ volume of solution [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** a small volume of solution would change concentration significantly as water moves into or out of the tissue **OR** it ensures the tissue is fully submerged [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ age **OR** type of potato [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** potato of different ages **OR** type may have different solute concentrations [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ presence of skin on potato [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** potato skin may have a different permeability to water compared to the inner tissue [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ same method of drying potato tissue before finding mass [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** drops of water on the potato tissue increase the mass [1 mark]\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Temperature** ($\\checkmark$) needs to be kept constant because **it affects** the **kinetic energy** of water **molecules** which influences the **rate of osmosis** ($\\checkmark$). Each cylinder of potato tissue also needs to be **dried** in the **same way** before recording the final mass, to ensure that **surface water** does not **artificially increase** the **final mass** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the data at 0.4 M show considerable variability relative to the small mean percentage change in mass, reducing confidence in that mean [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the data at 0.2 M have the lowest standard deviation, indicating the greatest consistency between repeated measurements [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the data at 0.8 M have the highest standard deviation, indicating the greatest absolute variability between repeated measurements [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The results at **0.2 M** are the **most consistent** because they have the **lowest standard deviation** (0.4), showing the **least variation** between repeated measurements ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **two** of the following *(correct value for osmolarity is needed for full marks)*:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 0.53 M (allow 0.51M to 0.55 M) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ osmolarity of the tissue is indicated where the trendline intersects the x-axis **BECAUSE** at this point y = 0 which means that there is no loss or gain of mass [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ osmolarity is where y = 0 **BECAUSE** no gain or loss of mass indicates that the solution is isotonic to the tissue so there is no net movement of water [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/BI2003dms.png\" alt=\"BI2003dms\">\n\n The estimated osmolarity is **0.53 M** because this is where the **line of best fit crosses** the **x-axis**, indicating **no net change** in mass ($\\checkmark$). This shows the solution is **isotonic** to the potato tissue, meaning **water movement into** and **out** of the tissue is **balanced** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ larger standard deviation error bars indicate greater variability in percentage change in mass between replicates [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the largest error bars occur at 0.8 M, indicating the greatest variability [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the smallest error bars occur at 0.2 M, indicating the least variability [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ variability differs between sucrose concentrations and does not show a consistent increase or decrease with concentration [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The data show **variability** as indicated by the standard deviation bars. The **variability** is **highest** at **0.8 M**, as shown by the **longest error bars** ($\\checkmark$), and **lowest** at **0.2 M**, where the **error bars** are **shortest** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2343","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2343\n\nWhich mechanism can be used by both unicellular and multicellular organisms to regulate water movement via active transport?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Control ion concentration of tissues |\n    | II. | Use aquaporins to increase water movement |\n    | III. | Use contractile vacuoles to release water|\n     | IV. | Use membrane proteins to control ion movement|\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ IV only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and IV only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement IV** is true because both unicellular and multicellular organisms can use membrane proteins, such as ion channels and pumps (requiring energy, and active transport) to regulate ion movement. This affects the solute concentration inside cells, influencing water movement via osmosis. For example, in multicellular organisms, the loop of Henle in mammalian kidneys uses ion transport to regulate water reabsorption. Similarly, unicellular organisms like Euglena and Chlamydomonas regulate ion movement to control water balance.\n\n**Statement I** is false because unicellular organisms consist of a single cell and do not form tissues, so they cannot regulate ion concentration in tissues either by active or passive transport.\n\n**Statement II** is false because aquaporins are specialised membrane proteins that facilitate water movement through passive transport rather than active transport. While used by multicellular organisms, some unicellular organisms (such as Paramecium) use aquaporins for water regulation.\n\n**Statement III** is false because contractile vacuoles, used by some unicellular organisms like Paramecium to expel excess water, are not utilised by multicellular organisms for water regulation. However, a contractile vacuole requires energy.\n\n**Choice C** is, therefore, the correct answer as it includes the true Statement IV.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **D** are incorrect as they include one or more false statements."},{"ref":"BI2412","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2412\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nA group of students was investigating the water potential of carrot cells. They cut ten cylinders of tissue from the same carrot, all the same diameter and length. They recorded the mass of each carrot cylinder and then placed each one into different solutions, with varying sucrose concentrations, as shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/eDITa.png\" alt=\"eDITa\">\n\nAfter 1 hr, each carrot cylinder was removed from its solution, dried, and its final mass recorded. The students then calculated the percentage change in mass and recorded this in the graph shown.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/EdiTb.png\" alt=\"EdiTb\">\n\n1. Draw a line of best fit onto the graph provided. [1 mark]\n\n2. Use the graph and the conversion table below to estimate the water potential of the carrot cells ($\\psi_{\\ce{cell}}$). Show your working. [3 marks]\n\n**mol** $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ **to solute potential conversion table**\n\n| Sucrose concentration / mol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ | Solute potential / kPA |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| 0.1 | -248.44 |\n| 0.2 | -496.86 |\n| 0.3 | -745.30 |\n| 0.4 | -993.74 |\n| 0.5 | -1242.11 |\n| 0.6 | -1490.62 |\n| 0.7 | -1739.06 |\n| 0.8 | -1987.50 |\n| 0.9 | -2235.94 |\n| 1.0 | -2484.38 |\n\n3. Explain what happens inside the carrot cells, in terms of pressure, osmotic and water potentials, when immersed in sucrose concentrations lower than 0.4 mol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ (hypotonic solutions). [3 marks]\n\nThe students repeated the experiment using turnip tissue. The solute potential ($\\psi_{\\ce{s}}$) of the turnip cell contents was given as -1550.40 kPa.\n\n$\\psi_{\\ce{cell}} = \\psi_{\\ce{s}} + \\psi_{\\ce{p}}$\n\n4. Use the equation and ‘mol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ to water potential conversion table’ provided to calculate the pressure potential ($\\psi_{\\ce{p}}$) of the turnip cells when they reached equilibrium in a 0.5 mol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ sucrose solution. [1 mark]\n\n5. The students studied the carrot cells under a light microscope after immersing them in the 1.0 mol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ sucrose solution for one hour. Describe what the students would have observed about the carrot cells. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ a straight line drawn so five points are above the line and five points are below the line [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/AnsA.png\" alt=\"AnsA\">\n\n2. any **three** of the following *(max **two** marks for correct answer without showing working, **OR** correct working without final correct answer)*:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ line crosses x-axis **OR** horizontal axis **OR** shows no change in mass at 0.47 mol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ (allow range 0.46 - 0.48) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 0.5 mol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ = -1242.11 kPa and 0.4 mol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ = -993.74 kPa [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 1242.11 - 993.74 = 248.37 kPa [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 0.47 mol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ = -993.74 + (0.7 $\\times$ 248.37) =-1167.60 kPa [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ -1167.60 kPa (allow in range -1140 to -1200) [1 mark]\n\n *Alternative method: identify any concentration value and its corresponding ΔG from the graph, then scale proportionally to the equilibrium concentration\ne.g. reading ΔG = –1242 kJ mol⁻¹ at [conc] = 0.50, then: ΔG (at equilibrium) = (0.47 / 0.50) × –1242 = –1167.6 kJ mol⁻¹\n Award full marks if the method is correct and consistent with read values*\n\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/BI2412bms.png\" alt=\"BI2412bms\">\n\n Line crosses x-axis at **0.47** mol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ ($\\checkmark$) sucrose solution, indicating no change in mass at this concentration (equilibrium is reached) \\\n At equilibrium water potential of cell (ψcell) equals water potential of external solution ($\\psi_{\\ce{solution}}$).\\\n The pressure potential ($\\psi_{\\ce{p}}$) of the sucrose solution is 0, because it is in an open container \\\n ψsolution = $\\psi_{\\ce{p}} + \\psi_{\\ce{s}} = 0 + \\psi_{\\ce{s}} = \\psi_{\\ce{s}}$ so, water potential of the sucrose solution (ψsolution) equals solute potential of the solution ($\\psi_{\\ce{s}}$)\n\n From table 0.5 mol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ sucrose solution = **-1242.11 kPa** \\\n From table 0.4 mol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ sucrose solution = **-993.74 kPa**\n\n Difference = **1242.11 kPa - 993.74 kPa** = **248.37 kPa** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n Calculating water potential of cell ($\\psi_{\\ce{cell}}$) using interpolation:\\\n 0.47 mol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ = **-993.74 + (0.7** $\\times$ **248.37**) = **-1167.599 kPa**\\\n = **1167.60 kPa** (to 2 d.p.) ($\\checkmark$)\n\n3. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as water moves in by osmosis, the increase in number of water molecules inside cell, increases pressure potential **OR** increase in water molecules increases turgor pressure inside the cell, leading to increase in pressure potential [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as water moves in by osmosis, number of solute molecules in cell decreases relative to water molecules, making solute potential less negative **OR** increasing solute potential [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ overall water potential of cell becomes less negative, **OR** increases, due to the combined effects of increased pressure potential and less negative **OR** increases solute potential [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n When immersed in hypotonic solutions, **water enters** the cells by **osmosis, turgor pressure increases**, and so there is an **increase in pressure potential** ($\\psi_{\\ce{p}}$) ($\\checkmark$). The influx of water **dilutes** the **solutes** inside the cell, reducing their concentration and making the **solute potential** ($\\psi_{\\ce{s}}$) **less negative** ($\\checkmark$). Overall, the **water potential** of the cell ($\\psi_{\\ce{cell}}$) becomes **less negative** due to the **combined effects** of **increased pressure potential** and **less negative solute potential** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 308.29 kPa [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n $\\psi_{\\ce{cell}} = \\psi_{\\ce{s}} + \\psi_{\\ce{p}}$\n\n $\\psi_{\\ce{s}}$ = -1550.40 kPa\\\n $\\psi_{\\ce{cell}}$ = -1242.11 kPa (from the mol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$to solute potential conversion table)\n\n $\\hspace{2.8em}$ -1242.11 kPa = -1550.40 kPa + $\\psi_{\\ce{p}}$\\\n $\\hspace{2.8em}$ $\\psi_{\\ce{p}}$ -1242.11 kPa + 1550.40 kPa = $\\psi_{\\ce{p}}$\\\n $\\hspace{2.8em}$ $\\psi_{\\ce{p}}$ = 308.29 kPa\n\n5. any **two** of the following *(annotated diagram acceptable)*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ plasmolysed cell [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cytoplasm **OR** cell membrane shrunken away from the cell wall **OR** gaps between the cell wall and cytoplasm [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ shrunken vacuoles **OR** vacuoles appear smaller [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ no loss of cell wall structure **OR** cell walls would still be visible **OR** maintain their shape [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/AnsE.png\" alt=\"AnsE\">"},{"ref":"BI2540","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2540\n\nWhich mechanism is used by both unicellular and multicellular organisms to regulate the direction of water movement across membranes?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Control the ion concentration of the tissues.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Use aquaporins to increase water movement.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Use contractile vacuoles to release water.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Use membrane proteins to control ion movement.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWater moves by osmosis, which depends on the concentration gradient of solutes, especially ions. Both unicellular and multicellular organisms use membrane proteins (such as ion pumps, ion channels, and co-transporters) to actively move ions across membranes. By controlling ion movement, cells establish osmotic gradients that regulate the direction of water flow. **Choice D** is the correct answer.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because whilst altering ion concentrations can indirectly affect water movement (through osmotic gradients), this option refers to controlling ions in “tissues,” which is not applicable to unicellular organisms. Unicellular organisms do not have tissues and regulate water movement at the cellular level.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because aquaporins increase the rate of water diffusion but do not control its direction. Water still moves passively through aquaporins in response to osmotic gradients, which are determined by ion transport.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because contractile vacuoles are found primarily in freshwater unicellular organisms as a means to expel excess water that enters the cell by osmosis. Contractile vacuoles are not present in multicellular organisms, making this mechanism unsuitable as a universal solution for both groups."},{"ref":"BI2672","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2672\n\nWhich of the following combinations of water potential values will result in the net movement of water into the cell?\n\n| | Outside the cell | Inside the cell |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 400 | 100 |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | -250 | -25 |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 0 | 52 |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | -20 | -263 |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because water potential values are always negative numbers when any amount of solute is present; the highest possible value (pure water) is zero. The value outside the cell (−20) is a higher number than the value inside the cell (−263), so water will diffuse down its concentration gradient, −20 $\\rarr$ −263 kPa.\n\n**Choice A** and **Choice C** are incorrect because water potential values are always negative numbers; the highest possible value, for pure water, is zero.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because −250 outside the cell is a lower number than −25 inside the cell. In such a case, water would diffuse out of the cell, not into it."},{"ref":"BI2673","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2673\n\nWhich statement(s) help(s) to explain why water potential ($\\psi_w$) is typically expressed in units of pressure (kPa)?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Because it is difficult to quantify potential energy in kJ $\\ce{dm^{-3}}$ in a water-based system.  |\n    | II. | Because the movement of water creates a pressure effect on cell membranes.  |\n    | III. | Because pressure always evens out between cells when their osmotic values are ideally regulated.  |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true. Water potential is a form of potential energy possessed by water molecules. While energy can be expressed in units like J or kJ per $\\ce{dm^3}$, it is difficult to measure directly in biological systems. Instead, pressure units (kPa) are used as a practical proxy, relative to the water potential of pure water at 20 $\\degree$C, which is defined as 0 kPa.\n\n**Statement II** is true because water movement across membranes generates pressure differences. For example, when water enters a plant cell, it increases the internal pressure (turgor pressure), which is resisted by the rigid cell wall. This pressure is a key component of the overall water potential.\n\n**Statement III** is false because pressures do not always equalize between cells. In fact, water potential differences are necessary for water to move from cell to cell, and pressure may vary depending on environmental conditions, cell type, or stage in the cell cycle.\n\n**Choice A** is correct because it includes the two true statements: **Statement I** and **Statement II**.\\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because it includes only one of the two correct statements.\\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because it includes only the false statement.\\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because it includes a false statement (**Statement III**)."},{"ref":"BI2674","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2674\n\nWhich row best describes the movement of water across cell membranes?\n\n| | Water concentration gradient | Solute concentration gradient |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | High $\\rarr$ Low | High $\\rarr$ Low|\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | High $\\rarr$ Low | Low $\\rarr$ High |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Low $\\rarr$ High | High $\\rarr$ Low|\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Low $\\rarr$ High | Low $\\rarr$ High |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because osmosis is a form of diffusion, and diffusion always involves movement from a region of higher concentration to a region of lower concentration. In the case of water, this means movement from high water concentration to low water concentration, which corresponds to low solute concentration to high solute concentration, since solutes reduce the free energy of water (i.e., lower its water potential, $\\psi$).\n\n**Choice A, Choice C**, and **Choice D** are incorrect because they show combinations that contradict the natural direction of osmosis—either by suggesting water moves from low to high concentration, or by reversing the solute gradient."},{"ref":"BI2676","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2676\n\nWhat is the water potential ($\\psi_w$) of a plant cell with a pressure potential ($\\psi_p$) of 778 kPa and solute potential ($\\psi_s$) of -627 kPa?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 1405 kPa\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ -151 kPa\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 151 kPa\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ -1405 kPa","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because we use the equation $\\psi_w = \\psi_s + \\psi_p$.\\\n$\\psi_w = \\psi_s + \\psi_p$ = -627 + 778 = 151 kPa.\\\nThis cell has an overall positive water potential, so it would be in a state of turgor, with a full vacuole and a well-hydrated cytoplasm. These will combine to exert pressure on the inside of the cell wall. It’s a common misconception that overall water potential is always negative. This is true of solute potential, but overall water potential has pressure potential contributing to it as well as solute potential, as indicated in the equation $\\psi_w = \\psi_s + \\psi_p$.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **D** are incorrect because they contain calculation errors."},{"ref":"BI2677","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2677\n\nThe pressure potential inside the tissue of a terrestrial plant was calculated to be -2877 kPa. Which type of tissue is this likely to have been?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Xylem vessel\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Phloem sieve tube\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Phloem companion cell\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Root hair cell","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because a strongly negative pressure potential (–2877 kPa) indicates tension, not actual physical compression. Such extreme negative pressure is typical of xylem vessels, especially in transpiring plants, where water is being pulled up from the roots through the stem. This tension is due to cohesion and adhesion of water molecules, as well as evaporation from leaf surfaces during transpiration.\n\n**Choice B, C** and **D** are incorrect because phloem and root hair cells operate under positive pressure. The phloem is responsible for the translocation of sugars and other nutrients around the plant; root hair cells absorb water from the soil by osmosis. Therefore, both cell types are typically under positive pressure, to ‘push’ materials around the plant."},{"ref":"BI2679","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2679\n\nA piece of tissue was removed from a healthy plant and examined using a light microscope. The diagram shows a single cell from this tissue, as observed.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/BI2679.png\" alt=\"BI2679\">\n\nA sample of this tissue was bathed in a hypotonic solution, left for 30 minutes and then re-examined under the microscope. Which diagram below represents the changes to the plant cell in that period?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A. | | $\\hspace{2cm}$ | |B.|\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/AnsA.png\" alt=\"AnsA\"> | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/AnsB.png\" alt=\"AnsB\">|\n|C. | | | |D. |\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/AnsC.png\" alt=\"AnsC\">| | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Continuity%20%26%20Change/Water%20Potential/images/AnsD.png\" alt=\"AnsD\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because the cell becomes turgid. Hypotonic solution means there is a higher water concentration outside the cell and, therefore a lower solute concentration. This causes water to diffuse in by osmosis. The influx of water causes the solute concentration inside the cell to reduce, and the extra water causes an increase in pressure. Overall, the water potential increases inside the cell. \\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the cell is unchanged in shape, i.e. still flaccid. This would be expected from placing the cell in an isotonic solution, not a hypotonic one. \\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because, while it correctly shows the cell becoming turgid, the solute concentration does not increase; the extra water moving into the cell causes the solute concentration to fall within the cell. \\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the cell is shown as plasmolysed; this would be the result of placing the tissue in a hypertonic solution, not a hypotonic one."}]}]},{"name":"Form & Function","subtopics":[{"name":"Adaptation to Environment","questions":[{"ref":"BI0108","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0108\n\nThe Arctic cod, ringed seal, killer whale, polar bear and other organisms live and interact with each other in the cold waters of the Arctic Ocean. Which of the following terms best describes them?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Population\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Community\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Habitat\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ecosystem","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA community is a group of populations living and interacting in a shared habitat (biotic factors). The abiotic factors for this community have not been described but could be the depth of the water, light intensity and the nutrients available for phytoplankton.\n\nA population (**Choice A**) is a group of organisms of the same species living in the same area at the same time which can  interbreed.  A habitat (**Choice C**) refers to the environment in which a species normally lives. An ecosystem (**Choice D**) is a group of populations living and interacting in a common habitat and includes both biotic and abiotic factors."},{"ref":"BI0677","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0677\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nThe photograph below shows a xerophyte which can live in extreme environments.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Adaptation%20to%20Environment/images/BI0677.png\" alt=\"BI0677\">\n[Source: Couleur, (2017, November). *File: flora, garden, desert, nature, cactus, green grass.* Pixnio. Retrieved December 22, 2022, from https: //pixnio.com/flora-plants/flowers/cactus-pictures/flora-garden-desert-nature-cactus-green-grass#img_info. Copyright under CC-Zero]\n\n1. State the process which accounts for the majority of water loss in plants. [1 mark]\n\n2. Outline the adaptations of the cactus which enables it to survive in extremely dry environmental conditions.          [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transpiration [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n2.    any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  small leaves **OR** spines    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  rolled leaves reduce exposure of stomata to moving air **OR** low humidity    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  reduced surface area for water evaporation, **OR** reduced transpiration [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  thick waxy cuticle on leaf **OR** stem [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  long roots     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  fewer stomata    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  stomata are sunken **OR** stomata are in recessed pits [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  water storage tissues in the stem    [1 mark]\n\n     $\\ce{ -> }$  plants grow lower to ground to reduce exposure to air    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  crassulacean acid metabolism **OR** CAM **OR** stomata open during the night and close during the day [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nTo adapt to life in extremely dry environments, xerophytes have **small leaves** ($\\checkmark$), which **reduces the surface area** for water loss by evaporation ($\\checkmark$). Stomata are **few** ($\\checkmark$), located in **recessed pits** ($\\checkmark$) and **open at night** when temperatures are cooler ($\\checkmark$).  These adaptations result in a decrease in water loss through stomata. Xerophytes also have **long roots** ($\\checkmark$) enabling them to absorb water located at a greater distance."},{"ref":"BI0818","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0818\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nDuring a field day, students were asked to investigate the effect of soil salinity on the distribution of plant species. Students designed an experiment that measured the number of plant species found up the shore at varying distances from the high tide line. Three groups of students investigated three sites on the same shore. The distance from the high tide line was taken as an indication of the salinity levels. The results are shown in the table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Adaptation%20to%20Environment/images/BI0818A.png\" alt=\"BI0818A\">\n\n1.\n    1. Identify the independent variable. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Outline the steps students could take, and the equipment they could use, to collect their results.                                [3 marks]\n\n2.\n    1. Suggest a reason for the discrepancy in the number of species sampled in areas 1, 2, and 3.                                     [1 mark]\n\n    2. Describe a way the students could reduce the impact of discrepancies on their collected data.                             [1 mark]\n\n3. The different species of plants found in each area were recorded in a table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Adaptation%20to%20Environment/images/BI0818B.png\" alt=\"BI0818B\">\n\nCalculate how many times greater the number of plant species found in Area 1 was compared to Area 2.                                 [1 mark]\n\n4. Evaluate the students’ investigation into salinity's effect on plant species distribution.                                 [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. (units are required for the mark):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ distance from the high tide line            [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **distance from the high tide line** ($\\checkmark$) is the independent variable.\n\n1.\n    2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use a measuring tape to measure 15m from the high tide line towards inland **OR** up shore                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use a belt transect (to mark out the area of investigation)    **OR**  transect line **OR** quadrats placed next to a measuring tape    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use a quadrat to sample plants every 3m **OR** at regular distances along the transect     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the distance between the transect and the quadrat should be kept constant [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ count the number of different plant species found within each quadrat     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use a clipboard **OR** notepad **OR** device for recording data   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use a dichotomous key **OR** plant identification guide **OR** expert to identify plant species [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    To carry out their investigation, students could use a measuring tape to **measure 15 m** up the shore away **from the high tide line** ($\\checkmark$). Place a **quadrat every 3 m** along the tape to **form a belt transect** ($\\checkmark$). Record the **number** of different **plant species** found in **each quadrat** ($\\checkmark$) in a results table.\n\n2.\n    1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some areas may have different soil **OR** soil structure **OR** more fertile soil **OR** more suitable soil pH **OR** salinity **OR** depth of soil **OR** mineral availability            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ human disturbances (footpaths, for example)        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light intensity                                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water availability                                 [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plant density                                     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ herbivory                                         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ accept any other valid description of habitat variation **OR** disturbance     [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    There may be discrepancies in the results because the areas sampled might have different soil types, with some being more **fertile** than others ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    2.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase the sampling frequency OR more quadrats OR more transects    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ decrease the distance between quadrats, so more taken within 15m [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase expertise **OR** use better resources to identify species [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ avoid using sites that clearly had foot trails                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ control more variables **OR** use areas with same angle of slope (accept any suitable example) [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Students could reduce the impact of discrepancies by increasing sampling frequency by adding more quadrats ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 1.29 **OR** 1.286            [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Adaptation%20to%20Environment/images/BI0818ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0818ANSA\">\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Area 1 - No. of plant species  = 9\\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Area 2 - No. of plant species = 7\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 9/7 = **1.29** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4. Any **three** of the following (must include strengths and weaknesses):\n\n    **Strengths**\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ students sampled three areas **OR** more than one area, **WHICH MEANS** they can calculate a mean from their results **OR** increases the reliability of their results        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ all areas sampled were at the same beach, **WHICH** decreases the effects of confounding variables between trials        [1 mark]\n\n    **Limitations**\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the salinity of the soil wasn’t measured, **OR** distance from the high tide line was used as an indirect measurement, **WHICH** means the research question is not addressed, **OR** a conclusion cannot be reached        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large discrepancies in students' results **MEANS** that the data isn’t reliable    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ more areas should be sampled to improve reliability    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lack of controlled variables, so students should identify variables such as soil moisture **OR** angle of slope **OR** soil pH **OR** light intensity, **OR** other valid controlled variable (example must be given to award mark)  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ all areas sampled were at the same beach, **WHICH** means the results may not be valid for other beaches            [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The students sampled **more than three** areas so a mean could be taken, and this increases the **reliability** ($\\checkmark$) of the results. However, there were **large discrepancies** between student results ($\\checkmark$), so **more areas need to be sampled** ($\\checkmark$). Also, the **salinity** of the soil was **not measured**; instead, they relied on **indirect measurements**, which means a conclusion can only be supported to a limited degree ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0918","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0918\n\nWhich of the following would not be considered an abiotic factor?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sunlight\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Disease\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Precipitation\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ pH","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because disease is a living factor and is, therefore, a biotic factor, not an abiotic one. Sunlight (**Choice A**), precipitation (**Choice C**), and pH (**Choice D**) are all non-living factors and are therefore abiotic."},{"ref":"BI0919","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0919\n\nWhat best defines a habitat?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The role an organism has within its ecosystem\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The community of organisms and their environment\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The place an organism lives\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ A group of organisms living in the same place","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because the habitat is a place a community, population, species, or organism lives. **Choice A** refers to an organism's niche, **Choice B** refers to the ecosystem, and **Choice D** refers to a community."},{"ref":"BI0920","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0920\n\nWhich of the following is not a factor required for coral reef formation?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Low pH\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Shallow enough water\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Temperatures between 20 :sup[o]C and 30 :sup[o]C\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ High enough salinity","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because low pH is not a factor corals need for reef formation, in fact, low pH is detrimental to coral growth. Shallow enough water (**Choice B**) is important to allow enough light to reach the photosynthetic Zooxanthellae found in the coral polyps. Temperatures between 20 :sup[o]C and 30 :sup[o]C (**Choice C**) are important as corals can only survive within this narrow range. High enough salinity (**Choice D**) is important for various reasons, including the maintenance of an osmotic balance for proper cellular function."},{"ref":"BI0921","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0921\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate to measure using a line transect?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Light intensity\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Soil pH\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Temperature\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Plant distribution","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because line transects can be used to show animal or plant distributions. **Choices A, B** and **C** are all factors that could be measured using different sensors that could provide meaning to plant or animal distributions."},{"ref":"BI0922","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0922\n\nWhich of the following factors create biomes?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Annual Precipitation |\n    | II. | Air Temperature |\n    | III. | Keystone species |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nBiomes are primarily determined by climatic and geographic conditions. **Choice C** is correct because air temperature (**Statement II**) and annual precipitation (**Statement I**) are both factors involved in biome formation.\n\nKeystone species (**Statement III**) are species that have a disproportionately large impact on their ecosystem relative to their abundance. While they play a crucial role in shaping and maintaining the structure of specific ecosystems, they are not a direct factor in creating biomes. **Choices A**, **B** and **D** are therefore incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0923","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0923\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nMarram grass, pictured below, is a plant species often found growing on sand dunes.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Adaptation%20to%20Environment/images/BI0923.png\" alt=\"BI0923\">\n[Source: Agnieszka K., Nova. (2020). *File: Ammophila arenaria Piaskownica zwyczajna 2020-06-29 04.jpg*\n. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved July 29, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Ammophila_arenaria_Piaskownica_zwyczajna_2020-06-29_04.jpg. Copyright under CC BY-SA 4.0]\n\n1.\n    1. Distinguish between abiotic and biotic factors.         [1 mark]\n\n    2. Suggest adaptations Marram grass could have in order to grow on sand dunes.     [2 marks]\n\n    3. For a named abiotic variable, outline how the range of tolerance for Marram grass could be measured in its natural habitat.             [3 marks]\n\n2. Describe an adaptation that allows a named organism to survive in desert habitats.  [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ abiotic factors are non-living components of an ecosystem **OR** non-living factors which affect organisms **WHEREAS** biotic factors are living components of an ecosystem **OR** living factors which affect organisms                    [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Abiotic factors are the **non-living components** of an ecosystem, whereas **biotic** factors are the **living components** of an ecosystem ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ dense root system **OR** long **OR** creeping rhizomes to maximise water uptake **OR** anchor plant in the sand      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ deep roots to tap into deeper water sources [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ narrow leaves **OR** thick cuticles to reduce transpiration        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stomata close if roots are dry **OR** during periods of drought        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ small hairs **OR** trichomes around stomata to reduce transpiration rate **OR** to build up humidity around stomata OR to reduce water concentration difference inside and outside the leaf     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can tolerate high salinity **OR** salt spray **OR** immersion in salt water     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stiff stems help it withstand strong winds     [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Marram grass could have a **dense root system** which helps maximise water absorption from the surface of the sand after it rains ($\\checkmark$). Their leaves are also typically **narrow** to help reduce the rate of transpiration ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    3. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature range **OR** pH range **OR** salinity **OR** water availability **OR** light intensity **OR** other named abiotic variable    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ using a transect **OR** line transect **OR** belt transect     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ measurements of the abiotic variable taken at regular intervals using an appropriate sensor   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ quadrat **OR** measuring stick **OR** measuring tape placed at regular intervals along the transect to count the number of individuals      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ measure characteristics of Marram grass, e.g. height, density or stem thickness     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ analyse data to identify trends between changes in the grass and the abiotic factor     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ repeat measurements     [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    In order to measure the effect of **pH** ($\\checkmark$) on the distribution of Marram grass, you could use a **line transect** ($\\checkmark$). A **pH probe** ($\\checkmark$) would be used to measure the pH at regular intervals along the line transect. A metre stick could then be used at regular intervals to determine whether Marram grass is present or not.\n\n2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ named organisms, e.g. Fennec fox    [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ description of suitable adaptation, e.g. large vascular ears help to dissipate heat    [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The **Fennec fox** ($\\checkmark$) is adapted to live in desert habitats because it has **large vascular ears**, which help to dissipate heat and keep it cool ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0924","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0924\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe kapok tree, pictured below, is found in the tropical rainforests of Central and South America. It has buttress roots that extend above the soil in order for it to grow tall.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Adaptation%20to%20Environment/images/BI0924A.png\" alt=\"BI0924A\">\n[Source: Fährtenleser. (2019). *File: Majestic kapok tree in Ecuador.jpg*\n. Retrieved July 29, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Majestic_kapok_tree_in_Ecuador.jpg.  Copyright under CC BY-SA 4.0\n]\n\n1.\n    1. Outline the climatic conditions which are typical of tropical rainforests.               [2 marks]\n\n    2. For a named organism other than the kapok tree, describe the adaptations it has to live in tropical rainforests.         [2 marks]\n\n    3. Tropical rainforests are an example of a biome. Define the term biome.     [1 mark]\n\n2. Explain the need for the kapok tree to grow tall and fast.     [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ high temperature **OR** high insolation        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ high precipitation **OR** rainfall             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ high humidity                             [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Tropical rainforests are characterised by **high temperatures** ($\\checkmark$) and **high** levels of **precipitation** ($\\checkmark$), which makes them very **humid** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    2.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ named plant or animal, e.g. lianas **OR** poison dart frog    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ correct adaptation for the named plant or animal, e.g. can attach to and grow up tree trunks in order to reach sunlight **OR** toe pads and strong limbs help poison dart frogs to climb and adhere to various surfaces, such as leaves and tree trunks     [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Lianas** ($\\checkmark$) are a species of vine that can **attach to tree trunks**, they grow up in order to reach the **sunlight** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    3.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ groups of ecosystems with similar communities, **OR** a large environment, characterised by specific abiotic factors    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    A biome is a group of ecosystems with **similar communities** characterised by specific abiotic conditions ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ high levels of competition (in tropical rainforest biome) **OR** high plant density         [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ **AND SO** the kapok tree must grow quickly to reach sunlight (in order to photosynthesise)    [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ growing tall allows it to reach the upper canopy     [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ **WHICH** provides a greater area for pollination and seed dispersal, enhancing reproductive success                                              [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ reach a more physically stable and resilient state faster [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ **IN ORDER TO**  withstand natural disturbances such as tree falls     [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ can elevate its foliage beyond the reach of some herbivores     [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ **WHICH** reduces the risk of herbivory                               [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ In tropical rainforests, there is a **high density of plants** ($\\checkmark$), so to **reach sunlight** ($\\checkmark$), the kapok tree must grow tall, and quickly, to photosynthesise."},{"ref":"BI0925","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0925\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Define the terms species, population, and community.    [3 marks]\n\n2. Explain the conditions required for coral reef formation. [6 marks]\n\n3. Using three named organisms, describe the adaptations they have to different\nenvironments.     [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a species is a group of organisms capable of interbreeding to produce fertile offspring         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a population is a group of individuals of the same species occupying the same area        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a community is all the populations of different species living in the same geographical area       [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    A species is often defined as a **group of organisms** that are capable of **interbreeding to produce fertile offspring** ($\\checkmark$). A population is a group of individuals of the **same species** which occupy the **same geographical area** ($\\checkmark$). A community is all the **populations of different species** living in the **same geographical area** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **six** of the following (maximum three marks for named conditions):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ suitable water temperature **ALLOWS** symbiotic algae to grow **OR** increased temperatures cause the algae to produce substances that are toxic to the coral so corals become stressed and expel zooxanthellae **OR** symbiotic algae, causing bleaching of the coral and making them more vulnerable to starvation and disease    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ clearwater **OR** water clarity **ALLOWS** sunlight through for photosynthesis     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ shallow water **OR** suitable water depth **ALLOWS** sunlight to reach symbiotic algae         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ suitable pH **ALLOWS** algae to survive **OR** skeleton to form **OR** as the pH drops, the availability of carbonate ions in seawater decreases, making it more challenging for corals to build their calcium carbonate skeletons **OR** calcium carbonate dissolves in acidic solutions, so the lowered pH of the water erodes the skeleton, removing its support and protection, leading to death **OR** increased acidity can cause coral bleaching, where corals expel the zooxanthellae    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ suitable salinity **OR** saltwater **ALLOWS** coral to survive and grow OR if the salinity is too high or too low, it can disrupt the osmotic balance, leading to dehydration or water overload within the coral cells **OR** the concentration of calcium and carbonate ions in the water is influenced by salinity **OR** the correct salinity plays a role in the chemical cues that attract larvae to appropriate settlement areas on the reef        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ coral larvae need a hard substrate on which they can attach and begin their growth        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gentle currents help to deliver nutrients and oxygen to the corals while flushing away sediment and waste        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adequate nutrients **BECAUSE** although corals have a symbiotic relationship with zooxanthellae, they also depend on capturing plankton and organic matter from the water for additional nutrition  [1 mark]\n    [6 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Corals require **suitable temperatures**, which allow the symbiotic algae to **survive** and grow ($\\checkmark$). They need to be in relatively **shallow water**, which allows the **sunlight** to reach the symbiotic algae ($\\checkmark$). Corals also need **clear water**, which also allows sunlight to reach them so that the symbiotic algae can **photosynthesise** ($\\checkmark$). Coral larvae also need a **hard substrate**, such as rock, on which they can **attach** and begin to grow ($\\checkmark$).Finally, Corals also need a suitable **salinity and pH**. Salinity directly influences **calcium carbonate** ion concentration ($\\checkmark$), and pH can influence the development of the **skeleton** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following (maximum **three marks** for named organisms and where it's found):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 3 named organisms and their correct habitat **OR** environment, e.g. Fennec fox found in the desert                 [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adaptation and advantage of adaptation for each organism, e.g. large vascular ears to dissipate heat             [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **Fennec fox** is adapted to live in **desert** ($\\checkmark$) environments because it has **large vascular ears** which allow it to **dissipate heat** ($\\checkmark$). It can also use its large ears to **detect small insects**, which are its prey ($\\checkmark$). **Marram grass** is adapted to live in **sand dunes** ($\\checkmark$) because it has **dense shallow roots** which allow it to **collect water** when it rains ($\\checkmark$). It can also **close the stomata** in its leaves to reduce water loss when water is in short supply ($\\checkmark$). The **kapok tree** is found in **tropical rainforests** ($\\checkmark$) it has **buttress roots** that extend above ground. This can help it to **grow tall** in shallow soil ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0949","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0949\n\nWhat is a common limiting factor for plant growth in arid (dry) environments?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ High humidity\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Excessive rainfall\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Soil compaction\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Low light intensity","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn arid environments, water availability is often a primary limiting factor. However, soil compaction (**Choice C**) can exacerbate this limitation by reducing water infiltration and root penetration. High humidity (**Choice A**), excessive rainfall (**Choice B**), and low light intensity (**Choice D**) are less likely to be limiting factors in arid environments."},{"ref":"BI0990","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0990\n\nWhich of the following biomes is best characterised by high temperatures, high rainfall, and high humidity?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Tropical rainforests\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Tundra\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Grasslands\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Temperate forests","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because tropical rainforests are characterised by high temperatures, high rainfall and high humidity. **Choice B** is incorrect because tundra would have low temperatures and low humidity. **Choice C** is incorrect because grasslands would have low humidity, and **Choice D** is incorrect because temperate forests would have lower temperatures and low humidity."},{"ref":"BI1424","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1424\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe photograph below is of the sea oat (*Unola paniculata*). This plant is found on sand dunes.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Adaptation%20to%20Environment/images/BI1424.png\" alt=\"BI1424\">\n[Source: Adapted from Daniel Di Palma (2 Feb 2017) *Miami Beach - Sand Dune Flora - Sea Oats in South Beach*. Retrieved 31 Jan 2024 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=59792482. Copyright CC BY-SA 4.0]\n\n1. Explain how the sea oat (*Unola paniculata*) is adapted to survive in its environment.    [3 marks]\n\n2. Describe the importance of the solvent properties of water for transport in plants and humans.            [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ wide shallow root system that helps anchor them securely in shifting sands **OR**  to allow rapid absorption of water from sand **OR** to stabilise sand reducing rate of erosion        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ salt tolerance to survive exposure to salt spray from the sea    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ narrow leaves that reduce water loss through transpiration    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ flexible **OR** bendy stems allow sea oats to sway with the wind without breaking    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ long bristle-like structures on seeds that catch the wind to aid seed dispersal    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some sea oat populations have a symbiotic relationship with nitrogen-fixing bacteria in their root nodules enabling them to survive in nutrient-poor beach soils    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nodes **OR** rhizomes allow regeneration after a fire **OR** to reproduce asexually to survive being covered by sand    [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **wide, shallow root** system of sea oats allows them to **anchor themselves** in the shifting sand ($\\checkmark$) of the dunes and to **absorb water rapidly** ($\\checkmark$). Their **narrow leaves reduce water loss** through **transpiration** ($\\checkmark$) and their **flexible stems** allow them to survive the **sea winds without breaking** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **three** of the following (*need **one** example for plants and **one** example for humans to gain maximum marks*):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water molecules are polar **OR** have an uneven distribution of charge so attract other polar molecules **OR** ions through hydrogen bonding **OR** water dissolves hydrophilic substances                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in cellular transport water allows movement of ions **OR** hydrophilic molecules **OR** polar molecules around the cell            [1 mark]\n\n    ***Plants*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ xylem **OR** transpiration stream carries minerals **OR** minerals **OR** potassium **OR** calcium **OR** magnesium from roots [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phloem carries nutrients **OR** sucrose **OR** carbohydrates **OR** glucose **OR** amino acids    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Humans*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ blood plasma dissolves and transports sodium chloride **OR** glucose **OR** amino acids **OR** wastes **OR** hormones **OR** oxygen **OR** carbon dioxide                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen dissolution in aqueous lining of lungs allows uptake of oxygen into blood [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ waste products are filtered out at the kidney **OR** wastes are transported in glomerular filtrate **OR** water allows the transport of wastes through the nephron to the bladder for excretion    [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Because **water molecules** are **polar, hydrophilic substances dissolve** in **aqueous solutions** ($\\checkmark$). In **plants**, water transports **minerals** up **from** the **roots**, where they have been absorbed from the soil, to where they are needed in the plant ($\\checkmark$).  In humans, water acts as the medium for **blood plasma, transporting** essential **nutrients** and **oxygen** around the body ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1596","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1596\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe graph below shows the influence of climate on the distribution of biomes on Earth.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Adaptation%20to%20Environment/images/BI1596.png\" alt=\"BI1596\">\n[Source: Adapted from Frankemann (2021, January 5).*File: climate influence on terrestrial biome.svg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved Apr 11, 2024 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Climate_influence_on_terrestrial_biome_no.svg Copyright under CC BY SA 4.0]\n\n1. Outline why precipitation has been considered important in influencing the type of biomes, as shown in the graph above.    [2 marks]\n\nThe following climograph shows the monthly precipitation and average monthly temperatures of Aklavik, Canada.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Adaptation%20to%20Environment/images/BI1596i.png\" alt=\"BI1596i\">\n[Source: Adapted from Image obtained from  NASA. (n.d.-a). *Temperature and precipitation graphs*. NASA. Retrieved April 11, 2024 from https://earthobservatory.nasa.gov/biome/graphs.php. Copyright free]\n\n2. Using the data from the two graphs, deduce, with a reason, which biome Aklavik, Canada, lies in. [2 marks]\n\n3. Predict two differences that would be seen between the pyramids of energy for the summer and winter months in Aklavik, Canada.    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ primary production is dependent on light **AND** water **OR** moisture available [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ precipitation is a direct source of water for plants, and so determines type **OR** amount of vegetation in a biome        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ soil moisture content influences soil chemistry **OR** microbial activity **OR** soil fertility     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ amount and timing of precipitation influence local climate conditions including temperature     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ areas with high precipitation tend to have more stable and moderate temperatures    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ areas with low precipitation often experience more extreme temperatures and aridity         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ availability of water from precipitation supports diverse ecosystems **OR** high levels of biodiversity  [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Precipitation is a **direct source** of **water** for plants, determining the **type** and **amount** of **vegetation** in a biome ($\\checkmark$). It influences **soil moisture content**, which affects **soil fertility** ($\\checkmark$) and, therefore, the types of plants that can grow there. Areas with **high precipitation**, such as **rainforests**, tend to have **more stable** and **moderate temperatures**  ($\\checkmark$). Areas with **low precipitation**, such as **deserts**, often experience more extreme temperatures and aridity ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tundra     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mean annual precipitation is 250 mm **OR** 25 cm (allow 22-29cm), **AND** mean yearly temperature is $-8^\\degree$C (allow -6 to 10)    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Adaptation%20to%20Environment/images/BI1596MS.png\" alt=\"BI1596MS\">\n    [Source: Adapted from Image obtained from  NASA. (n.d.-a). *Temperature and precipitation graphs*. NASA. Retrieved April 11, 2024 from https://earthobservatory.nasa.gov/biome/graphs.php. Copyright free]\n\n    Mean annual precipitation = 15 mm + 12 mm + 15 mm + 8 mm+ 15 mm + 19 mm + 40 mm + 42 mm + 30 mm + 33 mm + 22 mm+ 16 mm \\\n    = 267 mm \\\n    = 26.7 cm \\\n    = **27 cm** (to 2d.p.)\n\n    Mean annual temperature = $\\dfrac{-27^\\degree C -25^\\degree C - 21^\\degree C - 12^\\degree C + 0^\\degree C + 10^\\degree C + 15^\\degree C + 12^\\degree C+ 4^\\degree C - 7^\\degree C - 20^\\degree C - 24^\\degree C}\n    {12}$ \\\n    = **- 8$^\\degree C$**\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Adaptation%20to%20Environment/images/BI1596AnsC.png\" alt=\"BI1596AnsC\">\n    [Source: Adapted from Frankemann (2021, January 5).*File: climate influence on terrestrial biome.svg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved Apr 11, 2024 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Climate_influence_on_terrestrial_biome_no.svg Copyright under CC BY SA 4.0]\n\n    The mean annual **rainfall** for Aklavik is **27 cm**, and the mean **temperature** is  **-8**$^\\degree C$ ($\\checkmark$), which puts it in the **tundra** biome region ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ during summer base of pyramid **OR** primary producer level would be wider **OR** represent more energy **THAN** winter pyramid    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ during summer the higher trophic levels **OR** consumer trophic levels would be broader **OR** represent more energy **THAN** those in winter        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ summer pyramid is likely to include more trophic levels **THAN** winter pyramid        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ winter pyramid will have much steeper **OR** narrower shape **THAN** summer pyramid    [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    In Aklavik, Canada, the **summer** pyramid of energy would have a **wider base** ($\\checkmark$) than the winter pyramid. The **winter** pyramid would be much **steeper and narrower**  ($\\checkmark$), with **fewer trophic levels** supported than in the summer($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1882","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1882\n\nThe micrograph shows a cross-section of a marram grass leaf (*Ammophila arenaria*), a xerophyte often found on sand dunes.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Adaptation%20to%20Environment/images/BI1882.png\" alt=\"BI1882\">\n[Source: Adapted from Schools, S. and P. for. (2012, May 2). *Marram Grass*. Flickr. Retrieved 8 September, 2024 from https://www.flickr.com/photos/71183136@N08/6989003016. Copyright by CC 2.0]\n\nWhich row in the table correctly describes how this species is adapted to its environment?\n\n| | Adaptation | Purpose |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Thick, waxy cuticle on inside of rolled leaf | To prevent water loss |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Rolled leaf structure | To trap moisture to create a humid microclimate near the surface of the leaf |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Rolled leaf structure | To allow the wind to remove sand from stomata on the outer surface of the leaf |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Bumpy inner surface of rolled leaf | To increase the surface area of the leaf for photosynthesis |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nMarram grass leaves are rolled so that stomata are on the inner surface. The leaf's rolled structure creates a humid environment by trapping water vapour that exits the stomata, decreasing the water potential gradient between the stomata and the air. **Choice B** is correct.\n\nDue to the rolling of the leaf, the thick waxy cuticle is on the outer surface of the leaf to prevent water loss; **Choice A** is incorrect. Although the leaf is rolled, this is to reduce water loss as the majority of stomata are on the inner surface of the leaf; **Choice C**  is incorrect. Although the inner surface of the leaf is bumpy due to the rolling of the leaf, this does not increase the rate of photosynthesis as the inside of the leaf is not fully exposed to the sun; **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI2773","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2773\n\nWhich description best fits the definition of a habitat?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The Arctic Circle.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ An ocean.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ A peatland at low altitude.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ A coastline.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because a habitat is the specific place where an organism lives, characterized by particular physical conditions (such as moisture, temperature, and light) and the biological community it belongs to. A peatland at low altitude describes a defined environment with unique ecological conditions, making it a suitable example of a habitat.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the Arctic Circle is a geographical region, not a specific habitat. It encompasses a wide range of ecosystems and conditions, so it does not meet the criteria of a clearly defined habitat.\\\n**Choices B** and **D** are incorrect because oceans and coastlines are broad and general terms. They refer to large physical environments that contain many different habitats rather than describing one specific set of environmental conditions."},{"ref":"BI2774","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2774\n\nWhich adaptation is found in both grass species adapted to sand dunes and tree species found in mangroves?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Rolled leaves reduce transpiration.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Adaptations to increase water uptake by the roots.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Pneumatophores that grow vertically into the air.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Underground stems that grow deep into the sand or soil.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because both grass species in sand dunes and mangrove tree species have root adaptations that enhance water uptake in challenging conditions. These adaptations often involve the accumulation of solutes in root cells to increase osmotic potential, enabling more efficient water absorption despite low water availability in sandy, salty soils or high salinity in mangrove environments.\\\n**Choice A** is incorrect because rolled leaves are a xerophytic adaptation found in many sand dune grasses to reduce water loss through transpiration. However, mangrove trees typically have broad, flat leaves and use salt excretion or storage mechanisms, not rolled leaves, to manage water loss.\\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because pneumatophores are specialised aerial roots found in mangrove trees to facilitate gas exchange in anoxic, waterlogged soils. Sand dune grasses do not develop pneumatophores, as they grow in well-aerated sandy soils.\\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because rhizomes (horizontal underground stems) are found in some sand dune grasses to anchor the plant and access deep water. However, mangrove trees do not rely on rhizomes; they have other root adaptations like prop roots or pneumatophores to stabilise and function in muddy substrates."},{"ref":"BI2775","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2775\n\nWhich statement(s) are correct for the range of tolerance for a species?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Individuals are only found at the optimum condition. |\n    | II. | Population size gradually decreases the further the environmental condition moves beyond the range of tolerance. |\n    | III. | Population size varies within the range of tolerance. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement III** is true because the population is highest at the optimum condition and decreases as the condition moves away from the optimum. Population size is highest at the optimum condition but varies within the range of tolerance, because it decreases as the condition moves further from the optimum.\n\n**Statement I** is false because organisms are found within the zone of tolerance, including, but not limited to, when the condition is optimum.\n\n**Statement II** is false because the organism is not found beyond the range of tolerance. The range of tolerance is the range for a condition at which an organism can be found, for example, pH. When the pH is above or below this range, the organism cannot survive and will not be found.\n\n**Choice C** is correct because only **Statement III** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because **Statement III** is true. **Choices B** and **D** are incorrect because **Statement I** and **Statement II** are not true."},{"ref":"BI2776","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2776\n\nThe image shows a line transect being used on a rocky seashore.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Adaptation%20to%20Environment/images/BI2776.png\" alt=\"BI2776\">\n[Source: Adapted from ThalassLib (2019, June 6). *Intertidal transect sampling 02.jpg*. Wikimedia. Retrieved April 29, 2025, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Intertidal_transect_sampling_02.jpg. Copyright under Creative Commons CC0 1.0 Universal Public Domain]\n\nWhich abiotic factor could this transect be used to investigate?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The effect of herbivory on the distribution of seaweed species.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The effect of predation on the distribution of crab species.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The effect of wave action on the distribution of crab species.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The effect of wave action on the distribution of seaweed species.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because wave action is an abiotic factor that varies along a rocky seashore and can influence the distribution of seaweed species. Seaweeds are stationary organisms, making them suitable for surveying using quadrats placed at intervals along a line transect.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because although herbivory does affect the distribution of seaweed species, it is a biotic (living) factor, not an abiotic factor.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because predation is a biotic factor, and crabs are mobile, so they cannot be surveyed with a line transect.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because while wave action is an abiotic factor, crabs are mobile, so it is not possible to collect data about their distribution with a quadrat."},{"ref":"BI2777","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2777\n\nWhich set of conditions is the most suitable for coral reef formation?\n\n| | Water depth | Temperature | Water pH |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Less than 20m | 23 $\\degree$C to 29 $\\degree$C | Above 7.0 |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | More than 20m | 20 $\\degree$C to 23 $\\degree$C  | Above 7.8 |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | More than 50m | Above 30 $\\degree$C | 7.2 to 7.5 |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Less than 50m | 23 $\\degree$C to 29 $\\degree$C | Above 7.8 |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because the conditions it describes are ideal for coral reef formation. Water depth less than 50 m allows sufficient sunlight to reach the reef, which is essential for photosynthesis by zooxanthellae, the symbiotic algae within coral tissues. A temperature range of 23 $\\degree$C to 29 $\\degree$C is ideal for coral growth and the activity of zooxanthellae. A pH above 7.8 is necessary to maintain alkaline conditions, which favour the deposition of calcium carbonate ($\\ce{CaCO_3}$) for coral skeleton formation.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because although the temperature range is suitable, the pH is too low. A pH of 7- 7.8 is not sufficient for coral calcification; values above 7.8 are required. The depth range is acceptable but narrower than necessary, as corals can thrive down to 50 m.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the temperature range is too low. Coral reefs require warmer water, typically 23 $\\degree$C to 29 $\\degree$C, for optimal growth. The depth of ‘more than 20 m’ is also unsuitable because, at greater than 50m, the light levels become too low.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because all three factors fall outside the optimal range. A depth greater than 50 m limits light penetration, temperatures above 30 $\\degree$C can lead to coral bleaching, and a pH between 7.2 and 7.5 is too acidic for calcium carbonate deposition."}]},{"name":"Carbohydrates & Lipids","questions":[{"ref":"BI0282","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0282\n\nWhich row in the table below correctly identifies the constituents of the named macromolecules?\n\n| | Starch | Protein | Triglycerides |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| Beta glucose | Peptides | Fatty acids and glycerol |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Beta glucose | Amino acids | Fatty acids and glycogen |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Alpha glucose | Peptides | Fatty acids and glycogen |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Alpha glucose | Amino acids | Fatty acids and glycerol |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because starch is made of chains of alpha glucose, proteins are made of chains of amino acids and triglycerides are made of fatty acids and glycerol. **Choice A** is incorrect because starch is not composed of beta glucose and peptides are not the simplest units of proteins. **Choice B** is incorrect because starch is not composed of beta glucose and triglycerides do not contain glycogen. **Choice C** is incorrect because peptides are not the smallest unit of proteins and triglycerides do not contain glycogen."},{"ref":"BI0429","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0429\n\nWhich molecule diagram is correctly matched to the name?\n\n| | Name of molecule | Structure of molecule |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| Glucose | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Carbohydrates%20%26%20Lipids/images/429%20a.png\" alt=\"429 a\"> |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Ribose | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Carbohydrates%20%26%20Lipids/images/429b.png\" alt=\"429b\"> |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Deoxyribose | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Carbohydrates%20%26%20Lipids/images/429c.png\" alt=\"429c\"> |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Glycerol | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Carbohydrates%20%26%20Lipids/images/429d.png\" alt=\"429d\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\n**Choice A** is correct because the structure shown is cyclic glucose, a six-carbon monosaccharide (hexose). It contains a six-membered ring with one oxygen atom in the ring, a CH₂OH side group, and the arrangement of hydroxyl (–OH) groups characteristic of glucose.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because ribose has a hydroxyl (–OH) group on carbon 2, but the structure shown has a hydrogen (H) at this position. Therefore, the molecule shown is deoxyribose rather than ribose.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because deoxyribose lacks the hydroxyl (–OH) group on carbon 2. The structure shown contains an –OH group on carbon 2, so it represents ribose rather than deoxyribose.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because glycerol is a three-carbon alcohol with the formula C₃H₈O₃. The structure shown contains four carbon atoms, so it cannot be glycerol."},{"ref":"BI0435","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0435\n\nWhich of the following contain(s) covalent bonds between monomers?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n\n  | | |\n\n  | :-- | :-- |\n\n  | I. | Dipeptides |\n\n  | II. | Amino acids |\n\n  | III. | Triglycerides |\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nDipeptides (**molecule I**) contain covalent bonds between monomers. The condensation reaction between two amino acid molecules results in the formation of a peptide bond, which is a type of covalent bond. Since amino acids act as monomers in protein structures, this bond qualifies as a covalent bond between monomers.\n\nAmino acids (**molecule II**) themselves do not contain covalent bonds between monomers. Each amino acid is a single monomer, composed of covalently bonded atoms within its structure. However, it lacks a linkage to another monomer, meaning it does not satisfy the condition of having covalent bonds between monomers.\n\nTriglycerides (**molecule III**) are formed when one glycerol molecule reacts with three fatty acid molecules, creating ester bonds. Although ester bonds are covalent, glycerol and fatty acids are not identical monomers. Since polymers consist of repeating monomer units, triglycerides do not qualify as polymers, meaning the bonds in their structure do not count as covalent bonds between monomers.\n\nOnly dipeptides contain covalent bonds between monomers, so the correct answer to this question is **Choice A**.\n\n**Choices B, C** and **D** are incorrect because they include incorrect molecules."},{"ref":"BI0436","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0436\n\nWhich molecules form a triglyceride?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ A phosphate head and two fatty acid tails\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ One fatty acid and three glycerol molecules\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ One glycerol molecule and three fatty acids\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ One glycerol molecule and three monosaccharides","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nThe triglyceride molecule consists of one glycerol molecule and three fatty acids.\n\nA phosphate head and two fatty acid tails (**Choice A**) form a phospholipid, not a triglyceride. One fatty acid and three glycerol molecules (**Choice B**) or one glycerol molecule and three monosaccharides (**Choice D**) cannot form a triglyceride."},{"ref":"BI0437","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0437\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nThe diagram below shows the structure of 2 different monomers.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Carbohydrates%20%26%20Lipids/images/BI0437.png\" alt=\"BI0437\">\n[Source: Adapted from CNX OpenStax College. (2013). Illustration from Anatomy & Physiology, Connexions Website. Retrieved September 11, 2022, from https ://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:217_Five_Important_Monosaccharides-01.jpg Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n1. State the name of the following:\n\n    1. structure A    [1 mark]\n\n    2. structure B    [1 mark]\n\n2. State the name of a polymer which contains the following molecules:\n\n    1. structure A     [1 mark]\n\n    2. structure B    [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. \n\n    1. :br\n\n        $\\ce{->}$ structure A: α-glucose [1 mark]\n\n    2. :br\n\n        $\\ce{->}$ structure B: ribose [1 mark]\n\n2. \n\n    1. :br\n\n        $\\ce{->}$ structure A: starch **OR** glycogen **OR** amylose **OR** amylopectin [1 mark]\n\n    2. :br\n\n        $\\ce{->}$ structure B: ribonucleic acid **OR** RNA [1 mark]"},{"ref":"BI0438","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0438\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n1. Distinguish between the formation and breaking down of a polymer. [2 marks]\n\n2. Compare and contrast the structures of carbohydrates, lipids and proteins. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ in polymer formation, a dehydration **OR** condensation reaction occurs **WHILE** in the breaking down of a polymer a hydrolysis reaction occurs [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ in polymer formation, one molecule of water **OR** $\\ce{H2O}$ is produced **WHILE** in the breaking down of a polymer water **OR** $\\ce{H2O}$ molecules are split/added [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ polymer formation is an anabolic reaction **WHILE** breaking down a polymer is a catabolic reaction [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ polymer formation is an endergonic **OR** endothermic reaction or stores energy **WHILE** breaking down a polymer is an exergonic **OR** exothermic reaction or releases energy. [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    **To form a polymer, a dehydration reaction occurs** ($\\checkmark$) between monomers resulting in the formation of bonds between two molecules **while to break down a polymer, a hydrolysis reaction occurs** ($\\checkmark$) which breaks bonds between two monomers. Forming a polymer is an **anabolic reaction** while breaking down a polymer is a **catabolic reaction**. ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2. any **two** of the following (at least **one** from compare and **one** from contrast):\n\n    *Compare:* \\\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ carbohydrates, lipids and proteins are **ALL** organic compounds **OR** contain C,H, and O [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ carbohydrates, lipids and proteins are **ALL** macromolecules [1 mark]\n\n   \n\n    *Contrast:* \\\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ carbohydrates **AND** proteins are polymers **WHILST** lipids are not [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ carbohydrates and lipids are composed of carbon, hydrogen and oxygen **WHILE** proteins are composed of carbon, hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen and sometimes sulphur [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ the monomers for carbohydrates are monosaccharides **WHILE** the monomer for proteins are amino acids, and for lipids they are fatty acids and glycerol [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ carbohydrates contain glycosidic linkages between monomers **WHILE** proteins contain peptide linkages **WHILE** in lipids there are ester bonds [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ carbohydrates have aldehyde or ketone groups **WHILE** proteins have amino groups and carboxyl groups, and lipids only have carboxyl groups [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n| Carbohydrates | Proteins | Lipids | | \n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | \n\n| **Organic macromolecules** | **Organic macromolecules** | **Organic macromolecules** | ($\\checkmark$) |\n\n| Contain **glycosidic linkages** | Contain **peptide linkages** | Contain **ester bonds** | ($\\checkmark$) |\n\n| Composed of **C, H, and O** | Composed of **C, H, O, N and sometimes S** | Composed of **C, H, and O** | ($\\checkmark$) | \n\n| Monomers are **monosaccharides** | Monomers are **amino acids** | Made out of  **fatty acids** and **glycerol** | ($\\checkmark$) |"},{"ref":"BI0440","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0440\n\nWhich of the following is the correct composition of the named polysaccharide?\n\n| | $\\hspace{1em}$ Polysaccharide $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Glucose molecule $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Linkages $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ A. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Starch | $\\beta-$ glucose | 1,4 and 1,6 |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ B. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Glucagon | $\\alpha-$ glucose | 1,4 |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ C. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Glycogen | $\\alpha-$ glucose | 1,4 and 1,6 |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ D. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Cellulose | $\\beta-$ glucose | 1,6 |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as starch is made up of $\\alpha-$ glucose molecules. **Choice B**  is incorrect as glucagon is not a polysaccharide, and is therefore not made up of glucose. **Choice D**  is incorrect, as the linkages between the ꞵ- glucose molecules are 1,4 linkages only."},{"ref":"BI0441","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0441\n\nWhich reaction converts monosaccharides into disaccharides?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Condensation\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrolysis\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Catabolism\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Oxidation","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nWhen two monosaccharides are joined together to form a disaccharide, water is produced, therefore it is a condensation reaction. **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect as hydrolysis uses water to break bonds, in this case converting a disaccharide into a monosaccharides. **Choice C** is incorrect as catabolism breaks down large molecules into smaller molecules. **Choice D** is incorrect as oxidation means adding oxygen or losing electrons."},{"ref":"BI0442","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0442\n\nWhich diagram represents a polyunsaturated fatty acid?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |   |   |B.|\n|<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Carbohydrates%20%26%20Lipids/images/BI0442a.png\" alt=\"BI0442a\"> |   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Carbohydrates%20%26%20Lipids/images/BI0442b.png\" alt=\"BI0442b\">|\n|C. |   |   |  |D. |\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Carbohydrates%20%26%20Lipids/images/BI0442c.png\" alt=\"BI0442c\">|   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Carbohydrates%20%26%20Lipids/images/BI0442d.png\" alt=\"BI0442d\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nPolyunsaturated fatty acids have two or more double bonds. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as it only contains single bonds so it is a saturated fatty acid. **Choices B** and **D** are incorrect as they each contain only one double bond, making them monounsaturated fatty acids."},{"ref":"BI0444","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0444\n\nWhich statement(s) about starch and glycogen is/are correct?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n\n  | | |\n\n  | :-- | :-- |\n\n  | I. | Starch and glycogen are branched molecules. |\n\n  | II. | Starch is made up $\\beta-$ glucose molecules but glycogen is made up of $\\alpha-$ glucose molecules. |\n\n  | III. | Starch and glycogen are energy storing polysaccharides. |\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ II only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nBoth starch and glycogen are branched molecules (**Statement I**), though the degree and pattern of branching differ between them. Starch consists of two types of molecules: amylose (unbranched) and amylopectin (highly branched). Glycogen is more highly branched than amylopectin, which allows for rapid release of glucose when needed, especially important in animals for energy supply. \n\n**Statement II** is incorrect as both starch and glycogen are made up of α- glucose molecules.  \n\nBoth starch and glycogen serve as primary energy storage molecules (**Statement III**). Starch is the main form of stored carbohydrate in plants, while glycogen serves a similar function in animals and fungi.\n\n**Choice C** is therefore correct as it includes **Statements I** and **III** only.\n\n**Choices B** and **D** are incorrect as they include **Statement II**. **Choice A** is incorrect as it only includes one correct statement (**Statement I**)."},{"ref":"BI0445","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0445\n\nWhich molecule is shown in the diagram below?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Carbohydrates%20%26%20Lipids/images/BI0445.png\" alt=\"BI0445\">\n\n[Source: Bedin, T. (2022, February 28). *Plast - Karbon og hydrogen I Lange Baner - Naturfag (TP) - NDLA*. ndla.no. Retrieved June 9, 2022, from https ://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Figure_03_02_07.jpg. CC-BY-4.0]\n\n- A.    $\\hspace{1em}$ Cellulose\n\n- B.    $\\hspace{1em}$ Amylose\n- C.    $\\hspace{1em}$ Starch\n- D.    $\\hspace{1em}$ Glycogen","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nCellulose is an unbranched chain of  $\\beta-$ glucose molecules with 1,4 linkages and adjacent glucose molecules in alternating orientations. **Choice A**  is correct.\n\n**Choices C** and  **D** are incorrect as both starch and glycogen are branched molecules. **Choice B**  is incorrect as amylose is made of $\\alpha-$ glucose molecules."},{"ref":"BI0448","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0448\n\nWhat bond is formed between two monosaccharides to form a disaccharide?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Ester\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Peptide\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Glycosidic\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Sugar-phosphate","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nThe bond formed between two monosaccharides is a glycosidic bond. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because hydrogen bonds are weak interactions and do not form covalent links between monomers to create polymers. **Choice B** is incorrect because peptide bonds  link amino acids in polypeptides, not carbohydrates. **Choice D** is incorrect because sugar-phosphate (phosphodiester) bonds  link nucleotides in nucleic acids, connecting the ribose sugars and phosphate groups."},{"ref":"BI0449","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0449\n\nWhat is the product of this reaction?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Carbohydrates%20%26%20Lipids/images/BI0449.png\" alt=\"BI0449\">\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Fatty acid\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Starch\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Glycogen\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Triglyceride","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nThe diagram shows glycerol and 3 fatty acids. When these are joined in a condensation reaction, a triglyceride is formed. **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **C** are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0450","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0450\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe micrograph shows a part of a plant cell from the leaf of *Anemone sp.*\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Carbohydrates%20%26%20Lipids/images/EM%20of%20chloroplast%20no%20labels.png\" alt=\"EM of chloroplast no labels\">\n[Source: and3k, & caper437. (2009, June 25). *File: chloroplast in leaf of Anemone sp Tem 85000x.png - Wikimedia Commons*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 13, 2022, from https ://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Chloroplast_in_leaf_of_Anemone_sp_TEM_12000x.png Copyright free]\n\n1. Label structures in the plant cell above:\n 1. using the letter R where starch can be found; [1 mark]\n\n 2. using the letter S where lipids can be found; [1 mark]\n\n 3. using the letter T where cellulose can be found. [1 mark]\n\n2. Compare and contrast the structure and functions of glycogen and starch. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1. $\\ce{ -> }$ arrow labelled R pointing to a lighter oval [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Carbohydrates%20%26%20Lipids/images/450ai%201.png\" alt=\"450ai 1\">\n\n 2. $\\ce{ -> }$ arrow labelled S pointing to the cell membrane **OR** membrane around chloroplast **OR** thylakoid membrane **OR** lipid droplet **OR** vacuolar membrane [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Carbohydrates%20%26%20Lipids/images/450aii.png\" alt=\"450aii\">\n\n 3. $\\ce{ -> }$ arrow labelled T pointing to the cell wall\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Carbohydrates%20%26%20Lipids/images/450aiii.png\" alt=\"450aiii\">\n\n2. Any **four** of the following (should include **AT LEAST ONE** for compare and **AT LEAST ONE** for contrast):\n\n *Compare:*\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are polysaccharides [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are made up of $\\alpha$- glucose molecules [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have 1,4 and 1,6 linkages [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are energy storage molecules [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** amylopectin in starch and glycogen are branched molecules [1 mark]\n\n *Contrast:*\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycogen is more branched **IN CONTRAST TO** starch, **OR** amylose of starch is unbranched, amylopectin has few branched chains **WHEREAS** glycogen is more branched [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Starch is composed of two different polymers, amylose and amylopectin **WHEREAS** glycogen is composed of only one polymer [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ starch is found in plant cells **WHEREAS** glycogen is found in animal cells [1 mark]\n\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nBoth starch and glycogen are **polysaccharides** ($\\checkmark$) made of **$\\alpha$- glucose molecules** ($\\checkmark$), which have **1,4 and 1,6 linkages** ($\\checkmark$). Starch is composed of **two different** polymers; **amylose** (unbranched and forms a helix) and **amylopectin** (branched), whereas **glycogen** is composed of only **one type of polymer** ($\\checkmark$). Both amylopectin, in starch, and glycogen are **branched** molecules ($\\checkmark$), but **glycogen is more branched than starch** ($\\checkmark$). Both are **energy storage** ($\\checkmark$)molecules but **starch** is found in **plant cells** whereas **glycogen** is found in **animal cells** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{For additional detail}}$: see 4:30 in Carbohydrates Key Concept Video"},{"ref":"BI0451","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0451\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\n1. State the reaction that forms disaccharides from monosaccharides. [1 mark]\n\n2. Draw the products of the reaction between the two monosaccharides shown in the diagram below. $^*$ [2 marks]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Carbohydrates%20%26%20Lipids/images/BI0451.png\" alt=\"BI0451\">\n[Adapted from: SourceStolfi, J. (2020, February 1). *File: alpha-D-glucopyranose.svg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved June 13, 2022, from https ://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Alpha-D-Glucopyranose.svg. Copyright free]\n\n$^*$ *Note: Starting May 2028, this question may be assessed differently due to changes to the Biology guide, including the use of a data booklet.*","markScheme":"1.\n\n $\\hspace{1.5em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ condensation **OR** dehydration synthesis                    [1 mark]\n\n[1 mark]\n\n2.    **Two** products must be shown:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the disaccharide maltose is drawn correctly, showing the glycosidic bond [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  water [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Carbohydrates%20%26%20Lipids/images/Slide1.jpeg\" alt=\"Slide1\">\n\n    [Adapted from: SourceStolfi, J. (2020, February 1). *File: alpha-D-glucopyranose.svg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved June 13, 2022, from https ://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alpha-D-Glucopyranose.svg. Copyright free]"},{"ref":"BI0496","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0496\n\nA disaccharide is formed by a condensation reaction between a glucose molecule and a fructose molecule. Which molecule fits this description?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Maltose\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Lactose\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sucrose\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Galactose","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe disaccharide sucrose is made up of the monosaccharides glucose and fructose. Sucrose forms when glucose and fructose combine in a condensation reaction (a reaction that releases water). **Choice C** is the correct answer.\n\nMaltose (**Choice A**) is a disaccharide made up of two glucose molecules. Lactose (**Choice B**) is a disaccharide made up of glucose and galactose. Galactose (**Choice D**) is a monosaccharide, not a disaccharide."},{"ref":"BI0512","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0512\n\nIn the digestion of starch, amylase breaks down both amylose and amylopectin into simpler sugars. However, complete digestion to glucose requires additional enzymes due to certain structural characteristics.\n\nWhich of the following is the likely reason amylase alone cannot fully break down amylopectin into maltose, unlike its action on amylose?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ It contains β-glucose which amylase cannot interact with.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ It contains α-glucose which amylase cannot interact with.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ It has 1,4 glycosidic linkages that amylase cannot break.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ It has 1,6 glycosidic linkages that amylase cannot break.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D**  is correct because amylopectin contains both α-1,4 glycosidic linkages and α-1,6 glycosidic linkages. Amylase can hydrolyse α-1,4 linkages but cannot break α-1,6 linkages at the branch points. Additional enzymes are therefore required to completely digest amylopectin into glucose.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because amylopectin is composed of α-glucose monomers, not β-glucose. β-glucose is found in cellulose.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because amylase specifically acts on polysaccharides made from α-glucose, including starch components such as amylose and amylopectin.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because amylase can break α-1,4 glycosidic linkages. In fact, this is the main reaction catalysed by amylase during starch digestion."},{"ref":"BI0536","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0536\n\nA polymer of beta-glucose found in plants has only 1,4-glycosidic linkages. Which molecule is described by this statement?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Amylopectin\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Chitin\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cellulose\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Glycogen","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCellulose is a structural polysaccharide found in plants. Cellulose is a linear polymer of beta-glucose linked by only 1,4 glycosidic llinkages. Answer **Choice C** is therefore correct.\n\n**Choice A**: Amylopectin is a storage polysaccharide of plants. Amylopectin consists of alpha-glucose joined primarily by 1,4 glycosidic linkages with some 1,6 glycosidic linkages, giving the polysaccharide a branched structure.\n\n**Choice B**: Chitin is a structural polysaccharide found in animals and fungi. Chitin is a polymer of N-acetylglucosamine.\n\n**Choice D**: Glycogen is a storage polysaccharide in animals. Like amylopectin, glycogen consists of alpha-glucose joined by 1,4 glycosidic linkages and 1,6 glycosidic linkages."},{"ref":"BI0570","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0570\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nDistinguish between the structure of cellulose, starch and glycogen. [4 marks]","markScheme":"Any **four** of the following:\n\n$\\ce{->}$ cellulose is composed of beta-D-glucose **OR** glucose monomers **WHEREAS** starch and glycogen are composed of alpha-D-glucose monomers  [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{->}$ amylose and cellulose are linear chains **WHEREAS** amylopectin and glycogen are branched [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{->}$ amylose has alpha 1,4 glycosidic bonds **WHEREAS** amylopectin and glycogen have both alpha 1,4 and 1,6 glycosidic bonds [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{->}$ polymers of cellulose form many hydrogen bonds with each other **WHEREAS** starch and glycogen do not [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{->}$ **COMPARED TO**  starch **OR** amylopectin, glycogen is a more highly branched polymer   [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{->}$ every other monomer in cellulose is rotated 180° **WHEREAS** in starch and glycogen the monomers are all oriented the same  [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n|Cellulose | Starch | Glycogen| |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| Composed of **beta** glucose | Composed of **alpha** glucose | Composed of **alpha** glucose | ($\\checkmark$) $\\hspace{3em}$ |\n| **Linear** chains of glucose | **Branched** and linear chains of glucose | **Branched** chains of glucose | ($\\checkmark$) $\\hspace{3em}$ |\n| **Many hydrogen bonds** formed between polymers | **Does not form** large numbers of hydrogen bonds between polymers | **Does not form** large numbers of hydrogen bonds between polymers | ($\\checkmark$) $\\hspace{3em}$ |\n| Every other monomer **rotated** 180 degrees | All monomers have the **same** orientation | All monomers have the **same** orientation | ($\\checkmark$) $\\hspace{3em}$ |"},{"ref":"BI0831","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0831\n\nWhat bond is formed between two glucose molecules to form maltose?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrogen\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Peptide\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ α 1-4 glycosidic linkage\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sugar-phosphate","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe bond formed between two glucose molecules to form maltose is an α 1-4 glycosidic linkage. This is a covalent bond between two alpha glucose molecules, where the carbon #1 of one of the glucose molecules forms a bond with the carbon #4 of the other glucose molecule. **Choice C** is correct.\n\nA hydrogen bond (**Choice A**)  is a weak electrostatic attraction between a hydrogen atom that is covalently bonded to a highly electronegative atom and another electronegative atom in a different molecule or region of the same molecule. For example, between two water molecules. Peptide bonds (**Choice B**) are covalent bonds that hold amino acids together in polypeptide chains, and sugar-phosphate bonds (**Choice D**) are covalent bonds between the ribose sugars and phosphate groups, forming the backbone of DNA and RNA. **Choices A, B** and **D** are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0838","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0838\n\nWhich of the following is a steroid?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Carbohydrates%20%26%20Lipids/images/BI0838.png\" alt=\"BI0838\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because all steroids have four linked carbon rings.\n\n**Choice A** is an α-glucose molecule as shown by its hexagonal structure and molecular formula, C${_6}$H:sub[12]O${_6}$. **Choice B** is incorrect as it is an amino acid, as shown by the amine (NH${_2}$) and carboxyl (COOH) group on either end of the R group on the central carbon. **Choice C** is incorrect as it is a triglyceride, as shown by the three long fatty acids attached to the glycerol molecule."},{"ref":"BI1142","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1142\n\nWhich type of reaction is involved in the formation of polysaccharides?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrolysis\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Condensation\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Decarboxylation\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Phosphorylation","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe formation of polysaccharides involves the removal of water to form a glycosidic bond. This is a condensation reaction.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as water is a product, not a reactant, in the formation of polysaccharides. **Choice C** is incorrect as no carboxyl group is removed and no CO:sub[2] is produced in the reaction. **Choice D** is incorrect as the reaction does not require the addition of a phosphate group."},{"ref":"BI1157","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1157\n\nWhich row in the table below is correct for the digestion of macromolecules?\n\n| | Macromolecule | Monomers | Type of Reaction |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Polysaccharide | Alpha glucose | Hydrolysis |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Polysaccharide | Amino acid | Condensation |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Polypeptide | Alpha glucose | Hydrolysis |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Polypeptide | Amino acids | Condensation |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nPolysaccharides, such as starch, are broken down by hydrolysis reactions, and the digestion of starch eventually yields alpha glucose as a monomer.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the monomers of polysaccharides are not amino acids, and hydrolysis, not condensation reactions break down polysaccharides. **Choice C** is incorrect because alpha glucose is not a monomer of polypeptides. **Choice D** is incorrect because hydrolysis reactions, not condensation reactions, break down polypeptides."},{"ref":"BI1194","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1194\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nBelow is an image of *Solanum tuberosum*, commonly known as the potato plant.  Above ground are the photosynthetic organs of the plant, and below ground is a large tuber which stores energy in the form of carbohydrates.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Carbohydrates%20%26%20Lipids/images/BI1194-HL.png\" alt=\"BI1194-HL\">\n[Source: Adapted from H. Zell (2009, June 17). *Solanum tuberosum*. Retrieved September 28, 2023 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Solanum_tuberosum_001.JPG. Copyright under CC BY-SA 3.0]\n\n1. State which molecule serves as a source of electrons for photosystem II at the beginning of the electron transport chain in chloroplasts.    [1 mark]\n\n2. Outline how:\n    1. the structure of cellulose provides support to plants. [2 marks]\n\n    2. the structure of glycogen is suited to its storage role in animals. [2 marks]\n\n3. Explain how glucose is transported in the phloem, from the leaf to the roots.     [5 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ water    [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The **photolysis of water** in PS II provides the **electrons** ($\\checkmark$) for the electron transport chain in chloroplasts.\n\n2.\n    1. any **two** of the following (one for structure and one for how structure adds to the role):\n\n    ***structure*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ alternating arrangement of beta-glucose monomers gives a straight chain that can form groups [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ linear arrangement of cellulose molecules provides a fibrous structure that is rigid    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ groups of  cellulose molecules are arranged in parallel with hydrogen bonds forming cross-links [1 mark]\n\n    ***how structure supports the role*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ microfibrils of cellulose have tensile strength **OR** give strength to the plant cell walls [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Cellulose is an unbranched polymer of glucose, and **groups** of **cellulose molecules** are arranged in **parallel**, cross-linked by **hydrogen bonds** ($\\checkmark$)  to provide **strength** and **rigidity** ($\\checkmark$) to the cell walls of the plants.\n\n2.\n    2. any **two** of the following (one from each section):\n\n    ***structure*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glycogen has  α-1-6 glycosidic bonds **OR** α-1,6 linkages  between glucose molecules **OR** has  α-1-6 glycosidic bonds **OR** 1-6 linkages between α-glucose molecules **OR**  α-1-6 glycosidic bonds **OR** 1-6 linkages between α-D-glucose molecules **AND** making it branched  [1 mark]\n\n    ***how structure supports the role*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ branching leads to compact **OR** efficient storage of glucose **OR** maximises the number of  glucose molecules that can be stored **OR** glycogen takes up less room so it can be stored in large amounts  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glycogen is insoluble, so does not affect osmotic potential of the cell   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ branching means it can be broken down rapidly to release glucose for respiration **OR** rapid mobilisation of glucose when energy required **OR** glucose can be released more efficiently [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Glycogen contains **1,6 glycosidic bonds** between the α-glucose molecules making it **branched** ($\\checkmark$). This is beneficial as it means that enzymes can simultaneously act on multiple ends of the branches, making **glucose release rapid** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glucose is converted to sucrose by the addition of fructose     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sucrose is carried from source **OR** leaf to sink **OR** root by translocation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sucrose is loaded **OR** actively transported into phloem sieve tube elements    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ by companion cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lowers water potential **OR** makes water potential more negative **OR** increases solute potential in phloem sieve tubes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water moves into phloem sieve tubes **OR** water moves in by osmosis **WHICH** increases hydrostatic pressure            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at the sink **OR** root sucrose moves out of phloem sieve tube elements by diffusion **OR** active transport            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water moves out of phloem sieve tubes **OR** follows sucrose by osmosis **WHICH** lowers hydrostatic pressure                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sap **OR** sucrose flows **OR** moves from high to low hydrostatic pressure **OR** down pressure gradient                    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The carbohydrate transported in the phloem is the disaccharide **sucrose**, which is made up of **glucose** plus **fructose** ($\\checkmark$). The sucrose is **actively transported** into the phloem **sieve tube elements** ($\\checkmark$) by the **companion cells** ($\\checkmark$). The sucrose **reduces** the **water potential** inside the **sieve tube elements** ($\\checkmark$), so **water enters by osmosis, increasing** the **hydrostatic pressure** ($\\checkmark$). At the **sinks**, sucrose concentrations are low, so the **sucrose moves out** of the sieve tube elements by **diffusion** or **active transport** ($\\checkmark$). This **increases** the **water potential** in the sieve tube elements, so **water moves out** by osmosis, **reducing** the **hydrostatic pressure** ($\\checkmark$). The sap in the sieve tube elements flows from the source to the sink **down** the **hydrostatic pressure gradient** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1314","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1314\n\nWhich of the following statements describes α-Linolenate, as depicted in the diagram below?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Carbohydrates%20%26%20Lipids/images/BI1314.png\" alt=\"BI1314\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ A saturated fatty acid\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ A mono-unsaturated fatty acid\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ A polyunsaturated fatty acid\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ A triglyceride","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nα-Linolenate has more than one double bond in its carbon chain and is, therefore, a polyunsaturated fatty acid.\n\nSince there is more than one double bond in the carbon chain of α-Linolenate it cannot be a saturated fatty acid (**Choice A**) or a mono-unsaturated fatty acid (**Choice B**). The fatty acid is not attached to a glycerol molecule, along with 2 other fatty acids, and so is not a triglyceride (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI1379","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1379\n\nWhat type of reaction is involved in the digestion of the following molecule?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Carbohydrates%20%26%20Lipids/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Reduction\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Condensation\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Neutralisation\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrolysis","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because digestion involves catabolic reactions, such as hydrolysis, that break down larger molecules into smaller molecules. The molecule shown in the diagram is maltose, a disaccharide, which can be broken down into monomer subunits with the addition of water. This is a hydrolysis reaction.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because sugars are not reduced during digestion. A reduction reaction involves an atom or ion gaining one or more electrons. **Choice B** is incorrect since condensation reactions are a type of anabolic reaction, where two smaller molecules are joined together to produce a larger molecule whilst releasing water. This is the opposite of what we would expect for digestion, which involves catabolic reactions that break substances down. **Choice C** is incorrect as the breakdown of maltose involves neither acid nor alkali and thus cannot be a neutralisation reaction. A neutralisation reaction involves the reaction between an acid and base to produce a solution closer to pH7."},{"ref":"BI1475","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1475\n\nWhich biomolecule does **NOT** contain a carboxyl group?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Dipeptide\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Saturated fatty acid\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Amino acid\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Triglyceride","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nA carboxyl group (COOH) consists of a carbon molecule joined to an oxygen atom by a double bond and an OH/hydroxyl group by a single bond. Although carboxyl groups are found in one of the monomers in triglyceride; in fatty acids, the carboxyl group reacts with glycerol to form an ester bond in a condensation reaction. Therefore carboxyl groups are not found in triglycerides. **Choice D**  is correct.\n\nDipeptides, saturated fatty acids and amino acids all contain carboxyl groups. **Choices A, B** and **C**  are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1491","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1491\n\nWhich of the following statements about lipids is true?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Triglycerides are made up of a four-carbon glycerol molecule linked to three fatty acids.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ There is a carbon-carbon triple bond in monounsaturated fats.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Polyunsaturated fats are made up of carbon-carbon single bonds to keep them in a liquid state.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Saturated fats are made up of carbon-carbon single bonds.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSaturated fats are \"saturated\" with hydrogen atoms, meaning that the carbon atoms are connected by single bonds, and there are no double bonds. This structure allows the fat molecules to pack closely together, making them solid at room temperature. **Choice D** is correct.\n\nWhile triglycerides are made up of three fatty acids linked to a glycerol molecule, the glycerol molecule contains three carbon atoms, not four. **Choice A** is incorrect.\n\nMonounsaturated fats have one double bond (hence \"mono\") in the fatty acid chain. A triple bond is not characteristic of fats but is more often associated with certain specialised organic compounds. **Choice B** is incorrect.\n\nPolyunsaturated fats have more than one (hence \"poly\") carbon-carbon double bond in their structure. These multiple double bonds create kinks in the fatty acid chains, preventing the molecules from packing tightly together, which contributes to their liquid state at room temperature. **Choice C** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1547","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1547\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA student carried out an experiment to measure the energy content of different foods. Below is a diagram of the apparatus used.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Carbohydrates%20%26%20Lipids/images/BI1547ai.png\" alt=\"BI1547ai\">\n\nAt the start of the experiment, the initial temperature of the water and the mass of the food sample were recorded. The food was then set alight and immediately placed under the boiling tube of water. Once the food had finished burning, the final temperature of the water was recorded.\n\nIt takes 4.2J of energy to raise the temperature of 1g (1mL) of water by 1:sup[o]C.\n\nThe increase in the temperature of 10mL of water from 0.5g of potato crisp was 35:sup[o]C.\n\n1. Using the information provided, calculate the energy of the potato snack in kJ g:sup[-1] [2 marks]\n\nPart of the nutrition details from the bag of potato snacks is shown below:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Carbohydrates%20%26%20Lipids/images/BI1547b.png\" alt=\"BI1547b\">\n\n2. Assuming the students' repeated trials yielded similar results to those you have calculated, suggest two inaccuracies in the experimental method that could explain the discrepancy between the measured energy content in the experiment and the value listed on the food label. [2 marks]\n\nThe table below shows the energy and fat content on the packs of the other foods the student tested.\n\n| Food Type | Energy / kJ 100 $\\bf{g^{-1}}$ | Fat Content/%|\n| :-- | :-: | :-: |\n| Butterscotch popcorn| 1837 | 11.5 |\n| Pitta bread | 1005 | 2.8 |\n| Digestive biscuit | 2039 | 24.0 |\n| Smoked almonds | 2520 | 52.0 |\n| Oat cracker | 1807 | 14.2 |\n| Tortilla crisp | 2051 | 22.5 |\n\n3. Identify, with a reason, which food investigated by the student is least likely to increase the risk of obesity. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. *Full marks can only be awarded if units included*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ {(10g x 35:sup[o]C) x 4.2J) ÷ 0.5g} ÷ 1000 **OR** 2940J g:sup[-1] ÷ 1000 **OR** 2940J g:sup[-1] [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 2.940 **OR** 2.94 **OR** 2.9 kJ g:sup[-1] [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Energy transferred (J) per gram of food\n\n = {(mass of water (g) x temperature rise (:sup[o]C) x 4.2 (J g:sup[-1] :sup[o]C:sup[-1] ))÷\n Mass of food sample (g)}\n\n = **{(10g x 35:sup[o]C x 4.2J) ÷ 0.5g}** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n = **2940J g:sup[-1]** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n Energy in kJ g:sup[-1] = 2940J g:sup[-1] ÷ 1000\n\n = **2.940 kJ g:sup[-1]** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ heat is transferred to the boiling tube **OR** thermometer **OR** lost to air **OR** lost to surroundings instead of to water [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ incomplete combustion offood **OR** potato snack [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ food may fall off the needle **OR** holder before fully burning [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The energy calculated from the experiment is lower because **not all** the **heat** is **transferred to** the **water** in the boiling tube, **some** is **transferred to** the **apparatus** and **lost to** the surrounding **air** ($\\checkmark$). Also, the food may **not** have **fully combusted**, often indicated by smoke ($\\checkmark$)\n\n3.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ pitta bread [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ because it contains the least energy **AND** fat **OR** has the lowest energy **AND** fat content [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **pitta bread** ($\\checkmark$) is the least likely to lead to obesity if eaten as it has the **lowest energy and fat** ($\\checkmark$) content out of all the foods tested."},{"ref":"BI1561","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1561\n\nWhich of the following carbon compounds is **not** possible?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Carbohydrates%20%26%20Lipids/images/BI1561.png\" alt=\"BI1561\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because carbon can only form up to four covalent bonds with other atoms. This substance shows five covalent bonds for each carbon atom.\n\n**Choices B, C**, and **D** are incorrect because they represent real molecules (cyclopentane, acetamide, and propane, respectively) with the correct number of covalent bonds."},{"ref":"BI1563","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1563\n\nWhich row in the table correctly compares and contrasts the features of starch and glycogen?\n\n| | Similarity | Difference |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Both are highly branched polymers | Glycogen has a helical structure |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Both contain 1-4 and 1-6 glycosidic linkages | Glycogen is stored in animal and fungal, but not plant, cells |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Both are stored in plant cells | Starch is composed of both amylose and amylopectin |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Both are composed of alpha glucose monomers | Starch is more water soluble than glycogen |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because glycogen and amylopectin, one of the molecules found in starch, are both composed of 1-4 and 1-6 glycosidic linkages. Glycogen is used for energy storage in animal and fungal cells, as well as some bacteria, while amylopectin is used for energy storage in plants.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because amylose, a component of starch, has a helical structure; glycogen does not. **Choice C** is incorrect because glycogen is not found in plant cells. **Choice D** is incorrect because glycogen is more water soluble than starch."},{"ref":"BI2324","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2324\n\nWhich characteristics of triglycerides make them suitable for functions in organisms?\n\n| | Energy per gram | Solubility in water | Heat conduction |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Half compared to carbohydrates | Yes | High|\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Twice compared to carbohydrates | Yes | Low |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Half compared to carbohydrates | No | High |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Twice compared to carbohydrates | No  | Low |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because triglycerides contain twice the energy per gram compared to carbohydrates, making them an efficient long-term energy storage molecule. They are insoluble in water, so they can be stored without affecting the water potential of cells. Additionally, triglycerides are poor conductors of heat, which makes them effective thermal insulators, helping to reduce heat loss as a secondary function.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because triglycerides contain twice the energy per gram compared to carbohydrates, not half. Furthermore, they are insoluble in water and are poor conductors of heat, contrary to what is stated in this option.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because triglycerides are not soluble in water; their hydrophobic nature prevents them from dissolving.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because triglycerides provide twice the energy per gram compared to carbohydrates, not half, and they are poor conductors of heat, not good conductors.."},{"ref":"BI2439","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2439\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nBelow is a diagram of a phospholipid.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Carbohydrates%20%26%20Lipids/images/BI2439.png\" alt=\"BI2439\">\n\nDescribe how the properties of phospholipids allow for the formation of selectively permeable membranes. [4 marks]","markScheme":"any **four** of the following *(allow any of the following in an annotated diagram)*:\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ phospholipids are amphipathic **OR** have hydrophilic heads and hydrophobic tails [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ hydrophilic heads are orientated towards water **OR** face outwards **OR** towards the aqueous environment [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ hydrophobic tails are orientated away from water **OR** face inwards **OR** away from the aqueous environment [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ the hydrophobic core **OR** hydrophobic tails prevent the passage of polar **OR** charged molecules (through the membrane) [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ small non-polar molecules can pass through the membrane [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ larger **OR** polar **OR** charged molecules require protein channels to pass through the membrane [1 mark]\n [4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Phospholipids have **hydrophilic heads** and **hydrophobic tails** ($\\checkmark$), they are amphipathic. The hydrophilic heads attract water, so they are orientated **toward** the aqueous environment ($\\checkmark$), whilst the hydrophobic tails repel water and are orientated **away** from the aqueous environment ($\\checkmark$). The **hydrophobic core** of the bilayer allows only small, **non-polar molecules** such as oxygen to pass ($\\checkmark$), however larger or **charged molecules** require protein channels ($\\checkmark$).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Carbohydrates%20%26%20Lipids/images/BI2439Ans.png\" alt=\"BI2439Ans\">"}]},{"name":"Cell Specialization","questions":[{"ref":"BI0003","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0003\n\nWhich of the following statements about the effect of surface area and volume on cell processes is **false**?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$  The rate at which materials enter or leave a cell depends on it's surface area.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$  Small cells exchange materials more efficiently by diffusion than large cells.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$  The rate at which materials are used and produced depends on the surface area.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$  A large spherical cell may not be able to excrete waste products fast enough by diffusion.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is a false statement because the rate at which materials are used and produced depends mainly on the volume of the cell, not its surface area. As this is a false statement, it makes **Choice C** the correct answer to the question.\n\n**Choice A** is a true statement because diffusion occurs across the cell membrane, so the exchange rate depends on the surface area.\n\n**Choice B** is a true statement because smaller cells have a larger surface area-to-volume ratio, allowing more efficient diffusion.\n\n**Choice D** is a true statement because large cells have a smaller surface area-to-volume ratio, which can limit the diffusion of waste products out of the cell."},{"ref":"BI0007","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0007\n\nWhich statement best describes stem cells?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$  Stem cells cannot be found in the adult body.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$  The foetus is composed only of stem cells.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$  Stem cells can divide and differentiate into different types of specialised cells.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$  Stem cells have undergone specialisation and maturation.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** - There are still stem cells that can be found in the adult body.  For example, stem cells are present in the bone marrow, skin, and liver.\n\n**Choice B** - The foetal stage of development happens from the ninth week after conception until birth. In this stage, the organs have begun to form, and these developing organs come from differentiated stem cells.\n\n**Choice C** - Stem cells are characterised by their capacity to divide and differentiate. These cells are similar to a blank canvas with the possibility of becoming any type of cell.\n\n**Choice D** - Stem cells are yet to undergo specialisation through the process of differentiation."},{"ref":"BI0011","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0011\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe image below is a micrograph of blood.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Cell%20Specialization/images/micrograph%20of%20blood%20with%20phagocytes%20phagocytic%20white%20blood%20cells%20lymphocytes%20i.png\" alt=\"micrograph of blood with phagocytes phagocytic white blood cells lymphocytes i\">\nMagnification: 800x\n\n[Source: Adapted from Berkshire Community College Bioscience Image Library. (2018). *Connective Tissue: Human Blood Leukocyte Survey*. Flickr. Retrieved April 4, 2022, from\nhttps://www.flickr.com/photos/146824358@N03/26921278147. Copyright free]\n\n1. Identify the part of the cells labelled A and B. [2 marks]\n\n2. Outline why blood is classified as a tissue. [1 mark]\n\n3. State where the stem cells needed to produce red blood cells are located. [1 mark]\n\n4. Progenitor cells are produced by dividing stem cells. These cells will eventually differentiate even further. Blood cells have a common progenitor cell. Describe the process that happens to these progenitor cells. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ arrow A points to the plasma membrane   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ arrow B points to the nucleus (in white blood cell) [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ consists of different types of cells **OR** consists of specialised cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ consists of red blood cells, white blood cells and platelets   [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\nThe definition of tissue is **different types of cells** working together ($\\checkmark$). Blood consists of **red blood cells, white blood cells and platelets**,($\\checkmark$) which each have a different role and work together.\n\n3.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ bone marrow [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ through selective gene expression **OR** expression of only tissue-specific genes **OR** expression of specific genes needed for the development of the blood cells     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in response to changes in the environment  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ become specialised cells **OR** become cells with a specific role **OR** function  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ differentiate into red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets **OR** become the different types of blood cells   [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n**Through selective gene expression** ($\\checkmark$) common progenitor cells **become specialised cells** ($\\checkmark$). In the case of blood, these cells **differentiate into red blood cells, white blood cells and platelets** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0620","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0620\n\nWhich of the following characteristics of type I pneumocytes contribute to increasing the rate of gas exchange?\n\n-    I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Cells are flattened\n-    II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ Secretion of a surfactant\n-    III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ Cover less than 10\\% of the alveolar surface\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nType I pneumocytes are flattened cells found in the alveoli (**Statement I**). The cells are very thin which means gases have a shorter distance to diffuse.  This results in an increased rate of gas exchange.\n\nThe wall of an alveolus is covered mostly (>90\\%) by type I pneumocytes (**Statement III**). Type II pneumocytes cover less than 10\\% of an alveolus. Type II pneumocytes secrete a surfactant which aids gas exchange because oxygen first dissolves in the surfactant before diffusing across type I pneumocytes (**Statement II**)."},{"ref":"BI0754","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0754\n\nThe table shows the diameters of red blood cells from different organisms. Assuming the red blood cells are approximately the same shape, which organism's red blood cell will have the highest surface area to volume ratio?\n\n| | Organism | Diameter of red blood cell/µm)|\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Human | 7.7 |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Bird | 12.6 |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Fish | 13.6 |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Frog | 23.3 |\n\n[Source: Adapted from Elson, J. A. (2014, December 24). *File: Newbloodm.jpg.* Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved March 5, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Newbloodm.jpg Copyright under CC BY 3.0]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe cell with the smallest diameter is the smallest cell, and therefore will have the highest surface area to volume ratio. Choice **A** is correct.\n\nBirds, fish and frogs have red blood cells with a larger diameter than humans. Choices **B, C** and **D** are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0758","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0758\n\nWhich of the following could be a reason that adult haematopoietic stem cells rather than embryonic stem cells are used in the treatment of leukaemia?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Embryonic stem cells have limited differentiation potential.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ It is difficult to control which cell type embryonic stem cells differentiate into.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Adult haematopoietic stem cells divide faster than embryonic stem cells.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Embryonic stem cells have a higher chance of accumulated mutations.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nEmbryonic stem cells are pluripotent, meaning they can become almost any cell type. While this is useful in theory, it also makes it hard to control their differentiation precisely. In contrast, adult haematopoietic stem cells (from bone marrow or cord blood) are multipotent and are already partially specialised — they naturally form blood cells, which is ideal for treating leukaemia. Therefore, adult stem cells are safer and more targeted for this use. **Choice B** is the correct answer.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because embryonic stem cells actually have greater differentiation potential as they are pluripotent, and so not limited. Adult stem cells have more limited potential.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because it is embryonic stem cells that divide more rapidly than adult stem cells, meaning they have greater proliferative capacity than adult stem cells. Adult haematopoietic stem cells divide at a controlled, slower rate, which limits their use in some therapies but helps reduce risks like tumour formation.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because adult stem cells (especially from older donors) are more likely to have accumulated mutations due to age and environmental exposure. Embryonic stem cells are derived early in development and so typically have few mutations."},{"ref":"BI0759","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0759\n\nWhich row correctly describes the differentiation potential of a zygote, embryonic stem cells and adult stem cells?\n\n| | Zygote | Embryonic stem cells | Adult stem cells |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Can give rise to any cell type of the body and extra-embryonic tissue | Can give rise to a limited range of cell types | Can give rise to any cell type of the body |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Can give rise to any cell type of the body and extra-embryonic tissue | Can give rise to any cell type of the body | Can give rise to a limited range of cell types |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Can give rise to any cell type of the body | Can give rise to any cell type of the body and extra-embryonic tissue | Can give rise to a limited range of cell types |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Can give rise to any cell type of the body and extra-embryonic tissue | Can give rise to any cell type of the body and extra-embryonic tissue | Can give rise to a limited range of cell types |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe zygote is a totipotent cell that can give rise to all cell types of the body and to extra-embryonic tissues, such as the fetal part of the placenta. Embryonic stem cells are pluripotent cells that are derived from the inner cell mass of the blastocyst. These are capable of differentiating into any cell type of the body but not extra-embryonic tissues. Adult stem cells are multipotent cells found in specific tissues and are generally limited to differentiating into cell types of their tissue of origin. **Choice B** is therefore correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect, as embryonic stem cells are pluripotent and can give rise to any cell type of the body, whereas adult stem cells are multipotent and can only give rise to a limited range of cell types.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect, as the zygote is totipotent and can give rise to extra-embryonic tissues, but embryonic stem cells are pluripotent and cannot. \\\n**Choice D** is incorrect as embryonic stem cells are pluripotent, not totipotent. They can give rise to any cell type of the body but cannot give rise to extra-embryonic tissues."},{"ref":"BI0941","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0941\n\nWhat is this cell's most likely primary function, and where could it be located in a mammal?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Cell%20Specialization/images/BI0941.png\" alt=\"BI0941\">\n\n| | $\\hspace{1em}$ Function $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Location $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ absorption $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ nephron $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ absorption $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ capillary $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ immune defence $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ bone marrow $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ insulation $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ adipose tissue $\\hspace{1em}$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because microvilli are present, increasing the cell's surface area for absorption. Cells of the proximal convoluted tubule in the nephron require a large surface area for selected reabsorption of substances in the filtrate. **Choice B** is incorrect because cells lining capillaries are flat (squamous epithelial tissue). **Choice C** is incorrect because cells of the immune system do not have microvilli. **Choice D** is incorrect because the function of microvilli is not insulation, and adipose cells do not have microvilli."},{"ref":"BI0942","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0942\n\nBased on the Venn diagram, which identifies cardiac muscle cells and striated muscle fibres?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Cell%20Specialization/images/BI0942.png\" alt=\"BI0942\">\n\n| | I | II | III |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | striated muscle fibres | cardiac muscle | neither |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | both | striated muscle fibres | cardiac muscle |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ |cardiac muscle | both | skeletal muscle fibres |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | cardiac muscle | striated muscle fibres | neither |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because only cardiac muscle cells are both branched and contain contractile muscle fibres, while only striated muscle fibres have multiple nuclei and contractile muscle fibres. **Choice A** is incorrect because striated muscle fibres are not branched and because cardiac muscle cells do not contain multiple nuclei. **Choice B** is incorrect because only cardiac muscle cells are branched and do not contain multiple nuclei. **Choice C** is incorrect because only striated muscle fibres contain multiple nuclei and are not branched."},{"ref":"BI0943","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0943\n\nWhich is a correct description of the cell type adapted for gas exchange in the lungs?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Type II pneumocytes which contain many secretory vesicles\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Type I pneumocytes which contain many secretory vesicles\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Type I pneumocytes which are extremely thin\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Type II pneumocytes which are extremely thin","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** correctly describes the cell type adapted for gas exchange in the lungs.  **Choice A** is incorrect because although it is a correct description of type II pneumocytes, these cells are not adapted for gas exchange. **Choice B** is incorrect because type I pneumocytes do not contain many secretory vesicles. **Choice D** is incorrect because type II pneumocytes are not extremely thin."},{"ref":"BI0944","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0944\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Explain how the shape of erythrocytes and cells lining the proximal convoluted tubule make these cells more adapted to the functions they perform.  [4 marks]\n\n2. Compare and contrast the structure of striated muscle cells and cardiac muscle fibres.              [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain how the structure of egg and sperm cells are adapted to their functions.    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  erythrocytes are biconcave, which increases their surface area    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  increased surface area increases rate of gas exchange between erythrocyte and its surroundings **OR** blood plasma [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  PCT cells are columnar - allowing close packing of cells / efficient use of space [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  cells of the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) have microvilli which increase their surface area [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  increased surface area of PCT cells provides more places for aquaporins **OR** amino acid transport proteins **OR** glucose transport proteins **OR** ion transport proteins [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  greater number of aquaporins **OR** other correctly named transport protein increases the rate substances **OR** water **OR** amino acids **OR** glucose **OR** ions are reabsorbed from the filtrate [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  invaginations on the outer cell membrane, **OR** membrane on opposite side to lumen, of PCT cells provide a large SA for transport of substances out of the cell [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Erythrocytes are **biconcave**, which **increases** their **surface area** ($\\checkmark$).  Having a larger surface area **increases the rate** at which **gas exchange** occurs between the cell and its surroundings ($\\checkmark$).  Cells lining the proximal convoluted tubule have **microvilli**, increasing their **surface area** ($\\checkmark$).  This increased surface area means the cell can have **more aquaporins** and other **transport proteins** in its cell membrane ($\\checkmark$), which **increases the rate** at which substances such as water, glucose and amino acids are **reabsorbed** from the filtrate ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following (must contain at least 1 similarity and one difference for full marks):\n\n    **Differences:**\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ striated muscle cells have multiple nuclei **WHEREAS** cardiac muscle cells do not        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cardiac muscle cells are branched **WHEREAS** striated muscle cells are not        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ striated muscle cells are arranged side by side **WHEREAS** cardiac muscles are arranged side to side **AND** end to end [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ striated muscle fibres can be much larger than cardiac muscle cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cardiac muscle cells have intercalated discs **WHEREAS** striated muscle fibres do not [1 mark]\n\n    **Similarities:**\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contain contractile myofibrils [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contain sarcoplasm **OR** sarcoplasmic reticulum **OR** T-tubules [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Both** cardiac muscle cells and striated muscle fibres contain **contractile myofibrils** ($\\checkmark$), **T-tubules** and a **sarcolemma** ($\\checkmark$). However, **cardiac** muscle cells are **branched** and connected by **intercalated discs**, whereas **striated muscle** fibres are **not branched** ($\\checkmark$) and are formed from **fused cells** ($\\checkmark$).  Finally, **striated muscle** fibres have **multiple nuclei**, whereas **cardiac muscle** cells have only **one nucleus** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ egg cells are large, **OR** have a large volume of cytoplasm, which supplies the egg with nutrients **OR** food stores **OR** lipid stores **OR** makes it possible for multiple, rapid rounds of cell division after fertilisation  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ egg cells contain many mitochondria for energy production  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ eggs **OR** sperm cells are haploid, which allows them to fuse with another gamete to become diploid [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the zona pellucida prevents multiple sperm from fusing with the egg **OR** polyspermy **OR** prevents sperm from another species from fusing with the egg [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ corona radiata of egg protects the egg [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ egg cells contain cortical granules which undergo exocytosis after fertilisation which prevents polyspermy **OR** modifies zona pellucida **OR** results in the breakdown of sperm binding proteins [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ egg cells secrete chemicals which attract sperm [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sperm cells are streamlined, **OR** have few organelles, for swimming [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sperm cells have receptors for chemicals released by egg cells which guide sperm to the egg [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sperm cells have an acrosome which breaks down the zona pellucida [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sperm cells have receptor proteins which bind to the egg’s cell membrane [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sperm cells have a tail **OR** flagellum for locomotion [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sperm cells have many mitochondria which supply the energy for locomotion [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Egg** cells are large, so they have a **large volume of cytoplasm** containing **mitochondria** and **fat droplets** ($\\checkmark$) to supply the egg with energy.  Because it is large, egg cells are also prepared for relatively **rapid** rounds of **division** after they are fertilised ($\\checkmark$).  It is important that the zygote, formed after fertilisation, is diploid, so the **sperm and egg** are both **haploid** ($\\checkmark$).  The egg's **zona pellucida** also **prevents polyspermy** ($\\checkmark$) by hardening after fertilisation in response to the exocytosis of **enzymes** contained in **cortical granules** ($\\checkmark$).  Sperm are also adapted to their function, which is to fertilise egg cells.  They have **receptors** that bind to **chemicals released by egg** cells which help them move towards egg cells ($\\checkmark$).   Sperm cells also have an **acrosome** at the tip of their head, which **breaks down the zona pellucida** ($\\checkmark$), allowing receptors on the head of the sperm to **bind** to receptors on the cell **membrane of the egg** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0978","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0978\n\nA patient receives a bone marrow transplant from a 36 year old.  One of the functions of these cells is to produce red and white blood cells.  What type of stem cells has the patient received?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Pluripotent stem cells\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Totipotent stem cells\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Differentiated stem cells\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Multipotent stem cells","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because multipotent stem cells can produce only cells of a particular tissue or organ.  **Choice A** is incorrect because pluripotent stem cells are usually found in early embryos and are capable of differentiating to become any cell type found in the organism except for extra-embryonic tissues such as the placenta.  Additionally, they are not found in bone marrow. **Choice B** is incorrect because totipotent stem cells are only found in early stages of embryonic development and can produce all cell types of the organism as well as extra-embryonic tissues such as the placenta. **Choice C** is incorrect because stem cells are by definition, undifferentiated."},{"ref":"BI0979","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0979\n\nWhich of the following explains the limitations on cell size?\n\n| | Surface area to volume ratio decreases as cell size increases | Increases in cytoplasmic volume increase metabolic demands | Fewer transport proteins are present in the cell membranes of larger cells |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | ✔ | ✘ | ✘ |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | ✘ | ✔ | ✔ |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | ✔ |  ✔ | ✘ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because larger cells do not generally have fewer transport proteins. **Choice D** is also correct because as cell size increases, cytoplasmic volume increases, increasing the cell's metabolic demands  which increases the cell’s need to exchange substances.  Because cytoplasmic volume increases more rapidly than the surface area of the cell membrane, the surface area to volume ratio decreases.  This means that as cell size increases, the cell membrane's surface area is soon unable to excrete waste and intake nutrients rapidly enough.\n\n**Choice A** and **C** are incorrect as they include the statement that fewer transport proteins are present in the cell membranes of larger cells. **Choice B** is incorrect as it does not include the statement that increases in cytoplasmic volume increase metabolic demands"},{"ref":"BI0980","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0980\n\nIn humans, which of the following is the correct order from smallest to largest?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Male gamete → female gamete → red blood cell → striated muscle cell\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Red blood cells → white blood cells → female gamete → striated muscle cell\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ White blood cells → red blood cells → female gamete → striated muscle cell\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Male gamete → red blood cell → striated muscle cell → white blood cell","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because it is the correct order of cells from smallest to largest with red blood cells being the smallest. **Choice A** is incorrect because red blood cells are smaller than female gametes. **Choice C** is incorrect because red blood cells are smaller than white blood cells. **Choice D** is incorrect because white blood cells are smaller than striated muscle cells."},{"ref":"BI1016","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1016\n\nAn experiment investigating tidal volume in the lungs was conducted using a spirometer. In the experiment, the volume of air inhaled and exhaled with each breath was measured for one minute while the student was at rest. The results obtained from the spirometer are displayed in the graph below. The measurement was repeated for the student during exercise.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Cell%20Specialization/images/BI1016-SLHL.png\" alt=\"BI1016-SLHL\">\n\n1. Identify the independent and dependent variables in this experiment. [2 marks]\n\n2. State what is measured to give the tidal volume of the student. [1 mark]\n\n3. Calculate the mean tidal volume for one minute, giving the units. [2 marks]\n\n4. Suggest how the tidal volume would differ during exercise. [1 mark]\n\n5. State which cells of the alveoli are specialised to allow gaseous exchange to occur. [1 mark]\n\n6. Describe an experimental method, not involving a spirometer, that could be used by students to measure ventilation rate. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. independent variable: \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ effect of exercise **OR** at rest and exercise **OR** level of activity [1 mark]\n\n dependent variable: \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ tidal volume **OR** volume of air inhaled **OR** exhaled in one breath [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The independent variable is the effect **of exercise** ($\\checkmark$), and the dependent variable is the **volume of air** inhaled ($\\checkmark$) in the lungs.\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ measure the volume of air that moves into or out of the lungs during a normal breath, **OR** at rest [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The tidal volume can be measured by measuring the **volume of air** that flows **into the lungs** with a **normal breath** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ total volume in one minute / number of breaths in one minute **OR** 15.2 dm:sup[3] / 12 breaths [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 1.3 dm:sup[3] breath:sup[-1] [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Cell%20Specialization/images/BI1016-SLHLA.png\" alt=\"BI1016-SLHLA\">\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ total volume of air (calculated by adding together volume of each peak as indicated by the red marks on the graph above) dm:sup[3] / number of breaths per min\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ = 1.5 dm:sup[3] + 1.5 dm:sup[3] + 1.0 dm:sup[3] + 1.4 dm:sup[3] + 1.3 dm:sup[3] + 1.0 dm:sup[3] + 1.5 dm:sup[3] + 1.2 dm:sup[3] + 1.0 dm:sup[3] + 1.5 dm:sup[3] + 1.0 dm:sup[3] + 1.3 dm:sup[3]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ = **15.2 dm:sup[3] / 12 breaths** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ = **1.3** ($\\checkmark$) dm:sup[3] breath:sup[-1]\n\n4. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ greater **OR** increased, total volume, **OR** total volume of air inhaled **OR** exhaled, would be higher [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The tidal volume would be **greater** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ type 1 pneumocyte [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ **Type I pneumocytes** ($\\checkmark$) are specialised cells that allow gaseous exchange to take place.\n\n6. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ventilation rate is the number of breaths per given time **OR** per minute **OR** second [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ using a stopwatch [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ count the number of times air is breathed in **OR** breathed out through observation **OR** using differential pressure sensor **OR** inflatable chest belt around the thorax [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ measure **OR** repeat several times **OR** collect at least three sets of results **OR** calculate mean from several sets of results [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ any relevant controlled variable, such as using the same person to conduct the activity over a period of time **OR** sitting position **OR** breathing techniques are controlled that can affect the ventilation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ baseline measurement for comparison [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **ventilation rate** is the **number of breaths** over a **set period of time**, usually the number of **breaths per minute** ($\\checkmark$). This can be measured by **counting** the number of times air is **inhaled** and then **exhaled** in a **minute** at rest, through **observation** of **chest movements** ($\\checkmark$). A **stopwatch** is used to measure the time ($\\checkmark$). This is then **repeated** so that at least **three results** are recorded to **calculate the mean**. ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1057","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1057\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Describe how the use of stem cells may be used to treat type I diabetes in the future.     [5 marks]\n\n2. Explain the factors that limit cell size in humans.     [4 marks]\n\n3. Explain how red blood cells and striated muscle fibres are adapted to their function.    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ embryonic stem cells **OR** induced pluripotent stem cells **OR** iPSC used     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stem cells contain desired, **OR** insulin, allele     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stem cells differentiate to become pancreatic **OR** β-cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cells are implanted **OR** inserted into the pancreas    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ insulin produced in response to increase in blood glucose    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ immunity suppressing drugs used        [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Embryonic stem cells** ($\\checkmark$) with the **insulin gene** ($\\checkmark$) are stimulated to divide and **differentiate into β-cells** ($\\checkmark$) then **injected into the pancreas** ($\\checkmark$).  Implanted cells **produce insulin** ($\\checkmark$) in response to increasing blood glucose. **Immunosuppression drugs** ($\\checkmark$) may need to be taken if using embryonic stem cells.\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ surface area affects the rate at which materials enter and leave the cell     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ volume affects the rate at which materials are used and produced by the cell  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the surface area to volume ratio affects the rate of metabolism in a cell     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the bigger the cell, the smaller the SA to volume ratio is    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cells having a low SA to volume ratio cannot exchange materials fast enough\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some cells, **OR** motor neurons, **OR** muscle cells, **OR** other named example in humans, are long and thin, which maintains a high SA to volume ratio        [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **surface area** of a cell affects the **rate** at which **materials enter** and **leave** cells ($\\checkmark$). On the other hand, the cell’s **volume** affects the rate at which **materials** are **used and produced** ($\\checkmark$). A **small surface area to volume ratio** is not ideal for human cells because it **limits** the cell’s ability to **export waste from the cytoplasm** rapidly enough ($\\checkmark$) to meet the cytoplasm’s **metabolic needs** ($\\checkmark$), such as the availability of oxygen. A **large cell** has a **small surface area to volume ratio** ($\\checkmark$). Therefore, the transport of the substances is more efficient in small cells.\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    ***red blood cells:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ small and flexible to fit through narrow capillaries [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contain haemoglobin to bind to oxygen **OR** to carry oxygen [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ biconcave to increase surface area        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ no nucleus and lack other organelles to provide more space for haemoglobin    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Striated muscle fibres:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell membrane capable of electrical impulse **OR** action potential propagation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ long cells so produce movement when work together        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lots of mitochondria to supply ATP for contraction        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contain contractile proteins to bring about movement    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ multinucleate supports large size of cell **OR** aids in controlling protein synthesis **OR** repair sarcoplasmic reticulum to store and release calcium ions    [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Red blood cells** are adapted for **oxygen transport** ($\\checkmark$). They are **small** and **flexible** to squeeze through capillaries ($\\checkmark$) and have a **biconcave** shape to **increase** their **surface area** ($\\checkmark$). They **lack** a **nucleus** and most **other organelles** ($\\checkmark$) to make room for the **haemoglobin** which **binds**, and carries, **oxygen** ($\\checkmark$) around the body.\n\n    **Striated muscle fibres** are adapted for **voluntary movement** ($\\checkmark$). They contain **contractile proteins** ($\\checkmark$) and are **long** so when the fibres **contract together** they bring about significant **movement** ($\\checkmark$). They contain **many mitochondria** to provide **ATP** for contraction ($\\checkmark$) and also have **sarcoplasmic reticulum** which stores and releases **calcium ions** ($\\checkmark$) to control muscle contraction. The **many nuclei** are a result of the fusing of cells to produce the long fibres, and these **support** the large cell and **control protein production** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1058","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1058\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nBelow are diagrams of specialised cells (not to scale).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Cell%20Specialization/images/BI1058.png\" alt=\"BI1058\">\n\n1. Outline **one** functional reason for the difference in **size** between the human sperm and egg cell. [1 mark]\n\n2. Compare and contrast totipotent, pluripotent and multipotent stem cells. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. the following (must all be correct to award mark):\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the egg cell is larger because it contains nutrient reserves **OR** cytoplasm **OR** organelles to support early embryonic development after fertilisation [1 mark]\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the sperm cell is smaller to allow efficient swimming **OR** to reduce resistance **OR** reduce mass for movement towards the egg [1 mark]\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sperm cell is smaller because it contributes only DNA **OR** a haploid nucleus and does not need a large cytoplasm [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **egg** cell is much **larger** because it contains **nutrient reserves** needed to **support** the **early stages** of **embryonic development** after fertilisation ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **four** of the following (must include at least one similarity and one difference):\n\n **Similarities** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **ALL** three are undifferentiated **OR** are unspecialised cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ totipotent, pluripotent and multipotent stem cells are found in embryonic stages [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** pluripotent and multipotent stem cells have potential applications in regenerative medicine and research [1 mark]\n\n **Differences** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ totipotent stem cells can give rise to both embryonic and extraembryonic tissues, **WHILE** pluripotent stem cells can only differentiate into embryonic tissues [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **ONLY** multipotent stem cells are typically found in adult tissues [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ totipotent cells have the highest differentiation potential, followed by pluripotent cells, and then multipotent cells [1 mark]\n\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Totipotent, pluripotent and multipotent stem cells are all **undifferentiated** cells ($\\checkmark$). **All three** are found in **embryos** ($\\checkmark$), however, **only multipotent** stem cells are typically found in **adults** ($\\checkmark$). **Totipotent** stem cells can give rise to both embryonic and **extraembryonic** (e.g. placental) tissues, while **pluripotent** stem cells can **only** differentiate into **embryonic** tissues ($\\checkmark$). **Pluripotent** and **multipotent** stem cells have potential use in **regenerative medicine** and **research** ($\\checkmark$), but totipotent stem cells are not commonly used due to ethical considerations."},{"ref":"BI1135","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1135\n\nThe dimensions of a theoretical cube are doubled. By what factor would its volume increase?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 2\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 4\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 6\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 8","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIf x represents the unknown dimensions of the cube, then:\n\nVolume of cube = x:sup[3]\n\nDoubling the dimensions, the new volume would be:\n\nVolume of cube = (2x):sup[3 ]\n\n= 8x:sup[3]\n\nComparing the two volumes:\n\n8x:sup[3] / x:sup[3] = 8\n\nTherefore, the volume of the cube has increased by a factor of 8.\n\n**Choice A** would only be correct if one dimension of the cube were doubled.\n\n**Choice B** would only be correct if two dimensions of the cube were doubled.\n\n**Choice C** could be miscalculated using 2 $\\times$ 3 instead of 2:sup[3]."},{"ref":"BI1161","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1161\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Draw a labelled diagram of the fluid mosaic model of the plasma membrane of an animal cell.   [4 marks]\n\n2. Explain how type I and type II pneumocytes are adapted to their functions.    [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe how cell surface membranes regulate the movement of substances into and out of animal cells.     [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following, (drawing showing the following labels correctly marked on a clearly drawn diagram):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phospholipid bilayer with phosphate heads pointing outside and lipid tails pointing inwards        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ peripheral protein shown on the surface        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ integral protein shown within the phospholipid bilayer    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glycoprotein **OR** glycolipid shown with carbohydrate chain attached    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cholesterol in bilayer        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ protein channel across the bilayer    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Cell%20Specialization/images/BI1161.png\" alt=\"BI1161\">\n\n2. any **five** of the following (*must include at least **one** from each section to gain full marks*):\n\n    ***Type I pneumocytes*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ for gas exchange    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large surface area to maximise gas exchange        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ thin to reduce diffusion distance **OR** to reduce distance between capillaries and inside alveoli [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lack many organelles to reduce barriers for gas exchange     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tight junctions between adjacent cells to prevent fluid **OR** gas **OR** air leakage    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Type II pneumocytes*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ secrete **OR** produce surfactant **OR** a fluid to reduce surface tension in alveoli    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cuboidal to provide space for organelles **OR** numerous organelles **OR** secretory granules for production **OR** storage **OR** secretion of surfactant    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can differentiate into type 1 pneumocytes **OR** can regenerate type 1 cells [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n    **Type 1 pneumocytes** are **thin** so there is a **short diffusion distance** between the capillaries and the inside of the alveoli ($\\checkmark$). Their shape **increases** their **surface area**, and they have **fewer organelles** so there are **fewer barriers** to diffusion ($\\checkmark$). All these adaptations enable **rapid exchange of gases** ($\\checkmark$). The type 1 cells have **fewer organelles**, so there are fewer barriers, enabling rapid **exchange** of **gases** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    **Type II** pneumocytes secrete **surfactant** to reduce **surface tension** inside the alveoli ($\\checkmark$). **Numerous organelles** are found inside the type II cells to produce and store **surfactant** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    ***Passive transport*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ small molecules **OR** gases can move through the membrane **OR** pass across the bilayer by (simple) diffusion [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nonpolar **OR** lipid-soluble substances move through the membrane **OR** phospholipid bilayer by (simple) diffusion    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water molecules can pass across the membrane **OR** bilayer by osmosis    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ channel proteins allow facilitated diffusion of ions **OR** polar molecules **OR** water molecules **OR** water-soluble molecules **OR** named example of type of channel protein and molecules that pass through it        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gated proteins **OR** voltage-gated channels **OR** ligand-gated channels **OR** other suitable example control movement of substances due to changes in environment **OR** by specific signals    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ diffusion is the net movement of molecules down their concentration gradient **OR** from a higher concentration to a lower concentration, without use of energy **OR** is a passive process    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Active transport*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ protein pumps **OR** carrier proteins are involved in active transport of ions (but not channel proteins )        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ active transport of substances against a concentration gradient, which requires energy **OR** ATP                  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ indirect active transport **OR** secondary active transport when the movement of one substance against its concentration gradient is coupled to the movement of another substance down its concentration gradient         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ endocytosis is active transport of substances into cell by formation of vesicle from cell surface membrane                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phagocytosis is endocytosis of solid particles **AND** pinocytosis is endocytosis of dissolved substances **OR** liquid      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ exocytosis is the release of substances from the cell via vesicles fusing with the cell surface membrane                     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ different types of cells have different proteins in their cell membrane for transport of different substances                [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Nonpolar** ($\\checkmark$) and **small molecules**, such as **gases**, can pass across the phospholipid bilayer by **simple diffusion** ($\\checkmark$). **On** the other hand, polar molecules and ions tend to pass through **channel proteins** by **facilitated diffusion** ($\\checkmark$). These **channel proteins** may be **gated** to control when these substances pass through them, depending on the **environment** ($\\checkmark$). Whilst **diffusion** is a **passive process**, with the molecules passing **down** their **concentration gradient** ($\\checkmark$), some molecules need to be moved **against** their **concentration gradient** by **active transport**, which requires **energy** ($\\checkmark$). **Protein pumps** or **carrier proteins** embedded in the membrane are required for **active transport** ($\\checkmark$). Molecules may also **enter** the cell via **vesicles** pinched off from the cell membrane, a process known as **endocytosis** ($\\checkmark$). **Exocytosis** allows the **secretion** of substances **from cells**, when **vesicles** containing the substance **fuse with the cell membrane** and release their contents to the outside ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1222","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1222\n\nThe cubes represent models of unicellular organisms. Which cube has the lowest surface area to volume ratio?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Cell%20Specialization/images/BI1222.png\" alt=\"BI1222\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe surface area to volume ratio generally decreases as an organism or cuboid gets bigger unless the shapes of the cuboids are very different. Therefore, without calculation, the answer can be deduced. By calculation Surface area of C= 10mm $\\times$ 10mm $\\times$ 6mm = 600 mm:sup[2] and the volume of C = 10mm $\\times$ 10mm $\\times$ 10mm = 1000 mm:sup[3] . The surface area to volume ratio of C = 600 ÷ 1000, which is less than 1 (0.6). **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **D** are all incorrect as the SA:Vol of all three are greater than 1, and so greater than Choice C.\n\nSurface area of A = (2mm $\\times$ 2mm $\\times$ 2mm) + (4mm $\\times$ 2mm $\\times$ 4mm) = 40 mm:sup[2]. Volume of A = 2mm $\\times$ 4mm $\\times$ mm2 = 16 mm:sup[3]. Surface area to volume ratio of A = 40 ÷ 16 = 2.5\n\nSurface area of B = 2mm $\\times$ 2mm $\\times$ 6mm= 24 mm:sup[2]. Volume of B = 2mm $\\times$ 2mm $\\times$ 2mm = 8 mm:sup[3]. Surface area to volume ratio of B = 24 ÷ 8 = 3.\n\nSurface area of D = (4mm $\\times$ 4mm $\\times$ 2mm) + (1mm $\\times$ 4mm $\\times$ 4mm) = 48 mm:sup[2]. Volume of D = 1mm $\\times$ 4mm $\\times$ 4mm = 16 mm:sup[3]. Surface area to volume ratio of A = 48 ÷ 16 = 3."},{"ref":"BI1349","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1349\n\nHow can cells increase their surface area-to-volume ratio?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Flattening of cells |\n    | II. | Microvilli |\n    | III. | Invagination |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because **Statements I, II** and **III** are all true. The flattening of cells, the presence of microvilli and the invagination of the plasma membrane all increase the surface-area-to-volume ratio. **Choices A, B** and **C** are all incorrect because they do not include all statements."},{"ref":"BI1350","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1350\n\nWhich of these rows shows the correct functions of type I and type II pneumocytes?\n\n| | Type I pneumocytes | Type II pneumocytes |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Diffusion | Production of surfactants |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Prevent adhesion | Diffusion |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Production of surfactants | Active transport |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Active transport | Prevent adhesion |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because type I pneumocytes are flattened cells, meaning the diffusion distance for gaseous exchange is reduced, and type II pneumocytes produce surfactant to prevent adhesion of the alveolar walls. **Choice B** is incorrect because type I pneumocytes do not prevent adhesion, and the function of type II pneumocytes is not diffusion. **Choice C** is incorrect because type I pneumocytes do not produce surfactants. **Choice D** is incorrect because the function of type I pneumocytes is gas exchange, and gases are not exchanged by active transport."},{"ref":"BI1351","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1351\n\nWhich pair of characteristics are true for cardiac muscle cells?\n\n| | Type of fibres | Number of nuclei |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Branching fibres | Single nucleus |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Branching fibres | Many nuclei |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Parallel fibres | Single nucleus |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Parallel fibres | Many nuclei |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because cardiac muscle cells are composed of branched fibres with a single nucleus. **Choice B** is incorrect because cardiac muscle cells do not contain multiple nuclei. **Choice C** is incorrect because cardiac muscle cells are not composed of parallel fibres. **Choice D** is incorrect because cardiac muscle cells are not composed of parallel fibres and do not contain multiple nuclei."},{"ref":"BI1352","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1352\n\nWhich pair of characteristics are true for striated muscle cells?\n\n| | Type of fibres | Number of nuclei |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Branching fibres | Single nucleus |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Branching fibres | Many nuclei |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Parallel fibres | Single nucleus |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Parallel fibres | Many nuclei |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because striated muscle cells are composed of parallel fibres with many nuclei, sarcomeres and a sarcolemma. **Choice A** is incorrect because striated muscle cells are not composed of branching fibres and do not contain a single nucleus. **Choice B** is incorrect because striated muscle cells are not branched. **Choice C** is incorrect because a striated muscle cell contains more than one nucleus."},{"ref":"BI1353","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1353\n\nThe diagram below shows human gametes.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Cell%20Specialization/images/BI1353.png\" alt=\"BI1353\">\n\nWhich organelle is particularly abundant in the part labelled X?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ribosomes\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mitochondria\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Rough endoplasmic reticulum\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Nucleoids","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because the mid-piece of sperm cells contains a large number of mitochondria for the release of energy to enable the sperm cell to swim towards the egg. While ribosomes are present in sperm cells, they are not particularly abundant in the midpiece, which is primarily dedicated to providing energy for motility; **Choice A** is incorrect. The rough endoplasmic reticulum is involved in protein synthesis and is studded with ribosomes. It plays a role in the production of proteins within the cell but is not specifically abundant in the midpiece of sperm cells; **Choice C** is incorrect. **Choice D** is incorrect because sperm cells are not prokaryotic cells and so do not contain nucleoids."},{"ref":"BI1354","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1354\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe micrograph below shows a transverse section of an epithelial cell in the human intestine.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Cell%20Specialization/images/BI1354.png\" alt=\"BI1354\">\n[Source: Dartmouth Electron Microscope Facility, 2015. Microvilli.jpg. Wikimedia. Retrieved, September 14th 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Microvilli.jpg. Copyright under Public Domain]\n\n1.\n    1. Identify the adaptation that increases the surface area-to-volume ratio of the cells in the micrograph.        [1 mark]\n\n    2. State one other general adaptation of exchange surfaces, such as the small intestine, that increases the rate of simple diffusion.    [1 mark]\n\n    3. Outline how the surface area-to-volume ratio of a cell affects its ability to carry out its functions.        [2 marks]\n\n2. Explain how the adaptations of erythrocytes and sperm cells enable them to carry out their roles in the human body.    [4 marks]\n\n3. Identify two human body processes that require a high surface-area-to-volume ratio.\n[2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ microvilli        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    These cells have **microvilli** ($\\checkmark$), which increase the surface area.\n\n1.\n    2. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ short diffusion distance **OR** flattened cells **OR** wall only once cell thick        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ good blood supply **OR** many capillaries **OR** steep diffusion gradient **OR** counter-current mechanism            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ moist surface [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Exchange surfaces often have thin membranes of **flattened cells** that provide a **short diffusion distance** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    3. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large cell volume requires higher rate of exchange of substances **OR** need for exchange is increased with increased cell volume        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ higher surface area-to-volume ratio increases rate of exchange **OR** lower surface area-to-volume ratio decreases rate of exchange             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ciliated cells have a high surface area-to-volume ratio to give a large surface area for movement of mucous **OR** root hair cells have a high surface area-to-volume ratio for absorption of water **OR** type I pneumocytes in the alveoli are flattened to have a large surface area-to-volume ratio for gas exchange **OR** red blood cells have a biconcave shape which gives a large surface area-to-volume ratio for absorption and release of oxygen **OR** cells of the villi in the small intestine have a large surface area-to-volume ratio for absorption **OR** any other relevant example    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The surface area-to-volume ratio affects the need for the exchange of substances and the exchange rate. An increased cell volume **increases the need for exchange** ($\\checkmark$), but a lower surface area-to-volume ratio **decreases the rate of exchange** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following (must include **two** adaptations and explanations for **each** type of cell for full marks):\n\n    *Erythrocytes:* \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ erythrocytes do not have a nucleus **OR** lack organelles to provide maximum space for haemoglobin                        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ haemoglobin to bind with **OR** to carry oxygen        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ have a biconcave shape **OR** disc-shaped to increase surface area-to-volume ratio for gaseous exchange **OR** to increase diffusion rate        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ flexible to squeeze through capillaries            [1 mark]\n\n    *Sperm cells:* \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ flagellum **OR** tail to propel them forward **OR** allow them to swim to egg    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ midpiece has lots of mitochondria to provide energy for swimming to egg    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ acrosome contains enzymes to penetrate egg     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ streamlined shape to reduce drag **OR** friction **OR** to make it easier to swim to egg    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ small cells so can be produced in large quantities to increase chance of fertilisation **OR** to reach egg                        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ little cytoplasm **OR** few organelles **OR** no Golgi apparatus **OR** no ribosomes to reduce size **OR** weight to make it easier  for them to swim **OR** move    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ haploid nucleus **OR** half the normal number of chromosomes OR half the DNA for fertilisation of the egg        [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Erythrocytes are **biconcave** to increase their **surface area-to-volume ratio** for **gaseous exchange** ($\\checkmark$). They also **lack a nucleus** to provide **maximum space** for **haemoglobin** ($\\checkmark$) to be packed in; **haemoglobin** binds to **oxygen** to carry it from the lungs to the rest of the **body** ($\\checkmark$). Sperm have **flagella** to propel them forwards so they can **swim to the egg** to fertilise it ($\\checkmark$). They also have lots of **mitochondria** in their **midpiece** to provide **energy** for **swimming** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gas exchange **OR** diffusion of oxygen and carbon dioxide    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ absorption of nutrients                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ excretion of urea **OR** removal of wastes by kidney    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature regulation **OR** vasodilation **OR** vasoconstriction    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ movement of ions for transmission of nerve impulses        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell respiration **OR** aerobic respiration in the mitochondria **OR** oxidative phosphorylation on the inner membrane of the mitochondria        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ exchange of substances at the placenta **OR** exchange of substances between mother and foetus                        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **OR** any other relevant example        [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Increased surface area-to-volume ratio is important for the **absorption of nutrients** ($\\checkmark$) by villi and for **gas exchange** between alveoli and blood and body cells and blood ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1355","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1355\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast type I and type II pneumocytes.        [4 marks]\n\n2. Explain how human gametes are adapted for their reproductive roles.        [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain the properties and location of stem cells and distinguish between totipotent, pluripotent and multipotent stem cells.    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following: \\\nAnswer must include at least one similarity **and** one difference to receive full marks.\n\n    ***Similarities:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type I **AND** type II pneumocytes are **BOTH** found in the alveoli **OR** the lungs **OR** form part of the alveolar epithelium    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type I **AND** type II pneumocytes are **BOTH** adapted for their functions        [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type I pneumocytes have a flattened shape **WHEREAS** type II pneumocytes have a cuboidal **OR** rounded shape            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type I pneumocytes have minimal cytoplasm **OR** organelles **WHEREAS** type II pneumocytes have more cytoplasm **OR** organelles **OR** contain lamellar bodies\ntype I pneumocytes are adapted for diffusion of gases **WHEREAS** type II pneumocytes are not adapted for diffusion    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type II pneumocytes produce surfactants **WHEREAS** type I pneumocytes do not produce surfactants        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type I pneumocytes are approximately 95% of the surface of alveoli **WHEREAS** type II pneumocytes are approximately 5% of the surface of alveoli **OR** type I pneumocytes are the majority of the alveolar lining **WHEREAS** type II pneumocytes are only a small percentage of the alveolar lining            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type I pneumocytes have limited regenerative capacity **OR** are more vulnerable to damage **WHEREAS** type II pneumocytes can regenerate **OR** can differentiate into type I pneumocytes [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Type I and type II pneumocytes are **both found in the alveoli** ($\\checkmark$). Type **I pneumocytes are thin**, whereas **type II pneumocytes are cuboidal** ($\\checkmark$). This is because **type I** pneumocytes are adapted for **gaseous exchange** ($\\checkmark$), whereas **type II** pneumocytes are adapted to **produce surfactants** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** point for each cell type to gain full marks*):\n\n    ***Sperm cells:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ flagella **OR** tail for movement **OR** sperm cells can swim        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ acrosome containing enzymes for digesting the jelly coat of the egg **OR** for penetrating the egg                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large numbers of mitochondria for release of energy             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ haploid nucleus **OR** half the normal amount of genetic information for fertilisation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ small cells so can be produced in large quantities               [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ streamlined shape to reduce drag **OR** friction **OR** to make it easier to swim to egg    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ little cytoplasm **OR** few organelles OR no Golgi apparatus **OR** no ribosomes to reduce size **OR** weight to make it easier  for them to swim **OR** move        [1 mark]\n\n    ***Egg cells*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large as contain organelles **OR** resources **OR** nutrients  for early development of the zygote **OR** egg cells contain resources for early development of the embryo    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contain enzymes **OR** cortical granules which release enzymes to change the outer layers after fertilisation to prevent polyspermy                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ zona pellucida for sperm-egg recognition **OR** to prevent polyspermy    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ usually only one develops at a time to reduce risk of multiple fertilisations **OR** for more efficient use of resources                     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ haploid nucleus **OR** half the normal amount of genetic information for fertilisation    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Sperm cells have a **flagellum for movement** ($\\checkmark$), **enzymes in the acrosome** to digest the outer layers of the egg ($\\checkmark$) and **large numbers of mitochondria** in the midpiece for energy release for swimming ($\\checkmark$). Egg cells have **a large cytoplasm with the organelles** and **resources** for the early development of the zygote ($\\checkmark$).  **Both** sperm and egg cells have a **haploid nucleus** so that the normal **diploid number** of chromosomes is **restored at fertilisation** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following (*answer must include at least **two** points about properties and location of stem cells and at least **two** differences between totipotent, pluripotent and multipotent stem cells to gain full marks*):\n\n    ***Properties and locations of stem cells:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stem cells have the capacity to keep dividing            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stem cells have the ability to differentiate **OR** can differentiate into different types of cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stem cells are found in specific locations such as embryos **OR** umbilical cord **OR** stem cell niches [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adult stem cells are found in hair follicles **OR** bone marrow **OR** brain **OR** any relevant example of a location of a stem cell niche             [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences between totipotent, pluripotent and multipotent stem cells:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ totipotent and pluripotent stem cells are found in embryos **BUT** multipotent stem cells are found in adult tissues                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ totipotent stem cells are found in early-stage embryos **OR** totipotent stem cells can develop into both embryonic and extraembryonic cells  **WHEREAS** pluripotent cells are found in embryos at the blastocyst stage **OR** pluripotent cells can only differentiate into embryonic cells                     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ totipotent stem cells are stem cells with the greatest potential for differentiation **WHEREAS** pluripotent stem cells can differentiate into most types of cells OR totipotent stem cells can differentiate into any type of cell **WHEREAS** multipotent stem cells have the least capacity for differentiation              [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ multipotent stem cells can only develop into cells for the tissue where they are found **WHEREAS** totipotent and pluripotent stem cells can develop into cells for different tissues                            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ totipotent stem cells develop into pluripotent stem cells **BUT** multipotent stem cells do not develop into other types of stem cells    [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Stem cells have the capacity to **differentiate** ($\\checkmark$) and the **ability to continue dividing** ($\\checkmark$). Stem cells are found in **embryos and stem cell niches in adult tissue** ($\\checkmark$). Adult stem cells are found in stem cell niches such as **hair follicles and bone marrow** ($\\checkmark$). Totipotent and pluripotent stem cells are **found in embryos** but **multipotent** stem cells are found in **adult tissues** ($\\checkmark$). **Totipotent** cells are found in early-stage embryos and can develop into both **embryonic** and **extraembryonic cells**, but **pluripotent** cells are found in embryos at the **blastocyst stage** and can only differentiate into **embryonic** cells ($\\checkmark$).  **Pluripotent** and **totipotent** can develop into cells for **different tissues** but **multipotent** cells can only develop into **cells for the tissues where they are found** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1464","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1464\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe micrograph shows cardiac muscle tissue.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Cell%20Specialization/images/BI1464%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1464 fig1\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Goyitrina. (2007, January 22). File:Musculocardiaco.jpg. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved March 23, 2024 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Musculocardiaco.jpg. Copyright by CC 3.0]\n\n1.  Compare and contrast the **structure** of cardiac muscle cells and skeletal muscle cells.    [4 marks]\n\n2.  Within the right atrium wall is a group of cardiac cells with the ability to initiate an action potential. State the name of those groups of cells.    [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.  Any **four** of the following\\\n\n    *Answer must include at least **one** point from similarities and **one** from differences to gain full marks*\n\n    **Similarities**\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are striated    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have a similar arrangement of myosin **AND** actin    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contain many mitochondria    [1 mark]\n\n    **Differences**\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cardiac muscles are shorter and wider **THAN** skeletal muscle cells **OR** skeletal muscle cells are longer and narrower **THAN** cardiac muscle cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cardiac muscles can have one nucleus **OR** two nuclei per cell **WHEREAS** skeletal muscle cells are multinucleated    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cardiac muscles are branched **WHEREAS** skeletal muscles are unbranched    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ s there are gap junctions **OR** intercalated discs between cardiac muscle cells **WHEREAS** there are no gap junctions **OR** intercalated discs between skeletal muscle cells [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Both skeletal and cardiac muscle cells are **striated** ($\\checkmark$)  and contain **many mitochondria** ($\\checkmark$). However, cardiac muscle cells are **branched**, while skeletal muscle cells are **linear** ($\\checkmark$), and cardiac muscle cells only have **one or two nuclei per cell**, whereas skeletal muscle cells are **multinucleated** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.  Any **one** of the following\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sinoatrial **OR** SA node [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pacemaker [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    \n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    The **sinoatrial node**($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1548","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1548\n\n**Maximum mark: 1**\n\nWhat is the role of cell signalling gradients in early-stage embryonic development?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ They facilitate neuronal communication within the embryo.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ They control the rate of meiosis.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ They regulate gene expression.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ They ensure proper distribution of nutrients until organ systems begin to develop.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because cell signalling gradients modify gene expression in cells depending on where they are located in the developing embryo. Although all of the cells in an embryo have the same genome, the use of gradients and variable gene expression leads to differentiation and cell specialisation.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because early-stage embryos do not yet have neurons. **Choice B** is incorrect as early cell divisions are mitotic, not meiotic. **Choice D** is incorrect since at the embryonic stage, nutrient distribution is achieved by the concentration gradients of the nutrients themselves, not by cell signaling chemicals."},{"ref":"BI1549","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1549\n\nWhich of the following are features of stem cells?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | They have the capacity to divide continuously while remaining undifferentiated. |\n    | II. | They are present, in different states of potency, in all stages of an organism’s development. |\n    | III. | They are found in specialised cell niches such as the skin and bone marrow. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nStem cells have the ability to divide endlessly, some of which will remain undifferentiated in order to sustain the population of cells (**Statement I**). Although the degree of potency changes throughout life, with less flexibility as the organism ages, stem cells remain an integral part of tissue growth, maintenance, and repair in all stages of development (**Statement II**). Finally, in adult organisms, stem cells are located in stem cell niches, which contain different types of stem cells that can differentiate into appropriate specialised cells depending on the body region where they are found (**Statement III**). This includes stem cell populations located in the skin, hair follicles, and bone marrow, among others. Since all three statements are true, **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choices A, B**, and **C** are incorrect because they are missing one or more true statements."},{"ref":"BI1550","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1550\n\n**Maximum mark: 1**\n\nThe following diagram shows the sizes of human gametes (not drawn to scale).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Cell%20Specialization/images/BI1550A.png\" alt=\"BI1550A\">\n\nWhich of the following correctly describes how these cell sizes accommodate their functions?\n\n| | Oocyte | Spermatozoa |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Large volume to accommodate genetic material | Small volume to increase concentration of nutrients |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Large volume to provide nutrient reserves for developing embryo | Small volume to improve mobility |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Large surface area to improve chances of fertilisation | Small surface area to reduce heat loss |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Large surface area to improve chances of implantation | Small surface area to make fusion with oocyte more efficient |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct oocytes have a large volume to provide room for the nutrients and mitochondria required for embryonic development. This is particularly true for non-mammalian species that rely on external development following fertilisation. Spermatozoa are specialised for motility by having a streamlined, compact design with a small volume of cytoplasm, allowing faster and more efficient movement in order to reach the oocyte.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the oocyte does not need a large volume to contain the genetic material. The sperm cell contains the same amount of genetic material despite being far smaller. **Choice C** is incorrect because heat loss is not a significant concern for sperm cells. **Choice D** is incorrect since the size of the oocyte does not significantly impact the chances of implantation in the uterine wall."},{"ref":"BI1551","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1551\n\nThe micrograph image below shows simple cuboidal epithelial cells found in kidney tubules.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Cell%20Specialization/images/BI1551.png\" alt=\"BI1551\">\n[Source: Berkshire Community College Bioscience Image Library (2018, March 30). *File: Epithelial Tissues Simple Cuboidal Epithelium (41681552782).jpg. Wikimedia Commons*. Retrieved April 10, 2024, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Epithelial_Tissues_Simple_Cuboidal_Epithelium_(41681552782).jpg. Public domain]\n\nWhich graph depicts how the surface area to volume ratio of these cells changes as their dimensions increase?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A. | | $\\hspace{2cm}$ | |B.|\n| |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Cell%20Specialization/images/BI1551A.png\" alt=\"BI1551A\"> | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Cell%20Specialization/images/BI1551B.png\" alt=\"BI1551B\">|\n|C. | | | |D. |\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Cell%20Specialization/images/BI1551C.png\" alt=\"BI1551C\"> | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Cell%20Specialization/images/BI1551D.png\" alt=\"BI1551D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because for cuboidal cells, as side length increases, the volume increases at a greater rate than the surface area. This leads to a progressively lower surface area-to-volume ratio.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as the relationship between cell size and surface area to volume ratio is not linear. **Choice C** is incorrect as the relationship is neither linear nor positive. **Choice D** is incorrect since the surface area to volume ratio decreases rapidly rather than gradually as the dimensions of the cell increase."},{"ref":"BI1552","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1552\n\nWhich of the following correctly describes stem cell differentiation?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Totipotent stem cells can differentiate into any embryonic or adult cell type plus extraembryonic tissues such as the placenta, while multipotent stem cells can only differentiate into cell types within a specific lineage.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Multipotent stem cells can differentiate into any embryonic or adult cell type plus extraembryonic tissues such as the placenta, while pluripotent stem cells can only differentiate into cell types within a specific lineage.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Pluripotent stem cells can differentiate into any embryonic or adult cell type excluding extraembryonic tissues, while totipotent stem cells can only differentiate into cell types within a specific lineage.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Totipotent stem cells can differentiate into any embryonic or adult cell type plus extraembryonic tissues such as the placenta, while pluripotent stem cells can only differentiate into cell types within a specific lineage.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because totipotent stem cells, found in embryos, have the ability to differentiate into any cell type needed to form an entire organism, plus extraembryonic tissues such as the placenta. Pluripotent stem cells can become nearly any cell type in the organism except for extraembryonic tissues, whereas multipotent stem cells are limited to a particular cell lineage, such as blood cells produced by bone marrow stem cells.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect since multipotent stem cells are not able to become any cell type. **Choice C** is incorrect as pluripotent stem cells are not able to produce embryonic cells. Finally, **choice D** is incorrect since pluripotent cells are not limited to particular cell lineages."},{"ref":"BI1553","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1553\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA student wants to investigate how cell size affects the rate of diffusion. They carried out an experiment using alkaline agar cubes of different dimensions infused with pink phenolphthalein indicator, as shown in the diagram below. The phenolphthalein indicator becomes clear at pH values below 9.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Cell%20Specialization/images/BI1553.png\" alt=\"BI1553\">\n\nThree cubes of each size are submerged in an acetic acid solution and removed every two minutes so that the volume diffused can be calculated. The results of the experiment are summarised in the table below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Cell%20Specialization/images/Untitled%205.png\" alt=\"Untitled 5\">\n\n1. Explain how the student was able to determine the percentage volume diffused in this experiment. [3 marks]\n\n2. Evaluate, using the data, the student’s hypothesis that the rate of diffusion decreases linearly as cube side length increases. [2 marks]\n\n3. How do the results of this experiment relate to the functional limitations of cells based on their sizes? [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ phenolphthalein indicator turns clear **OR** changes colour **OR** goes from pink to clear when exposed to acetic acid **OR** ethanoic acid [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cubes become progressively clear as acetic acid diffuses in [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ dimensions of pink **OR** clear region can be measured with a ruler [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ volume is calculated by multiplying length, width, and height **OR** length cubed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ divide the volume of the diffusion zone by the total cube volume and multiply by 100% to express it as a percentage [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ repeat readings made **OR** outliers eliminated **AND** mean values calculated\n [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The student needs to **compare the dimensions of the diffused and undiffused portions** ($\\checkmark$) of each cube. This can be observed since **phenolphthalein changes** from **pink to clear** when **exposed to acetic acid** ($\\checkmark$). The pink undiffused region of each cube can be **measured with a ruler** ($\\checkmark$) and its **length cubed** to determine **volume** ($\\checkmark$). This value is then **subtracted from the total volume** ($\\checkmark$) in order to **calculate percentage diffusion**. Repeat readings allow outliers to be identified and eliminated so a **reliable mean can be calculated** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hypothesis rejected OR not supported [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ because The relationship is non-linear **OR** geometric **OR** exponential [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ doubling the dimensions of a cube results in a diffusion percentage **OR** rate of diffusion that is much less than expected for a linear decrease **OR** less than half [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ volume increases faster than surface area **OR** surface area to volume ratio decreases as cube size increases **OR** surface area is squared WHEREAS volume is cubed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ resulting in much slower rates of diffusion for larger cubes [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n As cube size increases, the **rate of diffusion decreases more than expected** ($\\checkmark$) based on the dimensional differences. For example, the 4 cm cube, despite being only twice as wide as the 2 cm cube, has a rate of diffusion and percentage volume diffusion **much less than half**. This would indicate that the student’s **hypothesis would not be supported** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ there is a size limitation on cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ because diffusion is less effective **OR** slower as cell size **OR** volume increases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ because they require nutrients **OR** gas exchange **OR** removal of waste products **OR** the efficient movement of substances into and out of the cytoplasm to survive [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ because surface area increases more slowly than volume **OR** volume increases more quickly than surface area **OR** surface area to volume ratio decreases as cells grow larger [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Cells are **unable to grow beyond a certain size** ($\\checkmark$) because **diffusion would be insufficient** ($\\checkmark$) to allow them to **receive** the **nutrients** they **require** to **survive** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1555","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1555\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe diagram shows a stem cell niche within the bone marrow of a human femur.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Cell%20Specialization/images/bone%20marrow%20femur%20haematopoietic%20stem%20cells%20ii%20BioRender.png\" alt=\"bone marrow femur haematopoietic stem cells ii BioRender\">\n\n1.\n 1. Name **two** types of specialised cells that could be produced from bone marrow stem cells. [2 marks]\n\n 2. State **one** other stem cell niche within the human body. [1 mark]\n\n 3. Outline the function of stem cell niches. [2 marks]\n\n2. Compare and contrast between adult and embryonic stem cells. [2 marks]\n\n3. Discuss why stem cell research may be beneficial to humans. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ leucocytes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ macrophages **OR** phagocytic cells **OR** monocytes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ granulocytes **OR** neutrophils **OR** eosinophils **OR** basophils [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ dendritic cells **OR** antigen-presenting cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ red blood cells **OR** erythrocytes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ platelets **OR** thrombocytes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ lymphocytes **OR** B cells **OR** T cells [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Macrophages**($\\checkmark$) and **erythrocytes** ($\\checkmark$) are examples of specialised cells that could be produced from hematopoietic stem cells.\n\n1.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hair follicles [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ skin epidermis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ testes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ intestinal epithelium [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ brain [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ liver [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ blood vessel walls [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cornea [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ dental pulp [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ striated OR skeletal muscle [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ lung [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Hair follicles** ($\\checkmark$) are another example of a stem cell niche.\n\n1.\n 3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ provides an environment in which stem cells can be kept in an inactive **OR** dormant **OR** undifferentiated state [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ regulation of stem cell self-renewal **OR** regulates the frequency and duration of stem cell division **OR** regulates stem cell proliferation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ directs the differentiation of stem cells into specialised cell types as needed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ support growth **OR** maintenance **OR** repair of tissues **OR** promotes tissue regeneration in response to injury **OR** damage [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ protection of stem cells from damage **OR** environmental stress [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n A stem cell niche provides an **environment** where **stem cells** can be m**aintained** in an **undifferentiated** state($\\checkmark$) and **regulates** their **self-renewal** ($\\checkmark$) ensuring the proper **balance** of stem cell **division** and **differentiation** into **specialised cell** types **as needed** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following (answer must contain at least one similarity and one difference in order to receive full marks):\n\n *Similarities:* \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** adult and embryonic stem cells can differentiate into multiple types of specialised cells [1 mark]\n\n *Differences:* \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ embryonic stem cells can divide indefinitely under correct conditions **WHEREAS** adult stem cells usually have a limited capacity for self-renewal [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ embryonic stem cells are pluripotent (stem cells of inner mass) **OR** totipotent (in zygote / early embryo up to 8-cell stage) **OR** capable of becoming any adult cell type **WHEREAS** adult stem cells are multipotent **OR** capable of becoming a limited number of cell types within a specific lineage [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ embryonic stem cells are found throughout the developing embryo **WHEREAS** adult stem cells are found in various stem cell niches throughout the body [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Both adult and embryonic stem cells have the ability to **differentiate into multiple cell types** ($\\checkmark$) as needed. However, embryonic stem cells are more versatile since they are usually **pluripotent** or able to **differentiate into nearly any cell type** ($\\checkmark$), whereas adult stem cells are generally **multipotent** or limited to a **smaller number of possible cell types** ($\\checkmark$) within a particular **cell lineage**.\n\n3. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ studying how organisms develop may provide evidence for evolutionary relationships **OR** help us understand developmental abnormalities [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ provides insights into the fundamental processes of embryonic development **OR** cellular differentiation **OR** enhances our understanding of developmental pathways [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can grow cells **OR** tissues **OR** organs in vitro **OR** in the laboratory, reducing the need for human **OR** animal test subjects [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ proliferate endlessly to provide a consistent source of cells for testing and research [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ due to their ability to differentiate, they could be used to replace damaged tissues **OR** treat currently incurable diseases **OR** degenerative diseases **OR** disorders **OR** injuries **OR** used for regenerating damaged **OR** diseased tissues **OR** organs **OR** in regenerative medicine [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can be used to model human diseases in vitro **OR** studying disease mechanisms **OR** identifying potential drug targets **OR** screening drugs for safety and efficacy [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can reduce tissue and organ rejection since a person’s own stem cells can be used to generate tissues **OR** organs for transplantation, reducing the risk of rejection [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$stem cell-based therapies have the potential to be tailored to individual patients **OR** used to develop personalised medicine [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Stem cells are of great potential value to scientists for a number of reasons. Firstly, they have the potential to **grow and divide endlessly**, providing a **reliable supply** of cells for **research** ($\\checkmark$). They can also be used to **grow tissues** and perhaps **organs in vitro**, which could one day **replace tissue and organ donation** ($\\checkmark$). Finally, stem cells could be used to **treat a variety of diseases** due to their **ability to differentiate** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1630","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1630\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe karyogram shows a karyotype of a healthy human foetus.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Cell%20Specialization/images/BI1630%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1630 fig1\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from National Cancer Institute (2013, January 21). File: Karyotype (normal). jpg. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved April 28, 2024. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karyotype_(normal).jpg. Copyright free]\n\n1.  Deduce, with a reason, the biological sex of the foetus.    [1 mark]\n\n2.  The biological father of the foetus has haemophilia, and the biological mother is a carrier of the disease. Using a Punnett square, calculate the probability that the foetus has haemophilia.    [3 marks]\n\n3.  Haemophilia occurs in approximately 1 in 6000 biological males and 1 in 100,000 biological females. Suggest a reason for this difference.    [1 mark]\n\n4.  Much research has been conducted into the use of stem cells to treat genetic diseases. Outline the advantages and disadvantages of the use of adult stem cells over embryonic ones in stem cell therapy.    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  any **one** of the following\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ biologically male **BECAUSE** they have a Y chromosome **OR** they have 22 pairs of homologous chromosomes **AND** 1 pair of non-homologous sex chromosomes        [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n    The foetus is biologically **male** because they have a **Y chromosome** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.  any **three** of the following\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ parental genotypes identified in the Punnett square; biological father: $\\ce{X^hY}$ **AND** biological mother: $\\ce{X^HX^h}$    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ offspring genotypes identified as $\\ce{X^HY, X^hY}$    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 0.50 **OR** 50% **OR** $\\frac{1}{2}$    [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Cell%20Specialization/images/1630.png\" alt=\"1630\">\n\n    The biological sex of the foetus is male, so it must inherit the Y chromosome from its biological father. The probability of a biologically male foetus having haemophilia is **50 %** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.  any **one** of the following\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it is a sex-linked recessive disease, **OR** the gene is carried on the X chromosome **AND** biological males only have one X chromosome, so if they have the recessive allele, they will have the disease, whereas biological females with one copy would be carriers    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ for a biological female to have haemophilia, they would need to have two copies of the recessive allele, whereas biological males only need one copy of the recessive allele to have haemophilia    [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n    Haemophilia is more common in biological males because it is a **sex-linked disease** on the **X chromosome**. **Biological males** have only **one X chromosome**, so inheriting the **recessive allele results** in the **disease**, while **biological females** with **two X chromosomes** can be **carriers without symptoms** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.  any **three** of the following (*at least* ***one*** *advantage and* ***one*** *disadvantage must be given to gain full marks*)\\\n\n    ***advantages:***\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adult stem cells can be obtained from the individual needing treatment, reducing the risk of immune rejection **WHILE** embryonic stem cells may be rejected by the patient’s immune system [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ harvesting adult stem cells is generally less controversial **OR** has fewer ethical implications compared to embryonic stem cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adult stem cells have a lower risk of producing tumours compared to embryonic stem cells  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adult stem cell therapies generally face fewer regulatory hurdles and greater public acceptance compared to therapies involving embryonic stem cells    [1 mark]\n\n    ***disadvantages:***\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adult stem cells are multipotent **OR** are often restricted in the number of cell types they can produce **WHILE** embryonic stem cells are pluripotent **OR** can differentiate into any bpdy cell type [1 mark]    \n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adult stem cells are limited in number within adult tissues **OR** can be challenging to obtain **OR** culture **WHILE** embryonic stem cells are more numerous OR easier to harvest    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adult stem cells have less potential for long-term proliferation **OR** durability in culture **COMPARED TO** embryonic stem cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adult stem cells are derived from fully developed tissues so typically more differentiated, **WHEREAS** embryonic stem cells are derived from a very early developmental stage so less differentiated    [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n    One **advantage** of using adult stem cells in therapy is that they can be obtained from the patient, **reducing the risk of immune rejection** ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, harvesting adult stem cells is **less controversial** and has **fewer ethical implications** ($\\checkmark$) compared to embryonic stem cells. However, adult stem cells are **multipotent** and **limited** in the **types of cells**($\\checkmark$)  they can produce, unlike pluripotent embryonic stem cells. Moreover, adult stem cells are **less numerous** and **harder to harvest** ($\\checkmark$) than embryonic stem cells."},{"ref":"BI1714","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1714\n\nHaematopoietic stem cells are found in the bone marrow. Which row in the table below is true of haematopoetic stem cells?\n\n| | $\\text{Pluripotent}$ | $\\text{Multipotent}$ |\n\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Yes | No |\n\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Yes | Yes |\n\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | No | Yes |\n\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | No | No |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nHaematopoietic stem cells (HSCs) are multipotent, meaning they can differentiate into multiple, but limited, cell types. Specifically, they can give rise to all the different types of blood cells, including red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **D** are all incorrect as haematopoietic stem cells are multipotent, but nor pluripotent."},{"ref":"BI2328","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2328\n\nWhich process is controlled by gradients of signalling chemicals in an embryo?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Growth of unspecialised cells in early-stage embryos.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Growth of unspecialised cells in late-stage embryos.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Differentiation of unspecialised cells in early-stage embryos.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Differentiation of unspecialised cells in late-stage foetuses.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because gradients of signalling chemicals in the early-stage embryo determine the differentiation of cells into different cell and tissue types. Cell positions in the embryo determine the gradient and, therefore, determine the pathways in which cells will differentiate.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because signalling chemicals primarily determine cell differentiation, not growth.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because signalling chemicals determine cell differentiation in early-stage embryos, not cell growth in late-stage embryos.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because chemical signalling controls differentiation in early-stage embryos, not late-stage foetuses."},{"ref":"BI2384","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2384\n\nThe diagram shows a simplified view of a human ovum (egg cell).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Cell%20Specialization/images/BI2384.png\" alt=\"BI2384\">\n\nWhich structures play a role in preventing polyspermy?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and IV only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ III and IV only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, III and IV","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nPolyspermy occurs when more than one sperm enters the egg cell, which stops fertilisation from taking place. A viable zygote would not be formed in such circumstances.\n\n**Structure I** is false. It is the egg’s nucleus. This is haploid as the egg is a gamete and only carries half of the mother’s genome. It plays no direct role in preventing polyspermy.\n\n**Structure II** is true. It is the zona pellucida, which has multiple roles, one of which is to prevent a second and subsequent sperm from entering the ovum after the first sperm has gained access. \\\n**Structure III** is false. It is a mitochondrion, an important organelle in many cell types where energy is released from aerobic respiration. Mitochondria do not play a direct role in preventing polyspermy.\n\n**Structure IV** is true. It is a cortical granule. These are vesicles that secrete their contents once a sperm has contacted the egg, and the contents modify the environment outside the cell so that further sperm cannot reach the egg.\n\n**Choice A** is correct because it contains the two true structures that prevent polyspermy (**II** and **IV**).\n\n**Choices B, C** and **D** are incorrect because they contain a combination of true and false structures."},{"ref":"BI2603","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2603\n\nThe micrograph shows the proximal convoluted tubule in the kidney. Which letter labels a structure that is an adaptation for exchange?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Cell%20Specialization/images/BI2603.png\" alt=\"BI2603\">\n[Source: Adapted from Othman, A. I., Abdel-Ghaffar, A., & Mahmoud, A. M. (2019, May 20). *Ketorolac- and warfarin-induced renal toxicity: Ultrastructural and biochemical study - the journal of Basic and Applied Zoology*. SpringerOpen. Retrieved February 22, 2025, from https://basicandappliedzoology.springeropen.com/articles/10.1186/s41936-019-0106-2 Copyright under CC BY 4.0]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** points to the brush border of microvilli, which greatly increases the surface area of the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) cells, facilitating efficient reabsorption of substances from the filtrate in the nephron; **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect, as it points to a mitochondrion which produces ATP during aerobic respiration, providing the energy needed for many processes in the nephron. **Choice B** is incorrect, as it points to the nucleus, which contains the genetic information and controls the activities of the cell. **Choice D** is incorrect, as it points to the lumen of the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) which is not an adaptation for exchange itself. The lumen is simply the central space through which filtrate flows."},{"ref":"BI2605","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2605\n\nWhich of the following best describes the role of surfactant in the alveoli?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Breaks down excess mucus in the alveoli, helping the airways to remain clear\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increases surface tension, preventing overexpansion during inhalation\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Maintains osmotic pressure in the alveoli, ensuring that pneumocytes maintain their shape\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Decreases surface tension, preventing alveolar collapse during exhalation","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSurfactant is a detergent secreted by Type 2 pneumocytes in the alveoli. The function of surfactant is to reduce surface tension in the alveoli so that the alveoli do not stick together or collapse during exhalation; **Choice D** is correct.\n\nSurfactant does not break up excess mucus in the alveoli. Mucus is swept away from the airways by ciliated cells in the airways; **Choice A** is incorrect. Surfactant does not increase surface tension; **Choice B** is incorrect. Surfactant’s function is not related to osmotic pressure regulation; **Choice C**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI2607","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2607\n\nWhich feature is **not** found in both skeletal and cardiac muscle cells?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Many mitochondria\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Striated appearance\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Branched cell structure\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sarcoplasmic reticulum","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCardiac muscle cells have a branched cell structure, but skeletal muscle cells do not; **Choice C** is correct.\n\nBoth skeletal and cardiac muscle cells contain many mitochondria to generate ATP for muscle contraction; **Choice A** is incorrect. Both skeletal and cardiac muscle cells have a striated appearance due to the arrangement of actin and myosin filaments; **Choice B** is incorrect. Both skeletal and cardiac muscle cells contain specialised endoplasmic reticulum called sarcoplasmic reticulum which stores and releases calcium ions for contraction; **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI2610","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2610\n\nWhich row of the table shows the correct functions of structures found in and around a mature secondary oocyte?\n\n| | Structure | Function |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Zona pellucida | Prevents polyspermy |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Zona pellucida | Nourishes and protects the oocyte |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Cortical granules | Contains microvilli to absorb nutrients |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Cortical granules | Contains enzymes which break the head of the sperm during fertilisation |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe zona pellucida is a jelly coat found around the oocyte. When a sperm reaches the egg, it binds to the zona pellucida, which triggers the acrosome reaction in the sperm cell. This allows the sperm membrane to fuse with the oocyte membrane and the nucleus of the sperm cell to enter for fertilisation to take place. Cortical granules release enzymes after fertilisation that modify the zona pellucida to prevent the entry of additional sperm and, so, prevent polyspermy. **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect as it is the role of follicle cells to nourish and protect the oocyte. **Choices C** and **D** are incorrect as the role of the cortical granules is to prevent polyspermy during fertilisation."},{"ref":"BI2855","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2855\n\nWhich of the following best explains why striated muscle fibres are significantly larger than many other human cells?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ They are specialised for contraction and require large volumes of cytoplasm to accommodate myofibrils.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ They contain multiple nuclei, allowing rapid oxygen exchange with surrounding capillaries.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ They must produce large volumes of calcium ions, requiring more cytoplasm.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ They undergo continuous mitosis, increasing their size beyond normal somatic cell limits.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct. Striated muscle fibres are extremely large and multinucleated because they are specialised for contraction. They need a large cytoplasmic volume to accommodate contractile proteins (myofibrils) and support their high metabolic demands.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because multiple nuclei support localised protein synthesis, not oxygen exchange. Oxygen delivery is primarily the role of red blood cells. \\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because muscle fibres store and release calcium ions for contraction, but they do not produce them. Calcium ions are stored primarily in the sarcoplasmic reticulum, and their presence does not explain the cell’s large size.\\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because striated muscle fibres do not undergo mitosis. In fact, mature muscle fibres are post-mitotic and arise from the fusion of many precursor cells (myoblasts), which contributes to their large size and multinucleation"}]},{"name":"Ecological Niches","questions":[{"ref":"BI0104","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0104\n\nSome fungi obtain nutrients by releasing digestive enzymes into their surroundings.  The enzymes break down things such as dead and decaying leaves and the fungi then absorb the products. Which of these terms best describes these fungi?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Autotrophic detritivores.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Autotrophic saprotrophs.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Heterotrophic detritivores.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Heterotrophic saprotrophs.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAutotrophs are producers (**Choice A, B**).  They are self-feeding whereas heterotrophs are organisms that gain nutrition by feeding on another organisms (**Choice C, D**). Detritivores consume dead organic matter by ingestion (**Choice C**) whereas saprotrophs  break down food outside their bodies by secreting enzymes into the environment."},{"ref":"BI0127","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0127\n\nWhich of the following is NOT a habitat for methanogenic archaeans?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Deep sea waters\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Stomachs of cows\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Well aerated loamy soils\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Swamps","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nMethanogenic archaea require anaerobic (oxygen-free) environments to survive, so well-aerated loamy soils are unsuitable habitats because they contain oxygen. This makes **Choice C** the correct answer.\n\nHabitats for methanogenic archaea include deep-sea waters  (**Choice A**), the stomachs of cows and other ruminants  (**Choice B**), and swampy areas that are deficient in oxygen (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0547","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0547\n\nChytrids are unicellular organisms that feed on pollen through extracellular secretion of digestive enzymes and absorption of the breakdown products. Which statement describes the nutritional mode of this organism?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Autotrophic producer\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Autotrophic consumer\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Heterotrophic detritivore\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Heterotrophic saprotroph","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe process of secreting digestive enzymes outside of an organism and absorbing the breakdown products is called external digestion. An organism that breaks down organic materials, such as pollen, by external digestion, is a saprotroph. All saprotrophs are heterotrophs (organisms that cannot fix carbon to produce their own food, but instead must consume sources of fixed organic carbon). So, chytrids are described as heterotrophic saprotrophs.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because an autotrophic producer synthesises its own food by fixing inorganic carbon (such as from carbon dioxide), which chytrids cannot do.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because an autotroph synthesises its own food, which chytrids do not do.  Furthermore autotrophs, by definition, are producers not consumers.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because detritivores use internal digestion, not external, to break down food."},{"ref":"BI0926","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0926\n\nWhich of the following is/are true for holozoic nutrition?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Demonstrated by saprotrophs |\n    | II. | Involves complete or partial ingestion |\n    | III. | Food is digested internally |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nHolozoic nutrition refers to the type of nutrition where organisms ingest whole or partially whole food items (**Statement II**). The ingested food is then broken down by the organism's internal digestive system (**Statement III**). Therefore **Choice B**  is correct.\nSaprotrophs (**Statement I**) are organisms that obtain their nutrients by decomposing dead and decaying organic matter externally and then absorbing the nutrients. Therefore **Choices A**, **C** and **D** are not correct as they all include **Statement I**."},{"ref":"BI0927","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0927\n\nWhich term refers to organisms that are both autotrophic and heterotrophic?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mixotrophic\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Multitrophic\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Saprotrophic\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Facultrophic","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because mixotrophic refers to both autotrophic and heterotrophic organisms.\n\n**Choices B** and **D** are incorrect because multitrophic refers to food webs or ecosystems involving multiple trophic levels, where organisms interact at different levels of the food chain, and facultrophic is not a standard scientific term. **Choice C** is incorrect because saprotrophic organisms are solely heterotrophic and obtain their nutrition by decomposing dead organic matter externally and absorbing the nutrients."},{"ref":"BI0928","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0928\n\nWhat is the purpose of incisors?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ripping and tearing food\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Biting pieces of food\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Crushing or slicing food\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Grinding food","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because incisors are used for biting pieces of food. **Choice A** is incorrect because tearing is the role of canines. **Choices C** and **D** are incorrect because crushing or slicing is the role of the premolars, and grinding is the role of molars."},{"ref":"BI0929","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0929\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe image below represents the skull of a gorilla, chimpanzee, and human.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Ecological%20Niches/images/BI0929.png\" alt=\"BI0929\">\n[Adapted from Source: Zhang, K. (2015). *File: The Skulls of the gorilla, chimpanzee and humans - Kaiwei Zhang.jpg*\n.Wikimedia commons. Retrieved July 30, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Skulls_of_the_gorilla,chimpanzee_and_humans_-_Kaiwei_Zhang.jpg.  Copyright under CC BY-SA 3.0\n]\n\n1.\n    1. Outline the roles of different types of teeth.    [3 marks]\n\n    2. Gorillas are primarily herbivorous but have large canines. Suggest a reason why.      [1 mark]\n\n    3. Compare and contrast the teeth of a carnivore with a herbivore.     [2 marks]\n\n2. In terms of diet, distinguish between a folivore and a frugivore. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ incisors are used for biting chunks of food                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ canines are used for tearing food                         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ premolars **OR** molars are used for slicing and crushing food [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ molars are used for grinding food                         [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Incisors are found at the front of the mouth and are used for **biting chunks** of food  ($\\checkmark$). Canines are located at the side of the mouth and are used for **tearing** food  ($\\checkmark$). Molars are found at the back of the mouth and are used for **grinding** food into a paste ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    2.\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ for display **OR** to appear intimidating to rivals **OR** to scare intruders **OR** to fight    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Gorillas have pronounced canines, possibly to scare away or **intimidate rivals** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    3. Any **two** of the following: (must include a similarity and a difference):\n\n    **Similarities**\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have the same types of teeth **OR** have incisors, canines, premolars and molars     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** use incisors for tearing chunks    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** use premolars for slicing or crushing food     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** use molars for grinding food                     [1 mark]\n\n    **Differences**\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ canines are more pronounced in carnivores **WHEREAS**, in most herbivores, canines are either small or absent         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carnivores have long, pointed canines designed for stabbing and piercing their prey, **WHEREAS** herbivores do not primarily rely on killing or tearing apart prey.     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ premolars **OR** molars are flatter/greater surface area in herbivores **WHEREAS** carnivores have sharper premolars and molars / less surface area.            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carnivores have premolars and molars that help slice through flesh and break bone, **WHEREAS** herbivores have premolars and molars with rough surfaces to break down tough plant cell walls and extract nutrients from vegetation [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ herbivores have a greater number of premolars and molars  [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Both** carnivores and herbivores have the **same types of teeth** ($\\checkmark$), but the canines are often **larger** and more pronounced in carnivores, whereas in **herbivores**, they are usually **small or absent** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ folivores eat mostly leaves **OR** foliage, **WHEREAS** frugivores eat mostly fruits    [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Frugivores are organisms that eat mostly **fruit**, but folivores eat mostly foliage like **leaves** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0950","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0950\n\nIdentify the row in the table which gives the correct information about the different types of organisms.\n\n| | Obligate anaerobes | Facultative anaerobes | Obligate aerobes |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | No tolerance to oxygen | Can carry out anaerobic and aerobic respiration | Require oxygen for respiration |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Can carry out anaerobic and aerobic respiration | Cannot survive in conditions low in oxygen | Require oxygen for respiration |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Cannot survive in conditions low in oxygen | Can respire when oxygen is present | Can carry out anaerobic and aerobic respiration |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Can respire when oxygen is present | Can carry out anaerobic and aerobic respiration | Cannot survive in conditions low in oxygen |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nObligate anaerobes are single-celled organisms that will not survive if oxygen is present. Facultative anaerobes are organisms that can either respire aerobically or anaerobically. Obligate aerobes can only respire if oxygen is present and cannot survive in conditions low in oxygen. Therefore **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choices B** and **D** are incorrect because obligate anaerobes cannot respire when oxygen is present. **Choice C** is incorrect as obligate anaerobes require anoxic conditions, and obligate aerobes cannot carry out anaerobic respiration."},{"ref":"BI0951","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0951\n\nWhich of the following is not a saprotroph?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mushroom\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Yeast\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ *Bacillus subtilis*\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Woodlouse or pill bug","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSaprotrophs are decomposers that secrete digestive enzymes onto organic molecules and then absorb the digested nutrients. This is a form of extra-cellular nutrition. Fungi and certain bacteria are examples of saprotrophs. Woodlice or pill bugs are detritivores. Detritivores are decomposers, but they ingest the organic molecules before digesting them into smaller, soluble ones.  **Choice D** is, therefore, correct.\nMushrooms and yeast are fungi, and *Bacillus subtilis* is a bacterium. Therefore **Choices A, B** and **C** are all saprotrophs and therefore are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1114","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1114\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the differences between mixotrophic and holozoic nutrition.  [4 marks]\n\n2. Describe how herbivores are adapted to eating plants. [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain the differences between obligate anaerobes, facultative anaerobes and obligate aerobes.    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ holozoic nutrition is a form of heterotrophic nutrition    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ holozoic nutrition involves ingesting an organism **OR** part of an organism, **OR** digesting an organism **OR** part of an organism,  internally **OR** energy source is ingestion of organism **OR** part of an organism        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mixotrophs are autotrophic and heterotrophic    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy **OR** nutrient source is sunlight **OR** photosynthesis **AND** ingestion of organisms **OR** parts of organisms  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mixotrophs can use whichever nutrient or energy source is most readily available **OR** require both forms of nutrition    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Holozoic nutrition** is a form of **heterotrophic** nutrition ($\\checkmark$). Organisms that obtain their food in this way are consumers and obtain their nutrients by **ingesting other organisms** ($\\checkmark$). **Mixotrophs** are both **autotrophs and heterotrophs** ($\\checkmark$). They obtain their nutrients by **photosynthesis** or by **ingesting** other organisms ($\\checkmark$). One type of mixotroph uses sunlight energy when it is available to make their own food, or if there is **little sunlight** energy available, they can **consume their food** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some are ruminants **OR** have fermentation chambers **OR** rumens in digestive system    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ long digestive systems **OR** large caecum for breakdown of cellulose **OR** plant material    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ coprophagy **OR** eat own faeces to re-digest plant material **OR** cellulose    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ back teeth **OR** molars **OR** premolars of herbivores are broad **OR** flat for grinding    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ bacteria **OR** microorganisms in digestive system help digest cellulose **OR** plant material    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tough tongue **OR** lips **OR** mouth to survive prickles **OR** thorns **OR** stings    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ modified mouth parts **OR** stylet to pierce phloem **OR** stem to obtain sap    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mandibles  of insects to cut leaves **OR** plant material        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ long necks **OR** legs to reach branches    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ detoxification mechanisms **OR** enzymes to remove plant toxins        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ specialised digestive enzymes to digest cellulose **OR** lignin **OR** cell walls    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Herbivores have many adaptations that allow them to consume and extract nutrients from plant material. Some possess **specialised teeth**, particularly **molars** and **premolars**, which are **flat** for **grinding** and **crushing** plant tissue ($\\checkmark$), as opposed to the sharp teeth used for tearing. Herbivores often have **complex digestive systems**, complete with **fermentation chambers** like the **rumen** in **ruminants** ($\\checkmark$). Within these chambers, **symbiotic microorganisms** help **break down** plant matter ($\\checkmark$), such as cellulose, in plant cell walls. Insects have evolved physiological adaptations such as the **stylets** of aphids that **pierce** the **phloem** to **extract sap** ($\\checkmark$) and the **mandibles** of the grasshopper, which can **cut through leaves** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **seven** of the following (at least two mark points needed for each type):\n\n    ***Obligate anaerobes*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ microorganisms **OR** unicellular organisms **OR** single-celled organisms that cannot survive or grow in the presence of oxygen    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen is toxic to them        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ obtain their energy through anaerobic respiration or fermentation processes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *Clostridium botulinum* **OR** *Clostridium tetani* **OR** other suitable example    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Facultative anaerobes*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ microorganisms OR unicellular organisms, **OR** single-celled organisms that can survive and grow in the presence or absence of oxygen    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can switch between aerobic respiration (using oxygen if available) and anaerobic respiration or fermentation (when oxygen is scarce)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *Escherichia coli* **OR** *Staphylococcus aureus* **OR** yeast **OR** other suitable example    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Obligate aerobes*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms that require oxygen for their growth and survival        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rely on aerobic respiration    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *Mycobacterium tuberculosis* **OR** *Pseudomonas aeruginosa* **OR** fish **OR** humans **OR** other suitable example    [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Microorganisms can be classified based on their oxygen requirements and how they produce ATP. First, there are **obligate anaerobes**, like ***Clostridium botulinum*** ($\\checkmark$), which **cannot survive** in the presence of **oxygen** ($\\checkmark$). They rely on **anaerobic respiration** ($\\checkmark$), or fermentation, for the production of ATP. Then, there are **facultative anaerobes**, such as ***Saccharomyces cerevisiae*** ($\\checkmark$), which are flexible. They can use **both aerobic and anaerobic** ($\\checkmark$) respiration depending on the available oxygen levels. Finally, **obligate aerobes** like ***Mycobacterium tuberculosis*** ($\\checkmark$) **cannot survive** for long **without oxygen** ($\\checkmark$) and primarily use **aerobic respiration** ($\\checkmark$) to release energy from organic compounds."},{"ref":"BI1198","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1198\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe African bush elephant, *Loxodonta africana*, pushes over trees, maintaining grasslands that provide essential resources such as food for many other animals. Its dung also fertilises the soil and provides a habitat for many species of beetle and fungi. The following diagram shows the flow of inorganic nutrients from elephants to herbivores within this ecosystem.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Ecological%20Niches/images/BI1198-SL.png\" alt=\"BI1198-SL\">\n\n1. Define the term ecosystem. [1 mark]\n\n2. Identify one abiotic factor and one biotic factor, other than food availability, affected by the presence of African bush elephants. [2 marks]\n\n3. The faeces buried by the dung beetle is broken down by decomposers. Outline how the action of decomposers increases soil nutrient content. [3 marks]\n\n4. Suggest one soil element, other than carbon, that would probably experience a reduction in concentration if dung beetles were absent from the ecosystem. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ all living organisms **OR** different living species, along with their physical environment **OR** abiotic factors [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n An ecosystem is **all** the **living organisms**, along with their **physical environment**, that **interact** and function together in a specific habitat ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **one** abiotic factor and any **one** biotic factor:\n\n ***abiotic factors:*** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increased soil nutrient content [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increased light intensity at ground level (due to reduced tree canopy) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ water availability (as a result of the digging of water holes) [1 mark]\n\n ***biotic factors:*** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increased abundance of beetles **OR** fungi **OR** decomposers **OR** soil microbes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ decreased abundance of trees **OR** increased area of grassland [1 mark]\n\n *Note: if a direction of change is given, it must be correct. Do not accept “increased abundance of trees”. Do not accept “vegetation” unless it is clear whether trees or grass is meant.*\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n African bush elephants push over trees, which increases light intensity at ground level, an **abiotic factor** ($\\checkmark$), and decreases the abundance of **trees**, a **biotic factor** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ decomposers break down organic compounds present in faeces using enzymes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ releases inorganic nutrients into the soil [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ saprotrophs break down organic compounds outside their bodies using enzymes, and some of the inorganic nutrients released remain in the soil [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ detritivores consume organic compounds present in faeces, digest them internally and release **OR** excrete **OR** egest inorganic nutrients into the soil [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Decomposers increase soil nutrient content by **breaking down organic compounds** present in faeces using **enzymes** ($\\checkmark$). The breakdown of these organic compounds **releases inorganic nutrients** into the soil ($\\checkmark$). For example, **saprotrophs** break down organic compounds **externally** using enzymes, and much of the resulting nutrients released **remain in the soil** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nitrogen **OR** phosphorus **OR** sulphur **OR** potassium **OR** calcium **OR** magnesium **OR** any other correct, named element [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Nitrogen** content in the soil would decrease if dung beetles were removed from the ecosystem ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1530","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1530\n\nWhich of the following is likely to result from competition between two species with the same ecological niche?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1.6em}$ Both species may compromise their fundamental niche in order to arrive at different realised niches\n\n- II. $\\hspace{1.3em}$ The population size of the less competitive species may decrease\n\n- III. $\\hspace{1em}$ The less competitive species may rapidly evolve to outcompete the more competitive species\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation:}}$\\\n\nCompetition between two species with the same ecological niche can result in competitive exclusion.\n\nThe principle of competitive exclusion states that no two species can occupy the same niche. If they share the same niche or resources at the same place and at the same time, then the species that is unable to compete successfully will die out (**Statement II**), or migrate, which may result in the two populations becoming geographically isolated or both species may compromise their fundamental niche, in order to arrive at different realised niches (**Statement I**). These outcomes would result in the two species not being in direct competition. **Choice C** is correct.\n\nEvolution is often a gradual process, therefore the less competitive species will most likely **not** evolve in time to outcompete the more competitive species, **Statement III**  is therefore not likely to happen. **Choice A**  is incorrect as it only includes **Statement I**. **Choices B** and **D**  are incorrect as they both include **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI1597","marks":"5","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1597\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n A university student decided to mimic the experiments conducted by the ecologist Joseph Connell to investigate the competition between two species of barnacles (*Sp. A* and *Sp. B*) living in intertidal zones. They specifically wanted to see if one species could outcompete the other for space. The diagram below shows where *Sp. A* and *Sp. B* live, *Sp. A* on the upper intertidal zone and *Sp. B* on the lower intertidal zone.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Ecological%20Niches/images/BI1597.png\" alt=\"BI1597\">\n\nThe student transferred 3 rocks containing *Sp. A* from the upper intertidal zone to the lower intertidal zone and fastened them down, as shown in the diagram on the left below. They then allowed *Sp. B* to colonise these rocks.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Ecological%20Niches/images/Joseph%20Connell%20colonisation%20by%20barnacles%20BI1597i.png\" alt=\"Joseph Connell colonisation by barnacles BI1597i\">\n\nAfter the colonisation period was over, the student removed *Sp. B* with a needle from one half of each of the 3 rocks that had been fastened down and left the other half undisturbed, as shown in the right-hand diagram above.\n\nThe student then monitored the percentage mortality of *Sp. A* on each half of these rocks for a year; the results are shown in the table below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Ecological%20Niches/images/BI1597T.png\" alt=\"BI1597T\">\n[Data adapted from original data obtained by the scientist]\n\n1. Deduce what effect *Sp. B* had on *Sp. A*. [1 mark]\n\n2. Outline the realised niches for the two species of barnacles. [2 marks]\n\nIn a second experiment, during unusually warm weather, the survival of both species was monitored at the margin where they coexisted. When there was no water for several days, mature *Sp. B* experienced a 50% mortality rate, while mature *Sp. A* suffered only about 2%.\n\n3. Using all the data, explain why both species can sometimes coexist in the same tidal zone. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **one** of the following :\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ *Sp. B* causes an increased mortality of *Sp. A* **OR** *Sp. B* has a negative impact on the survival of *Sp. A* [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ *Sp. B* outcompetes *Sp. A* in the lower intertidal zone [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n ***Sp. B*** caused an **increase** in the **death** of ***Sp. A***, as shown in the data ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. **Two** of the following :\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the realised niche for *Sp. A* is the upper intertidal zone [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the realised niche for *Sp. B* is the lower intertidal zone [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **realised niche** for ***Sp. A*** is the **upper intertidal** zone ($\\checkmark$), and for *Sp. B*, it is the **lower intertidal** zone ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **two** of the following :\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at the margins of the lower and upper intertidal zone, when there was a drought *Sp. A* showed lower mortality than *Sp. B* [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ *Sp. B* is not as resistant to desiccation as, **OR** requires more constant moisture than, *Sp. A* therefore can not survive on the upper rocks of the intertidal zone [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the intertidal zone environment varies allowing both species to exploit different microhabitats reducing direct competition and allowing coexistence [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fluctuating environmental conditions **OR** changes in temperature **OR** water exposure can give one species a temporary advantage preventing one species from completely excluding the other [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Based on the second experiment, *Sp. A*'s **higher resilience** to **lack of water**, as evidenced by its significantly **lower mortality rate** during **dry conditions** ($\\checkmark$), allows it to survive in the upper intertidal zone where exposure to air and the risk of desiccation are greater. In contrast, ***Sp. B's*** **higher mortality rate** in the **absence of water** indicates it **requires more constant moisture**, confining it to the **lower intertidal zone** ($\\checkmark$), where it is more frequently submerged."},{"ref":"BI1634","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1634\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe Sakhalin fir (*Abies sachalinensis*) and Ezo spruce (*Picea jezoensis*) are two of the most dominant conifers found throughout the northern Japanese island of Hokkaido. A quadrat survey was conducted to determine if there was an association between these two species. The results of the survey can be found in the table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Ecological%20Niches/images/Tab1.png\" alt=\"Tab1\">\n\n1.\n    1. Using the chi-square formula $χ^2 = \\sum \\dfrac{(O-E)^2}{E}$, calculate the $χ^2$ value for the data in the table, where O is the observed number of quadrats and E is the expected number of quadrats containing each combination of species.    [2 marks]\n\n    2. Using the table below, determine whether the observed and expected values differ significantly and explain whether or not an association exists between *A. sachalinensis* and *P. jezoensis*.                         [3 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Ecological%20Niches/images/Tab2.png\" alt=\"Tab2\">\n\n2. Both *A. sachalinensis* and *P. jezoensis* germinate on mossy fallen logs in the shady understorey of coniferous forests. Suggest **one** explanation for how these two species may be able to coexist in the same ecosystem.        [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. any **two** of the following (*answer must include correct final chi-square value to receive full marks*):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ correctly calculated expected values **OR** table of expected values            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chi-square formula calculations performed correctly for each of the four conditions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ $χ^2$ = 0.41 (accept values between 0.40 and 0.42)    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Expected values = (row total x column total) / grand total\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Ecological%20Niches/images/AnsTab1.png\" alt=\"AnsTab1\">\n\n    $χ^2 = \\sum \\dfrac{(O-E)^2}{E}$ = **0.07 + 0.10 + 0.10 + 0.14** ($\\checkmark$) = **0.41** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n    2. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ critical value for one degree of freedom at 5% level **OR** 0.05 significance is 3.84    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ since calculated chi-square value **OR**  0.41 is less than 3.84, the difference between observed and expected values is not statistically significant                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fail to **OR** cannot reject null hypothesis **OR** alternative hypothesis rejected    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ so no association between species **OR** *A. sachalinensis* and *P. jezoensis* **OR** observed differences in quadrat counts can be attributed to chance    [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Ecological%20Niches/images/1634aii.png\" alt=\"1634aii\">\n\n    The **calculated chi-square value** of **0.41** is **less than** the **critical value** of **3.84** at the **5% significance level** ($\\checkmark$). This indicates that there is **no significant difference** between the **observed** and **expected** values ($\\checkmark$), leading us to **fail to reject** the **null hypothesis** ($\\checkmark$). Therefore, it is **likely** that there is **no association** between ***A. sachalinensis*** and ***P. jezoensis*** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a mutualistic **OR** commensalistic relationship may exist **OR** minimal interspecific competition between the two species    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the two species occupy slightly different ecological niches        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  one tree species may prevent growth of other plants without affecting second tree species, thereby reducing competition for the two species        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the two species have different growth **OR** maturation times **OR** nutrient requirements **OR** light tolerance **OR** root depth **OR** any other reasonable physiological difference that would allow both species to coexist    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Despite being similar, these two species may be able to coexist because they have **different niches** ($\\checkmark$) and **do not compete** directly for **resources** such as **nutrients** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2331","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2331\n\nWhich statement is correct for adaptations of herbivores for feeding on plants?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Chewing mouthparts help insects to prevent damage from spines and thorns.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Chewing mouthparts allow insects to directly access vascular tissues.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Piercing-sucking mouthparts allow insects to feed directly on cell contents.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Piercing-sucking mouthparts allow direct feeding from the phloem sap.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because piercing-sucking mouthparts, such as stylets of aphids, are adapted to penetrate plant tissues and access phloem vessels directly. The phloem sap flows into the insect's digestive system without the need for further processing.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because chewing mouthparts, such as mandibles, are designed to physically cut and chew leaves but do not prevent damage from plant defences like spines or thorns.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because chewing mouthparts do not access vascular tissues such as the phloem; they are for consuming plant material at the surface.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because piercing-sucking mouthparts are adapted for feeding on phloem sap, not individual cell contents. Feeding on cell contents would require different adaptations, such as enzymes, to break cells open."},{"ref":"BI2827","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2827\n\nWhich of the following statements about holozoic nutrition is/are true?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | It occurs inside food vacuoles of all animals and involves the hydrolysis of macromolecules. |\n    | II. | Absorbed nutrients are transported to cells where assimilation occurs. |\n    | III. | All animals must ingest organic compounds for ATP synthesis. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is false. Food vacuoles are used for digestion in unicellular organisms (e.g., amoeba), but in multicellular animals, enzymatic hydrolysis occurs in the gut lumen or digestive tract, not inside vacuoles.\n\n**Statement II** is true. After digestion, nutrients are absorbed and transported via the circulatory system to cells, where they are used in assimilation.\n\n**Statement III** is true. As heterotrophs, all animals must ingest organic compounds to produce ATP through cell respiration.\n\n**Choice B** is correct as it includes only the true **Statements II** and **III**.        \\\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are incorrect as they all include the false **Statement I**."},{"ref":"BI2828","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2828\n\nThe data below shows the growth (in micrometres) of two protist species over 24 hours. Glucose was added to the medium *only* in the dark conditions.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Ecological%20Niches/images/BI2828.png\" alt=\"BI2828\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species X is a facultative mixotroph\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species Y is an obligate mixotroph\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species X is an obligate autotroph\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species Y is a facultative autotroph","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct. Species X grows in both the light and in the dark with glucose. This suggests it can switch between autotrophic nutrition via photosynthesis in light and heterotrophic nutrition in the dark with glucose (using external organic carbon). Thus, Species X is a facultative mixotroph (it can switch between both forms of nutrition depending on the environment).\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect. Species Y grows in the dark with glucose but shows no growth in the light when no glucose is present. This means it must be heterotrophic, relying entirely on external organic carbon, here in the form of glucose. Species Y is not a mixotroph since it doesn’t photosynthesise, so it does not use autotrophic nutrition.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect. Species X is not an obligate autotroph, because it also grows in the dark with glucose. An obligate autotroph would only grow in the light conditions.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect. Species Y shows growth in the dark with glucose, but it cannot grow in light, meaning it is heterotrophic and does not perform photosynthesis, so it cannot be called a facultative autotroph."},{"ref":"BI2829","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2829\n\nWhich condition would most strongly inhibit saprotrophic nutrition?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Low temperature and low oxygen availability\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Presence of antibacterial compounds secreted by the saprobiont\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Anaerobic conditions and high nitrogen availability\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Moderate warmth and access to cellulose-rich material","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct. Saprotrophic organisms like fungi and bacteria rely on enzymes to break down organic matter. Low temperatures reduce enzyme activity, and low oxygen limits aerobic respiration, which many saprotrophs depend on. Together, these conditions strongly inhibit decomposition.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect. Some saprotrophs produce antibacterial compounds to inhibit other microbes. These substances reduce competition but do not harm the saprotrophs that secrete them.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because although anaerobic conditions inhibit aerobic saprotrophs, some anaerobic bacteria can still function, and the presence of nitrogen may actually promote microbial growth.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because moderate temperatures support optimal enzyme activity, and cellulose provides a plentiful substrate for fungi and bacteria to decompose."},{"ref":"BI2830","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2830\n\nThe table below shows characteristics of nutrition for four unidentified microorganisms.\n\nWhich organism is **least** likely to be an archaeon?\n\n| | Light used as an energy source | Organic compounds used as a carbon source | Inorganic chemicals used as an energy source | Carbon dioxide used as a carbon source |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | No | Yes | No | No |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | No | No | Yes | Yes |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Yes | Yes | No | No |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Yes | No | No | Yes |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nArchaea are highly diverse in how they obtain energy for ATP production. Some obtain energy from organic compounds, others from inorganic chemicals, and some can use light. However, known light-using archaea do not use light energy to fix carbon dioxide by photosynthesis.\n\n**Choice D** is least likely to be an archaeon because the combination of light as the energy source and carbon dioxide as the carbon source indicates photoautotrophic nutrition. This is found among photosynthetic bacteria and eukaryotes but is not known among archaea.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because some archaea use organic compounds as sources of both energy and carbon.    \\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because some archaea obtain energy by oxidising inorganic chemicals and use carbon dioxide as their carbon source.    \\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because some archaea can use light to generate ATP while obtaining carbon from organic compounds. Therefore, the use of light does not by itself make an organism unlikely to be an archaeon."},{"ref":"BI2831","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2831\n\nWhich of the following statements illustrates coevolution between a plant and a herbivore?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Milkweed plants produce glycosides toxic to most insects and thus evolve resistance to herbivory. |\n    | II. | Milkweed plants produce glycosides toxic to most insects, and monarch caterpillars feed on milkweed, sequestering its toxins to deter predators. |\n    | III. | Aphids pierce phloem using stylets to avoid plant defences in leaves. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is false in the context of coevolution because it only describes a plant adaptation. There is no mention of an evolutionary response by herbivores.\\\n**Statement II** is true because it shows a reciprocal interaction: the plant produces toxins (a defence), and the monarch caterpillar evolves the ability to tolerate and sequester them. This is a classic coevolutionary dynamic.\\\n**Statement III** is false as it describes only a herbivore adaptation (aphid mouthparts) without evidence of a corresponding evolutionary response in the plant.\n\n**Choice A** is correct because only **Statement II** meets the definition of coevolution.\\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because **Statement I** does not show coevolution.\\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because neither **Statement I** nor **III** meets the full coevolution criteria.\\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because only **Statement II** qualifies."},{"ref":"BI2832","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2832\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe diagram below shows a trophic cascade in a forest ecosystem. The right side illustrates a food chain including wolves (apex predators), deer (primary consumers) and forest plants (producers). The left side shows the same system after the removal of the apex predator.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Ecological%20Niches/images/BI2827%20-%20BI2832%20B4.2%20MCQs%20and%20SA.jpeg.JPEG\" alt=\"BI2827 - BI2832 B4.2 MCQs and SA.jpeg\">\n\n[Source: adapted from Wikicommons contributors  (2020, October 15) *File: Trophic Cascade 1.svg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved May 12, 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=File:Trophic_Cascade_1.svg&oldid=490831526, CC-BY-3.0]\n\n1.\n    1. Using the diagram and your knowledge, describe one adaptation of the wolf that makes it an effective predator and one adaptation of the deer that helps it avoid predation.    [2 marks]\n\n    2. Use the diagram and your knowledge to explain the importance of wolves in this ecosystem.   [2 marks]\n\n2. Trees dominate the landscape in this forest ecosystem. Outline the strategies other plant species can use to ensure they can harvest light [3 marks]\n\n3. Explain whether the wolves being reintroduced into this habitat would occupy their fundamental or realised niche.  [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. any **two** of the following (*maximum **one** from each section*):\n\n        *Wolf adaptations:*\\\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ Wolves have forward-facing eyes, which provide binocular vision for accurately judging distance when hunting prey [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ strong jaw and sharp canines allow efficient killing and consumption of prey     [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ long limbs and muscular build enable endurance and speed for chasing prey [1 mark]\n\n        *Deer adaptations:*\\\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ deer have eyes on the sides of their head, giving a wide field of view to detect predators early   [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ long limbs and powerful muscles enable fast sprinting to escape predators   [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ keen senses such as hearing and smell help detect wolves at a distance  [1 mark]\n\n        [2 marks]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n        Wolves have **forward-facing eyes**, which provide **binocular vision** for accurately **judging distance** when hunting prey ($\\checkmark$). Deer have eyes on the **sides** of their head, giving a **wide field of view to detect predators early** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    2. any **two** of the following:\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ wolves reduce deer populations through predation  [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ predation by wolves keeps deer numbers from increasing above carrying capacity   [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ fewer deer leads to less grazing pressure on the forest vegetation  [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ this allows plant species in the deciduous forest to regenerate **OR** less damage to plants     [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ **THEREFORE** increased population size of other primary consumers  [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ **THEREFORE** increased population size of some secondary and tertiary consumers    [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ biodiversity increases   [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ removal of wolves can destabilise the ecosystem and reduce forest health  [1 mark]\n\n        [2 marks]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n        Predation by wolves keeps deer numbers from **increasing above carrying capacity** ($\\checkmark$). This means **less damage to plants** through overgrazing ($\\checkmark$). **Biodiversity increases** at all trophic levels ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lianas climb up other trees to reach sunlight without needing thick supportive trunks   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ epiphytes grow on high branches to access more light   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ shade-tolerant plants on the forest floor have large, flat leaves to maximise light absorption [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ strangler figs begin life in the canopy and grow downward to eventually dominate light access   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fast-growing plants reach the canopy quickly to access more light   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ any other valid adaptation that improves access to light, such as leaf orientation **OR** high chlorophyll density   [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Shade-tolerant** plants on the forest floor have **large, flat leaves** to maximise light absorption ($\\checkmark$). **Epiphytes** grow on tree branches **high up** near the tops of trees ($\\checkmark$) **Lianas climb up other trees** to reach sunlight, without needing **thick trunks** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.  any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ wolves' fundamental niche includes all potential roles and environments they could occupy in the absence of limiting factors like humans or competitors   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ their realised niche is what they actually occupy when biotic and abiotic pressures are considered [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reintroduction into natural habitats shows a return to part of their realised niche, as human presence, prey availability, and other species still affect their role    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it is not their full fundamental niche, as environmental and ecological **OR** biotic and abiotic constraints remain present   [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **fundamental niche of** wolves includes **all potential roles and environments they could occupy** in the absence of **limiting factors**. Their **realised niche** is what they **actually occupy** when **biotic** and **abiotic pressures** are present ($\\checkmark$). Reintroduction into forests is a return to **some parts of their realised niche** ($\\checkmark$) but it is **not** their **full fundamental niche**, as **biotic** and **abiotic** constraints remain present ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"​​BI1356","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# ​​BI1356\n\n\\[Maximum marks: 16\\]\n\n1. Distinguish between obligate aerobes, obligate anaerobes, and facultative anaerobes. [3 marks]\n\n2. Describe holozoic nutrition in animals. [5 marks]\n\n3. Using named examples, outline the relationship between dentition, musculature, and diet in herbivorous and omnivorous members of the family *Hominidae*. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. \\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ obligate aerobes require oxygen for respiration **OR** respire aerobically **OR** carry out aerobic respiration, **OR** require oxygen to grow **OR** survive, **OR** rely on aerobic respiration        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ obligate anaerobes do not require oxygen for respiration **OR** respire anaerobically **OR** respire in the absence of oxygen, **OR** are poisoned by oxygen-rich environments, **OR** cannot survive **OR** grow in the presence of oxygen [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ facultative aerobes can respire aerobically or anaerobically **OR** can respire in the presence or absence of oxygen, **OR** can survive **OR** grow in the presence or absence of oxygen [1 mark] \n\n[3 marks]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\n\nObligate aerobes **require oxygen** to respire and grow ($\\checkmark$), whereas obligate anaerobes do **not require oxygen** to respire ($\\checkmark$) and are, in fact, poisoned by oxygen due to a lack of defence mechanisms against oxidation. Facultative aerobes can respire **with or without oxygen** ($\\checkmark$), they have the ability to respire aerobically and anaerobically.\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    \n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ food is ingested            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ food is digested internally [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ food is absorbed [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ food is assimilated [1 mark] \n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ undigested **OR** indigestible food is egested [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ herbivores **OR** primary consumers feed on plant material     [1 mark] \n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carnivores **OR** omnivores **OR** secondary consumers **OR** any other trophic level above secondary consumers feed on animal material     [1 mark] \n\n[5 marks]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\n\nHolozoic nutrition refers to a mode of nutrition in which an organism **ingests** ($\\checkmark$) the food and then **digests it internally** ($\\checkmark$). After digestion, the food is **absorbed** ($\\checkmark$) and then **assimilated** ($\\checkmark$). **Herbivores** consume plant **material**($\\checkmark$), and **carnivores** consume **animal material**($\\checkmark$). \n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ herbivorous *Hominids* consume plants **AND** omnivorous hominids eat plants and animals        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ example of herbivorous *Hominids* e.g. gorilla (*Gorrila gorrila*) \n\n    [1 mark] \n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ herbivorous *Hominids* have large masseter muscles to grind food     \n\n[1 mark] \n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ herbivorous *Hominids* have large temporal muscles that allow for forcible bites     \n\n[1 mark]             \n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ herbivorous *Hominids* have larger **OR** broader premolars **OR** molars for grinding plant matter \n\n[1 mark]      \n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ herbivorous Hominids can use their incisors to bite chunks of vegetation \n\n[1 mark] \n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ herbivorous and omnivorous Hominids can have large canines for social interactions (e.g. fighting or ritual displays)\n\n[1 mark] \n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ example of omnivorous *Hominidae* e.g. chimpanzee\n\n[1 mark] \n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ omnivorous *Hominids* have relatively smaller masseter muscles\n\n[1 mark] \n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ omnivorous *Hominids* have relatively smaller temporal muscles \n\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ omnivorous *Hominids* can use canines to grab and tear prey     \n\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ omnivorous *Hominids* can use incisors to bite chunks from prey     \n\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ omnivorous *Hominids* have relatively smaller premolars **OR** molars \n\n[1 mark]\n\n[8 marks]\n\n :u[Sample answer]:\n\n**Herbivorous Hominids** such as the **gorilla** ($\\checkmark$) have **large masseter** muscles ($\\checkmark$) and **molars** to help them **grind vegetation** ($\\checkmark$). They also have **large temporal** muscles, which allow them to **bite down** on chunks of vegetation with force ($\\checkmark$). **Omnivorous** *Hominids*, such as **chimpanzees** ($\\checkmark$), have relatively **smaller** **masseter** muscles ($\\checkmark$) and **molars** ($\\checkmark$) compared to gorillas. Unlike gorillas, chimpanzees can use their **canines** to **hold prey** or **tear flesh** ($\\checkmark$). They can also use their **incisors** to **bite chunks** out of their prey ($\\checkmark$)."}]},{"name":"Gas Exchange","questions":[{"ref":"BI0613","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0613\n\nVentilation is important in maintaining the concentration gradients of oxygen and carbon dioxide between the air in the alveoli and blood flowing in adjacent capillaries.\n\nWhich of the following shows the **correct** concentration gradients when the diffusion of gases is taking place?\n\n| | Diffusion of oxygen into the blood | Diffusion of carbon dioxide into the alveoli |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| Higher oxygen concentration in alveoli | Lower carbon dioxide concentration in the alveoli |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Higher oxygen concentration in alveoli | Lower carbon dioxide concentration in the blood |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Lower oxygen concentration in the blood | Higher carbon dioxide concentration in the alveoli |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Lower oxygen concentration in the alveoli | Higher carbon dioxide concentration in the blood |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nFor oxygen to diffuse into the blood, there must be a higher concentration of oxygen in the alveoli than in the blood (**Choice D**).  This is because gases only diffuse down their concentration gradients.\n\nSimilarly, for carbon dioxide to diffuse into the alveoli, there should be a lower concentration of carbon dioxide in the alveoli (**Choice C**) and a higher concentration of carbon dioxide in the blood (**Choice B**)."},{"ref":"BI0614","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0614\n\nAir is carried to the lungs by entering through the nostrils into the nasal cavity, and then as shown in the pathway below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Gas%20Exchange/images/BI0614.png\" alt=\"BI0614\">\n\nWhich of the following statements are correct descriptions of structures **P** and **Q**?\n\n| | Characteristic of P | Characteristic of Q |\n| :-- | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| Large number of air sacs and a large total surface area | Wall containing C-shaped rings of cartilage |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Cells that secrete a surfactant | Muscles that relax to form a domed shape |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Muscles that relax to form a domed shape | Cells that secrete a surfactant |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Wall containing C-shaped rings of cartilage | Large number of air sacs and a large total surface area |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAir enters the nasal cavity and then passes down the trachea (**P**). The trachea’s wall consists of C shaped rings of cartilage. Air that passes down the trachea first enters the bronchi, then bronchioles and finally reaches the alveoli (**Q**). A characteristic of alveoli is the presence of a large number of air sacs and the presence of cells that secrete a surfactant.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect answer options are switched; characteristic P is referring to alveoli, not the trachea and characteristic of Q is referring to the trachea, not alveoli.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because cells that secrete a surfactant is referring to alveoli rather than trachea; and muscles that relax to form a dome is referring to the diaphragm, not alveoli.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because muscles that relax to form a dome is referring to the diaphragm, not the trachea."},{"ref":"BI0615","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0615\n\nThe photograph below shows lungs that have undergone excessive breakdown of alveoli by protein-digesting enzymes (part on the left) compared to normal lung tissue (on the right).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Gas%20Exchange/images/BI0615.png\" alt=\"BI0615\">\n[Source: Rosen, Y. (2010, August). *File: Bullous emphysema (4858473626).jpg*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved Jun 8, 2022, from https ://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Bullous_emphysema_%284858473626%29.jpg\n Copyright under CC-BY-SA-2.0]\n\nHow would this lung condition affect gaseous exchange?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Reduced surface area\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Reduced ventilation rate\n\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Reduced affinity of haemoglobin to oxygen\n\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Increased density of blood vessels at the alveoli","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThis condition, typically associated with emphysema, involves the destruction of alveoli, which greatly reduces the surface area available for gaseous exchange. A smaller surface area limits the diffusion of oxygen and carbon dioxide between the alveoli and blood, impairing the efficiency of gas exchange. **Choice A** is, therefore, the correct answer.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the breakdown of alveoli primarily affects gas exchange, not the rate of ventilation. Ventilation rate depends on factors like muscle function and neural control, which are not directly impacted by alveolar destruction.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because the affinity of haemoglobin for oxygen is not affected by the physical condition of the alveoli. Instead, it is influenced by factors like pH (Bohr effect), temperature, and levels of carbon dioxide. While less oxygen is available due to the reduced surface area, haemoglobin's intrinsic affinity remains unchanged.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the destruction of alveoli does not increase the density of blood vessels. In fact, the opposite may occur, where reduced blood flow and damaged capillaries further compromise gas exchange."},{"ref":"BI0616","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0616\n\nDuring ventilation, air from the atmosphere enters the lungs. Lungs consist of alveoli which lack muscular tissue. The lungs are attached to the ribcage through the pleural membranes which are filled with a fluid with a constant volume.\n\nWhat would be the most likely consequence if there was a puncture in the ribcage exposing the lungs?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Walls of the trachea would stick together\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Intercostal muscles and diaphragm would not function\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Pressure inside the thorax would be similar to atmospheric pressure\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Uncontrolled cell division in the lungs resulting in a tumour","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe inner pleural membrane covers the surface of the lungs, the outer membrane lines the inner surface of the ribs and diaphragm. Pleural fluid between the membranes acts as a lubricant and also helps to adhere the two membranes together through surface tension. However, the key factor that holds the two membranes together is the negative intrapleural pressure inside the thorax. The pressure between the two membranes is lower than atmospheric and alveolar pressure, thereby holding the membranes in close contact. The low pressure and surface tension allow the lungs to remain attached to the chest wall, facilitating lung expansion during inhalation.\n\nIf these pleural membranes were punctured pleural fluid would be able to get out and air would get in. The pressure inside the thorax would become similar to the atmospheric pressure, and the pleural membranes would no longer adhere to each other leading to lung collapse. **Choice C** is therefore correct.\n\nThe walls of the trachea cannot stick together under any circumstances as they contain cartilaginous rings which keep them open (**Choice A**).\n\nMost of the external and internal intercostal muscles and the diaphragm can work regardless of a puncture injury to the ribcage (**Choice B**).\n\nUncontrolled cell division which causes tumours is usually caused by carcinogens, not by the physical injury (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0617","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0617\n\nWhich of the following takes place during forced exhalation?\n\n-    I. $\\hspace{1em}$ The diaphragm forms a dome shape\n-    II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ Internal intercostal muscles contract\n-    III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ Pressure increases inside the lungs\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I**  is true because during exhalation, the diaphragm relaxes and lifts upwards to form a dome shape.\n**Statement II** is true because, although passive exhalation does not usually use internal intercostal muscles, during forced exhalation, internal intercostal muscles contract and pull the ribcage inwards and downwards.\n**Statement III** is true because as thoracic volume decreases, the pressure inside the lungs increases, pushing air out.\n\nThis makes **Choice D** the correct answer as it includes all three true statements.\n**Choices A, B** and **C** are all incorrect because they omit one or more true statements.\n\n**"},{"ref":"BI0618","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0618\n\nA spirometer is an apparatus that measures the volume of air (dm$^3$) inhaled and exhaled over a period of time. The graph below shows the spirometer reading of an individual after exercise.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Gas%20Exchange/images/bi0618.png\" alt=\"bi0618\">\n\nWhat would the spirometer reading look like for an individual at rest who has not recently exercised compared to the reading shown above?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Tidal volume would be higher\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Volume of air inhaled per breath would be the same\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Decrease in ventilation rate\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Graph would appear linear without fluctuations","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAfter mild or vigorous exercise spirometer readings show a higher ventilation rate with a higher number of breaths per minute and a larger amplitude. While at rest, the rate of ventilation would be comparatively less (**Choice C**).\n\nTidal volume, which is the volume of air that is inhaled or exhaled per breath, would be lower while at rest (**Choices A** and **B**).\n\nThe graph would not be linear while at rest because air would still be inhaled and exhaled unless a person was holding their breath. The volume would increase, showing an upward slope during inhalation and would then decrease during exhalation, showing a downward slope (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0619","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0619\n\nHow do different muscles contribute to the decrease in air pressure inside the lungs, causing inhalation?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Muscles on the abdominal wall contract\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Diaphragm relaxes\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ External intercostal muscles shorten\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Internal intercostal muscles shorten","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe process of air entering the lungs is known as inhalation.  The term $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$inspiration$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$ is used instead of inhalation sometimes. In order for inhalation to take place, the pressure inside the lungs must be lower compared to atmospheric pressure. To decrease the pressure inside the lungs, the external intercostal muscles must contract and become shorter to pull the ribs up and out, increasing the volume inside the thorax, and so increasing lung volume.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because abdominal wall muscles relax during inhalation. Lengthening of the abdominal muscles can contribute to inhalation by pushing the abdominal wall out which increases the volume inside the thorax, and so too the volume of the lungs.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because when the diaphragm relaxes it forms a dome shape and therefore reduces the volume of the thorax, and so the volume of the lungs. This occurs during exhalation.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because internal intercostal muscles relax and lengthen during inhalation. They contract and shorten during exhalation."},{"ref":"BI0622","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0622\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nPremature babies sometimes suffer from infant respiratory distress syndrome, which could be caused by a lack of sufficient surfactant in the pulmonary alveoli. The diagram below shows two different alveoli structures.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Gas%20Exchange/images/BI0622iii.png\" alt=\"BI0622iii\">\n\n1. Describe **one** feature of the surfactant. [1 mark]\n\n2. Suggest a treatment for infant respiratory distress syndrome. [1 mark]\n\n3. Outline how concentration gradients are maintained at the lungs to enable gaseous exchange. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ reduces surface tension in alveoli **OR** disrupts cohesive forces between water molecules lining alveoli [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ forms a monolayer on the alveolar surface [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrophilic heads facing water **OR** hydrophobic tails facing air [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nThe pulmonary surfactant is a lipoprotein complex that is secreted by type II pneumocytes. It reduces the **surface tension** in alveoli ($\\checkmark$) by orienting its **hydrophobic tails** towards the **air** and its **hydrophilic heads** towards the **water** lining the alveoli ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ provide pure oxygen **OR** breathing support [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ administer surfactant from other sources, **OR** from animal lungs [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nToo little surfactant in infants can lead to shortness of breath, so an **oxygen** mask and breathing assistance is necessary ($\\checkmark$). Doctors could also **extract the surfactant** from the lungs of animals or produce them in a lab and then administer the surfactant to the baby ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ continuous movement of blood [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ inhalation **OR** exhalation **OR** ventilation **OR** movement of air into and out of the lungs [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ binding of oxygen to haemoglobin [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ dense network of capillaries [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n The **continuous movement of blood** ($\\checkmark$)ensures that oxygenated blood is transported away from the lungs and deoxygenated blood is delivered to them. **Ventilation of the lungs** ($\\checkmark$) brings air rich in oxygen into the lungs and expels air high in carbon dioxide."},{"ref":"BI0623","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0623\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n1. Describe the structure of the bronchioles.  [2 marks]\n\n2. Distinguish between the external intercostal muscles and the internal intercostal muscles.    [2 marks]\n\n3. Outline how the concentration gradients of oxygen and carbon dioxide are maintained in the lungs. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.    Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ wall contains smooth muscle fibres [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  tree-like structure of narrow airways [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  connects alveoli to bronchi **OR** connects to alveoli at the end **OR** branch from bronchi      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  epithial cells OR goblet cells line the airways [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nThe walls of the bronchioles contain **smooth muscle fibres** ($\\checkmark$). They are smaller **branches** of bronchi ($\\checkmark$) and connect the bronchi with the **alveoli** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.    Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  during inhalation, external intercostal muscles contract, **OR** shorten, **WHEREAS** internal intercostal muscles relax, **OR** lengthen [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  during exhalation, external intercostal muscles relax, **OR** lengthen, **WHEREAS** internal intercostal muscles contract, **OR** shorten     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  during inhalation, external intercostal muscles pull ribcage upwards and outwards, **WHEREAS** internal intercostal muscles pull ribcage inwards and downwards during exhalation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  external intercostal muscles are located on the exterior surface of the ribs **WHILE** internal intercostal muscles are located on the interior surface of the ribs [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nThe difference between external intercostal muscles and internal intercostal muscles is that during **inhalation**, external intercostal muscles **contract**, whereas internal intercostal muscles **relax** ($\\checkmark$). When external intercostal muscles contract, the **ribcage** is pulled **upwards** and outwards, but when internal intercostal muscles contract, the ribcage is pulled inwards and **downwards** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.    Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  contraction of external intercostal muscles **OR** diaphragm brings about inhalation, **OR** contraction of internal intercostal muscle **OR** relaxation of external intercostal muscles **OR** relaxation of diaphragm brings about exhalation [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  after inhalation **OR** inspiration, alveoli have higher O$_2$ **OR** lower CO$_2$ concentration, **OR** after exhalation, alveoli have lower CO$_2$ concentration, than the blood [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  oxygen diffuses into blood **OR** capillaries from alveoli **OR** carbon dioxide diffuses from blood **OR** capillaries into alveoli   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  concentration gradients of gases **OR** oxygen **OR** carbon dioxide maintained by blood flow in capillaries    [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nThe **contraction** of the **external intercostal** muscles and **diaphragm** brings about **inhalation**  ($\\checkmark$). After **inhalation**, the **alveoli** have a **higher O$_2$** concentration and a **lower CO$_2$** concentration **than the blood** ($\\checkmark$). **Oxygen** diffuses from the **alveoli into** into the **blood** ($\\checkmark$). The constant **flow of blood** in the **capillaries** means oxygen is carried off to the body, **maintaining a steep diffusion gradient** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0666","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0666\n\nWhich of the following substances typically moves either in or out of a leaf\\'s inner air space via stomata?\n\n-    I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Water\n-    II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ Oxygen\n-    III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ Mineral ions\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWater diffuses out of stomata from the inner air space of leaves during transpiration (**Statement I**). Because oxygen is produced during photosynthesis, its concentration is generally higher in the inner air space than the atmosphere which means it diffuses out through the stomata (**Statement II**). Mineral ions (**Statement III**) enter plants through root cells, not through the stomata."},{"ref":"BI0669","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0669\n\nThe diagram below shows a simple apparatus which uses a porous pot to model water transport in plants.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Gas%20Exchange/images/BI0669.png\" alt=\"BI0669\">\n\nWhy is a porous pot used to model water transport in the xylem?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ It allows water molecules to flow down the capillary tube to represent water moving from leaves to roots.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ It allows water to leave the pot through the pores, which represents water evaporating out of the stomata.\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ It represents the thick, lignified walls of xylem vessels.\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ It generates adhesion and cohesion for transporting water inside plants.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA porous pot allows water to evaporate and diffuse out, which models water loss through the stomata in plant leaves. This evaporation creates tension, pulling water up the capillary tube, simulating how water is drawn up the xylem from roots to leaves during transpiration; **Choice B** is the correct answer.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because water in the xylem moves upward, from roots to leaves, not downward, and not from leaves to roots.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because the porous pot represents the leaves, where water is lost, not the xylem vessels. In this model, the capillary tube represents the xylem.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the porous pot does not generate adhesion and cohesion. These are intrinsic properties of water due to its polarity, which help maintain a continuous column of water in the xylem but are not caused by the porous pot."},{"ref":"BI0792","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0792\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nLung volumes and ventilation rates can be measured using a spirometer. During a school lab a student was asked to breathe normally at rest, then they were asked to take the deepest breath in that they could, followed by the largest breath out, before returning to breathing normally at rest. Below is the trace for this student.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Gas%20Exchange/images/BI0792aii.png\" alt=\"BI0792aii\">\n\n1. Calculate the total volume of air inhaled and exhaled during one minute by this student when breathing normally at rest. [2 marks]\n\nBelow is a diagram of the spirometer used by the students. The subject puts on a nose clip, to prevent air from escaping through the nose, and breathes in and out through the mouthpiece. The chamber is closed so the volume of air with each inhalation and exhalation can be recorded on the drum trace. The chamber is opened between trials to allow the air to refresh and the trace to be recalibrated.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Gas%20Exchange/images/BI0792B.png\" alt=\"BI0792B\">\n\n2. Explain why the trace decreased in height during the 100 seconds. [2 marks]\n\n3. Suggest a possible investigation that could be conducted using the spirometer. [2 marks]\n\n4. State two variables that must be controlled in the investigation you suggested in part (c). [2 marks]\n\nAnother student, who played the flute regularly, was then asked to record their results to compare them to student 1, who did not play a wind instrument. The trace for this second student is shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Gas%20Exchange/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n5. Discuss whether the data in the two graphs support the hypothesis that playing a wind instrument increases lung volume. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ tidal volume x ventilation rate **OR** litres per breath x breaths per minute **OR** 0.45 dm:sup[3] breath:sup[-1] x 13 breaths min:sup[-1] [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 5.85 **OR** 5.9 dm:sup[3] min:sup[-1] (allow range 5.6 - 6.3 dm:sup[3] min:sup[-1], dm:sup[3] units must be included) [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Gas%20Exchange/images/BI0792bi.png\" alt=\"BI0792bi\">\n\n **0.45 dm:sup[3]** breath:sup[-1] x **13** breaths min:sup[-1] ($\\checkmark$)\n\n = **5.85 dm:sup[3]** min:sup[-1] ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide is excreted in breath **OR** carbon dioxide is breathed out as a waste gas from respiration **OR** carbon dioxide is released with each breath [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide is absorbed by soda lime, and so volume of air in spirometer decreases **OR** carbon dioxide is absorbed in the spirometer, and so volume of gas decreases [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Carbon dioxide is **breathed** out due to it being a **waste gas** of respiration ($\\checkmark$). This is then **absorbed by the soda lime** in the spirometer, and so the **volume of gas** in the chamber **decreases** ($\\checkmark$) with each breath.\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the independent variable of the investigation is identified, e.g. levels of exercise, age, fitness, height or another suitable independent variable [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the dependent variable of the investigation is identified, e.g. air breathed per minute, tidal volume, volume of oxygen used in a fixed time, metabolic rate, vital capacity, ventilation rate or another suitable dependent variable [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The effect of **different levels of exercise** ($\\checkmark$) on the **volume of air** breathed per minute ($\\checkmark$) could be investigated.\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ environmental temperature [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ age of person **OR** sex of person **OR** fitness of person [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ way breathing conducted **OR** sealing of nose with clip [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ how the level of exercise controlled for each subject [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ subjects do not have respiratory disease [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ time of day investigation conducted **OR** time after meal investigation conducted [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ any other valid controlled variable [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The investigation will be carried out on **Year 11 students** ($\\checkmark$) using a treadmill. All students will **wear a nose clip** ($\\checkmark$) when breathing in and out of the apparatus.\n\n5. Any **two** of the following:\n\n **Supports the hypothesis:**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ vital capacity of student 2 is greater than student 1 **OR** vital capacity of student 1 is less than student 2 **OR** the inspiratory and expiratory reserve volumes are greater in student 2 **OR** the inspiratory and expiratory reserve volumes are less in student 1 [1 mark]\n\n **Does not support the hypothesis:**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ages of students may not be the same **OR** student 1 may be younger than student 2 **OR** sex of students may not be the same **OR** student 2 may be a biological male **OR** fitness of students may not be the same **OR** student 1 may not be as fit as student 2 [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ tidal volumes are the almost the same **OR** tidal volumes are the same [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Gas%20Exchange/images/792ms.png\" alt=\"792ms\">\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Gas%20Exchange/images/spirometer_graph_student2_047_medium.png\" alt=\"spirometer_graph_student2_047_medium\">\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **larger vital capacity** of the student who plays the **flute supports** ($\\checkmark$) the hypothesis. However, the **tidal volumes** of both students are almost **the same**, which **does not support** ($\\checkmark$) the hypothesis."},{"ref":"BI0800","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0800\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA student set up an experiment as in the diagram below, to investigate how temperature affects the rate of transpiration in plants.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Gas%20Exchange/images/BI0800.png\" alt=\"BI0800\">\n[Source: Adapted from Knott, T. (2006, August). *File: Potometer.png*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved May 3, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Potometer.png. CC-BY-SA-3.0-migrated]\n\n1.\n    1. Deduce in which direction the bubble will move as transpiration occurs.    [1 mark]\n\n    2. State one abiotic factor that needs to be kept constant in this experiment. [1 mark]\n\nThis table shows data collected by a student when they used the piece of apparatus in (a) to investigate the effect of temperature on the rate of transpiration.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Gas%20Exchange/images/1.JPEG\" alt=\"1\">\n\n2.\n    1. Describe, using the data provided, the relationship between temperature and the rate of water uptake.    [1 mark]\n\n    2. Explain the change in water uptake from 20 ⁰C to 35 ⁰C.    [2 marks]\n\n    3. Suggest a reason for the lower rate of transpiration at 40 ⁰C.    [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to the left **OR** towards the plant **OR** away from the beaker        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    As transpiration occurs, the bubble will move **towards the plant** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    2. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ humidity  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ air movement **OR** wind speed [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light intensity [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water availability [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ atmospheric pressure [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    One abiotic factor that needs to be kept constant in this experiment is **wind speed** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    1. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rate of water uptake increases up to 30 ⁰C **AND** then decreases above 30 ⁰C **OR** rate of water uptake is highest at 30 ⁰C with 2.2 mm min:sup[-1] **AND** rate of water uptake decreases at 35 ⁰C and 40 ⁰C with 2.0 and 1.2 mm min:sup[-1] respectively         [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Rate of water uptake **increases from** 1.6 mm min:sup[-1] at **20 ⁰C** to 2.2 mm min:sup[-1] at **30 ⁰C** and **then decreases** to 1.2 mm min:sup[-1] at **40 ⁰C** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    2. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water uptake is lower at low temperatures because water evaporates more slowly at low temperatures **OR** because molecules have less energy at low temperatures    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water uptake increases at higher temperatures because transpiration is faster at higher temperatures **OR** water evaporates more quickly at higher temperatures    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stomata close at higher temperatures to reduce water loss **OR** reduced transpiration at 35 ⁰C to conserve water in the plant **OR** reduced transpiration at 35 ⁰C because stomata are closed    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Water uptake increases at higher temperatures because **evaporation is faster at higher temperatures** ($\\checkmark$). There is reduced transpiration at 35 ⁰C because **stomata close to reduce water loss** ($\\checkmark$) to conserve water.\n\n2.\n    3.  any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Greater stomatal closure at 40 ⁰C to conserve water     [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    At 40 ⁰C **stomata are closed** ($\\checkmark$) to conserve water."},{"ref":"BI1045","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1045\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe image shows oxygen dissociation curves for an adult with sickle cell anaemia and an adult without sickle cell anaemia. Sickle cell anaemia is a genetic disease caused by a point mutation in the gene coding for haemoglobin, resulting in the substitution of a single amino acid in the haemoglobin molecule. Symptoms of sickle cell anaemia include fatigue, pain in joints and reduced growth rate.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Gas%20Exchange/images/BI1045.png\" alt=\"BI1045\">\n\n1.\n 1. Identify which oxygen dissociation curve represents a person who has sickle cell anaemia. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Explain a reason for your answer to part (i). [1 mark]\n\n2. Explain how the structure of normal haemoglobin enables cooperative binding. [3 marks]\n\n3.\n 1. Draw a curve on the graph that represents the oxygen saturation of foetal haemoglobin at different partial pressures of oxygen. $^*$ [1 mark]\n\n 2. Some people with sickle cell anaemia continue to express foetal haemoglobin into adulthood, a phenomenon known as foetal haemoglobin persistence. Suggest why people with sickle cell anaemia, who express foetal haemoglobin into adulthood, have less severe symptoms of sickle cell anaemia than those who switch to adult haemoglobin. [1 mark]\n\n3. Explain how intense exercise causes changes to the affinity of haemoglobin for oxygen.[3 marks]\n\n$^*$ *Note: Starting May 2028, this question may be assessed differently due to changes to the Biology guide, including the use of a data booklet.*","markScheme":"1.\n 1.\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the solid line **OR** curve **OR** the curve **OR** line on the right [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The curve on the **right** ($\\checkmark$) represents a person with sickle cell anaemia.\n\n1.\n 2.\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ people with sickle cell anaemia have altered haemoglobin, so have a lower affinity for oxygen at all partial pressures [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n People with sickle cell anaemia have **altered haemoglobin**, so they have a **lower affinity** for **oxygen** ($\\checkmark$), therefore, the curve is shifted to the right.\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ haemoglobin has four binding sites for oxygen [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it is difficult for the first oxygen to bind [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ once the first oxygen binds, it causes a conformational change that makes it easier for successive oxygens to bind [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ haemoglobin consists of four protein subunits arranged in a specific quaternary structure [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ each globin subunit contains a haem group that can bind to one oxygen molecule [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in the absence of oxygen binding, the binding sites for oxygen have lower affinity for oxygen molecules [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Haemoglobin has **four** binding sites for **oxygen** ($\\checkmark$). It is **difficult** for the **first oxygen** to bind to haemoglobin ($\\checkmark$), however, once the first oxygen binds, it causes a **conformational change** in the haemoglobin molecule that makes it **easier for successive oxygens** to bind ($\\checkmark$) until the haemoglobin is saturated.\n\n3.\n 1. of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ curve must be to the left of the dashed line **AND** start and end with the other curves [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Gas%20Exchange/images/BI1045ansb.png\" alt=\"BI1045ansb\">\n\n3.\n 2. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ foetal haemoglobin has a higher affinity for oxygen **OR** binds more oxygen at a lower partial pressure than adult haemoglobin so is able to transport oxygen **OR** unload **OR** load oxygen more efficiently [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ foetal haemoglobin has a lower tendency to polymerise **OR** stick together compared to adult haemoglobin, which means that red blood cells are less likely to become sickle-shaped and obstruct blood flow [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n People with sickle cell anaemia who express foetal haemoglobin into adulthood have less severe symptoms than those who switch to adult haemoglobin because foetal haemoglobin has a **higher affinity** for **oxygen**, so is able to **load and unload oxygen** more **efficiently** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ respiring tissues produce carbon dioxide [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ causes blood pH to lower **OR** blood to become more acidic [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen dissociation curve shifts to the right **OR** causes the Bohr effect [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ haemoglobin has a lower affinity for oxygen **AND** unloads oxygen at respiring tissues [1 mark]\n\n **OR**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ intense exercise results in increased body temperature [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ causes a decreased affinity of haemoglobin for oxygen [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen dissociation curve shifts to the right **OR** causes the Bohr effect [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Intense exercise causes changes to the affinity of haemoglobin for oxygen, as **respiring tissues** produce **carbon dioxide** ($\\checkmark$), which causes the blood **pH to drop** ($\\checkmark$). This causes the oxygen dissociation **curve** to shift to the **right** ($\\checkmark$), therefore haemoglobin has a **lower affinity for oxygen** and **unloads oxygen easily** at respiring tissues ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1059","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1059\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nGas exchange is very important for both plants and animals.\n\n1. Outline the properties of gas-exchange surfaces.    [4 marks]\n\n2. Describe how muscles bring about pressure changes during inspiration in humans.        [4 marks]\n\n3. Transpiration is a result of gas exchange in plants. Explain how different environmental factors affect the rate of transpiration.     [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ thin **OR** one cell thick for short diffusion distance     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large surface area for maximum diffusion rate                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ moist for gasses to dissolve, facilitating movement            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ often supplied with good transport system to maintain steep diffusion gradient    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ partially permeable membranes **OR** permeable to gases        [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Gas-exchange surfaces have a **large surface area** to maximise **diffusion** rate ($\\checkmark$). They are **thin**, often one cell thick, to make the **diffusion distance** as **short** as possible ($\\checkmark$). They are usually **moist** so the **gases** can **dissolve** in them ($\\checkmark$), to facilitate movement. The membranes are **permeable** to gases to **diffuse** through them ($\\checkmark$), and they often have a **good transport system** ($\\checkmark$) to maintain a **steep diffusion gradient** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ diaphragm contracts and moves downwards     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ external intercostal muscles contract **OR** internal intercostal muscles relax    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ribcage is pulled upwards and outwards        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increasing the volume of the thorax            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ air particles become more spread out **OR** lower pressure inside the thorax      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ air flows into the lungs     [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    During inspiration, the **diaphragm contracts** and moves downwards ($\\checkmark$).  **External intercostal muscles** contract ($\\checkmark$) and pull the ribcage **upwards** and outwards ($\\checkmark$) which **increases the volume** ($\\checkmark$) of the thoracic cavity. When the volume increases, the **pressure** inside the thoracic cavity **decreases** ($\\checkmark$) moving **air into** the lungs ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transpiration involves the loss of water through stomata    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ environmental factors that affect water loss through transpiration are temperature, humidity **and** wind **OR** air movement        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at higher temperatures, the rate of transpiration increases        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ because heat increases rate of evaporation in leaves **OR** rate water diffuses out of stomata **OR** the kinetic energy of the water molecules is greater    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at extremely high temperatures, the rate of transpiration may decrease    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ because stomata will close to prevent further water loss            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at higher humidity, the rate of transpiration decreases        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ because when surrounding humidity is high, the rate water evaporates in leaves decreases **OR** the rate water vapour diffuses out of stomata decreases **OR** the concentration gradient between the inside of the leaf and the surrounding air is lower [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at higher light intensity, the rate of transpiration increases        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ because light intensity stimulates stomata to open allowing water to diffuse out    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased wind speed increases the rate of transpiration            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ because wind carries the water vapour away from the stomata increasing the concentration gradient around the pores            [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Transpiration involves the loss of water from leaves through **stomata** ($\\checkmark$). The rate of transpiration is affected by environmental factors such as **temperature, humidity, and air movement** ($\\checkmark$). **Higher temperatures** cause the rate of transpiration to **increase** ($\\checkmark$) because **heat energy** absorbed by water in the inner air space of leaves causes water to **evaporate more rapidly** ($\\checkmark$). **Higher humidity** causes the rate of transpiration to **decrease** ($\\checkmark$). This is because air that is more humid carries more water particles in it which reduces the **concentration gradient** ($\\checkmark$) between the inside of the stomata and outside, thus reducing the rate water diffuses out of the stomata.  Lastly, **increases in wind speed** can **increase** the rate of transpiration ($\\checkmark$).  Wind carries away water vapour from the surface of leaves, which **increases the concentration gradient** ($\\checkmark$) of water between the inside and outside of the stomata."},{"ref":"BI1130","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1130\n\nWhich of the following statements about foetal haemoglobin is/are correct?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Foetal haemoglobin has a higher affinity for oxygen than adult haemoglobin. |\n    | II. | Foetal haemoglobin releases oxygen more readily than adult haemoglobin at the low partial pressures of oxygen found in the placenta. |\n    | III. | Foetal haemoglobin binds oxygen cooperatively, so its oxygen dissociation curve is sigmoid. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because foetal haemoglobin has a higher affinity for oxygen than adult haemoglobin, so its oxygen dissociation curve lies to the left. This allows it to load oxygen in the placenta at partial pressures at which maternal haemoglobin is unloading oxygen.\n\n**Statement II** is false because at the low partial pressures of oxygen in the placenta, foetal haemoglobin binds oxygen more readily than adult haemoglobin rather than releasing it.\n\n**Statement III** is true because, like adult haemoglobin, foetal haemoglobin binds oxygen cooperatively, giving a sigmoid curve.\n\n**Choice C** is correct as it only includes the two true **Statements I** and **III**. **Choice A** is incorrect because it omits **Statement III**. **Choices B** and **D** are incorrect as they include the false **Statement II**."},{"ref":"BI1131","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1131\n\nThe image shows an oxygen dissociation curve for human haemoglobin. Which sequence of events correctly describes how the Bohr shift occurs in heavily respiring tissues?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Gas%20Exchange/images/BI1131.png\" alt=\"BI1131\">\n[Source: Adapted from Militia, M. (2006, December 25). *File: HB Saturation curve.png - wikimedia commons*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved August 30, 2023 from URL  https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hb_saturation_curve.png.  Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increased carbon dioxide concentration in blood → higher pH → curve shifts to the left → haemoglobin has a decreased affinity for oxygen\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increased oxygen concentration in blood → lower pH → curve shifts to the left → haemoglobin has an increased affinity for oxygen\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increased carbon dioxide concentration in blood → lower pH → curve shifts to the right → haemoglobin has a decreased affinity for oxygen\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increased oxygen concentration in blood → higher pH → curve shifts to the right → haemoglobin has an increased affinity for oxygen","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C**  is correct, as heavily respiring tissues produce more carbon dioxide, which lowers the blood pH (or makes it more acidic), this causes the oxygen dissociation curve to shift to the right. This shift results in a decreased affinity of haemoglobin for oxygen, meaning that oxygen is more readily released to the heavily respiring tissues.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect, as a higher concentration of carbon dioxide in the blood does not result in a higher pH. **Choice B**  is incorrect, as an increase in the respiration rate of cells leads to a higher concentration of carbon dioxide in the blood, not oxygen. **Choice D**  is incorrect, as the Bohr shift results in haemoglobin having a decreased affinity for oxygen."},{"ref":"BI1132","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1132\n\nWhich statement explains why haemoglobin is described as an ‘allosteric protein’?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Conformational changes to oxygen influence the ability of it to bind to haemoglobin\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Haemoglobin has multiple binding sites for oxygen\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The binding of oxygen to haemoglobin causes the oxygen dissociation curve to shift to the right\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Changes in concentration of oxygen cause conformational changes in haemoglobin that influence the ability of oxygen to bind","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAllosteric proteins typically have multiple binding sites, where the binding of a ligand at one site induces a conformational change that influences binding at other sites. With haemoglobin, when one oxygen molecule binds to a subunit, it increases the affinity of the remaining subunits for oxygen, known as cooperative binding. Similarly, when oxygen is released, the affinity of the remaining subunits decreases. This property defines haemoglobin as an allosteric protein, therefore **Choice D**  is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect, as oxygen does not undergo conformational changes to bind to haemoglobin. **Choice B**  is incorrect as, although haemoglobin has multiple binding sites for oxygen, this doesn’t directly explain why haemoglobin is described as an allosteric protein. The multiple binding sites for oxygen are related to its quaternary structure but not specifically to its allosteric nature. **Choice C**  is incorrect, as the binding of oxygen to haemoglobin does not cause the oxygen dissociation curve to shift."},{"ref":"BI1133","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1133\n\nWhich is **not** a reason for oxygen dissociation curves to be S-shaped?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cooperative binding of oxygen\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The binding of the first oxygen molecule to haemoglobin causes the remaining oxygen molecules to bind more easily\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ It is easier for the first oxygen molecule to bind to haemoglobin than the others\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The shape of haemoglobin is changed as each oxygen molecule binds","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIt is more difficult for the first oxygen molecule to bind to haemoglobin, but its binding induces a conformational change that enhances the affinity of the other subunits for oxygen. This causes the curve to be S-shaped. Seeming as this is NOT a reason for oxygen dissociation curves to be S-shaped, **Choice C** is the correct answer to the question.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **D**  are all incorrect, as they are all reasons for the shape of the dissociation curve. When the first oxygen molecule binds to the haemoglobin, it increases the affinity of the remaining subunits for oxygen, making it easier for the subsequent oxygen molecules to bind, causing the S-shaped curve."},{"ref":"BI1134","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1134\n\nWhich statement(s) correctly describes a situation where a haemoglobin protein will have a high affinity for oxygen?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | In the capillaries surrounding the alveoli |\n    | II. | Near heavily respiring tissues |\n    | III. | When two oxygen molecules have bound to haemoglobin |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is correct, as haemoglobin will have a high affinity for oxygen because the partial pressure of oxygen is high due to the oxygen diffusing from the lungs into the blood. **Statement III** is correct, as when oxygen binds to haemoglobin, it leads to a conformational change in the haemoglobin molecule, making it easier for other oxygen molecules to bind. **Choice B**  is correct.\n\n**Statement II** is incorrect because, in heavily respiring tissues, oxygen is able to be loaded easily from haemoglobin to the respiring tissues due to haemoglobin having a low affinity for oxygen. **Choices A, C**  and **D**  are incorrect as they all include **statement II**."},{"ref":"BI1169","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1169\n\nWhich factor does **not** help maintain the oxygen concentration gradient in the alveoli?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ A dense network of capillaries surrounding the alveoli\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Thin-walled alveoli\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Continuous blood flow\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ventilation from the air","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because, while thin walls are essential for efficient respiration, they are a structural feature that facilitates the movement across the barrier, rather than a functional mechanism that prevents the oxygen concentrations on either side from reaching equilibrium.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because a dense network of capillaries ensures that oxygenated blood is rapidly carried away from the alveoli, maintaining a lower oxygen concentration in the blood and preserving the gradient.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because continuous blood flow replaces oxygenated blood with deoxygenated blood, preventing equilibrium from being reached and maintaining the gradient.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because ventilation continually supplies fresh air to the alveoli, maintaining a high oxygen concentration compared to the surrounding capillaries."},{"ref":"BI1175","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1175\n\nWhich one of the following statements is true of transpiration?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ With higher humidity rate increases\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ With greater wind speed rate decreases\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ With greater light intensity rate increases\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ With lower temperatures rate increases","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nTranspiration is the loss of water vapour from a plant via the stomata. At high light intensity, more stomata are likely to be open to allow more carbon dioxide to diffuse into the leaf for photosynthesis, while at the same time resulting in more loss of water from the plant via transpiration. **Choice C** is correct.\n\nHigher humidity means there is more moisture in the air, meaning there is a smaller difference in water concentration between the inside of the leaf and the surrounding air, so diffusion of water molecules out of the stomata decreases. **Choice A** is incorrect.\nAs wind speeds increase, more of the humid air around the leaves is blown away and replaced by drier air, therefore increasing the water concentration gradient between the inside of the leaf and the surrounding air, increasing the rate of diffusion of water molecules out of the stomata. **Choice B** is incorrect.\n\nAt lower temperatures, molecules have lower kinetic energy, so the rate of diffusion of water molecules out of the stomata will decrease. Also at lower temperatures plants tend to have fewer stomata open. **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1235","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1235\n\nThe diagram below shows an experiment to study the effect of light intensity on transpiration rates. What is the most likely effect of **high light intensity** on transpiration rates?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Gas%20Exchange/images/BI1235.png\" alt=\"BI1235\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Faster transpiration because more light increases the water potential in the xylem\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Slower transpiration because more heat will increase the humidity around the shoot\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Faster transpiration because more stomata are likely to be open\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Slower transpiration because heat prevents cohesion of water molecules","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nTranspiration is the loss of water vapour from a plant via the stomata. At high light intensity, more stomata are likely to be open to allow more carbon dioxide to diffuse into the leaf for photosynthesis, while at the same time resulting in more loss of water from the plant via transpiration. The lamp will also transfer heat energy to the plant, which will result in more evaporation from the leaf, increasing the rate of transpiration. **Choice C** is correct.\n\nLight has no direct effect on water potential in a plant; **Choice A** is incorrect. Humidity is a measure of the moisture content of the air. As temperature increases, the air will become drier, decreasing the humidity, and increasing the rate of evaporation and transpiration; **Choice B** is incorrect. Heat increases the rate of transpiration as it results in more evaporation from the surface of the leaf, resulting in a faster transpiration stream; **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1285","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1285\n\nThe graph shows a spirometer trace of a student who took part in a breathing experiment.\nWhich part of the trace represents the volume of air breathed in and out during normal, relaxed breathing?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Gas%20Exchange/images/BI1285.png\" alt=\"BI1285\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because the tidal volume is the volume of air breathed in or out during normal, relaxed breathing, which can be seen as the central wave that is constant throughout the graph, representing normal breathing at rest.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because this is the inspiratory reserve volume which represents the difference between the maximum inspiratory level and the tidal volume. **Choice B** is incorrect because this is the vital capacity is the maximum volume of air that can be forcefully exhaled after maximal inhalation. **Choice D** is incorrect because this is the expiratory reserve volume which represents the difference between the maximum expiratory level and the tidal volume."},{"ref":"BI1286","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1286\n\nThe graph shows a spirometer trace of a student who took part in a breathing experiment.\n\nThe vital capacity of the student is 4100 cm:sup[3].\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Gas%20Exchange/images/BI1286.png\" alt=\"BI1286\">\n\nWhat is the tidal volume of the student?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 500 cm:sup[3]\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 800 cm:sup[3]\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 2800 cm:sup[3]\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 3600 cm:sup[3]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because the tidal volume (TV) can be seen as the central wave that is constant throughout the graph.\n\nTV = VC $-$ IRV $-$ ERV \\\n$\\hspace{1.5em}$ = 4100 cm:sup[3] $-$ 2800 cm:sup[3] $-$ 800 cm:sup[3] \\\n$\\hspace{1.5em}$ = 500 cm:sup[3]\n\n**Choice B** 800 cm:sup[3] is the expiratory reserve volume (ERV) which can be obtained by finding the difference between the level after normal exhalation at rest and the maximum expiratory level.\n\n**Choice C** 2800 cm:sup[3] is the inspiratory reserve volume (IRV) which can be obtained by finding the difference between the level after normal inhalation at rest and the maximum inspiratory level.\n\n**Choice D** 3600cm:sup[3] is the sum of the expiratory reserve volume and the inspiratory reserve volume.\n\n**Choices B, C** and **D** are all incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1287","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1287\n\nHow is the concentration gradient of water maintained in the leaf?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Stomata opens to allow water vapour to escape. |\n    | II. | Air spaces in the spongy mesophyll allow water from the mesophyll cells to evaporate. |\n    | III. | Waxy cuticle layer of the upper epidermis reduces the amount of water escaping the leaf.  |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because the waxy cuticle layer of the upper epidermis reduces water loss through evaporation from the leaf (**Statement III**). The stomata found in the lower epidermis of the leaf would open to allow water vapour to escape during transpiration (**Statement I**). Water from the thin film of moisture surrounding the mesophyll cells can evaporate to form water vapour that will fill up the air spaces around the loosely packed spongy mesophyll cells (**Statement II**).\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it is missing **statements II** and **III**. \\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because it is missing **statements I** and **II**. \\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because it is missing **statement III**."},{"ref":"BI1288","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1288\n\nWhich leaf structure adaptation is correctly matched to its function?\n\n| | Adaptations | Function |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Waxy cuticle | Contains many pores to facilitate gas exchange |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Spongy mesophyll | Creates a large impermeable barrier to prevent loss of gases and water vapour from the surface |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Stomatal guard cells | Controls gas exchange and water loss by opening and closing |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Lower epidermis | Gives a large surface area for gas exchange |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because stomatal guard cells are found mostly at the lower epidermis, which controls gas exchange and water loss by opening and closing the stomata.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the waxy cuticle creates a large impermeable barrier to prevent gases and water vapour loss from the surface. **Choice B** is incorrect because spongy mesophyll provides a large surface area for gas exchange. **Choice D** is incorrect because the lower epidermis contains many stomata to facilitate gas exchange."},{"ref":"BI1289","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1289\n\nWhich method(s) could be used to measure ventilation rate?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Strap a belt around the chest and measure the pressure from the contraction and expansion of the chest using a data logger. |\n    | II. | Breathe into a mouthpiece connected to a spirometer and measure the volume of air inhaled and exhaled over a period of time. |\n    | III. | Place hands on the chest and count the number of times the chest rises over a period of time. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because the ventilation rate can be measured using a chest belt and pressure monitor connected to a data logger (**Statement I**). The pressure monitor would show the pressure based on the contraction and expansion of the chest. Ventilation rate can also be measured using a spirometer which would show the volume of air inhaled and exhaled when breathing is done through a mouthpiece connected to the spirometer (**Statement II**). Ventilation rate can also be measured in a simple way by observing and counting the number of times the chest rises over a period of time, for instance, 1 minute (**Statement III**).\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it is missing **statements II** and **III**. \\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because it is missing **statements I** and **II**. \\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because it is missing **statement III**."},{"ref":"BI1290","marks":"11","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1290\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe micrograph below shows a transverse section through the leaf of a monocot (*Lilium* sp.).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Gas%20Exchange/images/BI1290.png\" alt=\"BI1290\">\n[Source: Science and Plants for Schools, (2012, May 1). *Leaf of a monocot (Lilium sp.)*. Flickr. Retrieved October 11, 2023, from https://www.flickr.com/photos/71183136@N08/6985212944. CC BY-NC-SA 2.0 DEED]\n\n1. State the name and role of each of the structures **P** and **Q**. [2 marks]\n\n2. Explain how the movement of water into and out of the structure labelled Q affects its role.    [3 marks]\n\n3. Structure **R** represents the vascular bundle, which contains xylem and phloem.\n    1. Outline the role of xylem in transpiration.        [2 marks]\n    2. Describe the effect that high environmental humidity has on the rate of transpiration.        [2 marks]\n\n4. Structure **S** consists of cells that are loosely packed together. State one advantage of having these loosely packed cells in the leaf.         [1 mark]\n\n5. Using the correct name, label the photomicrograph to show the part of the leaf that would contain the greatest density of chloroplasts.    [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. ***both name and adaptation need to be correct to gain each mark***:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ P: waxy cuticle forms a water-resistant **OR** a protective layer to reduce water loss **OR** protect the leaf from pathogens **OR** physical damage **OR** UV radiation **OR** any other suitable example    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Q: guard cells which regulate opening and closing of stomata **OR** control gas exchange **OR** water loss     [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Structure P is the **waxy cuticle** which forms a **water-resistant** barrier to **reduce water loss** from the leaf ($\\checkmark$). Q points to **guard cells**, which **regulate** the **opening and closing** of the **stomata** and thus help **control gas exchange** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **three** of the following (must include at least **one** statement for water entering and at least **one** for water leaving to receive full marks):\n\n    **water movement into Q:** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ becomes turgid    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ bow outwards **OR** curve outwards due to increased internal pressure [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stomata open         [1 mark]\n\n    **water movement out of Q:** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ becomes flaccid         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ collapses inwards **OR** shrinks inwards due to decreased internal pressure    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stomata close    [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    When water enters the guard cells by osmosis, they become **turgid** ($\\checkmark$). This results in them **bending outwards** due to **increased internal pressure** ($\\checkmark$), **opening** the **stomata** (√). When the guard cells lose water, they become **flaccid** ($\\checkmark$), resulting in the **stomata closing** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. 1. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ xylem vessels carry water from the roots to the mesophyll cells **OR** leaves     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to replace water loss from evaporation **OR** transpiration        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ negative pressure gradient **OR** transpirational pull draws water up the xylem vessels     [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Xylem vessels** carry **water** from the **roots** to the **mesophyll cells** ($\\checkmark$). As **water** molecules **evaporate** from the leaves, they create a **negative pressure gradient** within the xylem, which **pulls up more water** from the roots ($\\checkmark$) to **replace that lost** through the stomata ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    2. the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reduces **OR** decreases **OR** slows down            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ because the water potential **OR** concentration gradient between leaf and air is reduced **OR** less steep     [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    High environmental humidity **reduces** the **rate** of transpiration ($\\checkmark$). This is because the **water potential gradient** between the **inside** of the **leaf** and the **surrounding air** is **decreased**  ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ provides a large surface area for gas exchange        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ air spaces for circulation of gases **OR** maintaining concentration gradient of gases **OR** efficient gas exchange            [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    A benefit to the plant of having spongy mesophyll cells, the loosely packed cells, is a **larger surface area** for **gas exchange** ($\\checkmark$) and **more air spaces** for the **circulation of gases** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ palisade mesophyll tissue **OR** layer **OR** cells labelled           [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Gas%20Exchange/images/BI1290e.png\" alt=\"BI1290e\">\n[Source: Science and Plants for Schools, (2012, May 1). *Leaf of a monocot (Lilium sp.)*. Flickr. Retrieved October 11, 2023, from https://www.flickr.com/photos/71183136@N08/6985212944. CC BY-NC-SA 2.0 DEED]"},{"ref":"BI1445","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1445\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Draw a labelled plan to show the distribution of different tissues in a transverse section of a dicotyledonous plant stem. [3 marks]\n\n2. Describe the movement of water in plants beginning with water in the soil and ending with water vapour in the atmosphere. [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain how different environmental factors affect the rate of transpiration. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **three** of the following:\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ correct positioning and labelling of xylem [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ correct positioning and labelling of phloem [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ correct positioning and labelling of cortex [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ correct positioning and labelling of epidermis [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ correct positioning and labelling of pith [1 mark]\n\n[3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Gas%20Exchange/images/drawing%20of%20TS%20of%20stem%20of%20dicot.png\" alt=\"drawing of TS of stem of dicot\">\n\n2. Any **five** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ concentration of mineral ions in the roots is higher than in the surrounding soil [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ (leads to) movement of water into roots by osmosis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ water is pulled upwards in xylem vessels **OR** the stem against gravity from the roots to the leaves [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ (due to) adhesive and cohesive properties of water [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ transpiration pull moves water towards the leaves **OR** up [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ structure of xylem vessels **OR** long continuous narrow tubes of xylem make capillary action possible [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ water evaporates in the inner air space of leaves **OR** from the surface of mesophyll cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ water diffuses out of stomata [1 mark]\n\n [5 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nWater **evaporates** from the surface of mesophyll cells in the inner air space of leaves ($\\checkmark$) and then **diffuses** out of the **stomata** ($\\checkmark$). This **pulls water upwards** ($\\checkmark$)in the stem because of water's **adhesive** and **cohesive properties** ($\\checkmark$). Water enters the roots by **osmosis** ($\\checkmark$) because the **concentration** of mineral **ions** is **higher** in the roots than in the soil ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **seven** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ transpiration involves the loss of water through stomata [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ environmental factors that affect water loss through transpiration are temperature, humidity and wind **OR** air movement [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at higher temperatures, the rate of transpiration increases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** heat increases rate of evaporation in leaves **OR** rate water diffuses out of stomata **OR** the kinetic energy of the water molecules is greater [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at extremely high temperatures, the rate of transpiration may decrease [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** stomata will close to prevent further water loss [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at higher humidity, the rate of transpiration decreases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** when surrounding humidity is high, the rate water evaporates in leaves decreases **OR** the rate water vapour diffuses out of stomata decreases **OR** the concentration gradient between the inside of the leaf and the surrounding air is lower [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at higher light intensity, the rate of transpiration increases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** light intensity stimulates stomata to open allowing water to diffuse out [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increased wind speed increases the rate of transpiration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** wind carries the water vapour away from the stomata increasing the concentration gradient around the pores [1 mark]\n\n [7 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nTranspiration involves the loss of water from leaves through **stomata** ($\\checkmark$). The rate of transpiration is affected by environmental factors such as **temperature, humidity, and air movement** ($\\checkmark$). **Higher temperatures** cause the rate of transpiration to **increase** ($\\checkmark$) because **heat energy** absorbed by water in the inner air space of leaves causes water to **evaporate more rapidly** ($\\checkmark$). **Higher humidity** causes the rate of transpiration to **decrease** ($\\checkmark$). This is because air that is more humid carries more water particles in it which reduces the **concentration gradient** ($\\checkmark$) between the inside of the stomata and outside, thus reducing the rate water diffuses out of the stomata. Lastly, **increases in wind speed** can **increase** the rate of transpiration ($\\checkmark$). Wind carries away water vapour from the surface of leaves, which **increases the concentration gradient** ($\\checkmark$) of water between the inside and outside of the stomata.\n\n*The quality mark is awarded if each part of the question (a, b, c) is answered to earn at least half of the points available, answers do not require re-reading to be understood, and there are almost no errors and little irrelevant information.* [1 mark]"},{"ref":"BI1466","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1466\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe frog’s life cycle is shown in the diagram below. The tadpole lives in water, and the adult frog can live on land or in the water.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Gas%20Exchange/images/BI1466%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1466 fig1\">\n\nThe graph above shows the oxygen dissociation curves of the frogs' and tadpoles' haemoglobin at pH 7.1 and 6.8.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Gas%20Exchange/images/BI1466%20fig2.png\" alt=\"BI1466 fig2\">\n\nDiscuss how the shape and position of the dissociation curves reflect the habitat in which these animals live. [6 marks]","markScheme":"Any **six** of the following\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ curve of tadpole is to the left of an adult frog's curve and is much steeper **OR** frogs, living both on land and in water, have an oxygen dissociation curve positioned more to the right [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ tadpole haemoglobin has higher affinity for oxygen **OR** tadpole haemoglobin binds oxygen more readily and releases it less readily than frog haemoglobin **OR** frog haemoglobin has a lower affinity for oxygen [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ in aquatic environments, partial pressure of oxygen is lower than in air, so tadpole haemoglobin adapted for more efficient extraction of oxygen from water [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ more oxygen in air than water, so frog haemoglobin is more adapted for releasing oxygen at tissues than tadpole haemoglobin [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ pH changes from 7.1 to 6.8 **OR** environment becomes more acidic with increased cellular respiration **OR** increase of carbon dioxide **OR** lactic acid due to increased respiration rate **OR** increased activity [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ tadpole's oxygen dissociation curve does not move with this reduction in pH, indicating their haemoglobin is less sensitive to changes in pH [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ could be an adaptation to the relatively stable levels of carbon dioxide **OR** oxygen in water [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ frog's curve shifts significantly to the right as pH drops **OR** environment becomes more acidic **OR** frog shows strong Bohr effect [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ frogs can, therefore,1465 and 1466 release oxygen more readily to their tissues in acidic conditions **OR** where respiration is occurring more rapidly due to increased movement **OR** stress [1 mark]\n\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nThe oxygen dissociation curve of a **tadpole**, positioned to the **left** and **steeper compared to** that of an **adult frog** ($\\checkmark$), indicates a **higher oxygen affinity** in **tadpole haemoglobin** ($\\checkmark$), essential for **efficient oxygen extraction** in the **aquatic environment** which has a **lower oxygen concentration than air** ($\\checkmark$). This adaptation allows **tadpoles** to **bind oxygen more readily** and **release it less readily**, which contrasts with **frog haemoglobin**, which is adapted more for **releasing oxygen** at **tissues**, an adaptation to the **higher oxygen concentration** in **air** ($\\checkmark$). When the **pH changes** from **7.1 to 6.8**, typically brought about by increased **carbon dioxide** or **lactic acid concentration** from **increased respiration** rate due to increased activity, the **tadpole’s** oxygen dissociation **curve** remains **unchanged**. This indicates **tadpole haemoglobin's reduced sensitivity to pH changes** ($\\checkmark$), possibly an adaptation to the relatively **stable levels** of **carbon dioxide** and **oxygen** in **water** ($\\checkmark$). Conversely, the **frog's** oxygen dissociation curve **shifts** significantly to the **right** under the same conditions, showing a **strong Bohr effect** ($\\checkmark$). This enables **frogs** to **release oxygen more readily** to their **tissues** during **increased activity** or stress. In conclusion, the tadpole's haemoglobin is optimised for acquiring oxygen in water, while the frog's haemoglobin prioritises oxygen delivery in tissues, reflecting the distinct challenges of their contrasting environments."},{"ref":"BI1587","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1587\n\nThe micrograph shows the stomata on the underside of a leaf.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Gas%20Exchange/images/BI1587.png\" alt=\"BI1587\">\n[Source: Adapted from Thongdumhyu, P. (n.d.). *Leaf Epidermis Stomata under microscope*. Dreamstime.com. Retrieved April 26, 2024, from https://www.dreamstime.com/close-up-plant-epidermis-stomata-leaf-under-microscope-image169274602. Royalty free image]\n\nWhich of the following describes the most typical state of the leaf in the micrograph?\n\n| | Guard Cells | Time | Transpiration Rate |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Turgid | Day | High |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Turgid | Night | Low |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Flaccid | Day | Low |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Flaccid | Night | High |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because the micrograph shows that the stomata are open. This is accomplished when the guard cells are turgid. The opening of the stomata allows gas exchange, which indicates that it is daytime and photosynthesis is taking place. A consequence of both photosynthesis and the opening of the stomata is the loss of water, which results in a higher rate of transpiration.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the open stomata indicate that this micrograph was taken during the day and the rate of transpiration would be high, rather than low. **Choice C** is incorrect because the guard cells are turgid to allow the stomata to open, not flaccid, and the transpiration rate would be high instead of low. **Choice D** is incorrect because the guard cells would not be flaccid and it would not be night."},{"ref":"BI2329","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2329\n\nThis micrograph shows a print from the underside of a leaf at 40x magnification. Each square of the grid represents 0.5 $\\ce{mm^2}$. \\\nWhich value is the most accurate for the stomatal density of the leaf tissue in this photomicrograph, based on squares I and II?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Gas%20Exchange/images/BI2329%20nail%20polish%20stomata%20imprint%20with%202%20boxes%20labelled.png\" alt=\"BI2329 nail polish stomata imprint with 2 boxes labelled\">\n[Adapted from Espt123 (2009 October 7). *Nail polish impression of stomata.jpg*. Retrieved November 25th, 2024 from: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Nail_polish_impression_of_stomata.jpg. Public domain.]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 23 stomata per $\\ce{mm^2}$\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 64 stomata per $\\ce{mm^2}$\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 28 stomata per $\\ce{mm^2}$\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 44 stomata per $\\ce{mm^2}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Gas%20Exchange/images/stomatal%20count.png\" alt=\"stomatal count\">\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$Square I = 23 stomata\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$Square II = 21 stomata\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$Total stomata in both squares: 23 + 21 = 44 stomata\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$Total area of both squares: 0.5 $\\ce{mm^2}$ + 0.5 $\\ce{mm^2}$ = 1.0 $\\ce{mm^2}$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$Stomatal density = 44 stomata  1 $\\ce{mm^2}$\n\nTherefore, 44 stomata per 1$\\ce{mm^2}$ is the most accurate value, making **Choice D** correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because this is the number of stomata in square I, which has an area of 0.5 $\\ce{mm^2}$, not 1 $\\ce{mm^2}$.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because 64 stomata per $\\ce{mm^2}$ is too high for the stomatal density in boxes I and II.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because 28 stomata per $\\ce{mm^2}$ is too low for the stomatal density in boxes I and II."},{"ref":"BI2385","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2385\n\nWhich of the following is an **inaccurate** statement about leaves?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Spongy mesophyll cells increase the surface area available for evaporation of water.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Stomata change their cell contents to allow opening and closing.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The waxy cuticle prevents excessive water loss from the leaf by evaporation.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Carbon dioxide absorption is maximised when the rate of photosynthesis is at its peak.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because it is not the stomata that change their cell contents, it is the guard cells. Stomata are just holes between guard cells, so as such are not tangible structures. Guard cells alter their water content so they are curved in shape when turgid; this causes a stoma to open. Guard cells become flaccid at night, and this causes them to straighten, so they lie flat against each other, closing the stoma between them.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are incorrect because they are all accurate statements."},{"ref":"BI2683","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2683\n\nThe table below shows % oxygen saturation at different partial pressures for adult and foetal haemoglobin.\n\n| Partial pressure (kPa) | % Saturation (Foetal Hb) | % Saturation (Adult Hb) |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| 2.0 | 60% | 30% |\n| 4.0 | 85% | 65% |\n| 8.0 | 98% | 90% |\n\nWhat conclusion can be drawn from the data?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Adult haemoglobin is better at binding oxygen at low oxygen pressures.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Foetal haemoglobin binds oxygen more effectively than adult haemoglobin at low oxygen pressures.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ At high oxygen pressures, foetal haemoglobin releases oxygen more easily than adult haemoglobin.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Adult haemoglobin always binds oxygen more strongly than foetal haemoglobin.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because the table shows that at every partial pressure of oxygen, foetal haemoglobin has a higher percentage saturation than adult haemoglobin. This means that foetal haemoglobin has a higher affinity for oxygen, which allows it to extract oxygen from maternal blood efficiently.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the table shows the opposite of this statement: adult haemoglobin has lower saturation than foetal haemoglobin at all partial pressures. This means foetal haemoglobin is better, not worse, at binding oxygen at low oxygen pressures.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because the table shows that at high oxygen pressures (e.g., 8.0 kPa), foetal haemoglobin remains more saturated (98%) than adult haemoglobin (90%). This means foetal haemoglobin holds onto oxygen more tightly, not that it releases oxygen more easily.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the table clearly shows that adult haemoglobin has lower oxygen saturation than foetal haemoglobin at all pressures, meaning foetal haemoglobin binds oxygen more strongly, not adult haemoglobin."},{"ref":"BI2684","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2684\n\nWhich of the following statements about haemoglobin and oxygen transport is/are true?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | The Bohr effect causes haemoglobin to release oxygen in tissues with high carbon dioxide concentrations. |\n    | II. | Cooperative binding means that after the first oxygen binds to haemoglobin, subsequent oxygen molecules bind more easily. |\n    | III. | Foetal haemoglobin has a lower affinity for oxygen than adult haemoglobin to ensure oxygen transfer from mother to fetus. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because the Bohr effect reduces haemoglobin's  affinity for oxygen in tissues with high carbon dioxide concentrations, facilitating oxygen release where it is most needed.\\\n**Statement II** is true because cooperative binding means that once the first oxygen molecule binds to haemoglobin, the molecule undergoes a conformational change, making it easier for subsequent oxygen molecules to bind.\\\n**Statement III** is false because foetal haemoglobin has a higher affinity for oxygen than adult haemoglobin, allowing efficient oxygen transfer from the maternal to foetal blood.\n\n**Choice A** is correct because it contains both true **Statements I** and **II**.\n\n**Choices B, C** and **D** are incorrect because they contain the false **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI2687","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2687\n\nHow does haemoglobin respond to increased carbon dioxide levels?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ It binds oxygen more strongly.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ It shifts to a low-affinity state, releasing more oxygen.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ It prevents $\\ce{CO_2}$ from being transported in the blood.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ It becomes saturated with oxygen more quickly.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because increased $\\ce{CO_2}$ levels cause the Bohr shift, where haemoglobin adopts a lower-affinity state, promoting oxygen release in tissues with high $\\ce{CO_2}$ concentrations.\\\n**Choice A** is incorrect because elevated $\\ce{CO_2}$ reduces haemoglobin’s affinity for oxygen, facilitating oxygen unloading rather than stronger binding.\\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because the Bohr shift does not affect $\\ce{CO_2}$ transport, only oxygen affinity. **Choice D** is incorrect because haemoglobin’s oxygen saturation is determined by oxygen partial pressure (p$\\ce{O_2}$), not $\\ce{CO_2}$ levels."},{"ref":"BI2689","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2689\n\nThe table below shows oxygen saturation levels at different partial pressures of oxygen in two conditions: normal $\\ce{CO_2}$ and high $\\ce{CO_2}$.\n\n|Partial pressure (kPa) | % Saturation (Normal $\\mathbf{CO_2}$) | % Saturation (High $\\mathbf{CO_2}$) |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| 2.0 | 35% | 20% |\n| 4.0 | 65% | 50% |\n| 8.0 | 90% | 80% |\n\nWhich conclusion can be drawn from the table?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ High $\\ce{CO_2}$ increases haemoglobin’s affinity for oxygen.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The Bohr shift reduces haemoglobin’s ability to release oxygen.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ High $\\ce{CO_2}$ promotes oxygen unloading by shifting the dissociation curve right.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The difference in oxygen saturation is due to temperature changes, not $\\ce{CO_2}$.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because the table shows that under high $\\ce{CO_2}$ conditions, haemoglobin has lower oxygen saturation at each partial pressure, indicating a rightward shift of the oxygen dissociation curve. This reflects the Bohr shift, where increased $\\ce{CO_2}$ lowers haemoglobin’s affinity for oxygen, promoting oxygen unloading in tissues.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because high $\\ce{CO_2}$ lowers haemoglobin’s affinity for oxygen, it doesn’t increase it. \\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the Bohr shift enhances, not reduces, oxygen unloading. \\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the data shows the effect of $\\ce{CO_2}$, not temperature."},{"ref":"BI2690","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2690\n\nThe graph below shows two oxygen dissociation curves.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Gas%20Exchange/images/BI2690.png\" alt=\"BI2690\">\n\nWhich of the following best explains why the right-shifted curve occurs?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ A decrease in temperature\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ A decrease in carbon dioxide concentration\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ An increase in blood pH\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ An increase in $\\ce{CO_2}$ levels","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because high $\\ce{CO_2}$ levels lower pH, shifting the curve right, meaning haemoglobin releases more oxygen at a given oxygen partial pressure.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because lower temperature shifts the curve left, increasing oxygen affinity, not right. **Choice B** is incorrect because lower $\\ce{CO_2}$ shifts the curve left, meaning haemoglobin holds onto oxygen more tightly. **Choice C** is incorrect because higher pH shifts the curve left, increasing haemoglobin’s oxygen affinity, opposite to the Bohr shift."},{"ref":"BI2691","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2691\n\nWhich of the following statements explains why the oxygen dissociation curve has this characteristic S-shape?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Haemoglobin’s first oxygen molecule binds with difficulty, but subsequent oxygen molecules bind more easily. |\n    | II. | The steep middle section of the curve results from cooperative binding, where haemoglobin’s affinity for oxygen increases. |\n    | III. | The plateau at high oxygen partial pressures occurs because haemoglobin is nearly fully saturated, limiting further oxygen binding.|\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because at low oxygen partial pressures, haemoglobin is in the Tense (T) state, meaning it has a low affinity for oxygen. This is because the first oxygen molecule binds with difficulty, as the haem groups are not yet exposed in an ideal conformation for oxygen attachment.\\\nThis slow initial binding is why the left end of the oxygen dissociation curve begins with a shallow slope.\n\n**Statement II** is true because the middle section of the curve (around 3–6 kPa) shows a steep increase in saturation. This happens because after the first oxygen binds, haemoglobin undergoes a conformational change that increases its affinity for oxygen. This positive feedback mechanism, known as cooperative binding, results in the rapid uptake of oxygen. As a result, small increases in oxygen partial pressure cause large increases in haemoglobin saturation in this region of the curve.\n\n**Statement III** is true because at high oxygen partial pressures (e.g., 8–10 kPa, found in the lungs), haemoglobin is already nearly fully saturated with oxygen. The oxygen dissociation curve plateaus because there are fewer unoccupied haem groups available for binding. Even though oxygen partial pressure continues to increase, oxygen binding slows down since nearly all haemoglobin molecules are already carrying their maximum of four oxygen molecules.\n\n**Choice D** is correct as it contains all three true statements.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **C** are incorrect as they are missing one or more true statement."}]},{"name":"Membranes & Membrane Transport","questions":[{"ref":"BI0014","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0014\n\nWhich of the structures below can transport water molecules across the cell membrane?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cholesterol\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Glycoproteins\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Protein pumps\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Channel proteins","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWater is transported passively via channel proteins known as aquaporins.\n\nCholesterol (**Choice A**) and glycoproteins (**Choice B**) are not components responsible for transport while protein pumps (**Choice C**) carry out active transport which is not utilised in the transport of water."},{"ref":"BI0015","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0015\n\nWhich of these membrane proteins carry out active transport?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Channel proteins\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Pump proteins\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Peripheral proteins\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Receptors","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nProtein pumps are membrane proteins that pump molecules up their concentration gradient using energy in the form of ATP.\n\nChannel proteins (**Choice A**) carry out passive transport while receptors (**Choice D**) and peripheral proteins  (**Choice C**) do not transport proteins."},{"ref":"BI0016","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0016\n\nWhich of the following is **not** a function of cholesterol in cell membranes?\n\n- A. It reduces membrane fluidity by restricting the movement of phospholipids.\n\n- B. It increases membrane flexibility.\n\n- C. It serves as a transport channel.\n\n- D. It reduces the membrane's  permeability to hydrophilic substances.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCholesterol is a steroid, not a transport channel protein.\n\nReducing membrane fluidity (**Choice A**), increasing membrane flexibility (**Choice B**), and reducing permeability to hydrophilic substances (**Choice D**) are all roles of cholesterol in cell membranes."},{"ref":"BI0017","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0017\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Membranes%20%26%20Membrane%20Transport/images/plasma%20membrane%20with%20peripheral%20protein%20labelled%20X.png\" alt=\"plasma membrane with peripheral protein labelled X\">\n\nWhich component of the cell membrane is represented by structure X?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Transmembrane protein\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Protein channel\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Peripheral protein\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cholesterol molecule","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nStructure X is a peripheral protein that is attached to one surface of the cell membrane.\n\nProtein channels span the width of membranes (**Choice B**) as do transmembrane proteins (**Choice A**). Cholesterol molecules do not have a globular structure (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0018","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0018\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Membranes%20%26%20Membrane%20Transport/images/BI0018.png\" alt=\"BI0018\">\n\nWhich structure represents a component responsible for reducing membrane fluidity at higher temperatures?","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nStructure **D** corresponds to a cholesterol molecule that can reduce membrane fluidity.\n\nStructure **A** corresponds to a glycoprotein, structure **B** corresponds to a glycolipid and structure **C** corresponds to an integral protein. None of these reduces the fluidity of membranes."},{"ref":"BI0019","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0019\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Membranes%20%26%20Membrane%20Transport/images/BI0019.png\" alt=\"BI0019\">\n\nWhich structure on the above diagram corresponds to the molecule below?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Membranes%20%26%20Membrane%20Transport/images/BI0019-.png\" alt=\"BI0019-\">\n[Source: Adapted from OpenStax College. (2013). *Other Important Lipids* [Diagram]. Anatomy and Physiology. Retrieved March 22, 2020, from https://openstax.org/apps/archive/20220228.174637/resources/f9ec6266887f5217851510612e5ddd14fd6486a1. Copyright under CC BY 3.0]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nStructure **D** corresponds to a phospholipid like the one whose chemical structure is represented in the diagram.\n\nStructure **A** corresponds to a glycoprotein, structure **B** corresponds to a glycolipid and structure **C** corresponds to a cholesterol molecule."},{"ref":"BI0020","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0020\n\nWhich statement correctly describes the cell membrane structure?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ A phospholipid bilayer where phosphate heads align facing each other and lipid tails point away.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ A phospholipid bilayer where lipid tails align facing each other and phosphate heads point away.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ A phospholipid bilayer where proteins line the outer layer and phospholipids line the inner layer.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ A layer of phospholipids with phosphate heads facing outside the cell and lipid tails facing the cell cytoplasm.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA cell membrane is formed of two layers of phospholipids whereby hydrophilic phosphate heads face the cytoplasm of the cell (cell interior) and the cell's environment (exterior) while the hydrophobic lipid tails face each other and form the interior of the membrane.\n\nHaving lipid tails pointing away (**Choice A**) is not a valid choice since lipid tails are hydrophobic and cannot face the cell's aqueous interior and exterior. Proteins are embedded in the membrane and do not line the outer layer (**Choice C**). The cell membrane is a bilayer and not just a monolayer (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0023","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0023\n\nWhich of the following is **not** a function of membrane proteins?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cell adhesion\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cell-to-cell communication\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Transport of oxygen gas\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Enzymatic action to catalyse chemical reactions","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nOxygen gas is exchanged by simple diffusion,  it diffuses freely across the lipid bilayer of the cell membrane due to its small size and nonpolar nature and so does not need to be transported by a membrane protein. **Choice C** is therefore the correct answer to this question.\n\nCell adhesion is a function of membrane proteins; this is fundamental for the formation of tissues and organs and is mediated by specific adhesion molecules. **Choice A** is, therefore, an incorrect answer to this question.\n\nMembrane proteins are essential for cell communication. They can act as receptors that bind signalling molecules (ligands) such as hormones and neurotransmitters, which trigger internal cellular responses. **Choice B** is, therefore, an incorrect answer to this question.\n\nSome membrane proteins are enzymes that catalyse biochemical reactions on the inner or outer surface of the cell membrane, e.g. ATP synthase. **Choice D** is, therefore, an incorrect answer to this question."},{"ref":"BI0402","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0402\n\nWhich of the following statements does **not** describe a step in the sodium-potassium pump cycle in the neuronal cells?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Three sodium ions attach to their binding sites in the pump from the interior of the cell.\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The pump changes shape upon being phosphorylated, and sodium ions are released to the outside.\n\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Three potassium ions attach to their binding sites in the pump from the exterior of the cell.\n\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The pump changes shape upon releasing the phosphate group, and potassium ions are released to the interior of the cell.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nTwo potassium ions, not three, are transported in the sodium-potassium pump cycle.\n\nThe sodium-potassium pump cycle is as follows: three sodium ions attach to their binding sites (**Choice A**), the pump changes shape to release the sodium ions to the exterior of the cell (**Choice B**) and finally, the pump changes shape again to release two potassium ions to the interior of the cell (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0403","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0403\n\nWhich one of the following transport proteins only carries out active transport?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Protein channels\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Protein pumps\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Protein carriers\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Aquaporins","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nProtein pumps are membrane proteins that carry out active transport.  They require the energy to pump molecules up their concentration gradient.\n\nProtein channels (**Choice A**), protein carriers (**Choice C**), and aquaporins (**Choice D**) all conduct passive transport."},{"ref":"BI0406","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0406\n\nWhich type of transport is represented by this diagram?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Membranes%20%26%20Membrane%20Transport/images/BI0406ii.png\" alt=\"BI0406ii\">\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Active transport\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Simple diffusion\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Facilitated diffusion\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Protein channel","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nFacilitated diffusion occurs with the help of protein channels or carrier proteins.\n\nActive transport (**Choice A**) requires energy expenditure in the form of ATP and moves substances up their concentration gradient. Simple diffusion (**Choice B**) occurs through the cell membrane without the use of membrane proteins. Protein channels (**Choice D**) often carry out facilitated diffusion, but they are not themselves a type of transport."},{"ref":"BI0407","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0407\n\nWhich of the following types of membrane transport do **NOT** require energy expenditure?\n\n-    I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Facilitated diffusion\n-    II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ Active transport\n-    III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ Osmosis\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nFacilitated diffusion and osmosis are both passive means of transport and therefore do not require energy.\n\nActive transport (**Choice A**) requires energy expenditure.  Facilitated diffusion is a passive form of transport but active transport requires energy (**Choice B**).  Facilitated diffusion (**Choice D**) does not require energy expenditure but this answer choice does not include osmosis."},{"ref":"BI0576","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0576\n\nThe important properties of animal cell membranes are fluidity and permeability. Which molecule is responsible for ensuring the membrane’s fluidity?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cholesterol\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Integral membrane proteins\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Glycolipids\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Peripheral membrane proteins","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** - Cholesterol is positioned between the phospholipids and maintains the fluidity of the cell membrane.\n\n**Choice B** - Integral Membrane Proteins are responsible for the transport of materials in and out of the cell as well as other functions such as cell-cell communication.\n\n**Choice C** - Glycolipids allow cells to recognize each other.\n\n**Choice D** - Peripheral membrane proteins are involved in maintaining the extracellular matrix and cytoskeleton, in cell-cell communication, and sometimes function as enzymes."},{"ref":"BI0812","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0812\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe table shows the results from a study on the percentage of cholesterol found in the cell membrane of epithelial skin cells of different animals.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Membranes%20%26%20Membrane%20Transport/images/BI0812A.png\" alt=\"BI0812A\">\n\n1. Calculate the mean percentage of cholesterol found in the cell membrane across all the animals listed in the table. [1 mark]\n\n2. State one role of cholesterol in the cell membrane. [1 mark]\n\n3. Suggest why arctic foxes have a greater mean percentage of cholesterol in their cell membranes than kangaroos. [2 marks]\n\n4. Estimate, with a reason, the percentage of cholesterol that would be found in the cell membranes of polar bears. [1 mark]\n\n5. Explain why the mass of cholesterol in the cell membranes is calculated as a percentage rather than a whole number. [2 marks]\n\n6. Suggest why the cells studied may contain a higher percentage of cholesterol than shown in the table. [1 mark]\n\n7.\n 1. Based on the data provided, suggest which animal in the study exhibits the highest fluidity in its epithelial skin cell membrane. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Explain your answer to part (i). [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 17.3 % **OR** 17.29 % **OR** 17.286 % [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n mean = sum of percentages / number of animals\n\n (10 + 12 + 15 + 18 + 28 + 8 + 30) ÷ 7 = **17.3** % ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to regulate the fluidity of the membrane **OR** to reduce rigidity of the membrane at lower temperatures **OR** to prevent excessive fluidity at higher temperatures [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ reduces the permeability of the membrane to hydrophilic particles **OR** reduces the permeability of the cell membrane to ions **OR** small polar molecules [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n One role of cholesterol in the cell membrane is to **regulate the fluidity** ($\\checkmark$) of the membrane.\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ arctic foxes live in cooler environments **OR** kangaroos live in warmer environments [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ a higher percentage of cholesterol will lower the temperature at which the membrane freezes **OR** becomes too rigid [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ so the membrane will be able to carry out normal functions efficiently **OR** maintain fluidity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ arctic foxes have a higher dietary intake of cholesterol than kangaroos **OR** kangaroos have a diet lower in cholesterol than arctic foxes [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Arctic foxes have more cholesterol in their cell membrane than kangaroos as **arctic foxes live in cooler environments** ($\\checkmark$) and a higher percentage of cholesterol in their membranes will **prevent** the membrane from **freezing** ($\\checkmark$), thus **maintaining the fluidity** of the membrane ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **one** of the following:\n\n *Answer must include a value in the correct range and a reason for the mark*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ value must be between 25% and 35% **BECAUSE** it lives in a similar climate to the arctic fox **OR** penguin [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ value must be between 25% and 35% **BECAUSE** they have a similar diet to the arctic fox **OR** a diet high in marine animals, which tend to have a higher cholesterol content [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **30%** ($\\checkmark$) because the polar bear lives in a similar, **cold** environment to the **arctic fox** and the **penguin** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cell membrane may be of different length, **OR** width, in different organisms [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to make the values comparable [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ easier to determine percent cholesterol from a sample of cells than to determine the mass of cholesterol in a single cell [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The mass of cholesterol is calculated as a percentage rather than a whole number, as the cell membrane may be of **different length and width** ($\\checkmark$) in different organisms and so it allows the values to be **compared** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the table only takes into account cholesterol present in the cell membrane [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ organelles **OR** endoplasmic reticulum **OR** nucleus **OR** Golgi apparatus **OR** mitochondria **OR** vacuoles **OR** lysosomes are membrane-bound and so also contain cholesterol [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The cells studied may contain a higher percentage of cholesterol than shown in the table, as the table **only** takes into account the **cholesterol that is present in the cell membrane** ($\\checkmark$) rather than the whole cell.\n\n7.\n 1. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ African elephant [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Arctic fox [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **African elephant** ($\\checkmark$) has the most fluid cell membrane.\n\n7.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n *African elephant because:*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at high temperatures cholesterol reduces ability of phospholipids to move, making the membrane more rigid **OR** less fluid **AND** African elephant has the lowest cholesterol [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cholesterol reduces the fluidity of plasma membrane at high temperatures **AND** African elephant has the lowest cholesterol and lives in warm environments [1 mark]\n\n *Actic fox because:*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at low temperatures cholesterol increases ability of phospholipids to move, making the membrane more fluid **AND** Arctic fox has the highest cholesterol and lives in cold environments [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Cholesterol **limits the fluidity** of the plasma membrane at **high temperatures**, and the African elephant, which lives in **warm environments**, has the **lowest percentage of cholesterol** in the epithelial cell membrane ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0935","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0935\n\nThe diagram shows a polar bear (*Ursus maritimus*), which is native to the Arctic. Which statement about the composition of its plasma membranes is correct?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Membranes%20%26%20Membrane%20Transport/images/BI0935A.png\" alt=\"BI0935A\">\n[Source: Adapted from Dungarees, E. (2011, November 13). *File: Dungarees the polar bear (Wapusk National Park) (6375873931).jpg*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved 31 July 2023 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dungarees_the_polar_bear_%28Wapusk_National_Park%29_%286375873931%29.jpg. Copyright free]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ They contain a higher percentage of saturated fatty acids compared to unsaturated fatty acids.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ They contain an equal percentage of saturated and unsaturated fatty acids.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ They contain a higher percentage of unsaturated fatty acids compared to saturated fatty acids.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ They only contain unsaturated fatty acids.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nUnsaturated fatty acids have one or more double bonds in their hydrocarbon chains, creating kinks and preventing the fatty acids from packing tightly. This means that unsaturated fatty acids help to maintain membrane fluidity at lower temperatures, preventing the membrane from becoming too rigid and allowing cells to function properly even in the cold. However, the membrane does not just contain unsaturated fatty acids (**Choice D**), as a combination of saturated and unsaturated fatty acids contributes to the overall properties of the plasma membrane, such as permeability, stability, and responsiveness to environmental changes. **Choice C** is therefore correct.\n\nSaturated fatty acids have no double bonds between the carbon atoms in their hydrocarbon chains, making them relatively straight and able to pack closely together. Having a high percentage of saturated fatty acids in the membrane (**Choice A**) would result in a more rigid and less fluid membrane. **Choice B** is incorrect as plasma membranes in polar bears do not have an equal percentage of saturated and unsaturated fatty acids as this, also,  would not allow plasma membranes to maintain the required fluidity."},{"ref":"BI0936","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0936\n\nThe diagram shows a plasma membrane.  Which letter indicates cholesterol?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Membranes%20%26%20Membrane%20Transport/images/BI0939image.png\" alt=\"BI0939image\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** indicates a cholesterol molecule between the phospholipids.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect because it is indicating a carbohydrate that is attached to a glycoprotein. **Choice B**  is incorrect because it is indicating a channel protein. **Choice C**  is incorrect as it is pointing to the lipid tail of a phospholipid."},{"ref":"BI0937","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0937\n\nWhich statement describes endocytosis?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Protein-containing vesicles from the Golgi apparatus fusing with the cell membrane\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The contractile vacuole in *Paramecium* expelling water\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Oxygen diffusing from a red blood cell to a respiring tissue\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ A bacterium being taken up by a macrophage.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe macrophage, a type of white blood cell, engulfs the bacterium through the process of phagocytosis, a specific form of endocytosis.  The cell membrane surrounds the bacterium and forms a vesicle, called a phagosome, which then fuses with a lysosome. The resulting phagolysosome contains enzymes that break down and destroy the bacterium.  **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it is describing exocytosis, the process by which substances are expelled from cells via vesicles merging with the plasma membrane and releasing their contents to the external environment. **Choice B** is incorrect as it is a process which involves the expelling of water from the cell rather than taking a substance into the cell. **Choice C** is incorrect as it is an example of simple diffusion, the random movement of particles from an area of high concentration to an area of low concentration."},{"ref":"BI0938","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0938\n\nHow is the action of sodium-dependent glucose transporters in the small intestine, an example of indirect active transport?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Facilitated diffusion of sodium provides energy for the active transport of glucose into the cells of the small intestine.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The Na:sup[+] / K:sup[+] pump actively transports sodium ions out of the cell to drive the co-transport of glucose into the cell via the sodium-dependent glucose transporter.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Active transport of sodium via the Na:sup[+] / K:sup[+] pump creates a sodium gradient allowing sodium to carry glucose into the cells of the small intestine.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Glucose is pumped into, and sodium is pumped out of the cells of the small intestine using the sodium-dependent glucose transporter.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSodium-dependent glucose transporters are membrane proteins found in the cells of the small intestine. The sodium-potassium pump actively transports sodium ions out of the cell and potassium ions into the cell. The sodium ions move back into the cell, down their concentration gradient through the sodium-dependent glucose transporter proteins, carrying glucose molecules with them. This is an example of indirect active transport, so **Choice B**  is correct.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect as facilitated diffusion does not provide energy; it allows molecules to move down their concentration gradient without energy input. **Choice C**  is incorrect because sodium itself does not carry glucose across the plasma membrane of cells, the sodium-dependent glucose transporter does. **Choice D**  is incorrect as the sodium-dependent glucose transporter does not pump glucose into the cell; it co-transports glucose along with sodium ions."},{"ref":"BI0939","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0939\n\nWhich row about the different methods of membrane transport is **incorrect**?\n\n| | Membrane transport | Requires ATP? | Is a cotransporter? | Transports ions |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Sodium potassium pump | ✓ | ✗ | ✓ |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Exocytosis | ✓ | ✗ | ✗ |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Sodium dependent glucose cotransporters | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Facilitated diffusion| ✗ | ✗ | ✓|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSodium-dependent glucose cotransporters (SGLTs) do not directly use ATP. Instead, they rely on the sodium ion (Na⁺) gradient, which is actively maintained by the sodium-potassium pump (a process that does require ATP). SGLTs are symporters, a type of cotransporter, as they move Na⁺ ions and glucose together in the same direction into the cell. Since row C incorrectly states that SGLTs directly require ATP, **Choice C** is the correct answer to the question.\n\nThe sodium-potassium pump is a primary active transport mechanism as it directly hydrolyses ATP to pump three sodium ions out and two potassium ions into the cell. It is not a cotransporter because it does not rely on an ion gradient to transport another substance—it actively moves ions against their concentration gradients using ATP.  Since row A is true, **Choice A** is an incorrect answer to the question.\n\nExocytosis requires ATP for the fusion of vesicles with the plasma membrane to release contents outside the cell. It is also a bulk transport process that does not involve cotransporters, and it does not specifically transport ions but instead releases larger molecules, like proteins or hormones. Since row B is true of exocytosis, **Choice B** is an incorrect answer to the question.\n\nFacilitated diffusion is a passive transport process, so it does not require ATP. It does not involve cotransporters but uses specific protein channels or carriers for the movement of substances, including ions. Since row D is true of facilitated diffusion, **Choice D** is an incorrect answer to the question."},{"ref":"BI0940","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0940\n\nWhich allows cells to stick together to form tissues?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cell cohesion molecules\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Channel proteins\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cell adhesion molecules\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cell-cell junction molecules","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCell adhesion molecules (CAMs) are cell surface proteins that help cells bind together and maintain the structural integrity of tissues, **Choice C**  is therefore correct.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect as it is a made up term. **Choice B**  is incorrect, as channel proteins allow molecules to move across the plasma membrane using facilitated diffusion. **Choice D**  is incorrect, as cell-cell junctions are specialised structures that physically connect adjacent cells, facilitating communication and coordinating cellular activities within tissues. Cell-cell junctions can only be formed after the process of cell adhesion has occurred."},{"ref":"BI1054","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1054\n\nWhich of the following is evidence to support the fluid mosaic model of the plasma membrane?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Membrane fusion experiments revealed membrane proteins can diffuse from one position to another in the phospholipid bilayer. |\n    | II. | Analysis of membrane proteins indicated that some amino acids of membrane proteins were hydrophobic. |\n    | III. | Freeze-etching studies of plasma membranes revealed no proteins embedded within the fatty acid tail region. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn the fluid mosaic model of the plasma membrane, shows proteins are able to move within the phospholipid bilayer. The finding that proteins can move throughout the membrane supported this model and refuted earlier models. **Statement I** is true.\n\nAdditionally, the discovery that some amino acids in membrane proteins are hydrophobic further supports the fluid mosaic model. Integral membrane proteins contain hydrophobic regions, which allow them to interact with the hydrophobic fatty acid tails of phospholipids and remain embedded in the bilayer. This structural adaptation is consistent with the fluid mosaic model. Therefore, **Statement II** is true.\n\nHowever, Statement III is not true. Freeze-etching studies actually provided evidence for embedded membrane proteins, rather than showing their absence. This technique, which involves rapidly freezing and fracturing the membrane, revealed integral proteins spanning the bilayer. If freeze-etching had shown no proteins within the fatty acid tail region, it would contradict the fluid mosaic model, which clearly describes integral proteins as embedded within the bilayer. Therefore, **Statement III** does not support the fluid mosaic model.\n\n**Choice B** is correct, as only **Statements I** and **II** are true.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as it is missing **Statement II**.\n\n**Choices C** and **D** are incorrect as they include the untrue **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI1055","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1055\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Draw and label the fluid mosaic model of a cell’s plasma membrane. [4 marks]\n\n2. Distinguish between integral and peripheral proteins. [4 marks]\n\n3. Compare and contrast the structure and function of glycoproteins and glycolipids in the plasma membrane. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ integral protein across the bilayer [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ protein channel across the bilayer [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ phospholipid bilayer with phosphate heads pointing outside and lipid tails pointing inwards [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ peripheral protein (on surface of the bilayer) **OR** embedded protein (half-way into the membrane) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycoprotein **OR** glycolipid [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cholesterol between the phospholipid tails [1 mark]\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Membranes%20%26%20Membrane%20Transport/images/BI1055.png\" alt=\"BI1055\">\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ integral proteins are permanently attached to the cell membrane **WHILE** peripheral proteins are temporarily attached [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ integral proteins are embedded across the cell membrane **WHILE** peripheral proteins are attached to one surface of the membrane only [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ integral proteins have both a hydrophobic region and a hydrophilic region **WHILE** peripheral proteins are only/mostly hydrophilic [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ integral proteins mainly serve as channels, transporters, or pumps **WHILE** peripheral proteins do not transport materials across the cell membrane [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ integral proteins are held in the cell membrane by hydrophobic interactions **WHILE** peripheral proteins held in place by integral proteins of the membrane, or cytoskeleton [1 mark]\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n | Integral Proteins | Peripheral Proteins | |\n | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n | Integral proteins are **embedded** across the cell membrane | Peripheral proteins are either attached to the **outer** surface of the membrane | $\\hspace{2em}$ ($\\checkmark$) $\\hspace{2em}$ |\n | Integral proteins have **both** a hydrophobic and a hydrophilic region | Peripheral proteins are **hydrophilic** | $\\hspace{2em}$ ($\\checkmark$) $\\hspace{2em}$ |\n | Integral proteins function as **channel** proteins, carrier proteins and protein pumps |peripheral proteins have **no transport** function across the cell membrane | $\\hspace{2em}$ ($\\checkmark$) $\\hspace{2em}$ |\n | Integral proteins are held in the membrane by **electrostatic bonds** (hydrophobic) | Peripheral proteins on the inside surface of the plasma membrane are **held by the cytoskeleton** | $\\hspace{2em}$ ($\\checkmark$) $\\hspace{2em}$ |\n\n3. any **eight** of the following (must include at least **two** similarities and **two** differences to gain full marks):\n\n ***Similarities:*** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have carbohydrate chains as part of their structure [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** glycoproteins and glycolipids are involved in cell recognition [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contribute to cell adhesion [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbohydrate chains on **BOTH** molecules act as unique markers that allow cells to interact with their environment [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** types of molecules contribute to the glycocalyx, providing protection and lubrication for the cell surface [1 mark]\n\n ***Differences:*** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycoproteins consist of a protein backbone with attached carbohydrate chains, **WHEREAS** glycolipids consist of phospholipid with attached carbohydrate chains [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycoproteins have a variety of roles **WHEREAS** glycolipids primarily contribute to membrane stability and lubrication [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycoproteins act as hormone receptors, **WHEREAS** glycolipids do not [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycoproteins can have enzymatic roles **WHEREAS** glycolipids do not [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycolipids play a more direct role **THAN** glycoproteins in affecting membrane fluidity due to their lipid component [1 mark]\n [7 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Both** glycoproteins and glycolipids have **carbohydrate chains** ($\\checkmark$) as part of their structure, which act as **unique markers** that allow **interaction** with the environment ($\\checkmark$). However, in **glycoproteins** the carbohydrate is attached to a protein whereas, in **glycolipids** it is attached to a **phospholipid** ($\\checkmark$). **Both** glycolipids and glycoproteins are involved in **cell recognition** ($\\checkmark$) and contribute to **cell adhesion** ($\\checkmark$). However, **glycoproteins** have a **greater variety** of roles than glycolipids ($\\checkmark$), which are **primarily** involved in **membrane stability** and lubrication ($\\checkmark$). **Glycoproteins** can also act as **hormone receptors** ($\\checkmark$) and **enzymes** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1138","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1138\n\nWhat is true about the membrane structure in the image below?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Membranes%20%26%20Membrane%20Transport/images/liposome.png\" alt=\"liposome\">\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n | | |\n | :-- | :-- |\n | I. | It is a lipid bilayer. |\n | II. | It contains cholesterol. |\n | III. | It contains both hydrophobic and hydrophilic components. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe image depicts a liposome, which has two layers of lipids (a bilayer), each containing hydrophilic heads and hydrophobic tails.\n\n**Choices A, B**, and **D** cannot be correct because this membrane does not include cholesterol."},{"ref":"BI1139","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1139\n\nWhat type of transport is depicted in the image below?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Membranes%20%26%20Membrane%20Transport/images/BI1139.png\" alt=\"BI1139\">\n[Source: Adapted from Laboratoires Servier (2019, September 29). *File: Endocytosis_2_--_Smart-Servier.png* - wikimedia commons. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved September 20, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Endocytosis_2_--_Smart-Servier.png Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Endocytosis\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Simple diffusion\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Facilitated diffusion\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Exocytosis","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe image shows endocytosis.\n\n**Choices B** and **C** are incorrect since they are types of passive transport. **Choice D** is incorrect since this is endocytosis, not exocytosis."},{"ref":"BI1378","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1378\n\nThe following diagram shows the structure of a plasma membrane. What is the function of the structure labelled X?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Membranes%20%26%20Membrane%20Transport/images/BI1378b.png\" alt=\"BI1378b\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Modulates membrane fluidity\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Aids in the facilitated diffusion of glucose\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increases membrane permeability to ions and polar molecules\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Acts as a surface receptor to communicate with other cells","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe molecule shown in the diagram is cholesterol. **Choice A** is correct because cholesterol plays an important role in maintaining the structural integrity and fluidity of the plasma membrane.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect since glucose transport is controlled by carrier proteins, not cholesterol. **Choice C** is incorrect since cholesterol reduces the membrane’s permeability to substances such as ions and polar molecules rather than increasing it. **Choice D** is incorrect as surface receptors are generally glycoproteins or glycolipids, not cholesterol."},{"ref":"BI1472","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1472\n\n**Maximum mark: 1**\n\nWhich of the following does not use amphipathic properties to help form the structure of animal cell membranes?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Cholesterol\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Glycoproteins\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Phospholipids\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Peripheral proteins","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nAmphipathic molecules are partly hydrophobic and partly hydrophilic. Although proteins are often amphipathic, peripheral proteins are not embedded **within** the phospholipid bilayer. They are loosely attached to the surface of the membrane, often by interacting with the hydrophilic head groups of phospholipids or other proteins. They don't rely on amphipathic properties for this attachment. **Choice D**  is correct.\n\nCholesterol is amphipathic as it consists of a polar hydroxyl (OH) group whereas the rest of the molecule is nonpolar. The hydroxyl group can form hydrogen bonds with the phospholipid heads, while the hydrophobic steroid rings sit between the fatty acid tails. **Choice A** is therefore an incorrect answer to this question. Glycoproteins, with attached carbohydrate chains, are embedded in the phospholipid bilayer, often with their hydrophilic parts facing the outside or inside of the cell, and their hydrophobic parts interacting with the fatty acid tails. So, they too use amphipathic properties therefore **Choice B** is incorrect. Phospholipids are amphipathic as they consist of a polar, hydrophilic head and non-polar hydrophobic tail. This allows them to arrange themselves in a double layer with the hydrophilic heads facing the watery environment inside and outside the cell, and the hydrophobic tails facing each other in the interior of the membrane; **Choice C**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1504","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1504\n\nCholesterol molecules found in membranes affect the fluidity of the membrane.  Which of the following statements is/are true for cholesterol found in membranes?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n\n    | | |\n\n    | :-- | :-- |\n\n    | I. | Interacts with the phosphate head and fatty acid tails |\n\n    | II. | Stabilises membranes at high temperatures |\n\n    | III. | Increases rigidity at lower temperatures |\n\n| | |\n\n| --- | :-- |\n\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | I only |\n\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | I and II only |\n\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | II and III only |\n\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | I, II and III |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\n**Statement I** is true because The polar hydroxyl group of cholesterol forms hydrogen bonds with the polar phosphate heads of phospholipids, positioning cholesterol in the membrane. The nonpolar, hydrophobic four-ring steroid structure and hydrocarbon tail interact with the fatty acid tails of phospholipids. Cholesterol fits between phospholipid molecules, affecting the packing of the fatty acid tails.\n\n**Statement II** is true because cholesterol stabilises membranes at high temperatures by reducing excessive fluidity. At elevated temperatures, lipid molecules move rapidly, increasing fluidity, which can harm the cell's integrity and function. Cholesterol disrupts the close packing of lipids, reducing their movement and overall membrane fluidity, thus maintaining membrane integrity.\n\n**Statements I** and **II** are the only true statements, making **Choice B** the correct answer. \n\n**Statement III** is not true, as at lower temperatures, cholesterol maintains membrane fluidity by preventing phospholipid tails from packing too tightly. This prevents the membrane from becoming too rigid and ensures it remains flexible enough to function properly. Instead of increasing rigidity, cholesterol ensures the membrane retains its flexibility in cold conditions.\n\n**Choice A** is missing **Statement II** so is not correct. **Choices C** and **D** both include **Statement III**, so are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1510","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1510\n\nWhich of the following are examples of molecules that are cotransported with sodium through indirect active transport?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n\n    | | |\n\n    | :-- | :-- |\n\n    | I. | Glucose absorption into small intestine epithelial cells |\n\n    | II. | Potassium through sodium-potassium pumps |\n\n    | III. | Glucose reabsorption in nephrons |\n\n| | |\n\n| --- | :-- |\n\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | I only |\n\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | I and II only |\n\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | I and III only |\n\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | I, II and III |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nIndirect active transport, also known as secondary active transport, involves the use of energy stored from an electrochemical gradient of one molecule to transport another molecule against the concentration gradient.\n\nGlucose is cotransported with sodium ions from the small intestine lumen into epithelial cells via sodium-glucose cotransporters (SGLTs). The sodium-potassium pump maintains a low intracellular sodium concentration by expelling sodium, indirectly providing the energy for glucose absorption against its concentration gradient; **Statement I** is true.\n\nThe sodium-potassium pump uses ATP for direct active transport, moving sodium out and potassium into the cell against their concentration gradients. It maintains the sodium gradient crucial for indirect active transport but does not involve the cotransport of sodium with another molecule. This gradient enables indirect active transport.; **Statement II** is therefore not true. \n\nGlucose reabsorption in nephrons is an example of indirect active transport. In the kidneys, particularly in the proximal tubule, glucose is reabsorbed from the filtrate alongside sodium ions via sodium-glucose cotransporters. The sodium-potassium pump maintains the sodium concentration gradient necessary for this cotransport, enabling glucose to be reabsorbed against its concentration gradient; **Statement III** is, therefore, true. \n\n**Statements I** and **III** are true, so **Choice C** is the correct answer to this question. \n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as it does not contain **Statement III**. **Choices B** and **D** include **Statement II** so are not correct."},{"ref":"BI1511","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1511\n\nThe diagram below represents a voltage-gated sodium channel in a neuron. Which is likely the **function** of structure I, after it has gone through this conformational change, and the **effect** of structure II in a neuron?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Membranes%20%26%20Membrane%20Transport/images/sodium%20v-g%20channel%20ii.png\" alt=\"sodium v-g channel ii\">\n\n| | I | II |\n\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$Inactivation of channel$\\hspace{3em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$Depolarisation$\\hspace{3em}$ |\n\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Inactivation of channel | Repolarisation |\n\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Activation of channel | Depolarisation |\n\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Activation of channel | Repolarisation |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\n**Label I** indicates the activation of the voltage-gated channel. **Label II** indicates Na:sup[+] diffusion, which leads to depolarisation in a neuron. **Choice C** is, therefore, correct.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **D** are all incorrect as they do not have both the correct function of structure I and the effect of structure II."},{"ref":"BI1538","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1538\n\nWhat type of transport is phagocytosis?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Endocytosis, which is a form of transport requiring energy\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Endocytosis, which is a form of passive transport\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Exocytosis, which is a form of transport requiring energy\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Exocytosis, which is a form of passive transport","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nPhagocytosis, carried out by phagocytic cells, is a type of bulk transport that involves moving large particles into the cell using energy. This process is a form of endocytosis and is classified as a transport mechanism that requires energy, similar to active transport. Choice A is therefore the correct answer to this question.\n\nEndocytosis requires energy expenditure and is therefore not passive (Choice B). Pinocytosis moves material into the cell, so is considered a type of endocytosis, not exocytosis (Choices C and D)."},{"ref":"BI1558","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1558\n\nWhich of the following structures plays an important role in modulating membrane fluidity at different temperatures?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Membranes%20%26%20Membrane%20Transport/images/BI1558ii.png\" alt=\"BI1558ii\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because the structure indicated is cholesterol. Cholesterol is embedded within the phospholipid bilayer and plays a critical role in modulating membrane fluidity by stabilising the membrane at higher temperatures and preventing it from becoming too rigid at lower temperatures.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it represents a glycoprotein, which is involved in cell recognition and immune responses. **Choice B** is incorrect because it represents a channel protein which is involved in transporting substances. **Choice C** is incorrect because it represents the phosphate head of a phospholipid, which does not directly influence membrane fluidity, but rather contributes to the overall structure and organisation of the membrane."},{"ref":"BI1559","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1559\n\nWhich of the following processes does **not** require active transport by membrane proteins?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Reabsorption of amino acids from the filtrate of the kidneys.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Movement of glucose into muscle cells.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Transport of calcium ions out of the presynaptic neuron following an action potential.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Transport of sodium ions out of the cytoplasm following an action potential.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn some areas of the body, such as the kidneys and intestines, glucose is actively transported against its concentration gradient. However, muscle cells typically use a lot of glucose in respiration, resulting in a low internal glucose concentration. Consequently, glucose moves from a high concentration outside the muscle cell to a low concentration inside the cell via facilitated diffusion; **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the reabsorption of amino acids from the filtrate in the proximal convoluted tubule of the kidney nephron is an example of indirect active transport. This process relies on the active transport of sodium ions out of the tubule wall cells, creating a concentration gradient that facilitates the movement of amino acids via cotransporter proteins into these cells from the filtrate. **Choice C** is incorrect because calcium ions are actively transported out of the neuron against their concentration gradient using calcium pumps.\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the action of the sodium-potassium pump following depolarisation relies on active transport due to the charges of sodium and potassium ions and the fact that these molecules are pumped against their concentration gradients."},{"ref":"BI2326","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2326\n\nMembrane permeability is determined by the mosaic of membrane components. Which structure in the membrane facilitates the transport of small, hydrophobic molecules?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Membranes%20%26%20Membrane%20Transport/images/Cell%20membrane%20labelled%20A-D%20BI2326.png\" alt=\"Cell membrane labelled A-D BI2326\">\n[Source:Adapted from LadyofHats (2012, June 13). *Cell membrane detailed diagram blank.svg*. Wikimedia. Retrieved December 6, 2024, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Cell_membrane_detailed_diagram_blank.svg. Public Domain]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because the phospholipid bilayer facilitates the transport of small, hydrophobic molecules via simple diffusion. Small and hydrophobic molecules can pass directly through the phospholipid bilayer's hydrophobic core without requiring a protein channel or carrier. The size and properties of the molecules determine the bilayer's selective permeability.\n\n**Choice  A** is incorrect because transmembrane proteins facilitate the transport of molecules via facilitated diffusion or active transport, which are required for larger, hydrophilic, or charged particles. These proteins are not involved in the simple diffusion of hydrophobic molecules.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because cholesterol primarily regulates the fluidity and stability of the membrane at different temperatures. It does not directly influence the movement of small, hydrophobic molecules through simple diffusion.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because glycoproteins are involved in cell recognition and adhesion. They play no role in the direct transport of molecules across the membrane."},{"ref":"BI2381","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2381\n\nIn neonatal mammals, intestinal epithelial cells acquire antibodies from maternal milk by taking in fluid via vesicle formation.\\\nWhich term best describes this process?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Phagocytosis\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Endocytosis\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Pinocytosis\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Exocytosis","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because pinocytosis is the uptake of liquid in bulk using vesicles - which carry material in quantities more than one molecule at a time. The latter would happen in active transport or diffusion across the bilayer.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because phagocytosis is the transport of solid particles into a cell such as the process that precedes the destruction of live pathogens in the human immune system.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because endocytosis is a broad term that refers to uptake into a cell, whether in liquid form (pinocytosis) or solid form (phagocytosis). As such, the more precise answer, **Choice C**, is a subset of **Choice B**.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because exocytosis occurs when vesicles inside the cell fuse with the cell membrane and eject material out of the cell."},{"ref":"BI2440","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2440\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nBelow is a diagram representing the interaction between two cells.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Membranes%20%26%20Membrane%20Transport/images/BI2440.png\" alt=\"BI2440\">\n\n1.\n Identify the type of molecule X represents in the diagram. [1 mark]\n\n2. Explain the importance of these molecules in facilitating cell adhesion. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycoprotein [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The molecule is likely to be a **glycoprotein** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbohydrate chains on glycoproteins interact with receptors **OR** complementary molecules on the surface of adjacent cells **WHICH** allows cells to recognise and adhere to each other [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycoproteins help form tight junctions **WHICH** creates a barrier that prevents the passage of molecules between cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycoproteins form desmosomes **WHICH** are specialised structures that maintain physical connections between cells **OR** anchor cells together **OR** provide mechanical strength [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycoproteins enable cell migration during development **OR** wound healing, where cells must adhere to one another to build **OR** regenerate tissues [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ disruptions in glycoprotein function can lead to impaired adhesion **WHICH** can lead to cells failing to adhere properly **OR** promoting metastasis in cancer **OR** other diseases [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Cells adhere to each other because the **carbohydrate chains** on the glycoproteins of one cell, recognise and then bind to **complementary** molecules on the other cell ($\\checkmark$). These glycoproteins help to form **tight connections** between cells, preventing molecules from passing between them ($\\checkmark$). **Desmosomes**, formed by glycoproteins, are specialised structures that provide additional strength, holding cells securely together ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2651","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2651\n\nThe diagram shows a membrane with four structures labelled I - IV.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Membranes%20%26%20Membrane%20Transport/images/BI2651.png\" alt=\"BI2651\">\n\nWhich row correctly names all four of the structures in the diagram?\n\n| | I. | II. | III. | IV. |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Glycolipid | Cholesterol | Channel protein | Glycoprotein |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Glycoprotein | Glycolipid | Channel protein | Cholesterol |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Glycoprotein | Glycolipid | Carrier protein | Cholesterol |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Glycoprotein | Cholesterol | Carrier protein | Glyoclipid |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**I** s glycoprotein, as it shows a carbohydrate chain attached to a protein. \\\n**II** is a glycolipid, as it shows a carbohydrate chain attached to a lipid. \\\n**III** is a channel protein, as it spans the membrane and has a pore through the middle. \\\n**IV** a cholesterol molecule, as it is a small molecule, made up of carbon rings, embedded in the bilayer.\\\n**Choice B** is therefore the correct row.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because **III**. is not a carrier protein. Carrier proteins have specific binding sites, change shape during transport and they open to one side at a time. This protein shows a straight pore, or channel, running through the middle of it so it is a channel protein.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as it only labels the channel protein correctly, and **Choice D** is incorrect as it only labels the glycoprotein correctly."},{"ref":"BI2652","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2652\n\nWhich of the following membrane proteins always uses ATP to transport particles across membranes?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Channel proteins\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Aquaporins\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Pump proteins\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Carrier proteins","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nPump proteins actively transport substances across membranes against their concentration gradient using energy from ATP; **Choice C** is correct.\n\nChannel proteins allow passive transport, by facilitated diffusion, of ions or molecules down their concentration gradient, so do not use ATP. **Choice A** is incorrect.\\\nAquaporins are specialised channel proteins which allow water to diffuse passively across the membrane, so again do not use ATP. **Choice B** is incorrect.\\\nCarrier proteins can be involved in passive or active transport., so not all carrier proteins use ATP, which means the term alone is too broad to be correct here. **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI2853","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2853\n\nThe table shows the results of an experiment that measured the diffusion rate of different particles across a phospholipid bilayer.\n\n| Particles | Relative Rate of Diffusion (arb) |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| Oxygen ($\\ce{O_2}$) | 15.08 |\n| Oestradiol (steroid hormone) | 20.45 |\n| Glucose | 1.02 |\n| Sodium ions ($\\ce{Na^+}$) | 0.0001(negligible) |\n\nWhich conclusion is best supported by the data?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Larger molecules diffuse faster than smaller ones across phospholipid bilayers.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Charged particles diffuse rapidly across phospholipid bilayers.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The phospholipid bilayer is selectively permeable to non-polar molecules.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Smaller molecules diffuse faster than larger ones across phospholipid bilayers.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct. The data show that non-polar molecules like oxygen and oestradiol diffuse rapidly, while charged/polar particles like glucose and $\\ce{Na^+}$ diffuse slowly or not at all. This supports the idea that the lipid bilayer is selectively permeable, and only allows non-polar molecules to move freely across.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because while oestradiol diffuses faster than oxygen, glucose is larger than oxygen but diffuses more slowly, contradicting the idea that larger molecules diffuse faster.\\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because sodium ions (charged) show a negligible rate of diffusion, but uncharged oxygen has a high rate of diffusion. This is because the hydrophobic tails of the phospholipids make it harder for charged or polar molecules to cross.\\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because sodium ions are smaller than oestradiol, but diffuse much more slowly."}]},{"name":"Muscle & Motility","questions":[{"ref":"BI0377","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0377\n\nBelow is a diagram of a synovial joint.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Muscle%20%26%20Motility/images/BI0377a.png\" alt=\"BI0377a\">\n[Diagram enhanced using Microsoft Copilot [AI-assisted educational diagram]]\n\nWhich letter points to the cartilage?","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is the correct answer because cartilage is a tissue that covers the ends of bones and reduces friction.\n\n The joint capsule keeps the joint together and keeps the synovial fluid in the joint (**Choice A**). The joint is filled with the synovial fluid that lubricates the joint (**Choice C**). Ligaments join bone to bone (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0378","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0378\n\nWhich of these statements are true for synovial joints?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. |Synovial fluid lubricates the joint. |\n    | II. | Synovial joints can only move in one direction (plane) |\n    | III. | Ligaments connect one bone to another |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statements I** and **III** are correct. **Statement II** is incorrect because only some joints (knee and elbow) allow movement in one direction; there are other synovial joints that allow movement in additional directions (hip and shoulder)."},{"ref":"BI0379","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0379\n\nWhich feature allows insects, such as crickets (*Gryllus bimaculatus*), to jump very high?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Strong tendons\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Strong exoskeleton\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Synovial joints\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Antagonistic muscles","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAntagonistic muscles work in pairs, with one muscle contracting while the other\nrelaxes. In insects, such as crickets, these muscles, particularly in their hind legs, are specialised for powerful and rapid contractions. This coordinated action of antagonistic muscles provides the force necessary for the rapid extension of the legs during a jump, propelling the insect into the air. **Choice D** is the correct answer to the question.\n\nTendons are fibrous tissues that connect muscles to bones, providing the mechanism for movement. While tendons do play a role in transmitting force from muscles to the exoskeleton, their strength alone isn't the primary reason for high jumps in insects. **Choice A** is not the correct answer to this question.\n\nThe exoskeleton of insects provides structural support and protection. While a strong exoskeleton is necessary for withstanding the stresses of jumping, it alone doesn't explain the high jumping ability. The exoskeleton serves as a rigid framework against which muscles can act, but it's not the main contributor to the jumping force. **Choice B** is not the correct answer to this question.\n\nSynovial joints are characterised by the presence of a fluid-filled joint cavity, allowing for smooth movement between bones. Insects do not have synovial joints, instead insects have joints called ‘arthrodial joints’ or ‘articulations’. **Choice C** is not the correct answer to this question."},{"ref":"BI0380","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0380\n\nThe diagram below represents a sarcomere. Which structures are labeled I and II ?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Muscle%20%26%20Motility/images/BI0380.png\" alt=\"BI0380\">\n\n| | I | II |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Tropomyosin | Actin |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Myosin | Troponin |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Actin | Myosin |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Myosin | Actin |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe protein filament that is thicker and has heads is made of myosin (arrow I). The thinner protein filaments are made of actin, troponin and tropomyosin. Arrow II is pointing to the actin component of the thin filament (**Choice D**). Tropomyosin is a protein in the thin filament that masks myosin-binding sites (shown as thin black lines). Troponin is also a protein found in thin filaments. It has binding sites for calcium ions (shown as small double circles attached to tropomyosin lines)."},{"ref":"BI0381","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0381\n\nThe diagram below represents two sarcomeres. Which structure does **NOT** shorten during muscle contraction?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Muscle%20%26%20Motility/images/BI0381a.png\" alt=\"BI0381a\">\n[Source: Adapted from Potte, Jack (19th August 2017). *File: Sarcomere chem114a.jpg*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved 9th June 2022 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Sarcomere_chem114a.jpg. Copyright-free]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDuring muscle contraction the thick filaments and thin filaments slide inwards across each other bringing the Z lines closer together, shortening the sarcomere (**Choice A**). As a result the I band (**Choice B**) and H zone (**Choice D**)  get narrower. However, seeming as the A band (**Choice C**) is where the thick filaments are found, whether on their own or with actin,  and the thick bands do not physically change length it means that the A band stays the same width. This means that **Choice C** is correct. **Choices A, B** and **D** all change length and so are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0382","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0382\n\nWhich type of ion binds to filaments in the sarcomere to initiate the process of muscle contraction?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Calcium\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Potassium\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sodium\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrogen","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe release of Calcium ions from the sarcoplasmic reticulum initiates the process of muscle contraction (**Choice A**). Potassium ions (**Choice B**) and sodium ions (**Choice C**) are involved in nerve transmission and raising the membrane potential of muscle fibres to bring about contraction, but they do not bind to the sarcomere. Hydrogen ions (**Choice D**) do not diffuse from the sarcoplasmic reticulum."},{"ref":"BI0383","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0383\n\nWhich of the following brings about movement of the skeleton?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cartilage\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ligaments\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Muscles\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Tendons","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe contraction and relaxation of muscles (**Choice C**) cause bones to move. Cartilage (**Choice A**) is the smooth tissue covering the end of bones in joints to reduce friction. Ligaments (**Choice B**) join bone to bone and help keep joints together. Tendons (**Choice D**) connect muscle to bone, but aren’t responsible for movement."},{"ref":"BI0384","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0384\n\nWhich of the following statements relates to myofibrils?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ A structure made of two proteins\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ A bundle of muscle fibres\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ A cylinder surrounded by the sarcolemma\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ A cylinder made of repeating sarcomeres","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nMyofibrils are cylinders made of repeating sarcomeres (**Choice D**). Myofibrils contain more than two types of proteins (**Choice A**). **Choice B** is incorrect because muscle fibres are made of myofibrils. **Choice C** is incorrect because the sarcolemma is the membrane that surrounds a muscle fibre."},{"ref":"BI0385","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0385\n\nWhich row shows the correct sequence of events in muscle contraction?\n\n| | I | II | III | IV |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Ca:sup[2+] ions released from sarcoplasmic reticulum | ATP breaks cross-bridge | Myosin heads attach to binding sites | Hydrolysis of ATP causes myosin heads to pull actin inwards |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Ca:sup[2+] ions released from sarcoplasmic reticulum | Troponin moves tropomyosin | Myosin heads attach to binding sites | ADP and energy is released, and myosin heads pull actin inwards |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Troponin moves tropomyosin | Ca:sup[2+] ions released from sarcoplasmic reticulum | Myosin heads attach to binding sites | ATP breaks cross-bridge |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Troponin moves tropomyosin | Myosin heads attach to binding sites | Hydrolysis of ATP causes myosin heads to pull actin inwards | Release of ADP breaks cross-bridge |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because Ca:sup[2+] ions are released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Calcium diffuses into the sarcomere, binds to troponin, and causes troponin to move the tropomyosin away from the binding sites on actin. Myosin heads then attach to actin, and when ADP and energy are released, myosin swivels and pulls the actin inwards. **Choice A** is incorrect because cross-bridges are broken after actin has been pulled inwards. **Choice C** is incorrect because Ca:sup[2+] ions are released before troponin moves tropomyosin. **Choice D** is incorrect because hydrolysis of ATP does not cause myosin heads to swivel and pull actin inwards."},{"ref":"BI0386","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0386\n\nWhich statement(s) are true for ATP hydrolysis during muscle contraction?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | ATP is hydrolysed by the sarcoplasmic reticulum |\n    | II. | ATP hydrolysis causes myosin heads to move into the ready position |\n    | III. | ATP hydrolysis causes the cross-bridges to break |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because **statement II** is the only correct statement. **Statement I** is incorrect because hydrolysis of ATP happens due to the ATPase enzyme in the myosin heads. **Statement II** is correct because hydrolysis of ATP does cause the myosin heads to move into the ready position. **Statement III** is incorrect because the attachment of ATP to myosin causes the cross bridges to break, not the hydrolysis of ATP."},{"ref":"BI0387","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0387\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nThe diagram below shows a synovial joint.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Muscle%20%26%20Motility/images/BI0387-11.2_B3.3.png\" alt=\"BI0387-11.2_B3.3\">\n\n1. Identify structures labelled I and II. [2 marks]\n\n2. Describe the role of each of the structures identified in (a). [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{->}$ I: cartilage [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{->}$ II: synovial fluid [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n2.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{->}$ cartilage reduces friction **OR** absorbs shock **OR** distributes load [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{->}$ synovial fluid acts as a shock absorber **OR** reduces friction **OR** lubricates joint **OR** provides oxygen to cartilage **OR** provides nutrients to cartilage [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ :u[Sample answer]:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Cartilage covers the end of bones and helps to reduce **friction** ($\\checkmark$). Synovial fluid acts as a **shock absorber** ($\\checkmark$) and reduces friction."},{"ref":"BI0388","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0388\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n1. Outline how antagonistic muscles move joints, giving an example. [3 marks]\n\n2. Describe how skeletal muscle fibres are adapted for their function.  [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (antagonistic muscles) move bones in opposite directions **OR** work in pairs [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when one muscle contracts, the other relaxes[1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antagonistic muscle pair has to be around the same joint **OR** must be moving the same bone [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ biceps and triceps in the upper arm, **OR** hamstring and quadriceps, (**OR** any other valid example), are examples of antagonistic muscles     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when the biceps contracts the triceps relaxes and the lower arm is pulled upwards **OR** any other valid example of antagonistic muscle movement         [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Antagonistic muscles are muscles that move bones in **opposite directions** ($\\checkmark$). When one muscle **contracts, the other muscle relaxes** ($\\checkmark$). The contraction and relaxation of antagonistic muscles move the **same bone in joints** ($\\checkmark$). The **biceps and triceps** in the upper arm are an example of antagonistic muscle pairs ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  long (multinucleated) fibres formed from fusin of cells, which support shortening and lengthening [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ many mitochondria to supply energy for the contraction [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sarcoplasmic reticulum stores calcium ions required for muscle contraction [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ thin and thick filaments, **OR** actin and myosin, slide past each other to shorten the sarcomere     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ T-tubules **OR** invaginations of sarcolemma allow action potential to be carried into the centre of muscle fibre [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ troponin and tropomyosin help control contraction [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Skeletal muscles have many adaptations that allow them to contract and relax to move bones around the joints. Muscle fibres are **long multinucleated cells** consisting of myofibrils. Myofibrils can **shorten or lengthen** ($\\checkmark$) due to the presence of sarcomeres. Sarcomeres contain **myosin and actin** filaments that can **slide past each other** ($\\checkmark$), causing the muscle fibre to shorten. This sliding action is dependent on the presence of ATP and Ca:sup[2+] ions. Muscle fibres contain a lot of **mitochondria** to supply **ATP** ($\\checkmark$). **Ca:sup[2+] ions are stored** until they are needed in a specially adapted **sarcoplasmic reticulum** ($\\checkmark$) inside the cell."},{"ref":"BI0389","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0389\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Distinguish between a myofibril, the sarcoplasmic reticulum and the sarcolemma.         [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline the proteins involved in muscle contraction.     [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe the process of muscle contraction. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following are correctly shown in a labelled diagram:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myofibrils are the basic unit inside the muscle fibres **OR** they are composed of  end-to-end sarcomeres **OR** they are long contractile fibres **OR** they are bundles of protein filaments, mainly actin and myosin   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the sarcolemma is the plasma membrane of the muscle fibres **OR** cell, **OR** it envelops the muscle fibre          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the sarcoplasmic reticulum is specialized endoplasmic reticulum that stores calcium **OR** releases calcium ions when stimulated by a nerve impulse **OR** action potential  [1 mark]\n[3 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Myofibrils, sarcolemma and sarcoplasmic reticulum are all found in muscle fibres. **Myofibrils** are the basic units of muscle fibres; they are made of **repeating sarcomeres** laid end-to-end and are responsible for muscle contraction ($\\checkmark$). The **sarcolemma** is the membrane that surrounds each **muscle fibre**, similar to the **plasma membrane** in any other cell ($\\checkmark$). The **sarcoplasmic** reticulum is a **specialized endoplasmic reticulum**. When a nerve impulse reaches the muscle fibre, depolarisation spreads to the sarcoplasmic reticulum. This activates voltage-sensitive calcium channels in the sarcoplasmic reticulum causing them to **release calcium ions** ($\\checkmark$) from the sarcoplasmic reticulum storage. This initiates the process of muscle contraction.\n\n2. any **five** of the following (answer can be in a table format):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ five types of protein involved in muscle contration - myosin, actin, troponin, tropomyosin and titin    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tropomyosin binds to actin    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ troponin moves tropomyosin from the binding sites, in presence of **OR** when it binds to, calcium ions      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ actin makes up thin filaments [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myosin makes up thick filaments    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myosin heads attach to the binding sites on actin **OR** forms cross-bridges with actin      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myosin heads rotate to pull the actin in **OR** carries out power stroke, shortening the sarcomere **OR** bringing about muscle contraction    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myosin and actin filaments, **OR** thin and thick filaments, slide past each other to cause muscle contraction     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ titin stabilises the sarcomere structure by anchoring myosin to the Z-lines    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ titin provides elasticity **OR** recoil, helping the sarcomere return to its resting length after contraction    [1 mark]\n[5 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    There are **four types of protein** involved in the muscle contraction: **troponin, tropomyosin, myosin and actin** ($\\checkmark$). They all together make up a sarcomere unit that is part of a muscle fiber. **Actin**, troponin and tropomyosin make up the **thin filaments** ($\\checkmark$) and **myosin** makes up the **thick filaments** in the sarcomere ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    **Tropomyosin is bound to the actin** ($\\checkmark$) and troponin and when the muscle is relaxed, troponin covers myosin binding sites on actin. When calcium ions are released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, **troponin moves the tropomyosin** from the actin, exposing myosin binding sites ($\\checkmark$). When the **binding sites** are exposed **myosin heads** attach to actin and **rotate to pull the actin in** ($\\checkmark$). This **slides thick filaments along thin filaments** ($\\checkmark$) and cases the **sarcomere to shorten** ($\\checkmark$) and muscle to contract.\n\n    **Titin stabilises** the sarcomere structure by **anchoring** the **myosin** filaments to the **Z-line**, **preventing overextension** during contraction ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sarcomeres are the repeating functional units of myofibrils   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sarcomeres can shorten       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a nerve impulse reaches the myofibril bringing about an action potential in the sarcolemma **OR** action potential passes down T-tubules of sarcolemma [1 mark]\n\n     $\\ce{ -> }$ calcium ions are released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ calcium ions bind to troponin  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ calcium attachment to troponin causes a movement of tropomyosin exposing the binding sites on actin     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myosin heads attach to the binding sites on actin **OR** cross bridges form between actin and myosin   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myosin heads release ADP, and rotate **OR** carry out power stroke, pulling the actin filament inwards     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP attaches to the myosin head and breaks the cross-bridge [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrolysis of ATP moves the myosin head into the cocked **OR** ready position [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ repeat of the process shortens the sarcomere [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the Z lines are pulled closer together [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ shortening of the sarcomeres in a myofibril causes muscle contraction     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ titin stabilises the sarcomere structure, anchoring myosin to the Z-line [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ titin provides elasticity **OR** recoil **OR** helps sarcomere return to its resting length after contraction [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    The structure of sarcomeres allows them to **shorten, which causes muscle contraction** ($\\checkmark$). When a nerve impulse reaches the sarcoplasmic reticulum of myofibril, **calcium ions** are released ($\\checkmark$). **Calcium ions bind** to a protein called **troponin** ($\\checkmark$) in the sarcomere. **Troponin moves** another protein called **tropomyosin** away from the binding sites on actin ($\\checkmark$). **Myosin heads** are in the **cocked position** and contain stored energy from **ATP hydrolysis** ($\\checkmark$). They bind to the **binding sites on actin** to form a **cross bridge** ($\\checkmark$). The stored energy and **ADP are released** ($\\checkmark$). When they are released, the **myosin heads move inwards** (“power stroke”) ($\\checkmark$) and pull on the actin. **ATP attaches** to myosin heads and breaks the cross-bridge ($\\checkmark$). Hydrolysis of ATP moves the myosin heads into the cocked position. This process repeats until the calcium ions are no longer available. **Repeating** this process **shortens the sarcomeres** in a myofibril and causes **muscle contraction** ($\\checkmark$). **Titin**, a **structural protein**, **anchors** the **myosin filaments** to the **Z-lines**, **stabilising** the **sarcomere** during contraction ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0820","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0820\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nChitin is an abundant polysaccharide in nature and is a major component of arthropod exoskeletons. In an experiment, scientists tested the effect of various chitin synthesis inhibitors - diflubenzuron (DFB), hexaflumuron (HX) and teflubenzuron (TFB) - on the moulting process of the salmon louse (*Lepeophtheirus salmonis*) which is a known parasite of salmon.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Muscle%20%26%20Motility/images/BI0820A.png\" alt=\"BI0820A\">\n[Source: Adapted from Harðardóttir, HM., Male, R., Nilsen, F., Dalvin, S.(2019). *Effects of chitin synthesis inhibitor treatment on Lepeophtheirus salmonis (Copepoda, Caligidae) larvae*. PLoS ONE 14(9): e0222520. Retrieved May 27, 2023.  https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0222520. Copyright free]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Muscle%20%26%20Motility/images/BI0820B.png\" alt=\"BI0820B\">\n[Source: Adapted from Harðardóttir, HM., Male, R., Nilsen, F., Dalvin, S.(2019). *Effects of chitin synthesis inhibitor treatment on Lepeophtheirus salmonis (Copepoda, Caligidae) larvae*. PLoS ONE 14(9): e0222520. Retrieved May 27, 2023.  https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0222520. Copyright free]\n\n1.\n    1. State, which is the independent variable and dependent variable. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Suggest which chitin synthesis inhibitor is most effective at reducing the moulting success of salmon louse. Justify your answer. [1 mark]\n\n    3. Suggest why the chitin synthesis inhibitors were administered at different concentrations.    [1 mark]\n\n2.\n    1. Exoskeletons provide an anchorage for muscles to attach to. In mammals, which type of tissue attaches muscle to the bone?     [1 mark]\n\n    2. Describe another function of the exoskeleton.     [1 mark]\n\n3. Outline the relationship between the type of chitin inhibitor, concentration and the percentage of incomplete moultings in salmon lice (*Lepeophtheirus salmonis*).     [3 marks]\n\n4. As shown, chitin is visible as dark areas present on the surface of arthropods. Suggest one apparatus that could have been used to view the exoskeletons of the moulting salmon lice accurately.     [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. (students must identify the type of variable to achieve the mark):\n\n    **independent variable**:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type of chitin inhibitor at different concentrations [1 mark]\n\n    **dependent variable**:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ % of animals with incomplete moulting  [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The independent variable is the **type of inhibitor** at different concentrations ($\\checkmark$) and the dependent variable is the **percentage of animals with incomplete moultings** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    2.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ HX **BECAUSE** at the lowest concentrations, it causes the greatest increase in incomplete moulting, **OR** it is the most effective at reducing moulting success at lower concentrations    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **HX** is the most effective chitin synthesis inhibitor because it’s the most effective at reducing moulting success at **lower concentrations** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some inhibitors may be lethal **OR** harmful at specific concentrations so data cannot be included for dead lice                            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some inhibitors may be ineffective at some concentrations    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to determine the lowest concentration necessary to achieve the desired percentage of incomplete moultings     [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The concentrations used may have been different because some of the inhibitors used might be **lethal** and kill the salmon louse larvae **at specific concentrations** , and therefore, **data cannot be included** for measurements of the percentage of animals with incomplete moulting ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    1.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tendons        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Tendons** ($\\checkmark$) attach muscle to bone.\n\n2.\n    2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ protection **OR** physical barrier to predators **OR** toxins **OR** pathogens    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prevents excessive water uptake **OR** loss     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ acts as a lever                                 [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sensory perception                                 [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ camouflage                                         [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The exoskeleton provides **protection** ($\\checkmark$) for the larvaes’ vital organs.\n\n3.\n    Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as the concentration of all inhibitors increases, the percentage of incomplete moultings increases **OR** positive relationship between the concentration of inhibitor and the percentage of incomplete moultings     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hexaflumuron is the most effective inhibitor at lower concentrations      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ diflubenzuron is required in relatively higher concentrations to have the same effect as other inhibitors       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at the relative highest concentration of each inhibitor, the percentage of incomplete moultings is approximately 90%       [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nFor all inhibitors, as the concentration increases, so does the percentage of incomplete moultings. This is a **positive relationship** ($\\checkmark$). Hexaflumuron impacts the percentage of incomplete moultings at **lower concentrations** relative to the other inhibitors ($\\checkmark$). Diflubenzuron is required in relatively **higher concentrations** compared to the other inhibitors in order to have a similar effect ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ viewed using a light microscope **OR** magnifying glass **OR** stereoscope     [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The salmon lice could be viewed using a **light microscope** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0873","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0873\n\nThe photo shows an Australian shelduck (*Tadorna tadornoides*).\nWhich is **not** a feature that is adapted to aid in locomotion?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Muscle%20%26%20Motility/images/BI0873.png\" alt=\"BI0873\">\n[Source: Adapted from Harrison, J. (2023, July 25). *Australian shelduck*. Wikipedia. Retrieved July 26, 202, from URL https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australian_shelduck. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Dense bones\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Wings\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Webbed feet\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Oily coating on feathers","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A**  is correct because dense bones would make the duck heavier, therefore making it harder for it to move.\n\n**Choice B**  is incorrect, as wings enable the duck to fly. **Choice C**  is incorrect, as webbed feet aid the duck in swimming more efficiently. **Choice D** is incorrect as the oily coating on feathers helps to waterproof the duck and keep it light so it does not become waterlogged.  This helps the duck to swim and fly more easily."},{"ref":"BI0874","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0874\n\nThe diagram shows a zebra (*Equus quagga*). Which is **not** a reason for it to move?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Muscle%20%26%20Motility/images/BI0874.png\" alt=\"BI0874\">\n[Source: Adapted from Katz, M. (2022, August 3). *Zebra*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved July 28, 2023, from URL https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Zebra. Copyright free]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ To find a mate\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ To escape grassland fires\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ To find a food source\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ To run towards predators","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because zebras run away rather than towards predators. **Choices A, B** and **C** are incorrect because zebras move to find mates, escape grassland fires and find sources of food."},{"ref":"BI0875","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0875\n\nWhich animal forelimb is adapted for swimming?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Muscle%20%26%20Motility/images/BI0875.png\" alt=\"BI0875\">\n[Source: Adapted from Владислав Петрович, В. (2019, February 16). *File:homology vertebrates-en-bw.svg.* Wikimedia Commons.  Retrieved July 26, 2023, from URL   http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Homology_vertebrates-en-bw.svg. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D**  is correct because it represents the bones in a  flipper. The bone structure is similar to that of a land mammal, but the bones are shortened and modified to give the flipper a larger surface area for increased propulsion. The human arm (**Choice A**) is adapted to reach and pick up objects, the dog foreleg (**Choice B**) is adapted for running and the bird wing (**Choice C**) is adapted for flying."},{"ref":"BI0876","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0876\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nThe image shows an African daisy (*Osteospermum acanthospermum*), a sessile organism and a Humboldt penguin (*Spheniscus humboldti*), a motile organism.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Muscle%20%26%20Motility/images/BI0876A.png\" alt=\"BI0876A\">\n[Source: Adapted from Pixabay. (2016, January 21). *Purple daisybush flower*· free stock photo - PEXELS. Pexels.  Retrieved July 26, 2023, from URL https://www.pexels.com/photo/purple-daisybush-flower-36753/. Copyright free]\n\n[Source: Adapted from Tolkein, T. (2010, October 25). *File : Humboldt Penguin spheniscus humboldti Newquay Zoo.jpg*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved July 26, 2023, from URL https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Humboldt_Penguin_Spheniscus_humboldti_Newquay_Zoo.jpg. Copyright under CC BY 2.0]\n\n1. State one way that a daisy moves.    [1 mark]\n\n2. State two life processes other than movement that the two organisms have in common.    [1 mark]\n\n3. Explain three adaptations that the penguin has for efficient movement in water and on land.    [3 marks]\n\n4. In order to reproduce, the penguin may have to travel to find a mate.\n\n    1. Outline one advantage of travelling to find a mate.            [1 mark]\n\n    2. Outline one disadvantage of travelling to find a mate.        [1 mark]\n\n6. State two reasons other than reproduction that the penguin may need to travel.    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stem grows upwards **OR** phototropism        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ root grows downwards **OR** geotropism **OR** hydrotropism **OR** chemotropism    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ closing of petals at night **OR** nyctinasty **OR** nastic response    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The daisy can move by the elongation of cells allowing **phototropism**, a growth response to light ($\\checkmark$) to occur.\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\\\n*Answer must have at least two points to gain one mark*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nutrition [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ metabolism **OR** respiration [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ growth [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ response [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ excretion [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ homeostasis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reproduction [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Nutrition** ($\\checkmark$) and **metabolism** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ streamlined body, which reduces resistance **OR** friction in wind **OR** water, allowing for faster movement    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ flat flippers, which allow the penguin to turn easily in water **OR** push water behind its body to propel itself through water **OR** can be placed against its body to make it more streamlined    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ webbed feet to move efficiently on the ice **OR** snow without sinking        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ has additional air sacs inside lungs that allow air to circulate so all oxygen can be extracted so that they can swim for great lengths of time        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ overlapping feathers that provide waterproofing so penguin remains light when moving [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ strong wing and chest muscles, enabling them to generate powerful flapping motions to propel themselves through water    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ countercurrent heat exchange allows warm arterial blood flowing to the extremities to transfer heat to the cooler venous blood returning to the body, minimising heat loss to the cold water, conserving heat during prolonged swimming        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ upright posture helps them maintain balance and conserve energy while walking    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ strong feet and claws that provide grip and stability on ice and rocky surfaces    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ special oil gland that produced waterproofing oil, which they spread over their feathers and beaks     [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Three adaptations that the penguin has for efficient movement are a **streamlined** body, which **reduces air or water resistance** ($\\checkmark$), so that it can move faster, **webbed feet** to move efficiently on ice and snow **without sinking** ($\\checkmark$) and **additional air sacs** in its lungs so that it can **extract all of the oxygen** from the air it breathes and **swim for longer periods of time** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.\n    1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased likelihood of genetic diversity in offspring **OR** avoids inbreeding    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increases the likelihood of finding a suitable mate with desirable traits **OR** strong genetics **OR** good health, **OR** effective parenting abilities    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can provide access to more abundant resources in other colonies, contributing to better reproductive success        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    One advantage of the penguin travelling to find a mate is an **increased likelihood of genetic diversity** in the offspring ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.\n    2.  Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ time-consuming    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ may require large amounts of energy    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased predation risks    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased competition from local individuals for mating opportunities    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a new social structure **OR** habitat could lead to stress **OR** disorientation    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    One disadvantage of the penguin travelling to find a mate is that it is **time-consuming** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to search for food    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to escape predators **OR** hunters    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ migration patterns    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ natural disasters **OR** changes in environmental conditions        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ loss of habitat due to climate change **OR** human activity            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to huddle for warmth    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ avoiding competition for space **OR** nesting ground **OR** nesting materials **OR** any other suitable suggestion    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ social interactions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Two reasons penguins may move are to **search for food** ($\\checkmark$) and **escape from predators** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1069","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1069\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Distinguish between a myofibril, the sarcoplasmic reticulum and the sarcolemma. [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline the different proteins and their position in the sarcomere when the muscle is relaxed.    [4 marks]\n\n3. Describe the process of muscle contraction.        [9 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myofibrils are the basic unit inside the muscle fibres **OR** they are composed of  end-to-end sarcomeres **OR** they are long contractile fibres **OR** they are bundles of protein filaments, mainly actin and myosin        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sarcolemma is the plasma membrane of the muscle fibre **OR** cell, **OR** it envelops the muscle fibre        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sarcoplasmic reticulum is a specialised endoplasmic reticulum that stores calcium, **OR** releases calcium ions when stimulated by a nerve impulse **OR** action potential        [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Myofibrils, sarcolemma and sarcoplasmic reticulum are all found in muscle fibres. **Myofibrils** are the basic units of muscle fibres ($\\checkmark$); they are made of **repeating sarcomeres** laid end-to-end and are responsible for muscle contraction. The **sarcolemma** is the membrane that surrounds each **muscle fibre**, similar to the **plasma membrane** ($\\checkmark$) in any other cell. The **sarcoplasmic** reticulum is a **specialised endoplasmic reticulum**. When a nerve impulse reaches the muscle fibre, depolarisation spreads to the sarcoplasmic reticulum. This activates voltage-sensitive calcium channels in the sarcoplasmic reticulum causing them to **release calcium ions** ($\\checkmark$) from the sarcoplasmic reticulum storage. This initiates the process of muscle contraction.\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ five types of protein involved in muscle contraction: titin, myosin, actin, troponin and tropomyosin    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tropomyosin binds to **OR** wraps around, actin, covering binding sites        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ actin, troponin and tropomyosin make up thin filaments            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myosin makes up majority of  thick filaments        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ titin holds myosin fibres in place in sarcomere     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ titin is an immense protein        [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    There are **five types of protein** involved in muscle contraction: **titin, troponin, tropomyosin, myosin and actin** ($\\checkmark$). They all together make up a sarcomere unit that is part of a muscle fibre. **Actin**, troponin and tropomyosin make up the **thin filaments** ($\\checkmark$) and **myosin** makes up the **thick filaments** ($\\checkmark$) in the sarcomere. **Titin** is an immense protein that **holds** the **myosin fibres** in place ($\\checkmark$) in the sarcomere.\n\n    **Tropomyosin is bound to the actin** and troponin ($\\checkmark$), and when the muscle is relaxed, troponin covers myosin binding sites on actin.\n\n3. any **nine** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sarcomeres are the repeating functional units of myofibrils        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sarcomeres can shorten    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a nerve impulse reaches the myofibril bringing about an action potential in the sarcolemma **OR** action potential passes down T-tubules of sarcolemma    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ calcium ions are released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ calcium ions bind to troponin     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ calcium attachment to troponin causes a movement of tropomyosin exposing the binding sites on actin    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myosin heads attach to the binding sites on actin **OR** cross-bridges form between actin and myosin        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myosin heads release ADP and rotate **OR** carry out power stroke, pulling the actin filament inwards    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP attaches to the myosin head and breaks the cross-bridge        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrolysis of ATP moves the myosin head into the cocked **OR** ready position    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ repeat of the process shortens the sarcomere        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the Z lines are pulled closer together        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ shortening of the sarcomeres in a myofibril causes muscle contraction    [1 mark]\n    [9 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The structure of sarcomeres allows them to **shorten, which causes muscle contraction** ($\\checkmark$). When a nerve impulse reaches the sarcoplasmic reticulum of myofibril, **calcium ions** are released ($\\checkmark$). **Calcium ions bind** to a protein called **troponin** in the sarcomere ($\\checkmark$). **Troponin moves** another protein called **tropomyosin** away from the binding sites on actin ($\\checkmark$). **Myosin heads** are in the **cocked position** and contain stored energy from **ATP hydrolysis** ($\\checkmark$). They bind to the **binding sites on actin** to form a **cross bridge** ($\\checkmark$). The stored energy and **ADP are released** ($\\checkmark$). When they are released, the **myosin heads move inwards** (“power stroke”) and pull on the actin ($\\checkmark$). **ATP attaches** to myosin heads and breaks the cross-bridge ($\\checkmark$). **Hydrolysis of ATP moves the myosin heads into the cocked position** ($\\checkmark$). This process repeats until the calcium ions are no longer available. **Repeating** this process **shortens the sarcomeres** ($\\checkmark$) in a myofibril and causes **muscle contraction** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1183","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1183\n\nThe diagram below shows part of a sarcomere. Which structure represents actin?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Muscle%20%26%20Motility/images/BI1183.png\" alt=\"BI1183\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDuring muscle contraction, myosin filaments pull the actin filaments inwards towards the centre of the sarcomere. Line B is pointing to an actin filament. **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choices A** and **C** are incorrect, as they are pointing to myosin filaments. **Choice D** is incorrect as it indicates a titin filament."},{"ref":"BI1762","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1762\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nBelow is an electron micrograph of skeletal muscle.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Muscle%20%26%20Motility/images/BI1762%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1762 fig1\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Louisa Howard. Human skeletal muscle tissue 1 - TEM. Retrieved 27 August 2024 from: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Human_skeletal_muscle_tissue_1_-_TEM.jpg. Copyright Public Domain]\n\n1.  Identify the region labelled X.        [1 mark]\n\n2.  Using the ruler that has been placed onto the electron micrograph to measure the image size, calculate the actual length of the sarcomere.    [2 marks]\n\n3.  Explain the role of calcium ions in the shortening of the sarcomere, after their release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum.    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Z$-$line    [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    X is the :answer[Z$-$line] ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.  any **two** of following *(units required to achieve full marks)*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 44,000μm / 20,000 (allow for 43,000 $-$ 45,000μm or equivalent in mm or cm)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 2.2μm (allow any values between 2.1 $-$ 2.3 μm)    [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    **Actual size** = $\\dfrac{\\textbf{image size}}{\\textbf{magnification}}$\n\n       \n\n    Image size = 4.4 cm = 44 mm\n\n    1mm = 1,000 μm\n\n    Image size = 44,000 μm\n\n    Actual size = **44,000 μm / 20,000** ($\\checkmark$)\\\n\n    $\\hspace{4.7em}$ = **2.2 μm** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ calcium ions bind to troponin bringing about conformational change     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ this change in troponin moves tropomyosin away from myosin-binding sites on actin **OR** exposes myosin-binding sites on actin        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allowing myosin to bind to actin **OR** form cross-bridges to enabling power stroke to occur [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Once calcium ions are released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, they **bind to troponin**, causing a **conformational change** in the protein ($\\checkmark$). This change **shifts tropomyosin away from** the **myosin-binding sites** on the **actin** filaments ($\\checkmark$). With these binding sites now exposed, **myosin heads** can **attach to actin**, forming **cross-bridges**, allowing the power stroke to occur ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2382","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2382\n\nThe extinct early animal genus *Dickinsonia* included large, flat organisms that fed on microbial mats on the sea bed.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Muscle%20%26%20Motility/images/BI2382.png\" alt=\"BI2382\">\n[© Susan H. Butts, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Dickinsonia_minima_(YPM_IP_035468).jpg Retrieved January 2025,\nCopyright free\n]\n\nSome *Dickinsonia* fossils , like the one shown above, exhibit single imprints, suggesting these organisms were sessile or had limited mobility. However, other fossils show multiple overlapping trace imprints, indicating that they were motile. No discernible sense organs have been found in the *Dickinsonia* genus.\n\\\nWhich of the following was an evolutionary advantage that motile *Dickinsonia* would have had over sessile ones?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The ability to specialise in different food types.\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The ability to detect and respond to predators.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The ability to increase the efficiency of nutrient absorption.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The ability to access more food by moving across the substrate.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because movement across the substrate would allow *Dickinsonia* to access new areas of microbial mats once local food sources were depleted. This provides a clear evolutionary advantage over sessile organisms, which remain fixed in one location and cannot reach additional resources.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because there is no evidence that motility leads to specialisation in different food types. Both motile and sessile *Dickinsonia* are thought to have fed on the same type of resource, namely microbial mats, so movement would not change diet.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the question stem states that no discernible sense organs have been found in *Dickinsonia*. Detecting and responding to predators requires sensory structures, so this advantage is not supported by the available evidence.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because nutrient absorption efficiency depends on physiological factors such as surface area and transport mechanisms, rather than on movement. Being motile would not directly increase the efficiency of nutrient absorption."},{"ref":"BI2416","marks":"9","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2416\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nPremature infants are often treated with caffeine to prevent episodes of apnea, a condition characterised by a temporary cessation of breathing. Although the exact mechanism of action is not fully understood, scientists have observed that treatment with caffeine leads to increased intracellular calcium levels in the muscle cells of the diaphragm.\n\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Muscle%20%26%20Motility/images/EditA.png\" alt=\"EditA\"> | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Muscle%20%26%20Motility/images/Edit%20B.png\" alt=\"Edit B\"> |\n| :-: | :-: |\n|[Source: *File: Premature infant CPAP.jpg*. (2020, October 9). Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 04:32, January 16, 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=File: Premature_infant_CPAP.jpg&oldid=484646381.] | [Adapted from *“Newborn baby organs”*, by BioRender.com (2025). Retrieved from https://app.biorender.com/biorender-templates] |\n\n1. State the organelle that is the most probable source of calcium. [1 mark]\n\n2. Suggest a possible mechanism for the treatment’s success. [1 mark]\n\nMultiple Sclerosis can also cause apnea, which is a temporary pause in breathing. MS attacks the myelin sheath around neurons and leaves behind lesions in the brain.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Muscle%20%26%20Motility/images/EditC.png\" alt=\"EditC\">\n\n3. Describe how MS could result in apnea. [3 marks]\n\nMultiple sclerosis can also result in impaired ability to sense heat or cold, which can lead to accidental burns or frostbite when handling hot or cold objects.\n\n4. Explain the process by which high temperatures detected in the fingers trigger an action potential in an undamaged sensory neuron. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ sarcoplasmic reticulum **OR** endoplasmic reticulum [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The most probable source of the calcium ions is the **sarcoplasmic reticulum** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ more calcium binding to troponin to allow actin-myosin connections increases diaphragm muscle contraction [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increased calcium increases the amount of neurotransmitter **OR** acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n More calcium in the diaphragm cells means more calcium is available to **bind troponin, which moves tropomyosin away from myosin binding sites on the actin filament, allowing more contractions** ($\\checkmark$) and better breathing.\n\n3. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ no **OR** reduced saltatory conduction **OR** action potential cannot ‘jump’ from node of Ranvier to node of Ranvier **OR** transmission of action potential slowed, due to damage **OR** no myelin sheath [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ damage to myelin sheath of motor neurons that innervate intercostal muscles **OR** diaphragm could lead to reduced coordination of contraction [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ lesions in the brainstem **OR** medulla oblongata **OR** pons, impair the respiratory centres that regulate rhythm **OR** depth of involuntary breathing [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ lesions in primary motor cortex **OR** cerebral hemispheres, disrupt voluntary control of breathing muscles [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ damage to myelin of sensory neurons could interfere with the detection of blood gas levels **OR** blood pH **OR** blood carbon dioxide concentration which would disrupt feedback to respiratory centres [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **damage** to the **myelin sheath** around the neurons would **disrupt saltatory conduction**, where **action potentials** normally **\"jump\"** between **nodes of Ranvier** ($\\checkmark$). This would **slow neural signalling**, impairing **motor neuron** function required for **controlling** the **intercostal muscles** and **diaphragm** ($\\checkmark$). The same applies to the **sensory neurons** involved in the **detection of blood pH** or **carbon dioxide levels**, so there would be **limited feedback** to the **respiratory centres** in the medulla oblongata ($\\checkmark$). Lesions in the **medulla oblongata** would also disrupt the **respiratory centres** that regulate the **rhythm** and **rate** of **involuntary breathing**, which could lead to irregular breathing patterns or **apnea** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **four** of the following *(ignore references to the flow of impulses beyond the sensory neuron, in a reflex arc*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nociceptors **OR** pain receptors sense pain from **OR** harmful temperature [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ high temperatures cause specific ion channels **OR** channel proteins **OR** transient receptor protein channels **OR** TRPs to open [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ channels found in skin epidermal layer [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ positively charged ions **OR** sodium ions **OR** $\\ce{Na^+}$ **OR** calcium ions **OR** $\\ce{Ca^{2+}}$, enter the neuron through the open channels [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ entry of positively charged ions reduces the negative charge inside the neuron leading to depolarisation of the membrane potential [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ if the depolarisation reaches threshold potential **OR** -55 mV **OR** other suitable value, voltage-gated sodium channels open [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ rapid influx of sodium ions **OR** $\\ce{Na^+}$, leads to spike in membrane potential **OR** membrane potential of +30 mV **OR** other suitable value [1 mark]\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Nociceptors** are a type of sensory receptor that senses pain ($\\checkmark$). High temperatures cause specific **ion channels**, known as **transient receptor protein channels**, to **open** in the free nerve endings of sensory neurons ($\\checkmark$). **Sodium ions** **enter** the **neuron** ($\\checkmark$), causing **depolarisation** of the **membrane potential** ($\\checkmark$). If the depolarisation **reaches** the **threshold potential**, **voltage-gated sodium channels open** ($\\checkmark$), and an action potential is generated."},{"ref":"BI2820","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2820\n\nWhich of the following is/are true about titin?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | It prevents overstretching of sarcomeres |\n    | II. | It generates active force for contraction |\n    | III. | It contributes to muscle elasticity |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nTitin is a large protein that connects the Z-disc to the M-line in sarcomeres. It acts as a molecular spring, helping to maintain the structural integrity of the sarcomere and preventing overstretching during muscle contraction and relaxation; **Statement I** is true.\n\nTitin does not generate active force for contraction. Active force during muscle contraction is primarily generated by myosin, the motor protein, interacting with actin filaments. Titin's role is structural and elastic, not force generation; **Statement II** is false.\n\nTitin contributes to the passive elasticity of muscle fibres. It acts like a spring, which helps return the muscle to its resting length after contraction. This elastic property is essential for maintaining muscle tone and preventing damage from excessive stretch.  **Statement III** is true.\n\n**Choice B** is correct because it only includes the two true **Statements I** and **III**.\\\n**Choice A** is incorrect as it only includes one of the true statements.\\\n**Choices C** and **D** are incorrect as they include the false **Statement II**."},{"ref":"BI2821","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2821\n\nWhich of the following statements about motor units is/are correct?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | All muscle fibres in a motor unit contract simultaneously |\n    | II. | The number of fibres in a motor unit determines the precision of control |\n    | III. | A single muscle fibre may be stimulated by multiple motor neurons |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA motor unit consists of one motor neuron and all the muscle fibres it innervates. When the motor neuron sends an action potential, all the fibres in that motor unit contract together, producing a coordinated contraction; **Statement I** is true.\n\nMotor units with fewer muscle fibres (e.g. in the fingers or eyes) allow for fine, precise control, while those with many fibres (e.g. in the legs) generate powerful but less precise movements;  **Statement II** is true.\n\nEach muscle fibre is innervated by only one motor neuron. This ensures that signals to contract are unambiguous and coordinated. The reverse is true: one motor neuron can stimulate multiple muscle fibres; **Statement III** is false.\n\n**Choice C** is correct because it only includes the two true **Statements I** and **II**.\\\n**Choice A** is incorrect as it only includes one of the true statements.\\\n**Choices B** and **D** are incorrect as they include the false **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI2916","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2916\n\nDuring deep dives, dolphins often encounter lower oxygen levels. Which of the following adaptations best helps them maintain swimming efficiency under low-oxygen conditions?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Myoglobin’s ability to store oxygen in muscles.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increased blood volume to carry more oxygen.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The ability to slow metabolic rate and conserve oxygen.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Modification of lung tissue to increase oxygen absorption.\n\n*Note: Starting May 2028, this question may be assessed differently due to changes to the Biology guide, including the use of a data booklet.*","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct.  Dolphins have high concentrations of myoglobin in their muscle tissue. Myoglobin is a protein that binds oxygen and stores it directly within the muscles, allowing dolphins to maintain muscle function during long dives without access to atmospheric oxygen. Unlike haemoglobin, which carries oxygen in the blood, myoglobin has a higher affinity for oxygen, meaning it holds onto oxygen more effectively and can supply it to muscles during periods of breath-holding.\nThis adaptation directly enables dolphins to maintain swimming efficiency during extended periods of submersion in low-oxygen conditions.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because, while blood volume is important for oxygen transport, dolphins do not adapt by increasing blood volume to dive efficiently; they rely on the capacity to store oxygen in myoglobin.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because, while dolphins can slow their metabolic rate during a dive to conserve oxygen, this is not the primary adaptation that enables them to maintain swimming efficiency in low-oxygen conditions. Slowing metabolism helps reduce overall oxygen consumption, but doesn’t allow dolphins to continue swimming efficiently. The key adaptation for maintaining swimming efficiency during long dives is myoglobin, which stores oxygen directly in muscles.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because there is no significant modification to lung tissue that allows for increased oxygen absorption to an extent that would support long dives. While lungs are essential for initial oxygen intake, they are not a key adaptation for swimming efficiency during extended periods underwater, especially under low-oxygen conditions. The primary adaptation for oxygen storage and efficient swimming during dives is myoglobin in the muscles, not modifications to lung tissue."}]},{"name":"Organelles & Compartmentalization","questions":[{"ref":"BI0154","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0154\n\nBelow is a micrograph of mitochondria with four structures or locations labelled. Which is a correct description of how one of these structures is adapted to its function?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Organelles%20%26%20Compartmentalization/images/BI0154.png\" alt=\"BI0154\">\n[Source: Bohler, et al. (2018, February 19). *Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image of mitochondria of a MEF cell in which the gene OMA1 has been knocked out.* [Photograph]. Wikimedia Retrieved April 02, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TEM_image_of_mitochondria_in_MEF_OMA1-KO.png#filelinks. Copyright under CC BY]\n\n| | Structure | Adaptation and function |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | P       | Projections to increase surface area for the electron transport chain |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Q       | Contains enzymes required by the link reaction and Krebs cycle |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | R       | Large surface area for oxidative phosphorylation  |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | S | Barrier between the inner parts of the mitochondrion and the cytoplasm which creates an ideal space with optimum conditions for cellular respiration |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nP is the outer mitochondrial membrane (**Choice A**). It acts as a barrier between the inner part of the mitochondrion and the cytoplasm which creates an ideal space with optimum conditions for cellular respiration.\n\nQ is the inner mitochondrial membrane (**Choice B**) where the electron transport chain and ATP synthase are located.\n\nR is the cristae, which are projections of the inner mitochondrial membrane which has a large surface area for oxidative phosphorylation to take place.\n\nS is the matrix (**Choice D**) where enzymes required for the Krebs cycle and link reaction are found."},{"ref":"BI0326","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0326\n\nThe image shows many ribosomes on a mRNA molecule.  How many unique polypeptide chains are being translated?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Organelles%20%26%20Compartmentalization/images/BI0326i_7.3.png\" alt=\"BI0326i_7.3\">\n\n[Adapted from Miller, C. L. (2020, December 30). *ribosome mrna translation pt.svg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved June 1, 2022, from https ://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Multiple_Ribosomes_Translation_Protein_Synthesis.png. Copyright free]\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 1\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 6\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 12\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nThe image shows 6 ribosomes on a single mRNA molecule, all translating polypeptide chains, it means that each ribosome is reading the same mRNA sequence and therefore synthesising the same polypeptide chain. The number of unique polypeptide chains being translated in this scenario would be 1, because all the ribosomes are translating the same mRNA into the same polypeptide sequence. **Choice A** is correct."},{"ref":"BI0330","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0330\n\nWhat is the main role of the ribosomes labelled in the micrograph?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Organelles%20%26%20Compartmentalization/images/rough%20endoplasmic%20reticulum%20RER%20rough%20ER.png\" alt=\"rough endoplasmic reticulum RER rough ER\">\n\n[Adapted from OpenStax. (2016, May 18). *File:0313 endoplasmic reticulum B en.png* - Wikimedia Commons. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 1, 2022, from https ://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0313_Endoplasmic_Reticulum_b_en.png. Copyright free]\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Synthesise enzymes to be used within the cytoplasm\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Transport mRNA from the nucleus to the cytoplasm\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Destroy proteins that the cell no longer needs\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Synthesise proteins for use in lysosomes","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nBound ribosomes synthesise proteins for secretion outside of the cell, membrane-associated proteins or proteins used in lysosomes (**Choice D**).\n\nWhile ribosomes do synthesize enzymes, the ribosomes attached to the rough ER specifically target the proteins they synthesize for secretion or for use in membrane-bound organelles (like lysosomes) or the cell membrane, rather than for immediate use within the cytoplasm. Proteins meant to function within the cytoplasm are typically synthesized by free ribosomes that are not attached to the rough ER. **Choice A**  is incorrect. \\\n\n**Choice B**  is incorrect as bound ribosomes do not transport mRNA from the nucleus to the cytoplasm. \\\n\n**Choice C**  is incorrect as ribosomes do not destroy proteins."},{"ref":"BI0334","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0334\n\nWhich statement about translation only applies to prokaryotes?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Translation occurs at the ribosomes.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Translation happens immediately after transcription.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Translation creates an amino acid sequence from the mRNA sequence.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Translation happens after mRNA processing.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\n**Choice B** is correct because in prokaryotes there are no post-transcriptional modifications made to the mRNA and thus mRNA is translated immediately after transcription. **Choices A** and **C** are incorrect as they apply to translation in both eukaryotes and prokaryotes. **Choice D**  is incorrect as it applies to eukaryotes, not prokaryotes. In eukaryotes, mRNA needs to be processed but not in prokaryotes."},{"ref":"BI0337","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0337\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast the structure and function of free and bound ribosomes. [3 marks]\n\n2. Describe the steps in the initiation of translation. [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain how the bonds between amino acids contribute to the structure of proteins. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following (answer can be in a table format):\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ **BOTH** free and bound ribosomes synthesise proteins [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ **BOTH** free and bound ribosomes consist of small and big subunits, **OR** both have A P E and mRNA binding sites [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ free ribosomes are found in the cytoplasm **WHILE** bound ribosomes are attached to the endoplasmic reticulum **OR** ER [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ free ribosomes synthesise proteins for use within the cell unattached to any structures, **BUT** bound ribosomes synthesise proteins for secretion **OR** use in the lysosomes **OR** membrane-bound proteins [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Both free and bound ribosomes **synthesise proteins** ($\\checkmark$). However, free ribosomes are found floating in the **cytoplasm** , and bound ribosomes are attached to the **endoplasmic reticulum** ($\\checkmark$). Free ribosomes synthesise proteins for use **within the cell** , but bound ribosomes synthesise proteins for **secretion** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ mRNA molecule binds to the small ribosomal subunit [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ initiator tRNA (carrying methionine) binds to the start codon [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ mRNA/tRNA **OR** codon/anticodon binding is facilitated by complementary base pairing [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ large ribosomal subunit binds to the initiator tRNA at the P site [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ amino acid carrying tRNA attaches to the next codon at the A site [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ peptide bond is formed between amino acids in the P and A site [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ ribosome moves a codon space on the mRNA, so that tRNA at start codon is now at E site [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Translation occurs in the cytoplasm. During the initiation stage, the mRNA molecule binds to the **small ribosomal subunit** ($\\checkmark$). Next, the **initiator tRNA** binds to the start codon ($\\checkmark$) on the mRNA, and finally, the **large ribosomal subunit** binds to the tRNA through the **P site** ($\\checkmark$) in the ribosome and then the next codon signals another tRNA to bind in the **A site** ($\\checkmark$), using complementary base pairing.  A **peptide bond** ($\\checkmark$) is formed between the amino acids carried by tRNAs in the P and A sites.\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ primary structure of a protein is the sequence of amino acids [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ peptide bonds link amino acids in the polypeptide chain [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ secondary structure is the formation of alpha helices **OR** beta pleated sheets within the same polypeptide [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ (secondary structure is) the result of hydrogen bonding **OR** hydrogen bonds form between the carboxyl and amino group **OR** C=O and N-H [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ tertiary structure is caused by interactions between R groups of amino acids [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ (in tertiary structure) positively charged R groups interact with negative charged R groups [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ (in tertiary structure) polar R groups will form hydrogen bonds with other polar R groups [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ (in tertiary structure) disulphide, **OR** S-S, bridges can form between cysteines in R groups [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ (tertiary structure) gives the polypeptide its 3D shape [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ quaternary structure is formed in proteins with more than one polypeptide chain [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ (quaternary structure is) formed by the interactions between R groups on different polypeptide chains [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ (quaternary structure is) supported by S-S bridges, **OR** any other examples of R group bounds in the previous marking statements  [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    The primary structure of a protein is the **sequence** of amino acids ($\\checkmark$), which are linked together with **peptide bonds** ($\\checkmark$). The secondary structure of a protein is the formation of **alpha-helices** ($\\checkmark$), which are stabilised by **hydrogen bonding** between the carboxyl and amino groups ($\\checkmark$) from peptide bonds between different amino acids in the same chain. The tertiary structure is caused by interactions between **R groups** ($\\checkmark$), which gives the polypeptide its **3D shape** ($\\checkmark$). Some of the bonds formed between R groups include **polar R groups**  forming hydrogen bonds with other polar R groups ($\\checkmark$) and cysteines in R groups, forming **disulphide bridges** ($\\checkmark$). The quaternary structure of proteins describes proteins with **more than one** polypeptide chain ($\\checkmark$) in their structure. The quaternary structure is supported by the same type of R-R group interactions as the tertiary level, such as **disulphide bridges** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0396","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0396\n\nWhich of the following is **not** a function of the Golgi apparatus?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Protein synthesis\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    Protein packaging into vesicles\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    Protein modification\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    Lipid packaging into vesicles","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nRibosomes carry out protein synthesis, not the Golgi apparatus. **Choice A** is correct. **Choices B**, **C** and **D** are incorrect as they are all functions of the Golgi apparatus."},{"ref":"BI0871","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0871\n\nThe diagram shows prophase, an early stage of mitosis.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Organelles%20%26%20Compartmentalization/images/BIO871ii.png\" alt=\"BIO871ii\">\n\nWhat is the fate of the nuclear membrane immediately after prophase is complete?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Forms pores to allow movement of molecules in and out of the nucleus\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Reassembles around the sister chromatids\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Breaks down into small vesicles\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Reforms at the poles of the cell","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because immediately after prophase is prometaphase, during which the nuclear membrane breaks down into small vesicles.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because, during prophase, the nuclear membrane already has existing pores. **Choice B** is incorrect because the nuclear membrane only reassembles during telophase, which is the last stage of mitosis, and not immediately after prophase. **Choice D** is incorrect because the mitotic spindle forms between the poles of the cell immediately after prophase."},{"ref":"BI0872","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0872\n\nWhich of the following substances **cannot** pass through the pores of the nuclear membrane?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ mRNA\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ DNA\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ ribosomal proteins\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ ions","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe main role of the nuclear envelope is to compartmentalise the DNA from the cytoplasm in eukaryotic cells. DNA is a large complex molecule which is too large to pass through the nuclear pores. Therefore **Choice B** is correct.\n\nThe pores in the nuclear envelope regulate the movement of molecules between the nucleus and cytosol. Nuclear pores allow small molecules and ions (**Choice D**) to diffuse through them. Many proteins (**Choice C**) required for transcription and DNA replication are made in the cytosol and selectively imported into the nucleus. Molecules, such as mRNA (**Choice A**) and tRNA are made in the nucleus and exported into the cytosol. Much of this transport across the membrane is selective, for example, mRNA can only be exported once it has been formed into mature RNA through the process of splicing."},{"ref":"BI0912","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0912\n\nThe micrograph below shows part of an animal cell.  Which statement is true about the parts labelled X and Y\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Organelles%20%26%20Compartmentalization/images/BI0912.png\" alt=\"BI0912\">\n[©Source: Adapted from T.Voekler (August 1986). File: Nucleus of a chloride cell.jpg. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved July 22nd, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Nucleus_of_a_chloride_cell.jpg. CC-BY-SA-3.0]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Both X and Y are primarily composed of water and solutes.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Only X has a specialised function, and Y does not.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Only Y is an organelle, and X is not.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Both X and Y are found in prokaryotes.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because Y is the nucleus and is an organelle, whereas X is the cytosol and not an organelle.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because only X, the cytosol, is primarily composed of water and solutes, Y, the nucleus, primarily contains the DNA. **Choice B** is incorrect because Y, the nucleus, has a specialised function. **Choice D** is incorrect because Y, the nucleus, is not found in prokaryotes, but both X, the cytosol, and Y, the nucleus, are found in eukaryotes."},{"ref":"BI0913","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0913\n\nWhich of the following processes occurs in prokaryotic cells but not eukaryotic cells?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Transcription takes place in the cytoplasm\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Post-transcriptional modification of mRNA\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Regulation of transcription\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Translation of mRNA into a polypeptide chain","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because there is no compartmentalisation in prokaryotes, hence transcription and translation both take place in the cytoplasm.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because post-transcriptional modification of mRNA does not occur in prokaryotes, but does occur in the nucleus of eukaryotic cells. **Choice C** and **D** are incorrect because the regulation of transcription and translation of mRNA into a polypeptide chain occurs in both prokaryotes and eukaryotes."},{"ref":"BI0914","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0914\n\nWhich statement applies to a lysosome?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ It usually fuses with a vacuole in the cytoplasm.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ It participates in anabolic reactions of cellular components.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ It forms from vesicles budding from the nucleus.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ It is an organelle which lacks a membrane and contains enzymes.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because a lysosome usually fuses with phagocytic vacuoles containing pathogens or cellular debris, or other vacuoles in the cytoplasm. The enzymes are then released from the lysosome into the vacuole to digest the contents.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because lysosomes contain digestive enzymes that break down cellular components, which involve catabolic reactions, not anabolic reactions. **Choice C** is incorrect because lysosomes form from vesicles budding off of the Golgi apparatus. **Choice D** is incorrect because lysosomes are membrane-bound organelles."},{"ref":"BI0915","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0915\n\nWhich technique was first developed to separate cellular components and isolate organelles?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Separation based on adhesion, using chromatography\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Separation based on charge, using gel electrophoresis\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Separation based on density, using the ultracentrifuge\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Separation based on size, using the Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR)","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because cellular components are separated using cell fractionation, which requires the ultracentrifuge to separate the cellular components based on density.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because chromatography separates mixtures of solutes, such as pigments, based on adhesion. **Choice B** is incorrect because gel electrophoresis separates molecules such as nucleic acids based on size, charge or both. **Choice D** is incorrect because PCR is used to amplify DNA fragments and is not a separation technique."},{"ref":"BI0916","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0916\n\nWhat is the name of the organelle on the surface of the rough endoplasmic reticulum that is needed to synthesise polypeptide chains?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Nucleus\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Vesicle\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ribosome\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mitochondrion","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because ribosomes are organelles found on the surface of the rough endoplasmic reticulum and are needed to synthesise polypeptide chains.\n\nThe nucleus (**Choice A**) contains DNA, some of which codes for the polypeptides made by the ribosomes. The role of vesicles (**Choice B**) is to store substances and to transport substances, including proteins, between cellular compartments. The mitochondria (**Choice D**) are the organelles responsible for aerobic respiration."},{"ref":"BI0917","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0917\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe diagram shows the process of phagocytosis.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Organelles%20%26%20Compartmentalization/images/BI0917.png\" alt=\"BI0917\">\n[Source: Mango Slices (June 2016). *File: Phagocytosis.svg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved July 24th, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Phagocytosis.svg. CC-BY-SA-4.0]\n\n1. On the diagram, label a lysosome using the letter L. [1 mark]\n\n2. State, with a reason, whether a lysosome is an organelle or not. [1 mark]\n\n3. Predict, with a reason, how a cell might be affected if the enzymes present in lysosomes were not stored within a phospholipid bilayer. [2 marks]\n\n4. Describe how ultracentrifugation is used in the study of organelles. [2 marks]\n\n5. Distinguish between prokaryotes and eukaryotes, in terms of compartmentalisation. [1 mark]\n\n6. Explain how compartmentalisation is beneficial during phagocytosis. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ letter L is written correctly beside the lysosome [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Organelles%20%26%20Compartmentalization/images/BI0917ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0917ANSA\">\n[Source: Mango Slices (June 2016). *File: Phagocytosis.svg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved July 24th, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Phagocytosis.svg. CC-BY-SA-4.0]\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ lysosomes are organelles because they have, a specific function [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ lysosomes are organelles because they are membrane-bound [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n A lysosome is an **organelle** because it is adapted to **perform a specific function** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **one** of the following predictions:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ waste **OR** cellular components **OR** pathogens are not digested **OR** broken down completely [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ digestive enzymes can cause damage **OR** break down **OR** digest other organelles **OR** cellular components in the cell [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ loss of, **OR** disrupted, cellular homeostasis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cell death [1 mark]\n\n Any **one** of the following reasons:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the digestive enzymes would diffuse throughout the cytoplasm [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the digestive enzymes would not be concentrated at an appropriate location **OR** released into vacuoles [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n If there was no compartmentalisation, the **digestive enzymes in the lysosome would cause damage** ($\\checkmark$) to the cell because the digestive enzymes would **diffuse throughout the cytoplasm** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ for cell fractionation **OR** to separate cellular components **OR** isolate organelles [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ separated based on size **OR** density [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ heaviest cell components **OR** organelle settle at the bottom as pellet [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ lightest cell components **OR** organelle remain at the top as supernatant [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cell lysis carried out to release cellular components **OR** a suitable process of cell lysis suggested [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Ultracentrifugation is used in the study of organelles in **cell fractionation** to **separate cellular components** ($\\checkmark$). The cell components can be separated **based on density**, where the **densest cell components settle to the bottom**, and the **least dense cell components remain at the top** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Ultracentrifugation is used in the study of organelles in **cell fractionation** to **separate cellular components** ($\\checkmark$). The cell components can be separated **based on density**, where the **densest cell components settle to the bottom**, and the **least dense cell components remain at the top** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n> Watch a key concept video on ultracentrifugation (SL): here\n\n> Watch a key concept video on ultracentrifugation (HL): here\n\n5. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in prokaryotes, nucleoid and cytoplasm are not separated, **WHEREAS** in eukaryotes, nucleus and cytoplasm are separated [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in prokaryotes, no membrane-bound organelles are present in the cytoplasm, **WHEREAS** in eukaryotes, there are membrane-bound organelles [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The difference between prokaryotes and eukaryotes in terms of compartmentalisation is that in **prokaryotes**, the **nucleoid and cytoplasm** are **not separated**, whereas in **eukaryotes**, the **nucleus and cytoplasm** are **separated** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ compartmentalisation is the separation of cellular components into compartments **OR** regions by a membrane **OR** phagocytosis is a type of endocytosis process where materials **OR** pathogens are ingested **OR** engulfed **OR** taken into another living cell within a membrane [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ endocytosis of pathogen **OR** material requires formation of vacuole using plasma membrane [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ phagosome, **OR** vesicle **OR** phagocytic vacuole, isolates, **OR** prevents spread of, the ingested, **OR** engulfed, pathogen, **OR** materials, from the rest of the cell's interior by a membrane [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ membrane of lysosome isolates digestive enzymes, **OR** prevents digestion of cell **OR** self-digestion [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ membrane of lysosome allows for ideal conditions **OR** pH for enzymes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ phagolysosome, **OR** fusing of phagosome and lysosome, allows targeting of enzymes **OR** provides controlled, **OR** isolated, environment for digestion of pathogen, **OR** material [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allows processing and presentation of antigens **OR** allows cell to present antigens on cell surface membrane [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allows for management, **OR** elimination, of waste of phagocytosis [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Compartmentalisation is the **separation of cellular components into compartments** by either a single or double **membrane** layer ($\\checkmark$). Compartmentalisation is important during phagocytosis because **pathogens are engulfed** by the formation of a **vacuole using the plasma membrane** ($\\checkmark$). The **membrane** around the **phagolysosome**, formed from the fusion of the phagosome and lysosome, provides an **isolated environment** for the digestion of the pathogen and **prevents spread of enzymes** into the rest of the cell’s interior ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0999","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0999\n\nWhich of the following is true of centrifugation?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ It separates substances by density\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ It separates substances by charge\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ It separates substances by chemical composition\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ It separates substances by colour","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCentrifugation is a technique that primarily separates substances based on their density. When a mixture is centrifuged, particles or components with higher density sediment to the bottom of the tube. **Choice A** is, therefore, correct.\n\nCentrifugation does not separate substances based on their charge. Electrophoresis is a technique that separates particles or molecules based on their charge. **Choice B** is, therefore, incorrect.\nCentrifugation does not directly separate substances based on their chemical composition. Other techniques like chromatography may be more suitable for separating substances by chemical composition. **Choice C** is, therefore, incorrect.\nCentrifugation does not separate substances based on their colour. Colour differences might be observed during centrifugation, but they are not the basis for separation. **Choice D** is, therefore, incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1000","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1000\n\nIdentify the row which is correct regarding the components of cells.\n\n| | Component | Is it an organelle? | Function\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Cell wall | Yes | Structural support and protection |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Cytoplasm | No | Production of ATP |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Golgi apparatus| Yes | Processes, modifies and packages proteins and lipids |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Lysosome | No | Digests macromolecules, pathogens, debris and old organelles|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe Golgi apparatus is a membrane-bound organelle that processes, modifies and packages proteins and lipids for secretion or delivery to various cellular compartments. **Choice C** is, therefore, correct.\n\nThe cell wall (**Choice A**) does provide structural support and protection to plant cells, but it is not an organelle, it is an extracellular structure external to the plasma membrane. The cytoplasm (**Choice B**) is not an organelle, but its role is to provide a medium for metabolic reactions to occur in. Although anaerobic respiration occurs in the cytoplasm, the cytoplasm does not produce ATP itself.. Lysosomes (**Choice D**) digest worn-out organelles, pathogens, macromolecules and debris, however, they are membrane-bound organelles."},{"ref":"BI1056","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1056\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline how gel electrophoresis separates molecules and suggest a use for this separation technique. [3 marks]\n\n2. Explain how compartmentalisation is advantageous to eukaryotic cells. [5 marks]\n\n3. In eukaryotic cells, transcription occurs in the nucleus and translation occurs in the cytoplasm. Explain the benefits to the cell of this physical separation.     [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ separates molecules by passing them through a gel using an electrical charge    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ separates molecules by size **OR** charge, **OR** smaller **OR** shorter molecules move further than larger **OR** longer ones                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ separating DNA **OR** protein **OR** RNA,  **OR** DNA profiling **OR** DNA fingerprinting     [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Gel electrophoresis separates molecules by **size or charge** ($\\checkmark$). The molecules are placed into wells in the gel, and an **electrical charge** is applied ($\\checkmark$). This process can be used to **separate DNA fragments**, in DNA profiling ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows for division of labour    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enzymes and substrates involved in the same processes can be in close proximity to each other          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$   reactions can happen faster **OR** more efficiently        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ optimal pH can be maintained in areas of the cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ substances that can cause damage to the cell can be safely stored **OR** reduces risk of damage from digestive **OR** hydrolytic enzymes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organelles can be moved around the cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ post-transcriptional modifications such as splicing can take place    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lower chance of non-specific interactions    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Eukaryotes having a compartmentalised structure means that enzymes and substrates involved in the same processes can be in close **proximity** ($\\checkmark$) to each other. **Optimal pH** ($\\checkmark$) can be maintained in certain areas of the cell so reactions can happen **faster** or more efficiently ($\\checkmark$) . It also means that substances that can damage the cell such as digestive enzymes can be **safely stored** ($\\checkmark$) , and organelles such as ribosomes can **move** around the cell ($\\checkmark$). It also allows important processes to occur such as **splicing** of pre-mRNA ($\\checkmark$), which increases the variety of polypeptides that can be made.\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleus provides a controlled environment for transcription        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA protected within nucleus    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transcription factors **OR** regulatory proteins can enter nucleus     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ this separation allows for precise control over when and where genes are transcribed    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ribosomes are located in the cytoplasm        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ newly transcribed RNA can be processed and modified in the nucleus before it is transported to the cytoplasm (for translation)         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ this spatial separation prevents potential conflicts between transcription and translation machinery    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ modifications of mRNA in nucleus are essential for the maturation and stability of the RNA before it is transported to the cytoplasm     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ribosomes can simultaneously synthesise multiple polypeptides from a single mRNA molecule, increasing efficiency             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA molecules that are not correctly processed **OR** modified in the nucleus can be prevented from entering the cytoplasm for translation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ helps maintain the accuracy of gene expression        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ regulatory proteins **OR** other factors can interact with mRNA molecules in the cytoplasm to influence their translation        [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The DNA is **protected inside the nucleus** ($\\checkmark$) and the nucleus provides a **controlled environment** for transcription ($\\checkmark$) to occur. **Transcription factors** can **enter** the nucleus ($\\checkmark$) but DNA cannot leave, so this provides **precise control** over where and when the genes are **transcribed** ($\\checkmark$). The **mRNA** is **modified** ($\\checkmark$) before leaving the nucleus, and this is essential for its **stability** ($\\checkmark$) and use in translation. **Ribosomes** are located in the **cytoplasm** ($\\checkmark$) and this **spatial separation prevents conflict** ($\\checkmark$) between transcription and translation machinery. Cytoplasmic ribosomes can **simultaneously synthesise multiple polypeptide** chains ($\\checkmark$) from one mRNA strand, **increasing efficiency** ($\\checkmark$). mRNA that has **not been properly processed** can be **prevented from leaving** the nucleus ($\\checkmark$) **preventing incorrect** proteins from being formed by **translation** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1260","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1260\n\nWhich list of cell organelles has a role in protein synthesis and modification?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Nucleus, ribosomes, smooth endoplasmic reticulum\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Nucleus, ribosomes, rough endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi apparatus\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Nucleus, smooth endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi apparatus\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Nucleus, ribosomes, rough endoplasmic reticulum, vacuoles","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because DNA in the nucleus has genes, the ribosomes assemble amino acids into polypeptides, and these polypeptides then enter the lumen of the rough endoplasmic reticulum where they are folded and modified, and the Golgi apparatus processes proteins. **Choice A** and **choice C** are incorrect because the smooth endoplasmic reticulum produces lipids, it does not produce proteins. **Choice D** is incorrect because vacuoles do not have a role in protein synthesis and release."},{"ref":"BI1264","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1264\n\nWhich of these is **not** an adaptation of mitochondria for the production of ATP?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Large surface area of cristae\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Small volumes of liquid inside thylakoids\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Small volume of intermembrane space\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Enzymes compartmentalised in matrix","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because thylakoids are found in chloroplasts, not mitochondria. **Choice A** is incorrect because the large surface area of cristae gives a large surface area for the electron transport chain. **Choice C** is incorrect because mitochondria do have a small volume of intermembrane space, which allows a concentration gradient to be established. **Choice D** is incorrect because enzymes are compartmentalised in the matrix for the reactions of the Krebs cycle."},{"ref":"BI1265","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1265\n\nWhich of these is not an adaptation of chloroplasts for photosynthesis?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Small volumes of fluid inside thylakoids\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Large surface area of thylakoids\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Contain own DNA for making enzymes for photosynthesis\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Infoldings of inner membranes","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because chloroplasts do not have infoldings, it is the mitochondria that have these.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because chloroplasts do have small volumes of liquids inside thylakoids. **Choice B** is incorrect because thylakoids in chloroplasts do have a large surface area. **Choice C** is incorrect because chloroplasts do contain their own DNA for making enzymes needed for photosynthesis."},{"ref":"BI1266","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1266\n\nWhich of these is not a benefit of the nuclear membrane?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Pores allow mRNA to move through between transcription and translation\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Regularly allow transcription factors to move out of the nucleus\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Able to dissociate into vesicles during mitosis\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Provides compartmentalisation to separate transcription from translation","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nTranscription factors regularly move into the nucleus, not out of it; **Choice B** is therefore **NOT** a benefit of the nuclear membrane and is the correct answer.\n\n**Choice A** is a benefit and so an incorrect answer. Nuclear pores allow mRNA to exit the nucleus after transcription so it can be translated in the cytoplasm.\n\n**Choice C** is a benefit and so an incorrect answer. During mitosis, the nuclear membrane breaks down into vesicles, facilitating chromosome segregation and later reassembly.\n\n**Choice D** is also a benefit, and so is an incorrect answer. The nuclear membrane allows transcription to occur in a controlled compartment, separated from translation in the cytoplasm, which is essential for gene regulation in eukaryotes."},{"ref":"BI1267","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1267\n\nWhich of these is a correct description of free ribosomes and bound ribosomes?\n\n| | Free ribosomes | Bound ribosomes |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Produce polypeptides to make proteins for use in the cell | Located on the smooth endoplasmic reticulum |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Produce polypeptides to make proteins for release from the cell | Located on the rough endoplasmic reticulum |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Produce polypeptides to make proteins for release from the cell | Located on the smooth endoplasmic reticulum |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Produce polypeptides to make proteins for use in the cell | Located on the rough endoplasmic reticulum |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because free ribosomes produce polypeptides for proteins that are used in the cell, and bound ribosomes attach to the rough endoplasmic reticulum.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because bound ribosomes are on the rough, not the smooth, endoplasmic reticulum.\n **Choice B** is incorrect because free ribosomes do not produce polypeptides for proteins that will be released from the cell. **Choice C** is incorrect because bound ribosomes are not bound to the smooth endoplasmic reticulum, they are bound to the rough endoplasmic reticulum."},{"ref":"BI1268","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1268\n\nIdentify the molecule that has a role in the formation of vesicles.\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Clathrin\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Surfactant\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cysteine\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Collagen","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because clathrin is a cell membrane protein that has an important role in forming vesicles for endocytosis. **Choice B** is incorrect because type II pneumocytes produce surfactant. **Choice C** is incorrect because cysteine is an amino acid that forms disulfide bonds in the tertiary structure of proteins. **Choice D** is incorrect because collagen is a non-conjugated protein found in connective tissue."},{"ref":"BI1269","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1269\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nOrganelles are adapted for their function(s). The diagram shows the basic structure of a chloroplast.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Organelles%20%26%20Compartmentalization/images/BI1269.png\" alt=\"BI1269\">\n\n1.\n 1. Identify the structures labelled II and IV in the diagram. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Using the labels from the diagram, identify a location with a large surface area and where a concentration gradient is established. [1 mark]\n\n2. Identify three similarities between the mitochondrial and chloroplast structure. [3 marks]\n\n3. Outline the roles of the rough endoplasmic reticulum and the Golgi apparatus in protein production. [3 marks]\n\n4. Outline the formation and function of vesicles. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1. **Both** structures must be identified:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ II is the inner membrane **AND** IV is a thylakoid **OR** granum [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n II is the **inner membrane**, and IV is a **thylakoid** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ IV has a large surface area and is a location where a concentration gradient can be established [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Part **IV** ($\\checkmark$) is the thylakoid membrane which has a large surface area and is a location where a concentration gradient can be established.\n\n2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have a double membrane **OR** inner and outer membrane [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have a large surface area [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have electron transport chains **OR** proteins that facilitate electron transport [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have their own DNA **OR** circular DNA molecules [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have 70S ribosomes **OR** can synthesise their own proteins [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have small intermembrane spaces where a concentration gradient can be established [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have enzymes **OR** ATP synthases [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Chloroplasts and mitochondria both have a **double membrane** ($\\checkmark$). Chloroplasts and mitochondria both have **70s ribosomes** ($\\checkmark$) and their **own, circular, DNA** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. **Three** from (at least one function for each organelle):\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ rough endoplasmic reticulum is the location of protein synthesis of proteins that will be released from the cell **OR** to be secreted [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ rough endoplasmic reticulum is the location of proteins for transport within the cell [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ rough endoplasmic is where fixed ribosomes attach to [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ribosomes on rough endoplasmic reticulum synthesise polypeptide chains [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ polypeptide chains enter the lumen of the rough endoplasmic reticulum **OR** interior of the rough endoplasmic reticulum where they are folded to form proteins [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ rough endoplasmic reticulum is involved with modification of proteins OR identification of misfolded **OR** improperly assembled proteins [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ rough endoplasmic reticulum packages proteins into transport vesicles to be sent to the Golgi apparatus [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Golgi apparatus processes **OR** modifies proteins from the rough endoplasmic reticulum [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Golgi apparatus processes **OR** modifies proteins for secretion [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Golgi apparatus produces vesicles for secretion of proteins [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Golgi apparatus has a role in the formation of lysosomes [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The rough endoplasmic reticulum produces **proteins to be secreted** ($\\checkmark$) and **proteins for transport in the cells** ($\\checkmark$). The Golgi apparatus **processes proteins for secretion** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Answer must include **one** point about the formation and **one** point about the function:\n\n *Formation:* \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles are surrounded by a lipid bilayer [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ clathrin is a protein that has a role in vesicle formation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ some vesicles are formed when the cell membrane invaginates **OR** some vesicles are formed by the Golgi apparatus **OR** some vesicles are formed by the rER [1 mark]\n\n *Function:* \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles can act as storage organelles [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles are used to transport substances inside cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles are used for endocytosis **OR** phagocytosis **OR** pinocytosis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles are used for exocytosis **OR** release of neurotransmitters **OR** any other suitable example of exocytosis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles can form lysosomes **OR** peroxisomes **OR** any other suitable example [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Vesicles are cell structures **surrounded by a lipid bilayer** ($\\checkmark$) that contain substances. Vesicles can be used to **transport substances** ($\\checkmark$) around cells, to release substances from cells and to take substances into cells."},{"ref":"BI1270","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1270\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nOrganelles are adapted for their functions.\n\n1. Outline the structure and function of vesicles.    [4 marks]\n\n2. Compare and contrast the structure and function of the rough endoplasmic reticulum and the Golgi apparatus.    [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain how the membranes of the mitochondria, chloroplasts and the nucleus are adapted for their functions.    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles are membrane-bound organelles    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles transport substances within cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles store substances    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles are used in exocytosis **OR** release of neurotransmitters **OR** any other suitable example of exocytosis    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles are used in endocytosis **OR** phagocytosis **OR** pinocytosis    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lysosomes are vesicles that contain digestive enzymes **OR** lysosomes digest material taken in by endocytosis **OR** phagocytosis **OR** lysosomes break down damaged cell organelles    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles are formed by the endoplasmic reticulum **OR** the Golgi apparatus    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the vesicle membrane fuses with the plasma membrane    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles are moved along microtubules by motor proteins    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ secretory vesicles transport substances to be released from the cell **OR** secretory vesicles transport substances such as hormones to the cell membrane to be released    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ clathrin is a protein that is important for vesicle formation **OR** clathrin coats the membrane and helps it bud inwards to form vesicles in endocytosis    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Vesicles are **membrane bound organelles** ($\\checkmark$). A protein called **clathrin has a role in the formation of vesicles** ($\\checkmark$). Vesicles **transport and store substances** ($\\checkmark$). Secretory vesicles **transport substances for release from the cell** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** similarity and **one** difference to gain full marks*):\n\n    ***Similarities:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the rough endoplasmic reticulum **AND** the Golgi apparatus are made of flattened membrane sacs **OR** **BOTH** the rough endoplasmic reticulum and the Golgi apparatus are membrane-bound organelles    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** the rough endoplasmic reticulum and the Golgi apparatus are involved in modification **OR** processing of proteins **OR** post-translational modification    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are involved in the transport of proteins after translation    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the Golgi apparatus membrane sacs are not interconnected **BUT** the rough endoplasmic reticulum is interconnected    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ribosomes are attached to the rough endoplasmic reticulum **BUT** ribosomes do not attach to the Golgi apparatus    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rough endoplasmic reticulum processes polypeptides formed by the ribosomes **WHEREAS** the Golgi apparatus processes proteins received from the rough endoplasmic reticulum    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the Golgi apparatus modifies (e.g. glycosylates), sorts and packages proteins into vesicles for secretion    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the rough endoplasmic reticulum synthesises proteins for secretion **OR** for membranes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the membrane of the rough endoplasmic reticulum is continuous with the nuclear envelope **BUT** the Golgi apparatus is not connected to the nuclear envelope    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Both** the rough endoplasmic reticulum and the Golgi apparatus are made of **flattened membrane sacs** ($\\checkmark$). The rough endoplasmic reticulum and the Golgi apparatus **both** have a **role in processing proteins** ($\\checkmark$) in cells, but the **rough endoplasmic reticulum processes polypeptides produced by the ribosomes**, whereas the **Golgi apparatus processes proteins** received **from** the **rough endoplasmic reticulum** ($\\checkmark$). The **rough endoplasmic reticulum** has **ribosomes** attached and its membrane is **continuous** with the **nuclear envelope**, whereas the **Golgi apparatus** is **not associated with ribosomes** ($\\checkmark$) and is **not connected to** the **nuclear envelope** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** mark point for each of the organelles to gain full marks*):\n\n    ***Mitochondria:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ membrane infoldings of mitochondria provide a large surface area    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electron transport chain proteins are located on the inner membrane (cristae) of mitochondria    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ inner membrane contains ATP synthases for ATP production    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ small intermembrane space between the inner and outer membranes allows a proton gradient to be established quickly    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ inner membrane of mitochondria is impermeable to most ions allowing for a proton gradient to be established    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ outer membrane of mitochondria separates the contents of the mitochondria from the rest of the cell    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Chloroplasts:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ thylakoid membranes give a large surface area in chloroplasts **OR** give a large surface area for the absorption of light    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ membranes of thylakoids in chloroplasts contain photosystems for converting light energy to chemical energy    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electron transport chain proteins in the thylakoid membranes pump protons into the thylakoid space, establishing a proton gradient    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP synthase in the thylakoid membranes produces ATP by chemiosmosis **OR** NADP is reduced to NADPH at the end of the electron transport chain, for use in the light-independent reactions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the small volume of fluid inside the thylakoids (thylakoid space) allows a proton gradient to be established quickly    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Nuclear membrane:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nuclear pores in the nuclear membrane allow substances to pass into and out of the nucleus **OR** nuclear pores allow mRNA to exit the nucleus    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the double membrane separates transcription from translation, allowing mRNA to be modified before translation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the nuclear membrane breaks down into vesicles during mitosis **OR** meiosis **AND** the vesicles fuse to reform the nuclear envelope afterwards    [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The membranes of **cristae** in mitochondria give a **large surface area** for **electron transport chains** ($\\checkmark$), the **small intermembrane** space allows **concentration gradients** to be **established** ($\\checkmark$), and the membranes of the cristae **contain ATP synthases** for the **production** of **ATP** ($\\checkmark$). The **nuclear membrane** is a double membrane that has **pores** for the **movement** of **substances into and out** of the **nucleus** ($\\checkmark$) and the membrane **breaks down into vesicles** during **mitosis** and **meiosis**, which **later fuse** to **reform** the nuclear **envelope** ($\\checkmark$). The membranes of the **thylakoids** in chloroplasts give a **large surface area** for **absorption of light** ($\\checkmark$) and the **small volume** of the **thylakoid space** allows a **proton gradient** to be **established** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1279","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1279\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe diagram shows the basic structure of a chloroplast.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Organelles%20%26%20Compartmentalization/images/BI1279.png\" alt=\"BI1279\">\n\n1. Identify the structures labelled W and Y. [1 mark]\n\n2. Identify the locations of (i) the increased proton concentration for chemiosmosis, (ii) the photosystems and (iii) the reactions of the light-independent stage. [1 mark]\n\n3. Explain why chloroplasts are green. [1 mark]\n\n4. Outline the adaptations of chloroplasts for their function. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. **BOTH** are needed:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ W is the thylakoid membrane **OR** lamella **AND** Y is the stroma [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n W is the **thylakoid membrane, and Y is the stroma** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. All **three** answers required:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ (i) the build-up of the proton concentration occurs in Z **AND** (ii) photosystems are located on the membrane of W **AND** (iii) reactions of the light-independent stage happen in Y [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n (i) The increased proton concentration for chemiosmosis occurs inside the **grana or in the intermembrane space (Z)**; (ii) the location of the photosystems is the **thylakoid membranes (W)**; (iii) the location of the independent stage of photosynthesis is the **stroma (Y)** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chloroplasts contain chlorophyll **WHICH** is green [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chloroplasts contain chlorophyll which reflects green light **OR** absorbs colours other than green [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Chloroplasts contain pigments for absorbing light and one of the pigments is **chlorophyll which reflects green light** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **four** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ outer membrane provides protection [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ inner membrane for regulation of the transport of molecules in and out [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chloroplasts contain chlorophyll **OR** chloroplasts contain pigments that can absorb light and convert it to chemical energy [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ many grana provide large surface area for light absorption **OR** thylakoid membranes provide large surface area for light absorption **OR** large surface area for electron transport chain **OR** reactions of the light-dependent stage [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ own DNA and ribosomes allow chloroplasts to synthesise their proteins **OR** enzymes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ compact arrangement of grana allows optimal light absorption in space provided [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ narrow intermembrane space of grana **OR** thylakoids for rapid formation of concentration gradient of protons [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ stroma contains enzymes that catalyse the reactions of the light-independent stage [1 mark]\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Chloroplasts **contain pigments that can absorb light** ($\\checkmark$) and convert it to chemical energy. **Thylakoid** membranes provide a **large surface area** for **light absorption** ($\\checkmark$). Chloroplasts have their **own DNA** and **ribosomes** to synthesise their **proteins**, such as **enzymes** needed for photosynthesis ($\\checkmark$). A **narrow intermembrane space** between the thylakoid membranes means a **high concentration of protons** can rapidly build up for chemiosmosis ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1319","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1319\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast transcription and translation between eukaryotes and prokaryotes.[5 marks]\n\n2. Describe how vesicles link translation and exocytosis.    [3 marks]\n\n3. Explain how errors in transcription may result in altered enzyme function.    [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **five** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** similarity and **one** difference to achieve full marks*):\n\n    ***Similarities*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ribosomes are the site of translation in **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ translation occurs in the cytoplasm  in **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA is synthesised in the 5’ to 3’ direction during transcription in  **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the mRNA code is read in the 5’ to 3’ direction in  **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** prokaryotes and eukaryotes have promoter regions **OR** sequences which enzymes **OR** transcription factors can bind to initiate **OR** enhance **OR** repress transcription    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA polymerase is involved in transcription in **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transcription involves the use of a DNA template to synthesise RNA in **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ translation involves the use of an RNA template **OR** mRNA to synthesise protein in **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the base pairing rules for DNA and RNA are the same **OR** Adenine (A) pairs with Thymine (T) in DNA **OR** Uracil (U) in RNA, while Cytosine (C) pairs with Guanine (G)  in **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there are specific stop signals that mark the end of transcription and translation in **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** prokaryotes and eukaryotes use transfer RNA (tRNA) molecules in translation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the same codons typically code for the same amino acids in **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes            [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transcription and translation are coupled **OR** occur simultaneously in prokaryotes **WHEREAS** in eukaryotes transcription occurs before translation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in eukaryotes transcription occurs in the nucleus **AND** translation occurs in the cytoplasm **WHEREAS** in prokaryotes **BOTH** processes occur in the cytoplasm    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleosomes are present in eukaryotic DNA and help to regulate transcription **BUT** are absent from prokaryotic DNA    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$eukaryotic mRNA undergoes post-transcriptional modification **OR** splicing **OR** addition of a poly-A tail **OR** addition of a 3’ cap **WHEREAS** prokaryotic DNA does not **OR** eukaryotes have introns **WHEREAS** prokaryotes do not        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 70S ribosomes carry out translation in prokaryotes **WHEREAS** 80S ribosomes carry out translation in eukaryotes                    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Ribosomes** are the site of **translation** in both eukaryotes and prokaryotes ($\\checkmark$), however, **70S ribosomes** carry out translation in prokaryotes, and **80S ribosomes** carry out translation in eukaryotes ($\\checkmark$). During transcription, **mRNA is synthesised** in the **5’ to 3’** direction in both eukaryotes and prokaryotes ($\\checkmark$), and both involve **RNA polymerase** ($\\checkmark$). However, in eukaryotes, the mRNA undergoes **post-transcriptional modification**, whereas this does not occur in prokaryotes ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polypeptides are translated by ribosomes on the rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER) **OR** after translation, polypeptide chains are folded into proteins **OR** modified inside RER    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles containing polypeptides bud off the RER **AND** are transported to the Golgi apparatus    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polypeptides are processed into their final form in the Golgi apparatus    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles bud off the Golgi apparatus        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles fuse with the plasma membrane of the cell     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ releasing proteins in the process of exocytosis    [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Exocytosis is the process by which **vesicles** (fluid-filled membrane sacs) within cells move towards the **plasma membrane** of the cell and **fuse** with it ($\\checkmark$), which allows them to **release their contents** ($\\checkmark$). This process can be linked to translation, as polypeptides are **translated** by ribosomes on the **RER** ($\\checkmark$), and then transferred in **vesicles** to the **Golgi** apparatus where they are **processed**  ($\\checkmark$) and then are transferred via vesicles to where they are needed outside the cell, via **exocytosis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a substitution mutation **OR** the incorrect RNA nucleotide is incorporated into the growing mRNA chain            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ an extra RNA nucleotide **OR** base is incorporated **OR** inserted **OR** removed **OR** deleted from the growing mRNA chain    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ may result in incorrect splicing of pre-mRNA     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ may result in translation **OR** production of a longer **OR** shorter polypeptide chain    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sequence of amino acids in the polypeptide chain can only be altered if the mutation occurs in the coding region of the gene **OR** exon    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic code dictates the correspondence between codons and specific amino acids [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ may result in the wrong amino acid being incorporated into the polypeptide chain **OR** wrong primary structure during translation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ leads to incorrect folding of the tertiary structure of the polypeptide chain        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as the incorrect amino acid may have an R-group that interacts differently with the rest of the chain            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the incorrect polypeptide chain is incorporated into the tertiary **OR** quaternary structure of the globular enzyme structure        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ results in an altered active site shape **OR** altered allosteric sites **OR** loss of active site [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ therefore substrate is unable to bind **OR** loss of catalytic activity        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ results in potential loss of stability in enzyme structure **OR** enzyme is more easily affected by changes in the environment **OR** enzyme denatures more easily        [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Transcription is the process by which an RNA copy of DNA is made. Errors in transcription can result in the **incorrect RNA** ($\\checkmark$) nucleotide, or an **extra RNA** nucleotide being incorporated into the growing mRNA chain ($\\checkmark$). This may result in **incorrect splicing** of the **pre-mRNA** molecule ($\\checkmark$) before it leaves the nucleus, which can result in the translation of a **truncated polypeptide** chain ($\\checkmark$) or the **wrong amino acid** being incorporated into the polypeptide chain ($\\checkmark$). This can result in the incorrect polypeptide chain being incorporated into the **tertiary structure** of the **globular** enzyme structure ($\\checkmark$) , which may result in an **altered active site** ($\\checkmark$), thereby **preventing** a **substrate** from successfully binding ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1366","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1366\n\nWhich of the following would be considered an organelle?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ cell wall\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ vesicles\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ cytoskeleton\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ cytoplasm","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nOrganelles are discrete parts of a cell specialised to carry out particular functions. **Choice B** is correct because vesicles are membrane-bound structures that have various roles within the cell, such as transporting proteins.\n\n**Choices A, C,** and **D** are incorrect as they are not considered organelles due to their non-discrete nature and non-specific roles within cells. With the exception of the cytoplasm (**Choice D**), they are also not enclosed within membranes, which is a typical feature of most organelles."},{"ref":"BI1367","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1367\n\nWhich of the following is capable of moving through nuclear pores?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | transcription proteins|\n    | II. | tRNA |\n    | III. | ribosomal subunits |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nNuclear pores control the movement of substances between the nucleus and cytoplasm. Substances required by the nucleus, such as the proteins involved in transcription (**Statement I**), can be transported into the nucleus through nuclear pores. Conversely, substances produced in the nucleus, such as various forms of RNA (**Statement II**) and ribosomal subunits composed of rRNA and ribosomal proteins (**Statement III**), are transported through nuclear pores to the cytoplasm, where they can perform their functions. **Choice D** is correct since all three statements are true.\n\n**Choices A, B**, and **C** are incorrect as they are missing one or more statements."},{"ref":"BI1368","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1368\n\nWhich of the following processes is correctly described for prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells?\n\n| | RNA splicing | Post-transcriptional modification | Translation |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Prokaryotes only | Prokaryotes only | Both |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Eukaryotes only | Eukaryotes only | Both |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Eukaryotes only | Prokaryotes only | Eukaryotes only |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ |Prokaryotes only | Eukaryotes only | Eukaryotes only |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn eukaryotes, post-transcriptional modification is possible as transcription occurs in the nucleus. Immature mRNA is modified by removing introns in a process called RNA splicing. Then, the mature mRNA leaves the nucleus to undergo translation in the cytoplasm. In prokaryotes, transcription and translation occur simultaneously in the cytoplasm, but no post-transcriptional modification or RNA splicing can occur. **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choices A, C**, and **D** are incorrect since prokaryotes do not perform post-transcriptional modification or RNA splicing, although they still carry out translation."},{"ref":"BI1369","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1369\n\nThe following diagram shows the results of an experiment to separate a cell fragment suspension using ultracentrifugation. Which of the following statements would not be true?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Organelles%20%26%20Compartmentalization/images/BI1369.png\" alt=\"BI1369\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Thylakoid membranes are less dense than mitochondria.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ There are more mitochondria than other organelles.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The cell fragment suspension must be from an organism capable of photosynthesis.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The cell fragment suspension must be from a eukaryotic organism.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nUltracentrifugation is a method used to separate cell components, such as organelles, based on their size and density. It does not, however, tell us much about the abundance of each organelle. **Choice B** is not true and so is the correct answer.\n\n**Choice A** is a true statement and so an incorrect answer, since thylakoid membranes do appear higher in the column and must, therefore, be less dense than mitochondria. **Choice C** is a true statement and so an incorrect answer, since the presence of thylakoids indicates that this sample must have contained chloroplasts and was thus obtained from an organism capable of photosynthesis. **Choice D** is a true statement and therefore an incorrect answer, since mitochondria are only found in eukaryotic organisms."},{"ref":"BI1370","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1370\n\nWhich of the following is **not** an advantage of cell compartmentalisation?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ability to control the distribution of metabolites and enzymes\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Improved nutrient storage\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Reduced energy costs\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Improved metabolic efficiency","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCompartmentalisation involves organising cell components into distinct and specialised organelles, typically using membranes. Although compartmentalisation has many advantages, maintaining organelles and their membranes to achieve this separation involves considerable energy costs. **Choice C** is correct.\n\nCompartmentalisation allows cells to separate different metabolites and enzymes, ensuring they are in the correct place and concentration, thus ensuring the efficiency of biochemical reactions. **Choice A** is, therefore, an incorrect answer, as the ability to control the distribution of metabolites and enzymes is an advantage. **Choice B** is an incorrect answer since nutrient storage is possible in vacuoles and other organelles. **Choice D** is an incorrect answer since cell compartmentalisation allows cells to prevent incompatible metabolic processes from taking place simultaneously in the same region of the cell."},{"ref":"BI1371","marks":"11","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1371\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nThe diagram below shows the organelles of a secretory cell.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Organelles%20%26%20Compartmentalization/images/BI1371A.png\" alt=\"BI1371A\">\n[Source: Adapted from Fischer, Holly (2013, January 31). *File: Organelles of the Secretory Pathway.png* - Wikimedia Commons. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved December 6, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Organelles_of_the_Secretory_Pathway.png Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n1. Identify the structures labelled X and Y on the diagram.    [2 marks]\n\n2.\n    1. Describe how secretory proteins found in structure X are produced and transported within the cell.                [4 marks]\n\n    2. State the transport mechanism used to release the contents of structure X.    [1 mark]\n\n3. Describe the advantages of cell compartmentalisation. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    *Structure X*:\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicle **OR** secretory vesicle [1 mark]\n\n    *Structure Y*:\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleus                [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Structure X is a **vesicle** ($\\checkmark$). Structure Y is the **nucleus** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ secretory proteins are synthesised by ribosomes [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ these ribosomes are attached to the rough endoplasmic reticulum [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ proteins enter the RER lumen, where they are folded and may be modified [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transport vesicles containing the proteins bud off from the RER [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ these vesicles carry the proteins to the Golgi apparatus [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in the Golgi apparatus, proteins undergo further processing **OR** are packaged into secretory vesicles, such as X [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ secretory vesicles transport the proteins towards the plasma membrane [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Secretory proteins are produced by the **ribosomes** ($\\checkmark$) on the **rough endoplasmic reticulum** ($\\checkmark$). **Vesicles** are **formed** from the membranes of the endoplasmic reticulum and are used to **transport these proteins** ($\\checkmark$)  to the Golgi apparatus ($\\checkmark$). At the **Golgi apparatus**, the proteins are **modified and repackaged into secretory vesicles** ($\\checkmark$) before finally reaching the **plasma membrane** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ active transport **OR** exocytosis                [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Proteins are released from vesicles using a form of **active transport** ($\\checkmark$) known as **exocytosis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows organelles to have specialised functions            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ such as the enzymatic digestion of substances in lysosomes **OR** the ingestion of foreign particles into phagocytic vacuoles **OR** any other named example of a compartmentalised organelle and its specialised function            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows metabolic processes to be isolated from each other    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ improves efficiency **OR** prevents incompatible reactions from occurring in the same part of the cell **OR** allows different cellular processes to occur simultaneously without interfering with each other **OR** ensures that energy production is focused and efficient, preventing wasteful reactions in other parts of the cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows the maintenance of concentration gradients **OR** internal conditions that differ from that of the cytoplasm **OR** create distinct microenvironments with specific pH levels **OR** ion concentrations **OR** chemical conditions crucial for particular biochemical reactions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows the cell to store **OR** control the distribution of substances such as enzymes and metabolites **OR** the products of metabolism **OR** nutrients **OR** ions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ protects cellular components from potentially damaging substances **OR** isolates toxic substances    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows sorting **OR** modification of molecules            [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Cell compartmentalisation allows **organelles** to have **specialised functions** ($\\checkmark$), such as the **digestion of wastes in lysosomes** ($\\checkmark$). The use of membranes also allows **metabolic processes** to be **isolated** from each other ($\\checkmark$) in order to **improve efficiency** and **prevent incompatible reactions** ($\\checkmark$) from occurring simultaneously."},{"ref":"BI1372","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1372\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Describe centrifugation and how developments in this technique allowed scientists to improve their understanding of the components of cells.  [4 marks]\n\n2. Distinguish between transcription and translation in prokaryotic and eukaryotic organisms.    [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain the advantages of cell compartmentalisation in eukaryotes.  [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ centrifugation allows the separation of molecular components based on differences in their relative densities **OR** sizes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a centrifuge **OR** ultracentrifuge is a device that spins at high speeds to separate substances in a suspension using centrifugal force    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows the separation of individual organelles            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell contents are homogenised **OR** broken up **OR** plasma membrane is broken to release organelles into the solution                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ substances that are less dense stay at the top **OR** form the supernatant, **WHILE** substances that are more dense settle at the bottom **OR** form the pellet    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ using cell fractionation, a multi-step process that involves successively higher speeds of centrifugation to collect progressively smaller cell components **OR** denser cell components can be separated at lower speeds    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows biologists to study **OR** determine the roles of individual organelles **OR** molecules **OR** specific chemical pathways in a controlled environment without the presence of confounding substances **OR** separate from the other reactions that occur in the cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ used in the famous experiment by Meselson and Stahl to discover the semi-conservative nature of DNA replication **OR** discovery of translation of mRNA **OR** any other example of an experiment or discovery involving centrifugation            [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Centrifugation** is a process used to **separate cell parts based on their relative densities** ($\\checkmark$). An **ultracentrifuge** is a device that **rotates at high speeds** to **separate cell components** using **centrifugal force** ($\\checkmark$). This allows scientists to **isolate** individual **organelles for experimentation** ($\\checkmark$). **Dense organelles settle to the bottom** of the sample following centrifugation, while **less dense organelles stay at the top** ($\\checkmark$). By **successively separating** the **supernatant** and **pellets** formed during centrifugation, many organelles can be isolated in a process known as **cell fractionation** ($\\checkmark$). These techniques have allowed scientists to make many **discoveries** regarding the nature of various organelles and cell processes, such as the **semi-conservative nature of DNA** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in eukaryotes, the genetic material **OR** DNA is separated from the cytoplasm by the nuclear membrane **OR** envelope **WHEREAS**, in prokaryotes, the genetic material is found in the cytoplasm [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in eukaryotes, transcription takes place in the nucleus, and translation occurs in the cytoplasm **WHEREAS** in prokaryotes, both processes take place in the cytoplasm    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transcription and translation are discontinuous **OR** completed separately in eukaryotes **WHEREAS** transcription and translation are continuous **OR** completed simultaneously in prokaryotes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ post-transcriptional modification occurs in eukaryotes **WHEREAS** no post-transcriptional modification **OR** minimal RNA modification occurs in prokaryotes     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ eukaryotic genes contain non-coding segments called introns **WHILE** prokaryotic genes do not **OR** eukaryotes have introns and exons **WHILE** prokaryotes do not [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in eukaryotes, introns are removed to produce mature mRNA **OR** RNA splicing occurs  **WHEREAS**, in prokaryotes, this process does not occur         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ movement of mRNA through nuclear pores into cytoplasm is controlled in eukaryotes, which helps regulate gene expression  **WHEREAS**, in prokaryotes, this form of gene expression regulation cannot happen    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    In **eukaryotic** organisms, the **DNA** is **separated from** the **cytoplasm** by the **nuclear membrane**, whereas the genetic material of **prokaryotes** is **within the cytoplasm** ($\\checkmark$).  In eukaryotes, **transcription** occurs **within the nucleus**, while **translation** occurs **in the cytoplasm**. In prokaryotes, however, **both processes** occur in the **cytoplasm** as these organisms do not have a nucleus ($\\checkmark$). Due to this lack of separation, both **transcription and translation happen simultaneously** in prokaryotes, while in eukaryotes, **transcription** must be **completed first** before **translation** can begin ($\\checkmark$). This allows **post-transcriptional modification** to take place in eukaryotes ($\\checkmark$), whereby the **immature mRNA is edited** through the process of **RNA splicing**, which **removes introns** ($\\checkmark$), sections of a gene that **do not code for proteins**. This **post-transcriptional modification** does not occur in **prokaryotes** as they **do not have introns** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows organelles to have specialised functions                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ such as the enzymatic digestion of substances in lysosomes **OR** the ingestion of foreign particles into phagocytic vacuoles **OR** any other named example of a compartmentalised organelle and its specialised function                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows metabolic processes to be isolated from each other        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ improves efficiency **OR** prevents incompatible reactions from occurring in the same part of the cell **OR** allows different cellular processes to occur simultaneously without interfering with each other **OR** ensures that ATP production is focused and efficient, preventing wasteful reactions in other parts of the cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows the maintenance of concentration gradients **OR** internal conditions that differ from that of the cytoplasm                        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ by controlling the distribution of substances such as enzymes and metabolites **OR** the products of metabolism **OR** nutrients    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ provides increased surface area for reactions                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ such as those that occur in the inner membrane of the mitochondria **OR** electron transport chain **OR** chemiosmosis **OR** other named example of a process within the cell that requires a high surface area    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows nutrients **OR** wastes to be stored separately from other cell components    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ such as starch **OR** cell sap in the vacuoles of plant cells             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows cellular communication **OR** allows precise control of cellular responses    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ using signaling molecules that are released from one compartment and target specific receptors in another            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ protects cellular components from potentially damaging substances **OR** isolates toxic substances, reducing cytotoxicity **OR** risk to the rest of the cell            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ provides an additional layer of protection for important cell structures        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ such as the DNA within the nucleus **OR** any other named example        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows transport **OR** sorting **OR** modification of molecules        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ including the transport of substances in vesicles from the rough endoplasmic reticulum to the Golgi apparatus                            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows the ability to respond to external stimuli using modification of the number **OR** structure of specific organelles **OR** cell compartments    [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Cell **compartmentalisation** uses **membranes** to separate cell components into **specialised organelles** ($\\checkmark$). This allows each organelle to take on unique roles, such as **absorbing foreign particles**, as seen in **phagocytic vacuoles** ($\\checkmark$). Various **cell processes** can be **completed separately** ($\\checkmark$), making them more **efficient** ($\\checkmark$). Compartmentalisation also allows the cell to **control** the production and **distribution** of substances such as **enzymes** ($\\checkmark$), directing them to areas where they are needed. Furthermore, compartmentalisation provides organelles such as the **mitochondria** with vastly **increased surface area** ($\\checkmark$) so that **metabolic reactions** can occur, allowing it to produce much more ATP than would otherwise be possible ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2327","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2327\n\nWhich organelle requires compartmentalisation to concentrate enzymes and prevent damage to the contents of cells?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Lysosome\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Chloroplast\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mitochondrion\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Contractile vacuole","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because lysosomes contain digestive enzymes that would digest the cell if they were not membrane-bound.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the membrane of a chloroplast maintains a high concentration of enzymes for the reactions of photosynthesis inside the chloroplast but these enzymes would not digest the cell.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because the membrane of a mitochondrion maintains a high concentration of enzymes for the reactions of respiration but these enzymes would not digest the cell.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because a contractile vacuole collects and removes excess water to maintain osmolarity, it does not contain digestive enzymes."},{"ref":"BI2653","marks":"11","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2653\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nThe diagram shows the cells that make up the various tissues in a dicotyledonous leaf.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Organelles%20%26%20Compartmentalization/images/EditA.png\" alt=\"EditA\">\n\n1. List **two** structures of plant cells that are not organelles. [2 marks]\n\n2. Explain why compartmentalisation is advantageous in eukaryotes. [5 marks]\n\nThis diagram shows a machine commonly used to study cells.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Organelles%20%26%20Compartmentalization/images/EditB.png\" alt=\"EditB\">\n\n3. Describe how the invention of the ultracentrifuge contributed to the study of organelles. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cell wall [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cytoskeleton [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cytoplasm [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Cell wall** ($\\checkmark$), **cytoplasm** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ enzymes and substrates for a specific process can be kept in close proximity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ optimal pH **OR** chemical environment can be maintained within organelles [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ reactions can proceed more quickly **OR** efficiently [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ damaging substances can be safely contained [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ organelles can be moved around the cell to meet localised energy **OR** functional demands [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ post-transcriptional modifications **OR** splicing **OR** other suitable example can occur in the nucleus [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ lower chance of non-specific interactions between molecules [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allows simultaneous and incompatible reactions to occur in the same cell [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ concentration gradients can be maintained across membranes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ specific targeting of proteins to organelles helps regulate functions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increased surface area for membrane-bound reactions [1 mark]\n\n [5 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Eukaryotes have a compartmentalised cell structure, meaning that **enzymes** and **substrates** for specific processes can be kept in **close proximity** ($\\checkmark$), and **optimal pH can be maintained** within certain **organelles** ($\\checkmark$), allowing **reactions** to proceed **more efficiently** ($\\checkmark$). **Harmful substances** such as hydrolytic enzymes can be **safely contained** ($\\checkmark$), and **organelles** such as ribosomes **can be repositioned** within the cytoplasm **as needed** ($\\checkmark$). Compartmentalisation also enables processes like pre-mRNA **splicing**, increasing the diversity of polypeptides produced from a single gene ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **four** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ultracentrifuge allows separation of cell components based on size **OR** density [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ heavier **OR** more dense organelles pellet first **OR** lighter **OR** less dense organelles pellet later [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ enabled isolation of individual organelles [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ function of organelles could be studied in isolation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ helped confirm compartmentalisation of cellular functions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ made biochemical analysis of specific organelles possible [1 mark]\n\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The ultracentrifuge allowed scientists to **separate cell components** based on their **size** and **density** ($\\checkmark$). **Heavier organelles** such as **nuclei pellet first**, while **lighter ones** like **ribosomes pellet later** ($\\checkmark$). This made it possible to **isolate** individual **organelles for study** ($\\checkmark$). As a result, the **functions** of **specific organelles** could be **investigated** in **isolation**, confirming that different organelles carry out **different roles** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2854","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2854\n\nWhich statement best explains a functional advantage eukaryotic cells gain from separating transcription and translation?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ It prevents mRNA from being translated before it is transcribed.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ It increases the speed of protein synthesis by separating the processes spatially.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ It ensures ribosomes are only assembled in the nucleus before being exported.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ It allows time for extensive post-transcriptional modification of mRNA before translation.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct. In eukaryotic cells, transcription occurs in the nucleus and translation in the cytoplasm. This spatial separation allows time for the mRNA transcript to undergo post-transcriptional modifications (such as 5' capping, splicing, and polyadenylation) before it interacts with ribosomes in the cytoplasm.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because transcription must occur before translation, regardless of compartmentalisation; the issue is not preventing premature translation of incomplete mRNA but enabling modification.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the separation actually slows the onset of translation compared to prokaryotes, but enables higher fidelity and regulation.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because ribosomes are assembled in the nucleolus but function in the cytoplasm. This does not directly relate to mRNA processing."}]},{"name":"Proteins","questions":[{"ref":"BI0328","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0328\n\nThe image shows part of a protein.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Proteins/images/BI0328%20tertiary%20structure%20with%20disulfide%20bond%20BR.png\" alt=\"BI0328 tertiary structure with disulfide bond BR\">\n\nWhich is the highest level of protein structure represented in this image?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Primary\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Secondary\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Tertiary\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Quaternary","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nThe image shows a single polypeptide chain folded into a complex 3D shape. It includes α-helices (secondary structures) and a disulfide bridge (a covalent bond between two cysteines), both of which contribute to the 3D conformation of the polypeptide chain. This overall folding, including interactions like hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds, hydrophobic interactions, and disulfide bonds, defines the tertiary structure. **Choice C** is, therefore, correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the primary structure refers only to the linear sequence of amino acids.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the secondary structure refers to local structures like α-helices and β-pleated sheets, but not the overall folding, and these structures do not contain disulfide bridges.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the quaternary structure involves two or more polypeptide chains, and the image shows only one."},{"ref":"BI0329","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0329\n\nWhich of the following accounts for the secondary structure of a protein?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrogen bonding between two different amino acids in different polypeptide chains\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrogen bonding between two different amino acids in the same polypeptide chain\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Covalent bonding between R groups of amino acids on the same polypeptide chain\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Peptide bonds between two different amino acids in the same polypeptide chain","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nThe secondary structure of a protein is formed by hydrogen bonding between two different amino acids within the same chain, which can result in the formation of alpha-helices and/or beta pleated sheets (**Choice B**). \n\n**Choice A** is incorrect, as any bonding between different polypeptide chains would be part of the quaternary structure. **Choice C** is incorrect as it describes the tertiary structure. **Choice D** is incorrect as it describes the primary structure."},{"ref":"BI0331","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0331\n\nWhat distinguishes the tertiary structure of a protein from the secondary structure?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The secondary structure includes hydrogen bonds between different amino acids.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The secondary structure does not contain bonds between the R- groups of amino acids.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ The secondary structure has peptide bonds between amino acids in the polypeptide chain.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The secondary structure contains disulfide bridges.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nThe secondary structure (α-helices and β-pleated sheets) is stabilised by hydrogen bonds between the backbone atoms of the polypeptide chain. It does not involve interactions between the side chains (R-groups).\n\nThe tertiary structure is the overall 3D folding of a protein and is stabilised by interactions between R-groups, including hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds, hydrophobic interactions, and disulfide bridges.\n\nTherefore, the key distinction is that tertiary structure depends on R-group interactions, whereas secondary structure does not. **Choice B**  is correct.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect because hydrogen bonds occur in both secondary and tertiary structures. \\\n\n**Choice C**  is incorrect because peptide bonds exist in all protein structures, so this is not unique to secondary structure. \\\n\n**Choice D**  is incorrect because disulfide bridges are part of the tertiary structure, not the secondary."},{"ref":"BI0431","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0431\n\nWhat type of molecule is represented in the diagram below?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Proteins/images/image.png\" alt=\"BI0431\">\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Fatty acid\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Phospholipid\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Nucleotide\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Amino acid","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nThe amino group and the carboxyl group indicate that this molecule is an amino acid.\n\nFatty acids and phospholipids (**Choices A** and **B**) do not have an amino group, while a nucleotide (**Choice C**) should have a phosphate group, a ribose or deoxyribose sugar and a nitrogenous base."},{"ref":"BI0432","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0432\n\nWhich of the structures A-D represents the -R group in the molecule below?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Proteins/images/BI0432.png\" alt=\"BI0432\">\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.  |    |     |B.||\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Proteins/images/BI0432a.png\" alt=\"BI0432a\"> | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Proteins/images/BI0432b.png\" alt=\"BI0432b\">|\n| | | | | |\n| | | | | |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$C. | | |D. ||\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Proteins/images/BI0432c.png\" alt=\"BI0432c\"> | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Proteins/images/BI0432d.png\" alt=\"BI0432d\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nThe -R group is connected to the carbon atom which the amino group is attached.\n\nThe amino group (**Choice A**) and the carboxyl group (**Choice B**) are functional groups but are not the -R group that distinguishes one amino acid from another. The part of the molecule shown in **Choice D** comprises part of the -R group but is missing a -CH$_2$."},{"ref":"BI0519","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0519\n\nWhich characteristics distinguish between an alpha helix and a beta-pleated sheet?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | The positions of hydrogen bonds between different amino acids |\n    | II. | The location of hydrophobic interactions between nonpolar R groups |\n    | III. | The positions of disulfide bridges between cysteine residues |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAlpha helices and beta-pleated sheets are different types of secondary structures in proteins.\n\nBoth alpha helices and beta sheets hold their shapes due to the hydrogen bonds that form between the O atom of the carbonyl (C=O) group of one amino acid and the H atom in the amino (N-H) group of the next. However, the arrangement of these hydrogen bonds differs between alpha-helices and beta-pleated sheets.\n- In an alpha helix, hydrogen bonds form between one amino acid and another that is four amino acids away (down the chain). This pattern pulls the chain into a helix.\n- A beta-pleated sheet consists of pairs of polypeptide chains lying side by side. Hydrogen bonds form between the carbonyl group on one chain and the amino group on the adjacent chain.\n\nHydrophobic interactions between nonpolar R groups (**Statement II**) and disulfide bridges between cysteine residues (**Statement III**) are features of tertiary and quaternary structures, not secondary structures."},{"ref":"BI0579","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0579\n\nBiomolecules have diverse forms and structures. Which of the following statements is **false**?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Deoxyribose can be differentiated from ribose because of the absence of the hydroxyl (-OH) group on the 2’ carbon.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Amino acids consist of an amino group, an acidic carboxyl group and an organic R group.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Phospholipids are lipid molecules with a hydrophobic “head” and two hydrophilic “tails.”\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Glucose is an example of a monosaccharide.\n\n *Note: Starting May 2028, this question may be assessed differently due to changes to the Biology guide, including the use of a data booklet.*","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** – The statement is false. Phospholipids are a class of lipids which have a hydrophilic “head” and two hydrophobic “tails.” The heads contain a phosphate group and the two tails each contain one fatty acid.\n\n**Choice A, B**, and **D** – These statements are all true."},{"ref":"BI0584","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0584\n\nWhich of the following contain peptide bonds?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Amino acids\n- II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ Dipeptides\n- III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ Proteins\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is false because amino acids do not contain peptide bonds by themselves. They are the basic building blocks of proteins, but a peptide bond forms only when two or more amino acids are linked together. Therefore, an amino acid on its own does not contain a peptide bond.\n\n**Statement II** is true because a dipeptide is formed when two amino acids join through a peptide bond. The bond occurs between the carboxyl group of one amino acid and the amino group of another. Thus, dipeptides do contain peptide bonds.\n\n**Statement III** is true because proteins are large molecules made up of many amino acids joined together by peptide bonds. These long chains of amino acids fold into specific structures, and the peptide bonds are essential for maintaining the protein's primary structure.\n\n**Choice C** is the correct answer because it only includes the two correct Statements II and III.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **D** are all incorrect as they include the false Statement I."},{"ref":"BI0585","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0585\n\nWhich of the following reactions results in the formation of a dipeptide from two amino acids?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Condensation\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Denaturation\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrolysis\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Catabolism","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nTwo amino acids form a dipeptide through a condensation reaction (**Choice A**), which involves the loss of hydrogen from the amine group of one amino acid and a hydroxyl group from the carboxyl group of another amino acid. This results in the formation of a peptide bond and the formation of one water molecule. \\\n**Choice B** is incorrect, as denaturation refers to a change in the 3D shape of a protein. \\\n**Choice C** is incorrect, as hydrolysis reactions produce smaller molecules, such as amino acids, from larger molecules, such as dipeptides. \\\n**Choice D** is incorrect, as catabolism is a breaking-down reaction, while dipeptide formation is an anabolic reaction."},{"ref":"BI0587","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0587\n\nWhich of the following are monomers that combine to form polypeptides?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$  Fatty acids\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$  Amino acids\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$  Proteins\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$  Nucleotides","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAmino acids (**Choice B**) can form polypeptides through the formation of peptide bonds as a result of condensation reactions. Fatty acids (**Choice A**) are involved in the formation of lipids and are not monomers. Proteins (**Choice C**) are composed of one or more polypeptides and are polymers. Nucleotides (**Choice D**) are the building blocks of DNA and RNA."},{"ref":"BI0588","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0588\n\nWhich of the following is a hormone?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$  Haemoglobin\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$  Insulin\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$  Collagen\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$  Glycogen","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nInsulin is a hormone produced by the pancreas (β-cells) that helps control blood glucose concentration; **Choice B** is the correct answer.\n\nHaemoglobin is a protein but not a hormone. It is found in red blood cells and transports oxygen from the lungs to body tissues; therefore, **Choice A** is not correct.\n\nCollagen is a structural protein, found in connective tissues like skin, tendons, and bones. It provides strength and elasticity but has no role in cell signalling or hormonal regulation; **Choice C** is, therefore, not correct.\n\nGlycogen is a storage polysaccharide found in animals and fungi. In humans, it stores glucose in liver and muscle cells. The hormone insulin promotes the formation of glycogen, while the hormone glucagon triggers its breakdown. Glycogen is not a hormone so **Choice D** is not correct."},{"ref":"BI0590","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0590\n\nThe diagram below shows a protein composed of three polypeptide chains, each chain contains 20 amino acids.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Proteins/images/BI0590.png\" alt=\"BI0590\">\n\nHow many water molecules would be released in the synthesis of one of these chains?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 17\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 18\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 19\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 20","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\\\n**Choice C** is correct because amino acids are joined by peptide bonds as a result of condensation reactions which release water. In order to join 20 amino acids together, 19 peptide bonds would have to form which would result in the release of 19 water molecules."},{"ref":"BI0596","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0596\n\nWhich of the following is a distinguishing characteristic of the quaternary structure of a protein?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The amino acid chain is arranged into an alpha helix\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The amino acid chain is arranged into beta-pleated sheets\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Polypeptide chains are held together by R-group interactions\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The amino acid sequence of the protein is held together by peptide bonds","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe R-groups, or side chains, of the amino acids in different polypeptide chains can interact with each other through various types of bonds and interactions, such as hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds, hydrophobic interactions, and disulfide bridges. These interactions hold the multiple subunits of a quaternary protein together; **Choice C** is therefore the correct answer.\n\nThe alpha-helix, where the polypeptide chain coils into a helix, and the beta-pleated sheet, where the polypeptide chain folds into sheet-like arrangements, are secondary structures of proteins, which are stabilised by hydrogen bonds. **Choices A** and **B** are therefore not correct. **Choice D** refers to the primary structure of a protein."},{"ref":"BI0597","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0597\n\nThe secondary structure of a protein is a result of which of the following:\n\n-    I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrogen bonding\n-    II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ Covalent bonding\n-    III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ Ionic bonding\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and II","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because the secondary structure of a protein includes α-helices and β-pleated sheets stabilised by hydrogen bonds between the oxygen atoms of carbonyl (C=O) groups and the hydrogen atoms of N–H groups in the polypeptide backbone. These hydrogen bonds occur in regular patterns that stabilise the coiling and pleating of the chain.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because covalent peptide bonds join amino acids into a polypeptide chain, but they are not the hydrogen bonds that stabilise its characteristic secondary folding. Covalent disulfide bonds between cysteine R-groups are associated with stabilisation of tertiary structure.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because ionic interactions between charged R-groups contribute to tertiary structure rather than forming the regular backbone hydrogen-bonding pattern of α-helices and β-pleated sheets.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because it includes both covalent and ionic bonding; only statement I identifies the characteristic stabilising interactions described in the question."},{"ref":"BI0598","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0598\n\nWhich level(s) of protein structure would be affected by denaturation due to high temperatures?\n\n-    I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Primary\n-    II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ Secondary\n-    III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ Tertiary\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because denaturation changes the secondary and tertiary structure of a protein (**Statement II** and **III**); however, the peptide bonds which hold the amino acid chain together are not affected, and thus neither is the primary structure (**Statement I**)."},{"ref":"BI1053","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1053\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Draw a diagram of a generalised amino acid, and label the functional groups. $^*$ [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline how a dipeptide is formed. [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain how extreme pH and extreme temperature affect the structure and function of proteins. [7 marks]\n\n$^*$ *Note: Starting May 2028, this question may be assessed differently due to changes to the Biology guide, including the use of a data booklet.*","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ amine group drawn correctly to one side of central carbon [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carboxyl group drawn correctly to other side of central carbon. [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ‘R’ group drawn correctly above or below central C and H drawn on opposite side. [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carboxyl group, amino groups and ‘R’ group all labelled correctly. [1 mark]\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Proteins/images/BI1053a.png\" alt=\"BI1053a\">\n\n2. any **five** of the following (accept a correctly labelled structural drawing showing the reaction between two amino acids):\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ dipeptide is composed of two amino acids [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ joined by a peptide bond [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ condensation reaction [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ water formed from hydroxyl group **OR** OH from carboxyl group of one amino acid and hydrogen ion **OR** H:sup[+] from the other amino acid [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sharing of electrons between C of carboxyl group of one amino acid and N of amino group of other amino acid [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ covalent bond [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carboxyl group of one amino acid **AND** amine OR amino group of the other [1 mark]\n [5 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Dipeptides are formed from **two amino acids** ($\\checkmark$). The **carboxyl group** from one amino acid joins the **amino group** of another amino acid ($\\checkmark$). This is a **condensation reaction** ($\\checkmark$) which results in the formation of a **peptide bond** ($\\checkmark$), a **covalent** bond ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperatures which are too high, **OR** above the optimum, can lead to denaturation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ pH levels which are too high **OR** too low, **OR** too different from the optimum pH, can lead to denaturation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ denaturation is a change in the 3-dimensional structure **OR** shape of a protein [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature increases motion **OR** kinetic energy of molecules in protein [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increased motion **OR** kinetic energy puts stress on bonds holding structure together [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ break hydrogen bonds so structure breaks down [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ change in pH can affect the charges holding protein structure together [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ protein cannot maintain **OR** take on normal shape [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ proteins shape **OR** structure is specific to its function [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ enzymes can’t bind to substrates when denatured **OR** other suitable example of a protein and its function being affected [1 mark]\n [7 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n When **temperature** increases **too far** above the optimal temperature for a protein, it **increases** the **kinetic energy of molecules** ($\\checkmark$) in the protein, putting **stress** on the **bonds** holding the protein together ($\\checkmark$), so **hydrogen bonds** begin to **break** ($\\checkmark$) and the **3-dimensional structure breaks** down ($\\checkmark$). A **pH** which is **too high** above, or **too low** below the optimum pH **affects the charges** ($\\checkmark$) holding the protein structure together. The protein can **no longer** form its **normal shape** ($\\checkmark$). As the structure of a protein is **specific to its function** ($\\checkmark$), a change in the structure will not permit it to carry out its function. When the normal protein shape is disrupted, leading to loss of activity, the protein is said to be **denatured** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1080","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1080\n\nWhich of the following  is/are correct for amino acid R-groups?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | R-groups can be hydrophobic or hydrophilic. |\n    | II. | Hydrophobic R-groups are polar. |\n    | III. | Hydrophilic R-groups can be acidic or basic.  |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because R-groups can be hydrophilic or hydrophobic (**Statement I**), and hydrophilic R-groups can be acidic or basic **(Statement III)**.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as it contains only one correct statement. **Choices B** and **D** are incorrect because they contain **Statement II**, which is incorrect because hydrophobic R-groups are non-polar."},{"ref":"BI1081","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1081\n\nWhich of the following is correct for amino acid R-groups?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrophobic R-groups can be polar\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrophobic R-groups can be acidic\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrophilic R-groups can be non-polar\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrophilic R-groups can be basic","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because hydrophilic R-groups can be basic. **Choice A** is incorrect because hydrophobic R-groups are non-polar. **Choice B** is incorrect because hydrophobic R-groups are not acidic (or basic). **Choice C** is incorrect because hydrophilic R-groups are polar or charged."},{"ref":"BI1082","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1082\n\nWhich of the following refers to the primary structure of a protein?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sequence and conformation of amino acids\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sequence and position of amino acids\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrogen bonds stabilise beta-pleated sheets\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrogen bonds stabilise alpha helices","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because the primary structure of a protein refers to the sequence and position of amino acids along the polypeptide chain, which determines the 3D shape. **Choice A** is incorrect as the primary structure does not refer to the conformation of amino acids. **Choices C** and **D** are incorrect as they refer to the secondary structure of a protein."},{"ref":"BI1083","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1083\n\nThe tertiary structure of a protein can be influenced by which of the following?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Disulfide covalent bonds |\n    | II. | Ionic bonds |\n    | III. | Hydrophobic interactions |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because **Statements I, II** and **III** are all correct. Disulfide covalent bonds, ionic bonds and hydrophobic interactions can influence the tertiary structure of a protein. Disulfide bonds form between non-adjacent cysteine amino acids when they get in close proximity to each other. Ionic bonds form between two amino acids with oppositely charged R groups. Non–polar amino acids are repelled by water and so end up in the centre of the folds of the polypeptide chain, away from the aqueous solution, and this is known as hydrophobic interactions. **Choices A, B** and **C** are incorrect because they do not contain all three statements."},{"ref":"BI1084","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1084\n\nWhich of the following rows in the table is correct for haemoglobin or collagen?\n\n| | Name of Protein | Conjugation | Type of Protein |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Haemoglobin | Non-conjugated | Fibrous |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Collagen | Conjugated | Fibrous |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Haemoglobin | Conjugated | Globular |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Collagen | Non-conjugated | Globular |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because haemoglobin is a conjugated globular protein. It is conjugated as it contains haem groups, which are non-protein groups. **Choice A** is incorrect, as haemoglobin is not a fibrous protein. **Choice B** is incorrect, as collagen is a non-conjugated protein. **Choice D** is incorrect because collagen is not a globular protein."},{"ref":"BI1085","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1085\n\nWhich of the following is an example of a non-conjugated fibrous protein?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Haemoglobin\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Collagen\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Amylase\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Insulin","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because collagen is a non-conjugated fibrous protein, as it does not contain non-protein groups. **Choice A** is incorrect because haemoglobin is a conjugated globular protein, as it contains haem groups. **Choices C** and **D** are incorrect because amylase and insulin are globular proteins."},{"ref":"BI1086","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1086\n\nWhich of the following are influenced by hydrogen bonding?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Protein primary structure |\n    | II. | Protein secondary structure |\n    | III. | Protein tertiary structure |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because only **Statements II** and **III** are correct. Hydrogen bonding can influence the secondary and tertiary structure of a protein. **Choices A** and **D** are incorrect because they contain **Statement I** which is incorrect. The primary structure of a protein is not influenced by hydrogen bonding. The primary structure is the sequence and position of the amino acids along a polypeptide chain, so the only bonds involved in this structure are the peptide bonds between the amino acids. **Choice B** is incorrect as it only contains one correct statement."},{"ref":"BI1087","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1087\n\nWhich row in the table is correct for amino acids?\n\n| | R-Group | Property | Location |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Polar | Hydrophobic | Exposed to the cytoplasm in integral proteins |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Polar | Hydrophilic | Clustered in the centre of globular proteins |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Non-Polar | Hydrophilic | Exposed to the cytoplasm in integral proteins |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Non-Polar | Hydrophobic | Clustered in the centre of globular proteins |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because amino acids with non-polar R-groups have hydrophobic properties. They can be found clustered in the centre of globular proteins. **Choice A** is incorrect because amino acids with polar R-groups are not hydrophobic. **Choice B** is incorrect because hydrophilic amino acids are not found clustered in the centre of globular proteins. **Choice C** is incorrect because amino acids with non-polar R-groups are not hydrophilic."},{"ref":"BI1088","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1088\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nThe properties of proteins influence the role they play within the body. The diagram below shows an integral protein in the plasma membrane of a cell.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Proteins/images/BI1088.png\" alt=\"BI1088\">\n\n1.\n 1. Describe the influence of polarity on the position of amino acids within integral proteins. [3 marks]\n\n 2. Other than polarity, state another chemical property of amino acids that can vary due to charge. [1 mark]\n\n3. Using named examples, distinguish between conjugated and non-conjugated proteins. [2 marks]\n\n4. Outline the role of interactions and bonding in the determination of protein 3D structure. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ polar amino acids are hydrophilic **OR** interact favourably with water molecules **OR** are attracted to water molecules [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ non-polar amino acids are hydrophobic **OR** are repelled by water molecules [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in integral proteins, hydrophobic amino acids help to embed them in the membrane **OR** hydrophobic amino acids are found in regions embedded within the membrane **OR** located within hydrophobic interior of lipid bilayer [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in integral proteins, hydrophilic amino acids are often found in the regions exposed to the cytoplasm **OR** extracellular fluid **OR** aqueous environment inside and outside cell [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Polar amino acids are **hydrophilic** ($\\checkmark$), whereas non-polar amino acids are **hydrophobic** ($\\checkmark$). In regards to integral proteins, the regions that are **exposed to the cytoplasm** and **extracellular fluid** contain amino acids that are **hydrophilic** ($\\checkmark$), but the regions that are **embedded** within the membrane have amino acids that are **hydrophobic** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrophilic amino acids can be acidic or basic [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ amino acids can be hydrophobic or hydrophilic [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can be aromatic or non-aromatic [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ functional groups [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ redox properties [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Hydrophilic amino acids, depending on their R-groups, can be **acidic or basic** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ example of conjugated protein e.g. haemoglobin [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ example of non-conjugated protein e.g. collagen [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ conjugated proteins are proteins that are associated with other chemical groups **OR** are composed of proteins and non-proteins **WHEREAS** non-conjugated proteins are not associated with other chemical groups [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Conjugated proteins**, such as **haemoglobin** ($\\checkmark$) are proteins that are associated with other chemical groups for example iron in haemoglobin. **Non-conjugated proteins**, such as **collagen** ($\\checkmark$), are **not associated with other chemical groups** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ peptide bonds form between amino acids to give primary structure that determines overall 3D structure [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen bonding in the formation of protein secondary structure **OR** formation of alpha helices **OR** beta-pleated sheets **OR** hydrogen bonding stabilising repeating patterns of secondary structures [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ tertiary structure is formed by interactions between amino acid R-groups [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen bonds between polar amino acids in the formation of tertiary structure [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ionic bonding in the formation of protein tertiary structure **OR** ionic bonding between oppositely charged amino acids [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ disulfide covalent bonding in the formation of protein tertiary structure OR disulfide bonds between pairs of cysteines [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrophobic interactions result in hydrophobic amino acids being orientated toward the inside of the protein molecule **OR** non-polar side chains cluster in protein’s core to minimise contact with water [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ quaternary strucure stabilised by hydrogen **OR** ionic **OR** disulfide bonds **OR** similar interactions to tertiary structure but between different polypeptide subunits [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Hydrogen bonding** is involved in stabilising the protein’s **secondary structure**, such as the formation of alpha-helices and beta-pleated sheets ($\\checkmark$). In the tertiary structure of proteins, **hydrogen bonds** can form between **polar amino acids** ($\\checkmark$), **ionic bonds** can also form between oppositely **charged amino acids** ($\\checkmark$) and **disulfide bonds** can form between sulfur atoms in **cysteine** R groups."},{"ref":"BI1089","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1089\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the chemical properties of amino acid R-groups based on their charge.[4 marks]\n\n2. With reference to bonding and association, compare and contrast conjugated and non-conjugated proteins.     [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe the four levels of protein structure.    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ non-polar R-groups are hydrophobic    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polar R-groups are hydrophilic         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ positively charged  R-groups are basic     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ negatively charged R-groups are acidic    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ amphoteric R-groups can be positively, negatively or neutrally charged **OR** change charge depending on pH            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ neutral polar R-groups are partially positively and partially negatively charged **OR** have polar functional groups        [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Non-polar R-groups are **hydrophobic** ($\\checkmark$), and polar R-groups are **hydrophilic** ($\\checkmark$). Hydrophilic R-groups can be **acidic (-'vely charge) ($\\checkmark$) or basic (+'vely charged)** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following (*must include at least **one** similarity and **one** difference to gain full marks*):\n\n    ***Similarities*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are composed of amino acids joined together by peptide bonds    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can contain ionic bonds         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can contain alpha-helices **OR** beta-pleated sheets     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can contain disulfide covalent bonds [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can contain hydrogen bonds         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can contain hydrophobic interactions [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can be fibrous **OR** globular             [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ conjugated proteins are associated with other chemical **OR** non-protein **OR** prosthetic groups **WHEREAS** non-conjugated proteins are not associated with other chemical **OR** non-protein **OR** prosthetic groups            [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    There are many similarities in the bonding of conjugated and non-conjugated proteins because they can both contain **ionic bonds** ($\\checkmark$), **disulfide covalent bonds** ($\\checkmark$), **hydrogen bonds** ($\\checkmark$), and **hydrophobic interactions** ($\\checkmark$). However, there are some differences in their associations because conjugated proteins are associated with other **chemical groups**, such as carbohydrates, whereas non-conjugated proteins are not ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ primary structure is the sequence of amino acids **OR** precise position of each amino acid       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ amino acids held together by covalent **OR** peptide bonds [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sequence of amino acids determines overall 3D shape of protein due to different R-groups   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ secondary structure has pleating and coiling **OR** local folding of polypeptide chain        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ alpha helices **OR** beta-pleated sheets formed by hydrogen bonding [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ all hydrogen bonding of secondary structure between non-adjacent amine and carboxyl groups in peptide bonds                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tertiary structure as a result of interactions between R-groups **OR** adjacent parts of the polypeptide chain            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ionic bonding as a result of charged R-groups [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pairs of cysteines can form disulfide bonds     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrophobic interactions responsible for protein folding **OR** hydrophobic regions are oriented towards the inside of the protein molecule **OR** hydrophobic amino acids can be found clustered together in the centre of globular proteins            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in integral proteins, hydrophilic amino acids can be found exposed to the cytoplasm or extracellular matrix, **OR** hydrophilic amino acids can be found exposed to aqueous solution     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in integral proteins, hydrophobic amino acids can be found embedded within the membrane    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ quaternary structure is the association of two or more polypeptide chains     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ quaternary structure stabilised by ionic **OR** hydrogen **OR** disulfide **OR** similar interactions to tertiary structure, but between different polypeptide chains            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ conjugated proteins are associated with other chemical **OR** non-protein **OR** prosthetic groups **OR** non-conjugated proteins are not associated with other chemical **OR** non-protein **OR** prosthetic groups [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The primary structure of a protein refers to the **sequence of amino acids** ($\\checkmark$) that make up the polypeptide chain and their precise position. The secondary structure of a protein is a result of hydrogen bonding between **non-adjacent carboxyl** and **amine groups** ($\\checkmark$), resulting in the formation of **alpha-helices and beta-pleated sheets** ($\\checkmark$). The tertiary structure of a protein is the result of interactions between the **R-groups** of amino acids ($\\checkmark$). Charged R-groups can form **ionic bonds** ($\\checkmark$). **Hydrophobic interactions** due to the presence of non-polar R-groups can also cause the protein to fold into different shapes ($\\checkmark$). In integral proteins, hydrophobic amino acids help to **embed** them in the plasma membrane ($\\checkmark$). The quaternary structure of a  protein is when it is composed of **more than one** polypeptide chain ($\\checkmark$). These proteins are referred to as **conjugated** if they are associated with other chemical groups, such as carbohydrates or **non-conjugated** if they are not associated with other chemical groups ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1141","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1141\n\nWhich of the following depicts an amino acid?\n\n| |  |  | |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Proteins/images/BI1141A.png\" alt=\"BI1141A\"> | B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Proteins/images/BI1141b.png\" alt=\"BI1141b\">|\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Proteins/images/BI1135C.png\" alt=\"BI1135C\"> | D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Proteins/images/BI1141D.png\" alt=\"BI1141D\"> |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAmino acids contain an amino group (H:sub[2]N) and a carboxyl group (COOH), which can be seen in the diagram (the diagram shows the amino acid valine).\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect since it is a fatty acid (valeric acid). **Choice B** is a carbohydrate (fructose). **Choice D** is glycerol."},{"ref":"BI1143","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1143\n\nWhich of the following statements is true regarding peptide bonds?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Ribosomes facilitate their formation. |\n    | II. | They form between an alcohol and a carboxyl group. |\n    | III. | They require the addition of water. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nOnly **Statement I** is correct since, during translation, ribosomes position the tRNA in the correct place along the mRNA, allowing peptide bonds to form between adjacent amino acids. Therefore, **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Statement II** is incorrect because peptide bonds form between amino and carboxyl groups. **Statement III** is false because water is produced during the condensation reaction instead of being required. Thus, **Choices B, C** and **D** cannot be correct."},{"ref":"BI1250","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1250\n\nPeanuts (*Arachis hypogaea*) are high in phenylalanine, an essential amino acid. What is true regarding this amino acid?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | It can only be obtained from the diet  |\n    | II. | It can be recycled following protein degradation by proteasomes  |\n    | III. | It cannot be used by the body to synthesise polypeptides  |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nEssential amino acids such as phenylalanine can only be obtained through the diet (**Statement I**). All amino acids can be recycled by proteasomes for further use within the cell (**Statement II**). Although the body cannot synthesise essential amino acids, it has no problem using the ones obtained from food to synthesise polypeptides such as enzymes and hormones. Thus, **Statement III** is false, and **Choice C** must be correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as it does not include **Statement II**. **Choices B** and **D** are not correct as they contain **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI1315","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1315\n\nWhich level of protein structure is represented by the area circled in the diagram below?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Proteins/images/1315a.png\" alt=\"1315a\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Tertiary structure held together by a covalent bond\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Quaternary structure held together by a covalent bond\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Secondary structure held together by a hydrogen bond\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Tertiary structure held together by an ionic bond","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because there are two polypeptide chains held together by disulfide bonds. Disulfide bonds are covalent bonds that form between two sulfur atoms in the R groups of cysteine amino acids. These bonds help to hold the polypeptide chains together in the quaternary structure of a protein.\n\nAlthough tertiary structures are held together by covalent bonds, a tertiary structure (**Choice A**) is the overall 3D shape of a single polypeptide chain. Although the α-helices and β-pleated sheets, which are secondary structures, are held together by hydrogen bonds (**Choice C**), the secondary structure of a protein again refers to a single polypeptide chain, and disulfide, not hydrogen bonds are shown in this diagram. Ionic bonds do help maintain the tertiary structure of a protein (**Choice D**), however, a tertiary structure refers to a single polypeptide chain, and the bonds shown in this diagram are disulfide, not ionic bonds."},{"ref":"BI1380","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1380\n\nWhich of the following would not lead to the denaturation of proteins?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Reduction in pH\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increase in temperature\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mechanical stress\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Dilution in water","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDenaturation is a change in the shape of a protein caused by a breakdown of the bonds that usually hold it together. Although some proteins are soluble in water, their shape remains intact and denaturation will not occur. Therefore, **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect because a reduction in pH is likely to cause denaturation for most proteins. This is because the attractive forces between amino acids, such as hydrogen bonds, are disrupted, leading to a change in the 3-dimensional shape of the protein. **Choice B** is incorrect because an increase in temperature tends to cause denaturation as the increased kinetic energy is likely to disrupt bonds. **Choice C** is incorrect because, while less common than pH or temperature changes, physical stress is another potential cause of denaturation."},{"ref":"BI1425","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1425\n\nWhich of the following proteins is a hormone?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$  Haemoglobin\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$  Collagen\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$  Glucagon\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$  Amylase","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nGlucagon is a hormone involved in maintaining blood glucose levels; **Choice C** is, therefore, the correct answer.\n\nHaemoglobin is a protein responsible for the transport of oxygen and carbon dioxide in red blood cells; **Choice A** is therefore not correct. Collagen is a structural protein, and amylase is an enzyme that catalyses the hydrolysis of starch; **Choices B** and **D** are, therefore, also not correct."},{"ref":"BI1461","marks":"5","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1461\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe table shows the recommended daily intake of some essential amino acids for infants aged 4 months. The mean concentrations of the same amino acids in cows’ milk and human milk are also provided. The mean mass of protein laid down by the body is 3.1g day-1 for infants from age 4 months to 12 months.\n\n| Amino acid | Recommended $\\\\$ infant (4 months) $\\\\$ daily intake/mg g$^{-1}$ $\\\\$ body protein gain| Mean $\\\\$ concentration of $\\\\$ amino acids in $\\\\$ cow’s milk/mg $\\\\$ g$^{-1}$ milk protein| Mean $\\\\$ concentration of $\\\\$amino acids in $\\\\$ human milk/mg g$^{-1}$ $\\\\$ milk protein|\n\n| :-- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| Histidine | 16 | 27 | 26 |\n\n| Isoleucine | 40 | 47 | 46 |\n\n| Leucine | 93 | 95 | 93 |\n\n| Methionine | 33 | 33 | 42 |\n\n| Lysine | 60 | 78 | 66 |\n\n| Phenylalanine | 72 | 102 | 72 |\n\n| Threonine | 50 | 44 | 43 |\n\n| Tryptophan | 10 | 14 | 17 |\n\n| Valine | 54 | 64 | 55 |\n\n[Source Adapted from: National Research Council (US) Subcommittee on the Tenth Edition of the Recommended Dietary Allowances. (1989). Protein and amino acids. Recommended Dietary Allowances: 10th Edition. Retrieved March 21, 2024, from https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK234922/. Copyright free]\n\n1. State the definition of an essential amino acid. [1 mark]\n\n2. Using only the data in the table, explain why cow’s milk is used as a replacement for human milk in infant nutrition. [2 marks]\n\nTyrosine, a conditionally essential amino acid, can be formed from phenylalanine by the addition of a hydroxyl group.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Proteins/images/phenylalanine%20to%20tyrosine%20no%20enzyme%20label%20i.png\" alt=\"phenylalanine to tyrosine no enzyme label i\">\n\n3.\n\n 1. Suggest why tyrosine is called a conditionally essential amino acid. [1 mark]\n\n 2. State **one** role of protein in the human body. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ amino acid that cannot be synthesised in cells **OR** tissues **OR** systems [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ need to be consumed **OR** ingested in the diet [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n An essential amino acid **cannot** be **synthesised** by the **organism that requires it** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it has a similar composition **OR** concentration of essential amino acids to human milk [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cows’ milk meets all the same essential amino acid needs of infants that human milk does if more than 3.1g of milk protein is consumed **OR** if consumed in same volume per day [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ lower mass of cows’ milk protein than human milk protein needs to be consumed to reach infants' daily threonine requirements for daily body mass increase [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 7 out of 9 essential amino acids are present in higher concentrations in cows’ milk than in human milk **OR** that valine, phenylalanine, and lysine are significantly higher in cows’ milk than in human milk [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n **Cows’ milk** is a good substitute for **human milk** for infants as it has a **similar concentration** of essential amino acids ($\\checkmark$) and **7 of the 9 essential amino acids** shown are in a **higher concentration** in **cows’ milk** than in human milk($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n\n 1. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ if phenylalanine is not available tyrosine cannot be synthesised [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the synthesis of tyrosine is conditional on **OR** depends on the presence (in the diet) of phenylalanine [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n Under normal circumstances, tyrosine can be synthesised from the essential amino acid phenylalanine, however, if there is **not enough phenylalanine** available then **tyrosine** needs to be **obtained from** the diet ($\\checkmark$).\n\n 2. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ structural support **OR** collagen in connective tissue **OR** any other suitable example [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ enzymes **OR** catalyse reactions **OR** suitable example [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ transport **OR** suitable example [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ antibodies **OR** defence against disease **OR** protect body from infection **OR** suitable example [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hormones **OR** part of endocrine system **OR** regulate blood glucose **OR** any other suitable example [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ movement **OR** contraction of muscles **OR** actin **OR** myosin are contractile proteins **OR** any other suitable example [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cellular communication **OR** receptor proteins in cell membranes **OR** facilitate cellular response [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ storage essential nutrients **OR** ferritin stores iron **OR** any other suitable example [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ regulation of gene expression **OR** transcription factors [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n One role of protein in the body is as **hormones**, such as **insulin** that helps **regulate blood glucose concentration** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1476","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1476\n\nWhich is an **incorrect** explanation for why a very low pH could cause a protein to denature?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Interactions between the R$-$groups could be changed\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The primary structure of the protein could be affected\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrogen bonds within the protein could be broken\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The folding patterns of polypeptides within the protein could change","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nLow pH can result in the denaturation of the protein, as the presence of hydrogen ions affects the bonds that hold the three dimensional structure of proteins together. The primary structure of a protein refers to the amino acid sequence.  pH does not affect the sequence of amino acids that form the polypeptide chain; **Choice B**  is correct.\n\nThe presence of hydrogen ions  can affect the interactions between R groups in proteins, which would change the three dimensional structure; **Choice A**  is incorrect. The presence of hydrogen ions would disrupt hydrogen bonds in the secondary structure of the protein (**Choice C**), which would affect the folding patterns of polypeptides within the protein (**Choice D**). **Choices C** and **D**  are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1564","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1564\n\nHuman growth hormone (HGH) is a single-chain polypeptide 191 amino acids long. How many water molecules would be required to completely hydrolyse this substance?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 190\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 191\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 192\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 383","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because each peptide bond must be hydrolysed by one water molecule, but a single-chain polypeptide always has one less peptide bond than the number of amino acids it contains. For HGH, this would mean 190 peptide bonds, and therefore, 190 water molecules.\n\n**Choices B, C**, and **D** are incorrect because there are only 190 bonds to be hydrolysed, the same as the number of water molecules required."},{"ref":"BI2325","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2325\n\nWhich statement is true for essential and non-essential amino acids?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Both must be synthesised from precursor molecules.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Essential amino acids can be synthesised in sufficient quantities by animals.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Non-essential amino acids cannot be synthesised by animals.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Non-essential amino acids can be synthesised from essential amino acids.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because non-essential amino acids can be synthesised by animals, often using essential amino acids or other precursor molecules. For example, tyrosine (a non-essential amino acid) can be synthesised from phenylalanine (an essential amino acid).\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because essential amino acids cannot be synthesised by animals and must be obtained through the diet. Only non-essential amino acids can be synthesised from precursor molecules.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because essential amino acids cannot be synthesised by animals at all (or in sufficient quantities) and must be obtained from the diet.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because non-essential amino acids can be synthesised by animals, typically using precursor molecules such as essential amino acids or intermediates from metabolic pathways."},{"ref":"BI2380","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2380\n\nWhich amino acid in a membrane protein is likely to be oriented close to the centre of the membrane bilayer?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A. | | $\\hspace{2cm}$ | |B.|\n| |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Proteins/images/aNSa.png\" alt=\"aNSa\"> $\\\\$ $\\hspace{1cm}$ alanine $\\hspace{1cm}$ | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Proteins/images/aNSb.png\" alt=\"aNSb\"> $\\\\$ $\\hspace{1cm}$ aspartic acid $\\hspace{1cm}$ |\n|C. | | | |D. |\n| |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Proteins/images/serine%20i.png\" alt=\"serine i\"> $\\\\$ $\\hspace{1cm}$ serine $\\hspace{1cm}$ | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Proteins/images/aNSd.png\" alt=\"aNSb\"> $\\\\$ $\\hspace{1cm}$ lysine $\\hspace{1cm}$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because alanine’s R group is an hydrocarbon-only side chain (a simple methyl group, $\\ce{-CH_3}$). This gives alanine’s R group a hydrophobic character which allows it to form hydrophobic bonds with the largely hydrophobic phospholipid tails at the centre of a typical membrane. Membrane-bound proteins will often have a high proportion of hydrophobic amino acids situated close to the centre of the phospholipid bilayer.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because aspartic acid has an acidic R group ($\\ce{-CH_2-COOH}$), which ionises and becomes negatively charged. This makes it hydrophilic and more likely to be found in regions of the protein that interact with aqueous environments, such as the cytoplasm or extracellular fluid.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because serine is classified as a polar amino acid because its side chain, –CH₂OH, contains a hydroxyl group that creates a dipole, enabling it to form hydrogen bonds with water molecules. This hydrogen bonding makes serine hydrophilic, in contrast to hydrophobic amino acids\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because lysine has a basic R group ($\\ce{-CH_2-CH_2-CH_2-CH_2-NH_2}$), which ionises to become positively charged. As a hydrophilic amino acid, lysine is more likely to be found in protein regions interacting with aqueous surroundings, not in the hydrophobic core of the membrane."},{"ref":"BI2415","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2415\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe diagram below shows two different types of receptor proteins binding their ligands.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Proteins/images/BI2415.png\" alt=\"BI2415\">\n[Adapted from *“Intracellular Receptors”*, by BioRender.com (2025). Retrieved from https://app.biorender.com/biorender-templates]\n\n1. Deduce the probable properties of the amino acids in the ligand-binding site of the extracellular and intracellular receptors.    [2 marks]\n\nEngineered receptors with transmembrane anchors, like the one shown in diagram B, are commonly used in research and therapeutic applications.\n\n2. Predict how the presence of a transmembrane anchor might affect the normal activity and localisation of an intracellular receptor.    [2 marks]\n\n3. Compare the signalling speed of integral membrane and intracellular receptors AND suggest a reason for the difference.     [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrophilic **OR** polar **OR** charged amino acids in the transmembrane receptor **OR** receptor A    [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrophobic **OR** non-polar amino acids in the intracellular receptor **OR** receptor B     [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The **intracellular receptor likely has hydrophobic** ($\\checkmark$) amino acids and the **transmembrane receptor likely has hydrophilic amino acids** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the receptor would be restricted to the membrane **OR** unable to diffuse freely in the cytoplasm [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ might prevent it from interacting with cytoplasmic signalling molecules **OR** cofactors required for its activity [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the receptor-ligand complex could not get to the nucleus [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gene regulation by the receptor would be inhibited    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ receptor could gain new functionality by interacting with membrane-bound ligands **OR** signalling pathways    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    If the intracellular receptor were anchored into the plasma membrane, it would **not be able to enter the nucleus** ($\\checkmark$) to **affect transcription** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ intracellular receptors signal more slowly **OR** transmembrane receptors act more quickly [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ intracellular receptors require ligand diffusion which is slow    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ membrane receptor signalling acts through second messengers which is faster than diffusion    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ intracellular receptors result in a change in gene transcription, which is a slow response    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Transmembrane receptor signalling is faster** ($\\checkmark$) because they use second messengers, which is a high-speed method of signal amplification, and they do not have to wait for the **ligand to diffuse through the membrane to bind as intracellular receptors do** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2838","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2838\n\nWhich of the following contributes most to the diversity of possible polypeptides?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The different types of peptide bonds\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The sequence and number of amino acids\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The strength of hydrogen bonding\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The number of codons in a gene","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because the diversity of possible polypeptides arises from the ability to vary both the number of amino acids in a chain and the order in which the 20 different amino acids are arranged. This allows for an enormous number of possible combinations.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because all peptide bonds are chemically the same; there are not different types contributing to diversity.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because hydrogen bonding affects the three-dimensional folding of the polypeptide, not the diversity of the primary structure (the sequence).\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because while codons determine which amino acids are used, the number of codons in a single gene is limited by the gene's length and does not account for the broader combinatorial possibilities of polypeptide sequences."},{"ref":"BI2839","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2839\n\nIn a sequence of 5 amino acids within a human protein, each position can be any amino acid (amino acids may repeat). How many possible sequences are there?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ $20^5$\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ $5^{20}$\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 5 x 20\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 20 + 5","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThere are 20 different amino acids available to choose from for every single \"slot\" in the peptide chain.\n\nThe Calculation:\n\n- For the 1st position, there are 20 choices.\n- For the 2nd position, there are 20 choices.\n- This continues for all 5 positions: $20 \\times 20 \\times 20 \\times 20 \\times 20\n- In mathematical terms, this is expressed as $20^5$.\n\nTherefore, **Choice A** is correct. This is equivalent to 3,200,000 possible sequences.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because it reverses the number of possible amino acids and the number of positions.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because multiplying 20 by 5 does not account for the 20 independent possibilities at each of the five positions.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the number of amino-acid choices and the number of positions must not simply be added."},{"ref":"BI2840","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2840\n\nWhich letter choice correctly matches all three polypeptides with their correct functions?\n\n| Polypeptide | Function|\n| :-: | :-: |\n| I. Insulin | a. Enzyme |\n| II. Collagen | b. Structural support |\n| III. Helicase | c. Hormone |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I $\\rarr$ a, II $\\rarr$ b, III $\\rarr$ c\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I $\\rarr$ c, II $\\rarr$ a, III $\\rarr$ b\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I $\\rarr$ b, II $\\rarr$ a, III $\\rarr$ c\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I $\\rarr$ c, II $\\rarr$ b, III $\\rarr$ a","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because insulin is a hormone involved in blood glucose regulation, collagen is a structural protein that provides tensile strength in connective tissues, and helicase is an enzyme that unwinds DNA during replication.\n\n**Choices A, B**, and **C** are incorrect because they each contain at least one incorrect match between a polypeptide and its function."}]},{"name":"Transport","questions":[{"ref":"BI0184","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0184\n\nWhich component of blood is mainly composed of water and transports water-soluble substances to different parts of the body?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Red blood cells\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ White blood cells\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Platelets\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Blood plasma","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nBlood plasma (**Choice D**) is the liquid component of blood and is composed of water which contains proteins, ions, and dissolved nutrients.\n\\\nRed blood cells (**Choice A**) transport oxygen. \\\nPlatelets (**Choice C**) are responsible for the process of blood clotting. \\\nWhite blood cells (**Choice B**) are part of the immune system."},{"ref":"BI0287","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0287\n\nWhich row correctly identifies a tissue where capillaries with fenestrations are present, along with an appropriate reason for their presence?\n\n| | Tissue | Reason |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Skeletal muscle | To allow contraction |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Brain | To maintain the blood-brain barrier |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Kidney | To enhance the filtration of plasma |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Skin | To increase thermoregulation |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCapillaries in the kidney glomerulus are fenestrated, allowing rapid filtration of plasma, including water, glucose, ions, and urea, while retaining plasma proteins and blood cells. The fenestrations in the endothelial layer increase permeability, but the basement membrane acts as a molecular filter, preventing larger molecules like proteins from being lost in urine. **Choice C** is correct.\n\nSkeletal muscle contains continuous capillaries, which lack fenestrations. These capillaries provide regulated exchange of gases and nutrients, such as oxygen and glucose, but do not require rapid permeability. Also, contraction is a function of muscle fibres, not capillaries; capillaries do not “allow” contraction. **Choice A** is incorrect.\n\nThe brain contains continuous, non-fenestrated capillaries with tight junctions. These create the blood–brain barrier, which restricts substance exchange to protect neural tissue. Fenestrations would compromise the barrier’s selectivity. **Choice B** is incorrect.\n\nSkin capillaries are typically continuous, not fenestrated, and they help regulate temperature through vasodilation and vasoconstriction, not by increased permeability. **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0288","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0288\n\nWhich row in the table below is **not** correct for the structure and function of different blood vessels?\n\n| | $\\hspace{1em}$Blood Vessel$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$Structure$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$Function$\\hspace{1em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| arteries | thick, non-elastic walls | receive blood at high pressure from the ventricles |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | veins | thin wall, wide lumen | receive blood at low pressure from the tissues |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | arteries | thick, elastic walls | receive blood at high pressure from the ventricles |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | capillaries | thin, permeable walls | allow exchange of materials between the blood and tissues |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is the correct response because arteries are not non-elastic, instead they do have a degree of elasticity. **Choices B**, **C** and **D** all correctly match the structure and function of each blood vessel."},{"ref":"BI0289","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0289\n\nThe diagram below shows the structure of the human heart.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/BI0289.png\" alt=\"BI0289\">\n[Wnauta, W. (2007, March 4th). *Human heart*. Wikimedia Commons.\nRetrieved August 6th 2022 from   https:// commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diagram_of_the_human_heart_%28clean%29.svg,\nCC-BY-SA-3.0-migrated] \n\nWhich of the following statement(s) is/are true?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1em}$ When the walls of the atria contract, blood flows from the atria to the ventricles because the atrioventricular valves are open\n-    II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ When the walls of the ventricles contract, the increase in pressure causes the atrioventricular valves to close which prevents the backflow of blood from the ventricles to the atria.\n-    III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ When the ventricles stop contracting, the pressure falls and the semilunar valves close preventing backflow from the arteries to the ventricles.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAll three statements are correct and describe the flow of blood in the cardiac cycle. **Choice B** includes all three statements.\nAll the other choices (**Choices A**, **C** and **D**) do not include all three statements."},{"ref":"BI0290","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0290\n\nThe diagram below shows the structure of the human heart.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/BI0290.png\" alt=\"BI0290\">\n[Adapted from Wnauta, W. (2007, March 4th). *Human heart*. Wikimedia Commons.\nRetrieved August 6th 2022 from   https:// commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diagram_of_the_human_heart_%28clean%29.svg,\nCC-BY-SA-3.0-migrated] \n\nBased on the diagram, which labels identify the aorta and the left ventricle?\n\n| | $\\hspace{1em}$Aorta$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$Left ventricle$\\hspace{1em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| IV | II |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | I | V |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | III | II |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | V | III |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn the diagram, **V** is the aorta and **III** is the left ventricle, therefore **Choice D** is correct.\\\n**IV** identifies the pulmonary veins, and **II** the right ventricle (**Choice A**). \\\n**I** identifies valves in the heart and **V** is the aorta (**Choice B**). \\\n**III** is the left ventricle and **II** is the right ventricle (**Choice C**)."},{"ref":"BI0291","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0291\n\nWhich of the following shows the direction of blood flow into and out of the heart?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$  Vena cava $\\rightarrow$ right ventricle $\\rightarrow$ right atrium $\\rightarrow$ lungs $\\rightarrow$ left ventricle $\\rightarrow$ left atrium $\\rightarrow$ aorta\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$  Right ventricle $\\rightarrow$ right atrium $\\rightarrow$ lungs $\\rightarrow$ aorta $\\rightarrow$ left atrium $\\rightarrow$ left ventricle $\\rightarrow$ vena cava\n\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$  Vena cava $\\rightarrow$ right atrium $\\rightarrow$ right ventricle $\\rightarrow$ lungs $\\rightarrow$ left atrium $\\rightarrow$ left ventricle $\\rightarrow$ aorta\n\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$  Lungs $\\rightarrow$ right atrium $\\rightarrow$ left atrium $\\rightarrow$ left ventricle $\\rightarrow$ right ventricle $\\rightarrow$ aorta","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nBlood flows from the vena cava into the right atrium of the heart, and then to the right ventricle.  Blood is then pumped to the lungs after which it flows back into the heart via the left atrium. It then flows into the left ventricle and from there it is pumped into the aorta before being carried away to the tissues (**Choice C**).\n\nBlood does not flow from the lungs into the left ventricle (**Choice A**). Blood does not flow from the lungs to the aorta (**Choice B**). Blood does not flow from the lungs to the right atrium (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0292","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0292\n\nWhich region of the heart is involved in controlling the frequency at which it beats?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$   Sinoatrial node\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$   The opening of the atrioventricular valves\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$   Pulmonary circulation\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$   The opening of the semilunar valves","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe sinoatrial node is a region of specialized cardiac muscle cells found in the wall of the right atrium. The sinoatrial node acts as a pacemaker and is involved in controlling heart rate (**Choice A**).  \\\nThe opening of the atrioventricular valves occurs when there is an increase in pressure inside the atria. Blood flows to the ventricles when these valves are open. This action does not control the heart rate (**Choice B**). \\\nPulmonary circulation is when blood flows into the lungs from the right ventricle and is returned to the left atrium after it is oxygenated, and does not control the heart rate (**Choice C**). \\\nThe opening of the semilunar valves occurs when the pressure in the ventricles increases (**Choice D**). This does not control heart rate."},{"ref":"BI0293","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0293\n\nWhich of the following carries oxygenated blood from the lungs to the heart?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Aorta\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Vena cava\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Pulmonary veins\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Pulmonary arteries","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nMany blood vessels enter and leave the heart. Two pulmonary veins, one from each lung lead into the left atrium of the heart to deliver oxygenated blood (**Choice C**). \\\nOxygenated blood is pumped out of the heart from the left ventricle to the aorta, which is the first route to all other parts of the body (**Choice A**). \\\nThe blood passes through body tissues and becomes deoxygenated, it is then brought to the heart by the two vena cavae (singular: vena cava) (**Choice B**). \\\nDeoxygenated blood from the vena cava enters the right atrium and then to the right ventricle. It is pushed out through the pulmonary arteries to the lungs (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0294","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0294\n\nWhich of the following does **not** cause coronary heart disease?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ High blood pressure\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ High blood sugar levels due to diabetes\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Genetic factors\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Regular exercise","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nRegular exercise prevents the buildup of fatty plaque in the coronary arteries thereby preventing heart disease (**Choice D**).\nSome of the factors that cause the buildup of fatty plaque and occlusion of the arteries causing chest pain are high blood pressure (**Choice A**), high blood sugar due to diabetes (**Choice B**) and some genetic factors (**Choice C**)."},{"ref":"BI0295","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0295\n\nWhich of the following statement(s) is/are true?\n\n-    I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Cardiac muscle can contract on its own even if not stimulated by a nerve.\n\n-    II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ The left atrium of the heart has the thickest walls.\n-    III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ The valves in the heart prevent the backflow of blood and aid circulation.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSpecialised cells, in the sinoatrial node of the right atrium, can generate electrical impulses spontaneously, initiating the heart's rhythmic contractions without requiring external nerve stimulation, so **Statement I** is correct. \\\nThe left ventricle has the thickest walls because it needs to produce more force so the blood can travel all over the body, **Statement II** is not correct. \\\nThe valves function to prevent the backflow of blood, so **Statement III** is correct. \\\nHence, **Choice B** is correct since **Statements I** and **III** are correct and not **Statement II**. Other choices (**Choices A**, and **C**) do not include all the correct statements. **Choice D** is incorrect because it includes **Statement II**."},{"ref":"BI0296","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0296\n\nThe image below represents the changes in pressure in the left atrium, left ventricle and the aorta.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/BI0296_2.png\" alt=\"BI0296_2\">\n[Adapted from OpenStax College, O. S. C. (2013, December 14th). *File:2029 cardiac cycle vs heart Sounds.jpg - Wikimedia Commons*. Cardiac cycle. Retrieved August 9th, 2022, from https:// commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2029_Cardiac_Cycle_vs_Heart_Sounds.jpg\nCC-BY-3.0]\n\nAt point II in the diagram, which of the following is likely to take place?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Atrioventricular valves close\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Semilunar valves open\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Semilunar valves close\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Atrioventricular valves open","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWhen the pressure in the ventricle increases, the semilunar valves (II) open and blood flows from the left ventricle to the aorta (**Choice B**).\nAs the pressure in the aorta increases, the semilunar valves close (III) and prevent backflow into the ventricles (**Choice C**).\nThe pressure in the ventricle drops, the AV valves open (IV) and the cycle starts again (**Choice D**).\nWhen the ventricle contracts, the pressure in the ventricle increases and the AV valves close (I), which prevents backflow from the ventricle to the atrium (**Choice A**)."},{"ref":"BI0297","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0297\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nThe diagram below represents the circulatory system of a fish.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/BI0297.png\" alt=\"BI0297\">\n\n1. Distinguish between a double circulatory system and a single circulatory system. [1 mark]\n\n2. Describe the advantages of a double circulatory system in humans. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the blood passes through the heart twice for every complete circuit of the body in a double circulatory system, **AND** it passes only once through the heart in a single circulatory system [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the pulmonary circulation is separate from the systemic circulation in a double circulatory system; the pulmonary circulation is not separate from the systemic circulation in a single circulatory system [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nWhile the blood passes **twice** through the heart for every complete circuit of the body in a double circulatory system, as found in humans, it passes only **once** in a single circulatory system ($\\checkmark$), as seen in a fish.\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ double circulation separates deoxygenated blood from oxygenated blood in the heart [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in single circulation, blood flows at lower pressure from the lungs to the tissues of the body [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in double circulation oxygenated blood from the lungs returns to the heart where it is pumped to tissues of the body under high pressure **OR** double circulation ensures that more highly oxygenated blood is transported under higher pressure [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nIn double circulation, **deoxygenated** blood is **separated from oxygenated** ($\\checkmark$) blood in the heart. After blood is **oxygenated** in the lungs, it comes **back to the heart** and is **pumped** out directly **to tissues** under **greater pressure** ($\\checkmark$). This ensures that more highly oxygenated blood reaches every part of the body."},{"ref":"BI0298","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0298\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe diagram below shows the structure of a heart.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/BI298.png\" alt=\"BI298\">\n[Wnauta, W. (2007, March 4th). *Human heart*. Wikimedia Commons.\nRetrieved August 6th, 2022 from     https:// commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diagram_of_the_human_heart_%28clean%29.svg.\nCC-BY-SA-3.0-migrated]\n\n1.    Label the following on the diagram. [4 marks]\n\n    -  right atrium\n    -    left ventricle\n    -    atrioventricular valves\n    -    semilunar valves\n\n2.\n    1.    Identify the location of the sinoatrial node.  [1 mark]\n\n    2.    Describe the function of the sinoatrial node. [1 mark]\n\n    3.    Describe how the heart responds to messages from the brain. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. all **four** of the following, labelled correctly:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ right atrium [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ left ventricle [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ atrioventricular (AV) valves [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ semilunar valves [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/298i.png\" alt=\"298i\">\n\n2.\n    1. $\\ce{ -> }$ right atrium [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nThe sinoatrial node is a group of specialized cardiac muscle cells located in the (walls of) **right atrium** of the heart ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ sinoatrial node acts as a pacemaker **OR** sets the pace of heartbeat [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ sends out electrical signal that originates excitation **OR** initiates contraction in the cardiac muscle **OR** right atrium [1 mark]\n\n        [1 mark]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n        Sinoatrial node acts as a **pacemaker** and can initiate contraction in the right atrium ($\\checkmark$).\n\n   3. Any **two** of the following\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ impulses are brought from the medulla of the brain to the SA node by two nerves [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$  impulses from one of the nerves to the SA node causes the pacemaker to slow down while impulses from the other causes it to increase [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ the sympathetic nerve (releases a neurotransmitter norepinephrine) that increases the heart rate [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ the parasympathetic nerve **OR** vagus nerve (releases the neurotransmitter acetylcholine) that decreases the heart rate [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ in response to signals from the brain, the hormone epinephrine is released that causes an increase in the heart rate [1 mark]\n\n        [2 marks]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nMessages are brought from the **medulla** of the brain to the SA node by **two nerves** ($\\checkmark$). Impulses from the **sympathetic nerve** causes the release of a neurotransmitter **norepinephrine** and increases the heart rate ($\\checkmark$). Impulses from the **parasympathetic** nerve causes the release of neurotransmitter **acetylcholine** and decreases the heart rate. To prepare for physical activity, the brain also stimulates the release of the hormone **epinephrine** that causes an increase in the heart rate."},{"ref":"BI0338","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0338\n\nWhich of the following is/are a feature of phloem?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Sieve plates |\n    | II. | Plasmodesmata |\n    | III. | High lignin content |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSieve plates (**Statement I**) and plasmodesmata (**Statement II**) are features found in phloem. Sieve plates possess pores which allow continual flow of sap through the sieve tubes. Plasmodesmata are small channels which connect the sieve tube elements to the adjacent companion cells. A high lignin content (**Statement III**) is found in xylem vessels. It provides support to withstand hydrostatic pressure, and is also impermeable to water."},{"ref":"BI0339","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0339\n\nWhich row in the table is correct for sugar transport in phloem?\n\n| | Direction of Sugar Transport | Flow |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Up and Down | Source → Sink |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Up and Down | Sink → Source |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Down ONLY | Source → Sink |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Down ONLY | Sink → Source |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSucrose is actively loaded into phloem at sites called **sources**, which are regions of the plant where photosynthesis occurs (such as the leaves) or where nutrients are stored (such as in storage organs). Sucrose is unloaded from phloem tubes in **sinks**, which are parts of the plant that can’t produce organic compounds themselves, such as the roots. The movement of sucrose in the phloem is **bidirectional**, meaning it can be transported upwards or downwards, depending on the location of the sink. This relates to **Choice A**.\n\n**Choice B** refers to sugar being transported from sink to the source which is incorrect. **Choices C** and **D** refer to sugar transport being down only, which is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0340","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0340\n\nThe image below shows a cross-section of a stem of the dicotyledonous plant *Helianthus*.\n\nWhich letter identifies phloem?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/dicot%20stem%20with%20labels%20A-D%20i.png\" alt=\"dicot stem with labels A-D i\">\n[Source: adapted from Berkshire Community College Bioscience Image Library (2014, February 10). *Herbaceous Dicot Stem: Young Helianthus*. Retrieved May 20, 2025, from  https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Herbaceous_Dicot_Stem_Young_Helianthus_(34731656652).jpg. CC0]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe vascular tissue in the stem is arranged into bundles of phloem and xylem (vascular bundles). Within the vascular bundle, the phloem is arranged closer to the outside of the stem, and the xylem vessels are located closer to the inside of the stem. **Choice D** represents phloem as it is inside the vascular bundles and is arranged closer to the outside of the stem.\n\n**Choice A**is not correct because this is the epidermis, a single layer of cells on the outside of the stem, not located in a vascular bundle.\n\n**Choice B** is not correct because this is the cortex, made up of medium-sized cells with thin walls, which help support the stem.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because although this tissue is inside a vascular bundle, it indicates the xylem vessels, which are orientated towards the inside of the stem."},{"ref":"BI0341","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0341\n\nThe image below shows a cross-section of a stem of the dicotyledonous plant *Helianthus*.\n\nWhat function is performed by the structure labelled **X**?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/BI0341%20i.png\" alt=\"BI0341 i\">\n[Source: adapted from Berkshire Community College Bioscience Image Library (2014, February 10). *Herbaceous Dicot Stem: Young Helianthus*. Retrieved May 20, 2025, from  https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Herbaceous_Dicot_Stem_Young_Helianthus_(34731656652).jpg. CC0]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Transport of water from the roots to the leaves\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Transport of organic compounds from sources to sinks\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Transport of organic compounds from sinks to sources\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Transport of water from sources to sinks","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nX in the diagram represents phloem, which is the part of the vascular bundle in the stem that is positioned towards the outside of the stem. Phloem’s role is to transport organic compounds such as sugars and amino acids from their area of production/storage (source) to the areas of the plant which require them but cannot produce them themselves e.g. roots (sinks).\n\n**Choices A** and **D** refer to the transport of water, which is the role of the xylem vessels. **Choice C** states that phloem transports organic compounds from the sink to the source which is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0342","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0342\n\nWhich row best represents the solute concentration and hydrostatic pressure within the sieve tubes near the leaves and roots, during the summer months?\n\n| | Solute concentration in leaves | Solute concentration in roots | Hydrostatic pressure in leaves | Hydrostatic pressure in roots|\n| --- | :-: | :-: |:-: |:-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | High | Low | High | Low |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | High | Low | Low | High |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Low| High | High | Low |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Low | High |Low | High |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDuring the summer months, green plants are usually actively growing and photosynthesising. This means that during this season, the leaves are sources, where organic compounds such as sucrose are loaded into the sieve tubes. This creates a high solute concentration in the sieve tubes and a concentration gradient between the sieve tubes and surrounding cells. The concentration gradient results in the movement of water by osmosis into the sieve tubes, which increases the hydrostatic pressure.\n\nRoots are sinks, where organic compounds such as sucrose are unloaded from the sieve tubes into the surrounding cells. This results in a decrease in the sucrose concentration in the sieve tubes and as a result, water moves out of the sieve tube by osmosis and the hydrostatic pressure decreases.\n\nThis makes **Choice A** the correct answer.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect as the hydrostatic pressure would be high in the leaves and low in the roots. **Choices C** and **D** are both incorrect as the solute concentration would be high in the sieve tubes near the leaves."},{"ref":"BI0343","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0343\n\nThe graph shows the results of an experiment which used radioactively labelled carbon dioxide (:sup[14]CO$_2$) to investigate the time taken for sap to travel to different colonies of aphids present on a plant.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/BI0343.png\" alt=\"BI0343\">\n\nWhich conclusion could be supported by these results?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ All colonies received an equal amount of sap.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ All colonies were equally spaced along the stem of the plant.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Colony 1 fed on the sap more quickly than other colonies.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Colony 1 was located closest to the leaves.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCarbon dioxide is absorbed through the stomata in leaves, where the carbon is fixed during the light-independent reactions of photosynthesis to form organic compounds. These organic compounds can then be loaded into phloem to be transported to areas of the plant which can’t produce their own (sinks).\n\nThe time taken for radioactive carbon to be found in samples taken from the stylets of aphids was lowest in colony 1. This is because Colony 1 was the closest colony to the leaves, which is where the organic compounds which contained the radioactive carbon were synthesised.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as the graph doesn’t provide any information to support the amount of sap each colony received. **Choice B** is incorrect because if the colonies were equally spaced along the stem, then we might expect to see a consistent time period between when radioactive carbon was found in each of their samples. **Choice C** is incorrect as we don’t have information to support how quickly the aphids were feeding, only the time taken for radioactive carbon to be found in their samples, which is not indicative of the feeding rate."},{"ref":"BI0344","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0344\n\nWhich structures does sucrose pass through during phloem loading?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Plasmodesmata |\n    | II. | Proton pumps |\n    | III. | Co-transporter proteins |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe active loading of phloem is brought about by **co-transport proteins**, which utilise the proton gradient created by the active transport of H:sup[+] ions out of companion cells. As companion cells pump protons out of the cell an electrochemical gradient is built up. The H:sup[+] ions therefore flow back into the cell, through **co-transporter proteins**, at the same time these proteins also carry sucrose molecules into the cell, against their concentration gradient. This builds up the concentration of sucrose in the companion cell, and the sucrose diffuses into the sieve tube elements through the **plasmodesmata**.\n\n**Proton pumps** are used to create the proton gradient used by the co-transporter proteins but sucrose does not move through them.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as it only refers to co-transporter proteins and not plasmodesmata. **Choices C** and **D** are incorrect as they both refer to proton pumps."},{"ref":"BI0345","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0345\n\nThe diagram shows sucrose being passively loaded into the sieve tube unaided, before being transported around the plant.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/BI0345.png\" alt=\"BI0345\">\n[Source: adapted from Xie, E. (2019, May 22). *An Annotated Diagram of the Vertical Cross-Sections for Apoplast and Symplast Pathways Involved in Phloem Loading.* Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved August 19, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: An_Annotated_Diagram_of_the_Vertical_Cross-Sections_for_Apoplast_and_Symplast_Pathways_Involved_in_Phloem_Loading.svg. Copyright free.]\n\nWhich structure is labelled X?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Plasmodesmata\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Proton pump\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Co-transporter protein\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Stomata","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSucrose can be loaded into the phloem passively through channels called plasmodesmata, which provide cytoplasmic continuity between the phloem and adjacent cells. Sucrose moves due to differences in its concentration gradient.\n\n**Choice B** refers to proton pumps which actively transport H:sup[+] ions. **Choice C** refers to co-transporter proteins which utilise the proton gradient created by the active transport of H:sup[+] to load sucrose into the phloem, so this loading is not unaided as referred to in the question. **Choice D** refers to the stomata which absorb carbon dioxide from the atmosphere and allow the evaporation of water from the leaf."},{"ref":"BI0346","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0346\n\nScientists can use aphids and radioactive carbon dioxide (:sup[14]CO$_2$) to measure the rate of sucrose transport in the phloem of plants. The aphids tap into the phloem and collect sap, allowing measurement of the time taken for radioactive sucrose to reach different parts of the plant.\n\nWhich factor could lead to a decrease in the time taken for radioactive carbon to be detected in samples of sap taken from the stylets of aphids, assuming all other factors remain constant?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increasing the light intensity in the growing environment\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Decreasing the light intensity in the growing environment\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Decreasing the number of leaves on the plant\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Decreasing the amount of water in the growing medium of the plant","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSources are the parts of the plant, such as leaves, which are capable of either producing or mobilising their own organic compounds through processes such as photosynthesis. These organic compounds, such as sucrose, can then be loaded into phloem to be transported to areas of the plant which can’t produce their own (sinks).     Therefore, any factor which is likely to increase the rate of photosynthesis will increase the rate at which organic compounds such as sucrose are synthesised and transported around the plant. If we relate this to the experiment, an increase in the light intensity will lead to an increase in the rate of production of sucrose molecules containing radioactively labelled carbon, and a decrease in the amount of time taken before they are detected in the sap samples taken from the stylets of aphids.\n\n**Choices B, C** and **D** would lead to a decrease in the rate of photosynthesis and therefore an increase in the time taken for radioactive carbon to be detected in samples."},{"ref":"BI0347","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0347\n\nWhich row correctly identifies features of **mature** sieve tube elements?\n\n| | Present | Absent|\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Plasmodesmata **and** sieve plate |  Nucleus|\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Plasmodesmata **and** complete end wall |   Lignin|\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Lignin **and** sieve plate |   Plasma membrane|\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Plasma membrane **and** nucleus |   Complete end wall |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nMature sieve tube elements contain sieve plates and are connected to adjacent companion cells by numerous plasmodesmata. Mature sieve tube elements lose their nucleus during development. Therefore, **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because mature sieve tube elements have sieve plates containing pores rather than complete, unperforated end walls. Although plasmodesmata are present and lignin is absent, the row is therefore incorrect.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because mature sieve tube elements retain a plasma membrane and their cell walls are not lignified. Although a sieve plate is present, the other two entries are incorrect.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because mature sieve tube elements lose their nucleus during development. Although they retain a plasma membrane and lack complete, unperforated end walls, the row is therefore incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0348","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0348\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\n*Zea mays*, pictured below, is a species of flowering plant which is often used as a model for the study of seed anatomy.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/BI0348A.png\" alt=\"BI0348A\">\n*Zea mays* (corn)\n\n[Source: adapted from Zell, H. (2009, July 16). *Zea mays 003.JPG*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved August 19, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Zea_mays_003.JPG. Copyright free.]\n\nA cross-section of the stem was taken from a specimen, and it was stained and viewed using a light microscope at 100x magnification. The resulting image is shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/BI0348B.png\" alt=\"BI0348B\">\n\nCross section of the stem of *Zea mays*.\n\n[Source: adapted from Reynolds, F.A. (2014, February 10). Berkshire Community College Bioscience Image. Flickr. Retrieved August 19, 2022, from  https://www.flickr.com/photos/146824358@N03/35716063792. Copyright free]\n\n1. Using the image of the stem cross-section above, draw a simple labelled diagram demonstrating the orientation and structure of a vascular bundle within the stem of a plant. [3 marks]\n\n2. State the name of the structure responsible for transporting organic compounds in plants.      [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ drawing includes reference to the outside of the stem/epidermis   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ phloem is correctly labelled (phloem is positioned towards the outside of the stem) [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ xylem is correctly labelled (positioned towards the inside of the stem)  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ cambium is correctly labelled (positioned between phloem and xylem)  [1 mark]\n[3 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/bi0348e.png\" alt=\"bi0348e\">\n\n2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{->}$ phloem [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ :u[Sample answer]:\n$\\hspace{1em}$ :br\n$\\hspace{1em}$ **Phloem** transports organic molecules in plants ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0513","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0513\n\nWhat is the position of the atrioventricular and semilunar valves at the point when the pressure in the left ventricle is the greatest?\n\n| | Atrioventricular Valves | Semilunar Valves |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | open | open |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | open | closed |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | closed | open |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | closed | closed |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe pressure in the ventricles is highest when the ventricles contract. The ventricular contraction causes the atrioventricular valves to close (to prevent backflow of blood to the atria) and the semilunar valves to open so blood may be sent to the arteries.\n\nAtrioventricular valves are open when blood is passing from the atria into the ventricles, not when the ventricles are contracting to push blood out of the heart (**Choice A** and **Choice B**). Semilunar valves have to open, not closed, to allow the blood out of the ventricles when they contract (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0556","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0556\n\nFrom which chamber of the heart is blood pumped into the pulmonary artery?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Right atrium\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Left atrium\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Right ventricle\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Left ventricle","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe right ventricle receives oxygen-poor blood from the right atrium and pumps this blood to the lungs via the pulmonary artery. So, blood is pumped into the pulmonary artery by the right ventricle.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the right atrium pumps blood to the right ventricle, not the pulmonary artery\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the left atrium pumps blood into the left ventricle.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the left ventricle pumps blood to the body via the aorta."},{"ref":"BI0559","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0559\n\nWhat helps prevent the backflow of blood in a vein?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Relaxation of skeletal muscles\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Venous valves\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Relaxation of the heart\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Thin vessel walls","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nVeins carry deoxygenated blood from the body back to the heart. As muscles surrounding the veins contract, they push the blood towards the heart and venous valves open to allow the flow of blood. When the muscles relax, the valves close to prevent the blood from flowing backwards in response to gravity. So, venous valves help prevent the backflow of blood away from the heart (**Choice B**).\n\nThe relaxation of skeletal muscles decreases blood pressure in veins and does not play a role in preventing the backflow of blood in veins (**Choice A**).  When the heart relaxes, this decreases blood pressure in arteries, it does not affect blood pressure in veins and plays no role in preventing the backflow of blood in veins (**Choice C**).  Thin vessel walls are most exemplified in capillaries whose walls are one cell thick and allow exchange of substances.  Thin walls play no role in preventing the backflow of blood (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0667","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0667\n\nWhat effect does the evaporation of water in leaves have on other parts of a plant?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ It causes the active uptake of mineral ions.\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ It causes water and mineral ions to move upwards in xylem vessels.\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ It helps to conserve water in dry environmental conditions.\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ It facilitates the formation of hydrogen bonds between water molecules.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWater loss by evaporation in the leaves is known as transpiration which helps move water and mineral ions upwards in xylem vessels (**Choice B**).\\\nWater loss by evaporation does not cause the active uptake of mineral ions (**Choice A**); instead, active uptake of mineral ions in roots causes absorption of water by osmosis. \\\nWater loss by evaporation is increased in dry environmental conditions (**Choice C**). Plants living in dry conditions have adaptations to reduce water loss by evaporation.\\\nThe formation of hydrogen bonds between water molecules (**Choice D**) is due to the cohesive property of water and is not related to whether or not water is evaporating in the leaves."},{"ref":"BI0671","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0671\n\nWhich of the following adaptations are seen in xerophytes for water conservation?\n\n| | Broad leaves | Thick cuticle | Sunken stomata |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| | | $\\checkmark$ |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\checkmark$ | $\\checkmark$ | |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\checkmark$ | | |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | | $\\checkmark$ | $\\checkmark$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nXerophyte adaptations for water conservation include thick cuticles and sunken stomata, which help reduce water loss through evaporation and transpiration. **Choice D** is correct, as it includes both of these key adaptations.\n\nBroad leaves are not typical of xerophytes, as they increase surface area and water loss.\nXerophyte leaves are narrow, small or rolled to reduce water loss by evaporation. **Choices B** and **C**  are incorrect as they include broad leaves.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because, although it correctly includes sunken stomata, it omits the thick cuticle, another major adaptation of xerophytes."},{"ref":"BI0673","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0673\n\nWhat is the main factor that causes the pressure inside xylem vessels to be lower than atmospheric pressure?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Cohesive properties of water inside xylem vessels\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Evaporation of water during transpiration\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Water molecules sticking to the walls of xylem vessels\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide through the stomata","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWater loss by evaporation due to transpiration in leaves causes the pressure inside xylem vessels to be lower than atmospheric pressure.\n\nThe cohesive properties of water do not significantly affect the pressure in xylem vessels (**Choice A**). They allow for the water column to move upwards in the stem.\n\nWater molecules sticking to the walls of xylem vessels is due to the adhesive properties of water. This does not significantly contribute to the low pressure inside the xylem (**Choice C**). Adhesive properties, together with cohesive properties, are responsible for capillary action.\n\nThe exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide through stomata does not affect the overall pressure in xylem vessels (**Choice D**).  However, the diffusion of water out of the stomata does contribute to decreasing the pressure in xylem vessels."},{"ref":"BI0674","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0674\n\nWhich of the following is likely to have the highest lignin content?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Xylem tissue\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Spongy mesophyll of leaves\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Phloem tissue\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Parenchyma tissue","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe cell walls of xylem are highly lignified (**Choice A**). Phloem tissue (**Choice C**), parenchyma tissue (**Choice D**) and spongy mesophyll cells (**Choice B**) have far less lignin compared to the cell walls of xylem."},{"ref":"BI0675","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0675\n\nHow are most mineral ions in the soil absorbed by roots?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Absorbed by osmosis\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Absorbed with water\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Active transport by root hair cells\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Diffuse into root hair cells from wet soil","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nMost mineral ions in the soil are absorbed by active transport in root hair cells (**Choice C**).\n\nActive uptake of mineral ions causes the absorption of water by osmosis.  By definition, osmosis is the movement of water, not ions (**Choice A**).\n\nWater gets absorbed passively, but most ions require active transport (**Choice B**).\n\nDiffusion is a type of passive transport and happens to some extent with mineral ion uptake. However the concentration of most minerals in the soil is low so active transport is generally required (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0676","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0676\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe micrograph below shows the xylem vessels in a developing shoot taken at 400$\\times$ magnification.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/BI0676_9.1.png\" alt=\"BI0676_9.1\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Berkshire Community College Bioscience Image Library, (2019, October). *File:Xylem vessels in developing Coleus shoot (35105390750).jpg*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 20, 2022, from https:// commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xylem_vessels_in_developing_Coleus_shoot_%2835105390750%29.jpg. Copyright under CC-Zero]\n\n1. Draw and label the structure of a primary xylem vessel in a developing shoot using the micrograph above. [2 marks]\n\n2. Describe the features of xylem vessels which enable them to withstand changes in pressure. [2 marks]\n\n3. Explain why the active uptake of mineral ions in roots leads to the absorption of water.    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lignin is drawn as rings within the xylem vessel and correctly labelled [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell wall correctly labelled and shown as one continuous line [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ remnants of fused end wall shown as indents [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/BI0676.png\" alt=\"BI0676\">\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ walls contain lignin [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ walls are thickened [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nXylem vessels have walls that are **thick** ($\\checkmark$) and impregnated with **lignin** in addition to cellulose ($\\checkmark$). This helps the xylem to withstand low pressures without collapsing.\n\n3.    Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ protein pumps, **OR** active transport, in root cells move the mineral ions into the root [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ higher solute, **OR** mineral ion concentration in the roots than in soil [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hypotonic conditions **OR** lower water potential in the roots compared to the soil [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water enters the roots from the soil by osmosis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water diffuses down its concentration gradient into the roots [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nRoot hair plasma membranes contain **protein pumps** specializing in the uptake of specific mineral ions needed by the plant ($\\checkmark$). The movement of minerals to the inside of the roots creates a **lower water potential** in the root cells compared to the surrounding environment ($\\checkmark$). This allows water to move into the roots by **osmosis**($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1121","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1121\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThis image shows an aphid with its stylet inserted into the stem of a plant, enabling it to feed.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/BI1121.png\" alt=\"BI1121\">\n[Source: https://www.flickr.com/photos/62295966@N07/24812950688/, 27th November 2017. *Banana aphid* (Pentalonia nigronervosa) (24812950688).jpg. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 27th June 2023 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: *Banana_aphid_(Pentalonia_nigronervosa)_-_24812950688.jpg*. Copyright under Creative Commons CC0 1.0]\n\n1. Name the tissue that aphids insert their stylets into. [1 mark]\n\n2. Draw a plan diagram to show the distribution of the vascular tissues in a transverse section of a dicotyledonous plant stem. [2 marks]\n\n3. Explain why the rate of flow of sap is faster at the sources than at the sinks.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ Phloem [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ This tissue is **phloem** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ vascular bundles arranged in ring shape around outer edge of stem [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ phloem labelled correctly in outer region of bundle **AND** xylem labelled correctly in inner region [1 mark]\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/BI1121ms.png\" alt=\"BI1121ms\">\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at sources sucrose **OR** organic compounds loaded into phloem AND at sinks sucrose **OR** organic compounds removed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ high sucrose **OR** organic compound concentration at source lowers water potential **OR** increases solute potential so water enters by osmosis **OR** low sucrose **OR** organic compound concentration at sink increases water potential **OR** decreases solute potential so water leaves by osmosis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ water entering phloem at source causes high hydrostatic pressure which increases rate of flow **OR** water leaving phloem at sinks causes low hydrostatic pressure reducing flow [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Sucrose is **actively transported** into the **phloem** at the **source**, which **increases** the **solute potential** (decreases water potential) ($\\checkmark$) of the sap, so **water follows** by **osmosis** ($\\checkmark$). This increase in water content of the sap causes a **high hydrostatic pressure**, **increasing** rate of **flow** ($\\checkmark$). At the **sink**, **sucrose leaves** the phloem ($\\checkmark$), so **solute potential decreases** (water potential increases), and **water leaves** the phloem by **osmosis**. This causes a **lower hydrostatic pressure** and so a **lower** rate of **flow** at the sinks."},{"ref":"BI1122","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1122\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nXylem and phloem are adapted for their functions. The image below is a longitudinal section of a stem showing phloem sieve tubes.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/BI1122.png\" alt=\"BI1122\">\n[Source: Berkshire Community College Bioscience Image Library. 10th February 2014. *Herbaceous Dicot Stem Tangential Section Phloem Plugs in Cucurbita (36826670444).jpg*. Retrieved 27th June 2023 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Herbaceous_Dicot_Stem_Tangential_Section_Phloem_Plugs_in_Cucurbita_%2836826668514%29.jpg Copyright under Creative Commons CC0 1.0]\n\n1.\n    1. Explain how phloem is adapted for its function.    [2 marks]\n\n    2. Identify the structures labelled I and II in this micrograph.     [1 mark]\n\n    3. Identify the features that you used to identify I and II.  [2 marks]\n\nBelow is a micrograph which shows a transport structure from a plant stem.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/BI1122b%20i.png\" alt=\"BI1122b i\">\n[Source: Adapted from Eugeenia Wen, 31st December 2019. File: Stem_of_Dicotyledon_C.S.png. Retrieved 13th May 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Stem_of_Dicotyledon_C.S.png. CC BY 4.0 ]\n\n2.\n    1. Name the group of tissues labelled X.    [1 mark]\n\n    2. Compare and contrast xylem and phloem tissue. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phloem    sieve tubes have a small amount of cytoplasm **OR** lack most cellular components, which gives space for transport of phloem sap **OR** to facilitate the flow of sap         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sieve plates between sieve tubes allow easy movement of phloem sap **OR** allow sap to flow freely between adjacent cells        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ companion cells carry out cellular functions **OR** provide metabolic support for the sieve tubes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell walls of cellulose and lignin **OR** sclerenchyma cells **OR** arrangement of cells, provide mechanical support and prevent collapse under high hydrostatic pressure [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ active transport mechanisms **OR** indirect active transport **OR**  sucrose-proton symport allow for the movement of nutrients **OR** sucrose against concentration gradients for loading at source    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Phloem sieve tubes have a **small amount of cytoplasm**, which gives **space for the transport of phloem sap** ($\\checkmark$) and **sieve plates** ($\\checkmark$) between the sieve tubes **allow the phloem sap to move easily** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    2. **BOTH** answers are required:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ I is a sieve plate **OR** sieve tube element, and II is a companion cell    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    I is a **sieve plate**, and II is a **companion cell** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    3. Answer must include **one** point for I and **one** point for II:\n\n    Any **one** of the following for I:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ I is located between sieve tube elements    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ I is perforated     [1 mark]\n\n    Any **one** of the following for II:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ II is adjacent to a sieve tube element        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ II is smaller in size than the sieve tube element        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ visible cytoplasm in II        [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Structure I is a sieve plate because it is **located between sieve tube elements** ($\\checkmark$) and II is a companion cell because it is **adjacent to a sieve tube element** and is smaller in size than the sieve tube element ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    1.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vascular bundle [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    X is a **vascular bundle** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    2. any **four** of the following (answer **must** include at least **one** difference **and one** similarity):\n\n    *Similarities*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are composed of tubes **OR** vascular tissues        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are both adapted for transport                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contain lignin in cell walls [1 mark]\n\n    *Differences*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ xylem is a non-living **OR** dead at maturity tissue, but phloem is a living tissue    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ xylem cells lack cell contents whereas in phloem sieve tube elements contain some cellular components **OR** companion cells contain normal cellular components    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ xylem contains more lignin **THAN** phloem        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ xylem vessels have a wider diameter than phloem tubes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ primary function of xylem is to transport water and minerals, whereas primary function of phloem is to transport organic compounds **OR** sucrose **OR** sugars    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ unidirectional transport in xylem, whereas bidirectional transport in phloem    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ main cell types in xylem are tracheids and vessel elements, whereas main cell types in phloem are sieve tube elements and companion cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transport through the xylem mainly relies on cohesion, adhesion and tension **OR** driven by transpiration, whereas transport through  phloem relies on active transport **OR** indirect active transport **OR**  sucrose-proton symport **OR** hydrostatic pressure [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Xylem and phloem are **both tubular structures**, or **vascular tissue**, ($\\checkmark$) and **adapted** to **transport substances** around the plant ($\\checkmark$). However, **xylem** is a **non-living tissue** that transports **water** around the plant in **one direction**, whereas **phloem** is a **living tissue** ($\\checkmark$) that transports **organic compounds** ($\\checkmark$)  both **up and down** the plant ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1158","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1158\n\nThe diagram below shows the structure of the human heart.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/BI1158.png\" alt=\"BI1158\">\n[Wnauta, W. (2007, March 4th). *Human heart*. Wikimedia Commons.\nRetrieved August 6th 2022 from   https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diagram_of_the_human_heart_%28clean%29.svg,\nCC-BY-SA-3.0-migrated\n]\n\nWhich pathway correctly describes the flow of blood after it leaves the lungs?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Pulmonary Vein → Right Atrium → Right Ventricle → Vena Cava\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Pulmonary Vein → Left Atrium → Left Ventricle → Aorta\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Pulmonary Artery → Right Atrium →  Right Ventricle → Vena Cava\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Pulmonary Artery → Left Atrium → Left Ventricle → Aorta","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nOxygenated blood from the lungs travels to the heart via the pulmonary vein. It then enters the left atrium before passing into the left ventricle before leaving the heart via the aorta on its way to the body. **Choice B** is, therefore, correct.\n\n**Choices A, C**, and **D** are incorrect as they all contain structures that are found on the right side of the heart (vena cava, right atrium, right ventricle, and pulmonary artery). The right-hand side of the heart is the side that pumps deoxygenated blood back to the heart."},{"ref":"BI1176","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1176\n\nA rose bush and radioactively labelled carbon dioxide (:sup[14]CO:sub[2]) were used in an experiment to investigate the time taken for sap to reach different groups of aphids located in different areas of the plant. The results are shown in the table below.\n\n| Aphid Colony | Time taken /min |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| X | 9.2 |\n| Y | 8.0 |\n| Z | 11.1 |\n\nWhat could be concluded from these results?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Colonies X, Y and Z are equally spaced along the stem of the rose bush.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Colony Y was located closest to the leaves.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Colonies X, Y and Z all received an equal volume of sap.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Colony Y fed on the sap more quickly than the other colonies.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCarbon dioxide enters the leaves, and is fixed during the light-independent stage of photosynthesis to form organic compounds. These organic compounds can then be loaded into phloem in the leaves (source) to be transported to areas of the plant which can’t produce their own (sinks).\n\nThe time taken for radioactive carbon to be found in samples taken from the stylets of aphids was lowest in colony Y. This is because Colony Y was the closest colony to the leaves, which is where the radioactively labelled organic compounds were synthesised. **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are not valid conclusions that could be drawn from these results."},{"ref":"BI1200","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1200\n\nHow is tissue fluid formed?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Plasma leaking out of capillaries due to hydrostatic pressure\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Plasma entering capillaries due to high pressure in the venules\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Plasma leaving the arteries under high pressure and entering cells\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Plasma leaking out of veins due to their high pressure","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because tissue fluid is formed by hydrostatic pressure, forcing plasma out of the capillaries so that it surrounds cells in the tissues of the body.\n\n​​It is osmotic pressure that causes the tissue fluid to re-enter the capillaries at the venous end, not hydrostatic pressure.  Also, this is the way tissue fluid is taken back up, not formed; **Choice B** is, therefore, incorrect. Tissue fluid cannot pass out of arteries or veins as their walls are too thick and do not contain pores, therefore **Choices C** and **D** are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1201","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1201\n\nDuring the formation of tissue fluid, which is **not** a transport method between the blood plasma and cells?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Diffusion\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Osmosis\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Active transport\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Filtration","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C**  is correct, as energy is not involved in the formation of tissue fluid.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect because gasses such as carbon dioxide and oxygen are exchanged between tissues and the capillaries via diffusion. **Choice B**  is incorrect, as the movement of water from the plasma between capillaries and tissues is via osmosis. **Choice D**  is incorrect, as tissue fluid is formed by pressure filtration of plasma from the capillaries."},{"ref":"BI1202","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1202\n\nWhich letter indicates tissue fluid?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/BI1202nv.png\" alt=\"BI1202nv\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct, as tissue fluid is the liquid that bathes cells.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect as it is pointing to lymph. **Choice C** is incorrect as it points to plasma. **Choice D** is incorrect as it points to a cell cytoplasm."},{"ref":"BI1203","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1203\n\nWhat is the role of the valves in the lymph vessels?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ To absorb tissue fluid from the surrounding cells\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ To prevent backflow of lymph\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ To allow lymph to flow in two directions\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ To aid the contraction of the lymph ducts","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B**  is correct, as valves prevent the backflow of lymph. **Choice A** is incorrect as the valves themselves do not absorb fluid, only control its flow. **Choice C**  is incorrect, as valves allow the lymph to only flow in one direction. **Choice  D** is incorrect, as valves do not control contraction, muscle tissue does."},{"ref":"BI1204","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1204\n\nWhich bodily substance is matched **incorrectly** to its description?\n\n| | Substance | Description |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Plasma | Part of blood that transports nutrients, proteins and hormones |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Tissue fluid | Intracellular fluid where most metabolic reactions take place |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Lymph | Fluid found in lymph ducts |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Surfactant | Lipids and proteins found in the fluid lining the surface of alveoli |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B**  is correct, as tissue fluid is the extracellular fluid that surrounds cells, not the intracellular, and it is not the site of most metabolic reactions. **Choices A, C** and **D**  are incorrect, as the substances are correctly described."},{"ref":"BI1205","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1205\n\nWhich description(s) is/are adaptations of the lymph ducts?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Valves to prevent backflow |\n    | II. | Thin walls to allow for efficient diffusion |\n    | III. | Many mitochondria to allow for strong muscle contractions |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct; lymph ducts contain valves to prevent backflow (**Statement I**) and have thin walls that allow for efficient transport of substances via diffusion (**Statement II**).  **Statement III** is incorrect, as lymph ducts do not need to contract strongly, so they do not contain many mitochondria. **Choices A, B** and **D**  are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1233","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1233\n\nThe diagram shows the frontal plane of the heart. Which letter indicates where the pacemaker would be found?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/BI1233.png\" alt=\"BI1233\">\n[Source: Adapted from spacefem. (2016, December 13). *Human heart*. openclipart.  Retrieved September 18, 2023, from https://openclipart.org/detail/268436/human-heart. Copyright free]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe heart diagram is always inverted so that the left is on the right and vice versa. The sinoatrial node or pacemaker is located in the right atrium of the heart. The atria are the two top chambers in the heart, and the right side of the heart can be identified as it has a thinner outer wall than the left side. **Choice A**  is correct.\n\nLine B indicates the right ventricle; **Choice B**  is incorrect. Line C indicates the pulmonary artery; **Choice C**  is incorrect. Line D indicates the left ventricle; **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1236","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1236\n\nThe image below is an electron micrograph of a cross-section of a stem of the monocot *Zea mays*. Which letter identifies the vessel that transports sucrose around the plant?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/BI1236.png\" alt=\"BI1236\">\n[Source: adapted from Reynolds, F.A. (2014, February 10). *Berkshire Community College Bioscience Image*. Flickr. Retrieved August 19, 2022, from  https://www.flickr.com/photos/146824358@N03/35716063792. Copy-right free]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn the stem, the vascular tissue is arranged into bundles of phloem and xylem (vascular bundles). Within the vascular bundle, the phloem is arranged closer to the outside of the stem and the xylem vessels are located closer to the inside of the stem. The phloem are also typically smaller than the xylem. The phloem transports sucrose around the plant and the xylem transports water from the roots to the rest of the leaves. **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**A** and **B** are not located within a vascular bundle, **Choices A**  and **B**  are incorrect. **C** indicates the xylem vessels, which are orientated towards the inside of the stem, **Choice C**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1488","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1488\n\nThe image shows a transverse section of an artery and vein. Which blood vessel carries oxygenated blood to body tissues?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/BI1488%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1488 fig1\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Company, C. B. S. (2015, January 7). Human artery & vein. Flickr. Retrieved February 12 , 2024,  https://www.flickr.com/photos/carolinabio/16196739466. Copyright free]\n\n| | Letter | Explanation |\n\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | X | It has a wider lumen to carry a higher volume of blood |\n\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | X | It has thin walls to allow diffusion of oxygen to body tissues |\n\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Y | It has thick walls to carry blood at high pressure |\n\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Y | It has valves to stop the backflow of blood |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nArteries have a narrow lumen and thick wall to carry oxygenated blood at high pressure to the body tissues; vessel Y is an artery. Veins have a wide lumen and thin walls, as they carry deoxygenated blood at lower pressure than the arteries; vessel X is a vein. **Choice C**  is correct.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect as although it has a wide lumen, it is a vein and does not carry oxygenated blood. **Choice B** is incorrect as capillaries allow diffusion of gases due to their thin walls, not arteries or veins, and most veins do not carry oxygenated blood. **Choice D** is incorrect as veins contain valves but arteries carry oxygenated blood."},{"ref":"BI1544","marks":"5","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1544\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nECGs are graphs that plot the electrical activity from the SA and AV nodes. The ECG below is from a person with atrial fibrillation, characterised by rapid, irregular beating of the atrial chambers of the heart.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/BI1544eDIT.png\" alt=\"BI1544eDIT\">\n\n1. Calculate the mean heart rate of this person. [1 mark]\n\n2. Suggest, with a reason, how atrial fibrillation will affect the pumping of the blood from the ventricles. [1 mark]\n\n3. Describe the passage of the electrical impulse and resulting mechanical actions in the heart during the QRS complex of a normal ECG. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. $\\ce{ -> }$ 23 bpm **OR** 23 beats min$^{-1}$ (allow 23 - 30) [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/BI1544AnsA.png\" alt=\"BI1544AnsA\">\n\n Time from end of the first QRS stage to end of last = 13s\n\n Number of beats in 13 seconds = 5\n\n Beats in one second = $(5 \\div 13) \\times 60$ = **23 beats min**$^{-1}$ ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ less blood is pumped from ventricles per beat **OR** decreased cardiac output **BECAUSE** incomplete filling of ventricles [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ inefficient and erratic ventricular contractions **BECAUSE** of irregular atrial signals [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ventricles have to contract more times per minute **OR** rate of ventricular systole increases **BECAUSE** lower volume of blood pumped out per beat [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Atrial fibrillation will **reduce** the **efficiency** of blood **pumping from** the **ventricles** because the **irregular** and **rapid beating** of the **atria** leads to **incomplete filling** of the ventricles ($\\checkmark$), resulting in **decreased cardiac output** ($\\checkmark$) and potentially insufficient blood supply to the body.\n\n3. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ electrical impulse passes through AV **OR** atrioventricular node [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ impulse passes down bundle of His **OR** down Purkinje fibres [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ventricles contract from apex upwards [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ventricular depolarisation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ventricular systole [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ blood pressure rises rapidly [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ atrioventricular valves shut **OR** semi-lunar valves open [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ atrial diastole **OR** atria begin to refill **OR** atrial repolarisation [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The QRS complex represents the **depolarisation** of the **ventricles** ($\\checkmark$), triggered as the **electrical impulse passes through** the **AV node** ($\\checkmark$). It travels down to the **apex** of the heart so the **ventricles** can **contract upwards** to push the blood out of the heart ($\\checkmark$). The phase corresponds to **ventricular systole** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1580","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1580\n\nAmphibians have a double circulatory system that carries blood between the heart, the lungs, and the rest of the body. Their heart consists of two atria and one ventricle, as seen in the diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/BI1580.png\" alt=\"BI1580\">\n[Source: Nefronus, (2020, March 28). *File: Amphibian circulatory system (numberated).svg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved April 25, 2024, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Amphibian_circulatory_system_(numberated).svg. Public domain]\n\nWhat is a consequence of this circulatory structure?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Oxygenated and deoxygenated blood mixes before it is received by the body.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increased blood pressure compared to mammals.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Amphibians have a less efficient circulatory system than fish.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Amphibians have a more efficient circulatory system than birds.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because the amphibian three-chambered heart causes oxygenated and deoxygenated blood to become mixed before it is pumped to the body.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because amphibians have much lower blood pressure than mammals. **Choice C** is incorrect because fish have a single circulatory system that reduces the supply of oxygen received by some tissues, making it less efficient than the amphibian circulatory system. **Choice D** is incorrect because birds, like humans, have a double circulatory system with a four-chambered heart. This makes their circulation much more efficient than an amphibian’s."},{"ref":"BI1588","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1588\n\nHow do companion cells support phloem sieve tubes in plants?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ They have no nucleus and reduced cytoplasm to aid in sucrose transport.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ They supply ATP for active transport.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ They maintain a higher sucrose concentration than the sieve tubes, allowing for more efficient loading at the source.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ They support hydrostatic flow by exchanging water with xylem vessels by osmosis.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because sieve tube elements lack a nucleus and most organelles. As a result, they require assistance from the companion cells in the form of ATP and proteins delivered via plasmodesmata. ATP is needed for active transport to load sucrose into the sieve tubes from the source or to unload at the sink. The proteins include structural proteins for the maintenance of the sieve tube and enzymes needed for biochemical processes within the sieve tubes.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it is the sieve tube elements rather than the companion cells that have no nucleus. **Choice C** is incorrect because the companion cells have a lower sucrose concentration than the phloem sieve tubes, requiring active transport to move sap from source to sink. **Choice D** is incorrect because osmosis does not occur between the xylem and companion cells. Instead, water is exchanged between the xylem and sieve tubes."},{"ref":"BI1632","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1632\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe image is a simple representation of the human circulatory system.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/BI1632%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1632 fig1\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Cancer Research UK. (2014, July 30). *File: Diagram showing the circulatory system of the body CRUK 299.SVG*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved April 30, 2024, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Diagram_showing_the_circulatory_system_of_the_body_CRUK_299.svg. Copyright free]\n\n1.  Label a vein on the image using the letter X.    [1 mark]\n\n2.  \n\n    1.  Describe **one** adaptation of veins that makes them suitable for their function.    [1 mark]\n\n    2.  Describe **one** adaptation of arteries that makes them suitable for their function. [1 mark]\n\n3.  Describe how the heart rate is lowered in response to high blood pressure in the aorta. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  any **one** of the following\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the blood vessel transporting blood from the capillaries in the lungs to the heart **OR** the blood vessel transporting blood from the capillaries in the rest of the body to the heart should be labelled with the letter X    [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/BI1632%20fig2.png\" alt=\"BI1632 fig2\">\n\n    [Source: Adapted from Cancer Research UK. (2014, July 30). *File:Diagram showing the circulatory system of the body CRUK 299.SVG*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved April 30, 2024, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Diagram_showing_the_circulatory_system_of_the_body_CRUK_299.svg. Copyright free]\n\n2.  \n\n    1.  any **one** of the following\\\n\n        *Answer must include* ***BOTH*** *the adaptation and function to receive one mark*\\\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ valves to prevent the backflow of blood        [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ thin **OR** less elastic walls to transport high volumes of blood at low pressure **OR** more flexible to accommodate changes in blood volume    [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ large lumen to carry high volumes of blood **OR** reduce resistance to blood flow    [1 mark]\n\n        [1 mark]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n        Veins have **valves** to **prevent** the **backflow** of blood ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    2.  any **one** of the following\\\n\n        *Answer must include* ***BOTH*** *the adaptation and function to receive one mark*\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ they have thick, muscular, elastic walls to withstand high pressure        [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ they have a narrow lumen to maintain high pressure    [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ smooth endothelium and minimises friction between the blood and the vessel walls    [1 mark]\n\n        [1 mark]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n        Arteries have **thick**, **elastic** walls to **withstand high pressure** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.  any **two** of the following\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ baroreceptors **OR** stretch receptors in the walls of the aorta **OR** carotid artery detect high blood pressure    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sensory neurons send impulses to the brain        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ processing by the cardiovascular centre **OR** medulla in the brain        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ impulses sent via the vagus nerve **OR** parasympathetic nervous system to lower the heart rate        [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n    **Baroreceptors** in the walls of the aorta **detect high blood pressure** ($\\checkmark$), which signals **sensory neurons** to **send impulses** to the brain($\\checkmark$). The impulses are processed by the **cardiovascular centre** in the brain ($\\checkmark$), which sends impulses via the **vagus nerve** to **lower the heart rate** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2330","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2330\n\nWhich row contains an adaptation with a matching advantage for xylem vessels?\n\n| | Adaptation | Advantage |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Incomplete end walls | Allow entry and exit of water to other tissues |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Lignified walls | Allows unimpeded flow of water |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Pits | Withstands tension due to upward movement of water |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Lack of cell contents | Allows unimpeded flow of water |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because xylem vessels contain significantly reduced cell contents which prevents obstruction to water flow in the xylem.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because while incomplete end walls allow water to flow easily from one xylem vessel to the next, water does not flow between xylem vessels and plant tissues through the incomplete end walls.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because xylem walls do have lignified walls, but the function of the lignified walls is to prevent the collapse of xylem vessels under tension caused by the transpiration stream, not to allow unimpeded water flow.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because pits in the walls of xylem vessels allow water to move between xylem vessels, pits do not withstand the tension created by water's upward movement."},{"ref":"BI2383","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2383\n\nWhich row of the table contains **three** correct statements about the circulatory systems of bony fish, amphibians and mammals?\n\n| | Bony fish | Amphibians | Mammals |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | open circulation | oxygen absorbed via capillaries in the lungs and skin | oxygen absorbed via capillaries in the lungs and skin |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | double loop | double loop | four-chambered heart |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | single loop | three-chambered heart | pulmonary circulation |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | closed circulation | four-chambered heart | double loop |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because all three statements are correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because all three types of organism have a closed circulation, where blood is kept separate from other body fluids. Also, mammals do not absorb oxygen through the skin (as amphibians do), just through the lungs. \\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because a bony fish’s circulation is a single circulation loop, not double. \\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the hearts of amphibians are 3-chambered (2 atria and 1 ventricle), not 4-chambered. \\\nThe correct statements are **highlighted** and the incorrect statements are ~struck out~ in the table below:\n\n| | Bony fish | Amphibians | Mammals |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | ~open circulation~ | **oxygen absorbed via capillaries in the lungs and skin** | ~oxygen absorbed via capillaries in the lungs and skin~ |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | ~double loop~ | **double loop** | **four-chambered heart** |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | **single loop** | **three-chambered heart** | **pulmonary circulation** |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | **closed circulation** | ~four-chambered heart~ | **double loop** |"},{"ref":"BI2817","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2817\n\nWhich of the following methods would most reduce uncertainty when measuring a resting heart rate using the radial pulse?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Use of thumb to feel for the pulse over 10 seconds\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Use of fingertips to measure for 30 seconds and multiply by 2\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Use of fingertips to measure for 60 seconds\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Use of stopwatch and estimation over 15 seconds","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nMeasuring the pulse with fingertips (not the thumb) for a full 60 seconds is the most accurate method for determining resting heart rate. This approach avoids the need to multiply or estimate, reducing uncertainty, and ensures that natural fluctuations in heart rhythm are accounted for. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the thumb has its own pulse, which can interfere with detecting the subject’s pulse accurately. Additionally, measuring for just 10 seconds increases uncertainty since small timing errors lead to a larger percentage error when extrapolated over a full minute.\\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because although using fingertips is good, multiplying a 30-second count by 2 increases uncertainty, especially if the pulse is irregular. Conditions such as sinus arrhythmia can cause beat-to-beat variations, making short-period extrapolations less reliable. Measuring for the full 60 seconds eliminates this issue.\\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because measuring over only 15 seconds significantly magnifies errors. A missed or extra beat during this brief window results in a fourfold discrepancy when multiplied, making it a much less reliable approach."},{"ref":"BI2818","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2818\n\nWhich structural feature of arteries allows them to withstand the pressure generated by ventricular systole?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Thin tunica media\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Elastic fibres in the tunica intima\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Smooth muscle in the tunica externa\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Thick tunica media with elastic fibres","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe tunica media is the middle layer of the artery wall and contains both smooth muscle and elastic fibres. During ventricular systole (when the heart contracts), blood is ejected into the arteries under high pressure.  A thick tunica media allows the artery to stretch (due to elastic fibres) and resist rupture (due to smooth muscle), helping to maintain the integrity of the vessel and buffer pressure surges. Additionally, elastic fibres enable recoil after stretching, ensuring consistent blood flow.  **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because this is the opposite of what allows arteries to withstand high pressure. A thin tunica media would lack the structural strength and elasticity needed to resist the force of blood ejected from the heart.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because although the tunica intima contains some elastic fibres, they are minimal compared to the tunica media, which plays the dominant role in stretch and recoil. The tunica intima primarily consists of an endothelial layer that reduces friction for smooth blood flow rather than providing strength or pressure resistance.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because the tunica externa (also called the adventitia) is primarily composed of collagen fibres, which provide structural support but do not actively regulate pressure. The smooth muscle responsible for vasoconstriction and withstanding pressure is located in the tunica media."},{"ref":"BI2819","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2819\n\nWhich of the following statements about venous blood flow is/are true?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | It depends on the contraction of skeletal muscles |\n    | II. | It occurs under low pressure compared to arterial flow|\n    | III. | It is aided by elastic recoil of vein walls |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because veins, especially in the limbs, are surrounded by skeletal muscle. Contraction of these muscles compresses the veins, pushing blood toward the heart. One-way valves in veins prevent backflow, making this mechanism essential for venous return.\\\n**Statement II** is true because after passing through capillaries, blood pressure in veins is much lower than in arteries. Veins do not have enough internal pressure to move blood effectively without help from muscle contractions, valves, and respiratory movements.\\\n**Statement III** is false because, unlike arteries, veins contain very few elastic fibres and do not recoil significantly. Elastic recoil is a feature of arterial walls, especially the aorta and large arteries, to maintain blood flow during diastole.\n\n**Choice A** is the correct answer because it only includes the two true **Statements I** and **II**.\\\n**Choices B, C** and **D** are all incorrect as they include the false **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI2858","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2858\n\nThe diagram shows a transverse section of an artery:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Transport/images/BI2858%20B3.2%20core%20MCQ.jpeg.JPEG\" alt=\"BI2858 B3.2 core MCQ.jpeg\">\n\nWhich row correctly matches the layer with its function?\n\n| | X | Y | Z |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Reduces resistance to flow | Provides strength and helps prevent bursting | Recoils to maintain flow |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Reduces resistance to flow | Recoils to maintain flow | Provides strength and helps prevent bursting |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Provides strength and helps prevent bursting | Recoils to maintain flow | Reduces resistance to flow |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Recoils to maintain flow | Reduces resistance to flow | Provides strength and helps prevent bursting |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Layer X** is the tunica intima. This innermost layer is made of smooth endothelium, providing a low-friction surface for efficient blood flow.\n\n**Layer Y** is the tunica media. This middle layer contains smooth muscle and elastic fibres, allowing the artery to stretch and recoil with each heartbeat, helping to maintain blood pressure and flow.\n\n**Layer Z** is the tunica externa. This outer layer contains collagen fibres that provide mechanical strength, and helps prevent rupturing under the high pressure.\n\n**Choice B** is therefore the correct answer to the question."}]},{"name":"Unassigned","questions":[{"ref":"BI0047","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0047\n\nWhich of the following processes is most directly involved in the formation of nucleic acids such as DNA?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrolysis\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Condensation\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Catabolism\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Translation","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nNucleotides are linked together by condensation reactions to form molecules of DNA (**Choice B**). The formation of DNA requires the removal of water for the joining of smaller molecules to form a larger molecule.\n\nThe joining of nucleotides together can also be classified as an anabolic reaction but not as a catabolic reaction (**Choice C**). Catabolism is the breaking down of a large molecule into a smaller molecule. An example of catabolism is hydrolysis which is a reaction that breaks down a large molecule into a smaller molecule by adding water (**Choice A**).\n\nTranslation is a process which follows the transcription of DNA (**Choice D**). It is where mRNA is used as a template by ribosomes to synthesize polypeptides."},{"ref":"BI0100","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0100\n\nRain forests are known to have tall trees and a large biomass with high productivity, despite infertile soils. The growing conditions have been known to nurture the presence of many interdependent organisms.  These species also interact with the physical environment they inhabit.\n\nWhich of the following is described here?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$  A habitat\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$  An ecosystem\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$  A population\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$  A community","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAn ecosystem refers to an area where organisms interact with each other and with the physical environment.\n\nA habitat (**Choice A**) refers to the environment in which a species normally lives. A population (**Choice C**) is a group of organisms of the same species living in the same area at the same time which can interbreed. A community (**Choice D**) is a group of populations living and interacting with each other in the same habitat."},{"ref":"BI0110","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0110\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA student wants to set up an aquatic mesocosm using seawater to simulate conditions in the food web (shown in the image below) and test its stability. He used seawater and sand from the area to set up the system and added some small organisms to the mesocosm.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI0110fw.png\" alt=\"BI0110fw\">\n[Source: Adapted from Cmglee. (2003, October). *File: Arctic Food Web.svg - Wikimedia Commons*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved April 14, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Arctic_food_web.svg. Copyright under CC BY-SA 3.0]\n\n1. In the food web shown, identify an autotroph. [1 mark]\n\n2. State a reason why phytoplankton shown in the food web would be a good addition to the mesocosm. [1 mark]\n\n3. Discuss how saprotrophs perform a necessary function for the mesocosm. [3 marks]\n\n4. Describe the disadvantages of using mesocosms in scientific investigations. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ phytoplankton [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phytoplankton are autotrophs **OR** can add energy to the mesocosm through photosynthesis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to provide energy continuously to the closed system [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    The phytoplankton **are autotrophs** ($\\checkmark$). Autotrophs are necessary for all ecosystems because they produce organic compounds through photosynthesis.\n\n3. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ saprotrophs secrete enzymes into the environment [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ saprotrophs break down food outside their bodies into simpler substances [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ saprotrophs are found in the water **OR**  sand [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ saprotrophs recycle nutrients in ecosystems [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ saprotrophs sometimes remove toxic compounds [1 mark]\n[3 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Saprotrophs **recycle nutrients in ecosystems** ($\\checkmark$) by **secreting enzymes into the environment** ($\\checkmark$) which **break down organic material into simpler substances** ($\\checkmark$) which they then absorb and use for food.  Saprotrophs can also **break down toxic compounds** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large organisms may not fit in the mesocosms **OR** sometimes not all organisms from the ecosystem can be added to the mesocosm **OR** mesocosms are sometimes limited in size[1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mesocosms are limited in their capacity to simulate natural conditions [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ small organisms added from the field to a mesocosm may be stressed [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mesocosms need to be monitored regularly [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Mesocosms are experiments set up in laboratories that are **limited in their physical space** ($\\checkmark$). Only small organisms can be used in the experiments and the organisms **can be stressed** once inside a mesocosm ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0299","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0299\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the risk factors and consequences associated with plaque buildup in the coronary arteries. [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline how the pressure changes in the left atrium and left ventricle affect the surrounding valves and blood flow.  [4 marks]\n3. Explain how the structural features of the arteries, veins and capillaries are adapted to support their functions. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following (at least **one** from each of the causes and consequences):\n\n    *Causes*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ high blood pressure **OR** high blood cholesterol [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  smoking **OR** diabetes **OR** genetic factors [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a diet high in LDL cholesterol **OR** saturated fats **OR** trans fats[1 mark]\n\n    *Consequences*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ blood flow restriction in the arteries **OR** narrowing of the arteries [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  lack of oxygen     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ heart inflammation **OR** angina [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ decreases the ability of the cardiac muscle to contract    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stimulates the formation of blood clots in the artery [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ heart attack  [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nOcclusion is caused by the **buildup of fatty plaques** in the inner lining of the coronary arteries ($\\checkmark$) which could be the effect of **smoking** ($\\checkmark$) or **high blood pressure** ($\\checkmark$). As a consequence, it can **restrict blood flow to the arteries** ($\\checkmark$) and lead to **chest pain** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.  any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when pressure increases in the left atrium **OR** pressure in the atrium is higher than the ventricle the atrioventricular **OR** bicuspid valve opens [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ blood flows from the left atrium to the left ventricle [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when the pressure increases in the left ventricle **OR** the pressure in the left ventricle is higher than the left atrium, the atrioventricular **OR** bicuspid valves close  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when the pressure increases in the left ventricle **OR** the pressure inside the left ventricle is higher than the aorta, the semilunar **OR** aortic valves open [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ blood flows from the left ventricle to the aorta [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    **Pressure** in the **left atrium increases** when the left atrium contracts, which causes the **atrioventricular** valve to **open** ($\\checkmark$), allowing **blood** to flow **into the left ventricle** ($\\checkmark$). The **pressure** in the **left ventricle increases** when the left ventricle contracts causing the **semilunar** valve to **open** ($\\checkmark$) and the **atrioventricular valve to close** ($\\checkmark$). This prevents the backflow of blood into the atrium from the ventricle. With the increase in pressure in the left ventricle, the **blood** is pumped **from the ventricle to the aorta** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.    any **eight** of the following (answer should contain at least **one** statement from each category, both the feature and explanation need to be present for each marking point)\n\n    *Arteries*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ thick walls to withstand high pressure **OR** prevent rupturing [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ walls have elastic fibers to allow them to, stretch **OR** expand, and recoil in response to, pulsatile blood flow **OR** heart beat  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ muscular walls for vasoconstriction **OR** vasodilation **OR** to regulate blood pressure **OR** control distribution of blood [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ narrow lumen helps maintain blood pressure [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ smooth inner lining, **OR** endothelium, to reduce friction [1 mark]\n\n    *Capillaries*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ thin walls **OR** one cells thick walls help to increase diffusion rate **OR** reduce diffusion distance between the blood and cells **OR** tissues [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ small diameter to allow passage of one blood cell at a time **OR** to optimize exchange [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ may contain pores, **OR** fenestrations,  to aid transport of materials [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ dense network, **OR** large surface area, to supply cells **OR** tissues with materials **OR** nutrients [1 mark]\n\n    *Veins*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ thin walls, **OR** close proximity to skeletal muscles, so muscle contraction can help support blood flow     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reduced elasticity (compared to arteries) due to lower pressure blood [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ valves to prevent the backflow of blood     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ wider lumen (compared to arteries) helps to reduce friction **OR** maximize blood flow [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    Arteries are adapted to suit their function because they have **thick walls** which help to **withstand the high pressure** blood they transport ($\\checkmark$), which is also maintained by their **narrow lumen** ($\\checkmark$). Their walls also have **elastic fibers** to allow them to **stretch and recoil** in response to **pulsatile blood flow**.  ($\\checkmark$).\nArteries branch into arterioles, which branch into capillaries, which are the site of exchange between the blood and surrounding tissue. They are adapted for their function because they have very **thin walls**, which **decreases the diffusion distance** ($\\checkmark$) for nutrients to travel and they have a very **dense network** to support the **delivery of nutrients** to all of the tissues ($\\checkmark$).\nVeins return blood from the tissues to the heart and they are adapted to their role because they have **valves to prevent the backflow** of blood ($\\checkmark$) which is traveling at lower pressure and often against gravity. They also have a **wider lumen** which helps to maximize blood flow by **reducing friction** ($\\checkmark$) and their walls are **less elastic compared to arteries**, because blood is flowing at a **lower pressure** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0350","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0350\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast the phloem found in the stem and the root of a plant.       [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline how root pressure is generated through the transport of minerals.       [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain the changes in hydrostatic pressure within the phloem that facilitate the transport of phloem sap from sources to sinks.  [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following (award 2 marks maximum if no contrast given):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phloem transports organic molecules **OR** amino acids **OR** sap **OR** sucrose in **BOTH** the root and stem  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phloem is part of **OR** found in the vascular bundle in **BOTH** the stem and root  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phloem is positioned outside the xylem in **BOTH** stem and roots  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phloem (as part of vascular bundles) is distributed **OR** scattered **OR** spread out **OR** located around the edge in the stem **BUT** located at the centre of roots   [1 mark]\n[3 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    In the stem and the root, the phloem is responsible for the transport of **organic molecules** ($\\checkmark$) such as sucrose and is found as part of the **vascular bundle** ($\\checkmark$). In the root, the phloem is located near the centre, however, it is more **spread out** in the stem ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ endodermal cells contain suberin **OR** have a Casparian strip [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ waterproof layer blocks movement through cell walls (apoplast pathway) [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water **AND** ions are forced through cytoplasm **OR** across cell membranes (**OR** symplast pathway) [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ensures regulated entry of water **AND** ions **OR** prevents uncontrolled loss from the stele [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ endodermal cells pump **OR** actively transport mineral ions into xylem [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ion accumulation lowers water potential **OR** increases solute concentration inside xylem [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water enters xylem down water potential gradient **OR** by osmosis. [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ influx of water generates positive pressure potential **OR** hydrostatic pressure [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Endodermal cells contain a **Casparian strip** ($\\checkmark$) that is waterproof and **blocks** the **apoplast pathway** ($\\checkmark$), **forcing water** and **ions** to move through the **symplast pathway** ($\\checkmark$). Endodermal cells then **actively transport ions into** the **xylem** ($\\checkmark$), **increasing** its **solute potential** ($\\checkmark$) causing **water** to **enter by osmosis** and generate a **positive pressure** known as root pressure ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sources synthesize organic compounds   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organic compounds **OR** named example, are loaded into phloem **OR** sieve tubes, at              sources       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ solute concentration increases (in the sieve tubes at sources)      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water from the xylem moves into the sieve tube **OR** phloem by osmosis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in hydrostatic pressure in the phloem  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sinks act as organic compound storage **OR** non-synthesising regions        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organic compounds, **OR** named example, are unloaded from phloem, **OR** sieve tubes  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ decreased solute concentration (in sieve tubes at sinks)       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water moves out of the sieve tube into the xylem by osmosis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrostatic pressure decreases     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrostatic pressure is the pressure exerted by a fluid as a result of gravity [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrostatic pressure gradient (as a result of the incompressibility of water) allows the sap to flow from source to sink **OR** differences in hydrostatic pressure between phloem at sources and sinks allows transport of sap        [1 mark]\n[8 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Sources are areas of the plant which can synthesise **organic compounds** ($\\checkmark$) such as sucrose. At sources, sucrose is **loaded** into the phloem ($\\checkmark$), which creates a **high solute concentration** ($\\checkmark$). This causes water to move, by **osmosis, into the sieve tubes** from xylem vessels ($\\checkmark$). The flow of water into the sieve tubes increases the **hydrostatic pressure** ($\\checkmark$) which causes water to **flow to areas of lower pressure** ($\\checkmark$), such as sinks. Sinks are areas of the plant which **don’t synthesise** organic molecules ($\\checkmark$), such as the roots. At sinks, sucrose is **unloaded** from the sieve tubes ($\\checkmark$), which **decreases the solute concentration** ($\\checkmark$). This leads to water moving back into the xylem vessels by osmosis and as a result, the **hydrostatic pressure** in the sieve tubes **decreases** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0412","marks":"10","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0412\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nNeuronal cells have special membrane properties that allow their axons to propagate action potential from one cell to another. The diagram below shows a portion of an axonal plasma membrane.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/sodium%20potassium%20pump.png\" alt=\"sodium potassium pump\">\n\n1.\n 1. State the role of a sodium-potassium pump in the axon. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Describe the action of the sodium-potassium pump. [3 marks]\n\n2. Distinguish between simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion and active transport. [2 marks]\n\n3. Compare and contrast sodium-potassium pumps and voltage-gated potassium channels. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ active transport **AND** higher concentration of sodium outside the cell **AND** higher concentration of potassium inside the cell **OR** actively transports three sodium ions out of the cell and two potassium ions into the cell for each cycle of its activity\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ helps establish **OR** restore **OR** maintain the resting membrane potential [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ facilitates the transport of other substances across the cell membrane **OR** enables secondary active transport **OR** cotransport **OR** antiport [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n The sodium-potassium pump **uses energy** to move Na$^{+}$ and K$^{+}$ ions against their concentration gradients **to maintain a higher concentration** of sodium **outside** the axon and a higher concentration of potassium **inside** the axon. ($\\checkmark$)\n\n 2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ three sodium ions attach to their binding sites on the pump which is open to the inside of the axon [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP transfers a phosphate group from itself to the pump causing the pump to change shape and the interior is then closed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the interior of the pump opens to the outside of the axon **OR** the three sodium ions are released [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ two potassium ions from the outside can then attach to their binding sites [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the binding of potassium causes the release of the phosphate group **OR** this causes the pump to change shape again so that it is only open to the inside of the axon [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the interior of the pump opens to the inside of the axon thus releasing the two potassium ions [1 mark]\n\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nThe sodium-potassium pump is responsible for active transport which maintains a high concentration of sodium and a low concentration of potassium inside the axon. When the pump is open to the inside of the axon, **three sodium ions attach to their binding sites** ($\\checkmark$). The **pump changes shape once ATP transfers a phosphate group** from itself to the pump ($\\checkmark$). **Three sodium ions are released** to the outside and **two potassium ions attach** to their binding sites ($\\checkmark$). The **pump changes shape again so that the two potassium ions are released towards the inside of the axon**. ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ simple diffusion and facilitated do not require energy **WHILE** active transport requires energy [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ simple diffusion and facilitated diffusion transport substances down their concentration gradient **WHILE** active transport transports substances up their concentration gradient [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ simple diffusion does not involve membrane proteins **WHILE** facilitated diffusion and active transport require membrane bound proteins [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n**Simple diffusion transports substances** down their concentration gradient requiring **neither energy nor membrane proteins**. Facilitated diffusion requires **membrane proteins** but does not use energy while active transport requires **both membrane proteins** ($\\checkmark$) and **energy** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Na$^{+}$-K$^{+}$ pump and K$^{+}$ channel are **BOTH** involved in the transport of potassium ions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Na$^{+}$-K$^{+}$ pump and K$^{+}$ channel are **BOTH** transmembrane **OR** integral proteins [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Na$^{+}$-K$^{+}$ pump and K$^{+}$ channel **BOTH** play an important role in the generation of action potentials in neurons [1 mark]\n\n Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Na$^{+}$-K$^{+}$ pumps require energy **WHILE** K$^{+}$ channels do not [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Na$^{+}$-K$^{+}$ pumps transport ions up their concentration gradient **WHILE** K$^{+}$ channels transport ions down their concentration gradient [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Na$^{+}$-K$^{+}$ pumps are not are not controlled by voltage **WHILE** K$^{+}$ channels open and close in response to changes in voltage [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Na$^{+}$-K$^{+}$ pumps transport potassium ions from the outside to the inside of the axon **WHILE** K$^{+}$ channels allow potassium ions to diffuse out of the axon [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ K$^{+}$ channels transport only potassium ions **WHEREAS** Na$^{+}$-K$^{+}$ pumps (anti-port) transport potassium and sodium ions [1 mark]\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n| Sodium-potassium pumps | Potassium channels | $\\hspace{1cm}$|\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| Transport potassium ions | Transport potassium ions | ($\\checkmark$) |\n| Is a transmembrane protein | Is a transmembrane protein | ($\\checkmark$) |\n| Require energy | No energy needed| ($\\checkmark$) |\n| Not voltage-gated | Voltage-gated | ($\\checkmark$) |\n| Transport potassium ions from the outside to the inside of an axon | Transport potassium ions from the inside to the outside of an axon | ($\\checkmark$) |"},{"ref":"BI0413","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0413\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nMembrane transport is important for allowing particles to move across the cell membrane and respond to changes in the chemical composition of the extracellular fluid.\n\n1. Outline the effects of placing a liver cell in a hypotonic solution.    [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline how the fluidity of membranes allows materials to be taken into cells or to be released from cells.  [6 marks]\n\n3. Compare and contrast four types of membrane transport. Include an example of each. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the liver cell swells **OR** enlarges **OR** expands **OR** increase in volume [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the liver cell ruptures **OR** burst **OR** lyses    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water diffuses into the liver cell **OR** water moves from the medium of lower solute concentration  to a medium where there is higher solute concentration  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ osmotic pressure favors the movement of water into the cell **OR** water will diffuse from a medium of lower osmotic pressure to higher osmotic pressure [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    Once a liver cell is placed in a hypotonic solution, **water** will diffuse from a **medium of lower solute concentration to a region of higher solute concentration** ($\\checkmark$) by osmosis. This results in **an influx of water into the liver cell** that **causes the cell to swell**($\\checkmark$) and eventually **rupture** ($\\checkmark$) depending on how steep the concentration gradient is.\n\n2. any **six** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the phospholipid composition of the cell membrane allows it to be in a fluid state [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the fluidity of the membrane allows membranes to change shape **OR** move [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the fluidity of the membrane allows the membrane to interact **OR** fuse with vesicles **OR** vesicles pinch off from  the membrane  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles are composed of phospholipids [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ during exocytosis, material to be exported by the cell is packaged in membrane-bound vesicles that fuse with the plasma membrane [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ during exocytosis, vesicles release their contents to the cell exterior and the membrane flattens out again [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ during endocytosis, materials to be imported into the cell is packaged in membrane-bound vesicles that pinch off  the plasma membrane and are transported to the cell interior     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ proteins in the membrane form vesicles using ATP [1 mark]\n\n    [6 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n    The cell membrane is a **phospholipid bilayer that is fluid** ($\\checkmark$). This allows **membranes to change shape** ($\\checkmark$) and to **form vesicles that pinch off of it or receive vesicles that fuse with it.** ($\\checkmark$)  These vesicles are involved in membrane transport known as endocytosis and exocytosis. **Exocytosis** is an active form of transport in which cells export material to the cell membrane or to the extracellular fluid via vesicles. These vesicles are transported to the cell surface where they **fuse with the plasma membrane and release their contents** to the extracellular fluid ($\\checkmark$). Vesicles fusing with the cell membrane help rebuild the cell membrane as they too are composed of **phospholipids**. ($\\checkmark$) In **endocytosis**, material entering the cell is engulfed by the cell membrane which then pinches inwards to form a vesicle surrounding the molecules. These vesicles are then **transported to the interior of the cell** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **ALL** four types of membrane transport move substances across a partially permeable membrane [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion **AND** osmosis are passive **OR** do not require ATP **WHILE** active transport uses energy **OR** requires ATP [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen diffuses across membranes by simple diffusion **OR** accept other valid examples [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the sodium potassium pump is an example of active transport and hydrolyzes ATP **OR** accept other valid examples [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ simple diffusion does not require membrane proteins to occur **WHILE** facilitated diffusion, osmosis, **AND** active transport all occur via membrane proteins **OR** facilitated diffusion requires protein channels or carrier proteins, osmosis sometimes uses aquaporins, **AND** active transport requires protein pumps    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the transport of chloride ions through channel proteins is an example of facilitated diffusion **OR** accept other valid examples [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the movement of water across a semipermeable membrane through aquaporins is an example of osmosis **OR** accept other valid examples [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion **AND** osmosis allow the diffusion of molecules down their concentration gradients **WHILE** active transport transports substances against their concentration gradient [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion **AND** osmosis allow the system to reach equilibrium **WHILE** active transport drives the system away from equilibrium [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ simple diffusion occurs with small polar particles **OR** hydrophobic molecules **OR** non-polar particles, **WHILE** facilitated diffusion occurs with larger or charged molecules, **WHEREAS** osmosis occurs with water molecules only **WHILE** active transport can transport any molecules [1 mark]\n\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    | Simple Diffusion | Facilitated Diffusion | Osmosis | Active Transport | $\\hspace{1cm}$ |\n    | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n    | passive | passive | passive | active | ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | does not require ATP hydrolysis | does not require ATP hydrolysis | does not require ATP hydrolysis | requires ATP hydrolysis | |\n    | does not require membrane proteins | requires membrane proteins | requires membrane proteins | requires membrane proteins | ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | transports substances down their concentration gradient | transports substances down their concentration gradient | transports water down its concentration gradient | transports substances up their concentration gradient | ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | applies to small polar, or small nonpolar, or hydrophobic molecules | applies to larger or charged molecules | applies to water molecules only | applies to any molecules  | ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | system eventually reaches equilibrium | system eventually reaches equilibrium | system eventually reaches equilibrium | does not allow the system to reach equilibrium | ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | Example: diffusion of **O$_2$** | Example: Diffusion of **Cl$^{-}$ ions** through **Cl$^{-}$ channels** | Example: diffusion of water through **aquaporins** in the kidneys| Example: pumping of **Na$^{+}$ and K$^{+}$ ions** through the **Na$^{+}$/K$^{+}$ pump** | ($\\checkmark$) |"},{"ref":"BI0505","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0505\n\nZooxanthellae are algae that live inside coral. Corals feed on the carbon compounds produced by zooxanthellae from carbon dioxide, water, and sunlight. Which describes the nutrition of these organisms?\n\n| | Zooxanthellae | Coral |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Autotroph | Heterotroph |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Autotroph | Detritivore |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Heterotroph | Autotroph |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Detritivore | Heterotroph |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nZooxanthellae make carbon compounds from carbon dioxide, water and energy from sunlight. So, zooxanthellae are autotrophs (organisms that make their own food).\nThe coral feed on the carbon compounds produced by zooxanthellae. So, the corals are heterotrophs (organisms that must obtain carbon compounds from other organisms).\n\nDetritivores feed on dead organic matter (**Choices B** and **D**).\\\n**Choice C** has the nutrition of these organisms backwards."},{"ref":"BI0573","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0573\n\nWhat is the substrate for the enzyme lactase?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Glucose\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Galactose\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Lactose\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Fructose","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe enzyme lactase breaks down the substrate lactose into glucose and galactose.\n\nGlucose and galactose are products of the reaction (**Choices A** and **B**).\n\nFructose is not a substrate for the enzyme lactase (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0594","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0594\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe diagram below represents the structure of the protein haemoglobin.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI0594i.png\" alt=\"BI0594i\">\n\n1.\n 1. State the function of haemoglobin. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Describe the structural differences that distinguish one protein from another. [2 marks]\n\n2. Haemoglobin is part of an organism’s proteome. Define proteome. [1 mark]\n\n3. Describe how the structure of collagen or insulin is related to its function. [2 marks]\n\n4.\n 1. Explain the impact high temperatures can have on the structure and function of proteins such as haemoglobin. [3 marks]\n\n 2. State another abiotic factor which can impact the structure of a protein. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n 1.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ transport of oxygen (around the body) [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nHaemoglobin is a protein found in red blood cells which **transports oxygen** around the body ($\\checkmark$).\n\n 2. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ large number of amino acids **OR** 20 amino acids allows for many combinations of amino acids between different proteins/polypeptides [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ length of polypeptides varies [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ proteins are composed of different amino acids [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ multiple polypeptides can be linked to make different proteins [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nBecause there are **20 different amino acids**, there is a large number of **different combinations** of amino acids possible ($\\checkmark$). Polypeptides can also **vary greatly in length** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. $\\ce{ -> }$ complete set of proteins expressed by a cell **OR** tissue **OR** organism [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nThe proteome is the complete **set of proteins** that a cell or an organism can express ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **two** of the following (answers must contain named example and function for full marks):\n\n ***Collagen***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fibrous protein that provides structural support in connective tissues **OR** resists stretching **OR** supports tissue integrity [1 mark]\n $\\ce{ -> }$ three polypeptide chains wound into a triple helix, giving it high tensile strength [1 mark]\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycine every third residue allows chains to pack tightly [1 mark]\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen bonds between chains **OR** covalent cross-links between molecules increase stability and strength [1 mark]\n\n ***Insulin***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ globular protein hormone involved in the regulation of blood glucose [1 mark]\n $\\ce{ -> }$ two polypeptide chains **OR** α and β chains giving quaternary structure [1 mark]\n $\\ce{ -> }$ specific shape allows insulin to bind precisely to insulin receptors on target cell membranes [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nCollagen is a **fibrous protein** that provides **structural support** in **connective tissue** ($\\checkmark$). Its **three polypeptide chains** are wound into a **triple helix**, which is reinforced by **hydrogen bonds**, giving it **high tensile strength** ($\\checkmark$) and making it **resistant to stretching** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.\n 1. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ extreme temperatures can lead to denaturation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ denaturation is the change in the 3-dimensional shape **OR** tertiary structure **OR** secondary **OR** quaternary structure of a protein [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ high temperatures break hydrogen bonds [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ protein can no longer function [1 mark]\n\n [3 marks]\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nTemperatures which are too high can lead to a protein becoming **denatured** ($\\checkmark$), which causes a change in the **3-dimensional shape** of the protein ($\\checkmark$). If the shape of a protein is changed, it may no longer be able to carry out its **function** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n 2. $\\ce{ -> }$ pH [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ other factor that disrupts hydrogen bonding such as salts or ethanol [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n**pH** ($\\checkmark$) levels outside the optimum range can lead to a protein becoming denatured."},{"ref":"BI0668","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0668\n\nThe cohesive and adhesive properties of water allow xylem vessels to transport water inside plants.\n\nWhich of the following are correct descriptions of how these properties are related to water transport in plants?\n\n| | Cohesive | Adhesive |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| Polar water molecules are attracted to one another and form hydrogen bonds. | Polar water molecules are attracted to the hydrophilic cell walls in xylem.\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Water molecules are polar, so are able to dissolve ions | Polar water molecules are attracted to one another and form hydrogen bonds. |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Polar water molecules are attracted to one another and form oxygen bonds. | Water molecules are polar, so are able to dissolve ions |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Polar water molecules are attracted to the hydrophilic cell walls in xylem. | Polar water molecules are attracted to one another and form hydrogen bonds. |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWater is cohesive because it is a polar molecule. The oxygen atom in one water molecule forms a hydrogen bond with the hydrogen atom of another water molecule. In xylem vessels water is attracted to the hydrophilic regions of the cell wall and hydrogen bonds are formed.  This an accurate description of the adhesive properties of water in the context of water transport in plants (**Choice A**). These statements are reversed in **Choice D**.\n\nIons dissolve in water, but this is not related to the cohesive (**Choice B**) or adhesive (**Choice C**) properties of water."},{"ref":"BI0755","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0755\n\nWhich statements about embryonic stem cells and specialised cells is/are **not** true?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Embryonic stem cells have higher levels of DNA methylation than differentiated cells |\n    | II. | Specialised cells are more differentiated than embryonic stem cells |\n    | III. | Most specialised cells and embryonic stem cells both contain the entire genome of an organism |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is not true because embryonic stem cells have relatively low levels of DNA methylation, which allows for greater plasticity and potential for differentiation into various cell types. In contrast, differentiated cells often have higher levels of DNA methylation, which contributes to their stable and specialized gene expression patterns. **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Statement II** is true, as specialised cells are more differentiated than embryonic stem cells as they are adapted to particular functions. **Statement III** is true, as the genome of an organism is the complete set of genes in an organism, and most cells in an organism contain the same set of genetic information. **Choices B, C** and **D**  are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0770","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0770\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nEating a healthy, balanced diet is important in order to maintain overall health and a strong immune system. A healthy diet means eating a wide variety of food whilst limiting the consumption of foods which are considered to be less healthy, such as those high in saturated fats.\n\nIn an investigation into the effects of diet on low-density lipoprotein levels (LDL) in the blood, scientists provided two groups of mice with varied diets. Each group was given 5g of food per day, containing a mixture of a standard food mix and either avocado or cheese. The ratios of the food were changed each day and blood LDL levels were recorded. The diets and results are shown in the tables and graph.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI0770A.png\" alt=\"BI0770A\">\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI0770B.png\" alt=\"BI0770B\">\n\n1.\n 1. Describe the impact on blood LDL levels when feeding mice diets supplemented with avocado or cheese. [2 marks]\n\n 2. Explain the differences between results from both groups. [2 marks]\n\n 3. Suggest a reason for the trend shown in LDL levels in group one. [1 mark]\n\n2. State which group is most at risk of heart disease and stroke. Give a reason for your answer. [2 marks]\n\n3.\n 1. Suggest one advantage of using mice in this experiment instead of humans. [1 mark]\n\n 2. State one variable, not mentioned in the question, which would need to be controlled in this experiment in order to achieve valid results. [1 mark]\n\n4. For mice fed with cheese, calculate how many times greater the LDL levels were on day 5 compared to day 2. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n 1. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mice fed with a mass of cheese per day have higher LDL levels than mice fed with a mass of avocado **OR** mice fed with a mass of avocado per day have lower LDL levels than mice fed with a mass of cheese [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as the proportion of cheese **OR** mass of cheese in the diet increases, so does blood LDL levels **OR** increases from 1000 - 1900 mg dm:sup[-3] [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as the proportion **OR** mass of avocado increases in the diet up to 3g day:sup[-1], o blood LDL levels increase **OR** blood LDL rise from about 500 to 1100 mg dm:sup[-3] **BUT** LDL decreases **OR** change from about 1100 - 650 mg dm:sup[-3], from day 3 - 5 [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n From the results, we can see that mice fed with cheese have **higher blood LDL levels** ($\\checkmark$) compared to mice fed with avocado. Furthermore, as the proportion of cheese in the diet **increases, so do blood LDL levels** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2.\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ saturated fats raise levels of LDL in the blood [1 mark]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ cheese is high in saturated fats **BUT** avocado is low in saturated fats [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n $\\hspace{1em}$ The mice fed with cheese have higher blood LDL levels because cheese contains higher levels of **saturated fats** ($\\checkmark$) than avocado and diets with high levels of saturated fats can **increase** blood LDL levels ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 3.\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ fats found in avocado reduce blood LDL levels **OR** avocado is plant based which contains mostly unsaturated fats which can reduce blood LDL levels **OR** avocado is lower in saturated fats than the standard food mix **OR** contains more unsaturated fats than the standard food mix which can help reduce LDL levels [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n $\\hspace{1em}$ Fats found in avocado are mostly **unsaturated**, because it is **plant based**, and unsaturated fats help to **reduce LDL levels** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. group must be stated for **one** mark:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ group 2 [1 mark]\n\n Then any **one**:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ high LDL levels are associated with heart disease and stroke **OR** high LDL levels are the result of a diet high in saturated fats which are associated with heart disease and strokes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ a diet high in saturated fats can lead to atherosclerosis which can result in heart disease **OR** strokes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ LDL can contribute to the formation of blood clots which can block blood flow [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Group 2** ($\\checkmark$) because they have **higher blood LDL levels** which make them more at risk of heart disease and strokes ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 1. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ less ethical considerations [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ easier to control as kept in a lab [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ require less space [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ less resources required OR more economical [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ shorter life span OR reproduce quickly so effects can be observed over several generations in a relatively short period [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Using mice in experiments has significantly **less ethical considerations** than using human subjects ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 2.\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ same type of standard food mix **OR** same environmental conditions **OR** same activity levels **OR** same time LDL levels are measured **OR** same age **OR** mass **OR** species of mouse **OR** same type of cheese **OR** variety of avocado [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n To achieve valid results, the **type of standard food mix** that mice are fed should be kept the same ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 1.58 (allow answers in the range of 1.57 - 1.59) [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI0770ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0770ANSA\">\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Day 5 = 1900\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Day 2 = 1200\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ = 1900/1200\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ = **1.58** ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"BI0775","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0775\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n1\\.\nA student had a small pond in the backyard and wanted to add some fish. The fish would be predators of the pond and feed on the zooplankton (prey) growing in the pond. Zooplankton are small floating or weakly swimming organisms that are dependent on the phytoplankton or algae for their supply of nutrition.\n\nShe wanted to first determine whether Fish A or Fish B would be more suitable, or if both fish could be added to the pond. As a trial, she set up four mesocosms using water from the pond. She monitored the mesocosms and then added the fish to three of the mesocosms as shown. She took samples of water from the different mesocosms and tested them for chlorophyll content at a research lab nearby.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI0775.png\" alt=\"BI0775\">\n\n1. Outline why the mesocosms used would be considered a ‘closed system’. [1 mark]\n\n2. Describe why adding mud or soil to the mesocosms would be advantageous. [1 mark]\n\n3. Outline the reason for including ‘Mesocosm A’ with the experiment. [1 mark]\n\n4. Explain the reason for monitoring chlorophyll content to analyse the effects of the fish as a predator. [1 mark]\n\n5. State another variable that could be monitored to see the effect of adding fish to the mesocosms. [1 mark]\n\nThe following table shows the results obtained for chlorophyll (μg dm:sup[-3]) with the different additions of fish.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI0775B.png\" alt=\"BI0775B\">\n[Source: Adapted from Froneman, P. W. (2022, July 22). *Predator diversity does not contribute to increased prey risk: Evidence from a mesocosm study*. MDPI. Retrieved March 23, 2023, from https://www.mdpi.com/1424-2818/14/8/584, Copyright under CC-BY 4.0]\n\n6. From the results obtained, suggest why the student decided to add both fish A and fish B to the pond. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ energy can enter and leave the mesocosm system,(even if mesocosms are covered) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ matter **OR** organisms, cannot enter or leave the mesocosm system, mesocosms are covered [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The mesocosms are a ‘closed system’ because they are covered, and though energy can enter and leave the system, the **matter is not added or removed from the system** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mud will contain saprotrophs **OR** detritus [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mud will aid decomposition and cycling of nutrients in the mesocosm [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mud provides nutrients for algae [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mud increases habitat complexity so more surfaces for microorganisms to attach [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ more realistic ecosystem- more closely mimics the pond ecosystem [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n If mud is added to the mesocosms, this would also add decomposed material and aid in the **decomposition and cycling of nutrients** ($\\checkmark$) by bacteria and other organisms.\n\n3. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ control for the experiment [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ obtain results without fish to compare results when fish are added [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Mesocosm A serves as a **control for the experiment** ($\\checkmark$) and shows the values for chlorophyll when no fish are added.\n\n4. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chlorophyll concentrations will indicate how much (green) algae is present in the system [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the zooplankton eat the algae, so it is an indirect measure of zooplankton levels [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the more the algae, the lesser the levels of zooplankton [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ It is possible **OR** easier to obtain algae levels by measuring chlorophyll concentrations [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n It is possible to **estimate algae levels** ($\\checkmark$) in the system by measuring chlorophyll concentrations. As the fish eat the zooplankton, algal levels might increase, so it is an indirect measure of the zooplankton present.\n\n5. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the zooplankton levels could be monitored [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the zooplankton could be counted directly (using microscopy and counting of the organisms) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ diversity of zooplankton could be measured [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ water quality **OR** dissolved oxygen levels **OR** pH **OR** nutrient levels **OR** turbidity **OR** nitrate levels **OR** phosphate levels [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **zooplankton populations** ($\\checkmark$) could be monitored directly by counting them.\n\n6. Any **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the prey **OR** zooplankton populations were maintained in the presence of the predator **OR** fish [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ both fish A and fish B did not decrease the zooplankton population significantly when both were added together [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the fish A and fish B added together did not interfere with the stability of the system [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ adding fish A and B together did not significantly affect primary productivity **OR** chlorophyll levels [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **zooplankton populations were maintained** ($\\checkmark$) when fish A and fish B were added together. The addition of **both fish did not decrease** ($\\checkmark$) the zooplankton population. Both fish could survive together in the mesocosms."},{"ref":"BI0780","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0780\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nIn an investigation into the effects of artery occlusion on blood pressure, students set up a simple experiment. A rigid, hard, plastic tube, representing an artery, was fixed to an electric water pump, and the end was sealed with a rubber stopper. A single hole was drilled into the plastic tube and the electric pump was turned on. Students measured the distance the water stream travelled.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI0780A.png\" alt=\"BI0780A\">\n\nIn subsequent trials, students added increasing numbers of equally sized and shaped clay-like masses to the inside of the plastic tube and the distance the water stream travelled was measured again.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI0780B.png\" alt=\"BI0780B\">\n\nResults are shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI0780C.png\" alt=\"BI0780C\">\n\n1.\n 1. Identify the independent variable in this experiment. [1 mark]\n\n 2. State two control variables, not mentioned in the question, that must be kept constant in order to achieve valid results. [1 mark]\n\n2.\n 1. Outline the relationship between the number of clay-like masses and the distance travelled by the water stream. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Explain how the results of this experiment can be used to understand artery occlusion. [2 marks]\n\n3. Based on the results, predict the distance travelled by the water stream if there were no clay-like masses present. [1 mark]\n\n4.\n 1. Using your knowledge of coronary heart disease, suggest a reason for the anomalous result seen when 5 clay-like masses were present. [2 marks]\n\n 2. State a risk factor associated with coronary heart disease. [1 mark]\n\n5. Identify a limitation of using the plastic tube to represent an artery in this experiment. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n 1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ number of clay-like masses [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The independent variable in this experiment is the **number of clay-like masses** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ position of the hole **OR** height of the hole (in relation to the plastic tube) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ size of the hole [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ size **OR** length **OR** diameter of the plastic tube [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ water pressure from the pump [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature of the water [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ stillness of the surrounding air [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n In the experiment, the **position of the hole** ($\\checkmark$) on the plastic pipe should be kept the same, the hole should also be kept the **same size** ($\\checkmark$) between trials.\n\n2.\n 1.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as the number of clay-like masses increases, up until 4 masses, so does the distance the water stream travels [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Up until 4 masses, as the number of clay-like masses **increases**, the distance the water stream travels also **increases** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 2.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the distance the water stream travels is a measure of water pressure **WHICH** represents blood pressure [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as the number of occlusions in an artery increases, the blood pressure increases [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n In this experiment, blood pressure is represented by the distance the **water stream travels** ($\\checkmark$). As the number of clay-like masses, which represent arterial occlusions, increase, so does the distance the water stream travels, which represents **increasing blood pressure** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. *Units must be included to gain the mark*\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 4.07 cm (accept answers in the range of 4.06 - 4.07 cm) [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI0780ANS%20C.png\" alt=\"BI0780ANS C\">\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ If…\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 1 - 2 clay masses = +0.14cm\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 2 - 3 clay masses = +0.15cm\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 3 - 4 clay masses = +0.16cm\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ then…\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 0 - 1 clay masses = either + 0.13 cm\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ so…\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Distance travelled by water for 1 clay mass = 4.20cm\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Distance travelled by water for 0 clay masses = 4.20cm - 0.13cm\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ = **4.07 cm** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4.\n 1.\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ clay-like masses represent fatty deposits **OR** plaques [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fatty deposits **OR** plaques can become dislodged and block **OR** restrict blood flow [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n In atherosclerosis, **fatty deposits** can build up in an artery, which is represented in this experiment by the **clay-like masses** ($\\checkmark$). These deposits, sometimes referred to as plaques, can become dislodged and block or **restrict blood flow** ($\\checkmark$), this could be the reason the distance the water stream travelled was reduced because there was a blockage.\n\n4.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ high LDL cholesterol [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ family history **OR** genetics [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ high blood pressure [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ lack of exercise [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ obesity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ diet high in saturated fats [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ alcohol consumption [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ smoking [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ diet high in salts [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ stress [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n A diet **high in saturated fats** ($\\checkmark$) can lead to high LDL cholesterol levels in the blood which is a risk factor for coronary heart disease.\n\n5. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ plastic tube lacks flexibility **OR** elasticity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ lumen width of plastic tube cannot change [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Arteries have **elastic walls** ($\\checkmark$), but the plastic tube lacks this."},{"ref":"BI0787","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0787\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nA group of students were asked to design an experiment to test the effect of the number of leaves on transpiration rates in plant shoots.\n\nStudents took cuttings of shoots with different numbers of leaves from the same plant and placed the stems in a simplified potometer. A potometer is a piece of apparatus used to measure the rate of water uptake by a plant.\n\nEach of the stems was placed by a window, and the water level was recorded every day for 3 days.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI0787A.png\" alt=\"BI0787A\">\n\nThe results are shown in the table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI0787B.png\" alt=\"BI0787B\">\n\n1. State clearly which is the dependent variable and independent variable in this investigation. [2 marks]\n\n2. Identify the two days between which the plant shoots lost the greatest volume of water. Suggest a reason for this. [2 marks]\n\n3. Calculate, as a percentage, the total water lost from the plant shoot with two leaves by day 3. [1 mark]\n\n4.\n 1. Suggest a reason for the similarity in results between the plant shoot with two leaves and three leaves. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Based on the results of the plant shoot with one leaf and assuming all other factors were controlled, predict the volume of water left in the graduated cylinder on day 2 for a plant shoot with 5 leaves. [1 mark]\n\n5.\n 1. Suggest a way in which the students could improve the reliability of their experiment. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Evaluate the use of the students' methodology in testing the effect of the number of leaves on the transpiration rate of plant shoots. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ number of leaves (independent variable) [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ volume of water in the graduated cylinder /cm:sup[3] (dependent variable) [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The independent variable is **the number of leaves** ($\\checkmark$) on the plant shoot and the dependent variable is the **volume of water in the graduated cylinder** which is measured in **cm:sup[3]** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ day 2 - 3 [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature of the room **OR** environment, was higher **OR** more sunlight **OR** humidity was lower **OR** lights were left on for longer in the lab **OR** window was opened and the wind increased [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The greatest loss of water was seen between **day 2 and 3** ($\\checkmark$). This may have been caused by an **increase** in the surrounding **environmental temperature** ($\\checkmark$) or more sunlight from the window.\n\n3. units are required in order to achieve the mark:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 42.5% [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI0787ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0787ANSA\">\n\n change\n = original - new\n = 20 - 11.5\n = 8.5\n\n % decrease\n = change/original x 100%\n = 8.5/20 x 100%\n = **42.5%** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4.\n 1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ similar TOTAL surface area of leaves **OR** the leaves had the same TOTAL surface area [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ incorrect cutting of the shoot with three leaves resulting in water uptake restriction **OR** a kink in the plastic tubing for the shoot with three leaves restricted water uptake **OR** the apparatus for the shoot with three leaves was not watertight [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The results for the shoots with two and three leaves were very similar because perhaps the leaves were different sizes and despite one having more leaves than the other, the total **surface area** of the leaves was the **same** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.\n 2. units are required in order to achieve the mark:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 10cm:sup[3] [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI0787ANSB.png\" alt=\"BI0787ANSB\">\n\n Water loss by day two for 1 leaf = original - new\n= 20 cm:sup[3] - 18 cm:sup[3]\n= 2 cm:sup[3]\n\n Water loss by day two for 5 leaves = 5 times the volume of 1 leaf\n = 5 x 2 cm:sup[3] = 10 cm:sup[3] ($\\checkmark$)\n\n5.\n 1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ repeat number of trials **OR** repeat at least twice more **OR** repeat to get at least 3 sets of results [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n To increase the reliability of the results you could repeat the trials until you have **at least three sets of results** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5.\n 2. Any **two** of the following (student answer must contain a strength and weakness of the methodology):\n\n **Strengths**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the burette is an accurate measuring device **OR** the burette was used to measure the volume of water lost [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ shoots from the same plant species were used **OR** any other controlled variable mentioned [1 mark]\n\n **Weaknesses**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the rate of transpiration was not calculated [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the surface area of the leaves **OR** the environment conditions were not controlled **OR** any other uncontrolled variable [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n A strength of this methodology is that the burette, which was used to measure the volume of water lost, is an **accurate measuring device** ($\\checkmark$). However, a weakness of this experiment is that some variables were not controlled such as the **surface area of the leaves** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0798","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0798\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe micrograph shows a transverse section through a leaf.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI0798A.png\" alt=\"BI0798A\">\n[Source:Adapted from Berkshire Community College Bioscience Image Library,2018. *Angiosperm Morphology Mesophyll Arrangement in Ligustrum (36198182664).jpg. Wikimedia Commons*. Retrieved 29th January 2023 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Angiosperm_Morphology_Mesophyll_Arrangement_in_Ligustrum_(36198182664).jpg.Copyright under Creative Commons CC0 1.0]\n\n1.\n    1. Identify an adaptation of this leaf for its function.    [1 mark]\n\n    2. Identify the cell organelle that is found in particularly high numbers in the palisade mesophyll cells. [1 mark]\n\nThis micrograph shows a transverse section through a pancreatic acinar cell. Acinar cells are found in the exocrine tissue of the pancreas.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI0798B.png\" alt=\"BI0798B\">\n[Source: Adapted from Howard, L., 2006. *Pancreatic acinar cells.jpg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 29th January 2023 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?search=pancreatic+cells&title=Special:MediaSearch&go=Go&type=image&haslicense=unrestricted. Copyright under Public Domain]\n\n2.\n    1. Explain how the structure of this cell is related to the function of the synthesis of proteins for release from the cell. [2 marks]\n\n    2. State why an electron microscope is needed to view the ultrastructure of the\nacinar cells. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large number of chloroplasts    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large surface area                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ air spaces **OR** spongy mesophyll                        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vascular tissues **OR** veins **OR** vascular bundles                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stomata **OR** guard cells                            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ waxy cuticle                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ columnar cells in top layer **OR** palisade mesophylll    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The leaf has a **large surface area** ($\\checkmark$) for absorption of light.\n\n1.\n    2.\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chloroplasts    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The organelle in greater abundance in the palisade mesophyll cells is **the chloroplast** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleus separated from cytoplasm by membrane **AND** allowing post-transcriptional modification of mRNA    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large numbers of membrane-bound ribosomes **OR** rough endoplasmic reticulum **AND** for translation **OR** synthesis of proteins for secretion    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large amount of rough endoplasmic reticulum **AND** lumen of rough endoplasmic reticulum is where polypeptides are folded and modified to form proteins    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ many vesicles **AND** used to transport proteins from rough endoplasmic reticulum to Golgi apparatus **OR** to carry proteins to plasma **OR** cell membrane for secretion    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Golgi apparatus **AND** receives proteins from rough endoplasmic reticulum and modifies **OR** packages proteins for release from  cell [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The structure of this cell is adapted to its function because it contains a large amount of **rough endoplasmic reticulum**. The **ribosomes** on the rough endoplasmic reticulum are the site of the **synthesis** of the **polypeptides** ($\\checkmark$) and these **polypeptides** are then **folded and modified into functional proteins** in the **lumen** of the **rough endoplasmic reticulum** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electron microscopes have a higher resolution than light microscopes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electron microscopes have a higher magnification than light microscopes    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Electron microscopes have **higher magnification** ($\\checkmark$) and **higher resolution** ($\\checkmark$)  than light microscopes, so they allow more detail to be seen in smaller objects."},{"ref":"BI0801","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0801\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nThe micrograph shows a transverse section through a dicot stem.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI0801A.png\" alt=\"BI0801A\">\n[Source: Adapted from Berkshire Community College Science Image Library, 2014. *Herbaceous Dicot Stem Younger Trifolium (3629423237).jpg*. Retrieved 30th January 2023. Copyright under Creative Commons CC0 1.0]\n\n1. Label xylem tissue with an ‘X’ in the micrograph. [1 mark]\n\n2. State how you identified the xylem tissue.        [1 mark]\n\n3. Explain how the xylem is adapted for its function.\n [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. :br\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ label line from the letter X pointing to the correct area showing xylem tissue    [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI0801ANSB.png\" alt=\"BI0801ANSB\">\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large diameter vessels    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tissue in the vascular bundles closer to the centre of the stem    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ thick lignified walls **OR** thick wall    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Xylem vessels are located in the vascular bundles and have a **large diameter** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ walls are thickened with lignin to withstand pressure **OR** to waterproof them    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vessels are hollow **OR** vessels have no cytoplasm to give space for the water column OR no obstruction to flow of water and minerals [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large diameter **OR** wide lumen, for movement of water and minerals **OR** to reduce resistance to flow of water and minerals        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pits in the walls of the vessels allow water to move between the vessels [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ end walls broken down **OR** largely broken down [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Xylem vessels are non-living and have **no cytoplasm** ($\\checkmark$) which allows space for movement of water and minerals. The walls of xylem vessels are **thickened with lignin** ($\\checkmark$) which allows them to withstand pressure."},{"ref":"BI0814","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0814\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nData was collected on the protein composition of venom samples taken from the Malayan Krait (*B. candidus*) found in three localities; Malaysia, Indonesia and Thailand. All samples were carefully transported and preserved at temperatures below 20 :sup[o]C. The graph shows the number of different types of protein found in each venom sample.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI0814A.png\" alt=\"BI0814A\">\n[Source: Adapted from Rusmili, M., Othman, L., Abidin, SA., Yusof, FA., Ratanabanangkoon, K., Chanhome, L., Hodgson, W., Chaisakul, J. (2019). *Variations in neurotoxicity and proteome profile of Malayan krait (Bungarus candidus) venoms*. PLoS ONE 14(12): e0227122. Retrieved May 14, 2023, from https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0227122.Copyright-free]\n\n1.\n 1. Identify the independent and dependent variables in this investigation. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Venom samples were collected from different snakes, in different localities. Suggest two controlled variables, other than temperature, that would support the collection of comparable samples. [1 mark]\n\nBelow are the results from gel electrophoresis of one group of venom proteins.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI0814B.png\" alt=\"BI0814B\">\n\n2.\n 1. Identify the group of venom proteins shown. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Explain how proteins are separated using gel electrophoresis. [2 marks]\n\nDifferent mice were envenomed with samples from each of the three localities. It was observed that the venom with the lowest neurotoxicity was from the Malayan krait sampled in Malaysia.\n\n3. Suggest why it was important to transport venom samples at temperatures below 20 :sup[o]C. [2 marks]\n\n4. List two advantages of using mice as model organisms for humans [1 mark]\n\nThe venom samples were further analysed, and it was suggested that the Three Finger Toxins group (3FTx) of proteins contributed significantly to the neurotoxicity of the venom. This prompted a closer analysis of the different types of 3FTx proteins found in each venom sample. The data was presented in a graph.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI0814C.png\" alt=\"BI0814C\">\n[Source: Adapted from Rusmili, M., Othman, L., Abidin, SA., Yusof, FA., Ratanabanangkoon, K., Chanhome, L., Hodgson, W., Chaisakul, J. (2019).*Variations in neurotoxicity and proteome profile of Malayan krait (Bungarus candidus) venoms*. PLoS ONE 14(12): e0227122. Retrieved May 14, 2023, from https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0227122.Copyright-free]\n\n5. Based on the data, suggest which type of 3FTx protein is most likely responsible for differences in the levels of toxicity observed between the different venom samples. Justify your answer. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1. **BOTH** of the following (Students must indicate which is the independent and dependent variable):\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the locality of Malayan krait (independent variable) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ protein composition of venom samples (dependent variable) [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The independent variable is the **locality** of the Malayan krait (Malaysia, Thailand, or Indonesia), and the dependent variable is the **protein composition** of the venom sample ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ samples collected from snakes of similar sizes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ samples collected from snakes of similar ages [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ samples collected from snakes of the same sex [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ samples collected using the same equipment **OR** apparatus [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ samples stored using the same methods [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ samples collected at the same time of day [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ samples collected from snakes in a similar feeding condition **OR** samples not collected from snakes who have recently fed [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n In order to collect valid samples from snakes in different localities, you could collect samples from snakes of a **similar size** ($\\checkmark$) or **age** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ PI **OR** PI proteins [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **PI** proteins ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 2.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ gel electrophoresis separates proteins based on charge **OR** size **OR** molecular weight **OR** conformation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ proteins move toward the positive end of the gel [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ smaller proteins move further **OR** larger proteins move less [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Gel electrophoresis separates proteins based on their **size** ($\\checkmark$). Proteins with a smaller molecular weight will travel **further** than proteins with a heavier molecular weight ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ proteins can denature at high temperatures **OR** temperatures above 20 :sup[o]C may denature the proteins [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ denatured proteins may affect the neurotoxicity **OR** affect the potency of venom [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ venom components may include enzymes, so keeping them at a lower temperature could reduce the degradation of venom [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ prevention of bacterial growth **OR** decomposition [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Proteins **denature** ($\\checkmark$) at high temperatures, which could **impact the neurotoxicity** ($\\checkmark$) of the samples which need to be used in the experiments.\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fewer ethical considerations when using mice [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mice are easier to keep in a lab **OR** require less space **OR** fewer resources **OR** are more cost-effective [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mice reproduce quickly [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ share similar physiology to humans **OR** mice have a similar genome to humans [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mice can be genetically modified to study neurotoxins [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Mice are good model organisms to use for humans in research because there are **fewer ethical considerations** when using them; they also require **less space** ($\\checkmark$) and resources to keep them, so they are more cost-effective.\n\n5. **BOTH** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ non-conventional [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** they are the only type of 3FTx proteins that vary amongst the different localities **OR** because the other types of 3FTx proteins are the same amongst the three localities **OR** samples from Malaysia had the lowest neurotoxicity and the Malaysian samples had the lowest level of non-conventional proteins [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI0814ANS.png\" alt=\"BI0814ANS\">\n\n **Non-conventional proteins** ($\\checkmark$) are most likely the cause of the differences in toxicity because they are the **only type of 3FTx** proteins that vary between the **different localities** ($\\checkmark$). In the Malaysian samples with the lowest neurotoxicity, the levels of non-conventional proteins were also the **lowest between all three localities** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1052","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1052\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Palmitic acid is an example of a saturated fatty acid with a 16 carbon chain. Draw the structure of a palmitic acid. $^*$ [3 marks]\n\n2. Testosterone is a steroid hormone, which is carried in the blood. Explain how, when testosterone reaches its target cells, it brings about its effect on the DNA of that cell. [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain how the structure of cellulose and glycogen are related to their functions. [8 marks]\n\n$^*$ *Note: Starting May 2028, this question may be assessed differently due to changes to the Biology guide, including the use of a data booklet.*","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ only single bonds in the carbon chain [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ a carboxyl group on the 1st or 16th carbon [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ no double bonds present (except in carboxyl group) and all carbons in the chain have four bonds [1 mark]\n\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI1052i.png\" alt=\"BI1052i\">\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nonpolar, so diffuses through the cell membrane\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ testosterone binds to a specific intracellular receptor in cytoplasm [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hormone-receptor complex is formed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ conformational change occurs allowing entry into nucleus **OR** complex enters nucleus [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ binds to specific DNA sequences of, **OR** to promoter regions of, target genes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ changes transcriptional activity of target genes\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ newly synthesised mRNA exits nucleus and enters cytoplasm for translation\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ synthesised proteins bring about response [1 mark]\n\n [5 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nWhen testosterone reaches its target cell it **diffuses** through the **cell membrane** ($\\checkmark$) into the cytoplasm of the cell. Once there it attaches to a **specific intracellular receptor** ($\\checkmark$), forming a **hormone-receptor complex** ($\\checkmark$). The conformational change of this complex enables it to **enter the nucleus** ($\\checkmark$) where it **binds** to the **promoter region** of the target genes ($\\checkmark$), bringing about **transcription**, and enabling the production of specific **proteins** to bring about the **response** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following (should include **AT LEAST ONE** for cellulose and **AT LEAST ONE** for glycogen):\n\n *Cellulose:*\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cellulose is made up of the monomer $\\beta$-glucose [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cellulose forms straight chains because the $\\beta$-glucose are oriented alternately upwards and downwards with $\\beta$-1,4 linkages [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the straight chains in cellulose provide structural support [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ $\\beta$-glucose molecules in cellulose form hydrogen bonds between polymers, which form microfibrils [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cellulose microfibrils have a high tensile strength which makes the cell wall strong [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ high tensile strength of cellulose prevents the cell from bursting under osmotic pressure [1 mark]\n\n *Glycogen:*\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycogen is made up of $\\alpha$-glucose molecules [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ $\\alpha$-glucose molecules are oriented the same way, resulting in a curved/twisted structure [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the compact structure of glycogen means a lot can be stored in a small space [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycogen is branched so that glucose can quickly be released for respiration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycogen does not have a fixed size so extra molecules of glucose can be added or taken away as needed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** cellulose and glycogen are insoluble in water due to their sizes so do not affect the osmotic pressure of the cell [1 mark]\n\n [8 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nCellulose is made up of **$\\beta$-glucose** ($\\checkmark$) molecules oriented alternately upwards and downwards which results in a **straight chain** ($\\checkmark$) and the **formation of hydrogen bonds**, contributing to the formation of **microfibrils** ($\\checkmark$). Microfibrils have a **high tensile strength** which contributes to a **strong cell wall** ($\\checkmark$). The **high tensile strength** ($\\checkmark$) of microfibrils prevents the cell from bursting **under osmotic pressure** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n Glycogen is made up of **$\\alpha$-glucose molecules** ($\\checkmark$), all oriented the same way, giving it a **twisted structure** ($\\checkmark$), which means it can be **stored in a small space** ($\\checkmark$). **Glycogen is branched** so that it can quickly be **digested by enzymes** and does not have a **fixed size** ($\\checkmark$), so glucose molecules can easily be **added and taken away** ($\\checkmark$). Both cellulose and glycogen are **insoluble in water** so do not affect the **osmotic pressure** of the cell ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1407","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1407\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe *Allium* genus contains leeks, garlics and chives. The images below show two members of the *Allium* genus that are native to Lithuania and their associated chromosome numbers. Polyploidy events have occurred to produce *Allium oleraeceum*.\n\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI1407A.png\" alt=\"BI1407A\"> | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI1407B.png\" alt=\"BI1407B\"> |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| *Allium angulosum* | *Allium oleraeceum* |\n| 2n = 16 | 4n = 32 |\n\n[Source: Adapted from Lefnaer, S. (2015, July 26). *Category: allium angulosum*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved January 8, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Allium_angulosum_sl3.jpg. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n[Source: Adapted from Leidus, I. (2013, July 7). *File: allium oleraceum - rohulauk.jpg* - wikimedia commons. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved January 8, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:  Allium_oleraceum_-_rohulauk.jpg. Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n1. Suggest how polyploidy may occur in plants.    [1 mark]\n\n2. Outline how polyploidy in a species can lead to speciation.        [2 marks]\n\n3. With reference to plant hormones, explain the directional growth observed in the image of *Allium oleraceum*.        [3 marks]\n\n4. Plants within the *Allium* genus often have bulbs which grow underground. Outline how bulbs can be considered sinks **and** sources in the process of translocation.        [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **one**  of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hybridisation events between closely related plant species **OR** gametes of two closely related plant species fuse        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ extra set of chromosomes formed during DNA replication stage in interphase **OR** cell cycle [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a diploid gamete fuses with a haploid gamete **OR** two diploid gametes fuse together [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ failed spindle formation **OR** non-disjunction **OR** failed separation of chromosomes **IN** meiosis leading to gametes with extra set of chromosomes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chromosomal duplications in cells involved in vegetative **OR** asexual reproduction [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Polyploidy can occur in plants if there are **hybridisation** events between **closely related plant** species ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms with a different number of chromosomes **OR** new tetraploid and original diploid cannot breed together to produce fertile offspring    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ results in reproductive isolation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polyploid plants may be larger **OR** more fertile **OR** better adapted than original populations allowing them to exploit different ecological niches leading to geographical isolation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ once isolated polyploid population diverges genetically from original populations    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The new polyploid organism has a different number of chromosomes than the original plant and so they **cannot breed** together to produce **fertile offspring** ($\\checkmark$). This can result in **reproductive isolation** ($\\checkmark$), which could lead to the formation of a new species.\n\n3. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plant stems **OR** *Allium oleraceum* stems respond to light by growing towards it **OR** plant stems are positively phototropic            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ auxin is produced by actively dividing cells in the tip of the stem **OR** in the meristem     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light causes auxin to accumulate on the shaded side of the stem     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ auxin efflux pumps bring about increased concentration on shaded side    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ this causes cells on the shaded side to elongate                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ auxin causes protons to be pumped from cytoplasm of cells into cell wall lowering pH    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lowering of pH loosens linkages between cellulose fibres so when water enters cell wall stretches and cell elongates    [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    *Allium oleraceum* has a **positive phototropic** response to light ($\\checkmark$).  **Auxin** is released from the **actively dividing cells** of the **meristem** ($\\checkmark$). Light stimulates auxin to **accumulate** on the **shaded** side of the **stem** ($\\checkmark$), so that **cells** on the **shaded side elongate** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **two**  of the following:\\\n    *Answer must include statements supporting bulbs being a sink and supporting bulbs being a source to gain full marks*\n\n    **Bulbs are a source because…**\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when the plant is not actively photosynthesising, it uses sucrose **OR** starch **OR** carbohydrate **OR** nutrients **OR** amino acids stored in bulb        [1 mark]\n\n    **Bulbs are a sink because…**\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when the plant is actively photosynthesising it will provide carbohydrates **OR** glucose **OR** sucrose **OR** starch, for storage in **OR** growth of, bulb    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    At the **beginning** of the **growing** season, the bulb is a **source** because the plant is **not** actively **photosynthesising**, so it needs to use the **sucrose and amino acids stored in the bulbs** for **growth** ($\\checkmark$).   However, during the **middle** of the growing season, when the plant is **actively photosynthesising**, **sucrose** is **transported** from the **leaves to the bulb** to **store** the **excess carbohydrate** and so the bulb becomes a **sink** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1637","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1637\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the structure and function of pneumocytes in the human lung.     [4 marks]\n\n2. Describe the roles of the internal and external intercostal muscles during exhalation.    [4 marks]\n\n3. Explain how the depolarisation of skeletal muscle fibres brings about the shortening of sarcomeres in muscle contraction.         [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **four** of the following:\n\n    ***Type I pneumocytes:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ flattened shape **OR**  thin **OR** minimal cytoplasm **OR** minimal organelles to reduce diffusion distance for gaseous exchange            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large surface area **OR** make up approximately 95% of the surface of alveoli for maximum rate of diffusion for gaseous exchange    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Type II pneumocytes:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ microvilli to increase surface area for secretion of surfactant    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ many Golgi apparatus **OR** extensive endoplasmic reticulum **OR** contain lamellar bodies to produce surfactants            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ surfactant reduces surface tension **OR** to prevent alveolar collapse        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can regenerate **OR** can differentiate into type I pneumocytes    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Type I** pneumocytes have a **flattened shape** with **minimal organelles** to **reduce** the **distance** that **gases** have to **diffuse** between the inside of the alveoli and the blood ($\\checkmark$), and have a **large surface area** to **maximise the rate** of **gaseous exchange** ($\\checkmark$). **Type II** pneumocytes are **cuboidal** with extensive **rough endoplasmic reticulum** and many **Golgi apparatus**. This is because **type II** pneumocytes are adapted to **produce surfactants** ($\\checkmark$) to **reduce** the **surface tension** inside the alveoli ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ intercostal muscles work in antagonistic muscle pairs        [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ internal intercostal muscles contract **WHILE** external intercostal muscles relax          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ribcage is pulled downwards and inwards            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ decreasing the volume of the thorax                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ air particles get pushed close together **OR** pressure inside the thorax increases     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ air moves out of the lungs         [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    During expiration, the **internal** intercostal muscles **contract** while the **external** intercostals **relax** ($\\checkmark$). This action pulls the ribcage **inwards** and **downwards** ($\\checkmark$), **decreasing the volume** ($\\checkmark$) of the thoracic cavity. As the volume decreases, the **pressure** inside the thoracic cavity increases ($\\checkmark$), forcing **air out** of the lungs ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ action potential passes down T-tubules of sarcolemma **OR** nerve fibre membrane    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ calcium ions are released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ calcium ions bind to troponin             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ calcium attachment to troponin causes a movement of tropomyosin, exposing the binding sites on actin        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myosin heads attach to the binding sites on actin **OR** cross-bridges form between actin and myosin        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myosin heads release ADP, and rotate **OR** carry out power stroke, pulling the actin filament inwards            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP attaches to the myosin head and breaks the cross-bridge    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrolysis of ATP moves the myosin head into the cocked **OR** ready position    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ repeat of the process shortens the sarcomere        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the Z lines are pulled closer together                [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    When an action potential passes down the **T-tubules** of the **muscle fibre membrane** ($\\checkmark$), **calcium ions** are released from the **sarcoplasmic reticulum** ($\\checkmark$). **Calcium ions bind** to a protein called **troponin** ($\\checkmark$) in the sarcomere. This causes troponin to **move** another protein, **tropomyosin** ($\\checkmark$), away from the myosin binding sites on actin. **Myosin heads** bind to these **sites** forming a **cross-bridge** ($\\checkmark$). **ADP** on the myosin heads is **released** ($\\checkmark$) and the **heads rotate** through about 45$\\degree$ in a **power stroke** ($\\checkmark$), pulling on the actin ($\\checkmark$). **ATP then attaches** to myosin heads, breaking the cross-bridge ($\\checkmark$). **Hydrolysis of ATP** moves the myosin heads into the **cocked position** ($\\checkmark$). This process repeats until the calcium ions are no longer available. **Repeating** this process **shortens the sarcomeres** ($\\checkmark$) in a myofibril and causes **muscle contraction**."},{"ref":"BI2411","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2411\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nA student wanted to compare the stomatal density on the leaves of two different species of tree. They took impressions of the lower surface of the leaves using clear nail varnish. Below are the student’s diagrams of two of the impressions, examined at a magnification of x400.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/stomatal%20density%20drawing%20species%20A%20and%20B.png\" alt=\"stomatal density drawing species A and B\">\n\nThe length of each side of the fields of view shown above is 250μm.\n1. For species B calculate the number of stomata per mm$^{2}$ and show your working. [2 marks]\n\nThe student then removed four more leaves from each tree and spread a waterproofing agent onto the top surface of each leaf. The leaves were then hung from a piece of string tied between two clamp stands as shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/drying%20leaves%20on%20washing%20line%20chemix.png\" alt=\"drying leaves on washing line chemix\">\n\nThe leaves were then weighed at regular intervals, and their masses were recorded. The percentage change in mass of each leaf was calculated. The mean results for each species are shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/graph%20to%20show%20percentage%20decrease%20in%20mass%20of%20leaves.png\" alt=\"graph to show percentage decrease in mass of leaves\">\n\n2. Explain why the percentage change in mass was recorded rather than just the change in mass. [2 marks]\n\n3. Identify **one** factor, not already mentioned that would need to be controlled in this experiment. [1 mark]\n\n4. Calculate the difference in percentage change in mass between species A and species B after 30 hours. [1 mark]\n\n5. Explain why the greater stomatal density of species B leads to the results shown in the graph. [1 mark]\n\n6. Suggest one reason why the rate of mass lost decreases towards the end of the experiment. [1 mark]\n\n7. When transpiration is not enough to bring about the movement of water through the plant, root pressure is required. Describe how root pressure is generated. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. **both** of the following (*units required to get full marks*):\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ size of field of view = 250μm x 250μm = 0.25mm x 0.25mm = 0.0625mm$^{2}$ **OR** 1mm$^{2}$/0.0625mm$^{2}$ = 16 **OR** number stomata in 1mm$^{2}$ = 12 x 16 [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 192 stomata mm$^{-2}$ [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Number of stomata in field of view = 12\n\n Size of field of view = 250μm x 250μm = 0.25mm x 0.25mm = 0.0625mm$^{2}$\n\n 1mm$^{2}$/0.0625mm$^{2}$ = 16\n\n If there are 12 stomata in 0.0625mm$^{2}$\n\n Number of stomata in 1mm$^{2}$ = **12 x 16** ($\\checkmark$)= **192 mm$^{-2}$** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ leaves may be of different lengths, **OR** widths **OR** sizes **OR** masses [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it provides a standardised measure by expressing the change in proportion of the starting mass [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allows for comparison between the changes in mass [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ percentage change reduces the impact of minor measurement inaccuracies as it is expressed as a ratio [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The change in mass was calculated as a percentage because the leaves may have had **different starting masses** ($\\checkmark$), and percentage change allows for **direct comparisons** ($\\checkmark$) between the two species despite these differences.\n\n3. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ wind speed **OR** direction [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ humidity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ light intensity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ health of leaves **OR** herbivory **OR** cleanliness of bottom surface of leaf [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ age **OR** stage of development, of leaf **OR** plant [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ any other valid controlled variable [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n One variable that would need to be controlled in this investigation is the **temperature** ($\\checkmark$) of the room where the experiment is conducted.\n\n4. *units must be included to gain the mark*:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 28% [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI2411ms.png\" alt=\"BI2411ms\">\n\n Percentage decrease in mass of Species A after 30 hours = 34%\n\n Percentage decrease in mass of Species B after 30 hours = 62%\n\n Difference in percentage decrease in mass = 62% - 34% = **28%** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n5. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ greater rate of evaporation of water **OR** transpiration from the leaves of species B compared to species A [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The greater stomatal density in the lower epidermis of species B leads to a **higher rate of water loss** through **evaporation** ($\\checkmark$), resulting in a greater percentage change in mass.\n\n6. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ decreased water availability inside the leaves **OR** decreased difference in water concentration gradient between inside the leaf and surrounding air [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fewer stomata open **OR** stomatal opening becomes less **OR** stomata not open as wide [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The decrease in mass loss towards the end of the experiment may be due to **fewer stomata being open** ($\\checkmark$) as a response to water stress.\n\n7. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Casparian **OR** suberin strip in endodermal cells blocks apoplast pathway, forcing water to move through symplast pathway (*not needed from May 2028*) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ active transport of mineral ions **OR** salts **OR** potassium ions **OR** nitrate ions **OR** any other suitable named mineral ion into the xylem by root **OR** endodermal cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mineral ions **OR** named mineral ion **OR** salts lower water potential in the xylem so water enters by osmosis from surrounding root cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ positive pressure potential in xylem develops, pushing water upwards [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Endodermal cells actively transport mineral ions into the xylem ($\\checkmark$), so that water follows, generating positive pressure potential that pushes the water upwards ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2413","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2413\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nHaem is the non-protein, iron-binding component found in haemoglobin.  Haem and chlorophyll are similar in structure but chlorophyll contains a magnesium ion instead of iron. The biochemical pathways that make haem and chlorophyll involve enzymes that are highly conserved across many distantly related species.  The diagram shows an amino acid sequence alignment for part of one such enzyme, delta-aminolevulinate dehydratase. Each amino acid is represented by a single-letter code.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/Edit1.png\" alt=\"Edit1\">\n[Source: Alignment created with and adapted from Cobalt with Refseq and Genbank sequences from National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI)[Internet]. *Bethesda (MD): National Library of Medicine (US), National Center for Biotechnology Information; [1988] – [cited 2024]*. Available from: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/]\n\n1. The diagram suggests that certain metabolic pathways are conserved across diverse organisms. Discuss how these conserved pathways provide evidence for LUCA.      [2 marks]\n\n2. Suggest two environmental conditions found in hydrothermal vents that may have facilitated the evolution of early metabolic pathways.        [2 marks]\n\n3. Explain how genomic analysis can be used to estimate the time period in which the last universal common ancestor (LUCA) existed.        [2 marks]\n\n4. Scientists discovered a bacterial enzyme with a significantly different amino acid sequence from its human counterpart but nearly identical function.  Suggest possible reasons why this function is conserved despite sequence divergence.     [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ conserved metabolic pathways **OR** similar enzymes in conserved pathways suggest a shared evolutionary origin **OR** fundamental processes like glycolysis **OR** ATP synthesis **OR** protein translation are found in all life forms    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ highly conserved pathways demonstrate biochemical efficiency favoured in natural selection **OR** strong selective pressure     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the lack of any other widespread metabolic mechanisms suggests competition and extinction of other forms of life    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Many **fundamental processes** like **glycolysis** are **conserved** across **all known life forms** ($\\checkmark$).  This indicates a shared evolutionary origin and supports the idea of LUCA (the last universal common ancestor).  The **lack of other metabolic mechanisms suggests** that they may have existed in early species but were outcompeted and **became extinct** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ heat energy to increase the rate of chemical reactions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ high pressure at thermal vents stabilised complex chemical reactions that could not occur in less extreme environments    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ minerals to serve as catalysts promoting biochemical reactions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mineral-rich vents may have mimicked cell membranes, concentrating molecules and enabling the development of metabolic pathways    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ proton gradients **OR** pH gradient for ATP synthesis via chemiosmosis    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy sources such as hydrogen **OR** methane **OR** sulfide gases as electron donors for metabolic reactions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ highly reduced compounds from vents coming into contact with the ocean’s oxidised compounds provided redox gradients driving early metabolism    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lack of oxygen prevented oxidative damage to early organic molecules **OR** supported anaerobic metabolism development    [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The heat and high-pressure environment of the hydrothermal vents may have enabled the evolution of metabolic pathways as **heat energy increases** the **rate** of **chemical reactions** ($\\checkmark$) and **high pressure stabilises complex chemical reactions** ($\\checkmark$) that may not have been able to occur in less extreme environments.\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phylogenetic trees constructed from genomic data illustrate evolutionary relationships    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ comparing conserved genetic sequences helps infer evolutionary timelines, as more conserved sequences indicate older ancestry    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genomic evidence combined with other evidence **OR** fossils helps establish LUCA’s timefram    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ molecular clocks estimate divergence time by assuming mutations accumulate at a constant rate\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ highly conserved ribosomal RNA **OR** rRNA sequences are useful for estimating divergence events\n [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Phylogenetic trees**, constructed from genomic data, illustrate **evolutionary relationships**, helping estimate when LUCA existed by identifying **divergence points** ($\\checkmark$)  **Molecular clocks** further refine this by comparing **genetic differences** and applying a **known mutation rate** to infer the **timing** of common ancestry ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ proteins with different primary sequences can fold into similar three-dimensional conformations **OR** structures    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ an enzyme's function depends on its 3-D structure    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ variations in sites other than the active site may not affect function    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ convergence in evolution can lead to functionally similar enzymes despite a difference in sequence    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some amino acid substitutions are neutral and do not alter protein folding **OR** stability **OR** active site configuration    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ other structural adaptations **OR** flexible binding sites **OR** other suitable named adaptation allow variations in sequence without affecting overall enzyme function    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Proteins with **different primary sequences** fold into **similar three-dimensional structures** ($\\checkmark$). Since an **enzyme’s function** depends on its **3D structure, especially** the shape of the **active site** ($\\checkmark$), as long as it has a **similar structure**, it should be able to perform a **similar function** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2521","marks":"11","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2521\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nA student is interested in comparing the protein concentration in cow’s milk and oat milk. They conduct a Biuret test (a chemical test that detects the presence of proteins in a sample) and use a spectrophotometer to measure their results.\n\nIf proteins are present in the milk sampled, copper ions in the Biuret reagent will bind to the nitrogen atoms of peptide bonds in the milk and cause a colour change from blue to purple. The intensity of the purple colour is directly proportional to the protein concentration in the sample.\n\nThe Biuret reaction forms a complex between copper ions and peptide bonds in the protein.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI2521a.png\" alt=\"BI2521a\">\n\n1. Label a peptide bond on the complex above. [1 mark]\n\n540 nm is the optimal wavelength for measuring the purple colour formed in the Biuret test, as it corresponds to the absorption peak of the copper-protein complex.\n\nThe student uses bovine serum albumin (BSA) to generate a standard curve that can be used to approximate the concentration of protein in the milk samples.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI2521bfull.png\" alt=\"BI2521bfull\">\n\n2. State the relationship between the BSA protein concentration and absorbance at 540nm.[1 mark]\n\n3. Explain what a higher absorbance value signifies in this experiment. [2 marks]\n\nThe student’s results are shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI2521cifull.png\" alt=\"BI2521cifull\">\n\n4. Determine the protein concentration in cow’s milk and oat milk. [2 marks]\n\n5. Suggest a potential source of error in spectrophotometry. [1 mark]\n\n6. Cow’s milk contains all nine essential amino acids. Define the term essential amino acid. [1 mark]\n\nCasein is the main protein found in cow’s milk, and avenin is the main protein found in oat milk. The student used the Biuret test to investigate the effect of temperature on the denaturation of these proteins by measuring the change in concentration of soluble proteins. The results are shown in the graph below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI2521dfull.png\" alt=\"BI2521dfull\">\n\n7. Compare and contrast the denaturation of casein and avenin milk proteins from the data. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ peptide bond labelled as between the C of the carboxyl group of one amino acid and the N of the amine group of another amino acid [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI2521amsi.png\" alt=\"BI2521amsi\">\n\n2. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ appears to be positive correlation **OR** absorbance increases as protein concentration increases [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n There appears to be a **positive correlation** as **absorbance increases** as protein **concentration increases**\n ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ more copper-protein complexes form **OR** more copper ions bind to protein [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ greater intensity of purple colour **OR** darker purple **OR** darker lilac [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the darker the sample **OR** the more complexes formed the more light absorbed [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n As protein concentration increases, **more copper-protein complexes** form ($\\checkmark$), producing a **darker purple** sample ($\\checkmark$), which absorbs more light.\n\n4. any **two** of the following *(units required for full marks, if correct values given for both but no units only **one** mark can be awarded)*:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cows milk 0.46 mg mL$^{-1}$ (allow answer in range 0.45 – 0.47) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ oat milk 0.27 mg mL$^{-1}$ (allow answer in range 0.26 – 0.28) [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI2521dms.png\" alt=\"BI2521dms\">\n\nThe concentration of protein in cows milk is **0.46 mg mL$^{-1}$** ($\\checkmark$), and in oat milk is **0.27 mg mL$^{-1}$** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ incorrect calibration **OR** not calibrating with water [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ dirty **OR** scratched cuvette [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ air bubbles in sample [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ accept any other reasonable answer\n [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The spectrophotometer must be **calibrated** properly in **between each sample** with distilled wate ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cannot be synthesised by the body in sufficient quantities **OR** not synthesised by the body **OR** must be consumed in the diet as cannot be made by body [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n An amino acid that the **body cannot synthesise** in **sufficient** quantities on its own, so it must be **obtained** through the **diet** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n7. any **three** of the following *(must include **one** similarity and **one** difference to gain full marks)*:\n\n ***Similarities***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the soluble protein content of **BOTH** decreases with increasing temperature [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as temperature increases protein denaturation increases in **BOTH** [1 mark]\n\n ***Differences***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ casein shows larger loss of solubility **OR** greater denaturation upon heating **OR** less steep decline in avenin's solubility **OR** less denaturation of avenin with heating [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ casein denatures steadily between 50$^{o}$C and 90$^{o}$C **WHEREAS** avenin does not show much denaturation until 70$^{o}$C is reached [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ casein soluble protein decreases by 1.0 mg mL$^{-1}$ OR from 2.0 to 1.0 mg mL$^{-1}$ between 50$^{o}$C and 90$^{o}$C **WHEREAS** avenin decreases by 0.3 mg mL$^{-1}$ OR from 0.8 to 0.5 mg mL$^{-1}$ [1 mark]\n\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n In **both** proteins, **denaturation** is **progressive** with **increasing temperature** ($\\checkmark$). However, **casein denatures more rapidly** ($\\checkmark$) and shows a **greater loss** of **soluble protein** mass (1.0 mg mL$^{-1}$) than **avenin** (0.3 mg mL$^{-1}$) ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2650","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI2650\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Draw a labelled diagram of the fluid mosaic model of membrane structure.    [5 marks]\n\n2. Compare and contrast glycoproteins and glycolipids.                [3 marks]\n\n3. Explain how plasma membranes are selectively permeable.            [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **five** of the following *(mark awarded if structure drawn in correct position **AND** labelled correctly):*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phospholipid bilayer with phosphate heads pointing outside and lipid tails pointing inwards        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ peripheral protein shown on the surface        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ integral protein shown within the phospholipid bilayer        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glycoprotein **OR** glycolipid shown with carbohydrate chain attached    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cholesterol in bilayer        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ protein channel across the bilayer        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Hydrophobic **OR** hydrophilic region indicated        [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/2650a.png\" alt=\"2650a\">\n\n2. any **three** of the following *(at least **one** similarity and **one** difference must be given to gain full marks):*\n\n    ***Similarities***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have a carbohydrate chain attached        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are found on the outer surface of the plasma membrane     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** involved in cell recognition or signalling        [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glycoproteins are composed of carbohydrate chain attached to proteins **WHEREAS** glycolipids are composed of carbohydrate chains attached to lipids            [1]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glycoproteins are part of integral **OR** peripheral proteins **WHEREAS** glycolipids are part of the phospholipid bilayer        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glycoproteins more commonly act as receptors **WHEREAS** glycolipids play a greater role in maintaining membrane stability            [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Glycoproteins and glycolipids **both** have a **carbohydrate chain attached** ($\\checkmark$) and are found on the **outer surface** of the plasma membrane ($\\checkmark$). However, **glycoproteins** consist of a **carbohydrate chain** attached to a **protein**, whereas **glycolipids** consist of a carbohydrate chain attached to a **lipid** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrophobic core of the phospholipid bilayer **AND** embedded **OR** transmembrane proteins are key structural features that enable selective absorption    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ non-polar or small uncharged molecules can cross the membrane by simple diffusion but this process is not selective            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the hydrophobic core of the bilayer prevents passage of polar **OR** charged molecules [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ channel proteins allow specific ions **OR** molecules to pass through the membrane [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some channel proteins are gated and can switch between open and closed states    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ voltage-gated channel proteins respond to changes in membrane potential     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ligand-gated channels open in response to binding of a specific molecule    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carrier proteins transport molecules by changing shape to move them across the membrane         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ facilitated diffusion through proteins allows transport of large polar molecules without using energy **OR** passively            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ active transport proteins **OR** protein pumps use ATP to move molecules against the concentration gradient             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ co-transport proteins couple the movement of one substance down its concentration gradient with the movement of another against its gradient     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the selective use of specific transport proteins allows the membrane to control which substances enter or leave the cell        [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The plasma membrane is made of a phospholipid bilayer which has a **hydrophobic core** and that, along with the **embedded proteins** are key in the selective absorption of substances into the cell ($\\checkmark$). **Non-polar** or small **uncharged molecules** can pass through by **simple diffusion**, but this process is **not selective** ($\\checkmark$). The **hydrophobic core blocks ions** and **polar molecules**, so specific transport proteins are needed ($\\checkmark$). **Channel proteins** allow certain **ions** or **molecules** to pass through the membrane ($\\checkmark$). Some of these channels are **gated** and only **open** under **specific conditions**, controlling the movement of substances ($\\checkmark$). **Carrier proteins** move **specific molecules** by **changing shape** as they transport them across the membrane ($\\checkmark$). **Active transport proteins** use **ATP** to pump substances **against** their **concentration gradient**, allowing selective uptake or removal of materials ($\\checkmark$).  **Co-transport proteins** couple the **movement of one** substance **down** its **concentration gradient** with the movement of **another against its gradient** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2816","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI2816\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Explain how xylem vessels are adapted for their role in plants.  [4 marks]\n\n2. Draw an annotated plan of the vascular tissues, cortex and epidermis in a transverse section of a dicotyledonous stem.  [4 marks]\n\n3. Describe the movement of water in plants, from soil to atmosphere.    [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ no cytoplasm **OR** organelles for unobstructed water flow  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell walls thickened with lignin for structural support **OR** prevents collapse under tension  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pits in walls to allow lateral movement of water between adjacent xylem vessels **OR** into surrounding tissues  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cells joined end-to-end with no end walls to form a continuous tube for water transport [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lignin deposited in spiral **OR** ring patterns to maintais flexibility of the vessel **OR** allows plant growth   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ narrow diameter enhances capillary action **OR** water cohesion  [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Xylem vessels are made of dead cells **joined end to end**, forming long **tubes** with **no cytoplasm**, allowing for **unimpeded water flow** ($\\checkmark$). Their **walls** are **thickened** with **lignin**, which **prevents collapse** and **provides support** ($\\checkmark$). The presence of **pits** in the walls enables **water** to **move sideways between vessels** ($\\checkmark$), and **spiral** or **ringed lignin** allows **flexibility** so the stem can bend without breaking ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ xylem correctly positioned and annotated    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phloem correctly positioned and annotated    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cortex correctly positioned and lannotated     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ epidermis correctly positioned and annotated    [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI2816%20B3.2%20PQ%20LA.jpeg.JPEG\" alt=\"BI2816 B3.2 PQ LA.jpeg\">\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water enters root hair cells from the soil by osmosis due to a water potential gradient    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large surface area of root hairs increases rate of water uptake      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water moves across the root cortex via the apoplast **OR** symplast pathways (*no longer needed from May 2028*)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ casparian strip blocks the apoplast pathway, forcing water into the symplast **OR** allowing selective uptake into the xylem (*no longer needed from May 2028*)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water moves up the xylem due to cohesion between water molecules  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adhesion between water molecules and xylem vessel walls helps maintain the column of water  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transpiration at the leaves creates negative pressure that pulls water up the xylem [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water evaporates from the cell walls of mesophyll cells into the air spaces of the leaf     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water vapour diffuses out of the leaf through the stomata down a water potential gradient   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stomata regulate the rate of water loss depending on environmental conditions [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Water **enters root hair cells** from the soil by **osmosis** due to a **water potential gradient** between the **soil** and the **cytoplasm** of the root hair cells ($\\checkmark$). The **large surface area** of **root hairs** **increases** the rate of water **uptake** ($\\checkmark$). Water moves across the root cortex  towards the xylem ($\\checkmark$). Within the xylem, water is **pulled upward** due to **cohesion** between **water molecules** ($\\checkmark$) and **adhesion** to the **walls** of the **xylem vessels** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    **Transpiration** at the leaves creates **negative pressure**, **drawing water upward** ($\\checkmark$). Water **evaporates** from the **surface of mesophyll cell walls** into **air spaces** ($\\checkmark$), and water **vapour** then **diffuses out** of the **stomata** down a **water potential gradient** into the atmosphere ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2836","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2836\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nA student investigates the effect of exercise on cardiovascular function by measuring a volunteer’s blood pressure and heart rate before, during, and after cycling on a stationary bike.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI2836a.png\" alt=\"BI2836a\">\n\n1. Deduce the time at which post-workout recovery appears to begin. Use evidence from the graph to support your answer. [2 marks]\n\n2. Compare and contrast the changes in systolic and diastolic pressure shown in this graph. [2 marks]\n\n3. Identify one reason for this change in systolic pressure during exercise. [1 mark]\n\n4. Heart rate can be measured by manually taking a pulse. State the name of one of the arteries which is usually used for measuring the pulse rate [1 mark]\n\n5. Explain how the valves in the heart are opened and closed during the cardiac cycle. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. **both** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 10-15 minutes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ heart rate **OR** diastolic pressure is elevated until that time and then begins to fall, indicating the exercise has stopped [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The volunteer exercised starting at 0 minutes and continued until **10 minutes** ($\\checkmark$) because the heart rate and pressure **increased** until 10 minutes and **then began to decrease**, which means they stopped exercising ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **two** of the following (*must include **one** similarity and **one** difference to gain full marks*):\n\n ***Similarities***:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** pressures increase during exercise [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** pressures start **AND** finish at baseline levels before exercise [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** pressures peak at the end of exercise [1 mark]\n\n ***Differences***:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ systolic pressure shows a large increase **OR** increases significantly **OR** increases by 60 mmHg **OR** by 50% **OR** by half **WHEREAS** diastolic pressure remains relatively constant **OR** increases slightly **OR** increases by 10 mmHg **OR** by 1/8 [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ systolic pressure starts at a higher pressure THAN diastolic pressure **OR** systolic pressure baseline is ~120 mmHg W**WHEREAS** diastolic pressure baseline is ~80 mmHg [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Both** systolic and diastolic pressure **increase during exercise**, which shows that the cardiovascular system responds to the increased demand for oxygen ($\\checkmark$). However, **systolic** pressure shows a **large increase** of about **60 mmHg**, whereas **diastolic** pressure **increases** only **slightly** by about **10 mmHg** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increase demand for oxygen [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increase cardiac output [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increased stroke volume **OR** heart rate [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ more blood is ejected into arteries during systole **OR** systolic pressure is caused by ventricular contractions [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The systolic pressure increases during exercise because the **heart pumps faster** and with a **higher volume** ($\\checkmark$) of blood with each pump to deliver more oxygen to muscles.\n\n4. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carotid [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ radial [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n One of the arteries used for measuring pulse rate is the **carotid** artery ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ during atrial systole **OR** when atria contract pressure increase causing atrioventricular **OR** AV **OR** tricuspid **OR** bicuspid valves to open [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ during ventricular systole OR when ventricles contract, pressure increases, forcing atrioventricular **OR** AV **OR** tricuspid **OR** bicuspid valves shut [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ during ventricular systole **OR** when ventricles contract **OR** as ventricular pressure increases further, semilunar valves open [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ during diastole, **OR** as ventricles relax, pressure decreases, closing semilunar valves [1 mark]\n\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n During **atrial** systole, the **atria contract**, **increasing** the **pressure** in them, which **forces** the **atrioventricular valves open** ($\\checkmark$). As the blood passes into the ventricles, the atria relax and the **ventricles contract, increasing** the **pressure** inside them. This increase in pressure inside the ventricles forces the **atrioventricular valves shut** ($\\checkmark$) and the **semilunar valves** in the pulmonary artery and aorta **open** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2837","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2837\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n| |  | |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| **Siberian Musk Deer** $\\\\$<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI2837i.jpg\" alt=\"BI2837i\">  $\\\\$ [Source: Porte musc Profil 2.jpg. (2024, August 1). *Wikimedia Commons*. Retrieved 09:30, May 15, 2025 from https://commons. wikimedia.org/w/index .php?title=File: Porte_musc_Profil_2.jpg&oldid=905679436. CC BY] | **Red Howler Monkey** $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI2837ii.jpg\" alt=\"BI2837ii\">  $\\\\$ [Source: *Alouatta seniculus* 5per!spective.jpg. (2024, August 10). *Wikimedia Commons*. Retrieved 09:32, May 15, 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=File: Alouatta_seniculus_5perspective.jpg&oldid=908225132. CC BY] | **Green Monkey** $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI2837iii.jpg\" alt=\"BI2837iii\"> $\\\\$ [Source: *Chlorocebus Global*.jpg. (2024, August 10). *Wikimedia Commons*. Retrieved 09:39, May 15, 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=File: Chlorocebus_Global.jpg&oldid=908309375. CC BY] |\n| **Warthog** $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI2837iv.jpg\" alt=\"BI2837iv\"> $\\\\$ [Source: Profil 8.jpg. (2021, August 22). *Wikimedia Commons*. Retrieved 09:38, May 15, 2025 from https://commons. wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=File: Profil_8.jpg&oldid=584169375. CC BY] | **Coyote** $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI2837v.png\" alt=\"BI2837v\"> $\\\\$ [Source: Coyote Skull 2.png. (2024, August 13). *Wikimedia Commons*. Retrieved 04:29, May 16, 2025 from https://commons.wiki media.org /w/index.php?title=File: Coyote_Skull_2.png&oldid=909107309. CC BY] | **Domestic Cat** $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Form%20%26%20Function/Unassigned/images/BI2837vi.jpg\" alt=\"BI2837vi\"> $\\\\$ [Source: Carnivora - *Vulpes vulpes, Felis domesticus, Martes martes* (skulls).jpg. (2024, February 4). *Wikimedia Commons*. Retrieved 04:36, May 16, 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=File: Carnivora_-_Vulpes_vulpes, _Felis_domesticus, _Martes_martes_(skulls).jpg&oldid=848861083. CC BY] |\n\nScientists can infer the dietary habits of animals by examining their skull structure and dentition.  The images show six different mammal species.\n\n1. Suggest what the differences in incisor shape between the Siberian Musk Deer and the Coyote indicate about their diets.     [2 marks]\n\n2. Other than incisors, describe **one** observable feature in the dentition of the Domestic Cat that suggests a carnivorous diet.       [2 marks]\n\n3. Explain a reason for the differing eye position in the skull of the Domestic Cat and the Siberian Musk Deer.         [1 mark]\n\n4. The Siberian Musk Deer, Green Monkey, and Warthog all possess prominent canine teeth or tusks largely unrelated to diet.  Suggest two possible functions of these canines/tusks which are unrelated to diet.       [2 marks]\n\n5. Besides dentition, describe **one** other source of evidence that palaeontologists can use to reconstruct the diets of extinct animals.         [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. **both** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the deer has broad, sloping incisors **WHILE** the coyote has smaller conical **OR** sharper incisors        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the deer incisors are more suitable for a herbivorous diet **OR** shearing vegetation WHILE the coyote incisors are adapted for grasping **OR** tearing small items, suggesting a carnivorous diet       [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **deer** has incisors that are **broad** and **slope outwards**, while the **coyote's** incisors are **small** and **sharp** ($\\checkmark$).  The **coyote's** incisors make it more adapted to **grabbing, tearing, and eating meat**, while the deer's incisors help the **deer shear vegetation** to eat it ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large, pointed canines for incapacitating prey **OR** piercing **OR** killing prey        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sharp premolars **OR** molars for tearing meat      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reduced **OR** absent flat grinding surfaces **OR** molars; not needed for carnivorous diet     [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The cat's **sharp molars** are adapted for **tearing** and **chewing** raw **meat** ($\\checkmark$) while that would not be possible with **flat molars** that are more suitable for **grinding vegetation** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Domestic Cats are predators, so their eyes are positioned forward, providing depth perception and accuracy of distance, which is important for acting on prey **OR** forward-facing eyes (cat) increase overlap of the visual fields, improving focus on a single target     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  The Siberian Musk Deer is prey, and has side-facing eyes giving them a wide field of view, which is important in detecting predators **OR** side-facing eyes increases peripheral vision, helping them escape threats      [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The Siberian Musk Deer are **prey animals**, so their eyes are on the **side** of the skull, allowing them to **detect predators** ($\\checkmark$).\nThe Domestic Cat is a **predator**, so their eyes are positioned on the **front**, giving them **binocular vision**, which is important for **stalking prey**\n ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fighting **OR** dominance **OR** aggression **OR** defence **OR** intraspecific competition [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sexual selection **OR** mating displays    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ digging **OR** foraging for food      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ territorial displays     [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Canines can be used in **fighting** against other members of the species for **dominance** ($\\checkmark$) or for a **mate** ($\\checkmark$).  The warthog can also use its tusks and canines for **digging**.\n\n5. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fossilised stomach contents **OR** faeces    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ teeth wear patterns **OR** tooth microstructure      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ skeletal structure      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ analysis of nearby fossils      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ isotopic **OR** chemical analysis of teeth enamel  [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    To determine what a fossilised species may have eaten, scientists can look at **fossils** of the **stomach contents** or faeces that are found nearby ($\\checkmark$).\n\n:"}]}]},{"name":"Interaction & Interdependence","subtopics":[{"name":"Cell Respiration","questions":[{"ref":"BI0052","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0052\n\nWhich of the following requires ATP as an immediate source of energy?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | Transmission of a nerve impulse |\n  | II. | Osmosis |\n  | III. | Condensation of a polypeptide |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$  I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$  I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$  II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$  I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nATP from cell respiration is an immediate source of energy for metabolic processes.\n\nNeurons transmit information in the form of electrical impulses (**Statement I**). This transmission can only happen when there is a difference in concentrations of Na:sup[+] and K:sup[+] ions across the axonal membrane of a neuron. Energy in the form of ATP is required for the Na:sup[+]/K:sup[+] pump to restore the ionic balance after the voltage-gated response.\n\nOsmosis (**Statement II**) does not require ATP. It is a passive transport process where water can move through a partially permeable membrane from an area of lower solute concentration to an area of higher solute concentration.\n\nThe condensation reaction of a polypeptide (**Statement III**) is the joining of amino acids together to form a polypeptide. It is an anabolic reaction where ATP is required to join amino acids together to form a peptide bond, and water is produced."},{"ref":"BI0053","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0053\n\nWhich pair of characteristics are correct descriptions of aerobic and anaerobic cellular respiration?\n\n| | Aerobic Respiration | Anaerobic Respiration |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$  | Requires oxygen | Requires oxygen |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$  | Produces a large yield of ATP | Produces a small yield of ATP |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$  | Substrate is glucose only | Substrate is glucose or lipid |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$  | Releases lactic acid | Releases carbon dioxide and water |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCell respiration is the controlled release of energy in the form of adenosine triphosphate (ATP) from organic compounds. Cell respiration occurs either in the presence of oxygen or in the absence of oxygen.  In the presence of oxygen, the process is known as aerobic cell respiration. In the absence of oxygen, the process is known as anaerobic cell respiration (**Choice A**).\n\nAnaerobic cell respiration produces a smaller yield of ATP from glucose which is 2 ATP molecules, compared to aerobic cell respiration’s larger yield of ATP, which is approximately 38 ATP molecules.\n\nThis is because aerobic cell respiration undergoes the complete metabolic pathway of cellular respiration which involves the reaction of organic compounds such as glucose, lipids or proteins (**Choice C**) in the presence of oxygen to produce carbon dioxide, water and a large yield of ATP.\n\nOn the other hand, anaerobic cell respiration only undergoes glycolysis, the first stage of the metabolic pathway of cell respiration. This involves the incomplete breakdown of glucose in the absence of oxygen to produce lactate, which is also known as lactic acid (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0054","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0054\n\nWhich substance is present in the brewing of beer but not found in baked bread?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Carbon dioxide\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Lactic acid\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ethanol\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Water","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because while both processes involve ethanol fermentation by yeast, the baking process involves high temperatures that cause the ethanol to evaporate. Therefore, ethanol remains as a primary constituent of beer but is not found in the final baked bread.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because carbon dioxide is present in both. In beer, it provides carbonation; in bread, it creates the air pockets that remain even after baking.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because lactic acid is a product of a different type of anaerobic respiration (lactic acid fermentation) found in humans and certain bacteria, not the yeast fermentation used in standard brewing or bread-making.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because water is a main ingredient and component of both the final liquid beer and the moisture content within baked bread."},{"ref":"BI0056","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0056\n\nThe diagram shows a respirometer. This device is used to measure the rate of respiration of germinating seeds.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Cell%20Respiration/images/BI0056-1.png\" alt=\"BI0056-1\">\n\nWhich manometer shows the position of the coloured liquid after ten minutes?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A. | | | |B.|\n|<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Cell%20Respiration/images/BI0056A.png\" alt=\"BI0056A\"> | | ||<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Cell%20Respiration/images/BI0056B.png\" alt=\"BI0056B\">\n|C. | | | |D. |\n|<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Cell%20Respiration/images/BI0056C.png\" alt=\"BI0056C\">| | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Cell%20Respiration/images/BI0056D.png\" alt=\"BI0056D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe direction of the movement of the coloured liquid in the manometer would be up the right side where the germinating seed is and down on the left side where the control tube is (**Choice A**). In the tube containing the germinating seed, the living organism undergoes respiration where oxygen is consumed and carbon dioxide and water are produced. The alkali at the bottom of the test tube functions to absorb carbon dioxide.\n\nThe test tube on the left side without the germinating seed acts as a control. There is no oxygen uptake and no carbon dioxide produced because there is no living organism present.\n\nWhen oxygen uptake in the tube containing the germinating seed occurs, the amount of oxygen gas in the tube decreases. This causes the air pressure inside the tube containing the germinating seed to decrease. Inside the manometer, the higher air pressure in the tube without the germinating seed will then push the coloured liquid in the direction of the tube containing the germinating seed. Hence, the coloured liquid on the right side will move up towards the tube containing the germinating seed and the coloured liquid on the left side will move down."},{"ref":"BI0057","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0057\n\nA respirometer is a device that can be used to measure the respiration rate of germinating seeds.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Cell%20Respiration/images/BI0057.png\" alt=\"BI0057\">\n\nIn the experimental set-up shown above, how is the rate of oxygen uptake measured?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The volume of soda-lime is measured.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The displacement of gas in the syringe is measured.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ A reading is taken from an oxygen sensor.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The temperature of the system is measured.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA respirometer is a device that is used to measure the rate of respiration. A basic feature of a respirometer is a sealed container containing the living organism and an alkaline substance such as soda-lime (**Choice A**) or potassium hydroxide to absorb the carbon dioxide gas, which is the product of respiration.\n\nThe rate of respiration is measured by a reduction in the volume of oxygen due to the oxygen consumed by the living organism. The reduction in the volume of oxygen inside the sealed container causes a reduction in pressure inside the container. This causes the syringe to move which is how oxygen consumption is measured (**Choice B**). The rate of respiration can then be calculated based on the amount of oxygen consumed over a period of time.\n\nAn oxygen gas sensor (**Choice C**) can also be used to measure the reduction in the amount of oxygen. However, the experimental setup for this question does not include a sealed bio chamber bottle that is designed to fit the gas sensor probe. Since this experimental setup used a bung to seal the container, removing it to place an oxygen gas sensor probe inside the flask will not provide an accurate measurement of the amount of oxygen consumed.\n\nWhen using a respirometer, the temperature is a controlled variable and should be kept constant (**Choice D**) to avoid changes to the volume of oxygen gas. Temperature also affects the rate of respiration. There should not be more than one independent variable being tested in an experimental setup."},{"ref":"BI0060","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0060\n\nThe graph shows the concentration of oxygen inside a chamber containing yeast cells and substrates. The experimental investigation compared the rate of oxygen consumption by the yeast cells during aerobic respiration using three different substrates: sucrose, fructose, and glucose.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Cell%20Respiration/images/BI0060QBi.png\" alt=\"BI0060QBi\">\n\nWhat can be concluded from the graph?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The hydrolysis of sucrose forms glucose and fructose\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The breakdown of glucose and fructose is much slower than sucrose\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The rate of cellular respiration decreases the more complex the substrate\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The cumulative number of oxygen molecules consumed by the yeast cells increases over time","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe graph shows that oxygen concentration decreases over time, indicating that oxygen is being consumed as the yeast cells perform aerobic respiration with each substrate. Since oxygen is required for aerobic respiration, this depletion aligns with a continuous and cumulative oxygen usage over time. **Choice D** is, therefore, correct.\n\nAlthough it's a biochemical fact that sucrose hydrolyses into glucose and fructose, this specific information cannot be inferred directly from the oxygen consumption data presented in the graph. The graph only shows how much oxygen is used, not the biochemical breakdown of sucrose. **Choice A** is, therefore, incorrect as it cannot be deduced from the graph alone.\n\nThe graph shows that all three substrates lead to similar oxygen consumption rates, with lines of best fit closely following each other. This indicates that metabolic breakdown does not vary significantly among the substrates. **Choice B** is incorrect.\n\nThe statement suggests that the rate of respiration decreases with substrate complexity, which would mean that sucrose, the more complex molecule, should be metabolised more slowly. However, the graph shows that oxygen is consumed slightly faster with sucrose, contradicting the idea that the respiration rate decreases with substrate complexity. Therefore, **Choice C** is also incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0061","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0061\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nCell respiration is a metabolic process that occurs in living organisms.\n\nThe electron micrograph shows a longitudinal section of a budding yeast cell.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Cell%20Respiration/images/BI0061ii.png\" alt=\"BI0061ii\">\n[Source: Adapted from Masur, 1 January 2010, File:S cerevisiae under DIC microscopy.jpg. Retrieved 15 May 2025 from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yeast#/media/File: S_cerevisiae_under_DIC_microscopy.jpg. Copyright Public Domain]\n\n1. ATP is a readily available source of energy for a cell. The primary substrate for the production of ATP is glucose. State **one** more possible substrate other than glucose. [1 mark]\n\n2. Identify the part of the yeast cell labelled A in the image. [1 mark]\n\n3. The ability of yeast to produce carbon dioxide is used in the making of food and beverages. Outline the role of carbon dioxide in the making of bread. [2 marks]\n\n4. Discuss which type of respiration is best adapted to short bursts of high-intensity activities such as weightlifting and sprinting.  [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lipid, **OR** fatty acid, **OR**  glycerol, **OR** protein, **OR** polypeptide, **OR** amino acid, **OR**  DNA, **OR** RNA, **OR** any other verifiable substrate [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n2.\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ cell wall [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide causes the bread to rise **OR** prevents the formation of a flat bread, **OR** causes the dough to increase in volume  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide forms pockets of gas, **OR** holes, in the dough **OR** carbon dioxide causes bread to be spongy, **OR** dough is leavened, **OR** dough is less dense [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Yeast undergoes anaerobic respiration to produce carbon dioxide and ethanol, which are important products needed to make food and beverages. In the making of bread, the carbon dioxide produced by the yeast is trapped inside the dough of the bread as **pockets of gas** ($\\checkmark$). The carbon dioxide gas expands and causes the **bread to rise** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ anaerobic respiration is better suited **BECAUSE** it can proceed in the absence of oxygen [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ during high-intensity exercise, demand for ATP exceeds the amount that can be produced by aerobic respiration per unit time [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ aerobic respiration is better suited **BECAUSE** it produces a high yield of ATP per glucose molecule, which powers muscle contractions     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ anaerobic respiration is less suited to sustained activity **BECAUSE** it produces lactate which can lower blood pH [1 mark]\n[3 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    During the initiation of intense exercise, oxygen is supplied to muscle cells so they can respire aerobically.  **Aerobic respiration produces a high yield of ATP** ($\\checkmark$) which is used to initially power muscle contractions.  However, high-intensity exercise **demands more ATP per unit time**  than aerobic respiration can produce alone ($\\checkmark$).  Therefore **anaerobic respiration is better suited**  to meeting the additional demand during short bursts of  high-intensity exercise because it **does not require oxygen** ($\\checkmark$) and produces ATP."},{"ref":"BI0152","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0152\n\nWhich of the following stages of cellular respiration takes place in the cytoplasm?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A. | | | |B.|\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Cell%20Respiration/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">| | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Cell%20Respiration/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">|\n|C. | | | |D. |\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Cell%20Respiration/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\"> | | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Cell%20Respiration/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe stage that takes place in the cytoplasm is glycolysis where glucose is converted to pyruvate (**Choice D**).\n\n**Choice A** is the Krebs cycle where acetyl CoA is oxidized in the mitochondrial matrix. **Choice B** is the link reaction where pyruvate gets converted to acetyl CoA. The link reaction occurs before the Krebs cycle. **Choice C** is the electron transport chain where most of the ATP is produced and takes place on the cristae of the mitochondria."},{"ref":"BI0153","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0153\n\nIn which step of respiration is pyruvate decarboxylated?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Glycolysis\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Link reaction\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Krebs cycle\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Oxidative phosphorylation","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn glycolysis (**Choice A**), glucose is broken down into pyruvate and this happens in the cytoplasm. Glycolysis is incorrect because pyruvate is only produced at the end of glycolysis and is not converted to acetyl CoA.\n\nPyruvate produced during glycolysis enters the mitochondria where it is decarboxylated and converted to acetyl CoA during the link reaction. (**Choice B**)\n\nThe Link reaction (**Choice B**) is the reaction that bridges glycolysis with the next stage which is the Krebs cycle (**Choice C**). Hence, the Krebs cycle is incorrect because acetyl CoA is the reactant and needs to be produced prior to entering the Krebs cycle.\n\nThe stage after the Krebs cycle is known as oxidative phosphorylation (**Choice D**) which involves the synthesis of ATP."},{"ref":"BI0156","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0156\n\nWhich of the following processes involve decarboxylation?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | Phosphorylation of glucose |\n  | II. | Oxidation of pyruvate to form acetyl CoA |\n  | III. | Oxidation of an acetate group to reform the four-carbon oxaloacetate |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDecarboxylation is a reaction where carbon dioxide is lost. There are a total of three decarboxylation reactions in the stages of aerobic cellular respiration. One of the decarboxylations happens in the link reaction where pyruvate gets oxidised to acetyl CoA (**Statement II**).\n\nThe other decarboxylation reactions happen in the Krebs cycle. The acetyl CoA which is produced in the link reaction then gets oxidised and the 4 carbon oxaloacetate is reformed (**Statement III**). Here, carbon dioxide is removed again.\n\nPhosphorylation of glucose (**Statement I**) takes place before the link reaction, where glucose is converted to pyruvate. Here, no carbon dioxide is removed so it is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0157","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0157\n\nIn chemiosmosis, what is the name of the enzyme which plays an important role in the diffusion of protons into the matrix of the mitochondrion?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cytochrome oxidase\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Coenzyme FAD:sup[+]\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Coenzyme NAD:sup[+]\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ ATP synthase\n\n*Note: Starting May 2028, this question may be assessed differently due to changes to the Biology guide.*","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nChemiosmosis is the last stage of aerobic cellular respiration and is part of oxidative phosphorylation. This is where ATP is synthesized. ATP synthase is the enzyme responsible for the diffusion of protons into the matrix.\n\nBoth coenzyme FAD:sup[+] (**Choice B**)  and NAD:sup[+] (**Choice C**) are known as electron and hydrogen ion carriers which are responsible for electron transport by gaining and losing electrons and hydrogen ions. The movement of electrons in the electron transport chain is a required step in the generation of ATP.\n\nCytochrome oxidase (**Choice A**) is the enzyme that is found in the inner mitochondrial membrane which is responsible for the movement of electrons that would then combine with both oxygen and hydrogen ions to produce water."},{"ref":"BI0158","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0158\n\nThe diagram shows part of the electron transport chain.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Cell%20Respiration/images/BI0158.jpg\" alt=\"BI0158\">\n\nWhat explains the role of oxygen?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Oxygen loses electrons and is oxidised to form water.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ As oxygen is reduced by electrons protons also bind, forming water and maintaining a proton gradient.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Oxygen stops the electron transport for ATP production.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Oxygen allows protons to diffuse into the matrix.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nElectrons are carried by the electron carriers coenzyme NADH (and FADH:sub[2]) from the Krebs cycle, which occurs in the matrix, to the cristae of the mitochondrion, where the electron transport chain takes place. NADH (and FADH:sub[2]) reduce protein complexes on the cristae by adding electrons to them.\n\nOxygen is the terminal electron acceptor. Oxygen gains electrons, and this is known as reduction, not oxidation (**Choice A**).\n\nThe chemical potential energy supplied by the proton gradient helps the protons to diffuse back to the matrix from the intermembrane space via ATP synthase and not through oxygen (**Choice D**).\n\nWhen oxygen is available, a high yield of ATP is produced (**Choice C**). Hence, electron transport only stops in the absence of oxygen."},{"ref":"BI0159","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0159\n\nThe Krebs cycle is one of the stages in aerobic cellular respiration. Which of the following correctly describes the electron carriers?\n\n| | Electron carriers | Oxidation or Reduction |Number of times produced in one cycle |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | NAD:sup[+] | Reduced to NADH | 1 |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | FAD:sup[+]| Oxidized to FADH:sub[2] | 3 |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | NAD:sup[+] | Oxidized to NADH | 3 |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | FAD:sup[+] | Reduced to FADH:sub[2] | 1 |\n\n*Note: Starting May 2028, this question may be assessed differently due to changes to the Biology guide.*","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn one turn of the Krebs cycle, the production of NADH occurs three times and the production of FADH:sub[2] happens once. Both NAD:sup[+] and FAD:sup[+] have to undergo reduction where they gain electrons and hydrogen ions to form NADH and FADH:sub[2]. Oxidation is the process where a substance loses electrons and hydrogen. NAD:sup[+] and FAD:sup[+] are the oxidised forms whereas NADH and FADH:sub[2] are the reduced forms."},{"ref":"BI0160","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0160\n\nIn respiration which process does **not** involve the conversion of ADP to ATP?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Reformation of oxaloacetate from a 5-carbon compound in the Krebs cycle\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Decarboxylation of pyruvate to acetyl-CoA in the link reaction\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Glycolysis\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Rotation of ATP synthase","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nADP combines with inorganic phosphate to form ATP, which is the usable form of energy. This conversion takes place during three stages of cellular respiration; glycolysis, Krebs cycle and oxidative phosphorylation.\n\nIn glycolysis, glucose is converted into two pyruvate molecules (**Choice C**). This  involves a net production of 2 molecules of ATP.\n\nThe next stage after glycolysis is the link reaction  (**Choice B**). Here the decarboxylation of pyruvate happens, producing acetyl-CoA and reduced NADH. This reaction does not involve any conversion of ADP to ATP.\n\nIn part of the Krebs cycle, a 5-carbon compound is decarboxylated and, through a series of reactions, the 4-carbon compound oxaloacetate is reformed (**Choice A**). This involves the production of 1 molecule of ATP.\n\nOn the cristae of mitochondria, ATP synthase rotates (**Choice D**) as protons diffuse from the intermembrane space to the matrix of the mitochondria (or from the thylakoids to the stroma in chloroplasts) and converts ADP to ATP."},{"ref":"BI0161","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0161\n\nHow many times are six-carbon carbohydrate intermediates phosphorylated during one complete glycolysis pathway?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Once\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Twice\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Four times\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ None","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn glycolysis, ATP is first used to phosphorylate glucose, producing glucose-6-phosphate. This molecule is then isomerised to fructose-6-phosphate. A second ATP is then used to add another phosphate group, forming fructose-1,6-bisphosphate, a sugar molecule with two phosphate groups attached. Although ADP undergoes substrate level phosphorylation in the second part of glycolysis, ADP is not a carbohydrate so does not count towards the total. **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are incorrect because they state the wrong number of times."},{"ref":"BI0162","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0162\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThere are four stages in cellular respiration. Some of these stages take place in the mitochondria. The electron micrograph shows an image of a mitochondrion from a mouse embryonic fibroblast (MEF) cell.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Cell%20Respiration/images/BI0162.png\" alt=\"BI01062\">\n[Source: Bohler, et al. (2018, February 19). *Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image of mitochondria of a MEF cell in which the gene OMA1 has been knocked out.* [Photograph]. Wikimedia Retrieved April 02, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: TEM_image_of_mitochondria_in_MEF_OMA1-KO.png#filelinks. Copyright under CC BY]\n\n1. Label the diagram with the letter **A** to indicate the location of ATP synthase. [1 mark]\n\n2. ATP is the energy carrier that provides an immediate source of energy to the cell. State **one** more energy carrier in cellular respiration other than ATP. [1 mark]\n\n3. Outline the roles of oxidation reactions in oxidative phosphorylation. [2 marks]\n\n4. Explain what would happen to the cell if cellular respiration was an uncontrolled release of energy from glucose. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Accept any of the cristae labelled A [1 mark]\n  <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Cell%20Respiration/images/BI0162a.jpg\" alt=\"BI0162a\">\n\n[1 mark]\n\n2. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ NAD:sup[+] [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (FAD:sup[+]) [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ NADH **OR** reduced NAD )**OR**\nFADH:sub[2]) loses electrons to electron carriers in electron transport chain [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ NADH **OR** reduced NAD (**OR** FADH:sub[2]) dissociates from hydrogen OR is oxidised resulting in a proton gradient between the intermembrane space and the matrix [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ NADH **OR** reduced NAD (**OR** FAD **OR** FADH:sub[2]) carries energy to the cristae of the mitochondrion [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen accepts electrons from reduced NAD (**OR** FAD) **OR** accepts electrons from the end of the electron transport chain, forming water [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP is produced from the energy supplied by the proton gradient which is the result of the oxidation of reduced NAD (**OR** FAD) [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n        Oxidative phosphorylation consists of the electron transport chain and chemiosmosis. In the electron transport chain, **NADH is oxidised** ($\\checkmark$) as it **loses electrons to electron carriers** ($\\checkmark$).  The movement of electrons from one electron carrier to the next releases energy which is used to pump hydrogen ions into the intermembrane space, creating a **proton gradient** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organism could not use all the energy at once [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large amounts of energy would be released all at once **OR** not released in small, controlled amounts [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glycolysis **OR** Krebs cycle **OR** anaerobic respiration would not happen [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ heat energy would be released all at once **OR** cell temperature cannot be maintained OR cell temperature would increase [1 mark]\n[3 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n        Cellular respiration is the controlled release of energy from organic compounds to produce ATP.  Without a series of controlled steps, large amounts of energy would be released **all at once** ($\\checkmark$), and this huge amount of energy would be mostly **lost as heat** and not trapped in ATP or electron carriers step-by-step ($\\checkmark$). Thus limiting the **amount of energy that could be used** from a single glucose molecule ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0520","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0520\n\nA mitochondrion is shown in the diagram. Which letter indicates the site where protons accumulate prior to the formation of ATP?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Cell%20Respiration/images/BI0520.png\" alt=\"BI0520\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Skuratovskaia, D., Komar, A., Vulf, M., & Litvinova, L. (2020). *Mitochondrial destiny in type 2 diabetes: the effects of oxidative stress on the dynamics and biogenesis of mitochondria*. PeerJ 8:e9741. Retrieved March 2, 2022, from https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.9741. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe concentration of H:sup[+] ions is the highest in the intermembrane space of the mitochondria (the site where protons build-up to establish the concentration gradient). The intermembrane space is pointed to by **Choice A**.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Cell%20Respiration/images/BI0520A.png\" alt=\"BI0520A\">\n\nThe outer membrane (**Choice B**) separates the interior of the mitochondrion from the rest of the cell. The mitochondrial matrix (**Choice C**) contains enzymes for the Krebs cycle. The inner membrane (**Choice D**) contains the electron transport chain and ATP synthases."},{"ref":"BI0583","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0583\n\nThe graph below shows the changes in the levels of lactate on the skin of athletes during pre-training (rest), training, and recovery.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Cell%20Respiration/images/BI0583QBi.png\" alt=\"BI0583QBi\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Ohkuwa, T., Tsukamoto, K., Yamai, K., Itoh, H., Yamazaki, Y., & Tsuda, T. (2009). *The relationship between exercise intensity and lactate concentration on the skin surface*. International Journal of Biomedical Science: IJBS, 5(1), 23. Retrieved March 27, 2022. From https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3614747/ Copyright © Tetsuo Ohkuwa et al. Licensee Master Publishing Group. Copyright under CC BY]\n\nBased on the graph, which is true?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ There was no lactate during pre-training because there was no cellular respiration.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ As exercise intensity increased, anaerobic respiration increased.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ During periods of exercise, lactate levels remained constant.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ During the recovery periods, aerobic and anaerobic respiration continuously increased.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** – A is wrong because there was approximately 0.39 mmol L$^{-1}$ during rest.\n\n**Choice B** - During the training period, there was an increase in the intensity of exercise which required an increase in ATP synthesis. Since the ATP output of aerobic respiration was inadequate, the body also used anaerobic respiration to produce ATP. The increased rate of anaerobic respiration was evidenced by the increasing levels of lactate on the skin.\n\n**Choice C** – During the training period, lactate levels increased because the intensity of exercise increased.\n\n**Choice D** – During the recovery period, lactate levels decreased because the body was transitioning back to utilising aerobic respiration only."},{"ref":"BI0995","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0995\n\nThere are four main stages of respiration.\n\nIdentify the correct row in the table below that is true of these four stages.\n\n| | Glycolysis | Link Reaction | Krebs Cycle | Electron Transport Chain\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-:\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | mitochondrial matrix | cytoplasm | cristae | mitochondrial matrix |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | cytoplasm | mitochondrial matrix | mitochondrial matrix | cristae|\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | mitochondrial matrix | cristae | cytoplasm | mitochondrial matrix |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | cytoplasm | cytoplasm | mitochondrial matrix| cristae |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nGlycolysis occurs in the cytoplasm of the cell. Then, in aerobic respiration, the product of glycolysis (pyruvate), then is transported into the mitochondrion, where it enters the link reaction. The link reaction takes place in the fluid part of the mitochondrion, the matrix. The product of the link reaction then enters the Krebs cycle, which also occurs in the matrix. The final stage, the electron transport chain, occurs in the membranes of the cristae. **Choice B** is, therefore, correct.\n\n**Choices A**, **C** and **D** are incorrect as they give one or more incorrect locations."},{"ref":"BI0996","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0996\n\nWhich of the following diagrams shows the correct structure of ATP?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Cell%20Respiration/images/BI0996a.png\" alt=\"BI0996a\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct, as ATP is short for adenosine triphosphate. As the name tells us, it has three phosphate groups, represented by the three circles attached to the central ribose sugar. The rectangle represents the adenine.\n\n**Choice A** is a cholesterol molecule, **Choice B** is a glucose molecule, and **Choice D** is a molecule of ADP (adenosine diphosphate), which is the molecule that results when ATP loses its third phosphate molecule, releasing energy."},{"ref":"BI0997","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0997\n\nWhich of the following processes does not require energy from ATP?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sodium-potassium pumps\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Facilitated diffusion\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Endocytosis\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Anabolic reactions","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nFacilitated diffusion is a passive process that allows the movement of molecules across a cell membrane with the help of transport proteins. It does not require energy input (ATP) because molecules move down their concentration gradient. Therefore **Choice B** is correct.\n\nSodium-potassium pumps (**Choice A**) actively transport sodium ions out of cells and potassium ions into cells against their concentration gradients. This process requires energy in the form of ATP.\nEndocytosis (**Choice C**) is an active process by which cells engulf substances by invagination of the cell membrane to form a vesicle around them. This process requires energy in the form of ATP.\nAnabolic reactions (**Choice D**) involve the synthesis of complex molecules from more simple ones. These reactions typically require energy input in the form of ATP."},{"ref":"BI1032","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1032\n\nThe diagram shows a respirometer that is being used to measure the oxygen consumption rate in germinating seeds. In 2 minutes, the ink in the syringe moved by 3 ml.\n\nWhat is the rate of respiration of the germinating seeds in ml s:sup[-1]?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Cell%20Respiration/images/BI1032-SLHL.png\" alt=\"BI1032-SLHL\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 1.5 ml s:sup[-1]\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 0.025 ml s:sup[-1]\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 6 ml s:sup[-1]\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 360 ml s:sup[-1]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe respiration rate is calculated by dividing the change in ink volume (3 ml) by the time (2 minutes = 120 seconds); 3ml ÷ 120s = 0.025 ml s:sup[-1], **Choice B** is correct.\n\nThe answer can be worked out by eliminating incorrect answers. **Choice A** is incorrect because 1.5 mL s⁻¹ is obtained by dividing 3 mL by 2 minutes without converting minutes into seconds. **Choice C** is incorrect, because 6 mL s⁻¹ is far greater than the measured oxygen consumption rate and does not result from the correct calculation. **Choice D** is incorrect because 360 mL s⁻¹ is an unrealistically large value and is not obtained using the correct method."},{"ref":"BI1033","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1033\n\nThe graph shows the total volume of oxygen consumed by a cricket over 1 minute, measured using a respirometer. What is the mean rate of respiration of the cricket?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Cell%20Respiration/images/BI1033.png\" alt=\"BI1033\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 0.01 mm:sup[3] s:sup[-1]\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 0.1 mm:sup[3] s:sup[-1]\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 1 mm:sup[3] s:sup[-1]\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 10 mm:sup[3] s:sup[-1]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe graph is linear, so the respiration rate can be calculated by choosing a point on the y-axis and dividing it by the corresponding point on the x-axis.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Cell%20Respiration/images/BI1033ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI1033ANSA\">\n\nRate of respiration = Total volume of oxygen consumed ÷ time\n\n$\\hspace{8em}$ = 4 mm:sup[3] ÷ 40 s\n\n$\\hspace{8em}$ = 0.1 mm:sup[3] s:sup[-1]\n\n**Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as to get 0.01 mm:sup[3] s:sup[-1], 4 mm:sup[3] would have to be collected over 400 seconds. **Choice C** is incorrect as to get 1 mm:sup[3] s:sup[-1], 4 mm:sup[3] would have to be collected over 4 seconds, and **Choice D** is incorrect as to get 10 mm:sup[3] s:sup[-1], 40 mm:sup[3] would have to be collected over 4 seconds."},{"ref":"BI1036","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1036\n\nWhich statement(s) are correct in explaining why lipids are used as a long-term energy store instead of carbohydrates?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Lipids contain more energy per gramme than carbohydrates |\n    | II. | Lipids are insoluble, whereas carbohydrates are not |\n    | III. | Lipids contain less oxygen than carbohydrates |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I**  is correct, as lipids transfer around twice as much energy as carbohydrates during respiration. **Statement II**  is correct as lipids are insoluble, so they can be stored without affecting the osmotic balance of tissues, whereas carbohydrates are soluble. **Statement III**  is correct, as lipids contain less oxygen than carbohydrates, allowing them to store more energy in more carbon-hydrogen bonds. **Choice D**  is correct.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **C**  are incorrect as they do not include all three statements."},{"ref":"BI1037","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1037\n\nWhich biological molecule is the substrate for glycolysis, the common initial stage of both aerobic and anaerobic cellular respiration?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Protein\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cholesterol\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Carbohydrate\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Water","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCarbohydrates are the only substrate that can be used in glycolysis, **Choice C**  is correct.\n\nProtein can be broken down into amino acids, some of which can enter the Krebs cycle, but they cannot enter glycolysis. **Choice A** is incorrect.  Cholesterol cannot be used directly in glycolysis. **Choice B**  is incorrect. Water is a waste product of aerobic respiration and does not feed into glycolysis. **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1219","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1219\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast anaerobic and aerobic respiration in humans.        [5 marks]\n\n2. Outline the roles of NAD:sup[+] **or** FAD in aerobic respiration.    $^*$ [3 marks]\n\n3. Describe the key biochemical reactions that occur in the mitochondrial matrix during aerobic respiration, excluding the roles of NAD and FAD.            [8 marks]\n\n$^*$ *Note: Starting May 2028, this question may be assessed differently due to changes to the Biology guide.*","markScheme":"1. any **five** of the following: \\\n*Answer must include at least **one** similarity and **one** difference to achieve full marks*\n\n    **Similarities** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** produce ATP    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** use glucose as a substrate **OR** reactant    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** involve glycolysis **OR** the oxidation of glucose **OR** involve the breakdown of one glucose molecule into 2 pyruvates        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are multi-step processes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** use enzymes     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** use NAD as a coenzyme **OR** electron carrier            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** initially take place in the cytoplasm    [1 mark]\n\n    **Differences** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ aerobic respiration requires oxygen **WHEREAS** anaerobic respiration does not    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ aerobic respiration produces 36-38 ATPs **OR** a high number of ATPs **WHEREAS** anaerobic respiration produces 2 ATPs **OR** a low number of ATPs    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the products of aerobic respiration are carbon dioxide and water, **WHEREAS** the product of anaerobic respiration is lactate    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mitochondria are required for aerobic respiration **WHEREAS** they are not for anaerobic respiration        [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Anaerobic and aerobic respiration are similar in that they **both** produce **ATP** ($\\checkmark$), they both use **glucose** as a **substrate** ($\\checkmark$), and both use **enzymes** ($\\checkmark$) to catalyse many reactions. However, aerobic respiration **requires oxygen**, whereas anaerobic respiration **does not** ($\\checkmark$), and aerobic respiration produces **36 ATPs**, whereas anaerobic respiration only produces **2 ATPs** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\n\n    ***NAD***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ NAD:sup[+] acts as electron carrier between metabolic pathways [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ involved  in glycolysis **OR** link reaction **OR** Krebs cycle [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reduced to NADH by accepting electrons and hydrogen ions so oxidation reactions can take place [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reduced NADH donates electrons to electron transport chain **OR** ETC where it is oxidised back to NAD:sup[+] [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ NADH contributes to the formation of ATP **OR** oxidative phosphorylation by generating the proton gradient across the inner mitochondrial membrane [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxidised NAD:sup[+] is used repeatedly in earlier stages **OR** glycolysis **OR**  link reaction **OR** Krebs cycle to accept electrons and continue cellular respiration [1 mark]\n\n    ***FAD***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ FAD is involved in the Krebs cycle    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reduced to FADH:sub[2] by accepting electrons and hydrogen ions, so oxidation reactions can take place[1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ FADH:sub[2] donates electrons to electron transport chain **OR** ETC, where it is oxidised back to FAD [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ FADH:sub[2] contributes to the formation of ATP **OR** oxidative phosphorylation by generating the proton gradient across the inner mitochondrial membrane [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxidised FAD is regenerated and used in the Krebs cycle to continue metabolic reactions    [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n   **NAD:sup[+]** is involved in **glycolysis**, the **link reaction**, and the **Krebs cycle** ($\\checkmark$), where it plays a crucial role in **oxidation reactions** by **accepting electrons** and **hydrogen ions**, **reducing** to **NADH** ($\\checkmark$). The reduced NADH then **donates electrons** to the **electron transport chain (ETC)**, where it is **oxidised** back to **NAD:sup[+]** ($\\checkmark$), providing **electrons** that help **generate** the **proton gradient** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ link reaction **AND** Krebs cycle    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as pyruvate enters matrix it is oxidised **AND** decarboxylated        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ an acetyl group **OR** acetate is formed        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the acetyl group **OR** acetate is combined with coenzyme A **OR** coA, forming acetyl-coenzyme A    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ acetyl-coenzyme A combines with a four-carbon compound **OR** oxaloacetate in the Krebs cycle    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ forming a six-carbon compound **OR** citrate         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ releasing coenzyme A **OR** coA to be reused in the link reaction        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the six-carbon compound **OR** citrate is decarboxylated **AND** oxidised into a five-carbon compound **OR** α-ketoglutarate    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the five-carbon compound **OR** α-ketoglutarate is decarboxylated **AND** oxidised into a four-carbon compound **OR**  succinate    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ through additional steps, oxaloacetate **OR** original four-carbon compound is regenerated        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ one molecule of ATP is generated per turn **OR** two per glucose molecule via substrate-level phosphorylation             [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The two reactions that occur in the mitochondrial matrix as a part of aerobic respiration are the **link reaction** and the **Krebs cycle**($\\checkmark$). The **pyruvate** from glycolysis **enters** the **link reaction** and is **oxidised** and **decarboxylated** ($\\checkmark$) to  form an **acetyl group** ($\\checkmark$). The acetyl group then **combines with coenzyme A** to form **acetyl coenzyme A** ($\\checkmark$). **Acetate** from acetyl coenzyme A acts as a **substrate** for the **Krebs cycle**, where it **combines** with the **four-carbon molecule, oxaloacetate** ($\\checkmark$), forming **citrate, a six-carbon molecule** ($\\checkmark$), This is then **decarboxylated and oxidised** into  **α-ketoglutarate**, a **five-carbon molecule** ($\\checkmark$), which is further **oxidised and decarboxylated**, to form **succinate, a four-carbon molecule**  ($\\checkmark$), before **oxaloacetate** is **reformed** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1225","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1225\n\nThe graph shows the results from a study on the effect of temperature on the respiration rate of germinating seeds, using a respirometer, which measures the volume of carbon dioxide produced over time. What can be concluded from the results?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Cell%20Respiration/images/BI1225i.png\" alt=\"BI1225i\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The rate of respiration decreases as the concentration of glucose decreases\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The rate of respiration is fastest between 0 and 10 °C\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ As temperature increases, the rate of respiration also increases\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Once the temperature is above 40 °C, the rate of respiration decreases","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nOnce the temperature is above 40°C the graph begins to fall, indicating that the rate of respiration is beginning to decrease. **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect, as the graph does not refer to glucose concentration. **Choice B** is incorrect as the highest rate of respiration is at 40°C, where the graph reaches its peak. **Choice C** is incorrect, as this statement is only true between 0 and 40°C, after 40°C the rate of respiration falls."},{"ref":"BI1228","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1228\n\nDuring which step of respiration is acetyl-CoA formed?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Krebs cycle\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Glycolysis\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Link reaction\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Oxidative phosphorylation","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDuring the link reaction pyruvate is decarboxylated and oxidised to form an acetyl group which is then combined with acetyl coenzyme A to form acetyl-CoA. **Choice C**  is correct.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **D**  are incorrect because they do not involve acetyl-CoA."},{"ref":"BI1300","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1300\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Describe how the structure of ATP is related to its function. [5 marks]\n\n2. Explain how temperature affects the rate of respiration, with reference to enzymes.    [4 marks]\n\n3. Compare and contrast anaerobic respiration in mice (*Mus musculus*) and yeast.    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **five** of the following (*answer must link structure to function to gain full marks*):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleotide made up of adenine **OR** nitrogenous base, **AND** a ribose **OR** pentose sugar, **AND** three phosphate groups    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cannot pass freely through the plasma membrane **SO** is retained inside the cell where it is needed    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrolysis of the terminal phosphate group releases energy (approximately 30 kJ per mole) **SO** energy is available immediately when needed    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ small relative to macromolecules **AND** soluble **SO** can diffuse freely within the cytoplasm to where energy is needed    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ soluble **OR** highly reactive **SO** can interact readily with other molecules **OR** participate in chemical reactions **OR** can easily be utilised by cells **OR** can react with other metabolites    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can lose a phosphate to make ADP, **AND** ADP can be phosphorylated to make ATP    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrolysis and phosphorylation means it is reusable **OR** recycled    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can transfer a phosphate group to other molecules (phosphorylation) **SO** they become more reactive **OR** less stable    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    ATP is a **nucleotide** consisting of a nitrogenous **base (adenine)**, a **ribose** and **three phosphate groups** ($\\checkmark$). It can easily **lose a phosphate group** via **hydrolysis** to **release energy** ($\\checkmark$), and **ADP** can be **phosphorylated** back to **ATP**, making it **reusable** ($\\checkmark$). The **polar** phosphate groups make ATP **soluble** in water, allowing it to **diffuse freely** ($\\checkmark$) within cells and enabling it to readily **participate in chemical reactions** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as temperature rises, the rate of respiration increases as enzymes **AND** substrates have more kinetic energy **OR** at lower temperatures, the rate of respiration is slower as enzymes **AND** substrates have less kinetic energy    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ so there is a higher chance of successful collisions between enzymes and substrates    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ until an optimum temperature is reached, at which the enzyme exhibits its maximum activity    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at optimum temperature, the kinetic energy of the molecules is sufficiently high to promote a high level of enzyme-substrate interaction **BUT** not so high to cause extensive denaturation of enzyme    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when the temperature is higher than the optimum, the rate of respiration begins to decrease as enzymes involved become denatured **OR** the active site changes shape so the substrate no longer fits    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    At **lower temperatures**, the rate of respiration is **slower** because the **enzymes** involved have **less kinetic energy** ($\\checkmark$), so there is a **lower chance** of successful **collisions** with **substrates** ($\\checkmark$). As **temperatures rise**, the **rate** of respiration **increases**, as the enzymes involved have **more kinetic energy** ($\\checkmark$) until an **optimum** temperature is reached, where the rate of respiration is **highest** ($\\checkmark$). **Above** this **optimum** temperature, the **respiration rate decreases** as the **enzymes** start to become **denatured** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** similarity and **one** difference to gain full marks*):\n\n    *Differences*\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the product of anaerobic respiration in mice is lactic acid **OR** lactate **WHEREAS**, in yeast, it is ethanol and carbon dioxide    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ yeast is better adapted to long-term anaerobic respiration **WHEREAS** mouse cells can perform anaerobic respiration but are limited in endurance    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ yeast converts pyruvate to ethanal, releasing carbon dioxide, then ethanal to ethanol **WHEREAS** mice convert pyruvate directly to lactate with no carbon dioxide released    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lactate can be converted back to pyruvate **OR** glucose when oxygen is available **WHEREAS** ethanol and carbon dioxide are released from yeast cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ products of yeast are used commercially, ethanol in brewing **AND** carbon dioxide in baking, **WHEREAS** lactate in mice is produced in muscles during vigorous activity    [1 mark]\n\n    *Similarities* \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** use glucose as a substrate    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** occur in the cytoplasm    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** occur in the absence **OR** shortage of oxygen    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the products of anaerobic respiration in **BOTH** mice and yeast can be toxic if they build up    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can occur alongside aerobic respiration    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** produce a small amount of ATP **OR** 2 ATPs    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ anaerobic respiration in **BOTH** mice and yeast allows ATP production to continue when oxygen is insufficient    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** begin with glycolysis, in which glucose is converted to pyruvate    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in **BOTH**, reduced NAD is oxidised to regenerate NAD for glycolysis    [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The product of anaerobic respiration in mice is **lactate**, whereas in yeast, it is **ethanol and carbon dioxide** ($\\checkmark$). However, in both the anaerobic respiration of mice and yeast, **glucose** is used as a **substrate** ($\\checkmark$), it occurs in the **cytoplasm** ($\\checkmark$), produces a **small** amount of **ATP** ($\\checkmark$) and in the **absence of oxygen** ($\\checkmark$). Also, in both mice and yeast, if the **products** of anaerobic respiration **build up**, they can be **toxic** to the cell ($\\checkmark$), and anaerobic respiration can occur **alongside aerobic respiration** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1426","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1426\n\nWhat results from the hydrolysis of ATP?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$  The products ADP and water\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$  The products oxygen, hydrogen, phosphorus, carbon and nitrogen\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$  The product pyruvate with the release of energy\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$  The products ADP and inorganic phosphate with the release of energy","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCellular respiration is the process by which chemical energy in organic compounds is transferred to chemical energy in adenosine triphosphate (ATP). ATP consists of the nitrogenous base adenine, a ribose sugar and three phosphate groups. These three components contain the elements oxygen, hydrogen, phosphorus, carbon and nitrogen. Even though ATP is made up of these five elements, they are not products of hydrolysis; therefore, **Choice B** is not correct.\n\nATP allows energy to be readily available for cellular processes. When ATP undergoes hydrolysis the energy which is stored in the third phosphate bond of ATP is released. This hydrolysis reaction leads to the formation of adenosine diphosphate (ADP) and inorganic phosphate. **Choice D** is, therefore, the correct answer.\n\nHydrolysis reactions require water molecules, they do not result in water molecules being formed, therefore **Choice A** is incorrect. Pyruvate is the product of glycolysis, not the product of the hydrolysis of ATP, therefore **Choice C** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1567","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1567\n\nWhy is yeast often included in bread recipes?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The production of ethanol keeps the bread from drying out.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The production of carbon dioxide makes the bread rise.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The production of glucose makes the bread sweeter.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The production of lactic acid makes the bread soft by denaturing proteins.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because the products of anaerobic respiration in yeast are ethanol and carbon dioxide. The ethanol evaporates during baking while the carbon dioxide creates bubbles, causing the bread to rise.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because ethanol evaporates during the baking process and does not prevent drying. **Choice C** is incorrect because glucose is a substrate of respiration, not a product. **Choice D** is incorrect because lactic acid is a product of anaerobic respiration in humans, not yeast."},{"ref":"BI1603","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1603\n\nMetabolic reactions can be exothermic, releasing energy to their surroundings, or endothermic, requiring an energy supply.\n\nWhich one of the following is an endothermic reaction?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The conversion of triose phosphate to pyruvate\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The conversion of glucose to fructose-1,6-bisphosphate\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The oxidation of glyceraldehyde by NAD:sup[+], in glycolysis\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The decarboxylation of citrate","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe conversion of glucose to fructose-1,6-bisphosphate occurs during glycolysis and involves two phosphorylation steps that require the input of ATP. Glucose is first converted to glucose-6-phosphate and then to fructose-6-phosphate before being phosphorylated again to form fructose-1,6-bisphosphate. These steps require energy in the form of ATP; **Choice B** is, therefore, correct.\n\nThe conversion of triose phosphate to pyruvate also occurs during glycolysis. Each triose phosphate molecule undergoes a series of enzyme-catalysed reactions, producing ATP and NADH while being oxidised to form pyruvate. This reaction is exothermic so **Choice A** is not the correct answer to this question.\n\nThe oxidation of glyceraldehyde by NAD+, in glycolysis is exothermic. This step also results in the reduction of NAD+ to NADH, which stores some of the energy released during the reaction; **Choice C** is not the correct answer to this question.\n\nThe decarboxylation of citrate occurs in the Krebs cycle, where citrate is converted to a 5-carbon molecule, producing carbon dioxide and forming NADH by transferring high-energy electrons to $\\ce{NAD^+}$. The breaking of bonds and the formation of NADH release more energy than is required, resulting in a net release of energy. This reaction is exothermic, so **Choice D** is not the correct answer to this question."},{"ref":"BI1636","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1636\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe diagram shows the structure responsible for ATP production toward the end of aerobic respiration in humans.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Cell%20Respiration/images/BI1636.png\" alt=\"BI1636\">\n\n1. State where the ATP synthase, represented in the diagram, is located within a cell. [1 mark]\n\n2. Describe how a proton gradient is established in the electron transport chain. [2 marks]\n\n3. Outline what happens to protons after they have been used to generate ATP. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ inner mitochondrial membrane **OR** cristae of mitochondria [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The ATP synthase involved in aerobic respiration is found in the **inner mitochondrial membrane** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ electrons from reduced NAD/FAD transferred to protein carriers in the electron transport chain [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as electrons are transferred between carriers, energy is released [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carriers act as proton pumps [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ protons **OR** hydrogen ions **OR** $\\ce{H^+}$ transferred from matrix to intermembrane space [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ using active transport **OR** against the concentration gradient [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ high concentration of hydrogen ions **OR** protons maintained in intermembrane space **OR** electrochemical gradient established between intermembrane space and matrix [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Electrons** from **reduced NAD** are **transferred between electron carriers** in the inner **membrane, releasing energy** ($\\checkmark$). The energy is used for the **active transport** ($\\checkmark$) of **protons** from the mitochondrial **matrix** to the **intermembrane space** ($\\checkmark$), generating a proton gradient.\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ protons **OR** hydrogen ions **OR** $\\ce{H^+}$ transferred back into **OR** accumulate in the matrix [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen functions as the final electron acceptor [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen binds with hydrogen ions **OR** protons **OR** $\\ce{H^+}$ and electrons, forming water [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Hydrogen ions** that have passed through ATP synthase **build up** in the mitochondrial **matrix** ($\\checkmark$). **Oxygen**, the **final electron acceptor** ($\\checkmark$) of the electron transport chain, **binds to hydrogen ions** and **electrons**, **forming water** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2034","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2034\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe image shows a respirometer that could be used to measure the rate of respiration in seeds. The experiment was used to compare the respiration rates at different temperatures in ml min$^{-1}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Cell%20Respiration/images/BI2034.png\" alt=\"BI2034\">\n\n1. Explain the purpose of the soda lime in this experiment. [2 marks]\n\n2. Outline how the rate of oxygen uptake of seeds can be calculated using the experimental set up seen in the diagram if the experiment were run for 5 minutes. [3 marks]\n\n3. Suggest one variable that should be controlled in this study. [1 mark]\n\nThe graph shows how much oxygen is consumed by germinating and non-germinating seeds at two different temperatures, over 5 minutes.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Cell%20Respiration/images/BI2034b.png\" alt=\"BI2034b\">\n\n4. Suggest reasons for the trends seen in the graph. [2 marks]\n\n5. Calculate the percentage increase in oxygen consumption by germinating seeds, over five minutes, due to the 10 $\\degree$C increase in temperature. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to absorb the carbon dioxide **OR** $\\ce{CO_2}$ produced by the seeds [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allows volume of oxygen used in respiration can be indirectly measured **OR** any volume change in the respirometer reflects oxygen consumption [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The purpose of the soda lime is to **absorb the carbon dioxide** produced by the respiring seeds ($\\checkmark$), so that the **oxygen uptake** is the only variable causing the **syringe to move** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ record the initial volume of oxygen in the syringe [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ after five minutes, record the new volume of oxygen in the syringe [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ subtract the initial volume of oxygen from the final volume of oxygen to determine volume of oxygen uptake **OR** oxygen consumption [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ divide volume of oxygen consumed in five minutes by five to calculate the rate [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Rate of oxygen uptake = $\\dfrac{\\ce{Initial vol (ml) - final volume (ml)}}{\\ce{5\\ min}}$\n\n The **initial volume of the oxygen** in the syringe should be recorded ($\\checkmark$). After the experiment has run for **five minutes** the **new volume of oxygen** in the syringe should be recorded ($\\checkmark$). The **initial volume** of oxygen should be **subtracted** from the **final volume** of oxygen to calculate the volume of oxygen taken up in five minutes ($\\checkmark$). This **volume** is then **divided by five** to give the oxygen consumption rate in **volume per minute** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ number of seeds [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mass of soda lime [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ time the experiment is run for [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ light intensity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ seed species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ age of seeds [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ humidity levels [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen availability [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ any other suitable variable [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Number of seeds** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ germinating seeds consume more oxygen than non-germinating seeds indicating they are more metabolically active **OR** are growing **OR** undergoing cell division **OR** non-germinating seeds consume less oxygen than germinating seeds indicating minimal metabolic activity **OR** they are dormant and require less oxygen for respiration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ both germinating and non-germinating seeds consume more oxygen at 30℃ compared to 20$\\degree$C indicating respiration is occurring at a higher rate due to increased enzyme activity **OR** indicating that 30℃ is closer to optimum temperature of respiratory enzymes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the rate of oxygen consumption for each condition is consistent **OR** constant over time indicating the energy needs **OR** respiration rate of seeds is stable throughout the duration of the study [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Germinating seeds** consume **more oxygen** than non-germinating seeds, **indicating** they are **more metabolically active**, growing, or undergoing cell division, while **non-germinating** seeds consume less oxygen, indicating **minimal metabolic activity** or dormancy ($\\checkmark$). **Both** germinating and non-germinating seeds consume **more oxygen** at **30**$\\degree$**C** compared to 20$\\degree$C, suggesting **respiration** occurs at a **higher rate** due to **increased enzyme activity** or that **30**$\\degree$**C** is **closer** to the **optimum temperature** for respiratory enzymes ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ $\\dfrac{\\ce{Final - Initial}}{\\ce{Initial}} \\times 100\\%$ **OR** $\\dfrac{\\ce{12\\ ml - 10\\ ml}}{\\ce{10\\ ml}} \\times 100\\%$ [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 20 (allow in range 18 - 22, % not required for mark) [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Cell%20Respiration/images/AnsExp.png\" alt=\"AnsExp\">\n\n Percentage increase = $\\dfrac{\\ce{Final - Initial}}{\\ce{Initial}} \\times 100\\%$\n\n percentage increase in oxygen consumption from 20$\\degree$C to 30$\\degree$C = $\\dfrac{\\textbf{12\\ ml - 10\\ ml}}{\\textbf{10\\ ml}} \\times$ **100**% ($\\checkmark$)\n\n $\\hspace{27em}$ = $\\dfrac{\\ce{2\\ ml}}{\\ce{10\\ ml}} \\times 100\\%$\\\n $\\hspace{27em}$ = 0.2 $\\times$ 100 %\\\n $\\hspace{27em}$ = **20** % ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"BI2333","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2333\n\nThe table shows data that was collected for respiring yeast. Which value is correct for the rate of cellular respiration between 30 and 60 seconds?\n\n| Time / s | Volume of carbon dioxide / ppm |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| 0 | 650 |\n| 30 | 910 |\n| 60 | 1390 |\n| 90 | 1670 |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 16 ppm $\\ce{s^{-1}}$ .\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 32 ppm $\\ce{s^{-1}}$.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 480 ppm $\\ce{s^{-1}}$ .\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 16 ppm $\\ce{min^{-1}}$ .","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because rate equals volume change divided by the time taken for change, \\\nRate of carbon dioxide production = $\\dfrac{\\ce{Volume change (ppm)}}{\\ce{Time for change (s)}} = \\dfrac{\\ce{1390-910ppm}}{\\ce{30s}} = \\dfrac{\\ce{480 ppm}}{\\ce{30 s}}= 16$ ppm $\\ce{s^{-1}}$.\n\n**Choices B, C** and **D** are incorrect because the volume change is 480, and when this is divided by 30, it does not give one of these answers."},{"ref":"BI2387","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2387\n\nWhich term best describes the chemical conversion step undergone by pyruvate prior to the Krebs cycle?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Reductive carboxylation\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Reductive decarboxylation\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Oxidative carboxylation\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Oxidative decarboxylation","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because the conversion of pyruvate to acetyl-CoA in the link reaction involves both oxidation (removal of electrons) and decarboxylation (loss of a carbon atom as $\\ce{CO_2}$). This step aligns with the overall oxidative nature of cellular respiration, where glucose and its intermediates are oxidised progressively. Pyruvate (3 carbons) is oxidised and decarboxylated to form acetate (2 carbons), which then combines with coenzyme A to produce acetyl-CoA.\n\n**Choice A** and **B** are incorrect because the process is oxidative, not reductive. While the reduction of $\\ce{NAD^+}$ to NADH occurs alongside pyruvate oxidation (as part of the redox process), pyruvate itself undergoes oxidation, not reduction.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because pyruvate is decarboxylated, meaning it loses a carbon atom as $\\ce{CO_2}$. Carboxylation refers to the addition of a carbon atom, which does not occur in this step."},{"ref":"BI2664","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2664\n\nThe graph below shows oxygen consumption, in arbitrary units, over time in two tissue types (X and Y):\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Cell%20Respiration/images/2664QB.png\" alt=\"2664QB\">\n\nWhich conclusion is best supported by the data?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Tissue Y performs anaerobic respiration only.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Tissue X is not undergoing respiration.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Tissue X likely has more mitochondria than Tissue Y.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Tissue Y is producing more ATP overall.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Tissue X** shows a higher rate of oxygen consumption over time, which indicates a greater reliance on aerobic respiration. Since aerobic respiration occurs in the mitochondria, it is likely that **Tissue X** contains more mitochondria than **Tissue Y**.\\\n**Choice C** is therefore correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because anaerobic respiration occurs in the absence of oxygen, yet **Tissue Y** is still consuming oxygen—just at a lower rate—so it is not relying solely on anaerobic respiration.\\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because **Tissue X** is clearly undergoing respiration, as shown by its high oxygen consumption.\\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because aerobic respiration (**Tissue X**) yields significantly more ATP per glucose molecule than anaerobic respiration (**Tissue Y**), so **Tissue X** is likely producing more ATP overall."}]},{"name":"Chemical Signalling","questions":[{"ref":"BI0485","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0485\n\nBelow is a diagram showing feedback mechanisms, labelled I, II and III, involving sex hormones.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Chemical%20Signalling/images/BI0485.png\" alt=\"BI0485\">\n\nWhich row in the table correctly names the type of feedback shown by I, II and III?\n\n| | I. | II. | III. |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| Negative | Positive | Negative |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Positive | Positive | Negative |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Positive | Negative | Negative |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Negative | Positive | Positive |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nFSH brings about follicle growth in the ovaries at the beginning of the menstrual cycle. The increase in follicle growth leads to increased levels of oestradiol (oestrogen). Normally, oestradiol exerts negative feedback on the anterior pituitary and hypothalamus to reduce the release of FSH and LH. However, when oestradiol levels reach a high threshold in the middle of the cycle, just before ovulation, the feedback on the anterior pituitary and hypothalamus becomes positive, leading to a surge in LH (and to some extent FSH). This LH surge triggers ovulation. Feedback loop **I** therefore shows positive feedback.\n\nAfter ovulation, the ruptured follicle becomes the corpus luteum, an endocrine gland in its own right. The corpus luteum secretes progesterone and some oestradiol. These hormones exert negative feedback on the anterior pituitary and hypothalamus, leading to decreased secretion of GnRH, LH, and FSH. The reduction in LH and FSH prevents the maturation of additional follicles during the luteal phase and is a classic example of a negative feedback loop. Therefore **II** and **III** are both examples of negative feedback mechanisms\n\n**Choice C** is, therefore, correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because I is positive feedback, and II is negative feedback.\n**Choice B** is incorrect as II is negative feedback.\n**Choice D** is incorrect as the types of feedback shown for I, II, and III are all incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0634","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0634\n\nWhat mode of transport do neurotransmitters use to travel across the synaptic cleft?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Exocytosis\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Simple diffusion\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Active transport\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Facilitated diffusion","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is incorrect as it describes how neurotransmitters are released from the presynaptic cleft. **Choice C** is incorrect as neurotransmitters do not require energy to move across the synaptic cleft. **Choice D** is incorrect as channels are not present in the synaptic cleft.  They are only present in the membranes of neurons."},{"ref":"BI0839","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0839\n\nWhich of the following is only able to trigger a response in a cell by binding to a receptor in the plasma membrane?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Insulin\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Testosterone\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Oestradiol\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Progesterone","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because insulin is a peptide hormone, which is polar so cannot directly pass through the plasma membrane.  Instead it  binds to receptors in the plasma membrane which triggers intracellular signalling pathways.\n\n**Choices B**, **C** and **D**  are all incorrect, as they are all non-polar steroids so can diffuse through the plasma membrane and bind directly to receptors inside the cell, affecting gene expression."},{"ref":"BI0952","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0952\n\nWhich of the following is true for hormones but not neurotransmitters?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ They are signalling chemicals found in animals\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ They act on distant target cells\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ They are transmitted across a synaptic gap\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ They result in a short-term effect","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because hormones are secreted into the blood and are transported by the plasma to act on distant target cells.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because both hormones and neurotransmitters are signalling chemicals found in animals. **Choices C** and **D** are incorrect because these are characteristics of neurotransmitters and not hormones. Hormones are transported by the blood, and they result in long-term effects."},{"ref":"BI0953","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0953\n\nWhich features describe the transmembrane receptor and intracellular receptor?\n\n| | Transmembrane receptor | Intracellular receptor\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Signalling chemical enters the cell and binds in the nucleus | Signalling chemical remains outside of the cell and binds with the receptor |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Located in the cytoplasm |Located in the cell membrane |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Transmembrane domain contains hydrophobic amino acids and extracellular domain contains  hydrophilic amino acids  | Domain contains hydrophobic amino acids  |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | An example is G protein-coupled receptor | An example is the tyrosine kinase receptor |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe extracellular domain of transmembrane receptors binds to the ligand binding and often contains hydrophilic amino acids, which are capable of forming hydrogen bonds with water molecules. The transmembrane domain is mainly composed of hydrophobic amino acids to form a stable, nonpolar region that spans the hydrophobic lipid bilayer of the cell membrane. ​​The Ligand-binding domain of intracellular receptors has a significant presence of hydrophobic amino acids as they bind with the hydrophobic, lipophilic, ligands. However, other types of amino acids are present in the ligand-binding domains of both types of receptors which contribute to the stability of the receptor-ligand complex and interactions with other molecules involved in the receptor's overall function. Therefore **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choices A** and **B** are incorrect because transmembrane receptors are found in the cell membrane. Therefore, the signalling chemicals remain outside the cell and bind with the receptor without entering the cell. On the other hand, intracellular receptors are found in the cytoplasm. Hence, signalling chemicals must enter the cell to bind with the receptor. **Choice D** is incorrect because both the G protein-coupled receptor and tyrosine kinase receptor are examples of transmembrane receptors."},{"ref":"BI0954","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0954\n\nWhich feature correctly describes the binding of a ligand to a receptor?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Receptors can bind to any ligand.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Both the ligand and receptor must have a complementary shape.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ All ligands can penetrate the cell to bind with intracellular receptors.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ All ligands bind irreversibly to receptors.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because both the ligand and receptor must have a complementary shape to bind together, just like enzyme substrate specificity.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because receptors bind to specific ligands and not any ligands. **Choice C** is incorrect because not all ligands can penetrate the cell to bind with intracellular receptors. Ligands have to be hydrophobic to enter the cell, and some ligands are hydrophilic, which means they will remain outside the cell. **Choice D** is incorrect because not all ligands bind irreversibly to receptors, some bind reversibly."},{"ref":"BI0955","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0955\n\nA signalling chemical is needed to activate the G protein by binding to the G protein-coupled receptor. What other substance is needed to activate the G protein?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ GTP\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ cAMP\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ GDP\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ ATP","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because GTP, guanosine triphosphate, needs to bind with the G protein and replace the GDP to activate the G protein.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because GDP is attached to an inactive G protein, which is then replaced by GTP to activate the G protein. **Choices B** and **D** are incorrect because cAMP is the secondary messenger that gets formed from ATP in reactions that take place after the G protein is activated."},{"ref":"BI0956","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0956\n\nThe diagram represents the regulation of glycogen metabolism by the epinephrine receptors. What occurs after cyclic AMP (cAMP) is formed?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Chemical%20Signalling/images/cAMP%20cyclic%20AMP.png\" alt=\"cAMP cyclic AMP\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Acts on ATP\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Binds to G protein-coupled receptor\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Activates G protein\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Binds to an inactive protein kinase (PKA)","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because cAMP binds to inactive PKA after being formed to activate PKA.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the enzyme adenyl cyclase (AC) acts on ATP to form cAMP. This has to take place first to form cAMP. **Choices B** and **C** are incorrect because these two reactions take place before cAMP is formed. A ligand such as epinephrine binds to the G protein-coupled receptor, resulting in guanosine triphosphate (GTP) binding to the G protein to activate it."},{"ref":"BI0957","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0957\n\nThe diagram shows a flow chart.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Chemical%20Signalling/images/BI0957a.png\" alt=\"BI0957a\">\n\nWhich is the correct sequence of reactions shown in the flow chart?\n\n| | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-:\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Insulin binds to the intracellular domain of the tyrosine kinase receptor | Phosphorylation of tyrosine kinase receptor | Glucose transporter vesicles move to the plasma membrane |  Glucose enters the cell |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Phosphorylation of transmembrane domain of tyrosine kinase receptor | Insulin binds to the intracellular domain of the tyrosine kinase receptor | Glucose enters the cell | Glucose transporter vesicles move to the plasma membrane|\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Insulin binds to the extracellular domain of the tyrosine kinase receptor | Phosphorylation of tyrosine kinase receptor | Glucose transporter vesicles move to the plasma membrane | Glucose enters the cell |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Glucose enters the cell | Glucose transporter vesicles move to the plasma membrane | Phosphorylation of transmembrane domain of tyrosine kinase receptor| Insulin binds to the intracellular domain of the tyrosine kinase receptor |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because the sequence is in the right order. Insulin first needs to bind to the extracellular domain of the tyrosine kinase receptor to activate the kinase.\n\n**Choices A**, **B** and **D** are incorrect because the sequence is in the wrong order. When specific ligands, such as insulin, activate the tyrosine kinase receptor, the phosphorylation of the intracellular domain occurs, which brings about further cascade events."},{"ref":"BI0958","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0958\n\nWhich of the following is an example of a positive feedback mechanism?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Control of blood glucose concentration\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Maintenance of body temperature in a homeotherm\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Absorption of more water from the collecting duct as a result of ADH secretion\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increase of oxytocin bringing about further uterine contractions.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA positive feedback mechanism is a physiological process in which a response amplifies the initial stimulus, leading to an accelerated and often self-reinforcing outcome. The dilation of the cervix during childbirth stimulates the release of the hormone oxytocin. Oxytocin then brings about further uterine contractions, which brings about further cervix dilation. This is, therefore, an example of positive feedback, and **Choice D** is correct.\n\nA negative feedback mechanism is a regulatory mechanism in which a response counteracts and reverses the initial stimulus, maintaining stability and homeostasis in the system. **Choices A**, **B**, and **C** are all examples of negative feedback mechanisms."},{"ref":"BI0972","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0972\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast the structure of transmembrane and intracellular receptors. [3 marks]\n\n2. Explain why a variety of chemically different substances are used as hormones and neurotransmitters in humans. [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe how adrenaline (epinephrine) brings about glycogen breakdown in liver cells and how progesterone acts on endometrial cells. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following (*must include at least **one** similarity and at least **one** difference to gain full marks; differences must refer to both receptor types*):\n\n    ***Similarities***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are proteins   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have binding sites for specific chemicals **OR** ligands   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can undergo conformational changes when their ligand binds   [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ binding site of transmembrane receptors is on the extracellular region **OR** faces the outside of the cell **WHEREAS** binding site of intracellular receptors is inside cell   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transmembrane receptors span the plasma membrane **OR** have hydrophobic amino acid side chains in the membrane-spanning region **WHEREAS** intracellular receptors do not span the membrane **OR** have predominantly hydrophilic amino acid side chains exposed on their surfaces   [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n**Both** transmembrane and intracellular receptors are **proteins** ($\\checkmark$) with **specific binding sites** for their ligands ($\\checkmark$). **Transmembrane** receptors have **hydrophobic amino acid side chains** exposed in their **membrane-spanning regions**, whereas **intracellular** receptors have predominantly **hydrophilic amino acid side chains** exposed on their surfaces ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following (*answer must link each reason to a type of chemical or named example*):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ steroid hormones/named steroid hormone are lipid soluble so diffuse through the plasma membrane to bind intracellular receptors   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ peptide/protein hormones/catecholamines/suitable named molecule are water soluble so bind transmembrane receptors in the plasma membrane   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ steroids alter gene expression so responses are slower/longer-lasting   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water-soluble hormones act through transmembrane receptors that trigger intracellular signalling pathways, so responses are rapid   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water-soluble hormones dissolve in plasma **WHEREAS** steroids need carrier proteins **OR** this affects duration of action   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chemically different signalling molecules bind to specific receptors, allowing target cells to distinguish between different signals   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ different neurotransmitters can produce excitatory **OR** inhibitory effects depending on the receptors activated, e.g. glutamate is usually excitatory **WHEREAS** GABA **OR** glycine is usually inhibitory   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ different neurotransmitters are removed from the synaptic cleft by enzymatic breakdown **OR** reuptake so signals can be terminated quickly   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ neuropeptides/endorphins have slower/longer-lasting/modulatory effects   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nitric oxide/NO is a gas so diffuses freely across membranes **OR** is not stored in vesicles **OR** acts locally on nearby cells, whereas hormones travel in the blood to distant target cells, allowing communication over different distances   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ different signalling molecules are synthesised from different precursors **OR** by different pathways, allowing their production and regulation in different tissues   [1 mark]\n\n    *(adrenaline / noradrenaline may be used as a hormone or neurotransmitter example, but credit the same idea only once)*\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n**Steroid** hormones such as **testosterone** are **lipid soluble**, allowing them to **diffuse through** the plasma **membrane** and bind to **intracellular receptors** ($\\checkmark$). In contrast, **protein hormones** such as **insulin** are **water soluble** and bind **transmembrane receptors** ($\\checkmark$). **Steroids** alter **gene expression**, so their effects are slower but **longer-lasting** ($\\checkmark$). Different **neurotransmitters** can produce **excitatory** or **inhibitory** effects depending on the **receptors** activated; for example, **glutamate** is usually excitatory while **GABA** is usually inhibitory ($\\checkmark$). **Nitric oxide** is a **gas**, so it **diffuses** freely **across membranes** and is **not stored in vesicles** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following (*maximum four marks for each hormone*):\n\n    ***Epinephrine/Adrenaline: G-protein-coupled receptor***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adrenaline/epinephrine binds to a G-protein-coupled receptor/GPCR in plasma membrane (of liver cells)   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ binding activates a G protein   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ activated G protein activates adenylyl/adenylate cyclase   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adenylyl/adenylate cyclase converts ATP to cAMP, which acts as a second messenger   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cAMP activates protein kinase A/PKA, which activates phosphorylase kinase **OR** initiates a phosphorylation cascade **OR** amplifies the signal   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glycogen phosphorylase/enzymes involved in glycogen breakdown are activated, increasing glucose availability in liver cells   [1 mark]\n\n    ***Progesterone: intracellular receptor***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ progesterone is lipid soluble **OR** hydrophobic and diffuses through the plasma membrane of endometrial cells   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ progesterone binds to a specific intracellular receptor in the cytoplasm **OR** nucleus, activating the receptor   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the receptor–hormone complex enters the nucleus if the receptor was in the cytoplasm   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the complex binds to specific DNA sequences **OR** hormone response elements   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the complex regulates **OR** promotes transcription of specific genes   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA is translated into proteins that contribute to the maintenance **OR** development of the endometrium/uterine lining   [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n**Epinephrine** binds to a **G-protein-coupled receptor** (GPCR) in the **plasma membrane** of liver cells ($\\checkmark$). This **activates** a **G protein** ($\\checkmark$), which activates **adenylate cyclase**. Adenylate cyclase converts **ATP into cAMP**, which acts as a **second messenger** ($\\checkmark$). cAMP activates **protein kinase A**, starting a **phosphorylation cascade** that activates **glycogen phosphorylase**, resulting in the **breakdown of glycogen** and increasing the **availability of glucose** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    **Progesterone** is **lipid soluble**, so it **diffuses through the plasma membrane** of endometrial cells ($\\checkmark$). It binds to a specific **intracellular receptor**, **activating** the **receptor** ($\\checkmark$). The **receptor–hormone complex** binds to **specific DNA sequences** in the nucleus, promoting **transcription** of specific genes ($\\checkmark$). The **mRNA** is **translated** into **proteins** that help **maintain the endometrium** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1052","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1052\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Palmitic acid is an example of a saturated fatty acid with a 16 carbon chain. Draw the structure of a palmitic acid. $^*$ [3 marks]\n\n2. Testosterone is a steroid hormone, which is carried in the blood. Explain how, when testosterone reaches its target cells, it brings about its effect on the DNA of that cell. [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain how the structure of cellulose and glycogen are related to their functions. [8 marks]\n\n$^*$ *Note: Starting May 2028, this question may be assessed differently due to changes to the Biology guide, including the use of a data booklet.*","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ only single bonds in the carbon chain [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ a carboxyl group on the 1st or 16th carbon [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ no double bonds present (except in carboxyl group) and all carbons in the chain have four bonds [1 mark]\n\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Chemical%20Signalling/images/BI1052i.png\" alt=\"BI1052i\">\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nonpolar, so diffuses through the cell membrane\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ testosterone binds to a specific intracellular receptor in cytoplasm [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hormone-receptor complex is formed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ conformational change occurs allowing entry into nucleus **OR** complex enters nucleus [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ binds to specific DNA sequences of, **OR** to promoter regions of, target genes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ changes transcriptional activity of target genes\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ newly synthesised mRNA exits nucleus and enters cytoplasm for translation\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ synthesised proteins bring about response [1 mark]\n\n [5 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nWhen testosterone reaches its target cell it **diffuses** through the **cell membrane** ($\\checkmark$) into the cytoplasm of the cell. Once there it attaches to a **specific intracellular receptor** ($\\checkmark$), forming a **hormone-receptor complex** ($\\checkmark$). The conformational change of this complex enables it to **enter the nucleus** ($\\checkmark$) where it **binds** to the **promoter region** of the target genes ($\\checkmark$), bringing about **transcription**, and enabling the production of specific **proteins** to bring about the **response** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following (should include **AT LEAST ONE** for cellulose and **AT LEAST ONE** for glycogen):\n\n *Cellulose:*\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cellulose is made up of the monomer $\\beta$-glucose [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cellulose forms straight chains because the $\\beta$-glucose are oriented alternately upwards and downwards with $\\beta$-1,4 linkages [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the straight chains in cellulose provide structural support [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ $\\beta$-glucose molecules in cellulose form hydrogen bonds between polymers, which form microfibrils [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cellulose microfibrils have a high tensile strength which makes the cell wall strong [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ high tensile strength of cellulose prevents the cell from bursting under osmotic pressure [1 mark]\n\n *Glycogen:*\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycogen is made up of $\\alpha$-glucose molecules [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ $\\alpha$-glucose molecules are oriented the same way, resulting in a curved/twisted structure [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the compact structure of glycogen means a lot can be stored in a small space [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycogen is branched so that glucose can quickly be released for respiration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycogen does not have a fixed size so extra molecules of glucose can be added or taken away as needed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** cellulose and glycogen are insoluble in water due to their sizes so do not affect the osmotic pressure of the cell [1 mark]\n\n [8 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nCellulose is made up of **$\\beta$-glucose** ($\\checkmark$) molecules oriented alternately upwards and downwards which results in a **straight chain** ($\\checkmark$) and the **formation of hydrogen bonds**, contributing to the formation of **microfibrils** ($\\checkmark$). Microfibrils have a **high tensile strength** which contributes to a **strong cell wall** ($\\checkmark$). The **high tensile strength** ($\\checkmark$) of microfibrils prevents the cell from bursting **under osmotic pressure** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n Glycogen is made up of **$\\alpha$-glucose molecules** ($\\checkmark$), all oriented the same way, giving it a **twisted structure** ($\\checkmark$), which means it can be **stored in a small space** ($\\checkmark$). **Glycogen is branched** so that it can quickly be **digested by enzymes** and does not have a **fixed size** ($\\checkmark$), so glucose molecules can easily be **added and taken away** ($\\checkmark$). Both cellulose and glycogen are **insoluble in water** so do not affect the **osmotic pressure** of the cell ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1162","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1162\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast anaerobic and aerobic respiration in animal cells, in terms of substrates, products and yield of ATP.     [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline the process of negative feedback, referring to either temperature regulation or control of blood glucose concentration, in humans as an example.    [4 marks]\n\n3. Define an ecosystem, and explain how the flow of energy and recycling of nutrients make a terrestrial ecosystem sustainable. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:( at least one from each section)\n\n    ***Similarities*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** use glucose as a substrate for energy release     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in **BOTH**, pyruvate is the end product of glycolysis    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** processes release energy **OR** produce ATP    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in aerobic respiration, oxygen is required **WHEREAS**  anaerobic respiration occurs in the absence of oxygen        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ aerobic respiration can use substrates other than glucose **OR** can use fatty acids **OR** amino acids **WHEREAS** in anaerobic respiration only substrate is glucose\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ end products of aerobic respiration are carbon dioxide and water, **WHEREAS** the end product of anaerobic respiration is lactic acid         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ aerobic respiration is more efficient at generating ATP **OR** generates more molecules of ATP per glucose molecule **THAN** anaerobic respiration    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Aerobic** and anaerobic respiration use **glucose** as the **substrate** ($\\checkmark$) to **release energy** in the form of **ATP** ($\\checkmark$).  In **aerobic** respiration, **oxygen** is required, whereas **anaerobic respiration** occurs in the **absence of oxygen** ($\\checkmark$). The **end products of aerobic respiration** in animal cells are **carbon dioxide and water**, whereas the end product of **anaerobic** respiration is **lactic acid** ($\\checkmark$). **Aerobic respiration** produces many **more** molecules of **ATP per glucose molecule** than anaerobic respiration ($\\checkmark$) (approximately 15 times more).\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ negative feedback is involved in maintaining an equilibrium **OR** involved in homeostasis, since it brings the value back to the original    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when there is a change from a set value **OR** optimal level, brings about change to return to set value **OR** reduce changes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ involves receptors detecting change and sending information to control centre **OR** brain    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ control centre **OR** brain sends information to effector **OR** muscle **OR** gland to carry out response [1 mark]\n\n    Any **one** of the following examples (max two marks):\n\n    ***Blood glucose*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in blood glucose detected by the pancreatic **OR** β-cells cells which release insulin, **OR** fall in blood glucose detected by the pancreatic **OR** α-cells which release of glucagon      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ insulin causes glucose to be converted to **OR** stored as glycogen in liver **OR** muscle, so decreasing blood glucose, **OR** glucagon causes glycogen to be broken down to glucose **OR** amino acids to be converted to glucose in liver, so increasing blood glucose [1 mark]\n\n    ***Body temperature*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase **OR** decrease in body temperature signals to hypothalamus in the brain      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (increase in temperature) leads to vasodilation **OR** arterioles supplying blood to the skin dilate **OR** sweat glands increase sweat production (do not allow hairs lie flat as not significant in humans) and so cooling **OR** decrease in body temperature, **OR** (decrease in body temperature) leads to vasoconstriction **OR** arterioles supplying blood to the skin constrict **OR** sweat glands decrease  sweat production (do not allow hairs stand up as not significant in humans) **OR** shivering, to help raise body temperature    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    When there is a change from the normal, **negative feedback** mechanisms are a form of **control** that brings the value back to **within a normal range** ($\\checkmark$). The **change** is **detected by receptors**, which send **signals** to the **brain** ($\\checkmark$) and then signals from the brain to the **effector** to bring about the **response** ($\\checkmark$).  In the control of blood glucose concentration, an **increase** in **blood glucose** will be detected by the **β-cells** and lead to the **release of insulin** ($\\checkmark$), causing **glucose** to be **converted** to and stored as **glycogen** in **liver** and skeletal **muscle** cells. This then restores the **blood glucose** concentration to **within normal levels** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ an ecosystem is a community interacting with its physical environment     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy from the Sun is used for photosynthesis **OR** light energy is converted to chemical energy by autotrophs **OR** plants        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ this initial capture of energy then moves through the trophic levels **OR** from producers to consumers in a food chain            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ only 5 to 10% of the energy is transferred from one trophic level to the next    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy transfer from one level to the next trophic level is not 100% efficient    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy is lost as heat  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy flow is depicted in a pyramid shape and shows the loss of energy   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms absorb nutrients from food that they consume        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ decomposers break down organic matter after death, releasing nutrients    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (saprophytic) bacteria and fungi are decomposers and help release nutrients back to the soil             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nutrients in soil are in simpler compounds and are absorbed by plants    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ recycling of nutrients in ecosystems takes place    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy needs to be supplied continuously for initial capture by plants    [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    An **ecosystem** is a **community** of living organisms interacting with its **physical environment** ($\\checkmark$). **Energy** from the Sun is used by plants for **photosynthesis** ($\\checkmark$) to make organic compounds, and this **energy moves** from **one trophic level** to the **next** ($\\checkmark$) when organisms consume plants or other organisms. This **transfer** of energy is not efficient, only about **5 to 10%** is transferred ($\\checkmark$) to the next level, much is **lost as heat** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    **Organisms** absorb the **nutrients** from the food they consume ($\\checkmark$). After their death, **decomposers break down** the **organic matter** ($\\checkmark$), releasing the nutrients. The **nutrients** are in **simpler compounds** and can be **absorbed by plants** ($\\checkmark$) from the soil to be used to make further organic compounds in plants."},{"ref":"BI1376","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1376\n\nDopamine acts as both neurotransmitter between synapses and a hormone. Parkinson’s disease is caused by the death of nerve cells that produce dopamine. To treat Parkinson’s, patients are given the drug Levodopa.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Chemical%20Signalling/images/BI1376.png\" alt=\"BI1376\">\n[Source: Adapted from 721tx, M. (2013, October 24). *File: L-DOPA-to-dopamine.svg*. Wikimedia Commons.  Retrieved December 04, 2023 from URL https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: L-DOPA-to-dopamine.svg. Copyright free]\n\n1. Suggest why Levodopa can be used to treat Parkinson’s disease.    [1 mark]\n\n2. Outline the processes that cause the stimulation and release of dopamine into the synaptic cleft.    [2 marks]\n\n3. Distinguish between hormones and neurotransmitters.        [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it has a similar shape **OR** chemical structure to dopamine    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it can bind to the dopaminergic **OR** dopamine receptors    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Levodopa has a **similar shape** to dopamine, so it can bind to dopamine **receptors** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ depolarisation **OR** arrival of action potential at presynaptic knob **OR** bulb    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stimulates the opening of calcium channels **OR** calcium ions diffuse into the presynaptic knob **OR** bulb    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stimulates vesicles containing dopamine to move towards the presynaptic membrane     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles fuse with presynaptic membrane releasing dopamine **OR**  dopamine is released by exocytosis    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Depolarisation** of the **presynaptic** knob ($\\checkmark$) stimulates the opening of **calcium channels**, so calcium ions **diffuse** into the presynaptic knob ($\\checkmark$). The diffusion of calcium ions stimulates dopamine to be released into the **synaptic cleft** via **exocytosis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hormones bring about a slower response **THAN** neurotransmitters **OR** neurotransmitters bring about a more rapid response **THAN** hormones        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hormones are can bring about a response **OR** bind to, distant target cells **WHEREAS** neurotransmitters only affect neighbouring neurons        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hormones affect a wider range of target cells **OR** tissues **OR** organs **WHEREAS** neurotransmitters only affect specific target cells **OR** tissues        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hormones have a longer-term effect on the body **COMPARED** neurotransmitters have a shorter-term effect on the body            [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n   **Hormones** bring about a slower ($\\checkmark$) **but longer-lasting response** than neurotransmitters ($\\checkmark$).  ($\\checkmark$). **Hormones** can bring about a **response** in **cells** or tissues **all over the body**, whereas **neurotransmitters** only bring about a **response** in **neighbouring neurons** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1410","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1410\n\nWhich of the following is/are true?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | Non-polar ligands have receptors in the cytoplasm  |\n  | II. | Polar ligands have receptors in the nucleus |\n  | III. | Insulin binds to transmembrane receptor proteins |\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true as non-polar ligands are lipid-soluble and so can pass through the plasma membrane into the cytoplasm, where they bind to receptors. **Statement III** is true as insulin is a protein hormone and, therefore, cannot enter the cytoplasm and has to bind to receptor proteins embedded in the phospholipid bilayer of the plasma membrane. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Statement II** is not true as polar ligands cannot pass through the plasma membrane and therefore cannot enter the nucleus, therefore **Choices B** and **D** are incorrect. **Choice A** is incorrect as it only gives one of the true statements."},{"ref":"BI1411","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1411\n\nA signalling molecule binds to a G protein-coupled receptor (GPCR) on the plasma membrane of a target cell, leading to activation of an intracellular signalling pathway.\n\nWhich sequence most accurately describes the events following binding of the signalling molecule?\n\nA. $\\hspace{1em}$ Ligand binds GPCR $\\rarr$ receptor changes conformation $\\rarr$ GTP is hydrolysed to GDP on the G protein $\\rarr$ G protein is activated $\\rarr$ effector protein is activated\n\nB. $\\hspace{1em}$ Ligand binds GPCR $\\rarr$ receptor changes conformation $\\rarr$ GDP is replaced by GTP on the receptor $\\rarr$ receptor is activated $\\rarr$ effector protein is activated\n\nC. $\\hspace{1em}$ Ligand binds GPCR $\\rarr$ receptor changes conformation $\\rarr$ G protein phosphorylates the receptor $\\rarr$ G protein is activated $\\rarr$ effector protein is activated\n\nD. $\\hspace{1em}$ Ligand binds GPCR $\\rarr$ receptor changes conformation $\\rarr$ GDP is replaced by GTP on the G protein $\\rarr$ G protein is activated $\\rarr$ effector protein is activated","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n**Choice D** is correct because binding of the signalling molecule causes a conformational change in the GPCR. This promotes the replacement of GDP with GTP on the G protein, activating it. The activated G protein then interacts with an effector protein, activating an intracellular signalling pathway.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because hydrolysis of GTP to GDP inactivates the G protein rather than activating it.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because GDP/GTP exchange occurs on the G protein, not the receptor. The GPCR changes conformation and activates the G protein.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because GPCR signalling does not involve the G protein phosphorylating the receptor. The receptor activates the G protein through a conformational change that promotes GDP-to-GTP exchange."},{"ref":"BI1412","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1412\n\nWhich of the following shows the correct order of events for cellular responses due to the release of epinephrine (adrenaline)?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Adrenaline binds to surface receptor $\\longrightarrow$ ATP converted to cAMP $\\longrightarrow$ activation of phosphorylase kinase $\\longrightarrow$ glycogen converted to glucose\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Adrenaline binds to surface receptor $\\longrightarrow$ activation of phosphorylase kinase $\\longrightarrow$ ATP converted to cAMP $\\longrightarrow$ glycogen converted to glucose\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Adrenaline binds to surface receptor $\\longrightarrow$ cAMP converted to ATP $\\longrightarrow$ activation of glycogen phosphorylase $\\longrightarrow$ glycogen converted to glucose\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Adrenaline binds to surface receptor $\\longrightarrow$ cAMP converted to ATP $\\longrightarrow$ activation of phosphorylase kinase $\\longrightarrow$ glycogen converted to glucose","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWhen epinephrine (adrenaline) is released into the blood, it binds to G protein-coupled receptors, activating G proteins, which in turn activate adenylyl (adenylate) cyclase. ATP molecules are then converted to cAMP, which activates phosphorylase kinase. The phosphorylase kinase activates glycogen phosphorylase, an enzyme that catalyses the breakdown of glycogen into glucose. **Choice A** is, therefore, correct.\n\n**Choices B, C** and **D** are incorrect, as the sequences of events are in the wrong order."},{"ref":"BI1419","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1419\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nOrganisms communicate in a variety of ways. The nervous system is a communication system found in animals. Below is a diagram of a motor neuron from a person suffering from multiple sclerosis. Multiple sclerosis disrupts the transmission of the electrical impulses along neurons.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Chemical%20Signalling/images/BI1419.png\" alt=\"BI1419\">\n\n1. Outline saltatory conduction and suggest how multiple sclerosis affects this process.[3 marks]\n\n2. Bacteria cells carry out a form of intercellular communication, called quorum sensing, which enables the control of the colony. Outline the mechanism of quorum sensing. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. Must include following to gain full marks:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ damage to myelin sheath reduces rate of transduction as axon no longer insulated **OR** saltatory conduction can no longer occur [1 mark]\n\n Then any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ myelin prevents changes in membrane potential where it is located [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nodes of Ranvier between Schwann cells where ion channels concentrated [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ depolarisation at one node of Ranvier causes local current through axon to next node [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ stimulates depolarisation **OR** action potential at next node [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ action potential jumps from node to node **OR** saltatory conduction [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n There are **many ion channels** at the **nodes of Ranvier** between the Schwann cells ($\\checkmark$). The depolarisation of the membrane, therefore, occurs rapidly at these nodes, and the rush of sodium ions into the cell causes a **local current** to flow passively through the **intracellular fluid** of the **axon** ($\\checkmark$). This current travels to the next node of Ranvier, **generating** a **new action potential** ($\\checkmark$). The action potential effectively jumps **from node to node**, a process known as **saltatory conduction** ($\\checkmark$). If there the **myelin sheath** is **degraded in Multiple Sclerosis**, saltatory conduction cannot happen and the **rate of transduction** of the impulse is **reduced** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **four** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ release of ligands **OR** autoinducer **OR** signalling molecules into environment by individual cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as colony increases in size concentration of ligands **OR** autoinducer **OR** signalling molecules increases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ bacteria detect increase in concentration of autoinducers OR ligands through cell surface receptors [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ once autoinducer **OR** ligand **OR** signalling molecule threshold reached binding of ligand **OR** autoinducer **OR** signalling molecules to receptor causes transduction cascade inside a bacterium [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ leads to change in gene expression **OR** activates autotransducer genes **OR** represses transcription of genes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ further increase in autoinducer **OR** ligand **OR** signalling molecules concentration example of positive feedback loop [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ controls virulence **OR** biofilm formation **OR** bioluminescence **OR** production of secondary metabolites **OR** sporulation [1 mark]\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n In quorum sensing, **individual bacteria release autoinducers** into the environment ($\\checkmark$). The **larger** the **colony**, the **more autoinducers** are released and these **bind to receptors** on the **cell surface** of each bacterial cell ($\\checkmark$). Once a **threshold autoinducer concentration** is reached, it causes a **transduction cascade** ($\\checkmark$) inside the cell leading to a **change in gene expression** ($\\checkmark$). This causes a further increase in the **autoinducer concentration** and so this is a **positive feedback** loop ($\\checkmark$). Quorum sensing is involved in the control of a variety of **functions**, such as **virulence** and **bioluminescence** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1465","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1465\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nOestradiol, shown below, is a hormone involved in human reproduction.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Chemical%20Signalling/images/BI1465%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1465 fig1\">\n\nExplain how oestradiol brings about a response in its target cells. [4 marks]","markScheme":"Any **four** of the following\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ lipid-soluble, so diffuses through the lipid bilayer of the cell membrane [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ binds to intracellular receptors **OR**, once inside cell, binds to specific oestrogen receptors in cytoplasm **OR** nucleus. [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ binding activates receptors [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ oestradiol-receptor complex acts as transcription factor and enters nucleus [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ oestradiol-receptor complex **OR** transcription factor binds to promoter region [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ this binding regulates the transcription of genes **OR** upregulates gene expression **OR** downregulates gene expression [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ altered levels of mRNA lead to changes in protein synthesis [1 mark]\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nOestradiol, a **steroid hormone**, **diffuses through** the **plasma membrane** of its target cells due to its **lipid-soluble** nature ($\\checkmark$). **Inside** the cell, it **binds to specific oestradiol receptors** located either **in** the **cytoplasm** or directly in the **nucleus** ($\\checkmark$). This **hormone-receptor complex** then acts as a **transcription factor** by **moving into** the nucleus ($\\checkmark$) (if not already there), where it binds to **promoter regions** on the DNA ($\\checkmark$). This binding **alters** the **transcription of certain genes**, either **upregulating or downregulating** their **expression** ($\\checkmark$). This change in gene expression **determines** whether certain **proteins** are **synthesised** ($\\checkmark$) or not."},{"ref":"BI1545","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1545\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nCompare and contrast how peptide and steroid hormones bring about a response once they have reached their target cells.    [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **four** of the following (*response must include at least one similarity and one difference to gain full marks*):\n\n    **Similarities:** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** control enzymes OR regulate cellular activity                    [1]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** bind to specific receptors                                [1]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** amplify the initial signal                                [1]\n\n    **Differences:** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ peptide hormones are hydrophilic **OR** polar and do not enter the cell **WHEREAS** steroid hormones are hydrophobic **OR** non-polar so can enter the cell                 [1]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ peptide hormones bind to receptors on the cell surface membrane **WHEREAS** steroid hormones bind to intracellular receptors **OR** receptors in the cytoplasm **OR** nucleus     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ peptide hormones activate a second messenger **OR** cyclic AMP  **WHEREAS** steroid hormones do not activate a second messenger    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ peptide hormones initiate cascade of reactions **OR** signal transduction **WHEREAS** steroid hormones directly activate gene expression    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ peptide hormones bring about a more rapid **OR** short-lived response **WHEREAS** steroid hormones bring about a slower **OR** longer-lasting response         [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Both** peptide and steroid hormones **regulate cellular activities** ($\\checkmark$) and **bind to specific receptors** ($\\checkmark$). **Peptide** hormones are **hydrophilic** so **cannot enter** the **cell** and instead **bind** to **cell surface receptors**, **activating** a **second messenger**, such as **cAMP** which brings about a **cascade response**.  In contrast, **steroid** hormones are **hydrophobic** and so can diffuse **through** the cell **membrane** ($\\checkmark$). Once inside the cell, they bind to **intracellular receptors** ($\\checkmark$), directly affecting **gene transcription** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2390","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2390\n\nWhat is the likely time taken for an adrenergic neurotransmitter to perform its function in chemical signalling?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 60s\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 1s\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 1ms\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 1$\\mu$s","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because there are usually open ligand-gated channels on the postsynaptic membrane, ready to bind to the neurotransmitter. The synapse is very narrow (typically 30nm) so diffusion across the synaptic gap is rapid. Adrenergic and cholinergic neurotransmitters are believed to have equal or near-equal transmission speeds.\n\n**Choice A** and **B** are incorrect because a transmission time measured in whole seconds would be unrealistically slow, given the overall speed of nervous transmission, even when an impulse has to pass through several synapses as it is directed to its target organ. For example, nervous transmission through myelinated neurons in humans can be as fast as 120 ms$^{-1}$ (432 kilometres per hour!); whilst the synapses cause some delay, voluntary reaction times to stimuli can be measured in split-seconds, which shows that the synaptic transmission must be in that order of magnitude of speed.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because this is too little time for a neurotransmitter to diffuse across the synaptic gap."},{"ref":"BI2457","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2457\n\nHow does the steroid hormone cortisol influence gene expression?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ By binding to cytoplasmic receptors and activating transcription factors in the nucleus.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ By directly binding to ribosomes to promote protein synthesis.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ By methylating DNA to silence genes.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ By increasing RNA polymerase activity across all genes.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because hormones affect gene expression by binding to cytoplasmic receptors, which activate transcription factors in the nucleus. These transcription factors cause the target genes to be expressed and specific proteins to be produced.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because hormones do not bind to ribosomes, they bind to cytoplasmic receptors.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because hormones do not cause methylation of DNA, they increase gene expression by activating transcription factors which are mainly in the cytoplasm.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because hormones do not increase RNA polymerase activity or have an effect on all genes."},{"ref":"BI2950","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2950\n\nWhich of the following is/are a possible effect of a ligand binding to a receptor protein?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Amplification of the signal via a second messenger|\n    | II. | Facilitated diffusion of the ligand into the cell, so it can bind to transcription factors |\n    | III. | Opening of a calcium ion channel|\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because when ligands bind to receptor proteins on the cell surface, this can trigger a signal transduction pathway that activates a second messenger. Second messengers amplify the original signal inside the cell.\\\n**Statement II**  is false because, while some non-polar ligands (like steroid hormones) diffuse across the membrane to bind to intracellular receptors, this is simple diffusion, not facilitated diffusion. Most ligands bind to extracellular receptors, initiating signal transduction. \\\n**Statement III**  is true because some receptor proteins are ligand-gated ion channels; binding of a ligand can open a calcium ion channel and allow $\\ce{Ca^2+}$ to flow into the cell.\n\n**Choice B** is correct because it contains only the true **Statements I** and **III**.\\\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it only contains one of the true statements; it is missing **Statement III**.\n**Choices C** and **D** are incorrect because they contain the false **Statement II**."},{"ref":"BI2952","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2952\n\nWhich is a primary reason for the existence of a wide range of chemically diverse signalling molecules in animals?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ To allow signalling molecules to pass easily through membranes\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ To enable cells to respond differently to various stimuli\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ To reduce the need for receptors on target cells\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ To ensure all signals result in increased gene expression","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA wide variety of chemically diverse signalling molecules allows cells to detect and respond to many different types of stimuli, ensuring specificity and appropriate physiological responses.\nTherefore, **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because signalling molecules do not need to cross membranes to function. Many, such as peptide hormones and cytokines, bind to extracellular receptors and trigger intracellular signalling pathways.\\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because greater chemical diversity in signalling molecules actually requires more receptor types, not fewer, to ensure accurate signal recognition.\\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because signalling does not always increase gene expression. Some pathways decrease transcription, and others trigger rapid cellular changes without affecting gene expression at all."},{"ref":"BI2953","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2953\n\nWhich of the following rows gives features that correctly compare hormones and neurotransmitters?\n\n| | Feature | Hormones | Neurotransmitters |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Distance of action | Short-distance | Long-distance |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Mode of transport | Carried by red blood cells | Carried in the plasma |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Duration of effect | Long lasting | Short-lived |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Site of release | Synaptic vesicles | Endocrine glands |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nHormones are carried in the blood to often distant target tissues and usually produce long-lasting effects. In contrast, neurotransmitters act rapidly and briefly across a synapse to enable fast neural communication. Therefore, **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because hormones typically act over long distances via the bloodstream, while neurotransmitters act over very short distances across the synaptic cleft.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because although hormones are transported in the blood, they are transported in the plasma, not by red blood cells. Also, neurotransmitters are not carried in the plasma; they diffuse freely across the synaptic cleft.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because hormones are not released by synaptic vesicles; they are released by specific glands or organs. Additionally, neurotransmitters are not released by endocrine glands; they are released by synaptic vesicles into the synaptic cleft."},{"ref":"BI2954","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2954\n\nThe image shows a transmembrane acetylcholine receptor on the postsynaptic membrane.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Chemical%20Signalling/images/2954Ed.png\" alt=\"2954Ed\">\n\nWhich row correctly describes what binds to the receptor, the substance that flows through it, and the effect on the postsynaptic membrane potential?\n\n| | What binds to the receptor? | Substance that flows through the receptor channel | Effect on the membrane potential |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Acetylcholine | Sodium ions | Depolarisation |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Acetylcholine | Chloride ions | Hyperpolarisation |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Acetylcholinesterase | Sodium ions | Depolarisation |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Calcium ions | Potassium ions | Hyperpolarisation |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWhen an action potential reaches the presynaptic membrane, acetylcholine is released, which diffuses across the synaptic cleft and binds to both binding sites on the receptor. This causes a conformational change in the receptor, opening the channel, allowing sodium ions to flow into the postsynaptic neuron down their electrochemical gradient, thereby depolarising the membrane. **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor is an excitatory channel; chloride ions flowing in and hyperpolarisation describe an inhibitory synapse. \\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because acetylcholinesterase does not bind to the receptor; it is an enzyme in the synaptic cleft that breaks down acetylcholine. \\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because calcium ions do not bind to this receptor; their entry into the presynaptic knob triggers acetylcholine release. Also, the main movement through the channel is Na$^+$ diffusing into the cell, which depolarises the membrane rather than hyperpolarising it."}]},{"name":"Defence Against Disease","questions":[{"ref":"BI0005","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0005\n\nAntibodies are proteins that bind to antigens. Which cell expresses the genes for antibody production?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Plasma B cells\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Macrophages\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Helper T cells\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Red blood cells","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** - Antibodies are globular proteins synthesized by plasma B cells.  The genes associated with antibody synthesis are expressed in plasma B cells.\n\n**Choice B** - Macrophages do not synthesize antibodies and therefore do not express these genes.  Macrophages are large phagocytic cells and present antigens to helper T cells.\n\n**Choice C** - Helper T cells do not synthesize antibodies.  They stimulate B cells to divide by mitosis to produce plasma B cells and memory B cells.\n\n**Choice D** - Red blood cells do not have a nucleus and therefore do not have the genes for antibody synthesis."},{"ref":"BI0243","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0243\n\nWhich of the following could lead to the development of antibiotic resistance?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Transfer of a plasmid from one bacterium to another\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    The development of new antibiotics\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    Human populations became resistant to the antibiotics\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    Antibiotics switch on resistance transcription factors","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because bacteria can gain antibiotic-resistance genes via horizontal gene transfer (often on plasmids), spreading resistance through a population.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because developing new antibiotics does not, in itself, cause resistance; rather, resistance increases when antibiotics act as a selection pressure that favours resistant bacteria.\n\n **Choice C** iis incorrect because antibiotics target bacteria, not human cells; humans do not “become resistant” to antibiotics in the evolutionary sense.\n\n **Choice D** is incorrect because antibiotic resistance develops mainly through genetic changes (such as mutation or the acquisition of genes), not because antibiotics generally “switch on transcription factors” to create resistance."},{"ref":"BI0300","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0300\n\nThe graph shows how the concentration of antibodies changed in an individual\\'s blood over time.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Defence%20Against%20Disease/images/BI0300i.png\" alt=\"BI0300i\">\n\nWhat is the most likely cause of the change in antibody levels at point X?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Primary infection\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Secondary infection by the same pathogen\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Infection by a different pathogen\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Antibiotics administered","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe graph shows two antibody peaks. The first smaller peak after the first arrow (pointing to the initial rise in antibody concentration) is typical of a primary immune response. The second, much larger and faster peak (at point X) is characteristic of a secondary immune response. When the same pathogen enters the body again, memory B cells created during the primary infection are rapidly activated. This causes a faster and more intense antibody production, which explains the sharp and high peak at point X. **Choice B** is the correct answer.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because a primary infection would result in a slower and lower rise in antibodies, as seen at the first peak, not the second.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because if infected by a different pathogen, the immune system would treat it as a new primary infection, and the antibody response would again be slow and small, not rapid and large.\n\n**Choice D**is incorrect because antibiotics do not directly cause an increase in antibody concentration. They target bacteria but do not stimulate antibody production, which is the job of the immune system"},{"ref":"BI0301","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0301\n\nWhich of the following is associated with the primary defence of the body?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Lymphocytes\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Antibodies\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Memory cells\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Mucus","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nMucus is part of the primary (first-line) defence of the body. It acts as a physical barrier to pathogens, trapping microbes in the respiratory and digestive tracts. Along with skin, cilia, stomach acid, and enzymes in tears and saliva, mucus helps prevent pathogens from entering the body in the first place. **Choice D** is the correct answer.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell involved in the secondary, not primary, immune response. They recognise and target specific pathogens.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because antibodies are produced by B lymphocytes in the adaptive immune response, which is part of the secondary, not primary, immune response.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because memory cells are specialised lymphocytes that \"remember\" a specific pathogen after a primary infection. They are crucial for a faster and stronger response during subsequent exposures to the same pathogen and are therefore part of the secondary immune response."},{"ref":"BI0302","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0302\n\nWhich of these statements about antibiotics are true?\n\n-    I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Antibiotics are ineffective against viruses.\n-    II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ Antibiotics block metabolic reactions in prokaryotes.\n-    III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ Antibiotic resistance is increasing in bacteria.\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAll three statements are true. Antibiotics are chemicals that affect metabolic processes in prokaryotes (**Statement II**) but are not effective against viruses (**Statement I**). Antibiotic resistance is an increasing global health problem (**Statement III**). **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choices B, C** and **D** are all incorrect as they are missing one or more of the true statements."},{"ref":"BI0303","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0303\n\nWhich row in the table correctly identifies substance X and the solubility of fibrinogen?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Defence%20Against%20Disease/images/BI0303.png\" alt=\"BI0303\">\n\n| | $\\hspace{1em}$ X $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Fibrinogen $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| Clotting factor V | Insoluble |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Dipeptidase | Soluble |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Thrombin | Soluble |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Surfactant | Insoluble |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSubstance X is thrombin, which converts soluble fibrinogen into insoluble fibrin; **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because clotting factors convert prothrombin to thrombin, and fibrinogen is soluble.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because, although both dipeptidase and thrombin are protease enzymes, dipeptidases function in digestion by breaking down dipeptides into amino acids, whereas thrombin is involved in blood clotting by converting fibrinogen into fibrin.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because surfactant is found in the alveoli, and fibrinogen is soluble."},{"ref":"BI0305","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0305\n\nWhat is the name of the process by which white blood cells engulf and destroy pathogens?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Lysis\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Phagocytosis\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Plasmolysis\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Crenation","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nPhagocytosis is the term for the process by which phagocytes engulf and destroy pathogens; **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because lysis is the breaking down or rupture of a cell, not the engulfing process.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because plasmolysis occurs in plant cells when water leaves the cell, causing the cell membrane to shrink away from the cell wall.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because crenation happens in animal cells when they shrink due to water loss in a hypertonic solution."},{"ref":"BI0306","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0306\n\nIn the diagram below, what type of molecule is X pointing to?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Defence%20Against%20Disease/images/BI0306%20i.png\" alt=\"BI0306 i\">\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Antibiotics\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Antigens\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Antibodies\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Receptors","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nExplanation:\nX is pointing to antigens, molecules that are recognised as \"foreign\" by the immune system and trigger an immune response. When a phagocytic cell engulfs a pathogen, it often displays the antigen of the pathogen on its cell surface membrane to help trigger an adaptive immune response; **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because antibiotics are substances used to treat bacterial infections.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because antibodies are proteins produced by plasma B cells in response to an antigen.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because receptors are specialised proteins or glycoproteins found on the surface of or within cells that bind to specific molecules (ligands), such as hormones, neurotransmitters, or antigens, and initiate a cellular response."},{"ref":"BI0307","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0307\n\nWhich of these does not contribute to antibiotic resistance?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The use of antibiotics in animal feed\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Vaccination\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ The use of antibiotics to treat viral infections\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Patients failing to complete the prescribed course of antibiotics","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because vaccinations involve the deliberate introduction of  antigens, which have been made harmless, to stimulate the production of antibodies and memory cells for long-term immunity. They do not contribute to antibiotic resistance. \\\n Increased and incorrect use of antibiotics contributes to antibiotic resistance. \\\n**Choice A** increases the use of antibiotics in raising livestock. \\\n**Choice C** is an incorrect use of antibiotics because they are ineffective against viruses.  \\\n**Choice D** can lead to the development of antibiotic resistance because patients who fail to complete a course of antibiotics may not completely eliminate the infection and instead allow the survival of resistant bacteria."},{"ref":"BI0308","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0308\n\nWhich of these statements are true for white blood cells?\n\n-    I. $\\hspace{1em}$ White blood cells are part of the non-specific and specific defence systems.\n-    II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ Antibodies are only produced by lymphocytes.\n-    III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ Lymphocytes provide long-term immunity.\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III\n\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because all three statements are true. Phagocytes and lymphocytes are both types of white blood cell. Phagocytes are part of the innate defence system, and lymphocytes are part of the adaptive defence system (**Statement I**). Antibodies are only produced by plasma B cells, a specific type of lymphocyte (**Statement II**). Lymphocytes provide long-term memory by forming memory B and T cells, which allow the immune system to respond more quickly and effectively to future infections by the same pathogen (**Statement III**).\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are incorrect as they are missing one or more of the true statements."},{"ref":"BI0309","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0309\n\nWhich row contains a cause and consequence of antibiotic resistance?\n\n| | $\\hspace{1em}$ Cause  $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Consequence $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| Patients not completing courses of antibiotics | Affects the ability of antibiotics to treat viral infections |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Overuse of antibiotics | Affects the ability of antibiotics to treat HIV |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Regular use of antibiotics in agriculture | Affects the ability of antibiotics to fight bacterial infections |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Mutation(s) in viral DNA | Affects the ability of antibiotics to fight bacterial infections |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because the use of antibiotics in agriculture exposes bacteria to antibiotics frequently, increasing selection for resistant strains. As a result, antibiotics become less effective at treating bacterial infections because resistant bacteria survive and spread. \\\n**Choice A** is incorrect because antibiotics are not used to treat viral infections. \\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because HIV is a virus, and antibiotics are ineffective against it. \\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because antibiotic resistance arises in bacteria, not from mutations in viruses."},{"ref":"BI0310","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0310\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nBelow is a diagram of Vibrio cholerae, the pathogen that causes the disease cholera.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Defence%20Against%20Disease/images/BI0310i.png\" alt=\"BI0310i\">\n\n1. Identify which group of pathogens Vibrio cholerae belongs to. [1 mark]\n\nStomach acid and the mucous membranes of the digestive system are important primary defences against cholera infection.\n\n2. Outline how mucous membranes defend the body against pathogens. [2 marks]\n3. Explain why blood clotting is described as a cascade reaction. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **one** of the following: \\\n$\\ce{ -> }$ bacteria [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotes [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n **Bacteria** $\\checkmark$.\n\n2. any **two** of the following: \\\n$\\ce{ -> }$ produce a sticky mucus to trap pathogens [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ act as a physical barrier preventing pathogens from reaching **OR** invading deeper tissues [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ contain enzymes such as lysozymes that chemically digest bacterial cell walls [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ cilia in respiratory tract move trapped pathogens up trachea to back of throat **OR** to be swallowed [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n Mucous membranes secrete **mucus** $\\checkmark$, which **traps** the **pathogens**, helping to **prevent** their **entry into tissues** $\\checkmark$.\n\n3. any **four** of the following: \\\n$\\ce{ -> }$ blood clotting is a cascade reaction in which a series of reactions occur where the product of one step activates the next [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ clotting factors are released from platelets [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ sequential activation of clotting factors leads to conversion of prothrombin to thrombin **OR** thrombin converts fibrinogen to fibrin [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ fibrin forms a mesh that traps erythrocytes to form a clot [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ the cascade results in amplification where a small initial signal produces a large response [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ the cascade allows rapid and efficient clot formation [1 mark]\n\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n Blood clotting is a **cascade reaction** in which a series of reactions occur where the **product of one step activates the next** $\\checkmark$. **Platelets** release **clotting factors** that initiate this sequence $\\checkmark$. These factors lead to the conversion of **prothrombin to thrombin**, which converts **fibrinogen into fibrin** $\\checkmark$. Fibrin forms a **mesh that traps erythrocytes**, resulting in **clot formation** $\\checkmark$."},{"ref":"BI0311","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0311\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe diagram below represents the interaction of antigens and antibodies in the human immune response.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Defence%20Against%20Disease/images/BI0311i.png\" alt=\"BI0311i\">\n\n1. Outline what an antigen is. [3 marks]\n2. Explain the role of memory B-cells in the human adaptive immune response. [3 marks]\n3. Describe four differences, other than those involving memory cells, between the innate and adaptive immune systems. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\\\n$\\ce{ -> }$ molecule **OR** substance that triggers an immune response **OR** antibody production [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ molecule recognised as foreign **OR** non-self [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ usually a protein **OR** glycoprotein **OR** polysaccharide [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ usually located on the surface of a pathogen **OR** bacteria **OR** virus **OR** a cancer cell **OR** pollen [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ has specific epitopes **OR** binding sites recognised by specific antibodies **OR** lymphocyte receptors [1 mark]\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nAn antigen is a **foreign molecule** $\\checkmark$ that is usually a **protein or glycoprotein** $\\checkmark$ found on the **surface of pathogens** $\\checkmark$, which triggers an **immune response** such as **antibody production** $\\checkmark$.\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\\\n$\\ce{ -> }$ produced during the primary immune response **OR** after clonal selection [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ remain in the blood **OR** lymph nodes for long periods [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ retain the ability to produce specific antibodies for the same antigen [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ upon re-infection **OR** secondary exposure, they provide a faster **OR** stronger response [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ rapidly proliferate **OR** undergo clonal expansion into plasma cells [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ ensures antibody concentration rises quickly to prevent disease symptoms [1 mark]\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nMemory B-cells are **long-lived lymphocytes** that **remain** in the body **after** the **primary immune response** $\\checkmark$, which on **secondary exposure** to the **same antigen** rapidly **divide** and **differentiate into plasma cells** $\\checkmark$, producing **large amounts** of **specific antibodies more quickly** than in primary response, leading to **immunity** $\\checkmark$.\n\n3. any **four** of the following:\\\n$\\ce{ -> }$ innate immunity is non-specific **WHEREAS** adaptive immunity is specific to particular antigens [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ innate immunity provides a rapid **OR** immediate response **WHEREAS** adaptive immunity has a delay **OR** lag phase on first exposure [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ innate immunity uses phagocytic white blood cells **WHEREAS** adaptive immunity involves lymphocytes **OR** antibody production [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ innate immunity recognises common pathogen-associated patterns **WHEREAS** adaptive immunity recognises specific antigens **OR** epitopes [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ innate immunity uses fixed receptors **WHEREAS** adaptive immunity has a diverse range of receptors [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ innate immunity includes physical **OR** chemical barriers **WHEREAS** adaptive immunity involves internal cellular **OR** molecular responses [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ innate immunity does not involve clonal selection **WHEREAS** adaptive immunity involves clonal selection and expansion of specific B cells **OR** T cells [1 mark]\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n**Innate** immunity is **non-specific**, whereas **adaptive** immunity is **specific** to particular antigens $\\checkmark$. **Innate** immunity provides a **rapid response**, whereas **adaptive** immunity has a **delay on first exposure** $\\checkmark$. **Innate** immunity involves **phagocytic cells**, whereas **adaptive** immunity involves **lymphocytes and antibody production** $\\checkmark$. **Innate** immunity does not involve clonal selection, whereas **adaptive** immunity involves **clonal selection and expansion** of specific lymphocytes $\\checkmark$."},{"ref":"BI0312","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0312\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the methods of HIV transmission and its effects on the immune system.     [3 marks]\n\n2.    Discuss the need for international cooperation to fight contagious diseases such as Ebola. [5 marks]\n\n3.    Antibiotics are an essential tool of modern medicine. Outline the strengths and limitations of antibiotics. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following (both the method of transmission and effects on the immune system are required for full marks):\n\n    *Method of transmission:*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ unprotected sexual intercourse **OR** sharing needles **OR** mother to child **OR** breast feeding    [1 mark]\n\n    *Effects of HIV on the immune system:*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ HIV targets helper T cells **OR** CD4 cells      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reduced number of helper T cells **OR** CD4 cells     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reduced ability to fight infection **OR** disease     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  (without treatment) can progress to acquired immunodeficiency syndrome [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    HIV can be transmitted through **unprotected sex** with infected person ($\\checkmark$). HIV targets cells of the immune system, specifically **helper T cells** ($\\checkmark$), which possess CD4 receptors. The virus **reduces** the **number of helper T cells** ($\\checkmark$) which help fight infection and thus a decrease in their number makes a person more **prone to infection** ($\\checkmark$). Without the correct treatment, HIV infection can progress to **AIDS** ($\\checkmark$), which is a result of the immune system becoming severely compromised.\n\n2.    any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contagious diseases can cross borders     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ international cooperation is needed to prevent the spread **OR** trace transmission    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to share knowledge and expertise [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ medicines need to be shared [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mass vaccination programs are needed to prevent spread [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mass vaccination programs are needed to protect the populations of different countries [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ monitoring and surveillance is needed to identify sources of infection    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ developed countries may need to give financial and medical support to developing countries [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nContagious diseases can move from one population to another and **across borders** ($\\checkmark$). Countries need to **share knowledge and expertise** to work together to prevent the spread of disease ($\\checkmark$). **Medicines and resources** need to be shared ($\\checkmark$), particularly from **developed to developing countries** ($\\checkmark$), to help to reduce and prevent transmission . Countries can also work together to **monitor the spread of disease and identify sources** ($\\checkmark$) of infection. Mass **vaccination** programs are needed **to protect populations** ($\\checkmark$) and need to happen in more than one country.\n\n3.    any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibiotics are chemicals that disrupt the metabolism of bacteria     [1 mark]\n\n    *Strengths*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can be used to treat bacterial infections     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can be applied using a variety of methods **OR** orally, topically and intravenously [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a number of antibiotics exist **OR** different antibiotics can be used to treat different infections [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibiotics do not harm eukaryotic cells **OR** body cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibiotics are fast acting [1 mark]\n\n    *Limitations*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibiotics are not effective against viruses [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibiotics can have side effects **OR** cause allergic reactions [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ misuse of antibiotics has led to increased antibiotic resistance **OR** antibiotic resistance is increasing     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ over prescription of antibiotics **OR** use of antibiotics in animal feed to increase muscle mass **OR** individuals not completing the course of antibiotics increases the prevalence of antibiotic resistance [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibiotic resistant bacterial strains cause illnesses that cannot be treated with current antibiotics [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ examples of antibiotic-resistant bacterial diseases are MRSA (methicillin-resistant *Staphylococcus aureus*) **OR** multi-drug resistant tuberculosis **OR** any other verifiable example [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ limited number of antibiotics to fight bacterial infections [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nAntibiotics are chemicals, which are produced by some species of fungi, that have the ability to disrupt the **metabolism** of bacteria ($\\checkmark$) and because of this they are used to treat **bacterial infections** ($\\checkmark$). The use of antibiotics has many benefits, but also some limitations.\n\n    *Benefits of Antibiotics*\n\n    Antibiotics are available in a **variety of forms**, such as oral pills, topical creams and intravenous injections ($\\checkmark$). They **work quickly** ($\\checkmark$) and despite being harmful to bacteria, they do not affect **eukaryotic cells** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    *Limitations of Antibiotics*\n\n    The use of antibiotics is restricted to treating bacterial infections as they have no effect on **viral** infections ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, the **overuse** of antibiotics in contexts such as agriculture, has led to development of antibiotic resistance ($\\checkmark$). This is when strains of bacteria become resistant to antibiotics, which renders them ineffective at treating the infection. Due to a **limited number** of antibiotics being currently available antibiotic resistant strains ($\\checkmark$), which are resistant to multiple antibiotics, such as **MRSA** are becoming a global health concern ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0527","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0527\n\nIn blood donation, an acute hemolytic transfusion reaction occurs when red blood cells are attacked by the recipient’s immune system. Which property of red blood cells explains this response?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ B cells recognize all red blood cells as foreign.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Foreign red blood cells have surface antibodies that interact with T cells.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Foreign red blood cells have surface antigens that stimulate antibody production.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Antibodies are unable to bind to foreign red blood cells.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAn acute hemolytic reaction occurs because red blood cells contain certain antigens on their surface. If the antigens on the donated red blood cells differ from the antigens present in the recipient’s blood cells (for example, if an individual with Type A blood receives a Type B blood donation), the donor’s immune system will recognise those red blood cells as foreign.\n\nB cells do not recognise all red blood cells as foreign (**Choice A**).  B cells only recognize red blood cells that have antigens different from those already in the body. Red blood cells have surface antigens, not surface antibodies (**Choice B**). Antibodies bind to the antigens on foreign red blood cells (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0653","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0653\n\nWhich is true for a zoonotic disease?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ It is caused by a pathogen that can cross species barriers\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ It is caused by a pathogen that affects domesticated animals\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ It is a disease that is caused by a virus\n\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ It is a disease that is caused by a pathogen that can only infect humans","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because zoonotic diseases are diseases that can be transmitted from one species to another, such as from chickens to humans. **Choice B** is incorrect because zoonotic diseases do not only affect domesticated animals. **Choice C** is incorrect because zoonotic diseases are not only caused by viruses. **Choice D** is incorrect because zoonotic diseases do not only affect humans."},{"ref":"BI0654","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0654\n\nWhich statement is true about B-lymphocytes in mammals?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ B-lymphocytes activate phagocytes\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ B-lymphocytes cause the release of histamine\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ T-lymphocytes activate B-lymphocytes\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Antibodies activate B-lymphocytes","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because activated helper T cells activate B lymphocytes. **Choice A** is incorrect because B-lymphocytes do not activate phagocytes. **Choice B** is incorrect because B-lymphocytes do not cause the release of histamine. **Choice D** is incorrect because antibodies are produced by B lymphocytes after they are activated."},{"ref":"BI0655","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0655\n\nWhich of the following statement(s) is/are true for plasma cells?\n\n-    I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Clones of B lymphocytes\n-    II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ Produce large numbers of antibodies\n-    III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ Have extensive rough endoplasmic reticulum\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$  I, II and III\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because all statements are true. Plasma cells are clones of B lymphocytes, produce large numbers of antibodies and have extensive rough endoplasmic reticulum."},{"ref":"BI0656","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0656\n\nWhich of these is not true of antibodies?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ They are globular proteins\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Antibodies have Y-shaped structures made up of two main heavy chains\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ They can make pathogens more detectable by phagocytes\n\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Each antibody has one binding site","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAn antibody is a Y-shaped globular protein. The two “arms” of the Y each contain a binding site that can attach to a specific antigen. Therefore, one antibody molecule can bind to two identical antigens at the same time. **Choice D** is an false statement, and so is the correct answer to this question.\n\n**Choice A**  is a true statement, and so is an incorrect answer to this question. Antibodies are globular proteins with a compact, rounded shape. Their shape helps them dissolve and move easily in blood plasma.\n\n**Choice B** is a true statement, and so is an incorrect answer to this question. Antibodies are made from 2 heavy chains and 2 light chains. These chains join together to form the characteristic Y shape.\n\n**Choice C**  is a true statement, and so is an incorrect answer to this question. When antibodies bind to antigens, they help the immune system remove or destroy foreign substances in several ways. They can neutralise toxins and viruses, cause pathogens to clump together, mark cells or pathogens for phagocytes to engulf more easily, and activate the complement system, which can damage cell membranes."},{"ref":"BI0657","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0657\n\nThis diagram shows the interaction between a helper T-cell and a B-lymphocyte.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Defence%20Against%20Disease/images/activated%20B%20cell%20by%20helper%20T%20cell.png\" alt=\"activated B cell by helper T cell\">\n\nWhat is X?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Fibrin\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Antibody\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Antigen\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Killer T-cell","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because antigens activate Helper T-cells when they bind to them and these then activate B lymphocytes. **Choice A** is incorrect because fibrin is an insoluble, fibrous protein that forms a mesh-like network during blood clotting; it is not involved in B-lymphocyte activation. **Choice D** is incorrect because killer T-cells are white blood cells that destroy infected body cells."},{"ref":"BI0660","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0660\n\nThe diagram shows a red blood cell with the A antigen.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Defence%20Against%20Disease/images/BI0660.png\" alt=\"BI0660\">\n\nWhich statement explains why a blood transfusion with blood group A cannot be given to someone with blood group O?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Type A antigens bind to O blood cells\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Type A antigen stimulates the production of anti-A antibodies in people with blood group O\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Type O antibodies bind to A antigen\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ People with blood group O do not produce antibodies against blood group A","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because individuals with blood group O produce anti-A antibodies in response to a transfusion with blood group A. **Choice A** is incorrect because the A antigen stimulates the production of anti-A antibodies in people with blood group O. **Choice C** is incorrect because it is anti-A antibodies that bind to the A antigen. **Choice D** is incorrect because blood group O does produce the correct antibodies for blood group A."},{"ref":"BI0661","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0661\n\nWhich human disease was the first to be eradicated by vaccination?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Polio\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Cholera\n\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Smallpox\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Bubonic plague","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because smallpox was the first human disease eradicated by vaccination. **Choice A** is incorrect because polio has not been fully eradicated. **Choice B** is incorrect because cholera has not been eradicated. **Choice D** is incorrect because there is no vaccine for bubonic plague."},{"ref":"BI0662","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0662\n\nWhich is a correct description of antigens?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ A protein produced by B lymphocytes\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$  A compound that binds to pathogens\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ A chemical that stimulates antibody production\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$A chemical produced by a hybridoma cell","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because antigens are chemicals that stimulate the production of antibodies. **Choice A** is incorrect because B lymphocytes produce antibodies, not antigens. **Choice B** is a description of antibodies, which binds to antigens so is incorrect. **Choice D** is incorrect because hybridoma cells produce antibodies, not antigens."},{"ref":"BI0663","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0663\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe graph shows the typical immune response to two doses of a vaccine.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Defence%20Against%20Disease/images/BI0663.png\" alt=\"BI0663\">\n\n1. State which type of molecule stimulates antibody production. [1 mark]\n2. Distinguish between the primary and secondary responses. [3 marks]\n\n3. Explain how vaccination leads to long-term immunity. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ antigens [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ a smaller number of antibodies are produced by the primary response **WHEREAS** a larger number of antibodies are produced by the secondary response [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the primary response is slower **BUT** the secondary response is faster [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ primary response occurs following first contact with the pathogen **OR** antigen **WHEREAS** secondary response occurs following second contact with the pathogen **OR** antigen [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nThe primary response is **slower** and produces a **smaller number** of antibodies, whereas a **larger number** ($\\checkmark$) of antibodies are produced more **rapidly** ($\\checkmark$) by the secondary response. The primary response occurs after an organism's **first exposure to the antigen**, whereas the secondary response occurs during **the second exposure** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **three** of the following (**AT LEAST** one point must link vaccination to long-term immunity):\n\n *Vaccination*\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ vaccines contain an attenuated version of the pathogen **OR** antigens of the pathogen [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the vaccine triggers an immune response that triggers antibody production [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ B and T lymphocytes are activated [1 mark]\n\n *Long-term immunity*\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ memory cells are produced, which give long-term immunity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ long-lived memory B cells, **OR** other correct memory cells, circulate in the blood [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ memory cells result in the production of antibodies at a greater rate if there is a second exposure to the same pathogen [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ antibodies specific to antigen continue to circulate in the blood [1 mark]\n\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n**Vaccines contain antigens** for a specific pathogen ($\\checkmark$). Vaccines give long-term immunity because they trigger an **immune response which produces antibodies** ($\\checkmark$) and because **memory B cells** are also produced which give long-term immunity ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0664","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0664\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nAntibodies are proteins with a specific shape that are produced by B lymphocytes.\n\nDescribe how antibodies defend against pathogens. [3 marks]","markScheme":"any **three** of the following:\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ prevent pathogens attaching to body cells [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ opsonisation **OR** help phagocytes to recognise pathogens      [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ complement activation **OR** cause pathogens to lyse    [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ cause agglutination **OR** cause pathogens to stick together so they cannot enter cells    [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ some antibodies neutralise ** bind to toxins that are produced by pathogens    [1 mark]\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nAntibodies bind to pathogens and **prevent pathogens from attaching to body cells** ($\\checkmark$). Some antibodies **neutralise toxins produced by pathogens** ($\\checkmark$). **Opsonisation** is when antibodies **help phagocytes to recognise pathogens** which allows more of them to be engulfed ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0665","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0665\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the importance of international cooperation in fighting infectious diseases. [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline the role of monoclonal antibodies in home pregnancy tests. [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain the specific immune response to a pathogen in terms of antibodies and lymphocytes.    [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ infectious diseases can cross borders [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if a disease is found in one population it can spread to other populations [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ herd immunity reduces rate of transmission [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ knowledge needs to be shared internationally so that all countries can take measures to prevent disease transmission **OR** so that countries know the best ways to prevent disease transmission [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ resources such as vaccinations and other medications need to be shared between richer and poorer countries  [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nInternational cooperation is needed to fight infectious diseases because diseases **cross borders** ($\\checkmark$). **Knowledge** ($\\checkmark$) and **vaccines** **need to be shared**  ($\\checkmark$) between richer and poorer countries so that all countries can **effectively reduce rates of transmission**.\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ home pregnancy tests contain monoclonal antibodies to hCG [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ home pregnancy tests have a test site and control site    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the urine of a pregnant woman contains hCG [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if hCG is present, it binds to monoclonal antibodies [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the monoclonal antibodies to hCG have a dye attached    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if there are antibodies with hCG attached, they will bind at the test site producing a line     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ monoclonal antibodies with dye attached bind to the control site regardless of the presence of hCG [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a line at the control site shows that the test is valid [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nHome pregnancy tests contain monoclonal antibodies for the hormone **hCG** ($\\checkmark$), which is found in the **urine of pregnant women** ($\\checkmark$). If hCG is present it **binds to monoclonal antibodies** ($\\checkmark$) near the start of the strip. The antibodies move along the strip with the urine. If there are antibodies with hCG attached, they bind to the **test site producing a line** ($\\checkmark$), which indicates a positive result. The test is considered to be valid if there is a line appearing at the **control site** ($\\checkmark$), regardless of the pregnancy status.\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ B lymphocytes and T lymphocytes are **both** part of the specific immune response [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ B lymphocytes and T lymphocytes **both** react to antigens [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ B lymphocytes and T lymphocytes **both** produce memory cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ B lymphocytes produce antibodies that are specific to one antigen [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ helper T cells activate B lymphocytes **OR** activated helper T cells activate B lymphocytes     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ B lymphocytes divide, producing plasma B cells     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibodies are produced by plasma B cells  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibodies bind to antigens on pathogens [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibodies cause agglutination **OR** antibodies cause pathogens to stick together [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibodies help phagocytes bind to and engulf pathogens [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibodies prevent pathogens from attaching to host cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibodies cause the destruction of pathogens [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ memory cells give long-term immunity [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n**B lymphocytes and T lymphocytes** are both part of the specific immune response ($\\checkmark$) and **both react to antigens** ($\\checkmark$). B lymphocytes and T lymphocytes **both produce memory cells** ($\\checkmark$). **Helper T cells** activate **B lymphocytes** ($\\checkmark$) which divide, producing memory B cells and **plasma B cells** ($\\checkmark$). Plasma B cells produce **antibodies** ($\\checkmark$). **Antibodies bind to antigens** ($\\checkmark$). Antibodies help **phagocytes** bind to and **engulf pathogens** ($\\checkmark$), **prevent pathogens from attaching to host cells** ($\\checkmark$) and cause **agglutination of pathogens** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0794","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0794\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe diagram shows how the effect of growing bacteria with different antibiotics can be investigated. A study was conducted to test the degree of antibiotic resistance that bacteria from a hospital door handle had developed. Measurements of the zone of inhibition were taken every 3 days, for 15 days.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Defence%20Against%20Disease/images/BI0793A.png\" alt=\"BI0793A\">\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Defence%20Against%20Disease/images/BI0794B.png\" alt=\"BI0794B\">\n\n1. State which antibiotic is the least effective at preventing bacterial growth. [1 mark]\n\n2. State two variables that must be controlled in this experiment, other than those already given. [2 marks]\n\n3.\n 1. Identify the anomalous result from the study. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Suggest how the anomalous result may have arisen. [1 mark]\n\n4. Explain how natural selection can lead to antibiotic resistance in bacteria. [2 marks]\n\n5. The researchers suggested that vancomycin could be used more extensively to treat bacterial infections. Discuss to what extent you agree with this. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ amoxicillin [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The least effective antibiotic is **amoxicillin** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature that bacteria are incubated at [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the type of agar [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the density of bacteria plated [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the concentration of antibiotic [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the humidity that the bacteria are incubated at [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the concentration of oxygen supplied to the bacteria [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the size of the petri dish [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sterilisation of equipment **AND** area that bacteria are studied in [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ size of the disc soaked in antibiotic [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Two variables that must be controlled in this experiment are the **temperature** ($\\checkmark$) that the petri dishes are stored in and the **type of agar** ($\\checkmark$) that the bacteria are grown on.\n\n3.\n 1. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 21.9 mm:sup[2] **OR** cefaclor on day 12 [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The anomalous result is **21.9** mm:sup[2] ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 2. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ not measuring the diameter **OR** radius accurately [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ not calculating the area of the zone of inhibition accurately [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ contamination [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ natural variability in bacterial growth [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The anomalous result may have arisen from the researcher **not measuring the diameter accurately** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ bacterium within a population develops a random mutation that confers resistance [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mutation spreads through population through transfer of plasmid **OR** horizontal transfer **OR** reproduction **OR** conjugation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ bacteria without the mutation **OR** that are susceptible to antibiotics are killed by antibiotics, reducing competition for those with the resistance gene [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ bacteria with the mutation survive and breed [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n A bacterium within a population develops a **random mutation** which confers resistance ($\\checkmark$) to antibiotics, and this mutation can **spread** throughout the population through **horizontal transfer** ($\\checkmark$), so that more bacteria are resistant to antibiotics. The bacteria with resistance are more likely to **survive** ($\\checkmark$) when treated with antibiotics, and so can go on to **reproduce** so that the population of antibiotic-resistant bacteria increases.\n\n5. Any **three** of the following:\n\n **Agree because…**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it had the largest zone of inhibition over the whole study [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the zone of inhibition didn’t change drastically in size over the 15 days [1 mark]\n\n **Disagree because…**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ if it is overused, it could lead to more resistance to vancomycin in a range of bacteria [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ only bacteria from door handles were tested [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the study does not include repeats [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the study only used a limited number of antibiotics [1 mark]\n\n *Answer must include at least one point from agree and one point from disagree to achieve full marks*\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The evidence from the study supports the suggestion of the researchers that vancomycin could be used more extensively to treat bacterial infections as it had a **larger zone of inhibition** compared to the rest of the antibiotics ($\\checkmark$) across the whole study. However, it shouldn’t be used to treat bacterial infections as **only bacteria from door handles** were tested ($\\checkmark$) and other locations in the hospital may have bacteria that are resistant to vancomycin. Also, its overuse could result in bacteria eventually **developing resistance** to it ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1120","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1120\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nFlu vaccinations are administered to millions of people every year and are often targeted at vulnerable members of the population, such as the elderly. To ascertain the effectiveness of vaccinations at reducing the spread of viruses, a study was conducted in which researchers investigated the effect of vaccination on the viral load of aerosols from pigs.\n\nIn the experiment, pigs were categorised and treated according to the following table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Defence%20Against%20Disease/images/BI1120.png\" alt=\"BI1120\">\n[Source: Adapted from Neira, V., Allerson, M., Corzo, C., Culhane, M., Rendah,l A., Torremorell, M. (2018). *Detection of influenza A virus in aerosols of vaccinated and non-vaccinated pigs in a warm environment*. PLoS ONE 13(5): e0197600. Retrieved May 24, 2023, from https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0197600. Copyright free]\n\nAt daily intervals after inoculation with the Influenza A virus, the viral load of aerosols from different orifices was sampled. Results are shown.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Defence%20Against%20Disease/images/BI1120b.png\" alt=\"BI1120b\">\n[Source: Adapted from Neira, V., Allerson, M., Corzo, C., Culhane, M., Rendah,l A., Torremorell, M. (2018). *Detection of influenza A virus in aerosols of vaccinated and non-vaccinated pigs in a warm environment*. PLoS ONE 13(5): e0197600. Retrieved May 24, 2023, from https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0197600. Copyright free]\n\nThe proportion of Influenza A negative pigs was also measured throughout the duration of the experiment.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Defence%20Against%20Disease/images/BI1120c.png\" alt=\"BI1120c\">\n[Source: Adapted from Neira, V., Allerson, M., Corzo, C., Culhane, M., Rendah,l A., Torremorell, M. (2018). *Detection of influenza A virus in aerosols of vaccinated and non-vaccinated pigs in a warm environment*. PLoS ONE 13(5): e0197600. Retrieved May 24, 2023, from https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0197600. Copyright free]\n\n1.\n    1. Suggest one environmental variable which could influence viral transmission between pigs that should be kept constant.     [1 mark]\n\n    2. State two ways in which the vaccination could be administered.     [1 mark]\n\n    3. Describe how the pig's non-specific immune system would recognise the inert viruses present in the vaccine as foreign molecules.     [2 marks]\n\n2.\n    1. Suggest two safety precautions that investigators could take to limit their health risks during sample collection.          [1 mark]\n\n    2. Describe an advantage to using pigs instead of humans in this experiment. [1 mark]\n\n3. State the advantage of increasing the number of oral aerosol samples taken to 9.      [1 mark]\n\n4. From the data, discuss whether vaccinating pigs with different strains of virus effectively reduces the transmission of viral particles in aerosols. Justify your answer.          [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  temperature  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  humidity    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  airflow **OR** ventilation        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  ground substrate             [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **temperature** of the environment should be kept constant ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ orally **OR** in food    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ intranasally **OR** nasal spray    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ injection **OR** intravenously    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The vaccine could be **injected intravenously** ($\\checkmark$) or given to the pigs **orally** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    3.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antigens are present on the surface of viruses **OR** viral particles **OR** viral protein coats [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ immune system **OR** immune cells **OR** macrophages **OR** phagocytes recognise the antigen as foreign **OR** non-self [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Within the vaccine, the attenuated virus particles have **antigens** ($\\checkmark$) on the surface of their protein coats. Once administered, the cells of the immune system, such as **macrophages**, recognise the antigens as being **non-self** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ wear gloves **OR** a mask **OR** protective clothing **OR** personal protective equipment (PPE)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ dispose of needles **OR** medical equipment safely     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ aseptic techniques **OR** sanitise equipment **OR** investigators         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vaccinate investigators [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Precautions could include wearing **protective clothing** ($\\checkmark$), such as a mask or gloves, and then safely **disposing of the equipment** ($\\checkmark$), such as needles, after administration.\n\n2.\n    2.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fewer ethical considerations compared to humans        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pigs are genetically **OR** physiologically similar to humans **OR** both are mammals     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ less resources required to care for pigs     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ susceptible to many of the same diseases **OR** illnesses, **OR** viruses         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ shorter life cycles so can be studied throughout their life        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Pigs share many **similarities** ($\\checkmark$) with humans and are **susceptible** to many of the **same viruses** ($\\checkmark$), such as influenza.\n\n3.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ increases the reliability of the results **OR** the experiment    [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Increasing the number of samples to 9 would increase the **reliability** of the results ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **three** of the following (answer must include effective, not effective **AND** conclusion):\n\n    **Not Effective**    \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** VAC-HETMU groups still test positive in oral samples **OR** nasal samples [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** VAC-HETMU group had a greater proportion of positive oral **OR** nasal samples compared to VAC-HETMO [1 mark]\n\n    **Effective** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** VAC-HETMU groups have fewer positive samples compared to the NON-VAC group [1 mark]\n\n    **Conclusion** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Mark awarded for the concluding statement with justification [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Treating pigs with different strains is **not effective** because there are still **positive oral and nasal samples in the VAC-HETMU group** ($\\checkmark$). However, there are **fewer positive samples** in these groups compared to the **NON-VAC group** ($\\checkmark$). In conclusion, the treatment is **not effective** because there are more positive samples in the **VAC-HETMU group** compared to the **VAC-HOMO group** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1159","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1159\n\nThe following graph shows the incidences (number of cases/10,000 admissions) of multidrug-resistant *Streptococcus dysgalactiae* obtained from six hospitals in the Kyoto-Shiga region of Japan between 2011 and 2020.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Defence%20Against%20Disease/images/BI1159.png\" alt=\"BI1159\">\n[Source: Adapted from Shinohara, K., Murase, K., Tsuchido, Y., Noguchi, T., Yukawa, S., Yamamoto, M.Nagao, M. (2023). *Clonal Expansion of Multidrug-Resistant Streptococcus dysgalactiae Subspecies equisimilis Causing Bacteremia, Japan, 2005–2021*. Emerging Infectious Diseases, 29(3), 528-539. Retrieved September 20 2023, from https://doi.org/10.3201/eid2903.221060. CC0]\n\nWhich of the following statements is **NOT** true regarding the above data?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The incidence of multi-resistant infections roughly doubled between 2014 and 2015.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 2012 represents the lowest incidence of multi-resistant infections throughout the study.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The incidences of multi-resistant infections decreased in 2012, 2014, 2016, and 2018 compared to the previous year.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ There were approximately 8 cases/10,000 admissions in 2018.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThere was a decrease in the incidence of cases compared to the previous year in 2012, 2014, 2016, and 2019. There was an increase in 2018, not a decrease as stated in **Choice C**. This makes **Choice C** false and therefore the correct answer.\n\n**Choices A, B**, and **D** are all true according to the graph."},{"ref":"BI1182","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1182\n\nThe diagram shows a red blood cell with the A antigen. What is the likely result if someone with blood group A is transfused with blood group AB?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Defence%20Against%20Disease/images/BI1182.png\" alt=\"BI1182\">\n\n| | Result | Reason |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Rejection | An immune response will be triggered in the recipient, where they will make anti-A antibodies |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Rejection | An immune response will be triggered in the recipient, where they will make anti-B antibodies |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Acceptance | Both the recipient and donor have A antigens present on their red blood cells |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Acceptance | Half of the red blood cells from the donor have A antigens on their surface |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nRed blood cells from people who have blood group A only have A antigens on their surface so the immune system recognises any other antigens as foreign. Red blood cells from people who have blood group AB, have both A and B antigens on their surface, therefore the B antigens are recognised as foreign, triggering an immune response and the recipient makes anti-B antibodies. **Choice B** is correct.\n\nThe recipient does not make anti-A antibodies, as the A antigen is not recognised as foreign; **Choice A** is incorrect. **Choices C** and **D** are incorrect, as blood group AB would not be accepted."},{"ref":"BI1234","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1234\n\nWhich is **not** a method to prevent antibiotic resistance?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Development of new antibiotics\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Patients taking the full course of antibiotics\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Farmers not using antibiotics in animal feeds\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Doctors prescribing antibiotics for viral infections","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAntibiotics are only effective against bacteria, not viruses. If antibiotics are taken for viral infections, then they are being overused, and bacteria are more likely to develop antibiotic resistance; **Choice D**  is correct.\n\nThe development of new antibiotics can prevent antibiotic resistance as new antibiotics may be more effective against bacteria that have developed resistance to older antibiotics; **Choice A**  is incorrect. Patients taking the full course of antibiotics means bacteria are less likely to develop antibiotic resistance; **Choice B**  is incorrect. Farmers not using antibiotics in animal feeds, means that antibiotics are not being overused, which lowers the chances of bacteria in livestock developing antibiotic resistance; **Choice C**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1357","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1357\n\nWhich factor does **not** contribute to the transmission of zoonotic diseases between animal and human species?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Destruction of animal habitats\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Humans and animals being bitten by infected mosquitoes\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Humans and animals being supplied with preventative antibiotics\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Humans eating animal byproducts","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nZoonotic diseases are diseases that can be transmitted from animals to humans. They may be bacterial, viral, or parasitic. Although antibiotics do not affect viral or parasitic infections, providing preventative antibiotics to humans and animals can help reduce the spread of bacterial zoonotic diseases. This practice can be beneficial in controlling disease transmission, especially in high-risk settings; **Choice C**  is correct.\n\nDestruction of animal habitats can result in animals being in closer proximity to humans, which can increase the risk of transmission of zoonotic diseases between animals and humans; **Choice A** is incorrect. Infected mosquitoes can transmit diseases like malaria, Zika, and West Nile virus from animals to humans. Mosquitoes can act as vectors, carrying the pathogens from one host to another during their blood-feeding process.; **Choice B**  is incorrect. Humans eating infected animal byproducts in raw forms e.g. unpasteurised milk or undercooked meat, can spread zoonotic diseases between animals and humans; **Choice D**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1358","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1358\n\nWhich of the following can contribute to increasing the chance of herd immunity within a population?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ A high proportion of the population being vaccinated\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Quarantining people who are infected with the disease \n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Providing free preventive drugs to the whole population\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Closing international borders so that no infected people can travel between countries","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nHerd immunity is achieved when a large proportion of a population is immune to a disease, either through vaccination or previous infection. This means that there is a much lower risk of someone who is susceptible to the disease becoming infected, as the chances of disease transmission are lowered. Vaccinating a large proportion of the population increases the chance of achieving herd immunity; **Choice A**  is correct.\n\n**Choice B**  is incorrect  because quarantine is a containment strategy. While it reduces the spread of the pathogen, it does not increase the number of immune individuals in the population.\n\n**Choice C**  is incorrect because preventive drugs (like antivirals or antibiotics) treat or prevent the symptoms or infection, but do not typically trigger the production of memory cells and antibodies required for long-term immunity.\n\n**Choice D**  is incorrect because closing international borders is a \"containment\" or \"mitigation\" strategy. While it limits the movement of a pathogen between populations, it does not change the biological immune status of the individuals within those populations. Herd immunity can only be built when individuals develop an active immune response (memory cells), which border closures do not provide."},{"ref":"BI1359","marks":"10","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1359\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe graphs show the global number of daily new confirmed deaths, and total confirmed deaths from COVID-19 between December 2019 and July 2020.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Defence%20Against%20Disease/images/BI1359.JPG\" alt=\"BI1359\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Our World in Data. (2020, March 14). File: daily and total confirmed covid-19 deaths, World.svg. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved August 14, 2023, from URL https: //commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Daily_and_total_confirmed_COVID-19_deaths,_World.svg. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n1.    Outline the trends in the graph of daily new confirmed deaths between December 2019 and July 2020. [2 marks]   \n\n    \n\n2.    Explain why the total confirmed deaths are much higher than daily new confirmed deaths. [1 mark]   \n\n3. 1.    COVID-19 has been described as a zoonotic disease. State a definition of the term $`$zoonotic disease'. [1 mark]\n\n    \n\n    2.   State the name of a zoonotic disease **other** than COVID-19. [1 mark]   \n\n4.  1. One strategy to prevent the transmission of COVID-19 is to increase the likelihood of herd immunity in the global population. State a definition for the term $`$herd immunity'. [1 mark]   \n\n    2.  Suggest one method to promote herd immunity against COVID-19 in the population. [1 mark]   \n\n    3.  Suggest why achieving herd immunity against COVID-19 may be difficult. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Any **two** of  the following\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$\n\n between December 2019 and January 2020 the daily new confirmed deaths increases OR goes from 0 to 1000 [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$\n\n daily new confirmed deaths is stable from January 2020 to March 2020 [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$\n\n from March 2020 onwards the number of daily new confirmed deaths increases rapidly [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$\n\n the number of daily new confirmed deaths reaches a peak in June 2020 **OR** peaks at 4000 [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$\n\n from June 2020 onwards the number of daily confirmed deaths decreases [1 mark]\n\n \n\n[2 marks]\n\n  :u[Sample answer]:\\\n\nThe first graph shows that between **December 2019** and **January 2020**, the daily new confirmed deaths **increases**  from 0 to 1000($\\checkmark$), but then remains **stable** between **January 2020**  and **March 2020**($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.  Any **one** of the following\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ total confirmed deaths is a cumulative value over a number of months, whereas the daily new confirmed deaths only takes into account one day [1 mark]\n\n[1 mark]\n\n  :u[Sample answer]:\\\n\nThe total confirmed deaths are much higher than the daily new confirmed deaths as the total confirmed deaths is a **cumulative** value, whereas the **daily** new confirmed deaths is a cumulative value over a **day** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.  1. Any **one** of the following\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ a disease that can be transmitted between animals and humans **OR** a disease that can cross the species barrier and affect humans [1 mark]\n\n[1 mark]\n\n  :u[Sample answer]:\\\n\n  A zoonotic disease is a disease that can be transmitted between **animals and humans** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n  3.  2. Any **one** of the following\n\n  $\\ce{ -> }$ tuberculosis **OR** TB **OR**     rabies **OR** Japanese encephalitis **OR** AIDS **OR** ebola **OR** swine $`$flu **OR** bird $`$flu **OR** Lyme disease **OR** West Nile disease **OR** leptospirosis **OR** any other zoonotic disease except COVID-19 [1 mark]\n\n  [1 mark]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\\\n\nAn example of a zoonotic disease is **tuberculosis**($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.  1. Any **one** of the following\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ when a large proportion of the population has immunity towards a disease lowering the transmission rate **OR** reducing the spread **OR** lowering the risk of someone who is susceptible to the disease contracting it [1 mark]\n\n  [1 mark]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\\\n\nHerd immunity is when a **large proportion** of the population has **immunity** towards a disease, which drastically **lowers the transmission** rate of the disease ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.  2.  Any **one** of the following\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ mass vaccinations so that enough people develop immunity [1 mark] \n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ allow the disease to spread unchecked throughout the population so that enough people develop immunity\n\n [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\\\n\nHerd immunity can be achieved by vaccinating the population for COVID-19, so enough people develop **immunity** to fight **future** infections ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.  3.  Any **three** of the following\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$\n\n might not be enough vaccines for the population [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$\n\n vaccines can be expensive to store so less wealthy countries may not be able to administer vaccinations before they expire [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$\n\n vaccines can be expensive to develop [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$\n\n people can be distrusting of vaccines **OR** reluctant to be vaccinated [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$\n\n COVID-19 virus may mutate **OR** new variants so current vaccines may not be effective long term [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$\n\n international travel can introduce the virus from areas with higher infection rates to areas with lower rates [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$\n\n there may be people with compromised immune systems, who are unable to be vaccinated [1 mark]\n\n \n\n$\\ce{ -> }$\n\n unclear how long immunity lasts **OR** waning immunity from vaccines **OR** boosters required [1 mark]\n\n \n\n[3 marks]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\\\n\nIt can be difficult to achieve herd immunity to COVID-19 because there might **not be enough vaccines** for the population, which means that **not enough people** in the population may develop immunity ($\\checkmark$) so COVID-19 is more likely to spread. Vaccinations can be **expensive** to develop. Therefore, COVID-19 may spread through populations **before vaccines are developed** ($\\checkmark$). The virus may also **mutate**, so **current vaccines** may **not** be **effective in the long term** ($\\checkmark$), resulting in a reduced chance of reaching herd immunity."},{"ref":"BI1362","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1362\n\nAt the height of the COVID-19 pandemic, the mean daily incidence of COVID-19 in Spain was 70 cases per 100 people, and the mean daily incidence of COVID-19 in England was 30 cases per 100 people. What was the percentage difference in the mean daily incidence between the two countries?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 65$\\%$\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 42$\\%$\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 80$\\%$\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 57$\\%$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nPercentage difference and percentage change are often confused. The percentage difference is looking at the percentage of the difference when compared to the mean between two numbers. The equation for percentage difference is (Difference in values $\\div$ Mean) $\\times$ 100$\\%$  \\\n\nThe difference in the values is: 70 $-$ 30 $=$ 40\\\n\nMean $=$ (70$+$30) $\\div$ 2 $=$ 50\\\n\nPercentage difference $=$ (40 $\\div$ 50) $\\times$ 100$\\%$ $=$ 80$\\%$.  **Choice C**  is correct.\n\n**Choice D** is the percentage change which is calculated by finding the difference between the two numbers and dividing by the original number, so here $\\{(30-70) / 70\\}$ $\\times$ 100$\\%$ $=$ $-$57$\\%$. Percentage change compares two values, an original value and a new or updated value and looks at the direction and relationship. A positive change represents an increase, and a negative change represents a decrease. Percentage change would be more useful if looking at changes in the number of incidences of COVID-19 in one country over a period of time.  **Choices A and B** are incorrect as they do not refer to percentage difference."},{"ref":"BI1374","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1374\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nThe diagram shows the results from PCR tests conducted on patients to find out who was infected with the COVID-19 virus.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Defence%20Against%20Disease/images/BI1374.png\" alt=\"BI1374\">\n\n1. Identify which patients have been infected with COVID-19.[1 mark]\n\n2. Determine, with a reason, whether COVID-19 should be treated with antibiotics. [1 mark]\n\n3. Another disease that affects humans is HIV.\n\n 1. Name one immune system cell that is targeted by HIV. [1 mark]\n\n 2. HIV is an enveloped virus. State the origin of the viral envelope. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ patients 1 **AND** 2 **AND** 4 [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Patients **1, 2** and **4** ($\\checkmark$) have been infected with COVID-19.\n\n2. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ no **BECAUSE** COVID-19 is a virus **AND** antibiotics are ineffective against viruses [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ no **BECAUSE** antibiotics are only effective against bacteria **OR** function by disrupting **OR** inhibiting the metabolism of bacteria but viruses do not have their own metabolism [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The COVID 19 virus should not be treated with antibiotics because antibiotics work by **inhibiting metabolic processes** specific to **bacteria**, so are **ineffective against viruses** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 1. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ T-cells **OR** T-lymphocytes **OR** helper T-cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ macrophages [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n A cell of the immune system targeted by HIV is a **T-lymphocyte** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n 2. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ host cell (plasma) membrane [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The viral envelope originates from the **host cell’s plasma membrane** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1403","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1403\n\nWhat features distinguish B-lymphocytes and T-lymphocytes?\n\n| | B-lymphocytes | T-lymphocytes |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Mature in the thymus | Mature in the bone marrow |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Produce antibodies | Multiply to form plasma cells |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Persist as memory cells | Activate B-lymphocytes |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Activate T-lymphocytes | Destroy infected cells |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because a small number of memory cells persist in the body following infections to rapidly respond to future pathogens. Helper T cells are a type of T-lymphocyte that activates B-lymphocytes to produce antibodies.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because B cells mature in the bone marrow while T cells mature in the thymus. **Choice B** is incorrect because T cells do not multiply to form plasma cells. **Choice D** is incorrect because B cells do not activate T-lymphocytes."},{"ref":"BI1562","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1562\n\nUpon injury, blood platelets release clotting factors, which lead to the production of thrombin. Thrombin is an enzyme that converts the protein fibrinogen, present in the blood, to fibrin, resulting in blood clotting. The graph shows the relative concentrations of these substances following an injury.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Defence%20Against%20Disease/images/BI1562iii.png\" alt=\"BI1562iii\">\n\nWhat property allows these proteins to perform their functions?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ All three substances are hydrophobic.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Thrombin functions optimally at a pH of 4.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Fibrinogen and thrombin, but not fibrin, are water-soluble.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrogen bonding between these three substances leads to blood clotting.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because both thrombin and fibrinogen are water-soluble proteins. This allows fibrinogen to be present in the blood and easily transported to the injured site when needed. As thrombin is released, it catalyses the conversion of fibrinogen to fibrin. Since fibrin is an insoluble, fibrous protein, it creates a physical barrier over the wound, which then facilitates clotting.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because fibrinogen is hydrophilic while thrombin and fibrin are somewhat hydrophobic. In essence, fibrinogen and thrombin are hydrophilic and remain soluble in the blood plasma, while fibrin, once formed, is a fibrous protein with hydrophobic characteristics. **Choice B** is incorrect since thrombin is optimally functional at a neutral pH similar to that of blood plasma and would likely denature at low pH values. Although hydrogen bonding between fibrin molecules does occur, **Choice D** is incorrect since these three substances do not all interact with each other simultaneously to form hydrogen bonds. Fibrinogen interacts with thrombin to form an enzyme-substrate complex, which involves hydrogen bonds among other interactions. Once fibrin is produced, it is released from thrombin and does not form hydrogen bonds with it. Instead, fibrin molecules interact with each other, including forming hydrogen bonds, to create a stable clot."},{"ref":"BI1593","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1593\n\nWhich of the following diagrams correctly depicts the steps required for antibody production in mammals?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A. | | $\\hspace{2cm}$ | |B.|\n| |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Defence%20Against%20Disease/images/BI1593A.png\" alt=\"BI1593A\"> | | | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Defence%20Against%20Disease/images/BI1593B.png\" alt=\"BI1593B\">|\n|C. | | | |D. |\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Defence%20Against%20Disease/images/BI1593C.png\" alt=\"BI1593C\"> | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Defence%20Against%20Disease/images/BI1593D.png\" alt=\"BI1593D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because, following infection, helper T lymphocytes activate B lymphocytes, which differentiate into plasma cells, which produce antibodies.\n\n**Choices B** and **C** are incorrect because plasma cells do not activate B or T lymphocytes. **Choice D** is incorrect because B lymphocytes do not activate T lymphocytes."},{"ref":"BI1687","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1687\n\nThe diagram below shows part of the immune response.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Defence%20Against%20Disease/images/BI1687%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1687 fig1\">\n\nWhich row is correct in the table below?\n\n| | Name of leucocyte Z | Label of organelle that synthesises antibody proteins |\n\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | B cell | I |\n\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | B cell | II |\n\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | T cell | I |\n\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | T cell | II |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\n**Choice B** is correct because B cells differentiate into the antibody$-$producing plasma B cells when they are activated, and antibodies are synthesised by the ribosomes on the rough endoplasmic reticulum.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because although leucocyte Z is a B cell, the Golgi apparatus only modifies the proteins that form antibodies, it does not synthesise the antibody proteins.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because although antibodies are synthesised by the ribosomes on the rough endoplasmic reticulum (**II**) leucocyte Z is not a T cell.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because leucocyte Z is not a T cell and the Golgi apparatus does not synthesise the antibody proteins, it only modifies and packages them."},{"ref":"BI1689","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1689\n\nWhich of the following is an example of an innate immune response?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Antibody production\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Agglutination\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Phagocytosis\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Neutralisation","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nThe innate immune response is the body's first line of defence against pathogens, characterised by a rapid, non$-$specific reaction through physical barriers, immune cells, and various proteins and molecules. Phagocytosis is an innate immune response in which phagocytic cells engulf and digest pathogens and debris. It is a non$-$specific and immediate defence mechanism that does not require previous exposure to the pathogen. **Choice C** is the correct answer. \n\nAntibody production is part of the adaptive immune response. It involves plasma B cells producing specific antibodies against antigens after being exposed to them. **Choice A** is incorrect.\n\nAgglutination refers to the clumping of particles, such as pathogens, which is usually mediated by antibodies binding to antigens. Since it involves antibodies, it is part of the adaptive immune response. **Choice B** is incorrect.\n\nNeutralisation is also an adaptive immune response where antibodies bind to pathogens or toxins, neutralising their harmful effects. **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1690","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1690\n\nBelow is a diagram of a blood smear, as observed under a microscope.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Defence%20Against%20Disease/images/BI1690%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1690 fig1\">\n\nWhich cell labelled A to D is a neutrophil, a type of phagocytic cell?","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nNeutrophils have a lobed nucleus to make them more flexible and manoeuvre through the narrow spaces and tight junctions in tissues and blood vessels and to facilitate the engulfment and digestion of pathogens. **Choice A** therefore indicates a neutrophil cell.\n\n**Choice B** indicates a red blood cell, or erythrocyte, which does not have a nucleus.\n\n**Choice C** indicates a cell with a very large round nucleus and so is likely to be a lymphocyte.\n\n**Choice D** indicates a platelet which is a small, disc-shaped cell fragment that plays a crucial role in blood clotting and wound repair."},{"ref":"BI1691","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1691\n\nWhich of the following statements is/are correct?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n\n    | | |\n\n    | :-- | :-- |\n\n    | I. | The innate immune system is non-specific and responds the same way to all pathogens. |\n\n    | II. | The adaptive immune system can improve its response after repeated exposures to the same type of pathogen. |\n\n    | III. | The innate immune system develops memory cells after encountering a pathogen. |\n\n    | IV. | The adaptive immune system is the first line of defence against pathogens. |\n\nWhich statements are correct?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and IV\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ III and IV","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nThe innate immune system is the body's first line of defence and responds the same way to all pathogen types. It provides a rapid, non-specific response that includes physical barriers, phagocytic cells, and various proteins and molecules. **Statement I** is, therefore, true of the innate immune system. \n\nThe adaptive immune system provides a specific response to particular pathogens and involves the activation of B and T lymphocytes. It builds up a memory of pathogens encountered, allowing for a more effective and rapid response upon subsequent exposures, making **Statement II** also true. **Choice A** is, therefore, correct.\n\n**Statement III** is not true, as it is the active immune system that develops memory after encountering a specific pathogen.\n\n**Statement IV** is not true, as the adaptive immune system is a third line of defence, not the first.\n\n**Choices B, C** and **D** are all incorrect as they include either **Statement III** or **Statement IV**."},{"ref":"BI1692","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1692\n\nBelow is a table showing the differences between the innate and adaptive immune systems\n\n| Feature | Innate Immune System | Adaptive Immune System |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| Response Time | Fast | Slow |\n\n| Specificity | Broad | Specific |\n\n| Memory | None | Present |\n\n| Primary Cells | I | II |\n\nPrimary cells are the main immune cells that are present in the body under normal conditions, ready to respond to infections. What primary cells do \"I\" and \"II\" represent in the table above?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I: T and B cells, II: Phagocytes\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I: Phagocytes, II: Lymphocytes\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I: Plasma cells, II: Phagocytes\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I: Macrophages, II: Plasma cells","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nPhagocytes are the primary cells in the innate immune system, while T and B lymphocytes are the primary cells in the adaptive immune system. **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because they are reversed; T and B lymphocytes are not part of the innate immune system, and phagocytes are not the primary cells of the adaptive immune system.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because plasma cells are not part of the innate immune system; they are differentiated cells derived from B lymphocytes in the adaptive immune system, and phagocytes are not primary cells of the adaptive immune system.\n\nAlthough macrophages are primary cells of the innate immune system, **Choice D**  is incorrect because plasma cells are not considered primary cells of the adaptive immune system. T and B lymphocytes are the primary cells of the adaptive immune system. Plasma cells are differentiated effector cells derived from B lymphocytes after the B lymphocytes have been activated."},{"ref":"BI2337","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2337\n\nThe World Health Organisation has identified 95% vaccination coverage as the threshold for herd immunity for measles. Which statement best describes the consequence of 95% measles vaccination coverage?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Reduces the need for measles vaccination programs in the future\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Eradicates measles in the population\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Protects individuals who cannot be vaccinated and significantly reduces transmission of measles\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Protects all vaccinated individuals from measles","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because herd immunity at 95% measles vaccination coverage reduces the number of people who can be infected and prevents transmission of measles. Lower infection rates reduce the chance of infection for people who cannot be vaccinated, such as very young children or individuals with a compromised immune system.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because while herd immunity can reduce transmission, continued vaccination programs are necessary to maintain herd immunity. Without ongoing vaccination, immunity levels in the population will drop over time, leading to outbreaks.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because vaccination can eradicate diseases, but measles is very difficult to eradicate due to the high 95% level needed for herd immunity, and some characteristics of the disease.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because while vaccination significantly reduces the chance of infection, it is not 100% effective, and therefore does not protect all vaccinated individuals from contracting measles."}]},{"name":"Enzymes & Metabolism","questions":[{"ref":"BI0427","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0427\n\nWhich of the following statements is best depicted by the figure below?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/image.png\" alt=\"BI0427\">\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Reaction X is a representation of anabolic reactions.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Reaction X is a representation of catabolic reactions.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Reaction X is a representation of all metabolic reactions.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Reaction Y is a representation of anabolic reactions.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nReaction X represents the synthesis of a more complex compound from simpler molecules and therefore is a depiction of an anabolic reaction. **Choice A** is threfore correct.\n\nReaction X cannot be catabolic (**Choice B**) since a more complex molecule is produced. Catabolism results in simpler products compared to the reactants. Reaction X cannot represent all metabolic reactions (**Choice C**) since metabolic reactions include both anabolic and catabolic reactions while reaction X represents only anabolic reactions. Reaction Y represents a catabolic reaction and not an anabolic reaction (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0428","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0428\n\nWhich of the following processes may be described as catabolism?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Reactions between many amino acids leading to the formation of a polypeptide.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Synthesis of a starch molecule from many glucose molecules.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Digestion of a single starch molecule resulting in many molecules of maltose.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Formation of DNA molecules from nucleotides.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nThe digestion of starch is a catabolic reaction where starch molecules are broken down into maltose by hydrolysis.\n\nPeptide formation from amino acids (**Choice A**), starch synthesis from glucose (**Choice B**), and DNA synthesis from nucleotides (**Choice D**) are all examples of anabolic reactions."},{"ref":"BI0433","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0433\n\nMetabolic reactions can break down or synthesise biomolecules. Which type(s) of metabolic reactions are condensation reactions?\n\n-    I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrolysis\n\n-    II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ Catabolic\n\n-    III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ Anabolic \n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$  II only\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$  III only\n\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$  I and II only\n\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$  II and III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nCondensation reactions are anabolic synthesis reactions in which a water molecule is produced while joining two other molecules to form one larger molecule.\n\nHydrolysis (**Statement I**) is an example of a catabolic reaction. Catabolic reactions (**Statement II**) are reactions in which molecules are broken down, and this does not happen via dehydration synthesis. Anabolic reactions (**Statement III**) are reactions in which larger molecules are synthesised from smaller molecules.\n\n**Statement III** is the only true statement, making **Choice B** the correct answer to this question.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are incorrect as they all include one or more untrue statement."},{"ref":"BI0453","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0453\n\nWhich of the following macromolecules describe a typical enzyme?\n\n| | Macromolecule | Type |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| Protein | Fibrous |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Lipid | Hydrophobic |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Protein | Globular |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Lipid | Hydrophilic |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAlmost all enzymes are globular proteins and differ from fibrous proteins in that they are sensitive to pH and temperature and are mostly water soluble.\n\nFibrous proteins (**Choice A**), unlike globular proteins, are long and narrow, less sensitive to changes in pH and temperature and are generally insoluble in water. Enzymes are not lipids (**Choices B, D**). Enzymes tend to be hydrophilic, not hydrophobic since they are mostly soluble in water."},{"ref":"BI0454","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0454\n\nWhich statement about enzymes is **not** true?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Enzymes are made of one or more polypeptides curled into very specific shapes.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The active site of an enzyme can accommodate many different substrates.\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ When the substrate fits into the active site, the shape of the whole enzyme changes slightly.\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Enzymes are responsible for catalysing most metabolic reactions that occur in living cells.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe active site of an enzyme is specific to a particular substrate. All other statements are true (**Choices A**, **C** and **D**)."},{"ref":"BI0455","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0455\n\nThe following image shows the structure of the lysozyme enzyme. The darkened structure in black is the substrate and is bound at the active site.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI0455.png\" alt=\"BI0455\">\n\n[Adapted from Shafee, T. (2015, December 22). *File: enzyme structure.svg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved May 13, 2022, from https: //commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Enzyme_structure.svg. Copyright under CC-BY 4.0]\n\nWhich of the following characterises the active site of an enzyme?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ It is rigid and cannot change its shape.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    It has a binding site for a substrate(s) and catalyses reactions.\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    It is always found in the middle of globular enzymes.\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ It is changed irreversibly in shape after binding with the substrate.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe active site of an enzyme has a binding site and a catalytic site. Once the substrate moves into the active site, the enzyme changes shape to accommodate the substrate better, making the substrate change shape so it is more likely to react and form a product.\nThe active site is not rigid (**Choice A**) as it can change shape slightly to fit a substrate. The active site can be found anywhere in the protein (**Choice C**) and there can be more than one active site on an enzyme. An enzyme is not changed irreversibly by the reaction (**Choice D**). Once the reaction is completed, the enzyme reverts back to its original conformation and is ready to catalyse another reaction."},{"ref":"BI0456","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0456\n\nWhich of the following statements describes how enzymes interact with substrates to catalyse reactions?\n\n-    I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Enzymes and substrates in solution collide, and the substrate binds to the active site.\n\n-    II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ There is greater substrate movement in a reaction because the substrate is smaller than the enzyme.\n\n-    III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ When the substrate binds to the active site, the enzyme's shape changes slightly to accommodate it.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$  I, II and III\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$  I and III only\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$  II only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nEnzymes are biological catalysts made up of one or more polypeptide chains, which fold into a specific 3D shape. This shape is very important for the enzyme's function: to speed up the reaction, the enzyme must be able to bind the substrate. When the enzyme and substrate are in solution, they collide with each other, and successful collisions result in the substrate binding to the enzyme’s active site (**Statment I**). As the substrate binds, the active site changes shape slightly to better fit the substrate and stabilise the transition state  (**Statement III**). As **Statements I** and **III** are true, **Choice C** is the correct answer.\n\n**Statement II** is incorrect because, in many catabolic reactions, the substrate is larger than the enzyme, for example, starch and amylase. Also, the movement of both substrates and enzymes in a solution is primarily determined by temperature and other physical factors, rather than their relative sizes. **Choices A, B** and **D** are incorrect as they include **Statement II**."},{"ref":"BI0457","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0457\n\nEnzyme A (Enz A) was immobilised on a matrix, and its activity was compared with that of a free enzyme in solution. The graph shows the relative enzyme activity (y-axis) with varying pH (x-axis).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI0457QB.png\" alt=\"BI0457QB\">\n\nWhich of the following statements is true?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Both soluble Enz A and immobilised Enz A are inactive at pH values higher than 8.\n- II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ The immobilised Enz A demonstrates greater activity than soluble Enz A at acidic pH values.\n- III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ Immobilising Enz A did not decrease its catalytic activity.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAt pH 8 the relative activity of both soluble Enz A and immobilised Enz A is 0% so **Statement I** is true.\n\nThe immobilised Enz A shows greater relative activity than the soluble Enz A at pHs up to 7, so **Statement II** is true.\n\nThe immobilised Enz A showed greater or approximately the same activity as soluble Enz A at the full range of pHs, apart from pH 7.5, so **Statement III** is true.\n\nAs all three statements are true, so **Choice C** is the correct answer to this question.\n\n**Choices A**, **B** and **D** do not include the three statements, so they are not correct."},{"ref":"BI0458","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0458\n\nPurified enzymes are easily denatured. Which of the following is the best way to keep enzymes active for a longer period of time?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ By adding acid to the solution containing the enzyme\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    By adding alcohol to the solution containing the enzyme slowly under cold conditions\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    Keeping the enzyme in a buffer solution at an appropriate pH in a refrigerator\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    Heating the enzyme to 100$^o$C and then storing it in an appropriate container","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nBy keeping the enzyme in a buffer solution at an optimum pH, the enzyme will be stabilized and remain active for a longer period of time.\nBy adding acid to the enzyme (**Choice A**), the pH will be lowered and may denature the enzyme, which is irreversible. The pH change will alter the charge on the enzyme, which can affect its solubility. pH changes can also prevent the enzyme from binding to the substrate by changing the shape of the active site.  Alcohol (**Choice B**) can disrupt the intramolecular bonding of proteins and may alter their tertiary structure. Alcohol can also denature proteins. Heating the enzyme to higher temperatures (**Choice D**) will cause the hydrogen bonds in the enzyme to break which affects the enzyme’s structure so that it can no longer bind to its substrate."},{"ref":"BI0459","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0459\n\nWhich of the following statements describes the advantages of using immobilized enzymes instead of free enzymes in the food, pharmaceutical and detergent industries?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Immobilization increases the stability of enzymes at a wider range of pH and temperatures.\n- II. $\\hspace{.7em}$    Immobilization makes reusing and recovering enzymes a simpler process.\n- III. $\\hspace{.4em}$    Immobilization makes the process more expensive.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$    I and II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    I only\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    II only\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWhen an enzyme is immobilized, it is attached to material that is insoluble and inert.  In addition to increasing the stability of enzymes at a wider range of pH and temperatures, immobilizing also makes reusing and recovering enzymes easier and makes the process less expensive.\n\n**Choices B** and **C** do not include statements I **and** II. Immobilization makes the process more expensive (**statement III**) is not an advantage."},{"ref":"BI0460","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0460\n\nMalate is converted to oxaloacetate (OAA) by the enzyme malate dehydrogenase. It is an example of metabolic reaction in the mitochondria.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI0460FC.png\" alt=\"BI0460FC\">\n[Huai, K. (2017, February 18). *File: Krebs Cycle 8 L-Malate to oxaloacetate.svg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved May 15, 2022. From https ://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Krebs_cycle_8_l-malate_to_oxaloacetate.svg Copyright under CC-BY-SA.]\n\nWhich of the following is applicable to this reaction?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The enzyme is not affected by higher temperatures and extreme pH conditions.\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    The enzyme can catalyze both forward and backward reactions.\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    With increasing enzyme concentration in the presence of sufficient substrate, there is no change in the reaction rate.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    The active site for the forward reaction is different from the one for the backward reaction.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nMost enzymes can catalyze both forward and backward reactions with the process being dependent on the concentration of substrates, products and other factors. \\\nBoth pH and temperature affect the rate of enzyme activity (**Choice A**). \\\nIncreasing enzyme concentration will increase the rate of reaction (**Choice C**). \\\nThe active site is where the substrate binds and is also where the product is released.  The same site is used in the forward and reverse reactions (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0461","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0461\n\nHow does denaturation change the properties of an enzyme?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The enzyme structure is changed but it can still bind the substrate.\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The structure of the enzyme is changed so that it can no longer function as a catalyst.\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ There can be a change in the amino acid sequence of the protein and its tertiary structure is altered.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ It makes the enzyme more active at high temperatures.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA denatured enzyme has lost its tertiary structure. It is no longer able to function and cannot catalyze reactions. \\\nThe active site will not be able to bind to the substrate in most cases (**Choice A**).\\\nUsually, the sequence of amino acids is not altered.  A denatured enzyme has had its three-dimensional structure altered (**Choice C**).\\\nHigh temperatures, specifically temperatures above the optimum temperature for a given enzyme cause denaturation and the enzyme can no longer catalyze reactions (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0463","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0463\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nMany enzymatic reactions happen as pathways involving multiple steps and multiple enzymes.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI0463.png\" alt=\"BI0463\">\n\n1. Describe how enzymes catalyze reactions. [2 marks]\n\n2. Outline the advantages of enzyme-substrate specificity. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following (accept specific examples if the mechanism is described correctly):\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ substrate binds to the active site on the enzyme [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ space of the active site is specific **OR** complementary to the shape of the substrate [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ enzymes decrease activation energy required to change a substrate into product [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the shape of the substrate changes in a way that stresses the bonds **OR** orients substrates thus facilitating bond formation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the products are released from the active site freeing the enzyme to bind to another substrate molecule [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n The **substrate** binds to the **active site** of the enzyme ($\\checkmark$). The enzyme **changes the shape** of the substrate in a way that **stresses bonds** in the substrate ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ enzymes are specific to one substrate **OR** only one specific substrate can bind to the active site of an enzyme [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the specificity helps to control where a reaction takes place [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the specificity helps to control when reactions takes place [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the specificity helps to control which reactions should take place **OR** prevents unnecessary reactions from taking place [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n**Only one specific substrate** can bind to the **active site of an enzyme** ($\\checkmark$), and this specificity helps to **control where** the reactions take place ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0464","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0464\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n1.    Outline the different ways the rate of enzyme catalyzed reactions can be measured. [2 marks]\n\n2. Draw a graph to show the effect of temperature on the reaction rate of an enzyme. [3 marks]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/axis%20iii.png\" alt=\"axis iii\">\n\n3. State how the optimum temperature can be determined using the graph drawn in part b. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.    Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ disappearance of the substrate    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ appearance of the product [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (rate is measured) over a set period of time [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nThe rate of enzyme catalyzed reactions can be measured by the **disappearance of the substrate over time** ($\\checkmark$), or by **the appearance of the product over time** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.    All **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the shape of the graph as drawn below showing a peak     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ x-axis is labeled $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$temperature$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$ with the appropriate units     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ y-axis is labeled  $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$rate of reaction$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$, **OR** $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$relative enzyme activity$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$ \\%, **OR** $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$product formed per min$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$ [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI0464.png\" alt=\"BI0464\">\n\n3.    Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the optimum temperature is the temperature at which the relative enzyme activity is at a maximum        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ optimum temperature as shown in the graph is 40$\\degree$C    [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    **The optimum temperature** is the temperature at which the **relative activity is at its maximum** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI0464%202.png\" alt=\"BI0464 2\">"},{"ref":"BI0497","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0497\n\nIn an experiment testing how increasing temperature affects the activity of the digestive enzyme lactase, which of the following is the most appropriate dependent variable?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Change in temperature\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Variation in pH\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Change in lactase concentration\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Rate of glucose production","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe lactase enzyme hydrolyzes the disaccharide lactose into the monosaccharides glucose and galactose. So, an appropriate dependent variable for the experiment is the rate at which one of these products is formed. Therefore, the rate of formation of glucose is an appropriate dependent variable for this experiment.\n\nThe change in temperature (**Choice A**) is the independent variable (the variable being tested) and so is not an appropriate dependent variable. pH (**Choice B**) does not change when lactose is broken down into glucose and galactose, so it would not indicate the activity of the enzyme. An enzyme is not consumed in a reaction, so there will be no change in lactase concentration (**Choice C**)."},{"ref":"BI0522","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0522\n\nThe diagram shows the pathway that converts threonine to isoleucine.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI0522FC.png\" alt=\"BI0522FC\">\n\nWhat is the name and mechanism of the process through which isoleucine interacts with threonine dehydratase?\n\n| | Name | Mechanism |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Competitive inhibition | Isoleucine binds to the active site on the enzyme. |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Competitive inhibition | Isoleucine binds to an allosteric site on the enzyme. |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | End-product inhibition | Isoleucine binds to the active site on the enzyme. |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | End-product inhibition | Isoleucine binds to an allosteric site on the enzyme. |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe diagram shows that threonine undergoes a series of enzyme-catalyzed reactions to produce isoleucine. At the end of this pathway, if isoleucine accumulates it inhibits threonine dehydratase. So, the product of the pathway inhibits the conversion of threonine to isoleucine. This is an example of end-product inhibition. Isoleucine inhibits the enzyme in the pathway by binding to an allosteric site on the enzyme, not the active site. The binding of isoleucine changes the conformation of the enzyme such that the active site is no longer able to bind to threonine. **Choice D** is correct.\n\nIn competitive inhibition (**Choices A** and **B**), a substance binds to the active site on the enzyme to prevent the substrate from binding. In end-product inhibition, the product binds to the allosteric site, not the active site (**Choice C**)."},{"ref":"BI0537","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0537\n\nAn experiment tests the effect of changing substrate concentration on an enzymatic reaction. What could be the independent and dependent variables in this experiment?\n\n| | Independent variable | Dependent variable |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | temperature | substrate concentration |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | substrate concentration | rate of product formation |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | enzyme concentration | temperature |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | rate of product formation | substrate concentration |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn a scientific experiment, the independent variable is the variable that is changed or controlled, while the dependent variable is the variable that is measured as an outcome, or response, to the changes in the independent variable.\n\nIn an experiment that tests the effect of changing substrate concentration on an enzymatic reaction, the substrate concentration would be varied by the experimenter. Thus, substrate concentration is the independent variable.\n\nDuring an enzymatic reaction, the enzyme catalyzes the formation of a product from the substrate. The rate at which product forms varies based on the activity of the enzyme, meaning this rate gives a measure of enzymatic activity. So, the rate of product formation could be the dependent variable in the experiment.\n\nIn this experiment, the effect of substrate concentration on rate of reaction is being tested, so enzyme concentration and temperature are controlled variables.  (**Choice A** and **Choice C**). **Choice D** is the wrong way round."},{"ref":"BI0540","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0540\n\nThe graph shows the effect of increasing pH on the rate of an enzymatic reaction. What is occurring during the phase labelled section X in the graph?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI0540.png\" alt=\"BI0540\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The substrate has all been converted to product.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The enzyme is saturated with substrate.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The active site of the enzyme has become denatured.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The optimum pH has been reached.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe graph shows how pH affects the rate of an enzyme controlled reaction. As the pH increases, the reaction rate initially increases from 0 to reach its maximum, the optimum (**Choice D**) and then decreases again to 0 in the region labelled X. If the reaction rate is 0, the enzyme is no longer catalysing the reaction.\n\nFor an enzyme to catalyse a reaction, the substrate must be able to bind to the active site. If the pH shifts too far away from the optimum pH for that enzyme, bonds holding the tertiary structure together start to break, and the enzyme structure is disrupted. This leads to a change in shape of the active site, the substrate can no longer bind and the enzyme is said to be denatured.The area labelled X on the graph shows this.\n\nThe graph provides no information on how much substrate is remaining so **Choice A** is incorrect. To determine if the enzyme is saturated with substrate, a graph showing substrate concentration on the x-axis and rate of reaction on the y-axis is needed. When the enzyme is saturated with substrate (**Choice B**) the graph plateaus , but no such graph is provided."},{"ref":"BI0573","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0573\n\nWhat is the substrate for the enzyme lactase?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Glucose\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Galactose\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Lactose\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Fructose","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe enzyme lactase breaks down the substrate lactose into glucose and galactose.\n\nGlucose and galactose are products of the reaction (**Choices A** and **B**).\n\nFructose is not a substrate for the enzyme lactase (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0580","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0580\n\nWhat type of reaction is the formation of glucose in photosynthesis?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Catabolic\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Fermentation\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Anabolic\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrolysis","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** – Catabolic reactions involve the breakdown of large molecules into smaller molecules.\n\n**Choice B** – Fermentation refers to anaerobic respiration which occurs in the cytoplasm.\n\n**Choice C** - Anabolic reactions build complex compounds from simpler molecules. In the case of photosynthesis, the reactants carbon dioxide and water are turned into glucose and oxygen gas.\n\n**Choice D** – Hydrolysis is an example of a catabolic reaction which involves the splitting of water."},{"ref":"BI0592","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0592\n\nWhich of the following graphs best illustrates the relationship between temperature and the activity of enzymes such as lipase?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A. | | | |B.|\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI0592_A.png\" alt=\"BI0592_A\">| | | | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI0592_B.png\" alt=\"BI0592_B\"> |\n|C. | | | |D. |\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI0592_C.png\" alt=\"BI0592_C\">| | | | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI0592_D.png\" alt=\"BI0592_D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because as temperature increases, the activity of enzymes, like lipase, also increases. However, when the temperature surpasses the optimum temperature of the enzyme, the enzyme rapidly denatures and the enzyme\\'s activity rapidly decreases. \\\n**Choice A** is incorrect because above the optimum temperature the enzyme becomes denatured, thus the rate of reaction wouldn\\'t continue to increase. \\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the rate of reaction wouldn\\'t plateau, it would start to decrease when the enzyme becomes denatured. \\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the rate of reaction wouldn't be highest at the lowest temperature."},{"ref":"BI0593","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0593\n\nWhich of the following are involved in denaturation?\n\n-    I. $\\hspace{1em}$   A change to the three dimensional structure of the protein\n-    II. $\\hspace{.7em}$   A change in the amino acid sequence of the protein\n- III. $\\hspace{.4em}$   Changes in the properties of the protein\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$   I and II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$   I and III only\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$   II and III only\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$   I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDenaturation impacts the three dimensional structure of a protein (**Statement I**), which in turn can impact its properties (**Statement III**). The amino acid sequence is not changed by denaturation (**Statement II**) as the peptide bonds between amino acids remain intact. **Choice B** is correct as it contains both correct statements.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are incorrect as they all include **Statement II**."},{"ref":"BI0639","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0639\n\nWhich are correct descriptions of the enzyme catalysed metabolic pathways shown below? Letters represent various products of individual reactions.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI0639.png\" alt=\"BI0639\">\n\n| | Pathway 1 | Pathway 2 |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| Chain: Intermediate A is constantly regenerated | Chain: End product is D |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Chain: End product is D | Cyclical: Intermediate A is constantly regenerated |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Chain: Intermediate A is constantly regenerated | Cyclical: End product is D |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Cyclical: End product is D | Cyclical: Intermediate A is constantly regenerated |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\\\nBoth metabolic pathways are catalysed by a series of different enzymes. Pathway 1 proceeds in a linear manner, and the end product is D. Pathway 2 is a cycle where the various products, such as A, are constantly regenerated. \\\nPathway 1 is a chain reaction and Pathway 2 is a cyclical reaction, so **Choices A** and **D** are not correct. \\\nThe end product for Pathway 1 is D, and the products keep cycling in Pathway 2, so **Choice C** is not correct."},{"ref":"BI0640","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0640\n\nThe Calvin cycle is a series of enzyme catalysed reactions.  A diagram outlining the Calvin cycle is shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI0640.png\" alt=\"BI0640\">\n[Source: Adapted from Jones, M. (2010, February 17). *File: Calvin-cycle4.svg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved August 8, 2022, from https:// commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Calvin-cycle4.svg. Copyright under CC BY-SA 3.0.]\n\nWhat is true about the Calvin cycle and most other metabolic pathways?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ It\\'s usually the same enzyme for all steps in one metabolic pathway.\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The product of one step cannot be the substrate for subsequent steps.\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Each step of the pathway is catalysed by a different enzyme.\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ All the enzymes are regulated by competitive inhibitors.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nMetabolic pathways consist of either a chain or a series of cyclical reactions where each reaction is catalysed by a different enzyme. \\\nThe same enzyme **is not** used for each reaction in a metabolic pathway (**Choice A**). \\\nThe product of one reaction or step **is** the substrate for the next or subsequent step in the pathway (**Choice B**). \\\nAlthough some enzymes are regulated by competitive inhibitors, this is not true for all enzymes in a metabolic pathway (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0641","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0641\n\nEnzymes play a role in catalysing reactions. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n-    I. $\\hspace{1em}$ The substrate is temporarily provided with energy before being converted to a product.\n-    II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ Enzymes reduce the activation energy needed for the reaction.\n-    III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ The substrate binds to the active site making it easier for bonds to be broken or formed.\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$  I only\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$  I, II only\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$  I, III only\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$  I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn an enzyme-catalysed reaction, the substrate first binds to the enzyme's active site, as described in **Statement III**. This binding positions the substrate in the correct orientation and stresses the bonds in the substrate, making it easier for the bonds to be broken or formed. Once the substrate is bound to the active site, the enzyme lowers the activation energy required for the reaction to proceed. **Statement II** is true because enzymes reduce the activation energy by providing an alternative reaction pathway with a lower energy requirement, allowing the reaction to proceed more efficiently at lower temperatures. **Statement I** is true because, in reaching the transition state, the substrate is temporarily provided with energy, which helps break or form bonds. This is the activation energy needed to drive the reaction forward, and the enzyme helps reduce the amount of energy needed to reach this state. Once the reaction occurs, the product is formed and released from the active site.\n\n**Choice D** is therefore correct because it includes all three true statements.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **C** are incorrect because they do not include all three statements."},{"ref":"BI0642","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0642\n\nWhich of the following describes the role of inhibitors in enzyme-catalysed reactions?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Activates the enzyme involved in the reaction.\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Facilitates binding of the substrate to the active site.\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Speeds up the rate of reaction.\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Reduces the rate of reaction.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nInhibitors  prevent enzymes from catalysing reactions and therefore decrease the rate of enzyme catalysed reactions.\\\nInhibitors do not activate enzymes, thereby increasing the rate of reaction (**Choice A**).\\\nInhibitors do not help the substrate bind to the active site (**Choice B**).  In fact they usually prevent the substrate from binding to the active site.\\\nInhibitors decrease the rate of reaction, they do not speed it up (**Choice C**)."},{"ref":"BI0643","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0643\n\nThere are several ways in which inhibitors work.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the inhibitor in the image below?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI0643.png\" alt=\"BI0643\">\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Competitive inhibitor\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Non-competitive inhibitor\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ End-product inhibitor\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Cofactor","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIt is a competitive inhibitor because it binds to the active site which prevents the substrate from binding.\\\nNon-competitive inhibitors (**Choice B**) do not bind to the active site. They bind at the allosteric site which alters the structure of the active site. This prevents the substrate from binding to the active site.\\\nEnd product inhibitors (**Choice C**) also regulate enzyme activity by binding at an allosteric site.\\\nA cofactor (**Choice D**) is usually a non-protein molecule reversibly bound to the enzyme. Cofactors are not inhibitors and can be inorganic (e.g. zinc or iron)."},{"ref":"BI0644","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0644\n\nThe graph below shows the rate of reaction of an enzyme with increasing substrate concentration. Similar concentrations of chemicals A, B, C and D were added independently to the reaction, and the activity was monitored for each of the reactions. The curve marked **A** coincides with the activity of the enzyme with no inhibitor added.\n\nWhich of the chemicals is likely to be a non-competitive inhibitor?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI0644.png\" alt=\"BI0644\">\n\n$\\small{\\textrm{}}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA non-competitive inhibitor does not compete with the substrate for the binding site, it slows down reactions by binding to the allosteric site.\n\\\nIncreasing the concentration of the substrate does not prevent the binding of the inhibitor, so the same proportion of inhibition is maintained at all concentrations of substrate as seen in curve C. This shows that inhibitor C is binding at a different site than the substrate and therefore is a non-competitive inhibitor. **Choice C** is correct.\n\\\nCurve A gives the same results as those obtained when there is no inhibitor present in the reaction. **Choice A** is incorrect. It is likely that inhibitor A does not bind to the enzyme under investigation.\n\\\nCurve B is the result of the presence of a competitive inhibitor. Increases in substrate concentration increase the rate of reaction since the substrate and inhibitor (chemical B) bind to the same site on the enzyme. **Choice B** is incorrect.\n\\\nCurve D increases the rate of reaction as compared to A (with no inhibitor present) and could be an activator for the reaction but not an inhibitor. **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0645","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0645\n\nA metabolic pathway that converts threonine to the end-product isoleucine consists of five reactions. Threonine is converted to various intermediate products sequentially by different enzymes before it is converted to isoleucine by the last enzyme in the pathway.\n\nIn the following diagram, the enzymes have been labelled as E1, E2, E3, E4 and E5.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI0645FCiii.png\" alt=\"BI0645FCiii\">\n\nWhich of the following shows how this pathway is regulated?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Isoleucine inhibits E1, the enzyme that catalyses the first step of the pathway.\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Isoleucine inhibits both E1 and E2, the enzymes that catalyse the first two steps.\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Isoleucine inhibits E5, the enzyme that catalyses the last step.\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Isoleucine inhibits all the enzymes in the pathway.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIsoleucine, the end product, inhibits E1 (threonine dehydratase) by binding to the allosteric site of the enzyme. E1 catalyses the first reaction of the pathway. When there is an excess of the end-product isoleucine, it inhibits the first enzyme and prevents the buildup of intermediate products.\\\nIsoleucine inhibits E1 but not E2, so **Choice B** is incorrect.\\\nIsoleucine does not inhibit E5, so **Choice C** is incorrect.\\\nIsoleucine does not inhibit all the enzymes in the pathway, only E1. **Choice D** is therefore not correct."},{"ref":"BI0646","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0646\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding regulation by end-product inhibition?\n\n-    I. $\\hspace{1em}$ The end product binds to the allosteric site of the enzyme.\n-    II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ The binding of the end product is reversible.\n-    III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ End product inhibition is an example of negative feedback regulation.\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II only\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, III only\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn many metabolic reactions, the product of the last reaction acts as an inhibitor and binds to the allosteric site of the enzyme that catalyses the first reaction in the pathway (**Statement I**). The binding is reversible, and when the end product is removed, the enzyme goes back to its original conformation where the active site can bind to the substrate (**Statement II**). It is an example of negative feedback. When there is an excess of the product, the pathway is switched off. When there is less of the end product, the pathway is switched on by the detachment of the end product from the enzyme that catalyses the first reaction (**Statement III**).\n\nOnly **Choice D** has all the correct statements mentioned (I, II and III)."},{"ref":"BI0647","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0647\n\nWhich of the following statements correctly describe(s) how penicillin helps fight infections?\n\n-    I. $\\hspace{1em}$ It involves the lysing of viral particles.\n\n-    II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ It involves the inhibition of transpeptidase.\n\n-    III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ It prevents peptide links between polysaccharide chains.\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nPenicillin is an antibiotic, not an antiviral, so it targets bacteria, not viruses; **Statement I** is false.\n\nBacterial cell walls are made of peptidoglycan, composed of polysaccharide chains cross-linked by peptides. Penicillin prevents the formation of peptide cross-links between these polysaccharide chains by inhibiting transpeptidase. This weakens the cell wall, leading to osmotic lysis of the bacterium; **Statements II** and **III**  are true.\n\nTherefore, **Choice B** is the correct answer because it only includes the two true **Statements II** and **III**.\n\n**Choices A** and **C** are incorrect because they are missing one of the true statements. **Choice D** is incorrect because it includes the false **Statement I**."},{"ref":"BI0648","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0648\n\nWhich of the following would **not** be useful in determining the rate of an enzyme-catalyzed reaction?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Mass of the substrate used per unit of time.\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Volume of product formed per unit of time.\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Mass of product formed per unit of time.\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Mass of enzyme used per unit of time.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe mass of enzyme added remains the same throughout because the enzyme acts as a catalyst and does not get used up during the reaction.\nAll other choices (**Choices A**, **B** and **C**) are used in obtaining the rate of an enzyme-catalysed reaction since they involve measuring the substrate used or product formed over a specified amount of time."},{"ref":"BI0649","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0649\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nThe image below shows the catalytic site of an enzyme. The substrate is shown in black and is bound to the active site.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI0649.png\" alt=\"BI0649\">\n[Adapted from Shafee, T. (2015, December 22). *File: enzyme structure.svg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved May 13, 2022, from https:// commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Enzyme_structure.svg. Copyright under CC-BY 4.0]\n\n1. State how the activation energy is changed by adding an enzyme to a reaction. [1 mark]\n\n2. Distinguish between anabolic and catabolic pathways. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enzymes reduce **OR** lower the activation energy of a reaction [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nEnzymes bind to their substrate which **lowers** the activation energy ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ anabolic pathways take in energy **WHILE** catabolic pathways release energy [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ anabolic pathways build complex compounds from simpler compounds **WHILE** catabolic pathways break down complex compounds to form simpler compounds [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the synthesis of glycogen from glucose is an anabolic reaction, **WHILE** the breakdown of food during digestion is a catabolic reaction **OR** any other valid reaction comparisons    [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nWhile anabolic pathways **take in energy**, catabolic pathways **release energy** during reactions ($\\checkmark$). Anabolic pathways **build complex compounds** from simple molecules, but catabolic pathways **break down** complex molecules to form simpler ones ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0650","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0650\n\n**Maximum mark: 2**\n\nOutline two ways enzyme catalysed reactions can be measured. [2 marks]","markScheme":"Any **two** of the following:\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ the quantity **OR** mass **OR** volume of substrate used over time **OR** per minute **OR** per second     [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ the quantity **OR** mass **OR** volume of product formed over time **OR** per minute **OR** per second  [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ volume of gas (oxygen) released over time **OR** per minute **OR** per second, **OR** any other valid example of a specific experiment [1 mark]\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nThe rate of reaction can be obtained by measuring the **quantity of substrate used per minute** ($\\checkmark$)and the **quantity of product formed per minute**. ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0651","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0651\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n1. Sketch a graph to show how increasing concentrations of substrate affect enzyme activity. [2 marks]\n\n2. Explain how the addition of a competitive inhibitor changes the activity of an enzyme. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. **Two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ graph with x-axis labelled substrate concentration and y-axis labelled relative activity **OR** rate of reaction [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ curve shows initial increase and then plateaus [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI0651-1.png\" alt=\"BI0651-1\">\n\n2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ competitive inhibitors have a shape similar to that of the substrate molecule [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ (competitive inhibitors) fit into the active site and prevent the substrate from binding [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ (competitive inhibitors) reduce the rate of reaction depending on the relative concentrations of inhibitor and substrate [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as the concentration of substrate molecules increases relative to the concentration of the inhibitor, the chances of substrate molecules binding to the active site also increases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as substrate concentration increases the rate of reaction increases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ maximum rate of reaction is still reached if substrate concentration increases enough [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ a clearly drawn and annotated graph showing the rate of reaction without an inhibitor and the rate of reaction with an inhibitor [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ x-axis on graph labelled $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$substrate concentration$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$ and y-axis labelled $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$relative activity$\\text{\\textquotedblright}$ [1 mark]\n\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI0651-2.png\" alt=\"BI0651-2\">\n\n The above graph shows the effect of increasing substrate concentration on the activity of an enzyme (**no inhibitor**) and on the activity of the same **enzyme in the presence of a competitive inhibitor**.\n\n The **shape** of competitive inhibitors is **similar to the substrate** ($\\checkmark$), so the inhibitor can also bind to the active site of the enzyme. By occupying the active site, competitive inhibitors **prevent the substrate from binding** ($\\checkmark$). The rate of reaction of **enzymes with an inhibitor present will still increase with increasing substrate concentration**($\\checkmark$). If substrate concentrations increase enough, the rate of reaction with an inhibitor present will reach the **same maximum rate** ($\\checkmark$) as when no inhibitor is present."},{"ref":"BI0797","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0797\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe equipment below can be used to study the effect of temperature on the activity of the enzyme amylase.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI0797A.png\" alt=\"BI0797A\">\n\n1. Outline how this equipment could be used to set up a study into the effect of temperature on amylase activity, up to the point of getting the results. [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline how the rate of reaction of the breakdown of starch by amylase could be measured using the equipment given. [2 marks]\n\n3. Describe a suitable control experiment for this study. [2 marks]\n4. Suggest **one** way to increase the reliability of the data that can be gathered from this experiment. [1 mark]\n\n5. Using the axis below draw a graph of the expected results from the study in part (a).[2 marks]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI0797B.png\" alt=\"BI0797B\">","markScheme":"1. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ measuring cylinder is used to measure equal volumes of substrate, **OR** starch, **AND** equal volumes of enzyme, **OR** amylase, into test tubes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$set up 5 different water baths at a range of at least 5 different temperatures **OR** set up water baths at 0, 20, 40, 60 and 80 :sup[o]C **OR** 5 sensible temperatures given [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ place test tubes containing starch and a test tube containing amylase in each of the water baths to equilibrate **OR** to get to the temperature of the water bath, before mixing together [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ enzyme **OR** amylase, and substrate **OR** starch, are mixed together in test tubes in the water bath for a fixed amount of time **OR** at least 5 minutes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ a variable that must be controlled is the pH **OR**, concentration of substrate **OR** starch **OR** substrate of enzyme **OR** amylase, **OR** the time that the substrate and enzyme are incubated together for in the water bath **OR** the volume of enzyme **OR** the volume of starch suspension [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the presence of starch can be tested for using iodine solution [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ a positive test for starch is iodine solution turning dark blue **OR** black [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n To study the effect of temperature on amylase activity, the **measuring cylinder** can be used to measure an **equal volume of starch suspension** and an **equal volume of amylase** ($\\checkmark$) into individual test tubes. Five different water baths should be set up with a **range of at least five temperatures** ($\\checkmark$) and a test tube containing starch and a test tube containing amylase, should be placed in each water bath to **equilibrate** before being mixed together ($\\checkmark$). Next, the amylase and starch should be **mixed together** and incubated at different temperatures for a **fixed time** ($\\checkmark$). The presence of starch can be tested for using **iodine solution** ($\\checkmark$), which turns dark **blue or black** in the presence of starch ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ take samples from the mixtures in each water bath at regular intervals **AND** test the solution with iodine solution **OR** place samples of the mixtures on a spotting tile at regular time intervals **AND** test the solution with iodine solution [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ record time when the iodine solution stays orange **OR** record time when the iodine solution no longer changes colour **OR** record time when no starch remains [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ calculate the rate of starch breakdown by taking the reciprocal of the time interval for each sample **OR** the rate can be calculated using the formula; rate = 1/ time [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The rate of reaction of the breakdown of starch by amylase can be measured by taking samples of the **mixture** of amylase and starch, from **each temperature every minute, for up to 15 minutes** and can be tested with **iodine solution** ($\\checkmark$). The **time taken for the iodine solution to no longer change colour** should be recorded ($\\checkmark$) and the rate of starch breakdown can be calculated using the formula; **1/time** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ using the same apparatus **AND** volume of solution [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ replace amylase **OR** enzyme [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at the same range of temperatures [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ there should be no change in the concentration of starch **OR** presence of starch can be confirmed by testing solution with iodine solution [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n A control experiment would be using the **same apparatus** and **volume of solutions** ($\\checkmark$), but **replace amylase with water** ($\\checkmark$) and conducting the same experiment with the **same range of temperatures** ($\\checkmark$). There should be **no change in the concentration of starch** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carry out repeats [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n To increase the reliability of the experiment, you could carry out **repeats** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature (°C) should be on the x-axis **AND** rate of reaction **OR** activity of enzyme should be on the y-axis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ line graph should rise rapidly to a maximum point and drop steeply [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI0797ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0797ANSA\">"},{"ref":"BI0930","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0930\n\nWhich of the following describes some types of extracellular enzymes?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Enzymes that are located in the mitochondria\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Enzymes that are involved in various metabolic pathways in the Krebs cycle\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Enzymes that are used for the chemical breakdown of nutrients in the digestive system\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Enzymes that are found in lysosomes","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nExtracellular enzymes are secreted by cells and function outside of cells, like digestive enzymes, which break down complex molecules into simpler products that can be absorbed.\n\nIntracellular enzymes are located inside the cell and can be cytosolic or within organelles like the mitochondria (**Choice A**)\n\nIntracellular enzymes are involved in metabolic pathways, such as the Krebs cycle,  and catalyse reactions that contribute to the growth, maintenance and survival of organisms (**Choice B**).\n\nLysosomes are membrane-bound vesicles containing digestive enzymes such as glycosidases and peptidases. The enzymes are in membrane-bound sacs, mainly inside animal cells, so therefore are intracellular enzymes. (**Choice D**).\n\nExamples of extracellular enzymes are digestive enzymes like amylases and lipases, and examples of intracellular enzymes are Krebs cycle enzymes, DNA or RNA polymerases, and hexokinases."},{"ref":"BI0931","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0931\n\nWhat is a characteristic that separates mammals or endotherms from ectotherms that have fluctuating body temperatures?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ They are able to absorb heat from external sources to maintain their body temperatures.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ They use the heat generated from metabolic processes in their bodies to maintain their body temperature above a set lower limit.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ They have a very efficient metabolism that does not release much waste as heat energy.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ They can survive without eating for longer periods because they do not depend on metabolising what they eat.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nEndotherms tend to have a more consistent and higher baseline metabolic rate than ectotherms.   Metabolic reactions are not very efficient and so energy is dissipated as heat, therefore, **Choice C**  is incorrect. Endotherms use the heat released to maintain their body temperature above a lower set limit, therefore **Choice B** is correct.\n\nBoth ectotherms and endotherms absorb heat from external sources.  However, ectotherms rely on this heat to maintain their body temperature as they cannot maintain their own body temperature (**Choice A**).\n\nIt’s the ectotherms that can survive for long periods without eating (**Choice D**) as they are not dependent on metabolising what they eat to generate heat. They also tolerate variations in internal temperatures better than endotherms."},{"ref":"BI0932","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0932\n\nThe mevalonate pathway is shown below. Inhibitors to the enzyme HMG- CoA reductase can lower the production of cholesterol.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI0932.png\" alt=\"BI0932\">\n\nStatins lower cholesterol, reduce the risk of heart attacks, and prevent strokes. They are known to inhibit HMG-CoA reductase, which is the rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol biosynthesis in the mevalonate pathway (image above).\n\nWhich of the following describes the mechanism of action of statins in lowering cholesterol?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Statins are competitive inhibitors of HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol biosynthesis.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Statins bind to an allosteric site on HMG – CoA reductase, an enzyme in the pathway to cholesterol biosynthesis.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Statins bind to the enzyme-substrate complex of HMG- CoA reductase and thereby inhibit enzyme activity.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Statins are irreversible inhibitors of the enzyme HMG- CoA reductase.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nStatins are competitive inhibitors of the substrate for HMG-CoA reductase and bind at the active site of the enzyme (**Choice A**).\n\nStatins do not bind at an allosteric site of the enzyme HMG-CoA reductase (**Choice B**), so they are not non-competitive inhibitors.\n\nStatins do not inhibit by binding to the enzyme-substrate complex to inhibit activity (**Choice C**).\n\nStatins are competitive inhibitors, so inhibition is reversible in the presence of increasing substrate concentrations (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI1092","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1092\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast intracellular and extracellular chemical reactions, given one example of each type of reaction.     [5 marks]\n\n2. Enzymes are regulated by inhibitors that are classified as competitive or non-competitive.  Distinguish between these two types of inhibitors.     [4 marks]\n\n3. Describe, using an example, how metabolic pathways can be regulated by end-product inhibition.         [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **five** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** similarity and **one** difference **AND** **one** example of **each** type of reaction to gain full marks*):\n\n    ***Similarities*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are controlled by enzymes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** types of reactions are tightly regulated    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ intracellular reactions occur inside cells **WHEREAS** extracellular reactions occur outside a cell        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ intracellular reactions are more focused on the specific needs of the cell, **WHEREAS** extracellular reactions often involve interactions between cells **OR**, tissues **OR** contribute to broader physiological processes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ intracellular reactions are primarily controlled by cellular mechanisms, **WHEREAS**  extracellular reactions might involve the integration of signals from multiple cells **OR** systems [1 mark]\n\n    ***Examples*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ intracellular example is glycolysis **OR** Krebs cycle **OR** photosynthesis **OR** any other suitable example        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ extracellular example is chemical breakdown in digestive system **OR** any other suitable example [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Intracellular and extracellular chemical reactions are **both tightly regulated** ($\\checkmark$) by **enzymes** ($\\checkmark$), however, **intracellular** reactions go on **inside cells**, and **extracellular** reactions go on **outside** of cells ($\\checkmark$). **Glycolysis** is an example of an **intracellular** reaction ($\\checkmark$) as it occurs in the cytoplasm of cells. The **chemical breakdown of food** is an example of **extracellular** reactions ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ competitive inhibitors have a shape similar to that of the substrate **WHILE** non-competitive inhibitors have a completely different shape from the substrate        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ competitive inhibitors bind to the active site **WHILE** non-competitive inhibitors bind to a site other than the active site, **OR** allosteric site     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ competitive inhibitors have no effect on **OR** do not change the shape of the active site **WHILE** binding of non-competitive inhibitors changes the shape of the active site     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in competitive inhibition, increasing substrate concentration can overcome inhibition **WHILE** in non-competitive inhibition increasing substrate concentration will not have this effect     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ example of competitive inhibitor is disulfiram inhibition of alcohol dehydrogenase **WHILE** example of non-competitive inhibitor is isoleucine end-product inhibition of threonine deaminase, **OR** any other valid examples of corresponding inhibitors        [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    | competitive inhibition | non-competitive inhibition |\n    | :-: | :-: |\n    | substrate and inhibitor have a **similar** shape | substrate and inhibitor have **different** shapes ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | the inhibitor can **bind to the active** site | inhibitor **binds to an allosteric** site ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | inhibitor has **no effect on the shape** of the active site | inhibitor **changes the shape of the active site** after binding ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | increasing substrate concentration **reduces** inhibition | increasing substrate concentration **does not reduce** inhibition ($\\checkmark$) |\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the product of the last reaction will inhibit an enzyme at the beginning of the pathway **OR** a clearly drawn and labelled diagram     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the end-product binds to the allosteric site of the enzyme        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the end-product acts as a non-competitive inhibitor        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when bound the inhibitor changes the shape of the active enzyme so the substrate can no longer bind        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ end-product inhibition is reversible         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as the concentration of the end-product decreases inhibition decreases as well, so the pathway can resume             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ end product inhibition is an example of negative feedback inhibition     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ intermediate products **OR** substrates do not build up if there is an excess of the end-product [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ threonine is converted to isoleucine in a series of enzyme-controlled steps **OR** any other valid example of end-product inhibition    [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI1092.png\" alt=\"BI1092\">\n\n    In some metabolic pathways, regulation is achieved by end-product inhibition. The product of the last reaction will **inhibit the first enzyme** ($\\checkmark$) in the pathway. **Threonine is converted to isoleucine**, and this pathway is an example\n($\\checkmark$) of end-product inhibition\n\n    The end-product (isoleucine) **binds to the allosteric site** ($\\checkmark$) of the first enzyme and changes the shape of the active site of the enzyme so the **substrate can no longer bind** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    Isoleucine acts as a **non-competitive inhibitor** ($\\checkmark$). The binding of the **end product is reversible** ($\\checkmark$). Once the end-product detaches from the enzyme, the enzyme can once again catalyse reactions.\n\n    End-product inhibition is an **example of negative feedback inhibition** ($\\checkmark$). If there is an excess of the end product, **intermediate products do not build up** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1144","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1144\n\nWhat would happen to the rate of an enzyme catalysed reaction at high substrate concentrations, as seen in the diagram below?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI1144.png\" alt=\"BI1144\">\n[Source: Adapted from YassineMrabet (2007, August 13). *File: KinEnzymo(en).svg* - wikimedia commons. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved September 20, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: KinEnzymo(en).svg Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The reaction rate slows because the enzymes begin to denature.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The reaction rate speeds up because there is more substrate available.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The reaction rate reaches a plateau because the enzymes become saturated.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The reaction stops because the enzymes are used up.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAt high substrate concentrations, all enzymes are occupied (saturated) and are working at maximum capacity. Thus, the reaction rate plateaus regardless of how much excess substrate is present.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the enzymes do not denature in response to increasing substrate concentrations. **Choice B** is true up to the point where the enzymes become saturated, as depicted in the diagram. Thus, the reaction rate would not increase further and **Choice B** cannot be correct. **Choice D** is incorrect since enzymes are not used up during the reactions they catalyse."},{"ref":"BI1174","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1174\n\nBelow are four graphs with the solid lines showing the rate of an enzyme-controlled reaction with increasing substrate concentrations, in the absence of an inhibitor. The dashed lines show possible rates of reaction in the presence of a non-competitive inhibitor. Which graph correctly shows the effect of a non-competitive inhibitor on reaction rate?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI1174.png\" alt=\"BI1174\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA non-competitive inhibitor has a dissimilar shape to the substrate and binds to the allosteric site of an enzyme, which causes a conformational change in the enzyme so that the substrate can no longer bind. This means that some of the enzyme molecules are unable to bind to the substrate regardless of the substrate concentration, so the reaction rate will remain below the uninhibited enzyme; **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as the relationship between substrate concentration and rate of reaction is not linear, and the rate of reaction with a non-competitive inhibitor will not reach the rate of an uninhibited enzyme. A competitive inhibitor is an inhibitor with a similar shape to a substrate that reversibly binds to the active site of an enzyme, decreasing the rate of reaction. However, increasing the concentration of substrate will overcome the effect of a competitive inhibitor. The dashed line in graph C represents a competitive inhibitor; **Choice C** is incorrect. Graph D suggests that an inhibitor increases the rate of reaction compared to an uninhibited enzyme; **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1381","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1381\n\nThe following graph shows the relative reaction rates of several common enzymes tested at different pH values. Which of the following statements is/are correct?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n | | |\n | :-- | :-- |\n | I. | Comparing the relative activity of each enzyme at their optimum pH, amylase has the highest percentage activity. |\n | II. | Catalase functions over the smallest pH range at relative activity levels over 80%. |\n | III. | Amylase has a lower optimal pH than pepsin. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAll the enzymes have a maximum relative activity of 100% so the maximum relative activity of amylase is not higher than the other enzymes; **Statement I** is, therefore, false. Catalase does have the narrowest pH range at relative activity levels over 80% (approximately pH 8.5 -9.5) when compared to the other enzymes (pepsin 0.8 - 3.0 and amylase 4.7 - 7.2 approximately) **Statement II** is, therefore, true. Amylase has a higher, not lower, optimal pH than pepsin (pH 6 compared to pH 2), so **Statement III** is false. **Choice B** is, therefore, correct.\n\n**Choices A, C**, and **D** are incorrect as they include one or more incorrect statements."},{"ref":"BI1393","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1393\n\nThe following diagram shows an enzyme-catalysed reaction. Which model best describes the interaction between the enzyme and its substrate?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI1393.png\" alt=\"BI1393\">\n[Source: Adapted from TimVickers, (2006, October 11). *File: Induced fit diagram.svg - Wikimedia Commons*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved January 2, 2024, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Induced_fit_diagram.svg Copyright under public domai]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Lock and key model\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ End-product inhibition model\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Davson-Danielli model\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Induced fit model","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because he induced fit model, introduced by Daniel Koshland in 1958, proposes that an enzyme’s active site is not rigid but flexible, adjusting its shape to closely match the substrate when binding occurs. Unlike the fixed lock-and-key model, this process is dynamic, with both the enzyme and substrate undergoing slight conformational changes that facilitate catalysis\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the lock and key model suggests that enzymes fit specific substrates without the need to modify their shape. **Choice B** is incorrect because end-product inhibition involves the last product in a metabolic pathway becoming an inhibitor of one of the enzymes earlier on in the sequence, which is not shown in the diagram. **Choice C** is incorrect since the Davson-Danielli model refers to a proposed model of the cell membrane and is unrelated to enzyme function."},{"ref":"BI1397","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1397\n\nMetabolic reactions in mammals are not completely efficient. What happens to the energy wasted during these reactions?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Used to maintain body temperature.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Used for active transport.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Used to generate action potentials.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Used for muscle contractions.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because much of the energy from metabolic processes is wasted in the form of heat, which is used to maintain a consistent body temperature in mammals (and birds).\n\n**Choices B, C**, and **D** are incorrect because active transport, action potentials, and muscle contractions all rely on energy from ATP, produced through cellular respiration."},{"ref":"BI1584","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1584\n\nThe diagram shows a metabolic pathway catalysed by enzymes.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI1584.png\" alt=\"BI1584\">\n\nWhich of the following would be a correct example of end-product inhibition?\n\n| | Inhibitor | Enzyme |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | W | U|\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Z | S |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | T | V |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | U | V |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because the end product, Z, binds to and inhibits the enzyme S early in the metabolic pathway, slowing the overall reaction.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because W is not the end product in the metabolic pathway and cannot inhibit enzymes that come after it. **Choices C** and **D** are incorrect because products inhibit enzymes in end-product inhibition, not the other way around."},{"ref":"BI1633","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1633\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nGaucher disease is a genetic disorder characterised by a defective enzyme, glucocerebrosidase, which normally breaks down lipids in lysosomes. This leads to a buildup of lipids in some organs, including the liver and spleen.\n\nA study was conducted on 12 patients with Gaucher disease who received enzyme replacement therapy (ERT) injections. The graph shows the effect of ERT on liver and spleen size, measured as a percentage of body mass, over 6 months.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/1633aqui.png\" alt=\"1633aqui\">\n\n1. Calculate the decrease in mean spleen size, as a percentage of body mass, between the beginning and end of the study. [1 mark]\n\n2. Explain why ERT is not a cure for patients with Gaucher disease. [2 marks]\n\n Substrate reduction therapy (SRT), which uses oral medications to reduce the amount of the lipid glucocerebroside synthesised, can also be used in combination with ERT to treat Gaucher disease. Common side effects of SRT treatment include digestive problems and diarrhoea.\n\n The graph shows the results of a follow-up study in which four groups of patients were given no treatment, ERT treatment, SRT treatment, or a combination of both ERT and SRT.\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI1633%20graph%202.png\" alt=\"BI1633 graph 2\">\n\n3. Using all the data provided, discuss whether a combined ERT and SRT treatment is more effective than ERT or SRT alone. [3 marks]\n\n4. Explain why patients receiving a combined ERT and SRT treatment would not likely benefit from very high doses of glucocerebrosidase. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. *answer **must include units** to gain full marks. Do not accept 44% as that is a percentage change, not a difference in % body mass*\n:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 4.4% **OR** 4.4% body mass (accept answers between 4.0-4.8%) [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/AnsA.png\" alt=\"AnsA\">\n\n 10% - 5.6% = **4.4%** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ defective enzyme is still produced **OR** defective gene is still transcribed **OR** expressed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ERT treats symptoms of Gaucher disease, not the cause [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ effect of the treatment is temporary **OR** normal enzyme breaks down over time **OR** regular injections would be needed to maintain normal function **OR** lifelong infusions required to maintain therapeutic level of the enzyme [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ERT may reduce organ sizes, but since a normal **OR** control liver and spleen size are not included in the study, we cannot conclude that treatment has been effective [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n ERT is not a cure for Gaucher disease because the **effect** is **temporary** ($\\checkmark$), as the **defective enzyme** will continue to be **produced** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **three** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** mark supporting combined ERT and SRT treatment and at least **one** mark supporting either SRT or ERT treatment alone)*:\n\n ***Support for combined ERT and SRT treatment:*** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ combined treatment works more quickly **OR** is more effective within the first few weeks compared to ERT or SRT treatment alone [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ combined treatment reaches a stable concentration of blood glucocerebroside within 6 months [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ combined treatment reduces organ size **AND** substrate production, alleviating symptoms **OR** SRT alone may not reduce organ size **OR** size of liver **OR** spleen [1 mark]\n\n ***Support for individual ERT or SRT treatment:*** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ERT injections may be difficult to administer, **WHILE** SRT treatment can be taken orally at home [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ SRT treatment alone resulted in similar **OR** lower blood glucocerebroside concentrations than combined treatment after 6 months **OR** 25 weeks [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ SRT has significant side effects **OR** digestive side effects **OR** ERT has fewer side effects [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Combined ERT** and **SRT** treatment **begins working effectively within** the **first few weeks** ($\\checkmark$) of treatment and **reaches** a **stable** level of **blood glucocerebroside within 6 months** ($\\checkmark$). On the other hand, **ERT** treatment relies on **regular injections**, which may **not** be **easy** or realistic to obtain, **while SRT** medication can be **taken orally** at **home** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ SRT results in significantly reduced production of substrate **OR** lipids **OR** blood glucocerebroside [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ so high doses of enzyme **OR** glucocerebrosidase would have limited **OR** no substrate to act on **OR** bind with [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ since enzymes are not used up in metabolic reactions **OR** can be reused [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ low doses of enzyme **OR** glucocerebrosidase may still be necessary to break down the existing substrate effectively **OR** reduce organ size [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Since **SRT treatment reduces** the **production** of **lipid substrates** ($\\checkmark$), **high doses** of enzymes from **ERT treatment** would be **unnecessary** as the **enzymes** would have **limited substrate to bind with** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1760","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1760\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe enzyme catalase breaks down hydrogen peroxide, a metabolic waste product, into water and oxygen, preventing potential harm to the cell. One catalase molecule can catabolise six million hydrogen peroxide molecules per minute. Catalase is located within membrane-bound organelles called peroxisomes, where it breaks down hydrogen peroxide.\n\nStudents conducted an experiment to investigate how increasing substrate concentration affects catalase activity. They ground 1 g of liver tissue (a tissue rich in peroxisomes) with 5 ml of water and soaked a filter paper disc in this mixture for 5 seconds to absorb catalase. The disc was then placed at the bottom of a tube with 10 ml of 1% hydrogen peroxide solution. The time it took for the disc to rise to the surface of the hydrogen peroxide was recorded. This process was repeated twice more, and the mean time was calculated. The experiment was then repeated with four different hydrogen peroxide concentrations using the same liver mixture. The results are shown in the table below.\n\n| Hydrogen Peroxide Concentration / M| Mean time taken for disc to rise / s | Rate of reaction / s$^{-1}$ |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| 0.4 | 62 | 0.016 |\n\n| 0.8 | 41 | 0.024 |\n\n| 1.2 | 13 |  |\n\n| 1.6 | 8 | 0.125 |\n\n| 2.0 | 7 | 0.143 |\n\n1.  Calculate the rate of reaction for the 1.2 M hydrogen peroxide solution.        [1 mark]\n\n2.  In addition to the controlled variables specified in the method, identify **two** other variables that should be kept constant to ensure a fair test in this experiment.    [1 mark]\n\n3.  Describe the relationship between increasing hydrogen peroxide concentration and catalase activity.        [1 mark]\n\n4.  Suggest why catalase is held in peroxisomes and not released directly into the cytoplasm.    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  the follwoing\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 0.077 s$^{-1}$    [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Rate of reaction with 1.2M hydrogen peroxide = 1/13 s\\\n\n    $\\hspace{19.75em}$ = **0.077 s$^{-1}$**\n\n    The rate of reaction for 1.2 M hydrogen peroxide is **0.077 s$^{-1}$** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.  any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pH        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ surface area **OR** size of paper disc    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ time between preparing hydrogen peroxide and using it    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ any other reasonable answer    [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Two other variables that need to be controlled are the **pH** of the solution and the **temperature** of the hydrogen peroxide. ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.  any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as concentration of hydrogen peroxide increases catalase activity increases [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ results observed suggest direct relationship **OR** positive correlation between hydrogen peroxide concentration and catalase activity     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large **OR** greatest increase in rate of reaction between 0.4 M to 1.2 M and then smaller increase in rate of reaction from 1.2 M  to 2.0 M hydrogen peroxide **OR** reaction rate rises significantly at low substrate concentrations but begins to plateau at higher substrate concentrations    [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    As the **concentration** of **hydrogen peroxide increases**, the rate of reaction, i.e. **catalase activity, increases**, indicating a **positive correlation** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.  any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to prevent too much oxygen being produced at once which could damage cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ only way to control rate of reaction as cells cannot control temperature **OR** substrate concentration **OR** hydrogen peroxide concentration    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to prevent catalase from interacting with other cellular molecules **OR** organelles, potentially disrupting normal cellular processes **OR** to ensure that the breakdown of hydrogen peroxide occurs in a specific location, protecting other parts of the cell from damage    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ peroxisomes provide an optimal environment **OR** optimum pH for catalase activity    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows cells to quickly respond to changes in hydrogen peroxide levels, preventing its accumulation **OR** minimising potential cytotoxic effects    [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Catalase is kept in peroxisomes to **prevent** it from **interacting** with other **cellular molecules** or **organelles**, which could **disrupt** normal **cellular processes** ($\\checkmark$). Peroxisomes also provide an **optimal environment** for **catalase activity**, ensuring efficient breakdown of hydrogen peroxide ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1944","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1944\n\nWhich statements are true for denaturation?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | The product cannot separate from the active site|\n    | II. | It is irreversible |\n    | III. | It affects enzyme-substrate specificity |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is not true because denaturation does not cause the product to remain bound to the active site. Instead, denaturation disrupts the enzyme's tertiary structure, leading to the active site losing its specific shape and function. This means the enzyme-substrate complex cannot occur, and no products are formed.\n\n**Statement II** is true. It is important to distinguish between changes in enzyme activity and denaturation. When temperature increases within the enzyme’s normal working range, activity rises and falls in a reversible manner, so cooling the enzyme restores normal function. However, once the temperature becomes high enough to cause denaturation, the enzyme’s three-dimensional structure is permanently altered. The active site loses its specific shape, hydrophobic regions may aggregate, and the polypeptide cannot spontaneously refold under biological conditions. For almost all enzymes in living systems, heat-induced denaturation is irreversible. A few proteins can refold under special laboratory conditions, but NOT under normal physiological conditions.\n\n**Statement III** is true because denaturation changes the shape of the active site, preventing the enzyme from binding to its specific substrate, which directly affects enzyme-substrate specificity.\n\n**Choice B** is, therefore, correct as it includes only **Statements II** and **III**.\n**Choices A, C**, and **D**  are incorrect because they all include **Statement I**."},{"ref":"BI1996","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1996\n\nWhich type of bonds are disrupted when an enzyme denatures?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Covalent bonds in the substrate.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrogen bonds and ionic interactions in the enzyme.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Peptide bonds in the enzyme.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Disulfide bridges in the substrate.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDenaturation disrupts hydrogen bonds and ionic interactions within the enzyme that maintain its specific three-dimensional (tertiary and quaternary) structure. These changes render the enzyme non-functional as its active site loses its shape. **Choice B** is correct because it specifies the bonds and interactions disrupted during denaturation.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as covalent bonds in the substrate are unrelated to enzyme denaturation. Denaturation involves changes in the enzyme, not the substrate.\\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because peptide bonds in the enzyme are not broken during denaturation; breaking peptide bonds would result in complete hydrolysis of the protein, not just structural changes.\\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because disulfide bridges in the substrate are irrelevant; the question is asking about the denaturation of enzymes."},{"ref":"BI2332","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2332\n\nThe graph shows the energy profile diagram for a reaction with and without an enzyme.\nWhich line is correct for activation energy with an enzyme?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/Caption.png\" alt=\"Caption\">\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A. | | $\\hspace{2cm}$ | |B.|\n| |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/Ans%20A.png\" alt=\"Ans A\"> | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/Ansb.png\" alt=\"Ansb\">|\n|C. | | | |D. |\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/aNSc.png\" alt=\"aNSc\"> | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/aNSd.png\" alt=\"aNSd\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because the activation energy with an enzyme is lower than without an enzyme. Activation energy is defined as the difference between the energy of the reactants and the highest energy point (maximum energy level) reached during the reaction.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because this line represents the activation energy without an enzyme.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because this line represents the activation energy with an enzyme plus the energy difference between reactants and products.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because this line represents the difference between activation energy without an enzyme and activation energy with an enzyme."},{"ref":"BI2386","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2386\n\nWhich of the following does penicillin inhibit the formation of?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Short cross-linking peptides between peptidoglycan polymers\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Transpeptidases\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Glycosidic bonds within peptidoglycan\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Enzyme-antibiotic complexes","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because the disruption of the formation of transpeptides that lie between peptidoglycan’s polysaccharide chains weakens the bacterial cell wall and makes it susceptible to rupture via osmotic pressure.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because transpeptidases are the enzymes that catalyse transpeptidation, the formation of peptide cross-links. Penicillin binds to and inhibits these enzymes but does not prevent their synthesis.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because the main polysaccharide structure of peptidoglycan is unaffected by penicillin. Rather, penicillin disrupts the formation of the short peptides that link peptidoglycan strands together.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because penicillin is an antibiotic that forms very effective complexes with the transpeptidase enzyme. The two molecules bond irreversibly, rendering the enzyme ineffective."},{"ref":"BI2441","marks":"3","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2441\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nThe image below shows an example of a transamination and an oxidative deamination reaction.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Enzymes%20%26%20Metabolism/images/BI2441.png\" alt=\"BI2441\">\n\n1.\n Deduce whether oxidative deamination is a catabolic or anabolic reaction. [1 mark]\n\n2. Justify your response to (a). [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ catabolic [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Oxidative deamination is a **catabolic** reaction ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ catabolic reactions involve the breakdown of macromolecules **OR** larger molecules **OR** complex molecules into monomers **OR** smaller products **OR** simpler products [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ during oxidative deamination glutamic acid is broken down into $\\ce{NH_3}$ **OR** ammonium and keto acid **OR** is broken down into smaller **OR** simpler products **OR** ammonium is often released as a product of amino acid catabolism [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Catabolic reactions are when larger molecules are **broken down** into monomers or smaller molecules ($\\checkmark$). Based on the diagram, glutamic acid is broken down into **ammonium and keto acid**, so this would be catabolic ($\\checkmark$)."}]},{"name":"Integration of Body Systems","questions":[{"ref":"BI0004","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0004\n\nWhy do multicellular organisms exhibit emergent properties?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ All the cells of multicellular organisms have the same function.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cells of multicellular organisms do not communicate with one another.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cells of multicellular organisms interact with one another.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cells of multicellular organisms have similar structures.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** - Cells of multicellular organisms have different functions.  They interact with one another to perform more complex functions such as responding to the environment, movement and homeostasis.\n\n**Choice B** - Cells of multicellular organisms communicate with each other. These cells communicate to coordinate the activities of organs, tissues and other cells.\n\n**Choice C** - Cells interact with one another forming complex structures and functions that any one cell type alone could not perform\n\n**Choice D** - Cells have different structures based on the cell’s specialised roles."},{"ref":"BI0286","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0286\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Describe how the enteric nervous system ensures the coordinated movement of food through the digestive tract. [5 marks]\n\n2. Explain how emergent properties arise from the integration of subsystems in a multicellular organism.                [4 marks]\n\n3. Compare and contrast how the nervous system controls conscious and reflex responses. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ controls involuntary peristalsis from mid **OR** lower oesophagus to rectum  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ peristalsis is the wave-like contraction of circular and longitudinal muscles in the gut wall                     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ coordinates contraction and relaxation of smooth muscle of gut walls [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contraction of circular muscles behind bolus of food to push food along **OR** stops food moving backwards  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contraction of longitudinal muscles in front of a bolus of food shortens gut, moving food along [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ acts independently of central nervous system **OR** CNS **OR** is influenced by autonomic nervous system **OR** ANS     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ modulates peristalsis speed based on food composition and digestive needs [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ responds to stretch receptors in the gut walls [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nThe enteric nervous system (ENS) controls **involuntary peristalsis** from the **lower oesophagus** to the **rectum** ($\\checkmark$) . It **coordinates** the **contraction** of **circular muscles behind** the **food** to **prevent backflow** ($\\checkmark$) and **longitudinal muscles ahead** of it, pushing the **food forward** ($\\checkmark$) . Neural **signals** between different **regions** of the **gut** ensure that this **movement** is **synchronised** throughout the digestive tract ($\\checkmark$) . This coordination ensures that food moves in **one direction** and at a **speed** that allows for proper **digestion** and **absorption** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ emergent properties are characteristics **OR** abilities that arise from the interaction of parts within a system                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in multicellular organisms, cells are organised into tissues, tissues into organs, and organs into systems    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ each higher level has properties that emerge from the interactions within the levels below    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the organisms systems must communicate and coordinate to perform higher-level functions **OR** Integration between systems enables functions that individual systems cannot perform alone                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ emergent properties depend on timing **OR** coordination between systems     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ macromolecular **OR** genetic interactions within cells contribute to emergent properties at the cellular level        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ whole organism relies on coordination between all levels of biological organisation     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ failure of integration at any level disrupts the organism's function         [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nEmergent properties are **characteristics** that **arise** from the **interaction** of **parts** in a system ($\\checkmark$). In **multicellular organisms**, **cells** are organised into **tissues**, which form **organs**, and organs into **systems** ($\\checkmark$). Each level in this hierarchy has **new properties** that are **not found** at the **lower** levels ($\\checkmark$). **Failure** of integration at **any level disrupts** the organism's **function**, leading to disease or dysfunction ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following (at least **two** rsimilarities and **two** differences are needed to achieve full marks)\n\n    ***Similarities***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** involve sensory neurons conveying input from receptors to the central nervous system, **OR** CNS    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** result in a response via motor neurons, either as muscle contraction **OR** glandular secretion    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** rely on synaptic transmission **OR** neurotransmitter release    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can involve the spinal cord as part of the neural pathway        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are coordinated by the nervous system to produce appropriate responses to stimuli     [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ conscious responses are always controlled by the brain **OR** cerebral hemispheres, **WHEREAS** reflex responses are often integrated in the spinal cord **OR** do not always pass through the brain        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ conscious responses involve voluntary control **WHEREAS** reflex responses are involuntary **OR** automatic [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reflex responses are faster because they involve fewer synapses **WHEREAS** conscious responses are slower due to more complex processing    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reflex responses cannot be consciously initiated **WHEREAS** conscious responses require awareness **OR** decision-making    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ conscious responses can be modified by learning **OR** memory **WHEREAS** reflex responses are usually fixed **OR** innate        [1 mark]\n\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    **Both** conscious and reflex responses **begin with sensory neurons** detecting a stimulus and sending **signals** to the **central nervous system (CNS)** ($\\checkmark$). In **both** cases, **motor neurons** carry **signals to effectors**, which may be **muscles or glands** ($\\checkmark$). **Synapses and neurotransmitters** are involved in **transmitting impulses** between **neurons** in both types of response ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    However, **reflex** responses are usually **integrated** in the **spinal cord**, while **conscious** responses are processed in the **cerebral hemispheres** ($\\checkmark$). **Reflexes** are **involuntary** and occur **automatically**, whereas **conscious** responses involve **voluntary** control ($\\checkmark$). **Reflexes** are typically **faster** because they involve **fewer neurons** and **synapses**, while **conscious** responses are **slower** due to more complex **processing** ($\\checkmark$). **Conscious** responses can also be **modified by learning** or past **experience**, but **reflexes** are generally **fixed** and not influenced by memory ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0352","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0352\n\nWhich statement defines tropisms in plants?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Growth of a plant away from gravity\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Growth of a plant in response to a change in day length\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Growth of a plant towards light\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Growth of a plant towards or away from a stimulus","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because a tropism is any growth response of a plant towards or away from a stimulus. **Choice A** is incorrect because this is the definition for only one type of tropism, geotropism. **Choice B** is incorrect because day length does not cause a plant to grow in one direction. **Choice C** is incorrect because this is a specific definition for phototropism whereas tropism in general is a growth response towards or away from a stimulus, and not only light."},{"ref":"BI0353","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0353\n\nWhich of the following is a function of auxin efflux pumps?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ To move auxin upwards in a stem\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ To move auxin to the shaded side of a stem\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ To move auxin to the sunny side of a stem\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ To move auxin into flowers","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because in phototropism auxin efflux pumps move auxin to the shady side of the stem, which stimulates growth and results in the bending of the stem towards the light source. **Choice A** is incorrect because auxin moves downwards in stems. **Choice C** is incorrect because auxin efflux pumps move auxin to the shady side of a stem. **Choice D** is incorrect because auxin does not move into flowers."},{"ref":"BI0355","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0355\n\nWhich statement identifies the direct effect of auxin?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Development of leaves\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Stimulates meiosis\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Inhibits flowering\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Changes gene expression","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because auxin affects gene expression which leads to changes in growth. **Choice A** is incorrect because cell differentiation causes development of leaves and this is not a direct effect of auxin. **Choice B** is incorrect because one effect of auxin is that it stimulates mitosis, it does not stimulate meiosis. **Choice C** is incorrect because auxin stimulates flowering."},{"ref":"BI0359","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0359\n\nWhich statement correctly describes the role of auxin in phototropism?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ High concentrations stimulate cell elongation in the stem.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Low concentrations stimulate cell elongation in the stem.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ High concentrations inhibit cell division in the stem.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Low concentrations inhibit cell division in the stem.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because high auxin concentrations stimulate cell elongation in the stem tip. **Choice B** is incorrect because low auxin concentrations do not stimulate cell elongation in the stem tip. **Choice C** and **Choice D** are incorrect because in phototropism auxin does not inhibit cell division, it affects cell elongation."},{"ref":"BI0360","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0360\n\nWhich sequence shows the route taken by auxin from its production to its role in phototropism?\n\n| | Step 1 | Step 2| Step 3 |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Produced in meristem | Moves to shaded side| Inhibits cell elongation\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Produced on shady side | Changes gene expression | Promotes cell elongation |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Produced on sunny side | Changes gene expression | Inhibits cell elongation |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Produced in meristem |Moves to shaded side | Promotes cell elongation|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because auxin is produced at the meristem in the stem tip and is moved to the shady side by efflux pumps, and the high concentrations on the shady side cause cell elongation. **Choice A** is incorrect because auxin does not inhibit cell elongation on the shady side. **Choice B** is incorrect because auxin is not produced on the shady side. **Choice C** is incorrect because auxin is produced at the meristem in the stem tip, not only on the shady side, and it does not inhibit cell elongation in stems."},{"ref":"BI0361","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0361\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe diagram represents a stem tip and the position of the Sun.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Integration%20of%20Body%20Systems/images/BI0361ai.png\" alt=\"BI0361ai\">\n\n1. On the diagram above, draw an arrow to indicate the direction of movement of auxin. [1 mark]\n\n2.\n 1. State the term used to describe the directional growth of a plant in response to light. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Outline how this auxin affects growth in the stem tip.[4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{->}$ arrow is drawn from left to right **OR** from the sunny side to the shady side of the tip [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Integration%20of%20Body%20Systems/images/BI0361ams.png\" alt=\"BI0361ams\">\n\n2.\n 1. $\\hspace{1em}$\n $\\ce{->}$ phototropism [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n The directional growth response of plants to light is **phototropism** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n 2. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{->}$ phototropins in the stem tip detect higher light intensity on one side [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{->}$ auxin efflux carriers **OR** pumps become repositioned on shaded side **OR** to transport auxin laterally toward the shaded side [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{->}$ auxin diffuses freely **OR** passively moves into the cells of the stem tip but efflux **OR** movement out is active **OR** ATP-driven **OR** pumped out [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{->}$ a auxin accumulates on the shaded side of the stem tip, creating a concentration gradien [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{->}$ auxin binds to receptors in target cells initiating changes in gene expression **OR** tiggereing transcription of genes related to elongation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{->}$ auxin stimulates proton pumps to secrete H⁺ ions into the cell wall **OR** to acidify cell wall [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{->}$ acidification loosens cellulose cross-links [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{->}$ allows cells on the shaded side to elongate more than those on the lit side the stem bends toward the light **OR** causes greater elongation on shaded side [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{->}$ this growth response increases exposure of leaves to sunlight **OR** causes stem tip to grow towards sun [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Phototropins** in the stem tip **detect higher light intensity** on **one side** of the shoot ($\\checkmark$). **Auxin efflux carriers** are repositioned to **transport auxin** toward the **shaded side** ($\\checkmark$). This causes **auxin** to **accumulate** on the **shaded side**, creating a **concentration gradient** ($\\checkmark$). **Auxin stimulates proton pumps** to secrete **hydrogen ions** into the **cell wall**, **acidifying it** and allowing **cell elongation** ($\\checkmark$).\n If higher light intensities are detected, by **phototropins**, on **one side** of the plant ($\\checkmark$), then **efflux pumps** transport auxin to the more **shaded** side ($\\checkmark$). This leads to an **increase in auxin concentration** on the more shaded side of the plant ($\\checkmark$), which stimulates **cell elongation** ($\\checkmark$) and results in the stem tip growing towards the **sun** or light source ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0484","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0484\n\nWhich hormone is secreted by the gland labelled X?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Integration%20of%20Body%20Systems/images/BI0484.png\" alt=\"BI0484\">\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Adrenaline\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Thyroxin\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Progesterone\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Melatonin","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because the gland X is the pineal gland, and it produces melatonin, a hormone that regulates circadian rhythms (sleep-wake cycles).\n\n **Choice A** is incorrect because adrenaline (epinephrine) is produced by the adrenal glands.\n\n **Choice B** is incorrect because thyroxin is produced by the thyroid gland.\n\n **Choice C** is incorrect because progesterone is produced by the corpus luteum in the ovary and by the placenta during pregnancy."},{"ref":"BI0490","marks":"4","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0490\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nHormones can be used as medical treatments in some situations.\n\n1. Name the hormone that can be used to treat jet lag and state its effect on the body. [2 marks]\n\n2. Outline two uses of hormones in IVF. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following (*the name must be given*):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ melatonin is the hormone that can be used to treat jet lag [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ melatonin regulates the sleep-wake cycle **OR** melatonin regulates circadian rhythms     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ melatonin concentration increases in the evening [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ melatonin promotes sleepiness [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ changing time zones affects the release of melatonin so the sleep-wake cycle is affected     [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n**Melatonin** is the hormone used to treat jet lag ($\\checkmark$). It is produced by the pineal gland and **affects the circadian rhythm** of the body ($\\checkmark$). The **concentration of melatonin increases** in the **evening** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ daily FSH injections are given to increase the development of follicles **OR** daily LH injections are given daily to increase the development of follicles **OR** daily FSH and LH injections are given to increase the development of follicles [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ injection of hCG **OR** LH is given which stimulates the follicles to mature before ova are collected [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oestrogen used to prepare endometrium for implantation **OR** to prepare for embryo transfer [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ after embryos are placed in the uterus a progesterone pessary **OR** tablet is placed in the vagina to maintain the endometrium to allow implantation [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    IVF uses hormones in a number of ways. After a nasal spray is used to stop the release of FSH and LH from the pituitary gland a woman is given **daily FSH and LH injections** for approximately ten days to stimulate the **development of follicles** ($\\checkmark$). An **injection of hCG** is then given to **stimulate the follicles to mature** ($\\checkmark$). After embryos are placed in the uterus **a progesterone pessary** is placed in the vagina to **maintain the endometrium to allow implantation** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0516","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0516\n\nThe graph shows the circadian rhythm in body temperature of an individual under their normal light-dark cycle and after exposure to a 1-hour pulse of 1000-lux light at their typical bedtime.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Integration%20of%20Body%20Systems/images/BI0516.png\" alt=\"BI0516\">\n[Source: Adapted from Coiffard, B., Diallo, A.B., Mezouar, S., Leone, M., & Mege, J.-L. (2021). *A Tangled Threesome: Circadian Rhythm, Body Temperature Variations, and the Immune System*. Biology 2021, 10, 65. Retrieved March 2, 2022, from  https://doi.org/10.3390/biology10010065. Copyright under CC BY 4.0; Cho, C.H., Moon, J.H., Yoon, H.K. et al. (2016). *Molecular circadian rhythm shift due to bright light exposure before bedtime is related to subthreshold bipolarity*. Sci Rep 6, 31846 (2016). Retrieved March 2, 2022, from https://doi.org/10.1038/srep31846. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\nWhich statement correctly describes the event responsible for the change in the rhythm of body temperature after exposure to light?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The secretion of melatonin from the thyroid gland was elevated by light exposure.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The secretion of melatonin from the pineal gland was suppressed by light exposure.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The secretion of thyroxin from the thyroid gland was elevated by light exposure.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The secretion of thyroxin from the pineal gland was suppressed by light exposure.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe graph shows the time of day on the horizontal axis and body temperature on the vertical axis. The black line indicates the body temperature rhythm of an individual following their normal light-dark cycle, and the dashed line indicates the body temperature rhythm of that same individual after exposure to a pulse of 1000-lux light at their typical bedtime. The body temperature rhythm after exposure to light has the same shape as that before exposure, but the peak of this rhythm is shifted later in the day relative to the individual’s typical body temperature.\n\nThe hormone melatonin regulates an individual’s circadian rhythm and sleep-wake cycle. Melatonin is released by the pineal gland, not the thyroid gland (**Choice A**), at night to control circadian cycles. If the circadian cycle is shifted to later in the day, it is likely that the exposure to light suppressed the release of melatonin from the pineal gland.\n\nThyroxin is secreted by the thyroid gland, not the pineal gland (**Choice D**) and regulates metabolic rate and body temperature, not the sleep cycle (**Choice C**)."},{"ref":"BI0524","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0524\n\nPlant shoots detect and respond to light. Which phytohormone is responsible for positive phototropism in shoots, and what mechanism is responsible for its directional movement?\n\n| | Protein | Mechanism |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Auxin | Passive efflux channels |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Cytokinin  | Passive efflux channels |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Auxin | Active efflux pumps |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ |  Cytokinin | Active efflux pumps |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAuxin is the phytohormone responsible for positive phototropism in shoots. Active efflux pumps transport auxin laterally from the illuminated side to the shaded side of the shoot. This causes differential growth, bending the shoot toward the light. **Choice C** is the correct answer.\n\nPassive auxin-influx channels allow facilitated diffusion of auxin into the cells, but it is efflux pumps that actively transport auxin out of the cell and direct its redistribution. Cytokinins are not involved in phototropism; they primarily regulate cell division and shoot/root development. **Choices A, B** and **D** are all incorrect because they include either passive movement, cytokinins or both."},{"ref":"BI0680","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0680\n\nBelow is a photograph of a lily flower (*Lilium candidum*), which begins to flower as days get longer in summer. Gibberellins, a type of phytohormone, trigger the flowering in meristem tissue.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Integration%20of%20Body%20Systems/images/lilly%20petal%20anther%20stigma%20style.jpg\" alt=\"lilly petal anther stigma style\">\n\nHow do gibberellins bring about flowering in lily plants?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ They bring about differentiation in the lateral meristem.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ They cause changes in gene expression in the apical meristem.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ They stimulate the production of cytokinins.\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ They bring about gene mutations in the apical meristem.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because gibberellins activate genes in the shoot apical meristem, promoting the transition from vegetative growth to flowering.\n\n**Choice A** is Incorrect as gibberellins act on the shoot apical meristem, not the lateral meristem, which is responsible for secondary growth and not flowering.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect as gibberellins do not directly stimulate cytokinins. Cytokinins interact with auxins and are involved in processes like cell division, but they are not central to flowering in long-day plants.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because gibberellins do not cause gene mutations; they regulate gene expression to activate flowering-related genes."},{"ref":"BI0760","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0760\n\nWhat is an example of an emergent property?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ A unicellular organism uses active transport to absorb nutrients.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ A retinal cell detecting changes in light intensity.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The endocrine and nervous system working cooperatively to respond to the environment.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ A single muscle cell contracting.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nEmergent properties are properties that arise from the interaction between component parts in a complex system. **Choice A** is incorrect as it only describes a function of active transport. **Choice B**  is incorrect as a retinal cell detecting light is a basic function of that cell and does not involve the interaction of multiple cells and no new property has emerged. **Choice D** is incorrect because a muscle cell contracting does not involve component parts and no new property emerges."},{"ref":"BI0788","marks":"10","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0788\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe graph shows the effect of differing concentrations of the growth hormone Indole-3-acetic acid (IAA) on the shoot growth rate of the sesame seed plant (*Sesamum indicum*). IAA is an auxin.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Integration%20of%20Body%20Systems/images/BI0788.png\" alt=\"BI0788\">\n\n1.\n 1. Describe the trend shown in the graph. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Outline why percentage change was used rather than change in length. [2 marks]\n\n2.\n 1. State two variables that would need to be controlled in this experiment. [2 marks]\n\n 2. Explain why it is important that one of the factors you chose in (i) needs to be controlled in this experiment. [2 marks]\n\n3.\n 1. Explain how auxins such as IAA promote shoot growth. [2 marks]\n\n 2. Deduce, with a reason, which concentration of IAA should be used to grow sesame seed plants. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n 1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ between 0 µM and 75 µM, the percentage change in shoot length increased **AND** between 75 µM and 100 µM, the percentage change in shoot length decreased [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ up to 75μΜ percentage change in length increases with IAA concentration **AND** above 75μM percentage change in length decreases [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The graph shows that between **0 µM and 75 µM**, the percentage change in shoot length **increased** and between **75 µM and 100 µM**, the percentage change in shoot length **decreased** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the shoots may not all be the same length at the beginning [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to compare the relative changes in shoot length **OR** to make the results comparable **OR** provide a standardised measure of change [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Percentage change is used rather than change in length, as all of the shoots may **not be the same length** at the beginning of the experiment ($\\checkmark$); therefore percentage change allows for the **comparison** of relative changes in shoot length ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ light intensity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$volume of water [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ concentration of nutrients [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide concentration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ length of time between auxin application and measurement of shoot length [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature of environment [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ number of seeds grown at each concentration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ distance between seeds [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ wind speed **OR** air movement [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Two controlled variables in this experiment are **light intensity** ($\\checkmark$) and the **volume of water** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n *Must refer to **one** of the factors that were chosen in (b) (i) for one mark*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ there should only be one independent variable in an experiment [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ for fair comparison between the effect of different concentrations of IAA **OR** the effect of changing the stated variable on the experiment and how this would affect fair comparison [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n It is important that **light intensity** is controlled as it affects the rate of photosynthesis and so affects the **growth rate** ($\\checkmark$) of the shoot, and there should only be **one independent variable** ($\\checkmark$) in an experiment.\n\n3.\n 1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ IAA **OR** auxin enters the cells of shoot and upregulates gene expression for production of expansins [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ expansins increase elasticity of cell wall [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ IAA **OR** auxin activates proton pumps in the plasma membrane so hydrogen ions are pumped into the cell wall [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen ions cause bonds holding cellulose fibres together to break [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ breaking bonds in cell wall makes it more elastic so, as water enters the vacuole, the cell elongates [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ IAA **OR** auxin stimulates cell division [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Auxin is a positive phototropic growth hormone in plants. It enters the cells in the shoot and **upregulates gene expression of expansins** ($\\checkmark$), which **increase the elasticity** of the cell wall ($\\checkmark$). Auxin activates **proton pumps** in the plasma membrane of cells in the shoot so **hydrogen ions are pumped** into the cell wall ($\\checkmark$). The hydrogen ions cause bonds holding **cellulose fibres together in the cell wall to break** ($\\checkmark$), making the **cell walls more elastic**, which allows **water to enter the vacuole** , and causes the cell to **elongate** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n *Both a value and a reason are needed for one mark*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 75 µM because it is the concentration with the highest percentage change [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The concentration of IAA that should be used to grow sesame seed plants is **75 µM** because it is the concentration that results in the **highest percentage increase** ($\\checkmark$) in shoot length."},{"ref":"BI1003","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1003\n\nWhich of the following gives the correct hierarchy of body subsystems?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Tissues → cells → organs → organ systems → organism\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cells → tissues → organs → organ systems → organism\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Tissues → cells → organ systems → organs → organism\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Organism → cells → tissues → organs → organ system","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCells are the basic structural and functional units of life. They are the fundamental building blocks of all living organisms. Tissues are groups of specialised cells that work together to perform a specific function. Different types of tissues combine to form organs. Organs are composed of multiple tissues that work together to carry out complex functions. Organs have specific roles within the body. Organ systems are groups of organs that collaborate to perform broader functions necessary for the survival of the organism. Organism refers to the complete living individual made up of various organ systems working together. Therefore **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choices A**, **C** and **D** are all in the incorrect order."},{"ref":"BI1004","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1004\n\nWhen a person is frightened, various changes in the body occur. Which of the following are involved in this fight or flight response?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Transmembrane receptors |\n    | II. | The endocrine system |\n    | III. | The autonomic nervous system |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe endocrine system (**Statement II**) is involved in the fight or flight response through the release of hormones from endocrine glands, such as adrenaline (epinephrine), from the adrenal glands. Adrenaline (epinephrine) is released into the bloodstream and travels throughout the body to target organs and tissues, preparing the body for immediate action.  When adrenaline (epinephrine) reaches its target tissues it binds to specific transmembrane receptors on the surface of cells (**Statement I**). This binding triggers a cascade of intracellular events that ultimately lead to physiological changes associated with the fight or flight response, such as increased heart rate and dilated airways.  The autonomic nervous system (**Statement III**) plays a central role in the fight or flight response. In a frightening situation, the nervous system is activated, leading to the release of adrenaline (epinephrine), increased heart rate, increased blood flow to muscles, and other physiological changes that prepare the body for a rapid response. **Choice D** is therefore correct.\n\n**Choices A**, **B** and **C** are all incorrect as they do not include all three statements."},{"ref":"BI1005","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1005\n\nWhich of the labels (A-D) points to the cerebellum on the diagram of the brain below?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Integration%20of%20Body%20Systems/images/BI1005.png\" alt=\"BI1005\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** points to the cerebellum, which is the part of the brain that controls voluntary muscle movements, balance and posture.\n\n**Choice B** points to the medulla oblongata which controls vital functions such as breathing, heart rate, and blood pressure. **Choice C** points to the pituitary gland which is often referred to as the ‘master gland’. The pituitary gland is an endocrine gland located at the base of the brain that regulates and controls various hormonal functions in the body. **Choice D** points to the cerebrum, the largest and most developed part of the brain, responsible for higher cognitive functions, sensory perception, and voluntary motor control."},{"ref":"BI1060","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1060\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe autonomic nervous system helps regulate the control of heart rate. Below is a diagram of the human brain.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Integration%20of%20Body%20Systems/images/BI1060a.png\" alt=\"BI1060a\">\n\n1. State the name of the part of the brain that is involved in the control of heart rate. [1 mark]\n\n2. Baroreceptors and chemoreceptors play an important role in this control. Compare and contrast how these two types of receptors help with the regulation of heart rate. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ medulla **OR** medulla oblongata [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The part of the brain responsible for the control of heart rate is the **medulla** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **four** of the following (must include at least one similarity and one difference):\n\n **Similarities:** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** located at aorta and carotid arteries [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** send information to medulla [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ stimulation of **BOTH** baroreceptors and chemoreceptors can cause decrease in heart rate **OR** stroke volume [1 mark]\n\n **Differences:** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ baroreceptors located inside aorta and carotid arteries **WHEREAS** chemoreceptors are located in tissue on outside of aorta and carotid arteries [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ baroreceptors detect changes in blood pressure **WHEREAS** chemoreceptors detect changes in pH of blood **OR** oxygen concentration **OR** carbon dioxide concentration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ baroreceptors only send action potentials when there is an increase in blood pressure **OR** stretching of artery walls **WHEREAS** chemoreceptors send action potentials when there is an increase or decrease away from the ‘norm’ [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ three different types of chemoreceptors **OR** chemoreceptors to detect oxygen levels, carbon dioxide levels and pH **WHEREAS** baroreceptors only detect stretching of walls of arteries [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Both baroreceptors and chemoreceptors send impulses to the **medulla oblongata** ($\\checkmark$) of the brain when stimulated to control heart rate. Also both types of receptors are located at the **aorta and carotid arteries** ($\\checkmark$), but exact location is different, while baroreceptors are **inside the arterial tissue**, chemoreceptors are located in the tissues **adjacent to the arteries** ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the signal those receptors detect are different: baroreceptors detect the changes in **pressure** by measuring the stretch of the walls of the arteries, while chemoreceptors can detect the change in the **pH levels** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1116","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1116\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline how treading on a drawing pin can bring about the reflex of lifting the foot.    [5 marks]\n\n2. Describe how the cerebellum exerts overall control over the voluntary movements of the body.    [4 marks]\n\n3. Explain how melatonin plays a part in the control of circadian rhythms.    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ specialised pain receptors **OR** nociceptors, are activated by drawing pin entering skin [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ activation of nociceptors **OR** sensory receptors generates action potentials [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ action potentials travel along sensory neurons (located in the foot) [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ axons enter spinal cord, transmitting action potentials to spinal cord [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ synapse with interneurons **OR** relay neurons within spinal cord [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ interneurons synapse with **OR** transmit information **OR** action potentials to motor neurons  [1 mark]\n\n     $\\ce{ -> }$ motor neurons synapse with **OR** transmit information **OR** action potentials to leg muscles **OR** effectors  [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ leg muscles **OR** effectors which bring about response **OR** lift the foot [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    When you step on a drawing pin, specialised **pain receptors**, called **nociceptors, are activated** ($\\checkmark$). These trigger **action potentials** in **sensory neurons** ($\\checkmark$), and the **axons** of these neurons **enter** the **spinal cord** ($\\checkmark$), carrying the action potential with them. The sensory neurons **synapse with interneurons** ($\\checkmark$), which in turn **synapse with motor neurons** ($\\checkmark$). The **motor neurons carry** the **information to** the **effectors** ($\\checkmark$), the leg muscles in this case, which bring about the **response** ($\\checkmark$) of lifting the leg.\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cerebellum receives sensory input from sensory receptors when body moves [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ provides information about current position of body **OR** tension of muscles [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cerebellum processes this information to form model of body's position in space [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ compares the intended movement, instructed by the cerebral cortex, with the actual sensory feedback from the body **OR** allows detection of errors in motor execution [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sends corrective signals to the motor centres in the brainstem **OR** cerebral cortex [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ controls timing and sequencing of movement **OR** controls precise sequence of muscle contractions required for complex motor tasks [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ important for maintaining balance and posture [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ involved in motor learning and memory **OR** muslce memory **OR** improved motor performance through practice and repetition  [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The cerebellum is important in **controlling** the **balance** ($\\checkmark$) of the body and the **coordination of voluntary movements** ($\\checkmark$). It **receives information from sensory receptors** around the body which allow it to work out the **current position** ($\\checkmark$) of the body. It then **processes this information**, compares it with the instruction from the cerebral cortex, and **detects any errors** ($\\checkmark$). It then sends out signals to the correct these errors. It is the cerebellum that allows us to **improve** our **motor performance** through **practice and repetition** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ melatonin is secreted **OR** produced by the pineal gland [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ production is controlled by an area in the hypothalamus [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ secretion is controlled by light and dark [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ controls sleep cycle [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cells in retina **OR** photoreceptors in retina are sensitive to light [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ triggering these cells **OR** presence of light causes signal from these cells to cause pineal gland to adjust melatonin production [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ for diurnal animals **OR** animals that are active in day melatonin levels are high at night **OR** for nocturnal animals melatonin levels are hiigh in day [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as light increases, melatonin levels drop and the animal becomes active **OR** alert  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ helps synchronise circadium rhythms as is cue for other physiological processes  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ E.g.  hormone release **OR** mating **OR** hibernation **OR** other suitable example  [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Melatonin is a hormone produced by the **pineal gland** ($\\checkmark$) which is involved in regulating the **sleep-wake cycle** ($\\checkmark$). Melatonin production is **controlled by the hypothalamus** ($\\checkmark$). **Photoreceptors in the retina** detect **light** and send impulses to the brain that **controls** the **secretion** of the melatonin ($\\checkmark$). In diurnal animals, such as humans, the **absence of light increases melatonin production** ($\\checkmark$), and this promotes a state of sleepiness, contributing to the onset of sleep. Conversely, as **morning approaches** and melatonin **levels decline**, the body receives a signal to wake up and become **alert** ($\\checkmark$). Beyond the sleep-wake cycle, melatonin helps **synchronise** various **circadian rhythms**  throughout the body, ensuring that different **physiological processes** ($\\checkmark$) occur at the correct time, such as **mating** and **hibernation** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1177","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1177\n\nThe photomicrograph shows a section through a shoot apex, with the sunlight coming from the left side. Which sequence shows the route taken by auxin from its production to its role in phototropism?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Integration%20of%20Body%20Systems/images/BI1177.png\" alt=\"BI1177\">\n[Source: Adapted from Bohle, K. (2022, January 24). *Forsøk: Tre forsøk med plantehormon - biologi 1 - NDLA. ndla.no.*  Retrieved September 16, 2023, from https://ndla.no/nn/subject:1:83ce68bc-19c9-4f2b-8dba-caf401428f21/topic:b0d8201d-66cc-4b29-b74b-6f7bd88a4d99/topic:6e4ccf8c-36a0-45f1-aade-857b63ae2557/resource:7807f765-dfec-4f22-90df-7c70943fbf00. Copyright under CC BY-SA 4.0]\n\n| | Produced in… | Moves to… | Effect on plant shoot |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 1 | 3 | Promotes cell elongation |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 4 | 2 | Inhibits mitosis |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 1 | 2 | Inhibits cell elongation |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 4 | 3 | Directly promotes mitosis |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n1 is the meristem, 4 is the base of the shoot and 2 and 3 are the sunny and shaded side, respectively. Auxin is produced in the meristem of the stem tip and is moved to the shady side by efflux pumps. The resulting high concentrations on the shady sides cause cell elongation; **Choice A**  is correct.\n\nAuxin is not produced in the base of the shoot, nor does it move to the sunny side or inhibit mitosis; **Choice B**  is incorrect. Auxin does not move to the sunny side or inhibit cell elongation; **Choice C**  is incorrect.  Auxin does not directly promote mitosis; **Choice D**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1297","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1297\n\nWhich row correctly identifies how auxin moves into and out of shoot cells during phototropism?\n\n| | Movement into cell | Movement out of cell |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Facilitated diffusion | Simple diffusion |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Simple diffusion | Active transport |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Endocytosis | Active transport |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Active transport | Simple Diffusion |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAuxin can diffuse freely into plant cells but cannot diffuse out through the plasma membrane. Auxin efflux carriers actively transport auxin out of cells. When these carriers are concentrated on the same side of successive cells, they produce directional transport of auxin through the shoot and allow auxin to accumulate on the shaded side. Therefore, **Choice B**  is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because auxin enters cells by simple diffusion rather than facilitated diffusion and does not leave by simple diffusion.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because auxin does not enter cells by endocytosis, although its movement out of cells involves active transport.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because auxin enters cells by simple diffusion and leaves through efflux carriers by active transport, not the reverse."},{"ref":"BI1298","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1298\n\nWhich phytohormone’s production brings about the ripening of fruit?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Auxin\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cytokinin\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Gibberellin\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ethylene","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nEthylene is a phytohormone whose production starts to increase as the fruit approaches maturity, and it plays a vital role in initiating and regulating the ripening process; **Choice D**  is correct.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **C**  are incorrect as they are all involved in plant growth and development."},{"ref":"BI1299","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1299\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast the principal sources of auxin and cytokinins and their roles in plant growth.        [4 marks]\n\n2. Describe how positive feedback is involved in synchronised and rapid fruit ripening.        [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain how the phototropic response, mediated by auxin in the shoot, triggers changes in the cell wall structure.    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** similarity and **one** difference to gain full marks*):\n\n***Differences***\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ auxin is produced mainly in shoot apical meristems **OR** young leaves, **WHEREAS** cytokinins are produced mainly in root apical meristems      [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ auxin primarily promotes cell elongation, **WHEREAS** cytokinins primarily promote cell division     [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ auxin from the shoot apex promotes apical dominance **OR** inhibits lateral bud growth, **WHEREAS** cytokinins promote lateral bud growth         [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ auxin promotes the initiation of lateral **OR** adventitious roots, **WHEREAS** a relatively high cytokinin concentration promotes shoot formation     [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ auxin delays leaf **OR** fruit abscission, **WHEREAS** cytokinins delay leaf senescence     [1 mark]\n\n***Similarities***\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are chemical messengers that regulate plant growth and development at low concentrations      [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are produced in actively growing plant tissues      [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** regulate the growth and development of roots and shoots      [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ the relative concentrations of **BOTH** hormones help coordinate root and shoot development      [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can be transported from their principal sites of production to other parts of the plant      [1 mark]\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n**Auxin** is produced mainly in **shoot apical meristems and young leaves**, whereas **cytokinins** are produced mainly in **root apical meristems** ($\\checkmark$). **Both hormones** regulate the growth and development of **roots and shoots** ($\\checkmark$). Auxin primarily promotes **cell elongation**, whereas cytokinins primarily promote **cell division** ($\\checkmark$). **Auxin** from the **shoot apex** inhibits **lateral bud growth**, whereas **cytokinins** promote lateral bud growth ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ positive feedback is when a change stimulates further change in the same direction **OR** the output of a process increases the rate of that process        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ethylene **OR** ethene is produced in ripening fruit **OR** maturing fruit        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ethylene **OR** ethene speeds up the ripening process of the fruit that produced it     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as the fruit ripens further, it produces more ethylene    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nearby fruit detect ethylene **OR** ethene        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nearby fruit starts to ripen                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ overall ethylene **OR** ethene production increases        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ more fruit ripen **OR** a large number of fruit ripen **OR** fruit ripening is synchronised due to the amplifying nature of the positive feedback loop        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ synchronised ripening means many fruits are ripe at the same time, attracting seed-dispersing animals **OR** making seed dispersal more efficient    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Positive feedback** is when a **change** stimulates **further change** in the **same direction** ($\\checkmark$). This can occur in synchronised and rapid fruit ripening, where **ethylene** is produced in **ripening fruits**  ($\\checkmark$), and other nearby fruits **detect** the ethylene ($\\checkmark$), so they **also** begin to **ripen** ($\\checkmark$). The ripe fruit then releases its **own ethylene**, so that even **more** nearby fruit **ripens**, so ripening is **rapid** and **synchronised** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ auxin is positively phototropic **OR** is produced in the shoot tip **OR** coleoptile in response to light            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light causes auxin to diffuse into cells on the shaded side of the shoot, **OR** light causes auxin to be actively transported out of cells on the illuminated side of the shoot     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ auxin accumulates on the dark side of the shoot    **OR** auxin redistributes to the shaded side [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ auxin stimulates the activity of proton pumps in the cell membrane    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ proton pumps actively transport protons (H:sup[+]) from the cytoplasm into the cell wall    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ auxin promotes hydrogen ion secretion into  non-living part of the cells **OR** cell walls/cell wall junctions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ acidifying the cell wall    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ low pH activates expansins    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ altering the pattern of hydrogen bonding in polysaccharides in the cell wall **OR** expansins trigger the breakage of crosslinks within cellulose **OR** expansins break hydrogen bonds between cellulose microfibrils and other cell wall components        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the resistance of the cell wall to stretching **OR** cell elongation is decreased, **OR** cell wall becomes more flexible, **OR** cell can take up water more readily, leading to turgor pressure-driven cell expansion or elongation                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell turgor causes stretching **OR** elongation of the cell wall            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ auxin regulates gene expression to promote cell elongation on the shaded side    [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Auxin is a phytohormone, produced in **shoot tips** in response to **light**  ($\\checkmark$). Light causes **auxin** to **diffuse** into cells on the **shaded** side of the shoot ($\\checkmark$) so that it **accumulates** in the **shaded** side ($\\checkmark$). Auxin promotes **hydrogen ion secretion** into the **cell walls and cell wall junctions**  ($\\checkmark$), which **acidifies** the **cell wall** ($\\checkmark$). Low pH activates proteins called **expansins** ($\\checkmark$), and expansins alter the pattern of **hydrogen bonding in polysaccharides** in the cell wall ($\\checkmark$), which **lowers** the resistance of cell walls to **stretching**  ($\\checkmark$). As **water enters** the cell the **turgor pressure** causes the **stretching** and **elongation** of the cell ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1301","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1301\n\nWhat function is under the voluntary control of the somatic nervous system?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Control of heart rate\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Control of skeletal muscle\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Reaction to pain\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Peristalsis in the small intestine","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe somatic nervous system controls voluntary responses, whereas the autonomic nervous system controls involuntary responses. Skeletal muscle coordinates movement, which is a voluntary response; **Choice B**  is correct.\n\nControl of heart rate, the reaction to pain and peristalsis in the small intestine are all involuntary responses and are controlled by the autonomic nervous system; **Choices A, C** and **D**  are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1302","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1302\n\nWhich is the correct order for a pain reflex arc?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Pain stimulus → pain receptor in skin → relay neuron → motor neuron →  sensory neuron → muscle movement\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Pain stimulus → pain receptor in skin → motor neuron → brain →  sensory neuron → muscle movement\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Pain stimulus → sensory neuron → relay neuron→ pain receptor in skin →  motor neuron → muscle movement\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Pain stimulus → pain receptor in skin → sensory neuron → relay neuron →  motor neuron → muscle movement","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe response to pain is involuntary and is, therefore, a reflex. Pain receptors in the skin detect pain, and the signal is transmitted through a sensory neuron to a relay neuron in the spinal cord. Next, the signal is transmitted through the motor neuron, and this coordinates muscle movement away from the source of pain. **Choice D**  is correct.\n\n**Choices A** and **C**  are incorrect, as the order of the reflex arc is incorrect. **Choice B**  is incorrect as the order is incorrect, and the brain is not involved in reflex responses."},{"ref":"BI1303","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1303\n\nWhat is the role of the part of the brain labelled **X** in the diagram?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Integration%20of%20Body%20Systems/images/BI1303.png\" alt=\"BI1303\">\n[Source: Adapted from Injurymap. (2019, April 24). *File: human Brain.png* - Wikimedia Commons. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved October 02, 2023 from URL https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Human_Brain.png.  Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Responsible for long-term memory\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Controls emotional responses\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Controls posture and balance\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Modulates the hunger response","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nX is pointing to the cerebellum, which controls involuntary muscle movements of posture and balance as well as precise voluntary hand movements such as writing. **Choice C** is correct.\n\nLong-term memory is controlled by the hippocampus and related structures in the temporal lobe; **Choice A**  is incorrect. Emotional responses are controlled by the limbic system, which does not contain the cerebellum; **Choice B** is incorrect. The hypothalamus controls the hunger response in the brain; **Choice D**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1304","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1304\n\nWhich of the following statements describes the effect of epinephrine on the body?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Causes the sinoatrial node to increase the heart rate |\n    | II. | Stimulates the liver cells to convert glucagon into glucose |\n    | III. | Causes the pupils to dilate |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nEpinephrine is a peptide hormone secreted by the adrenal glands to prepare for vigorous activity. It causes the sinoatrial node (pacemaker) to increase the heart rate (**Statement I**) and the pupils to dilate (**Statement III**). **Choice C**  is correct.\n\nGlucagon is a hormone released by the pancreas in response to low blood glucose levels; it converts stored glycogen into glucose. Epinephrine initiates the conversion of glycogen into glucose; **Statement II**  is incorrect. **Choices A, B** and **D**  are incorrect as they all include **Statement II**."},{"ref":"BI1305","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1305\n\nBaroreceptors are sensory receptors which respond to stretching in the cardiovascular system in response to changes in blood pressure. In what parts of the cardiovascular system are they **not** located?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Veins\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Walls of the aorta\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Carotid arteries\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Wall of the right atrium","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nBaroreceptors are not located in the veins, as they carry blood around the body at low pressure, and so would not provide the brain with accurate signals to modulate blood pressure; **Choice A**  is correct.\n\nBaroreceptors are in the walls of the aorta, carotid arteries and walls of the right atrium, as stretching in these parts of the cardiovascular system sends signals to the brain to adjust blood pressure. **Choices B, C** and **D**  are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1306","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1306\n\nHow do impulses from the expiratory and inspiratory centres work together to cause rhythmic breathing?\n\n| | Expiratory centre | Inspiratory centre |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Sends impulses to inhibit the inspiratory centre | Sends impulses to inhibit the expiratory centre |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Sends impulses to inhibit the inspiratory centre | Sends impulses to increase rate and depth of breathing |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Sends impulses to increase the depth of breathing | Sends impulses to increase the rate of expiration |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Sends impulses to stimulate expiration | Sends impulses to decrease the volume of the alveoli |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe respiratory centre is located in the medulla of the hindbrain and contains two groups of interacting nerve cells: the inspiratory and expiratory centres. The inspiratory centre sends impulses to increase the rate and depth of breathing, and the expiratory centre sends impulses to inhibit the inspiratory centre and stimulate expiration. **Choice B**  is correct.\n\nThe inspiratory centre does not send impulses to inhibit the expiratory centre; **Choice A**  is incorrect. The expiratory centre does not send impulses to increase the depth of breathing; this is the role of the inspiratory centre, and the inspiratory centre does send impulses to increase the rate of expiration, it instead stimulates inspiration; **Choice C**  is incorrect. **Choice D**  is incorrect as although the expiratory centre does send impulses to stimulate expiration, the action of the inspiratory centre would not promote the alveoli to decrease in volume. Instead, it would increase the volume of the alveoli."},{"ref":"BI1307","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1307\n\nWhat effect does strenuous exercise have on blood carbon dioxide concentration, which receptors detect it, and what changes does this cause in the respiratory system?\n\n| | Effect on blood carbon dioxide concentration | Receptors that detect changes in carbon dioxide concentration | Changes to respiratory system |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Decreases | Chemoreceptors | Intercostal muscles contract less rapidly |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Increases | Baroreceptors | Diaphragm contracts more rapidly |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Decreases | Baroreceptors | Diaphragm contracts less rapidly |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Increases | Chemoreceptors | Intercostal muscles contract more rapidly |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nRespiration is a reaction that occurs in cells to release energy from the reaction between oxygen and glucose and produces carbon dioxide and water as byproducts. When conducting strenuous exercise, respiration rates increase; therefore, blood carbon dioxide concentration increases. Changes to blood carbon dioxide levels are detected by chemoreceptors and cause the intercostal muscles and diaphragm to contract more rapidly to provide cells with more oxygen and also to get rid of carbon dioxide. **Choice D**  is correct.\n\nStrenuous exercise does not cause the blood carbon dioxide concentration to decrease, nor does it cause the intercostal muscles to contract less rapidly; **Choice A**  is incorrect. Baroreceptors detect changes in the stretch of blood vessels, not carbon dioxide concentration; **Choice B**  is incorrect. During strenuous exercise, blood carbon dioxide concentration increases. Changes in blood carbon dioxide levels are detected by chemoreceptors, which send signals that result in the diaphragm contracting more rapidly; **Choice C**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1308","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1308\n\nHow is the movement of food through the alimentary canal controlled?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The enteric nervous system relaxes the muscles in the alimentary canal, so food can easily glide through.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The enteric nervous system coordinates peristalsis through the alimentary canal.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The enteric nervous system powerfully contracts the lower third of the oesophagus, so food is propelled through the alimentary canal.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The somatic nervous system coordinates peristalsis through the alimentary canal.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nPeristalsis are waves of muscular contraction and relaxation in the circular and longitudinal muscles of the alimentary canal, which propel digested food through the alimentary canal from the oesophagus to the rectum. Peristalsis is controlled by a part of the autonomic nervous system called the enteric nervous system. **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect, as food is propelled through the alimentary canal through coordinated waves of muscle contraction and relaxation. **Choice C** is incorrect, as peristalsis occurs throughout most of the alimentary canal, not just in the oesophagus. **Choice D** is incorrect as the somatic nervous system controls voluntary movements, and the movement of food through the alimentary canal is mostly an autonomic process."},{"ref":"BI1309","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1309\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe image shows a pain reflex arc in the hand.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Integration%20of%20Body%20Systems/images/BI1309iii.JPEG\" alt=\"BI1309iii\">\n\n1. State what type of neuron is indicated by the letter X. [1 mark]\n\n2.\n 1. State the region of the spinal cord where neuron X is located. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Explain why it is important that a pain reflex is an involuntary response. [2 marks]\n\nTrauma to the brain can disrupt the coordination of movement, even when spinal reflexes such as the pain reflex remain intact.\n\n3. Suggest one effect of damage to the cerebellum on a person's ability to carry out everyday activities. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ relay neuron **OR** interneuron [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n X is a **relay** neuron ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ grey matter [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Grey matter** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 2. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the response is rapid, so it reduces **OR** prevents injury [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the signal does not need to travel to the brain for conscious processing [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ only a few synapses are involved, so transmission is fast [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the response is automatic **OR** consistent, so it does not depend on a decision [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The pain reflex is involuntary, so the signal does **not need to be processed consciously** by the brain ($\\checkmark$). This makes the response **rapid**, so the hand is withdrawn before serious injury occurs ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ loss of balance **OR** unsteady walking **OR** staggering [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ poor coordination of movements **OR** clumsiness [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ difficulty with fine motor tasks [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ tremor when reaching for objects **OR** overshooting a target [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ difficulty maintaining posture [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Damage to the cerebellum would cause a **loss of balance** ($\\checkmark$), so the person would walk unsteadily and may fall."},{"ref":"BI1310","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1310\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe image shows the adrenal glands.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Integration%20of%20Body%20Systems/images/BI1310.png\" alt=\"BI1310\">\n[Source: Adapted from Poghosyan97. (2020, July 8). *Category: adrenal glands*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved October 09, 2023 from URL https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category: Adrenal_glands. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n1.\n    1. State the name of the hormone released from the adrenal glands that is responsible for the ‘fight or flight’ response.    [1 mark]\n\n    2. Explain how secretion of the hormone you named in part (i) affects the heart to prepare the body for vigorous exercise.    [3 marks]\n\n2. Suggest an effect on the body if the adrenal glands do not function properly.    [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. any **one** of the following:\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ epinephrine **OR** adrenaline    [1 mark]\n        [1 mark]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n        Epinephrine ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    2. any **three** of the following:\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ binds to receptors at the sinoatrial node, increasing heart rate    [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ an increased heart rate pumps blood more rapidly to working muscles        [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ delivers more oxygen and glucose for increased aerobic respiration        [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ greater ATP supply prepares the muscles for vigorous activity [1 mark]\n        [3 marks]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n        Epinephrine binds to the **sinoatrial node** of the heart and **increases heart rate**  ($\\checkmark$). This allows **blood** to reach the **muscles more rapidly**, delivering more **oxygen** and **glucose** ($\\checkmark$). The increase in **aerobic respiration**  produces **more ATP**, preparing the body for vigorous exercise  ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ altered **OR** slow response to stress **OR** stress response impairment [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ low energy levels **OR** tiredness                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ low blood pressure                            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stomach pain when stressed                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ metabolic disruption **OR** change in body weight **OR** blood sugar irregularities     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ immune system suppression                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mood swings **OR** changes in emotions            [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    If the adrenal glands did not work properly, then a person might have an **altered response to stress** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1313","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1313\n\nWhich of these statements **does not** relate to peristalsis?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Longitudinal muscles and circular muscles work together.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    Circular muscles prevent the bolus from moving backwards.\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    Longitudinal muscles shorten the alimentary canal.\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    It is under the voluntary control of the enteric nervous system","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nLongitudinal and circular muscles work together to move the bolus of food along the alimentary canal (**Choice A**). The longitudinal muscles contract to shorten the canal (**Choice C**) and circular muscles contract to narrow and push the contents forward, preventing backwards movement (**Choice B**).  The series of contractions of these smooth muscles is known as peristalsis. The bolus of food stretches the walls of the alimentary canal, which stimulates stretch receptors in the walls. The response is to cause muscles behind the bolus to contract to push it along and cause the muscles ahead of the bolus to relax, allowing the bolus to move forward. This type of response is under involuntary control as it is not under conscious thought, therefore **Choice D** is not a true statement and is, therefore, the correct answer."},{"ref":"BI1398","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1398\n\nWhich plant tropism is correctly matched with its response?\n\n| | Tropism | Response |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Positive phototropism | Shoot growth towards light |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Positive phototropism | Root growth towards light |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Negative phototropism | Root growth towards light |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Negative phototropism | Shoot growth towards light |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because phototropism is the ability of plants to respond to light. Differential auxin concentrations in the stem allow plants to grow towards the light. This is positive phototropism.\n\n**Choices B, C** and **D** are incorrect, as the tropisms are mismatched with the responses."},{"ref":"BI1546","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1546\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nExplain how the ventilation rate in humans changes with strenuous exercise.         [6 marks]","markScheme":"**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nAny **six** of the following:\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ rate of cellular respiration increases with exercise    [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ increased concentration of carbon dioxide in blood        [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ blood becomes more acidic **OR** blood pH decreases        [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ decrease in pH **OR** increase in hydrogen ion concentration detected by chemoreceptors  [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ impulse **OR** signal sent to respiratory control centre **OR** inspiratory centre     [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ located in the **OR** medulla oblongata **OR** brainstem        [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ impulse **OR** signal sent to intercostal muscles **OR** diaphragm to increase rate **OR** strength of contraction [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ breathing **OR** ventilation rate increases        [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ tidal volume **OR** depth of breathing increases        [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ brings carbon dioxide concentration down **OR** pH up back within normal range    [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ Negative feedback loop **OR** mechanism            [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ long-term exercise increases number of alveoli **OR** surface area **OR** vital capacity     [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ training reduces resting breathing **OR** ventilation rate        [1 mark]\n[6 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nDuring strenuous exercise, the **rate** of **cellular respiration increases** ($\\checkmark$), leading to a **higher concentration** of **carbon dioxide** in the blood ($\\checkmark$), which **lowers blood pH** ($\\checkmark$) and makes it more acidic. **Chemoreceptors** such as those in the **aorta**, **detect** this **decrease in pH** ($\\checkmark$) and send **nervous impulses** to the **respiratory control centre** in the **medulla oblongata** ($\\checkmark$). The **medulla** then sends nervous **impulses** to the **intercostal muscles** and **diaphragm** to **increase** their **contraction rate** ($\\checkmark$), resulting in an **increased breathing rate** ($\\checkmark$) to excrete more carbon dioxide and take up more oxygen."},{"ref":"BI2336","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2336\n\nChemoreceptors in the body detect and monitor various blood variables to regulate ventilation rate. Which of the following is the factor that most directly drives changes in ventilation rate under normal conditions?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Blood oxygen concentration\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Blood pH\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Blood carbon dioxide concentration\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Blood osmolarity","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nChemoreceptors primarily monitor blood pH to regulate ventilation rate. Since carbon dioxide dissolves in blood to form carbonic acid, which dissociates into H⁺ and HCO₃⁻ ions, an increase in carbon dioxide lowers blood pH. This results in an increase in ventilation rate to excrete carbon dioxide and restore pH balance. **Choice B** is the correct answer.\n\nBlood oxygen concentration plays a secondary role, with chemoreceptors only responding to severe hypoxia (very low oxygen levels). Variations in oxygen concentration in the blood are, therefore, not involved in the main feedback control of ventilation rate; **Choice A** is incorrect.\n\nWhile blood carbon dioxide concentration indirectly affects ventilation, chemoreceptors detect pH changes caused by variations in carbon dioxide concentrations, rather than carbon dioxide directly; **Choice C** is incorrect.\n\n Blood osmolarity is not directly involved in ventilation control; **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI2579","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2579\n\nThe sweet pea plant (*Lathyrus oleraceus*) coils around other plants or structures for support.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Integration%20of%20Body%20Systems/images/BI2579.png\" alt=\"BI2579\">\n\nWhen a tendril touches another plant or support, a phytohormone is transported and accumulates on the opposite side (the side not in contact). This causes cell elongation on the non-touching side and less growth on the touching side, leading to the coiling effect.\n\nWhich hypotheses could be inferred from the information given?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n | | |\n | :-- | :-- |\n | I. | The phytohormone is likely to be an auxin. |\n | II. | The coiling effect is likely a form of tropism. |\n | III. | The phytohormone is likely to move to the opposite side of the plant via diffusion. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** could be inferred from the information. Auxin is the primary phytohormone involved in directional growth responses such as phototropism and gravitropism. The description states that a phytohormone accumulates on the opposite side and causes differential growth, which matches the known action of auxin.\n\n**Statement II** could be inferred from the information. Tropisms are directional growth responses in plants that occur in response to external stimuli. In this example, the tendril grows in response to touch, which is characteristic of thigmotropism (a growth response to mechanical stimulation).\n\n**Statement III** cannot be inferred from the information. Although the description states that the phytohormone moves to the opposite side of the tendril, it does not specify the mechanism of transport. Auxin transport occurs through specialised transport proteins and active transport mechanisms rather than simple diffusion.\n\n**Choice B** is correct because **Statements I** and **II** are supported by the information given, whereas **Statement III** is not."},{"ref":"BI2581","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2581\n\nA scientist treated a plant shoot with an inhibitor that blocked auxin efflux carriers. What is the most likely effect on the plant’s growth?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The shoot will bend towards the light more quickly.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The shoot will fail to bend towards the light.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The shoot will bend away from the light.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The shoot will grow at the same rate but without directional bending.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because auxin efflux carriers are necessary for redistributing auxin to the shaded side of the shoot, where auxin stimulates cell elongation. If these carriers are blocked, auxin remains evenly distributed, and no differential growth occurs, meaning the shoot grows straight instead of bending toward the light.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because blocking auxin efflux carriers prevents the normal redistribution of auxin to the shaded side of the shoot. If auxin is not concentrated on one side, differential cell elongation cannot occur, meaning phototropic bending will not happen.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because blocking auxin efflux carriers disrupts auxin distribution but does not cause an inversion of the response (i.e., bending away from light).\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because if auxin cannot be redistributed, the plant may still grow, but without bending (which makes this option seem plausible). However, auxin cannot be efficiently transported down the shoot, which leads to auxin accumulation in the shoot tip rather than being redistributed to the elongation zone. With less auxin reaching the elongation zone, less cell expansion and slower growth occur."},{"ref":"BI2583","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2583\n\nA plant which has a mutation that inhibits auxin production is treated with a synthetic auxin-like compound. This compound, which is much larger than natural auxin molecules, is applied to the extracellular spaces in a plant stem. Which of the following diagrams best represents the most likely result?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A. | | $\\hspace{2cm}$ | |B.|\n| |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Integration%20of%20Body%20Systems/images/AnsA.png\" alt=\"AnsA\">| | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Integration%20of%20Body%20Systems/images/AnsB.png\" alt=\"AnsB\">|\n|C. | | | |D. |\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Integration%20of%20Body%20Systems/images/AnsC.png\" alt=\"AnsC\">| | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Integration%20of%20Body%20Systems/images/Ans%20D.png\" alt=\"Ans D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because the question states that the synthetic compound is much larger than natural auxin. If it is too large to pass through auxin transporters, it will remain in the extracellular spaces in a plant stem (the apoplast) and cannot enter. Since auxin must be transported inside cells to activate proton pumps and trigger the acid-growth mechanism, this synthetic compound will be unable to induce cell elongation or phototropic bending, so the stem will grow without bending. Other phytohormones, such as gibberellins and cytokinins, will still promote some growth even in the absence of auxin.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because this would happen only if auxin successfully entered cells and promoted elongation. Since this compound is large and stays outside the cells of the stem (in the apoplast), it won’t induce bending. This could happen if the synthetic compound was able to bind extracellularly, but there is nothing in the question to indicate this.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because if the synthetic auxin compound was able to enter cells on the treated side, then we would see bending in the other direction (away from the treated side).\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the plant may still have other hormones (e.g., gibberellins, cytokinins) that promote some growth, even without auxin. Growth would be stunted but not completely halted."},{"ref":"BI2585","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2585\n\nThe table shows the data collected from an experiment which investigated the effect of cytokinin concentration on root and shoot growth.\n\n| Cytokinin concentration / mg dm$^{-3}$ | Root growth / mm | Shoot growth / mm |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| 0.0 | 11 | 78 |\n| 0.2 | 32 | 57 |\n| 0.5 | 49 | 54 |\n| 1.0 | 70 | 38 |\n| 2.0 | 79 | 33 |\n| 4.0 | 62 | 19 |\n\nIf a plant is treated with 5 mg dm$^{-3}$ cytokinin, what would be expected?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Shoot growth will be at its lowest and root growth will increase.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Shoot growth will be at its highest and root growth will decrease.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Root growth will increase and shoot growth will be its lowest.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Root growth and shoot growth will both decrease.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because based on the data in the table, root growth and shoot growth decrease from $2.0-4.0$ mg dm$^{-3}$, so this trend will likely continue if increased to 5 mg dm$^{-3}$.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because we are unable to determine if shoot growth will reach its lowest point, and root growth is unlikely to increase. **Choice B** is incorrect because we are unable to determine if shoot growth will be at its highest point; this is also very unlikely based on the data. **Choice C** is incorrect because root growth is unlikely to increase."},{"ref":"BI2888","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2888\n\nPlant shoots detect and respond to light. What membrane protein is involved in phototropism, and what mechanism does it control?\n\n| | Protein | Mechanism |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Efflux carriers | Movement of  auxin into cells |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Protein channels  | Movement of hydrogen ions into cell wall |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Efflux carriers | Movement of auxin out of cells |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ |  Protein channels | Movement of cytokinin from roots to shoots |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn phototropism, auxin is redistributed to the shaded side of the plant shoot in response to light. The efflux carriers (such as PIN proteins) are membrane proteins that actively transport auxin out of the cells. The auxin accumulates on the shaded side of the shoot, where it promotes cell elongation, causing the plant to bend towards the light. Efflux carriers are necessary because, once auxin enters the cell, it becomes negatively charged (as it loses a proton), and cannot diffuse out passively. Therefore, efflux carriers are essential for moving auxin out of the cell and contributing to the phototropic response. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because efflux carriers are responsible for moving auxin out of cells, not into them. Auxin diffuses freely into cells.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because hydrogen ion (H⁺) movement into the cell wall is mediated by proton pumps, not protein channels. Proton pumps actively transport hydrogen ions from inside the cell into the apoplast (the cell wall region), which acidifies the wall and facilitates cell elongation.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because cytokinin is not involved in phototropism. Cytokinin is produced in the roots and travels to the shoots via the xylem to promote cell division and growth. It is unrelated to the movement of auxin or the mechanism of phototropism, where the key hormone is auxin."}]},{"name":"Neural Signalling","questions":[{"ref":"BI0412","marks":"10","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0412\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nNeuronal cells have special membrane properties that allow their axons to propagate action potential from one cell to another. The diagram below shows a portion of an axonal plasma membrane.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Neural%20Signalling/images/sodium%20potassium%20pump.png\" alt=\"sodium potassium pump\">\n\n1.\n 1. State the role of a sodium-potassium pump in the axon. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Describe the action of the sodium-potassium pump. [3 marks]\n\n2. Distinguish between simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion and active transport. [2 marks]\n\n3. Compare and contrast sodium-potassium pumps and voltage-gated potassium channels. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ active transport **AND** higher concentration of sodium outside the cell **AND** higher concentration of potassium inside the cell **OR** actively transports three sodium ions out of the cell and two potassium ions into the cell for each cycle of its activity\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ helps establish **OR** restore **OR** maintain the resting membrane potential [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ facilitates the transport of other substances across the cell membrane **OR** enables secondary active transport **OR** cotransport **OR** antiport [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n The sodium-potassium pump **uses energy** to move Na$^{+}$ and K$^{+}$ ions against their concentration gradients **to maintain a higher concentration** of sodium **outside** the axon and a higher concentration of potassium **inside** the axon. ($\\checkmark$)\n\n 2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ three sodium ions attach to their binding sites on the pump which is open to the inside of the axon [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP transfers a phosphate group from itself to the pump causing the pump to change shape and the interior is then closed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the interior of the pump opens to the outside of the axon **OR** the three sodium ions are released [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ two potassium ions from the outside can then attach to their binding sites [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the binding of potassium causes the release of the phosphate group **OR** this causes the pump to change shape again so that it is only open to the inside of the axon [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the interior of the pump opens to the inside of the axon thus releasing the two potassium ions [1 mark]\n\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nThe sodium-potassium pump is responsible for active transport which maintains a high concentration of sodium and a low concentration of potassium inside the axon. When the pump is open to the inside of the axon, **three sodium ions attach to their binding sites** ($\\checkmark$). The **pump changes shape once ATP transfers a phosphate group** from itself to the pump ($\\checkmark$). **Three sodium ions are released** to the outside and **two potassium ions attach** to their binding sites ($\\checkmark$). The **pump changes shape again so that the two potassium ions are released towards the inside of the axon**. ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ simple diffusion and facilitated do not require energy **WHILE** active transport requires energy [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ simple diffusion and facilitated diffusion transport substances down their concentration gradient **WHILE** active transport transports substances up their concentration gradient [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ simple diffusion does not involve membrane proteins **WHILE** facilitated diffusion and active transport require membrane bound proteins [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n**Simple diffusion transports substances** down their concentration gradient requiring **neither energy nor membrane proteins**. Facilitated diffusion requires **membrane proteins** but does not use energy while active transport requires **both membrane proteins** ($\\checkmark$) and **energy** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Na$^{+}$-K$^{+}$ pump and K$^{+}$ channel are **BOTH** involved in the transport of potassium ions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Na$^{+}$-K$^{+}$ pump and K$^{+}$ channel are **BOTH** transmembrane **OR** integral proteins [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Na$^{+}$-K$^{+}$ pump and K$^{+}$ channel **BOTH** play an important role in the generation of action potentials in neurons [1 mark]\n\n Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Na$^{+}$-K$^{+}$ pumps require energy **WHILE** K$^{+}$ channels do not [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Na$^{+}$-K$^{+}$ pumps transport ions up their concentration gradient **WHILE** K$^{+}$ channels transport ions down their concentration gradient [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Na$^{+}$-K$^{+}$ pumps are not are not controlled by voltage **WHILE** K$^{+}$ channels open and close in response to changes in voltage [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Na$^{+}$-K$^{+}$ pumps transport potassium ions from the outside to the inside of the axon **WHILE** K$^{+}$ channels allow potassium ions to diffuse out of the axon [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ K$^{+}$ channels transport only potassium ions **WHEREAS** Na$^{+}$-K$^{+}$ pumps (anti-port) transport potassium and sodium ions [1 mark]\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n| Sodium-potassium pumps | Potassium channels | $\\hspace{1cm}$|\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| Transport potassium ions | Transport potassium ions | ($\\checkmark$) |\n| Is a transmembrane protein | Is a transmembrane protein | ($\\checkmark$) |\n| Require energy | No energy needed| ($\\checkmark$) |\n| Not voltage-gated | Voltage-gated | ($\\checkmark$) |\n| Transport potassium ions from the outside to the inside of an axon | Transport potassium ions from the inside to the outside of an axon | ($\\checkmark$) |"},{"ref":"BI0511","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0511\n\nThe graph below shows membrane voltage over time during an action potential. Which letter represents the segment of the action potential when voltage-gated sodium channels are open and voltage-gated potassium channels are closed?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Neural%20Signalling/images/511_6.5.png\" alt=\"511_6.5\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nOnce a neuron reaches threshold potential, the voltage-gated sodium channels in the membrane open. Once these channels are open, sodium diffuses down its concentration gradient into the cell. The rapid influx of positively charged sodium ions depolarises the cell to a positive voltage. **Choice B** on the graph indicates the depolarisation phase, so corresponds to the time during the action potential in which voltage-gated potassium channels are closed and voltage-gated sodium channels are open.\n\nAt resting potential (**Choice A**), both voltage-gated sodium and potassium channels are closed. During the repolarisation phase (**Choice C**), voltage-gated potassium channels are open and voltage-gated sodium channels are closed. During the hyperpolarisation phase (**Choice D**), voltage-gated sodium channels are closed and voltage-gated potassium channels are in the process of closing."},{"ref":"BI0554","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0554\n\nThe diagram shows a process that occurs in the membranes of neurons during a stage of the action potential. What process does the diagram depict?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Neural%20Signalling/images/554_6.5.png\" alt=\"554_6.5\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Open sodium channels initiating depolarisation.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The sodium/potassium pump initiating depolarisation.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Open sodium channels restoring the resting potential.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The sodium/potassium pump restoring the resting potential.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe four labelled steps in the diagram show the following:\n\n$\\hspace{0.5em}$ 1. The binding of three sodium ions (Na:sup[+]) on the inside of the neuron to the protein pump embedded in the membrane of the neuron\n\n$\\hspace{0.5em}$ 2. The hydrolysis of ATP to ADP, releasing energy which allows the change in conformation of the protein to transport the three sodium ions out of the neuron\n\n$\\hspace{0.5em}$ 3. The binding of two potassium ions (K:sup[+]) on the outside of the neuron to the protein pump embedded in the membrane of the neuron\n\n$\\hspace{0.5em}$ 4. The release of the two potassium ions into the inside of the neuron\n\nThe diagram shows the active transport of three sodium ions out of the neuron and the transport of two potassium ions into the neuron. Therefore, this diagram shows the sodium/potassium pump establishing the resting potential; **Choice D** is correct.\n\nDuring depolarisation sodium ions enter the neuron through sodium channels, which is not being shown by this diagram; **Choice A** is incorrect.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the sodium/potassium pump is responsible for establishing the resting potential, not initiating depolarisation\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because the diagram shows the sodium/potassium pump, not open sodium channels. Furthermore, open sodium channels depolarise the cell, they do not restore the resting membrane potential."},{"ref":"BI0555","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0555\n\nSome acetylcholine released by a motor neuron at the neuromuscular junction stimulates muscle contraction, while the remainder is rapidly removed from the synapse.\n\nWhich processes are required for each of these to happen?\n\n| | Stimulation of contraction | Removal of excess acetylcholine |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | receptor binding | enzymatic degradation followed by reuptake of choline |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | endocytosis | endocytosis followed by lysosomal degradation |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | receptor binding | endocytosis followed by lysosomal degradation |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | endocytosis | enzymatic degradation followed by reuptake of choline |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAcetylcholine is a neurotransmitter released by motor neurons at neuromuscular junctions. Once released, acetylcholine diffuses across the synaptic cleft to bind to receptors on the muscle cell. The binding of acetylcholine to receptors initiates the depolarization of the muscle cell that leads to contraction. So, receptor binding is required for the stimulation of muscle contraction (**Choice A** and **Choice C**).\n\nThe excess acetylcholine in the synaptic cleft needs to be removed to prevent overstimulation of the muscle. This removal happens through the hydrolysis of acetylcholine by the enzyme acetylcholinesterase to produce acetic acid and choline. The choline is taken up by the motor neuron so it may be used to make more acetylcholine. So, the removal of excess acetylcholine is achieved by enzymatic degradation followed by the reuptake of choline (**Choice A**).\n\n**Choice B** and **Choice D** are incorrect because endocytosis is not involved in muscle stimulation. **Choice C** is incorrect as acetylcholine is broken down before choline diffuses back across the synaptic cleft to be taken up and reused by the neuron."},{"ref":"BI0625","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0625\n\nThe diagram shows a neuron.\n\nWhich arrow indicates a dendrite?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Neural%20Signalling/images/BI0625.png\" alt=\"BI0625\">\n[Source: Adapted from Dhp1080. (2008, June 29). *File:derived neuron schema with no labels.svg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved August 6, 2022, from https ://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Derived_Neuron_schema_with_no_labels.svg  CC-BY-3.0]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n**Choice A** is correct because it points to a dendrite, a branching extension of the cell body that receives signals from other neurons and carries electrical changes towards the cell body.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because it points to a node of Ranvier, a gap between adjacent segments of the myelin sheath where the axon membrane is not covered by myelin.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because it points to a Schwann cell, which wraps around a section of the axon and forms a segment of the myelin sheath.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because it points to a terminal branch of the axon, not a dendrite. These branches occur at the far end of the axon and form connections with target cells."},{"ref":"BI0626","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0626\n\nBelow is an action potential graph.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Neural%20Signalling/images/BI0626.png\" alt=\"BI0626\">\n\nWhat is happening at point X?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Repolarisation due to potassium ions diffusing out\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Repolarisation due to sodium ions diffusing in\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Depolarisation due to sodium ions diffusing out\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Depolarisation due to potassium ions diffusing in","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWhen voltage-gated potassium channels open, K$^+$ ions diffuse out down their concentration gradient, causing the inside of the neuron to become more negative. **Choice A** is correct.\\\n**Choice B** is incorrect as when sodium ions diffuse into the neuron the membrane potential rises due to the increase in positive charge inside the neuron. \\\n**Choices C** and **D** are incorrect as the term depolarisation means an increase in the membrane potential."},{"ref":"BI0627","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0627\n\nWhat are the main factors that maintain a neuron’s resting potential?\n\n| | Sodium ions | Potassium ions |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| Diffuse out | Diffuse in |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Pumped in | Pumped out |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Pumped out | Pumped in |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Diffuse in | Diffuse out |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choices A** and **D** are incorrect as the main movement of ions that contributes to the resting potential is done by the Na$^+\\text{\\textendash}$ K$^+$ pump which is an example of active transport (as opposed to diffusion, which is passive transport).  **Choice B** is incorrect as sodium ions are pumped out of the neuron, and potassium ions are pumped in, which creates the resting potential."},{"ref":"BI0628","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0628\n\nWhich membrane potential triggers the opening of the voltage-gated sodium channels?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ -70 mV\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ -50mV\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ +30 mV\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0 mV","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n-50mV is the threshold potential that must be reached for voltage-gated sodium channels to open.\\\n**Choice A** is incorrect, as -70 mV is the resting potential of a neuron. **Choice C** is incorrect as at +30 mV, voltage-gated potassium channels are triggered to open. **Choice D** is incorrect as at 0mV the voltage-gated sodium channels are already open."},{"ref":"BI0629","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0629\n\nWhich is the correct definition of saltatory conduction?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Electrical impulses jumping between nodes of Ranvier\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Neurotransmitters diffusing across the synaptic cleft\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Sodium and potassium ions being pumped across membranes\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Electrical impulses moving down an unmyelinated axon","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSaltatory conduction is when electrical impulses jump between nodes of Ranvier (the gaps between sections of the myelin sheath that surrounds axons). \\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because it is a description of synaptic transmission.\\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because it is a description of how the resting potential is maintained.\\\n**Choice D** is incorrect as it describes how an action potential travels down an unmyelinated neuron."},{"ref":"BI0630","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0630\n\nWhat is **not** a function of the myelin sheath?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ To increase impulse transmission rate\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ To insulate the axon\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ To prevent depolarisation\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ To release neurotransmitters","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nNeurotransmitters are not transferred by the myelin sheath.\\\n**Choices A**, **B** and **C** are incorrect, as they are all functions of the myelin sheath."},{"ref":"BI0631","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0631\n\nWhat causes the exocytosis of neurotransmitters at the presynaptic membrane?\n\n-    I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Depolarisation of the postsynaptic membrane\n-    II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ Depolarisation of the presynaptic membrane\n-    III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ The diffusion of calcium ions\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDepolarisation of the presynaptic membrane (**Statement II**) and the resulting influx of calcium ions into the presynaptic neuron (**Statement III**) cause the exocytosis (release) of neurotransmitters at the presynaptic membrane.\n\n**Statement I** is false, as the postsynaptic membrane receives neurotransmitters that are released from the presynaptic membrane. **Choices A**, **C** and **D** are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0632","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0632\n\nWhat happens if the threshold potential is not reached ?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ No action potential is triggered.\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ A lower amplitude action potential is triggered.\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Neurotransmitters are released at the synaptic cleft.\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The action potential moves backwards to the dendrites.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe all-or-none law states that an action potential will only be transmitted if a threshold potential is reached, and the size of the action potential does not vary. **Choice A** is correct.\n**Choice B** is incorrect as if the threshold potential isn’t reached, then no action potential is triggered. **Choice C** is incorrect because if the threshold potential is not reached, then an action potential is not triggered and so neurotransmitters are not released at the synaptic cleft.  **Choice D**  is incorrect as an action potential only moves in one direction."},{"ref":"BI0633","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0633\n\nHow do neonicotinoid pesticides work?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Binds to receptors in synapses, preventing neurotransmitters from binding\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Inhibits neurotransmitter release at the presynaptic cleft\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Reverses the direction of action potentials\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Damages the receptors on synapses","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nNeonicotinoids bind irreversibly to acetylcholine receptors at cholinergic synapses so that acetylcholine (a neurotransmitter) is unable to bind and an action potential cannot be generated.\\\n**Choices B**, **C** and **D** are incorrect as they do not describe how neonicotinoids work."},{"ref":"BI0635","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0635\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe diagram represents a synaptic cleft between two neurons in an insect\\'s nervous system.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Neural%20Signalling/images/BI0635.png\" alt=\"BI0635\">\n[Adapted from: Source Rcchang16. (2018, November 21). *Category: neurotransmitters*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved August 6, 2022, from https ://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fe/Synapse-Neurotransmitter_Release.png  CC-BY-4.0]\n\n1.\n    1. Gardeners use pesticides that contain neonicotinoids to kill pests. Label with the letter **X** where neonicotinoids would bind. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Explain how neonicotinoids work on insects.     [4 marks]\n\n    3. Suggest what the effect would be on synaptic transmission in cholinergic synapses if gardeners used pesticides that were broken down by acetylcholinesterase. [1 mark]\n\n2. Explain why neonicotinoid containing pesticides are less toxic to mammals than insects [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. $\\ce{ -> }$ an arrow labelled with **X** pointing to a receptor (any part) on the postsynaptic membrane [1 mark]\n\n        [1 mark]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Neural%20Signalling/images/BI0635_MS.png\" alt=\"BI0635_MS\">\n\n    2. any **four** of the following:\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ neonicotinoids bind to acetylcholine receptors [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ in the postsynaptic membrane [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ in cholinergic synapses [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ they bind irreversibly, so they cannot be broken down by acetylcholinesterase [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ so acetylcholine cannot bind    [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$  synaptic transmission is blocked [1 mark]\n\n        [4 marks]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n        Neonicotinoids **bind to acetylcholine receptors** ($\\checkmark$) in the **postsynaptic membranes** ($\\checkmark$) of **cholinergic synapses** ($\\checkmark$). They **bind irreversibly**, so they cannot be broken down by the enzyme acetylcholinesterase ($\\checkmark$), which normally breaks down acetylcholine. Because the neonicotinoids are bound to the receptor, the neurotransmitter **acetylcholine cannot bind** ($\\checkmark$); therefore, **synaptic transmission is blocked** ($\\checkmark$) and the insect gets paralysed and dies.\n\n    3.    any **one** of the following:\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$   acetylcholine will bind to receptors after the pesticide is broken down by acetylcholinesterase [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$   synaptic transmission will occur **OR** take place **OR** not be blocked [1 mark]\n\n        [1 mark]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nIf the pesticides used by farmers were broken down by acetylcholinesterase, they would be less effective and **synaptic transmission would occur** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ insects have proportionally more cholinergic synapses than mammal [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ neonicotinoids bind more strongly to acetylcholine receptors in insects than in mammals [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nPesticides containing neonicotinoids are less toxic to mammals because neonicotinoids **bind more strongly** to acetylcholine receptors in insects than in mammals ($\\checkmark$), preventing synaptic transmission."},{"ref":"BI0636","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0636\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nExplain how the resting potential is maintained in a neuron.     [3 marks]","markScheme":"Any **three** of the following:\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ a neuron that is not transmitting an impulse has a resting potential of -70mV [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ it is due to the difference in positive and negative charges across the membrane [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ sodium-potassium pump pumps three Na$^+$ ions out and two K$^+$ ions into the cell [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ the membrane is more permeable to K$^+$ ions than Na$^+$ ions [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ K$^+$ ions diffuse down their concentration gradient out of the neuron [1 mark]\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nA neuron that is not transmitting an impulse has a resting potential of **-70mV** ($\\checkmark$) due to the **imbalance** of positive and negative charges across the membrane ($\\checkmark$) which is maintained by the action of sodium-potassium pumps. The **sodium-potassium pump** pumps three Na$^+$ ions out and two K$^+$  ions into the cell ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0637","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0637\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Describe the structure of a typical human motor neuron. [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline why myelination is essential for the rapid transmission of impulses. [4 marks]\n\n3. Explain the importance of positive ions in the generation and propagation of an action potential. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1.    any **four** of the following (***accept a correctly annotated diagram as an answer***)  :\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ long axon extends from cell body [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myelin sheath **OR** Schwann cells wrapped around axon    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  nodes of Ranvier are gaps between Schwann cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  cell body contains nucleus and other organelles [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ dendrons extend from **OR** lead towards cell body [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ dendrites are short fibres branching from dendrons [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  axon terminal **OR** synaptic bulb **OR** synaptic knob at end of axon     [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    A typical motor neuron consists of a **cell body** containing the **nucleus** and other **organelles** ($\\checkmark$). **Dendrons extend** from the **cell body** ($\\checkmark$) and **branch** into **dendrites** ($\\checkmark$), which receive incoming signals. The **axon**, which also **extends** from the **cell body**,($\\checkmark$)  is **myelinated** by **Schwann cells** ($\\checkmark$) with **nodes of Ranvier** in between ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.    any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  myelin sheath is composed of proteins and lipids **OR** myelin is made up of Schwann cells, **OR** oligodendrocytes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  myelin acts as an insulator     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  there are gaps **OR** nodes of Ranvier between Schwann cells on the axon [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  (myelin) allows for nerve impulses to jump from node to node **OR** saltatory conduction [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  sodium ions diffuse from node to node without leaking out of the axon **OR** local currents stimulate downstream nodes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  sodium and potassium channels are concentrated at the nodes (increasing the speed of neurotransmission)    [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nSchwann cells deposit layers of **proteins and lipids** ($\\checkmark$) called myelin around axons. Gaps between myelin-containing Schwann cells on the axon are called **nodes of Ranvier** ($\\checkmark$).  Myelin acts as an **insulator** ($\\checkmark$),  so nerve impulses can **jump from node to node** ($\\checkmark$), making neurotransmission faster than in an unmyelinated neuron. **Sodium and potassium channels** are concentrated at the nodes ($\\checkmark$), which increases the speed of neurotransmission.\n\n3.    any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  an action potential is the depolarisation and repolarisation of a neuron due to the movement of positive ions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  inside the neuron, there is a high concentration of  K$^+$ ions and a low concentration of Na$^+$ ions     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  depolarisation is due to  Na$^+$ ions diffusing into the neuron [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  Na$^+$ ions diffuse in through ligand-gated channels on dendrites, **OR** cell body, **OR** synaptic transmission, causes initial Na$^+$ ion influx    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  if threshold potential is reached, **OR** at -55mV, voltage-gated Na$^+$ channels open [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  the voltage-gated influx of Na$^+$ ions makes the inside more positive compared to the outside, **OR** raising the membrane potential up to +30mV    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  (at +30mV) voltage-gated sodium channels close    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  (at +30mV) voltage-gated potassium channels open    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  repolarization happens due to K$^+$ ions diffusing out of the neuron     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  cell interior becomes more negative compared to the outside of the cell **OR** reaches -75mV **OR** hyperpolarisation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  (after -75mV) voltage-gated K$^+$ channels close    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  Na$^+\\text{\\textendash}$K$^+$ pump restores the relative concentrations of Na$^+$ and K$^+$ in the cell [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nAn **action potential** is the **depolarisation and repolarisation** of a neuron due to the movement of positive ions ($\\checkmark$). The initial depolarisation occurs on the cell body or on dendrites at synapses. Here **neurotransmitters** cause sodium channels to open and **Na$^+$ ions diffuse** into the neuron ($\\checkmark$). There is an ion imbalance in the neuron where the concentration of **Na$^+$ is higher outside the neuron** and the concentration of **K$^+$ is higher inside** the neuron ($\\checkmark$). The resting potential of a neuron is about -70 mV.\n\n    If the initial depolarisation reaches the **threshold potential**, an action potential is generated and **voltage-gated Na$^+$ channels open** ($\\checkmark$).  **Na$^+$ ions diffuse into** the neuron, leading to the membrane potential rising to **+30mV** ($\\checkmark$).  At +30mV, voltage-gated **sodium channels close** ($\\checkmark$), and voltage-gated **potassium channels open** ($\\checkmark$). This is followed by **repolarisation**, where K$^+$ ions diffuse out of the neuron ($\\checkmark$), making the inside of the cell **more negative** compared to the outside of the cell ($\\checkmark$).  During the final stage of an action potential, the **Na$^+\\text{\\textendash}$K$^+$ pump** re-establishes the initial relative concentrations ($\\checkmark$) of sodium and potassium in and out of the neuron."},{"ref":"BI0809","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0809\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nNeonicotinoids are synthetic compounds that are widely used as pesticides.  However, there are concerns about their effect on honey bees and other pollinating insects.  The table below shows the results from a study where honey bees (*Apis mellifera carnica*) were treated with non-lethal doses of two neonicotinoids, Imidacloprid and Clothianidin, and the effect it had on their homing instinct (ability to fly back to their hive).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Neural%20Signalling/images/BI0809A.png\" alt=\"BI0809A\">\n[Source: Adapted from Fischer, J., Müller, T., Spatz, A.-K., Greggers, U., Grünewald, B., & Menzel, R. (2014). *Neonicotinoids interfere with specific components of navigation in honeybees*. PLOS ONE. Retrieved June 7, 2023, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0091364 . Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n1. Describe the mode of action of pesticides containing neonicotinoids on cholinergic synapses in insects.    [2 marks]\n\n2. Using the data, outline the effect of the neonicotinoids on honey bees.    [2 marks]\n\n3. Calculate the percentage increase between the control and those treated with 0.6 μM of Imidacloprid which did **not** arrive back at their hive.     [1 mark]\n\n4. State what the dependent variable is in this study.    [1 mark]\n\n5.\n    1. Based on the data, predict the effect that exposing honey bees to neonicotinoids will have on the fitness of the honey bee colony.        [1 mark]\n\n    2. Explain your answer to part (i).                    [1 mark]\n\n6. The researchers concluded that neonicotinoids should not be used as pesticides due to their unintended effects on honey bees. Evaluate whether this data supports their conclusion.    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ neonicotinoids bind to acetylcholine receptors irreversibly **OR** cannot be broken down by acetylcholinesterase [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ acetylcholine is unable to bind to acetylcholine receptors [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ synaptic transmission is prevented [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Neonicotinoids work by **binding** to **acetylcholine receptors** that are present on cholinergic postsynaptic membranes **irreversibly** ($\\checkmark$) which **prevents acetylcholine from binding** to its own receptors ($\\checkmark$), **preventing synaptic transmission** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ treatment with neonicotinoids decreases the percentage of honey bees that arrive back at the hive **OR** increases the percentage of bees that do not arrive back at the hive [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ with higher doses of imidacloprid **OR** 0.9 µM of imidacloprid the percentage of bees that arrived back at the hive decreased dramatically, **OR** the percentage of bees that did not arrive back at the hive increased dramatically [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Treating honeybees with neonicotinoids **decreases the percentage of honey bees that arrive back** ($\\checkmark$) at the hive compared to the controls, and with the **highest dose of imidacloprid** the percentage of bees that arrived back at the hive **decreased by almost two thirds** ($\\checkmark$) compared to the controls.\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 124% **OR** 124. 4% **OR** 124.39% [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    percentage increase = ((new value - original value) ÷ original value) x 100 %\n\n    percentage increase = ((27.6 - 12.3) ÷ 12.3) x 100 % = **124** % ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the number of bees that arrive or do not arrive back at the hive [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nThe dependent variable in this study is the **number of bees** that **arrive** or **do not arrive** back at the hive ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5.\n    1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ decrease **OR** detrimental effect **OR** negative impact on health **OR** productivity [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Exposing the honey bees to neonicotinoids will **decrease** ($\\checkmark$) the fitness of the colony.\n\n5.\n    2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ less bees return to the hive lowering the number of matings [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ less bees return to the hive, so less nectar **OR** pollen **OR** food is brought back to the colony [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ less resources could impact the fertility and survival rates of queen bees [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ colony may be more susceptible to other stressors such as diseases **OR** parasites, **OR** environmental challenges [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    If less bees return to the hive, then there will be a **lower number of matings** ($\\checkmark$), lowering the fitness of the hive.\n\n6. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    *Answer must include at least one positive and one negative statement to achieve full marks.*\n\n    *Agree because…*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ neonicotinoids affect the ability of honey bees to return to their hives [1 mark]\n\n    *Disagree because…*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ only tested one species of honey bees [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ small sample size [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ only two types of neonicotinoids were tested [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Neonicotinoids should not be used as pesticides because they **affect the ability of honey bees to return to their hive** ($\\checkmark$), which decreases the fitness of the colony and may also affect their ability to pollinate. However, the study only tested a **small sample of honey bees** ($\\checkmark$), therefore, it doesn’t mean that neonicotinoids should not be used."},{"ref":"BI0877","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0877\n\nWhich two features are more abundant per unit length in a non-myelinated neuron than in a myelinated neuron?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sodium-potassium pumps and synaptic vesicles\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Potassium ion channels and sodium-potassium pumps\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Rough endoplasmic reticulum and Golgi apparatus\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Nodes of Ranvier and Schwann cells","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because cell membranes of myelinated neurons largely lack ion channels and pumps in regions of the axon that are myelinated. **Choice A** is incorrect because synaptic vesicles are not present in axons. **Choice C** is incorrect because the rough endoplasmic reticulum and Golgi apparatus are present in the cell body, not the axon.  **Choice D** is incorrect because Nodes of Ranvier and Schwann cells are present on myelinated neurons, not non-myelinated neurons."},{"ref":"BI0878","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0878\n\nWhich of the following statements correctly describe(s) the factors affecting the speed of nerve impulse conduction?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | As the diameter of axons increases, the speed at which impulses are conducted increases. |\n    | II. | There is a negative correlation between animal size and the speed at which impulses are conducted. |\n    | III. | Impulse conduction in myelinated neurons is slower than that in non-myelinated neurons.|\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because larger-diameter axons offer less resistance to ion flow, allowing impulses to travel faster.\n\n**Statement II** is false because impulse speed is more influenced by axon diameter and myelination than by body size. In fact, large animals often have adaptations like highly myelinated neurons to ensure efficient conduction over longer distances.\n\n**Statement III** is false because myelination greatly increases conduction speed via saltatory conduction, where impulses \"jump\" between nodes of Ranvier. This is significantly faster than continuous conduction in non-myelinated neurons.\n**Choice D** is correct because only **Statement I** is correct.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **C** are all incorrect because they include one or more false statements."},{"ref":"BI1070","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1070\n\nThe image shows neurons that are found in dog brains. What does letter X indicate?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Neural%20Signalling/images/BI1070.png\" alt=\"BI1070\">\n[Source: Adapted from Lennart, R. (2021, September 14). *File: neurons (Purkinje cells)*.JPG - Wikimedia Commons. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved August 07, 2023, from URL  https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Neurons_(Purkinje_cells).jpg. Copyright free]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cell body\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Axon\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Dendrites\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Nerve fibres","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nLetter X indicates the cell body, which contains the cytoplasm and the nucleus. **Choice A**  is correct.\n\nAn axon is a long, thin projection of a neuron that carries action potentials away from the cell body, **Choice B** is incorrect. Dendrites are multiple specialised extensions branching from a neuron’s cell body or dendron, responsible for receiving incoming signals from other neurons or sensory receptors, **Choice C**  is incorrect. Nerve fibres are a general term for any long, slender projection from a cell body that is capable of transmitting electrical signals - it can be an axon or dendron, **Choice D**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1071","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1071\n\nWhich are correct reasons for a negative resting potential in a neuron?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | The action of sodium-potassium pumps |\n    | II. | The permeability of the membrane to potassium ions |\n    | III. | Opening of sodium channels in the membrane |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA neuron that is not transmitting a signal will have a negative resting potential due to an imbalance of charges across the membrane. The resting potential is maintained by the action of sodium-potassium pumps, which pump  3 Na:sup[+] ions out for every 2 K:sup[+] ions (**Statement I**) that they pump in and the membrane being 50$\\times$ more permeable to K:sup[+] ions than  Na:sup[+] ions (**Statement II**), causing  K:sup[+] ions to leak back out across the membrane, increasing the concentration gradient, **Choice B**  is correct.\n\n**Statement III**  is incorrect, as the opening of sodium channels begins the depolarisation process and raises the membrane potential to a positive value, **Choices C** and **D**  are incorrect. **Choice A**  is incorrect as it only includes **Statement I**."},{"ref":"BI1072","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1072\n\nWhich statement explains why a nerve impulse is electrical?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ It releases hormones from glands into the bloodstream\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Neurotransmitters carry electrical charge between neurons\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Electrons jump from node to node as electrical signals are transferred across neurons\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ It involves the movement of positively charged ions","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA nerve impulse is described as electrical because it involves the movement of positively charged ions, which causes a charge imbalance, **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect as it describes how chemicals are released via the endocrine system. **Choice B**  is incorrect as neurotransmitters do not carry electrical charges themselves, they just trigger an action potential when they bind to receptors on the postsynaptic membrane. **Choice C**  is incorrect, as nerve impulses are primarily carried by the movement of positive ions across the cell membrane, not electrons."},{"ref":"BI1073","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1073\n\nThe diagram shows two neurons of equal length and diameter. Which neuron will transfer an electrical impulse faster?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Neural%20Signalling/images/BI1073.png\" alt=\"BI1073\">\n[Source: Adapted from: Gorton, N. (2012, March 28). *File:Myelinated unmylinated neurons.PNG.* Wikimedia commons. Retrieved August 07 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Myelinated_unmylinated_neurons.PNG. CC BY-SA 3.0]\n\n| | Neuron | Reason |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | X | because it has more dendrites |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Y | because it is unmyelinated |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | X | because it is myelinated |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Y | because it has more axon terminals |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nElectrical impulses can jump between nodes of Ranvier (the gaps in the myelin sheath) in a process called saltatory conduction; this process is faster than continuous transmission along an unmyelinated fibre, **Choice C**  is correct.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect as neurons X and Y have the same number of dendrites, and neuron Y is unmyelinated. **Choice B**  is incorrect, as unmyelinated neurons have a slower rate of electrical transmission. **Choice D**  is incorrect as it is myelination, not the number of axon terminals, that speeds up electrical transmission."},{"ref":"BI1074","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1074\n\nWhich statement is **not**  a reason that a signal normally passes along a neuron or across a cholinergic synapse in one direction?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Specific neurotransmitter receptors are only present on the postsynaptic membrane\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Straight after an action potential, there is a refractory period during which it is difficult or impossible to fire another action potential\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ There is a higher concentration of neurotransmitters released at the postsynaptic membrane\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Vesicles are formed in, and neurotransmitters are released from, the presynaptic knob","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThere is a higher concentration of neurotransmitters released at the presynaptic membrane, rather than the postsynaptic membrane, which then diffuse towards the postsynaptic membrane and bind to receptors, **Choice C** is an untrue statement, and so is the correct answer.\n\nNeurotransmitters that are released from the presynaptic membrane bind to specific receptors on the postsynaptic membrane. These receptors receive the neurotransmitters and initiate a response in the postsynaptic membrane. The signal transmission is unidirectional since the specific receptors are present only on the postsynaptic membrane, **Choice A**  is a true statement and, therefore, not a correct answer to this question. Electrical impulses can only travel in one direction through a neuron due to hyperpolarisation and the refractory period, **Choice B**  is a true statement and, therefore, not a correct answer to this question. Vesicles are only formed in and released from the presynaptic knob meaning that the impulse can only be transmitted in one direction, **Choice D**  is a true statement and, therefore, not a correct answer to this question."},{"ref":"BI1075","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1075\n\nWhat triggers the release of neurotransmitters from the presynaptic membrane?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Depolarisation of the presynaptic membrane\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ A refractory period after depolarisation of the presynaptic membrane\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Repolarisation of the presynaptic membrane\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Activation of receptors on the postsynaptic neuron","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDepolarisation  triggers the influx of calcium ions into the presynaptic knob, which brings about the release of neurotransmitters from the presynaptic membrane, **Choice A**  is correct.\n\n**Choices B** and **C**  are incorrect, as neither depolarisation nor repolarisation can occur during the refractory period. **Choice D**  is incorrect as activation of receptors on the postsynaptic membrane is caused by neurotransmitter binding; it does not trigger their release."},{"ref":"BI1076","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1076\n\nWhich mode of transport do neurotransmitters use to travel across a synapse?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Facilitated diffusion\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Active transport\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Simple diffusion\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Exocytosis","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nNeurotransmitters travel freely without energy from an area of high concentration at the presynaptic membrane to a low concentration at the postsynaptic membrane via simple diffusion, **Choice C**  is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect, as neurotransmitters do not use a channel to diffuse across the synapse. **Choice B**  is incorrect, as neurotransmitters do not travel against the concentration gradient using energy across the synapse. **Choice D**  is incorrect as although neurotransmitters are released from the presynaptic membrane via exocytosis, they do not travel across the synapse using this method."},{"ref":"BI1077","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1077\n\nWhich statement does **not** apply to an axon undergoing depolarisation with a membrane potential of 10mV?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Simultaneously, other parts of the axon will be at resting potential\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The neuron will be more negative on the inside than on the outside\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sodium channels will be open, while potassium channels will remain closed\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The threshold potential would have been surpassed","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDuring depolarisation, the inside of the neuron is more positive than the outside due to the influx of sodium ions via sodium channels. **Choice B**  is the statement that does not apply to this axon and is, therefore, the correct answer to this question.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect as nerve impulses travel through a neuron via local currents, so the depolarisation of one part of an axon causes the next part of the axon to rise from its resting potential. **Choice C**   is incorrect as the opening of sodium channels causes sodium to diffuse into the neuron, causing the membrane potential to rise, **Choice D**  is incorrect as in order for depolarisation to occur, the threshold potential must have been surpassed."},{"ref":"BI1078","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1078\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe diagram represents the release of a neurotransmitter that triggers at the presynaptic membrane of a neuron.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Neural%20Signalling/images/BI1078.png\" alt=\"BI1078\">\n[Adapted from: Source Rcchang16. (2018, November 21). *Category: neurotransmitters*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved August 6, 2022, from https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fe/Synapse-Neurotransmitter_Release.png  CC-BY-4.0]\n\n1. Describe the events in the presynaptic neuron that trigger the release of neurotransmitters.        [3 marks]\n\n2.\n    1. State two things that happen to neurotransmitters once they reach the postsynaptic membrane.                [2 marks]\n\n    2. Predict what would happen to the postsynaptic neuron if the neurotransmitter did not do what you described in part (i).        [2 marks]\n\n3. Outline one possible effect on neuronal transmission if a compound similar in shape to the neurotransmitter was added to the synaptic cleft.    [1 mark]\n\n4. Explain why a nervous impulse can only be conducted in one direction across the synaptic cleft.    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nerve impulse reaches the presynaptic membrane **OR** presynaptic knob **OR** bulb, **OR** depolarisation occurs in the presynaptic membrane  **OR** presynaptic knob **OR** bulb [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ causes calcium channels to open **OR** voltage-gated calcium channels open    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ calcium ions enter presynaptic knob **OR** bulb        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ causes vesicles containing the neurotransmitter to move towards the presynaptic membrane [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles fuse with presynaptic membrane        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ neurotransmitter released by exocytosis        [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    When a **nerve impulse** reaches the presynaptic membrane ($\\checkmark$), it causes **calcium ions** to diffuse through **channels** into the neuron ($\\checkmark$). Subsequently, this causes **vesicles** containing the neurotransmitters to **move towards the presynaptic membrane** ($\\checkmark$) and **fuse** with it ($\\checkmark$), **releasing the neurotransmitter** into the synapse via **exocytosis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ binds to receptors                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ broken down **OR** enzymatic degradation        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reabsorbed into vesicles **OR** reuptake        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ diffuse away from the synaptic cleft **OR** gap    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Once a neurotransmitter reaches the postsynaptic membrane, it **binds** to specific **receptors** ($\\checkmark$) and then the signal is terminated by the neurotransmitter being **broken down** ($\\checkmark$) by an enzyme.\n\n2.\n    2. Any **two** of the following (must include **one** result for **each** statement given in **(bi)**):\n\n    *If it didn’t bind to receptors:* \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ action potential **OR** nerve impulse would not be generated in next axon     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ postsynaptic neuron wouldn’t be able to process and integrate signals from multiple sources effectively                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ disruptions in the flow of information within neural circuits, affecting brain functions and behaviours                    [1 mark]\n\n    *If it did’t break down, get reabsorbed back into vesicles, or diffuse away:* \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ postsynaptic neuron will be continuously activated, **OR** an action potential will be continuously fired            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ postsynaptic receptors could become desensitised **OR** downregulated, resulting is less responsiveness to the neurotransmitter over time **OR** reducing the\neffectiveness of future signalling at that synapse        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ accumulation of neurotransmitters in the synaptic cleft  could interfere with subsequent signals **OR** result in overstimulation **OR** crosstalk between neighbouring synapses        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the ability to distinguish between different synaptic inputs could be compromised, leading to a loss of specificity in neuronal communication     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ synapses may not be able to repeatedly transmit signals, as the accumulation of neurotransmitters might prevent the proper resetting of the synapse for subsequent signalling            [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    If the neurotransmitter did not bind to receptors on the postsynaptic membrane, then an **action potential would not be generated** ($\\checkmark$) in the postsynaptic neuron. If the neurotransmitter wasn’t broken down in the synaptic cleft, the postsynaptic neuron would **continuously fire an action potential** ($\\checkmark$) without returning to rest.\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ slow down **OR** stop **BECAUSE** compound would bind to receptors on postsynaptic membrane blocking the receptor **OR** preventing the neurotransmitter from binding **OR** it could act as a competitive inhibitor **OR** could bind to a different site on the receptor, causing allosteric changes that inhibit the receptor’s ability to bind        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ agonistic effect **OR** compound could activate the receptors and produce a response similar to the actual neurotransmitter            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the compound might bind to the receptors with low efficacy **OR** affinity compared to actual neurotransmitter, triggering a partial response, which might result in a weaker **OR** modified transmission                        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ compound might not be able to bind effectively **OR** activate the receptors, and so may have no significant impact                    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    If a compound similar in shape was added to the synaptic cleft, then neuronal transmission would **slow down** because some of the **receptors** on the postsynaptic membrane would be **blocked** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles of neurotransmitter only found in presynaptic bulb **OR** knob **OR** neurotransmitter only released from presynaptic membrane        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ receptors for neurotransmitter are only found on postsynaptic membrane    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ voltage-gated calcium channels are concentrated in the presynaptic knob **OR** bulb and trigger release of neurotransmitter        [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Due to the **refractory** period, nerve impulses only travel in one direction, and they stimulate the release of neurotransmitters ($\\checkmark$).  Additionally, the **receptors** for neurotransmitters are only found on the **postsynaptic membrane** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1079","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1079\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Explain why a resting potential is negative.    [3 marks]\n\n2. Explain how the structure of a neuron is adapted to its function. [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe the events that occur to propagate an action potential within a neuron and between two neurons.        [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sodium-potassium pumps move 3 Na$^+$ ions out of the cell and 2 K$^+$ ions into the cell     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ membrane is more permeable to  K$^+$ ions, so  K$^+$ ions leak back out of the cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there are negative ions **OR** anions, **OR** substances carrying negative charge,  inside the cell than outside    [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    A resting potential is negative because **sodium-potassium pumps** in the membrane pump **3 Na$^+$** ions **out** of the cell for every **2 K$^+$** ions that are pumped **in** ($\\checkmark$), making the outside of the cell more positive and the inside less positive. Additionally, the membrane is **more permeable to  K$^+$ ions**, so  K$^+$ ions **leak back out** of the cell ($\\checkmark$), adding further to the charge imbalance. Finally, there is higher concentration of **negative charge**, such as Cl$^-$ ions, **inside the cell** ($\\checkmark$), which make the inside of the cell more negative.\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ long axon fibre to carry electrical impulses over relatively long distances in the body    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ many branched dendrites to receive **OR** send electrical impulses from many neurons    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myelination **OR** Schwann cells provide insulation so that electrical impulses are conducted faster    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ saltatory conduction **OR** nodes of Ranvier allow impulses to jump from node to node so that the impulse is transported faster        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contain many mitochondria which provide energy to construct neurotransmitters    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell body processes and integrates incoming signals from dendrites before determining whether to generate an action potential     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at the end of the axon, terminal branches form synapses with other neurons **OR** target cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the presence of synaptic vesicles within axon terminals enables the storage and release of neurotransmitters, facilitating efficient communication        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ neurons possess a variety of ion channels, including voltage-gated channels, which regulate the movement of ions across the cell membrane    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ postsynaptic membrane contains receptors that specifically bind to neurotransmitters released by other neurons        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ neurons can undergo structural and functional changes **OR** neuronal plasticity in response to experience or learning        [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The structure of a neuron is adapted to its function by having **long** axon fibres to carry electrical impulses over relatively **long distances in the body** ($\\checkmark$). Neurons have many-branched **dendrites** to receive multiple signals from **other neurons** ($\\checkmark$). In the axons **myelination** provides **insulation** so that electrical impulses are conducted **faster** ($\\checkmark$).  Inbetween the myelin sheaths there are myelin-free gaps called **nodes of Ranvier** which allow action potential to move **faster** ($\\checkmark$) by. **jumping from node to node**.\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stimulus, such as change in membrane voltage or neurotransmitter binding to a receptor, triggers the opening of sodium channels in the axon’s initial segment    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ leads to depolarising of membrane    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if change in potential reaches threshold potential **OR** -55 to -50mV, it triggers the opening of voltage-gated sodium channels    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rapid influx of sodium ions, causing action potential        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ positive feedback loop **OR** greater change in potential results in more sodium voltage-gated channels opening        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ after reaching peak positive potential **OR** +40 mV, voltage-gated sodium channels start to close, and voltage-gated potassium channels open    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ potassium ions move out of the cell, repolarising the membrane and bringing it back towards the resting potential    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hyperpolarisation **OR** refractory period prevents further action potential in that region until sodium-potassium pump restores the resting membrane potential and ionic balance    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ action potential in one region means sodium ions diffuse along inside of axon to neighbouring regions, which changes membrane potential in this region    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ causing this region to surpass the threshold potential        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ action potential cannot go backwards, despite diffusion of sodium ions as sodium voltage-gated channels can’t open during absolute refractory period        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ action potential reaching the presynaptic knob, leading to the opening of voltage-gated calcium channels        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ influx of  Ca:sup[2+] ions causes vesicles containing neurotransmitter to fuse with the presynaptic membrane        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ neurotransmitter is released into the synapse by exocytosis    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ neurotransmitter diffuses across the synapse **AND** binds to receptors on the postsynaptic membrane    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ triggers the opening of sodium channels in the postsynaptic membrane    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if postsynaptic potential reaches threshold potential (-55 mV to -50 mV), an action potential is generated in the next neuron [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Local currents control the propagation of an action potential within a neuron. This occurs when **sodium channels open**, letting Na:sup[+] ions into the cell ($\\checkmark$), which raises the membrane potential so that it meets the **threshold potential** ($\\checkmark$), this triggers an **action potential** ($\\checkmark$). However, due to the presence of a higher concentration of Na:sup[+] ions within the axon, **Na:sup[+] ions diffuse inside** the axon to the next region of the axon, which is at resting potential ($\\checkmark$), this causes the next region to depolarise and **reach the threshold potential** ($\\checkmark$), so that the voltage-gated **sodium channels open** ($\\checkmark$), and the process repeats. Once the wave of **depolarisation** reaches the presynaptic membrane, it **causes the voltage-gated calcium channels** in the presynaptic knob to **open** ($\\checkmark$). **Calcium ions** therefore **diffuse into the cell**, which triggers **vesicles containing neurotransmitters** to **fuse with** the **presynaptic membrane** ($\\checkmark$).  The neurotransmitters **diffuse** across the **synapse and bind to receptors** on the postsynaptic membrane ($\\checkmark$), which triggers sodium channels to open, and the process begins again."},{"ref":"BI1160","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1160\n\nThe graph below shows membrane voltage over time during an action potential. Which letter represents the segment of the action potential when voltage-gated sodium channels are closed and voltage-gated potassium channels are open?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Neural%20Signalling/images/BI1160.png\" alt=\"BI1160\">\n[Source: Adapted from Losh, M., & Llamocca, D. (2019) *A Low-Power Spike-Like Neural Network Design*. Electronics 8, 1479. Retrieved March 16, 2022, from https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics8121479. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDuring the repolarisation phase of an action potential, voltage-gated potassium channels are open and the voltage-gated sodium channels are closed. This allows K:sup[+] ions to leave the cell, returning the cell to a negative state.\n\n**Choice B** on the graph indicates the depolarisation phase, so corresponds to the time during the action potential in which voltage-gated potassium channels are closed and voltage-gated sodium channels are open. Between the resting potential and threshold potential (**Choice A**), both voltage-gated sodium and potassium channels are closed. During the hyperpolarisation phase (**Choice D**), voltage-gated sodium channels are closed and voltage-gated potassium channels are either closed or in the process of closing."},{"ref":"BI1189","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1189\n\nThe diagram shows a neuron. What are structures X and Y?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Neural%20Signalling/images/BI1189.png\" alt=\"BI1189\">\n[Source: Adapted from Dhp1080. (2008, June 29). *File: derived neuron schema with no labels.svg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved August 6, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Derived_Neuron_schema_with_no_labels.svg  CC-BY-3.0]\n\n| | X | Y |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Axon terminal | Node of Ranvier |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Dendrite | Schwann cell |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Axon | Nerve fibre |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Cell body | Nucleus |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nX is a dendrite and Y is a Schwann cell. **Choice B**  is correct.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D**  are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1196","marks":"9","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1196\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nAlpha-methyltyrosine inhibits the synthesis of dopamine, which is a neurotransmitter.  Alpha-methyltyrosine is also structurally similar to tyrosine, a substrate involved in dopamine synthesis.\n\n1. Based on the information above, deduce, with a reason, what type of inhibitor alpha-methyltyrosine is.         [1 mark]\n\n2. Describe the events in the presynaptic neuron that trigger the release of neurotransmitters.            [3 marks]\n\n3. Dopamine is an excitatory neurotransmitter, which means that it increases the chances of an action potential occurring. Describe what happens to the neurotransmitter after it has been released from the presynaptic knob.      [3 marks]\n\n4. If alpha-methyltyrosine concentration increases significantly, the resulting decreasing synthesis of dopamine can cause negative health effects. Suggest how dopamine synthesis could be returned to normal levels even if alpha-methyltyrosine concentrations remain elevated.  [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ competitive inhibitor **BECAUSE** similar shape so can bind to same receptor        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Alpha-methyltyrosine is a **competitive inhibitor** and binds to the enzyme at the tyrosine (substrate) binding site ($\\checkmark$) as it is a similar shape.\n\n2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nerve impulse reaches the presynaptic membrane **OR** presynaptic knob **OR** bulb, OR depolarisation occurs in the presynaptic membrane  **OR** presynaptic knob **OR** bulb    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ causes calcium channels to open **OR** voltage-gated calcium ions open    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ calcium ions enter presynaptic knob **OR** bulb        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ causes vesicles containing the neurotransmitter to move towards the presynaptic membrane **AND** fuse with it **OR** vesicles move towards the presynaptic membrane, and  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ neurotransmitter is released by exocytosis        [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    When a **nerve impulse** reaches the presynaptic membrane ($\\checkmark$), it causes **calcium ions** to diffuse through **channels** into the neuron ($\\checkmark$). Subsequently, this causes **vesicles** containing the neurotransmitters to **move towards the presynaptic membrane** and **fuse** with it ($\\checkmark$), releasing neurotransmitters into the synapse via **exocytosis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ dopamine diffuses across synaptic cleft    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ dopamine binds to receptors on the post-synaptic membrane    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sodium channels open, and sodium ions    enter post-synaptic neuron    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ entry of sodium ions causes an action potential [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enzymatic inactivation of the neurotransmitter **OR** neurotransmitter is broken down by enzymes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reformation of neurotransmitter in the presynaptic neuron    [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    When dopamine is secreted, it **diffuses across the synaptic cleft** ($\\checkmark$) and binds to receptors on the **post-synaptic membrane** ($\\checkmark$). This opens sodium ion channels, allowing the **entry** of **sodium ions** ($\\checkmark$), which **trigger** an **action potential** ($\\checkmark$). Further, the **neurotransmitter** is **broken down** by enzymes ($\\checkmark$), diffuses back to the presynaptic neuron, and is **reformed** as a neurotransmitter ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as alpha-methyltyrosine is a competitive inhibitor it binds reversibly at the substrate binding site            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if the concentration of substrate is increased, rate of dopamine production will increase **OR** increasing concentrations of tyrosine will reverse the effects of alpha-methyltyrosine **OR** if enough of the substrate is provided, eventually it may outcompete inhibitor **OR** may restore the original production rate (of dopamine) [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ administration of substrate **OR** tyrosine **OR** dopamine therapeutically could relieve symptoms [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Alpha-methyltyrosine is a **competitive inhibitor**, and so **binds reversibly** at the **substrate binding site** ($\\checkmark$) in place of substrate tyrosine, thereby preventing the synthesis of dopamine. If **more of the substrate tyrosine** is added, it will **reverse** the **effects** of the **alpha-methyltyrosine** ($\\checkmark$) since the **chances** of the **substrate binding** at the active site are **more than** the chances of **inhibitor binding** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1409","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1409\n\nWhich row is correct regarding the repolarisation of a neuron during an action potential?\n\n| | Voltage-gated sodium ion channels | Voltage-gated potassium ion channels | Membrane potential |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| open | closed | becomes more negative |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | closed | open | becomes more negative |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | open | closed | becomes more positive |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | closed | open | becomes more positive |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nRepolarisation occurs after depolarisation during an action potential, and it is when the inside of the neuron returns to a negative charge compared to the outside. When the membrane potential reaches the peak of its positive charge, the voltage-gated sodium ion channels shut, and the voltage-gated potassium ion channels open. The potassium ions flood out of the neuron, making the inside more negative on the inside compared to the outside. **Choice B** is the correct answer.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as the voltage-gated sodium ion channels are closed during repolarisation, and the voltage-gated potassium ion channels are open.\\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because during repolarisation, the voltage-gated sodium ion channels are closed and the voltage-gated potassium ion channels are open, and the membrane potential becomes more negative, not more positive.\\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the membrane potential becomes more negative, not positive during repolarisation."},{"ref":"BI1635","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1635\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nOligodendrocytes are cells found in the central nervous systems of mammals that are responsible for myelin sheath formation. Learning and memory are thought to be influenced by the ability of oligodendrocytes to adjust the size and location of myelin in order to synchronise action potentials. The diagram below shows two mechanisms for myelin sheath formation that are associated with learning in the central nervous system of mice (*Mus musculus*).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Neural%20Signalling/images/BI1635.png\" alt=\"BI1635\">\n[Adapted from *“Myelin Sheath Formation and Remodeling in Learning”*, by BioRender.com (2024). Retrieved from https://app.biorender.com/biorender-templates]\n\n1. Outline the role of myelin in the nervous system. [2 marks]\n\n2. Using the diagrams, distinguish between the two mechanisms of myelin sheath formation associated with learning. [2 marks]\n\n The oscilloscope trace below shows several nerve signals measured in a postsynaptic neuron.\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Neural%20Signalling/images/1635qu.png\" alt=\"1635qu\">\n\n3.\n 1. Identify Y on the oscilloscope trace. [1 mark]\n 2. Explain the relationship between X and Y. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fatty substance that insulates **OR** surrounds the axon **OR** nerve fibres [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allows for saltatory conduction **OR** jumping of nerve impulses between nodes **OR** nodes of Ranvier [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increases the speed **OR** velocity of nerve impulses **OR** neural transmission [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ preserves the strength of nerve impulses along the length of a neuron [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Myelin** is a **fatty substance** that **insulates nerve fibres** ($\\checkmark$), allowing for **saltatory conduction** ($\\checkmark$) to **increase** the **speed** of **nerve impulses** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mechanism 1 involves new oligodendrocytes **WHEREAS** mechanism 2 relies on existing oligodendrocytes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ In mechanism 1, new myelin is produced **OR** myelin fills in the gaps along neurons **WHEREAS** in mechanism 2, the size and location of myelin are remodelled **OR** adjusted **OR** changed to synchronise the speed of action potentials [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n In **mechanism 1, new oligodendrocytes** are used, while mechanism 2 uses existing oligodendrocytes ($\\checkmark$). Mechanism 1 also involves the formation of new myelin to fill in the gaps along neurons, whereas in **mechanism 2, myelin** is simply **remodelled** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 1.\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ threshold potential [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Y represents the **threshold potential** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n 2.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ X represents failed initiations **OR** nerve impulses **AND** these do not initiate an action potential **OR** when X reaches Y **OR** threshold potential, nerve impulses will generate an action potential [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **X** represents the **failed initiations** due to the depolarisation **not reaching Y**, so the neuron will **not fire** an **action potential**, and the membrane potential will return to its resting state ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2335","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2335\n\nWhat is the correct order of events for nerve transmission at a synapse?\n\n|  $\\ce{A}.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{Neurotransmitter released}$ $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\rarr$ $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{Neurotransmitter diffuses}$ $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\rarr$ $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\ce{Neurotransmitter binds to}$ $\\\\$ $\\ce{transmembrane receptors}$  |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Neurotransmitter diffuses |$\\rarr$ | Neurotransmitter binds to $\\\\$ transmembrane receptors | $\\rarr$ | Neurotransmitter released |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Neurotransmitter binds to $\\\\$ transmembrane receptors | $\\rarr$ | Neurotransmitter released | $\\rarr$ |Neurotransmitter diffuses |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Neurotransmitter diffuses | $\\rarr$ | Neurotransmitter released | $\\rarr$ | Neurotransmitter binds to $\\\\$ transmembrane receptors |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because when a nerve impulse reaches the synaptic terminal, calcium ions enter the presynaptic knob, triggering the release of neurotransmitters via exocytosis. The released neurotransmitter diffuses across the synaptic cleft and binds to specific receptors on the postsynaptic membrane, leading to the initiation of a response in the postsynaptic neuron.\n\n**Choices B** and **D** are incorrect because neurotransmitter diffusion cannot occur before it is released.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because neurotransmitter binding cannot happen before it is released or diffused."},{"ref":"BI2389","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2389\n\nA teacher drew the following unlabelled diagram of a synapse to illustrate the mode of action of cocaine.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Neural%20Signalling/images/%27teachers%27%20drawing%20of%20synapse%20labelled%20A-D%20i.png\" alt=\"'teachers' drawing of synapse labelled A-D i\">\n\nWhich label (**A - D**) shows the site of action of cocaine in the teacher’s drawing?","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because this is the transporter protein that normally allows the reuptake of the neurotransmitters (principally dopamine, serotonin and norepinephrine) from the synaptic gap. Cocaine’s mode of action is to block this receptor. The resulting high concentration of neurotransmitters in the synaptic gap amplifies the response in the postsynaptic cell, giving short-term feelings of euphoria and high energy. This is only short term, in that a dangerous dependency can ensue quickly with repeated cocaine misuse.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because this shows the presynaptic vesicles that carry the neurotransmitter; these are unaffected by cocaine.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because this shows the neurotransmitter molecules crossing the synaptic gap. Cocaine has no direct molecular effect on neurotransmitters.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because these are the receptors that bind to the neurotransmitter on the postsynaptic membrane in order to relay the impulse."},{"ref":"BI2693","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2693\n\nWhich processes are responsible for sodium channels adjacent to an area of depolarisation reaching threshold potential?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Diffusion of sodium and potassium ions inside the axon\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Diffusion of sodium ions inside and outside the axon\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Diffusion of sodium ions inside the axon and potassium ions outside the axon\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Diffusion of potassium ions inside and outside the axon","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because, when sodium ions ($\\ce{Na^+}$) enter the axon during depolarisation, they increase the concentration of $\\ce{Na^+}$ inside that region. These sodium ions diffuse along the inside of the axon towards the next resting region, reducing the membrane potential there. Meanwhile, outside the axon, sodium ions move back toward the previously depolarised region to replace the sodium ions that moved into the axon. These movements, known as local currents, help propagate the action potential by bringing the adjacent section of the axon to the threshold potential.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because only sodium ions, not potassium ions, are involved in generating local currents.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because the action potential is propagated by the diffusion of sodium ions inside and outside the neuron, not potassium ions.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because local currents of sodium ions propagate the impulse, not the diffusion of potassium ions."},{"ref":"BI2695","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2695\n\nWhich labelled structure facilitates saltatory conduction?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Neural%20Signalling/images/BI2695.png\" alt=\"BI2695\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because the nodes of Ranvier are unmyelinated sections between myelinated sheaths, where action potentials can occur. These structures facilitate saltatory conduction because the nerve impulse moves from one node to the next.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it is the axon, the structure that the nerve impulse travels along. **Choice B** is incorrect because it is the cell body that contains the nucleus which controls the activities of the neuron. **Choice D** is incorrect because this is an axon terminal that releases neurotransmitters to transfer nerve impulses from one neuron to the next."},{"ref":"BI2698","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2698\n\nWhich statements are true for the effect of cocaine on synapses?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Cocaine enhances synaptic activity by blocking dopamine reuptake transporters. |\n    | II. | Cocaine leads to prolonged activation of postsynaptic dopamine receptors. |\n    | III. | Cocaine increases dopamine production in presynaptic neurons. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because cocaine blocks dopamine reuptake transporters (DAT) on the presynaptic neuron, preventing reabsorption of dopamine. This increases dopamine levels in the synaptic cleft, enhancing synaptic transmission.\n\n**Statement II** is true because the excess dopamine in the synaptic cleft leads to prolonged activation of postsynaptic dopamine receptors, resulting in heightened stimulation associated with euphoria and reward.\n\n**Statement III** is false because cocaine does not increase dopamine production; it prevents dopamine from being reabsorbed into the presynaptic neuron. Over time, due to dopamine buildup in the synapse, the presynaptic neuron actually reduces dopamine synthesis to compensate, leading to withdrawal symptoms when cocaine use stops.\n\n**Choice B** is correct because it contains both true statements.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are incorrect because they do not contain the correct combination of true statements (s)."},{"ref":"BI2700","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2700\n\nWhich row correctly describes the effect and consequence of inhibitory neurotransmitter binding to the postsynaptic neuron?\n\n| | Effect | Consequence |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Increases permeability to negative ions | Hypo-polarisation |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Increases permeability to negative ions | Hyperpolarisation |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Decreases permeability to positive ions | Hypo-polarisation |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Increases permeability to positive ions | Hyperpolarisation |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because inhibitory neurotransmitters increase membrane permeability to negative ions, allowing them to enter the neuron. This makes the inside of the neuron more negative than the resting potential, resulting in hyperpolarisation, which reduces the likelihood of an action potential.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the movement of more negative ions into the postsynaptic neuron causes hyperpolarisation, not hypo-polarisation.\n\n**Choices C** and **D** are incorrect because inhibitory neurotransmitters increase permeability to negative ions, not positive ions."},{"ref":"BI2701","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2701\n\nWhich statements correctly describe summation in synaptic transmission?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Summation results from the combined effects of excitatory and inhibitory neurotransmitters on a postsynaptic neuron. |\n    | II. | Summation requires the involvement of multiple postsynaptic neurons.|\n    | III. | Spatial summation involves multiple presynaptic neurons releasing neurotransmitters onto a single postsynaptic neuron. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because summation occurs when multiple excitatory (EPSPs) and inhibitory (IPSPs) neurotransmitters interact at a single postsynaptic neuron. If excitatory inputs outweigh inhibitory inputs, the neuron reaches the threshold and fires an action potential. If inhibitory inputs dominate, the neuron is less likely to fire.\n\n**Statement II** is false because summation occurs within a single postsynaptic neuron, integrating signals from multiple presynaptic neurons or repeated signals from one. It does not involve multiple postsynaptic neurons interacting with each other.\n\n**Statement III** is true because spatial summation occurs when multiple presynaptic neurons release neurotransmitters onto a single postsynaptic neuron, collectively bringing it to the threshold.\n\n**Choice C** is correct because it contains both true statements.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **D** are incorrect as they do not contain the correct combination of true statements."},{"ref":"BI2702","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2702\n\nWhich row contains suitable conditions for summation?\n\n| | Excitatory vs. inhibitory neurotransmitters | Threshold reached | Depolarisation |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Unequal | Yes | Yes |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Unequal | No | Yes |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Equal | Yes | No |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Equal | Yes | Yes","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because summation depends on the net effect of excitatory and inhibitory neurotransmitters. If there are more excitatory inputs than inhibitory ones, and this net excitation is strong enough to reach threshold, it will trigger depolarisation of the postsynaptic neuron.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because although inputs are unequal, the threshold is not reached, so depolarisation cannot occur.\n\n**Choices C** and **D** are incorrect because if the concentrations of excitatory and inhibitory neurotransmitters are equal, the threshold potential will not be reached."},{"ref":"BI2704","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2704\n\nHow do stimuli such as skin damage, heat, and certain chemicals affect pain-sensitive neurons with free nerve endings in the skin?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Trigger an influx of positively charged ions into the neuron\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Trigger an efflux of positively charged ions out of the neuron\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Trigger an influx of negatively charged ions into the neuron\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Trigger an efflux of negatively charged ions out of the neuron","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because stimuli such as skin damage, heat, or certain chemicals activate pain-sensitive neurons by opening ion channels in free nerve endings. This allows an influx of positively charged ions into the neuron. The entry of these ions makes the inside of the neuron less negative, bringing it to threshold potential and triggering an action potential that carries the pain signal to the brain.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because stimuli at the skin cause positive ions to move into the neuron, not out of the neuron.\n\n**Choices C** and **D** are incorrect because stimuli at the skin increase the inward movement of positive ions to bring the neuron to threshold potential; they do not affect the movement of negative ions."}]},{"name":"Photosynthesis","questions":[{"ref":"BI0063","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0063\n\nThis graph shows the rate of photosynthesis at different temperatures.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/BI0063.png\" alt=\"BI0063\">\n\nWhat conclusion can be made?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ There is more carbon dioxide produced at **x** than **w**.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The rate of photosynthesis increases with temperature.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The maximum volume of oxygen is produced at **x**.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The concentration of carbon dioxide is a limiting factor at **y**.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nPhotosynthesis is a process where organic compounds are produced in cells using light energy, carbon dioxide and water. A product of photosynthesis is oxygen gas. Hence, the rate of photosynthesis can be measured by the rate of carbon dioxide gas uptake or by the rate of oxygen gas production (**Choice A**).\n\nAt x, this is known as the optimum temperature. The rate of photosynthesis is the highest here. In other words, x is the temperature where the maximum rate of carbon dioxide absorption and oxygen production occurs.\n\nAt y, light intensity and carbon dioxide concentration are not the limiting factors because, beyond the optimum temperature, the graph does not plateau but instead, it slopes downwards (**Choice D**). At y, the rate of photosynthesis decreases because the temperature is affecting the enzymes involved in photosynthesis (**Choice B**). Hence, the rate of photosynthesis does not just increase with temperature, it also decreases above the optimum temperature."},{"ref":"BI0064","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0064\n\nWhich visible spectrum represents the **correct** range of wavelengths of light that are absorbed and reflected by chlorophyll for photosynthesis?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |   |   |B.|\n|<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/BI0064A.png\" alt=\"BI0064A\"> |   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/BI0064B.png\" alt=\"BI0064B\">|\n|C. |   |   |  |D. |\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/BI0064c.png\" alt=\"BI0064c\"> |   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/BI0064d.png\" alt=\"BI0064d\">|\n\n[Source: Adapted from Beckett, M (2014). *Colours of the visible light spectrum* [Diagram]  Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved April 9, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Colours_of_the_visible_light_spectrum.png. Copyright-free]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe wavelength of the visible spectrum falls between 400 and 700 nanometres (nm), with 400 being the shortest and 700 being the longest. Chlorophyll is a green pigment that is used in photosynthesis. Chlorophyll is what makes the leaf of a plant appear green because chlorophyll reflects green light. It absorbs red and blue light for photosynthesis.\nThe wavelength of red light falls between 600 and 700 nm, green light falls between 500 and 600 nm, and blue light falls between 400 and 500 nm.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it includes blue and green light in the “absorbed” category.\n**Choice B** is incorrect because it includes only red light in the “absorbed” category.\n**Choice D** is incorrect because it includes green light in the “absorbed” category.\nOnly **Choice C** is correct because it includes red and blue light in the “absorbed” category."},{"ref":"BI0065","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0065\n\nWhich term describes the splitting of water molecules in the presence of light to produce electrons, hydrogen ions and oxygen molecules?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Photolysis\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Chemiosmosis\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The Calvin cycle\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The electron transport chain","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nPhotolysis is the splitting of water molecules in the presence of light to produce electrons, hydrogen ions and oxygen. This is part of the light-dependent reaction of photosynthesis.\n\nChemiosmosis (**Choice B**) happens in both photosynthesis and cellular respiration. In both processes, ATP is formed from ADP and inorganic phosphate. Hence, the products are not electrons, hydrogen ions and oxygen molecules.\n\nThe Calvin cycle (**Choice C**) is part of the light-independent reaction of photosynthesis. It produces carbon compounds and does not require light.\n\nThe electron transport chain (**Choice D**) is a component of both photosynthesis and cellular respiration. In photosynthesis, it takes place after photolysis which is when water molecules are split, producing electrons, hydrogen ions and oxygen. Photolysis needs to take place first before these electrons can move down the electron transport chain."},{"ref":"BI0066","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0066\n\nWhich of the following **correctly** describes photosynthesis or respiration?\n\n| | Reaction | Products | Anabolic or Catabolic | Endothermic or Exothermic |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Photosynthesis | Carbon dioxide, water | Catabolic | Exothermic |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Respiration | Oxygen, glucose | Catabolic | Exothermic |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Photosynthesis | Oxygen, glucose | Anabolic | Endothermic |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Respiration | Carbon dioxide, water | Anabolic | Endothermic |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nPhotosynthesis is the production of carbon compounds such as glucose using light energy. Oxygen is produced as a waste product, not carbon dioxide (**Choice A**) which then diffuses out of the cell. Plants use smaller molecules such as carbon dioxide and water to build larger molecules like glucose. Hence, photosynthesis is an anabolic reaction. An anabolic reaction requires energy to build larger molecules. Photosynthesis takes in energy so is known as an endothermic reaction. **Choice C** is, therefore, the correct answer.\n\nCell respiration (**Choices B and D**) is the production of ATP, carbon dioxide and water using glucose and oxygen. Glucose is broken down to form smaller molecules so it is categorised as a catabolic reaction. A catabolic reaction releases energy when larger molecules are broken down to form smaller molecules. Reactions that release energy are known as exothermic reactions."},{"ref":"BI0068","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0068\n\nThe graph shows the action spectrum for photosynthesis.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/BI0068.png\" alt=\"BI0068\">\n\nWhat can be directly inferred from the graph?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The highest rate of photosynthesis is in the presence of red light.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The highest rate of photosynthesis is in the presence of blue light.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The peaks mean that there are two types of chlorophyll pigments.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Different amounts of light are absorbed by chlorophyll at different wavelengths.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe graph shows a clear peak between ~430–480 nm, which corresponds to blue light. This is the highest point on the entire curve, and therefore, the highest photosynthesis rate occurs under blue wavelengths. **Choice B** is correct.\n\nThere is a peak in the red region (~650–680 nm), it is lower than the peak in blue light. Therefore red light does not give the highest photosynthesis rate in this graph, so **Choice A** is incorrect.\n\nThe action spectrum only shows photosynthetic rate, not pigment identity. Peaks indicate wavelengths effective for photosynthesis, but do not prove how many pigments are involved. The presence of multiple pigments (chlorophyll a, chlorophyll b, carotenoids) is a biological explanation, but it cannot be directly inferred from the action spectrum alone. **Choice C** is, therefore, incorrect.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because it describes the absorption spectrum, not the action spectrum. Although absorption and action spectra are related, the graph does not directly measure absorption, so we cannot infer this statement directly."},{"ref":"BI0069","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0069\n\nThe graph shows the effect of light intensity on the rate of photosynthesis.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/BI0069.png\" alt=\"BI0069\">\n\nWhat can be concluded from the results presented in this graph?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Light intensity is a limiting factor if all other conditions are kept the same.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The maximum rate of photosynthesis is reached at 40% light intensity if all other conditions are kept the same.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Rubisco enzymes are saturated with the substrate at 40% light intensity.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Enzymes are denatured at 65% light intensity.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nLight is needed by plants to perform photosynthesis. This can be seen in the graph where the light intensity increases and the rate of photosynthesis also increases. At around 40% is the light intensity where the rate of photosynthesis reaches a maximum. From 40% onwards, the rate of photosynthesis plateaus to show that increasing the light intensity any further will not have an effect on the rate. Hence, light intensity is not a limiting factor here (**Choice A**). Instead, the concentration of carbon dioxide or temperature could be the limiting factors.\n\nIn a carbon dioxide concentration graph, when the rate of photosynthesis plateaus, it means that the rubisco enzyme is saturated with the carbon dioxide substrate. Hence, carbon fixation has reached the maximum level. Therefore, **Choice C** is only applicable for a graph that shows the effect of carbon dioxide concentration on the rate of photosynthesis.\n\nIn a temperature graph, the rate of photosynthesis does not plateau but instead decreases beyond the optimum temperature. This means that the enzymes are denatured due to high temperatures. Therefore, **Choice D** is applicable for a graph that shows the effect of temperature on the rate of photosynthesis."},{"ref":"BI0070","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0070\n\nThe diagram below shows an experiment investigating the effect of temperature on the rate of photosynthesis. Which of the following is **not** a method of keeping a controlled variable at a constant value throughout this particular experiment?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/BI0070.png\" alt=\"BI0070\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Placing the tube containing *Elodea* in a water bath.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Using the same volume of sodium hydrogen carbonate solution for each trial.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Placing the *Elodea* at a constant distance from the same light source for each trial.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Using the same concentration of sodium hydrogen carbonate solution for each trial.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSince the experiment is about testing the temperature; the temperature would be the independent variable, not the controlled variable. A water bath would be used in this experiment to set up different increments of the independent variable.\n\nWhen investigating the effect of temperature on photosynthesis, the other limiting factors (concentration of carbon dioxide and light intensity) must be controlled. The light intensity can be controlled by placing the test tube containing the Elodea at a constant distance from the same light source for each trial (**Choice C**) to make sure that the light intensity is the same for all trials.\n\nUsing the same volume and concentration of sodium hydrogen carbonate solution (**Choice B** and **Choice D**)ensures that the source of carbon dioxide is controlled, and it is not a limiting factor."},{"ref":"BI0071","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0071\n\nWhat does X represent?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/BI0071.png\" alt=\"BI0071\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The ratio of the distance moved by the pigment and solvent\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The distance moved by the solvent\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The distance moved by the pigment\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The baseline","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThin-layer chromatography is a method that can be used to separate pigments extracted from a leaf. Paper chromatography uses paper with a line drawn approximately 1 cm from one end, which is called the baseline (**Choice D**). This is where a drop of leaf extract is placed. Then the paper is immersed in a solvent with the level of solvent being right below the baseline. This solvent will then move up the paper to separate the pigments found in the leaf extract.\n\nThe coloured spots show the distance moved by each pigment (**Choice C**). The solvent will then move further up and will eventually stop moving. The mark where the solvent stops is known as the solvent front and shows the distance moved by the solvent (**Choice B**).\n\nIn order to find the Rf value for a given pigment, the distance moved by the pigment is divided by the distance moved by the solvent (**Choice A**)."},{"ref":"BI0073","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0073\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n*Coleus blumei* is an attractive houseplant with red leaves.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/BI0073.png\" alt=\"BI0073\">\n[Source: Juni (2004, October 13). *Coleus blumei*. [Image]. Flickr. Retrieved April 11, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Coleus_blumei.jpg. Copyright under CC BY]\n\n1. Determine with a reason whether **or** not this plant is still able to conduct photosynthesis with red leaves. [1 mark]\n\n2. Red leaves absorb and reflect different coloured light than green leaves. Distinguish between red light and green light in the visible spectrum. [1 mark]\n\n3. Outline how limiting factors affect the rate of photosynthesis. [3 marks]\n\n4. Explain how oxygen produced during photosynthesis can be used during respiration. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can conduct photosynthesis **BECAUSE** plants have multiple photosynthetic pigments **OR** plants do not only have chlorophyll **OR** green pigment [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can conduct photosynthesis **BECAUSE** there is anthocyanin [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can conduct photosynthesis **BECAUSE** chlorophyll is present in the leaves [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    The plant can still photosynthesize because chlorophyll is not the only pigment capable of trapping photons. When chlorophyll is not available, **other pigments** ($\\checkmark$) take over that role.\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ red light has a longer wavelength **THAN** green light [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ red light has less energy **THAN** green light [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Wavelength of red light is typically around 650nm, while green light is around **550 nm** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  limiting factors include carbon dioxide concentration, temperature **AND** light intensity [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increases in carbon dioxide concentration **OR** light intensity increase the rate of photosynthesis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light energy is needed for photolysis of water **OR** to excite electrons in chlorophyll **OR** to excite electrons in the light dependent stage **OR** to excite electrons to enter the electron transport chain [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide is needed to form glucose **OR** carbohydrates **OR** organic molecules [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  increases in temperature up to the optimum temperature increase the frequency of successful collisions between substrates and active sites increasing rate of photosynthesis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ high temperatures **OR** temperatures beyond the optimum temperature, alter the tertiary structure **OR** start to denature enzymes **OR** denature enzymes **OR** decrease the enzymes activity OR decrease rate of photosynthesis  [1 mark]\n[3 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Limiting factors affect the rate of photosynthesis and are the **concentration of carbon dioxide**, **temperature and light intensity** ($\\checkmark$). **Carbon dioxide** is needed as a substrate for photosynthesis to form **glucose** ($\\checkmark$), so an increase in carbon dioxide concentration would increase the rate of photosynthesis. There is an **optimum temperature** for photosynthesis when there are **maximum successful collisions** between substrates and enzymes ($\\checkmark$). **Temperatures that are too high denature** ($\\checkmark$) the enzymes, reducing the rate of photosynthesis. Light intensity is important for the **excitation of electrons in chlorophyll a** ($\\checkmark$). An **increase in light intensity increases the rate** of photosynthesis ($\\checkmark$) until another limiting factor comes into play.\n\n4. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ photolysis of water produces oxygen during photosynthesis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen is used in aerobic cellular respiration, **OR** in mitochondria [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen is the terminal electron acceptor (in aerobic respiration) [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen is transformed into water (as a result of aerobic cellular respiration) [1 mark]\n[3 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    In photosynthesis, carbon dioxide and water are used, and glucose and oxygen are produced. **Photolysis of water produces oxygen**  during photosynthesis ($\\checkmark$), and this oxygen is then used in **aerobic cellular respiration** ($\\checkmark$) to produce water in **mitochondria** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0164","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0164\n\nThe micrograph below shows part of a chloroplast in a leaf.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/bi0164.png\" alt=\"bi0164\">\n\n[Source: *File: chloroplast in leaf of Anemone sp tem 85000x.png ... (n.d.)*. Retrieved April 23, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Chloroplast_in_leaf_of_Anemone_sp_TEM_85000x.png. Copyright under CC BY]\n\nWhich of the following structures are found in the part of a chloroplast labelled X?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. |  Photosystem I  |\n  | II. | ATP Synthase |\n  | III. | Ribosomes |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe part of the chloroplast labelled X is the thylakoid membrane. The membrane contains photosystem I (**Statement I**), photosystem II, ATP synthase (**Statement II**) and electron carriers which are important structures in the light-dependent reactions.\n\nRibosomes (**Statement III**) are found in the stroma of chloroplasts."},{"ref":"BI0165","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0165\n\nWhat is used to generate a proton gradient across the thylakoid membrane in the light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Triose phosphate\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Electrons from Photosystem II\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ ATP synthase\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Reduced NADP","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nExcited electrons from Photosystem II release energy as they pass along the electron transport chain. This energy is used to pump protons (H⁺ ions) from the stroma into the thylakoid lumen, generating a proton gradient across the thylakoid membrane. **Choice B** is the correct answer.\n\nATP synthase does not generate the proton gradient. Instead, it uses the proton gradient to synthesise ATP from ADP and inorganic phosphate via chemiosmosis. **Choice C** is incorrect.\n\nExcited electrons from Photosystem I are used to reduce NADP⁺, which is not involved in generating the proton gradient. Instead, NADPH provides hydrogen atoms to reduce glycerate 3-phosphate (GP) to triose phosphate (TP) in the Calvin cycle. **Choice D** is incorrect.\n\nTriose phosphate is produced in the Calvin cycle and is used to regenerate ribulose bisphosphate (RuBP) and to synthesise carbohydrates. It plays no role in the light-dependent reactions or in establishing the proton gradient. **Choice A** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0166","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0166\n\nWhich type of reaction is occurring in this light-independent reaction of photosynthesis?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/BI0166.png\" alt=\"BI0166\">\n[Source: Adapted from Gathman, A. (2010, February 25). *Carbon fixation*. Flickr. Retrieved April 23, 2022, from https://www.flickr.com/photos/agathman/4388295140. Copyright under CC BY]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Reduction of glycerate 3-phosphate\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Regeneration of ribulose bisphosphate\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Photolysis of water\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Carboxylation of ribulose bisphosphate","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCarbon fixation is a light-independent reaction where carbon dioxide reacts with ribulose bisphosphate to form two molecules of glycerate 3-phosphate. This is known as the carboxylation of ribulose bisphosphate.\n\nIn this diagram, the product on the right-hand side of the equation is glycerate 3-phosphate, hence, it is not the reduction of glycerate 3-phosphate (**Choice A**). Reduction of glycerate 3-phosphate would require glycerate 3-phosphate to be a reactant on the left-hand side of the equation which would then get reduced to form triose phosphate.\n\nSimilarly, in this diagram, the reactant on the left-hand side of the equation is ribulose bisphosphate, hence, it is not the regeneration of ribulose bisphosphate (**Choice B**). If this diagram were showing the regeneration of ribulose bisphosphate, triose phosphate would be on the left and ribulose bisphosphate, the product, would be on the right.\n\nPhotolysis of water (**Choice C**) involves light and happens in the first part of photosynthesis. However, in this diagram, it has been indicated that it is a light-independent reaction."},{"ref":"BI0167","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0167\n\nWhich of the following **correctly** shows where chemiosmosis takes place in photosynthesis and aerobic cell respiration?\n\n| | Photosynthesis | Aerobic cell respiration |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Thylakoid membrane | Inner mitochondrion membrane |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Stroma | Mitochondrial matrix |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Starch grain | Cytoplasm  |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Chloroplast envelope | Cell membrane |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nChemiosmosis is a process that is common to both photosynthesis and aerobic cell respiration. In photosynthesis, chemiosmosis takes place along thylakoid membranes. In aerobic cell respiration, chemiosmosis takes place along the inner mitochondrial membrane.\n\nChemiosmosis does not take place inside the stroma but instead, the Calvin cycle occurs here. Chemiosmosis does not take place inside the mitochondrial matrix but instead, the Krebs cycle and the link reaction of aerobic cell respiration take place here (**Choice B**).\n\nChemiosmosis also does not take place on or in starch grains.  Glucose, produced by photosynthesis, is stored as starch. Similarly, chemiosmosis does not take place in the cytoplasm during aerobic cell respiration; glycolysis takes place here (**Choice C**).\n\nThe chloroplast envelope is the boundary of the chloroplast and the cell membrane similarly is the boundary of the cell.  Chemiosmosis does not take place in either of these structures (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0168","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0168\n\nThe diagram below shows Calvin’s lollipop apparatus used to investigate the light-independent reactions of photosynthesis. The experiment used radioactive ¹⁴CO₂ to trace carbon in photosynthesis. Algae were killed at timed intervals with hot alcohol, and compounds were separated by chromatography and detected by X-ray film.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/calvins%20lollipop%20with%20correct%20carbon%2014%20label%20i.png\" alt=\"calvins lollipop with correct carbon 14 label i\">\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/Calvin%27s%20lollipop%20results%20i.png\" alt=\"Calvin's lollipop results i\">\n\nDeduce which of the following were results that enabled the determination of the sequence of reactions in the Calvin cycle.\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Algae *Chlorella* were killed by being added to hot alcohol.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ A high percentage of radioactivity was found in glycerate-3-phosphate in the first sample.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ A large amount of triose phosphate and other sugar phosphates were found in the first sample.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ :sup[12]CO$_2$ was replaced by :sup[14]CO$_2$, a radioactive isotope of carbon which was supplied to the algae *Chlorella*.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nBecause glycerate-3-phosphate is the first stable product of carbon fixation in the Calvin cycle, it makes sense that a high percentage of radioactivity was found in glycerate-3-phosphate in the first sample. **Choice B** is the correct answer.\n\nHot alcohol was used to kill the algae and stop metabolism instantly, allowing researchers to analyse the intermediates at specific time points. This step was part of the method, not the results. **Choice A** is incorrect.\n\nTriose phosphate is not the first product of the Calvin cycle, it is produced in later steps. Therefore, radioactive triose phosphate and other sugar phosphates would not be found in the first sample, they would be found in later samples. **Choice C** is incorrect.\n\n¹²CO₂ was replaced by ¹⁴CO₂, a radioactive isotope of carbon which was supplied to the algae. This allowed the tracking of carbon atoms through the cycle. However, this was part of the method, not a result. **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0169","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0169\n\nThe following processes occur in the Calvin cycle.\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. |  Ribulose bisphosphate regeneration  |\n  | II. | Carbon dioxide fixation |\n  | III. | Glycerate-3-phosphate is reduced to triose phosphate |\n\nWhich is the **incorrect** sequence for these processes?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I. → II. → III.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I. → III. → II.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II. → III. → I.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ III. → I. → II.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn the Calvin cycle, there are three important stages. Firstly, the fixation of carbon dioxide (**Statement II**) where ribulose bisphosphate is carboxylated by a Rubisco enzyme.\n\nCarbon fixation is followed by the production of triose phosphate through the reduction of glycerate-3-phosphate  (**Statement III**) using reduced NADP and ATP.\n\nFinally, the regeneration of the acceptor compound, ribulose bisphosphate (**Statement I**) which uses triose phosphate and ATP.  The starting point in the Calvin cycle is arbitrary because it is a cyclic process. Thus all answer choices except **Choice B** show the correct order of events in the Calvin cycle."},{"ref":"BI0170","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0170\n\nThe Hill reaction shows electron transfer in isolated chloroplasts for the light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis. A blue dye, DCPIP (2,6-dichlorophenolindophenol) is used instead of the natural final electron acceptor. What is the final electron acceptor in photosynthesis?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ CO$_2$\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Oxygen\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ ATP\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ NADP","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn the light-dependent reaction, NADP is the final electron acceptor in Photosystem I and this is reduced to form NADPH.\n\nCO$_2$  is fixed by the enzyme Rubisco and is not an electron acceptor  (**Choice A**).\n\nATP is synthesized by ATP synthase using the proton gradient in the thylakoids and is not an electron acceptor (**Choice C**).\n\nOxygen (**Choice B**) is the terminal electron acceptor in aerobic cell respiration, but not in photosynthesis."},{"ref":"BI0171","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0171\n\nWhat is the main source of hydrogen ions in the light-dependent reactions for photosynthesis?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ H$_2$O\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ ATP\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ C$_6$H:sub[12]O$_6$\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ NADPH","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn the light-dependent reactions for photosynthesis, hydrogen ions, also known as the protons, are important in creating a proton gradient.\n\nThe main source of the hydrogen ions in the light-dependent reactions is water, H$_2$O. Photolysis of water results in the release of electrons, hydrogen ions and oxygen.\n\nATP (**Choice B**) is not a source of hydrogen ions.  ATP is generated through the use of a proton gradient and chemiosmosis.\n\nC$_6$H:sub[12]O$_6$, (**Choice C**) or glucose is a product formed during the light-independent reactions. Glucose is not a source of hydrogen ions.  However, it does require the hydrogen ions from NADPH in order to be synthesized in the Calvin cycle.\n\nNADPH (**Choice D**) is a source of hydrogen ions in the Calvin cycle, where glucose is produced. This takes place in the light-independent reactions, however, and not in the light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis."},{"ref":"BI0172","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0172\n\nWhat is the process that takes place before excited electrons are transferred from photosystem II to photosystem I?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Chemiosmosis\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Photophosphorylation\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Photoactivation of photosystem II\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Reduction of NADP","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nPhotoactivation of photosystem II needs to take place first before the transfer of excited electrons from photosystem II to photosystem I. Light energy needs to be absorbed by chlorophyll in photosystem II for the electrons to be excited to high-energy electrons.\n\nChemiosmosis (**Choice A**), photophosphorylation (**Choice B**) and reduction of NADP (**Choice D**) are processes that take place after, or during, the transfer of excited electrons from photosystem II to photosystem I.\n\nThe transfer of excited electrons from photosystem II to photosystem I causes a release of energy which is used to move protons into the thylakoid space. The movement of protons generates a proton gradient. Chemiosmosis then occurs, which leads to the synthesis of ATP, a process known as photophosphorylation. These excited electrons from photosystem I are then used to reduce NADP."},{"ref":"BI0173","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0173\n\nWhich products are formed at the end of the light-dependent and light-independent reactions?\n\n| | Light-dependent | Light-independent |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Water, NADP | ATP, reduced NADP  |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | ATP, reduced NADP, oxygen | carbohydrates |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ |  ATP, reduced NADP | Water, NADP |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ |  Carbohydrates | ATP, reduced NADP, oxygen |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn light-dependent reactions, the products from the reactions are ATP, reduced NADP and oxygen. For the light-independent reactions, the products are carbohydrates. Carbohydrates are not the products of the light-dependent reactions (**Choice D**).\n\nWater and NADP are reactants that are needed to form the products of the light-dependent reactions. The products of the light-dependent reactions, ATP and reduced NADP are the reactants for the light-independent reactions (**Choice A** and **Choice C**)."},{"ref":"BI0174","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0174\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe diagram below shows the ultrastructure of a chloroplast.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/BI0174.png\" alt=\"BI0174\">\n\n1. Identify the enzyme that catalyses the process of carbon fixation and is found at Y. [1 mark]\n\n2. Deduce, with a reason, which structure of the chloroplast has the lowest pH value. [1 mark]\n\n3. State the name of the structure found in chloroplasts that consists of stacks of fluid-filled sacs, which give the chloroplast a larger surface area for light absorption. [1 mark]\n\n4. Describe the factors that would increase the number of starch grains in structure Y. [2 marks]\n\n5. Distinguish between photosystem II and photosystem I. [2 marks]\n\n6. Describe the process of photophosphorylation in photosynthesis. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ rubisco **OR** ribulose bisphosphate carboxylase [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ thylakoid space because there is a high concentration of protons **OR** hydrogen ions in the thylakoid space [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n3.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ granum **OR** grana [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n4. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ high light intensity increases rate of photosynthesis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ high carbon dioxide concentration increases rate of photosynthesis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ optimal temperature for photosynthesis **OR** enzymes **OR** Rubisco [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ rate of photosynthesis is higher than the rate of respiration **OR** low metabolic demand for glucose in the cell **OR** more glucose is produced than is immediately used [1 mark]\n\n[2 marks]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\n\n To increase the glucose concentration so it can be converted to more starch grains, the **rate of photosynthesis** needs to be **greater** than the rate of cellular **respiration** ($\\checkmark$). An **increase in carbon dioxide** concentration and **light energy** would enable the rate of photosynthesis to increase ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ photosystem II absorbs the highest percentage of light at wavelengths of 680nm **WHILE** photosystem I absorbs the highest percentage of light at wavelengths of 700nm [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ photosystem II is a structure before the electron transport chain **WHEREAS** photosystem I is after the electron transport chain [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ photosystem II passes the electrons to the electron transport chain **WHILE** photosystem I pass the electrons to NADP [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ lost electrons in the reaction centres of photosystem II are filled by electrons from the photolysis of water **WHEREAS** lost electrons in the reaction centres of photosystem I are replaced by electrons from photosystem II [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n :u[Sample answer]:\n\n **Photosystem II** is activated by light that has a **wavelength of 680nm** whereas **photosystem I** is activated by light that has a **wavelength of 700nm** ($\\checkmark$). The excited electrons in photosystem II are transferred to the **electron transport chain** whereas the excited electrons in photosystem I are transferred to **NADP** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cyclic photophosphorylation **OR** non-cyclic photophosphorylation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ takes place in thylakoid membrane [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ involves light energy **OR** energy from photons of light **OR** light-dependent reactions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ photoactivation of photosystem II **OR** light absorption of light by photosystem II [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ transfer of excited electrons from photosystem II to photosystem I [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ excited electrons provide energy to make ATP **OR** excited electrons generate proton gradient which leads to ATP synthesis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ phosphorylation of ADP to ATP **OR** make ATP from ADP and Pi [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP is synthesized by ATP synthase [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ excited electrons from photosystem I reduces NADP **OR** NADP to NADPH [1 mark]\n[3 marks]\n\n :u[Sample answer]:\n\n Photophosphorylation is a **light-dependent reaction** ($\\checkmark$) that takes place in the **thylakoid membrane** ($\\checkmark$). The transport of **excited electrons from photosystem II to photosystem I** ($\\checkmark$) in a linear path produces a **proton gradient** ($\\checkmark$) in thylakoids that leads to the phosphorylation of **ADP to form ATP by ATP synthase** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0521","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0521\n\nWhich structure of the chloroplast is most involved in the production of reduced NADP **and** ATP?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Thylakoid space\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Stroma\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Thylakoid membrane\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Chloroplast membrane","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis use energy from sunlight to produce reduced NADP (NADPH) and ATP. These reactions take place on the thylakoid membranes within chloroplasts. **Choice C** is therefore correct.\n\nDuring the light-dependent reactions, protons accumulate within the thylakoid space (**Choice A**) to create the proton gradient that will power the production of ATP. However, the thylakoid space is not involved in the production of reduced NADP, and the actual production of ATP occurs in the thylakoid membrane where the ATP synthase enzyme is located. The stroma (**Choice B**) contains the enzymes of the Calvin cycle for the light-independent reactions. The chloroplast membrane (**Choice D**) separates the chloroplast from the rest of the cell."},{"ref":"BI0539","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0539\n\nThe most abundant catalytic protein in a vascular plant is found in the leaves. What is the name of this protein?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Insulin\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Rubisco\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Rhodopsin\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Collagen","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA catalytic protein is an enzyme. The most abundant enzyme in plants is rubisco, the enzyme that catalyzes the first step of carbon fixation during the light-independent reactions of photosynthesis.\n\n**Choice A**: Insulin is a hormone found in humans. Insulin binds to receptors on cells to allow them to take up glucose.\n\n**Choice C**: Rhodopsin is a pigment found in the photoreceptors of the retina. Rhodopsin is involved in initiating the response of the cell to light.\n\n**Choice D**: Collagen is a structural protein that gives strength to skin, bones, tendons, and ligaments."},{"ref":"BI0561","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0561\n\nIn which structure of the chloroplast does ribulose bisphosphate undergo carboxylation?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Thylakoid space\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Stroma\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Thylakoid membrane\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Chloroplast membrane","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe carboxylation of ribulose bisphosphate is the first step in the light-independent reactions (the Calvin cycle) of photosynthesis.\n\nDuring the light-dependent reactions, protons accumulate within the thylakoid space (**Choice A**) to create the proton gradient that powers the production of NADPH and ATP. The light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis take place on the thylakoid membrane (**Choice C**). The chloroplast membrane (**Choice D**) separates the chloroplast from the rest of the cell."},{"ref":"BI0591","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0591\n\nWhich of the following catalyses carbon fixation during photosynthesis?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$   RuBP\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$   Rubisco\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$   NADP\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$   Decarboxylase","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\\\n**Choice B** is correct  because Rubisco is the name of the enzyme which catalyses the fixation of carbon during photosynthesis.\n\nRuBP (ribulose bisphosphate) is a 5-carbon molecule involved in the Calvin cycle. It reacts with carbon dioxide but does not catalyse the reaction; **Choice A** is incorrect.\n\nNADP is a coenzyme, not an enzyme. It plays a role in the light-dependent reactions, accepting electrons and protons to form NADPH. NADPH is then used in the Calvin cycle, but NADP does not catalyse carbon fixation; **Choice C** is incorrect.\n\nDecarboxylase enzymes remove carbon dioxide from molecules, so they perform decarboxylation which is the opposite of carbon fixation; **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0815","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0815\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe diagram shows the separation of photosynthetic pigments using the thin-layer chromatography (TLC) technique in the solvent propanone.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/BI0815A.png\" alt=\"BI0815A\">\n\n1. Identify a pigment that is present in cabbage but **not** in spinach. [1 mark]\n\n2. Outline the method used to extract the pigments from the plant leaves. [2 marks]\n\n3.\n 1. Outline what information the Rf value provides in this experiment. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Calculate the Rf value of Xanthophyll, showing your working. [2 marks]\n\n4. Spinach also contains Lycopene, an orange/red pigment. Suggest why it is absent from the chromatogram. [1 mark]\n\n5. Deduce, with a reason, what pigment X is. [1 mark]\n\n6. Explain why the origin must be above the solvent at the start of the experiment. [1 mark]\n\n7. Explain why it is important that the TLC plate is held by its edges when setting up this experiment. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ xanthophyll [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n A photosynthetic pigment that is present in cabbage but not in spinach is **xanthophyll** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ grind the leaves up finely in propanone **OR** add propanone after grinding leaves up finely **OR** chop or finely cut the plant material into small pieces, add propanone and allow the mixture to stand for several hours [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ filter mixture to remove ground leaves **OR** separate propanone from leaf debris [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allow pigment to dry and rehydrate it using propanone [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n To extract pigment from the plant leaves, the plant leaves are **ground up finely in propanone** ($\\checkmark$) and then the mixture is **filtered** to remove the ground leaves ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ preliminary indication of the name of the pigment **OR** name of the pigment when compared to a reference table [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ relative mobility of a substance within a chromatographic system [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The Rf value indicates the **name of the pigment** when compared to a reference table ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n *1 mark can be awarded for the correct working, but an incorrect answer*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 2.1 cm ÷ 3.4 cm **OR** Rf value = distance moved by pigment from origin ÷ distance moved by solvent [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 0.6 **OR** 0.62 [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/BI0815ansA.png\" alt=\"BI0815ansA\">\n\n Rf value = distance moved by pigment from origin ÷ distance moved by solvent\n\n Distance moved by pigment from origin = 2.7 - 0.6 = 2.1 cm \\\n Distance moved by solvent front from origin = 4.0 - 0.6 = 3.4 cm\n\n Rf value = 2.1 ÷ 3.4 = **0.6** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the pigment did not dissolve **OR** is insoluble in propanone **OR** pigment may have degraded during extraction [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Lycopene may be absent from the chromatogram as it is **insoluble** ($\\checkmark$) in propanone.\n\n5. Any **one** of the following:\n\n *Answer must include a pigment and a reason to gain the mark*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carotene **BECAUSE** it moved the same distance as X **OR** same Rf value as X [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Pigment X is likely to be **carotene** as it moved the **same distance** as X ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ so that the pigment only runs in one direction **OR** so that the pigment moves with the solvent [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to stop the pigment from dissolving into the propanone **OR** solvent [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it promotes even separation **OR** achieves better resolution between different pigments [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The origin is placed above the solvent at the start of the experiment to **stop the pigment from dissolving into the solvent** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n7. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ contamination from skin **OR** gloves can alter the results [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ minimises the risk of damaging or scratching the stationary phase, which could affect separation of pigments [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n It is important that TLC plates are held by the edges as **contamination from the skin** can **alter** the results ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0998","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0998\n\nThin-layer chromatography can be used to separate photosynthetic pigments. Below is a diagram showing the separation of pigments in this way.\nRf values are used to work out what pigments are present in the leaf extracts.\n\nWhich of the following are the measurements that need to be taken to calculate the Rf values?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/BI0998.png\" alt=\"BI0998\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ From the pencil line to the solvent front and from the pencil line to the centre of each pigment spot\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ From the centre of the pigment spot to the solvent front and from the base of the chromatography plate to the centre of the pigment spot\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ From the top of the pigment spot to the solvent front and from the bottom of the pigment spot to the pencil line\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ From the bottom of the chromatography plate to the centre of the pigment spot and from the bottom of the chromatography plate to the solvent front","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nFor thin-layer chromatography, the Rf value of a substance is the ratio of the distance the substance travels to the distance the solvent travels on the TLC plate.\nTherefore you need to measure the distance between the pencil line and the solvent front and the distance between the pencil line and the centre of each pigment spot.\nBy taking these measurements, you can calculate the Rf value for each pigment spot. Therefore **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choices B**, **C** and **D** are not the correct measurements for calculating Rf values."},{"ref":"BI1038","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1038\n\nCarbon dioxide enrichment experiments are used to predict future rates of photosynthesis and plant growth. Which of the following is an advantage of enclosed greenhouse experiments over free-air carbon dioxide  enrichment  (FACE) experiments, when conducting carbon dioxide enrichment experiments?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The wavelength of light can be controlled\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ It mimics a natural ecosystem\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Large trees can be studied\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ It is difficult to control rainfall","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nEnclosed greenhouse experiments allow for wavelengths of light to be controlled by exposing the plants to different colours of light. By adjusting the spectrum of light, researchers can investigate the isolated effects of distinct wavelengths on plant responses to elevated carbon dioxide levels. Such control is typically unattainable in open-air experiments like the free-air carbon dioxide enrichment (FACE) system, where natural light conditions predominate. **Choice A**  is correct.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect as an enclosed greenhouse space does not mimic a natural ecosystem as it is a man-made enclosed structure. **Choice C**  is incorrect, as large trees cannot fit or grow freely in a greenhouse. **Choice D**  is incorrect as in an enclosed greenhouse, water can be controlled, mimicking rainfall."},{"ref":"BI1039","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1039\n\nThe graph shows the results from an enclosed greenhouse experiment studying the effect of increasing carbon dioxide concentration on the rate of photosynthesis. What conclusion can be drawn from the graph?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/BI1039.png\" alt=\"BI1039\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ As carbon dioxide concentration increases, the rate of photosynthesis increases.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Light intensity is a limiting factor.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ As carbon dioxide concentration increases, the concentration of oxygen decreases.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ When the rate of photosynthesis plateaus, carbon dioxide is no longer a limiting factor.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAbove a certain carbon dioxide concentration, the graph plateaus. This indicates that carbon dioxide is no longer a limiting factor, and another factor, such as temperature or light intensity, has become a limiting factor. **Choice D** is correct.\n\nAt the beginning of the graph, as the concentration of carbon dioxide increases, the rate of photosynthesis increases, however, this is not the case for the whole graph; **Choice A** is incorrect. When the graph plateaus this indicates that carbon dioxide is no longer a limiting factor, another factor is limiting photosynthesis, however, light intensity might not be that factor; **Choice B** is incorrect. Oxygen is a waste product from photosynthesis, so as the rate of photosynthesis increases, oxygen concentration should also increase. However, the graph does not refer to oxygen concentration, so we do not know what is happening to this in the experiment; **Choice C** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1040","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1040\n\nWhich of the following is **not** a way light is used in the light-dependent reactions in photosynthesis?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ In the breakdown of water\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ To produce ATP and reduced NADP\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ To release carbon dioxide from photosystem I\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ To excite electrons in photosystem II","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe carbon dioxide used during photosynthesis comes from the atmosphere, not from photosystem I, **Choice C**  is, therefore, correct.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect as light is used in the photolysis reaction (the breakdown of water using light), which is a light-dependent reaction. **Choice B**  is incorrect as the products of the light-dependent reaction that are used in the light-independent reaction are ATP and reduced NADP. **Choice D**  is incorrect, as light is used to excite electrons in photosystem II."},{"ref":"BI1041","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1041\n\nAlgae commonly live in water. In what form do they obtain carbon dioxide from their surroundings, and what do they use this carbon dioxide for?\n\n| | Form carbon dioxide is in | Role of Carbon Dioxide |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Carbon dioxide gas | Binds to RuBP, catalysed by Rubisco |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Hydrogen carbonate ions | Fixed into an organic molecule, catalysed by RuBisCO |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Carbon dioxide gas | Fixed into an organic molecule, catalysed by RuBisCO |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Hydrogen carbonate ions | Binds to Rubisco, catalysed by RuBP |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAquatic algae obtain their carbon dioxide from the water in the form of dissolved carbon dioxide or hydrogen carbonate ions. Once in the stroma, the carbon dioxide enters the Calvin cycle and binds to RuBP, a reaction catalysed by the enzyme RuBisCO; **Choice B**  is correct.\n\nWhen carbon dioxide enters the water it is no longer in a gaseous state. It forms dissolved carbon dioxide and carbonic acid, which dissociates into hydrogen carbonate ions and to a lesser extent carbonate ions.  Once in the stroma, the carbon dioxide binds to RuBP, not RuBisCO, and the carbon dioxide is fixed into glucose and other organic molecules. **Choices A, C** and **D** are therefore incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1197","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1197\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe micrograph below shows *Cyanobacteria*, which contain chlorophyll.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/BI1197-SL.png\" alt=\"BI1197-SL\">\n[Source: Adapted from Thompson L. and Whatson N. (2007, January 1).  *Cyanobacteria*.  Wikipedia.  Retrieved on September 6, 2023, from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyanobacteria#. Copyright under CCO]\n\n1. Using your knowledge and the micrograph, state two features that would indicate that this is a bacterium.     [2 marks]\n\n2. Outline the structural differences between animal and plant cells.  [3 marks]\n\n3. Explain how light energy is used in photosynthesis to carry out anabolic reactions. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **two** of the following\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell wall of peptidoglycan            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ naked DNA **OR** DNA not associated with histones **OR** proteins    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleoid **OR** DNA free in cytoplasm                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ no membrane bound organelles                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 70s ribosomes                                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell smaller than 10μm                            [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Two features that would indicate that this is a bacterium are it has **no membrane-bound organelles** ($\\checkmark$) and **70s ribosomes** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plant cells have a cell wall, **WHEREAS** animal cells do not    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plant cells generally have a central vacuole **WHEREAS** animal cells do not        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plant cells have chloroplasts **WHEREAS** animal cells do not    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ animal cells may have lysosomes **WHEREAS** plant cells do not    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ animal cells may have cilia **OR** flagella **WHEREAS** plant cells do not    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ animal cells have centrioles **WHEREAS** plant cells do not        [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Plant cells have a **cell wall** ($\\checkmark$), **central vacuole** ($\\checkmark$) and **chloroplasts**, whereas **animal** cells **do not** ($\\checkmark$) .\n\n3. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light energy is absorbed by chlorophyll            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light energy is transferred to electrons **OR** is used to excite electrons    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light energy splitting water molecules **OR** photolysis releases electrons for the electron transport chain **OR** hydrogen ions for use in ATP production    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light energy is used to synthesise ATP from ADP and inorganic phosphate **OR** NADPH from electrons and one proton            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ NADPH **OR** ATP is used to make larger organic compounds **OR** glucose **OR** starch **OR** other named organic compound from carbon dioxide        [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Light energy is **absorbed** by **chlorophyll** ($\\checkmark$) and is used to **excite electrons** ($\\checkmark$).  These electrons reduce **NADP:sup[+]** to form **NADPH**, which is an anabolic reaction ($\\checkmark$).  Energy present in **NADPH** is then used to **produce glucose** from **carbon dioxide** ($\\checkmark$).  This is also an anabolic reaction."},{"ref":"BI1226","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1226\n\nThe graph shows the rate of photosynthesis for a plant placed in a greenhouse where temperature and carbon dioxide concentration are controlled. At what time would light intensity be **least likely** to limit the rate of photosynthesis?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/BI1226.png\" alt=\"BI1226\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 12:00\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 20:00\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 10:00\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 00:00","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAt 12:00, the plant is receiving maximum sunlight and has the highest rate of photosynthesis, therefore light is unlikely to be limiting the rate of photosynthesis; **Choice A** is correct.\n\nAt 20:00, 10:00 and 00:00, the rates of photosynthesis are lower than at 12:00, suggesting that light intensity is the limiting factor since carbon dioxide concentration and temperature are controlled. **Choices B, C** and **D** are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1229","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1229\n\nATP and reduced NADP from the light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis are used in the light-independent reactions in photosynthesis. What is used to reduce NADP and provide the energy for ATP synthesis in light-dependent reactions?\n\n| | Source of NADP reduction | Source of energy for ATP synthesis |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Protons from the thylakoid space | Energy released from the movement of electrons from carrier to carrier |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Electrons from photosystem II | Active transport  of protons through ATP synthase |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Electrons from the photolysis of water | Simple diffusion of protons across the thylakoid membrane |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Electrons from photosystem I | Facilitated diffusion of protons through ATP synthase |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe excited electrons from photosystem I are passed two at a time to NADP, an electron acceptor, which with the addition of a hydrogen ion from the stroma, is reduced. The energy for ATP production comes from protons moving down ATP synthase, which is used to phosphorylate ADP to form ATP. **Choice D**  is correct.\n\nProtons from the thylakoid space create the electrochemical gradient, which provides the energy for ATP to be made; **Choice A**  is incorrect. The movement of electrons from photosystem II provides the energy to pump electrons into the thylakoid space; **Choice B**  is incorrect. Electrons from the photolysis of water are passed from electron carrier to carrier, and protons move by facilitated diffusion through ATP synthase; **Choice C**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1568","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1568\n\nWhich of the following graphs correctly depicts the action spectrum for chlorophyll a?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A. | | $\\hspace{2cm}$ | |B.|\n| |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/BI1568A.png\" alt=\"BI1568A\"> | | | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/BI1568B.png\" alt=\"BI1568B\">|\n|C. | | | |D. |\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/BI1568C.png\" alt=\"BI1568C\"> | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/BI1568D.png\" alt=\"BI1568D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because the action spectrum of a photosynthetic pigment is determined by the rate of photosynthesis at each wavelength of light. By contrast, the absorption spectrum is based on the light absorption by a pigment at each wavelength. For chlorophyll A, there is increased photosynthetic activity when exposed to red and blue light, corresponding to the upper and lower end of the visible spectrum from 400-700nm.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because this graph represents the absorption spectrum, not the action spectrum. The shape of the curve is also slightly different. **Choice B** is incorrect because the wavelengths of light are incorrect and the shape of the curve is different. **Choice C** is incorrect because the y-axis shows absorption rather than the rate of photosynthesis."},{"ref":"BI1586","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1586\n\nWhere is chlorophyll located and what is its role in photosynthesis?\n\n| | Location | Function |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Thylakoid membrane | Facilitates photolysis of water |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Stroma | Facilitates photolysis of water |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Thylakoid membrane | Uses proton gradient to generate ATP |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Stroma | Uses proton gradient to generate ATP |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because chlorophyll is a photosynthetic pigment found in the photosystems of the thylakoid membrane. It absorbs sunlight to facilitate the photolysis, or splitting, of water molecules.\n\n**Choices B** and **D** are incorrect because chlorophyll is not present in the stroma. **Choice C** is incorrect because chemiosmosis, or the production of ATP using proton gradients, is accomplished with ATP synthase, not chlorophyll."},{"ref":"BI1629","marks":"3","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1629\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nThe diagram shows the results of a chromatogram created using pigments extracted from a geranium leaf (*Geranium albanum*).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/1629iii.png\" alt=\"1629iii\">\n\nThe table shows Rf values for photosynthetic pigments.\n\n| Photosynthetic pigment | Rf |\n\n| :-- | :-- |\n\n| Chlorophyll a | 0.68 |\n\n| Chlorophyll b | 0.54 |\n\n| $\\beta$- carotene | 0.94 |\n\n| Fucoxanthine | 0.51 |\n\n| Lutein | 0.43 |\n\n| Neoxanthine | 0.22 |\n\n| Violaxanthine | 0.08 |\n\n[Source: Adapted from: Bhatia, S., Nagpal, K., Bera, T., Sharma, A., & Sharma, K. Evaluation of pharmacognostical, phytochemical and anti-microbial properties of Porphyra vietnamensis (2015). International Journal of Green Pharmacy, 9(2), 131. Retrieved April 28, 2024, from https://doi.org/10.4103/0973-8258.155065. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n1.\n\n 1. Calculate the Rf value of pigment X. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Identify pigment X using the data from the table. [1 mark]\n\n2. Neoxanthine appears light green on the chromatogram. Explain why it appears this colour. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n\n 1. any **one** of the following\\\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 0.69 [1 mark]\n\n *Accept answers in the range of 0.68 - 0.70*\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/1629%20MSii.png\" alt=\"1629 MSii\">\n\n Rf value = distance moved by pigment from origin ÷ distance moved by solvent front from the origin\\\n\n Rf value = (29 - 5) ÷ (40 - 5)\\\n\n Rf value = 24 ÷ 35 = 0.69 ($\\checkmark$)\n\n 2. any **one** of the following\\\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chlorophyll a [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n **Chlorophyll a** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **one** of the following\\\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it reflects light green light **AND** absorbs all other colours of light **OR** wavelengths of green light are reflected **AND** all other wavelengths of light are absorbed **OR** reflects wavelengths 594 - 570nm and absorbs all other wavelengths [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n Neoxanthine **reflects green** light and **absorbs** all **other colours** of light ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2002","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2002\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nPlant growth chambers are contained and controllable environments used to strictly control growth conditions. A researcher planted *Arabidopsis thaliana* in several growth chambers at six different carbon dioxide concentrations. After 3 weeks of growth, the plants were dried and massed.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/BI2002A.png\" alt=\"BI2002A\">\n\n1. State the independent variable. [1 mark]\n\n2. Suggest **one** reason for reporting biomass units as grams per 10 plants. [1 mark]\n\n3. Explain the trend seen in the graph. [3 marks]\n\n4. Discuss a probable result if the $\\ce{CO_2}$ concentration were raised above\n2,000 mg m$^{-3}$. [2 marks]\n\n5. Explain the use of dried biomass in the experiment. [2 marks]\n\n6. State **one** light variable that can be adjusted in the growth chamber to potentially change the biomass produced by the plant. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ $\\ce{CO_2}$ concentration **OR** mg m$^{-3}$ of $\\ce{CO_2}$ [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The independent variable is the **concentration of carbon dioxide in mg m**$^{-3}$ ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ measuring individual plants may be less precise due to the limitations of the scale **OR** instrument used **OR** combining plants into groups improves accuracy [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ reduces impact of outliers **OR** non-germination improving data reliability [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allows larger sample sizes to be used which improves reliability **OR** reduces impact of variability between individual plants [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ provides a more reliable mean by averaging natural variation in growth between individual plants **OR** provides a more representative measure of biomass across plants [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ any other reasonable suggestion [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n This would simplify measurements and **reduce variability** ($\\checkmark$) between individual plants in large-scale experiments, ensuring a more **reliable mean** that accounts for **natural variation** across a population ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as $\\ce{CO_2}$ concentration increases, dry biomass also increases, indicating a possible positive correlation **OR** indicating a positive relationship [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ $\\ce{CO_2}$ is a substrate for photosynthesis, so increasing its availability can enhance rate of photosynthesis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ photosynthesis reactions are catalysed by enzymes, and higher $\\ce{CO_2}$ concentrations can initially increase the rate of these reactions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in biomass suggests increase in $\\ce{CO_2}$ concentration increases rate of photosynthesis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at higher $\\ce{CO_2}$ concentrations, the biomass plateaus, suggesting limiting factor **OR** enzyme concentration **OR** light intensity **OR** temperature is limiting [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n From 600 to 1,200 mg m$^{-3}$ $\\ce{CO_2}$, biomass increases, indicating a **positive relationship** ($\\checkmark$). This occurs because **CO**$_2$ is a **substrate for photosynthesis**, and its availability **enhances** the **rate of photosynthesis** ($\\checkmark$). However, **beyond 1,200 mg m**$^{-3}$, the biomass **plateaus**, suggesting that **enzymes** are **saturated** or another **factor**, such as **light intensity**, has become **limiting** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **two** of the following *(result must be supported by reasoning)*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ biomass production would not change **OR** would stay constant [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ photosynthetic enzymes (rubisco) are saturated **OR** carbon dioxide is no longer the limiting factor [1 mark]\n **OR**\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ biomass production would decrease [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ excessive $\\ce{CO_2}$ could disrupt cellular processes **OR** lead to toxic effects **OR** metabolic imbalances **OR** limit access to oxygen for respiration [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The biomass measurements would stay at the **plateau** ($\\checkmark$) because **carbon dioxide** is **no longer** the **limiting factor** once the photosynthetic **enzymes** are **saturated** ($\\checkmark$) with the substrate.\n\n5. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ dry biomass is an indirect measure of photosynthetic rate [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ drying plants ensures results are based only on organic matter [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ removes variability of water content between plants [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ using gas sensors for weeks to measure photosynthetic rate is impractical **OR** would be labour intensive **OR** require constant monitoring [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ only endpoint data is required to measure three weeks of growth [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ any other reasonable explanation [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Dried biomass is an **indirect measure of photosynthetic rate** ($\\checkmark$), because it reflects the organic molecules produced by the plant. Drying **ensures results** are based only on **organic molecules** by **removing variability** caused by **water content** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ light intensity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ day length [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ wavelength(s) [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n One way the light delivered by the growth chambers can impact the dried biomass produced is if they change the **intensity of the light** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2334","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2334\n\nWhich of these is the purpose of FACE experiments?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Investigate rates of photosynthesis and growth of aquatic plants at higher concentrations of dissolved carbon dioxide.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Investigate rates of photosynthesis and plant growth in natural ecosystems at higher temperatures.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Investigate rates of photosynthesis and plant growth in natural ecosystems at higher concentrations of atmospheric carbon dioxide.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Investigate energy loss from natural ecosystems at higher concentrations of atmospheric carbon dioxide.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because FACE (Free-Air Carbon Dioxide Enrichment) experiments are designed to study how increased atmospheric carbon dioxide affects photosynthesis, plant growth, and overall ecosystem dynamics in natural and agricultural environments. This matches the purpose of these experiments.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because FACE experiments investigate the effect of increased carbon dioxide concentration on terrestrial ecosystems.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because FACE experiments do not investigate the effect of temperature on natural ecosystems.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because FACE experiments do investigate the effect of higher atmospheric concentrations of carbon dioxide but do not investigate the effect of this on energy loss."},{"ref":"BI2388","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2388\n\nWhich of the following are the advantages of photosynthetic pigments being held in photosystems within the thylakoid membrane?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | More electrons are available for excitation.|\n    | II. | Light energy can be captured from a wide variety of wavelengths of the visible spectrum. |\n    | III. | Spatial channelling of excited electrons towards the reaction centre can be achieved.  |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is false because having the photosynthetic pigment molecules aggregated in a photosystem does not in itself affect the molecular structure of those pigments, and in turn, does not affect the number of electrons within each pigment molecule that would be available for excitation by light energy.\n\n**Statement II** is true because different photosynthetic pigments have different absorption and action spectra and so can work together to absorb a wider range of wavelengths of light from the spectrum found in sunlight.\n\n**Statement III** is true because the spatial arrangement of pigments within the photosystem ensures that excited electrons are transferred efficiently to the reaction centre, where they can be passed on for further steps in the photosynthetic process. This spatial organisation is essential for the function of photosystems and allows photosynthesis to proceed efficiently.\n\n**Choice C** is correct because it contains the two true statements (**II** and **III**).\n\n**Choices A** and **D** are incorrect because they contain the false statement (**I**).\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because it omits one of the true statements (**III**)."},{"ref":"BI2445","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2445\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nBelow is an electron micrograph of a chloroplast.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/BI2445.png\" alt=\"BI2445\">\n[Source:Adapted from: and3k, & caper437. (2009, June 25). *File: chloroplast in leaf of Anemone sp Tem 85000x.png* - Wikimedia Commons. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 13, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Chloroplast_in_leaf_of_Anemone_sp_TEM_12000x.png Copyright free]\n\n1. State the molecule that supplies the electrons to replace those lost from chlorophyll in photosystem II at the beginning of the electron transport chain in chloroplasts.    [1 mark]\n\n2. Outline the role of carbon dioxide in photosynthesis.                      [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$  water    [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The **photolysis of water** in PS II provides the **electrons** ($\\checkmark$) for the electron transport chain in chloroplasts.\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide is a substrate in the light-independent reactions **OR** Calvin cycle    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Calvin cycle takes place in stroma                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ requires the enzyme rubisco        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon fixation **OR** carboxylation of ribulose bisphosphate **OR** carbon from carbon dioxide is added to ribulose bisphosphate **OR** a five-carbon compound        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to form an unstable six-carbon compound which splits into two G3P **OR** two, 3 carbon compounds         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide binds more strongly with rubisco **OR** ribulose bisphosphate carboxylase than oxygen does with rubisco     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ one sixth **OR** some molecules of triose phosphate leaves the cycle to form glucose while five sixths **OR** rest of molecules are used to regenerate ribulose bisphosphate molecules            [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Carbon dioxide is needed as a substrate in the **light-independent reactions** ($\\checkmark$) to form carbohydrates. **Carboxylation of ribulose bisphosphate** ($\\checkmark$) takes place in the **stroma** ($\\checkmark$) where carbon dioxide is added to ribulose bisphosphate to form an unstable **six- carbon compound** which splits into **two molecules** of **glycerate-3-phosphate** ($\\checkmark$). These are then further reduced to form **triose phosphate**, some of which are used to **form glucose**, and the **rest** are used to **regenerate RuBP** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2669","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI2669\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline how light energy is converted into chemical energy during photosynthesis.            [4 marks]\n\n2. Describe the benefits and challenges of FACE experiments for investigating photosynthesis.    [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain how valid results can be obtained when investigating the effect of varying light intensity on the rate of photosynthesis.    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light energy is absorbed by photosynthetic pigments **OR** chlorophyll    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ absorbed light energy excites electrons in chlorophyll        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ excited electrons provide energy to convert carbon dioxide into glucose **OR** carbohydrate [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electrons lost from chlorophyll are replaced by electrons from the splitting of water **OR** photolysis        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ photolysis is the splitting of water molecules in the presence of light [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ photolysis produces electrons **AND** hydrogen ions **AND** oxygen     [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Light energy** is **absorbed** by **chlorophyll** in the chloroplasts ($\\checkmark$). This **energy excites electrons** in the **chlorophyll** molecules ($\\checkmark$). The excited electrons provide energy to **convert carbon dioxide into glucose** ($\\checkmark$). The **electrons lost** from chlorophyll are **replaced** by electrons from the **splitting of water** in a process called **photolysis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following *(must include at least **one** benefit and **one** challenge to gain full marks):*\n\n    ***Benefits:***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ take place in natural ecosystems, increasing ecological validity    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allow observation of long-term plant responses to elevated $\\ce{CO_2}$        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ simulate future atmospheric $\\ce{CO_2}$ conditions predicted under climate change    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plants experience natural interactions with pests, competitors, and pollinators, giving realistic outcomes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enable study of whole-ecosystem responses, not just individual plants    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Challenges:***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ difficult to control environmental variables like temperature and rainfall        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ expensive **OR** requires large infrastructure    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ differences in the environmental conditions **OR** characteristics of different experimental plots may cause unintended variability in the results        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ wind or weather may disperse added $\\ce{CO_2}$ unevenly        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ monitoring **OR** data collection over large areas is logistically demanding    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ harder to isolate the effect of $\\ce{CO_2}$ alone due to multiple interacting variables        [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    FACE experiments are beneficial because they are conducted in **natural ecosystems**, so they provide **more valid data** about how **plants respond** to **elevated** $\\mathbf{CO_2}$ levels ($\\checkmark$).  They also allow **long-term observations** of plant **growth** and **photosynthesis** under **future climate** conditions ($\\checkmark$).  However, a major challenge is that **environmental variables** like temperature and rainfall are **difficult to control** in open-air settings ($\\checkmark$).  In addition, differences between plots, such as soil type or microclimate, can **introduce variation** in the **data** that is **unrelated** to $\\ce{CO_2}$ levels ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use a light meter to measure light intensity    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allow plant time to acclimatise at each light level before taking measurements        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carry out multiple trials **OR** repeats **OR** collect at least three results at each light intensity    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ take mean of results **OR** remove any anomalous results [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use  same plant **OR** same species **OR** size **OR** condition of plant            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ maintain a constant $\\ce{CO_2}$ concentration, **OR** use a fixed mass **OR** concentration of sodium hydrogen carbonate        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ keep temperature constant **OR** use a water bath to maintain stable **OR** suitable temperature [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ record data in a consistent **OR** quantifiable way **OR** count  bubbles of oxygen per minute **OR** collect volume of oxygen in same set time            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carry out the experiment in a darkened room to eliminate interference from other light sources [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ calibrate equipment before use to reduce systematic error    [1 mark]\n\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    To ensure valid results a **light meter** should be used to **accurately measure** light intensity ($\\checkmark$). The **plant** should be allowed to **acclimatise** at **each** light **intensity before recording data** ($\\checkmark$). Multiple trials should be carried out at **each light intensity** ($\\checkmark$), and a **mean** should be **calculated** to provide a more reliable estimate of the true photosynthetic rate ($\\checkmark$). The **same species** and **size of plant** should be used in **all trials** ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, **carbon dioxide concentration** must be **kept constant**, for example by using the **same mass** of **sodium hydrogen carbonate** for each trial ($\\checkmark$). **Temperature** should also be **kept constant**, possibly using a **water bath** to maintain a suitable temperature for the plant’s enzymes and to prevent heat from the lamp from affecting results ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2822","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2822\n\nWhich row correctly describes the source and role of oxygen in photosynthesis?\n\n| | Source | Description |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Reduction of water | By-product |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Chemiosmosis | Reactant |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Photolysis | By-product |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Photolysis | Reactant |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because photolysis is the splitting of water using light energy. This process releases oxygen gas ($\\ce{O_2}$), protons ($\\ce{H^+}$), and electrons.  The oxygen originates from water and is released into the atmosphere, playing no further role in the photosynthesis process, which makes it a by-product.\\\n**Choice A** is incorrect because although oxygen is a by-product of photosynthesis, it is not produced by the reduction of water; it is produced by photolysis (splitting of water).\\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because chemiosmosis involves the synthesis of ATP driven by a proton gradient across the thylakoid membrane, and oxygen is not a reactant of photosynthesis; it is a by-product.\\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because although oxygen is produced by photolysis, it is a by-product, not a reactant."},{"ref":"BI2823","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2823\n\nTable 1 shows data collected using thin-layer chromatography to separate photosynthetic pigments. Table 2 shows the retention factors for four photosynthetic pigments using the same solvent.\n\nUse the information to identify pigment II.\n\n| Distance travelled | mm |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| Solvent | 178 |\n| Pigment I | 98 |\n| Pigment II | 91 |\n| Pigment III | 121 |\n\n| Pigment | Retention factor ( R$\\mathbf{_f}$ ) |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| Chlorophyll a | 0.68 |\n| Chlorophyll b | 0.55 |\n| Fucoxanthin | 0.51 |\n| $\\beta$-carotene | 0.98\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Chlorophyll a\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Chlorophyll b\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Fucoxanthin\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\beta$-carotene","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because the retention factor ($\\ce{R_f}$) for fucoxanthin is 0.51, which matches the calculated $\\ce{R_f}$ value for Pigment II.\n\nRetention factor ($\\ce{R_f}$) =  $\\dfrac{\\ce{Distance travelled by pigment}}{\\ce{Distance travelled by solvent}}$\n\n$\\ce{R_f}$ of pigment II = $\\dfrac{91}{178}$  = 0.51\n\n$\\ce{R_f}$ of pigment I = $\\dfrac{98}{178}$  = 0.55\n\n$\\ce{R_f}$ of pigment III = $\\dfrac{121}{178}$  = 0.68\n\n**Choices A, B** and **D** are incorrect because their $\\ce{R_f}$ values do not match the calculated $\\ce{R_f}$ for Pigment II."},{"ref":"BI2824","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2824\n\nWhich of the following statement(s) correctly describe(s) the absorption of light by photosynthetic pigments?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Light absorption causes electrons to become excited. |\n    | II. | Absorption is highest at wavelengths 550 nm to 600 nm. |\n    | III. | Light energy absorbed is transformed into chemical energy. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because electrons within a pigment molecule are excited by light energy.\n\n**Statement II** is false because absorption is lowest between 550 nm and 600 nm. It is highest between 400 nm and 500 nm and between 650 nm and 700 nm.\n\n**Statement III** is true because the light energy, which is absorbed by photosynthetic pigments is converted to chemical energy.\n\n**Choice C** is correct because it only includes the true **Statements I** and **III**.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because is it missing the true **Statement III**. **Choices B** and **D** are incorrect because **Statement II** is false."},{"ref":"BI2825","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2825\n\nWhich graph best represents the action spectrum for photosynthesis?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A. | | $\\hspace{2cm}$ | |B.|\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/AnsA.png\" alt=\"AnsA\"> | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/AnsB.png\" alt=\"AnsB\"> |\n|C. | | | |D. |\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/AnsC.png\" alt=\"AnsC\"> | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/AnsD.png\" alt=\"AnsD\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because the rate of photosynthesis is highest at wavelengths that pigments such as chlorophyll have the highest rate of absorption, between approximately 400 and 500 nm and between approximately 650 nm and 700 nm. The axes are correctly labelled, with wavelength on the x-axis and rate of photosynthesis on the y-axis.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the axes are incorrectly labelled, the x-axis should be wavelength and the y-axis should be photosynthesis rate. **Choice B** is incorrect because the rate of photosynthesis is low between 500 nm and 600 nm, but this graph shows the opposite. **Choice C** is incorrect because the values on the x-axis are incorrect, and the highest rates of photosynthesis are not between 200 nm and 300 nm and 400 nm and 450 nm."},{"ref":"BI2826","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2826\n\nWhich description is correct for FACE experiments?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Carbon dioxide concentration is increased, and all other variables are controlled.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Use enclosed greenhouses to predict rates of photosynthesis at increased concentrations of carbon dioxide.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Require control plots under carefully controlled conditions.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Use agricultural situations and natural ecosystems to predict rates of photosynthesis at increased concentrations of carbon dioxide.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because FACE experiments are free-air carbon dioxide enrichment experiments, which involve increasing carbon dioxide concentration to plants in an open scenario where other environmental factors cannot be controlled. For FACE, carbon dioxide is artificially increased for the plants in the experiment plot and a separate plot is used as a control group under normal atmospheric conditions.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because in FACE experiments, carbon dioxide concentration is increased, but apart from carbon dioxide concentration, the environmental conditions are natural. This means that factors such as temperature, rainfall, humidity and wind are all variables. **Choice B** is incorrect because FACE experiments are conducted outside under environmental conditions where many factors vary. **Choice C** is incorrect because FACE experiments use control plots, but the control plots are open to environmental conditions and are not controlled."},{"ref":"BI2887","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2887\n\nWhich of the following statements best describe(s) the energy transformation during photosynthesis?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Light energy is absorbed by chlorophyll and converted into chemical energy.  |\n    | II. | Chemical energy stored in glucose is released as heat energy during photosynthesis. |\n    | III. | The energy from sunlight is stored in the bonds of glucose molecules. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDuring photosynthesis, light energy is absorbed by chlorophyll and converted into chemical energy, which is stored in molecules like ATP and NADPH. This energy is then used to convert carbon dioxide into glucose. This transformation of light energy into chemical energy is a key part of photosynthesis. **Statement I** is true.\n\nPhotosynthesis is the process of storing energy in glucose molecules, not releasing it as heat. The chemical energy in glucose is stored and can later be used by the plant for growth, development, or metabolism, but it is not released as heat during the process of photosynthesis. **Statement II** is false.\n\nDuring photosynthesis, light energy is used to form chemical bonds in glucose molecules. These bonds store energy in the form of glucose, which can later be broken down to release energy when needed by the plant. **Statement III** is true.\n\n**Choice C** is the correct answer because it includes only the true **Statements I** and **III**.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as it misses the true **Statement III**.\\\n**Choices B** and **D** are incorrect because they include the false **Statement II**."},{"ref":"BI3216","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI3216\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nPhotosynthesis is a process where carbon compounds are produced in the presence of light.\n\n1. Draw and label a plan diagram to show the distribution of the main tissues in a transverse section of a dicotyledonous leaf. [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline how light energy is converted into chemical energy during photosynthesis. [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe an experiment investigating the effect of carbon dioxide concentration on the rate of photosynthesis. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. diagram to show any **four** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ correctly positioned **AND** labelled palisade mesophyll layer [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ correctly positioned **AND** labelled spongy mesophyll layer [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ correctly positioned **AND** labelled upper epidermis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ correctly positioned **AND** labelled lower epidermis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ correctly positioned **AND** labelled vascular bundle **OR** xylem **OR** phloem [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n :u[Sample answer]:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Photosynthesis/images/BI3216_leaf_plan_diagram_v2.png\" alt=\"TS of tissues in dicot leaf plan diagram\">\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ light is absorbed by chlorophyll **OR** pigments arranged in photosystems in the thylakoid membrane [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ absorbed energy excites electrons, which are emitted from the reaction centre of photosystem II [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ excited electrons pass along an electron transport chain **OR** chain of electron carriers [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ energy from the electrons is used to pump protons (H:sup[+]) into the thylakoid space [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ protons (H:sup[+]) diffuse down their concentration gradient through ATP synthase, driving ATP synthesis by chemiosmosis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ photolysis of water produces electrons that replace those lost from photosystem II, together with oxygen **AND** H:sup[+] [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ electrons are re-excited at photosystem I and used to reduce NADP **OR** produce reduced NADP [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP and reduced NADP are used in the Calvin cycle **OR** light-independent reactions to convert glycerate 3-phosphate into triose phosphate (accept to produce triose phosphate **OR** carbohydrate) [1 mark]\n[5 marks]\n\n :u[Sample answer]:\n\n **Light** is **absorbed** by **pigments** in **photosystem II** in the **thylakoid membrane**, **exciting electrons** ($\\checkmark$). The excited electrons pass along an **electron transport chain** ($\\checkmark$), **releasing energy** that **pumps protons** into the **thylakoid space** ($\\checkmark$). The **protons diffuse** back through **ATP synthase**, producing **ATP** by **chemiosmosis** ($\\checkmark$). **Electrons lost** from photosystem II are **replaced** by the **photolysis** of **water**, which releases **oxygen** ($\\checkmark$). At **photosystem I**, electrons are **re-excited** and used to **reduce NADP** ($\\checkmark$). **ATP** and **reduced NADP** are then used in the **Calvin cycle** to convert **glycerate 3-phosphate** into **triose phosphate**, which is used to synthesise **carbohydrates** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ select a range of five different concentrations of sodium hydrogen carbonate/sodium bicarbonate solution/other suitable source of carbon dioxide, including control with no added sodium hydrogen carbonate/no added source of carbon dioxide **OR** prepare 0.000 M, 0.005 M, 0.010 M, 0.015 M and 0.020 M sodium hydrogen carbonate/any acceptable range with consistent intervals provided [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ use aquatic plants/*Elodea*/pondweed **OR** land plants [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ for aquatic plants, submerge the plant in water with the cut end of the stem facing upwards [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ for land-based plants, measure oxygen gas production using a gas sensor **OR** for aquatic plants, count the number of bubbles/water displacement [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ use boiled and cooled water to reduce dissolved carbon dioxide **OR** use a tray with sodium hydroxide to absorb CO:sub[2] from the air in terrestrial plants set-up [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ for land plants, enclose the plant in a sealed chamber and vary CO:sub[2] by adding CO:sub[2] gas **OR** absorbing CO:sub[2] with soda lime/sodium hydroxide, checking concentrations with a CO:sub[2] sensor [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ multiple trials to obtain reliable results **OR** five trials for each concentration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ use a thermometer to check for temperature changes **OR** place the beaker/boiling tube containing aquatic plants in water bath [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ place light source at a constant distance from the plant [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ description of appropriate controlled variable, e.g. plant size in different trials and experimental groups is the same [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ rate of photosynthesis can be calculated as oxygen gas percentage over time **OR** number of bubbles over time **OR** water displacement over time [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide concentration is the independent variable **AND** rate of oxygen production is the dependent variable [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allow the plant to acclimatise for a few minutes at each new concentration before measuring [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ collect the gas in a gas syringe **OR** photosynthometer to measure volume, as bubble size varies [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ check pH **OR** use a buffer, as sodium hydrogen carbonate can change the pH of the water [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ plot a graph of rate of photosynthesis against carbon dioxide concentration [1 mark]\n[7 marks]\n\n :u[Sample answer]:\n\n Select **five** different **concentrations** of carbon dioxide (0.000 M to 0.020 M sodium hydrogen carbonate) ($\\checkmark$). For the control with no added sodium hydrogen carbonate, use **boiled** and then **cooled water** to **reduce dissolved carbon dioxide** ($\\checkmark$). Use **pondweed** ($\\checkmark$) and **submerge** it in the **water**, with the **cut end** of the **stem pointing upwards** ($\\checkmark$). The **oxygen** produced can be **collected** in a small **gas syringe** ($\\checkmark$). The **rate of photosynthesis** can then be calculated by **dividing** the **volume** collected **by** the **time** taken ($\\checkmark$). **Repeat** the experiment **five times** at **each concentration** and **calculate** a **mean rate** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n The **other limiting factors**, temperature and light intensity, must be **controlled**. **Temperature** can be controlled by placing the beaker containing the plant in a **thermostatically controlled water bath** ($\\checkmark$), and **light intensity** by placing the **light source** at a **fixed distance** ($\\checkmark$)."}]},{"name":"Populations & Communities","questions":[{"ref":"BI0100","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0100\n\nRain forests are known to have tall trees and a large biomass with high productivity, despite infertile soils. The growing conditions have been known to nurture the presence of many interdependent organisms.  These species also interact with the physical environment they inhabit.\n\nWhich of the following is described here?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$  A habitat\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$  An ecosystem\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$  A population\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$  A community","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAn ecosystem refers to an area where organisms interact with each other and with the physical environment.\n\nA habitat (**Choice A**) refers to the environment in which a species normally lives. A population (**Choice C**) is a group of organisms of the same species living in the same area at the same time which can interbreed. A community (**Choice D**) is a group of populations living and interacting with each other in the same habitat."},{"ref":"BI0106","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0106\n\nA wetland plant called sea-blite, which has narrow succulent leaves, and a tough perenial salt grass both grow on the edge of a salt marsh in Bolsa Chica, California. What statistical test can be used to determine if the two species are found growing together more than would be expected by random chance?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Simpson's diversity index\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ T-test\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Chi-square analysis\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Frequency found in quadrats","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDiversity indices are used to measure the diversity of organisms in an area (**Choice A**). A t-test (**Choice B**) is used to see if there is a significant difference between the means of two groups.  Chi-square analysis (**Choice C**) can be used to check for the association of two species after collecting data on the presence or absence of either or both in defined areas.  More generally, Chi-square analysis is used to determine if there is a significant difference between observed and expected.  Frequency found in quadrats (**Choice D**) records how frequently one or more species are found in quadrats and is not a statistical test."},{"ref":"BI0109","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0109\n\nWhich of the following can be determined using the chi-squared test?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The biodiversity found in an area of a forest.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The range of values obtained for heights of trees in an area.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The probability of finding certain species of plants in adjoining plots based on observations made for one area.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Testing for a statistically significant association between the occurrences of two plant species","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe presence or absence of two plant species can be recorded in a series of sampling quadrats. A chi-squared test compares the observed frequencies with the expected frequencies if the species occur independently. This allows scientists to test for a statistically significant association between the occurrences of the two species. Therefore, **Choice D** is correct.\n\nC**Choice A** is incorrect because a chi-squared test does not calculate biodiversity. Species diversity can instead be quantified using an index, such as Simpson’s diversity index, which takes account of the number of species and their relative abundances.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the range of tree heights is calculated by subtracting the smallest recorded height from the largest recorded height. A chi-squared test is not needed for this calculation.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because a chi-squared test does not directly predict the probability of finding plant species in unsampled adjoining plots. It evaluates observed frequency data against the frequencies expected under a null hypothesis, such as the hypothesis that two species occur independently."},{"ref":"BI0110","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0110\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA student wants to set up an aquatic mesocosm using seawater to simulate conditions in the food web (shown in the image below) and test its stability. He used seawater and sand from the area to set up the system and added some small organisms to the mesocosm.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Populations%20%26%20Communities/images/BI0110fw.png\" alt=\"BI0110fw\">\n[Source: Adapted from Cmglee. (2003, October). *File: Arctic Food Web.svg - Wikimedia Commons*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved April 14, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Arctic_food_web.svg. Copyright under CC BY-SA 3.0]\n\n1. In the food web shown, identify an autotroph. [1 mark]\n\n2. State a reason why phytoplankton shown in the food web would be a good addition to the mesocosm. [1 mark]\n\n3. Discuss how saprotrophs perform a necessary function for the mesocosm. [3 marks]\n\n4. Describe the disadvantages of using mesocosms in scientific investigations. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ phytoplankton [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phytoplankton are autotrophs **OR** can add energy to the mesocosm through photosynthesis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to provide energy continuously to the closed system [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    The phytoplankton **are autotrophs** ($\\checkmark$). Autotrophs are necessary for all ecosystems because they produce organic compounds through photosynthesis.\n\n3. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ saprotrophs secrete enzymes into the environment [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ saprotrophs break down food outside their bodies into simpler substances [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ saprotrophs are found in the water **OR**  sand [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ saprotrophs recycle nutrients in ecosystems [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ saprotrophs sometimes remove toxic compounds [1 mark]\n[3 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Saprotrophs **recycle nutrients in ecosystems** ($\\checkmark$) by **secreting enzymes into the environment** ($\\checkmark$) which **break down organic material into simpler substances** ($\\checkmark$) which they then absorb and use for food.  Saprotrophs can also **break down toxic compounds** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large organisms may not fit in the mesocosms **OR** sometimes not all organisms from the ecosystem can be added to the mesocosm **OR** mesocosms are sometimes limited in size[1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mesocosms are limited in their capacity to simulate natural conditions [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ small organisms added from the field to a mesocosm may be stressed [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mesocosms need to be monitored regularly [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Mesocosms are experiments set up in laboratories that are **limited in their physical space** ($\\checkmark$). Only small organisms can be used in the experiments and the organisms **can be stressed** once inside a mesocosm ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0685","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0685\n\nWhich parts of insect-pollinated flowers are adaptations for mutualistic relationships with pollinators?\n\n-    I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Nectary\n-    II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ Coloured petals\n-    III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ Stigma\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because insect-pollinated flowers have coloured petals (**Statement II**) to attract pollinators and a nectary (**Statement I**) to reward them, whereas all flowers have a stigma (**Statement III**). **Choice A** is incorrect because both statements I and II are correct. **Choices C** and **D** are incorrect because the stigma is not adapted for a mutualistic relationship with insects.  Only the flower benefits from the presence of a stigma."},{"ref":"BI0959","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0959\n\nThe cyclical variations in the population densities of a predator and its prey are shown in the image below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Populations%20%26%20Communities/images/BI0959.png\" alt=\"BI0959\">\n\nWhich of the following factors may have the least direct effect on the populations of the predator and prey species depicted here?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Pressure due to predation\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Reproductive rate of predators\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Food availability\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Weather conditions","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe weather (**Choice D**)does not play a significant role in the growth of the predator-prey population but could influence it. It may impact the behaviour or visibility of the predator or prey indirectly. The other factors play a more prominent role.\n\nPredators sometimes exert a significant and direct influence on prey populations by consuming them, decreasing the prey population.\nAs the predator population increases, the predation pressure (**Choice A**) on the prey increases, leading to a decline in the prey population. The reproductive rates of predators and prey play a role in the growth of the populations. The prey usually has a higher reproductive rate (**Choice B**). The availability of food impacts both predator and prey. A decrease in the prey population will cause a decline in the predator population, leading to cyclical variations in predator and prey population densities (**Choice C**)."},{"ref":"BI0960","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0960\n\nFrom the statements listed, which can be considered true of invasive but not native species?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species that have evolved and adapted to specific conditions\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species mainly found in a specific region\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species that are a significant threat to biodiversity worldwide\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species that are more vulnerable to environmental changes","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nInvasive species are introduced deliberately or accidentally to new areas outside of their native range, and so have no natural predators.  An example would be the zebra mussel which originates from the lakes of Russia and Ukraine.  It was accidentally introduced to the Great Lakes of the US through the discharge of a contaminated cargo ship's ballast water. Invasive species have been recognised as a major threat to biodiversity and ecosystems worldwide. They can disrupt native ecosystems, outcompete native species for resources, and lead to declines in native populations. Therefore **Choice C** is correct.\nWhile it is true that some invasive species have evolved and adapted to specific conditions (**Choice A**) in their native range, this characteristic is also true of native species. Invasive species often have a native region (**Choice B**), as do native species. Also, some invasive species have a broad native distribution, and when they are introduced to new areas, they can spread widely. Invasive species can be influenced by environmental changes (**Choice D**), but native species can also be affected by environmental changes, and vulnerability to these changes can vary widely among both invasive and native species."},{"ref":"BI0961","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0961\n\nWhich statement(s) describes allelopathy in plants?\n\n-    I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Allelopathy is the process by which a plant releases secondary metabolites that inhibit the growth of other plants.\n-    II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ Allelopathy is the presence of mechanical defences such as thorns and spines in plants.\n-    III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ Allelopathy may involve a decreased cell elongation rate in one plant species in response to a chemical secreted by another plant species.\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statements I** and **III** describe the process of allelopathy, which is\nthe release of secondary metabolites by plants to prevent the growth and development of other plants. The decreased rate of cell elongation is one of the physiological effects that can occur as a result of allelopathic interactions. Therefore **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because it only includes one true statement.\n**Statement II** is incorrect because, in addition to chemical defences, some plants may have mechanical defences, like thorns which provide physical protection against herbivores. **Choices B** and **C** are incorrect as they include this statement."},{"ref":"BI0962","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0962\n\nWhat is the correct description of 'interspecific competition’?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Interactions between predator and prey\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Individuals of the same species competing for limited resources\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Individuals of different species competing for limited resources\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Individuals of different species cooperating for limited resources","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCompetition between different species in the same ecosystem (**Choice C**) is referred to as interspecific competition. Different species may compete for resources such as food, water and shelter.\n\nIndividuals of the same species competing for resources (**Choice B**) is ‘intraspecific\nCompetition.’  **Choice A** refers to predator-prey interactions and is not any type of competition. Individuals of different species cooperating for limited resources (**Choice D**) describes a mutualistic relationship, not a competitive one."},{"ref":"BI0963","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0963\n\nWhich of the following does **NOT** apply to predator-prey relationships?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Density-dependent control of the population\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Negative feedback control of predator and prey populations\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Interspecific competition between predator and prey\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Predators and prey occupy different trophic levels in an ecosystem","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nInterspecific competition (**Choice C**) refers to competition between different species for the same resources. In predator-prey relationships, the interactions are primarily based on predation and consumption of one species by another, rather than competition for the same resources. Therefore this statement is not true of predator-prey relationships, and **Choice C** is the correct answer.\n\nPredator and prey living in the same ecosystem leads to density-dependent control of their populations (**Choice A**). As prey populations increase, predator populations may also increase due to the availability of more food. However, as predator populations increase, they put more pressure on prey populations, which can lead to a decrease in prey numbers. **Choice A** is therefore true of predator-prey relationships and so not the correct answer.\n\nNegative feedback control occurs when changes in one population lead to changes in the other population that oppose the initial change. Increases in the prey population will lead to an increase in the predator population, which then causes a decrease in the prey population, leading to a reduction in the predator population. The population sizes of both predator and prey are maintained by negative feedback loops. **Choice B** is therefore true of predator-prey relationships and so not the correct answer.\n\nPredators are typically higher in the food chain than their prey, so they usually occupy different trophic levels. **Choice D** is therefore true of predator-prey relationships and so not the correct answer."},{"ref":"BI0964","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0964\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nTwo different mesocosms were used for a study to understand the population dynamics of two ecosystems, one without fish (C ) and one with fish (F). Each mesocosm had the following added to it: two different species of snails and the same (one) species of phytoplankton. The snails feed on the phytoplankton and are, in turn, preyed upon by the fish.\n\nThe following diagrams show the mesocosms used for the study. C is the control without fish, and F is the experimental mesocosm with one fish added. Both mesocosms were maintained at the same temperature and light conditions.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Populations%20%26%20Communities/images/mesocosms%20phytoplankton%2C%20snails%2C%20fish%20black%20and%20white%20created%20in%20BR.png\" alt=\"mesocosms phytoplankton, snails, fish black and white created in BR\">\n\nAfter a period of time, as shown in the graph below, the density of phytoplankton was seen to vary in the two mesocosms used in the study.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Populations%20%26%20Communities/images/BI0964B.png\" alt=\"BI0964B\">\n[Source: adapted from Cheng, H., Feng, M., Zhang, P., Zhang, H., Wang, H., Xu, J., & Zhang, M. (2022, December 30). *Effects of warming on aquatic snails and periphyton in freshwater ecosystems with and without predation by common carp*. MDPI. Retrieved January 10, 2023, from https://www.mdpi.com/2073-4441/15/1/153, Copyright under CC-BY 4.0]\n\n1. Outline a reason for the variation seen in the density of the phytoplankton in the two mesocosms, as shown above. [1 mark]\n\n2. Explain whether this is indicative of a top-down or bottom–up control of the simulated ecosystem. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the snails are eaten by the fish in mesocosm F, and therefore there is an increase in phytoplankton density [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in the absence of fish, as in mesocosm C, the snails eat the phytoplankton, and the density of phytoplankton is controlled and remains lower than in mesocosm F. [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n In **mesocosm F**, the **snails are eaten by the fish** ($\\checkmark$); therefore, the phytoplankton density is higher than in mesocosm C.\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the fish control the abundance **OR** density of the snail, which controls the density of the producer [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ this is indicative of a top-down control [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in the case of bottom-up control, with increasing light **OR** temperature **OR** nutrients, increasing algae will cause growth of more snails, thus more fish can be supported [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ no data on nutrient content in either mesocosm, so cannot determine if there are bottom-up limiting factors **OR** if there is bottom-up control [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n In mesocosm F, the **fish reduce** the abundance of **snails** ($\\checkmark$), and therefore phytoplankton density increases in the presence of the fish. This is indicative of **top-down control of the ecosystem** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0965","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0965\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nScientists conducted studies to understand interspecific foraging behaviour overlap in Fiji’s forest birds. They observed the foraging behaviour of three types of birds on the main island of Viti Levu: nectarivores, insectivores and omnivores. The recorded data on the vertical partitioning of the different birds are shown below.\n\nOn the island of Viti Levu, the box and whisker plots below show the range, interquartile range and median for the results obtained for three species of nectarivores, two species of insectivores and two species of omnivores.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Populations%20%26%20Communities/images/BI0965A.png\" alt=\"BI0965A\">\n[Source: Adapted from Naikatini, A. N., Keppel, G., Brodie, G., & Kleindorfer, S. (2022, March 18). *Interspecific competition and vertical niche partitioning in Fiji’s forest birds*. MDPI. Retrieved 1 July 2023 from https://doi.org/10.3390/d14030223 Copyright under CC-BY 4.0]\n\n1. Distinguish between the terms ‘intraspecific’ and ‘interspecific’.        [1 mark]\n\n2. Describe the differences seen in the foraging heights of the different species of nectarivores, as seen in the interquartile range shown in the box and whisker plots.    [2 marks]\n\n3. Outline the similarities in foraging heights of nectarivores, insectivores and omnivores, based on the interquartile range shown in the box and whisker plots.    [2 marks]\n\nFurther studies were carried out on the birds. The vertical foraging height of the Orange-breasted Myzomela honeyeater bird was recorded in the coastal forest in Leleuvia island, where they are the only honeyeater species, and on the main island of Viti Levu, where it lives alongside two other honeyeater species. The average height of the trees on Leleuvia was 15 m and on Viti Levu it was 26 m. The data is therefore normalised for comparison and shown as relative foraging height (foraging height/mean height of tree). The box and whisker plots show the range, interquartile range and median for the results when competitors are absent (Leleuvia island) and when competitors are present (Viti Levu).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Populations%20%26%20Communities/images/BI0965B.png\" alt=\"BI0965B\">\n[Source: Adapted from Naikatini, A. N., Keppel, G., Brodie, G., & Kleindorfer, S. (2022, March 18). *Interspecific competition and vertical niche partitioning in Fiji’s forest birds*. MDPI. Retrieved 1 July 2023 https://doi.org/10.3390/d14030223 Copyright under CC-BY 4.0]\n\n4. Discuss the foraging behaviour of the Myzomela honeyeater species with and without the presence of competing nectarivore bird species.        [1 mark]\n5. Comment on the challenges of obtaining data in field studies compared to laboratory experiments.        [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ intraspecific refers to interactions within the same species **WHEREAS** interspecific refers to interactions between two different species        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ intraspecific refers to interactions between the individuals of a species **WHEREAS** interspecific refers to interactions between individuals of different species     [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Intraspecific refers to **interactions** between individuals of the **same species**, whereas interspecific refers to **interactions** between individuals of **different species** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Myzomela species eat at the lower (median) heights **OR** between (approximately) 2 and 8 (+/- 1) m **OR** with a median of 5m  **AND** Giant Forest species at the higher (median) heights **OR** between 10 and 16 (+/- 1) m **OR** with a median of 12m        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is overlap in the foraging heights of Kikau species with Myzomela at its lower (median) heights **OR** between 6 and 8 (+/- 1) m **OR** between median and lower quartile, **OR** there is overlap in the foraging heights of Kikau species with the Giant Forest at its higher (median) heights **OR** between 9 and 10 (+/- 1) m **OR** between the median and upper quartile    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is no overlap of the interquartile range of foraging heights for Myzomela species and Giant Forest species     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ interquartile range for foraging heights of  Kikau species is narrower than those of Myzomela and Giant Forest **OR** interquartile range for foraging heights of  Giant Forest species is wider than those of  Myzomela and Kikau    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Populations%20%26%20Communities/images/BI0965ANSBi.png\" alt=\"BI0965ANSBi\">\n    [Source: Adapted from Naikatini, A. N., Keppel, G., Brodie, G., & Kleindorfer, S. (2022, March 18). *Interspecific competition and vertical niche partitioning in Fiji’s forest birds*. MDPI. Retrieved 1 July 2023 https://doi.org/10.3390/d14030223 Copyright under CC-BY 4.0]\n\n    The median foraging height of the **Myzomela** species is the **lowest, at 5m**, and the **Giant Forest** species is the **highest, at 12m** ($\\checkmark$). There is an **overlap** in the foraging heights of the **Kikau** species with the **Myzomela** species between the Kikau’s **median and lower quartile** and with the **Giant Forest** species between **9 and 10m** ($\\checkmark$).  There is **no overlap** of the interquartile range for the **foraging** heights of the **Myzomela** and the **Giant Forest species** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there are at least two species in each group that forage at different heights with no overlap in the interquartile range shown    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the species in each group at the lower heights forage from around 2 to 7 m, and the species at the higher heights from 7 or 8 to 15m in the interquartile range    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ starting from 2m, at least one species was feeding at every height for insectivores, nectarivores and omnivores when the range is included    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ interquartile range for species feeding at higher levels is wider than at the lower levels for all three groups            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ species appear to be  separated vertically to help with resource partitioning in each feeding group     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ median for species feeding at the highest part of the trees is about 12m for all 3 feeding groups\n[1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Populations%20%26%20Communities/images/BI0965ANSC.png\" alt=\"BI0965ANSC\">\n    [Source: Adapted from Naikatini, A. N., Keppel, G., Brodie, G., & Kleindorfer, S. (2022, March 18). *Interspecific competition and vertical niche partitioning in Fiji’s forest birds*. MDPI. Retrieved 1 July 2023 https://doi.org/10.3390/d14030223 Copyright under CC-BY 4.0]\n\n    At least **two species** in each group **forage at different heights** with **no overlap** in the **interquartile range** ($\\checkmark$). For each group, the interquartile range for species feeding at the **highest levels** of the tree is **wider** than those feeding at the lowest levels ($\\checkmark$). The median for species feeding at the **highest levels** is **12m** for all three feeding groups ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in the absence of competing nectivore species, Myzomela has a much broader range of relative foraging height **OR** ranges from 0.05 to 0.92 (+/- 0.02) **OR** has a range of 0.87 (+/- 0.04) **OR** has an interquartile range of 0.13 - 0.60 (+/- 0.02) OR 0.47 (+/- 0.04) in the presence of competitors, Myzomela has a much narrower range of relative foraging heights **OR** ranges from 0.04 to 0.45 (+/- 0.02) **OR** has a range of 0.41 (+/- 0.04) **OR** has an interquartile range of 0.08 - 0.30 (+/- 0.02) **OR** 0.22 (+/- 0.04)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is overlap in the interquartile range of foraging heights with and without competitors [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ interspecific competition likely causes Myzomela to forage at lower heights in the presence of other species    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vertical niche partitioning may occur as a result of the  presence of more than one species of the bird    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Populations%20%26%20Communities/images/BI0965ANSD.png\" alt=\"BI0965ANSD\">\n    [Source: Adapted from Naikatini, A. N., Keppel, G., Brodie, G., & Kleindorfer, S. (2022, March 18). *Interspecific competition and vertical niche partitioning in Fiji’s forest birds*. MDPI. Retrieved 1 July 2023 from https://doi.org/10.3390/d14030223 Copyright under CC-BY 4.0]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The presence of competing species likely causes a **reduction in the height** ($\\checkmark$) at which Myzomela feeds.\n\n5. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in the laboratory, researchers have more control over variables and conditions, making it easier to replicate experiments [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in the field, uncontrollable factors can skew **OR** influence the results    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it is difficult to manipulate **OR** adjust the variables in the field, unlike the laboratory    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ could be more sampling errors in field conditions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ extrapolating results from sampling a small area to a large area is often inaccurate   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ field experiments need more planning in terms of locations    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ field experiments could be more expensive    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ weather changes could affect field experiments         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in the field, it is difficult to manage ethical concerns, unlike in the laboratory     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ equipment needs to be transported and is difficult to repair if needed in the field    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ more coordination with participants is needed for field experiments        [1 mark]\n\n     $\\ce{ -> }$ maybe no sighting of species being studied OR difficult to find species to be studied        [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **In the laboratory, researchers have more control** over variables and conditions, making it **easier to replicate experiments** ($\\checkmark$), whereas in the field, it is difficult to control confounding variables. **Field** experiments tend to be **more expensive** ($\\checkmark$) and need more planning, such as **identifying locations** where they will be carried out ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1209","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1209\n\nWhich statement is an example of intraspecific competition that could lead to evolution by natural selection?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sharks and dolphins eat the same types of fish\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Parasitic vines growing on tall kapok trees in the rainforest\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Roses and weeds grow in the same areas and compete for nutrients\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Male zebras fight over territory, and female partners","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIntraspecific competition is competition between organisms of the same species. Male zebras are from the same species and compete with each other over territory and mates; **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choices A** and **C** are incorrect as they describe interspecific competition, which is competition between organisms from different species. The growth of vines on tall kapok trees represents a parasitic relationship in which the vines derive nutrients and water from the kapok trees. This interaction does not involve intraspecific competition; **Choice B**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1317","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1317\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nA capture-mark-release-recapture method was used to determine the population size of bighorn sheep in the areas of Yellowstone National Park, Montana, USA.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Populations%20%26%20Communities/images/BI1317.png\" alt=\"BI1317\">\n[@ 2023]\n\nThe sheep were captured, tagged, and released. A week later, they were recaptured. The number of sheep caught and the number of sheep marked among the ones caught were recorded. The data obtained is shown in the following table.\n\n| Stage of Investigation | Number of sheep |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| First capture | 42 |\n| Second capture or recapture | 40 |\n| Marked in the second capture | 16 |\n\n1. Calculate the number of sheep likely to be living in this area. [2 marks]\n\n2. Evaluate the method used to calculate the number of sheep. [4 marks]\n\nIn Yellowstone National Park, there are numerous rivers and lakes where cutthroat trout are found. The capture-mark-release-recapture method has also been used to monitor the number of fish in lakes in the park. There are various programs to restore cutthroat trout populations in these water bodies in N. America. In one of these programs, cutthroat trout were introduced into a lake. The trout population was recorded in this lake over the next 10 years. The results are shown in the growth curve below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Populations%20%26%20Communities/images/BI1317b.png\" alt=\"BI1317b\">\n[@ 2023]\n\n3.\n 1. Outline what is happening to the population size during the phase marked X in the graph above. [1 mark]\n 2. Outline what is happening to the trout population, in regards to birth rate, death rate and resources, in section Y of the graph. [2 marks]\n4. In a lake with snails, fish, algae and phytoplankton, discuss how top-down control involving herbivory is different from bottom-up control. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. The following (calculation and right answer):\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ (42 $\\times$ 40) ÷ 16 [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ = 105 [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n | | |\n | :-- | :-- |\n | Number of bighorn sheep caught and marked initially | N:sub[1] = 42 |\n | Total number of sheep caught in the second capture |N:sub[2] = 40 |\n |Number of marked sheep recaptured | M:sub[2] = 16 |\n\n Estimated population size = $\\dfrac{N_{1} \\times N_{2}} {M_{2}}$\n\n $\\hspace{11.1em}$ = $\\dfrac{\\textbf{42} \\times \\textbf{40}} {\\textbf{16}}$ ($\\checkmark$)\n\n $\\hspace{11.1em}$ = **105** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2. Any **four** of the following (**one** point for advantage and **one** point for disadvantage)\n\n ***advantages*** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ provides a reasonable estimate size of the population [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can be used to study changes in population size over a period of time [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can be used to study the effectiveness of conservation strategies [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can be used to study a wide variety of animals, especially those that are motile [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it is cost-effective **OR** cheaper than other techniques **OR** aerial survey **OR** genetic analysis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can help study population structure [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can be used in a wide range of habitats [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ enables age **OR** sex **OR** mass **OR** other information to be recorded at same time [1 mark]\n\n ***disadvantages*** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ marking could be more visible in some animals and could lead to a biased recapture [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ could cause stress to animals being captured and marked [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ marking could be erased before the second capture making it difficult to estimate **OR** causing errors in recognising the marks [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ marking could increase risk of predation affecting accuracy of estimation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ does not account for births, deaths, immigration or emigration of animals in the area [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ not all species can be marked because of location, or size [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ if population is small, estimates could be less reliable [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ if individuals don’t mix randomly in the population, then it could lead to a biased estimate [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to account for seasonal variation in behaviour and distribution- an estimate needs to be done at different times of the year [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ assumption made that all animals have a similar chance of getting captured and recaptured **OR** animals may become habituated to marking so be easier **OR** harder to recapture [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ very time consuming **OR** can be difficult **OR** potentially dangerous [1 mark]\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The capture-mark-release-recapture method provides a reliable method of **estimation** of **size** of the **population** ($\\checkmark$), and can be used to study the **effectiveness** of strategies used for **conservation** ($\\checkmark$). Sometimes the **marking** could be **erased** ($\\checkmark$), or marking could **increase the chance** of **predation between** the **first capture**, release and the **second capture**, making it **difficult** to make an **accurate estimate** ($\\checkmark$) of the population size.\n\n3.\n 1. The following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ phase marked ‘X’ is the ‘log’ **OR** ‘exponential’ growth phase **OR** rapid growth of fish (trout) takes place [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The phase marked ‘X’ shows the exponential growth phase, and **rapid growth of the fish (trout) takes place** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 2. Any **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ when population is at carrying capacity birth and death rate stabilise over time because there are enough resources for the population but no excess [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as population numbers increase, competition for resources increases, so death rate is higher than birth rate, and population decreases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as population decreases, there is reduced competition for resources so birth rate is higher than death rate, leading to an increase in population size [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The population of trout is likely to fluctuate around the carrying capacity of the lake. When the population **numbers increase**, there is **more competition** for **resources**, causing the **death rate** to be **higher** than **birth rate** ($\\checkmark$) which decreases the size of the population to below carrying capacity.\n\n When the number of **fish decreases**, there is **less competition** for **resources**, birth rate is higher than death rate and **population size increases** ($\\checkmark$) (above that of carrying capacity).\n\n4. Any **three** of the following ( at least **one** from each section):\n\n ***top down control*** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ top-down control is when higher trophic level **OR** predator **OR** fish abundance **OR** behaviour influences **OR** controls the lower trophic levels abundance **OR** community structure [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fish feed on snails, which, in turn, feed on algae **OR** phytoplankton [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ high fish population means more snails consumed so less snails to feed on algae **OR** phytoplankton **AND** algae **OR** phytoplankton abundance increases **OR** decreased grazing pressure on algae **OR** phytoplankton [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ presence of top level predators **OR** fish helps maintain balance in ecosystem by preventing unchecked growth of herbivores **OR** snails, and so controlling algal populations [1 mark]\n\n ***bottom up control*** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ bottom-up control refers to regulation of abundance **OR** community structure of higher trophic levels **OR** fish by nutrient availability **OR** primary production **OR** resource limitation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ algal abundance is influenced **OR** limited by nutrient availability **OR** light **OR** other suitable factors [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ if nutrient availability increases it can promote growth of algal **OR** phytoplankton **OR** if nutrient availability decreases leads to reduced population size of algae **OR** phytoplankton [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ if nutrients are restricted it can lead to reduced populations of algae **OR** phytoplankton, which leads to reduction in population size of snails **OR** fish [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increased algal population, provides food source for snails so snail population increases providing more food for fish and fish population increase [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The difference between top-down control and bottom-up control is the direction of influence within a lake ecosystem. **Top-down** control occurs when **predators**, such as **fish**, exert **control** over **herbivores**, like **snails**, which, in turn, **indirectly** influence the abundance of **lower** trophic level **organisms**, including **algae** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n Conversely, **bottom up** control is characterised by the **dependence** of **fish** populations **on** environmental factors, such as **nutrient availability** ($\\checkmark$) which directly impacts the **growth** of primary producers such as **algae** ($\\checkmark$). The increased abundance of **algae** then **promotes** the growth of herbivores, like **snails** and **subsequently** affects other consumers, including **fish** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1324","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1324\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nPopulations within a community are interdependent due to the many interactions among organisms.\n\n1. Sketch a sigmoid population growth curve. Annotate each phase with the reason for the change in growth. [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline how the data needed to estimate population size using the Lincoln index are collected. [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain competition and cooperation using examples. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. the following:\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ axes suitably labelled **AND** sigmoid curve drawn [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ exponential phase: rapid growth because resources are plentiful **OR** little competition / predation / disease [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ transitional phase: growth rate slows as resources become limiting **OR** competition / predation / disease increase [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ plateau phase: population is stable because birth rate = death rate / birth + immigration = death + emigration **OR** carrying capacity / K is reached **OR** maximum population the environment can support [1 mark]\n\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Populations%20%26%20Communities/images/BI1324ams.png\" alt=\"BI1324ams\">\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ first sample of the population is captured **AND** counted [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ captured individuals are marked in a way that does not harm them / affect their behaviour / make them more visible to predators [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ marked individuals are released at the site where they were captured [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ time is allowed for marked individuals to mix randomly back into the population [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ second sample is captured from the same area / using the same method **AND** the total number counted [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ number of marked individuals in the second sample is counted [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ large samples are captured so that the estimate is more representative / less affected by chance **OR** procedure is repeated / several recaptures are carried out **AND** a mean estimate is calculated to improve reliability [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ one assumption stated **OR** no immigration / emigration / births / deaths between samples **OR** marks are not lost **OR** marked and unmarked individuals are equally likely to be caught [1 mark]\n\n [5 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n A **first sample** is **captured** and the number of individuals is **counted** ($\\checkmark$). Each individual is **marked** in a way that **does not harm** it or make it **more visible** to predators, and then **released** ($\\checkmark$). Time is allowed for the **marked individuals** to **mix back** into the population ($\\checkmark$). A **second sample** is then **captured**, and the **total number** is **counted**, along with the **number** of **marked individuals** ($\\checkmark$). To improve **reliability**, the process is **repeated** two more times, and the **mean is calculated** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** statement and **one** example for **both** competition **and** cooperation to gain full marks*)\n:\n\n ***Competition:*** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms compete for a limited resource such as food / space / light / water / mates [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ competition is a cost to one or both organisms because a resource used by one is not available to the other **OR** because it reduces survival / reproduction [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ intraspecific competition is between members of the same species **OR** interspecific competition is between members of different species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ named example of interspecific competition with the resource stated, e.g. grey squirrels compete with red squirrels for food and are more successful, so red squirrel populations decline **OR** any other named example [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ named example of intraspecific competition with the resource stated, e.g. plants of the same species competing for light **OR** male red deer competing for mates [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ competition limits population size **OR** acts as a density-dependent factor as the population nears carrying capacity [1 mark]\n\n ***Cooperation:*** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cooperation is when organisms work together so that they benefit [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cooperation increases the chance of survival **OR** reproduction [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ named example of intraspecific cooperation, e.g. wolves hunting in packs to catch larger prey **OR** worker bees caring for the colony’s young [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mutualism is an interspecific relationship where two species live in close association **AND** both benefit [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ named example of mutualism with the benefit to both partners stated, e.g. *zooxanthellae* provide hard corals with products of photosynthesis and the coral provides a protected habitat **OR** *Rhizobium* in the root nodules of legumes fixes nitrogen and receives sugars **OR** mycorrhizal fungi supply minerals to orchids and receive sugars [1 mark]\n\n [7 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Competition happens when organisms need the same **limited resource**, such as **food, space or mates** ($\\checkmark$). It is a **cost** to one or both organisms, because resources used by one are not available to the other, which reduces survival or reproduction ($\\checkmark$). Competition can be **intraspecific**, between members of the **same species**, or **interspecific**, between members of **different species** ($\\checkmark$). For example, grey squirrels **compete with red squirrels for food**. Grey squirrels are more successful, so red squirrel populations have declined where grey squirrels have been introduced ($\\checkmark$).\n\n Cooperation is when organisms **work together** so that they **benefit** ($\\checkmark$). Mutualism is when organisms of **two species** live in **close association** and **both benefit** ($\\checkmark$). For example, ***zooxanthellae*** live inside **hard corals** and provide them with **nutrients** made by photosynthesis, while the coral provides the *zooxanthellae* with a **protected habitat** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1325","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1325\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe diagram below shows a sigmoid population curve for duckweed over a period of 5 weeks.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Populations%20%26%20Communities/images/BI1325.png\" alt=\"BI1325\">\n\n1.\n 1. Name the type of growth labelled ‘X’ on the graph.\n[1 mark]\n\n 2. Suggest possible reasons for the population change that is happening at ‘X’ in the graph. [3 marks]\n\nDifferent sampling methods are needed for motile and non-motile organisms. \\\nA biologist used the capture-mark-release-recapture method to collect the data below for the purpose of applying the Lincoln index to estimate the size of a snail population.\n\n| Snails captured initially | Snails recaptured | Number of recaptured snails marked |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| 140 | 120 | 70 |\n\n2. Use the Lincoln index to estimate the snail population size. Show your working. [2 marks]\n\n3.\n 1. Explain intraspecific competition using snails or another named species as an example. [2 marks]\n\n 2. Compare and contrast interspecific and intraspecific competition. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1.\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ exponential growth phase [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n X is the **exponential growth phase** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ high reproductive rate **OR** primarily reproduces asexually [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms have adapted to the environment [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ resources are available **OR** resources such as nutrients **OR** water **OR** space are available **OR** there could be little or no competition for resources **OR** abundant nutrients **OR** little competition for resources **OR** nutrients **OR** space [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ possibly due to absence of disease [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ possibly due to absence of predators [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature **OR** pH is suitable for growth [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ plenty of sunlight [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ absence of pollution **OR** herbicides, **OR** tolerance of plant to pollution **OR** herbicides [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n At point X population size could be rapidly increasing because the **temperature is suitable** ($\\checkmark$) and **resources** such as **water, nutrients** and **space** are **available** ($\\checkmark$). At point X there could also be an **absence of disease** ($\\checkmark$) and **predators** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Correct formula must be used **and** correct answer must be given to get full marks:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ $\\dfrac{140 \\times 120} {70}$ [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 240 [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n Estimated population size = $\\dfrac{\\textbf{Number of individuals marked initially} \\times \\textbf{Number of individuals recaptured}} {\\textbf{Number of recaptured individuals that are marked}}$ ($\\checkmark$)\n\n Estimated population size = $\\dfrac{\\textbf{140} \\times \\textbf{120}} {\\textbf{70}}$ ($\\checkmark$)\n\n $\\hspace{11.1em}$ = **240** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n3.\n 1. Answer **must** include **one** mark for defining intraspecific competition and **one** mark for an example:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ intraspecific competition is competition between individuals of the same species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ snails **OR** other named species competing for space [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ snails **OR** other named species competing for food **OR** nutrients [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ snails **OR** other named species competing for mating **OR** reproduction competition [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ any other reasonable example of intraspecific competition [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Intraspecific **competition is competition between individuals of the same species** ($\\checkmark$). Snails compete for **food** ($\\checkmark$) and **space** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 2. Answer must include **one** similarity and **one** difference:\n\n *Similarities* \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in **BOTH** intraspecific competition **AND** interspecific competition organisms are competing for resources **OR** food **OR** nutrients **OR** shelter **OR** space **OR** light [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** types of competition help regulate the population sizes **OR** structure within ecosystem **OR** influence distribution **OR** abundance of species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can influence fitness of population **OR** drive natural selection by favouring individuals with advantageous traits [1 mark]\n\n *Differences* \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ intraspecific competition is competition between individuals of the same species **WHEREAS** interspecific is competition between individuals of different species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ competition for mates **ONLY** happens in intraspecific competition **OR** between individuals of the same species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ interspecific competition can lead to niche differentiation **OR** impact species diversity in an ecosystem **WHEREAS** intraspecific competition primarily affects the population dynamics of a single species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ competitive exclusion principle **OR** one species excluding another applies to interspecific but **NOT** to intraspecific competition [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Intraspecific competition** is competition between individuals of the **same species** whereas **interspecific** competition is between individuals of **different species** ($\\checkmark$). Individuals **compete for resources in both** ($\\checkmark$) intraspecific and interspecific competition."},{"ref":"BI1459","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1459\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nBelow is a graph representing the population size of a bacteria colony.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Populations%20%26%20Communities/images/growth%20curve%20copy%20ii.png\" alt=\"growth curve copy ii\">\n\nDescribe what is happening at stages X and Y on the graph. [4 marks]","markScheme":"Any **four** of the following (there must be at least **one** statement for X and **one** statement for Y to achieve full marks)\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ X shows exponential growth **OR** the log phase [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ at X bacteria are dividing at the maximum rate **OR** natality exceeds mortality [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ at X there are plenty of nutrients **OR** plenty of resources **OR** little competition [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ at X temperature **OR** pH **OR** oxygen levels are optimal [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ Y is the transition phase of the growth curve [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ at Y mortality rate is almost as high as natality rate [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ at Y essential nutrients have been consumed **OR** reduction in nutrients available **OR** less resources **OR** competition has increased [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ at Y metabolic wastes **OR** toxins have accumulated [1 mark]\n\n[4 marks]"},{"ref":"BI1578","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1578\n\nEastern Egg Rock Island, Maine is home to a population of Atlantic puffins (*Fratercula arctica*). The colony was reintroduced to the island in 1973 by translocating six chicks from Newfoundland. The Audubon society has recorded the number of breeding pairs for more than fifty years, some of the results are provided in the table.\n\n| Year | Number of Breeding Pairs |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| 1981 | 4 |\n| 2001 | 37 |\n| 2005 | 71 |\n| 2013 | 111|\n| 2015 | 150 |\n| 2017 | 172 |\n| 2019 | 188 |\n| 2021 | 180 |\n\nWhich of the following statements does the data support?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Puffins exhibit sexual selection.|\n    | II. | The puffin population exhibited sigmoid growth following reintroduction.|\n    | III. | The puffin population has stabilised near the carrying capacity of the environment. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because the puffin population has risen sharply before becoming stable in recent years, characteristic of sigmoid growth (**Statement II**). The recent slight decline in the number of breeding pairs suggests that the population has begun to stabilise near the environment’s carrying capacity, leading to the death of some puffins and an overall plateau in population growth (**Statement III**).\n\n**Statement I** is likely to be true, but the data does not describe sexual selection taking place in the puffin population. **Choice A** is incorrect because it is missing **Statement II**. **Choices B** and **D** are incorrect because they include **Statement I**."},{"ref":"BI2920","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2920\n\nA team of ecologists used random sampling to estimate the population of rare beetle species in a temperate deciduous forest.  Their estimated population size was 5,000 beetles.  The true population size is 5,352 beetles.\nWhat does the difference between these two numbers represent?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Systematic error in the sampling method.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Bias introduced by non-random sampling.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Natural variation is expected in population density.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sampling error is inherent in the estimation process.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because the difference between the estimated population size (5,000 beetles) and the true population size (5,352 beetles) represents sampling error, which is an inherent part of any estimation process using a sample. This discrepancy arises because a sample can only approximate the true population size, and the result will vary due to random chance.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because systematic error refers to consistent, repeatable measurement errors due to flaws in the methodology or equipment. For example, if the measuring tape used to mark a quadrat were stretched, the quadrat size would be incorrect, leading to over- or underestimates in every measurement taken. This is not the case here, as random sampling was used.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because bias arises from non-random sampling, which could skew the results by disproportionately representing certain individuals or areas. For example, if quadrats are placed only in easily accessible areas (like near a path), the sample would not represent the entire area. In this case, random sampling was used, so bias is not the issue.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because natural variation refers to fluctuations in population density over time due to environmental factors (e.g., light or water availability) and is not the cause of the discrepancy between the estimated and true population sizes in a stable population. A sufficiently large sample size should help minimise the effect of such natural variation in the estimate."},{"ref":"BI2921","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2921\n\nEcologists are comparing the population distribution of two different plant species, X and Y, using random quadrat sampling.  They calculate the mean number of individuals per quadrat and the standard deviation for both species.\n\n| | Density (plants/quadrat) |Standard Deviation |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| **Species X** | 15 | 2 |\n| **Species Y** | 15 | 10 |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species X has a larger total population size than Species Y.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species Y is more evenly distributed throughout the sampled area than Species X.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species X shows less variation in population density over the sampled area compared to Species Y.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Both species have the same population density, so their distribution patterns must be identical.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because standard deviation measures how spread out the data are around the mean. A smaller standard deviation indicates that the quadrat counts are more tightly clustered around the mean, suggesting that Species X has a more uniform distribution of individuals across the sampled area. In contrast, Species Y has a larger standard deviation, meaning the counts are more spread out and show greater variation, implying a less uniform or more clumped distribution.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the mean population density for both species is the same (15 plants per quadrat), so this does not indicate any difference in total population size between the two species.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because Species Y has a larger standard deviation than Species X, which actually indicates greater variation in its distribution, meaning it is less evenly spread out.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because even though the two species have the same mean density, their distribution patterns can differ, as reflected in the different standard deviations. A species can have the same mean but different patterns of distribution (e.g., clumped vs. uniform)."},{"ref":"BI2923","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2923\n\nA student grows a yeast culture to study a sigmoid growth curve.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Populations%20%26%20Communities/images/2923.png\" alt=\"2923\">\n\nWhich label identifies the stage during which the availability of glucose in the culture becomes a primary limiting factor for the yeast population's growth?","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because it refers to the decelerating phase of the sigmoid growth curve, where the growth rate starts to slow as resources like glucose become limited. In this phase, density-dependent factors, such as competition for nutrients, begin to have a noticeable impact on the population's growth, causing the curve to flatten as the population approaches its carrying capacity.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it represents the lag phase, during which the population is adjusting to the new environment and growth is slow due to factors like adaptation to the environment. There is not yet a significant resource limitation.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because it refers to the log phase (also known as the exponential phase), where the population is growing at its fastest rate. In this phase, resources are still relatively abundant, and the graph shows the steepest slope.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because it corresponds to the stationary phase, where the population has levelled off after reaching carrying capacity. At this point, the birth rate and death rate are approximately equal, and resources cannot support further growth."},{"ref":"BI2924","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2924\n\nWhich of the following would be considered part of a forest's ecological community?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Deer, wolves, rabbits|\n    | II. | Oak trees, ferns, mosses |\n    | III. | Detritus on the forest floor |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA community comprises all the interacting populations, which are living organisms, in a given area.\n\n**Statement I** is true because it lists living animals which are part of the community.\\\n**Statement II** is true because it lists living plants which are also a part of the community. \\\n**Statement III** is not true because detritus is dead organic matter.  Even though it originated from living organisms and is considered part of the ecosystem, it is not considered part of the community.\n\n**Choice B** is correct because it includes true **Statements I** and **II** and does not include untrue **Statement III**.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it does not include true **Statement II**.\\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because it does not include true **Statement I**, and it includes untrue **Statement III**.\\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because it includes the untrue **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI2925","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2925\n\nWhich of the following statements describes the mutualistic relationship between mycorrhizal fungi and orchid plants?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | The fungi provide the orchid with nutrients essential for germination and early growth. |\n    | II. | The orchid supplies the fungi with carbohydrates produced through photosynthesis. |\n    | III. | The fungi perform nitrogen fixation for the orchid, converting atmospheric nitrogen into usable ammonia. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because orchid seeds are small and lack energy reserves to support germination and early development.  The seeds rely on mycorrhizal fungi to provide these initial nutrients.\\\n**Statement II** is true.  The orchid is photosynthetic and provides the fungi with carbohydrates in return for the nutrients and water relayed through the fungi to the orchid.\\\n**Statement III** is not true because nitrogen fixation is carried out primarily by bacteria like Rhizobium in legume root nodules.  While mycorrhizal fungi enhance nitrogen and phosphorus uptake, they do not perform nitrogen fixation.\n\n**Choice C** is correct because it contains true **Statements I** and **II** and does not contain the untrue **Statement III**.\\\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it does not include true **Statement II**.\\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because it does not include true **Statement I**.\\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because it includes the untrue **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI2926","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2926\n\nMexican petunia (*Ruellia simplex*), introduced from Mexico in the 1930s as an ornamental flower, is a favourite among Florida gardeners. Its hardiness, rapid growth, and attractive purple flowers make it a popular choice. Gardeners appreciate its ability to outcompete weeds due to its fast growth, dense clusters, and vast root structure, which effectively suppresses other, less desirable plants in a garden setting.\n\nBased on this description, how would Ruellia simplex be classified?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Endemic\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Native\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Opportunistic\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Invasive","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because the Mexican petunia was introduced from Mexico, and it suppresses the growth of other plants.  These characteristics describe it as outcompeting other plants for resources.  Being introduced to a new region and outcompeting native and other species are the two defining features of an invasive species.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because endemic species are native species found only in one geographic region.\\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the Mexican petunia was introduced to Florida and is therefore not native.\\\n**Choice C**  is incorrect because the description goes beyond that of an opportunistic species, which may take advantage of a disturbed environment, and it highlights aggressive, competitive behaviour that actively suppresses the growth of other plants."},{"ref":"BI2927","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2927\n\nA group of students surveyed several forest plots, recording the presence or absence of two plant species.  They then used a chi-squared test to analyse their data.  What is the purpose of performing a chi-squared test in this investigation?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ To determine if there's a significant difference in the allele frequencies of the two species across the plots.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ To determine the statistical relationship between the distribution of the two species.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ To establish a causal link between the distribution of the two species.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ To predict future population trends and estimate the carrying capacity for each species.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because the chi-squared test for association is used to determine if the observed distribution of the two species is statistically different from what would be expected by chance, indicating a statistically significant association.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the chi-squared test for association is not used to analyse allele frequencies.  This distractor was designed to be confused with Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium.\\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because the chi-squared test for association does not indicate a direct cause-and-effect relationship.  This would require more experimental data and analysis.\\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the data collection is a snapshot of current distribution patterns and cannot provide any longitudinal data.  Population growth models would be needed to predict future population trends."},{"ref":"BI2928","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2928\n\nThe graph below illustrates the population dynamics of Species X and Species Y over several years in a stable ecosystem.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Populations%20%26%20Communities/images/2928.png\" alt=\"2928\">\n\nY as represented in the graph?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species X is the prey, species Y is the predator.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Both Species X and Species Y are experiencing density-independent population control.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species X and Species Y are engaged in competitive exclusion.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species X is the parasite, and Species Y is its host.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because the graph illustrates the characteristic predator-prey cycle, where the population peak of Species Y (the predator) follows the peak of Species X (the prey). In a predator-prey dynamic, an increase in the prey population leads to a rise in the predator population. As the predator population grows, it exerts pressure on the prey, causing a subsequent decline in the prey population. This, in turn, leads to a decline in the predator population. This cyclic relationship is driven by density-dependent factors, specifically predation.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the cyclical nature of the graph is indicative of density-dependent factors, such as predation, where one population directly influences the other. Density-independent factors (e.g., weather, natural disasters) would not produce such predictable fluctuations.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because competitive exclusion refers to one species outcompeting and driving the other to extinction, which is not represented in the graph. Instead, both species are fluctuating in a cyclical manner without one being eliminated.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because if Species X were a parasite, its population would typically follow the decline of the host species. In a parasite-host relationship, the host population (in this case, Species Y) would typically be larger and experience a decline only after the parasite population increases, which is not reflected in the graph."},{"ref":"BI2930","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2930\n\nFarmers often use rye, planting it before their main cash crop, as it suppresses weed growth in their fields by releasing chemicals.  Which term best describes this biological interaction?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Parasitism\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Commensalism\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Antibody secretion\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Allelopathy","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because allelopathy refers to the biological interaction where a plant releases chemical compounds that inhibit the growth of other plants. In this case, the rye suppresses weed growth by releasing chemicals, making allelopathy the best description of this interaction.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because parasitism involves one organism benefiting at the expense of another, often by living on or inside the host to derive nutrients. The rye does not derive nutrients from the weeds, so this interaction does not fit the definition of parasitism.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because commensalism refers to a relationship where one organism benefits, and the other is neither harmed nor helped. In the case of the rye and the weeds, the weeds are being harmed by the chemicals released by the rye, which makes commensalism an inappropriate choice.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because antibody secretion refers to the production of antibodies by an organism to defend against pathogens. While allelopathy is a form of antibiosis (chemical defence), it specifically applies to plants and the release of chemicals to inhibit the growth of other plants, which makes allelopathy the more accurate term in this context."}]},{"name":"Transfers of Energy & Matter","questions":[{"ref":"BI0103","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0103\n\nWoodlice, earthworms, sea cucumbers and springtails feed on the remains of dead plants and animals.\n\nWhat term describes the mode of nutrition of these organisms?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Autotrophs\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mixotrophs\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Detritivores\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Saprotrophs","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C**  is correct because these organisms feed on detritus (dead plant and animal remains) by ingesting it (taking it into the body) and digesting it internally. Earthworms, woodlice, sea cucumbers and springtails are all examples of detritivores.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because autotrophs make their own organic molecules from inorganic substances (e.g., photosynthesis in plants and chemosynthesis in some bacteria). The organisms listed are feeding on existing organic matter, so they are not autotrophs.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because mixotrophs are organisms that obtain energy and nutrients through both autotrophic **and** heterotrophic modes of nutrition. The named organisms in the question can only obtain nutrients through consuming their food.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because saprotrophs (e.g., many fungi and bacteria) feed on dead organic matter by external digestion: they secrete enzymes onto the material, break it down outside the body, and then absorb the soluble products. The named organisms here feed by ingestion, not external digestion and absorption."},{"ref":"BI0105","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0105\n\nThe illustration shows a rice cultivation system that eliminates the need for fertilizers, herbicides and pesticides by using ducks in the organic cultivation of rice. In addition, *Azolla*, an aquatic floating fern, is grown on these fields because it harbours blue-green algae which can fix atmospheric nitrogen. Using this system, rice, ducks, and fish can all be produced for human consumption in one location.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/Picture%201.png\" alt=\"Picture 1\">\n\nWhich option includes two of the autotrophs in the system?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ *Azolla* and rice\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ *Azolla* and ducks\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Rice and fish\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Fish and ducks","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDucks and fish are consumers (**Choice B, C and D**), which is a type of heterotroph, whereas *Azolla* and rice can carry out photosynthesis and are, therefore, autotrophs (self-feeding)."},{"ref":"BI0112","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0112\n\nWhich of the following statements is a correct description of a food chain?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The most abundant organisms in a food chain are the secondary consumers.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Autotrophs are producers and form the base of the food chain.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Energy enters a food chain via tertiary consumers.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The flow of energy is from secondary consumers to primary consumers to producers.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAutotrophs are producers and form the base of food chains. They carry out photosynthesis and get their energy from the Sun.\n\nThe most abundant organisms (**Choice A**) are usually the producers but there are exceptions.\nEnergy always enters the food chain via the autotrophs, not consumers (**Choice C**).\n\nThe flow of energy (**Choice D**) is from producers to primary to secondary to tertiary consumers."},{"ref":"BI0113","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0113\n\nThe illustration shows a rice cultivation system that eliminates the need for fertilizers, herbicides and pesticides by raising ducks in rice paddies. Additionally, *Azolla*, an aquatic floating fern, is grown in these fields because they harbour blue-green algae that fixes N$_2$. This system produces duck, fish and rice for human consumption.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/Picture%201.png\" alt=\"Picture 1\">\n\nWhat would be a possible food chain for the rice cultivation system shown in the image?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ algae $\\rightarrow$ fish $\\rightarrow$ duck $\\rightarrow$ human\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ algae $\\leftarrow$ fish $\\leftarrow$ duck $\\leftarrow$ human\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ insects $\\rightarrow$ algae $\\rightarrow$ duck $\\rightarrow$ human\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ insects $\\leftarrow$ algae $\\leftarrow$ duck $\\leftarrow$ human","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAlgae is a producer, (**Choice B**), but the arrows show the flow of energy in the wrong direction.\nThe food chain cannot start with insects (**Choice C**) as it is not an autotroph. Algae do not eat ducks and ducks do not eat humans (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0114","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0114\n\nWhich of the following statements is **NOT** a **TRUE** description of energy flow in terrestrial ecosystems?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Trophic relationships between plants and animals do not exist as a simple chain, but tend to be more elaborate and interwoven as a food web.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ In food webs, different herbivores may feed on the same plant. Also, the same species of herbivore may feed on different plants.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Some consumers that feed on the dead remains of plants and animals are called detritivores.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ In food webs, a typical food chain consists of approximately 6 organisms.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is not a true statement as typically, food chains in ecosystems tend not to be very long due to energy loss at each trophic level. Approximately 90% of the energy is lost through metabolic processes and as heat at each level, leaving only about 10% available to the next level. This energy loss limits the number of trophic levels (and hence the number of organisms in a straightforward food chain) to usually fewer than 6. Most food chains have between 3 and 5 trophic levels.\n\n**Choice A** is a true statement. In nature, trophic relationships (i.e., feeding relationships between organisms) often form complex networks rather than simple, linear chains. These networks are termed food webs, and they illustrate the multiple feeding connections (who eats whom) in ecosystems.\n\n**Choice B** is a true statement. Food webs demonstrate the diversity of feeding relationships in an ecosystem. It is common for multiple herbivores to feed on the same plant species and for individual herbivores to consume various types of plants. This flexibility increases the stability and resilience of the ecosystem.\n\n**Choice C** is a true statement. Detritivores, such as earthworms and certain insects, play a critical role in ecosystems by breaking down dead organic material. This process helps in nutrient cycling and energy flow through the ecosystem, facilitating decomposition and the release of nutrients back into the soil for use by plants."},{"ref":"BI0115","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0115\n\nWhich of the organisms in the food web below is both a secondary and a tertiary consumer?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/Picture%201.png\" alt=\"Picture 1\">\n[Source: Cmglee. (2003, October). *File: Arctic Food Web.svg - Wikimedia Commons*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved April 14, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Arctic_food_web.svg. Copyright under CC BY-SA 3.0]\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Harbour seal\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Polar bear\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Arctic cod\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Capelin","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nArctic cod feeds on herbivorous zooplankton which is a primary consumer which makes Arctic cod a secondary consumer.  Arctic cod also consumes carnivorous zooplankton which is a secondary consumer, making Arctic cod both a tertiary and secondary consumer.\n\nHarbour seals (**Choice A**), polar bears (**Choice B**) and capelins (**Choice D**) are all tertiary consumers, not secondary consumers."},{"ref":"BI0116","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0116\n\nWhich of the following diagrams shows the flow of energy in an ecosystem having four trophic levels? The size of each level represents energy values (in kJm$^{-2}$yr$^{-1}$) in the energy pyramids below.\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A. | | | |B.|\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/Picture%201.png\" alt=\"Picture A\">| | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/Picture%201.png\" alt=\"Picture B\">|\n|C. | | | |D. |\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/Picture%201.png\" alt=\"Picture 1\">| | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/Picture%201.png\" alt=\"Picture D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn an ecosystem, energy pyramids illustrate how energy decreases as it moves through trophic levels. Producers have the most energy, converting sunlight into chemical energy through photosynthesis. Primary consumers gain only about 10% of the energy from producers, and this pattern continues up the trophic levels, with only 10% of energy passing to the next level.\n**Choice C** correctly represents this, with the base (producers) widest and each successive level narrower.\n\n**Choice A** is upside down.\n**Choice B** has the primary consumer level too narrow.\n**Choice D** has incorrect secondary and tertiary consumer levels."},{"ref":"BI0117","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0117\n\nThe figure below shows the flow of energy per unit area per year in arbitrary units. Gross production is the total kJ obtained through photosynthesis, and net production is the kJ remaining after respiration.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/bi0117.png\" alt=\"bi0117\">\n\nWhat percentage of net production (kJ after respiration) is lost to decomposers by primary consumers?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 100%\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 75%\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 50%\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 25%","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe primary consumers lose 1200 kJ out of 1600 kJ to decomposers, which translates to 75%."},{"ref":"BI0118","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0118\n\nThe figure below shows the flow of energy per area per year in an ecosystem.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/bi0117.png\" alt=\"bi0117\">\n\nAproximately what percentage of energy from the producers reaches the tertiary consumers?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 100%\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 10%\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 1%\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.1%","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAs energy moves from producers to consumers, only about 10% is transferred to the next trophic level. So only about 0.1 % of the energy produced by the producers reaches the tertiary consumers. All other choices (**Choice A, B, C**) are not correct. Looking at the values in the diagram, producers start with 20 000 units of energy and tertiary consumers receive only 25 units. 25 divided by 20 000 is approximately 0.1%."},{"ref":"BI0119","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0119\n\nWhich of the following statements are **true**?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | Living organisms release energy from organic compounds and convert it to heat during the process of respiration. |\n  | II. | Since heat cannot be directly transformed into other forms of energy by the organisms it is lost from ecosystems. |\n  | III. | Light energy from the Sun is converted to chemical energy by producers and then converted to heat during respiration. |\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAll the statements above are true. In most ecosystems, light energy from the Sun is captured by producers via photosynthesis and transferred to consumers through feeding. As organisms respire, they lose energy in the form of heat which cannot be reused by organisms. There is a constant input of energy from the Sun."},{"ref":"BI0120","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0120\n\nWhat units are typically used for pyramids of energy?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Calories\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Kilocalories\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ kJm$^{-2}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ kJm$^{-2}$y$^{-1}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCalories (**Choice A**) and Kilocalories (**Choice B**) are measures for energy but do not include area and time which is how productivity is measured in an ecosystem.  kJm$^{-2}$ (**Choice C**) does include the area but not time. To see energy values over a specified time, usually a year, the units kJm$^{-2}$y$^{-1}$ are used."},{"ref":"BI0121","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0121\n\nWhich of the following correctly describes how energy is lost from ecosystems?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Lost as light\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Lost as heat\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Lost as carbon dioxide\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Lost as chemical energy","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWhen organisms respire, they lose energy in the form of heat. This form of energy cannot be recovered and is finally lost from the system.\n\nInitially, during photosynthesis, energy is captured from light (**Choice A**). Carbon dioxide is not a form of energy (**Choice C**), and chemical energy (**Choice D**) is held in the bonds of organic molecules made during photosynthesis."},{"ref":"BI0122","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0122\n\n**Maximum mark: 2**\n\nThe food web below shows the feeding relationships in a soil ecosystem.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/BI0122.png\" alt=\"BI0122\">\n[Adapted from Ingham, E. R. (n.d.). *Natural Resources Conservation Service*. Soil Food Web | NRCS Soils. Retrieved April 16, 2022, from https://www.nrcs.usda.gov/wps/portal/nrcs/detailfull/soils/health/biology/?cid=nrcs142p2_053868\nCopyright -free]\n\nDraw a food chain with five trophic levels. [2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\ce{ -> }$ Five levels in the correct order starting with Leaf litter:\n\n$\\hspace{1.5em}$ Leaf litter $\\rightarrow$ fungi **OR** bacteria $\\rightarrow$ arthropods (small) **OR** nematodes(small) $\\rightarrow$     arthropods (predator) $\\rightarrow$ birds [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ Arrows pointing in the direction of the flow of energy from leaf litter to fungi **OR** bacteria and so on     [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1.5em}$:u[Sample answer]:\n\n$\\hspace{1.5em}$**Leaf litter $\\rightarrow$ fungi  $\\rightarrow$ arthropods (small) $\\rightarrow$  arthropods (predator) $\\rightarrow$ ($\\checkmark$) birds** ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"BI0123","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0123\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe following diagram shows a generalised pyramid of energy.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/BI0123ii.png\" alt=\"BI0123ii\">\n\n1. Label the lowest level (**I**) and the highest level (**IV**) with the correct trophic classifications. [2 marks]\n\n2. State which trophic level, **Level I** or **Level II**, can sustain the largest human population.[1 mark]\n\n3. Explain why animals at Level IV are most vulnerable to extinction. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ Level I is producers **OR** primary producers [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ Level IV is tertiary consumers [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/BI0123%20MSi%20a.png\" alt=\"BI0123 MSi a\">\n\n2.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ Level I or primary producers [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\n\n **Level I**, the **producers** ($\\checkmark$), can sustain the largest human population.\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ energy loss limits population size **OR** only small percentage **OR** approximately 10% of energy is transferred between trophic levels [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ dependence on prey species **OR** top predators rely on populations of lower trophic levels, making them vulnerable to fluctuations in prey availability **OR** animals in Level IV require large habitats to support sufficient prey populations [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ greater impact of human and environmental threats **OR** habitat destruction **OR** hunting **OR** bioaccumulation of toxins **OR** other suitable human impact disproportionately affect top predatorss [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n :u[Sample answer]:\n\n Animals at Level IV are most vulnerable to extinction because only a **small fraction** (about **10%**) of **energy** is **transferred between trophic levels** ($\\checkmark$), leading to lower population sizes. They require **large habitats** to provide **enough prey**, making them more **susceptible to habitat loss** and fragmentation ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0125","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0125\n\nWhat is the name of the process that converts carbon dioxide in the air to carbohydrates?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Respiration\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Photosynthesis\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Decomposition\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Transpiration","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nPhotosynthesis (**Choice B**) is the process by which carbon dioxide in the air is converted to carbon compounds in green plants.\n\nRespiration releases carbon dioxide (**Choice A**), and decomposition by decomposers  (**Choice C**) also releases carbon dioxide.\nTranspiration is the process by which water is drawn up the stem of a plant to the leaves where it evaporates (**Choice D**) and is not involved directly in the carbon cycle."},{"ref":"BI0126","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0126\n\nThere is continuous cycling of carbon from the atmosphere to other carbon sinks. Which is the main source of energy for the carbon cycle?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Heat\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Sunlight\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Chemical energy\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Energy from respiration","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nEnergy from the Sun (**Choice B**) is used to form carbon compounds through the process of photosynthesis.\nHeat (**Choice A**) is a form of energy that cannot be converted to chemical energy by living organisms.\n\nChemical energy (**Choice C**) is held in the bonds of organic compounds which are produced by photosynthesis, or comes from hydrothermal vents deep in the oceans. Even though it is an energy source, it is not the main source of energy for the carbon cycle.\n\nEnergy stored in chemical bonds is released (**Choice D**) during respiration forming ATP which is used to power the cells metabolic processes."},{"ref":"BI0130","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0130\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/BI0130.png\" alt=\"BI0130\">\n[Source: Webb, R. (2007, January 28). *File: soil profile*, Murieston Water - geograph.org.uk - 325135.JPG. Retrieved May 9, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Soil_profile,\\_Murieston_Water_-_geograph.org.uk\\_-_325135.jpg  Copyright under  Creative Commons Attribution-Share Alike 2.0 Generic]\n\nWhat kind of fuels can be formed in conditions such as those shown in the picture above?  The soil is anaerobic, acidic and waterlogged.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$  Peat\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$  Crude Oil\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$  Natural gas\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$  Tar sands","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWhen soil is waterlogged, anaerobic conditions can develop and the soil sometimes becomes acidic.  In these conditions, partially decomposed organic matter will form peat.\n\nCrude oil is formed when organic matter buried deep in the earth is compressed by pressure and heat over millions of years. (**Choice B**)\n\nNatural gas (**Choice C**) is found in underground shale formations and sandstone beds.\nTar sand is a mix of sand, clay, water and bitumen (used for road surfacing) (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0132","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0132\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/BI0132.png\" alt=\"BI0132\">\n[Source: Sémhur, S. (2008). *Atmospheric carbon dioxide concentrations as directly measured at Mauna Loa, Hawaii*. Retrieved April 27, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Mauna_Loa_Carbon_Dioxide-en.svg. Copyright under CC- SA 4.0]\n\nWhich of the following statements explains the annual fluctuations of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Decrease in the combustion of fossil fuels in the summer months.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Carbon dioxide dissolves in the oceans more rapidly during the summer months.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ More carbon dioxide is fixed through photosynthesis in the northern hemisphere during the summer.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ More carbon dioxide is released in the summer in the northern hemisphere.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn the summer months, the rate of photosynthesis increases in the northern hemisphere and more atmospheric carbon dioxide is used to make organic compounds by photosynthesizing organisms such as green plants and trees.\n\nThere is no substantial decrease in the consumption of fossil fuels during the summer, which could explain the fluctuation of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere (**Choice A**).\nCarbon dioxide dissolution in the oceans cannot account for the fluctuations either (**Choice B**).\n\nThe rate of photosynthesis is on average greater than the rate of respiration in the northern hemisphere during the summer which means more carbon dioxide is absorbed, not released at this time (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0133","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0133\n\nNutrients are constantly cycled in ecosystems, including in the atmosphere as a gaseous form. Which of the following elements is not found in the atmosphere?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Carbon\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Sulfur\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Nitrogen\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Phosphorus","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nUnlike the other elements, phosphorus is not found as a gas,\n\nCarbon exists as a gas in the atmosphere in the form of carbon dioxide, carbon monoxide, and methane (**Choice A**).\n\nSulfur can be found as sulfur dioxide which mainly comes from burning coal (**Choice B**). Nitrogen gas is inert and found in the atmosphere and oxides of nitrogen are emitted during the combustion of fossil fuels. (**Choice C**)"},{"ref":"BI0134","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0134\n\nWhich of the following processes in the Carbon cycle requires the longest time?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Compounds present in plankton and plants turn into fossil fuels.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Respiration breaks down organic compounds into carbon dioxide.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Photosynthesis captures solar energy and fixes CO$_2$.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Forest fires, which occur naturally and burn the underbrush clean the forest floor of debris.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nHeat and pressure from the Earth’s crust decompose the remains of dead plants and animals over millions of years to form oil, natural gas or coal (fossil fuels).\n\nRespiration (**Choice B**) takes place in a relatively short time as does photosynthesis which requires light to synthesize organic compounds (**Choice  C**). Forest fires (**Choice D**) often occur naturally and release carbon dioxide into the atmosphere.  Global warming has increased the number of wildfires."},{"ref":"BI0505","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0505\n\nZooxanthellae are algae that live inside coral. Corals feed on the carbon compounds produced by zooxanthellae from carbon dioxide, water, and sunlight. Which describes the nutrition of these organisms?\n\n| | Zooxanthellae | Coral |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Autotroph | Heterotroph |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Autotroph | Detritivore |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Heterotroph | Autotroph |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Detritivore | Heterotroph |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nZooxanthellae make carbon compounds from carbon dioxide, water and energy from sunlight. So, zooxanthellae are autotrophs (organisms that make their own food).\nThe coral feed on the carbon compounds produced by zooxanthellae. So, the corals are heterotrophs (organisms that must obtain carbon compounds from other organisms).\n\nDetritivores feed on dead organic matter (**Choices B** and **D**).\\\n**Choice C** has the nutrition of these organisms backwards."},{"ref":"BI0506","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0506\n\nThe diagram shows the carbon stored in some of Earth’s carbon reservoirs, and the estimated annual carbon flux, in GT/yr, between reservoirs.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/BI0506.png\" alt=\"BI0506\">\n[Source: Adapted from NOAA Global Monitoring Laboratory (n.d.) *Measuring & Analyzing Greenhouse Gases: Behind the Scenes*. Retrieved March 20, 2022, from https://gml.noaa.gov/outreach/behind_the_scenes/gases.html . Copyright under Public Domain]\n\nWhich is a net flux between two reservoirs in the carbon cycle?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Vegetation/soil releases 1 GT of carbon to the atmosphere each year.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Vegetation/soil absorbs 1 GT of carbon from the atmosphere each year.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Marine biota gain 11 GT of carbon each year.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Marine biota lose 11 GT of carbon each year.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe diagram shows that soil/vegetation absorbs 123 GT of carbon from the atmosphere per year and releases 122 GT of carbon into the atmosphere per year. So, soil/vegetation absorbs a net of 123 GT - 122 GT  =  1 GT of carbon per year.\n\nSoil/vegetation gains 1 GT of carbon each year, rather than releasing 1 GT of carbon to the atmosphere (**Choice A**). Marine biota gain a net total of 11 GT of carbon from the surface ocean but lose 11 GT of carbon to the intermediate/deep ocean so have a net flux of 0 GT of carbon per year. Therefore, marine biota neither gains 11 GT of carbon per year (**Choice C**) nor loses 11 GT of carbon per year (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0507","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0507\n\nAquatic autotrophs require inorganic carbon. In what forms is inorganic carbon present in aquatic ecosystems?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Dissolved carbon dioxide and hydrogen carbonate ions\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Dissolved carbon monoxide and calcium carbonate\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Dissolved methane and glucose\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Dissolved carbon dioxide and methane","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAquatic ecosystems take in inorganic carbon when carbon dioxide dissolves in water. Dissolved carbon dioxide can combine with water to form carbonic acid (H$_2$CO$_3$) which dissociates into hydrogen ions (H:sup[+]) and hydrogen carbonate ions (HCO$_3$:sup[-]). So, inorganic carbon is present as both dissolved carbon dioxide and hydrogen carbonate ions in aquatic ecosystems.\n\nCarbon dioxide, not carbon monoxide (**Choice B**) is the form of inorganic carbon that autotrophs can use. Methane (**Choice C** and **D**) is a form of organic carbon, not inorganic carbon."},{"ref":"BI0548","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0548\n\nThe diagram illustrates the carbon cycle in a swamp. What is represented by the letters?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/BI0548.png\" alt=\"BI0548\">\n\n| | X |Y | Z|\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Anaerobic respiration by Archaeans | Photosynthesis by producers | Incomplete decomposition of organic matter\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Cell respiration by autotrophs | Cell respiration by saprotrophs | Cell respiration by saprotrophs |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Anaerobic respiration by Archaeans | Cell respiration by autotrophs | Incomplete decomposition of organic matter |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Cell respiration by autotrophs | Photosynthesis by producers | Cell respiration by saprotrophs |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe letter “**X**” in the diagram represents the process through which methane is released from peat. Peat forms in acidic and/or anaerobic conditions in marshes and other waterlogged areas. Methanogenic Archaeans present in peat use anaerobic respiration to decompose organic compounds releasing methane to the atmosphere. So, the process represented by the letter X is anaerobic respiration by Archaeans.\n\nThe letter “**Y**” in the diagram represents the process through which carbon dioxide is released to the atmosphere by marsh plants. Plants are autotrophs, meaning they are organisms that produce their own food (sugar and other carbon-based molecules) from inorganic carbon. Plants break down some of the sugar they produce through aerobic cellular respiration which releases carbon dioxide as a byproduct. So, the process represented by the letter Y is cell respiration by autotrophs.\n\nThe letter “**Z**” in the diagram represents the formation of peat from organic carbon litter. Peat forms when organic material is not fully decomposed in acidic and/or anaerobic wetlands. So, the process represented by the letter Z is the incomplete decomposition of organic matter."},{"ref":"BI0808","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0808\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe diagram below shows the food web in a salt marsh, one of the most productive habitats on Earth.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/BI0808A.png\" alt=\"BI0808A\">\n\nThe numbers on the arrows show the percentage of gross primary production that flows into different trophic levels or is used in plant respiration.\n\n1. Define gross and net primary production. [2 marks]\n\n2. Identify the herbivores shown in the system. [1 mark]\n\n3. If the energy available to consumers from producers in organic compounds is 300 g m:sup[-2] yr:sup[-1] (dry), calculate the amount of energy that is lost by export to the tide. [1 mark]\n\n4.\n 1. The table below shows the NPP for three trophic levels in a food web. Draw a pyramid of energy from the information in this table, labelling it appropriately with the trophic levels and corresponding NPP values (rounded to 3 significant figures as in the table). [2 marks]\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/BI0808B.png\" alt=\"BI0808B\">\n\n 2. Outline a limitation of the ecological pyramid of energy. [1 mark]\n\n5. Explain how a food web provides a more accurate representation of an ecosystem’s feeding relationships than a food chain. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following (one point for GPP and one point for NPP):\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ gross primary productivity is the energy produced by the process of photosynthesis by green plants **OR** primary producers [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ GPP represents the total rate at which primary producers convert light energy into chemical energy [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ net primary productivity is the actual growth of the producer [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ net primary productivity is the energy available after the producer has carried out respiration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ net primary productivity is the Gross Primary Productivity minus energy lost in respiration **OR** NPP = GPP -R **OR** Net primary productivity = Gross Primary Productivity - Respiration [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Gross primary productivity is the total **rate** at which primary producers **convert** light energy into **chemical energy** ($\\checkmark$) in the form of organic compounds.\n\n Net primary productivity is the **energy** available **after** the producer has carried out **respiration** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. **Two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ aphids **AND** grasshoppers [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The two herbivores in the food web are **aphids** and **grasshoppers** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. The following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 120 (dry) gram m:sup[-2] yr:sup[-1] [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/BI0808ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0808ANSA\">\n\n 25% = 300 (dry) g m:sup[-2] yr:sup[-1]\n\n proportion of the 300 (dry) g m:sup[-2] yr:sup[-1] that is exported by tide = 10/25\n\n 10/25 X 300 g m:sup[-2] yr:sup[-1] = 120 (dry) g m:sup[-2] yr:sup[-1]\n\n The net primary productivity exported to the tide is **120 (dry) g m:sup[-2] yr:sup[-1]** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.\n 1. The following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 3 appropriately sized rectangles representing the 3 trophic levels [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ levels labelled to show trophic level names, **OR** abbreviations from table, **AND** labelled with the corresponding numbers [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/BI0808ANSB.png\" alt=\"BI0808ANSB\">\n\n4.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it does not address the same species belonging to two or more trophic levels [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it is not truly reflective of energy flow in an ecosystem because in nature, it is not usually a simple food chain [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it does not account for detritivores (usually) or saprophytes [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The pyramid of energy does not address species that belong to **two or more trophic levels** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ food chains include only one type of food the organism eats, whereas a food web is a network of interconnecting food chains **OR** more realistic way of showing interconnections between organisms [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ food webs reflect how interactions affect the energy transfer between trophic levels, whereas food chains show a simplified flow of energy [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ food webs indicate how stable, **OR** resilient, the ecosystem is [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ food webs account for the fact that organisms, **OR** omnivores, **OR** detritivores, can feed at multiple, **OR** many, trophic levels [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ food webs show interdependence **OR** show how the change in one population can affect another population [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Food chains only include **one type of food** the organism eats ($\\checkmark$), but the food web shows **all the different food sources** of the organism. Food webs show the **energy transfer between trophic levels** ($\\checkmark$), and also indicate how complex and **resilient the ecosystem is** ($\\checkmark$) to unforeseen changes.\n\n ​*​Check out our Key Concept video on Energy in Ecosystems for more.*"},{"ref":"BI0966","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0966\n\nWhich of the following statements describe a chemoautotroph?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Bacteria and extremophiles living in hostile environments like hydrothermal vents |\n    | II. | Organisms which obtain their energy from oxidation reactions and form organic molecules primarily from carbon dioxide |\n    | III. | Chemoautotrophs are dependent on the Sun for survival |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nChemoautotrophs are organisms that obtain their energy by oxidising inorganic compounds, such as hydrogen sulfide or ammonia. They use this energy to synthesise organic molecules primarily from carbon dioxide via reduction reactions, a process known as autotrophy (**Statement II**). They live in extreme environments where sunlight is scarce (**Statement I**). They are important producers in deep sea hydrothermal vents and form the base of the food chain. Therefore **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as it only includes **Statement I**.\n\nChemoautotrophs can make organic compounds without sunlight (**Statement III**), unlike green plants and algae which are called photoautotrophs. **Choices B** and **D** are therefore incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0967","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0967\n\nThe image below shows an Arctic food web.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/BI0967A.png\" alt=\"BI0967A\">\n[Source: Cmglee. (2003, October). *File: Arctic Food Web.svg - Wikimedia Commons*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved August 1, 2023,, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arctic_food_web.svg. Copyright under CC BY-SA 3.0]\n\nWhich of the following organisms is **not** a heterotroph?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Polar bear\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Phytoplankton\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Killer whale\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Capelin","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nHeterotrophs are organisms that cannot produce their own food. They obtain organic nutrients by consuming other organic matter, such as plants, animals, fungi, or decomposing organic material. Polar bears (**Choice A**), killer whales (**Choice C**) and capelins (**Choice D**) are all heterotrophs, as can be seen on the food web.\n\nThe producer, or photoautotroph for this marine ecosystem is the phytoplankton (**Choice B**), which forms the food web base. Photoautotrophs have the pigment chlorophyll and can absorb sunlight energy for photosynthesis to produce organic molecules.  Because there are no arrows pointing to phytoplankton, phytoplankton can be identified as an autotroph."},{"ref":"BI0968","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0968\n\nThe following image is a graph obtained by plotting values for net primary productivity (x-axis) and the biomass of herbivores (y-axis). The black dots represent one biome and the grey dots represent another biome.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/BI0968A.png\" alt=\"BI0968A\">\n\nThe x-axis shows the increasing net primary productivity of biomes, and the y-axis shows the increasing biomass of herbivores. Which of the following choices correctly identifies the biomes represented in the graph above?\n\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/BI0968BTAB10.png\" alt=\"BI0968BTAB10\"> | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/BI0968TAB11.png\" alt=\"BI0968TAB11\"> |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Taiga $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Desert $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Rainforest $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Savanna $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Savanna $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Tundra $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Tundra $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Savanna $\\hspace{1em}$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe net primary productivities of the taiga, tundra and desert biomes are relatively lower than the rainforest and savanna biomes. Lower net primary productivity leads to lower secondary productivity. The biomass of the herbivores is therefore lower in the taiga, tundra and desert biomes compared to the savanna and rainforest biomes.\n\n**Choice D** is the correct choice because the tundra has lower net primary productivity so therefore supports a lesser biomass of herbivores. The savanna has vast dry grasslands and therefore more primary productivity overall so can support a larger biomass of herbivores.\n\nDeserts tend to have the lowest productivity of these biomes due to limited water availability, and rainforests have the highest productivity due to their abundant rainfall and sunlight. **Choices A** and **B** are therefore incorrect. The other ecosystems fall in between based on factors like climate, nutrient availability, and plant diversity. The next lowest net productivity tends to be seen in the tundra, then the taiga. The savannah has a high net productivity, but it is lower than rainforests. **Choice C** is therefore incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0969","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0969\n\nThe following graph shows the concentration of atmospheric carbon dioxide measured at Mauna Loa Observatory in Hawaii, USA.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/BI0969.png\" alt=\"BI0969\">\n[Source: Adapted from US Department of Commerce, NOAA. “Global Monitoring Laboratory - Carbon Cycle Greenhouse Gases.” GML, ( Oct. 2005). Retrieved 18 May,2023 from gml.noaa.gov/ccgg/trends/. Copyright under CC0 4.0]\n\nWhich of the following statements do not explain the rise and fall of the carbon dioxide levels shown in the graph above?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ During the winter months, there is a decrease in photosynthesis due to lower temperatures and therefore, an overall increase in atmospheric carbon dioxide.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ As temperatures rise in the summers, there is decreased fixation of carbon dioxide, causing a rise in atmospheric carbon dioxide.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The fluctuations are repeated every year, but the amplitude has been increasing due to the cumulative effects of increasing carbon dioxide from the combustion of fossil fuels.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Leaves and other detritus decay in winter months which releases carbon dioxide.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAs temperatures rise in the summers, more carbon dioxide is fixed, causing a decrease in atmospheric carbon dioxide. There is more photosynthesis and more growth of vegetation. Overall, more carbon dioxide is removed from the atmosphere, causing a seasonal decrease. Therefore **Choice B** is not a correct explanation of the fluctuations of carbon dioxide, so is the correct answer.\n\nDuring the winter months, photosynthesis decreases due to lower temperatures, leading to a decrease in the removal of carbon dioxide from the atmosphere which can contribute to increases in atmospheric carbon dioxide levels.  Therefore **Choice A** is a correct explanation of fluctuating carbon dioxide levels, and so is not the correct answer choice.  Fluctuations in carbon dioxide levels are indeed repeated every year due to natural seasonal cycles, but the amplitude of these fluctuations has been increasing due to the cumulative effects of human activities, particularly the combustion of fossil fuels, which releases additional carbon dioxide into the atmosphere. **Choice C** is therefore a true statement about fluctuating carbon dioxide levels and so is not the correct choice.  The decay of leaves and detritus in the winter months can lead to the release of carbon dioxide as organic matter decomposes, contributing to the observed increase in carbon dioxide levels. **Choice D** is therefore another true statement and so is not the correct choice."},{"ref":"BI0970","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0970\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nThe image below shows a hydrothermal vent deep in the oceans, where a diverse ecosystem is supported. A number of organisms thrive in these areas of high pressure and darkness.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/BI0970A.png\" alt=\"BI0970A\">\n[Source: Adapted from US Department of Commerce, N. O. and A. A. (2008, February 1). *What is an extremophile?. NOAA’s National Ocean Service*. https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/extremophile.html Copyright free CC BY 4.0]\n\n1. State an example of a chemoautotroph that is likely to be found in the hydrothermal vent shown above.     [1 mark]\n\n2. Compare and contrast photoautotrophs and chemoautotrophs.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ iron bacteria **OR** iron-oxidising bacteria **OR** named species of iron bacteria        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sulfur bacteria **OR** sulfur-oxidising bacteria **OR** named species of sulfur bacteria    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nitrogen bacteria **OR** nitrogen-oxidising bacteria **OR** named species of nitorgen bacteria    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (methanogenic) archae **OR** named species of methanogen    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Some **iron bacteria** ($\\checkmark$) are organisms that are chemoautotrophs.\n\n2. Any **three** of the following (at least one from each section):\n\n    **Similarities** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they are both producers **OR** form the base of food chains **OR** food webs     [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ both contribute to the flow of energy **OR** nutrients in ecosystems             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ both use carbon dioxide from their environments to synthesise organic compounds      [1 mark]\n\n    **Differences** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ photoautotrophs convert light energy to chemical energy in the process of photosynthesis **WHEREAS** chemoautotrophs convert energy obtained from chemical reactions involving inorganic compounds into chemical energy [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ photoautotrophs are found in ecosystems like grasslands, forests, oceans, freshwater bodies **WHEREAS** chemoautotrophs are found in extreme environments living in places with no light, and having high temperatures or at high pressure [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ photoautotrophs use pigments such as chlorophyll **WHEREAS** pigments are not required for the reactions carried out by chemoautotrophs [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Both photoautotrophs and chemoautotrophs are **producers**  ($\\checkmark$) and are at the base of the food chain. Both types of organisms use **carbon dioxide from their respective environments to make organic compounds** ($\\checkmark$). **Photoautotrophs** convert **light energy to chemical energy** whereas **chemoautotrophs** convert the energy from **chemical reactions involving inorganic compounds to chemical energy** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0971","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0971\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe image shows a food web in a salt marsh ecosystem.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/BI0971.png\" alt=\"BI0971\">\n\n1. Identify all the detritivores in the food web shown above.[1 mark]\n2. Define secondary productivity. [1 mark]\n\n3. Explain why energy is lost during the transfer from producers to herbivores. [1 mark]\n\n4. Describe a method a farmer could use to estimate the gross primary productivity per month of his wheat crop. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ bacteria **AND** crabs **AND** nematodes [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Bacteria, crabs** and **nematodes** are detritivores ($\\checkmark$) in this food web.\n\n2. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ gain in biomass, of heterotrophs **OR** consumers, **OR** the rate at which consumers convert the organic matter they consume into their own biomass over a given period of time [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Secondary productivity is the **gain in biomass of heterotrophs** ($\\checkmark$) in an ecosystem.\n\n3. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ transfer of energy from one trophic level to another is not 100% efficient [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ energy is lost as heat **OR** during respiration **OR** during metabolic reactions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ not all parts of organism digestible **OR** herbivores do not assimilate all the energy from plant materials that they consume OR lack of break down of cellulose **OR** lignin by consumers **OR** parts of animal are not digested OR suitable example [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ not all parts of organism are eaten [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ waste production **OR** loss of energy in faeces **OR** urine **OR** egestion **OR** excretion [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n During transfer of energy from one trophic level to another, **energy is lost as heat during respiration** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ from a one metre squared area **OR** suitable sized area harvest all above plant material from that area [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ dry the plant material in an oven until it reaches constant mass and record mass [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ one month later repeat the process removing the crop from same sized in same field [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ calculate gross primary production by calculating increase in biomass per m:sup[2] per month **OR** GPP = (total dry biomass of second sample - total dry biomass of first sample) / area for the month [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Harvest all **plant material** above ground from a **1m:sup[2] area** of land ($\\checkmark$) and **dry it** out in an oven until it reaches a constant mass and then **record the mass** ($\\checkmark$). **Repeat the process** in the same field **one month** later. **Calculate** the gross primary production as **increase in dry biomass per m:sup[2] per month** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1151","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1151\n\nThe image below shows a pyramid of energy for a grassland ecosystem.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/BI1151.png\" alt=\"BI1151\">\n\nWhich organism(s) in the diagram synthesise carbon compounds from simple inorganic substances?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ IV only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II, III, and IV\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, III, and IV","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nOnly autotrophs can synthesize their own carbon compounds from inorganic substances. Autotrophs are always found at the base of a pyramid of energy, so **Choice B** is the correct response.\n\n**Choices A, C**, and **D** cannot be correct as they all involve heterotrophs."},{"ref":"BI1152","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1152\n\nWhich of the organisms in the food web below occupies more than one trophic level?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/BI1152correcti.png\" alt=\"BI1152correcti\">\n[Source: Adapted from Cmglee. (2003, October). *File: Arctic Food Web.svg* - Wikimedia Commons. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved April 14, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Arctic_food_web.svg. Copyright under CC BY-SA 3.0]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Arctic char\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Carnivorous zooplankton\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Arctic cod\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Capelin","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nArctic cod feed on both herbivorous zooplankton (primary consumers) and carnivorous zooplankton (secondary consumers). This means that Arctic cod function as both secondary and tertiary consumers, occupying both the third and fourth trophic levels. Therefore, **Choice C** is correct.\n\nArctic char feed only on carnivorous zooplankton, which are secondary consumers. This means Arctic char are tertiary consumers in this food web, occupying only the fourth trophic level. **Choice A** is incorrect because Arctic char do not feed at multiple trophic levels.\nIn this food web, carnivorous zooplankton feed exclusively on herbivorous zooplankton, which are primary consumers. As a result, they are secondary consumers and occupy the third trophic level. **Choice B** is incorrect because carnivorous zooplankton only occupy one trophic level.\nCapelin feed only on carnivorous zooplankton, which are secondary consumers. This means Capelin function as tertiary consumers, occupying only the fourth trophic level. **Choice D** is incorrect because Capelin also do not occupy more than one trophic level."},{"ref":"BI1153","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1153\n\nThe graph below depicts the changes in atmospheric carbon dioxide levels measured in Hawaii over a 50 year period. The inset shows an example of annual carbon dioxide changes.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/BI1153.png\" alt=\"BI1153\">\n[Source: Adapted from Robert A. Rohde (2006, July 2). *File: Mauna Loa Carbon Dioxide.png* - wikimedia commons. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved September 21, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Mauna_Loa_Carbon_Dioxide.png Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\nWhat accounts for the majority of annual cycling observed in atmospheric carbon dioxide levels?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Variations in combustion levels due to human energy and transportation needs\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Variations in respiration due to seasonal reproduction of animals\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Variations in photosynthesis and carbon storage by land plants\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Variations in surface-level gas exchange between the sea and atmosphere","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nPlants account for the majority of seasonal changes observed in atmospheric carbon dioxide levels. The Earth’s landmass is concentrated in the northern hemisphere, as are its forests, so a large amount of photosynthesis and carbon uptake occurs in the summer. As those plants die or become dormant in the fall and winter, their carbon is released back into the atmosphere, causing a subsequent rise.\n\nAlthough minor fluctuations in combustion (**Choice A**), respiration (**Choice B**), and gas exchange (**Choice D**) could be expected over time, these do not account for the seasonal patterns observed in the graph."},{"ref":"BI1187","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1187\n\nThe energy pyramid represents organisms in a tropical grassland. What is a reason that the lions have less energy available to them than the giraffes?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/BI1187.png\" alt=\"BI1187\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Energy is lost as heat, a waste product of respiration, at each level\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ There are a higher number of giraffes in tropical grasslands than lions\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Giraffes have a higher rate of respiration than lions\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Giraffes can derive more energy from acacia trees than lions are able to from hyenas","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThere is a loss of energy between trophic levels for a number of reasons including heat from respiration; **Choice A** is correct.\n\nThe size of the trophic levels in pyramids of energy do not represent the number of organisms; **Choice B** is incorrect. Pyramids of energy offer no information on respiration rates; **Choice C** is incorrect. Approximately 90% of energy is lost between trophic levels, regardless of the organisms involved; **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1231","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1231\n\nPhytoplankton, shown below, are organisms that obtain their energy from the sun and live in the ocean and freshwater systems. What type of nutrition do they use?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/BI1231.png\" alt=\"BI1231\">\n[Source: Adapted from Anderson, S. (2018, August 15). *Phytoplankton*. Wikipedia.  Retrieved September 18, 2023, from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phytoplankton. Copyright free]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Heterotrophic\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Autotrophic\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Saprotrophic\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Detritivorous","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nOrganisms that use autotrophic nutrition make their own food, often using photosynthetic mechanisms; **Choice B**  is correct.\n\nOrganisms that use heterotrophic nutrition feed on other organisms for nutrition; **Choice A** is incorrect. Organisms that use saprotrophic nutrition feed on dead organic matter and digest it externally; **Choice C**  is incorrect. Organisms that are detritivorous also feed on dead organic matter but digest it internally; **Choice D**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1441","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1441\n\nA researcher observed an apple with apple rot fungus growing on it for 48 hours. There was no visible water loss from the apple, and there was free movement of air. Which of the following is the most likely outcome based on the information provided?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The mass of the apple with apple rot will remain unchanged as the fungal growth balances the breakdown of apple tissue.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The mass of the apple with apple rot will increase significantly due to rapid fungal growth.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The mass of the apple with apple rot will decrease slightly due to the fungus converting apple biomass into fungal biomass and carbon dioxide.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The mass of the apple with apple rot will decrease significantly due to the fungus converting apple biomass into ethanol, carbon dioxide and ATP.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAs the fungus breaks down the apple biomass to form its own, it carries our respiration to release the energy required for the metabolic processes. We are told that conditions are aerobic, therefore the waste product is carbon dioxide, which is being released into the atmosphere. The growth of fungal biomass does not entirely offset this loss of carbon dioxide. **Choice C** is the correct answer to this question.\n\nBecause carbon dioxide and heat energy are lost to the atmosphere during respiration, as the fungus converts apple biomass into fungal biomass, the overall mass cannot increase or remain the same. **Choices A** and **B** are not the correct answers.\n\nEthanol is produced during anaerobic respiration in fungi, but we are told conditions are aerobic; therefore, **Choice D** is not the correct answer."},{"ref":"BI1482","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1482\n\nWhich is not an energy conversion that can occur in living organisms?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Light energy into chemical energy\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Heat energy into kinetic energy\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Chemical energy to electrical energy\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Chemical energy to chemical energy","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nLiving organisms cannot convert heat energy into other forms, heat energy is lost to the environment; **Choice B**  is correct.\n\nLight energy is converted into chemical energy during photosynthesis; **Choice A**  is incorrect. Chemical energy is converted into electrical energy in nerve cells; **Choice C**  is incorrect.  Chemical energy is converted into chemical energy in chemical reactions such as respiration or photosynthesis; **Choice D**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1483","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1483\n\nWhich process releases carbon dioxide into the atmosphere?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Photosynthesis\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Peat formation\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Respiration in saprotrophs\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Ingestion","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nDuring respiration carbon dioxide is released into the atmosphere; **Choice C**  is correct.\n\nPhotosynthesis takes in carbon dioxide from the atmosphere; **Choice A** is incorrect. Peat formation traps carbon dioxide in waterlogged soils; **Choice B**  is incorrect. Ingestion is the consumption of carbon containing compounds; **Choice D**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1484","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1484\n\nThe graph shows the annual carbon emissions by different global regions. Which conclusion is correct?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/BI1484i.png\" alt=\"BI1484i\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from DB, P. (2009, January 29). File: Carbon Emissions by Country.jpg. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved February 11 , 2024,  from https://en.wikiversity.org/wiki/File:Carbon_Emissions_by_Country.jpg. Copyright by CC2.0]\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Asia and Oceania contribute the most towards climate change\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ There is a steady increase in carbon emissions in North America between 1980 and 2005\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Europe has the lowest carbon emissions between 2000 and 2005\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Eurasia burns the least fossil fuels compared to other regions","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nThe graph shows that there is a steady increase in carbon emissions in North America between 1980 and 2005. **Choice B** is correct.\n\nAlthough there is a correlation between carbon emissions and climate change, a direct causation cannot be concluded based on this data alone. Also, although Asia and Oceania have higher emissions than the other global regions in later years, they were below the other regions in previous time periods; **Choice A**  is incorrect. **Choice C**  is incorrect as between 2000 and 2005, Eurasia has the lowest carbon emissions, not Europe. **Choice D**  is incorrect as carbon emissions do not solely come from the burning of fossil fuels."},{"ref":"BI1574","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1574\n\nThe diagram shows a biomass pyramid for an arctic ecosystem.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/BI1574ii.png\" alt=\"BI1574ii\">\n\nWhich of the following causes the biomass of organisms to decrease at the higher trophic levels?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Decreased primary productivity due to competition for resources.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Energy loss through respiration and heat production.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Enhanced efficiency of energy transfer through the food chain.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Elevated rates of predation and mortality among higher trophic levels.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because biomass is lost at each trophic level due to the inefficiency of energy transfer resulting from heat generation and respiration. Only a fraction of the energy contained within each trophic level is transferred to the level above it.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because decreased primary productivity would lead to a reduced number of autotrophs, which would affect all trophic levels equally, not just the highest ones. **Choice C** is incorrect because energy efficiency does not increase at higher trophic levels. **Choice D** is incorrect as rates of predation and mortality should be relatively consistent at each trophic level in a balanced ecosystem."},{"ref":"BI1575","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1575\n\nThe diagram shows the carbon cycle.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Transfers%20of%20Energy%20%26%20Matter/images/BI1575.png\" alt=\"BI1575\">\n[Adapted from *“The Carbon Cycle”*, by BioRender.com (2024). Retrieved from https://app.biorender.com/biorender-template]\n\nIdentify the processes X, Y, and Z in the diagram.\n\n| | X | Y | Z |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Ingestion | Fossilisation | Combustion |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Egestion | Fossilisation | Evaporation |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Ingestion | Compaction | Combustion |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Egestion | Compaction | Evaporation |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because processes X, Y, and Z represent ingestion, fossilisation, and combustion, respectively. Carbon compounds are ingested by animals and converted into complex organic molecules. Following decomposition, organic matter becomes fossilised over millions of years underground. After it is extracted and refined, it is burned through combustion, releasing carbon dioxide into the atmosphere.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because process X is ingestion and process Z is combustion. **Choice C** is incorrect because process Y is fossilisation, not compaction. **Choice D** is incorrect because all three processes are mislabelled."},{"ref":"BI2338","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2338\n\nWhich term describes biomass, peat, coal, oil and natural gas before combustion?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Trophic level\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Carbon flux\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Carbon source\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Carbon sink","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is the correct answer because before combustion, biomass, peat, coal, oil and natural gas all contain large quantities of carbon in stable forms, they are carbon sinks. Combustion changes these carbon sinks to carbon sources. Combustion moves carbon from one part of the ecosystem to another, it is a carbon flux.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because a trophic level is a feeding level in a food chain.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because a carbon flux is a process that moves carbon from one organism, or part of an ecosystem, to another.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because a carbon source releases carbon dioxide into the atmosphere. Biomass, peat, oil and natural gas are only carbon sources when combusted."}]},{"name":"Unassigned","questions":[{"ref":"BI0055","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0055\n\nThe graph shows the changes in blood pH levels of an individual before exercise, during exercise and after exercise.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0055.png\" alt=\"BI0055\">\n\nWhich statement may explain the changes in blood pH shown in the graph?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The blood pH decreases due to the build-up of lactate.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The blood pH decreases due to muscle cells undergoing anaerobic respiration in the presence of oxygen.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The blood pH decreases due to the continued anaerobic respiration caused by oxygen debt.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The blood pH decreases due to the large yield of ATP produced during strenuous exercise.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because during strenuous exercise, muscle cells may not receive enough oxygen to meet energy demands through aerobic respiration alone. As a result, some muscle cells begin anaerobic respiration, producing lactate. Lactate accumulation lowers blood pH, explaining the decrease shown in the graph.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because anaerobic respiration occurs in the absence of oxygen, not in the presence of oxygen.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because oxygen debt develops after strenuous exercise due to insufficient oxygen availability, but it is not the direct cause of the decrease in blood pH. The decrease in pH is caused by lactate accumulation.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because anaerobic respiration produces only a small yield of ATP per glucose molecule. ATP production itself does not directly decrease blood pH."},{"ref":"BI0062","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0062\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast anaerobic respiration in muscle and yeast cells. [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline the role of ATP in cells. [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain how a respirometer could be used to investigate the effect of temperature on the rate of respiration.     [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** similarity and **one** difference*):\n\n   ***Similarities***\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** processes occur in the cytoplasm and do not require oxygen [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** involve glycolysis, producing a net yield of two ATP molecules per glucose molecule [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** regenerate NAD from reduced NAD, allowing glycolysis to continue [1 mark]\n\n   ***Differences***\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ in muscle cells, lactate is produced, **WHEREAS** in yeast cells, ethanol and carbon dioxide are produced **OR** in muscle cells, pyruvate is directly reduced to lactate, **WHEREAS** in yeast cells, pyruvate is first decarboxylated (to ethanal) then reduced to ethanol [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ in muscle cells, pyruvate accepts hydrogen from reduced NAD, **WHEREAS** in yeast cells, ethanal accepts hydrogen from reduced NAD [1 mark]\n\n[3 marks]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\n\nBoth muscle and yeast cells carry out anaerobic respiration **in the cytoplasm without requiring oxygen** ($\\checkmark$). Both processes involve **glycolysis, producing a net yield of two ATP molecules per glucose molecule** ($\\checkmark$). In both cells, **reduced NAD is oxidised to regenerate NAD, allowing glycolysis to continue** ($\\checkmark$). However, in muscle cells, **pyruvate is reduced directly to lactate, whereas in yeast cells, pyruvate is first decarboxylated to ethanal and then reduced to ethanol, releasing carbon dioxide** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP is the molecule that distributes energy within cells [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrolysis of ATP to ADP and inorganic phosphate / Pi releases a small / usable amount of energy [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP can transfer a phosphate group to another molecule, changing its properties / reactivity / stability, enabling cellular processes [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP is regenerated from ADP and Pi using energy released by cellular respiration [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ most ATP during aerobic respiration is produced by ATP synthase in the inner mitochondrial membrane **OR** by chemiosmosis [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ protons flow through ATP synthase down their concentration gradient, providing energy to phosphorylate ADP [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ used for active transport across membranes **OR** suitable example [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ used for synthesis of macromolecules **OR** anabolism **OR** suitable example [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ used for movement of the whole cell **OR** cell components **OR** suitable example [1 mark]\n\n[5 marks]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\n\n**Hydrolysis of ATP** to **ADP** and **inorganic phosphate** releases a small, **usable amount of energy** ($\\checkmark$). ATP is **regenerated** from **ADP and Pi** during cellular respiration ($\\checkmark$), mostly by **ATP synthase** in the **inner mitochondrial membrane** ($\\checkmark$), using energy from the **flow of protons down their concentration gradient (chemiosmosis)** ($\\checkmark$). ATP provides energy for **active transport** ($\\checkmark$) and **anabolic reactions** such as **protein synthesis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ respirometer containing germinating seeds **OR** invertebrates with an alkali / soda lime / potassium hydroxide to absorb carbon dioxide [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen uptake reduces the volume / pressure of gas so the coloured liquid in the manometer moves towards the organisms [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature is the independent variable, set using water baths [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ a range of at least five temperatures is used (eg. 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 °C) [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ apparatus left to equilibrate at each temperature before readings are taken [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ distance moved by the liquid is measured over a fixed time [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ volume of oxygen consumed is calculated using the cross-sectional area of the capillary tube multiplied by the distance moved / using $\\pi r^2h$ [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ rate of oxygen uptake is calculated by dividing the volume of oxygen consumed by the time taken **OR** rate expressed per unit mass (eg. cm³ O₂ g⁻¹ min⁻¹) [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ controlled variables / named controlled variable kept constant [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ control respirometer with glass beads / dead seeds of equal volume at each temperature to account for changes in gas pressure unrelated to respiration [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ at least three repeats at each temperature and mean calculated [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ mean respiration rate is plotted against temperature to identify the relationship between temperature and respiration rate [1 mark]\n\n[8 marks]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\n\nA **respirometer** is set up containing **germinating seeds** and an **alkali such as soda lime to absorb carbon dioxide** produced during respiration ($\\checkmark$). As the seeds respire, **oxygen is taken up**, so the **gas pressure in the tube decreases and the coloured liquid in the manometer moves towards the seeds** ($\\checkmark$). **Temperature is the independent variable** and is controlled using **thermostatically controlled water baths** ($\\checkmark$). A **range of five temperatures** is used, eg. 10, 20, 30, 40 and 50 °C ($\\checkmark$), and at each temperature the apparatus is left to **equilibrate** before readings are taken ($\\checkmark$). The **distance moved by the liquid in a fixed time** is measured ($\\checkmark$), and the **volume of oxygen consumed is calculated using the cross-sectional area of the capillary tube** ($\\checkmark$). A **control tube containing glass beads** is used at each temperature to correct for changes in gas volume caused by temperature ($\\checkmark$). Measurements are **repeated at each temperature three times**, the **mean calculated** ($\\checkmark$), and the **results are plotted against temperature** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0107","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0107\n\nA student set up an aquatic mesocosm using a commercially available seawater-filled glass orb, which included algae, shrimp, some corals and gravel. The student placed it near a window to provide a light source. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | The algae are autotrophs, produce carbon compounds and provide oxygen for organisms in the mesocosm |\n  | II. | Saprotrophs present in the water and gravel help to recycle nutrients found in dead organisms |\n  | III. | Herbivores usually present in a natural ecosystem can be excluded in the mesocosm |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$  I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$  I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$  II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$  I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAll the statements are true. A mesocosm is a stable, small representation of an ecosystem and can be used to study how one factor in an ecosystem affects other factors in an ecosystem. In all ecosystems, energy is lost and autotrophs such as algae or green plants generate the energy needed for the ecosystem. Saprotrophs are necessary for nutrient cycling, which is why no external addition of nutrients is generally required. Herbivores are not necessary for mesocosms to be stable as autotroph abundance is also regulated by the availability of resources such as nutrients and light. The only choice that includes all three statements is **Choice C**. All other choices (**A, B and C**) are therefore incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0108","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0108\n\nThe Arctic cod, ringed seal, killer whale, polar bear and other organisms live and interact with each other in the cold waters of the Arctic Ocean. Which of the following terms best describes them?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Population\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Community\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Habitat\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ecosystem","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA community is a group of populations living and interacting in a shared habitat (biotic factors). The abiotic factors for this community have not been described but could be the depth of the water, light intensity and the nutrients available for phytoplankton.\n\nA population (**Choice A**) is a group of organisms of the same species living in the same area at the same time which can  interbreed.  A habitat (**Choice C**) refers to the environment in which a species normally lives. An ecosystem (**Choice D**) is a group of populations living and interacting in a common habitat and includes both biotic and abiotic factors."},{"ref":"BI0111","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0111\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast how detritivores and saprotrophs obtain nutrition. [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline how a quadrat can be used to estimate the population of poppies in a field. [5 marks]\n\n3. Energy and nutrients move through ecosystems differently.  Describe how energy and nutrients move through terrestrial ecosystems. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following (must include at least one similarity and one difference to gain full marks):\n\n    ***Similarities***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** obtain energy/nutrients exclusively from dead organic matter/detritus/dead leaves/feathers/animal carcasses [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are heterotrophic [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ detritivores ingest dead organic matter **WHEREAS** saprotrophs secrete enzymes that digest dead organic matter outside their body [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ saprotrophs absorb soluble/digested nutrients externally across their cell membranes/walls, **WHEREAS** detritivores absorb digested nutrients internally across a gut/digestive system wall [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ example of detritivore e.g. earthworms **AND** example of saprotrophs e.g. fungi/bacteria [1 mark]\n[3 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Both detritivores and saprotrophs feed on **dead organic matter** ($\\checkmark$) such as leaves. **Detritivores** such as **earthworms**, which are found in soil, **ingest dead organic matter**, and digestion occurs in their digestive system. **Saprotrophs such as fungi and bacteria** ($\\checkmark$) **secrete enzymes** into the environment, **digest** the organic matter **outside of themselves** and then **absorb** some of the **resulting products** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ drawing shown of a field roughly divided to show where quadrats can be placed to sample the number of poppies [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ description of quadrat as a square with known dimensions [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ random **OR** systematic placing of quadrats in the field to measure poppy abundance [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ abundance of poppies in each quadrat recorded [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ repeated sampling [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ average (mean) number of poppies in quadrats is calculated [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ extrapolate to estimate the size of the population in the field [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ quadrats are easy to carry to the study site [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ population of poppies in the field using quadrat sampling is only an estimate [1 mark]\n[5 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/Picture%201.png\" alt=\"Picture 1\">\n\n    **(Quadrats placed randomly used to estimate numbers of poppies in the area shown)**\n\n    Quadrats, which **have a known dimension** ($\\checkmark$), can be used in fields  to estimate the abundance of sessile plants and animals. They are placed **randomly or in a systematic pattern** ($\\checkmark$), and the **number of poppies** in **each quadrat** is recorded ($\\checkmark$). This process is **repeated to obtain a representative sample** ($\\checkmark$) and the **mean abundance of poppies per quadrat is calculated** ($\\checkmark$).  The mean abundance of poppies per quadrat is used to **extrapolate an estimate of the population** ($\\checkmark$) of poppies in the field.\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy is not recycled in ecosystems [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light energy is converted to chemical energy by autotrophs through photosynthesis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chemical energy moves from autotrophs to heterotrophs through feeding [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when energy is transferred between trophic levels most of the energy is lost [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy is lost between trophic levels as heat **OR** excreted waste products **OR** indigestible parts of a plants or animals such as hair [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plants absorb nutrients and water from the soil [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nutrients move from one trophic level to the next through feeding [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ saprotrophs are decomposers, break down dead organic matter and release nutrients into the environment **OR** detritivores consume dead organic matter and release nutrients into the environment [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ saprotrophs secrete enzymes into the environment and digest their food externally **OR** detritivores ingest their food and break it down internally [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plants absorb nutrients released through decomposition [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nutrients are recycled in ecosystems [1 mark]\n[8 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Plants **are autotrophs** and **make organic compounds via photosynthesis using light energy** ($\\checkmark$).  **Primary consumers feed on autotrophs** and **energy flows** ($\\checkmark$) from primary to secondary to tertiary consumers. All organisms that feed on others are known as **heterotrophs**. **As energy is transferred** from one trophic level to the next, energy is **lost as heat** ($\\checkmark$) and **cannot be reused** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    **Saprotrophs are decomposers** and break **down dead organic matter** in the soil by **secreting enzymes** ($\\checkmark$).  This process **releases nutrients into the environment** ($\\checkmark$) and **plants absorb and use them** ($\\checkmark$). In this manner **nutrients are recycled** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0124","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0124\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Draw and label a trophic pyramid of energy showing four levels. [4 marks]\n\n2. Explain how the presence of a food web in an ecosystem makes it more stable than the presence of a single food chain. [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe how energy flows in and out of terrestrial ecosystems. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the pyramid shows 4 trophic levels [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the level at the base is labelled as producer and is the biggest [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the step above producers is labeled as primary consumer and is smaller than the first level [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the third level is labeled as secondary consumer and is smaller than primary consumer [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the fourth level is labeled as tertiary consumer, and is smaller than secondary consumer [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pyramid shows similar rate of decrease at each level [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0124a.png\" alt=\"BI0124a\">\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in a food chain, each organism is dependent on only one other organism for its food  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ food chains **AND/OR** food webs show the relationship between organisms [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ multiple food chains are linked by common organisms to form a food web     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in a food web, herbivores tend to eat different plants so they survive if one plant disappears  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in a food web, consumers tend to eat different herbivores **OR** consumers so survive if one prey species disappears    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in a food web, a single consumer may eat herbivores **OR** consumers from different trophic levels [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Ecosystems with a food web are not destabilized by the disappearance of one species **WHILE** in food chains the removal of one species will lead to the collapse of the food chain [1 mark]\n[5 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Each organism in a food chain is **dependent on only one organism from the previous trophic level** for food ($\\checkmark$). A food web includes many different food chains that are **interlinked by common organisms** ($\\checkmark$). Since a herbivore **eats different plants** in a food web, it will never be short of food if one plant species disappears ($\\checkmark$). Similarly, **a single consumer may eat multiple different herbivores or other consumers** making it less dependent on only one prey species for food ($\\checkmark$). If a **predator or a consumer disappears** due to disease or other factors, the ecosystem is less likely to be destabilized as other species occupy similar niches ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy from the Sun is captured by autotrophs **OR** producers through the process of photosynthesis     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy assimilated by organisms is stored as biomass [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy transfer from one trophic level to the next is not 100% efficient [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy moves from producers to primary consumers to secondary consumers to tertiary consumers in most ecosystems [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the number of trophic levels differs in different ecosystems [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ all organisms lose energy through the process of cell respiration **OR** in the form of heat [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some energy is lost because some body parts (accept any valid example) are indigestible **OR** because some compounds are excreted or egested  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ egested **AND/OR** excreted organic material is consumed by decomposers [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the efficiency of energy production and transfer varies among  ecosystems [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy has to be continuously produced to maintain an ecosystem [1 mark]\n[7 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    In terrestrial ecosystems, energy is captured by producers from the Sun through **photosynthesis** ($\\checkmark$). This energy is **stored as biomass** ($\\checkmark$) in plants and is transferred to herbivores or **primary consumers**, then to secondary and tertiary consumers ($\\checkmark$).  Energy transfer is **not 100% efficient** ($\\checkmark$). The length of the food chain varies in food webs depending on the initial production of energy by producers. The efficiency of production and transfer **varies** between terrestrial ecosystems ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    All organisms use energy for growth and maintenance, and **energy is lost through respiration** in the form of heat ($\\checkmark$). Energy from the dead parts of organisms is consumed by **decomposers** and lost as heat ($\\checkmark$). Energy from the Sun has to be **continuously captured** by producers to maintain the ecosystem ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0128","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0128\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0128.png\" alt=\"BI0128\">\n\nThe diagram above shows a simplified diagram of the movement of carbon in the ocean. Which of the following rows correctly identifies processes involved in the carbon cycle?\n\n| | I | II | III | IV |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A. $\\hspace{1em}$ | Respiration | Eaten by | Decomposition | Photosynthesis |\n| B. $\\hspace{1em}$ | Decomposition | Photosynthesis | Respiration | Eaten by |\n| C. $\\hspace{1em}$ | Photosynthesis | Respiration | Decomposition | Eaten by |\n| D. $\\hspace{1em}$ | Photosynthesis | Respiration | Eaten by | Decomposition |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nProcess I is photosynthesis and shows the capture of energy by phytoplankton from the Sun. Process II is respiration where carbon dioxide is released into the atmosphere. Process III shows the movement of carbon when organisms are eaten by members of the next trophic level, and process IV is the decomposition of dead organisms by bacteria which eventually causes a release of carbon dioxide.\n\nAll other choices (**Choice A, Choice B and Choice C**) do not identify the processes correctly."},{"ref":"BI0135","marks":"2","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0135\n\n**Maximum mark: 2**\n\nThe diagram below shows a simplified scheme of the movement of carbon in the ocean.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0135.png\" alt=\"BI0135\">\n\nIdentify the processes marked I and II. [2 marks]","markScheme":"$\\ce{ -> }$ Process **I** is photosynthesis [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ Process **II** is consumed by **OR** eaten by **OR** predated by [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1.5em}$:u[Sample answer]:\n\n$\\hspace{1.5em}$Process I is **photosynthesis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n$\\hspace{1.5em}$Process II is zooplankton being **consumed by** secondary consumers ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0136","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0136\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\n1. Compare and contrast forests and marine phytoplankton in their role in the carbon cycle. [2 marks]\n\n2. Describe how aquatic autotrophs absorb carbon dioxide from the surroundings.  [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **both** are autotrophs **OR  both** carry out photosynthesis     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ trees in forests absorb carbon dioxide from the air **WHILE**  marine phytoplankton absorbs carbon dioxide dissolved in water [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ancient trees were compressed beneath the soil and formed coal **WHILE** phytoplankton were compressed beneath sediment and formed oil and natural gas [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Both marine phytoplankton and trees carry out **photosynthesis** ($\\checkmark$).  However, the trees in the forests take in carbon dioxide from the **air** while marine phytoplankton take in **dissolved carbon dioxide** from the water ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide in water is absorbed by aquatic autotrophs by diffusion  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide dissolves in water to form carbonic acid     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbonic acid can dissociate to form hydrogen and hydrogen carbonate ions  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ aquatic autotrophs use both dissolved carbon dioxide and hydrogen carbonate ions for photosynthesis [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    In water; **carbon dioxide forms carbonic acid** ($\\checkmark$) which can **dissociate** to form hydrogen and **hydrogen carbonate ions** ($\\checkmark$). The aquatic **autotrophs** can use both dissolved **carbon dioxide** and  **hydrogen carbonate ions** for **photosynthesis** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0138","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0138\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Describe how decomposers contribute to energy transformations in an ecosystem. [3 marks]\n\n2. Compare and contrast primary production by autotrophs and secondary production by consumers in an ecosystem. [5 marks]\n\n3. Nutrients are cycled in all ecosystems.  Draw and label the carbon cycle showing at least four stores or sinks and at least four flows or processes. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ break down organic matter **OR** dead organisms **OR** waste material **OR** prevent accumulation of dead material     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ release nutrients into the environment **OR** release inorganic nutrients **OR** named inorganic nutrient back into the soil **OR** water for uptake by autotrophs **OR** increase soil fertility **OR**  enhance soil composition so supporting plant growth **OR** primary production   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ facilitate detritivore activity **OR** make it easier for detritivores **OR** named detritivore to consume **OR** process organic matter  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ convert organic carbon to inorganic carbon **OR** release carbon dioxide during respiration which re-enters the atmosphere     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ release energy as heat **OR** heat is lost as a byproduct of respiration   [1 mark]\n\n[3 marks]\n\n :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Decomposers **break down dead organisms** and **waste material**, **preventing** the **accumulation** of organic matter ($\\checkmark$). They **release nutrients** such as **nitrogen** and **phosphorus** into the **soil**, increasing **fertility** and **supporting plant growth** ($\\checkmark$). During **respiration**, they release **heat energy** as a by-product ($\\checkmark$) and **carbon dioxide**, which re-enters the atmosphere and can be used by autotrophs in **photosynthesis**($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** similarity and **one** difference to gain full marks*):\n\n***Similarities***:\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** involve the accumulation of carbon compounds in biomass        [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ In **BOTH** energy is lost as biomass is transferred to the next trophic level       [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are typically measured in grammes per metre cubed per year OR  g m⁻² yr⁻¹     [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contribute to biomass availability in an ecosystem     [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** depend on energy input [1 mark]\n\n***Differences***:\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ primary production occurs in autotrophs **WHEREAS** secondary production occurs in heterotrophs      [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ primary production is greater **THAN** secondary production   [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ primary production occurs at the first trophic level **WHEREAS** secondary production occurs at higher trophic levels      [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ primary production usually depends on sunlight for energy  **WHEREAS** for secondary production depends on consuming other organisms  [1 mark]\n[5 marks]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\n\n    **Both** primary and secondary production involve the **accumulation** of **carbon compounds in biomass** ($\\checkmark$). In **both** cases, **energy** is **lost** as **biomass** is **transferred** to the next **trophic level** ($\\checkmark$). However, **primary production** occurs in **autotrophs**, which **convert energy** from **light** or **chemical** sources into **organic molecules**, whereas **secondary** production occurs in **heterotrophs** ($\\checkmark$), which obtain **energy** by **consuming** other **organisms** ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, primary production is greater than secondary production due to energy losses at each trophic level ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following shown on the diagram (if a diagram is not shown, award a maximum of 4 marks):\n\n    **Stores or sinks shown in diagram**\n\n    Any **four** of the following shown on the diagram:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide in air **OR** water       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organic carbon compounds in plants **OR** producers  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organic carbon compounds in consumers **OR** animals      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon in sediments **OR** seabed   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (carbon) in limestone  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (carbon in)  coal **OR** oil **OR** gas **OR** fossil fuels      [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n    **Flows or processes shown by arrows in the correct direction**\n\n    Any **four** of the following shown on the diagram:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ arrow from carbon dioxide in the air **OR** water to producers is labelled **photosynthesis** [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ arrow from any of the organisms pointing to carbon dioxide in the air **OR** water is labelled **respiration** [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ arrow from plants **OR** producers to primary consumers  is labelled eaten by **OR** consumed by  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ arrow from the primary consumers to secondary consumers is labelled **eaten by OR** consumed by   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ arrow from plants **OR** animals to saprotrophs **OR** bacteria **OR** fungi is labelled **decomposition**     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ arrow from plants **OR** animals to limestone **OR** coal **OR** oil **OR** gas **OR** fossil fuels is labelled **fossilization** [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ arrow from the oil **OR** coal **OR** gas **OR** fossil fuels to the CO$_2$ in the air  is labelled **combustion** [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n    $\\hspace{1.5em}$:u[Sample answer]:\n\n    $\\hspace{1.5em}$Stores or sinks are shown in boxes and flows or processes are shown as arrows.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0138.png\" alt=\"BI0138\">"},{"ref":"BI0143","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0143\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0143.png\" alt=\"BI0143\">\n[Source: Adapted from  Semhur, S.(n.d.). *File: Mauna Loa carbon dioxide-en svg.* Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved April 27, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Mauna_Loa_Carbon_Dioxide-en.svg. Copyright free]\n\nThe above data obtained from Mauna Loa show trends in atmospheric CO$_{2}$ concentrations. What conclusions can be made from this graph?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | There is an annual fluctuation of carbon dioxide concentration in the atmosphere. |\n  | II. | There is a continuous increase in the average concentration of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere over the years. |\n  | III. | Increased use of fossil fuels has caused an increase in carbon dioxide in the atmosphere as recorded in Mauna Loa. |\n\n- A. I only\n\n- B. I and II only\n\n- C. I and III only\n\n- D. I, II and III","markScheme":"Explanation:\n\nThe data show an annual fluctuation in the concentration of carbon dioxide **(I)** and a constant increase in average concentration over the many years measured **(II)**.\n\n**Choice A** includes annual fluctuations but not a constant increase in carbon dioxide.\n\n**Choice C** includes a conclusion about annual fluctuations but also the conclusion that says increased use of fossil fuels has caused the increase in carbon dioxide in the atmosphere **(III)** which cannot be determined based on this graph.\n\n**Choice D** includes all three conclusions and since conclusion III is incorrect, it is not the right choice."},{"ref":"BI0163","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0163\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nOxidative phosphorylation, which generates ATP for the cell, is comprised of the electron transport chain and chemiosmosis.\n\n1. Distinguish between the electron transport chain in aerobic cellular respiration and the electron transport chain in photosynthesis.   [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline how ATP can be recycled after being used by the cell.      [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain how 38 molecules of ATP can be produced from one glucose molecule in a cell.     [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ETC happens in the inner mitochondrial membrane, **WHILE** it happens in the thylakoid membrane in the chloroplast   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electrons are donated by the reduced NAD (**OR** FAD) at the beginning of the ETC in aerobic cellular respiration, **WHILE** in photosynthesis electrons are donated by chlorophyll a in PSII **OR** water  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ NADH (**OR** FADH$_2$) is oxidised **OR** loses electrons in the ETC of aerobic cellular respiration, **WHILE** NADP:sup[+] is reduced in the ETC of chloroplasts  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen is the final electron acceptor in the ETC of aerobic cellular respiration **BUT** NADP:sup[+] is the final electron acceptor in the ETC of photosynthesis   [1 mark]\n[3 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    In aerobic cellular respiration, the **ETC takes place in the inner mitochondrial membrane**, while in **photosynthesis it take place in the thylakoid membrane of the chloroplast** ($\\checkmark$). In **aerobic cellular respiration, electrons are donated by NADH (and FADH$_2$**), while in **photosynthesis they are donated by chlorophyll a in photosystem II** ($\\checkmark$). **NADH (and FADH$_2$) undergo oxidation in aerobic cellular respiration** when they donate the electrons at the beginning of the ETC, but in **photosynthesis NADP:sup[+] is reduced** as it accepts electrons at the end of the ETC ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrolysis of ATP to ADP and Pi **OR** inorganic phosphate    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when ATP breaks, energy is released  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ inorganic phosphate combines with ADP to form ATP [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when ATP is synthesized, energy is added [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxidative phosphorylation, **OR** energy released from the oxidation of glucose, **OR** energy from the proton, **OR** H:sup[+] gradient, **OR** chemiosmosis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electron transport chain, **OR** electron movement along the carriers, provides energy [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy released from ETC generates proton **OR** H:sup[+] gradient **OR** moves H:sup[+] across the inner mitochondrial membrane [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP synthase synthesizes ATP, **OR** ATP synthase allows protons, **OR** H:sup[+], to diffuse      through the inner mitochondrial, **OR** thylakoid, membrane [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the energy to convert ADP and inorganic phosphate to ATP comes from organic compounds **OR** sunlight [1 mark]\n[5 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    ATP can be recycled continuously to meet the energy demands of cells. This is done after the **hydrolysis of ATP**, which **releases the energy** ($\\checkmark$) needed to fuel cell processes. Hydrolysis of ATP produces **ADP and inorganic phosphate** ($\\checkmark$). This inorganic phosphate can be **recombined with ADP** ($\\checkmark$) with the addition of **energy** ($\\checkmark$) from either **organic compounds or sunlight** ($\\checkmark$) and then stored within the bonds formed. ATP is formed by **ATP synthase** ($\\checkmark$) in oxidative phosphorylation , which involves **electron movement** and proton transport ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ aerobic respiration requires oxygen and leads to a high yield of ATP   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ products (of aerobic cell respiration) are CO$_2$, water and ATP   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the four stages of cellular respiration are glycolysis, link reaction, Krebs cycle and oxidative phosphorylation     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glycolysis happens in cytoplasm **OR** outside of mitochondria [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ products of glycolysis are pyruvate, NADH, and net of 2 ATP  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pyruvate is converted into acetyl CoA in the link reaction   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxidative phosphorylation happens in mitochondria **OR** Krebs cycle in the matrix of mitochondrion **OR** ETC on the inner mitochondrial membrane (**OR** cristae)  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Krebs cycle results in 2 ATP, reduced NADH (**OR** FADH$_2$), **OR** reduced electron carriers [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ NADH (**OR*  FADH$_2$) donate electrons and hydrogen to ETC    (**OR** electron transport chain) [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electron movement in ETC moves H:sup[+] across the inner mitochondrial membrane **OR** produces an H:sup[+] gradient     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP synthase uses H:sup[+] concentration gradient, **OR** proton gradient, to synthesize ATP [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ inorganic phosphate is added to ADP to form ATP       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 34 ATP formed in oxidative phosphorylation  [1 mark]\n[8 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Cellular respiration consists of **4 stages: glycolysis (which occurs in the cytoplasm** ($\\checkmark$) **and is anaerobic, not needing oxygen)**, and then the other 3 stages, which occur in the mitochondrion and make up aerobic respiration: **the link reaction, the Krebs cycle and oxidative phosphorylation** ($\\checkmark$).   In glycolysis, glucose is broken down into **pyruvate, NADH and a net of 2 ATP is produced** ($\\checkmark$). The pyruvate then enters the mitochondrion, and the **link reaction converts it into acetyl-CoA** ($\\checkmark$). The acetyl-CoA is delivered to the Krebs cycle in the **matrix of mitochondria** ($\\checkmark$). Here **6 NADH, (2 FADH$_2$) and 2 ATP** ($\\checkmark$) are produced per molecule of glucose . **NADH (and FADH$_2$) carry electrons to the electron transport chain (ETC)** ($\\checkmark$), the first step in oxidative phosphorylation. The 2nd step is chemiosmosis, where **ATP synthase produces 34 molecules of ATP** ($\\checkmark$), using the **energy from the proton gradient generated by the ETC** ($\\checkmark$) to combine **ADP and inorganic phosphate** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0175","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0175\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nCompare and contrast the light-dependent and light-independent reactions. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **seven** of the following (*must include at least **two** similarities and **two** differences to gain full marks*):\n\n    ***Similarities***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** reactions take place in the chloroplast   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** reactions involve enzymes   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** reactions can take place during day time     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** reactions are part of photosynthesis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** reactions are linked OR dependent on the activity of the other [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** rely on coenzymes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** involve energy transfer [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light-dependent reactions take place in the intermembrane space of the thylakoid and thylakoid membrane **WHEREAS** light-independent reactions take place in the stroma  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light-dependent reactions require light, **WHEREAS**  the light-independent reactions do not require light       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen is formed in the light-dependent reactions **WHEREAS**  sugar **OR** carbohydrates are formed in the light-independent reactions  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electrons and protons for the light-dependent reactions are supplied from the photolysis of water **WHEREAS**   electrons and protons in the light-independent reaction come from reduced NADPH     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP is formed in the light-dependent reactions **WHEREAS**  ATP is broken down to supply energy in the light-independent reactions [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ NADP is reduced **OR** NADP accepts electrons and protons in the light-dependent reactions **WHEREAS**  NADPH is oxidised **OR** reduced NADP donates electrons and protons in light-independent reaction        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light-dependent reactions involve an electron transport chain and chemiosmosis WHEREAS light-independent reactions involve carbon fixation and the Calvin cycle\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light-dependent reactions produce short-term energy molecules **OR** ATP and NADP **WHEREAS**   light-independent reactions produce long-term energy storage molecules\n [1 mark]\n[7 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    The similarities between the light-dependent reactions and light-independent reactions are that both of these reactions are **part of photosynthesis** ($\\checkmark$) which is conducted by photosynthesizing organisms. Both reactions **involve enzymes** ($\\checkmark$) such as the cytochrome b6f complex in the light-dependent reactions and rubisco in the light-independent reactions.\n\n    The differences include that the light-dependent reactions take place in the **intermembrane space as well as the thylakoid membranes** whereas the light-independent reactions take place in the **stroma** of the chloroplast ($\\checkmark$). The light-dependent reactions **need light** for photophosphorylation, but the light-independent reactions **do not need light** ($\\checkmark$). In the light-dependent reactions, **oxygen** and **ATP are formed** ($\\checkmark$) whereas in the light-independent reactions, **ATP is broken down** to supply energy to form **sugar** ($\\checkmark$). **NADP is reduced** in light-dependent reactions, whereas in the light-independent reactions, the reduced NADP is **oxidised to form NADP** ($\\checkmark$)  ."},{"ref":"BI0402","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0402\n\nWhich of the following statements does **not** describe a step in the sodium-potassium pump cycle in the neuronal cells?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Three sodium ions attach to their binding sites in the pump from the interior of the cell.\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The pump changes shape upon being phosphorylated, and sodium ions are released to the outside.\n\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Three potassium ions attach to their binding sites in the pump from the exterior of the cell.\n\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The pump changes shape upon releasing the phosphate group, and potassium ions are released to the interior of the cell.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nTwo potassium ions, not three, are transported in the sodium-potassium pump cycle.\n\nThe sodium-potassium pump cycle is as follows: three sodium ions attach to their binding sites (**Choice A**), the pump changes shape to release the sodium ions to the exterior of the cell (**Choice B**) and finally, the pump changes shape again to release two potassium ions to the interior of the cell (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0757","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0757\n\nWhich statements about unicellular and multicellular organisms are correct?\n\n| | Unicellular organisms | Multicellular organisms|\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | They only reproduce asexually | Contains differentiated cells |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Consist of only one cell | All cellular processes are carried out by one organelle |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Can reproduce sexually | They have emergent properties |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Contains differentiated cells | Can reproduce sexually |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A**  is incorrect, as not all unicellular organisms reproduce asexually. **Choice B** is incorrect, as both unicellular and multicellular organisms contain multiple organelles that carry out different functions. **Choice D**  is incorrect, because unicellular organisms do not contain more than one cell, and so do not contain multiple differentiated cells."},{"ref":"BI0771","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0771\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe enzyme pepsin hydrolyses proteins to release peptides in the stomach. A study was carried out to determine the effect of increasing substrate concentration on enzyme activity at a constant temperature of 30°C.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0771A.png\" alt=\"BI0771A\">\n\n1. Describe the trends shown by the graph. [2 marks]\n\n2.\n 1. Outline why it is important that temperature is kept constant in this experiment. [2 marks]\n\n 2. State one factor other than temperature that would need to be controlled in this experiment. [1 mark]\n\n 3. Suggest a method that can be used to maintain a constant temperature in this experiment. [1 mark]\n\n3. Calculate the rate of reaction during the first 4 minutes when the enzyme pepsin is exposed to 5 mM of protein. Make sure you show your working. [2 marks]\n\n4. Explain the observed results. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as time increases the concentration of peptides made also increases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the higher the concentration of protein, the faster the reaction rate **OR** the greater the number of peptides formed per unit time **OR** the lower the concentration of protein, the slower the reaction rate **OR** the lower the number of peptides formed per unit time [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the reaction rate is fastest at the beginning **OR** reaction rate slows down towards the end [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ for every 10 mM increase in protein concentration there is almost an identical increase in the concentration of peptides produced at 20 minutes [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The graph shows that the **higher the concentration of protein** (substrate), **the faster the reaction rate** ($\\checkmark$), however the **reaction rate is fastest at the beginning** and **slows down towards the end** ($\\checkmark$), as indicated by the steepness of the graph. For all the concentrations of substrate **as time progresses, the concentration of peptides** (products) **increases** ($\\checkmark$). For every **10 mM increase in the concentration of protein**, there is an almost identical **increase in the concentration of peptides** produced at 20 minutes ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as temperature increases (up to optimum), reaction rate increases **OR** as temperature decreases, reaction rate decreases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ if the temperature is too high **OR** above the optimum temperature, enzyme starts to denature [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to see the effects of change in substrate concentration **OR** to make results valid **OR** comparable, there should only be one independent variable in an experiment [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n As **temperature increases, reaction rate also increases** ($\\checkmark$) up until the optimum temperature. Therefore there should only be **one independent variable** ($\\checkmark$) in the experiment (substrate concentration), to allow for a **fair comparison** between the different substrate concentrations. If the **temperature is too high, this can cause the enzyme to denature** ($\\checkmark$), stopping the reaction from continuing.\n\n2.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ pH [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ enzyme **OR** pepsin concentration **OR** volume of enzyme [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ volume of substrate solution [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ same protein [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **pH** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2.\n 3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ waterbath [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ metal hot blocks [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Placing the samples in a **waterbath** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ (6 mM - 0 mM) ÷ (4 min - 0 min) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 1.5 mM min:sup[-1] [1 mark]\n\n *1 mark should be awarded for correct working, if incorrect numbers are extracted from the graph*\n\n *Units must be included*\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/bi0771ans%20b.png\" alt=\"bi0771ans b\">\n\n (**6** mM - **0** mM) ÷ (**4** min - **0** min) ($\\checkmark$) = **1.5 mM min:sup[-1]** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at a higher concentration of protein (25mM), initially peptides are made at a faster rate as there is a greater chance of successful collisions between substrate and enzyme **OR** at a lower concentration of protein (5mM), initially peptides are made at a comparably slower rate as there is a lower chance of successful collisions between enzyme and substrate [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ for all concentrations of protein, there is a faster rate of reaction within the first 6 minutes compared to the latter half of the reaction, as there is a higher concentration of substrate available to bind to the enzyme [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ for all concentrations of protein, the reaction rate slows down as the substrate reduces in concentration and the concentration of product increases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ The highest concentration of protein (25mM) produces the highest concentration of peptides because there is higher initial concentration of substrate so more product can be made **OR** the lowest concentration of protein (5mM) produces the lowest concentration of peptides because there is a lower initial concentration of substrate so less product can be made [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n With the **highest concentration** of protein (25mM), peptides are initially made at a **faster rate** compared to the lower concentrations, as there is a great chance of **successful collisions** ($\\checkmark$) between substrate and enzyme. For all concentrations of protein, there is a **faster rate of reaction within the first 6 minutes** compared to the latter half of the reaction, as there is **more substrate available to bind to the enzyme** ($\\checkmark$). However, as all of the reactions progress, the reaction rate begins to **slow down** and eventually become stops, as the concentration of **substrate reduces** and the concentration of **product increases** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0772","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0772\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nIn an investigation into the impacts of climate change on the rate of photosynthesis, pondweed was placed in a number of sealed conical flasks, each containing a solution of water and carbonic acid.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0772A.png\" alt=\"BI0772A\">\n[ 2022. Created with Chemix (https://chemix.org)]\n\nEach conical flask was placed in a water bath maintained at a constant temperature. At regular time intervals, samples of the solution were taken from each conical flask and measured using a pH probe.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0772B.png\" alt=\"BI0772B\">\n[ 2022. Created with Chemix (https://chemix.org)]\n\nThe results are shown in the table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0772C.png\" alt=\"BI0772C\">\n\n1.\n 1. Identify the dependent and independent variables in this investigation. [2 marks]\n\n 2. State one variable that must be kept constant in this investigation to ensure the results are valid. [1 mark]\n\n2. Describe the relationship between the temperature, time and pH of the solution. [2 marks]\n\n3. Predict the pH of the dilute carbonic acid solution at 40 :sup[o]C after 60 minutes. [1 mark]\n\n4.\n 1. Calculate the rate of change in pH between 30 - 40 minutes and 40 - 50 minutes for the trial kept at 40 :sup[o]C. [2 marks]\n\n 2. Suggest a reason for the difference in the rate of change you calculated in (i). [1 mark]\n\n5. Suggest one improvement to the design of this experiment and state whether it would increase the **accuracy** or **reliability** of the results. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n 1.\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ independent variable is temperature [1 mark]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ dependent variable is pH [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n $\\hspace{1em}$ The independent variable is **temperature** ($\\checkmark$) and the dependent variable is **pH** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2.\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ concentration of carbonic acid **OR** volume of solution **OR** light intensity **OR** mass of pondweed **OR** size of pondweed **OR** species of pondweed **OR** other suitable control [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n $\\hspace{1em}$ The **concentration of carbonic acid** ($\\checkmark$) in the solution should remain constant.\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ for 20 :sup[o]C - 40 :sup[o]C as the time increases up to 50 minutes, the pH of the solution increases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ for 50 :sup[o]C as the time increases, the pH remains fairly constant **OR** increases less than 20 :sup[o]C - 40 :sup[o]C **OR** above 40 :sup[o]C there is very little, or no change in pH up to 50 minutes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ for 60 :sup[o]C as time increases, the pH remains constant [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n In the experiment, as time increases, the pH of the solution **increases** for trials kept at temperatures between **20 - 40 :sup[o]C** ($\\checkmark$), but at **60 :sup[o]C** the pH remains **unchanged** ($\\checkmark$) as time increases.\n\n3.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ accept answers in the range of 6.82 - 6.87 [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0772ANS%20A.png\" alt=\"BI0772ANS A\">\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 30 min 6.47\n\n$\\hspace{6em}$ + 0.30\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 40 min 6.77\n\n$\\hspace{6em}$ + 0.05\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 50 min 6.82\n\n$\\hspace{6em}$ + 0.05 or less\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 60 min **6.83** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4.\n 1.\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 0.03 pH min:sup[-1] (30 - 40 :sup[o]C) [1 mark]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 0.005 pH min:sup[-1] (40 - 50 :sup[o]C) [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0772ANSC.png\" alt=\"BI0772ANSC\">\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ rate of change = change (pH) ÷ time (min)\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ 30 = pH 6.47\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ 40 = pH 6.77\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ change in pH = (6.77 - 6.47) = pH 0.3\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ pH 0.3 ÷ 10 min = **0.03 pH min:sup[-1]** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ 40 = pH 6.77\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ 50 = pH 6.82\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ Change in pH = (6.82 - 6.77) = pH 0.05\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ pH 0.05 ÷ 10 min = **0.005 pH min:sup[-1]** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4.\n 2.\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ reduced rate of photosynthesis **OR** less photosynthesis [1 mark]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ less carbon dioxide available **OR** lower concentration of carbon dioxide [1 mark]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ the increase in pH may have started to denature plant enzymes [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n $\\hspace{1em}$ The rate of change is slowing down because the **source of carbon dioxide** in the solution is becoming **exhausted** ($\\checkmark$) so less is being absorbed for photosynthesis and thus the pH of the solution is increasing more slowly.\n\n5. Any **one** of the following (students should relate answers to accuracy or reliability):\n\n **Increase accuracy:**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ smaller time intervals between measurements **OR** more frequent measurements [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ use smaller intervals between test temperatures [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ pH probe inside the conical flask **OR** pH measurements taken inside the conical flask **OR** do not remove stopper to test pH [1 mark]\n\n **Increase reliability:**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ repeat the experiment two more times **OR** at least 3 times [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ include a control group without pondweed in flask [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n To improve the accuracy of the experiment, scientists could take **measurements at shorter time intervals** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0774","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0774\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe white clover (*Trifolium repens*) is native to the fields around Cornell University. The image shows a 10m x 15m area of the park, where quadrats (1m:sup[2]) were used to measure the number of white clover in the fields. The results from 10 of the quadrats are also shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0774A.png\" alt=\"BI0774A\">\n[Source: adapted from: NY State IPM Program at Cornell University, N. Y. S. I. P. M. P. at C. U. (2014, June 19). *White Clover in soccer field*. Flickr. Retrieved March 8, 2023, from https://www.flickr.com/photos/99758165@N06/14273941729 Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n1.\n    1. Describe how quadrats could be used to estimate the mean number of white clover in the field. [3 marks]\n\n    2. alculate the mean number of white clover in the field, showing your working. [1 mark]\n\n    3. Explain why this type of sampling can only be used for sessile or immobile organisms. [1 mark]\n\n    4. Suggest two limitations of quadrat sampling. [2 marks]\n\n2. State the statistical test that could be conducted to check if white clover are associated with another organism in the field. [1 mark]\n\n3. There are nitrogen fixing bacteria in the root nodules of the white clover. State two ways that the white clover uses the nitrogen provided by the bacteria. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ divide the field into a grid using a measuring tape **OR** other suitable apparatus [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use a random number generator **OR** other suitable apparatus to generate ten coordinates     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ place quadrats at the ten coordinates **AND** count the number of white clovers in each quadrat    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ calculate the mean number of white clover per quadrat    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ multiply the mean number of white clover by 150        [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    To estimate the number of white clover in the field, the field should be **divided into a grid** using a measuring tape ($\\checkmark$) and each grid should be assigned a coordinate. Next, using a **random number generator** ($\\checkmark$), you should generate **10 coordinates** and then place a quadrat at one coordinate and count the number of white clovers at that coordinate. Then place the quadrat at another coordinate and **count the number of white clover** ($\\checkmark$) there, this should be repeated for all of the coordinates. Finally,  **the mean number** ($\\checkmark$) of white clover per quadrat should be calculated and the mean should be **multiplied by 150** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    2. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 4290     [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ Mean number of white clover per 1 m:sup[2] quadrat=  **(13 + 44 + 33 + 42 + 10 + 18 + 34 + 29 + 7 + 56)  ÷ 10 = 28.6**\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ Area of field = 10 m x 15 m  = **150** m:sup[2]\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ Mean number of white clovers per field = 28.6 x 150 = **4290** ($\\checkmark$) white clover\n\n1.\n    3. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ immobile organisms do not move out of the sample area making counting accurate **OR**  highly mobile organisms can move out of the sample area making counting inaccurate     [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ Quadrats are mostly used for counting sessile or immobile organisms, as they **do not move**  out of the sample area, so counting is **accurate** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    4. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ the number of organisms may be underrepresented **OR** overrepresented **OR** size of quadrat can greatly influence the results    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ researcher can make mistakes when counting    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ if a white clover is on the edge of a quadrat, then it might be difficult to know whether to include it    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ there is only a small sample size    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ Two limitations of quadrat sampling are that the number of organisms may be **underrepresented** ($\\checkmark$) or the researcher can make **mistakes when counting** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chi squared [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    A **chi squared** ($\\checkmark$) test can be used to identify if white clovers are associated with another organism in the field.\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to make amino acids    **OR** protein    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to make nitrogenous bases **OR** nucleic acids **OR** DNA **OR** RNA **OR** ATP **OR** NADP **OR** NAD **OR** FAD    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to make chlorophyll    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    White clover use nitrogen to make **amino acids** ($\\checkmark$) for protein synthesis and to make nitrogenous **bases in DNA** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0775","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0775\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n1\\.\nA student had a small pond in the backyard and wanted to add some fish. The fish would be predators of the pond and feed on the zooplankton (prey) growing in the pond. Zooplankton are small floating or weakly swimming organisms that are dependent on the phytoplankton or algae for their supply of nutrition.\n\nShe wanted to first determine whether Fish A or Fish B would be more suitable, or if both fish could be added to the pond. As a trial, she set up four mesocosms using water from the pond. She monitored the mesocosms and then added the fish to three of the mesocosms as shown. She took samples of water from the different mesocosms and tested them for chlorophyll content at a research lab nearby.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0775.png\" alt=\"BI0775\">\n\n1. Outline why the mesocosms used would be considered a ‘closed system’. [1 mark]\n\n2. Describe why adding mud or soil to the mesocosms would be advantageous. [1 mark]\n\n3. Outline the reason for including ‘Mesocosm A’ with the experiment. [1 mark]\n\n4. Explain the reason for monitoring chlorophyll content to analyse the effects of the fish as a predator. [1 mark]\n\n5. State another variable that could be monitored to see the effect of adding fish to the mesocosms. [1 mark]\n\nThe following table shows the results obtained for chlorophyll (μg dm:sup[-3]) with the different additions of fish.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0775B.png\" alt=\"BI0775B\">\n[Source: Adapted from Froneman, P. W. (2022, July 22). *Predator diversity does not contribute to increased prey risk: Evidence from a mesocosm study*. MDPI. Retrieved March 23, 2023, from https://www.mdpi.com/1424-2818/14/8/584, Copyright under CC-BY 4.0]\n\n6. From the results obtained, suggest why the student decided to add both fish A and fish B to the pond. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ energy can enter and leave the mesocosm system,(even if mesocosms are covered) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ matter **OR** organisms, cannot enter or leave the mesocosm system, mesocosms are covered [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The mesocosms are a ‘closed system’ because they are covered, and though energy can enter and leave the system, the **matter is not added or removed from the system** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mud will contain saprotrophs **OR** detritus [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mud will aid decomposition and cycling of nutrients in the mesocosm [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mud provides nutrients for algae [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mud increases habitat complexity so more surfaces for microorganisms to attach [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ more realistic ecosystem- more closely mimics the pond ecosystem [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n If mud is added to the mesocosms, this would also add decomposed material and aid in the **decomposition and cycling of nutrients** ($\\checkmark$) by bacteria and other organisms.\n\n3. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ control for the experiment [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ obtain results without fish to compare results when fish are added [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Mesocosm A serves as a **control for the experiment** ($\\checkmark$) and shows the values for chlorophyll when no fish are added.\n\n4. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chlorophyll concentrations will indicate how much (green) algae is present in the system [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the zooplankton eat the algae, so it is an indirect measure of zooplankton levels [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the more the algae, the lesser the levels of zooplankton [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ It is possible **OR** easier to obtain algae levels by measuring chlorophyll concentrations [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n It is possible to **estimate algae levels** ($\\checkmark$) in the system by measuring chlorophyll concentrations. As the fish eat the zooplankton, algal levels might increase, so it is an indirect measure of the zooplankton present.\n\n5. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the zooplankton levels could be monitored [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the zooplankton could be counted directly (using microscopy and counting of the organisms) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ diversity of zooplankton could be measured [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ water quality **OR** dissolved oxygen levels **OR** pH **OR** nutrient levels **OR** turbidity **OR** nitrate levels **OR** phosphate levels [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **zooplankton populations** ($\\checkmark$) could be monitored directly by counting them.\n\n6. Any **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the prey **OR** zooplankton populations were maintained in the presence of the predator **OR** fish [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ both fish A and fish B did not decrease the zooplankton population significantly when both were added together [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the fish A and fish B added together did not interfere with the stability of the system [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ adding fish A and B together did not significantly affect primary productivity **OR** chlorophyll levels [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **zooplankton populations were maintained** ($\\checkmark$) when fish A and fish B were added together. The addition of **both fish did not decrease** ($\\checkmark$) the zooplankton population. Both fish could survive together in the mesocosms."},{"ref":"BI0776","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0776\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA student wanted to study how food chains in leaf litter can be affected by the mass of initial leaf litter added. The student placed an increasing mass of leaf litter in three different tree holes and waited a few weeks. To collect data, the student collected all the leaf litter and placed it in an apparatus, as shown below. The heat from the light bulb dried out the litter from above and the heat and light drove the invertebrates through the wire mesh to the beaker below. The invertebrates were collected, the number of species counted, the species were identified and then they were put into their respective trophic groups.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0776A.png\" alt=\"BI0776A\">\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0776B.png\" alt=\"BI0776B\">\n\n1.\n 1. State the independent variable in the experiment. [1 mark]\n\n 2. State the dependent variable in the experiment. [1 mark]\n\n2. Suggest how the dry biomass of the leaf litter can be obtained. [2 marks]\n\n3. Explain why using the dry biomass of leaf litter is advantageous to using the fresh biomass. [1 mark]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0776C.png\" alt=\"BI0776C\">\n\n4. From the two graphs provided, describe the results of the experiment. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1. the following:\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ mass of leaf litter **OR** g of leaf litter [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n $\\hspace{1em}$ The independent variable is **‘mass of leaf litter’** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2. the following:\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ number of species [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n $\\hspace{1em}$ The dependent variable is **‘number of species’** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ leave the leaf litter and the filter (after the experiment and invertebrates removed) under lamp to dry **OR** dry in oven [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ weigh the leaf litter and the filter using a balance after it is dry [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the mass of the leaf litter is obtained by subtracting the mass of the filter [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The leaf litter, along with the filter, is **left to dry out under the lamp** ($\\checkmark$). The leaf litter and filter are weighed and then the mass of the **leaf litter is obtained by subtracting the mass of the filter** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fresh leaf litter may have different proportions of moisture [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ dry biomass gives a better measure of the mass of organic matter in the leaf litter available to the invertebrates [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ different proportions of water in fresh leaf litter can skew the results obtained **OR** make it difficult to compare results between samples [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n To compare the results obtained, it is better to use the biomass of dry leaf litter. Based on the position of the tree hole, such as the sunny or shady side, **the different samples of leaf litter could have different proportions of water** ($\\checkmark$) and will affect the results obtained.\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ with increasing mass of leaf litter, the length of the food chain increases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ with increasing mass of leaf litter, the number of species in the food chain increases [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n With increasing mass of leaf litter put in the tree holes, there is an increase in both **the length of the food chain** ($\\checkmark$) and **number of species present in the food chain** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0777","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0777\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nThe following image shows the productivity of a small terrestrial ecosystem with the numbers showing the flow of energy (kJ m:sup[-2] yr:sup[-1]).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0777CORA.png\" alt=\"BI0777CORA\">\n\nNet production = Gross production – energy lost to respiration\n\n1. Outline how light energy is sequentially transformed into other forms of energy in the terrestrial ecosystem shown in the image. [2 marks]\n\n2. Calculate the percentage of energy captured by the producers that is available to the secondary consumers in the ecosystem shown above. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ light energy $\\ce{ -> }$ chemical energy [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chemical energy $\\ce{ -> }$ heat energy [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ light energy from the sun is converted to chemical energy in organic molecules during photosynthesis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in respiration chemical energy in organic molecules is converted to chemical energy in ATP [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ during respiration some chemical energy is converted to heat energy [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n During photosynthesis **light energy** from the Sun **is converted into chemical energy** ($\\checkmark$) and then during **respiration** some of the **chemical energy is converted into heat energy** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. the following (units are required for marks to be awarded):\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 6.8 % [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0777MS.png\" alt=\"BI0777MS\">\n\n % (percentage) energy initially captured by producers available to secondary consumers :\n\n (1500/22000) x 100% = **6.82 %** ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"BI0781","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0781\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nIn a classroom investigation into the effects of herbal remedies on bacterial infections, students selected a range of herbal teas, marketed as possessing “Immune System Boosting” properties. Teas each contained one main ingredient, in addition to other secondary ingredients, present in lower concentrations.\n\nA concentrated solution of each tea was prepared and a 0.2 g mass of cotton wool was soaked in each.\n\nA petri dish was prepared using nutrient agar gel and inoculated with a strain of *Staphylococcus aureus*. The masses of cotton wool were each placed on the petri dish, which was then incubated at 20 :sup[o]C for 5 days, after which the zone of inhibition for each mass of cotton wool was measured.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0781A.png\" alt=\"BI0781A\">\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0781B.png\" alt=\"BI0781B\">\n\nThe experiment was repeated 3 times, a mean and standard deviation was calculated. Results are shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0781C.png\" alt=\"BI0781C\">\n\n1.\n 1. State the dependent variable in this investigation. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Identify one control variable from the methodology. [1 mark]\n\n2.\n 1. Based on the results, suggest which ingredient is most effective at inhibiting the growth of bacteria. Give a reason for your answer. [1 mark]\n\n 2. State the **TWO** brands of tea whose results are unlikely to show statistically significant differences. Give a reason for your answer. [2 marks]\n\n3. Calculate the size of the inhibition zone for EterniTea as a percentage of the size of that for ResisTea. [1 mark]\n\n4. Outline the techniques which should be employed in order to reduce risk when working with bacterial cultures. [2 marks]\n\n5.\n 1. Identify a limitation of the methodology in allowing a valid comparison of each tea brand. [1 mark]\n\n 2. State how the size of the error bars could be reduced. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n 1. (units are required in order for the mark to be awarded)\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ zone of inhibition /mm [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The dependent variable in this investigation is the **zone of inhibition** which is measured in **mm** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ green tea leaves used in all brands [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cultures incubated at 20 :sup[o]C **OR** for 5 days [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mass of cotton wool **OR** 0.2 g of cotton wool [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ *Staphylococcus aureus* used in all experiments **OR** same bacteria used [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cultures grown on nutrient agar **OR** the same medium [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n In this experiment, each brand of tea used was made from **green tea leaves** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ turmeric **BECAUSE** the tea with turmeric as the primary ingredient had the largest zone of inhibition [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ **Turmeric** is the most effective at inhibiting bacteria because it's the main ingredient in the tea with the **largest zone of inhibition** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ EterniTea **AND** ImmuBooster [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** the error bars are overlapping **OR** there is significant overlap in the spread of data [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The difference in the results from **EterniTea** and **ImmuBooster** ($\\checkmark$) are not likely to be statistically significant because as shown in the graph, the **error bars are overlapping** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 333% [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0781%20ANS%20A.png\" alt=\"BI0781 ANS A\">\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ EterniTea = 20mm \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ ResisTea = 6mm\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 20 / 6 = 3.33 \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 3.33 x 100% \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ = **333%** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ prepare a risk assessment [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ wear lab coat **OR** gloves **OR** protective clothing **OR** eye protection **OR** mask [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ wash hands before and after [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ use aseptic techniques **OR** sterile equipment [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ incubate below 30 :sup[o]C [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ no eating or drinking in the lab [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ label cultures clearly [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ autoclave waste materials [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ use disinfectants to clean surfaces [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n When working with bacterial cultures, you should create a **risk assessment** ($\\checkmark$) and take measures to reduce risk such as employing **aseptic techniques** ($\\checkmark$) like sterilising equipment and the work surface with alcohol.\n\n5.\n 1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ concentration of primary **OR** secondary ingredients in each tea could be different **OR** was not controlled **OR** other ingredients may be present in each tea **OR** some of the secondary ingredients in the tea types were the same as the primary ingredients of other teas [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ volume of tea solution absorbed by the cotton wool could be different **OR** shape of the mass of cotton wool may vary [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ only one bacterial strain was used [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n A potential limitation of the methodology in this experiment is that the **concentration** of the primary and secondary ingredients **may vary** between the different tea brands ($\\checkmark$), which makes it difficult to compare them.\n\n5.\n 2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ increase the number of trials **OR** repeats [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ remove anomalous data [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ reduce sources of error [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ reduce uncontrolled variables [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ improve precision of measurement of diameter **OR** use more accurate instrument of measurement **OR** use vernier callipers **OR** other suitable equipment [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ To reduce the size of the error bars, the experiment could be **repeated more times** ($\\checkmark$) to give a larger data set in which to spot anomalies."},{"ref":"BI0784","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0784\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nDissolved CO:sub[2] is increasing in standing waters of lakes and ponds. Research has shown this will impact the nutritional quality of phytoplankton (primary producers) and may further affect zooplankton (consumers) that feed on the phytoplankton.\n\nMesocosms were used to monitor three species of zooplankton, the water flea (*Daphnia magna*), the seed shrimp (*Heterocypris incongruens*) and the rotifer (*Brachionus calyciflorus*) under three different conditions of CO:sub[2] in ponds and shallow lakes:\n\n- Treatment C - present day levels of CO:sub[2], (control)\n- Treatment 1 - moderate increase of CO:sub[2] (possible future levels)\n- Treatment 2 - high increase of CO:sub[2]  (more extreme future levels)\n\nGlass jars filled with filtered pond water were injected with both carbon dioxide and air under pressure, and the representative species of zooplankton, water flea, seed shrimp or rotifer were added to the jars. They were monitored for survival, growth rate and body size over the next several days.\n\nThe image below shows the three species of zooplankton that were studied in the mesocosms.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0784%20a.png\" alt=\"BI0784 a\">\n[Adapted from Ramaekers, L., Pinceel, T., Brendonck, L., & Vanschoenwinkel, B. (2022, April 12). *Direct effects of elevated dissolved CO2 can alter the life history of Freshwater Zooplankton*. Nature News. Retrieved March 22, 2023, from https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-10094-2, Copyright under CC-BY 4.0]\n\nThe survival probability of the three species of zooplankton were monitored over several days in the mesocosms and the results are shown in the graphs below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0784b.png\" alt=\"BI0784b\">\n[Adapted from Ramaekers, L., Pinceel, T., Brendonck, L., & Vanschoenwinkel, B. (2022, April 12). Direct effects of elevated dissolved CO2 can alter the life history of Freshwater Zooplankton. Nature News. Retrieved March 22, 2023, from https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-10094-2,Copyright under CC-BY 4.0]\n\n1. Giving a reason, predict which zooplankton is the least resilient to the effects of increased carbon dioxide.    [1 mark]\n\n2. Describe how using mesocosms for research is both an advantage and a disadvantage in predicting the effects of increasing carbon dioxide in the atmosphere.     [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the water flea, because it could not survive treatment T2 or extreme future levels of CO:sub[2] and therefore was the most sensitive  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ both seed shrimp and rotifer could survive treatment T2 for more days and therefore the    water flea is the most sensitive to elevated levels of CO:sub[2]  [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Unlike the seed shrimp and rotifer, **the water flea could not survive treatment T2** ($\\checkmark$)  after the first two days and is therefore the most sensitive to future elevated levels of CO:sub[2].\n\n2. Any **one** from each section\n\n    **Advantages:**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ with a closed system it is easier to increase levels of CO:sub[2]     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mesocosms can be monitored to see the effects of elevated CO2    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mesocosms will yield results in a short period of time     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mesocosms allow small scale experiments to be set up    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reproducibility - can do repeats more easily to collect more data    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ costs - can be cheaper to set up [1 mark]\n\n    **Disadvantages:**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it is not possible to simulate all that happens in the environment     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it is unethical to add larger organisms to a mesocosm    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it can be difficult to set up mesocosms and monitor the changes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ often short term experiments do not capture the long term effects of changes in carbon dioxide [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    An advantage is that it is **easier to increase levels of CO:sub[2] in a closed system** ($\\checkmark$) like a mesocosm to study the effects. A disadvantage is that it is **not possible to simulate** ($\\checkmark$) exactly all that happens in the environment."},{"ref":"BI0785","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0785\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nTo study catalase enzyme activity, a student cut a turnip root into small cubes. They measured approximately 5 g in a weighing dish, added 50 ml water and used a blender to homogenize the turnip root. The homogenate was filtered and the liquid extract was stored in a test tube at room temperature.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0785A.png\" alt=\"BI0785A\">\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0785B.png\" alt=\"BI0785B\">\n\nThe turnip extract and water were added to the conical flask first. Then 3% hydrogen peroxide solution was added to the conical flask, as shown in the image above. The oxygen produced is collected over 1 minute and measured in an inverted measuring cylinder, as shown.\n\nThe student wanted to conduct an experiment to see the effect of increasing enzyme concentration on the reaction. She made up the different concentrations as shown in the table below and added 1 ml of 3% hydrogen peroxide to each.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0785C.png\" alt=\"BI0785C\">\n\n1. Suggest why the process of grinding the turnip root and extracting the filtrate was carried out. [1 mark]\n\n2. State the dependent variable. [1 mark]\n\n3. Explain why the volume of water was varied, as shown. [1 mark]\n\nThe following graph was obtained when the student varied the substrate (hydrogen peroxide) concentration, keeping the enzyme concentration constant.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0785D.png\" alt=\"BI0785D\">\n\n4. From the graph shown above, calculate the percentage increase in volume of oxygen gas produced when using 1% hydrogen peroxide compared to 0.5% hydrogen peroxide. [2 marks]\n\n5. Outline the reasons for the shape of the curve obtained. [2 marks]\n\nThe student repeated the same experiment but added an inhibitor along with the enzyme to the reaction mix. She got the following results.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0785E.png\" alt=\"BI0785E\">\n\n6. Deduce, with an explanation, whether a competitive or non-competitive inhibitor was used in the investigation. [2 marks]\n\n7. Other than the factors already mentioned, state one other factor that can lower the rate of an enzyme controlled reaction. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the catalase enzyme is extracted, **OR** released, from the cells by grinding [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ filtering remove, the parts of the plant cells that are not soluble, **OR** the cell walls, **OR** only allowed the solution through [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it is easier to vary the volume of enzyme added by using a filtrate, **OR** homogenate, of the turnip root cells [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The enzyme is **extracted from the turnip root cells** ($\\checkmark$) by grinding them and filtering to remove the parts of the plant cells that are not soluble.\n\n2. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the volume, **OR** ml min:sup[-1], of oxygen produced [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The dependent variable is the **volume of oxygen produced in 1 minute** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ different volumes of enzyme extract and water were used in each sample to provide different concentrations of enzyme [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ volume of water was added to get a final volume of 10ml for all concentrations of enzyme [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The different volumes of enzyme extract and water added were used **to produce different concentrations of enzyme** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. **Two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ values shown correctly, 1.6 for volume of O:sub[2] gas /ml min:sup[-1] for 0.5% H:sub[2]O:sub[2] and 3.2 for volume of O:sub[2] gas /ml min:sup[-1] for 1% H:sub[2]O:sub[2] [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 100% [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0785F.png\" alt=\"BI0785F\">\n\n % increase in oxygen gas released = [(3.2 - 1.6)/ 1.6] x 100% = **100%** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n5. **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ rate of reaction, **OR** volume of gas, increases with increased substrate concentration (up to 1.7%) **BECAUSE** more substrate molecules means more chances of successful collisions **OR** more enzyme-substrate-complexes form per unit time [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at higher concentrations gas production plateaus **BECAUSE** concentration of enzyme, **OR** number of enzyme molecules, become limiting **OR** at higher substrate concentrations the active sites on the enzyme are saturated [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n As more substrate is added, the rate of reaction **increases** ($\\checkmark$) up to 1.7% concentration of hydrogen peroxide. At higher concentrations of substrate, the enzyme concentration becomes limiting and therefore the curve **plateaus** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. Any **two** of the following (one for stating inhibitor is competitive)\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ inhibitor is competitive [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ competitive inhibitor competes with the substrate for the active site, so as substrate concentration increases, the activity of the enzyme **OR** rate of reaction, increases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ with a non-competitive inhibitor, even at high substrate concentrations the enzyme activity, **OR** rate of reaction, is always lower than when there is no inhibitor. [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at highest substrate concentrations enzyme activity rate, **OR** rate of reaction, is restored to, **OR** is the same as, levels without inhibitor [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ a graph sketched and annotated to show how each point above can be awarded the appropriate marks [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BIO785G.png\" alt=\"BIO785G\">\n\n At higher concentrations of substrate **the rate of enzyme activity is nearly as high as without an inhibitor** ($\\checkmark$) which shows that the inhibitor is competitive. With a non-competitive inhibitor, even at higher concentrations of substrate , **the enzyme activity rate is lower** ($\\checkmark$) than when there is no inhibitor.\n\n7. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperatures either side of the optimum temperature **OR** exposure to extreme temperatures [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ pH away from the optimum [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ surface area if in solid form [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Varying the pH **away from the optimum** ($\\checkmark$) (by adding acid or alkali to the enzyme extract) can lower the activity of the enzyme."},{"ref":"BI0786","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0786\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nCancer studies have shown that many tumours have abnormal patterns of aerobic respiration, showing increased levels of glycolysis and lower levels of oxidative phosphorylation, even in the presence of high oxygen levels. A breast cancer cell line (MDA-MB-231) shows variable protein expression of complex III in the electron transport chain compared to normal breast tissue.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0786.png\" alt=\"BI0786\">\n[Source: Adapted from : Owens, K. M., Kulawiec, M., Desouki, M. M., Vanniarajan, A., & Singh, K. K. (2011). *Impaired Oxphos Complex III in breast cancer. PLoS ONE, 6(8)*. Retrieved April 1, 2023, from https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0023846 Copyright free]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0786CHGCOR.png\" alt=\"BI0786CHGCOR\">\n\n1. State the independent variable in this study. [1 mark]\n\n2. Outline the role of the complexes in the electron transport chain. [2 marks]\n\n3. Suggest one advantage and one disadvantage of breast cancer cells using glycolysis to generate ATP rather than oxidative phosphorylation. [2 marks]\n\n4. Predict, with a reason, what would happen to the levels of oxidative phosphorylation in the non-cancerous cells if they were treated with an inhibitor of glycolysis. [1 mark]\n5. Explain why non-cancerous breast cancer cells were used as a control in this study. [1 mark]\n\n6. The researchers concluded that downregulation of complex III in breast cancer cells is a cause of breast cancer. Evaluate this conclusion. [2 marks]\n\n7. Outline one measurement that could be taken to determine the activity of Complex III in this study. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the type of cell [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The independent variable is whether the **cell type** ($\\checkmark$) is cancerous or not.\n\n2. any **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ oxidises reduced NAD/NADH **OR** oxidises reduced FAD/FADH:sub[2] [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ releases electrons [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ electrons move from complex to complex, releasing energy **OR** electrons are passed along complexes by redox reactions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ energy released is used to pump protons from the matrix to the intermembrane space [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ protons pass through ATP synthase down a concentration gradient **OR** using facilitated diffusion releasing energy, which is used to phosphorylate ADP [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ last complex **OR** complex IV, passes electrons onto oxygen **OR** reduces oxygen, so more electrons can enter electron transport chain [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The complexes **oxidise reduced NAD** ($\\checkmark$), which **releases electrons** ($\\checkmark$). The electrons move from complex to complex, **releasing energy** ($\\checkmark$) which is used to **pump protons** from the matrix into the **intermembrane space** ($\\checkmark$). This creates a **concentration gradient**, so protons move down the concentration gradient, through ATP synthase releasing energy which is used to **phosphorylate ADP to ATP** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **two** of the following (*answer must include **one** disadvantage and **one** advantage to gain full marks*):\n\n ***Advantage***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycolysis releases ATP more quickly than oxidative phosphorylation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycolysis is able to occur in the presence and absence of oxygen, whereas oxidative phosphorylation requires oxygen [1 mark]\n\n ***Disadvantage***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycolysis releases less energy per glucose molecule than oxidative phosphorylation **OR** glycolysis releases 2 ATP per glucose molecule whereas oxidative phosphorylation releases 36-38 ATP per glucose molecule [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n One advantage of breast cancer cells using glycolysis rather than oxidative phosphorylation to generate ATP is that **glycolysis generates ATP faster** than oxidative phosphorylation ($\\checkmark$). One disadvantage is that **glycolysis releases less ATP per glucose molecule** than oxidative phosphorylation ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **one** of the following (*answer must include a reason to gain one mark*):\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ decrease **BECAUSE** oxidative phosphorylation relies on the production of pyruvate from glycolysis [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The levels of oxidative phosphorylation would decrease, because oxidative phosphorylation **relies on the production of pyruvate** from glycolysis ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ indicates that any differences between the two cell types are only due to cancer [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Non-cancerous breast cancer cells were used as a control in this study as this indicates that any **differences between the two cell types are only due to cancer** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. any **two** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** reason for and **one** against to gain full marks*):\n\n ***Reasons for***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ there is a clear difference between expression of complex III in the breast cancer cell line compared to the non-cancerous cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ altered respiration in the cancer cell line can contribute to uncontrolled tumour growth [1 mark]\n\n ***Reasons against***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ study shows correlation not causation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ study was only conducted on one cell line [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ there may be other causes of breast cancer [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ there is no information on what other variables are being controlled [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The study shows the downregulation of complex III may be a cause of breast cancer as **altered respiration** in the cancer cell line can contribute to **uncontrolled tumour growth** ($\\checkmark$), however, there may be **another factor** which is causing uncontrolled cell division ($\\checkmark$). The study was conducted on only **one cell line** ($\\checkmark$) so it is unknown whether other cancer cell lines also have reduced levels of complex III.\n\n7. any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the concentration of ATP in mitochondria extracted from cancer cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the proportion of cyt-c **OR** cytochrome-c that is reduced [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ change in pH between the intermembrane space and matrix [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n One measurement that could be taken to determine the activity of complex III is the concentration of **ATP in mitochondria extracted from cancer cells** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0796","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0796\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nStudents were investigating metabolic reactions in the classroom and were given an experiment which demonstrated the effect of temperature on a catabolic reaction.\n\nA sample of fat was added to a beaker, which contained 150ml of a 1% sodium chloride solution.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0796A.png\" alt=\"BI0796A\">\n\nThe beaker and its contents were then added to a water bath, which was set at 15 :sup[o]C. The beaker was given 5 minutes to equalise with the temperature of the water bath. After 5 minutes, 5 ml of a 2 % lipase solution, at the same temperature as the water bath, was added to the beaker.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0796B.png\" alt=\"BI0796B\">\n\n5 ml of 2 % sodium hydroxide (NaOH), pH 8.4, was added to a test tube and phenolphthalein, a colourless solution which turns pink in basic environments, was then added. The intensity of the pink colour is proportionate to the strength of the basic solution.\n\nAt exactly 20 minutes, a 5ml sample was taken from the beaker incubated in the water bath and added to the test tube.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0796C.png\" alt=\"BI0796C\">\n\nThe contents of the test tube were then immediately transferred into a cuvette and placed in a spectrophotometer set at 420 nm. The percentage transmission of light able to pass through the sample was measured and recorded.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0796D.png\" alt=\"BI0796D\">\n\nThe procedure was then repeated using water baths set at a range of different temperatures. The measurements were recorded in a table and presented in a graph.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0796E.png\" alt=\"BI0796E\">\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0796F.png\" alt=\"BI0796F\">\n\n1.\n 1. State the independent variable in this experiment. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Identify two factors, not already mentioned, that would need to be controlled in this experiment. [1 mark]\n\n2.\n 1. Explain how the spectrophotometer is able to measure the breakdown of fat. [3 marks]\n\n 2. Explain the effect of increasing the temperature from 15 :sup[o]C to 35 :sup[o]C on the rate of the catabolic reaction. [2 marks]\n\n3. Predict the percentage transmission for a sample incubated at 20 :sup[o]C. [1 mark]\n\n4. Evaluate the claim that based on the data, the optimum temperature for lipase activity is 35 :sup[o]C. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1. (units are required for the mark to be awarded)\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature :sup[o]C [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The independent variable in this investigation is the **temperature (:sup[o]C)** of the water bath that the beakers are incubated in ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2. Any **two** of the following needed to gain the mark:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mass **OR** surface area of fat samples, **OR** type of fat **OR** size of beaker **OR** volume of phenolphthalein [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n In this experiment, the **mass of the fat sample** and the **volume of phenolphthalein** should be kept the same ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ enzyme lipase breaks down fat into fatty acids and glycerol [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fatty acids are acidic [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ phenolphthalein is pink in basic environments **OR** is pink when added to sodium hydroxide [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the intensity of the pink colour reduces as the pH of the solution decreases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the pH of the solution can be used as a measure of the concentration of fatty acids in a sample **OR** a lower pH can indicate a higher concentration of fatty acids [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the intensity of the pink colour **OR** the percentage of light transmission can be measured using the spectrophotometer [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ a higher percentage of % of light passes through the sample **OR** a higher light transmission result **OR** a lower colour intensity, correlates to a higher concentration of fatty acids in the sample [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The enzyme lipase breaks down fat into **fatty acids and glycerol** ($\\checkmark$). Fatty acids are **acidic** ($\\checkmark$) because they have a carboxyl group. When the sample containing the fatty acids is added to the solution with NaOH and phenolphthalein, the intensity of the pink colour will decrease depending on the concentration of fatty acids in the sample. The higher the percentage of **light transmission**, which is measured using the **spectrophotometer** ($\\checkmark$), the greater the **concentration of fatty acids** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as the temperature increases (from 15 :sup[o]C to 35 :sup[o]C) the kinetic energy of the molecules increases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as kinetic energy increases there are more successful collisions between the enzyme and the substrate **OR** between lipase and fat [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as temperature increases (from 15 :sup[o]C to 35 :sup[o]C), the rate of reaction increases **OR** rate of fat digestion increases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ more enzyme-substrate complexes form [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n As the temperature increased from 15 :sup[o]C to 35 :sup[o]C, the **kinetic energy** of the molecules **increased** ($\\checkmark$). An increase in the kinetic energy resulted in an increase in the number of **successful collisions** between the enzyme lipase and its substrate fat ($\\checkmark$), which in turn, **increased** the rate of reaction ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. (units are required for the mark to be awarded)\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 31.5% [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0796ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0796ANSA\">\n\n Mean % transmission at 15 :sup[o]C = $\\left(\\dfrac{\\text{22 +20+20}}{\\text{3}}\\right) = 20.67 = 21\\% $ (2s.f.)\n\n Mean % transmission at 25 :sup[o]C = $\\left(\\dfrac{\\text{42+44+41}}{\\text{3}}\\right) = 42.33 = 42\\% $ (2s.f.)\n\n 20 :sup[o]C is half way between 15 :sup[o]C and 25 :sup[o]C\n\n Predicted % transmission value at 20 :sup[o]C\n\n = mean at 15 :sup[o]C + half the difference between mean % transmission at25 :sup[o]C and 15 :sup[o]C\n\n = 21% + 10.5%\n\n = **31.5%** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4. Any **TWO** of the following:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 35 :sup[o]C has the highest percentage transmission of all temperatures used in the experiment **OR** the greatest rate of breakdown in this experiment [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ **HOWEVER** the optimum temperature may be between 25 :sup[o]C and 35 :sup[o]C **OR** 35 :sup[o]C and 55 :sup[o]C [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ to find the optimal temperature, more experiments would need to be carried out at narrower increments of temperature around 35 :sup[o]C **OR** to get closer to finding the optimum temperature repeat the experiment at temperatures of 30, 32, 34, 36, 38, 40 :sup[o]C [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 35 :sup[o]C may be the optimum temperature for lipase activity because in the experiment it yielded the **highest percentage transmission** ($\\checkmark$). However, because temperatures between 25 :sup[o]C - 35 :sup[o]C and 35 :sup[o]C - 55 :sup[o]C **were not tested** ($\\checkmark$), the optimum temperature could lie between these temperatures."},{"ref":"BI0806","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0806\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nThe following graphs show the concentration of atmospheric carbon dioxide over time at Mauna Loa Observatory in Hawaii, United States.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0806A.png\" alt=\"BI0806A\">\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0806B.png\" alt=\"BI0806B\">\n[Source: Adapted from US Department of Commerce, NOAA. *“Global Monitoring Laboratory - Carbon Cycle Greenhouse Gases.”* GML, ( Oct. 2005). Retrieved 18 May from gml.noaa.gov/ccgg/trends/. Copyright under CC0 4.0]\n\n1. Describe the overall trend in atmospheric carbon dioxide levels over time. [1 mark]\n\n2. State one factor that has significantly contributed to increases in atmospheric carbon dioxide concentration as seen in the graphs.    [1 mark]\n\n3. Explain why the concentration of atmospheric carbon dioxide fluctuates annually. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. The following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide levels in the atmosphere have been rising continuously since the 1960s [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Carbon dioxide levels in the atmosphere **have increased from 1960 to 2020** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increasing use **OR** increase in combustion of fossil fuels to provide energy    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in the numbers of cars and other vehicles [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ deforestation **OR** land use changes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ improper waste management **OR** decomposition of organic waste in landfills   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ industrial activities    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The main cause has been the **increased number of cars** and other vehicles ($\\checkmark$) being used.\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ during the summer (in the northern hemisphere), **OR** spring (in the northern hemisphere) **OR** growing season, an increase in vegetation **OR** biomass **OR** temperature causes an increase in photosynthesis which results in increased fixation of carbon dioxide, causing a decrease in atmospheric concentration     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ during the autumn (in the northern hemisphere) **OR** winter (in the northern hemisphere), there is a decrease in photosynthesis and an increase in respiration due to cooler temperatures which results in an increase of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vegetation decay  during the dormant season **OR** autumn **OR** winter (in the northern hemisphere) and carbon dioxide is released        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fluctuations in ocean carbon exchange in response to temperature changes **OR** cooler temperatures more carbon dioxide absorbed by oceans, reducing atmospheric carbon dioxide **OR** warmer temperatures ocean releases carbon dioxide into the atmosphere [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The annual fluctuations in atmospheric carbon dioxide concentrations are primarily driven by the **seasonal patterns** of **photosynthesis** and **respiration in plants**.  In the northern hemisphere, there is an **increase** in **plant growth** in the **summer**, which **increases carbon dioxide fixation** by **photosynthesis** ($\\checkmark$). In **winter**, there is a **decrease in photosynthesis**, so **reduced carbon dioxide uptake** by plants ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0807","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0807\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe image below shows global sea levels from the years 1880 to 2020.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0807.png\" alt=\"BI0807\">\n[Source: Adapted from Wikipedia, ( May 2023), *“Sea Level Rise.”*\nRetrieved 17 May 2023, from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea_level_rise Copyright under CC0 3.0]\n\n1. There is a trend line showing changes in sea level from 1920 to 1960 and another trend line showing changes in sea level between 1980 and 2020. Describe the differences in sea level change between these two periods of time. [1 mark]\n\n2. Outline the reasons for linking atmospheric carbon dioxide levels to the rise in sea levels. [2 marks]\n\nEcosystems growing in salt marshes are able to help reduce the effects of climate change by the sequestration (capture) of carbon. Sea level rise (SLR) has affected these ecosystems by flooding and eroding coastal areas.\n\nThe results from a recent study modelling the effects of a one-metre sea level rise on carbon sequestration by salt marshes in New York, United States, are shown in the graph below. The data shows the predicted effects by the year 2100.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0807B.png\" alt=\"BI0807B\">\n[Source: Adapted from Tanner, Katherine, and Aaron L. Strong. ( Mar, 2023) *Sustainability 2023, 15(6), 4733; “Assessing the Impact of Future Sea Level Rise on Blue Carbon Ecosystem Services on Long Island, New York.” MDPI*, Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute. Retrieved May 15, 2023, from doi.org/10.3390/su15064733, Copyright under CC0 4.0]\n\n3. Suggest an explanation for the difference in carbon sequestration between no SLR and one meter SLR, shown in the prediction graph. [1 mark]\n\n4. Draw a diagram of the carbon cycle for a terrestrial ecosystem, and include carbon sinks, and processes associated with the cycle. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. The following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the trend lines show that the increase in sea levels for the years 1920 to 1960 is not as steep as seen for the years 1980 to 2020 [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The increase in sea levels (as shown by the trend lines) **is steeper for the years from 1980 to 2020** ($\\checkmark$) compared to the years from 1920 to 1960.\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increasing carbon dioxide levels cause an increase in the temperature of the atmosphere [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increasing atmospheric temperatures cause glaciers **OR** ice sheets to melt more rapidly which raises sea levels [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increasing ocean temperatures cause thermal expansion of the ocean waters causing sea levels to rise [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Increasing carbon dioxide levels **cause an increase in atmospheric temperature** ($\\checkmark$) which can lead to **increased melting of ice sheets** ($\\checkmark$) and a rise in sea levels.\n\n3. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the sea level rise may damage photosynthesising organisms **OR** producers, in the salt marshes **OR** coastal ecosystems so decrease the capture of carbon [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ erosion may release carbon into the atmosphere [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ rising sea level increases the depth of water in salt marshes resulting in less light reaching aquatic photosynthesising organisms' habitats [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Sea level rises may kill some photosynthesising organisms and so **decrease the capture of carbon by photosynthesis** ($\\checkmark$) thereby affecting the carbon cycle.\n\n4. Any **three** of the following sinks or storages with at least **three** different connecting processes relevant to those sinks or storages:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ CO$_2$ in the air **AND** combustion **OR** respiration **OR** photosynthesis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon compounds in producers **AND** photosynthesis **OR** respiration **OR** consumed by **OR** death [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon compounds in consumers **AND** respiration **OR** consumed by **OR** death [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon compounds in saprotrophs OR decomposers **OR** detritivores **AND** decomposition **OR** respiration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon compounds in dead organisms **AND** fossilisation **OR** death **OR** decomposition [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon compounds in fossil fuels **AND** combustion **OR** fossilisation [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0807BII.png\" alt=\"BI0807BII\">"},{"ref":"BI0818","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0818\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nDuring a field day, students were asked to investigate the effect of soil salinity on the distribution of plant species. Students designed an experiment that measured the number of plant species found up the shore at varying distances from the high tide line. Three groups of students investigated three sites on the same shore. The distance from the high tide line was taken as an indication of the salinity levels. The results are shown in the table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0818A.png\" alt=\"BI0818A\">\n\n1.\n    1. Identify the independent variable. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Outline the steps students could take, and the equipment they could use, to collect their results.                                [3 marks]\n\n2.\n    1. Suggest a reason for the discrepancy in the number of species sampled in areas 1, 2, and 3.                                     [1 mark]\n\n    2. Describe a way the students could reduce the impact of discrepancies on their collected data.                             [1 mark]\n\n3. The different species of plants found in each area were recorded in a table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0818B.png\" alt=\"BI0818B\">\n\nCalculate how many times greater the number of plant species found in Area 1 was compared to Area 2.                                 [1 mark]\n\n4. Evaluate the students’ investigation into salinity's effect on plant species distribution.                                 [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. (units are required for the mark):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ distance from the high tide line            [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **distance from the high tide line** ($\\checkmark$) is the independent variable.\n\n1.\n    2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use a measuring tape to measure 15m from the high tide line towards inland **OR** up shore                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use a belt transect (to mark out the area of investigation)    **OR**  transect line **OR** quadrats placed next to a measuring tape    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use a quadrat to sample plants every 3m **OR** at regular distances along the transect     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the distance between the transect and the quadrat should be kept constant [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ count the number of different plant species found within each quadrat     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use a clipboard **OR** notepad **OR** device for recording data   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use a dichotomous key **OR** plant identification guide **OR** expert to identify plant species [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    To carry out their investigation, students could use a measuring tape to **measure 15 m** up the shore away **from the high tide line** ($\\checkmark$). Place a **quadrat every 3 m** along the tape to **form a belt transect** ($\\checkmark$). Record the **number** of different **plant species** found in **each quadrat** ($\\checkmark$) in a results table.\n\n2.\n    1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some areas may have different soil **OR** soil structure **OR** more fertile soil **OR** more suitable soil pH **OR** salinity **OR** depth of soil **OR** mineral availability            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ human disturbances (footpaths, for example)        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light intensity                                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water availability                                 [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plant density                                     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ herbivory                                         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ accept any other valid description of habitat variation **OR** disturbance     [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    There may be discrepancies in the results because the areas sampled might have different soil types, with some being more **fertile** than others ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    2.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase the sampling frequency OR more quadrats OR more transects    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ decrease the distance between quadrats, so more taken within 15m [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase expertise **OR** use better resources to identify species [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ avoid using sites that clearly had foot trails                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ control more variables **OR** use areas with same angle of slope (accept any suitable example) [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Students could reduce the impact of discrepancies by increasing sampling frequency by adding more quadrats ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 1.29 **OR** 1.286            [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0818ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0818ANSA\">\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Area 1 - No. of plant species  = 9\\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Area 2 - No. of plant species = 7\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 9/7 = **1.29** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4. Any **three** of the following (must include strengths and weaknesses):\n\n    **Strengths**\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ students sampled three areas **OR** more than one area, **WHICH MEANS** they can calculate a mean from their results **OR** increases the reliability of their results        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ all areas sampled were at the same beach, **WHICH** decreases the effects of confounding variables between trials        [1 mark]\n\n    **Limitations**\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the salinity of the soil wasn’t measured, **OR** distance from the high tide line was used as an indirect measurement, **WHICH** means the research question is not addressed, **OR** a conclusion cannot be reached        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large discrepancies in students' results **MEANS** that the data isn’t reliable    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ more areas should be sampled to improve reliability    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lack of controlled variables, so students should identify variables such as soil moisture **OR** angle of slope **OR** soil pH **OR** light intensity, **OR** other valid controlled variable (example must be given to award mark)  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ all areas sampled were at the same beach, **WHICH** means the results may not be valid for other beaches            [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The students sampled **more than three** areas so a mean could be taken, and this increases the **reliability** ($\\checkmark$) of the results. However, there were **large discrepancies** between student results ($\\checkmark$), so **more areas need to be sampled** ($\\checkmark$). Also, the **salinity** of the soil was **not measured**; instead, they relied on **indirect measurements**, which means a conclusion can only be supported to a limited degree ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0820","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0820\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nChitin is an abundant polysaccharide in nature and is a major component of arthropod exoskeletons. In an experiment, scientists tested the effect of various chitin synthesis inhibitors - diflubenzuron (DFB), hexaflumuron (HX) and teflubenzuron (TFB) - on the moulting process of the salmon louse (*Lepeophtheirus salmonis*) which is a known parasite of salmon.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0820A.png\" alt=\"BI0820A\">\n[Source: Adapted from Harðardóttir, HM., Male, R., Nilsen, F., Dalvin, S.(2019). *Effects of chitin synthesis inhibitor treatment on Lepeophtheirus salmonis (Copepoda, Caligidae) larvae*. PLoS ONE 14(9): e0222520. Retrieved May 27, 2023.  https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0222520. Copyright free]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI0820B.png\" alt=\"BI0820B\">\n[Source: Adapted from Harðardóttir, HM., Male, R., Nilsen, F., Dalvin, S.(2019). *Effects of chitin synthesis inhibitor treatment on Lepeophtheirus salmonis (Copepoda, Caligidae) larvae*. PLoS ONE 14(9): e0222520. Retrieved May 27, 2023.  https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0222520. Copyright free]\n\n1.\n    1. State, which is the independent variable and dependent variable. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Suggest which chitin synthesis inhibitor is most effective at reducing the moulting success of salmon louse. Justify your answer. [1 mark]\n\n    3. Suggest why the chitin synthesis inhibitors were administered at different concentrations.    [1 mark]\n\n2.\n    1. Exoskeletons provide an anchorage for muscles to attach to. In mammals, which type of tissue attaches muscle to the bone?     [1 mark]\n\n    2. Describe another function of the exoskeleton.     [1 mark]\n\n3. Outline the relationship between the type of chitin inhibitor, concentration and the percentage of incomplete moultings in salmon lice (*Lepeophtheirus salmonis*).     [3 marks]\n\n4. As shown, chitin is visible as dark areas present on the surface of arthropods. Suggest one apparatus that could have been used to view the exoskeletons of the moulting salmon lice accurately.     [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. (students must identify the type of variable to achieve the mark):\n\n    **independent variable**:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type of chitin inhibitor at different concentrations [1 mark]\n\n    **dependent variable**:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ % of animals with incomplete moulting  [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The independent variable is the **type of inhibitor** at different concentrations ($\\checkmark$) and the dependent variable is the **percentage of animals with incomplete moultings** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    2.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ HX **BECAUSE** at the lowest concentrations, it causes the greatest increase in incomplete moulting, **OR** it is the most effective at reducing moulting success at lower concentrations    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **HX** is the most effective chitin synthesis inhibitor because it’s the most effective at reducing moulting success at **lower concentrations** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some inhibitors may be lethal **OR** harmful at specific concentrations so data cannot be included for dead lice                            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some inhibitors may be ineffective at some concentrations    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to determine the lowest concentration necessary to achieve the desired percentage of incomplete moultings     [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The concentrations used may have been different because some of the inhibitors used might be **lethal** and kill the salmon louse larvae **at specific concentrations** , and therefore, **data cannot be included** for measurements of the percentage of animals with incomplete moulting ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    1.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tendons        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Tendons** ($\\checkmark$) attach muscle to bone.\n\n2.\n    2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ protection **OR** physical barrier to predators **OR** toxins **OR** pathogens    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prevents excessive water uptake **OR** loss     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ acts as a lever                                 [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sensory perception                                 [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ camouflage                                         [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The exoskeleton provides **protection** ($\\checkmark$) for the larvaes’ vital organs.\n\n3.\n    Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as the concentration of all inhibitors increases, the percentage of incomplete moultings increases **OR** positive relationship between the concentration of inhibitor and the percentage of incomplete moultings     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hexaflumuron is the most effective inhibitor at lower concentrations      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ diflubenzuron is required in relatively higher concentrations to have the same effect as other inhibitors       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at the relative highest concentration of each inhibitor, the percentage of incomplete moultings is approximately 90%       [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nFor all inhibitors, as the concentration increases, so does the percentage of incomplete moultings. This is a **positive relationship** ($\\checkmark$). Hexaflumuron impacts the percentage of incomplete moultings at **lower concentrations** relative to the other inhibitors ($\\checkmark$). Diflubenzuron is required in relatively **higher concentrations** compared to the other inhibitors in order to have a similar effect ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ viewed using a light microscope **OR** magnifying glass **OR** stereoscope     [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The salmon lice could be viewed using a **light microscope** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1142","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1142\n\nWhich type of reaction is involved in the formation of polysaccharides?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrolysis\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Condensation\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Decarboxylation\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Phosphorylation","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe formation of polysaccharides involves the removal of water to form a glycosidic bond. This is a condensation reaction.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as water is a product, not a reactant, in the formation of polysaccharides. **Choice C** is incorrect as no carboxyl group is removed and no CO:sub[2] is produced in the reaction. **Choice D** is incorrect as the reaction does not require the addition of a phosphate group."},{"ref":"BI1198","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1198\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe African bush elephant, *Loxodonta africana*, pushes over trees, maintaining grasslands that provide essential resources such as food for many other animals. Its dung also fertilises the soil and provides a habitat for many species of beetle and fungi. The following diagram shows the flow of inorganic nutrients from elephants to herbivores within this ecosystem.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI1198-SL.png\" alt=\"BI1198-SL\">\n\n1. Define the term ecosystem. [1 mark]\n\n2. Identify one abiotic factor and one biotic factor, other than food availability, affected by the presence of African bush elephants. [2 marks]\n\n3. The faeces buried by the dung beetle is broken down by decomposers. Outline how the action of decomposers increases soil nutrient content. [3 marks]\n\n4. Suggest one soil element, other than carbon, that would probably experience a reduction in concentration if dung beetles were absent from the ecosystem. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ all living organisms **OR** different living species, along with their physical environment **OR** abiotic factors [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n An ecosystem is **all** the **living organisms**, along with their **physical environment**, that **interact** and function together in a specific habitat ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **one** abiotic factor and any **one** biotic factor:\n\n ***abiotic factors:*** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increased soil nutrient content [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increased light intensity at ground level (due to reduced tree canopy) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ water availability (as a result of the digging of water holes) [1 mark]\n\n ***biotic factors:*** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increased abundance of beetles **OR** fungi **OR** decomposers **OR** soil microbes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ decreased abundance of trees **OR** increased area of grassland [1 mark]\n\n *Note: if a direction of change is given, it must be correct. Do not accept “increased abundance of trees”. Do not accept “vegetation” unless it is clear whether trees or grass is meant.*\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n African bush elephants push over trees, which increases light intensity at ground level, an **abiotic factor** ($\\checkmark$), and decreases the abundance of **trees**, a **biotic factor** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ decomposers break down organic compounds present in faeces using enzymes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ releases inorganic nutrients into the soil [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ saprotrophs break down organic compounds outside their bodies using enzymes, and some of the inorganic nutrients released remain in the soil [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ detritivores consume organic compounds present in faeces, digest them internally and release **OR** excrete **OR** egest inorganic nutrients into the soil [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Decomposers increase soil nutrient content by **breaking down organic compounds** present in faeces using **enzymes** ($\\checkmark$). The breakdown of these organic compounds **releases inorganic nutrients** into the soil ($\\checkmark$). For example, **saprotrophs** break down organic compounds **externally** using enzymes, and much of the resulting nutrients released **remain in the soil** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nitrogen **OR** phosphorus **OR** sulphur **OR** potassium **OR** calcium **OR** magnesium **OR** any other correct, named element [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Nitrogen** content in the soil would decrease if dung beetles were removed from the ecosystem ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1221","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1221\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nDNA and RNA are nucleic acids that are essential to all living organisms. ATP is a type of nucleotide.\n\n1. Distinguish between ATP and the monomers of DNA in humans. [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline the roles of the different types of RNA involved in translation.     [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe the structure of the DNA double helix.  [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP contains three phosphate groups **WHEREAS** each DNA nucleotide contains only one phosphate group         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP has adenine for a nitrogenous base **WHEREAS** a DNA nucleotide can contain adenine, cytosine, guanine or thymine    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a component of ATP is ribose **WHEREAS** DNA nucleotides have deoxyribose instead        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP stores energy **OR** provides energy, **WHEREAS** DNA carries **OR** transmits genetic information                          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP can perform its function as a single monomer **WHEREAS** DNA can only perform its function, **OR** carry genetic material as a polymer            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP is found throughout the cell **OR** primarily in the cytoplasm **OR** mitochondria **WHEREAS** DNA is found primarily in the nucleus **OR** only found in the nucleus AND mitochondria            [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Both ATP and DNA molecules are nucleic acids which contain a phosphate group, a sugar and a base. The structural difference between a molecule of ATP and a molecule of DNA is that one ATP molecule contains **three phosphate groups** whereas one DNA nucleotide contains **one phosphate group** ($\\checkmark$). In addition, the nitrogenous base in ATP is **adenine** whereas there are four possible nitrogenous bases in one nucleotide of DNA which are **adenine, cytosine, guanine and thymine** ($\\checkmark$). The functional difference is that ATP **stores energy**, whereas DNA **carries genetic material** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following (there must be at least one statement for each of mRNA, tRNA and rRNA to achieve maximum marks):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA carries the genetic information from DNA to the ribosome            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA serves as a template for protein synthesis        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ codons of mRNA  specify positioning of  amino acids along polypeptide **OR** codons guide the assembly of amino acids into a polypeptide         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tRNA molecules carry amino acids to mRNA **OR** ribosome    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ anticodons on tRNA complementary to codons on mRNA allow amino acids to be positioned correctly along the polypeptide **OR** anticodons bind to codons to bring corresponding amino acid to the ribosome             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rRNA provides the ribosome's structural framework **OR** forms ribosomes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rRNA catalyses the formation of peptide bonds between adjacent amino acids     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rRNA facilitates binding of mRNA and tRNA        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA, tRNA, and rRNA orchestrate protein synthesis during translation    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Together, mRNA, tRNA, and rRNA** orchestrate the process of **protein synthesis** ($\\checkmark$) during translation. **mRNA carries** the genetic **information** from **DNA** to the **ribosome**  ($\\checkmark$). **Codons** on the mRNA **specify the positioning** of **amino acids** along the **polypeptide** chain  ($\\checkmark$). Each **tRNA** molecule has a specific **anticodon** sequence that is **complementary** to a **codon** on the mRNA  ($\\checkmark$). This base-pairing specificity allows tRNA to bring the **corresponding amino acid** to the **correct position** to form the polypeptide  ($\\checkmark$). The rRNA **catalyses** the formation of **peptide bonds** between adjacent amino acids  ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following: \\\n    *(appropriately labelled and correctly drawn DNA segment can be used in the answer)*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ each strand of DNA is a polymer of nucleotides         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ each strand has a sugar-phosphate backbone            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ four nitrogenous bases A **OR** adenine **AND** T **OR** thymine **AND** C **OR** cytosine **AND** G **OR** guanine              [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ two strands are anti-parallel **OR** parallel but in opposite directions **OR** one strand is in the 5’ to 3’ direction and the other strand is in the 3’ to 5’ direction    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ strands are twisted **OR** right-handed and turn into a double helix with around 10 base pairs per turn          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleotides consist of one phosphate group, one deoxyribose sugar and one nitrogenous base                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phosphate group is bound to the 5’ carbon of the sugar **OR** deoxyribose **OR** pentose                           [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phosphate group is bound to the 3’ carbon OH group of deoxyribose (of the next nucleotide)                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ covalent **OR** sugar-phosphate bond between phosphate and sugar        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nitrogenous base **OR** base is bound to the 1’ carbon of the sugar **OR** deoxyribose **OR** pentose sugar             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ covalent bond between nitrogenous base and deoxyribose **OR** sugar **OR** pentose sugar                     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ two strands are held together by hydrogen bonds between nitrogen bases of two nucleotides **OR** 3 hydrogen bonds between guanine and cytosine **OR** 2 hydrogen bonds between adenine and thymine    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ complementary base pairing between nitrogenous bases    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ A pairs up with T AND C pairs up with G        [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    DNA is a nucleic acid, also known as a **polymer of nucleotides** ($\\checkmark$) . Many nucleotides are joined together to form it. **One nucleotide** consists of three parts; a **phosphate group, deoxyribose sugar and a nitrogenous base** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    One end of the **phosphate group** is bonded to the **5’ carbon** of the **deoxyribose sugar** ($\\checkmark$). On the other side of the nucleotide, **the phosphate group is bonded to the 3’ carbon sugar** ($\\checkmark$) of another nucleotide. Both bonds are covalent **sugar-phosphate bonds** ($\\checkmark$). Repeated alternating sugar-phosphate bonds form the **sugar-phosphate backbone** ($\\checkmark$).  The **1’ carbon of the sugar is covalently bonded to the nitrogenous base** ($\\checkmark$) also through a condensation reaction to make up one complete nucleotide.\n\n    **DNA is a double helix** ($\\checkmark$) in which nitrogenous bases form the centre of the structure, and **both strands are held together by hydrogen bonds** ($\\checkmark$) between the complementary bases from each strand. **A** pairs up **with T**, and **C** always pairs up **with G** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1407","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1407\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe *Allium* genus contains leeks, garlics and chives. The images below show two members of the *Allium* genus that are native to Lithuania and their associated chromosome numbers. Polyploidy events have occurred to produce *Allium oleraeceum*.\n\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI1407A.png\" alt=\"BI1407A\"> | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI1407B.png\" alt=\"BI1407B\"> |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| *Allium angulosum* | *Allium oleraeceum* |\n| 2n = 16 | 4n = 32 |\n\n[Source: Adapted from Lefnaer, S. (2015, July 26). *Category: allium angulosum*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved January 8, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Allium_angulosum_sl3.jpg. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n[Source: Adapted from Leidus, I. (2013, July 7). *File: allium oleraceum - rohulauk.jpg* - wikimedia commons. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved January 8, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:  Allium_oleraceum_-_rohulauk.jpg. Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n1. Suggest how polyploidy may occur in plants.    [1 mark]\n\n2. Outline how polyploidy in a species can lead to speciation.        [2 marks]\n\n3. With reference to plant hormones, explain the directional growth observed in the image of *Allium oleraceum*.        [3 marks]\n\n4. Plants within the *Allium* genus often have bulbs which grow underground. Outline how bulbs can be considered sinks **and** sources in the process of translocation.        [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **one**  of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hybridisation events between closely related plant species **OR** gametes of two closely related plant species fuse        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ extra set of chromosomes formed during DNA replication stage in interphase **OR** cell cycle [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a diploid gamete fuses with a haploid gamete **OR** two diploid gametes fuse together [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ failed spindle formation **OR** non-disjunction **OR** failed separation of chromosomes **IN** meiosis leading to gametes with extra set of chromosomes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chromosomal duplications in cells involved in vegetative **OR** asexual reproduction [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Polyploidy can occur in plants if there are **hybridisation** events between **closely related plant** species ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms with a different number of chromosomes **OR** new tetraploid and original diploid cannot breed together to produce fertile offspring    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ results in reproductive isolation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polyploid plants may be larger **OR** more fertile **OR** better adapted than original populations allowing them to exploit different ecological niches leading to geographical isolation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ once isolated polyploid population diverges genetically from original populations    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The new polyploid organism has a different number of chromosomes than the original plant and so they **cannot breed** together to produce **fertile offspring** ($\\checkmark$). This can result in **reproductive isolation** ($\\checkmark$), which could lead to the formation of a new species.\n\n3. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plant stems **OR** *Allium oleraceum* stems respond to light by growing towards it **OR** plant stems are positively phototropic            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ auxin is produced by actively dividing cells in the tip of the stem **OR** in the meristem     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light causes auxin to accumulate on the shaded side of the stem     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ auxin efflux pumps bring about increased concentration on shaded side    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ this causes cells on the shaded side to elongate                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ auxin causes protons to be pumped from cytoplasm of cells into cell wall lowering pH    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lowering of pH loosens linkages between cellulose fibres so when water enters cell wall stretches and cell elongates    [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    *Allium oleraceum* has a **positive phototropic** response to light ($\\checkmark$).  **Auxin** is released from the **actively dividing cells** of the **meristem** ($\\checkmark$). Light stimulates auxin to **accumulate** on the **shaded** side of the **stem** ($\\checkmark$), so that **cells** on the **shaded side elongate** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **two**  of the following:\\\n    *Answer must include statements supporting bulbs being a sink and supporting bulbs being a source to gain full marks*\n\n    **Bulbs are a source because…**\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when the plant is not actively photosynthesising, it uses sucrose **OR** starch **OR** carbohydrate **OR** nutrients **OR** amino acids stored in bulb        [1 mark]\n\n    **Bulbs are a sink because…**\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when the plant is actively photosynthesising it will provide carbohydrates **OR** glucose **OR** sucrose **OR** starch, for storage in **OR** growth of, bulb    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    At the **beginning** of the **growing** season, the bulb is a **source** because the plant is **not** actively **photosynthesising**, so it needs to use the **sucrose and amino acids stored in the bulbs** for **growth** ($\\checkmark$).   However, during the **middle** of the growing season, when the plant is **actively photosynthesising**, **sucrose** is **transported** from the **leaves to the bulb** to **store** the **excess carbohydrate** and so the bulb becomes a **sink** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1457","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1457\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe Food Conversion Ratio (FCR) is a measure commonly used in livestock production to assess the efficiency at which animals convert feed into the desired output, such as weight gain. Below is a graph showing the mean food conversion ratios of four different farmed animals.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI1457%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1457 fig1\">\n\n1. Deduce, with a reason, which animal is most sustainable to farm. [1 mark]\n\n2. Suggest why there is such a large difference between the food conversion ratio of cattle and fish. [2 marks]\n\nArtificial fertilisers are often used in agriculture and can be washed into watercourses, leading to eutrophication.\n\n3. Describe the process of eutrophication. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. The following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ crickets **BECAUSE** they have the highest food-to-body mass conversion ratio **OR** they have the lowest food-conversion ratio [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n **Crickets** as they have they are the **most efficient** at **converting food to body mass** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cattle have a higher respiration **OR** metabolic rate than fish [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cattle are endothermic, so need to expend a significant portion of their ingested energy to maintain a constant body temperature, whereas fish are ectothermic temperature of their bodies adjusts to match the water in which they live [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ digestion in cattle is less energy-efficient compared to the digestion processes in fish [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cattle spend energy on locomotion and supporting their body weight against gravity, whereas fish benefit from buoyancy, which reduces the energy needed for supporting their body weight and movement [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ growth rate of cattle is slower than that of fish [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n **Cattle**, being **endothermic**, expend **significant energy** to maintain a **constant body temperature**, contrasting with **ectothermic fish** whose body temperature adapts to their aquatic environment, leading to **lower metabolic demands** ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, **cattle** need to support their **body weight** against **gravity**, and their locomotion on land consumes **more energy** than **fish**, which are supported by **buoyancy** in water, **reducing** the **energy required** for **movement** ($\\checkmark$) and contributing to their lower food conversion ratio. This results in **cattle** having a notably **higher respiration** rate than fish ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **four** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nitrates **OR** phosphates enter water course [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ excess nutrients to the water bodies will cause algae to grow rapidly **OR** algal blooms [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ growth of algae will block sunlight to underwater plants, causing them to die [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in nutrients **OR** dead organic matter leads to more algal growth [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in decomposing dead organic matter [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ aerobic bacteria are decomposers [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ greater oxygen demand **OR** biochemical oxygen demand **OR** BOD [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen-depleted water can lead to fish **OR** other aquatic organisms dying [1 mark]\n\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n The runoff from using **fertilisers** in agricultural fields can contaminate local water bodies, leading to an **increase** in the **growth of algae** ($\\checkmark$) due to added nutrients. The algal growth will **block light** from penetrating the water bodies, so the underwater plants die and decompose ($\\checkmark$). The death of **underwater plants** will cause an **increase** in dead organic matter($\\checkmark$), promoting the **growth** of **bacteria** ($\\checkmark$). These aerobic bacteria will cause a **decrease** in **dissolved oxygen** ($\\checkmark$) in the water as they respire and may lead to the **death** of the **fish** and other aquatic **organisms** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1634","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1634\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe Sakhalin fir (*Abies sachalinensis*) and Ezo spruce (*Picea jezoensis*) are two of the most dominant conifers found throughout the northern Japanese island of Hokkaido. A quadrat survey was conducted to determine if there was an association between these two species. The results of the survey can be found in the table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/Tab1.png\" alt=\"Tab1\">\n\n1.\n    1. Using the chi-square formula $χ^2 = \\sum \\dfrac{(O-E)^2}{E}$, calculate the $χ^2$ value for the data in the table, where O is the observed number of quadrats and E is the expected number of quadrats containing each combination of species.    [2 marks]\n\n    2. Using the table below, determine whether the observed and expected values differ significantly and explain whether or not an association exists between *A. sachalinensis* and *P. jezoensis*.                         [3 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/Tab2.png\" alt=\"Tab2\">\n\n2. Both *A. sachalinensis* and *P. jezoensis* germinate on mossy fallen logs in the shady understorey of coniferous forests. Suggest **one** explanation for how these two species may be able to coexist in the same ecosystem.        [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. any **two** of the following (*answer must include correct final chi-square value to receive full marks*):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ correctly calculated expected values **OR** table of expected values            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chi-square formula calculations performed correctly for each of the four conditions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ $χ^2$ = 0.41 (accept values between 0.40 and 0.42)    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Expected values = (row total x column total) / grand total\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/AnsTab1.png\" alt=\"AnsTab1\">\n\n    $χ^2 = \\sum \\dfrac{(O-E)^2}{E}$ = **0.07 + 0.10 + 0.10 + 0.14** ($\\checkmark$) = **0.41** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n    2. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ critical value for one degree of freedom at 5% level **OR** 0.05 significance is 3.84    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ since calculated chi-square value **OR**  0.41 is less than 3.84, the difference between observed and expected values is not statistically significant                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fail to **OR** cannot reject null hypothesis **OR** alternative hypothesis rejected    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ so no association between species **OR** *A. sachalinensis* and *P. jezoensis* **OR** observed differences in quadrat counts can be attributed to chance    [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/1634aii.png\" alt=\"1634aii\">\n\n    The **calculated chi-square value** of **0.41** is **less than** the **critical value** of **3.84** at the **5% significance level** ($\\checkmark$). This indicates that there is **no significant difference** between the **observed** and **expected** values ($\\checkmark$), leading us to **fail to reject** the **null hypothesis** ($\\checkmark$). Therefore, it is **likely** that there is **no association** between ***A. sachalinensis*** and ***P. jezoensis*** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a mutualistic **OR** commensalistic relationship may exist **OR** minimal interspecific competition between the two species    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the two species occupy slightly different ecological niches        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  one tree species may prevent growth of other plants without affecting second tree species, thereby reducing competition for the two species        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the two species have different growth **OR** maturation times **OR** nutrient requirements **OR** light tolerance **OR** root depth **OR** any other reasonable physiological difference that would allow both species to coexist    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Despite being similar, these two species may be able to coexist because they have **different niches** ($\\checkmark$) and **do not compete** directly for **resources** such as **nutrients** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1688","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1688\n\nAntibodies pass across the placenta from mother to baby. What type of immunity is this an example of?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Natural active\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Natural passive\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Artificial active\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Artificial passive","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nPassive immunity occurs when antibodies are transferred from one individual to another, rather than being produced by the recipient’s immune system. Natural immunity refers to immunity acquired without medical intervention. Since the antibodies are naturally transferred from mother to baby via the placenta, this classifies as passive natural immunity.   **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because active immunity requires the body to produce its own antibodies after exposure to a pathogen. In this case, the baby receives pre-made antibodies from the mother, making it passive, not active, immunity.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because artificial immunity involves medical interventions, such as vaccines, which stimulate the immune system to produce its own antibodies. Since no vaccine or medical treatment is involved, this is not artificial active immunity.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because, although passive immunity is correct, artificial passive immunity involves medical treatment (e.g., antibody injections). Since the antibodies were transferred naturally across the placenta, this is natural, not artificial, passive immunity."},{"ref":"BI2066","marks":"14","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2066\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nGerminating seeds can respire aerobically or anaerobically. A student used the apparatus shown below to measure the rate of respiration in germinating seeds, by measuring the volume of $\\ce{CO_2}$ produced.\n\nThey:\n\n- positioned the apparatus in a water bath at 25 $\\degree$C\n- left the apparatus for one hour before starting the investigation\n- placed the apparatus in complete darkness.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI2066.png\" alt=\"BI2066\">\n\n1. State **two** reasons why the apparatus was maintained at 25$\\degree$C. [2 marks]\n\n2. Give **one** reason why it was important that the student left the apparatus for one hour, excluding any temperature-related factors. [1 mark]\n\n3. Suggest why it was important that the apparatus was placed in complete darkness. [1 mark]\n\nEvery 10 minutes a reading was taken from the apparatus shown above, and the coloured liquid was reset.\n\nBelow is a graph showing the results.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI2066b.png\" alt=\"BI2066b\">\n\n4. Suggest why the rate of $\\ce{CO_2}$ produced decreased after 2.5 hours. [1 mark]\n\nThe rate of aerobic respiration in germinating seeds can be calculated by repeating the experiment above but using potassium hydroxide (KOH) instead of alkaline pyrogallol. This time the coloured liquid moved to the left.\n\n5. Explain why the coloured liquid moved to the left. [3 marks]\n\nThe student wanted to calculate the rate of oxygen uptake in this experiment. They reset the coloured liquid and timed its movement for 5 minutes. The distance moved along the capillary tubing was measured as 12cm, and the diameter as 2mm\n\n6. Using 3.14 for the value of $\\pi$, and the equation provided below,calculate the rate of oxygen uptake in cm$^3$ min$^{-1}$, giving your answer to three significant figures. Show your working. [2 marks]\n\nvolume of cylinder = $\\pi$ r$^{2}$ x h\n\n7. Explain why, in plants, the rate of $\\ce{CO_2}$ production during aerobic respiration is different to the rate during anaerobic respiration. [2 marks]\n\n8. Suggest, with a reason, what difference would be expected if small invertebrates were used in this second experiment instead of germinating seeds. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ prevents changes in gas pressure **OR** volume due to temperature fluctuations, **OR** higher temperatures increase volume **OR** pressure of gas, **OR** lower temperatures decrease volume **OR** pressure of gas [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ rate of respiration depends on the activity of enzymes, which is temperature-dependent [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ prevents heat produced by respiration from affecting overall temperature of the system [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ maintains a constant temperature to ensure comparability across different trials [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n If the **temperature changes**, it could **affect** the **pressure** and so the **volume** of **gases** inside the apparatus ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, fluctuations in temperature could **alter** the **rate** of **enzyme activity**, affecting the rate of **respiration** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ so any oxygen present in the system is used **OR** absorbed **OR** respired [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to ensure anaerobic conditions are fully established [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to allow gases to equilibrate **OR** allows gas pressures to stabilise within the apparatus [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n This allows time for all the **oxygen** in the system to be **used up**, ensuring **anaerobic conditions** are fully established ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to prevent photosynthesis occurring which would absorb $\\ce{CO_2}$ **OR** produce $\\ce{O_2}$ affecting results [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ placing the setup in darkness eliminates any chance of unintended light exposure affecting metabolic processes, ensuring consistent conditions **OR** prevents light being a confounding variable [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Even though **photosynthesis** in seeds is minimal, any that does occur would **use CO**$_2$ produced during respiration, **affecting the results** of the experiment. Placing the apparatus in darkness ensures this does not happen ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$glucose decreases **OR** is used up **OR** glucose is a limiting factor **OR** substrate of respiration is depleted [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in ethanol **OR** accumulation of by-products **OR** seeds start to die **OR** activity of enzymes involved in respiratory pathway reduced [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ accumulation ofcarbon dioxide **OR** CO₂ lowers pH, reducing enzyme activity [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Accumulation of **toxic by-products**, such as **ethanol** during **anaerobic respiration**, may **inhibit enzyme activity** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen used by seeds in aerobic respiration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide produced in aerobic respiration is absorbed by potassium hydroxide **OR** KOH [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ this decreases the volume of gas **OR** pressure inside the apparatus [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The germinating seeds **respired aerobically** and **absorbed oxygen** from the apparatus ($\\checkmark$). The **carbon dioxide** they **produced** was **absorbed** by the **potassium hydroxide** ($\\checkmark$). This caused the overall **gas volume and pressure** in the tube to **decrease**, so the coloured liquid moved to the left ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. any **two** of the following (*units not required for full marks, as units in question*)\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ working shows 0.3768 cm$^3$ **OR** shows division by 5 min **OR** 0.07 cm3 min-1 **OR** 0.075 cm$^3$ min$^{-1}$ **OR** any other wrong number of significant figures [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 0.0754 cm$^3$ min$^{-1}$ [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Volume of cylinder = $\\pi \\ce{r^2} \\times \\ce{h}$\n\n $\\pi \\times$ 0.12 cm $\\times$ 12 cm = 0.3768 cm$^3$\n\n Rate of oxygen uptake = $\\dfrac{\\ce{volume} (\\textbf{0.3768}\\ \\ce{cm^3)}}{{\\ce{Time} (\\textbf{5}\\ \\ce{min)}}}$ = **0.07536** cm$^3$ min$^{-1}$ ($\\checkmark$)\n\n **0.0754** cm$^3$ min$^{-1}$ ($\\checkmark$)\n\n7. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ rate of $\\ce{CO_2}$ production is higher during aerobic **THAN** anaerobic respiration in plants [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ only glycolysis occurs during anaerobic respiration **WHEREAS** glycolysis, link reaction, Krebs cycle and oxidative phosphorylation occur during aerobic respiration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ one molecule of glucose yields two $\\ce{CO_2}$ molecules in alcoholic fermentation **OR** anaerobic respiration **WHEREAS** six $\\ce{CO_2}$ molecules produced in aerobic respiration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in aerobic respiration, glucose is fully broken down into $\\ce{CO_2}$ and water, **WHEREAS** anaerobic respiration only partially breaks down glucose into ethanol and $\\ce{CO_2}$ [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n In plants, **anaerobic** respiration involves **glycolysis**, where glucose is converted into **ethanol** and **two CO**$_2$ molecules ($\\checkmark$). **Aerobic** respiration, however, involves **glycolysis**, the **link reaction**, the **Krebs cycle**, and **oxidative phosphorylation** ($\\checkmark$), **completely oxidising glucose** into **six CO**$_2$ molecules and **water** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n8. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ coloured liquid would move at a greater rate **OR** a greater rate of O₂ uptake **OR** consumption would be observed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ invertebrates typically have a higher metabolic rate than germinating seeds **OR** invertebrates require more energy for movement **OR** temperature regulation **OR** other valid example [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The coloured liquid would move **more rapidly**($\\checkmark$) because invertebrates would **use O₂** at a **greater rate** than germinating seeds, due to them having a **higher metabolic rate** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2219","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2219\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe beach strawberry (*Fragaria chiloensis*) and yellow sand verbena (*Abronia latifolia*) are two plant species found in the coastal region of Oregon, USA. Some students decided to record the presence and absence of the two species in 200 quadrats and use the chi-squared test to examine whether their distributions were associated with each other. The table below shows the observed results obtained.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI2219a.png\" alt=\"BI2219a\">\n\n1. Outline the purpose of a Chi-Square test. [1 mark]\n\n2. Suggest a null hypothesis for this investigation. [1 mark]\n\nThe expected results and chi-square values were calculated:\n\n| Species absent / present | Expected value | Chi-square value |\n| :-- | :-: | :-: |\n| Yellow sand verbena present $\\\\$ Beach strawberry present(A) | 80 | 1.25 |\n| Yellow sand verbena present $\\\\$ Beach strawberry absent | 54 | 1.85 |\n| Yellow sand verbena absent $\\\\$ Beach strawberry present | 40 | 2.50 |\n| Yellow sand verbena absent $\\\\$ Beach strawberry absent |26 |\n\n3. Calculate the chi-square value for the final quadrant using the formula $\\ce{χ^2}= \\Sigma \\dfrac{\\ce{(O-E)^2}}{\\ce{E}}$. [1 mark]\n\n4. State the number of degrees of freedom for this data set to determine the critical value of chi-squared. [1 mark]\n\n| Degrees of freedom | Critical value for p=0.05 |\n| :-- | :-- |\n| 1 | 3.84 |\n| 2 | 5.99 |\n| 3 | 7.81 |\n| 4 | 9.49 |\n\n5. Using the **total** chi-squared value, explain the conclusion that can be drawn from the calculated and critical values obtained from the data. [4 marks]\n\n6. Suggest **two** types of additional data that could be collected to determine if there is interspecific competition between beach strawberry (*Fragaria chiloensis*) and yellow sand verbena (*Abronia latifolia*). [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to determine whether there is a statistically significant association between two categorical variables [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to evaluate whether the observed data significantly deviate from expected data under the null hypothesis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to test if the difference between observed and expected data is due to chance [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n To determine whether there is a **significant association between two** categorical **variables** ($\\checkmark$) or whether **observed** data **significantly deviate** from the **expected** data under the **null hypothesis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ there is no significant association **OR** no association between the presence of beach strawberry **OR** *Fragaria chiloensis* and yellow sand verbena **OR** *Abronia latifolia* in the coastal region of Oregon **OR** in the quadrats [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n There is **no** significant **association** between the **presence of yellow sand verbena and beach strawberry** ($\\checkmark$) in the data collected using quadrats.\n\n3. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 3.85 (*do not allow answers to anything other than 2d.p.*) [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n $\\ce{χ^2}= \\Sigma \\dfrac{\\ce{(O-E)^2}}{\\ce{E}}$\n\n $\\ce{χ^2}= \\Sigma \\dfrac{(36-26)^2}{26} = \\dfrac{100}{26}$ = **3.85** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 1 [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n degrees of freedom = (r - 1) $\\times$ (c - 1)\\\n Where:\\\n r = number of rows\\\n c = number of columns\n\n For this data set:\\\n df = (2-1) $\\times$ (2-1) = 1 $\\times$ 1 = **1** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n5. any **four** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ $\\ce{χ^2_{(total)}}$ = 9.45 [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ $\\ce{χ^2_{(critical)}}$ = 3.84 (at 1 d.f.) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ since $\\ce{χ^2_{(total)}}$ = 9.45 is greater than $\\ce{χ^2_{(critical)}}$ = 3.84 , null hypothesis is rejected [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ indicates a statistically significant difference between observed and expected frequencies [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ evidence of a statistical association between the presence of beach strawberry and yellow sand verbena **OR** distribution not independent of each other [1 mark]\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The total chi-squared value is calculated as follows:\n\n $\\ce{χ^2_{(total)}}$ = (1.25 +1.85+2.5+3.85) = **9.45** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n The **critical value** at **1 degree of freedom** (p = 0.05) = **3.84** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n Since $\\textbf{χ}^2_{\\textbf{(total)}}$ = **9.45 is greater than** $\\textbf{x}^2_{\\textbf{(critical)}}$ **= 3.84**, the **null hypothesis** is **rejected** ($\\checkmark$). This indicates that there is a **significant difference** between the **observed** and **expected values** in the data set ($\\checkmark$).\n\n There is **evidence** of a **statistical association** between the **presence** of **beach strawberry** and **yellow sand verbena**, suggesting that their **distributions** are **not independent** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ field observations to track the survival of the two species in single-species **OR** mixed-species plots [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ field observations to monitor growth **OR** seed production in single-species **OR** mixed-species plots [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ remove one of the species in half of the field study area and compare the growth of the other species in the presence and absence of the species removed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ observe positive interactions, such as by checking if one species provides shade **OR** nutrients **OR** other benefit to the other species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ measure competition for resources **OR** competition for soil nutrients **OR** water **OR** light availability in areas where the two species coexist versus areas where they are isolated [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ conduct controlled experiments in a greenhouse to control abiotic factors and test competitive interactions between the two species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ investigate the soil microbiome composition in areas dominated by one species versus mixed-species areas to assess indirect competition through microbial interactions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ track pollinator activity to determine if the two species compete for the same pollinators [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ any other suitable suggestion [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Interspecific competition can be studied by observing the **survival** of the **two species** in **single-species** or **mixed-species** plots ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, **one species** can be **removed** from **half** of the field **study area**, and the **growth** of the **other species** can be **monitored** to **compare** their **performance** in the **presence** and **absence** of the **competing species** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2415","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2415\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe diagram below shows two different types of receptor proteins binding their ligands.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/BI2415.png\" alt=\"BI2415\">\n[Adapted from *“Intracellular Receptors”*, by BioRender.com (2025). Retrieved from https://app.biorender.com/biorender-templates]\n\n1. Deduce the probable properties of the amino acids in the ligand-binding site of the extracellular and intracellular receptors.    [2 marks]\n\nEngineered receptors with transmembrane anchors, like the one shown in diagram B, are commonly used in research and therapeutic applications.\n\n2. Predict how the presence of a transmembrane anchor might affect the normal activity and localisation of an intracellular receptor.    [2 marks]\n\n3. Compare the signalling speed of integral membrane and intracellular receptors AND suggest a reason for the difference.     [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrophilic **OR** polar **OR** charged amino acids in the transmembrane receptor **OR** receptor A    [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrophobic **OR** non-polar amino acids in the intracellular receptor **OR** receptor B     [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The **intracellular receptor likely has hydrophobic** ($\\checkmark$) amino acids and the **transmembrane receptor likely has hydrophilic amino acids** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the receptor would be restricted to the membrane **OR** unable to diffuse freely in the cytoplasm [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ might prevent it from interacting with cytoplasmic signalling molecules **OR** cofactors required for its activity [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the receptor-ligand complex could not get to the nucleus [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gene regulation by the receptor would be inhibited    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ receptor could gain new functionality by interacting with membrane-bound ligands **OR** signalling pathways    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    If the intracellular receptor were anchored into the plasma membrane, it would **not be able to enter the nucleus** ($\\checkmark$) to **affect transcription** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ intracellular receptors signal more slowly **OR** transmembrane receptors act more quickly [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ intracellular receptors require ligand diffusion which is slow    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ membrane receptor signalling acts through second messengers which is faster than diffusion    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ intracellular receptors result in a change in gene transcription, which is a slow response    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Transmembrane receptor signalling is faster** ($\\checkmark$) because they use second messengers, which is a high-speed method of signal amplification, and they do not have to wait for the **ligand to diffuse through the membrane to bind as intracellular receptors do** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2416","marks":"9","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2416\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nPremature infants are often treated with caffeine to prevent episodes of apnea, a condition characterised by a temporary cessation of breathing. Although the exact mechanism of action is not fully understood, scientists have observed that treatment with caffeine leads to increased intracellular calcium levels in the muscle cells of the diaphragm.\n\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/EditA.png\" alt=\"EditA\"> | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/Edit%20B.png\" alt=\"Edit B\"> |\n| :-: | :-: |\n|[Source: *File: Premature infant CPAP.jpg*. (2020, October 9). Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 04:32, January 16, 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=File: Premature_infant_CPAP.jpg&oldid=484646381.] | [Adapted from *“Newborn baby organs”*, by BioRender.com (2025). Retrieved from https://app.biorender.com/biorender-templates] |\n\n1. State the organelle that is the most probable source of calcium. [1 mark]\n\n2. Suggest a possible mechanism for the treatment’s success. [1 mark]\n\nMultiple Sclerosis can also cause apnea, which is a temporary pause in breathing. MS attacks the myelin sheath around neurons and leaves behind lesions in the brain.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/EditC.png\" alt=\"EditC\">\n\n3. Describe how MS could result in apnea. [3 marks]\n\nMultiple sclerosis can also result in impaired ability to sense heat or cold, which can lead to accidental burns or frostbite when handling hot or cold objects.\n\n4. Explain the process by which high temperatures detected in the fingers trigger an action potential in an undamaged sensory neuron. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ sarcoplasmic reticulum **OR** endoplasmic reticulum [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The most probable source of the calcium ions is the **sarcoplasmic reticulum** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ more calcium binding to troponin to allow actin-myosin connections increases diaphragm muscle contraction [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increased calcium increases the amount of neurotransmitter **OR** acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n More calcium in the diaphragm cells means more calcium is available to **bind troponin, which moves tropomyosin away from myosin binding sites on the actin filament, allowing more contractions** ($\\checkmark$) and better breathing.\n\n3. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ no **OR** reduced saltatory conduction **OR** action potential cannot ‘jump’ from node of Ranvier to node of Ranvier **OR** transmission of action potential slowed, due to damage **OR** no myelin sheath [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ damage to myelin sheath of motor neurons that innervate intercostal muscles **OR** diaphragm could lead to reduced coordination of contraction [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ lesions in the brainstem **OR** medulla oblongata **OR** pons, impair the respiratory centres that regulate rhythm **OR** depth of involuntary breathing [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ lesions in primary motor cortex **OR** cerebral hemispheres, disrupt voluntary control of breathing muscles [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ damage to myelin of sensory neurons could interfere with the detection of blood gas levels **OR** blood pH **OR** blood carbon dioxide concentration which would disrupt feedback to respiratory centres [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **damage** to the **myelin sheath** around the neurons would **disrupt saltatory conduction**, where **action potentials** normally **\"jump\"** between **nodes of Ranvier** ($\\checkmark$). This would **slow neural signalling**, impairing **motor neuron** function required for **controlling** the **intercostal muscles** and **diaphragm** ($\\checkmark$). The same applies to the **sensory neurons** involved in the **detection of blood pH** or **carbon dioxide levels**, so there would be **limited feedback** to the **respiratory centres** in the medulla oblongata ($\\checkmark$). Lesions in the **medulla oblongata** would also disrupt the **respiratory centres** that regulate the **rhythm** and **rate** of **involuntary breathing**, which could lead to irregular breathing patterns or **apnea** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **four** of the following *(ignore references to the flow of impulses beyond the sensory neuron, in a reflex arc*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nociceptors **OR** pain receptors sense pain from **OR** harmful temperature [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ high temperatures cause specific ion channels **OR** channel proteins **OR** transient receptor protein channels **OR** TRPs to open [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ channels found in skin epidermal layer [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ positively charged ions **OR** sodium ions **OR** $\\ce{Na^+}$ **OR** calcium ions **OR** $\\ce{Ca^{2+}}$, enter the neuron through the open channels [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ entry of positively charged ions reduces the negative charge inside the neuron leading to depolarisation of the membrane potential [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ if the depolarisation reaches threshold potential **OR** -55 mV **OR** other suitable value, voltage-gated sodium channels open [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ rapid influx of sodium ions **OR** $\\ce{Na^+}$, leads to spike in membrane potential **OR** membrane potential of +30 mV **OR** other suitable value [1 mark]\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Nociceptors** are a type of sensory receptor that senses pain ($\\checkmark$). High temperatures cause specific **ion channels**, known as **transient receptor protein channels**, to **open** in the free nerve endings of sensory neurons ($\\checkmark$). **Sodium ions** **enter** the **neuron** ($\\checkmark$), causing **depolarisation** of the **membrane potential** ($\\checkmark$). If the depolarisation **reaches** the **threshold potential**, **voltage-gated sodium channels open** ($\\checkmark$), and an action potential is generated."},{"ref":"BI2670","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI2670\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline how other organic molecules are formed using glucose produced in the Calvin cycle and substances absorbed from the soil.        [4 marks]\n\n2. Describe the overall role of ATP and NADPH in the Calvin cycle.            [4 marks]\n\n3. Explain how light energy is used to produce the molecules required in the light-independent reactions.            [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fatty acids  **OR** amino acids can be synthesised from glycerate-3-phosphate **OR** triose phosphate         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mineral ions from the soil are required for the synthesis of some organic molecules    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phosphorus for synthesis of  phospholipids **OR** nucleotide **OR** ATP **OR** nucleic acids **OR** DNA **OR** RNA comes from phosphate ions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nitrogen for synthesis of amino acids **OR** proteins comes from nitrate **OR** ammonium ions [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sulfur for synthesis of some amino acids **OR** proteins comes from sulfate ions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon fixed during the Calvin cycle provides the carbon backbone for biosynthetic pathways [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Triose phosphate** from the Calvin cycle can be used to make **fatty acids** or **amino acids** ($\\checkmark$). **Phosphate ions** absorbed from the **soil** are needed to make **ATP** and other **nucleotides** ($\\checkmark$). **Nitrate** from the soil provides **nitrogen** for making **amino acids** and **proteins** ($\\checkmark$). The **carbon backbone** for these molecules comes from **carbon dioxide fixed** during the Calvin cycle ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP provides energy for converting glycerate 3-phosphate **OR** GP to triose phosphate **OR** TP            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ NADPH reduces glycerate 3-phosphate **OR** GP to triose phosphate **OR** TP by donating electrons AND a hydrogen ion    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP is also used to regenerate ribulose bisphosphate **OR** RuBP from triose phosphate **OR** TP            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ for each $\\ce{CO_2}$ fixed, 3 ATP and 2 NADPH are used        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to make one glucose molecule, 18 ATP and 12 NADPH are required [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ resulting ADP and $\\ce{NADP^+}$ return to the light-dependent reactions so more ATP and NADPH can be produced        [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **ATP** provides **energy** to convert **glycerate 3-phosphate** (GP) into **triose phosphate** (TP) ($\\checkmark$). **NADPH reduces GP to TP** by donating two **electrons** and a **hydrogen ion** ($\\checkmark$). **ATP** is also required to **regenerate ribulose bisphosphate** (RuBP) **from TP** ($\\checkmark$). For each molecule of **carbon dioxide fixed, 3 ATP** and **2 NADPH** are **used** in the Calvin cycle ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light energy is absorbed by pigments **OR** chlorophyll **OR** accessory pigments in photosystems **OR** photosystems I **OR** II        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ photosystems are photosynthetic pigment complexes **OR** protein-pigment complexes embedded  in thylakoid membranes            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ absorption of light excites electrons in the reaction centre of photosystem **OR** excites electrons in chlorophyll a    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ excited electrons from Photosystem II are transferred through an electron transport chain [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy released from electrons is used to pump hydrogen ions **OR** protons **OR**  $\\ce{H^+}$  into thylakoid space                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ an electrochemical gradient **OR** proton **OR** hydrogen ions **OR** $\\ce{H^+}$ gradient is built up across thylakoid membrane                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ protons **OR** hydrogen ions **OR** $\\ce{H^+}$ diffuse back into stroma down their concentration gradient **OR** electrochemical gradient through ATP synthase **OR** by chemiosmosis        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ flow of protons **OR** hydrogen ions **OR** $\\ce{H^+}$ **OR** chemiosmosis brings about the phosphorylation of ADP to form ATP **OR** brings about the production of ATP from ADP and inorganic phosphate        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ photolysis splits water into electrons **AND** protons **OR** hydrogen ions  **OR** $\\ce{H^+}$ **AND** oxygen                        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electrons from photolysis replace those lost from Photosystem II         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ protons **OR** hydrogen ions **OR** $\\ce{H^+}$ from photolysis are used in chemiosmosis **OR** in the reduction of $\\ce{NADP^+}$ **OR** the production of NADPH            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light re-excites **OR** also excites electrons in Photosystem I     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electrons from Photosystem I are used to reduce $\\ce{NADP^+}$ to NADPH [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ $\\ce{NADP^+}$ accepts two electrons **AND** one proton **OR** hydrogen ion **OR** $\\ce{H^+}$ to form NADPH    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP and NADPH are the molecules required for the light-independent reactions    [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Light energy** is **absorbed** by **pigments** in **photosystems I and II** embedded in the thylakoid membranes ($\\checkmark$).  This **energy excites electrons** in **chlorophyll a** at the **reaction centre** of each photosystem  ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    **Electrons** from **Photosystem II** are transferred through an **electron transport chain**  ($\\checkmark$). The **energy released** is used to **pump protons** ($\\ce{H^+}$) into the **thylakoid space**, creating a **proton gradient** ($\\checkmark$). **Protons** move **back into the stroma** through **ATP synthase**, driving the production of **ATP from ADP** and **inorganic phosphate**  ($\\checkmark$). **Water** is **split** in **photolysis**, producing **electrons, protons** ($\\ce{H^+}$), and **oxygen**  ($\\checkmark$). The **electrons** from photolysis **replace** those lost from **Photosystem II**  ($\\checkmark$). **Light** also **excites electrons** in **Photosystem I**  ($\\checkmark$).  These electrons are used to **reduce** $\\mathbf{NADP^+}$ **to NADPH**  ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2743","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2743\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe oscilloscope trace shows changes in the membrane potential of a neuron during an action potential.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/2743.png\" alt=\"2743\">\n\n1. Explain the changes in membrane potential between II and IV. [2 marks]\n\n2. Use the graph to estimate the number of impulses per minute that this neuron would likely transmit if there were 2 ms between the end of one action potential and the start of the next. [2 marks]\n\n3. Researchers used oscilloscope traces to study how the membrane potential changed in the fingertips of professional basketball players when different levels of pressure were applied. Five players participated in the study, with three trials conducted for each of the three pressure levels.\n\n 1. State **two** factors that need to be controlled in this investigation. [2 marks]\n\n 2. Suggest a control experiment that could be used for this investigation. [1 mark]\n\n 3. Suggest, with a reason, an improvement to the experimental method used in this investigation that would increase the reliability of the data collected. [2 marks]\n\n 4. Identify an appropriate statistical test for significance that could be used if the data were normally distributed and a larger sample size were available. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. any **two** of the following *(must include **one** statement for depoarisation, and **one** statement for between repolarisation to gain full marks)*:\n\n **Depolarisation:**\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at II, the membrane potential is -55 mV **OR** reaches the threshold potential, **WHICH** causes the voltage-gated sodium channels to open [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at III, the diffusion of sodium ions into the neuron causes depolarisation **OR** the membrane to change from negative to positive **OR** to between +30 mV and +40 mV [1 mark]\n\n **Repolarisation**\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at approximately +30 mV voltage-gated sodium channels shut and voltage-gated potassium channels open, **WHICH** causes potassium ions to diffuse out of the neuron [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ diffusion of potassium ions out of the neuron through potassium channels causes repolarisation **OR** makes membrane potential negative again **OR** makes the inside negative compared to the outside **OR** changes the membrane potential towards -70mV **OR** resting potential **OR** -80mV [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n At point II, the membrane potential reaches the **threshold** (-55 mV), **causing voltage-gated sodium channels to open** ($\\checkmark$) and **sodium ions** to **enter** the neuron, leading to **depolarisation** ($\\checkmark$). Around **+40mV voltage-gated potassium channels close** and **voltage-gated potassium channels open** ($\\checkmark$) allowing **potassium ions to move out** of the neuron, which causes **repolarisation** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. **both** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 2.6 ms, *(accept answers in range 2.3 ms - 2.8 ms)* **OR** 4.6 ms, *(accept answers in teh range 4.3 ms - 4.8 ms)* [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 13,043 min$^{-1}$ *(accept answers in range 12,500 - 14,000)* [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Interaction%20%26%20Interdependence/Unassigned/images/Ans.png\" alt=\"Ans\">\n\n Start of depolarisation =1.2 ms,\\\n Restoration of resting potential = 3.8 ms\n\n Time for one action potential = 3.8 ms - 1.2 ms = **2.6 ms** ($\\checkmark$)\\\n Interval between each action potential = 2ms \\\n Total time between start of one action potential and start of next = 2.6 ms + 2.0 ms = **4.6 ms** ($\\checkmark$)\\\n 1 min = 60,000 ms\\\n Number of impulses in one minute = $\\dfrac{\\mathbf{60,000}}{\\mathbf{4.6}}$ = **13,043 min**$^{-1}$ ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 1. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ same environmental conditions **OR** room temperature **OR** lighting **OR** same noise level to control distractions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ consistent placement of electrode on each participant’s finger [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ same hand **OR** dominant hand **OR** non-dominant hand used by all participants **OR** all participants have same dominant hand **OR** are all left handed **OR** are all right handed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ standardised instructions to participants to reduce behavioural variation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allow adequate rest period between trials to avoid fatigue effects [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ensure equipment calibration before each session [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ randomise order of pressure levels to prevent anticipatory responses [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ no visual prompts **OR** no sound prompts [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ basketball players of the same experience **OR** age **OR** training hours [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ same measuring equipment for changes in membrane potential **OR** same equipment for applying pressure **OR** pressure applied must be equal for all participants [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ finger temperature must be consistent because temperature affects the speed of nerve conduction [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ duration of pressure must be the same for all trials [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ any other suitable factor [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n For this investigation, **the duration of the pressure must be the same** for each trial ($\\checkmark$). The **same instructions** must also be given to all participants ($\\checkmark$).\n\n 2. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ people of the same age who do not play basketball **OR** non-athlete participants under identical conditions for comparison [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ record changes in membrane potential when no pressure is applied to measure baseline membrane potential changes not caused by mechanical stimulation **OR** for comparison [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Collect data for individuals who are not basketball players** using the same pressures and following the same method ($\\checkmark$).\n\n 3. any **two** of the following *(improvement with the appropriate reasoning must both be given to gain full marks)*:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increase the number of trials at each pressure **OR** complete at least ten trials for each pressure [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** three trials for each participant at each pressure are insufficient to generate reliable data **OR** to give sufficient data points for analysis [1 mark]\n\n **OR**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increase the number of participants **OR** test changes in membrane potential for at least ten basketball players [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to allow data processing, such as mean **OR** mode **OR** median, to be calculated **OR** ANOVA **OR** t-tests could be used to test for statistically significant results [1 mark]\n\n **OR**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ use double-blind design **OR** use a method where neither the participant nor the person collecting the data knows which treatment is being applied [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to reduce observer bias **OR** to make the results more objective **OR** to increase reliability of the data [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Increasing the number of trials to ten** ($\\checkmark$) for each pressure to **give sufficient data points for analysis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n 4. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ t test **OR** paired t test [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ANOVA [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **t-test** ($\\checkmark$) could test for statistically significant differences between the data sets for different pressure levels."}]}]},{"name":"Practical-based and Extended Response Questions","subtopics":[{"name":"Extended Response Questions","questions":[{"ref":"BI0062","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0062\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast anaerobic respiration in muscle and yeast cells. [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline the role of ATP in cells. [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain how a respirometer could be used to investigate the effect of temperature on the rate of respiration.     [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** similarity and **one** difference*):\n\n   ***Similarities***\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** processes occur in the cytoplasm and do not require oxygen [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** involve glycolysis, producing a net yield of two ATP molecules per glucose molecule [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** regenerate NAD from reduced NAD, allowing glycolysis to continue [1 mark]\n\n   ***Differences***\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ in muscle cells, lactate is produced, **WHEREAS** in yeast cells, ethanol and carbon dioxide are produced **OR** in muscle cells, pyruvate is directly reduced to lactate, **WHEREAS** in yeast cells, pyruvate is first decarboxylated (to ethanal) then reduced to ethanol [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ in muscle cells, pyruvate accepts hydrogen from reduced NAD, **WHEREAS** in yeast cells, ethanal accepts hydrogen from reduced NAD [1 mark]\n\n[3 marks]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\n\nBoth muscle and yeast cells carry out anaerobic respiration **in the cytoplasm without requiring oxygen** ($\\checkmark$). Both processes involve **glycolysis, producing a net yield of two ATP molecules per glucose molecule** ($\\checkmark$). In both cells, **reduced NAD is oxidised to regenerate NAD, allowing glycolysis to continue** ($\\checkmark$). However, in muscle cells, **pyruvate is reduced directly to lactate, whereas in yeast cells, pyruvate is first decarboxylated to ethanal and then reduced to ethanol, releasing carbon dioxide** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP is the molecule that distributes energy within cells [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrolysis of ATP to ADP and inorganic phosphate / Pi releases a small / usable amount of energy [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP can transfer a phosphate group to another molecule, changing its properties / reactivity / stability, enabling cellular processes [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP is regenerated from ADP and Pi using energy released by cellular respiration [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ most ATP during aerobic respiration is produced by ATP synthase in the inner mitochondrial membrane **OR** by chemiosmosis [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ protons flow through ATP synthase down their concentration gradient, providing energy to phosphorylate ADP [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ used for active transport across membranes **OR** suitable example [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ used for synthesis of macromolecules **OR** anabolism **OR** suitable example [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ used for movement of the whole cell **OR** cell components **OR** suitable example [1 mark]\n\n[5 marks]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\n\n**Hydrolysis of ATP** to **ADP** and **inorganic phosphate** releases a small, **usable amount of energy** ($\\checkmark$). ATP is **regenerated** from **ADP and Pi** during cellular respiration ($\\checkmark$), mostly by **ATP synthase** in the **inner mitochondrial membrane** ($\\checkmark$), using energy from the **flow of protons down their concentration gradient (chemiosmosis)** ($\\checkmark$). ATP provides energy for **active transport** ($\\checkmark$) and **anabolic reactions** such as **protein synthesis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ respirometer containing germinating seeds **OR** invertebrates with an alkali / soda lime / potassium hydroxide to absorb carbon dioxide [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen uptake reduces the volume / pressure of gas so the coloured liquid in the manometer moves towards the organisms [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature is the independent variable, set using water baths [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ a range of at least five temperatures is used (eg. 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 °C) [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ apparatus left to equilibrate at each temperature before readings are taken [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ distance moved by the liquid is measured over a fixed time [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ volume of oxygen consumed is calculated using the cross-sectional area of the capillary tube multiplied by the distance moved / using $\\pi r^2h$ [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ rate of oxygen uptake is calculated by dividing the volume of oxygen consumed by the time taken **OR** rate expressed per unit mass (eg. cm³ O₂ g⁻¹ min⁻¹) [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ controlled variables / named controlled variable kept constant [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ control respirometer with glass beads / dead seeds of equal volume at each temperature to account for changes in gas pressure unrelated to respiration [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ at least three repeats at each temperature and mean calculated [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ mean respiration rate is plotted against temperature to identify the relationship between temperature and respiration rate [1 mark]\n\n[8 marks]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\n\nA **respirometer** is set up containing **germinating seeds** and an **alkali such as soda lime to absorb carbon dioxide** produced during respiration ($\\checkmark$). As the seeds respire, **oxygen is taken up**, so the **gas pressure in the tube decreases and the coloured liquid in the manometer moves towards the seeds** ($\\checkmark$). **Temperature is the independent variable** and is controlled using **thermostatically controlled water baths** ($\\checkmark$). A **range of five temperatures** is used, eg. 10, 20, 30, 40 and 50 °C ($\\checkmark$), and at each temperature the apparatus is left to **equilibrate** before readings are taken ($\\checkmark$). The **distance moved by the liquid in a fixed time** is measured ($\\checkmark$), and the **volume of oxygen consumed is calculated using the cross-sectional area of the capillary tube** ($\\checkmark$). A **control tube containing glass beads** is used at each temperature to correct for changes in gas volume caused by temperature ($\\checkmark$). Measurements are **repeated at each temperature three times**, the **mean calculated** ($\\checkmark$), and the **results are plotted against temperature** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0111","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0111\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast how detritivores and saprotrophs obtain nutrition. [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline how a quadrat can be used to estimate the population of poppies in a field. [5 marks]\n\n3. Energy and nutrients move through ecosystems differently.  Describe how energy and nutrients move through terrestrial ecosystems. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following (must include at least one similarity and one difference to gain full marks):\n\n    ***Similarities***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** obtain energy/nutrients exclusively from dead organic matter/detritus/dead leaves/feathers/animal carcasses [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are heterotrophic [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ detritivores ingest dead organic matter **WHEREAS** saprotrophs secrete enzymes that digest dead organic matter outside their body [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ saprotrophs absorb soluble/digested nutrients externally across their cell membranes/walls, **WHEREAS** detritivores absorb digested nutrients internally across a gut/digestive system wall [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ example of detritivore e.g. earthworms **AND** example of saprotrophs e.g. fungi/bacteria [1 mark]\n[3 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Both detritivores and saprotrophs feed on **dead organic matter** ($\\checkmark$) such as leaves. **Detritivores** such as **earthworms**, which are found in soil, **ingest dead organic matter**, and digestion occurs in their digestive system. **Saprotrophs such as fungi and bacteria** ($\\checkmark$) **secrete enzymes** into the environment, **digest** the organic matter **outside of themselves** and then **absorb** some of the **resulting products** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ drawing shown of a field roughly divided to show where quadrats can be placed to sample the number of poppies [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ description of quadrat as a square with known dimensions [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ random **OR** systematic placing of quadrats in the field to measure poppy abundance [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ abundance of poppies in each quadrat recorded [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ repeated sampling [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ average (mean) number of poppies in quadrats is calculated [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ extrapolate to estimate the size of the population in the field [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ quadrats are easy to carry to the study site [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ population of poppies in the field using quadrat sampling is only an estimate [1 mark]\n[5 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Extended%20Response%20Questions/images/Picture%201.png\" alt=\"Picture 1\">\n\n    **(Quadrats placed randomly used to estimate numbers of poppies in the area shown)**\n\n    Quadrats, which **have a known dimension** ($\\checkmark$), can be used in fields  to estimate the abundance of sessile plants and animals. They are placed **randomly or in a systematic pattern** ($\\checkmark$), and the **number of poppies** in **each quadrat** is recorded ($\\checkmark$). This process is **repeated to obtain a representative sample** ($\\checkmark$) and the **mean abundance of poppies per quadrat is calculated** ($\\checkmark$).  The mean abundance of poppies per quadrat is used to **extrapolate an estimate of the population** ($\\checkmark$) of poppies in the field.\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy is not recycled in ecosystems [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light energy is converted to chemical energy by autotrophs through photosynthesis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chemical energy moves from autotrophs to heterotrophs through feeding [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when energy is transferred between trophic levels most of the energy is lost [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy is lost between trophic levels as heat **OR** excreted waste products **OR** indigestible parts of a plants or animals such as hair [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plants absorb nutrients and water from the soil [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nutrients move from one trophic level to the next through feeding [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ saprotrophs are decomposers, break down dead organic matter and release nutrients into the environment **OR** detritivores consume dead organic matter and release nutrients into the environment [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ saprotrophs secrete enzymes into the environment and digest their food externally **OR** detritivores ingest their food and break it down internally [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plants absorb nutrients released through decomposition [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nutrients are recycled in ecosystems [1 mark]\n[8 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Plants **are autotrophs** and **make organic compounds via photosynthesis using light energy** ($\\checkmark$).  **Primary consumers feed on autotrophs** and **energy flows** ($\\checkmark$) from primary to secondary to tertiary consumers. All organisms that feed on others are known as **heterotrophs**. **As energy is transferred** from one trophic level to the next, energy is **lost as heat** ($\\checkmark$) and **cannot be reused** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    **Saprotrophs are decomposers** and break **down dead organic matter** in the soil by **secreting enzymes** ($\\checkmark$).  This process **releases nutrients into the environment** ($\\checkmark$) and **plants absorb and use them** ($\\checkmark$). In this manner **nutrients are recycled** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0124","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0124\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Draw and label a trophic pyramid of energy showing four levels. [4 marks]\n\n2. Explain how the presence of a food web in an ecosystem makes it more stable than the presence of a single food chain. [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe how energy flows in and out of terrestrial ecosystems. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the pyramid shows 4 trophic levels [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the level at the base is labelled as producer and is the biggest [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the step above producers is labeled as primary consumer and is smaller than the first level [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the third level is labeled as secondary consumer and is smaller than primary consumer [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the fourth level is labeled as tertiary consumer, and is smaller than secondary consumer [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pyramid shows similar rate of decrease at each level [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Extended%20Response%20Questions/images/BI0124a.png\" alt=\"BI0124a\">\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in a food chain, each organism is dependent on only one other organism for its food  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ food chains **AND/OR** food webs show the relationship between organisms [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ multiple food chains are linked by common organisms to form a food web     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in a food web, herbivores tend to eat different plants so they survive if one plant disappears  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in a food web, consumers tend to eat different herbivores **OR** consumers so survive if one prey species disappears    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in a food web, a single consumer may eat herbivores **OR** consumers from different trophic levels [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Ecosystems with a food web are not destabilized by the disappearance of one species **WHILE** in food chains the removal of one species will lead to the collapse of the food chain [1 mark]\n[5 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Each organism in a food chain is **dependent on only one organism from the previous trophic level** for food ($\\checkmark$). A food web includes many different food chains that are **interlinked by common organisms** ($\\checkmark$). Since a herbivore **eats different plants** in a food web, it will never be short of food if one plant species disappears ($\\checkmark$). Similarly, **a single consumer may eat multiple different herbivores or other consumers** making it less dependent on only one prey species for food ($\\checkmark$). If a **predator or a consumer disappears** due to disease or other factors, the ecosystem is less likely to be destabilized as other species occupy similar niches ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy from the Sun is captured by autotrophs **OR** producers through the process of photosynthesis     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy assimilated by organisms is stored as biomass [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy transfer from one trophic level to the next is not 100% efficient [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy moves from producers to primary consumers to secondary consumers to tertiary consumers in most ecosystems [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the number of trophic levels differs in different ecosystems [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ all organisms lose energy through the process of cell respiration **OR** in the form of heat [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some energy is lost because some body parts (accept any valid example) are indigestible **OR** because some compounds are excreted or egested  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ egested **AND/OR** excreted organic material is consumed by decomposers [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the efficiency of energy production and transfer varies among  ecosystems [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy has to be continuously produced to maintain an ecosystem [1 mark]\n[7 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    In terrestrial ecosystems, energy is captured by producers from the Sun through **photosynthesis** ($\\checkmark$). This energy is **stored as biomass** ($\\checkmark$) in plants and is transferred to herbivores or **primary consumers**, then to secondary and tertiary consumers ($\\checkmark$).  Energy transfer is **not 100% efficient** ($\\checkmark$). The length of the food chain varies in food webs depending on the initial production of energy by producers. The efficiency of production and transfer **varies** between terrestrial ecosystems ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    All organisms use energy for growth and maintenance, and **energy is lost through respiration** in the form of heat ($\\checkmark$). Energy from the dead parts of organisms is consumed by **decomposers** and lost as heat ($\\checkmark$). Energy from the Sun has to be **continuously captured** by producers to maintain the ecosystem ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0138","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0138\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Describe how decomposers contribute to energy transformations in an ecosystem. [3 marks]\n\n2. Compare and contrast primary production by autotrophs and secondary production by consumers in an ecosystem. [5 marks]\n\n3. Nutrients are cycled in all ecosystems.  Draw and label the carbon cycle showing at least four stores or sinks and at least four flows or processes. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ break down organic matter **OR** dead organisms **OR** waste material **OR** prevent accumulation of dead material     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ release nutrients into the environment **OR** release inorganic nutrients **OR** named inorganic nutrient back into the soil **OR** water for uptake by autotrophs **OR** increase soil fertility **OR**  enhance soil composition so supporting plant growth **OR** primary production   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ facilitate detritivore activity **OR** make it easier for detritivores **OR** named detritivore to consume **OR** process organic matter  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ convert organic carbon to inorganic carbon **OR** release carbon dioxide during respiration which re-enters the atmosphere     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ release energy as heat **OR** heat is lost as a byproduct of respiration   [1 mark]\n\n[3 marks]\n\n :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Decomposers **break down dead organisms** and **waste material**, **preventing** the **accumulation** of organic matter ($\\checkmark$). They **release nutrients** such as **nitrogen** and **phosphorus** into the **soil**, increasing **fertility** and **supporting plant growth** ($\\checkmark$). During **respiration**, they release **heat energy** as a by-product ($\\checkmark$) and **carbon dioxide**, which re-enters the atmosphere and can be used by autotrophs in **photosynthesis**($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** similarity and **one** difference to gain full marks*):\n\n***Similarities***:\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** involve the accumulation of carbon compounds in biomass        [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ In **BOTH** energy is lost as biomass is transferred to the next trophic level       [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are typically measured in grammes per metre cubed per year OR  g m⁻² yr⁻¹     [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contribute to biomass availability in an ecosystem     [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** depend on energy input [1 mark]\n\n***Differences***:\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ primary production occurs in autotrophs **WHEREAS** secondary production occurs in heterotrophs      [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ primary production is greater **THAN** secondary production   [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ primary production occurs at the first trophic level **WHEREAS** secondary production occurs at higher trophic levels      [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ primary production usually depends on sunlight for energy  **WHEREAS** for secondary production depends on consuming other organisms  [1 mark]\n[5 marks]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\n\n    **Both** primary and secondary production involve the **accumulation** of **carbon compounds in biomass** ($\\checkmark$). In **both** cases, **energy** is **lost** as **biomass** is **transferred** to the next **trophic level** ($\\checkmark$). However, **primary production** occurs in **autotrophs**, which **convert energy** from **light** or **chemical** sources into **organic molecules**, whereas **secondary** production occurs in **heterotrophs** ($\\checkmark$), which obtain **energy** by **consuming** other **organisms** ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, primary production is greater than secondary production due to energy losses at each trophic level ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following shown on the diagram (if a diagram is not shown, award a maximum of 4 marks):\n\n    **Stores or sinks shown in diagram**\n\n    Any **four** of the following shown on the diagram:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide in air **OR** water       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organic carbon compounds in plants **OR** producers  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organic carbon compounds in consumers **OR** animals      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon in sediments **OR** seabed   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (carbon) in limestone  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (carbon in)  coal **OR** oil **OR** gas **OR** fossil fuels      [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n    **Flows or processes shown by arrows in the correct direction**\n\n    Any **four** of the following shown on the diagram:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ arrow from carbon dioxide in the air **OR** water to producers is labelled **photosynthesis** [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ arrow from any of the organisms pointing to carbon dioxide in the air **OR** water is labelled **respiration** [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ arrow from plants **OR** producers to primary consumers  is labelled eaten by **OR** consumed by  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ arrow from the primary consumers to secondary consumers is labelled **eaten by OR** consumed by   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ arrow from plants **OR** animals to saprotrophs **OR** bacteria **OR** fungi is labelled **decomposition**     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ arrow from plants **OR** animals to limestone **OR** coal **OR** oil **OR** gas **OR** fossil fuels is labelled **fossilization** [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ arrow from the oil **OR** coal **OR** gas **OR** fossil fuels to the CO$_2$ in the air  is labelled **combustion** [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n    $\\hspace{1.5em}$:u[Sample answer]:\n\n    $\\hspace{1.5em}$Stores or sinks are shown in boxes and flows or processes are shown as arrows.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Extended%20Response%20Questions/images/BI0138.png\" alt=\"BI0138\">"},{"ref":"BI0151","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0151\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare the concentration and potency of carbon dioxide and methane as greenhouse gases in the atmosphere.  [3 marks]\n\n2. Explain the reasoning of climate scientists for identifying human activity as the main cause of climate change. [5 marks]\n\n3. Discuss the consequences of global warming with reference to the climate, marine organisms, and changes to coastal regions and Arctic ecosystems. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide concentrations are higher **THAN** those of methane   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ methane is a more potent greenhouse gas **THAN** carbon dioxide (20:1)  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide stays in the atmosphere for a longer time **THAN** methane [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ methane is converted to carbon dioxide and water in the atmosphere   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide is the main greenhouse gas  [1 mark]\n[3 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    **The levels of carbon dioxide are more than methane** in the atmosphere ($\\checkmark$). **Carbon dioxide persists for a longer time than methane** ($\\checkmark$). **Methane is converted to carbon dioxide and water in the atmosphere** after a while ($\\checkmark$). The potency or **warming potential of methane is more than that of carbon dioxide**, almost 20 times more ($\\checkmark$). It can trap more heat energy.\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ scientists have calculated that Earth would be much cooler without the added greenhouse gases (GHG) [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ global temperatures are influenced by GHG concentrations  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the burning of fossil fuels has added carbon dioxide to the atmosphere [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ global fluctuations **OR** increase and decrease of carbon dioxide and temperature are strongly correlated [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ for hundreds of years, CO$_2$ concentrations were between 200 and 300 parts per million [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ currently CO$_2$ concentrations have risen to almost 400 parts per million [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ human activity is causing the increase **OR** release of GHG in the atmosphere [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the GHGs carbon dioxide, methane, halocarbons, and nitrogen oxides are associated with global warming [1 mark]\n[5 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Scientists have calculated **that the Earth would be much cooler without the effects of warming** caused by greenhouse gases ($\\checkmark$). **Global temperatures are influenced by the concentration of greenhouse gases** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    Starting about 200 years ago which was the start of the industrial age, carbon dioxide levels on Earth have been steadily increasing. This shows that **burning fossil fuels has added extra carbon dioxide to the atmosphere** and this will continue unless alternative technologies are established ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    Ice core data has shown that **carbon dioxide levels and temperature are strongly correlated** in the past ($\\checkmark$). Therefore, **human activities which release carbon dioxide and other greenhouse gases** are clearly responsible for the rising temperatures associated with climate change ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Maximum of **two** marks from each category\n\n    Climate (any **two** of the following):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ consequences will vary from region to region   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some colder countries will have warmer temperatures and may benefit through increased agricultural production   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased global temperatures cause heat waves **OR** typhoons **OR** hurricanes **OR** change of ocean currents     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased ice melting may disrupt ocean currents [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in frequency or severity of droughts **OR** desertification in some areas  [1 mark]\n\n    Marine organisms (any **two** of the following):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ coral reefs will undergo bleaching  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ loss of biodiversity in the oceans  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ higher temperatures will lead to ocean acidification [1 mark]\n\n    Change to coastal regions (any **two** of the following):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ coastal flooding **OR** coastal cities could be submerged [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some islands may disappear completely     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ existing coastal ecosystems may be lost    [1 mark]\n\n    Arctic ecosystems (any **two** of the following):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Arctic **OR** Antarctic ice could melt **OR** melting of glaciers   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ loss of habitat for animals (like polar bears)  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ permafrost melting **OR** permafrost melting will result in the release of methane  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the latitude and altitude of biomes may shift [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased spread of tropical diseases  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ other correct consequences can be accepted   [1 mark]\n[8 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    The **consequences of global warming will vary from region to region** ($\\checkmark$). Though mostly detrimental, some countries will have **warmer temperatures and may benefit from increased agricultural production** ($\\checkmark$). Increased temperatures are occurring in most places and in some places, **increased rainfall is causing flooding**.\n\n    **Coral reefs will undergo bleaching** ($\\checkmark$) due to **ocean acidification** ($\\checkmark$). There will be a **loss of biodiversity in oceans** as well ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    **Coastal cities will be submerged** ($\\checkmark$) when sea levels rise due to the melting of ice sheets and increased rainfall.  Some **islands may be completely submerged** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    Global warming will lead to the **loss of habitat for animals like polar bears** ($\\checkmark$) in the Arctic and penguins in the **Antarctic due to large areas of ice melting** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    Overall, scientists foresee an increase in catastrophic weather events for humans everywhere as temperatures continue to rise globally."},{"ref":"BI0163","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0163\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nOxidative phosphorylation, which generates ATP for the cell, is comprised of the electron transport chain and chemiosmosis.\n\n1. Distinguish between the electron transport chain in aerobic cellular respiration and the electron transport chain in photosynthesis.   [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline how ATP can be recycled after being used by the cell.      [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain how 38 molecules of ATP can be produced from one glucose molecule in a cell.     [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ETC happens in the inner mitochondrial membrane, **WHILE** it happens in the thylakoid membrane in the chloroplast   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electrons are donated by the reduced NAD (**OR** FAD) at the beginning of the ETC in aerobic cellular respiration, **WHILE** in photosynthesis electrons are donated by chlorophyll a in PSII **OR** water  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ NADH (**OR** FADH$_2$) is oxidised **OR** loses electrons in the ETC of aerobic cellular respiration, **WHILE** NADP:sup[+] is reduced in the ETC of chloroplasts  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen is the final electron acceptor in the ETC of aerobic cellular respiration **BUT** NADP:sup[+] is the final electron acceptor in the ETC of photosynthesis   [1 mark]\n[3 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    In aerobic cellular respiration, the **ETC takes place in the inner mitochondrial membrane**, while in **photosynthesis it take place in the thylakoid membrane of the chloroplast** ($\\checkmark$). In **aerobic cellular respiration, electrons are donated by NADH (and FADH$_2$**), while in **photosynthesis they are donated by chlorophyll a in photosystem II** ($\\checkmark$). **NADH (and FADH$_2$) undergo oxidation in aerobic cellular respiration** when they donate the electrons at the beginning of the ETC, but in **photosynthesis NADP:sup[+] is reduced** as it accepts electrons at the end of the ETC ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrolysis of ATP to ADP and Pi **OR** inorganic phosphate    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when ATP breaks, energy is released  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ inorganic phosphate combines with ADP to form ATP [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when ATP is synthesized, energy is added [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxidative phosphorylation, **OR** energy released from the oxidation of glucose, **OR** energy from the proton, **OR** H:sup[+] gradient, **OR** chemiosmosis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electron transport chain, **OR** electron movement along the carriers, provides energy [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy released from ETC generates proton **OR** H:sup[+] gradient **OR** moves H:sup[+] across the inner mitochondrial membrane [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP synthase synthesizes ATP, **OR** ATP synthase allows protons, **OR** H:sup[+], to diffuse      through the inner mitochondrial, **OR** thylakoid, membrane [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the energy to convert ADP and inorganic phosphate to ATP comes from organic compounds **OR** sunlight [1 mark]\n[5 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    ATP can be recycled continuously to meet the energy demands of cells. This is done after the **hydrolysis of ATP**, which **releases the energy** ($\\checkmark$) needed to fuel cell processes. Hydrolysis of ATP produces **ADP and inorganic phosphate** ($\\checkmark$). This inorganic phosphate can be **recombined with ADP** ($\\checkmark$) with the addition of **energy** ($\\checkmark$) from either **organic compounds or sunlight** ($\\checkmark$) and then stored within the bonds formed. ATP is formed by **ATP synthase** ($\\checkmark$) in oxidative phosphorylation , which involves **electron movement** and proton transport ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ aerobic respiration requires oxygen and leads to a high yield of ATP   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ products (of aerobic cell respiration) are CO$_2$, water and ATP   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the four stages of cellular respiration are glycolysis, link reaction, Krebs cycle and oxidative phosphorylation     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glycolysis happens in cytoplasm **OR** outside of mitochondria [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ products of glycolysis are pyruvate, NADH, and net of 2 ATP  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pyruvate is converted into acetyl CoA in the link reaction   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxidative phosphorylation happens in mitochondria **OR** Krebs cycle in the matrix of mitochondrion **OR** ETC on the inner mitochondrial membrane (**OR** cristae)  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Krebs cycle results in 2 ATP, reduced NADH (**OR** FADH$_2$), **OR** reduced electron carriers [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ NADH (**OR*  FADH$_2$) donate electrons and hydrogen to ETC    (**OR** electron transport chain) [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electron movement in ETC moves H:sup[+] across the inner mitochondrial membrane **OR** produces an H:sup[+] gradient     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP synthase uses H:sup[+] concentration gradient, **OR** proton gradient, to synthesize ATP [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ inorganic phosphate is added to ADP to form ATP       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 34 ATP formed in oxidative phosphorylation  [1 mark]\n[8 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Cellular respiration consists of **4 stages: glycolysis (which occurs in the cytoplasm** ($\\checkmark$) **and is anaerobic, not needing oxygen)**, and then the other 3 stages, which occur in the mitochondrion and make up aerobic respiration: **the link reaction, the Krebs cycle and oxidative phosphorylation** ($\\checkmark$).   In glycolysis, glucose is broken down into **pyruvate, NADH and a net of 2 ATP is produced** ($\\checkmark$). The pyruvate then enters the mitochondrion, and the **link reaction converts it into acetyl-CoA** ($\\checkmark$). The acetyl-CoA is delivered to the Krebs cycle in the **matrix of mitochondria** ($\\checkmark$). Here **6 NADH, (2 FADH$_2$) and 2 ATP** ($\\checkmark$) are produced per molecule of glucose . **NADH (and FADH$_2$) carry electrons to the electron transport chain (ETC)** ($\\checkmark$), the first step in oxidative phosphorylation. The 2nd step is chemiosmosis, where **ATP synthase produces 34 molecules of ATP** ($\\checkmark$), using the **energy from the proton gradient generated by the ETC** ($\\checkmark$) to combine **ADP and inorganic phosphate** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0175","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0175\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nCompare and contrast the light-dependent and light-independent reactions. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **seven** of the following (*must include at least **two** similarities and **two** differences to gain full marks*):\n\n    ***Similarities***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** reactions take place in the chloroplast   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** reactions involve enzymes   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** reactions can take place during day time     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** reactions are part of photosynthesis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** reactions are linked OR dependent on the activity of the other [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** rely on coenzymes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** involve energy transfer [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light-dependent reactions take place in the intermembrane space of the thylakoid and thylakoid membrane **WHEREAS** light-independent reactions take place in the stroma  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light-dependent reactions require light, **WHEREAS**  the light-independent reactions do not require light       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen is formed in the light-dependent reactions **WHEREAS**  sugar **OR** carbohydrates are formed in the light-independent reactions  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electrons and protons for the light-dependent reactions are supplied from the photolysis of water **WHEREAS**   electrons and protons in the light-independent reaction come from reduced NADPH     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP is formed in the light-dependent reactions **WHEREAS**  ATP is broken down to supply energy in the light-independent reactions [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ NADP is reduced **OR** NADP accepts electrons and protons in the light-dependent reactions **WHEREAS**  NADPH is oxidised **OR** reduced NADP donates electrons and protons in light-independent reaction        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light-dependent reactions involve an electron transport chain and chemiosmosis WHEREAS light-independent reactions involve carbon fixation and the Calvin cycle\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light-dependent reactions produce short-term energy molecules **OR** ATP and NADP **WHEREAS**   light-independent reactions produce long-term energy storage molecules\n [1 mark]\n[7 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    The similarities between the light-dependent reactions and light-independent reactions are that both of these reactions are **part of photosynthesis** ($\\checkmark$) which is conducted by photosynthesizing organisms. Both reactions **involve enzymes** ($\\checkmark$) such as the cytochrome b6f complex in the light-dependent reactions and rubisco in the light-independent reactions.\n\n    The differences include that the light-dependent reactions take place in the **intermembrane space as well as the thylakoid membranes** whereas the light-independent reactions take place in the **stroma** of the chloroplast ($\\checkmark$). The light-dependent reactions **need light** for photophosphorylation, but the light-independent reactions **do not need light** ($\\checkmark$). In the light-dependent reactions, **oxygen** and **ATP are formed** ($\\checkmark$) whereas in the light-independent reactions, **ATP is broken down** to supply energy to form **sugar** ($\\checkmark$). **NADP is reduced** in light-dependent reactions, whereas in the light-independent reactions, the reduced NADP is **oxidised to form NADP** ($\\checkmark$)  ."},{"ref":"BI0201","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0201\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the different uses of karyograms. [4 marks]\n\n2. Distinguish between cells with haploid and diploid nuclei. [4 marks]\n\n3. Compare and contrast different types of chromosomes. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ karyograms are images of the chromosomes in an organism [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous chromosomes are arranged in pairs of decreasing length OR Sex chromosomes are the 23rd pair in human karyograms        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ karyograms can be used to identify the sex of an individual    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ karyograms can be used to identify conditions caused by trisomy, e.g. Down syndrome **OR** Patau syndrome **OR** Klinefelter syndrome             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ karyograms can be used to determine if a chromosome is missing **OR** if part of a chromosome is missing                        [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    **Karyograms** ($\\checkmark$) are images of the chromosomes in an organism. In a karyogram, the pairs of homologous chromosomes are arranged in pairs of **decreasing length** ($\\checkmark$). The sex chromosomes are shown as the 23rd pair in human karyograms.\nKaryograms can be used to determine if an individual is a biological **male or female** ($\\checkmark$). Two X chromosomes are present in a human female cell, and one X chromosome and one Y chromosome are present in a human male cell.\nKaryograms can be used to **identify** conditions caused by **trisomy** (three copies of a chromosome) e.g. **Down syndrome**, Patau syndrome, Edward’s syndrome and Klinefelter syndrome ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ haploid nuclei have one set of chromosomes, **OR** one chromosome per type **WHEREAS** diploid nuclei have two sets **OR** two chromosomes of each type [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ haploid nuclei have one allele for each gene **WHEREAS** diploid nuclei have two alleles for each gene [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ haploid nuclei in humans have 23 chromosomes, and diploid nuclei in humans have 46 chromosomes **OR** any other valid species example [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ haploid cells are usually gametes **WHEREAS** diploid cells are usually somatic cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ haploid cells are usually the result of meiosis, **WHILE** diploid cells are usually the result of mitosis.  [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    Haploid nuclei have **one set of chromosomes**($\\checkmark$), whereas diploid nuclei have two sets of chromosomes.  For example, in **humans**($\\checkmark$), gametes have 23 chromosomes, while somatic cells have 46 chromosomes. Haploid nuclei have **one allele for each gene**, ($\\checkmark$)  whereas diploid nuclei have two alleles for each gene. In humans, only **gametes**($\\checkmark$)  have haploid nuclei, whereas the rest of the somatic cells have diploid nuclei.\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (within one species) different chromosomes are composed of DNA molecules with different lengths     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (within one species) different chromosomes have different numbers of genes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (within one species) different chromosomes have different types **OR** different numbers of genes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ both prokaryotic **AND** eukaryotic chromosomes contain DNA    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ bacterial chromosomes are not associated with proteins, **WHILE** eukaryotic and archaeal chromosomes are associated with proteins [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic chromosomes are looped molecules of DNA, **WHILE** eukaryotic chromosomes are linear molecules of DNA [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** autosomes and sex chromosomes contain genes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the human Y chromosome carries information mainly coding for male sex determination and development, **WHILE** the X chromosome carries information for other body characteristics [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is only one pair of sex chromosomes, **WHILE** there can be more than one pair of autosomes (in different species)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ autosomes are pairs of homologous chromosomes of equal length, **WHILE** two sex chromosomes can be different lengths [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chromosomes can be homologous and non-homologous [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous chromosomes are pairs of chromosomes of equal length carrying the same genes, but different alleles, **WHILE** non-homologous chromosomes, carry different genes [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    All chromosomes are made of DNA and contain genes, but they **differ in length** ($\\checkmark$) and in the **number of genes** ($\\checkmark$) and **type of genes** ($\\checkmark$). Similarities and differences between prokaryotic and eukaryotic chromosomes: Both prokaryotic and eukaryotic chromosomes **contain DNA** ($\\checkmark$). Bacterial chromosomes are not associated with proteins, but eukaryotic chromosomes are **associated with proteins** ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotic chromosomes are looped, while eukaryotic chromosomes are **linear** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    Similarities and differences between autosomes and sex chromosomes: \\\n    Both autosomes and sex chromosomes are made of DNA and contain genes. Autosomes are chromosomes that do not determine sex and do not have genes for sex-linked characteristics; sex chromosomes determine sex and have genes for sex-linked traits. There is one pair of sex chromosomes in a somatic nucleus, but there can be **more than one pair of autosomes** ($\\checkmark$) in a somatic nucleus. X and Y chromosomes are of different lengths, while autosomal homologous chromosome pairs are **equal in length** ($\\checkmark$) \\\n    Comparing non-homologous chromosomes and homologous chromosomes:\\\n    Homologous chromosomes are pairs of chromosomes that have genes for the same characteristics and can have the same or different alleles. Non-homologous chromosomes have genes for **different characteristics** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0213","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0213\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the events which would lead to an individual being born with Down syndrome, which is characterised by the presence of three copies of chromosome 21. [4 marks]\n\n2. Explain how meiosis increases variation.    [3 marks]\n\n3. Outline the stages of meiosis. [9 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ non-disjunction as the cause of trisomy **OR** having three copies of a chromosome **OR** an extra chromosome [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous chromosomes do not separate properly during cell division     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ both chromosomes from a homologous pair move to the same pole **OR** end of the cell [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some gametes receive an extra chromosome    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the chances of non-disjunction increase with the mother’s age **OR** there is a positive correlation between the mother’s age and non-disjunction [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    **Nondisjunction** ($\\checkmark$) is the reason individuals are born with an **extra chromosome**  which is the cause of the condition down syndrome. Nondisjunction can happen when the chromosomes **do not separate properly** during the meiosis. ($\\checkmark$) For example, **homologous pair moves to the same end of the cell** ($\\checkmark$). The likelihood of non-disjunction increases for individuals with **older mothers** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.    any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ random orientation of **homologous** pairs in metaphase I [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ independent assortment increases the number of potential combinations of alleles [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ crossing over during prophase I **OR** exchange of sections of DNA between non-identical sister chromatids leads to new combinations of alleles **OR** recombinant chromatids     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reduction division **OR** production of haploid cells allowing for fertilisation   [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nMeiosis increases genetic variation through three primary mechanisms: **crossing over during prophase I**, where **non-identical chromatids** of homologous pairs exchange segments of genetic material ($\\checkmark$); **independent assortment during metaphase I**, leading to a multitude of possible genetic combinations ($\\checkmark$); and the formation of unique **haploid gametes** allowing for **fertilisation** during sexual reproduction ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.    any **nine** of the following (accept marking points clearly shown in an annotated diagram):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ meiosis has eight stages, prophase I, metaphase I, anaphase I and telophase I, and prophase II, metaphase II, anaphase II and telophase II    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ meiosis I is called a reduction division; it reduces the number of chromosomes by half **OR** produces haploid cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ meiosis II separates sister chromatids to produce cells with a single copy of one chromosome from each homologous pair [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ meiosis produces four unique haploid nuclei [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in $\\underline{\\textrm{Prophase I}}$ nuclear membrane breaks down and condensed chromosomes are clearly visible [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in $\\underline{\\textrm{Metaphase I}}$, homologous chromosomes line up on the equator [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in $\\underline{\\textrm{Anaphase I}}$, spindle fibres contract and separate the chromosomes of each homologous pair **OR** homologous chromosomes are pulled to opposite poles [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in $\\underline{\\textrm{Telophase I}}$ two haploid nuclei are formed    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in $\\underline{\\textrm{Prophase II}}$, chromosomes condense, and the nuclear membrane breaks down [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in $\\underline{\\textrm{Metaphase II}}$, chromosomes line up on the equator **OR** each chromosome is attached to both poles (at the centromere)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in $\\underline{\\textrm{Anaphase II}}$, spindle fibres contract and sister chromatids are separated and move to opposite poles [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in $\\underline{\\textrm{Telophase II}}$ two haploid nuclei are formed from each cell, 4 haploid cells in total     [1 mark]\n\n    [9 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    In meiosis, there are **two cycles of prophase, metaphase, anaphase and telophase** (meiosis I and meiosis II). Meiosis I separates homologous chromosomes and produces 2 haploid nuclei; this is called a **reduction division.** ($\\checkmark$) Meiosis II separates **sister chromatids** ($\\checkmark$) and produces **four haploid nuclei** ($\\checkmark$) in total.\n\n    **Meiosis I**\n\n    In **prophase I**, chromosomes are made of sister chromatids and are **visible** (e.g. 46 chromosomes with two strands in human cells). **The nuclear membrane breaks down** ($\\checkmark$). In **metaphase I**, **homologous chromosomes line up on the equator** ($\\checkmark$). The centromere of each chromosome is attached to one pole. In **anaphase I** spindle fibres contract and **homologous chromosomes are pulled to opposite poles** ($\\checkmark$). In **telophase I, two haploid nuclei form** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    **Meiosis II**\n\n    In **prophase II** the **nuclear membrane breaks down, and chromosomes condense** (haploid number, e.g. 23 in a human cell) ($\\checkmark$). In **metaphase II, chromosomes are lined up on the equator** ($\\checkmark$), and each centromere is attached to both poles. In **anaphase II, spindle fibres contract** and **sister chromatids are separated** and are pulled to opposite poles ($\\checkmark$). **In telophase II**, two haploid nuclei form from each cell containing haploid number of chromosomes ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0225","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0225\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n1. Describe how parents with blood type A and blood type B can have a child with blood type O.     [4 marks]\n\n2.    Outline, using a named example, how polygenic inheritance results in continuous variation [4 marks]\n\n3.    Explain the role of inheritance in genetic diseases.    [8 marks]","markScheme":"1.    any **four** of the following (accept any marking point clearly shown in a genetic diagram)\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the allele for blood group A **OR** allele I$^A$, is dominant over the allele for blood group O **OR** allele i  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the allele for blood group B **OR** allele I$^B$, is dominant over the allele for blood group O **OR** allele i  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ parents with blood group A or blood group B could be heterozygous **OR** I$^A$i and I$^B$i [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a parent who is heterozygous for blood group A can pass on the allele for blood group O **OR** allele i    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a parent who is heterozygous for blood group B can pass on the allele for blood group O **OR** allele i     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ offspring that receive the allele for blood type O **OR** i allele from both parents will be blood group O    [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    The alleles for blood type A and blood type B are **dominant** to the allele for blood type O ($\\checkmark$). Parents with blood type A or B could be **heterozygous** ($\\checkmark$) and possess the recessive allele for blood type O ($\\checkmark$). If the offspring **inherits the recessive allele** ($\\checkmark$) from both parents, then they will have blood type O.\n\n    Parents:     $\\hspace{2.5em}$      **I$^A$ i**  $\\hspace{3em}$ x   $\\hspace{2.5em}$  **I$^B$ i**\n\n    Gametes:        $\\hspace{1.5em}$     **I$^A$ and i**  $\\hspace{1.5em}$ x   $\\hspace{1.5em}$  **I$^B$ and i**\n\n    Punnett square:\n\n    | | I$^B$ | i |\n    | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n    | **I$^A$**|  $\\hspace{1.5em}$ I$^A$ I$^B$ $\\hspace{1.5em}$  |  $\\hspace{1.5em}$ I$^A$ i  $\\hspace{1.5em}$  |\n    | **i** |  $\\hspace{1.5em}$ I$^B$ i $\\hspace{1.5em}$  |  $\\hspace{1.5em}$ i i $\\hspace{1.5em}$  |\n\n    Genotypes of offspring: $\\hspace{1.5em}$ $\\hspace{1.5em}$ **I$^A$ I$^B$ $\\hspace{1.5em}$ :  $\\hspace{1.5em}$ I$^A$ i  $\\hspace{1.5em}$ : $\\hspace{1.5em}$ I$^B$ i $\\hspace{1.5em}$ : $\\hspace{1.5em}$ i i $\\hspace{1.5em}$**\n\n    $\\hspace{14em}$ **1 $\\hspace{2.8em}$ : $\\hspace{2em}$ 1 $\\hspace{2.2em}$: $\\hspace{2.5em}$1$\\hspace{2.1em}$ :$\\hspace{1.8em}$ 1**\n\n    Phenotypes of offspring:  $\\hspace{1.5em}$ **blood type AB : blood type A : blood type B : blood type O**\n\n    $\\hspace{15.5em}$ **1 $\\hspace{3em}$ : $\\hspace{3em}$ 1 $\\hspace{2.5em}$: $\\hspace{2.5em}$1$\\hspace{3.2em}$ :$\\hspace{3.5em}$ 1**\n\n2.  any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ human skin colour OR any other suitable example                [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at least three OR several genes control phenotype                [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ each gene has two or more alleles contributing to the phenotype            [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ no single allele is solely responsible for the phenotype                [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ many possible allele combinations                         [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ additive OR cumulative effect of alleles results in continuous range of phenotypes    [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phenotype shows a bell-shaped OR normal distribution in the population        [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $ \\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    **Human skin colour** ($\\checkmark$) is controlled by **several genes**  ($\\checkmark$).  **Each allele** has a small **dditive effect** on the amount of melanin produced.  The **many possible combinations** of **alleles** ($\\checkmark$) result in a **continuous range**  ($\\checkmark$) of skin colours.\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic diseases can be caused by inherited mutant alleles [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ alleles are passed to offspring via haploid gametes **OR** sperm and eggs [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gametes carry one allele per gene [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fertilisation restores diploidy **OR** combines parental alleles [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allele combination **OR** genotype influences whether genetic disease is expressed [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ autosomal dominant disease require one disease allele to be expressed [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ huntington’s disease **OR** any other valid autosomal dominant example, is an autosomal dominant condition [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ autosomal recessive disease requires two disease alleles to be expressed [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ heterozygotes can be carriers of autosomal recessive diseases [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phenylketonuria (PKU) **OR**  any other valid autosomal recessive example, is an autosomal recessive condition [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ many sex-linked genes are carried on the X chromosome [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ X-linked recessive diseases are more common in biological males [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ haemophilia **OR**  any other valid X-linked recessive example, is an X-linked recessive condition [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ affected X-linked recessive biological mother will pass diesease to all biological sons [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carrier X-linked recessive biological mother will pass disease to 50% of biological sons **OR** 50% biological daughters, if father unaffected [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    Genetic diseases can be caused by **inheriting mutant alleles** of genes ($\\checkmark$). Alleles are passed from parents to **offspring in haploid gametes** ($\\checkmark$), and **fertilisation** combines **one allele** from **each parent** to form the offspring’s genotype ($\\checkmark$). If a disease allele is **dominant**, only **one copy** is needed for the condition to be **expressed** ($\\checkmark$), for example, **Huntington’s disease** ($\\checkmark$). If a disease allele is **recessive, two copies** are needed for the condition to be **expressed** ($\\checkmark$), and people with **one recessive** allele are **carriers** who can pass it on ($\\checkmark$). Some genetic diseases are **sex-linked** because the gene is on the **X chromosome**, so X-linked recessive diseases are **more common** in biological **males** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0249","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0249\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n1. Describe how mutations can result in natural selection. [4 marks]\n\n2. Discuss the advantages and disadvantages of a population producing more offspring than the environment can support. [4 marks]\n\n3. Explain how the misuse of antibiotics can lead to the development of bacterial resistance. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1.    any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mutations are a random change in the DNA sequence [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ natural selection as the mechanism by which organisms better adapted survive and pass on their traits **OR** characteristics **OR** genes to their offspring     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase variation **OR** diversity within a population **OR** species  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mutations can be neutral, advantageous or detrimental    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ individuals with advantageous mutations are better adapted for the environment  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ individuals with advantageous mutations, survive and reproduce **OR** they pass these mutations onto their offspring [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    Mutations are **random changes in the DNA sequence** ($\\checkmark$)\n that can lead to an increase in the **variation** within a species ($\\checkmark$). Individuals with advantageous mutations that make them **better adapted** to their environment ($\\checkmark$) **survive and reproduce**, so they are able to pass these advantageous mutations onto their **offspring** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.    any **four** of the following (advantages and disadvantages must be present for full marks):\n\n    **Advantages:**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increases genetic diversity  **OR** variation [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ individuals with favourable characteristics **OR** advantageous traits **OR** better adapted, survive and reproduce    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ species becomes better adapted (to the environment) over time [1 mark]\n\n    **Disadvantages:**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increases competition for resources    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ higher population densities increase disease transmission **OR** environmental degradation [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ struggle for survival **OR** suffering of individuals less adapted **OR** with unfavourable characteristics    [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    Producing more offspring than the environment can support can be advantageous because it can **increase the genetic diversity** ($\\checkmark$) within a population\nand increasing diversity allows individuals with more **favourable characteristics** to survive ($\\checkmark$)\n, which leads to the population becoming **better adapted** to the environment over time ($\\checkmark$)\n. However, this can also be a disadvantage because if there are a large number of individuals within a population it could increase the **spread of disease** as the population is more dense ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.    any **eight** of the following (maximum of 7 marks if no example of misuse is stated):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibiotics as a selective pressure [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some bacteria are resistant to some antibiotics **OR** possess a mutation **OR** gene for antibiotic resistance    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ bacteria (with antibiotic resistance) are not killed by antibiotics **OR** antibiotics are ineffective [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transfers of the gene for antibiotic resistance to another bacterium through conjugation **OR** using plasmids     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ resistance gene can pass to next generation **OR** offspring, through cell division **OR** binary fission [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ resistant bacteria have no competition and proliferate in the absence of other bacteria **OR** proportion of resistance bacteria in a population increase [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ MRSA (methicillin resistant *Staphylococcus aureus*) is an example of antibiotic resistant bacteria **OR** any other valid example [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ example of misuses such as over prescription of antibiotics **OR** failure to complete the full course    [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    **Overprescribing antibiotics** to patients who don’t need them can lead to the development of bacterial resistance ($\\checkmark$). This is an issue which is becoming a global health concern.\n\n    Some bacteria may **possess a mutation** which confers resistance to an antibiotic ($\\checkmark$). If this antibiotic is used to treat a bacterial infection, then it acts as a **selective pressure** ($\\checkmark$), which results in the death of bacteria that do not possess resistance, but the **survival** of those that do ($\\checkmark$). Surviving bacteria are able to **pass on the gene for resistance** to the next generation through **binary fission** ($\\checkmark$) or to other bacteria through **plasmids** ($\\checkmark$). This would result in the **increase in the proportion** of resistant bacteria in a population ($\\checkmark$), which means that another antibiotic would have to be used to help treat the infection. Some bacteria, such as **MRSA**, can have multiple resistances, ($\\checkmark$) which means that a combination of antibiotics must be used."},{"ref":"BI0274","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0274\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline how mitochondrial DNA can be used to construct a cladogram.     [4 marks]\n\n2. Explain why organisms classified in the past may be reclassified. [4 marks]\n\n3. Compare and contrast cladograms made using molecular evidence and structural evidence. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1.    any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mitochondrial DNA from different organisms can be sequenced [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mutations occur at a roughly constant rate **OR** can act as a molecular clock [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the lower the number of differences in the base sequence between organisms, the more closely related the organisms are [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the number of differences in the base sequence can be used to determine how long ago species diverged from a common ancestor [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ which can be used to deduce where the nodes are on cladograms [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nSections of mitochondrial DNA from **different organisms can be sequenced** ($\\checkmark$), which allows scientists to observe differences in the base sequence. The **lower the number of differences** in the base sequence between organisms, the **more closely related** the organisms are ($\\checkmark$). The **number of differences** in the sequence of mitochondrial DNA can also be used to determine **how long ago species diverged** from a common ancestor ($\\checkmark$), which can be used to deduce **position of nodes** on cladograms ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.    any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms (used to be) classified based on shared anatomical characteristics **OR** structures **OR** physical features [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ shared anatomical characteristics **OR** structures **OR** physical features may be analogous **OR** not due to common ancestry [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ analogous structures as a result of convergent evolution [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms (are now) classified using amino acid **OR** DNA sequences [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased accuracy **OR** reliability of cladograms made using DNA **OR** amino acid sequences [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nIn the past, organisms were classified based on shared **physical features** ($\\checkmark$), however **physical features could be analogous** and therefore not the result of common ancestry ($\\checkmark$). Cladograms which are constructed using **DNA or amino acid sequences** ($\\checkmark$) are more **reliable** ($\\checkmark$) and can highlight new evolutionary relationships which would lead to organisms potentially being reclassified.\n\n3.    any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** seek to demonstrate **OR** deduce evolutionary relationships [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contain a root **OR** ancestor common to all organisms within the cladogram [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contain nodes **OR** points at which species diverged from one another [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contain clades **OR** an ancestor and all of its evolutionary descendants [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can contain outgroups **OR** the most distantly related species [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ species in **BOTH** may be reclassified (in response to new evidence) [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ species organised based on anatomical structures **OR** physical characteristics **WHEREAS** species are organised based on amino acid **OR** DNA sequence [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in the past cladograms were constructed using similarities in physical features **WHEREAS** now DNA **OR** amino acid sequences are used [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ molecular evidence **OR** DNA sequences **OR** amino sequences more reliable **OR** accurate than structural evidence **OR** physical characteristics [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ analogous structures can be an issue in cladograms made using structural similarities **BUT** not in cladograms using molecular evidence **OR** DNA **OR** amino acid sequences [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cladograms made using structural evidence require less technology **COMPARED** to cladograms using molecular evidence **OR** DNA or amino acid sequences [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nCladograms which are made using structural evidence and those made using molecular evidence have several things in common, for example, **both have** features such as a **root** ($\\checkmark$), **nodes** ($\\checkmark$) and **clades** ($\\checkmark$), they **both** also attempt to show **evolutionary relationships** between species ($\\checkmark$). There are also some differences in these cladograms. In cladograms made using structural evidence, the **physical characteristics** of species are used to organise them into groups, which **does not require much technology**. However, in cladograms using molecular evidence, which requires more **advanced technology** ($\\checkmark$), scientists use the number of differences in the DNA or **amino acid sequence** ($\\checkmark$), which is known to be the more **reliable** ($\\checkmark$) method and is more commonly used now, compared to structural features being used more in the **past** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0299","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0299\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the risk factors and consequences associated with plaque buildup in the coronary arteries. [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline how the pressure changes in the left atrium and left ventricle affect the surrounding valves and blood flow.  [4 marks]\n3. Explain how the structural features of the arteries, veins and capillaries are adapted to support their functions. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following (at least **one** from each of the causes and consequences):\n\n    *Causes*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ high blood pressure **OR** high blood cholesterol [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  smoking **OR** diabetes **OR** genetic factors [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a diet high in LDL cholesterol **OR** saturated fats **OR** trans fats[1 mark]\n\n    *Consequences*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ blood flow restriction in the arteries **OR** narrowing of the arteries [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  lack of oxygen     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ heart inflammation **OR** angina [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ decreases the ability of the cardiac muscle to contract    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stimulates the formation of blood clots in the artery [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ heart attack  [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nOcclusion is caused by the **buildup of fatty plaques** in the inner lining of the coronary arteries ($\\checkmark$) which could be the effect of **smoking** ($\\checkmark$) or **high blood pressure** ($\\checkmark$). As a consequence, it can **restrict blood flow to the arteries** ($\\checkmark$) and lead to **chest pain** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.  any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when pressure increases in the left atrium **OR** pressure in the atrium is higher than the ventricle the atrioventricular **OR** bicuspid valve opens [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ blood flows from the left atrium to the left ventricle [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when the pressure increases in the left ventricle **OR** the pressure in the left ventricle is higher than the left atrium, the atrioventricular **OR** bicuspid valves close  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when the pressure increases in the left ventricle **OR** the pressure inside the left ventricle is higher than the aorta, the semilunar **OR** aortic valves open [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ blood flows from the left ventricle to the aorta [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    **Pressure** in the **left atrium increases** when the left atrium contracts, which causes the **atrioventricular** valve to **open** ($\\checkmark$), allowing **blood** to flow **into the left ventricle** ($\\checkmark$). The **pressure** in the **left ventricle increases** when the left ventricle contracts causing the **semilunar** valve to **open** ($\\checkmark$) and the **atrioventricular valve to close** ($\\checkmark$). This prevents the backflow of blood into the atrium from the ventricle. With the increase in pressure in the left ventricle, the **blood** is pumped **from the ventricle to the aorta** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.    any **eight** of the following (answer should contain at least **one** statement from each category, both the feature and explanation need to be present for each marking point)\n\n    *Arteries*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ thick walls to withstand high pressure **OR** prevent rupturing [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ walls have elastic fibers to allow them to, stretch **OR** expand, and recoil in response to, pulsatile blood flow **OR** heart beat  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ muscular walls for vasoconstriction **OR** vasodilation **OR** to regulate blood pressure **OR** control distribution of blood [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ narrow lumen helps maintain blood pressure [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ smooth inner lining, **OR** endothelium, to reduce friction [1 mark]\n\n    *Capillaries*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ thin walls **OR** one cells thick walls help to increase diffusion rate **OR** reduce diffusion distance between the blood and cells **OR** tissues [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ small diameter to allow passage of one blood cell at a time **OR** to optimize exchange [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ may contain pores, **OR** fenestrations,  to aid transport of materials [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ dense network, **OR** large surface area, to supply cells **OR** tissues with materials **OR** nutrients [1 mark]\n\n    *Veins*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ thin walls, **OR** close proximity to skeletal muscles, so muscle contraction can help support blood flow     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reduced elasticity (compared to arteries) due to lower pressure blood [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ valves to prevent the backflow of blood     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ wider lumen (compared to arteries) helps to reduce friction **OR** maximize blood flow [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    Arteries are adapted to suit their function because they have **thick walls** which help to **withstand the high pressure** blood they transport ($\\checkmark$), which is also maintained by their **narrow lumen** ($\\checkmark$). Their walls also have **elastic fibers** to allow them to **stretch and recoil** in response to **pulsatile blood flow**.  ($\\checkmark$).\nArteries branch into arterioles, which branch into capillaries, which are the site of exchange between the blood and surrounding tissue. They are adapted for their function because they have very **thin walls**, which **decreases the diffusion distance** ($\\checkmark$) for nutrients to travel and they have a very **dense network** to support the **delivery of nutrients** to all of the tissues ($\\checkmark$).\nVeins return blood from the tissues to the heart and they are adapted to their role because they have **valves to prevent the backflow** of blood ($\\checkmark$) which is traveling at lower pressure and often against gravity. They also have a **wider lumen** which helps to maximize blood flow by **reducing friction** ($\\checkmark$) and their walls are **less elastic compared to arteries**, because blood is flowing at a **lower pressure** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0350","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0350\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast the phloem found in the stem and the root of a plant.       [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline how root pressure is generated through the transport of minerals.       [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain the changes in hydrostatic pressure within the phloem that facilitate the transport of phloem sap from sources to sinks.  [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following (award 2 marks maximum if no contrast given):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phloem transports organic molecules **OR** amino acids **OR** sap **OR** sucrose in **BOTH** the root and stem  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phloem is part of **OR** found in the vascular bundle in **BOTH** the stem and root  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phloem is positioned outside the xylem in **BOTH** stem and roots  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phloem (as part of vascular bundles) is distributed **OR** scattered **OR** spread out **OR** located around the edge in the stem **BUT** located at the centre of roots   [1 mark]\n[3 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    In the stem and the root, the phloem is responsible for the transport of **organic molecules** ($\\checkmark$) such as sucrose and is found as part of the **vascular bundle** ($\\checkmark$). In the root, the phloem is located near the centre, however, it is more **spread out** in the stem ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ endodermal cells contain suberin **OR** have a Casparian strip [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ waterproof layer blocks movement through cell walls (apoplast pathway) [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water **AND** ions are forced through cytoplasm **OR** across cell membranes (**OR** symplast pathway) [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ensures regulated entry of water **AND** ions **OR** prevents uncontrolled loss from the stele [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ endodermal cells pump **OR** actively transport mineral ions into xylem [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ion accumulation lowers water potential **OR** increases solute concentration inside xylem [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water enters xylem down water potential gradient **OR** by osmosis. [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ influx of water generates positive pressure potential **OR** hydrostatic pressure [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Endodermal cells contain a **Casparian strip** ($\\checkmark$) that is waterproof and **blocks** the **apoplast pathway** ($\\checkmark$), **forcing water** and **ions** to move through the **symplast pathway** ($\\checkmark$). Endodermal cells then **actively transport ions into** the **xylem** ($\\checkmark$), **increasing** its **solute potential** ($\\checkmark$) causing **water** to **enter by osmosis** and generate a **positive pressure** known as root pressure ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sources synthesize organic compounds   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organic compounds **OR** named example, are loaded into phloem **OR** sieve tubes, at              sources       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ solute concentration increases (in the sieve tubes at sources)      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water from the xylem moves into the sieve tube **OR** phloem by osmosis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in hydrostatic pressure in the phloem  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sinks act as organic compound storage **OR** non-synthesising regions        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organic compounds, **OR** named example, are unloaded from phloem, **OR** sieve tubes  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ decreased solute concentration (in sieve tubes at sinks)       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water moves out of the sieve tube into the xylem by osmosis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrostatic pressure decreases     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrostatic pressure is the pressure exerted by a fluid as a result of gravity [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrostatic pressure gradient (as a result of the incompressibility of water) allows the sap to flow from source to sink **OR** differences in hydrostatic pressure between phloem at sources and sinks allows transport of sap        [1 mark]\n[8 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Sources are areas of the plant which can synthesise **organic compounds** ($\\checkmark$) such as sucrose. At sources, sucrose is **loaded** into the phloem ($\\checkmark$), which creates a **high solute concentration** ($\\checkmark$). This causes water to move, by **osmosis, into the sieve tubes** from xylem vessels ($\\checkmark$). The flow of water into the sieve tubes increases the **hydrostatic pressure** ($\\checkmark$) which causes water to **flow to areas of lower pressure** ($\\checkmark$), such as sinks. Sinks are areas of the plant which **don’t synthesise** organic molecules ($\\checkmark$), such as the roots. At sinks, sucrose is **unloaded** from the sieve tubes ($\\checkmark$), which **decreases the solute concentration** ($\\checkmark$). This leads to water moving back into the xylem vessels by osmosis and as a result, the **hydrostatic pressure** in the sieve tubes **decreases** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0376","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0376\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Draw a labelled diagram to represent the process of crossing over.     [4 marks]\n\n2. Compare and contrast random orientation and crossing over. [4 marks]\n\n3. Outline the stages of meiosis. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following are correctly shown in a labelled diagram:\n\n    **Labels**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous chromosome pair   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ synapsis (chromosomes attached together) **OR** tetrad      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chiasmata (between non-sister chromatids)      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ recombinant chromatids    [1 mark]\n\n    **Drawing**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ diagram is shown as at least three stages –  aligned chromosome pair, chiasmata between homologous chromosomes, resulting in two chromosomes with swapped material       [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Extended%20Response%20Questions/images/BI0376.png\" alt=\"BI0376\">\n\n2. any **four** of the following (answer can be in a table format):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** occur during meiosis I    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** promote variation      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are random processes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Crossing over occurs during prophase I **WHEREAS** random orientation occurs during metaphase I [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ During crossing over, there is an exchange of genetic information **OR** alleles **WHEREAS** during random orientation pairs of homologous chromosomes align randomly along the equator         [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Both random orientation and crossing over occur during **meiosis I** ($\\checkmark$), and are **random processes** ($\\checkmark$) that promote **variation** ($\\checkmark$), as they encourage the formation of genetically distinct gametes at the end of meiosis. However, crossing over occurs during **prophase I** and random orientation occurs during **metaphase I** ($\\checkmark$). During crossing over there is an **exchange** of genetic information between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes, whereas during random orientation homologous chromosomes **independently and randomly align** on either side of the equator ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 4 stages in meiosis I and meiosis II; prophase, metaphase, anaphase, telophase   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prophase I: homologous chromosomes condense **OR** become visible       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prophase I: crossing over occurs between homologous chromosomes **OR** non-sister chromatids              [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ metaphase I: homologous chromosomes align on the equator     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ metaphase I:  random orientation **OR** random alignment of homologous chromosomes     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ anaphase I: spindle fibres attach to the centromeres of each chromosome        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ anaphase I: each chromosome in a pair **OR** each pair of sister chromatids, is pulled to either side of the cell      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ telophase I: cytokinesis occurs **OR** two nuclear envelopes form **OR** cells go from diploid to haploid      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prophase II: chromosomes condense and become visible [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ metaphase II: chromosomes **OR** pairs of sister chromatids, align on the equator of the haploid cells     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ anaphase II: spindle fibres attach to the centromeres of each chromosome     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ anaphase II: sister chromatids are pulled apart (now chromosomes in own right)         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ anaphase II: chromosomes are pulled to either pole of the cell         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ telophase II: cytokinesis occurs **OR**  four nuclear envelopes are formed         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ telophase II: four haploid cells are formed         [1 mark]\n[8 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Meiosis is a form of cell division that results in the production of four haploid gametes. It is split into meiosis I and II, which both consist of four stages; **prophase, metaphase, anaphase and telophase** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    **Meiosis I**\n\n    In prophase I, homologous chromosomes **condense** ($\\checkmark$) and become visible, they form tetrads and **crossing over** ($\\checkmark$) can occur between non-sister chromatids. In metaphase I, the homologous chromosomes align on the **equator** ($\\checkmark$) and are subject to **random orientation** ($\\checkmark$). In anaphase I, the chromosomes, each composed of a pair of sister chromatids, are pulled to **opposite sides** ($\\checkmark$) of the cell and then in telophase I the nuclear membrane reforms and the cytoplasm divides during **cytokinesis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    **Meiosis II**\n\n    In prophase II, chromosomes **condense** ($\\checkmark$) and become visible, they then align at the **equator** ($\\checkmark$) during metaphase II. In anaphase II the **sister chromatids are pulled apart** ($\\checkmark$) and now, as chromsomes in their own right, move to **opposite sides** of the cell ($\\checkmark$) and finally in telophase, the nuclear membrane **reforms** in each of the haploid cells ($\\checkmark$), resulting in the production of four **haploid gametes** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0704","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0704\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline how polygenic traits can show a normal distribution. [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline the differences and similarities between homologous chromosomes.     [4 marks]\n\n3. Explain why expected and observed Mendelian ratios may differ. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polygenic traits are caused by two or more genes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the greater the number of genes that are responsible for a trait, the greater the number of phenotypes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polygenic traits often show continuous variation [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ which is a continuous range of variation rather than distinct categories [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is a large range of phenotypes, where the majority of organisms are in the middle of the normal distribution curve [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polygenic traits are often influenced by the environment [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nPolygenic traits are caused by **two or more genes** ($\\checkmark$). The **higher the number of genes** that are responsible for a trait, the **higher the number of possible phenotypes** ($\\checkmark$) which means that the traits show **continuous variation** ($\\checkmark$). Continuous variation is when a phenotype shows a **continuous range of variation**, rather than having distinct categories ($\\checkmark$) and usually shows a normal distribution, where the majority of organisms are in the **middle of the normal distribution curve** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following (at least **ONE** marking statement should come from the similarities and **ONE** from the differences):\n\n    *Similarities:*\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ length [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ centromeres are  in the same position [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sequence of genes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gene loci **OR** position of genes [1 mark]\n\n    *Differences:*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ alleles [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ one chromosome is inherited maternally and one chromosome is inherited paternally [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nHomologous chromosomes are pairs of chromosomes with the same genes, but **different alleles** ($\\checkmark$). In a pair, one chromosome is inherited **paternally** and one is inherited **maternally** ($\\checkmark$). Homologous chromosomes are similar in that they are the **same length** ($\\checkmark$), have their **centromeres in the same place** ($\\checkmark$) and have the **same sequence** ($\\checkmark$) and **loci** of genes ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mendelian ratios are the proportions of genotypes that would be expected from a punnett square     [1 mark]\n\n    *Observed and expected ratio may be different due to:* \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mutations [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genes are not inherited as expected [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ interactions between genes and the environment     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ environment affects some phenotypes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ autosomal linkage **OR** when genes are found on the same autosome [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sex linkage **OR** when genes are found on the sex chromosomes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ so do not segregate independently during meiosis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when linked genes are far apart on the same chromosome, crossing over can occur making them seem unlinked  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms inheriting diseases that are fatal to embryos disrupts phenotypic ratios    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polygenic inheritance **OR** inheritance caused by two or more genes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when scientists incorrectly assume that a trait is determined by one gene     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if inheritance is monogenic, but two different genes result in the same phenotype     [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nMendelian ratios are the **proportions of genotypes or phenotypes that are expected from a given cross** ($\\checkmark$).  However, observed ratios are not always the same as expected ratios.  This can be due to a number of factors including random **mutations** ($\\checkmark$), and **interactions between genes and the environment** ($\\checkmark$);  the **environment** sometimes alters a **phenotype** ($\\checkmark$). Observed and expected values can also be affected by **autosomal gene linkage** ($\\checkmark$) which is when genes are found on the same autosome.  Autosomally linked genes **do not segregate independently during meiosis** ($\\checkmark$). **Linked genes** that are found **far apart** from each other ont he same chromosome are more likely to **become unlinked** due to **crossing over** ($\\checkmark$). Observed and expected values can also differ if organisms inherit diseases that are **fatal to embryos, which changes phenotypic ratios** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1191","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1191\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the role of auxin in positive phototropism. [4 marks]\n\n2. Explain the evidence that supports the theory that eukaryotic cells evolved from prokaryotic cells. [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain how the products of the light-dependent stage of photosynthesis are used in the light-independent stage to convert carbon dioxide into organic substances. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ light is detected by group of proteins **OR** phototropins which trigger the movement of auxins by active transport [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ auxin efflux pumps move auxin so the concentration of auxins is more on the shadier side\nand lesser on the side with more light [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ plant cells have auxin receptors [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ when auxin binds to these receptors ,transcription of genes for enzyme **OR** expansin [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ auxin causes movement of hydrogen ions from cytoplasm into the cell walls [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen ions and expansion **OR** enzyme loosens cellulose fibres **OR** fibres of cell wall [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ causes cells on the shaded side of shoot tip to elongate [1 mark]\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Light is detected** by **phototropins** and this triggers the movement of auxins by **active transport** ($\\checkmark$). Auxin **efflux pumps** move auxins from **cytoplasm** into **cell walls**, so that the **concentration** of auxin is **more** on the **shadier side** ($\\checkmark$) and less on the side with more light. When auxin binds to **receptors** on the cells, **genes** for the enzyme **expansin** are **expressed** ($\\checkmark$). **Expansin**, and the **pumping of protons** from the cytoplasm into the cell wall cause the **loosening** of **cellulose fibres** ($\\checkmark$) in the cell walls, leading to **elongation** of the **cells** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ theory of endosymbiosis proposes that mitochondria **OR** chloroplasts have evolved from free-living prokaryotes that were engulfed **OR** taken in by endocytosis, by larger cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mitochondria **OR** chloroplasts have naked DNA [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mitochondria **OR** chloroplasts divide by binary fission [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mitochondria **OR** chloroplasts divide independently of cell cycle **OR** cell [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mitochondria **OR** chloroplasts about same size as prokaryotes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mitochondria **OR** chloroplasts have 70S ribosomes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mitochondria **OR** chloroplasts can synthesise their own proteins [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mitochondria **OR** chloroplasts have double membranes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mitochondria **OR** chloroplasts have inner membrane like **OR** similar in structure to prokaryotes **AND** outer membrane like **OR** similar in structure to eukaryotes\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA of mitochondria **OR** chloroplasts is more closely related **OR** more similar to prokaryotic DNA than to nuclear DNA of eukaryotic cells, **OR** rRNA **OR** ribosomal RNA more closely related **OR** more similar to prokaryotes than eukaryotes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ observations of modern examples of endosymbiosis OR given examples of present day endosymbiosis, provide support for the theory [1 mark]\n\n [5 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The endosymbiotic **theory** supports the evidence that **mitochondria** and **chloroplasts** in eukaryotic cells **evolved from prokaryotic** cells, which were taken in by larger cells through **endocytosis** ($\\checkmark$). The evidence supporting this theory includes the fact that these organelles have **naked DNA** ($\\checkmark$), divide by **binary fission** ($\\checkmark$), can **synthesise** their **own proteins** ($\\checkmark$) and have **double membranes** around the outside ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **seven** of the following: (drawing showing the Calvin cycle with the steps described below):\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ products of the light-dependent reaction are essential for the light-independent reactions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ light-dependent reactions produce ATP, NADPH and oxygen [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP and NADPH are used in the light-independent reactions **OR** Calvin cycle [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ light-independent reactions convert carbon dioxide into glucose and other organic molecules [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ enzyme RuBisCO fixes carbon dioxide [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ causes 1 x RuBP to bind to 1 x carbon dioxide [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 6 C compound is unstable and forms two 3C compounds of glycerate-3-phosphate **OR** GP [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP and NADPH are used to convert glycerate-3-phosphate **OR** GP to triose phosphate **OR** TP [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ some of the triose phosphate **OR** TP leaves the cycle to form carbohydrates, most is used to reform RuBP [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ regeneration of RuBP requires energy in the form of ATP [1 mark]\n [7 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Light-dependent** reactions of photosynthesis produce **ATP, NADPH** ($\\checkmark$) and oxygen. Oxygen is a by-product, ATP and NADPH are used in the light-independent reactions. In the stroma of the chloroplast, **carbon dioxide** combines with **RuBP** ($\\checkmark$), a 5C compound catalysed by the enzyme **Rubisco** ($\\checkmark$). The **6C compound** formed is **unstable** and splits into **two molecules of glycerate-3-phosphate** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n **ATP and NADPH** ($\\checkmark$) are used to convert **glycerate-3-phosphate into triose phosphate** ($\\checkmark$). Some of the **molecules** of **triose phosphate leave** the **cycle** to form **glucose** ($\\checkmark$) or other carbohydrates, **most** of it is **used** to **regenerate** the **5C RuBP** ($\\checkmark$). **Regeneration** of **RuBP** again requires **ATP** ($\\checkmark$) obtained from the light-dependent reactions.\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{For additional detail}}$: see 5:26 in Photosynthesis Key Concept Video: https://www..com/ib-biology/hl/key-concepts/?keyConceptId=cd710a11-a37d-4f93-aed3-30e4e1d75e55"},{"ref":"BI1192","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1192\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Explain the effects of ADH in the kidney. [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline the functions of enzymes involved in DNA replication. [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe how memory cells and antibodies are produced in response to an antigen.  [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ADH is involved in osmoregulation **OR** the control of solute concentration in the blood plasma     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if the water content of the blood is low **OR** solute concentration is high, the pituitary gland secretes ADH    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ADH causes the cells of the collecting duct to be more permeable to water        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water channels **OR** protein channels called aquaporins are put into cell membranes   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as filtrate moves through collecting ducts, water is reabsorbed due to the solute concentration in the medulla being high    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ADH causes the blood to become more dilute by reabsorption of water in the collecting ducts of the nephrons            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ADH causes a small volume of concentrated urine to be formed for excretion              [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ no **OR** low ADH causes dilute urine to be excreted    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    ADH is involved in **osmoregulation** of the blood plasma, the control of solute concentration of the blood ($\\checkmark$). If the **water content** of the **blood** is **low**, the **pituitary gland** secretes **ADH** ($\\checkmark$). ADH causes the cells of the **collecting duct** of the **nephrons** to become **more permeable** to **water** ($\\checkmark$). Water channels called **aquaporins** are put into the **membranes** of the cells of the **collecting duct walls** ($\\checkmark$). The **high solute** concentration in the **medulla** will allow the **reabsorption** of **water** by **osmosis** ($\\checkmark$) from the collecting ducts. It causes a **small volume** of **concentrated** urine ($\\checkmark$) to be produced.\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA helicase unwinds **OR** uncoils the DNA (by breaking the hydrogen bonds between complementary bases) and separates it into two template strands    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA polymerase III adds nucleotides in the 5’ to 3’ direction on the leading strand OR adds nucleotides to the 3’ end    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$v DNA primase adds a primer or a short strand of RNA to the template strand where DNA polymerase can bind and begin replication     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA polymerase 1 removes the RNA primer and replaces it with DNA        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA ligase seals the gaps between the Okazaki fragments on the lagging strand         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA gyrase moves in front of the helicase that help in relieving the supercoiling of DNA     [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n     **DNA helicase unwinds** the DNA into **two strands** ($\\checkmark$) by breaking the hydrogen bonds between complementary bases. **DNA Polymerase III** adds **nucleotides** in the **5’ to 3’ direction** on the **leading strand** ($\\checkmark$). **DNA primase** adds a **short strand of RNA** or a primer to the template strand **where DNA polymerase** can bind and **start replication** ($\\checkmark$).**DNA Polymerase I removes** the **RNA primer** ($\\checkmark$) and replaces it with DNA.   **DNA ligase seals the gaps** between the **Okazaki fragments** ($\\checkmark$) on the lagging strand.\n\n3. any **seven** of the following (at least one from each section, presented in a logical sequence):\n\n    ***Helper T-cells*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ helper T-cells have  receptor proteins in their plasma membranes, to which a specific  antigen can bind [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the antigen is presented to the helper T cell by a macrophage **OR** antigen-presenting cell [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when the antigen binds, the helper T-cell is activated [1 mark]\n\n    ***B-cells*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ inactive B-cells have antibodies **OR** receptors for antigens **OR** B-cell receptors in their plasma membrane  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when complementary antigen binds **OR** when activated T-cell with the antigen binds B-cell is activated   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ T-cell can send chemical message **OR** cytokines to help activate B-cells [1 mark]\n\n    ***Production of plasma cells*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ activated B-cells start to divide once activated **OR** go through clonal expansion [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ differentiation into plasma cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ these cells have large volumes of cytoplasm and are called plasma cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they synthesise antibodies  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibodies are released by exocytosis [1 mark]\n\n    ***Production of memory cells*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ activated T-cells and B-cells can also differentiate into memory T-cells and B-cells  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ these cells persist once the disease is gone [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they can be activated very quickly in response to the same antigen  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ long term immunity to a disease is thus developed by the body [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    In the initial phase of the disease, the **antigen** is presented by a **macrophage** to a **helper T-cell** with the **specific receptor** ($\\checkmark$). This **helper T-cell** can bind the **antigen** and is **activated** ($\\checkmark$), and **can now** bind to a **specific B -cell** containing the **complementary receptor** ($\\checkmark$). The T-cell sends a message to the B-cell, thus activating it.\n\n    **Activated B-cells** start to **divide** ($\\checkmark$) and can **differentiate** into **plasma B-cells**, which have large amounts of cytoplasm ($\\checkmark$). They **synthesise antibodies** ($\\checkmark$) which are **released** by **exocytosis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    **Memory cells** are **T-cells and B-cells** that are **formed** when the **respective helper cells** are **made** ($\\checkmark$). These **cells persist** in the body and are **activated** in response to the **same antigen** ($\\checkmark$). **Long-term immunity** to the disease ($\\checkmark$) is established."},{"ref":"BI1193","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1193\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline how reproductive isolation can result in changes in allele frequencies between populations. [4 marks]\n\n2. Compare and contrast spermatogenesis and oogenesis. [5 marks]\n\n3. Outline how muscle contraction is brought about according to the sliding filament model. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allopatric speciation is caused by geographical isolation and, therefore reproductive isolation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sympatric speciation takes place in the same habitat due to different niches **OR** differences in feeding behaviour **OR** behavioural differences **OR** courtship differences [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ both processes lead to isolation of populations [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic variation occurs due to genetic drift **OR** mutation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ reproductive isolation prevents or limits gene flow between populations [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ leads to changes in allele frequencies within those populations [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ over time this leads to formation of a new species due to inability to breed with the original population of the species [1 mark]\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Geographic isolation**, such as that caused by tectonic plate movement, can lead to **isolation** of a **population** ($\\checkmark$). **Reproductive isolation prevents** or limits **gene flow** ($\\checkmark$) between populations of the same species.\n\n This **leads** to **changes** in **allele frequencies** within those populations ($\\checkmark$), and **over time** could lead to the **formation** of a **new species** due to its inability to breed with the original population that it separated from ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following: (at least one from each):\n\n ***Similarities*** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** produce haploid cells **OR** nuclei [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** involve meiosis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** occur in reproductive organs [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** involve maturation of immature germ cells [1 mark]\n\n ***Differences*** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ spermatogenesis occurs in testes **WHEREAS** oogenesis occurs in the ovaries [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ spermatogenesis produces many gametes **WHEREAS** oogenesis produces one gamete per month or cycle [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ spermatogenesis produces **OR** results in four mature sperms from one precursor cell ( and no polar bodies) **WHEREAS** oogenesis produces one mature egg from the precursor cell, (the other cells forming polar bodies) **OR** spermatogenesis involves mitotic and meiotic divisions **WHEREAS** oogenesis involves meiotic divisions with uneven cytokinesis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ spermatogenesis starts at puberty **WHEREAS** oogenesis begins during fetal development [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ spermatogenesis continues through adult life **WHEREAS** oogenesis stops at menopause [1 mark]\n [5 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Both **spermatogenesis** and **oogenesis** take place in **reproductive organs** ($\\checkmark$), however **spermatogenesis** occurs in the **testes** of biologically male animals whereas **oogenesis** occurs in the **ovaries** of biologically female animals ($\\checkmark$). **Both** processes **produce haploid cells** ($\\checkmark$) via the process of **meiosis** ($\\checkmark$). While **spermatogenesis** produces **four mature sperms** from one **precursor cell**, **oogenesis** produces **one mature egg** ($\\checkmark$) from the precursor cell, the other cells becoming polar bodies. **Spermatogenesis continues** throughout **adult life** in the biological male, whereas **oogenesis stops** at **menopause** ($\\checkmark$) in biological females. **Spermatogenesis** produces **many gametes**, whereas **oogenesis** produces **one gamete per month** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ muscle fibres consist of myofibrils made up of myosin **AND** actin **AND** troponin **AND** tropomyosin protein [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ tropomyosin winds around actin filaments covering binding sites for myosin heads [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ action potential passes along cell membrane of muscle fibre **OR** sarcolemma **OR** down T-tubules [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ causes release of calcium ions **OR** Ca:sup[2+] from sarcoplasmic reticulum [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Ca:sup[2+] bind troponin protein on the actin filaments causing a conformational change [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ change lifts tropomyosin away for myosin binding sites on actin **OR** leads to a change in the tropomyosin protein on the actin filament exposing sites to which myosin can bind [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ myosin head binds to actin forming cross bridges [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ inorganic phosphate **OR** Pi released from myosin head and myosin head changes angle, pulling actin filament along **OR** leading to power stroke [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ‘power stroke’ **OR** movement of myosin head shortens the sarcomere leading to a muscle contraction [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ADP is released from myosin head, allowing ATP to bind [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ causes cross-bridge to break **OR** cause myosin head to release actin [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrolysis of ATP releases energy to return myosin head to original position **OR** to ‘re-cock’ head [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ titin stabilises the sarcomere by anchoring myosin to the Z-line **OR** provides elasticity **OR** helps the sarcomere return to its resting length after contraction [1 mark]\n\n [7 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Muscle fibres consist of **myofibrils** made up of thick filaments containing **myosin**, and thin filaments made up of **actin, tropomyosin** and **troponin** ($\\checkmark$). **An action potential** travelling along the sarcolemma causes the **release of calcium ions** from the **sarcoplasmic reticulum. The calcium ions bind to troponin**, which causes a **conformational change** ($\\checkmark$), lifting the **tropomyosin** and, exposing **sites** to which **myosin** can **bind** ($\\checkmark$). The **binding** of the myosin head to the actin causes the **release of inorganic phosphate**, and the **change** in the **angle** of the **myosin head**, pulling the actin filament along ($\\checkmark$). **ADP is released** from the myosin head, which allows **ATP** to **bind** to the myosin in its place ($\\checkmark$), causing the **cross-bridge** between the actin and myosin to **break** ($\\checkmark$). The **ATP** molecule is then **hydrolysed** to ADP and Pi, **releasing** the **energy** needed for the **myosin** head to return to its **original position** ($\\checkmark$). It is now ready for the whole process to occur again.\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{For additional detail}}$: see 6:00 in Muscle and Motility II Key Concept Video"},{"ref":"BI1311","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1311\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline how the cerebellum helps control movement.    [3 marks]\n\n2. Describe how the hypothalamus coordinates nervous and hormonal responses to regulate body temperature in cold environmental conditions. [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain how negative feedback helps control the rate and depth of ventilation during strenuous exercise.    [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ helps maintain posture by adjusting the activity of muscles that support the body [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ helps maintain balance by coordinating muscular adjustments when body position changes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ coordinates the timing **OR** force of skeletal muscle contractions, producing smooth, precise movements **OR** fine-tunes movements, such as precise hand movements [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contributes to motor learning, improving the accuracy and coordination of movements through practice [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    The cerebellum helps **maintain posture** by adjusting the **activity of muscles that support the body** ($\\checkmark$). It helps **maintain balance** by coordinating **muscular adjustments when body position changes** ($\\checkmark$). It also **coordinates the timing and force of skeletal muscle contractions**, making movements **smooth and precise** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ receives sensory nerve impulses from peripheral thermoreceptors, which detect changes in skin temperature [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ thermoreceptors within hypothalamus detect changes in the blood temperature [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ coordinates shivering in skeletal muscles which increases heat production [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stimulates brown adipose tissue, increasing heat production through uncoupled respiration [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ coordinates vasoconstriction of skin arterioles, reducing blood flow to skin surface, reducing heat loss [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stimulates pituitary hormone release, which stimulates thyroid gland to release thyroxin [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ thyroxin increases metabolic rate of target cells, increasing heat production [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ responses operate through negative feedback, counteracting initial temperature change **OR** as body temperature returns towards normal range, the corrective responses are reduced [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    In cold conditions, **peripheral thermoreceptors** detect a **decrease in skin temperature** and send **sensory nerve impulses to the hypothalamus** ($\\checkmark$). The hypothalamus coordinates **shivering in skeletal muscles**, increasing **heat production** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    It also stimulates the **pituitary gland** to release a hormone that stimulates the **thyroid gland to release thyroxin** ($\\checkmark$). Thyroxin **increases the metabolic rate** of target cells, increasing **heat production** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    As body temperature **returns towards its normal range**, these heat-producing responses are reduced through **negative feedback** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the respiratory centre is in the medulla of the hindbrain [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ strenuous exercise results in increased rate of aerobic cell respiration in active muscle cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide is produced more rapidly by active muscle cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ results in increased blood carbon dioxide concentration [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide forms carbonic acid with water, which dissociates; hydrogen ions released which lower blood pH [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ high carbon dioxide levels **OR** low blood pH detected by chemoreceptors / hydrogen ion detectors in brain [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ peripheral chemoreceptors **OR** chemoreceptors in carotid arteries / aortic bodies, detect a fall in blood pH [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chemoreceptors **OR** hydrogen ion detectors send impulses to the inspiratory centre [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ inspiratory centre alters the signals sent through motor neurons to the diaphragm **AND** external intercostal muscles [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ these muscles contract more frequently **AND** more forcefully **OR** increase rate **AND** depth of ventilation [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased ventilation removes carbon dioxide more rapidly, helping blood pH to return towards normal range [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as carbon dioxide concentration / pH return towards normal ranges, chemoreceptor stimulation decreases, reducing response through negative feedback [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    During strenuous exercise, active muscles **produce carbon dioxide more rapidly**, releasing it into the **bloodstream** ($\\checkmark$). If carbon dioxide accumulates, it **reacts with water to form carbonic acid, which dissociates** ($\\checkmark$) and **releases hydrogen ions and lowers pH** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    **Chemoreceptors** in the brainstem detect the associated **decrease in the pH** of their surrounding fluid ($\\checkmark$). The **respiratory centre** responds by altering **motor nerve signals to the diaphragm and external intercostal muscles** ($\\checkmark$). These muscles **contract more frequently and forcefully**, increasing the **rate and depth of ventilation** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    **Increased ventilation** removes carbon dioxide more rapidly, helping to **counteract the fall in pH** ($\\checkmark$). As **carbon dioxide concentration and pH return towards their normal ranges**, chemoreceptor stimulation decreases, reducing the corrective response through **negative feedback** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1320","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1320\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline how water is formed at the end of aerobic respiration.    [3 marks]\n\n2. Explain how the properties of water allow it to be transported around a plant.    [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe how plants transport carbohydrates from source to sink.    [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electrons donated by reduced NAD and FAD **OR** NADH and FADH at the beginning of the electron transport chain    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electrons are passed along electron carriers    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ final electron acceptor is oxygen **OR** :sup[1]/:sub[2] O:sub[2]      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ excess hydrogen ions **OR** protons, from intermembrane space **OR** coenzymes **OR** reduced NAD **OR** reduced FAD found in matrix **OR** accumulate in the matrix            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen ions **OR** protons react with oxygen to form water    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 4e:sup[-] + 4H:sup[+] + O:sub[2] → 2H:sub[2]O **OR** 4 electrons + 4 protons + 1 molecule of oxygen →  2 molecules of water    [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Electrons** donated by **reduced NAD** and **FAD** at the beginning of the electron transport chain ($\\checkmark$) are passed along **electron carriers** ($\\checkmark$), with the **final** electron acceptor being **oxygen** ($\\checkmark$). **Hydrogen ions** in the **matrix** react with **oxygen** to form water ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polar so can form hydrogen bonds             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ strong cohesive forces between water molecules help pull other water molecules up the xylem                                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ strong adhesive properties mean water can stick to lignin **OR** cellulose **OR** inside of the xylem                                        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cohesion and adhesion work together to enable capillary action **OR** allow water to move against gravity in xylem vessels            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ evaporation from leaves creates a negative pressure **OR** tension in the xylem vessels, which helps to “pull” more water up the plant            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cohesion-tension theory **OR** cohesive forces between water molecules and the tension created in the xylem vessels create a continuous column of water    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ high latent heat of vaporisation so water can evaporate from the stomata and stimulate the transpiration stream                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ low viscosity, so can flow easily through small pores in the plant        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ root pressure results from the active pumping of ions into the root cells, creating an osmotic gradient that causes water to enter the roots    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Water is able to move around a plant because it is **polar**, so **hydrogen bonds** can form ($\\checkmark$) between different water molecules, which allow them to be **cohesive** so they can **stick together** to be pulled up the **xylem** ($\\checkmark$) at low pressure. Water molecules are **adhesive** which allows them to stick to **cellulose** in the plant ($\\checkmark$). The **cohesion** and **adhesion** work together to enable **capillary action** up the xylem vessels ($\\checkmark$).  Water has a **high latent heat of vaporisation**, so water can **evaporate** from the **stomata** and maintain the transpiration stream ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sources are where carbohydrates are synthesised     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sinks are where carbohydrates are utilised            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sucrose and H:sup[+] are co-transported into the companion cells (in apoplastic route) near the source                                            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sucrose concentration increases in the companion cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sucrose diffuses into sieve tube via plasmodesmata        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sucrose diffuses through cytoplasm of neighbouring cells, via plasmodesmata into phloem (in symplastic route)                        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the increase in concentration of carbohydrates **OR** sucrose **OR** decrease in solute potential results in water flowing into the phloem via osmosis            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ raises the hydrostatic pressure in the sieve tube elements near the source cell        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organic compounds, **OR** carbohydrates **OR** named example, are unloaded from phloem, **OR** sieve tubes near the sink                        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ decreases the solute concentration (in sieve tubes at sinks)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water moves out of the sieve tubes reducing hydrostatic pressure [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water **OR** sap containing the sucrose **OR** carbohydrates moves down the hydrostatic pressure gradient towards the sink                    [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Sources** are where carbohydrates are **synthesised** in plants, such as photosynthesising leaves ($\\checkmark$) and **sinks** are where carbohydrates are **used**, such as growing shoots ($\\checkmark$). In plants, **sucrose and H:sup[+]** are **co-transported** into companion cells near the source ($\\checkmark$), this causes the **sucrose concentration** to **increase** in companion cells ($\\checkmark$). Sucrose then **diffuses** into **sieve tubes** via **plasmodesmata** ($\\checkmark$) and then **diffuses** through the **cytoplasm of neighbouring cells**, into the phloem ($\\checkmark$). This causes water to move into the phloem via **osmosis** from the **xylem** ($\\checkmark$), **raising** the **hydrostatic pressure** ($\\checkmark$). At the **sink**, the **carbohydrates** are **unloaded** from the phloem ($\\checkmark$), **decreasing** the **solute concentration** ($\\checkmark$), **water follows**, thereby **reducing hydrostatic pressure** ($\\checkmark$). The sap containing the carbohydrates **move down** the **hydrostatic pressure gradient** towards the sink ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1321","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1321\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Explain why the processes that occur in interphase are necessary for meiosis.    [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline the process of DNA replication in prokaryotes.        [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain the factors that can result in increased and decreased genetic variation within a population.                    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ replication of DNA **OR** chromosomes **OR** genetic material is necessary to produce sister chromatids for homologous pair separation in meiosis I **OR** to ensure gametes receive one chromosome of each homologous pair            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell growth so that daughter cells are the correct size **OR** can fit all of the organelles    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ duplication of organelles **OR** mitochondria **OR** any other named necessary organelle for gametes, to ensure that daughter cells **OR** gametes have the organelles required for division        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ duplication of centrosomes **OR** microtubule organising structure so that homologous pairs can be separated in meiosis I **OR** sister chromatids can be separated during meiosis II    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ protein synthesis in the cytoplasm to ensure that the required proteins are found in the daughter cells **OR** gametes                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ respiration to provide energy **OR** ATP for interphase **OR** for meiosis     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ checkpoint control repairs errors in DNA replication which prevents transmission of damaged DNA to gametes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cyclin production regulates the progression through different phases of the cell cycle    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    During interphase, the **DNA** is **replicated** to produce **sister chromatids** for the **separation** of **homologous pairs**  in **meiosis I**  ($\\checkmark$) and the **cell grows** so that **daughter cells** are **large enough** to receive all the **organelles** they require ($\\checkmark$). The **organelles** are **duplicated** so that the **gametes** receive all the **required organelles** to be **functional**  ($\\checkmark$). In animal cells, the **centrosomes** are **duplicated** to allow **spindle formation** for the **separation** of **homologous pairs** in **meiosis I** and of **sister chromatids** in **meiosis II** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA is unwound by helicase **OR** DNA helicase breaks hydrogen bonds between base pairs **OR** strands of DNA                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ topoisomerase **OR** gyrase prevents DNA from becoming supercoiled [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ each parental strand acts as a template for the synthesis of a new complementary strand    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (RNA) primase adds one primer to the leading strand **AND** many primers to the lagging strand **OR** (RNA) primase synthesises a short RNA primer complementary to the DNA template strand            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ replication is continuous on the leading strand **AND** discontinuous on the lagging strand    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA polymerase III binds to RNA primer(s)         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA polymerase III synthesises the new DNA strand         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ new DNA strand synthesised in the 5’-3’ direction        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ using complementary base pairing between nucleotides **OR** A with T and G with C on the template strand                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ on the lagging strand, DNA polymerase III creates Okazaki fragments        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA polymerase I replaces the RNA primers with DNA    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA polymerases carry out proofreading to detect and correct errors during replication, increasing replication accuracy                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ligase sticks the DNA fragments together            [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    During DNA replication, the DNA strands are **separated** by DNA **helicase** ($\\checkmark$), and the enzyme **topoisomerase prevents** the DNA from becoming **supercoiled** ($\\checkmark$), which would damage the DNA. DNA **primase** adds **one** primer to the **leading** strand ($\\checkmark$), which it replicates **continuously** ($\\checkmark$) and many primers to the **lagging** strand, which it replicates **discontinuously**. DNA **polymerase III** binds to the RNA **primers** ($\\checkmark$) and **synthesises** the new DNA strand in the **5’-3’** direction.\n\n3. any **seven** of the following (*Answer must include at least **one** factor from each category to achieve full marks*):\n\n    ***Factors that increase variation*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ crossing over of non-sister chromatids in prophase 1 of meiosis results in recombination of alleles **OR** exchange of genetic material                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ independent assortment  **OR** random assortment of homologous chromosomes in metaphase 1 of meiosis, results in different combinations of chromosomes in gametes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sexual reproduction results in the random fusion of gametes during fertilisation        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ migration of organisms **OR** gene flow can lead to speciation by reproductive isolation **OR** geographic isolation            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ behavioural isolation **OR** temporal isolation can lead to speciation        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mutations can lead to differences between individuals in a population **OR** introduce new alleles into the gene pool            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a larger population means that there is a higher likelihood of different mutations arising **OR** different selection pressures acting on the population **OR** more mates to choose from                            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ errors during cell division can cause polyploid organisms    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Factors that decrease variation*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ small population size means that there is likely to be less mutations **OR** less mates to choose from **OR** a higher chance of inbreeding            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ barriers to migration **OR** genetic drift **OR** geographic isolation **OR** ecological isolation **OR** behavioural isolation can lead to reduced gene flow    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ asexual reproduction **OR** budding results in clones **OR** organisms with the same genetic makeup in a population                            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ artificial selection **OR** selective breeding results in organisms in a population having similar traits                            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ natural selection **OR** directional selection **OR** stabilising selection tend to decrease variation                                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ population bottlenecks result in only a small subset of individuals surviving, leading to a loss of genetic diversity        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ founder effect **OR** when a small group of individuals establishes a new population in a different location, the gene pool of the founder population may not accurately represent the genetic diversity of the larger original population    [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Factors that can result in increased genetic variation are **crossing over** during prophase 1 of meiosis, which results in **new combinations** of **alleles** ($\\checkmark$), **random assortment** of homologous chromosomes during metaphase 1, which results in **different combinations** of **chromosomes** in gametes ($\\checkmark$), **sexual reproduction** which results in the **random fusion of gametes** during fertilisation ($\\checkmark$) and **migration** of individuals of a population which could lead to **speciation** through **reproductive isolation** ($\\checkmark$). Factors that can result in decreased genetic variation are a **small population** size which means that there are **fewer mates** to choose from, which can lead to **inbreeding** ($\\checkmark$), **barriers to migration**, such as physical changes to the environment, which **prevent** individuals from **leaving** their population ($\\checkmark$) and **asexual reproduction** which results in a **genetically identical** population ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1323","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1323\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Draw the general structure of an amino acid. $^*$ [3 marks]\n\n2. Explain the processes in the carbon cycle that are vital for life on Earth. [9 marks]\n\n3. Explain why carbon is present in so many biological molecules. [4 marks]\n\n$^*$ *Note: Starting May 2028, this question may be assessed differently due to changes to the Biology guide, including the use of a data booklet.*","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following: \\\n*Accept the simplified or expanded version of a general amino acid*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ central carbon with four single bonds [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ an amine group (NH:sub[2]) **AND** carboxyl group (COOH) on opposite sides of the central carbon [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ a hydrogen **AND** R group on opposite sides of the central carbon [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Extended%20Response%20Questions/images/BI1323.png\" alt=\"BI1323\">\n\n2. any **nine** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the carbon cycle keeps the long-term concentration of carbon in the atmosphere **AND** Earth in balance which helps to keep the Earth’s temperature relatively stable [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ climate change can be damaging to organisms on Earth by making some places uninhabitable **OR** resulting in flooding **OR** causing droughts **OR** increasing the risk of extreme weather events **OR** increasing the spread of pathogens **OR** contributing to the death of organisms that are unable to adapt [1 mark]\n\n ***Terrestrial heterotrophs***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms consume carbon-containing compounds to use for energy **OR** respiration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms release carbon dioxide during respiration, which contributes to carbon dioxide in the atmosphere which helps to regulate the Earth’s temperature [1 mark]\n\n ***Photosynthetic autotrophs***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide from the atmosphere is used by terrestrial autotrophs **OR** plants for photosynthesis **OR** to make carbohydrates **OR** necessary carbon compounds [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ autotrophs fix carbon **OR** store carbon which prevents too much carbon dioxide from being released into the atmosphere, mitigating the greenhouse effect **OR** reducing the impacts of climate change [1 mark]\n\n ***Archaeans***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ archaeans use carbon dioxide **OR** acetate **OR** carbon-containing compounds for methanogenesis **AND** the presence of methane in the atmosphere can help to regulate the Earth’s temperature [1 mark]\n\n ***Oceanic processes***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ soluble carbon dioxide **OR** hydrogen carbonate ions can be absorbed by aquatic plants **OR** aquatic autotrophs so they can carry out photosynthesis **OR** make carbohydrates [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ corals **OR** molluscs have hard outer layers made of calcium carbonate that act as protection [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ gas exchange of carbon dioxide gas between the ocean and atmosphere helps to maintain the Earth’s temperature **AND** maintain the pH of the oceans for aquatic life [1 mark]\n\n ***Decomposition***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ saprotrophs digest non-living organic matter (containing carbon) for nutrition **OR** energy [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ without saprotrophs, organic waste would accumulate on Earth [1 mark]\n\n ***Geological***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fossilised organic matter **OR** coal **OR** oil **OR** natural gas are formed from large deposits of carbon from past geological eras and can provide fuel for cooking **OR** warmth [1 mark]\n [9 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The carbon cycle keeps the **long-term concentration of carbon** in the atmosphere and Earth in **balance**, which helps to keep the Earth’s **temperature** relatively **stable** ($\\checkmark$). **Carbon dioxide** in the **atmosphere** is used by plants for **photosynthesis** ($\\checkmark$). Plants can also **store** or fix carbon which prevents too much carbon dioxide from being **released into the atmosphere**, contributing to the **greenhouse effect** ($\\checkmark$). This is useful, as drastic and uncontrolled **increases** to global **temperatures** can be **damaging to organisms** on Earth by making some places uninhabitable for organisms to live ($\\checkmark$). **Heterotrophs** **consume** carbon-containing compounds to use for **energy** ($\\checkmark$) and also release carbon dioxide during **respiration**, which contributes to carbon dioxide in the atmosphere, which helps to **regulate** the **Earth’s temperature** ($\\checkmark$). **Soluble** carbon dioxide can be absorbed by **aquatic plants** for **photosynthesis** ($\\checkmark$), which uses light energy to convert it into chemical energy. **Molluscs** have **hard outer shells** made of **calcium carbonate** that act as **protection** ($\\checkmark$). **Saprotrophs** **digest** dead **organic matter** containing carbon for **nutrition** ($\\checkmark$), releasing carbon dioxide back into the atmosphere through respiration.\n\n3. any **four** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it can form up to four covalent bonds with a range of different atoms so can form complex structures **OR** it can form bonds with other carbon molecules to create large macromolecules [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ covalent bonds are strong, so it can form stable molecules [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it can form single **AND** double bonds so can form a diverse range of molecules [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon atoms can link together in long chains **OR** branched structures **OR** rings [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it is an abundant element on Earth **OR** is the 15th most abundant element on Earth, so is available to bond with many elements [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it can bond with ions as well as elements to allow for increased diversity of molecular compounds **OR** can add functional groups to it to make a diverse range of compounds [1 mark]\n\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Carbon is present in many biological molecules, because it can form up to **four, strong covalent** bonds ($\\checkmark$), with a range of **different atoms** so it can form **stable complex** structures ($\\checkmark$). Carbon can form **single** and **double bonds** to form a **diverse range** of molecules ($\\checkmark$) and can bond with **ions** as well as **elements** to allow for **increased diversity** of molecular compounds ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1417","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1417\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nEnzymes are central to the control of the majority of biochemical processes in an organism, including the cell cycle.\n\n1. Explain how the rate of an enzyme-controlled reaction is affected by changes in the enzyme concentration.             [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline how polyploidy results in reproductive isolation. [4 marks]\n\n3. Explain the reasons for changes in allele frequency in different populations.      [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following (an annotated graph correctly drawn is acceptable to achieve full marks)\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at lower enzyme concentrations, the rate of reaction is relatively slower    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as enzyme concentration increases, the reaction rate increases             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the greater the concentration of an enzyme is, the higher the probability of substrate molecules binding with  **OR** colliding with the active site **OR** forming enzyme-substrate complexes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at very low concentrations of substrate, increasing enzyme concentration has little effect on reaction rate                       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enzyme saturation **OR** if all active sites have substrate attached, substrate is the limiting factor                                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ eventually, if substrate limited, rate of reaction plateaus        [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    As **enzyme concentration increases**, the rate of reaction also increases ($\\checkmark$).   The **more enzyme molecules** present, the **greater the chances** are of a **substrate** molecule **binding** to the **active site** ($\\checkmark$).  If there is a very **low concentration of substrate**, adding more enzymes has **little effect** on the **rate** of reaction ($\\checkmark$) since there is no more substrate to bind. Once **all** the **substrate** molecules are **attached to active sites** the **rate** of reaction **will plateau** ($\\checkmark$) as the **substrate** is the **limiting factor** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polyploids possess more than two sets of chromosomes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can be caused by hybridisation events between two closely related species     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can result from failed spindle formation **OR** non-disjunction **OR** failed separation of chromosomes **OR** chromosomes replicate and cell does not divide **OR** when a diploid gamete fuses with a haploid gamete **OR** two sperm may fertilise an egg [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms with a different number of chromosomes **OR** new tetraploid and original diploid cannot breed together to produce fertile offspring        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ results in being reproductively isolated from the original population    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ once isolated polyploid population diverges genetically from original population [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the polyploid organism is a new species            [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Polyploidy is when an organism has **more than two sets of chromosomes**  ($\\checkmark$), this is often the result of chromosomes **failing to separate properly** during anaphase I or II of meiosis ($\\checkmark$). The polyploid organism becomes **reproductively isolated** from the original population  ($\\checkmark$) and thus becomes a **new species**  ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allele frequency is the proportion of an allele in a population        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ affected by natural selection, where the frequency of more favourable alleles will increase **OR** the frequency of less favourable alleles will decrease    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ three types of natural selection: disruptive, stabilising and directional    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ disruptive selection is when selection pressures act to remove intermediate varieties in a population, causing the alleles for extreme traits to increase in frequency **OR** the alleles for intermediate traits to decrease in frequency            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increasing the variation in a population        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stabilising selection is when selection pressures act to remove extreme varieties, causing the alleles for extreme traits to decrease    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ decreasing the variation within a population        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ directional selection is when selection pressures act to remove one extreme, which results in the increase of the favoured allele        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mutations are random changes to the DNA base sequence        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if they result in favourable phenotypes, then the frequency of those alleles can increase in the population        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic drift is random changes in the number of alleles in a population  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can lead to rapid changes in allele frequency in a short period of time    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gene migration **OR** gene flow is the transfer of genetic information from one population to another, often via migration        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ introduces new alleles to populations, thereby changing the allele frequency    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ crossing over can result in new allele combinations that may be favourable to     offspring            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can result in the allele frequency of the favourable allele becoming more prominent in a population    [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Allele frequency is the **proportion of an allele in a population** ($\\checkmark$) and it can be affected by many factors, including **natural selection**, which is when organisms that are **more adapted** to an environment are **more likely** to **survive** and breed ($\\checkmark$). There are **three types** of natural selection; **disruptive, stabilising and directional** ($\\checkmark$). **Disruptive selection** is when selection pressures act to **remove intermediate varieties**,  causing the alleles for extreme traits to **increase in frequency** ($\\checkmark$), which **increases the variation in a population** ($\\checkmark$). Stabilising selection is when selection pressures act to **remove extreme varieties**, causing the **alleles for extreme traits to decrease** ($\\checkmark$), which **decreases the variation in a population** ($\\checkmark$). Directional selection is when **selection pressures** act to **remove one extreme**, which results in the **increase of the favoured allele** ($\\checkmark$). Mutations are **random changes to the DNA base sequence** ($\\checkmark$), if they **result in favourable phenotypes, then the frequency of those alleles can increase in the population** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1428","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1428\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nOrganisms have adapted over time to derive nutrients from their environments and protect themselves from being preyed upon.\n\n1. Compare and contrast between chemosynthesis and photosynthesis.             [5 marks]\n\n2. Outline how differences in dentition and jaw structure between chimpanzees (*Pan troglodytes*) and gorillas (*Gorilla gorilla*) are directly related to their respective diets. [3 marks]\n\n3. The ‘evolutionary arms race’ between predators and prey is an ongoing process of adaptation and counter-adaptation. Explain how named predators and named prey have adapted physical traits that prevent or aid predation.      [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **five** of the following (answer must include at least **one** difference and **one** similarity to gain full marks):\n\n    ***Similarities***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** result in the release of energy **OR** ATP **OR** transfer energy from one form to another\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can use the energy released to synthesise glucose **OR** transfer energy into chemical energy stored in organic compounds             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** use inorganic molecules as substrates **OR** use carbon dioxide as a substrate             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can be carried out by bacteria             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** processes are carried out by autotrophs             [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chemosynthesis uses the energy from the oxidation of inorganic compounds **WHEREAS** photosynthesis uses energy from light to excite electrons to release energy        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ photosynthesis requires light to catalyse the reaction **WHEREAS** chemosynthesis does not                       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ photosynthesis produces oxygen **WHEREAS** chemosynthesis produces sulphide **OR** nitrogen compounds                                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ photosynthesis is carried out by plants, algae and bacteria OR cyanobacteria **WHEREAS** chemosynthesis is carried out by archaea and bacteria        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chemosynthesis can take place in extreme conditions, such as near hydrothermal vents under high pressure, **WHEREAS** photosynthesis cannot        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ photosynthesis requires chlorophyll **WHEREA**S chemosynthesis does not        [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Both** chemosynthesis and photosynthesis are carried out by **autotrophs** ($\\checkmark$) and result in the **synthesis of glucose** ($\\checkmark$). However, **chemosynthesis** is a process in which organisms use the energy from the **oxidation of inorganic compounds** to produce glucose, with the release of **sulfur or nitrogen compounds** as **by-products**, whereas **photosynthesis** uses **light energy** ($\\checkmark$) to convert carbon dioxide and water into glucose, with the **release of oxygen** ($\\checkmark$). **Photosynthesis** is carried out by **plants, algae** and **cyanobacteria**, whereas **chemosynthesis** is carried out by certain **archaea** and **bacteria** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gorillas are mainly herbivores **WHEREAS** chimpanzees are mainly omnivores **OR** fruit eaters with occasional meat consumption        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gorillas have large masseter muscles **OR** temporal muscles which allow them to grind up **OR** bite through plants **OR** fibrous material **WHEREAS** chimpanzees have less developed masseter muscles **OR** temporal muscles as they do not need to grind up **OR** bite through plants **OR** fibrous material         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gorillas have a sagittal crest **OR** ridge of bone running along the centre of their skull for their strong temporal muscles to attach to **WHEREAS** chimpanzees have a less developed sagittal crest as they do not need to strongly bite through fibrous plant material [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gorillas have broader **OR** flatter premolars and molars **COMPARED** to chimpanzees to help them grind fibrous plants **OR** vice versa        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chimpanzees have more pronounced canines **COMPARED** to gorillas to allow cutting **OR** tearing of meat **OR** vice versa    [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Gorillas** are predominantly **herbivores**, whereas **chimpanzees** are mainly **omnivores**($\\checkmark$). Therefore **gorillas** have **larger** and **stronger masseter** and **temporal muscles**, compared to chimpanzees,  which allows them to **grind up** and **bite** through **fibrous plant tissue** ($\\checkmark$). **Gorillas** also have **wider, flatter premolars** and **molars** compared to chimpanzees, which enables gorillas to **grind up fibrous plants**($\\checkmark$). **Chimpanzees** have more **pronounced canines** than gorillas to support their **omnivorous diet**, including the **cutting** and **tearing** of **meat**($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following (*answer must include at least **two** examples of predators and **two** examples of prey to gain full marks*):\n\n    ***Adaptations of predators  (each adaptation must have a suitable named example of an organism to gain the mark)***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ eyes on front of head to accurately judge distances **OR** depth perception **OR** give more focused field of view for calculating strikes **OR** detecting prey        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sense organ adapted for detecting prey    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ strong muscles allow them to move **OR** run **OR** swim fast to catch prey    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ streamlined shape to move **OR** run **OR** swim quickly to catch prey            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ camouflage which allows them to stalk **OR** surprise prey        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ have sharp **OR** serrated teeth **OR** large canines to allow them to bite through their prey **OR** tear their prey apart    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ venom **OR** poison **OR** toxins to immobilise **OR** kill prey        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ have sharp claws to tear their prey apart **OR** capture prey **OR** dig **OR** climb to access prey species            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ accept any other reasonable named example of a physical adaptation with named example of an organism with this type of adaptation        [1 mark]\n\n    ***Adaptations of prey  (each adaptation must have a suitable named example of an organism to gain the mark)***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ eyes on side of head to spot predators approaching from all directions        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ camouflage to avoid detection  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ strong muscles to move fast **OR** be agile to escape predators    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mimic appearance of more dangerous **OR** unpalatable species        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ physical defenses to harm predators if they try to consume them    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fur **OR** skin **OR** exoskeleton **OR** outer casing in warning colours **OR** aposematic colouration to indicate they may be toxic            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ release pungent **OR** noxious chemicals that deter predators from attacking them    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ accept any other reasonable named example of a physical adaptation with named example of an organism with this type of adaptation        [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Adaptations of **predators** include having **eyes** on the **front** of their **heads**, which improves their **depth perception** so they can accurately gauge how far away prey are, for example, **eagles** can spot prey from a **great height** and **dive accurately** to capture it ($\\checkmark$). **Predators** may also have **strong muscles** and a **streamlined shape** which allow them to **move quickly** to catch their prey, for example, the shape of **penguins** reduces drag, allowing them to **swim fast** ($\\checkmark$), and **cheetahs** have **powerful leg muscles** to provide **power** for **rapid acceleration** ($\\checkmark$). **Prey** also have **strong muscles**, which allow them to **move fast** and escape predators, for example, **rabbits** have powerful muscles for **bursts** of **speed** and to **turn quickly** ($\\checkmark$). **Prey** often have **eyes** on the **sides** of their **heads**, which allows them to **spot predators** coming from **all directions**, for example, **frogs** have eyes on the side of their heads so they can **remain alert** to predators while **remaining stationary** ($\\checkmark$). **Prey** may also have the ability to **camouflage** with their surroundings to **prevent detection**, for example, the **peppered moth** evolved **darker colouration** during the Industrial Revolution to better hide on **soot-covered trees** ($\\checkmark$). **Predators** can also use the ability to **camouflage** with their surroundings to their advantage by blending in with their surroundings so that they are **not spotted by prey**, for example, the **arctic fox changes colour** with the **seasons**, so is **white** in **winter** to blend in with the snow and **brown** in **summer** to match the rocks and plants ($\\checkmark$). **Mimicry** is also used by **prey** to avoid being attacked by predators. An example of this is the **larvae** of some **butterfly** species resemble the **plants** they feed on which are **poisonous** to many predators, and as they feed, they **sequester toxins** from the plant, becoming **toxic** themselves ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1527","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1527\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1.  Describe how the primordial soup hypothesis (an aqueous mix of simple organic molecules) explains the origin of carbon compounds on Earth. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Mitochondria are organelles that are thought to have evolved from early prokaryotes. Discuss whether mitochondria can be considered living or nonliving. [4 marks]\n\n3.  Discuss the evidence and limitations for the existence of a last universal common ancestor. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ early atmosphere **OR** oceans contained a mixture of gases **OR** carbon dioxide AND methane **OR** water vapour **OR** ammonia **OR** hydrogen gas [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ high levels of UV **OR** ultraviolet light **OR** electrical discharges **OR** lightning catalysed reactions in these gases    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the gases broke down into smaller, highly reactive components    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the smaller components reacted to form larger carbon compounds **OR** more complex organic molecules    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ abiogenesis **OR** chemical evolution    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in water **OR** pools **OR** oceans    [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    The primordial soup hypothesis states that billions of years ago, there were much **higher levels of UV light** on Earth which **catalysed** reactions in the **gases** of the early atmosphere ($\\checkmark$). The UV radiation **broke down** inorganic molecules into **smaller, highly reactive** components ($\\checkmark$). The smaller, highly reactive components reacted to **form larger carbon compounds** ($\\checkmark$).    \n\n2.  Any **four** of the following:\\\n\n    *Answers must include at least one argument for living and one argument for non-living to gain full marks*\\\n\n    **Living because…**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they can replicate independently of the host cell **OR** carry our binary fission    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they contain their own ribosomes to make their own proteins    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they contain their own DNA **OR** genetic material    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they can make their own ATP **OR** release energy from organic molecules **OR** carry out a series of metabolic reactions independent of the host cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they have their own membranes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they can grow larger    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they can excrete waste **OR** carbon dioxide    [1 mark]\n\n    **Non-living because…**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they cannot survive independently outside of a host cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the majority of proteins needed for mitochondrial function are synthesised in the host cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they do not regulate homeostasis independently of the host cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they cannot respond to stimuli **OR** adapt to changing environments    [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Mitochondria can be thought of as non-living because they **cannot survive independently** outside the host cell ($\\checkmark$) and the majority of **proteins** needed for mitochondrial function are **made in the host cell** ($\\checkmark$). However, it could be argued that they are living as they carry out several life processes, such as **reproduction, independently** of the host cell ($\\checkmark$), and contain their own **ribosomes** which they use to **make** their own **proteins** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.  Any **eight** of the following:\\\n\n    *Answers must include at least one argument in support of a last universal common ancestor (LUCA) and at least one gap in the evidence to gain full marks*\n\n    ***Evidence that exists for the existence of a last universal common ancestor***\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ universal genetic code **OR** all organisms have the same nucleotide bases **OR** genetic code     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ conserved molecular structures **OR** all organisms have the same degenerate amino acid code **OR** the same codons code for the same amino acids in all organisms **OR** there are highly conserved DNA sequences that are common across many domains of life    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genomic analysis of fossils shows that there are many similarities in the DNA sequences between modern and extinct organisms    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ specific proteins produced by different organisms can be used interchangeably between species without any major side effects    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ all organisms have a similar process for gene transcription and protein synthesis **OR** transcription and translation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mutations in DNA **OR** amino acid sequences can be tracked over time and used as a ‘molecular clock’ to estimate the age of the last universal common ancestor    [1 mark]\n\n    **Gaps in the evidence for the existence of a last universal common ancestor**\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ incomplete fossil record makes it difficult to link all species together anatomically    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ very early organisms were unicellular **OR** made of soft tissues that do not readily fossilise    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lack of direct fossil evidence for the last universal common ancestor    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the last universal common ancestor does not exist today    [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]        \n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    The last universal common ancestor (LUCA) is proposed to be the evolutionary link between the abiotic phase of Earth’s history and the biotic phase. It is the last ancestor that links all biological life on Earth. Evidence for the LUCA’s existence comes from the fact that organisms are all biochemically similar; they share the **same DNA bases** ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, the **same codons** code for the **same amino acids** in all organisms ($\\checkmark$), and all organisms have a similar process for **gene transcription and protein synthesis**($\\checkmark$). Also, there are some **highly conserved sequences** across the majority of organisms such as **ribosomal RNA** which is common across all domains of life ($\\checkmark$). **Genomic analysis** of fossils also shows that there are many similar DNA sequences between **extinct and modern organisms** ($\\checkmark$). However, there **isn’t** a **complete fossil record** which makes it difficult to link all species anatomically ($\\checkmark$), there is **no direct fossil evidence** for the existence of the LUCA ($\\checkmark$) and the LUCA does **not exist** on Earth **today** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1609","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1609\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1.  Outline the role of the active site in an enzyme.    [3 marks]\n\n2.  Outline how populations of the same species can diverge to form new species        [4 marks]\n\n3.  Describe the regulation and use of hormones during in vitro fertilisation (IVF).    [9 marks]","markScheme":"1.  any **three** of the following:\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it is the binding site for a substrate **OR** specific region where the substrate binds    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it allows for induced fit binding of the substrate    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it facilitates enzyme-substrate specificity **OR** has a specific shape that allows the substrate to bind effectively    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it causes a conformational change in the enzyme-substrate complex, which leads to the formation of products        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it is the site where a substrate is converted into a product **OR** where a substrate is held while a chemical reaction takes place        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it lowers the energy of the transition state **OR** activation energy so that a chemical reaction can take place more efficiently    [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n    The active site of an enzyme is a specific region where a **substrate binds** ($\\checkmark$). Within the active site, the enzyme **lowers** the **energy of the transition state** ($\\checkmark$), facilitating the chemical reaction so that the substrate can be successfully **converted into a product** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.  any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a population consists of all organisms of one species living in the same place at the same time, capable of breeding together    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ members of the species can become geographically isolated **OR** separated by a physical barrier such as a river **OR** mountain    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ members of the species can become temporally isolated **OR** become separated by producing gametes at different times **OR** seasons    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ members of the species can become behaviourally isolated **OR** become separated by displaying distinctive courtship behaviours        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when populations of the same species become separated, genetic drift will cause the allele frequencies to change    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reproductive isolation mechanisms, such as incompatibility of reproductive organs, can prevent interbreeding between separated populations        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mutations can introduce new genetic variations in each isolated population, contributing to genetic divergence over time    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ selective pressures will act on the separated species, leading to an accumulation of genetic differences in each population **OR** natural selection will act on each population so that traits that are beneficial to each group will accumulate     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ over many generations, speciation will occur **OR** the separate populations will not be able to breed to produce fertile offspring        [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n    Members of the same species can become separate populations if they become **geographically isolated**($\\checkmark$) or **behaviourally isolated** ($\\checkmark$). Once populations of the same species are separated, **genetic drift** will cause the **allele frequencies to change** ($\\checkmark$). **Selective pressures** will act on the separated populations, leading to an **accumulation of genetic differences** ($\\checkmark$). Eventually, **speciation**($\\checkmark$) will occur, resulting in populations that can no longer interbreed to produce fertile offspring.\n\n3.  any **nine**  of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ drugs are used to downregulate several hormones **OR** FSH **OR** follicle stimulating hormone AND LH **OR** luteinising hormone, to better control the release **OR** maturation of follicles later on        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the function of FSH **OR** follicle-stimulating hormone is to promote follicle growth **OR** mature eggs     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the function of LH **OR** luteinising hormone is to trigger ovulation **OR** trigger the release of mature eggs from the ovaries    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the downregulation of LH and FSH also results in the downregulation of oestrogen and progesterone    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oestrogen normally stimulates the uterine **OR** endometrial lining to build up        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ progesterone normally stimulates the maintenance of the uterine **OR** endometrial lining for implantation        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ high doses of FSH are injected to stimulate superovulation **OR** the development of many follicles        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the development of many follicles increases the chances of successful fertilisation during the IVF process        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) agonists are injected to prevent premature ovulation **OR** control the timing of ovulation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ human chorionic gonadotropin **OR** hCG is injected to stimulate the follicles to mature    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ progesterone is injected before the fertilised egg is implanted to improve the chances of successful implantation        [1 mark]\n\n    [9 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n    In vitro fertilisation (IVF) is a process used by couples with fertility issues, where mature eggs are fertilised outside the body. To start the process, **drugs** are used to **downregulate several reproductive hormones** to better control the **release and maturation of follicles** later on ($\\checkmark$). The downregulation of **FSH stops follicles from growing** ($\\checkmark$). Drugs are also used to **downregulate LH**, which results in the **prevention of ovulation**  ($\\checkmark$). The downregulation of LH and FSH also results in the **downregulation** of **oestrogen and progesterone**  ($\\checkmark$), which normally function to stimulate the **build-up of the uterine lining**($\\checkmark$) and **maintain the uterine lining** ($\\checkmark$), respectively. Once these hormones are successfully downregulated, **high doses of FSH** are injected to trigger the **development of multiple follicles** in the ovary($\\checkmark$). The development of many follicles **increases the chance of successful fertilisation** during the IVF process ($\\checkmark$). **GnRH agonists** are injected to **prevent premature ovulation** ($\\checkmark$), and **hCG** is injected to stimulate the **follicles to mature** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1637","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1637\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the structure and function of pneumocytes in the human lung.     [4 marks]\n\n2. Describe the roles of the internal and external intercostal muscles during exhalation.    [4 marks]\n\n3. Explain how the depolarisation of skeletal muscle fibres brings about the shortening of sarcomeres in muscle contraction.         [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **four** of the following:\n\n    ***Type I pneumocytes:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ flattened shape **OR**  thin **OR** minimal cytoplasm **OR** minimal organelles to reduce diffusion distance for gaseous exchange            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large surface area **OR** make up approximately 95% of the surface of alveoli for maximum rate of diffusion for gaseous exchange    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Type II pneumocytes:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ microvilli to increase surface area for secretion of surfactant    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ many Golgi apparatus **OR** extensive endoplasmic reticulum **OR** contain lamellar bodies to produce surfactants            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ surfactant reduces surface tension **OR** to prevent alveolar collapse        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can regenerate **OR** can differentiate into type I pneumocytes    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Type I** pneumocytes have a **flattened shape** with **minimal organelles** to **reduce** the **distance** that **gases** have to **diffuse** between the inside of the alveoli and the blood ($\\checkmark$), and have a **large surface area** to **maximise the rate** of **gaseous exchange** ($\\checkmark$). **Type II** pneumocytes are **cuboidal** with extensive **rough endoplasmic reticulum** and many **Golgi apparatus**. This is because **type II** pneumocytes are adapted to **produce surfactants** ($\\checkmark$) to **reduce** the **surface tension** inside the alveoli ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ intercostal muscles work in antagonistic muscle pairs        [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ internal intercostal muscles contract **WHILE** external intercostal muscles relax          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ribcage is pulled downwards and inwards            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ decreasing the volume of the thorax                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ air particles get pushed close together **OR** pressure inside the thorax increases     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ air moves out of the lungs         [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    During expiration, the **internal** intercostal muscles **contract** while the **external** intercostals **relax** ($\\checkmark$). This action pulls the ribcage **inwards** and **downwards** ($\\checkmark$), **decreasing the volume** ($\\checkmark$) of the thoracic cavity. As the volume decreases, the **pressure** inside the thoracic cavity increases ($\\checkmark$), forcing **air out** of the lungs ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ action potential passes down T-tubules of sarcolemma **OR** nerve fibre membrane    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ calcium ions are released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ calcium ions bind to troponin             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ calcium attachment to troponin causes a movement of tropomyosin, exposing the binding sites on actin        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myosin heads attach to the binding sites on actin **OR** cross-bridges form between actin and myosin        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myosin heads release ADP, and rotate **OR** carry out power stroke, pulling the actin filament inwards            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP attaches to the myosin head and breaks the cross-bridge    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrolysis of ATP moves the myosin head into the cocked **OR** ready position    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ repeat of the process shortens the sarcomere        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the Z lines are pulled closer together                [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    When an action potential passes down the **T-tubules** of the **muscle fibre membrane** ($\\checkmark$), **calcium ions** are released from the **sarcoplasmic reticulum** ($\\checkmark$). **Calcium ions bind** to a protein called **troponin** ($\\checkmark$) in the sarcomere. This causes troponin to **move** another protein, **tropomyosin** ($\\checkmark$), away from the myosin binding sites on actin. **Myosin heads** bind to these **sites** forming a **cross-bridge** ($\\checkmark$). **ADP** on the myosin heads is **released** ($\\checkmark$) and the **heads rotate** through about 45$\\degree$ in a **power stroke** ($\\checkmark$), pulling on the actin ($\\checkmark$). **ATP then attaches** to myosin heads, breaking the cross-bridge ($\\checkmark$). **Hydrolysis of ATP** moves the myosin heads into the **cocked position** ($\\checkmark$). This process repeats until the calcium ions are no longer available. **Repeating** this process **shortens the sarcomeres** ($\\checkmark$) in a myofibril and causes **muscle contraction**."},{"ref":"BI1638","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1638\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline how some flowers are adapted to increase their chance of pollination by insects. [4 marks]\n\n2. Describe how the structure of the placenta is adapted for its role in the development of the foetus. [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain the process of IVF (in vitro fertilisation) in humans.     [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ coloured petals to attract insects     towards them **OR** large petals for insects to land on     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ insect-pollinated flowers have nectar that provides nutrients **OR** carbohydrates **OR** sugars for insects    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ anthers produce pollen that is consumed by **OR** provides proteins for insects        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nectary is inside the flower so that insects touch the anthers and stigma when feeding    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pollen grains have spines which stick to insects    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ smell **OR** scent to attract insects        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ filament holds anther in best position to touch insect body    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ top of stigma is sticky so holds pollen in place when insect brushes it        [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Coloured petals** ($\\checkmark$) and the **scent** attract insects to the flower ($\\checkmark$). When insects **feed** on nectar and **pollen** on one plant, some of the **pollen** from the anther **sticks** to the insect due to **spikes** on the pollen ($\\checkmark$).  Then, when the same insect **feeds** on another plant's nectar or pollen, some of the pollen lands on the sticky **stigma** ($\\checkmark$) because the nectary is inside the flower. **Nectar provides carbohydrates** ($\\checkmark$) to the insect, and **pollen provides protein** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chorionic villi **OR** large surface area for exchange of materials        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ thin exchange membrane facilitates efficient diffusion of gases **OR** nutrients    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rich blood supply **OR** maternal blood pools around villi for efficient nutrient **OR** gas exchange [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ intervillous spaces allow maternal blood to flow freely for exchange of substances    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ foetal capillaries close to maternal blood for effective exchange of substances        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ selective permeability protects foetus by allowing essential nutrients **OR** gases **OR** substances through **WHILE** blocking harmful substances    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hormone production **OR** progesterone **OR** hCG secretion to maintain uterine lining **OR** support pregnancy                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ active transport of nutrients **OR** glucose **OR** amino acids    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ immune protection due to transfer of maternal antibodies to foetus        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ removal of foetal waste products **OR** carbon dioxide **OR** urea    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The placenta is adapted for foetal development with **chorionic villi**, providing a **large surface area** for **material exchange** ($\\checkmark$). It has a **thin exchange membrane** that facilitates **efficient diffusion** of **gases** and **nutrients** ($\\checkmark$). A **rich blood supply** surrounds the **villi**, and **intervillous spaces** allow **maternal blood** to **flow freely** ($\\checkmark$) for effective nutrient and gas exchange. **Foetal capillaries** are **close to maternal blood** ($\\checkmark$), enhancing **substance exchange**. The placenta's **selective permeability** permits essential nutrients while **blocking harmful substances** ($\\checkmark$). It produces **hormones** like **progesterone** and **hCG** to **maintain** the **uterine lining** ($\\checkmark$) and support pregnancy.\n\n3. Any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fertilisation outside the body **OR** in the lab        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ drug used to arrest normal menstrual cycle        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ high levels of FSH injected to stimulate maturation of multiple ovules **OR** follicles **OR** superovulation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) agonists are injected to prevent premature ovulation **OR** control the timing of ovulation        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hCG injected to complete maturation of follicles    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ova **OR** eggs harvested **OR** removed from ovaries    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sperm sample collected **OR** concentrated for fertilisation            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ova **OR** eggs mixed with sperm  **OR** ova **OR** eggs fertilised with selected sperm        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fertilised ova **OR** eggs **OR** zygotes **OR** embryos cultured in lab for several days        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ progesterone injected before the fertilised egg is implanted to improve the chances of successful implantation                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ several embryos selected for implantation into uterus        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pregnancy test used to check if pregnancy has occurred **OR** to check if embryo implanted                        [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    In vitro fertilisation (IVF) involves **fertilisation outside the body** ($\\checkmark$) in a laboratory. First, **drugs** are used to **suppress** the **menstrual cycle**  ($\\checkmark$), followed by injection of high levels of **FSH** injections to **stimulate** multiple **ova to mature** ($\\checkmark$). **Gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) agonists** are injected to **prevent premature ovulation** ($\\checkmark$), and **hCG** is injected to complete the **maturation of follicles** ($\\checkmark$). The **mature eggs** are **retrieved from** the **ovaries**  ($\\checkmark$) and **mixed** with **sperm** in the **lab** ($\\checkmark$). Selected **embryos** are then **implanted** into the **uterus**  ($\\checkmark$), and a **pregnancy test** is performed to confirm **implantation** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1639","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1639\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast between the alpha-helices and beta-pleated sheets in polypeptide structure. [5 marks]\n\n2. Outline the processes during meiosis that lead to genetic variation.    [4 marks]\n\n3. Explain the roles of the different enzymes involved in the replication of a DNA molecule.        [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **five** of the following (at least **one** similarity and **one** difference required to achieve full marks):\n\n    ***Similarities*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are secondary protein structures        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are stabilised by hydrogen bonds        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contribute to the overall 3D **OR** tertiary structure of proteins        [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ alpha-helices are coiled **WHILE** beta-pleated sheets are ribbon-like            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen bonding occurs within a single strand in alpha-helices **WHILE** hydrogen bonding occurs between strands in beta-pleated sheets        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ alpha-helices are common in  fibrous proteins **WHILE** beta-pleated sheets are common in globular proteins                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ alpha-helices provide elasticity **OR** flexibility **WHILE** beta-pleated sheets provide strength **OR** rigidity                [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Alpha-helices and beta-pleated sheets are **both secondary structures** ($\\checkmark$) in proteins, **stabilised** by **hydrogen bonds** ($\\checkmark$). **Alpha-helices** are **coiled** and **provide flexibility**, while **beta-pleated sheets** are **sheet-like** ($\\checkmark$) and provide **rigidity** ($\\checkmark$). In **alpha-helices**, **hydrogen bonding** occurs **within** a **single strand**, whereas in **beta-pleated** sheets, it occurs **between strands** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ crossing over **OR** chiasma formation between non-identical sister chromatids **OR** homologous chromosomes in prophase I        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ exchanges segments of DNA **OR** results in new combination of alleles         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ random orientation of homologous chromosomes in metaphase I **OR** random orientation of sister chromatids in metaphase II            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ leads to different combinations of maternal and paternal chromosomes in gametes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ independent assortment             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ produces different combinations of alleles in the gametes **OR** $\\ce{2^n}$ possible combinations    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mutations introduce genetic variations        [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    During meiosis, genetic variation is achieved through **crossing over** in **prophase I** ($\\checkmark$), where **non-identical sister chromatids exchange genetic material** ($\\checkmark$). **Random orientation** of **homologous chromosomes** during **metaphase I** and **sister chromatids** during **metaphase II** ($\\checkmark$) leads to different combinations of maternal and paternal chromosomes in the gametes ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ helicase unwinds the DNA double helix **OR** forms replication fork **OR** breaks hydrogen bonds between strands            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows each strand to be used as a template        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gyrase relieves strain caused by unwinding of the DNA [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA primase synthesises a short RNA primer        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ which provides a starting point with a free 3’ hydroxyl group for DNA polymerase III to begin adding DNA nucleotides        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA polymerase III adds nucleotides to the growing DNA strand    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in the 5’ to 3’ direction **OR** adds nucleotides to the 3’ end            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ continuously on leading strand **AND** discontinuously on lagging strand    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA polymerase III has proofreading activity **OR** removes any mismatched base and replaces it with correct nucleotide            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA polymerase I removes RNA primers and replaces them with DNA    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA ligase joins Okazaki fragments on the lagging strand    [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    During DNA replication, **helicase unwinds** the **double helix**, creating a **replication fork** ($\\checkmark$), **allowing** the DNA **strands** to be **separated** ($\\checkmark$). **Gyrase relieves** the **torsional strain** ($\\checkmark$) caused by the unwinding of the helix. **DNA primase** synthesises a **short RNA primer** ($\\checkmark$), providing a starting point for **DNA polymerase III**, which **adds nucleotides** ($\\checkmark$) in the **5’ to 3’ direction** ($\\checkmark$). **DNA polymerase I replaces RNA primers** with **DNA nucleotides** ($\\checkmark$), ensuring the newly synthesised strand is continuous. **DNA ligase joins** the **Okazaki fragments** together on the **lagging strand** ($\\checkmark$), forming a complete DNA strand."},{"ref":"BI2650","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI2650\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Draw a labelled diagram of the fluid mosaic model of membrane structure.    [5 marks]\n\n2. Compare and contrast glycoproteins and glycolipids.                [3 marks]\n\n3. Explain how plasma membranes are selectively permeable.            [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **five** of the following *(mark awarded if structure drawn in correct position **AND** labelled correctly):*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phospholipid bilayer with phosphate heads pointing outside and lipid tails pointing inwards        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ peripheral protein shown on the surface        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ integral protein shown within the phospholipid bilayer        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glycoprotein **OR** glycolipid shown with carbohydrate chain attached    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cholesterol in bilayer        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ protein channel across the bilayer        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Hydrophobic **OR** hydrophilic region indicated        [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Extended%20Response%20Questions/images/2650a.png\" alt=\"2650a\">\n\n2. any **three** of the following *(at least **one** similarity and **one** difference must be given to gain full marks):*\n\n    ***Similarities***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have a carbohydrate chain attached        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are found on the outer surface of the plasma membrane     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** involved in cell recognition or signalling        [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glycoproteins are composed of carbohydrate chain attached to proteins **WHEREAS** glycolipids are composed of carbohydrate chains attached to lipids            [1]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glycoproteins are part of integral **OR** peripheral proteins **WHEREAS** glycolipids are part of the phospholipid bilayer        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glycoproteins more commonly act as receptors **WHEREAS** glycolipids play a greater role in maintaining membrane stability            [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Glycoproteins and glycolipids **both** have a **carbohydrate chain attached** ($\\checkmark$) and are found on the **outer surface** of the plasma membrane ($\\checkmark$). However, **glycoproteins** consist of a **carbohydrate chain** attached to a **protein**, whereas **glycolipids** consist of a carbohydrate chain attached to a **lipid** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrophobic core of the phospholipid bilayer **AND** embedded **OR** transmembrane proteins are key structural features that enable selective absorption    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ non-polar or small uncharged molecules can cross the membrane by simple diffusion but this process is not selective            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the hydrophobic core of the bilayer prevents passage of polar **OR** charged molecules [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ channel proteins allow specific ions **OR** molecules to pass through the membrane [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some channel proteins are gated and can switch between open and closed states    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ voltage-gated channel proteins respond to changes in membrane potential     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ligand-gated channels open in response to binding of a specific molecule    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carrier proteins transport molecules by changing shape to move them across the membrane         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ facilitated diffusion through proteins allows transport of large polar molecules without using energy **OR** passively            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ active transport proteins **OR** protein pumps use ATP to move molecules against the concentration gradient             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ co-transport proteins couple the movement of one substance down its concentration gradient with the movement of another against its gradient     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the selective use of specific transport proteins allows the membrane to control which substances enter or leave the cell        [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The plasma membrane is made of a phospholipid bilayer which has a **hydrophobic core** and that, along with the **embedded proteins** are key in the selective absorption of substances into the cell ($\\checkmark$). **Non-polar** or small **uncharged molecules** can pass through by **simple diffusion**, but this process is **not selective** ($\\checkmark$). The **hydrophobic core blocks ions** and **polar molecules**, so specific transport proteins are needed ($\\checkmark$). **Channel proteins** allow certain **ions** or **molecules** to pass through the membrane ($\\checkmark$). Some of these channels are **gated** and only **open** under **specific conditions**, controlling the movement of substances ($\\checkmark$). **Carrier proteins** move **specific molecules** by **changing shape** as they transport them across the membrane ($\\checkmark$). **Active transport proteins** use **ATP** to pump substances **against** their **concentration gradient**, allowing selective uptake or removal of materials ($\\checkmark$).  **Co-transport proteins** couple the **movement of one** substance **down** its **concentration gradient** with the movement of **another against its gradient** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2670","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI2670\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline how other organic molecules are formed using glucose produced in the Calvin cycle and substances absorbed from the soil.        [4 marks]\n\n2. Describe the overall role of ATP and NADPH in the Calvin cycle.            [4 marks]\n\n3. Explain how light energy is used to produce the molecules required in the light-independent reactions.            [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fatty acids  **OR** amino acids can be synthesised from glycerate-3-phosphate **OR** triose phosphate         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mineral ions from the soil are required for the synthesis of some organic molecules    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phosphorus for synthesis of  phospholipids **OR** nucleotide **OR** ATP **OR** nucleic acids **OR** DNA **OR** RNA comes from phosphate ions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nitrogen for synthesis of amino acids **OR** proteins comes from nitrate **OR** ammonium ions [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sulfur for synthesis of some amino acids **OR** proteins comes from sulfate ions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon fixed during the Calvin cycle provides the carbon backbone for biosynthetic pathways [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Triose phosphate** from the Calvin cycle can be used to make **fatty acids** or **amino acids** ($\\checkmark$). **Phosphate ions** absorbed from the **soil** are needed to make **ATP** and other **nucleotides** ($\\checkmark$). **Nitrate** from the soil provides **nitrogen** for making **amino acids** and **proteins** ($\\checkmark$). The **carbon backbone** for these molecules comes from **carbon dioxide fixed** during the Calvin cycle ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP provides energy for converting glycerate 3-phosphate **OR** GP to triose phosphate **OR** TP            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ NADPH reduces glycerate 3-phosphate **OR** GP to triose phosphate **OR** TP by donating electrons AND a hydrogen ion    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP is also used to regenerate ribulose bisphosphate **OR** RuBP from triose phosphate **OR** TP            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ for each $\\ce{CO_2}$ fixed, 3 ATP and 2 NADPH are used        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to make one glucose molecule, 18 ATP and 12 NADPH are required [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ resulting ADP and $\\ce{NADP^+}$ return to the light-dependent reactions so more ATP and NADPH can be produced        [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **ATP** provides **energy** to convert **glycerate 3-phosphate** (GP) into **triose phosphate** (TP) ($\\checkmark$). **NADPH reduces GP to TP** by donating two **electrons** and a **hydrogen ion** ($\\checkmark$). **ATP** is also required to **regenerate ribulose bisphosphate** (RuBP) **from TP** ($\\checkmark$). For each molecule of **carbon dioxide fixed, 3 ATP** and **2 NADPH** are **used** in the Calvin cycle ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light energy is absorbed by pigments **OR** chlorophyll **OR** accessory pigments in photosystems **OR** photosystems I **OR** II        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ photosystems are photosynthetic pigment complexes **OR** protein-pigment complexes embedded  in thylakoid membranes            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ absorption of light excites electrons in the reaction centre of photosystem **OR** excites electrons in chlorophyll a    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ excited electrons from Photosystem II are transferred through an electron transport chain [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy released from electrons is used to pump hydrogen ions **OR** protons **OR**  $\\ce{H^+}$  into thylakoid space                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ an electrochemical gradient **OR** proton **OR** hydrogen ions **OR** $\\ce{H^+}$ gradient is built up across thylakoid membrane                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ protons **OR** hydrogen ions **OR** $\\ce{H^+}$ diffuse back into stroma down their concentration gradient **OR** electrochemical gradient through ATP synthase **OR** by chemiosmosis        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ flow of protons **OR** hydrogen ions **OR** $\\ce{H^+}$ **OR** chemiosmosis brings about the phosphorylation of ADP to form ATP **OR** brings about the production of ATP from ADP and inorganic phosphate        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ photolysis splits water into electrons **AND** protons **OR** hydrogen ions  **OR** $\\ce{H^+}$ **AND** oxygen                        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electrons from photolysis replace those lost from Photosystem II         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ protons **OR** hydrogen ions **OR** $\\ce{H^+}$ from photolysis are used in chemiosmosis **OR** in the reduction of $\\ce{NADP^+}$ **OR** the production of NADPH            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light re-excites **OR** also excites electrons in Photosystem I     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electrons from Photosystem I are used to reduce $\\ce{NADP^+}$ to NADPH [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ $\\ce{NADP^+}$ accepts two electrons **AND** one proton **OR** hydrogen ion **OR** $\\ce{H^+}$ to form NADPH    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP and NADPH are the molecules required for the light-independent reactions    [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Light energy** is **absorbed** by **pigments** in **photosystems I and II** embedded in the thylakoid membranes ($\\checkmark$).  This **energy excites electrons** in **chlorophyll a** at the **reaction centre** of each photosystem  ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    **Electrons** from **Photosystem II** are transferred through an **electron transport chain**  ($\\checkmark$). The **energy released** is used to **pump protons** ($\\ce{H^+}$) into the **thylakoid space**, creating a **proton gradient** ($\\checkmark$). **Protons** move **back into the stroma** through **ATP synthase**, driving the production of **ATP from ADP** and **inorganic phosphate**  ($\\checkmark$). **Water** is **split** in **photolysis**, producing **electrons, protons** ($\\ce{H^+}$), and **oxygen**  ($\\checkmark$). The **electrons** from photolysis **replace** those lost from **Photosystem II**  ($\\checkmark$). **Light** also **excites electrons** in **Photosystem I**  ($\\checkmark$).  These electrons are used to **reduce** $\\mathbf{NADP^+}$ **to NADPH**  ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2798","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI2798\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Electron microscopy provides images of cells with significantly higher resolution when compared to light microscopy. Evaluate the implications and limitations of relying primarily on electron micrographs to understand cell function.            [3 marks]\n\n2. Explain how advances in microscopy have contributed to our understanding of cell structure and function.                                [5 marks]\n\n3. Discuss the evidence for and against the idea that prokaryotic cells are “simple” compared to eukaryotic cells.                             [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following, *must include at least **one** implication and **one** limitation*\n\n    ***Implications***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fine detail resolution has led to a more detailed understanding of structure-function relationships    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ discovery of new organelles and structures    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Limitations***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ requires complex sample preparation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sample preparation can leave artefacts    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ samples must be fixed, dead, meaning no dynamic cellular processes can be observed    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pigmentation or colour is not detected    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ maximum understanding comes from combining information from both light and electron microscopy    [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Electron microscopy gives us very **detailed images**, which let us see how the tiny parts of a cell are **structured to fulfil** their **functions** ($\\checkmark$). It has also led to the discovery of **new things inside** cells ($\\checkmark$). Unfortunately, to **prepare specimens** for electron microscopy, they must be treated in such a way that they are **killed**, and so we **cannot observe processes** happening in real time ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light microscopy allowed for the initial visualisation of cells and basic structures    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ staining techniques have enhanced the visibility of cell components    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fluorescence microscopy enables visualisation and localisation of specific molecules     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ live-imaging techniques allow dynamic processes to be observed    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electron microscopy provides significantly higher resolution than light microscopy    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transmission electron microscopy reveals details of the internal structure of organelles    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ scanning electron microscopy provides 3-D images of cell surfaces **OR** cell shape    [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Light microscopy first allowed us to see **cells** and their **basic** parts ($\\checkmark$). Later, **staining** made it easier to see these parts more **clearly** ($\\checkmark$). **Fluorescence** microscopy has allowed scientists to visualise and pinpoint the **locations** of specific molecules within cells ($\\checkmark$).  **Electron** microscopy provides **far greater resolution** than light microscopy ($\\checkmark$), making it possible to see **internal structures** like mitochondria and membranes in **fine detail** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    ***In favour***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells lack membrane-bound organelles **OR** have less compartmentalisation        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells have a single, circular chromosome; eukaryotic cells have multiple linear chromosomes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells are generally smaller        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells reproduce by binary fission, which is less complex than mitosis/meiosis        [1 mark]\n\n    ***Against***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells efficiently carry out all life processes within a single cell, demonstrating functional sophistication        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells exhibit diverse and complex metabolic pathways        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells exhibit unique metabolic pathways, ie chemosynthesis        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells can engage in cell signaling        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells have highly specialised adaptations (ie flagellae)        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells have a high surface area to volume ratio, increasing the exchange rate with the surroundings         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells are structurally simpler but functionally complex        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ evaluation of the application of the word “simple”        [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Prokaryotes are generally **smaller** ($\\checkmark$) than most eukaryotic cells, and they reproduce by **binary fission** ($\\checkmark$) instead of mitosis or meiosis. Prokaryotic cells are often called more simple than eukaryotic cells, but it is important to **distinguish between structure and function** ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotic cells may appear simple, but they still carry out all of the **functions of life** ($\\checkmark$) and have some that are unique to prokaryotes. Although prokaryotes **do not have membrane-bound organelles** ($\\checkmark$), they still have **complex metabolic** pathways like cellular respiration and even pathways eukaryotes do not have, like **chemosynthesis** ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotes can communicate with each other via **cell signalling** pathways like quorum sensing ($\\checkmark$) and also have highly **specialised structures** such as flagellae for motility ($\\checkmark$). The term “simple” is an oversimplification of a superficial observation."},{"ref":"BI2816","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI2816\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Explain how xylem vessels are adapted for their role in plants.  [4 marks]\n\n2. Draw an annotated plan of the vascular tissues, cortex and epidermis in a transverse section of a dicotyledonous stem.  [4 marks]\n\n3. Describe the movement of water in plants, from soil to atmosphere.    [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ no cytoplasm **OR** organelles for unobstructed water flow  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell walls thickened with lignin for structural support **OR** prevents collapse under tension  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pits in walls to allow lateral movement of water between adjacent xylem vessels **OR** into surrounding tissues  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cells joined end-to-end with no end walls to form a continuous tube for water transport [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lignin deposited in spiral **OR** ring patterns to maintais flexibility of the vessel **OR** allows plant growth   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ narrow diameter enhances capillary action **OR** water cohesion  [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Xylem vessels are made of dead cells **joined end to end**, forming long **tubes** with **no cytoplasm**, allowing for **unimpeded water flow** ($\\checkmark$). Their **walls** are **thickened** with **lignin**, which **prevents collapse** and **provides support** ($\\checkmark$). The presence of **pits** in the walls enables **water** to **move sideways between vessels** ($\\checkmark$), and **spiral** or **ringed lignin** allows **flexibility** so the stem can bend without breaking ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ xylem correctly positioned and annotated    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phloem correctly positioned and annotated    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cortex correctly positioned and lannotated     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ epidermis correctly positioned and annotated    [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Extended%20Response%20Questions/images/BI2816%20B3.2%20PQ%20LA.jpeg.JPEG\" alt=\"BI2816 B3.2 PQ LA.jpeg\">\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water enters root hair cells from the soil by osmosis due to a water potential gradient    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large surface area of root hairs increases rate of water uptake      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water moves across the root cortex via the apoplast **OR** symplast pathways (*no longer needed from May 2028*)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ casparian strip blocks the apoplast pathway, forcing water into the symplast **OR** allowing selective uptake into the xylem (*no longer needed from May 2028*)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water moves up the xylem due to cohesion between water molecules  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adhesion between water molecules and xylem vessel walls helps maintain the column of water  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transpiration at the leaves creates negative pressure that pulls water up the xylem [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water evaporates from the cell walls of mesophyll cells into the air spaces of the leaf     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water vapour diffuses out of the leaf through the stomata down a water potential gradient   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stomata regulate the rate of water loss depending on environmental conditions [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Water **enters root hair cells** from the soil by **osmosis** due to a **water potential gradient** between the **soil** and the **cytoplasm** of the root hair cells ($\\checkmark$). The **large surface area** of **root hairs** **increases** the rate of water **uptake** ($\\checkmark$). Water moves across the root cortex  towards the xylem ($\\checkmark$). Within the xylem, water is **pulled upward** due to **cohesion** between **water molecules** ($\\checkmark$) and **adhesion** to the **walls** of the **xylem vessels** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    **Transpiration** at the leaves creates **negative pressure**, **drawing water upward** ($\\checkmark$). Water **evaporates** from the **surface of mesophyll cell walls** into **air spaces** ($\\checkmark$), and water **vapour** then **diffuses out** of the **stomata** down a **water potential gradient** into the atmosphere ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2881","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI2881\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Explain how community vaccination programs contribute to herd immunity but may not result in the eradication of the disease    [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline the features that are common to all viruses.        [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe how mutations in proto-oncogenes and tumour suppressor genes can lead to the formation of a secondary tumour.                        [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following *(must include at least **one** explanation of herd immunity and **one** statement for why it may not eradicate the disease to gain full marks)*:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ herd immunity occurs when enough individuals in a population are immune either via vaccination **OR** previous exposure reducing disease transmission    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ this reduces chance of unvaccinated people **OR** vulnerable individuals **OR** people within the population who do not have immunity, from getting the disease        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ herd immunity reduces the basic reproductive number **OR** $\\ce{R_0}$ below 1, causing infection rates to decline over time                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vaccination disrupts transmission chains, especially for diseases with human-only transmission                [1 mark]\n\n    ***It may not lead to the eradication of the disease if…***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ not enough of the population are vaccinated, so the disease is still able to spread between susceptible people        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pathogen mutates frequently **OR** antigenic drift, reducing vaccine effectiveness        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the immunity provided by the vaccine is relatively short-lived, so even vaccinated people become vulnerable to the disease over time    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ asymptomatic carriers can transmit  disease to susceptible people, even if carriers have been vaccinated        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pathogen has animal reservoirs **OR** zoonotic disease, so it can re-enter the human population        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vaccine coverage is uneven geographically, allowing disease to persist in under-vaccinated areas            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ socioeconomic **OR** political barriers prevent effective vaccine delivery **OR** uptake in some regions                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ public vaccine hesitancy **OR** misinformation reduces uptake, keeping immunity levels below the threshold        [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Herd immunity occurs when a  **large enough proportion**  of the population is **immune** to a disease, either via **prior infection** or **vaccination**, which reduces the overall transmission of the pathogen ($\\checkmark$). This **indirectly protects** individuals who are **not immune** ($\\checkmark$). However, if the level of immunity within the population is **not high enough**, the disease can still **circulate** between **susceptible individuals** ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the **immunity** gained from vaccines may **decline over time**, so without booster doses, even **previously vaccinated** individuals can become **susceptible again** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five**  of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ non-cellular **OR** acellular structure            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ smaller than bacteria **OR** ultramicroscopic **OR** have a diameter range of 20 - 400 nm    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contain DNA **OR** RNA **OR** genetic material        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ have no cytoplasm **OR** organelles **OR** plasma membranes **OR** ribosomes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ no metabolism **OR** do not carry out metabolic processes independently     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ surrounded by capsid **OR** protein coat        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ obligate intracellular parasites **OR** reproduction depends on infecting host cells    [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Features common to all viruses include their **acellular structure** ($\\checkmark$) and the presence of **either DNA** or **RNA** as genetic material ($\\checkmark$). They **lack organelles** and **do not carry out metabolism** ($\\checkmark$). Viruses are also **extremely small**, much **smaller than bacteria** ($\\checkmark$), and are enclosed in a **protein coat** called a **capsid** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following *(must include at least **one** statement  from each of the three sections to gain full marks)*:\n\n    ***Proto-oncogenes*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ proto-oncogenes are genes that code for proteins that stimulate normal cell division    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Mutations can convert proto-oncogenes into oncogenes, leading to overexpression **OR** increased translation of the proto-oncogenes’ active protein **OR** a permanently activated protein that drives continuous cell division                [1 mark]\n\n    ***Tumour suppressor genes***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tumour suppressor genes encode proteins that inhibit cell division **OR** repair damaged DNA **OR** promote controlled cell death **OR** apoptosis        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mutations in tumour suppressor genes result in no **OR** decrease in protein being translated **OR** the translation of an inactive **OR** non-functional protein        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ resulting in uncontrolled OR unregulated cell division OR tumour formation    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Formation of a secondary tumour***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a primary tumour forms at the original mutation site    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some cells in the primary tumour acquire additional mutations, enabling invasion of local tissues **OR** detachment        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cancerous cells from the primary tumour metastasise **OR** move through the bloodstream **OR** lymphatic system to other sites in the body    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ these cells can establish **OR** grow into a secondary tumour at a new site        [1 mark]\n\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Proto-oncogenes** are normal genes that code for proteins that **promote normal cell division** ($\\checkmark$). When proto-oncogenes undergo **mutation**, they can become **oncogenes**, resulting in **increased translation** of the protein and therefore **uncontrolled cell division** ($\\checkmark$). In contrast, **tumour suppressor genes** code for proteins that **inhibit cell cycle progression** ($\\checkmark$), which can **prevent the translation** of a protein, removing important cell cycle checkpoints ($\\checkmark$). Mutations in either tumour suppressor or proto-oncogenes can result in **uncontrolled cell division** ($\\checkmark$), forming a **primary tumour** at the **original mutation site** ($\\checkmark$). Gradually, some cells within the primary tumour may accumulate **additional mutations**, enabling them to **metastasise** ($\\checkmark$) and form a **secondary tumour** in another part of the body ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2917","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI2917\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Describe the process of epigenetic inheritance.      [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline    the epigenetic origins of phenotypic differences in lion-tiger hybrids.     [6 marks]\n\n3. Explain how transcription in eukaryotic cells is regulated by proteins that bind to specific DNA sequences.        [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ epigenetic inheritance involves passing on changes in gene expression without altering the DNA sequence            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can result in phenotypic differences being passed to daughter cells **OR** offspring        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA methylation is a chemical tag added to DNA            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ histone modification alters chromatin structure [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can lead to phenotypic differences between genetically identical cells or organisms [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA methylation can prevent transcription factor binding, blocking RNA polymerase from initiating transcription       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ histone acetylation can loosen chromatin, increasing gene expression        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ histone methylation can tighten **OR** loosen chromatin, affecting transcription    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ epigenetic tags **OR** DNA methylation **OR** histone modification can remain in place during mitosis or meiosis     [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Epigenetic inheritance occurs when **changes** in **gene expression** are passed to **daughter cells** or **offspring without altering** the **DNA nucleotide sequence** ($\\checkmark$). Chemical modifications such as **DNA methylation** can **prevent transcription factors** from **binding** to **promoters** ($\\checkmark$), while **histone modification**, such as **methylation**, alters **chromatin structure**, affecting whether **genes** are **accessible** for **transcription** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **six** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ligers are the offspring of a male lion and a female tiger **AND** tigons are the hybrid offspring of a female lion and a male tiger    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ IGF2 is a growth-promoting gene involved in cell division **OR** development     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ IGF2 is expressed differently in the gametes **OR** sex cells of male and female lions due to epigenetic imprinting        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the IGF2 allele in a lion’s sperm is active and promotes rapid foetal growth     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the IGF2 allele in a lioness’ egg is silenced via imprinting **OR** DNA methylation **OR** epigenetic tags        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in tigers, both maternal and paternal IGF2 alleles are active **OR** expressed **OR** there is no imprinting on **OR** silencing of the tiger maternal IGF2 gene         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ligers are larger than both parents because both IGF2 alleles are active, leading to excessive growth        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tigons are smaller **OR** normal size because the maternal IGF2 allele from the lioness is silenced **OR** only the paternal IGF2 allele from the tiger is expressed     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phenotypic differences arise from mismatched epigenetic imprinting systems between lions and tigers        [1 mark]\n\n    [6 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    When lions and tigers interbreed, the resulting hybrids, **ligers** from **male lions** and **female tigers** and **tigons** from **male tigers** and **female lions** ($\\checkmark$), show distinct phenotypic differences. These differences arise from **epigenetic imprinting** of the **IGF2 gene**, which **promotes growth** ($\\checkmark$). In **lions**, the **maternal IGF2 allele** is **silenced** by **DNA methylation**, so only the **paternal allele** is **expressed** ($\\checkmark$). In tigers, **both parental IGF2 alleles** are **active** ($\\checkmark$). As a result, **ligers** inherit **two active IGF2 alleles**, one from the lion and one from the tigress, leading to **excessive growth** ($\\checkmark$). **Tigons** are the **same size**, or slightly **smaller**, than their parents because **only** the **paternal IGF2 allele** is **expressed** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    |Feature | Lion | Tiger | Liger | Tigon  |\n    | :-- | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n    | Parental combination | — | — | **Male lion** $\\times$ **Female tiger** | **Female lion** $\\times$ **Male tiger**   ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | Maternal IGF2  | **Silenced via methylation** ($\\checkmark$) | **Active** | Active (from tigress) | Silenced (from lioness) |\n    | Paternal IGF2  | Active | **Active** ($\\checkmark$) | Active (from lion) | Active (from tiger) |\n    | Total IGF2 expression | One active allele | Two active alleles | **Two active alleles** |  **One active allele** |\n    | Phenotypic outcome | Normal growth | Normal growth | **Excessive growth (larger)** |  **Normal or smaller size** |\n    | Cause of hybrid difference | — | — | **No silencing of maternal IGF2** ($\\checkmark$) | **Silencing of maternal IGF2** ($\\checkmark$) |\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA polymerase II and general transcription factors are required to initiate transcription    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA polymerase II reads the DNA template to build a strand of mRNA    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ general transcription factors help RNA polymerase II bind correctly to the promoter [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a promoter is a specific DNA sequence located near the start of a gene    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ promoter indicates where RNA polymerase should bind **AND** where transcription begins [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enhancers are DNA sequences that increase transcription of a gene    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enhancers can be located far upstream  **OR**  downstream from the gene to be transcribed    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ activator proteins bind to enhancers to increase transcription of the gene [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ external signals **OR** hormones **OR** growth factors **OR** stress activate activator transcription factors         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA looping brings enhancer-bound activators close to promoter-bound factors to initiate transcription            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ repressor factors bind to silencers to inhibit transcription        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ co-activators **OR** mediator proteins facilitate communication between transcription factors and RNA polymerase     [1 mark]\n\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Transcription in eukaryotic cells is tightly regulated by proteins that bind to specific DNA sequences. **RNA polymerase II** and **general transcription factors** are essential for **initiating transcription** ($\\checkmark$). General **transcription factors** help **RNA polymerase bind** to the **promoter** ($\\checkmark$), a **DNA sequence** near the **start** of a **gene** that marks **where transcription begins** ($\\checkmark$). **Enhancers** are regulatory DNA elements that **increase transcription rates** ($\\checkmark$), even when located **far upstream** or **downstream** from the **gene** ($\\checkmark$). **Activator proteins bind** to **enhancers** and are **activated** by **external signals** such as **hormones** or **stress** ($\\checkmark$). **DNA looping** brings **enhancer-bound activators** into proximity with **promoter-bound transcription factors**, facilitating the **assembly** of the **transcription initiation complex** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2931","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI2931\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast the structures of cellulose, starch and glycogen.        [5 marks]\n\n2. Draw an annotated diagram showing the distribution of tissues in a transverse section through the root of a dicotyledonous plant.                    [4 marks]\n\n3. Explain how the phytohormones control phototropism in plant shoots.        [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **five** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** similarity and **one** difference to gain full marks*):\n\n    ***Similarities***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **ALL** are polysaccharides        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **ALL** are made from glucose monomers            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **ALL** are held together by **OR** contain glycosidic bonds        [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ starch is made up of amylose and amylopectin **OR** two different polysaccharides **WHEREAS** cellulose and glycogen are made up of a single type of polymer chain         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cellulose is composed of $\\beta$-glucose monomers **WHEREAS** starch and glycogen are composed of $\\alpha$-glucose monomers        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ amylose and cellulose form linear chains **WHEREAS** amylopectin and glycogen are branched polymers             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ amylose has only $\\alpha$-1-4-glycosidic bonds **WHEREAS** amylopectin and glycogen have both $\\alpha$-1-4 and $\\alpha$-1-6 glycosidic bonds         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polymers of cellulose form many hydrogen bonds with each other **WHEREAS** starch and glycogen do not         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ compared to starch **OR** amylopectin **OR** amylose and cellulose, glycogen is more highly branched     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ every other monomer in cellulose is rotated 180$\\degree$ **WHEREAS** in starch and glycogen the monomers are all oriented the same way         [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Cellulose, starch, and glycogen are **all polymers** of **glucose** monomers ($\\checkmark$) joined by **glycosidic bonds** ($\\checkmark$), with **cellulose** consisting of $\\beta$**-glucose** and **starch and glycogen** composed of $\\alpha$**-glucose** monomers ($\\checkmark$). **Cellulose** and **amylose** form **linear chains**, whereas **amylopectin** and **glycogen** are **branched** polymers ($\\checkmark$). **Amylose** contains $\\alpha$**(1-4) glycosidic** bonds, while both **amylopectin** and **glycogen** contain both $\\alpha$**(1-4)** and $\\alpha$**(1-6)** glycosidic bonds, leading to their branching ($\\checkmark$). **Cellulose** forms many **hydrogen bonds** between **polymer chains**, giving it structural strength, whereas **starch and glycogen do not** form these extensive bonds ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    All three are **polymers**, made up of only **glucose** monomers ($\\checkmark$), joined together with **glycosidic bonds** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    | Feature | Cellulose | Starch | Glycogen |\n    | :-: | :-- | :-- | :-- |\n    | Monomer | $\\beta$**-glucose** | $\\alpha$**-glucose** | $\\alpha$**-glucose**  ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | Polymer Structure | **Unbranched** (linear) chains | Amylose (**unbranched**) and amylopectin (**branched**) ($\\checkmark$) | **Highly branched** ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | Glycosidic Bonds | $\\beta$**-1,4** glycosidic bonds | Amylose: $\\alpha$**-1,4** glycosidic bonds  $\\\\$Amylopectin: $\\alpha$**-1,4** and $\\alpha$**-1,6** glycosidic bonds | $\\alpha$**-1,4** and $\\alpha$**-1,6** glycosidic bonds ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | Orientation of Monomers | Every other monomer is **rotated 180**$\\degree$ | All monomers **oriented** the **same way** | All monomers oriented the **same way** ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | Hydrogen Bonding | Forms **many hydrogen bonds** between parallel chains to form microfibrils | **Do not** form extensive hydrogen bonds between chains | **Do not** form extensive hydrogen bonds between chains ($\\checkmark$) |\n\n2. any **four** of the following correctly labelled and drawn:\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n   <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Extended%20Response%20Questions/images/BI2931b%20ms.png\" alt=\"BI2931b ms\">\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phototropism is the directional growth response of plant shoots towards or away from light [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ auxin is produced in shoot tip **OR** coleoptile in response to light        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ auxin efflux carriers actively transport auxin to shaded side of shoot     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ auxin binds to auxin receptors on target cells         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ auxin stimulates activity of proton pumps in cell membrane **OR** causes movement of hydrogen ions into cell wall         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ auxin activates genes for expansins     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ low pH activates expansins         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ expansin enzymes break hydrogen bond **OR** cross links between cellulose molecules         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ change in cell wall allows more water to enter cell     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell turgor **OR** pressure from water in vacuole causes stretching **OR** elongation of cell wall  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ differential cell elongation on shaded side causes plant to bend towards light        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ feedback mechanism adjusts auxin distribution as shoot bends    [1 mark]\n\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Phototropism is the **directional growth response** of plant shoots **to light**, with the shoot bending towards the light source ($\\checkmark$). The response is regulated by the phytohormone **auxin**, which is produced in the **shoot tip** or **coleoptile** in **response to light** ($\\checkmark$). Auxin **efflux carriers actively transport auxin** to the **shaded side** of the shoot, where the **concentration of auxin** becomes **higher** ($\\checkmark$). **Auxin binds** to auxin **receptors** on the **target cells**, triggering a cascade of cellular events ($\\checkmark$). This binding **stimulates** the activity of **proton pumps** in the **cell membrane**, which causes **hydrogen ions** ($\\ce{H^+}$) to be moved **into** the **cell wall, lowering** the **pH** ($\\checkmark$). The **lowered pH activates expansins**, enzymes that play a key role in cell wall loosening ($\\checkmark$). **Expansin enzymes break hydrogen bonds** or **cross-links** between **cellulose** molecules, making the **cell wall more flexible** ($\\checkmark$). The change in the cell wall allows **more water** to **enter** the **cell** ($\\checkmark$), **increasing** cell **turgor** or pressure from the vacuole, which causes the **stretching** or **elongation** of the **cell wall** ($\\checkmark$). This **differential cell elongation** on the **shaded side** causes the **shoot** to **bend towards** the **light** ($\\checkmark$)."}]},{"name":"Practical-based Questions","questions":[{"ref":"BI0572","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0572\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n1. Outline the steps in calculating the magnification of a micrograph.  [4 marks]\n\n2. Describe the steps taken to focus on a specimen using the 40x objective lens of a light microscope.      [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ measure (using a ruler) the scale bar on the drawing **OR** micrograph   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ note the stated length which the scale bar represents [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ convert the represented and real length or scale bar into the same units [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ M= I/A **OR** any correct example of the calculation     [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    To calculate the magnification of a microphotograph, the first step is to measure **the scale bar using a ruler** ($\\checkmark$). The second step is **to note the length the scale bar represents** ($\\checkmark$). This gives you the actual size of the image. These two measurements should be converted to **the same units** ($\\checkmark$). The last step is to **divide the length measured by the ruler by the length represented by the scale bar** ($\\checkmark$). During this calculation, units cancel out.  Therefore, the **magnification does not have units**.\n\n2. Any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$  use the lowest power **OR** 4X objective lens first        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ place the specimen **OR** slide directly below the objective lens **OR** on the centre of the microscope stage   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$  turn the coarse focus knob to focus on the specimen at 4X  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ adjust the turret **OR** change the objective lens so the medium power **OR** 10X objective lens is used     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ refocus on the specimen using a fine focus knob at 10X and 40X [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$  adjust the turret **OR** change the objective lens, so the high power **OR** 40X objective lens is used [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ while fine focusing always move the lens away from the slide [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nPlace the specimen **in the centre** of the microscope stage  ($\\checkmark$).  Use the **4x objective lens first**  ($\\checkmark$) and use the **coarse focus knob**  ($\\checkmark$) to focus on the specimen.  Adjust the position of the specimen so it is in the middle of the field of view and then **switch to the 10x**  ($\\checkmark$) objective lens and **refocus using the fine focus knob**, ($\\checkmark$) by moving lens **away from the specimen**  ($\\checkmark$) to prevent lens breakage.  Finally, switch to the **40x objective lens** ($\\checkmark$) and use the **fine focus knob** to refocus on the specimen."},{"ref":"BI0764","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0764\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nThe mitotic index is an indirect way of measuring plant growth. A study was conducted on white onions (*Allium cepa*), under controlled conditions in a lab, to investigate the effect of soil pH on the mitotic index of the roots.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0764A.png\" alt=\"BI0764A\">\n\n1. Describe the trends shown in the graph. [2 marks]\n\n2. Outline how the mitotic index is calculated. [1 mark]\n\n3. Discuss whether the data support the hypothesis that onion plants should be grown in soil with a pH of 5.5. [2 marks]\n\n4. Suggest how a pH of 9 affects the process of mitosis. [2 marks]\n\n5.\n 1. Explain why measuring the mitotic index in the root is an indirect way of measuring the overall growth rate of the onion plant. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Suggest another method, apart from measuring mitotic index, that could be used to measure plant growth. [1 mark]\n\n6. State one of the variables that must be controlled to make this experiment valid. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ from pH 3 to pH 5.5, the mitotic index increases **AND** when the pH is higher than 5.5 the mitotic index decreases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ pH 5.5 shows the highest mitotic index [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the steepest fall in mitotic index is between pH 7 and 7.5 [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The graph shows that from **pH 3 to 5.5, the mitotic index increases** and when the **pH is higher than 5.5, the mitotic index decreases** ($\\checkmark$). At pH **5.5**, the onion root has the highest **mitotic index** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ number of cells in mitosis **OR** prophase, metaphase, anaphase and telophase divided by the total number of cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Mitotic index = $\\left(\\dfrac{\\text{Number of cells in mitosis}}{\\text{Total number of cells}}\\right) $ **OR**\n Mitotic index = $\\left(\\dfrac{\\text{Number of cells in mitosis}}{\\text{Total number of cells}}\\right) $X100%\n[1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The mitotic index can be calculated by counting the **number of cells** undergoing **mitosis** and **dividing** that by the **total number of cells** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n *Answer must include at least one positive and one negative statement to achieve full marks*\n\n *Reasons it supports hypothesis:*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 5.5 is the pH with the highest mean mitotic index **OR** results indicate that root growth rate is highest at pH 5.5 [1 mark]\n\n *Reasons it does not support hypothesis:*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the error bars for pH 5.5 overlap with the error bars for pH 5 and 6 [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mitotic index is an indirect method of measuring growth **OR** in this study only the rate of growth of the root was investigated [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in the study, the pH increases in increments of 0.5, however the optimum pH for growth in roots could be either side of 5.5 [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ there is no data showing an increase in dry mass [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The data supports the hypothesis that onion plants should be grown at a **pH of 5.5**, as at pH 5.5 the onion root has the **highest mitotic index** ($\\checkmark$), however, the **error bars for pH overlap** with the error bars for **pH 5 and 6** ($\\checkmark$) suggesting that there is no significant difference between the mitotic index at those pHs.\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ enzymes **OR** cyclin-dependent kinases control the progression of cells through mitosis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at pH 9, enzymes may begin to denature **OR** the active site changes shape irreversibly [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at pH 9, the formation of the mitotic spindle may be adversely affected **OR** any other named process involved in mitosis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ so less cells will be actively undergoing mitosis [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The mitotic index at pH 9 is relatively lower, as progression through the cell cycle is controlled by the activity of **cyclin dependent kinases** ($\\checkmark$). Cyclin dependent kinases are enzymes so may begin to **denature** at pH 9 ($\\checkmark$), so **less cells** will undergo **mitosis** ($\\checkmark$), reducing the mitotic index.\n\n5.\n 1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ does not indicate that the onion plant is growing **OR** the growth rate of the onion plant is not directly proportional to the growth rate of the root **OR** does not indicate the dry mass of the plants [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ growth does not only rely on producing more cells but also an increase in the size of cells [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The mitotic index of the root is an indirect way of measuring the growth rate of the onion plant as the growth rate of the onion plant is **not directly proportional to the growth rate of the root** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ measuring the length of the shoot [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ measuring the length of the root [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ counting the number of leaves [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ measuring the size of the leaves [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ measuring stem diameter [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ measuring dry mass [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ An alternative way of measuring plant growth is by **measuring the length of the shoot** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ light intensity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide concentration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ volume of water [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature of soil OR air [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ concentration of nutrients [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ time that onion plant is grown in soil before mitotic index is measured [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ distance between onion plants in the soil [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ type of soil [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ age of onion plant [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ humidity [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n One variable that must be controlled in this experiment is the **temperature of the soil** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0768","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0768\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe graph shows the results of an experiment on osmosis in potato tissue (*Solanum tuberosum*), cactus tissue (*Carnegiea gigantea*) and sunflower seeds (*Helianthus annuus*). Similar sized pieces of tissue were immersed in different solutions of sucrose for 30 hours.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0768A.png\" alt=\"BI0768A\">\n\n1. Estimate the concentration of sucrose that is isotonic to the interior of the potato tissue. [1 mark]\n\n2. Using the graph deduce, with a reason, the tissue that has the highest solute concentration. [1 mark]\n\n3. Suggest one reason for the difference in internal solute concentration between the sunflower seed and cacti tissue. [1 mark]\n\n4. The epidermis of the potato and cactus and the testa of the sunflowers seeds were removed. Suggest a reason for this. [1 mark]\n\n5. Predict what the appearance of the cacti tissue would be like at 0.5 mol dm-3 sucrose solution. [1 mark]\n\n6.\n 1. State how percentage change in mass is calculated. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Explain why percentage change in mass is calculated rather than change in mass. [2 marks]\n\n7. Explain why temperature has to be controlled in this study. [1 mark]\n\n8. State one way in which this experiment could be made more reliable. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 0.4 mol dm:sup[-3] [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0768ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0768ANSA\">\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The concentration of sucrose in the potato tissue is **0.4 mol dm:sup[-3]** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n ***Answer must include a reason to gain a mark***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sunflower seed **BECAUSE** it gained the most mass **OR** it still gains mass at the most concentrated solution whereas the other tissues are losing mass at higher sucrose concentrations **OR** the sunflower seed is the only tissue to gain mass at all sucrose concentrations [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The tissue with the highest solute concentration is the **sunflower seed**, because it **gained the most mass** via osmosis ($\\checkmark$), as it has a higher solute concentration compared to the solution that it was immersed in.\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sunflower seed is a storage organ for carbohydrates and other molecules **WHEREAS** the cacti tissue is a storage organ for water [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sunflower seed acts as a sink for sucrose **WHEREAS** the cacti tissue contains a higher concentration of water [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sunflower plants may prioritise synthesis and accumulation of various solutes **OR** carbohydrates **OR** proteins, **WHEREAS** cacti prioritise water conservation [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The sunflower seed has a higher concentration of solutes, because it is a **storage organ for carbohydrates, proteins** and other molecules, which can be used for plant growth, whereas the cacti tissue is a **storage organ for water** ($\\checkmark$), which cacti need to grow in dry areas.\n\n4. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the rate of osmosis **OR** movement of water may be different through the testa or epidermis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to eliminate potential barriers to water molecules [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ removing testa and epidermis from all tissue samples minimises confounding variables **OR** makes the results more valid [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The rate of osmosis through the epidermis or testa may **vary from the rate of osmosis** ($\\checkmark$) in underlying tissue. Removing the epidermis or testa from all samples makes the results **more valid** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ shrivelled **OR** smaller in size **OR** wilted **OR** dehydrated [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n At 0.5 mol dm:sup[-3], the cacti tissue would appear **shrivelled** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6.\n 1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ((final mass - initial mass) ÷ initial mass) x 100% [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Percentage change in mass = **((final mass - initial mass) ÷ initial mass) x 100%** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6.\n 2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it provides a standardised measure by expressing the change in proportion to the starting mass [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the pieces of tissue may not have the same mass at the start of the experiment [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allows for comparison between the changes in mass of samples of different initial masses [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ percentage change in mass is less sensitive to small variations in the mass measurement (as it calculated as a ratio of two values) [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Percentage change in mass is used rather than mass in this experiment, as the pieces of tissue may **not** be the **same mass at the beginning** ($\\checkmark$) of the experiment, so it allows for accurate **comparison** ($\\checkmark$) in the changes in mass.\n\n7. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the higher the temperature, the faster the rate of osmosis **OR** the lower the temperature, the slower the rate of osmosis **OR** the higher the temperature, the higher the kinetic energy of the water molecules, so they move in or out of the cells at a faster rate [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature affects the structure of the plasma membrane, which can alter the permeability of the membrane, thus altering the rate of osmosis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature affects the rate of metabolic reactions, which can alter the concentration of solutes inside the cells, changing the water potential [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allows for accurate comparison of results [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Temperature has to be controlled in this study because the **higher** the **temperature**, the **faster** the rate of **osmosis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n8.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ do repeats **OR** record at least three sets of results for each tissue sample [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ To make the results more reliable **repeats** ($\\checkmark$) need to be carried out to make sure that there are at least three results for each type of tissue at each sucrose concentration."},{"ref":"BI0769","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0769\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nAn investigation was carried out into the effects of household detergents on aquatic habitats. Scientists tested the effect of different household detergents on the surface tension of water in order to better understand the impact they may have on organisms living on the surface.\n\nA paper clip was folded slightly on one side and rested on the surface of the water, maintained at a constant temperature and contained within a water tank. Drops of detergent solution were slowly added to the water tank until the paperclip sank to the bottom. The volume of detergent solution taken to sink the paperclip was recorded.\n\nDetergent solutions were made by adding 5 ml of detergent to 15 ml of water.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0769A.png\" alt=\"BI0769A\">\n\nThe results are shown in the table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0769B.png\" alt=\"BI0769B\">\n\n1. State the relationship between the volume of detergent solution added and the surface tension of water. [1 mark]\n\n2.\n 1. For the **Easy Clean** detergent, calculate the mean volume of detergent needed to sink the paper clip. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Estimate the volume of **Easy Clean** detergent solution it would take to sink 5 paper clips. [1 mark]\n\n3.\n 1. State **one** variable, other than those given, which would need to be controlled in this experiment in order to achieve a valid result. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Suggest **one** method that could be used to keep the water at a constant temperature. [1 mark]\n\n 3. Explain the importance of maintaining water at a constant temperature. [2 marks]\n\n 4. Identify **one** potential source of error in the methodology. [1 mark]\n\n4. Suggest why different numbers of drops of detergent solution were needed to sink the paperclip between each type of detergent. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ surface tension decreases as detergent solution volume increases [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ As the volume of detergent solution **increases** the **surface tension** of the water **decreases** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1. (units must be included in order to be awarded the mark)\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 9.25ml [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0769ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0769ANSA\">\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n Mean = total/number of trials\n\n = (8 ml+10 ml+10 ml+9 ml) / 4\n\n = 37 ml / 4\n\n = **9.25ml** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2.\n 2. (units must be included in order to be awarded the mark)\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 9.25ml [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n If it takes 9.25 ml of detergent solution to reduce the surface tension enough to sink one paper clip, then the **same volume** would sink 5 paper clips ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ volume of the water (in the water tank) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ size **OR** mass **OR** brand **OR** material of paper clip **OR** way it is folded [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ distance of burette from water surface [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **size of the paper clip** ($\\checkmark$) in the experiment would need to be controlled.\n\n3.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ water bath [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ heating/cooling blocks [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n A **water bath** ($\\checkmark$) could be used to maintain a constant temperature.\n\n3.\n 3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the cohesive properties of water create surface tension **OR** surface tension is due to hydrogen bonding between water molecules [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cohesive forces **OR** hydrogen bonding decrease with increasing temperature **BECAUSE** there is more kinetic energy so molecules move more and interact less **OR** less attractive forces form between them, **OR** cohesive forces **OR** hydrogen bonding increase with decreasing temperature **BECAUSE** there is less kinetic energy so molecules move less and interact more **OR** more attractive forces form between them [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n It’s important to maintain a constant temperature in this experiment because surface tension is due to **cohesion** ($\\checkmark$) between water molecules which is a result of hydrogen bonding. High temperatures can break hydrogen bonds and **reduce the cohesive properties** ($\\checkmark$) of water which in turn reduces surface tension.\n\n3.\n 4.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ surface tension broken by the droplets of detergent solution **OR** ripples created by droplets of detergent [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ paper clips folded incorrectly **OR** differently (if different paper clips used for each trial) [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ paper clip has residual detergent between trials (if same paper clip is used for each trial) [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ paper clips are different materials **OR** brands **OR** texture (if different paper clips are used for each trial) [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ A source of error could be that the surface tension of the water is broken by the **droplets of detergent** ($\\checkmark$) which create ripples. This could result in the paperclip sinking.\n\n4.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ detergents are made using different chemicals **OR** detergents have different starting concentrations **OR** different concentrations of chemical ingredients [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ some chemicals **OR** detergents affect the cohesion between water molecules more **AND** therefore reduce surface tension more [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ There may be a difference between results because each detergent is a different brand which may be made using **different chemicals** ($\\checkmark$). Some of the chemicals found in some detergents may have a **greater effect on cohesion** between water molecules which means they could reduce the **surface tension** more ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0770","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0770\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nEating a healthy, balanced diet is important in order to maintain overall health and a strong immune system. A healthy diet means eating a wide variety of food whilst limiting the consumption of foods which are considered to be less healthy, such as those high in saturated fats.\n\nIn an investigation into the effects of diet on low-density lipoprotein levels (LDL) in the blood, scientists provided two groups of mice with varied diets. Each group was given 5g of food per day, containing a mixture of a standard food mix and either avocado or cheese. The ratios of the food were changed each day and blood LDL levels were recorded. The diets and results are shown in the tables and graph.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0770A.png\" alt=\"BI0770A\">\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0770B.png\" alt=\"BI0770B\">\n\n1.\n 1. Describe the impact on blood LDL levels when feeding mice diets supplemented with avocado or cheese. [2 marks]\n\n 2. Explain the differences between results from both groups. [2 marks]\n\n 3. Suggest a reason for the trend shown in LDL levels in group one. [1 mark]\n\n2. State which group is most at risk of heart disease and stroke. Give a reason for your answer. [2 marks]\n\n3.\n 1. Suggest one advantage of using mice in this experiment instead of humans. [1 mark]\n\n 2. State one variable, not mentioned in the question, which would need to be controlled in this experiment in order to achieve valid results. [1 mark]\n\n4. For mice fed with cheese, calculate how many times greater the LDL levels were on day 5 compared to day 2. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n 1. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mice fed with a mass of cheese per day have higher LDL levels than mice fed with a mass of avocado **OR** mice fed with a mass of avocado per day have lower LDL levels than mice fed with a mass of cheese [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as the proportion of cheese **OR** mass of cheese in the diet increases, so does blood LDL levels **OR** increases from 1000 - 1900 mg dm:sup[-3] [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as the proportion **OR** mass of avocado increases in the diet up to 3g day:sup[-1], o blood LDL levels increase **OR** blood LDL rise from about 500 to 1100 mg dm:sup[-3] **BUT** LDL decreases **OR** change from about 1100 - 650 mg dm:sup[-3], from day 3 - 5 [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n From the results, we can see that mice fed with cheese have **higher blood LDL levels** ($\\checkmark$) compared to mice fed with avocado. Furthermore, as the proportion of cheese in the diet **increases, so do blood LDL levels** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2.\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ saturated fats raise levels of LDL in the blood [1 mark]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ cheese is high in saturated fats **BUT** avocado is low in saturated fats [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n $\\hspace{1em}$ The mice fed with cheese have higher blood LDL levels because cheese contains higher levels of **saturated fats** ($\\checkmark$) than avocado and diets with high levels of saturated fats can **increase** blood LDL levels ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 3.\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ fats found in avocado reduce blood LDL levels **OR** avocado is plant based which contains mostly unsaturated fats which can reduce blood LDL levels **OR** avocado is lower in saturated fats than the standard food mix **OR** contains more unsaturated fats than the standard food mix which can help reduce LDL levels [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n $\\hspace{1em}$ Fats found in avocado are mostly **unsaturated**, because it is **plant based**, and unsaturated fats help to **reduce LDL levels** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. group must be stated for **one** mark:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ group 2 [1 mark]\n\n Then any **one**:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ high LDL levels are associated with heart disease and stroke **OR** high LDL levels are the result of a diet high in saturated fats which are associated with heart disease and strokes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ a diet high in saturated fats can lead to atherosclerosis which can result in heart disease **OR** strokes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ LDL can contribute to the formation of blood clots which can block blood flow [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Group 2** ($\\checkmark$) because they have **higher blood LDL levels** which make them more at risk of heart disease and strokes ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 1. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ less ethical considerations [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ easier to control as kept in a lab [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ require less space [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ less resources required OR more economical [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ shorter life span OR reproduce quickly so effects can be observed over several generations in a relatively short period [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Using mice in experiments has significantly **less ethical considerations** than using human subjects ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 2.\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ same type of standard food mix **OR** same environmental conditions **OR** same activity levels **OR** same time LDL levels are measured **OR** same age **OR** mass **OR** species of mouse **OR** same type of cheese **OR** variety of avocado [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n To achieve valid results, the **type of standard food mix** that mice are fed should be kept the same ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 1.58 (allow answers in the range of 1.57 - 1.59) [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0770ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0770ANSA\">\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Day 5 = 1900\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Day 2 = 1200\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ = 1900/1200\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ = **1.58** ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"BI0771","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0771\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe enzyme pepsin hydrolyses proteins to release peptides in the stomach. A study was carried out to determine the effect of increasing substrate concentration on enzyme activity at a constant temperature of 30°C.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0771A.png\" alt=\"BI0771A\">\n\n1. Describe the trends shown by the graph. [2 marks]\n\n2.\n 1. Outline why it is important that temperature is kept constant in this experiment. [2 marks]\n\n 2. State one factor other than temperature that would need to be controlled in this experiment. [1 mark]\n\n 3. Suggest a method that can be used to maintain a constant temperature in this experiment. [1 mark]\n\n3. Calculate the rate of reaction during the first 4 minutes when the enzyme pepsin is exposed to 5 mM of protein. Make sure you show your working. [2 marks]\n\n4. Explain the observed results. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as time increases the concentration of peptides made also increases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the higher the concentration of protein, the faster the reaction rate **OR** the greater the number of peptides formed per unit time **OR** the lower the concentration of protein, the slower the reaction rate **OR** the lower the number of peptides formed per unit time [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the reaction rate is fastest at the beginning **OR** reaction rate slows down towards the end [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ for every 10 mM increase in protein concentration there is almost an identical increase in the concentration of peptides produced at 20 minutes [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The graph shows that the **higher the concentration of protein** (substrate), **the faster the reaction rate** ($\\checkmark$), however the **reaction rate is fastest at the beginning** and **slows down towards the end** ($\\checkmark$), as indicated by the steepness of the graph. For all the concentrations of substrate **as time progresses, the concentration of peptides** (products) **increases** ($\\checkmark$). For every **10 mM increase in the concentration of protein**, there is an almost identical **increase in the concentration of peptides** produced at 20 minutes ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as temperature increases (up to optimum), reaction rate increases **OR** as temperature decreases, reaction rate decreases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ if the temperature is too high **OR** above the optimum temperature, enzyme starts to denature [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to see the effects of change in substrate concentration **OR** to make results valid **OR** comparable, there should only be one independent variable in an experiment [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n As **temperature increases, reaction rate also increases** ($\\checkmark$) up until the optimum temperature. Therefore there should only be **one independent variable** ($\\checkmark$) in the experiment (substrate concentration), to allow for a **fair comparison** between the different substrate concentrations. If the **temperature is too high, this can cause the enzyme to denature** ($\\checkmark$), stopping the reaction from continuing.\n\n2.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ pH [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ enzyme **OR** pepsin concentration **OR** volume of enzyme [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ volume of substrate solution [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ same protein [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **pH** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2.\n 3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ waterbath [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ metal hot blocks [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Placing the samples in a **waterbath** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ (6 mM - 0 mM) ÷ (4 min - 0 min) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 1.5 mM min:sup[-1] [1 mark]\n\n *1 mark should be awarded for correct working, if incorrect numbers are extracted from the graph*\n\n *Units must be included*\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/bi0771ans%20b.png\" alt=\"bi0771ans b\">\n\n (**6** mM - **0** mM) ÷ (**4** min - **0** min) ($\\checkmark$) = **1.5 mM min:sup[-1]** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at a higher concentration of protein (25mM), initially peptides are made at a faster rate as there is a greater chance of successful collisions between substrate and enzyme **OR** at a lower concentration of protein (5mM), initially peptides are made at a comparably slower rate as there is a lower chance of successful collisions between enzyme and substrate [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ for all concentrations of protein, there is a faster rate of reaction within the first 6 minutes compared to the latter half of the reaction, as there is a higher concentration of substrate available to bind to the enzyme [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ for all concentrations of protein, the reaction rate slows down as the substrate reduces in concentration and the concentration of product increases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ The highest concentration of protein (25mM) produces the highest concentration of peptides because there is higher initial concentration of substrate so more product can be made **OR** the lowest concentration of protein (5mM) produces the lowest concentration of peptides because there is a lower initial concentration of substrate so less product can be made [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n With the **highest concentration** of protein (25mM), peptides are initially made at a **faster rate** compared to the lower concentrations, as there is a great chance of **successful collisions** ($\\checkmark$) between substrate and enzyme. For all concentrations of protein, there is a **faster rate of reaction within the first 6 minutes** compared to the latter half of the reaction, as there is **more substrate available to bind to the enzyme** ($\\checkmark$). However, as all of the reactions progress, the reaction rate begins to **slow down** and eventually become stops, as the concentration of **substrate reduces** and the concentration of **product increases** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0772","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0772\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nIn an investigation into the impacts of climate change on the rate of photosynthesis, pondweed was placed in a number of sealed conical flasks, each containing a solution of water and carbonic acid.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0772A.png\" alt=\"BI0772A\">\n[ 2022. Created with Chemix (https://chemix.org)]\n\nEach conical flask was placed in a water bath maintained at a constant temperature. At regular time intervals, samples of the solution were taken from each conical flask and measured using a pH probe.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0772B.png\" alt=\"BI0772B\">\n[ 2022. Created with Chemix (https://chemix.org)]\n\nThe results are shown in the table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0772C.png\" alt=\"BI0772C\">\n\n1.\n 1. Identify the dependent and independent variables in this investigation. [2 marks]\n\n 2. State one variable that must be kept constant in this investigation to ensure the results are valid. [1 mark]\n\n2. Describe the relationship between the temperature, time and pH of the solution. [2 marks]\n\n3. Predict the pH of the dilute carbonic acid solution at 40 :sup[o]C after 60 minutes. [1 mark]\n\n4.\n 1. Calculate the rate of change in pH between 30 - 40 minutes and 40 - 50 minutes for the trial kept at 40 :sup[o]C. [2 marks]\n\n 2. Suggest a reason for the difference in the rate of change you calculated in (i). [1 mark]\n\n5. Suggest one improvement to the design of this experiment and state whether it would increase the **accuracy** or **reliability** of the results. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n 1.\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ independent variable is temperature [1 mark]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ dependent variable is pH [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n $\\hspace{1em}$ The independent variable is **temperature** ($\\checkmark$) and the dependent variable is **pH** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2.\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ concentration of carbonic acid **OR** volume of solution **OR** light intensity **OR** mass of pondweed **OR** size of pondweed **OR** species of pondweed **OR** other suitable control [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n $\\hspace{1em}$ The **concentration of carbonic acid** ($\\checkmark$) in the solution should remain constant.\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ for 20 :sup[o]C - 40 :sup[o]C as the time increases up to 50 minutes, the pH of the solution increases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ for 50 :sup[o]C as the time increases, the pH remains fairly constant **OR** increases less than 20 :sup[o]C - 40 :sup[o]C **OR** above 40 :sup[o]C there is very little, or no change in pH up to 50 minutes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ for 60 :sup[o]C as time increases, the pH remains constant [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n In the experiment, as time increases, the pH of the solution **increases** for trials kept at temperatures between **20 - 40 :sup[o]C** ($\\checkmark$), but at **60 :sup[o]C** the pH remains **unchanged** ($\\checkmark$) as time increases.\n\n3.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ accept answers in the range of 6.82 - 6.87 [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0772ANS%20A.png\" alt=\"BI0772ANS A\">\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 30 min 6.47\n\n$\\hspace{6em}$ + 0.30\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 40 min 6.77\n\n$\\hspace{6em}$ + 0.05\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 50 min 6.82\n\n$\\hspace{6em}$ + 0.05 or less\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 60 min **6.83** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4.\n 1.\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 0.03 pH min:sup[-1] (30 - 40 :sup[o]C) [1 mark]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 0.005 pH min:sup[-1] (40 - 50 :sup[o]C) [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0772ANSC.png\" alt=\"BI0772ANSC\">\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ rate of change = change (pH) ÷ time (min)\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ 30 = pH 6.47\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ 40 = pH 6.77\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ change in pH = (6.77 - 6.47) = pH 0.3\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ pH 0.3 ÷ 10 min = **0.03 pH min:sup[-1]** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ 40 = pH 6.77\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ 50 = pH 6.82\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ Change in pH = (6.82 - 6.77) = pH 0.05\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ pH 0.05 ÷ 10 min = **0.005 pH min:sup[-1]** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4.\n 2.\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ reduced rate of photosynthesis **OR** less photosynthesis [1 mark]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ less carbon dioxide available **OR** lower concentration of carbon dioxide [1 mark]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ the increase in pH may have started to denature plant enzymes [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n $\\hspace{1em}$ The rate of change is slowing down because the **source of carbon dioxide** in the solution is becoming **exhausted** ($\\checkmark$) so less is being absorbed for photosynthesis and thus the pH of the solution is increasing more slowly.\n\n5. Any **one** of the following (students should relate answers to accuracy or reliability):\n\n **Increase accuracy:**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ smaller time intervals between measurements **OR** more frequent measurements [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ use smaller intervals between test temperatures [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ pH probe inside the conical flask **OR** pH measurements taken inside the conical flask **OR** do not remove stopper to test pH [1 mark]\n\n **Increase reliability:**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ repeat the experiment two more times **OR** at least 3 times [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ include a control group without pondweed in flask [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n To improve the accuracy of the experiment, scientists could take **measurements at shorter time intervals** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0774","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0774\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe white clover (*Trifolium repens*) is native to the fields around Cornell University. The image shows a 10m x 15m area of the park, where quadrats (1m:sup[2]) were used to measure the number of white clover in the fields. The results from 10 of the quadrats are also shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0774A.png\" alt=\"BI0774A\">\n[Source: adapted from: NY State IPM Program at Cornell University, N. Y. S. I. P. M. P. at C. U. (2014, June 19). *White Clover in soccer field*. Flickr. Retrieved March 8, 2023, from https://www.flickr.com/photos/99758165@N06/14273941729 Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n1.\n    1. Describe how quadrats could be used to estimate the mean number of white clover in the field. [3 marks]\n\n    2. alculate the mean number of white clover in the field, showing your working. [1 mark]\n\n    3. Explain why this type of sampling can only be used for sessile or immobile organisms. [1 mark]\n\n    4. Suggest two limitations of quadrat sampling. [2 marks]\n\n2. State the statistical test that could be conducted to check if white clover are associated with another organism in the field. [1 mark]\n\n3. There are nitrogen fixing bacteria in the root nodules of the white clover. State two ways that the white clover uses the nitrogen provided by the bacteria. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ divide the field into a grid using a measuring tape **OR** other suitable apparatus [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use a random number generator **OR** other suitable apparatus to generate ten coordinates     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ place quadrats at the ten coordinates **AND** count the number of white clovers in each quadrat    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ calculate the mean number of white clover per quadrat    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ multiply the mean number of white clover by 150        [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    To estimate the number of white clover in the field, the field should be **divided into a grid** using a measuring tape ($\\checkmark$) and each grid should be assigned a coordinate. Next, using a **random number generator** ($\\checkmark$), you should generate **10 coordinates** and then place a quadrat at one coordinate and count the number of white clovers at that coordinate. Then place the quadrat at another coordinate and **count the number of white clover** ($\\checkmark$) there, this should be repeated for all of the coordinates. Finally,  **the mean number** ($\\checkmark$) of white clover per quadrat should be calculated and the mean should be **multiplied by 150** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    2. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 4290     [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ Mean number of white clover per 1 m:sup[2] quadrat=  **(13 + 44 + 33 + 42 + 10 + 18 + 34 + 29 + 7 + 56)  ÷ 10 = 28.6**\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ Area of field = 10 m x 15 m  = **150** m:sup[2]\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ Mean number of white clovers per field = 28.6 x 150 = **4290** ($\\checkmark$) white clover\n\n1.\n    3. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ immobile organisms do not move out of the sample area making counting accurate **OR**  highly mobile organisms can move out of the sample area making counting inaccurate     [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ Quadrats are mostly used for counting sessile or immobile organisms, as they **do not move**  out of the sample area, so counting is **accurate** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    4. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ the number of organisms may be underrepresented **OR** overrepresented **OR** size of quadrat can greatly influence the results    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ researcher can make mistakes when counting    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ if a white clover is on the edge of a quadrat, then it might be difficult to know whether to include it    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ there is only a small sample size    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ Two limitations of quadrat sampling are that the number of organisms may be **underrepresented** ($\\checkmark$) or the researcher can make **mistakes when counting** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chi squared [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    A **chi squared** ($\\checkmark$) test can be used to identify if white clovers are associated with another organism in the field.\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to make amino acids    **OR** protein    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to make nitrogenous bases **OR** nucleic acids **OR** DNA **OR** RNA **OR** ATP **OR** NADP **OR** NAD **OR** FAD    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to make chlorophyll    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    White clover use nitrogen to make **amino acids** ($\\checkmark$) for protein synthesis and to make nitrogenous **bases in DNA** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0775","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0775\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n1\\.\nA student had a small pond in the backyard and wanted to add some fish. The fish would be predators of the pond and feed on the zooplankton (prey) growing in the pond. Zooplankton are small floating or weakly swimming organisms that are dependent on the phytoplankton or algae for their supply of nutrition.\n\nShe wanted to first determine whether Fish A or Fish B would be more suitable, or if both fish could be added to the pond. As a trial, she set up four mesocosms using water from the pond. She monitored the mesocosms and then added the fish to three of the mesocosms as shown. She took samples of water from the different mesocosms and tested them for chlorophyll content at a research lab nearby.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0775.png\" alt=\"BI0775\">\n\n1. Outline why the mesocosms used would be considered a ‘closed system’. [1 mark]\n\n2. Describe why adding mud or soil to the mesocosms would be advantageous. [1 mark]\n\n3. Outline the reason for including ‘Mesocosm A’ with the experiment. [1 mark]\n\n4. Explain the reason for monitoring chlorophyll content to analyse the effects of the fish as a predator. [1 mark]\n\n5. State another variable that could be monitored to see the effect of adding fish to the mesocosms. [1 mark]\n\nThe following table shows the results obtained for chlorophyll (μg dm:sup[-3]) with the different additions of fish.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0775B.png\" alt=\"BI0775B\">\n[Source: Adapted from Froneman, P. W. (2022, July 22). *Predator diversity does not contribute to increased prey risk: Evidence from a mesocosm study*. MDPI. Retrieved March 23, 2023, from https://www.mdpi.com/1424-2818/14/8/584, Copyright under CC-BY 4.0]\n\n6. From the results obtained, suggest why the student decided to add both fish A and fish B to the pond. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ energy can enter and leave the mesocosm system,(even if mesocosms are covered) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ matter **OR** organisms, cannot enter or leave the mesocosm system, mesocosms are covered [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The mesocosms are a ‘closed system’ because they are covered, and though energy can enter and leave the system, the **matter is not added or removed from the system** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mud will contain saprotrophs **OR** detritus [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mud will aid decomposition and cycling of nutrients in the mesocosm [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mud provides nutrients for algae [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mud increases habitat complexity so more surfaces for microorganisms to attach [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ more realistic ecosystem- more closely mimics the pond ecosystem [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n If mud is added to the mesocosms, this would also add decomposed material and aid in the **decomposition and cycling of nutrients** ($\\checkmark$) by bacteria and other organisms.\n\n3. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ control for the experiment [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ obtain results without fish to compare results when fish are added [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Mesocosm A serves as a **control for the experiment** ($\\checkmark$) and shows the values for chlorophyll when no fish are added.\n\n4. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chlorophyll concentrations will indicate how much (green) algae is present in the system [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the zooplankton eat the algae, so it is an indirect measure of zooplankton levels [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the more the algae, the lesser the levels of zooplankton [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ It is possible **OR** easier to obtain algae levels by measuring chlorophyll concentrations [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n It is possible to **estimate algae levels** ($\\checkmark$) in the system by measuring chlorophyll concentrations. As the fish eat the zooplankton, algal levels might increase, so it is an indirect measure of the zooplankton present.\n\n5. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the zooplankton levels could be monitored [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the zooplankton could be counted directly (using microscopy and counting of the organisms) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ diversity of zooplankton could be measured [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ water quality **OR** dissolved oxygen levels **OR** pH **OR** nutrient levels **OR** turbidity **OR** nitrate levels **OR** phosphate levels [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **zooplankton populations** ($\\checkmark$) could be monitored directly by counting them.\n\n6. Any **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the prey **OR** zooplankton populations were maintained in the presence of the predator **OR** fish [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ both fish A and fish B did not decrease the zooplankton population significantly when both were added together [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the fish A and fish B added together did not interfere with the stability of the system [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ adding fish A and B together did not significantly affect primary productivity **OR** chlorophyll levels [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **zooplankton populations were maintained** ($\\checkmark$) when fish A and fish B were added together. The addition of **both fish did not decrease** ($\\checkmark$) the zooplankton population. Both fish could survive together in the mesocosms."},{"ref":"BI0776","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0776\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA student wanted to study how food chains in leaf litter can be affected by the mass of initial leaf litter added. The student placed an increasing mass of leaf litter in three different tree holes and waited a few weeks. To collect data, the student collected all the leaf litter and placed it in an apparatus, as shown below. The heat from the light bulb dried out the litter from above and the heat and light drove the invertebrates through the wire mesh to the beaker below. The invertebrates were collected, the number of species counted, the species were identified and then they were put into their respective trophic groups.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0776A.png\" alt=\"BI0776A\">\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0776B.png\" alt=\"BI0776B\">\n\n1.\n 1. State the independent variable in the experiment. [1 mark]\n\n 2. State the dependent variable in the experiment. [1 mark]\n\n2. Suggest how the dry biomass of the leaf litter can be obtained. [2 marks]\n\n3. Explain why using the dry biomass of leaf litter is advantageous to using the fresh biomass. [1 mark]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0776C.png\" alt=\"BI0776C\">\n\n4. From the two graphs provided, describe the results of the experiment. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1. the following:\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ mass of leaf litter **OR** g of leaf litter [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n $\\hspace{1em}$ The independent variable is **‘mass of leaf litter’** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2. the following:\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ number of species [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n $\\hspace{1em}$ The dependent variable is **‘number of species’** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ leave the leaf litter and the filter (after the experiment and invertebrates removed) under lamp to dry **OR** dry in oven [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ weigh the leaf litter and the filter using a balance after it is dry [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the mass of the leaf litter is obtained by subtracting the mass of the filter [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The leaf litter, along with the filter, is **left to dry out under the lamp** ($\\checkmark$). The leaf litter and filter are weighed and then the mass of the **leaf litter is obtained by subtracting the mass of the filter** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fresh leaf litter may have different proportions of moisture [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ dry biomass gives a better measure of the mass of organic matter in the leaf litter available to the invertebrates [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ different proportions of water in fresh leaf litter can skew the results obtained **OR** make it difficult to compare results between samples [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n To compare the results obtained, it is better to use the biomass of dry leaf litter. Based on the position of the tree hole, such as the sunny or shady side, **the different samples of leaf litter could have different proportions of water** ($\\checkmark$) and will affect the results obtained.\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ with increasing mass of leaf litter, the length of the food chain increases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ with increasing mass of leaf litter, the number of species in the food chain increases [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n With increasing mass of leaf litter put in the tree holes, there is an increase in both **the length of the food chain** ($\\checkmark$) and **number of species present in the food chain** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0777","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0777\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nThe following image shows the productivity of a small terrestrial ecosystem with the numbers showing the flow of energy (kJ m:sup[-2] yr:sup[-1]).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0777CORA.png\" alt=\"BI0777CORA\">\n\nNet production = Gross production – energy lost to respiration\n\n1. Outline how light energy is sequentially transformed into other forms of energy in the terrestrial ecosystem shown in the image. [2 marks]\n\n2. Calculate the percentage of energy captured by the producers that is available to the secondary consumers in the ecosystem shown above. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ light energy $\\ce{ -> }$ chemical energy [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chemical energy $\\ce{ -> }$ heat energy [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ light energy from the sun is converted to chemical energy in organic molecules during photosynthesis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in respiration chemical energy in organic molecules is converted to chemical energy in ATP [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ during respiration some chemical energy is converted to heat energy [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n During photosynthesis **light energy** from the Sun **is converted into chemical energy** ($\\checkmark$) and then during **respiration** some of the **chemical energy is converted into heat energy** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. the following (units are required for marks to be awarded):\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 6.8 % [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0777MS.png\" alt=\"BI0777MS\">\n\n % (percentage) energy initially captured by producers available to secondary consumers :\n\n (1500/22000) x 100% = **6.82 %** ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"BI0778","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0778\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nMesocosms were used to study the response of freshwater snails and algae to warming in two different systems, one with fish present and one without fish. Each mesocosm had the following added to it: two different species of snails *Bellamya sp.* and *Radix sp.*, and the same (one) species of phytoplankton. The snails feed on phytoplankton and are in turn preyed upon by the fish.\n\nThe following diagrams show the mesocosms used for the study. **C** is the control without fish at environmental temperature and **F** is the control with fish at environmental temperature. **W** is the mesocosm at increased (+4.5 °C) temperature without fish, and **WF** is the mesocosm at increased temperature with fish. The temperature increase of +4.5 °C was chosen to simulate the possible environmental warming that could happen in this region by the end of the century.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0778ai.png\" alt=\"BI0778ai\">\n\n1. State two other variables, other than those given, that need to be controlled in the mesocosms. [2 marks]\n\nThe following graphs show the fluctuations in the density of the two snails against the period of time (x axis) the study took place, along with the treatments.\n\n(**C** - control, **W** - warming , **F** - with fish , **WF** - warming with fish)\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI077B.png\" alt=\"BI077B\">\n[Source: adapted from Cheng, H., Feng, M., Zhang, P., Zhang, H., Wang, H., Xu, J., & Zhang, M. (2022, December 30). *Effects of warming on aquatic snails and periphyton in freshwater ecosystems with and without predation by common carp*. MDPI. Retrieved January 10, 2023, from https://www.mdpi.com/2073-4441/15/1/153, Copyright under CC-BY 4.0]\n\n2. Draw a line in graph A to show the **peak** density value for both the control (C) and warming (W) for the snail *Bellamya sp.*, as shown in graph B for the snail *Radix sp.* [1 mark]\n\n3. Compare and contrast the effects of **warming** on each species of snails in the mesocosms (W and WF). [2 marks]\n\n4. State which is the preferred snail for consumption by the fish. [1 mark]\n\n5. Predict the effect of warming in the presence of fish on the biodiversity (of snails) in open ecosystems. [1 mark]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0778C.png\" alt=\"BI0778C\">\n[Source: adapted from Cheng, H., Feng, M., Zhang, P., Zhang, H., Wang, H., Xu, J., & Zhang, M. (2022, December 30). *Effects of warming on aquatic snails and periphyton in freshwater ecosystems with and without predation by common carp*. MDPI. Retrieved January 10, 2023, from https://www.mdpi.com/2073-4441/15/1/153, Copyright under CC-BY 4.0 ]\n\n6. A study was carried out on variation in periphyton density in the four different mesocosms, the results of which are shown in the graph above. Periphyton is a mixture of autotrophic microorganisms, heterotrophic microorganisms and detritus. Suggest a reason for the increase in periphyton density shown in the warmer mesocosm containing fish. [1 mark]\n\n7. Mesocosms have been used to simulate conditions for climate change and other studies. Outline an advantage and a disadvantage of using mesocosms to carry out these investigations. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ number/ biomass of snails added initially [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ biomass of phytoplankton added initially [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mass of water /dimensions of tanks used [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ light intensity/temperature [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ time span of experiment monitored [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ pH of **OR** dissolved oxygen **OR** nutrient levels in water [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Two variables that need to be controlled are the number **of the two different snails added initially** ($\\checkmark$) and the **time span** ($\\checkmark$) of the experiment.\n\n2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ line drawn vertically through 200 days (allow range of 190-200 days) [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0778ANS%20B.png\" alt=\"BI0778ANS B\">\n\n3. Any **two** of the following (at least **one** similarity and **one** difference):\n\n **similarities**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** snails decrease in density with warming of the environment, W, as compared to the control, C [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ In **BOTH** *Bellamya* and *Radix* there is virtually no difference between warmed mesocosms, W, and controls, C, by the end of the study time [1 mark]\n\n **differences**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ for *Bellamya*, the peak day/time for C and W is equal **WHEREAS** for *Radix* the peak day/time for C and W is different **OR** For *Bellamya*, the peak day/time for C and W is at the same time **WHEREAS** for *Radix* the peak day/time for C is earlier than W [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in W density of *Radix* was approximately half that of C around day 150 **WHEREAS**, after day 100, although density of *Bellamya* was always lower in W than in C it was never as low as half of C [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in the first 200 days, *Bellamya* density is greater in F than WF, **WHEREAS** in the first 200 days *Radix* sp. density is similar for F and WF [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in *Bellamya* there are snails present at end of experiment for W and C, **WHEREAS** in *Radix*, there are likely no snails present for W and C [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in *Bellamya* the density of snails remains relatively constant for W and C, **WHEREAS** in *Radix*, the density of snails increases and drops for W and C [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Both snails decrease in density** ($\\checkmark$) with warming in the mesocosms without fish, W.\n\n In ***Radix*** snails, the population density is **0 in warmed** mesocosms, W, and **controls**, C, by day **240** whereas in ***Bellamya*** **snails**, the population density is **similar** in **W and C** by day **300** and there are still snails present ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ *Radix* snail [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The preferred snail for consumption by the fish is the ***Radix*** **snail** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ since the *Radix* snail is preferred by the fish, overtime *Radix* snail will decrease and *Bellamya* will increase in numbers [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ *Bellamya* snail will out compete *Radix* snail, and become the dominant snail the ecosystem [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ biodiversity of snails will decrease over time [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n ***Bellamya*** **snails will out compete** ***Radix*** **snails** ($\\checkmark$) in the presence of fish, over time, in the ecosystems.\n\n6. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in the presence of fish, the snails are eaten **OR** are fewer in number, and therefore there is an increase of periphyton [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n In the presence of fish, **snails are eaten** ($\\checkmark$) and therefore there is an increase in periphyton as less is eaten by snails, (showing a top down control of the ecosystem).\n\n7. Any **one** of the following:\n\n **advantages**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ easier to simulate (ecological) conditions for research in a small (and defined) environment **OR** in a laboratory setting [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ different conditions can be set for varying periods as needed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ particularly useful to make predictions for effects of climate change [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can be made sustainable for long periods of time with only inputs of energy, nutrients being recycled **OR** easy to collect data for a continuous period of time [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can be more realistic representation of an ecosystem than experiments in lab [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can easily be replicated **OR** reproducible [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can be more ethical than manipulating the natural ecosystem of an organism [1 mark]\n\n and any **one** of the following :\n\n **disadvantages**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ may not reproduce conditions as in the wild for ecological studies **OR** usually smaller than natural ecosystems which limits findings [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ difficult to work with large animals since it is not considered ethical [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ there are limitations, as setting up mesocosms may be complex [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ usually limited duration so may not be useful for investigating long-term effects [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can be expensive to set up and maintain [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n An advantage of using a mesocosm is that it is easier **to simulate conditions** ($\\checkmark$) for research. A disadvantage is that mesocosms **may not reproduce all the conditions** ($\\checkmark$) that occur in natural open ecosystems."},{"ref":"BI0780","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0780\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nIn an investigation into the effects of artery occlusion on blood pressure, students set up a simple experiment. A rigid, hard, plastic tube, representing an artery, was fixed to an electric water pump, and the end was sealed with a rubber stopper. A single hole was drilled into the plastic tube and the electric pump was turned on. Students measured the distance the water stream travelled.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0780A.png\" alt=\"BI0780A\">\n\nIn subsequent trials, students added increasing numbers of equally sized and shaped clay-like masses to the inside of the plastic tube and the distance the water stream travelled was measured again.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0780B.png\" alt=\"BI0780B\">\n\nResults are shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0780C.png\" alt=\"BI0780C\">\n\n1.\n 1. Identify the independent variable in this experiment. [1 mark]\n\n 2. State two control variables, not mentioned in the question, that must be kept constant in order to achieve valid results. [1 mark]\n\n2.\n 1. Outline the relationship between the number of clay-like masses and the distance travelled by the water stream. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Explain how the results of this experiment can be used to understand artery occlusion. [2 marks]\n\n3. Based on the results, predict the distance travelled by the water stream if there were no clay-like masses present. [1 mark]\n\n4.\n 1. Using your knowledge of coronary heart disease, suggest a reason for the anomalous result seen when 5 clay-like masses were present. [2 marks]\n\n 2. State a risk factor associated with coronary heart disease. [1 mark]\n\n5. Identify a limitation of using the plastic tube to represent an artery in this experiment. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n 1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ number of clay-like masses [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The independent variable in this experiment is the **number of clay-like masses** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ position of the hole **OR** height of the hole (in relation to the plastic tube) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ size of the hole [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ size **OR** length **OR** diameter of the plastic tube [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ water pressure from the pump [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature of the water [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ stillness of the surrounding air [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n In the experiment, the **position of the hole** ($\\checkmark$) on the plastic pipe should be kept the same, the hole should also be kept the **same size** ($\\checkmark$) between trials.\n\n2.\n 1.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as the number of clay-like masses increases, up until 4 masses, so does the distance the water stream travels [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Up until 4 masses, as the number of clay-like masses **increases**, the distance the water stream travels also **increases** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 2.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the distance the water stream travels is a measure of water pressure **WHICH** represents blood pressure [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as the number of occlusions in an artery increases, the blood pressure increases [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n In this experiment, blood pressure is represented by the distance the **water stream travels** ($\\checkmark$). As the number of clay-like masses, which represent arterial occlusions, increase, so does the distance the water stream travels, which represents **increasing blood pressure** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. *Units must be included to gain the mark*\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 4.07 cm (accept answers in the range of 4.06 - 4.07 cm) [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0780ANS%20C.png\" alt=\"BI0780ANS C\">\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ If…\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 1 - 2 clay masses = +0.14cm\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 2 - 3 clay masses = +0.15cm\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 3 - 4 clay masses = +0.16cm\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ then…\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 0 - 1 clay masses = either + 0.13 cm\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ so…\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Distance travelled by water for 1 clay mass = 4.20cm\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Distance travelled by water for 0 clay masses = 4.20cm - 0.13cm\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ = **4.07 cm** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4.\n 1.\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ clay-like masses represent fatty deposits **OR** plaques [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fatty deposits **OR** plaques can become dislodged and block **OR** restrict blood flow [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n In atherosclerosis, **fatty deposits** can build up in an artery, which is represented in this experiment by the **clay-like masses** ($\\checkmark$). These deposits, sometimes referred to as plaques, can become dislodged and block or **restrict blood flow** ($\\checkmark$), this could be the reason the distance the water stream travelled was reduced because there was a blockage.\n\n4.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ high LDL cholesterol [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ family history **OR** genetics [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ high blood pressure [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ lack of exercise [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ obesity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ diet high in saturated fats [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ alcohol consumption [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ smoking [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ diet high in salts [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ stress [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n A diet **high in saturated fats** ($\\checkmark$) can lead to high LDL cholesterol levels in the blood which is a risk factor for coronary heart disease.\n\n5. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ plastic tube lacks flexibility **OR** elasticity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ lumen width of plastic tube cannot change [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Arteries have **elastic walls** ($\\checkmark$), but the plastic tube lacks this."},{"ref":"BI0781","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0781\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nIn a classroom investigation into the effects of herbal remedies on bacterial infections, students selected a range of herbal teas, marketed as possessing “Immune System Boosting” properties. Teas each contained one main ingredient, in addition to other secondary ingredients, present in lower concentrations.\n\nA concentrated solution of each tea was prepared and a 0.2 g mass of cotton wool was soaked in each.\n\nA petri dish was prepared using nutrient agar gel and inoculated with a strain of *Staphylococcus aureus*. The masses of cotton wool were each placed on the petri dish, which was then incubated at 20 :sup[o]C for 5 days, after which the zone of inhibition for each mass of cotton wool was measured.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0781A.png\" alt=\"BI0781A\">\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0781B.png\" alt=\"BI0781B\">\n\nThe experiment was repeated 3 times, a mean and standard deviation was calculated. Results are shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0781C.png\" alt=\"BI0781C\">\n\n1.\n 1. State the dependent variable in this investigation. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Identify one control variable from the methodology. [1 mark]\n\n2.\n 1. Based on the results, suggest which ingredient is most effective at inhibiting the growth of bacteria. Give a reason for your answer. [1 mark]\n\n 2. State the **TWO** brands of tea whose results are unlikely to show statistically significant differences. Give a reason for your answer. [2 marks]\n\n3. Calculate the size of the inhibition zone for EterniTea as a percentage of the size of that for ResisTea. [1 mark]\n\n4. Outline the techniques which should be employed in order to reduce risk when working with bacterial cultures. [2 marks]\n\n5.\n 1. Identify a limitation of the methodology in allowing a valid comparison of each tea brand. [1 mark]\n\n 2. State how the size of the error bars could be reduced. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n 1. (units are required in order for the mark to be awarded)\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ zone of inhibition /mm [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The dependent variable in this investigation is the **zone of inhibition** which is measured in **mm** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ green tea leaves used in all brands [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cultures incubated at 20 :sup[o]C **OR** for 5 days [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mass of cotton wool **OR** 0.2 g of cotton wool [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ *Staphylococcus aureus* used in all experiments **OR** same bacteria used [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cultures grown on nutrient agar **OR** the same medium [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n In this experiment, each brand of tea used was made from **green tea leaves** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ turmeric **BECAUSE** the tea with turmeric as the primary ingredient had the largest zone of inhibition [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ **Turmeric** is the most effective at inhibiting bacteria because it's the main ingredient in the tea with the **largest zone of inhibition** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ EterniTea **AND** ImmuBooster [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** the error bars are overlapping **OR** there is significant overlap in the spread of data [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The difference in the results from **EterniTea** and **ImmuBooster** ($\\checkmark$) are not likely to be statistically significant because as shown in the graph, the **error bars are overlapping** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 333% [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0781%20ANS%20A.png\" alt=\"BI0781 ANS A\">\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ EterniTea = 20mm \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ ResisTea = 6mm\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 20 / 6 = 3.33 \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 3.33 x 100% \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ = **333%** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ prepare a risk assessment [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ wear lab coat **OR** gloves **OR** protective clothing **OR** eye protection **OR** mask [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ wash hands before and after [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ use aseptic techniques **OR** sterile equipment [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ incubate below 30 :sup[o]C [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ no eating or drinking in the lab [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ label cultures clearly [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ autoclave waste materials [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ use disinfectants to clean surfaces [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n When working with bacterial cultures, you should create a **risk assessment** ($\\checkmark$) and take measures to reduce risk such as employing **aseptic techniques** ($\\checkmark$) like sterilising equipment and the work surface with alcohol.\n\n5.\n 1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ concentration of primary **OR** secondary ingredients in each tea could be different **OR** was not controlled **OR** other ingredients may be present in each tea **OR** some of the secondary ingredients in the tea types were the same as the primary ingredients of other teas [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ volume of tea solution absorbed by the cotton wool could be different **OR** shape of the mass of cotton wool may vary [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ only one bacterial strain was used [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n A potential limitation of the methodology in this experiment is that the **concentration** of the primary and secondary ingredients **may vary** between the different tea brands ($\\checkmark$), which makes it difficult to compare them.\n\n5.\n 2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ increase the number of trials **OR** repeats [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ remove anomalous data [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ reduce sources of error [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ reduce uncontrolled variables [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ improve precision of measurement of diameter **OR** use more accurate instrument of measurement **OR** use vernier callipers **OR** other suitable equipment [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ To reduce the size of the error bars, the experiment could be **repeated more times** ($\\checkmark$) to give a larger data set in which to spot anomalies."},{"ref":"BI0782","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0782\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe graph shows the mean blood glucose levels in a study of 300 people, 10 minutes after eating a meal and 3 hours after eating a meal. Of the 300 people studied, 100 people were non-diabetic, 100 people had untreated Type 1 diabetes and 100 people had untreated Type 2 diabetes.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0782A.png\" alt=\"BI0782A\">\n\n1. Compare and contrast the blood glucose levels of people without diabetes and those with diabetes. [2 marks]\n\n2. Suggest one variable, other than those already mentioned in the question, that needs to be controlled to make this study valid. [1 mark]\n\n3. Predict how the mean blood glucose concentration after 3 hours would change if the Type 1 diabetics were injected with insulin 15 minutes after eating. [1 mark]\n\n4.\n 1. Explain the effect of insulin on blood glucose levels. [2 marks]\n\n 2. State the name of the cells that make and secrete insulin. [1 mark]\n\n5. Calculate the percentage change between the blood glucose concentration of people with Type 2 diabetes 10 minutes after eating a meal and 3 hours after eating a meal. [1 mark]\n\n6. Explain why Type 2 diabetics have a lower blood glucose concentration 3 hours after eating a meal, compared to Type 1 diabetics. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following\n\n **Similarities**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** diabetics and non diabetics have a higher blood glucose concentration 10 minutes after eating compared to 3 hours after the meal [1 mark]\n\n **Differences**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ People with diabetes have a higher blood glucose concentration 3 hours after a meal **COMPARED TO** people without diabetes. [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ People with diabetes have a higher blood glucose concentration, 10 minutes after a meal **COMPARED TO** people without diabetes. [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Both** diabetics and non diabetics have a **higher blood glucose concentration 10 minutes after eating** compared to 3 hours after the meal ($\\checkmark$). However, people with diabetes have a **higher blood glucose concentration 3 hours after a meal compared** to people without diabetes ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ biological sex [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ age [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ type of meal eaten [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mass of food eaten during meal [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mass of glucose in meal eaten [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ body mass of individuals taking part in study [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ activities conducted by participants [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ period of time since last meal [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ same volume of water consumed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ time of day study conducted [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ same hours of sleep before study [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ no medication or type of medication taken [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Mass of glucose** in meal eaten ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it would be lower than 180 mg dL:sup[-1] **OR** it would be similar to that of the non-diabetics [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n It would be **lower** than 180 mg L:sup[-1] ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.\n 1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ insulin lowers blood glucose levels [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ stimulates uptake of glucose by cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ activates enzymes involved in the conversion of glucose to glycogen [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glucose is converted into glycogen **OR** glycogenesis, in the liver **OR** skeletal muscle [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ causes an increase in respiration rate, increasing glucose breakdown [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increases conversion of glucose to lipids for storage in adipose tissue [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Insulin **lowers** blood glucose levels ($\\checkmark$) by stimulating the **uptake of glucose** by cells ($\\checkmark$). Insulin also **activates enzymes** involved in the conversion of **glucose to glycogen** in the **liver** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ beta (β) cells [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Beta cells** ($\\checkmark$) in the pancreas make and secrete insulin.\n\n5. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ((200 - 130) ÷ 200) x 100 % = 35 % [1 mark]\n *Units are required to gain the mark* \\\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0782ANS%20A.png\" alt=\"BI0782ANS A\">\n\n (200 - 130) ÷ 200 = 0.35 x 100% = **35 %** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n6. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Type 1 diabetes is caused by an inability to produce insulin **OR** in Type 1 diabetes there is destruction of ꞵ cells that produce insulin **OR** with Type 2 diabetes there is reduced ability of the body to respond or process insulin **OR** with Type 2 diabetes there is a deficiency of insulin receptors **OR** glucose transporters [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ with Type 1 diabetes little glucose is taken up and stored as glycogen in liver **OR** muscle cells **OR** in Type 2 diabetics some glucose can still be taken up and stored as glycogen in liver **OR** muscle cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ with Type 1 diabetes little glucose is taken up and stored as fat in adipose cells **OR** with Type 2 diabetes some glucose is still stored as fat in adipose cells [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The Type 1 diabetics have a higher blood glucose level after three hours compared to the Type 2 diabetics, as Type 1 diabetes is caused by an **inability to produce insulin** and Type 2 diabetes is caused by a **reduced ability of the body to respond to insulin** ($\\checkmark$) effectively. **Therefore Type 1 diabetics produce much less insulin** ($\\checkmark$) and so convert much less glucose into glycogen."},{"ref":"BI0785","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0785\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nTo study catalase enzyme activity, a student cut a turnip root into small cubes. They measured approximately 5 g in a weighing dish, added 50 ml water and used a blender to homogenize the turnip root. The homogenate was filtered and the liquid extract was stored in a test tube at room temperature.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0785A.png\" alt=\"BI0785A\">\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0785B.png\" alt=\"BI0785B\">\n\nThe turnip extract and water were added to the conical flask first. Then 3% hydrogen peroxide solution was added to the conical flask, as shown in the image above. The oxygen produced is collected over 1 minute and measured in an inverted measuring cylinder, as shown.\n\nThe student wanted to conduct an experiment to see the effect of increasing enzyme concentration on the reaction. She made up the different concentrations as shown in the table below and added 1 ml of 3% hydrogen peroxide to each.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0785C.png\" alt=\"BI0785C\">\n\n1. Suggest why the process of grinding the turnip root and extracting the filtrate was carried out. [1 mark]\n\n2. State the dependent variable. [1 mark]\n\n3. Explain why the volume of water was varied, as shown. [1 mark]\n\nThe following graph was obtained when the student varied the substrate (hydrogen peroxide) concentration, keeping the enzyme concentration constant.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0785D.png\" alt=\"BI0785D\">\n\n4. From the graph shown above, calculate the percentage increase in volume of oxygen gas produced when using 1% hydrogen peroxide compared to 0.5% hydrogen peroxide. [2 marks]\n\n5. Outline the reasons for the shape of the curve obtained. [2 marks]\n\nThe student repeated the same experiment but added an inhibitor along with the enzyme to the reaction mix. She got the following results.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0785E.png\" alt=\"BI0785E\">\n\n6. Deduce, with an explanation, whether a competitive or non-competitive inhibitor was used in the investigation. [2 marks]\n\n7. Other than the factors already mentioned, state one other factor that can lower the rate of an enzyme controlled reaction. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the catalase enzyme is extracted, **OR** released, from the cells by grinding [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ filtering remove, the parts of the plant cells that are not soluble, **OR** the cell walls, **OR** only allowed the solution through [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it is easier to vary the volume of enzyme added by using a filtrate, **OR** homogenate, of the turnip root cells [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The enzyme is **extracted from the turnip root cells** ($\\checkmark$) by grinding them and filtering to remove the parts of the plant cells that are not soluble.\n\n2. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the volume, **OR** ml min:sup[-1], of oxygen produced [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The dependent variable is the **volume of oxygen produced in 1 minute** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ different volumes of enzyme extract and water were used in each sample to provide different concentrations of enzyme [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ volume of water was added to get a final volume of 10ml for all concentrations of enzyme [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The different volumes of enzyme extract and water added were used **to produce different concentrations of enzyme** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. **Two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ values shown correctly, 1.6 for volume of O:sub[2] gas /ml min:sup[-1] for 0.5% H:sub[2]O:sub[2] and 3.2 for volume of O:sub[2] gas /ml min:sup[-1] for 1% H:sub[2]O:sub[2] [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 100% [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0785F.png\" alt=\"BI0785F\">\n\n % increase in oxygen gas released = [(3.2 - 1.6)/ 1.6] x 100% = **100%** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n5. **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ rate of reaction, **OR** volume of gas, increases with increased substrate concentration (up to 1.7%) **BECAUSE** more substrate molecules means more chances of successful collisions **OR** more enzyme-substrate-complexes form per unit time [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at higher concentrations gas production plateaus **BECAUSE** concentration of enzyme, **OR** number of enzyme molecules, become limiting **OR** at higher substrate concentrations the active sites on the enzyme are saturated [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n As more substrate is added, the rate of reaction **increases** ($\\checkmark$) up to 1.7% concentration of hydrogen peroxide. At higher concentrations of substrate, the enzyme concentration becomes limiting and therefore the curve **plateaus** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. Any **two** of the following (one for stating inhibitor is competitive)\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ inhibitor is competitive [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ competitive inhibitor competes with the substrate for the active site, so as substrate concentration increases, the activity of the enzyme **OR** rate of reaction, increases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ with a non-competitive inhibitor, even at high substrate concentrations the enzyme activity, **OR** rate of reaction, is always lower than when there is no inhibitor. [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at highest substrate concentrations enzyme activity rate, **OR** rate of reaction, is restored to, **OR** is the same as, levels without inhibitor [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ a graph sketched and annotated to show how each point above can be awarded the appropriate marks [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BIO785G.png\" alt=\"BIO785G\">\n\n At higher concentrations of substrate **the rate of enzyme activity is nearly as high as without an inhibitor** ($\\checkmark$) which shows that the inhibitor is competitive. With a non-competitive inhibitor, even at higher concentrations of substrate , **the enzyme activity rate is lower** ($\\checkmark$) than when there is no inhibitor.\n\n7. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperatures either side of the optimum temperature **OR** exposure to extreme temperatures [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ pH away from the optimum [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ surface area if in solid form [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Varying the pH **away from the optimum** ($\\checkmark$) (by adding acid or alkali to the enzyme extract) can lower the activity of the enzyme."},{"ref":"BI0786","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0786\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nCancer studies have shown that many tumours have abnormal patterns of aerobic respiration, showing increased levels of glycolysis and lower levels of oxidative phosphorylation, even in the presence of high oxygen levels. A breast cancer cell line (MDA-MB-231) shows variable protein expression of complex III in the electron transport chain compared to normal breast tissue.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0786.png\" alt=\"BI0786\">\n[Source: Adapted from : Owens, K. M., Kulawiec, M., Desouki, M. M., Vanniarajan, A., & Singh, K. K. (2011). *Impaired Oxphos Complex III in breast cancer. PLoS ONE, 6(8)*. Retrieved April 1, 2023, from https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0023846 Copyright free]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0786CHGCOR.png\" alt=\"BI0786CHGCOR\">\n\n1. State the independent variable in this study. [1 mark]\n\n2. Outline the role of the complexes in the electron transport chain. [2 marks]\n\n3. Suggest one advantage and one disadvantage of breast cancer cells using glycolysis to generate ATP rather than oxidative phosphorylation. [2 marks]\n\n4. Predict, with a reason, what would happen to the levels of oxidative phosphorylation in the non-cancerous cells if they were treated with an inhibitor of glycolysis. [1 mark]\n5. Explain why non-cancerous breast cancer cells were used as a control in this study. [1 mark]\n\n6. The researchers concluded that downregulation of complex III in breast cancer cells is a cause of breast cancer. Evaluate this conclusion. [2 marks]\n\n7. Outline one measurement that could be taken to determine the activity of Complex III in this study. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the type of cell [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The independent variable is whether the **cell type** ($\\checkmark$) is cancerous or not.\n\n2. any **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ oxidises reduced NAD/NADH **OR** oxidises reduced FAD/FADH:sub[2] [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ releases electrons [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ electrons move from complex to complex, releasing energy **OR** electrons are passed along complexes by redox reactions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ energy released is used to pump protons from the matrix to the intermembrane space [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ protons pass through ATP synthase down a concentration gradient **OR** using facilitated diffusion releasing energy, which is used to phosphorylate ADP [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ last complex **OR** complex IV, passes electrons onto oxygen **OR** reduces oxygen, so more electrons can enter electron transport chain [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The complexes **oxidise reduced NAD** ($\\checkmark$), which **releases electrons** ($\\checkmark$). The electrons move from complex to complex, **releasing energy** ($\\checkmark$) which is used to **pump protons** from the matrix into the **intermembrane space** ($\\checkmark$). This creates a **concentration gradient**, so protons move down the concentration gradient, through ATP synthase releasing energy which is used to **phosphorylate ADP to ATP** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **two** of the following (*answer must include **one** disadvantage and **one** advantage to gain full marks*):\n\n ***Advantage***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycolysis releases ATP more quickly than oxidative phosphorylation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycolysis is able to occur in the presence and absence of oxygen, whereas oxidative phosphorylation requires oxygen [1 mark]\n\n ***Disadvantage***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycolysis releases less energy per glucose molecule than oxidative phosphorylation **OR** glycolysis releases 2 ATP per glucose molecule whereas oxidative phosphorylation releases 36-38 ATP per glucose molecule [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n One advantage of breast cancer cells using glycolysis rather than oxidative phosphorylation to generate ATP is that **glycolysis generates ATP faster** than oxidative phosphorylation ($\\checkmark$). One disadvantage is that **glycolysis releases less ATP per glucose molecule** than oxidative phosphorylation ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **one** of the following (*answer must include a reason to gain one mark*):\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ decrease **BECAUSE** oxidative phosphorylation relies on the production of pyruvate from glycolysis [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The levels of oxidative phosphorylation would decrease, because oxidative phosphorylation **relies on the production of pyruvate** from glycolysis ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ indicates that any differences between the two cell types are only due to cancer [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Non-cancerous breast cancer cells were used as a control in this study as this indicates that any **differences between the two cell types are only due to cancer** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. any **two** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** reason for and **one** against to gain full marks*):\n\n ***Reasons for***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ there is a clear difference between expression of complex III in the breast cancer cell line compared to the non-cancerous cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ altered respiration in the cancer cell line can contribute to uncontrolled tumour growth [1 mark]\n\n ***Reasons against***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ study shows correlation not causation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ study was only conducted on one cell line [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ there may be other causes of breast cancer [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ there is no information on what other variables are being controlled [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The study shows the downregulation of complex III may be a cause of breast cancer as **altered respiration** in the cancer cell line can contribute to **uncontrolled tumour growth** ($\\checkmark$), however, there may be **another factor** which is causing uncontrolled cell division ($\\checkmark$). The study was conducted on only **one cell line** ($\\checkmark$) so it is unknown whether other cancer cell lines also have reduced levels of complex III.\n\n7. any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the concentration of ATP in mitochondria extracted from cancer cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the proportion of cyt-c **OR** cytochrome-c that is reduced [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ change in pH between the intermembrane space and matrix [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n One measurement that could be taken to determine the activity of complex III is the concentration of **ATP in mitochondria extracted from cancer cells** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0787","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0787\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nA group of students were asked to design an experiment to test the effect of the number of leaves on transpiration rates in plant shoots.\n\nStudents took cuttings of shoots with different numbers of leaves from the same plant and placed the stems in a simplified potometer. A potometer is a piece of apparatus used to measure the rate of water uptake by a plant.\n\nEach of the stems was placed by a window, and the water level was recorded every day for 3 days.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0787A.png\" alt=\"BI0787A\">\n\nThe results are shown in the table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0787B.png\" alt=\"BI0787B\">\n\n1. State clearly which is the dependent variable and independent variable in this investigation. [2 marks]\n\n2. Identify the two days between which the plant shoots lost the greatest volume of water. Suggest a reason for this. [2 marks]\n\n3. Calculate, as a percentage, the total water lost from the plant shoot with two leaves by day 3. [1 mark]\n\n4.\n 1. Suggest a reason for the similarity in results between the plant shoot with two leaves and three leaves. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Based on the results of the plant shoot with one leaf and assuming all other factors were controlled, predict the volume of water left in the graduated cylinder on day 2 for a plant shoot with 5 leaves. [1 mark]\n\n5.\n 1. Suggest a way in which the students could improve the reliability of their experiment. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Evaluate the use of the students' methodology in testing the effect of the number of leaves on the transpiration rate of plant shoots. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ number of leaves (independent variable) [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ volume of water in the graduated cylinder /cm:sup[3] (dependent variable) [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The independent variable is **the number of leaves** ($\\checkmark$) on the plant shoot and the dependent variable is the **volume of water in the graduated cylinder** which is measured in **cm:sup[3]** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ day 2 - 3 [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature of the room **OR** environment, was higher **OR** more sunlight **OR** humidity was lower **OR** lights were left on for longer in the lab **OR** window was opened and the wind increased [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The greatest loss of water was seen between **day 2 and 3** ($\\checkmark$). This may have been caused by an **increase** in the surrounding **environmental temperature** ($\\checkmark$) or more sunlight from the window.\n\n3. units are required in order to achieve the mark:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 42.5% [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0787ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0787ANSA\">\n\n change\n = original - new\n = 20 - 11.5\n = 8.5\n\n % decrease\n = change/original x 100%\n = 8.5/20 x 100%\n = **42.5%** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4.\n 1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ similar TOTAL surface area of leaves **OR** the leaves had the same TOTAL surface area [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ incorrect cutting of the shoot with three leaves resulting in water uptake restriction **OR** a kink in the plastic tubing for the shoot with three leaves restricted water uptake **OR** the apparatus for the shoot with three leaves was not watertight [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The results for the shoots with two and three leaves were very similar because perhaps the leaves were different sizes and despite one having more leaves than the other, the total **surface area** of the leaves was the **same** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.\n 2. units are required in order to achieve the mark:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 10cm:sup[3] [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0787ANSB.png\" alt=\"BI0787ANSB\">\n\n Water loss by day two for 1 leaf = original - new\n= 20 cm:sup[3] - 18 cm:sup[3]\n= 2 cm:sup[3]\n\n Water loss by day two for 5 leaves = 5 times the volume of 1 leaf\n = 5 x 2 cm:sup[3] = 10 cm:sup[3] ($\\checkmark$)\n\n5.\n 1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ repeat number of trials **OR** repeat at least twice more **OR** repeat to get at least 3 sets of results [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n To increase the reliability of the results you could repeat the trials until you have **at least three sets of results** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5.\n 2. Any **two** of the following (student answer must contain a strength and weakness of the methodology):\n\n **Strengths**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the burette is an accurate measuring device **OR** the burette was used to measure the volume of water lost [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ shoots from the same plant species were used **OR** any other controlled variable mentioned [1 mark]\n\n **Weaknesses**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the rate of transpiration was not calculated [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the surface area of the leaves **OR** the environment conditions were not controlled **OR** any other uncontrolled variable [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n A strength of this methodology is that the burette, which was used to measure the volume of water lost, is an **accurate measuring device** ($\\checkmark$). However, a weakness of this experiment is that some variables were not controlled such as the **surface area of the leaves** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0796","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0796\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nStudents were investigating metabolic reactions in the classroom and were given an experiment which demonstrated the effect of temperature on a catabolic reaction.\n\nA sample of fat was added to a beaker, which contained 150ml of a 1% sodium chloride solution.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0796A.png\" alt=\"BI0796A\">\n\nThe beaker and its contents were then added to a water bath, which was set at 15 :sup[o]C. The beaker was given 5 minutes to equalise with the temperature of the water bath. After 5 minutes, 5 ml of a 2 % lipase solution, at the same temperature as the water bath, was added to the beaker.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0796B.png\" alt=\"BI0796B\">\n\n5 ml of 2 % sodium hydroxide (NaOH), pH 8.4, was added to a test tube and phenolphthalein, a colourless solution which turns pink in basic environments, was then added. The intensity of the pink colour is proportionate to the strength of the basic solution.\n\nAt exactly 20 minutes, a 5ml sample was taken from the beaker incubated in the water bath and added to the test tube.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0796C.png\" alt=\"BI0796C\">\n\nThe contents of the test tube were then immediately transferred into a cuvette and placed in a spectrophotometer set at 420 nm. The percentage transmission of light able to pass through the sample was measured and recorded.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0796D.png\" alt=\"BI0796D\">\n\nThe procedure was then repeated using water baths set at a range of different temperatures. The measurements were recorded in a table and presented in a graph.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0796E.png\" alt=\"BI0796E\">\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0796F.png\" alt=\"BI0796F\">\n\n1.\n 1. State the independent variable in this experiment. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Identify two factors, not already mentioned, that would need to be controlled in this experiment. [1 mark]\n\n2.\n 1. Explain how the spectrophotometer is able to measure the breakdown of fat. [3 marks]\n\n 2. Explain the effect of increasing the temperature from 15 :sup[o]C to 35 :sup[o]C on the rate of the catabolic reaction. [2 marks]\n\n3. Predict the percentage transmission for a sample incubated at 20 :sup[o]C. [1 mark]\n\n4. Evaluate the claim that based on the data, the optimum temperature for lipase activity is 35 :sup[o]C. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1. (units are required for the mark to be awarded)\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature :sup[o]C [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The independent variable in this investigation is the **temperature (:sup[o]C)** of the water bath that the beakers are incubated in ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2. Any **two** of the following needed to gain the mark:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mass **OR** surface area of fat samples, **OR** type of fat **OR** size of beaker **OR** volume of phenolphthalein [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n In this experiment, the **mass of the fat sample** and the **volume of phenolphthalein** should be kept the same ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ enzyme lipase breaks down fat into fatty acids and glycerol [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fatty acids are acidic [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ phenolphthalein is pink in basic environments **OR** is pink when added to sodium hydroxide [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the intensity of the pink colour reduces as the pH of the solution decreases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the pH of the solution can be used as a measure of the concentration of fatty acids in a sample **OR** a lower pH can indicate a higher concentration of fatty acids [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the intensity of the pink colour **OR** the percentage of light transmission can be measured using the spectrophotometer [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ a higher percentage of % of light passes through the sample **OR** a higher light transmission result **OR** a lower colour intensity, correlates to a higher concentration of fatty acids in the sample [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The enzyme lipase breaks down fat into **fatty acids and glycerol** ($\\checkmark$). Fatty acids are **acidic** ($\\checkmark$) because they have a carboxyl group. When the sample containing the fatty acids is added to the solution with NaOH and phenolphthalein, the intensity of the pink colour will decrease depending on the concentration of fatty acids in the sample. The higher the percentage of **light transmission**, which is measured using the **spectrophotometer** ($\\checkmark$), the greater the **concentration of fatty acids** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as the temperature increases (from 15 :sup[o]C to 35 :sup[o]C) the kinetic energy of the molecules increases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as kinetic energy increases there are more successful collisions between the enzyme and the substrate **OR** between lipase and fat [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as temperature increases (from 15 :sup[o]C to 35 :sup[o]C), the rate of reaction increases **OR** rate of fat digestion increases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ more enzyme-substrate complexes form [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n As the temperature increased from 15 :sup[o]C to 35 :sup[o]C, the **kinetic energy** of the molecules **increased** ($\\checkmark$). An increase in the kinetic energy resulted in an increase in the number of **successful collisions** between the enzyme lipase and its substrate fat ($\\checkmark$), which in turn, **increased** the rate of reaction ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. (units are required for the mark to be awarded)\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 31.5% [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0796ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0796ANSA\">\n\n Mean % transmission at 15 :sup[o]C = $\\left(\\dfrac{\\text{22 +20+20}}{\\text{3}}\\right) = 20.67 = 21\\% $ (2s.f.)\n\n Mean % transmission at 25 :sup[o]C = $\\left(\\dfrac{\\text{42+44+41}}{\\text{3}}\\right) = 42.33 = 42\\% $ (2s.f.)\n\n 20 :sup[o]C is half way between 15 :sup[o]C and 25 :sup[o]C\n\n Predicted % transmission value at 20 :sup[o]C\n\n = mean at 15 :sup[o]C + half the difference between mean % transmission at25 :sup[o]C and 15 :sup[o]C\n\n = 21% + 10.5%\n\n = **31.5%** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4. Any **TWO** of the following:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 35 :sup[o]C has the highest percentage transmission of all temperatures used in the experiment **OR** the greatest rate of breakdown in this experiment [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ **HOWEVER** the optimum temperature may be between 25 :sup[o]C and 35 :sup[o]C **OR** 35 :sup[o]C and 55 :sup[o]C [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ to find the optimal temperature, more experiments would need to be carried out at narrower increments of temperature around 35 :sup[o]C **OR** to get closer to finding the optimum temperature repeat the experiment at temperatures of 30, 32, 34, 36, 38, 40 :sup[o]C [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 35 :sup[o]C may be the optimum temperature for lipase activity because in the experiment it yielded the **highest percentage transmission** ($\\checkmark$). However, because temperatures between 25 :sup[o]C - 35 :sup[o]C and 35 :sup[o]C - 55 :sup[o]C **were not tested** ($\\checkmark$), the optimum temperature could lie between these temperatures."},{"ref":"BI0801","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0801\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nThe micrograph shows a transverse section through a dicot stem.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0801A.png\" alt=\"BI0801A\">\n[Source: Adapted from Berkshire Community College Science Image Library, 2014. *Herbaceous Dicot Stem Younger Trifolium (3629423237).jpg*. Retrieved 30th January 2023. Copyright under Creative Commons CC0 1.0]\n\n1. Label xylem tissue with an ‘X’ in the micrograph. [1 mark]\n\n2. State how you identified the xylem tissue.        [1 mark]\n\n3. Explain how the xylem is adapted for its function.\n [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. :br\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ label line from the letter X pointing to the correct area showing xylem tissue    [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI0801ANSB.png\" alt=\"BI0801ANSB\">\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large diameter vessels    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tissue in the vascular bundles closer to the centre of the stem    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ thick lignified walls **OR** thick wall    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Xylem vessels are located in the vascular bundles and have a **large diameter** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ walls are thickened with lignin to withstand pressure **OR** to waterproof them    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vessels are hollow **OR** vessels have no cytoplasm to give space for the water column OR no obstruction to flow of water and minerals [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large diameter **OR** wide lumen, for movement of water and minerals **OR** to reduce resistance to flow of water and minerals        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pits in the walls of the vessels allow water to move between the vessels [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ end walls broken down **OR** largely broken down [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Xylem vessels are non-living and have **no cytoplasm** ($\\checkmark$) which allows space for movement of water and minerals. The walls of xylem vessels are **thickened with lignin** ($\\checkmark$) which allows them to withstand pressure."},{"ref":"BI2066","marks":"14","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2066\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nGerminating seeds can respire aerobically or anaerobically. A student used the apparatus shown below to measure the rate of respiration in germinating seeds, by measuring the volume of $\\ce{CO_2}$ produced.\n\nThey:\n\n- positioned the apparatus in a water bath at 25 $\\degree$C\n- left the apparatus for one hour before starting the investigation\n- placed the apparatus in complete darkness.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2066.png\" alt=\"BI2066\">\n\n1. State **two** reasons why the apparatus was maintained at 25$\\degree$C. [2 marks]\n\n2. Give **one** reason why it was important that the student left the apparatus for one hour, excluding any temperature-related factors. [1 mark]\n\n3. Suggest why it was important that the apparatus was placed in complete darkness. [1 mark]\n\nEvery 10 minutes a reading was taken from the apparatus shown above, and the coloured liquid was reset.\n\nBelow is a graph showing the results.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2066b.png\" alt=\"BI2066b\">\n\n4. Suggest why the rate of $\\ce{CO_2}$ produced decreased after 2.5 hours. [1 mark]\n\nThe rate of aerobic respiration in germinating seeds can be calculated by repeating the experiment above but using potassium hydroxide (KOH) instead of alkaline pyrogallol. This time the coloured liquid moved to the left.\n\n5. Explain why the coloured liquid moved to the left. [3 marks]\n\nThe student wanted to calculate the rate of oxygen uptake in this experiment. They reset the coloured liquid and timed its movement for 5 minutes. The distance moved along the capillary tubing was measured as 12cm, and the diameter as 2mm\n\n6. Using 3.14 for the value of $\\pi$, and the equation provided below,calculate the rate of oxygen uptake in cm$^3$ min$^{-1}$, giving your answer to three significant figures. Show your working. [2 marks]\n\nvolume of cylinder = $\\pi$ r$^{2}$ x h\n\n7. Explain why, in plants, the rate of $\\ce{CO_2}$ production during aerobic respiration is different to the rate during anaerobic respiration. [2 marks]\n\n8. Suggest, with a reason, what difference would be expected if small invertebrates were used in this second experiment instead of germinating seeds. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ prevents changes in gas pressure **OR** volume due to temperature fluctuations, **OR** higher temperatures increase volume **OR** pressure of gas, **OR** lower temperatures decrease volume **OR** pressure of gas [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ rate of respiration depends on the activity of enzymes, which is temperature-dependent [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ prevents heat produced by respiration from affecting overall temperature of the system [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ maintains a constant temperature to ensure comparability across different trials [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n If the **temperature changes**, it could **affect** the **pressure** and so the **volume** of **gases** inside the apparatus ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, fluctuations in temperature could **alter** the **rate** of **enzyme activity**, affecting the rate of **respiration** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ so any oxygen present in the system is used **OR** absorbed **OR** respired [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to ensure anaerobic conditions are fully established [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to allow gases to equilibrate **OR** allows gas pressures to stabilise within the apparatus [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n This allows time for all the **oxygen** in the system to be **used up**, ensuring **anaerobic conditions** are fully established ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to prevent photosynthesis occurring which would absorb $\\ce{CO_2}$ **OR** produce $\\ce{O_2}$ affecting results [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ placing the setup in darkness eliminates any chance of unintended light exposure affecting metabolic processes, ensuring consistent conditions **OR** prevents light being a confounding variable [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Even though **photosynthesis** in seeds is minimal, any that does occur would **use CO**$_2$ produced during respiration, **affecting the results** of the experiment. Placing the apparatus in darkness ensures this does not happen ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$glucose decreases **OR** is used up **OR** glucose is a limiting factor **OR** substrate of respiration is depleted [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in ethanol **OR** accumulation of by-products **OR** seeds start to die **OR** activity of enzymes involved in respiratory pathway reduced [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ accumulation ofcarbon dioxide **OR** CO₂ lowers pH, reducing enzyme activity [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Accumulation of **toxic by-products**, such as **ethanol** during **anaerobic respiration**, may **inhibit enzyme activity** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen used by seeds in aerobic respiration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide produced in aerobic respiration is absorbed by potassium hydroxide **OR** KOH [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ this decreases the volume of gas **OR** pressure inside the apparatus [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The germinating seeds **respired aerobically** and **absorbed oxygen** from the apparatus ($\\checkmark$). The **carbon dioxide** they **produced** was **absorbed** by the **potassium hydroxide** ($\\checkmark$). This caused the overall **gas volume and pressure** in the tube to **decrease**, so the coloured liquid moved to the left ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. any **two** of the following (*units not required for full marks, as units in question*)\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ working shows 0.3768 cm$^3$ **OR** shows division by 5 min **OR** 0.07 cm3 min-1 **OR** 0.075 cm$^3$ min$^{-1}$ **OR** any other wrong number of significant figures [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 0.0754 cm$^3$ min$^{-1}$ [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Volume of cylinder = $\\pi \\ce{r^2} \\times \\ce{h}$\n\n $\\pi \\times$ 0.12 cm $\\times$ 12 cm = 0.3768 cm$^3$\n\n Rate of oxygen uptake = $\\dfrac{\\ce{volume} (\\textbf{0.3768}\\ \\ce{cm^3)}}{{\\ce{Time} (\\textbf{5}\\ \\ce{min)}}}$ = **0.07536** cm$^3$ min$^{-1}$ ($\\checkmark$)\n\n **0.0754** cm$^3$ min$^{-1}$ ($\\checkmark$)\n\n7. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ rate of $\\ce{CO_2}$ production is higher during aerobic **THAN** anaerobic respiration in plants [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ only glycolysis occurs during anaerobic respiration **WHEREAS** glycolysis, link reaction, Krebs cycle and oxidative phosphorylation occur during aerobic respiration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ one molecule of glucose yields two $\\ce{CO_2}$ molecules in alcoholic fermentation **OR** anaerobic respiration **WHEREAS** six $\\ce{CO_2}$ molecules produced in aerobic respiration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in aerobic respiration, glucose is fully broken down into $\\ce{CO_2}$ and water, **WHEREAS** anaerobic respiration only partially breaks down glucose into ethanol and $\\ce{CO_2}$ [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n In plants, **anaerobic** respiration involves **glycolysis**, where glucose is converted into **ethanol** and **two CO**$_2$ molecules ($\\checkmark$). **Aerobic** respiration, however, involves **glycolysis**, the **link reaction**, the **Krebs cycle**, and **oxidative phosphorylation** ($\\checkmark$), **completely oxidising glucose** into **six CO**$_2$ molecules and **water** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n8. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ coloured liquid would move at a greater rate **OR** a greater rate of O₂ uptake **OR** consumption would be observed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ invertebrates typically have a higher metabolic rate than germinating seeds **OR** invertebrates require more energy for movement **OR** temperature regulation **OR** other valid example [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The coloured liquid would move **more rapidly**($\\checkmark$) because invertebrates would **use O₂** at a **greater rate** than germinating seeds, due to them having a **higher metabolic rate** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2219","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2219\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe beach strawberry (*Fragaria chiloensis*) and yellow sand verbena (*Abronia latifolia*) are two plant species found in the coastal region of Oregon, USA. Some students decided to record the presence and absence of the two species in 200 quadrats and use the chi-squared test to examine whether their distributions were associated with each other. The table below shows the observed results obtained.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2219a.png\" alt=\"BI2219a\">\n\n1. Outline the purpose of a Chi-Square test. [1 mark]\n\n2. Suggest a null hypothesis for this investigation. [1 mark]\n\nThe expected results and chi-square values were calculated:\n\n| Species absent / present | Expected value | Chi-square value |\n| :-- | :-: | :-: |\n| Yellow sand verbena present $\\\\$ Beach strawberry present(A) | 80 | 1.25 |\n| Yellow sand verbena present $\\\\$ Beach strawberry absent | 54 | 1.85 |\n| Yellow sand verbena absent $\\\\$ Beach strawberry present | 40 | 2.50 |\n| Yellow sand verbena absent $\\\\$ Beach strawberry absent |26 |\n\n3. Calculate the chi-square value for the final quadrant using the formula $\\ce{χ^2}= \\Sigma \\dfrac{\\ce{(O-E)^2}}{\\ce{E}}$. [1 mark]\n\n4. State the number of degrees of freedom for this data set to determine the critical value of chi-squared. [1 mark]\n\n| Degrees of freedom | Critical value for p=0.05 |\n| :-- | :-- |\n| 1 | 3.84 |\n| 2 | 5.99 |\n| 3 | 7.81 |\n| 4 | 9.49 |\n\n5. Using the **total** chi-squared value, explain the conclusion that can be drawn from the calculated and critical values obtained from the data. [4 marks]\n\n6. Suggest **two** types of additional data that could be collected to determine if there is interspecific competition between beach strawberry (*Fragaria chiloensis*) and yellow sand verbena (*Abronia latifolia*). [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to determine whether there is a statistically significant association between two categorical variables [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to evaluate whether the observed data significantly deviate from expected data under the null hypothesis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to test if the difference between observed and expected data is due to chance [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n To determine whether there is a **significant association between two** categorical **variables** ($\\checkmark$) or whether **observed** data **significantly deviate** from the **expected** data under the **null hypothesis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ there is no significant association **OR** no association between the presence of beach strawberry **OR** *Fragaria chiloensis* and yellow sand verbena **OR** *Abronia latifolia* in the coastal region of Oregon **OR** in the quadrats [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n There is **no** significant **association** between the **presence of yellow sand verbena and beach strawberry** ($\\checkmark$) in the data collected using quadrats.\n\n3. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 3.85 (*do not allow answers to anything other than 2d.p.*) [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n $\\ce{χ^2}= \\Sigma \\dfrac{\\ce{(O-E)^2}}{\\ce{E}}$\n\n $\\ce{χ^2}= \\Sigma \\dfrac{(36-26)^2}{26} = \\dfrac{100}{26}$ = **3.85** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 1 [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n degrees of freedom = (r - 1) $\\times$ (c - 1)\\\n Where:\\\n r = number of rows\\\n c = number of columns\n\n For this data set:\\\n df = (2-1) $\\times$ (2-1) = 1 $\\times$ 1 = **1** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n5. any **four** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ $\\ce{χ^2_{(total)}}$ = 9.45 [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ $\\ce{χ^2_{(critical)}}$ = 3.84 (at 1 d.f.) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ since $\\ce{χ^2_{(total)}}$ = 9.45 is greater than $\\ce{χ^2_{(critical)}}$ = 3.84 , null hypothesis is rejected [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ indicates a statistically significant difference between observed and expected frequencies [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ evidence of a statistical association between the presence of beach strawberry and yellow sand verbena **OR** distribution not independent of each other [1 mark]\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The total chi-squared value is calculated as follows:\n\n $\\ce{χ^2_{(total)}}$ = (1.25 +1.85+2.5+3.85) = **9.45** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n The **critical value** at **1 degree of freedom** (p = 0.05) = **3.84** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n Since $\\textbf{χ}^2_{\\textbf{(total)}}$ = **9.45 is greater than** $\\textbf{x}^2_{\\textbf{(critical)}}$ **= 3.84**, the **null hypothesis** is **rejected** ($\\checkmark$). This indicates that there is a **significant difference** between the **observed** and **expected values** in the data set ($\\checkmark$).\n\n There is **evidence** of a **statistical association** between the **presence** of **beach strawberry** and **yellow sand verbena**, suggesting that their **distributions** are **not independent** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ field observations to track the survival of the two species in single-species **OR** mixed-species plots [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ field observations to monitor growth **OR** seed production in single-species **OR** mixed-species plots [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ remove one of the species in half of the field study area and compare the growth of the other species in the presence and absence of the species removed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ observe positive interactions, such as by checking if one species provides shade **OR** nutrients **OR** other benefit to the other species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ measure competition for resources **OR** competition for soil nutrients **OR** water **OR** light availability in areas where the two species coexist versus areas where they are isolated [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ conduct controlled experiments in a greenhouse to control abiotic factors and test competitive interactions between the two species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ investigate the soil microbiome composition in areas dominated by one species versus mixed-species areas to assess indirect competition through microbial interactions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ track pollinator activity to determine if the two species compete for the same pollinators [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ any other suitable suggestion [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Interspecific competition can be studied by observing the **survival** of the **two species** in **single-species** or **mixed-species** plots ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, **one species** can be **removed** from **half** of the field **study area**, and the **growth** of the **other species** can be **monitored** to **compare** their **performance** in the **presence** and **absence** of the **competing species** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2411","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2411\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nA student wanted to compare the stomatal density on the leaves of two different species of tree. They took impressions of the lower surface of the leaves using clear nail varnish. Below are the student’s diagrams of two of the impressions, examined at a magnification of x400.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/stomatal%20density%20drawing%20species%20A%20and%20B.png\" alt=\"stomatal density drawing species A and B\">\n\nThe length of each side of the fields of view shown above is 250μm.\n1. For species B calculate the number of stomata per mm$^{2}$ and show your working. [2 marks]\n\nThe student then removed four more leaves from each tree and spread a waterproofing agent onto the top surface of each leaf. The leaves were then hung from a piece of string tied between two clamp stands as shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/drying%20leaves%20on%20washing%20line%20chemix.png\" alt=\"drying leaves on washing line chemix\">\n\nThe leaves were then weighed at regular intervals, and their masses were recorded. The percentage change in mass of each leaf was calculated. The mean results for each species are shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/graph%20to%20show%20percentage%20decrease%20in%20mass%20of%20leaves.png\" alt=\"graph to show percentage decrease in mass of leaves\">\n\n2. Explain why the percentage change in mass was recorded rather than just the change in mass. [2 marks]\n\n3. Identify **one** factor, not already mentioned that would need to be controlled in this experiment. [1 mark]\n\n4. Calculate the difference in percentage change in mass between species A and species B after 30 hours. [1 mark]\n\n5. Explain why the greater stomatal density of species B leads to the results shown in the graph. [1 mark]\n\n6. Suggest one reason why the rate of mass lost decreases towards the end of the experiment. [1 mark]\n\n7. When transpiration is not enough to bring about the movement of water through the plant, root pressure is required. Describe how root pressure is generated. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. **both** of the following (*units required to get full marks*):\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ size of field of view = 250μm x 250μm = 0.25mm x 0.25mm = 0.0625mm$^{2}$ **OR** 1mm$^{2}$/0.0625mm$^{2}$ = 16 **OR** number stomata in 1mm$^{2}$ = 12 x 16 [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 192 stomata mm$^{-2}$ [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Number of stomata in field of view = 12\n\n Size of field of view = 250μm x 250μm = 0.25mm x 0.25mm = 0.0625mm$^{2}$\n\n 1mm$^{2}$/0.0625mm$^{2}$ = 16\n\n If there are 12 stomata in 0.0625mm$^{2}$\n\n Number of stomata in 1mm$^{2}$ = **12 x 16** ($\\checkmark$)= **192 mm$^{-2}$** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ leaves may be of different lengths, **OR** widths **OR** sizes **OR** masses [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it provides a standardised measure by expressing the change in proportion of the starting mass [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allows for comparison between the changes in mass [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ percentage change reduces the impact of minor measurement inaccuracies as it is expressed as a ratio [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The change in mass was calculated as a percentage because the leaves may have had **different starting masses** ($\\checkmark$), and percentage change allows for **direct comparisons** ($\\checkmark$) between the two species despite these differences.\n\n3. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ wind speed **OR** direction [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ humidity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ light intensity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ health of leaves **OR** herbivory **OR** cleanliness of bottom surface of leaf [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ age **OR** stage of development, of leaf **OR** plant [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ any other valid controlled variable [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n One variable that would need to be controlled in this investigation is the **temperature** ($\\checkmark$) of the room where the experiment is conducted.\n\n4. *units must be included to gain the mark*:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 28% [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2411ms.png\" alt=\"BI2411ms\">\n\n Percentage decrease in mass of Species A after 30 hours = 34%\n\n Percentage decrease in mass of Species B after 30 hours = 62%\n\n Difference in percentage decrease in mass = 62% - 34% = **28%** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n5. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ greater rate of evaporation of water **OR** transpiration from the leaves of species B compared to species A [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The greater stomatal density in the lower epidermis of species B leads to a **higher rate of water loss** through **evaporation** ($\\checkmark$), resulting in a greater percentage change in mass.\n\n6. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ decreased water availability inside the leaves **OR** decreased difference in water concentration gradient between inside the leaf and surrounding air [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fewer stomata open **OR** stomatal opening becomes less **OR** stomata not open as wide [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The decrease in mass loss towards the end of the experiment may be due to **fewer stomata being open** ($\\checkmark$) as a response to water stress.\n\n7. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Casparian **OR** suberin strip in endodermal cells blocks apoplast pathway, forcing water to move through symplast pathway (*not needed from May 2028*) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ active transport of mineral ions **OR** salts **OR** potassium ions **OR** nitrate ions **OR** any other suitable named mineral ion into the xylem by root **OR** endodermal cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mineral ions **OR** named mineral ion **OR** salts lower water potential in the xylem so water enters by osmosis from surrounding root cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ positive pressure potential in xylem develops, pushing water upwards [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Endodermal cells actively transport mineral ions into the xylem ($\\checkmark$), so that water follows, generating positive pressure potential that pushes the water upwards ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2446","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2446\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nMonozygotic twin studies have been used to investigate the effect of environmental factors on epigenetic tags.\n\nThe table below summarises data from three twin studies.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2446.png\" alt=\"BI2446\">\n\n1. Define the term epigenesis. [1 mark]\n\n2. Explain why monozygotic twins are suitable to investigate the effect of the environment on methylation patterns. [3 marks]\n\n3. Describe how methylation affects gene expression. [2 marks]\n\n4. Evaluate the validity of the following statement:\n“Stress has the most significant impact on methylation in humans.” [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ development of differentiation patterns in cells of multicellular organisms [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Epigenesis is the process by which **cells** in a **multicellular organism** develop **different functions** through controlled **gene expression**, influenced by **epigenetic modifications** without changing the DNA sequence ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ monozygotic twins share 100% of their DNA [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allows comparison of identical DNA **OR** DNA is a control variable [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ environmental factors are the independent factors [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ differences between the methylation patterns are the dependent variable **OR** can be attributed to the environment [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ methylation differences can be quantified **OR** extent of DNA methylation at specific genes can be measured [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Monozygotic twins have **identical DNA** ($\\checkmark$), making genetic differences **a controlled variable**, and **environmental factors** the **independent variable** ($\\checkmark$). This means that differences between the methylation patterns of the twins can be **attributed to the environment** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ methyl tags attach to cytosine in gene promoters [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ methylation of promoters reduces transcription [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ methyl tags attach to amino acids in histones [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ methylation of histones can repress **OR** activate gene expression [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Methyl tags **attach** to **cytosine** in **promoters** ($\\checkmark$) and **amino acids** in **histones** ($\\checkmark$). Methylation of promoters **reduces transcription** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **four** of the following:\n\n ***Results could be valid because:***\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ difference in methylation between the low- and high-stress environment is highest compared to the other variables **OR** the difference in methylation between the low and high-stress environment is 25%, but it is only 10% **AND** 15% for exercise and pollution respectively [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ experiments used monozygotic twins wo share one hundred percent of their DNA **OR** are suitable to be used as a control [1 mark]\n\n ***Results may not be valid because:***\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ no test for significance of results **OR** statistical analysis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ small data set means there is limited confidence in results **OR** results may not be generalisable **OR** fit the wider population [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ only three environmental factors were investigated **OR** other factors not investigated may have more of an impact [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ uncontrolled variables may have influenced results **OR** it is difficult to control all the environmental variables to ensure the validity of results [1 mark]\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n There is a **greater difference** in **% methylation** between the **high-stress** and **low-stress** environmental conditions compared to the other variables tested ($\\checkmark$). The data could be valid because **monozygotic twins share 100% of their DNA** so they are a suitable control ($\\checkmark$). The results may not be valid because there is **no statistical analysis** ($\\checkmark$) and **uncontrolled variables may have influenced the data** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2521","marks":"11","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2521\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nA student is interested in comparing the protein concentration in cow’s milk and oat milk. They conduct a Biuret test (a chemical test that detects the presence of proteins in a sample) and use a spectrophotometer to measure their results.\n\nIf proteins are present in the milk sampled, copper ions in the Biuret reagent will bind to the nitrogen atoms of peptide bonds in the milk and cause a colour change from blue to purple. The intensity of the purple colour is directly proportional to the protein concentration in the sample.\n\nThe Biuret reaction forms a complex between copper ions and peptide bonds in the protein.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2521a.png\" alt=\"BI2521a\">\n\n1. Label a peptide bond on the complex above. [1 mark]\n\n540 nm is the optimal wavelength for measuring the purple colour formed in the Biuret test, as it corresponds to the absorption peak of the copper-protein complex.\n\nThe student uses bovine serum albumin (BSA) to generate a standard curve that can be used to approximate the concentration of protein in the milk samples.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2521bfull.png\" alt=\"BI2521bfull\">\n\n2. State the relationship between the BSA protein concentration and absorbance at 540nm.[1 mark]\n\n3. Explain what a higher absorbance value signifies in this experiment. [2 marks]\n\nThe student’s results are shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2521cifull.png\" alt=\"BI2521cifull\">\n\n4. Determine the protein concentration in cow’s milk and oat milk. [2 marks]\n\n5. Suggest a potential source of error in spectrophotometry. [1 mark]\n\n6. Cow’s milk contains all nine essential amino acids. Define the term essential amino acid. [1 mark]\n\nCasein is the main protein found in cow’s milk, and avenin is the main protein found in oat milk. The student used the Biuret test to investigate the effect of temperature on the denaturation of these proteins by measuring the change in concentration of soluble proteins. The results are shown in the graph below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2521dfull.png\" alt=\"BI2521dfull\">\n\n7. Compare and contrast the denaturation of casein and avenin milk proteins from the data. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ peptide bond labelled as between the C of the carboxyl group of one amino acid and the N of the amine group of another amino acid [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2521amsi.png\" alt=\"BI2521amsi\">\n\n2. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ appears to be positive correlation **OR** absorbance increases as protein concentration increases [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n There appears to be a **positive correlation** as **absorbance increases** as protein **concentration increases**\n ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ more copper-protein complexes form **OR** more copper ions bind to protein [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ greater intensity of purple colour **OR** darker purple **OR** darker lilac [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the darker the sample **OR** the more complexes formed the more light absorbed [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n As protein concentration increases, **more copper-protein complexes** form ($\\checkmark$), producing a **darker purple** sample ($\\checkmark$), which absorbs more light.\n\n4. any **two** of the following *(units required for full marks, if correct values given for both but no units only **one** mark can be awarded)*:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cows milk 0.46 mg mL$^{-1}$ (allow answer in range 0.45 – 0.47) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ oat milk 0.27 mg mL$^{-1}$ (allow answer in range 0.26 – 0.28) [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2521dms.png\" alt=\"BI2521dms\">\n\nThe concentration of protein in cows milk is **0.46 mg mL$^{-1}$** ($\\checkmark$), and in oat milk is **0.27 mg mL$^{-1}$** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ incorrect calibration **OR** not calibrating with water [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ dirty **OR** scratched cuvette [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ air bubbles in sample [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ accept any other reasonable answer\n [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The spectrophotometer must be **calibrated** properly in **between each sample** with distilled wate ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cannot be synthesised by the body in sufficient quantities **OR** not synthesised by the body **OR** must be consumed in the diet as cannot be made by body [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n An amino acid that the **body cannot synthesise** in **sufficient** quantities on its own, so it must be **obtained** through the **diet** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n7. any **three** of the following *(must include **one** similarity and **one** difference to gain full marks)*:\n\n ***Similarities***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the soluble protein content of **BOTH** decreases with increasing temperature [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as temperature increases protein denaturation increases in **BOTH** [1 mark]\n\n ***Differences***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ casein shows larger loss of solubility **OR** greater denaturation upon heating **OR** less steep decline in avenin's solubility **OR** less denaturation of avenin with heating [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ casein denatures steadily between 50$^{o}$C and 90$^{o}$C **WHEREAS** avenin does not show much denaturation until 70$^{o}$C is reached [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ casein soluble protein decreases by 1.0 mg mL$^{-1}$ OR from 2.0 to 1.0 mg mL$^{-1}$ between 50$^{o}$C and 90$^{o}$C **WHEREAS** avenin decreases by 0.3 mg mL$^{-1}$ OR from 0.8 to 0.5 mg mL$^{-1}$ [1 mark]\n\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n In **both** proteins, **denaturation** is **progressive** with **increasing temperature** ($\\checkmark$). However, **casein denatures more rapidly** ($\\checkmark$) and shows a **greater loss** of **soluble protein** mass (1.0 mg mL$^{-1}$) than **avenin** (0.3 mg mL$^{-1}$) ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2623","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2623\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nHospital doctors compared the mitotic index of some tumour cells with that of some non-tumour cells retrieved from a patient. They prepared a light microscope slide for each specimen. The doctors diagnosed the patient’s tumour as melanoma, a skin cancer characterised by abnormally shaped, sometimes coloured moles on the skin surface.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/2623%20Edit.png\" alt=\"2623 Edit\">\n\n1. Calculate the mitotic index for each sample.        [2 marks]\n\n2. Describe **three** other requirements of the slide and microscope setup that enable the mitotic index to be calculated by light microscopy.         [3 marks]\n\n3. Melanomas have a high probability of giving rise to secondary tumours. State the name of the process by which secondary tumours form.    [1 mark]\n\n4. Suggest how the doctors could have improved the accuracy and reliability of their determination of the mitotic index.   [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ tumour sample, mitotic index 9.41 **OR** 9.4%         [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ non-tumour sample, 2.17 **OR** 2.2%        [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ *ignore omission of %*\\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ *do not credit decimal values e.g. 0.91, 0.02*\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Mitotic index formula = $(\\dfrac{\\ce{number of cells in the active stages of mitosis}}{\\ce{total number of cells observed}}) \\times 100$%\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ For the tumour sample, mitotic index = $\\dfrac{8}{85} \\times 100$% = **9.4%** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ For the non-tumour sample, mitotic index = $\\dfrac{2}{92} \\times 100$% = **2.2%** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sample must be one cell thick to allow enough light to pass through the sample            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sample must be stained    to colour the cell nuclei **OR** to provide visual contrast            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cells must be fixed **OR** dead **OR** no longer actively dividing so that no further cell division takes place **OR** to prevent the mitotic index from fluctuating            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ high magnification (200$\\times$ to 1000$\\times$) to allow enough detail to be observed within cell nuclei **OR** to be able to see condensed chromosomes of cells undergoing mitosis        [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The sample must be **dead** so that no cells are still actively dividing; this **removes the risk** of the mitotic index **fluctuating** during the study ($\\checkmark$). Tissue samples must be **one cell thick** so that **enough light** can pass through the sample to give an adequate visual picture ($\\checkmark$). The sample should be **stained** appropriately to provide **visual contrast** so that chromosomes can be observed ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ metastasis [1 mark]\n\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ This is called **metastasis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **four** of the following:\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ examined additional fields of view **OR** calculated the mitotic index from different areas of the same tissue samples [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ taken additional biopsies **OR** tissue samples from different regions **AND** analysed them microscopically [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ counted a larger number of cells **OR** increased the sample size [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ identified anomalous results **AND** investigated them [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ calculated mean mitotic indices from replicate measurements [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ used higher-resolution imaging **OR** microscopy to identify cells undergoing mitosis more accurately [1 mark]\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ carried out an appropriate statistical test to determine whether the difference between tumour and non-tumour samples was statistically significant [1 mark]\n\n   [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The doctors could have examined **additional fields of view** from each tissue sample ($\\checkmark$). They could have taken **additional biopsies from different regions** of the tumour and comparable non-tumour tissue ($\\checkmark$). They could have **counted a larger number of cells** ($\\checkmark$) and **calculated mean mitotic indices** from **replicate measurements** ($\\checkmark$). They could also have used **higher-resolution microscopy** to identify cells undergoing mitosis more accurately ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2743","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2743\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe oscilloscope trace shows changes in the membrane potential of a neuron during an action potential.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/2743.png\" alt=\"2743\">\n\n1. Explain the changes in membrane potential between II and IV. [2 marks]\n\n2. Use the graph to estimate the number of impulses per minute that this neuron would likely transmit if there were 2 ms between the end of one action potential and the start of the next. [2 marks]\n\n3. Researchers used oscilloscope traces to study how the membrane potential changed in the fingertips of professional basketball players when different levels of pressure were applied. Five players participated in the study, with three trials conducted for each of the three pressure levels.\n\n 1. State **two** factors that need to be controlled in this investigation. [2 marks]\n\n 2. Suggest a control experiment that could be used for this investigation. [1 mark]\n\n 3. Suggest, with a reason, an improvement to the experimental method used in this investigation that would increase the reliability of the data collected. [2 marks]\n\n 4. Identify an appropriate statistical test for significance that could be used if the data were normally distributed and a larger sample size were available. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. any **two** of the following *(must include **one** statement for depoarisation, and **one** statement for between repolarisation to gain full marks)*:\n\n **Depolarisation:**\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at II, the membrane potential is -55 mV **OR** reaches the threshold potential, **WHICH** causes the voltage-gated sodium channels to open [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at III, the diffusion of sodium ions into the neuron causes depolarisation **OR** the membrane to change from negative to positive **OR** to between +30 mV and +40 mV [1 mark]\n\n **Repolarisation**\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at approximately +30 mV voltage-gated sodium channels shut and voltage-gated potassium channels open, **WHICH** causes potassium ions to diffuse out of the neuron [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ diffusion of potassium ions out of the neuron through potassium channels causes repolarisation **OR** makes membrane potential negative again **OR** makes the inside negative compared to the outside **OR** changes the membrane potential towards -70mV **OR** resting potential **OR** -80mV [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n At point II, the membrane potential reaches the **threshold** (-55 mV), **causing voltage-gated sodium channels to open** ($\\checkmark$) and **sodium ions** to **enter** the neuron, leading to **depolarisation** ($\\checkmark$). Around **+40mV voltage-gated potassium channels close** and **voltage-gated potassium channels open** ($\\checkmark$) allowing **potassium ions to move out** of the neuron, which causes **repolarisation** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. **both** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 2.6 ms, *(accept answers in range 2.3 ms - 2.8 ms)* **OR** 4.6 ms, *(accept answers in teh range 4.3 ms - 4.8 ms)* [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 13,043 min$^{-1}$ *(accept answers in range 12,500 - 14,000)* [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/Ans.png\" alt=\"Ans\">\n\n Start of depolarisation =1.2 ms,\\\n Restoration of resting potential = 3.8 ms\n\n Time for one action potential = 3.8 ms - 1.2 ms = **2.6 ms** ($\\checkmark$)\\\n Interval between each action potential = 2ms \\\n Total time between start of one action potential and start of next = 2.6 ms + 2.0 ms = **4.6 ms** ($\\checkmark$)\\\n 1 min = 60,000 ms\\\n Number of impulses in one minute = $\\dfrac{\\mathbf{60,000}}{\\mathbf{4.6}}$ = **13,043 min**$^{-1}$ ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 1. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ same environmental conditions **OR** room temperature **OR** lighting **OR** same noise level to control distractions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ consistent placement of electrode on each participant’s finger [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ same hand **OR** dominant hand **OR** non-dominant hand used by all participants **OR** all participants have same dominant hand **OR** are all left handed **OR** are all right handed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ standardised instructions to participants to reduce behavioural variation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allow adequate rest period between trials to avoid fatigue effects [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ensure equipment calibration before each session [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ randomise order of pressure levels to prevent anticipatory responses [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ no visual prompts **OR** no sound prompts [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ basketball players of the same experience **OR** age **OR** training hours [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ same measuring equipment for changes in membrane potential **OR** same equipment for applying pressure **OR** pressure applied must be equal for all participants [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ finger temperature must be consistent because temperature affects the speed of nerve conduction [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ duration of pressure must be the same for all trials [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ any other suitable factor [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n For this investigation, **the duration of the pressure must be the same** for each trial ($\\checkmark$). The **same instructions** must also be given to all participants ($\\checkmark$).\n\n 2. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ people of the same age who do not play basketball **OR** non-athlete participants under identical conditions for comparison [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ record changes in membrane potential when no pressure is applied to measure baseline membrane potential changes not caused by mechanical stimulation **OR** for comparison [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Collect data for individuals who are not basketball players** using the same pressures and following the same method ($\\checkmark$).\n\n 3. any **two** of the following *(improvement with the appropriate reasoning must both be given to gain full marks)*:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increase the number of trials at each pressure **OR** complete at least ten trials for each pressure [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** three trials for each participant at each pressure are insufficient to generate reliable data **OR** to give sufficient data points for analysis [1 mark]\n\n **OR**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increase the number of participants **OR** test changes in membrane potential for at least ten basketball players [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to allow data processing, such as mean **OR** mode **OR** median, to be calculated **OR** ANOVA **OR** t-tests could be used to test for statistically significant results [1 mark]\n\n **OR**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ use double-blind design **OR** use a method where neither the participant nor the person collecting the data knows which treatment is being applied [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to reduce observer bias **OR** to make the results more objective **OR** to increase reliability of the data [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Increasing the number of trials to ten** ($\\checkmark$) for each pressure to **give sufficient data points for analysis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n 4. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ t test **OR** paired t test [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ANOVA [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **t-test** ($\\checkmark$) could test for statistically significant differences between the data sets for different pressure levels."},{"ref":"BI2772","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2772\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nJohn Endler experimented on guppies, investigating sexual and natural selection. In one experiment, he raised guppies for six months in tanks with no predators, then transferred them to three different pools. In one pool, no predators were added, in another, a strong predator was added, and in a third, a weak predator was added. Six months later, he collected data on the number and colour of spots on the guppies.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/2772.png\" alt=\"2772\">\n\n1. Determine which colour showed the greatest decrease in mean number of spots when predators were added. [1 mark]\n\n2. Using the data and your own knowledge, discuss the hypothesis that having spots makes individuals more fit. [3 marks]\n\n3. Suggest with reasons what selection pressures may have led to variation in spot colour in guppies. [2 marks]\n\nA fourth group of guppies was transferred from a pool back into the wild, and the mean number of spots was determined intermittently over 7 months.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/2772b.png\" alt=\"2772b\">\n\n4. Describe how the mean number of spots changed after the guppies were released into the wild. [2 marks]\n\n5. Suggest reasons for the trend seen in the mean number of spots between 5 and 7 months. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ blue [1 mark]\n\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The mean number of **blue** spots decreased the most when predators were added ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in guppies females tend to mate with males that have more spots [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ males with more spots are more reproductively successful [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mean number of red spots did not show a large decrease under predation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ having red spots in the presence of predators is adaptive because females may prefer red spots [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ spots remained for all colours when predators were added suggesting sexual selection may favour having spots even in the presence of predators [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ predators may not always be present in the wild, so selection pressure from predation may vary [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ having blue spots decreases fitness in the presence of predators, **OR** having red spots slightly decreases fitness in the presence of predators [1 mark]\n\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Having spots** makes males more **reproductively fit** ($\\checkmark$) because **females tend to mate** with males with **more spots** ($\\checkmark$). On the other hand, **having coloured spots** makes males **more visible to predators**, which would make them **less fit** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n ***Sexual selection:***\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ different females may prefer different spot colours [1 mark]\n\n ***Natural selection:***\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ different colours may be more **OR** less visible to different predators [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ different environments **OR** backgrounds **OR** rock colours may influence which colours provide effective camouflage [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n If **females prefer different** spot **colours**, then **sexual selection** may sometimes select for a new colour of spot ($\\checkmark$). Some spot colours may be **less visible** to different **predators**, so **natural selection** may select for different colours depending on what predator is present ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mean number of spots decreased until month 5 [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ between months 5 and 7, the mean number of spots stayed the same [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ during the first month mean number of spots did not change [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The mean number of **spots decreased** for the **first 5 months** ($\\checkmark$) then **stayed the same** between **months 5 and 7** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ individuals with one spot may have higher fitness than those with no spots or many spots [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ having one spot does not increase guppy visibility to predators much [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ although one spot may increase visibility of guppies to predators, the benefit of finding a mate outweighs the risk [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ individuals with alleles for more spots may have been removed by predation, reducing the frequency of those alleles in the gene pool [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ natural selection has acted on the population over time, favouring guppies with intermediate spotting [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Having **one spot** makes individual guppies **more fit** than having no spots or many spots ($\\checkmark$). Over time, individuals with **more spots** may have been **eaten**, so the **gene pool** now mostly includes **alleles** for **fewer spots** ($\\checkmark$). This means the **population trends** toward **intermediate spotting** because it's the **best trade-off** between **avoiding predators** and **attracting mates** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2799","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2799\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe transmission electron micrograph shows a mammalian lung mitochondrion.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2799.png\" alt=\"BI2799\">\n[Source: Adapted from Howard, L (n.d.). *Mitochondria,_mammalian_lung_-_TEM* [Photograph]. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 5 Sept. 2024 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Mitochondria,_mammalian_lung_-_TEM.jpg. Copyright Public Domain]\n\n1. Draw an annotated diagram of the mitochondrion to scale.                [4 marks]\n\n2. Calculate the magnification of this image.                            [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ drawing shape resembles the image, including overall outline and internal structures    [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ single lines, no sketched lines, no shading            [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ matrix correctly labelled **AND** site of Krebs cycle **OR** contains enzymes for Krebs cycle **OR** metabolic reactions **OR** provides medium for substrate-enzymes interaction **OR** any other suitable function of matrix [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ crista correctly labelled **AND** increases surface area for electron transport chain **OR** ATP synthases **OR** oxidative phosphorylation **OR** site of ATP production  [1 mark]\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/Ans.png\" alt=\"Ans\">\n\n2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ measured length of scale bar divided by 50 nm for magnification calculation **OR** $\\dfrac{7,000,000}{\\ce{50 nm}}$ (accept image line length in range 6,000,000 - 8,000,000 nm)    [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 140,000 $\\times$ (accept answers in range 120,000 - 160,000 x)        [1 mark]\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Length of scale bar in image = 7 mm \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 1mm = 1,000,000 nm \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 7mm = 7,000,000 nm\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Actual length scale bar represents = 50 nm\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Magnification  = $\\dfrac{\\mathbf{7,000,000}}{\\mathbf{50\\ nm}}$ ($\\checkmark$) = **140,000** $\\times$ ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"BI2835","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2835\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n| | | |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| **Rose of Sharon** ***(Hibiscus syriacus)*** $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2835i.png\" alt=\"BI2835i\">$\\\\$ | **Singapore Graveyard Flower** ***(Plumeria obtusa)*** $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2835ii.png\" alt=\"BI2835ii\">$\\\\$ | **Purple Loosestrife** ***(Lythrum salicaria)*** $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2835iii.png\" alt=\"BI2835iii\">$\\\\$ |\n| **Daffodil** ***(Narcissus pseudonarcissus)*** $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2835iv.png\" alt=\"BI2835iv\"> $\\\\$ | **Primrose** ***(Primula vulgaris)*** $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2835v.png\" alt=\"BI2835v\"> $\\\\$ | **Balkan Anemone** ***(Anemone blanda)*** $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2835vi.png\" alt=\"BI2835vi\"> $\\\\$ |\n| **Rose** ***(Rosa hybrid)*** $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2835vii.png\" alt=\"BI2835vii\"> $\\\\$ | **Sunflower** ***(Helianthus annuus)*** $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2835viii.png\" alt=\"BI2835viii\"> $\\\\$ | **Garden Peony** ***(Paeonia lactiflora)*** $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2835ix.png\" alt=\"BI2835ix\"> $\\\\$\n\n1. State **two** qualities that make a characteristic suitable for use as the divisions of a dichotomous key. [2 marks]\n\n2. Evaluate the use of flower colour in the construction of a dichotomous key. [3 marks]\n\n3. Construct a dichotomous key for four of the flowers in the diagram. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ clearly observable [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ binary **OR** providing a clear either/or choice **OR** two mutually exclusive options [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the characteristic should be consistently present in the organism it aims to group and consistently absent in the alternative group [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the two states of the characteristic should not be overlapping **OR** ambiguous [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the characteristic used should be present **OR** interpretable across all items being identified by the key [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n A good characteristic for a dichotomous key should be **clearly visible** ($\\checkmark$) so that it can be easily observed in all organisms being identified. It should also offer a clear **either/or option**, such as presence or absence, so the user can make a **definitive choice** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. **three** of the following (*must include at least **one** advantage and **one** disadvantage to gain full marks*):\n\n ***advantages***\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ease of observation of colour [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ there are a wide variety of flower colours, increasing the possibility of differentiating between species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can be a genetically fixed trait in some species [1 mark]\n\n ***disadvantages***\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ subjective perception of colour [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ variability within a species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ environmental influence [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ image limitations (black and white) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ colour change with flower developmental stage [1 mark]\n\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Using colour for a dichotomous key has advantages and disadvantages. On the positive side, colour is **easy to observe** ($\\checkmark$) and there is a **wide variety of colours** so that it is easy to distinguish between species ($\\checkmark$). Colour can also be a disadvantage because different people may describe or **interpret the same colour differently** ($\\checkmark$), leading to inconsistent results. Also, **flower colour can vary** within a single plant ($\\checkmark$), and the colour of a single flower can change as it grows.\n\n3. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ use of appropriate observable characteristics **OR** each step uses clear binary, and non-overlapping characteristics [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ structure of the key is logical and dichotomous **OR** the key follows a binary format at each step [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ accurate identification and separation of all four flowers [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ clear formatting and correct labelling of the key **OR** steps are numbered or lettered correctly and lead logically to species names [1 mark]\n\n *students may use any observable traits visible in the diagram*\n\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n **1a**. Flower head with a prominent central disk surrounded by many petals— **Sunflower**\\\n **1b**. ($\\checkmark$) Flower without a prominent central disk… **go to 2** ($\\checkmark$)\\\n 2a. Flower with petals fused into a distinct central trumpet or funnel shape… **Daffodil**\\\n 2b Flower without petals fused into a distinct central trumpet or funnel shape… go to 3\\\n 3a Numerous and overlapping petals… **Garden Peony**\\\n 3b Flowers clustered together on the stem… **Purple Loosestrife** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Additional Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2835cms.png\" alt=\"BI2835cms\">"},{"ref":"BI2836","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2836\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nA student investigates the effect of exercise on cardiovascular function by measuring a volunteer’s blood pressure and heart rate before, during, and after cycling on a stationary bike.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2836a.png\" alt=\"BI2836a\">\n\n1. Deduce the time at which post-workout recovery appears to begin. Use evidence from the graph to support your answer. [2 marks]\n\n2. Compare and contrast the changes in systolic and diastolic pressure shown in this graph. [2 marks]\n\n3. Identify one reason for this change in systolic pressure during exercise. [1 mark]\n\n4. Heart rate can be measured by manually taking a pulse. State the name of one of the arteries which is usually used for measuring the pulse rate [1 mark]\n\n5. Explain how the valves in the heart are opened and closed during the cardiac cycle. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. **both** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 10-15 minutes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ heart rate **OR** diastolic pressure is elevated until that time and then begins to fall, indicating the exercise has stopped [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The volunteer exercised starting at 0 minutes and continued until **10 minutes** ($\\checkmark$) because the heart rate and pressure **increased** until 10 minutes and **then began to decrease**, which means they stopped exercising ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **two** of the following (*must include **one** similarity and **one** difference to gain full marks*):\n\n ***Similarities***:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** pressures increase during exercise [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** pressures start **AND** finish at baseline levels before exercise [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** pressures peak at the end of exercise [1 mark]\n\n ***Differences***:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ systolic pressure shows a large increase **OR** increases significantly **OR** increases by 60 mmHg **OR** by 50% **OR** by half **WHEREAS** diastolic pressure remains relatively constant **OR** increases slightly **OR** increases by 10 mmHg **OR** by 1/8 [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ systolic pressure starts at a higher pressure THAN diastolic pressure **OR** systolic pressure baseline is ~120 mmHg W**WHEREAS** diastolic pressure baseline is ~80 mmHg [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Both** systolic and diastolic pressure **increase during exercise**, which shows that the cardiovascular system responds to the increased demand for oxygen ($\\checkmark$). However, **systolic** pressure shows a **large increase** of about **60 mmHg**, whereas **diastolic** pressure **increases** only **slightly** by about **10 mmHg** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increase demand for oxygen [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increase cardiac output [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increased stroke volume **OR** heart rate [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ more blood is ejected into arteries during systole **OR** systolic pressure is caused by ventricular contractions [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The systolic pressure increases during exercise because the **heart pumps faster** and with a **higher volume** ($\\checkmark$) of blood with each pump to deliver more oxygen to muscles.\n\n4. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carotid [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ radial [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n One of the arteries used for measuring pulse rate is the **carotid** artery ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ during atrial systole **OR** when atria contract pressure increase causing atrioventricular **OR** AV **OR** tricuspid **OR** bicuspid valves to open [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ during ventricular systole OR when ventricles contract, pressure increases, forcing atrioventricular **OR** AV **OR** tricuspid **OR** bicuspid valves shut [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ during ventricular systole **OR** when ventricles contract **OR** as ventricular pressure increases further, semilunar valves open [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ during diastole, **OR** as ventricles relax, pressure decreases, closing semilunar valves [1 mark]\n\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n During **atrial** systole, the **atria contract**, **increasing** the **pressure** in them, which **forces** the **atrioventricular valves open** ($\\checkmark$). As the blood passes into the ventricles, the atria relax and the **ventricles contract, increasing** the **pressure** inside them. This increase in pressure inside the ventricles forces the **atrioventricular valves shut** ($\\checkmark$) and the **semilunar valves** in the pulmonary artery and aorta **open** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2837","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2837\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n| |  | |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| **Siberian Musk Deer** $\\\\$<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2837i.jpg\" alt=\"BI2837i\">  $\\\\$ [Source: Porte musc Profil 2.jpg. (2024, August 1). *Wikimedia Commons*. Retrieved 09:30, May 15, 2025 from https://commons. wikimedia.org/w/index .php?title=File: Porte_musc_Profil_2.jpg&oldid=905679436. CC BY] | **Red Howler Monkey** $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2837ii.jpg\" alt=\"BI2837ii\">  $\\\\$ [Source: *Alouatta seniculus* 5per!spective.jpg. (2024, August 10). *Wikimedia Commons*. Retrieved 09:32, May 15, 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=File: Alouatta_seniculus_5perspective.jpg&oldid=908225132. CC BY] | **Green Monkey** $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2837iii.jpg\" alt=\"BI2837iii\"> $\\\\$ [Source: *Chlorocebus Global*.jpg. (2024, August 10). *Wikimedia Commons*. Retrieved 09:39, May 15, 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=File: Chlorocebus_Global.jpg&oldid=908309375. CC BY] |\n| **Warthog** $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2837iv.jpg\" alt=\"BI2837iv\"> $\\\\$ [Source: Profil 8.jpg. (2021, August 22). *Wikimedia Commons*. Retrieved 09:38, May 15, 2025 from https://commons. wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=File: Profil_8.jpg&oldid=584169375. CC BY] | **Coyote** $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2837v.png\" alt=\"BI2837v\"> $\\\\$ [Source: Coyote Skull 2.png. (2024, August 13). *Wikimedia Commons*. Retrieved 04:29, May 16, 2025 from https://commons.wiki media.org /w/index.php?title=File: Coyote_Skull_2.png&oldid=909107309. CC BY] | **Domestic Cat** $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2837vi.jpg\" alt=\"BI2837vi\"> $\\\\$ [Source: Carnivora - *Vulpes vulpes, Felis domesticus, Martes martes* (skulls).jpg. (2024, February 4). *Wikimedia Commons*. Retrieved 04:36, May 16, 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=File: Carnivora_-_Vulpes_vulpes, _Felis_domesticus, _Martes_martes_(skulls).jpg&oldid=848861083. CC BY] |\n\nScientists can infer the dietary habits of animals by examining their skull structure and dentition.  The images show six different mammal species.\n\n1. Suggest what the differences in incisor shape between the Siberian Musk Deer and the Coyote indicate about their diets.     [2 marks]\n\n2. Other than incisors, describe **one** observable feature in the dentition of the Domestic Cat that suggests a carnivorous diet.       [2 marks]\n\n3. Explain a reason for the differing eye position in the skull of the Domestic Cat and the Siberian Musk Deer.         [1 mark]\n\n4. The Siberian Musk Deer, Green Monkey, and Warthog all possess prominent canine teeth or tusks largely unrelated to diet.  Suggest two possible functions of these canines/tusks which are unrelated to diet.       [2 marks]\n\n5. Besides dentition, describe **one** other source of evidence that palaeontologists can use to reconstruct the diets of extinct animals.         [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. **both** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the deer has broad, sloping incisors **WHILE** the coyote has smaller conical **OR** sharper incisors        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the deer incisors are more suitable for a herbivorous diet **OR** shearing vegetation WHILE the coyote incisors are adapted for grasping **OR** tearing small items, suggesting a carnivorous diet       [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **deer** has incisors that are **broad** and **slope outwards**, while the **coyote's** incisors are **small** and **sharp** ($\\checkmark$).  The **coyote's** incisors make it more adapted to **grabbing, tearing, and eating meat**, while the deer's incisors help the **deer shear vegetation** to eat it ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large, pointed canines for incapacitating prey **OR** piercing **OR** killing prey        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sharp premolars **OR** molars for tearing meat      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reduced **OR** absent flat grinding surfaces **OR** molars; not needed for carnivorous diet     [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The cat's **sharp molars** are adapted for **tearing** and **chewing** raw **meat** ($\\checkmark$) while that would not be possible with **flat molars** that are more suitable for **grinding vegetation** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Domestic Cats are predators, so their eyes are positioned forward, providing depth perception and accuracy of distance, which is important for acting on prey **OR** forward-facing eyes (cat) increase overlap of the visual fields, improving focus on a single target     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  The Siberian Musk Deer is prey, and has side-facing eyes giving them a wide field of view, which is important in detecting predators **OR** side-facing eyes increases peripheral vision, helping them escape threats      [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The Siberian Musk Deer are **prey animals**, so their eyes are on the **side** of the skull, allowing them to **detect predators** ($\\checkmark$).\nThe Domestic Cat is a **predator**, so their eyes are positioned on the **front**, giving them **binocular vision**, which is important for **stalking prey**\n ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fighting **OR** dominance **OR** aggression **OR** defence **OR** intraspecific competition [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sexual selection **OR** mating displays    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ digging **OR** foraging for food      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ territorial displays     [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Canines can be used in **fighting** against other members of the species for **dominance** ($\\checkmark$) or for a **mate** ($\\checkmark$).  The warthog can also use its tusks and canines for **digging**.\n\n5. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fossilised stomach contents **OR** faeces    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ teeth wear patterns **OR** tooth microstructure      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ skeletal structure      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ analysis of nearby fossils      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ isotopic **OR** chemical analysis of teeth enamel  [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    To determine what a fossilised species may have eaten, scientists can look at **fossils** of the **stomach contents** or faeces that are found nearby ($\\checkmark$).\n\n:"},{"ref":"BI2886","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2886\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA student extracted photosynthetic pigments from hibiscus leaves and separated them using thin layer chromatography. Their results are shown in the diagram, and the table gives the Rf values, along with the colours, of some of the pigments.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2886ai.png\" alt=\"BI2886ai\">\n\n| | Pigment | Rf value | Colour\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$ | β-carotene | 0.94 | yellow-orange |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$ | Chlorophyll a | 0.68 | blue-green |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$ | Chlorophyll b | 0.47 | green |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$ | Xanthophylls | 0.32 - 0.38 | yellow|\n\n1. 1. Calculate the Rf value of spot Y, giving your answer to significant figures. Show your working. [2 marks]\n\n 2. Identify pigment Y. [1 mark]\n\n2. The solvent used in the experiment was non-polar. Identify the spot that is most polar, and give a reason for your choice. [1 mark]\n\nThe graph shows an action spectrum for photosynthetic pigments.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2886bi.png\" alt=\"BI2886bi\">\n\n3. From the graph, identify the wavelength of light at which the rate of photosynthesis is lowest. [1 mark]\n\n4. Outline the reason for this low photosynthesis rate. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1.\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Rf = distance travelled by pigment ÷ distance travelled by solvent **OR** any appropriate numbers if in correct ratio [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 0.46 (*allow in range 0.45 - 0.47*) [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2886ams.png\" alt=\"BI2886ams\">\n\n **Rf = distance travelled by pigment ÷ distance travelled by solvent** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n = 55 mm ÷ 118.5 m\n = **0.46** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n 2.\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chlorophyll b [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n Spot Y is **chlorophyll b** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Z **BECAUSE** it is least soluble in non-polar solvent so moves the slowest **OR** interacts most strongly with the stationary phase so moves least [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n Pigment **Z** is likely to be the most polar as it **moves the least distance**, indicating that, out of all the pigments, it **interacts** the **most strongly** with the **stationary phase** (√)\n\n3. *answer must include **units** to gain mark*:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 530 nm (*allow answers in range of 525 - 540*) [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Practical-based%20Questions/images/BI2886cms.png\" alt=\"BI2886cms\">\n\n 100nm = 40mm\n\n 1mm = 100nm/40\n\n 1mm = 2.5nm\n\n 11mm = 27.5nm\n\n 500 + 27.5 = 530nm (to 2 s.f.)\n\n The wavelength at which the rate of photosynthesis is lowest is **530 nm** (√).\n\n4. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ less absorption of this wavelength of light **OR** greater reflection of this wavelength of light by photosynthetic pigments **OR** this is green light which is reflected by the photosynthetic pigments [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ less light energy is available for photolysis at this wavelength [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ less **OR** insufficient light energy to excite electrons in chlorophyll ) [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n This wavelength of light is **reflected** by the photosynthetic pigments **more than other wavelengths** (√) meaning there is **less light energy** available to **excite** the **electrons** in **chlorophyll a** (√)."}]},{"name":"Unassigned","questions":[{"ref":"BI0286","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0286\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Describe how the enteric nervous system ensures the coordinated movement of food through the digestive tract. [5 marks]\n\n2. Explain how emergent properties arise from the integration of subsystems in a multicellular organism.                [4 marks]\n\n3. Compare and contrast how the nervous system controls conscious and reflex responses. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ controls involuntary peristalsis from mid **OR** lower oesophagus to rectum  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ peristalsis is the wave-like contraction of circular and longitudinal muscles in the gut wall                     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ coordinates contraction and relaxation of smooth muscle of gut walls [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contraction of circular muscles behind bolus of food to push food along **OR** stops food moving backwards  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contraction of longitudinal muscles in front of a bolus of food shortens gut, moving food along [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ acts independently of central nervous system **OR** CNS **OR** is influenced by autonomic nervous system **OR** ANS     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ modulates peristalsis speed based on food composition and digestive needs [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ responds to stretch receptors in the gut walls [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nThe enteric nervous system (ENS) controls **involuntary peristalsis** from the **lower oesophagus** to the **rectum** ($\\checkmark$) . It **coordinates** the **contraction** of **circular muscles behind** the **food** to **prevent backflow** ($\\checkmark$) and **longitudinal muscles ahead** of it, pushing the **food forward** ($\\checkmark$) . Neural **signals** between different **regions** of the **gut** ensure that this **movement** is **synchronised** throughout the digestive tract ($\\checkmark$) . This coordination ensures that food moves in **one direction** and at a **speed** that allows for proper **digestion** and **absorption** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ emergent properties are characteristics **OR** abilities that arise from the interaction of parts within a system                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in multicellular organisms, cells are organised into tissues, tissues into organs, and organs into systems    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ each higher level has properties that emerge from the interactions within the levels below    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the organisms systems must communicate and coordinate to perform higher-level functions **OR** Integration between systems enables functions that individual systems cannot perform alone                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ emergent properties depend on timing **OR** coordination between systems     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ macromolecular **OR** genetic interactions within cells contribute to emergent properties at the cellular level        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ whole organism relies on coordination between all levels of biological organisation     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ failure of integration at any level disrupts the organism's function         [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nEmergent properties are **characteristics** that **arise** from the **interaction** of **parts** in a system ($\\checkmark$). In **multicellular organisms**, **cells** are organised into **tissues**, which form **organs**, and organs into **systems** ($\\checkmark$). Each level in this hierarchy has **new properties** that are **not found** at the **lower** levels ($\\checkmark$). **Failure** of integration at **any level disrupts** the organism's **function**, leading to disease or dysfunction ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following (at least **two** rsimilarities and **two** differences are needed to achieve full marks)\n\n    ***Similarities***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** involve sensory neurons conveying input from receptors to the central nervous system, **OR** CNS    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** result in a response via motor neurons, either as muscle contraction **OR** glandular secretion    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** rely on synaptic transmission **OR** neurotransmitter release    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can involve the spinal cord as part of the neural pathway        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are coordinated by the nervous system to produce appropriate responses to stimuli     [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ conscious responses are always controlled by the brain **OR** cerebral hemispheres, **WHEREAS** reflex responses are often integrated in the spinal cord **OR** do not always pass through the brain        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ conscious responses involve voluntary control **WHEREAS** reflex responses are involuntary **OR** automatic [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reflex responses are faster because they involve fewer synapses **WHEREAS** conscious responses are slower due to more complex processing    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reflex responses cannot be consciously initiated **WHEREAS** conscious responses require awareness **OR** decision-making    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ conscious responses can be modified by learning **OR** memory **WHEREAS** reflex responses are usually fixed **OR** innate        [1 mark]\n\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    **Both** conscious and reflex responses **begin with sensory neurons** detecting a stimulus and sending **signals** to the **central nervous system (CNS)** ($\\checkmark$). In **both** cases, **motor neurons** carry **signals to effectors**, which may be **muscles or glands** ($\\checkmark$). **Synapses and neurotransmitters** are involved in **transmitting impulses** between **neurons** in both types of response ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    However, **reflex** responses are usually **integrated** in the **spinal cord**, while **conscious** responses are processed in the **cerebral hemispheres** ($\\checkmark$). **Reflexes** are **involuntary** and occur **automatically**, whereas **conscious** responses involve **voluntary** control ($\\checkmark$). **Reflexes** are typically **faster** because they involve **fewer neurons** and **synapses**, while **conscious** responses are **slower** due to more complex **processing** ($\\checkmark$). **Conscious** responses can also be **modified by learning** or past **experience**, but **reflexes** are generally **fixed** and not influenced by memory ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0312","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0312\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the methods of HIV transmission and its effects on the immune system.     [3 marks]\n\n2.    Discuss the need for international cooperation to fight contagious diseases such as Ebola. [5 marks]\n\n3.    Antibiotics are an essential tool of modern medicine. Outline the strengths and limitations of antibiotics. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following (both the method of transmission and effects on the immune system are required for full marks):\n\n    *Method of transmission:*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ unprotected sexual intercourse **OR** sharing needles **OR** mother to child **OR** breast feeding    [1 mark]\n\n    *Effects of HIV on the immune system:*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ HIV targets helper T cells **OR** CD4 cells      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reduced number of helper T cells **OR** CD4 cells     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reduced ability to fight infection **OR** disease     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  (without treatment) can progress to acquired immunodeficiency syndrome [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    HIV can be transmitted through **unprotected sex** with infected person ($\\checkmark$). HIV targets cells of the immune system, specifically **helper T cells** ($\\checkmark$), which possess CD4 receptors. The virus **reduces** the **number of helper T cells** ($\\checkmark$) which help fight infection and thus a decrease in their number makes a person more **prone to infection** ($\\checkmark$). Without the correct treatment, HIV infection can progress to **AIDS** ($\\checkmark$), which is a result of the immune system becoming severely compromised.\n\n2.    any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contagious diseases can cross borders     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ international cooperation is needed to prevent the spread **OR** trace transmission    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to share knowledge and expertise [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ medicines need to be shared [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mass vaccination programs are needed to prevent spread [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mass vaccination programs are needed to protect the populations of different countries [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ monitoring and surveillance is needed to identify sources of infection    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ developed countries may need to give financial and medical support to developing countries [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nContagious diseases can move from one population to another and **across borders** ($\\checkmark$). Countries need to **share knowledge and expertise** to work together to prevent the spread of disease ($\\checkmark$). **Medicines and resources** need to be shared ($\\checkmark$), particularly from **developed to developing countries** ($\\checkmark$), to help to reduce and prevent transmission . Countries can also work together to **monitor the spread of disease and identify sources** ($\\checkmark$) of infection. Mass **vaccination** programs are needed **to protect populations** ($\\checkmark$) and need to happen in more than one country.\n\n3.    any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibiotics are chemicals that disrupt the metabolism of bacteria     [1 mark]\n\n    *Strengths*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can be used to treat bacterial infections     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can be applied using a variety of methods **OR** orally, topically and intravenously [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a number of antibiotics exist **OR** different antibiotics can be used to treat different infections [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibiotics do not harm eukaryotic cells **OR** body cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibiotics are fast acting [1 mark]\n\n    *Limitations*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibiotics are not effective against viruses [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibiotics can have side effects **OR** cause allergic reactions [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ misuse of antibiotics has led to increased antibiotic resistance **OR** antibiotic resistance is increasing     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ over prescription of antibiotics **OR** use of antibiotics in animal feed to increase muscle mass **OR** individuals not completing the course of antibiotics increases the prevalence of antibiotic resistance [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibiotic resistant bacterial strains cause illnesses that cannot be treated with current antibiotics [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ examples of antibiotic-resistant bacterial diseases are MRSA (methicillin-resistant *Staphylococcus aureus*) **OR** multi-drug resistant tuberculosis **OR** any other verifiable example [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ limited number of antibiotics to fight bacterial infections [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nAntibiotics are chemicals, which are produced by some species of fungi, that have the ability to disrupt the **metabolism** of bacteria ($\\checkmark$) and because of this they are used to treat **bacterial infections** ($\\checkmark$). The use of antibiotics has many benefits, but also some limitations.\n\n    *Benefits of Antibiotics*\n\n    Antibiotics are available in a **variety of forms**, such as oral pills, topical creams and intravenous injections ($\\checkmark$). They **work quickly** ($\\checkmark$) and despite being harmful to bacteria, they do not affect **eukaryotic cells** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    *Limitations of Antibiotics*\n\n    The use of antibiotics is restricted to treating bacterial infections as they have no effect on **viral** infections ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, the **overuse** of antibiotics in contexts such as agriculture, has led to development of antibiotic resistance ($\\checkmark$). This is when strains of bacteria become resistant to antibiotics, which renders them ineffective at treating the infection. Due to a **limited number** of antibiotics being currently available antibiotic resistant strains ($\\checkmark$), which are resistant to multiple antibiotics, such as **MRSA** are becoming a global health concern ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0337","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0337\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast the structure and function of free and bound ribosomes. [3 marks]\n\n2. Describe the steps in the initiation of translation. [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain how the bonds between amino acids contribute to the structure of proteins. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following (answer can be in a table format):\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ **BOTH** free and bound ribosomes synthesise proteins [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ **BOTH** free and bound ribosomes consist of small and big subunits, **OR** both have A P E and mRNA binding sites [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ free ribosomes are found in the cytoplasm **WHILE** bound ribosomes are attached to the endoplasmic reticulum **OR** ER [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ free ribosomes synthesise proteins for use within the cell unattached to any structures, **BUT** bound ribosomes synthesise proteins for secretion **OR** use in the lysosomes **OR** membrane-bound proteins [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Both free and bound ribosomes **synthesise proteins** ($\\checkmark$). However, free ribosomes are found floating in the **cytoplasm** , and bound ribosomes are attached to the **endoplasmic reticulum** ($\\checkmark$). Free ribosomes synthesise proteins for use **within the cell** , but bound ribosomes synthesise proteins for **secretion** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ mRNA molecule binds to the small ribosomal subunit [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ initiator tRNA (carrying methionine) binds to the start codon [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ mRNA/tRNA **OR** codon/anticodon binding is facilitated by complementary base pairing [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ large ribosomal subunit binds to the initiator tRNA at the P site [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ amino acid carrying tRNA attaches to the next codon at the A site [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ peptide bond is formed between amino acids in the P and A site [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ ribosome moves a codon space on the mRNA, so that tRNA at start codon is now at E site [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Translation occurs in the cytoplasm. During the initiation stage, the mRNA molecule binds to the **small ribosomal subunit** ($\\checkmark$). Next, the **initiator tRNA** binds to the start codon ($\\checkmark$) on the mRNA, and finally, the **large ribosomal subunit** binds to the tRNA through the **P site** ($\\checkmark$) in the ribosome and then the next codon signals another tRNA to bind in the **A site** ($\\checkmark$), using complementary base pairing.  A **peptide bond** ($\\checkmark$) is formed between the amino acids carried by tRNAs in the P and A sites.\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ primary structure of a protein is the sequence of amino acids [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ peptide bonds link amino acids in the polypeptide chain [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ secondary structure is the formation of alpha helices **OR** beta pleated sheets within the same polypeptide [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ (secondary structure is) the result of hydrogen bonding **OR** hydrogen bonds form between the carboxyl and amino group **OR** C=O and N-H [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ tertiary structure is caused by interactions between R groups of amino acids [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ (in tertiary structure) positively charged R groups interact with negative charged R groups [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ (in tertiary structure) polar R groups will form hydrogen bonds with other polar R groups [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ (in tertiary structure) disulphide, **OR** S-S, bridges can form between cysteines in R groups [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ (tertiary structure) gives the polypeptide its 3D shape [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ quaternary structure is formed in proteins with more than one polypeptide chain [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ (quaternary structure is) formed by the interactions between R groups on different polypeptide chains [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ (quaternary structure is) supported by S-S bridges, **OR** any other examples of R group bounds in the previous marking statements  [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    The primary structure of a protein is the **sequence** of amino acids ($\\checkmark$), which are linked together with **peptide bonds** ($\\checkmark$). The secondary structure of a protein is the formation of **alpha-helices** ($\\checkmark$), which are stabilised by **hydrogen bonding** between the carboxyl and amino groups ($\\checkmark$) from peptide bonds between different amino acids in the same chain. The tertiary structure is caused by interactions between **R groups** ($\\checkmark$), which gives the polypeptide its **3D shape** ($\\checkmark$). Some of the bonds formed between R groups include **polar R groups**  forming hydrogen bonds with other polar R groups ($\\checkmark$) and cysteines in R groups, forming **disulphide bridges** ($\\checkmark$). The quaternary structure of proteins describes proteins with **more than one** polypeptide chain ($\\checkmark$) in their structure. The quaternary structure is supported by the same type of R-R group interactions as the tertiary level, such as **disulphide bridges** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0389","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0389\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Distinguish between a myofibril, the sarcoplasmic reticulum and the sarcolemma.         [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline the proteins involved in muscle contraction.     [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe the process of muscle contraction. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following are correctly shown in a labelled diagram:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myofibrils are the basic unit inside the muscle fibres **OR** they are composed of  end-to-end sarcomeres **OR** they are long contractile fibres **OR** they are bundles of protein filaments, mainly actin and myosin   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the sarcolemma is the plasma membrane of the muscle fibres **OR** cell, **OR** it envelops the muscle fibre          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the sarcoplasmic reticulum is specialized endoplasmic reticulum that stores calcium **OR** releases calcium ions when stimulated by a nerve impulse **OR** action potential  [1 mark]\n[3 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Myofibrils, sarcolemma and sarcoplasmic reticulum are all found in muscle fibres. **Myofibrils** are the basic units of muscle fibres; they are made of **repeating sarcomeres** laid end-to-end and are responsible for muscle contraction ($\\checkmark$). The **sarcolemma** is the membrane that surrounds each **muscle fibre**, similar to the **plasma membrane** in any other cell ($\\checkmark$). The **sarcoplasmic** reticulum is a **specialized endoplasmic reticulum**. When a nerve impulse reaches the muscle fibre, depolarisation spreads to the sarcoplasmic reticulum. This activates voltage-sensitive calcium channels in the sarcoplasmic reticulum causing them to **release calcium ions** ($\\checkmark$) from the sarcoplasmic reticulum storage. This initiates the process of muscle contraction.\n\n2. any **five** of the following (answer can be in a table format):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ five types of protein involved in muscle contration - myosin, actin, troponin, tropomyosin and titin    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tropomyosin binds to actin    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ troponin moves tropomyosin from the binding sites, in presence of **OR** when it binds to, calcium ions      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ actin makes up thin filaments [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myosin makes up thick filaments    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myosin heads attach to the binding sites on actin **OR** forms cross-bridges with actin      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myosin heads rotate to pull the actin in **OR** carries out power stroke, shortening the sarcomere **OR** bringing about muscle contraction    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myosin and actin filaments, **OR** thin and thick filaments, slide past each other to cause muscle contraction     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ titin stabilises the sarcomere structure by anchoring myosin to the Z-lines    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ titin provides elasticity **OR** recoil, helping the sarcomere return to its resting length after contraction    [1 mark]\n[5 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    There are **four types of protein** involved in the muscle contraction: **troponin, tropomyosin, myosin and actin** ($\\checkmark$). They all together make up a sarcomere unit that is part of a muscle fiber. **Actin**, troponin and tropomyosin make up the **thin filaments** ($\\checkmark$) and **myosin** makes up the **thick filaments** in the sarcomere ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    **Tropomyosin is bound to the actin** ($\\checkmark$) and troponin and when the muscle is relaxed, troponin covers myosin binding sites on actin. When calcium ions are released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, **troponin moves the tropomyosin** from the actin, exposing myosin binding sites ($\\checkmark$). When the **binding sites** are exposed **myosin heads** attach to actin and **rotate to pull the actin in** ($\\checkmark$). This **slides thick filaments along thin filaments** ($\\checkmark$) and cases the **sarcomere to shorten** ($\\checkmark$) and muscle to contract.\n\n    **Titin stabilises** the sarcomere structure by **anchoring** the **myosin** filaments to the **Z-line**, **preventing overextension** during contraction ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sarcomeres are the repeating functional units of myofibrils   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sarcomeres can shorten       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a nerve impulse reaches the myofibril bringing about an action potential in the sarcolemma **OR** action potential passes down T-tubules of sarcolemma [1 mark]\n\n     $\\ce{ -> }$ calcium ions are released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ calcium ions bind to troponin  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ calcium attachment to troponin causes a movement of tropomyosin exposing the binding sites on actin     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myosin heads attach to the binding sites on actin **OR** cross bridges form between actin and myosin   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myosin heads release ADP, and rotate **OR** carry out power stroke, pulling the actin filament inwards     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP attaches to the myosin head and breaks the cross-bridge [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrolysis of ATP moves the myosin head into the cocked **OR** ready position [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ repeat of the process shortens the sarcomere [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the Z lines are pulled closer together [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ shortening of the sarcomeres in a myofibril causes muscle contraction     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ titin stabilises the sarcomere structure, anchoring myosin to the Z-line [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ titin provides elasticity **OR** recoil **OR** helps sarcomere return to its resting length after contraction [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    The structure of sarcomeres allows them to **shorten, which causes muscle contraction** ($\\checkmark$). When a nerve impulse reaches the sarcoplasmic reticulum of myofibril, **calcium ions** are released ($\\checkmark$). **Calcium ions bind** to a protein called **troponin** ($\\checkmark$) in the sarcomere. **Troponin moves** another protein called **tropomyosin** away from the binding sites on actin ($\\checkmark$). **Myosin heads** are in the **cocked position** and contain stored energy from **ATP hydrolysis** ($\\checkmark$). They bind to the **binding sites on actin** to form a **cross bridge** ($\\checkmark$). The stored energy and **ADP are released** ($\\checkmark$). When they are released, the **myosin heads move inwards** (“power stroke”) ($\\checkmark$) and pull on the actin. **ATP attaches** to myosin heads and breaks the cross-bridge ($\\checkmark$). Hydrolysis of ATP moves the myosin heads into the cocked position. This process repeats until the calcium ions are no longer available. **Repeating** this process **shortens the sarcomeres** in a myofibril and causes **muscle contraction** ($\\checkmark$). **Titin**, a **structural protein**, **anchors** the **myosin filaments** to the **Z-lines**, **stabilising** the **sarcomere** during contraction ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0413","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0413\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nMembrane transport is important for allowing particles to move across the cell membrane and respond to changes in the chemical composition of the extracellular fluid.\n\n1. Outline the effects of placing a liver cell in a hypotonic solution.    [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline how the fluidity of membranes allows materials to be taken into cells or to be released from cells.  [6 marks]\n\n3. Compare and contrast four types of membrane transport. Include an example of each. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the liver cell swells **OR** enlarges **OR** expands **OR** increase in volume [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the liver cell ruptures **OR** burst **OR** lyses    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water diffuses into the liver cell **OR** water moves from the medium of lower solute concentration  to a medium where there is higher solute concentration  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ osmotic pressure favors the movement of water into the cell **OR** water will diffuse from a medium of lower osmotic pressure to higher osmotic pressure [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    Once a liver cell is placed in a hypotonic solution, **water** will diffuse from a **medium of lower solute concentration to a region of higher solute concentration** ($\\checkmark$) by osmosis. This results in **an influx of water into the liver cell** that **causes the cell to swell**($\\checkmark$) and eventually **rupture** ($\\checkmark$) depending on how steep the concentration gradient is.\n\n2. any **six** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the phospholipid composition of the cell membrane allows it to be in a fluid state [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the fluidity of the membrane allows membranes to change shape **OR** move [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the fluidity of the membrane allows the membrane to interact **OR** fuse with vesicles **OR** vesicles pinch off from  the membrane  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles are composed of phospholipids [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ during exocytosis, material to be exported by the cell is packaged in membrane-bound vesicles that fuse with the plasma membrane [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ during exocytosis, vesicles release their contents to the cell exterior and the membrane flattens out again [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ during endocytosis, materials to be imported into the cell is packaged in membrane-bound vesicles that pinch off  the plasma membrane and are transported to the cell interior     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ proteins in the membrane form vesicles using ATP [1 mark]\n\n    [6 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n    The cell membrane is a **phospholipid bilayer that is fluid** ($\\checkmark$). This allows **membranes to change shape** ($\\checkmark$) and to **form vesicles that pinch off of it or receive vesicles that fuse with it.** ($\\checkmark$)  These vesicles are involved in membrane transport known as endocytosis and exocytosis. **Exocytosis** is an active form of transport in which cells export material to the cell membrane or to the extracellular fluid via vesicles. These vesicles are transported to the cell surface where they **fuse with the plasma membrane and release their contents** to the extracellular fluid ($\\checkmark$). Vesicles fusing with the cell membrane help rebuild the cell membrane as they too are composed of **phospholipids**. ($\\checkmark$) In **endocytosis**, material entering the cell is engulfed by the cell membrane which then pinches inwards to form a vesicle surrounding the molecules. These vesicles are then **transported to the interior of the cell** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **ALL** four types of membrane transport move substances across a partially permeable membrane [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion **AND** osmosis are passive **OR** do not require ATP **WHILE** active transport uses energy **OR** requires ATP [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen diffuses across membranes by simple diffusion **OR** accept other valid examples [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the sodium potassium pump is an example of active transport and hydrolyzes ATP **OR** accept other valid examples [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ simple diffusion does not require membrane proteins to occur **WHILE** facilitated diffusion, osmosis, **AND** active transport all occur via membrane proteins **OR** facilitated diffusion requires protein channels or carrier proteins, osmosis sometimes uses aquaporins, **AND** active transport requires protein pumps    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the transport of chloride ions through channel proteins is an example of facilitated diffusion **OR** accept other valid examples [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the movement of water across a semipermeable membrane through aquaporins is an example of osmosis **OR** accept other valid examples [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion **AND** osmosis allow the diffusion of molecules down their concentration gradients **WHILE** active transport transports substances against their concentration gradient [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion **AND** osmosis allow the system to reach equilibrium **WHILE** active transport drives the system away from equilibrium [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ simple diffusion occurs with small polar particles **OR** hydrophobic molecules **OR** non-polar particles, **WHILE** facilitated diffusion occurs with larger or charged molecules, **WHEREAS** osmosis occurs with water molecules only **WHILE** active transport can transport any molecules [1 mark]\n\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    | Simple Diffusion | Facilitated Diffusion | Osmosis | Active Transport | $\\hspace{1cm}$ |\n    | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n    | passive | passive | passive | active | ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | does not require ATP hydrolysis | does not require ATP hydrolysis | does not require ATP hydrolysis | requires ATP hydrolysis | |\n    | does not require membrane proteins | requires membrane proteins | requires membrane proteins | requires membrane proteins | ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | transports substances down their concentration gradient | transports substances down their concentration gradient | transports water down its concentration gradient | transports substances up their concentration gradient | ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | applies to small polar, or small nonpolar, or hydrophobic molecules | applies to larger or charged molecules | applies to water molecules only | applies to any molecules  | ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | system eventually reaches equilibrium | system eventually reaches equilibrium | system eventually reaches equilibrium | does not allow the system to reach equilibrium | ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | Example: diffusion of **O$_2$** | Example: Diffusion of **Cl$^{-}$ ions** through **Cl$^{-}$ channels** | Example: diffusion of water through **aquaporins** in the kidneys| Example: pumping of **Na$^{+}$ and K$^{+}$ ions** through the **Na$^{+}$/K$^{+}$ pump** | ($\\checkmark$) |"},{"ref":"BI0611","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0611\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Distinguish between eukaryotic and prokaryotic transcription. [3 marks]\n\n2. Describe the roles of non-coding DNA sequences.  [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe the role of DNA-binding proteins in gene expression. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1.    any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in prokaryotic cells transcription and translation of a gene can happen simultaneously, **WHEREAS** in eukaryotes they occur separately [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in eukaryotic cells there are post-transcriptional modifications, **OR** addition of 5\\' cap and poly-A tail, **OR** RNA splicing, **OR** removal of introns and joining of exons, **BUT** in prokaryotes, there are no post-transcriptional modifications [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in eukaryotic cells transcription takes place in the nucleus, **BUT** in prokaryotes, transcription takes place in the cytoplasm [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in eukaryotic cells, mature mRNA is produced from pre-mRNA **WHEREAS** in prokaryotes, mRNA is formed immediately through the transcription of the DNA strand [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nA difference between transcription in eukaryotes and prokaryotes is that transcription takes place in the **nucleus** for eukaryotes, whereas, in prokaryotes, transcription takes place in the **cytoplasm** ($\\checkmark$). **Prokaryotic** cells can also transcribe and translate a gene at the **same time**, whereas in **eukaryotes**, these two processes are **separated in time and place** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    Another difference is that in eukaryotes, there are **post-transcriptional modifications** such as RNA splicing, where introns are removed and exons are joined, while in prokaryotes, there are **no post-transcriptional modifications** ($\\checkmark$) due to the absence of introns.\n\n2.    any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ do not code for polypeptides [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA polymerase binds to promoters to initiate transcription **OR** promoters are where transcription is initiated     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ proteins **OR** activators bind to enhancers to increase transcription [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ proteins, **OR** repressors bind to silencers to prevent transcription [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ promoter-proximal elements regulate transcription [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ introns allow for alternative mRNA splicing [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ telomeres protect the ends of chromosomes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some DNA sequences code for tRNA and rRNA [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some functions are still unknown **OR** accept any other valid example of non-coding DNA and its function [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nNon-coding DNA sequences **do not code for polypeptides** ($\\checkmark$). For example the promoter is a section of DNA **where RNA polymerase binds** to initiate transcription ($\\checkmark$). Enhancers **increase transcription** ($\\checkmark$) when proteins bind to them and silencers **prevent transcription** ($\\checkmark$) when proteins bind to them. There are also genes that **code for tRNA and rRNA** ($\\checkmark$) as the final product.\n\n3.    any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transcription factors are proteins that bind to DNA sequences to regulate transcription [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ activator proteins bind to enhancer sequences [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  (this) increases the rate of transcription    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ repressor proteins bind to silencer sequences [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  (this) decreases the rate of transcription [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ proteins bind to promoter-proximal elements [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  (this is) to modulate **OR** regulate the transcription of genes     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA polymerase binds to the promoter [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  (this) initiates  transcription    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ histones bind to DNA in order to pack the DNA more tightly [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (this is)  to prevent gene expression [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nMany proteins bind to DNA sequences to regulate gene expression. For example, **activator proteins** bind to enhancers ($\\checkmark$) which are non-coding sections of DNA close to the promoter.  This **increases** the rate of **transcription** ($\\checkmark$). Regulatory proteins also bind to **promoter-proximal elements** ($\\checkmark$). The binding of proteins to this region **regulates transcription** ($\\checkmark$) of the gene.  **Repressor proteins** bind to the **silencer** ($\\checkmark$) and when this happens, there is a **decrease in the rate of transcription** ($\\checkmark$). Finally, **RNA polymerase** binds to the **promoter** ($\\checkmark$) to **initiate transcription** ($\\checkmark$) of the gene."},{"ref":"BI0637","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0637\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Describe the structure of a typical human motor neuron. [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline why myelination is essential for the rapid transmission of impulses. [4 marks]\n\n3. Explain the importance of positive ions in the generation and propagation of an action potential. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1.    any **four** of the following (***accept a correctly annotated diagram as an answer***)  :\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ long axon extends from cell body [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myelin sheath **OR** Schwann cells wrapped around axon    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  nodes of Ranvier are gaps between Schwann cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  cell body contains nucleus and other organelles [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ dendrons extend from **OR** lead towards cell body [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ dendrites are short fibres branching from dendrons [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  axon terminal **OR** synaptic bulb **OR** synaptic knob at end of axon     [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    A typical motor neuron consists of a **cell body** containing the **nucleus** and other **organelles** ($\\checkmark$). **Dendrons extend** from the **cell body** ($\\checkmark$) and **branch** into **dendrites** ($\\checkmark$), which receive incoming signals. The **axon**, which also **extends** from the **cell body**,($\\checkmark$)  is **myelinated** by **Schwann cells** ($\\checkmark$) with **nodes of Ranvier** in between ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.    any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  myelin sheath is composed of proteins and lipids **OR** myelin is made up of Schwann cells, **OR** oligodendrocytes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  myelin acts as an insulator     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  there are gaps **OR** nodes of Ranvier between Schwann cells on the axon [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  (myelin) allows for nerve impulses to jump from node to node **OR** saltatory conduction [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  sodium ions diffuse from node to node without leaking out of the axon **OR** local currents stimulate downstream nodes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  sodium and potassium channels are concentrated at the nodes (increasing the speed of neurotransmission)    [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nSchwann cells deposit layers of **proteins and lipids** ($\\checkmark$) called myelin around axons. Gaps between myelin-containing Schwann cells on the axon are called **nodes of Ranvier** ($\\checkmark$).  Myelin acts as an **insulator** ($\\checkmark$),  so nerve impulses can **jump from node to node** ($\\checkmark$), making neurotransmission faster than in an unmyelinated neuron. **Sodium and potassium channels** are concentrated at the nodes ($\\checkmark$), which increases the speed of neurotransmission.\n\n3.    any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  an action potential is the depolarisation and repolarisation of a neuron due to the movement of positive ions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  inside the neuron, there is a high concentration of  K$^+$ ions and a low concentration of Na$^+$ ions     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  depolarisation is due to  Na$^+$ ions diffusing into the neuron [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  Na$^+$ ions diffuse in through ligand-gated channels on dendrites, **OR** cell body, **OR** synaptic transmission, causes initial Na$^+$ ion influx    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  if threshold potential is reached, **OR** at -55mV, voltage-gated Na$^+$ channels open [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  the voltage-gated influx of Na$^+$ ions makes the inside more positive compared to the outside, **OR** raising the membrane potential up to +30mV    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  (at +30mV) voltage-gated sodium channels close    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  (at +30mV) voltage-gated potassium channels open    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  repolarization happens due to K$^+$ ions diffusing out of the neuron     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  cell interior becomes more negative compared to the outside of the cell **OR** reaches -75mV **OR** hyperpolarisation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  (after -75mV) voltage-gated K$^+$ channels close    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  Na$^+\\text{\\textendash}$K$^+$ pump restores the relative concentrations of Na$^+$ and K$^+$ in the cell [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nAn **action potential** is the **depolarisation and repolarisation** of a neuron due to the movement of positive ions ($\\checkmark$). The initial depolarisation occurs on the cell body or on dendrites at synapses. Here **neurotransmitters** cause sodium channels to open and **Na$^+$ ions diffuse** into the neuron ($\\checkmark$). There is an ion imbalance in the neuron where the concentration of **Na$^+$ is higher outside the neuron** and the concentration of **K$^+$ is higher inside** the neuron ($\\checkmark$). The resting potential of a neuron is about -70 mV.\n\n    If the initial depolarisation reaches the **threshold potential**, an action potential is generated and **voltage-gated Na$^+$ channels open** ($\\checkmark$).  **Na$^+$ ions diffuse into** the neuron, leading to the membrane potential rising to **+30mV** ($\\checkmark$).  At +30mV, voltage-gated **sodium channels close** ($\\checkmark$), and voltage-gated **potassium channels open** ($\\checkmark$). This is followed by **repolarisation**, where K$^+$ ions diffuse out of the neuron ($\\checkmark$), making the inside of the cell **more negative** compared to the outside of the cell ($\\checkmark$).  During the final stage of an action potential, the **Na$^+\\text{\\textendash}$K$^+$ pump** re-establishes the initial relative concentrations ($\\checkmark$) of sodium and potassium in and out of the neuron."},{"ref":"BI0665","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0665\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the importance of international cooperation in fighting infectious diseases. [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline the role of monoclonal antibodies in home pregnancy tests. [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain the specific immune response to a pathogen in terms of antibodies and lymphocytes.    [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ infectious diseases can cross borders [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if a disease is found in one population it can spread to other populations [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ herd immunity reduces rate of transmission [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ knowledge needs to be shared internationally so that all countries can take measures to prevent disease transmission **OR** so that countries know the best ways to prevent disease transmission [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ resources such as vaccinations and other medications need to be shared between richer and poorer countries  [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nInternational cooperation is needed to fight infectious diseases because diseases **cross borders** ($\\checkmark$). **Knowledge** ($\\checkmark$) and **vaccines** **need to be shared**  ($\\checkmark$) between richer and poorer countries so that all countries can **effectively reduce rates of transmission**.\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ home pregnancy tests contain monoclonal antibodies to hCG [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ home pregnancy tests have a test site and control site    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the urine of a pregnant woman contains hCG [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if hCG is present, it binds to monoclonal antibodies [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the monoclonal antibodies to hCG have a dye attached    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if there are antibodies with hCG attached, they will bind at the test site producing a line     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ monoclonal antibodies with dye attached bind to the control site regardless of the presence of hCG [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a line at the control site shows that the test is valid [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nHome pregnancy tests contain monoclonal antibodies for the hormone **hCG** ($\\checkmark$), which is found in the **urine of pregnant women** ($\\checkmark$). If hCG is present it **binds to monoclonal antibodies** ($\\checkmark$) near the start of the strip. The antibodies move along the strip with the urine. If there are antibodies with hCG attached, they bind to the **test site producing a line** ($\\checkmark$), which indicates a positive result. The test is considered to be valid if there is a line appearing at the **control site** ($\\checkmark$), regardless of the pregnancy status.\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ B lymphocytes and T lymphocytes are **both** part of the specific immune response [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ B lymphocytes and T lymphocytes **both** react to antigens [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ B lymphocytes and T lymphocytes **both** produce memory cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ B lymphocytes produce antibodies that are specific to one antigen [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ helper T cells activate B lymphocytes **OR** activated helper T cells activate B lymphocytes     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ B lymphocytes divide, producing plasma B cells     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibodies are produced by plasma B cells  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibodies bind to antigens on pathogens [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibodies cause agglutination **OR** antibodies cause pathogens to stick together [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibodies help phagocytes bind to and engulf pathogens [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibodies prevent pathogens from attaching to host cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antibodies cause the destruction of pathogens [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ memory cells give long-term immunity [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n**B lymphocytes and T lymphocytes** are both part of the specific immune response ($\\checkmark$) and **both react to antigens** ($\\checkmark$). B lymphocytes and T lymphocytes **both produce memory cells** ($\\checkmark$). **Helper T cells** activate **B lymphocytes** ($\\checkmark$) which divide, producing memory B cells and **plasma B cells** ($\\checkmark$). Plasma B cells produce **antibodies** ($\\checkmark$). **Antibodies bind to antigens** ($\\checkmark$). Antibodies help **phagocytes** bind to and **engulf pathogens** ($\\checkmark$), **prevent pathogens from attaching to host cells** ($\\checkmark$) and cause **agglutination of pathogens** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0730","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0730\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the adaptations of sperm cells.      [4 marks]\n\n2. Describe how positive feedback results in contractions during pregnancy.         [4 marks]\n\n3. Discuss the importance of cell division in pregnancy. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a flagellum **OR** tail enables the sperm cell to swim towards the egg [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ numerous mitochondria in the midpiece produce ATP to provide energy for movement [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ an acrosome contains enzymes that help the sperm penetrate the outer layers surrounding the egg [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a haploid nucleus contains one set of chromosomes, allowing the diploid chromosome number to be restored at fertilisation [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a streamlined shape **OR** reduced cytoplasm reduces resistance during movement through fluid [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ receptors on the sperm cell membrane enable recognition of **OR** binding to the egg [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Sperm cells have **a flagellum that enables them to swim towards the egg** ($\\checkmark$). They contain **numerous mitochondria in the midpiece that produce ATP to provide energy for movement** ($\\checkmark$). **The acrosome contains enzymes that help the sperm penetrate the outer layers surrounding the egg** ($\\checkmark$). Sperm cells also have **a haploid nucleus containing one set of chromosomes, allowing the diploid chromosome number to be restored at fertilisation** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ positive feedback acts to increase an effect    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxytocin stimulates contractions in the uterus **OR** myometrium     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the contractions are detected by stretch receptors  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ signal to the pituitary gland to release more oxytocin    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ more oxytocin makes the contractions more vigorous and frequent     [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    **Positive feedback acts to increase an effect** ($\\checkmark$). Towards the end of pregnancy, levels of progesterone fall and levels of oxytocin increase. **Oxytocin stimulates contractions in the myometrium** ($\\checkmark$), which is detected by **stretch receptors** ($\\checkmark$), this sends a signal to the **pituitary gland to release more oxytocin** ($\\checkmark$), which makes the **contractions even more vigorous** and frequent ($\\checkmark$), causing even more oxytocin secretion, allowing the baby to be born more rapidly.\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ occurs during oogenesis in the ovaries **OR** spermatogenesis in the testes       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mitosis occurs first, allowing spermatocytes **OR** oocytes to be made       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ precursor to two rounds of meiosis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allowing mature gametes to be made  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ without mature gametes, there would be no fertilisation **OR** continuation of life [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ post fertilisation, the zygote divides many times via mitosis   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ forming pluripotent cells with high differentiation potential  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if extracted at this point, they can be used for stem cell treatments or research     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if they continue to divide via mitosis, a blastocyst will eventually form [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ outer layer of blastocyst implants into the endometrium [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ outer layer of blastocyst will eventually differentiate into the placenta, which allows for exchange of materials between mother and foetus [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allowing the foetus to grow and develop     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ inner part of blastocyst will eventually differentiate into the foetus     [1 mark]\n[8 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Cell division occurs during both **oogenesis and spermatogenesis** ($\\checkmark$), in the ovaries and testes, respectively. **Mitosis occurs first** ($\\checkmark$), allowing spermatocytes or oocytes to be made, next the cells undergo **two rounds of meiosis** ($\\checkmark$), which allows **mature egg and sperm cells to be made** ($\\checkmark$). Without the mature gametes, **there would be no fertilisation** ($\\checkmark$). **Post fertilisation**, the zygote (first cell formed after fertilisation) **divides many times via mitosis** ($\\checkmark$) forming identical **pluripotent cells** with the ability to differentiate into most cell types ($\\checkmark$), if these cells are extracted at this point, they can be used for **stem cell treatments** ($\\checkmark$) such as treating Stargardt disease. If the cells are not extracted, at this point they will continue to divide via **mitosis**, until a **blastocyst** ($\\checkmark$) (a hollow ball of cells)  is formed. The **outer layer** of the blastocyst will **implant into the endometrium** ($\\checkmark$) and will eventually **differentiate into the placenta** which allows for **exchange of materials** ($\\checkmark$) such as nutrients, waste and oxygen between the mother and child, **sustaining the foetus’s life** ($\\checkmark$). The **inner part of the blastocyst will differentiate into the foetus** ($\\checkmark$) which will eventually develop into a child."},{"ref":"BI0742","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0742\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline how the glomerulus and Bowman’s capsule are adapted for their function.     [4 marks]\n\n2. Explain the differences between the composition of blood in the renal artery and renal vein.         [4 marks]\n\n3. Describe the processes in the nephron that leads to the production of urine. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large surface area [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fenestrations **OR** large pores in the walls of the capillaries that allow ultrafiltration      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ podocytes allow fluid to pass through freely [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ basement membrane only allows molecules below a certain size to pass through    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ afferent arterioles are wider than efferent arterioles, so (hydrostatic) pressure is increased in the glomerulus    [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    The glomerulus and Bowman’s capsule are adapted for their function because they have a **large surface area** ($\\checkmark$). There are **fenestrations** ($\\checkmark$) in the walls of the capillaries that allow ultrafiltration, the **basement membrane** only allow molecules below a certain size to pass through ($\\checkmark$) and **podocytes** allow fluid to pass through freely ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following (difference and reason both need to be mentioned in order to get a marking point):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ blood in the renal artery has a higher concentration of oxygen due to aerobic respiration in the cells of the kidney    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (blood in the renal artery has a) higher concentration of glucose due to cell respiration in the kidney     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ blood in the renal artery has a higher concentration of urea because urea is filtered out of the blood by the nephron     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ blood in the renal vein has a higher concentration of carbon dioxide because it is a waste product of aerobic respiration in the cells of the kidney    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ blood in the renal vein has balanced optimum levels of water and ions compared to the renal artery because the ultra-filtration and selective reabsorption in the nephron results in the optimum concentrations     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the renal artery may contain toxins and undigestible pigments that are not found in the renal vein due to ultrafiltration in the nephron         [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    Blood in the renal vein has lower concentrations of **oxygen** ($\\checkmark$) and **glucose** ($\\checkmark$) and a higher concentration of **carbon dioxide** due to **aerobic respiration** ($\\checkmark$) in the kidney cells. Blood in the renal artery has a higher concentration of **urea** because urea is **filtered** out ($\\checkmark$) of the blood in the glomerulus. Blood in the renal artery does not have **optimum water and ions concentrations** ($\\checkmark$). Those get balanced out by the ultrafiltration and consecutive re-absorption of both. The renal artery may contain **toxins** ($\\checkmark$) which are not found in the renal vein because they would be filtered out in the glomerulus and have no way of being re-absorbed.\n\n3. any **eight** of the following (answers could be accepted in the form of an annotated diagram):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ultrafiltration happens in Bowman’s capsule  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glomerulus has structural features for ultrafiltration **OR** glomerulus has fenestrations, podocytes and basal membrane to increase permeability **OR** substances are filtered from the blood due to the high pressure in the glomerulus caused by afferent arteriole being wider than the efferent [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water, salts, glucose, urea, water and mineral ions are filtered out of the blood [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ substances from the filtrate are reabsorbed by osmosis, simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion and active transport  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in the proximal convoluted tubule, all of the glucose is reabsorbed (into the blood) [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in the proximal convoluted tubule, most of the water, **OR** most of the salts, is, **OR** are, reabsorbed [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in the descending limb of the loop of Henle, water is reabsorbed  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in the ascending limb of the loop of Henle, sodium ions are reabsorbed by active transport **OR** are actively pumped out of the nephron **OR** glomerular filtrate     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the active transport of sodium ions in the ascending limb of the loop of Henle increases the solute concentration of the medulla, which increases water reabsorption in the descending limb and collecting duct  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in the distal convoluted tubule, mineral ions are reabsorbed into the blood, and blood pH and ion concentrations are balanced   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when blood water concentration falls, ADH is released from the pituitary gland **OR** when blood water potential decreases, osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus detect it and stimulate the release of ADH  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ADH results in more aquaporins in the plasma membrane of cells of the collecting duct wall, allowing more water to be reabsorbed         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a nephron consists of Bowman’s capsule and glomerulus, a proximal convoluted tubule, a loop of Henle, a distal convoluted tubule and a collecting duct (all have to be named and in the correct order for this marking point)     [1 mark]\n[8 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    **Ultrafiltration** happens in Bowman’s capsule ($\\checkmark$), and **reabsorption** of substances through osmosis, simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion, and active transport happens in the rest of the nephron ($\\checkmark$). The filtrate contains **water, salts, glucose, urea, water and mineral ions** ($\\checkmark$). In the **proximal convoluted tubule**, most water and salt ($\\checkmark$) are reabsorbed, as well as **all of the glucose** ($\\checkmark$). In the **descending limb** of the loop of Henle, water is reabsorbed ($\\checkmark$). In the **ascending limb** of the loop of Henle, sodium ions are reabsorbed by active transport ($\\checkmark$). The active transport of salts **increases the solute concentration of the medulla** ($\\checkmark$), which allows a higher volume of water to be reabsorbed in the descending limb and collecting duct. In the **distal convoluted tubule**, mineral ions are reabsorbed, and the pH and ion concentration of the blood is balanced ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0765","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0765\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nIn an investigation into the effect of solute concentration on diffusion rate, starch solutions, of varying concentrations, were placed inside starch permeable membrane balloons. Each balloon was put into a beaker of distilled water and at 3 minute intervals, samples were taken from the solution surrounding the membrane balloons in each beaker, placed on spotting tiles and tested for the presence of starch. The time taken for a positive test to show was recorded for each trial.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0765A.png\" alt=\"BI0765A\">\n\nThe results are shown in the graph.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0765COR.png\" alt=\"BI0765COR\">\n\n1.\n 1. Identify the dependent and independent variable in this investigation. [2 marks]\n\n 2. State one variable, other than time of testing, that should be kept constant in this investigation to ensure the results are valid. [1 mark]\n\n2. Describe a positive test for starch. [1 mark]\n\n3. Explain why the method used in this study would not be appropriate for an investigation into the effects of solute concentration on active transport. [2 marks]\n\n4.\n 1. Samples taken from the 5% and 10% starch solution both tested positive for starch after 3 minutes. Suggest a way in which the experiment could be improved to provide a more accurate result. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Predict the time taken for a positive starch test for a starch concentration of 3%. [1 mark]\n\n5. Suggest modifications to the methodology that would make it suitable to investigate the effect of glucose concentration on the rate of diffusion. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ independent variable is starch concentration **OR** solute concentration [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ dependent variable is time taken for a positive starch test **OR** diffusion rate [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The independent variable in this investigation is the **starch concentration** ($\\checkmark$) and the dependent variable is the **time taken for a positive starch test** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2. Any **one** of following:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ volume of distilled water [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ volume of starch solution [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ size of beaker [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ surface area of membrane balloon **OR** size and shape of membrane balloon [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ In the experiment, **the volume of distilled water** ($\\checkmark$) used should be kept constant.\n\n2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ orange **OR** brown to blue **OR** black [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ A positive test for starch would result in the iodine changing colour from **brown to black** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ active transport requires energy **OR** proteins [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ membrane balloon is non-living **OR** artificial **OR** lacks requirements for active transport [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ starch does not move by active transport **OR** starch only moves actively by exocytosis **OR** endocytosis (which the membrane balloon cannot replicate) [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Active transport **requires energy** ($\\checkmark$), but this methodology, which uses an **artificial** membrane balloon, does **not have** a source of energy ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.\n 1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ decrease time between samples **OR** sample at shorter time intervals **OR** increase the frequency of samples\n[1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The accuracy of the experiment could be improved by **increasing the frequency** ($\\checkmark$) of samples, for example, every one minute, instead of every three minutes.\n\n4.\n 2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 6 minutes [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0765ANSACHG1.png\" alt=\"BI0765ANSACHG1\">\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ A positive test for starch, at 3%, would take approximately **6 minutes** ($\\checkmark$) based on the data in the graph.\n\n5. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ dialysis tubing **OR** visking tubing (instead of starch permeable membrane balloon) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ put different concentrations of glucose solution in dialysis **OR** visking tubing [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Benedict’s reagent (instead of iodine solution) **OR** glucose test strips [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ heat samples to above 70°C for positive (glucose) test with Benedicts [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ enzyme **OR** amylase to break down starch OR different concentrations of glucose in tubing at start [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ same concentration **OR** same volume, of enzyme OR same volume of starting glucose concentrations [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n To modify the methodology, we could use **dialysis tubing** ($\\checkmark$) instead of the starch permeable membrane balloon. We would also need to use **Benedict’s reagent** ($\\checkmark$) instead of iodine to test for glucose."},{"ref":"BI0773","marks":"10","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0773\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nBelow is an image showing the results from a paternity test.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0773A.png\" alt=\"BI0773A\">\n\n1. State the two properties of DNA fragments that allow them to be separated on a gel. [1 mark]\n\n2. Outline the method used in DNA profiling in paternity testing to reach the stage shown in the diagram. [3 marks]\n\n3. Deduce, with a reason, who is most likely to be the father of:\n\n 1. Child A [1 mark]\n\n 2. Child B [1 mark]\n\n4. State one use other than paternity testing for DNA profiling. [1 mark]\n\n5. Draw a circle around the heaviest DNA band in father 4’s sample. [1 mark]\n\n6. Suggest why it is important to analyse the DNA of the mother, as well as the father in paternity testing. [1 mark]\n\n7. State one potential source of error in DNA profiling. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ charge **AND** size [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n DNA fragments are separated by their **charge** and **size** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA sample is obtained from child, alleged biological father and mother [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA is extracted from the sample **OR** separated from other cellular components [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sequences that vary between individuals **OR** VNTRs **OR** variable number of tandem repeats **OR** STRs **OR** short tandem repeats are selected [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA is amplified **OR** copied using PCR [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA is fragmented using restriction endonucleases **OR** enzymes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fragments are separated by charge and size **OR** using gel electrophoresis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ stain the DNA fragments with a dye to make visible [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the gel is observed under UV light to see the DNA bands [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n To create a DNA profile, a **DNA sample is obtained** from the child, alleged biological father and mother ($\\checkmark$), and analysed. **Sequences that vary** a lot between individuals such as **VNTRs** ($\\checkmark$) are selected and amplified using **PCR** ($\\checkmark$). The DNA is then split into **fragments** using **restriction endonucleases** ($\\checkmark$) and the fragments are separated by size and charge, using **gel electrophoresis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 1. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ *Answer must include a reason to gain a mark*\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ father 4 **BECAUSE** their 5th **OR** 6th fragment are unique to child A and them [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0773ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0773ANSA\">\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ **Father 4** because their **5th and 6th fragments** from the top of the gel, are **unique** to them and Child A ($\\checkmark$). The remaining fragments for Child A either match to their mother’s fragments or are not unique to Child A, so cannot be used to distinguish between fathers.\n\n3.\n 2. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ *Answer must include a reason to gain a mark*\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ father 1 **BECAUSE** their 2nd **OR** 4th **OR** 9th fragment are unique to child B and them [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0773ANSB.png\" alt=\"BI0773ANSB\">\n\n $\\hspace{1em}$ **Father 1** because their **2nd and 4th fragments** from the top of the gel, are **unique** to them and Child B ($\\checkmark$). The remaining fragments for Child B either match to their mother’s fragments or are not unique to Child B, so cannot be used to distinguish between fathers.\n\n4. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ forensic testing [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to study genealogy **OR** anthropology **OR** population genetics [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to identify disaster victims **OR** identification of human remains [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ immigration and citizenship [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ disease diagnosis **OR** tailoring medical treatment [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n To identify **perpetrators** or **victims** at a **crime scene** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the band nearest to the top of the gel for father 4 should be circled [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0773C.png\" alt=\"BI0773C\">\n\n6. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ half of the DNA fragments in offspring will be shared with the mother **AND** the rest must come from the father [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ helps to identify any potential errors **OR** contamination of samples by comparing child’s and mother’s DNA markers [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ some genetic markers are common in the general population, which can lead to false positives if child’s DNA is not compared to both mother and alleged father [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ father may have similar fragment pattern to another male relative [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n It is important to analyse maternal DNA as well as paternal DNA, in paternity testing as **half** of the DNA fragments in an offspring’s samples will be the **same as the mother** , which will show which **DNA fragments come from the biological father**($\\checkmark$).\n\n7. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ contamination from other sources of DNA [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ degradation of DNA OR fragmentation of DNA **OR** poor storage of DNA leading to damage [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ some genetic markers being more common in the population than expected giving false positives [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n One potential source of error in DNA profiling is **contamination** from other sources of DNA ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0783","marks":"9","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0783\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe graphs show the results from a long-term study on 86 pairs of monozygotic twins, over 80 years.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0783A.png\" alt=\"BI0783A\">\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0783B.png\" alt=\"BI0783B\">\n\n1. Describe the relationship between the percentage similarity in DNA methylation pattern and age. [2 marks]\n\n2. Explain the relationship that you referred to in part (a). [1 mark]\n\n3. Explain the effect that DNA methylation most likely has on gene expression. [1 mark]\n\n4. Explain why monozygotic twins were used in this study. [2 marks]\n\n5. Suggest one limitation of conducting a study in humans over 80 years. [1 mark]\n\n6. Evaluate whether this study provides evidence that decreases in the percentage of DNA methylation is a predictor of an early death. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as age increases, the percentage similarity in the DNA methylation pattern decreases **OR** there is a negative correlation between age and percentage similarity in the DNA methylation pattern [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ between the ages of 20 - 40, the percentage similarity in DNA methylation pattern remains relatively stable **OR** the percentage similarity changed the most between ages 0 - 20 **OR** the greatest change was between 10 - 20 years [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n As **age increases**, the percentage similarity in the DNA methylation pattern **decreases** ($\\checkmark$), however between the ages of **20 - 40**, the percentage similarity remains **relatively stable** ($\\checkmark$), at approximately 82%.\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ differences in environment **OR** changes in lifestyle **OR** diet **OR** exposure to toxins cause differential expression of genes **OR** in the epigenome [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ errors in DNA replication **OR** damage to DNA over time [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Monozygotic twins have more differences in their patterns of methylation as they age, as the **differences**in their **environments** cause **changes** in their **gene expression** patterns ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ downregulates gene expression by inhibiting transcription **OR** preventing transcription factors from binding **OR** silences gene expression [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Direct DNA methylation will most likely **downregulate** gene expression by **inhibiting** gene **transcription** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allows for the control of genetic variation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ identical twins **OR** monozygotic twins are often raised in the same environment so helps isolate the effects of age [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ born with the same sequence of genes **OR** share the same genetic material **OR** develop from a single fertilised ovum **OR** develop from the same zygote that splits [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can be used to study how the environment and genes interact **OR** the epigenome [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Monozygotic twins were used in this study, as they are born with the **same alleles** ($\\checkmark$), allowing for the **control of genetic variation**, so studies conducted on them can be used to study how **genes and the environment interact** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ researchers cannot control the subject’s lifestyle **OR** difficulty in controlling environmental factors [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ subjects may die before the age of 80 [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ subjects may leave the study **OR** be difficult to keep track of [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ small sample size [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ethical considerations as older participants may not be capable of providing informed consent [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n One limitation of conducting a human study over 80 years is that the subjects may **die before the age of 80** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. Any **two** of the following:\n\n *Answer must include at least one positive and one negative statement to achieve full marks*\n\n **Yes because…**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as you age the degree of methylation decreases [1 mark]\n\n **No because…**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ correlation is not causation **OR** the graph does not indicate that hypomethylation is a predictor of death [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the sample size is too small [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Hypomethylation may be an indicator of an early death because **as you age** the degree of **methylation decreases** ($\\checkmark$), however, the graph **does not refer to death rate** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0784","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0784\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nDissolved CO:sub[2] is increasing in standing waters of lakes and ponds. Research has shown this will impact the nutritional quality of phytoplankton (primary producers) and may further affect zooplankton (consumers) that feed on the phytoplankton.\n\nMesocosms were used to monitor three species of zooplankton, the water flea (*Daphnia magna*), the seed shrimp (*Heterocypris incongruens*) and the rotifer (*Brachionus calyciflorus*) under three different conditions of CO:sub[2] in ponds and shallow lakes:\n\n- Treatment C - present day levels of CO:sub[2], (control)\n- Treatment 1 - moderate increase of CO:sub[2] (possible future levels)\n- Treatment 2 - high increase of CO:sub[2]  (more extreme future levels)\n\nGlass jars filled with filtered pond water were injected with both carbon dioxide and air under pressure, and the representative species of zooplankton, water flea, seed shrimp or rotifer were added to the jars. They were monitored for survival, growth rate and body size over the next several days.\n\nThe image below shows the three species of zooplankton that were studied in the mesocosms.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0784%20a.png\" alt=\"BI0784 a\">\n[Adapted from Ramaekers, L., Pinceel, T., Brendonck, L., & Vanschoenwinkel, B. (2022, April 12). *Direct effects of elevated dissolved CO2 can alter the life history of Freshwater Zooplankton*. Nature News. Retrieved March 22, 2023, from https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-10094-2, Copyright under CC-BY 4.0]\n\nThe survival probability of the three species of zooplankton were monitored over several days in the mesocosms and the results are shown in the graphs below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0784b.png\" alt=\"BI0784b\">\n[Adapted from Ramaekers, L., Pinceel, T., Brendonck, L., & Vanschoenwinkel, B. (2022, April 12). Direct effects of elevated dissolved CO2 can alter the life history of Freshwater Zooplankton. Nature News. Retrieved March 22, 2023, from https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-10094-2,Copyright under CC-BY 4.0]\n\n1. Giving a reason, predict which zooplankton is the least resilient to the effects of increased carbon dioxide.    [1 mark]\n\n2. Describe how using mesocosms for research is both an advantage and a disadvantage in predicting the effects of increasing carbon dioxide in the atmosphere.     [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the water flea, because it could not survive treatment T2 or extreme future levels of CO:sub[2] and therefore was the most sensitive  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ both seed shrimp and rotifer could survive treatment T2 for more days and therefore the    water flea is the most sensitive to elevated levels of CO:sub[2]  [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Unlike the seed shrimp and rotifer, **the water flea could not survive treatment T2** ($\\checkmark$)  after the first two days and is therefore the most sensitive to future elevated levels of CO:sub[2].\n\n2. Any **one** from each section\n\n    **Advantages:**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ with a closed system it is easier to increase levels of CO:sub[2]     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mesocosms can be monitored to see the effects of elevated CO2    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mesocosms will yield results in a short period of time     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mesocosms allow small scale experiments to be set up    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reproducibility - can do repeats more easily to collect more data    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ costs - can be cheaper to set up [1 mark]\n\n    **Disadvantages:**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it is not possible to simulate all that happens in the environment     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it is unethical to add larger organisms to a mesocosm    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it can be difficult to set up mesocosms and monitor the changes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ often short term experiments do not capture the long term effects of changes in carbon dioxide [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    An advantage is that it is **easier to increase levels of CO:sub[2] in a closed system** ($\\checkmark$) like a mesocosm to study the effects. A disadvantage is that it is **not possible to simulate** ($\\checkmark$) exactly all that happens in the environment."},{"ref":"BI0788","marks":"10","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0788\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe graph shows the effect of differing concentrations of the growth hormone Indole-3-acetic acid (IAA) on the shoot growth rate of the sesame seed plant (*Sesamum indicum*). IAA is an auxin.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0788.png\" alt=\"BI0788\">\n\n1.\n 1. Describe the trend shown in the graph. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Outline why percentage change was used rather than change in length. [2 marks]\n\n2.\n 1. State two variables that would need to be controlled in this experiment. [2 marks]\n\n 2. Explain why it is important that one of the factors you chose in (i) needs to be controlled in this experiment. [2 marks]\n\n3.\n 1. Explain how auxins such as IAA promote shoot growth. [2 marks]\n\n 2. Deduce, with a reason, which concentration of IAA should be used to grow sesame seed plants. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n 1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ between 0 µM and 75 µM, the percentage change in shoot length increased **AND** between 75 µM and 100 µM, the percentage change in shoot length decreased [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ up to 75μΜ percentage change in length increases with IAA concentration **AND** above 75μM percentage change in length decreases [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The graph shows that between **0 µM and 75 µM**, the percentage change in shoot length **increased** and between **75 µM and 100 µM**, the percentage change in shoot length **decreased** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the shoots may not all be the same length at the beginning [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to compare the relative changes in shoot length **OR** to make the results comparable **OR** provide a standardised measure of change [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Percentage change is used rather than change in length, as all of the shoots may **not be the same length** at the beginning of the experiment ($\\checkmark$); therefore percentage change allows for the **comparison** of relative changes in shoot length ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ light intensity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$volume of water [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ concentration of nutrients [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide concentration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ length of time between auxin application and measurement of shoot length [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature of environment [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ number of seeds grown at each concentration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ distance between seeds [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ wind speed **OR** air movement [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Two controlled variables in this experiment are **light intensity** ($\\checkmark$) and the **volume of water** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n *Must refer to **one** of the factors that were chosen in (b) (i) for one mark*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ there should only be one independent variable in an experiment [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ for fair comparison between the effect of different concentrations of IAA **OR** the effect of changing the stated variable on the experiment and how this would affect fair comparison [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n It is important that **light intensity** is controlled as it affects the rate of photosynthesis and so affects the **growth rate** ($\\checkmark$) of the shoot, and there should only be **one independent variable** ($\\checkmark$) in an experiment.\n\n3.\n 1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ IAA **OR** auxin enters the cells of shoot and upregulates gene expression for production of expansins [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ expansins increase elasticity of cell wall [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ IAA **OR** auxin activates proton pumps in the plasma membrane so hydrogen ions are pumped into the cell wall [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen ions cause bonds holding cellulose fibres together to break [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ breaking bonds in cell wall makes it more elastic so, as water enters the vacuole, the cell elongates [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ IAA **OR** auxin stimulates cell division [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Auxin is a positive phototropic growth hormone in plants. It enters the cells in the shoot and **upregulates gene expression of expansins** ($\\checkmark$), which **increase the elasticity** of the cell wall ($\\checkmark$). Auxin activates **proton pumps** in the plasma membrane of cells in the shoot so **hydrogen ions are pumped** into the cell wall ($\\checkmark$). The hydrogen ions cause bonds holding **cellulose fibres together in the cell wall to break** ($\\checkmark$), making the **cell walls more elastic**, which allows **water to enter the vacuole** , and causes the cell to **elongate** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n *Both a value and a reason are needed for one mark*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 75 µM because it is the concentration with the highest percentage change [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The concentration of IAA that should be used to grow sesame seed plants is **75 µM** because it is the concentration that results in the **highest percentage increase** ($\\checkmark$) in shoot length."},{"ref":"BI0792","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0792\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nLung volumes and ventilation rates can be measured using a spirometer. During a school lab a student was asked to breathe normally at rest, then they were asked to take the deepest breath in that they could, followed by the largest breath out, before returning to breathing normally at rest. Below is the trace for this student.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0792aii.png\" alt=\"BI0792aii\">\n\n1. Calculate the total volume of air inhaled and exhaled during one minute by this student when breathing normally at rest. [2 marks]\n\nBelow is a diagram of the spirometer used by the students. The subject puts on a nose clip, to prevent air from escaping through the nose, and breathes in and out through the mouthpiece. The chamber is closed so the volume of air with each inhalation and exhalation can be recorded on the drum trace. The chamber is opened between trials to allow the air to refresh and the trace to be recalibrated.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0792B.png\" alt=\"BI0792B\">\n\n2. Explain why the trace decreased in height during the 100 seconds. [2 marks]\n\n3. Suggest a possible investigation that could be conducted using the spirometer. [2 marks]\n\n4. State two variables that must be controlled in the investigation you suggested in part (c). [2 marks]\n\nAnother student, who played the flute regularly, was then asked to record their results to compare them to student 1, who did not play a wind instrument. The trace for this second student is shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n5. Discuss whether the data in the two graphs support the hypothesis that playing a wind instrument increases lung volume. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ tidal volume x ventilation rate **OR** litres per breath x breaths per minute **OR** 0.45 dm:sup[3] breath:sup[-1] x 13 breaths min:sup[-1] [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 5.85 **OR** 5.9 dm:sup[3] min:sup[-1] (allow range 5.6 - 6.3 dm:sup[3] min:sup[-1], dm:sup[3] units must be included) [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0792bi.png\" alt=\"BI0792bi\">\n\n **0.45 dm:sup[3]** breath:sup[-1] x **13** breaths min:sup[-1] ($\\checkmark$)\n\n = **5.85 dm:sup[3]** min:sup[-1] ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide is excreted in breath **OR** carbon dioxide is breathed out as a waste gas from respiration **OR** carbon dioxide is released with each breath [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide is absorbed by soda lime, and so volume of air in spirometer decreases **OR** carbon dioxide is absorbed in the spirometer, and so volume of gas decreases [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Carbon dioxide is **breathed** out due to it being a **waste gas** of respiration ($\\checkmark$). This is then **absorbed by the soda lime** in the spirometer, and so the **volume of gas** in the chamber **decreases** ($\\checkmark$) with each breath.\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the independent variable of the investigation is identified, e.g. levels of exercise, age, fitness, height or another suitable independent variable [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the dependent variable of the investigation is identified, e.g. air breathed per minute, tidal volume, volume of oxygen used in a fixed time, metabolic rate, vital capacity, ventilation rate or another suitable dependent variable [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The effect of **different levels of exercise** ($\\checkmark$) on the **volume of air** breathed per minute ($\\checkmark$) could be investigated.\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ environmental temperature [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ age of person **OR** sex of person **OR** fitness of person [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ way breathing conducted **OR** sealing of nose with clip [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ how the level of exercise controlled for each subject [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ subjects do not have respiratory disease [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ time of day investigation conducted **OR** time after meal investigation conducted [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ any other valid controlled variable [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The investigation will be carried out on **Year 11 students** ($\\checkmark$) using a treadmill. All students will **wear a nose clip** ($\\checkmark$) when breathing in and out of the apparatus.\n\n5. Any **two** of the following:\n\n **Supports the hypothesis:**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ vital capacity of student 2 is greater than student 1 **OR** vital capacity of student 1 is less than student 2 **OR** the inspiratory and expiratory reserve volumes are greater in student 2 **OR** the inspiratory and expiratory reserve volumes are less in student 1 [1 mark]\n\n **Does not support the hypothesis:**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ages of students may not be the same **OR** student 1 may be younger than student 2 **OR** sex of students may not be the same **OR** student 2 may be a biological male **OR** fitness of students may not be the same **OR** student 1 may not be as fit as student 2 [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ tidal volumes are the almost the same **OR** tidal volumes are the same [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/792ms.png\" alt=\"792ms\">\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/spirometer_graph_student2_047_medium.png\" alt=\"spirometer_graph_student2_047_medium\">\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **larger vital capacity** of the student who plays the **flute supports** ($\\checkmark$) the hypothesis. However, the **tidal volumes** of both students are almost **the same**, which **does not support** ($\\checkmark$) the hypothesis."},{"ref":"BI0793","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0793\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe maps show the incidence of sickle cell anaemia and the spread of malaria across the continent of Africa.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0793A.png\" alt=\"BI0793A\">\n[Source: Adapted from Allison, A. (2009, December 10). *File: Malaria versus sickle-cell trait distributions.png*. Retrieved May 3, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malaria_versus_sickle-cell_trait_distributions.png. Copyright free]\n\nThe graph shows the average density of malarial parasites present in the blood of Hb AA (not affected by sickle cell anaemia) and Hb AS (carriers of sickle cell anaemia) people in Sub-Saharan Africa.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BIO793B.png\" alt=\"BIO793B\">\n[Source: Adapted from Luzzatto, L. (2012, October 3). *Sickle cell anaemia and malaria*. Mediterranean journal of hematology and infectious diseases. Retrieved May 4, 2023, from https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3499995/ Copyright under CC BY 2.0]\n\n1. Compare and contrast between the distribution of sickle cell trait and malaria in Africa.    [2 marks]\n\n2.\n    1. State the mode of inheritance of sickle cell anaemia.    [1 mark]\n\n    2. Using a genetic diagram, determine the probability of a child having sickle cell anaemia, if both their parents are carriers of the disease.        [2 marks]\n\n3. Blood samples were taken from children to create the graph. Suggest one difference between the appearance of blood cells in Hb AA and Hb AS samples.    [1 mark]\n\n4. State at which age there is the largest difference between the number of parasites found in the blood of Hb AA and Hb AS samples.    [1 mark]\n\n5. Suggest one difficulty of collecting sufficient data for this study. [1 mark]\n\n6. Using your own knowledge and the data provided, discuss whether carrying the sickle cell allele offers protection from malaria.    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    **Similarities**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is an overlap in the distribution of malaria and distribution of people with the sickle cell trait in the middle of Africa **OR** Sub-Saharan Africa        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is an overlap in a small region of East Africa, in the distribution of malaria and distribution of people with the sickle cell trait in the middle of Africa **OR** Sub-Saharan Africa [1 mark]\n\n    **Differences**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ malaria is present in Northern Africa **BUT** there is less than 1% **OR** a very low percentage of people with the sickle cell trait in Northern Africa    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ malaria is present in Northern Africa **BUT** there is less than 1% **OR** a very low percentage of people with the sickle cell trait in Northern Africa    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is a much wider distribution of malaria across the continent **COMPARED** to the distribution of the sickle cell trait **OR** sickle cell trait is mainly found in western and central Africa **WHEREAS** malaria is distributed as a band across the central third of Africa and at the Northern tip                             [1 mark]\n\n    *Answer must include at least one difference and one similarity to gain full marks*\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    There is an **overlap** in the **distribution of malaria and distribution of people with the sickle cell trait** ($\\checkmark$) across Sub-Saharan Africa  However across the whole continent, there is a **much wider distribution of malaria** across the continent **compared** to the distribution of the sickle cell trait ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ autosomal recessive    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The mode of inheritance for the sickle cell trait is **autosomal recessive** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    2.  Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ alleles must be written correctly for both parents; Hb:sup[A] Hb:sup[S] **OR** AS    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ correct genotypes for all four potential offspring in a punnett square; Hb:sup[A] Hb:sup[A], Hb:sup[S] Hb:sup[A], Hb:sup[S] Hb:sup[A], Hb:sup[S]Hb:sup[S] OR AA, SA, SA, SS        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  25 % **OR** 0.25  **OR** ¼     [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0793ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0793ANSA\">\n\n    There is a **25 %** ($\\checkmark$)  chance of having offspring who have sickle cell anaemia.\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Hb AS blood samples would have a mixture of sickle cell shaped red blood cells and normal **OR** biconcave red blood cells **WHEREAS** HbAA blood samples would have only normal **OR** biconcave shaped red blood cells    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    One difference between the blood samples is that Hb AS blood samples would have a **mixture** of **sickle cell shaped** red blood cells and **normal shaped** red blood cells whereas HbAA blood samples would have **only normal shaped red blood cells** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **one** of the following\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 3 years old        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0793ANSB.png\" alt=\"BI0793ANSB\">\n\n    The age at which there is the largest difference between the number of parasites found in the Hb AA and Hb AS samples is **3 years old** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ have to collect data from a large continent    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ children cannot always give consent, so consent has to be sought from parents    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ limited access to healthcare facilities to collect blood samples [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ collecting and analysing blood samples for malarial parasites can be time consuming **OR** costly    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cultural barriers **OR** collecting blood samples may be viewed with suspicion **OR** reluctance in some communities    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    One difficulty in collecting sufficient data for this study is **collecting data from a large continent** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    **Yes because…**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the presence of the sickle cell allele in the population overlaps with the prevalence of malaria, due to selective pressures    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ from ages 3 - 9 children carrying the sickle cell anaemia trait have a lower density of malarial parasites in their blood compared to those that don’t carry the trait [1 mark]\n\n    **No because…**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is incomplete overlap between the prevalence of malaria and carrying the sickle cell anaemia trait [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the study comparing malarial parasite density between Hb AA and Hb AS children, only focuses on the first ten years of life     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at ten years old there is no difference in malarial parasite density between the Hb AA and Hb AS children    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at two years old Hb AS children have a slightly higher malarial parasite density than Hb AA children [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Having the sickle cell anaemia trait may offer some protection from malaria, as the **sickle cell trait overlaps with the persistent prevalence of malaria** due to selective pressures ($\\checkmark$), however the study comparing parasite density in HbAA and HbAS children, shows that at **ten years old** there is **no difference** in malarial parasite density between the Hb AA and Hb AS children ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0794","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0794\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe diagram shows how the effect of growing bacteria with different antibiotics can be investigated. A study was conducted to test the degree of antibiotic resistance that bacteria from a hospital door handle had developed. Measurements of the zone of inhibition were taken every 3 days, for 15 days.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0793A.png\" alt=\"BI0793A\">\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0794B.png\" alt=\"BI0794B\">\n\n1. State which antibiotic is the least effective at preventing bacterial growth. [1 mark]\n\n2. State two variables that must be controlled in this experiment, other than those already given. [2 marks]\n\n3.\n 1. Identify the anomalous result from the study. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Suggest how the anomalous result may have arisen. [1 mark]\n\n4. Explain how natural selection can lead to antibiotic resistance in bacteria. [2 marks]\n\n5. The researchers suggested that vancomycin could be used more extensively to treat bacterial infections. Discuss to what extent you agree with this. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ amoxicillin [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The least effective antibiotic is **amoxicillin** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature that bacteria are incubated at [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the type of agar [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the density of bacteria plated [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the concentration of antibiotic [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the humidity that the bacteria are incubated at [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the concentration of oxygen supplied to the bacteria [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the size of the petri dish [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sterilisation of equipment **AND** area that bacteria are studied in [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ size of the disc soaked in antibiotic [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Two variables that must be controlled in this experiment are the **temperature** ($\\checkmark$) that the petri dishes are stored in and the **type of agar** ($\\checkmark$) that the bacteria are grown on.\n\n3.\n 1. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 21.9 mm:sup[2] **OR** cefaclor on day 12 [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The anomalous result is **21.9** mm:sup[2] ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 2. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ not measuring the diameter **OR** radius accurately [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ not calculating the area of the zone of inhibition accurately [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ contamination [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ natural variability in bacterial growth [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The anomalous result may have arisen from the researcher **not measuring the diameter accurately** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ bacterium within a population develops a random mutation that confers resistance [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mutation spreads through population through transfer of plasmid **OR** horizontal transfer **OR** reproduction **OR** conjugation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ bacteria without the mutation **OR** that are susceptible to antibiotics are killed by antibiotics, reducing competition for those with the resistance gene [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ bacteria with the mutation survive and breed [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n A bacterium within a population develops a **random mutation** which confers resistance ($\\checkmark$) to antibiotics, and this mutation can **spread** throughout the population through **horizontal transfer** ($\\checkmark$), so that more bacteria are resistant to antibiotics. The bacteria with resistance are more likely to **survive** ($\\checkmark$) when treated with antibiotics, and so can go on to **reproduce** so that the population of antibiotic-resistant bacteria increases.\n\n5. Any **three** of the following:\n\n **Agree because…**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it had the largest zone of inhibition over the whole study [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the zone of inhibition didn’t change drastically in size over the 15 days [1 mark]\n\n **Disagree because…**\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ if it is overused, it could lead to more resistance to vancomycin in a range of bacteria [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ only bacteria from door handles were tested [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the study does not include repeats [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the study only used a limited number of antibiotics [1 mark]\n\n *Answer must include at least one point from agree and one point from disagree to achieve full marks*\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The evidence from the study supports the suggestion of the researchers that vancomycin could be used more extensively to treat bacterial infections as it had a **larger zone of inhibition** compared to the rest of the antibiotics ($\\checkmark$) across the whole study. However, it shouldn’t be used to treat bacterial infections as **only bacteria from door handles** were tested ($\\checkmark$) and other locations in the hospital may have bacteria that are resistant to vancomycin. Also, its overuse could result in bacteria eventually **developing resistance** to it ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0797","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0797\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe equipment below can be used to study the effect of temperature on the activity of the enzyme amylase.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0797A.png\" alt=\"BI0797A\">\n\n1. Outline how this equipment could be used to set up a study into the effect of temperature on amylase activity, up to the point of getting the results. [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline how the rate of reaction of the breakdown of starch by amylase could be measured using the equipment given. [2 marks]\n\n3. Describe a suitable control experiment for this study. [2 marks]\n4. Suggest **one** way to increase the reliability of the data that can be gathered from this experiment. [1 mark]\n\n5. Using the axis below draw a graph of the expected results from the study in part (a).[2 marks]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0797B.png\" alt=\"BI0797B\">","markScheme":"1. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ measuring cylinder is used to measure equal volumes of substrate, **OR** starch, **AND** equal volumes of enzyme, **OR** amylase, into test tubes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$set up 5 different water baths at a range of at least 5 different temperatures **OR** set up water baths at 0, 20, 40, 60 and 80 :sup[o]C **OR** 5 sensible temperatures given [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ place test tubes containing starch and a test tube containing amylase in each of the water baths to equilibrate **OR** to get to the temperature of the water bath, before mixing together [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ enzyme **OR** amylase, and substrate **OR** starch, are mixed together in test tubes in the water bath for a fixed amount of time **OR** at least 5 minutes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ a variable that must be controlled is the pH **OR**, concentration of substrate **OR** starch **OR** substrate of enzyme **OR** amylase, **OR** the time that the substrate and enzyme are incubated together for in the water bath **OR** the volume of enzyme **OR** the volume of starch suspension [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the presence of starch can be tested for using iodine solution [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ a positive test for starch is iodine solution turning dark blue **OR** black [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n To study the effect of temperature on amylase activity, the **measuring cylinder** can be used to measure an **equal volume of starch suspension** and an **equal volume of amylase** ($\\checkmark$) into individual test tubes. Five different water baths should be set up with a **range of at least five temperatures** ($\\checkmark$) and a test tube containing starch and a test tube containing amylase, should be placed in each water bath to **equilibrate** before being mixed together ($\\checkmark$). Next, the amylase and starch should be **mixed together** and incubated at different temperatures for a **fixed time** ($\\checkmark$). The presence of starch can be tested for using **iodine solution** ($\\checkmark$), which turns dark **blue or black** in the presence of starch ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ take samples from the mixtures in each water bath at regular intervals **AND** test the solution with iodine solution **OR** place samples of the mixtures on a spotting tile at regular time intervals **AND** test the solution with iodine solution [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ record time when the iodine solution stays orange **OR** record time when the iodine solution no longer changes colour **OR** record time when no starch remains [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ calculate the rate of starch breakdown by taking the reciprocal of the time interval for each sample **OR** the rate can be calculated using the formula; rate = 1/ time [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The rate of reaction of the breakdown of starch by amylase can be measured by taking samples of the **mixture** of amylase and starch, from **each temperature every minute, for up to 15 minutes** and can be tested with **iodine solution** ($\\checkmark$). The **time taken for the iodine solution to no longer change colour** should be recorded ($\\checkmark$) and the rate of starch breakdown can be calculated using the formula; **1/time** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ using the same apparatus **AND** volume of solution [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ replace amylase **OR** enzyme [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at the same range of temperatures [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ there should be no change in the concentration of starch **OR** presence of starch can be confirmed by testing solution with iodine solution [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n A control experiment would be using the **same apparatus** and **volume of solutions** ($\\checkmark$), but **replace amylase with water** ($\\checkmark$) and conducting the same experiment with the **same range of temperatures** ($\\checkmark$). There should be **no change in the concentration of starch** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carry out repeats [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n To increase the reliability of the experiment, you could carry out **repeats** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature (°C) should be on the x-axis **AND** rate of reaction **OR** activity of enzyme should be on the y-axis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ line graph should rise rapidly to a maximum point and drop steeply [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0797ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0797ANSA\">"},{"ref":"BI0799","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0799\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nThe micrograph below shows *E. coli* cells.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0799A.png\" alt=\"BI0799A\">\n[Source: Adapted from Highton, P., 1968. *E.coli image.jpeg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 29th January 2023 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:E.coli_image.jpg. Copyright under Creative CommonsCC0 1.0]\n\n1. Identify the structure labelled I in the micrograph. [1 mark]\n\n2. If the magnification of the image is x36,000 and the length of cell II in the image is 54 mm, calculate the actual length of cell II in micrometres (µm). [2 marks]\n\n3. State **one** feature, other than the structure labelled I, that can be used to identify the cells in this micrograph as prokaryotic cells. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ Nucleoid **OR** region of circular DNA **OR** region of bacterial chromosome **OR** circular DNA    [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:  \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Structure I is the **nucleoid** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ = $\\left(\\dfrac{\\text{54000 µm}}{\\text{36000}}\\right) $\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ = 1.5µm    [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:  \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Magnification = x36000\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Length in image = 54mm x 1000 = 54000 µm\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Actual length\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ = $\\left(\\dfrac{\\textbf{54000 µm}}{\\textbf{36000}}\\right) $  ($\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ = **1.5 µm** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell wall of peptidoglycan    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell in binary fission        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ no compartments in cell **OR** no membrane bound organelles **OR** no nucleus    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:  \\\n    The cell does **not have** a nucleus, or any other **membrane bound organelles** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0800","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0800\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA student set up an experiment as in the diagram below, to investigate how temperature affects the rate of transpiration in plants.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0800.png\" alt=\"BI0800\">\n[Source: Adapted from Knott, T. (2006, August). *File: Potometer.png*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved May 3, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Potometer.png. CC-BY-SA-3.0-migrated]\n\n1.\n    1. Deduce in which direction the bubble will move as transpiration occurs.    [1 mark]\n\n    2. State one abiotic factor that needs to be kept constant in this experiment. [1 mark]\n\nThis table shows data collected by a student when they used the piece of apparatus in (a) to investigate the effect of temperature on the rate of transpiration.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/1.JPEG\" alt=\"1\">\n\n2.\n    1. Describe, using the data provided, the relationship between temperature and the rate of water uptake.    [1 mark]\n\n    2. Explain the change in water uptake from 20 ⁰C to 35 ⁰C.    [2 marks]\n\n    3. Suggest a reason for the lower rate of transpiration at 40 ⁰C.    [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to the left **OR** towards the plant **OR** away from the beaker        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    As transpiration occurs, the bubble will move **towards the plant** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    2. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ humidity  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ air movement **OR** wind speed [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light intensity [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water availability [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ atmospheric pressure [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    One abiotic factor that needs to be kept constant in this experiment is **wind speed** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    1. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rate of water uptake increases up to 30 ⁰C **AND** then decreases above 30 ⁰C **OR** rate of water uptake is highest at 30 ⁰C with 2.2 mm min:sup[-1] **AND** rate of water uptake decreases at 35 ⁰C and 40 ⁰C with 2.0 and 1.2 mm min:sup[-1] respectively         [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Rate of water uptake **increases from** 1.6 mm min:sup[-1] at **20 ⁰C** to 2.2 mm min:sup[-1] at **30 ⁰C** and **then decreases** to 1.2 mm min:sup[-1] at **40 ⁰C** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    2. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water uptake is lower at low temperatures because water evaporates more slowly at low temperatures **OR** because molecules have less energy at low temperatures    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water uptake increases at higher temperatures because transpiration is faster at higher temperatures **OR** water evaporates more quickly at higher temperatures    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stomata close at higher temperatures to reduce water loss **OR** reduced transpiration at 35 ⁰C to conserve water in the plant **OR** reduced transpiration at 35 ⁰C because stomata are closed    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Water uptake increases at higher temperatures because **evaporation is faster at higher temperatures** ($\\checkmark$). There is reduced transpiration at 35 ⁰C because **stomata close to reduce water loss** ($\\checkmark$) to conserve water.\n\n2.\n    3.  any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Greater stomatal closure at 40 ⁰C to conserve water     [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    At 40 ⁰C **stomata are closed** ($\\checkmark$) to conserve water."},{"ref":"BI0802","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0802\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0802A.png\" alt=\"BI0802A\">\n\nThe following results were obtained when a researcher investigated the effect of temperature and pH on the germination of black-eyed peas. He placed the seeds in petri dishes with some cotton wool spread out evenly at the bottom. He then added water every day and monitored the growth by counting the seeds that had germinated.\n\nThe results below on the left show the cumulative percentage of seeds that germinated over a 28 day period. Different temperatures (5, 10, 15, 20 and 25 :sup[o]C) were investigated by placing the petri dishes in ovens at the appropriate temperature.\nAgain, pH was investigated by adding water (with pH adjusted to 2, 3.5, 5, 7, 9, 11 and 12.5) as shown in the results.\n\nThe results on the right show the final percentage of seeds that germinated at the different temperatures and pH.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0802B.png\" alt=\"BI0802B\">\n[Source:Adapted from Cherrate, Mustapha, et al.( Mar,2023) *“Effects of Temperature, Ph, and Salinity on Seed Germination of Acinos Alpinus Subsp*. Meridionalis and FTIR Analysis of Molecular Composition Changes.” MDPI, Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute, Retrieved May 5, 2023, from https://www.mdpi.com/2071-1050/15/6/4793, Copyright under CC0 4.0]\n\nThe percentage germination rate was calculated as follows, for the days the experiment was monitored.\n\nGermination rate = (number of seeds that germinated / total number of seeds) x 100 %\n\nA student wanted to set up an experiment to check the effect of salinity on germination and plant growth, using the data from above.\n\n1. Based on the results shown,\n 1. State the optimum temperature for germination of black-eyed peas. [1 mark]\n\n 2. State the optimum pH for growth of black-eyed peas. [1 mark]\n\n2. As an extension of the studies shown above, state what the student would\nneed to replace the pH solutions with if they wanted to investigate the effect of salinity on the germination of peas? [1 mark]\n\n3. Other than pH and temperature, identify two controlled variables for setting up the experiment [2 marks]\n\n4. Other than germination rate, state two other dependent variables that could be monitored to see the effect of salinity on the growth of black-eyed peas. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 15 :sup[o]C [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The optimum temperature for the germination of black eyed peas is **15 :sup[o]C** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ pH7 [1 mark]\n\n (6.75 to 7.25 acceptable)\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The optimum pH for the germination of black eyed peas is **pH 7** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ by making equal volumes of aqueous solutions containing different masses of salt (NaCl) and adding this to the different petri dishes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ adding water to the control experiment, and adding at least five different concentrations of saline solutions to subsequent petri dishes [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The student can set up different petri dishes with the same numbers of black eyed peas. The student can then add water to the control plate, and **five different concentrations of saline solutions** ($\\checkmark$) to the other petri dishes.\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ adding equal volumes of water and salt solutions (regularly) to all the petri dishes on a daily basis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ counting the germinated seeds for all the petri dishes at the same time of day [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ all the petri dishes are so placed that they receive the same light intensity on a daily basis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ number of seeds placed in the petri dishes **OR** placed same distance apart [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sizes of the petri dishes are the same [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ same mass of cotton wool [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ peas from the same source **OR** stored in similar conditions **OR** same age [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ accept any other valid control [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Other than pH and temperature, controlled variables are **the number of seeds** ($\\checkmark$) placed in the petri dishes, and making sure **the germinated seeds are counted for all the petri dishes at the same time** ($\\checkmark$) on a daily basis.\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fresh **OR** dry weight (of plants at end of experiment) [1]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ shoot length [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ root length [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ shoot to root ratio [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ number of root hairs [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ shoot **OR** root, width [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Other than germination rate, two dependent variables that could be measured are **shoot length** ($\\checkmark$) and **root length** ($\\checkmark$) at the end of the experiment."},{"ref":"BI0803","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0803\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nTo understand the effects of abiotic stress, investigations were carried out using barley seeds. The researchers wanted to select strains tolerant to drought. Petri dishes were set up with some cotton wool spread out evenly at the bottom and the barley seeds placed on top, as shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0803.png\" alt=\"BI0803\">\n\nTo simulate the effects of drought, polyethylene glycol (PEG) was added at a concentration of 20% to experimental petri dishes while distilled water (without PEG) was added to control petri dishes.\n\nGermination percentage was calculated as follows:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0803A.png\" alt=\"BI0803A\">\n\nwhere ‘n’ is number of seeds germinated on the final day and ‘N’ is the total number of seeds.\n\nGermination pace is expressed as GP\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0803B.png\" alt=\"BI0803B\">\n\nWhere **N** is the total number of germinated seeds at the end of the experiment and n is the number of germinated seeds on day **g** (1, 2, 3..).\n\nThe results obtained are shown below:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0803C.png\" alt=\"BI0803C\">\n[Source: Adapted from Thabet, Samar G., et al.(Nov, 2018) *Genetic Basis of Drought Tolerance during Seed Germination in Barley*.PLOS ONE, PLoS ONE 13(11):e0206682.Retrieved May 5, 2023, from https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0206682 Copyright under CC0 4.0]\n\n1. Based on the results shown, explain how a drought is likely to affect a crop such as barley. [2 marks]\n\n2. State another abiotic factor, apart from water, that is required for germination of seeds. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the germination rate of the barley crop will decrease overall [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the percentage of seeds that germinate is reduced to approximately 80% [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the pace at which the seeds germinate will decrease [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ delay in the time taken by the seeds to germinate will delay growth of the crop [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **germination rate will decrease** ($\\checkmark$), meaning fewer seeds will germinate than when there is no drought and there will be a **delay in the time taken** ($\\checkmark$) for the seeds to germinate during a drought period.\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ suitable temperature **OR** warmth [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ suitable pH [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Another abiotic factor required for seed germination is **oxygen** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0806","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0806\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nThe following graphs show the concentration of atmospheric carbon dioxide over time at Mauna Loa Observatory in Hawaii, United States.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0806A.png\" alt=\"BI0806A\">\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0806B.png\" alt=\"BI0806B\">\n[Source: Adapted from US Department of Commerce, NOAA. *“Global Monitoring Laboratory - Carbon Cycle Greenhouse Gases.”* GML, ( Oct. 2005). Retrieved 18 May from gml.noaa.gov/ccgg/trends/. Copyright under CC0 4.0]\n\n1. Describe the overall trend in atmospheric carbon dioxide levels over time. [1 mark]\n\n2. State one factor that has significantly contributed to increases in atmospheric carbon dioxide concentration as seen in the graphs.    [1 mark]\n\n3. Explain why the concentration of atmospheric carbon dioxide fluctuates annually. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. The following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide levels in the atmosphere have been rising continuously since the 1960s [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Carbon dioxide levels in the atmosphere **have increased from 1960 to 2020** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increasing use **OR** increase in combustion of fossil fuels to provide energy    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in the numbers of cars and other vehicles [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ deforestation **OR** land use changes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ improper waste management **OR** decomposition of organic waste in landfills   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ industrial activities    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The main cause has been the **increased number of cars** and other vehicles ($\\checkmark$) being used.\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ during the summer (in the northern hemisphere), **OR** spring (in the northern hemisphere) **OR** growing season, an increase in vegetation **OR** biomass **OR** temperature causes an increase in photosynthesis which results in increased fixation of carbon dioxide, causing a decrease in atmospheric concentration     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ during the autumn (in the northern hemisphere) **OR** winter (in the northern hemisphere), there is a decrease in photosynthesis and an increase in respiration due to cooler temperatures which results in an increase of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vegetation decay  during the dormant season **OR** autumn **OR** winter (in the northern hemisphere) and carbon dioxide is released        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fluctuations in ocean carbon exchange in response to temperature changes **OR** cooler temperatures more carbon dioxide absorbed by oceans, reducing atmospheric carbon dioxide **OR** warmer temperatures ocean releases carbon dioxide into the atmosphere [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The annual fluctuations in atmospheric carbon dioxide concentrations are primarily driven by the **seasonal patterns** of **photosynthesis** and **respiration in plants**.  In the northern hemisphere, there is an **increase** in **plant growth** in the **summer**, which **increases carbon dioxide fixation** by **photosynthesis** ($\\checkmark$). In **winter**, there is a **decrease in photosynthesis**, so **reduced carbon dioxide uptake** by plants ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0807","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0807\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe image below shows global sea levels from the years 1880 to 2020.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0807.png\" alt=\"BI0807\">\n[Source: Adapted from Wikipedia, ( May 2023), *“Sea Level Rise.”*\nRetrieved 17 May 2023, from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea_level_rise Copyright under CC0 3.0]\n\n1. There is a trend line showing changes in sea level from 1920 to 1960 and another trend line showing changes in sea level between 1980 and 2020. Describe the differences in sea level change between these two periods of time. [1 mark]\n\n2. Outline the reasons for linking atmospheric carbon dioxide levels to the rise in sea levels. [2 marks]\n\nEcosystems growing in salt marshes are able to help reduce the effects of climate change by the sequestration (capture) of carbon. Sea level rise (SLR) has affected these ecosystems by flooding and eroding coastal areas.\n\nThe results from a recent study modelling the effects of a one-metre sea level rise on carbon sequestration by salt marshes in New York, United States, are shown in the graph below. The data shows the predicted effects by the year 2100.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0807B.png\" alt=\"BI0807B\">\n[Source: Adapted from Tanner, Katherine, and Aaron L. Strong. ( Mar, 2023) *Sustainability 2023, 15(6), 4733; “Assessing the Impact of Future Sea Level Rise on Blue Carbon Ecosystem Services on Long Island, New York.” MDPI*, Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute. Retrieved May 15, 2023, from doi.org/10.3390/su15064733, Copyright under CC0 4.0]\n\n3. Suggest an explanation for the difference in carbon sequestration between no SLR and one meter SLR, shown in the prediction graph. [1 mark]\n\n4. Draw a diagram of the carbon cycle for a terrestrial ecosystem, and include carbon sinks, and processes associated with the cycle. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. The following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the trend lines show that the increase in sea levels for the years 1920 to 1960 is not as steep as seen for the years 1980 to 2020 [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The increase in sea levels (as shown by the trend lines) **is steeper for the years from 1980 to 2020** ($\\checkmark$) compared to the years from 1920 to 1960.\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increasing carbon dioxide levels cause an increase in the temperature of the atmosphere [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increasing atmospheric temperatures cause glaciers **OR** ice sheets to melt more rapidly which raises sea levels [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increasing ocean temperatures cause thermal expansion of the ocean waters causing sea levels to rise [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Increasing carbon dioxide levels **cause an increase in atmospheric temperature** ($\\checkmark$) which can lead to **increased melting of ice sheets** ($\\checkmark$) and a rise in sea levels.\n\n3. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the sea level rise may damage photosynthesising organisms **OR** producers, in the salt marshes **OR** coastal ecosystems so decrease the capture of carbon [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ erosion may release carbon into the atmosphere [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ rising sea level increases the depth of water in salt marshes resulting in less light reaching aquatic photosynthesising organisms' habitats [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Sea level rises may kill some photosynthesising organisms and so **decrease the capture of carbon by photosynthesis** ($\\checkmark$) thereby affecting the carbon cycle.\n\n4. Any **three** of the following sinks or storages with at least **three** different connecting processes relevant to those sinks or storages:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ CO$_2$ in the air **AND** combustion **OR** respiration **OR** photosynthesis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon compounds in producers **AND** photosynthesis **OR** respiration **OR** consumed by **OR** death [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon compounds in consumers **AND** respiration **OR** consumed by **OR** death [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon compounds in saprotrophs OR decomposers **OR** detritivores **AND** decomposition **OR** respiration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon compounds in dead organisms **AND** fossilisation **OR** death **OR** decomposition [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon compounds in fossil fuels **AND** combustion **OR** fossilisation [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0807BII.png\" alt=\"BI0807BII\">"},{"ref":"BI0808","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0808\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe diagram below shows the food web in a salt marsh, one of the most productive habitats on Earth.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0808A.png\" alt=\"BI0808A\">\n\nThe numbers on the arrows show the percentage of gross primary production that flows into different trophic levels or is used in plant respiration.\n\n1. Define gross and net primary production. [2 marks]\n\n2. Identify the herbivores shown in the system. [1 mark]\n\n3. If the energy available to consumers from producers in organic compounds is 300 g m:sup[-2] yr:sup[-1] (dry), calculate the amount of energy that is lost by export to the tide. [1 mark]\n\n4.\n 1. The table below shows the NPP for three trophic levels in a food web. Draw a pyramid of energy from the information in this table, labelling it appropriately with the trophic levels and corresponding NPP values (rounded to 3 significant figures as in the table). [2 marks]\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0808B.png\" alt=\"BI0808B\">\n\n 2. Outline a limitation of the ecological pyramid of energy. [1 mark]\n\n5. Explain how a food web provides a more accurate representation of an ecosystem’s feeding relationships than a food chain. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following (one point for GPP and one point for NPP):\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ gross primary productivity is the energy produced by the process of photosynthesis by green plants **OR** primary producers [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ GPP represents the total rate at which primary producers convert light energy into chemical energy [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ net primary productivity is the actual growth of the producer [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ net primary productivity is the energy available after the producer has carried out respiration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ net primary productivity is the Gross Primary Productivity minus energy lost in respiration **OR** NPP = GPP -R **OR** Net primary productivity = Gross Primary Productivity - Respiration [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Gross primary productivity is the total **rate** at which primary producers **convert** light energy into **chemical energy** ($\\checkmark$) in the form of organic compounds.\n\n Net primary productivity is the **energy** available **after** the producer has carried out **respiration** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. **Two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ aphids **AND** grasshoppers [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The two herbivores in the food web are **aphids** and **grasshoppers** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. The following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 120 (dry) gram m:sup[-2] yr:sup[-1] [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0808ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0808ANSA\">\n\n 25% = 300 (dry) g m:sup[-2] yr:sup[-1]\n\n proportion of the 300 (dry) g m:sup[-2] yr:sup[-1] that is exported by tide = 10/25\n\n 10/25 X 300 g m:sup[-2] yr:sup[-1] = 120 (dry) g m:sup[-2] yr:sup[-1]\n\n The net primary productivity exported to the tide is **120 (dry) g m:sup[-2] yr:sup[-1]** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.\n 1. The following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 3 appropriately sized rectangles representing the 3 trophic levels [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ levels labelled to show trophic level names, **OR** abbreviations from table, **AND** labelled with the corresponding numbers [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0808ANSB.png\" alt=\"BI0808ANSB\">\n\n4.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it does not address the same species belonging to two or more trophic levels [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it is not truly reflective of energy flow in an ecosystem because in nature, it is not usually a simple food chain [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it does not account for detritivores (usually) or saprophytes [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The pyramid of energy does not address species that belong to **two or more trophic levels** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ food chains include only one type of food the organism eats, whereas a food web is a network of interconnecting food chains **OR** more realistic way of showing interconnections between organisms [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ food webs reflect how interactions affect the energy transfer between trophic levels, whereas food chains show a simplified flow of energy [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ food webs indicate how stable, **OR** resilient, the ecosystem is [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ food webs account for the fact that organisms, **OR** omnivores, **OR** detritivores, can feed at multiple, **OR** many, trophic levels [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ food webs show interdependence **OR** show how the change in one population can affect another population [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Food chains only include **one type of food** the organism eats ($\\checkmark$), but the food web shows **all the different food sources** of the organism. Food webs show the **energy transfer between trophic levels** ($\\checkmark$), and also indicate how complex and **resilient the ecosystem is** ($\\checkmark$) to unforeseen changes.\n\n ​*​Check out our Key Concept video on Energy in Ecosystems for more.*"},{"ref":"BI0809","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0809\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nNeonicotinoids are synthetic compounds that are widely used as pesticides.  However, there are concerns about their effect on honey bees and other pollinating insects.  The table below shows the results from a study where honey bees (*Apis mellifera carnica*) were treated with non-lethal doses of two neonicotinoids, Imidacloprid and Clothianidin, and the effect it had on their homing instinct (ability to fly back to their hive).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0809A.png\" alt=\"BI0809A\">\n[Source: Adapted from Fischer, J., Müller, T., Spatz, A.-K., Greggers, U., Grünewald, B., & Menzel, R. (2014). *Neonicotinoids interfere with specific components of navigation in honeybees*. PLOS ONE. Retrieved June 7, 2023, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0091364 . Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n1. Describe the mode of action of pesticides containing neonicotinoids on cholinergic synapses in insects.    [2 marks]\n\n2. Using the data, outline the effect of the neonicotinoids on honey bees.    [2 marks]\n\n3. Calculate the percentage increase between the control and those treated with 0.6 μM of Imidacloprid which did **not** arrive back at their hive.     [1 mark]\n\n4. State what the dependent variable is in this study.    [1 mark]\n\n5.\n    1. Based on the data, predict the effect that exposing honey bees to neonicotinoids will have on the fitness of the honey bee colony.        [1 mark]\n\n    2. Explain your answer to part (i).                    [1 mark]\n\n6. The researchers concluded that neonicotinoids should not be used as pesticides due to their unintended effects on honey bees. Evaluate whether this data supports their conclusion.    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ neonicotinoids bind to acetylcholine receptors irreversibly **OR** cannot be broken down by acetylcholinesterase [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ acetylcholine is unable to bind to acetylcholine receptors [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ synaptic transmission is prevented [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Neonicotinoids work by **binding** to **acetylcholine receptors** that are present on cholinergic postsynaptic membranes **irreversibly** ($\\checkmark$) which **prevents acetylcholine from binding** to its own receptors ($\\checkmark$), **preventing synaptic transmission** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ treatment with neonicotinoids decreases the percentage of honey bees that arrive back at the hive **OR** increases the percentage of bees that do not arrive back at the hive [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ with higher doses of imidacloprid **OR** 0.9 µM of imidacloprid the percentage of bees that arrived back at the hive decreased dramatically, **OR** the percentage of bees that did not arrive back at the hive increased dramatically [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Treating honeybees with neonicotinoids **decreases the percentage of honey bees that arrive back** ($\\checkmark$) at the hive compared to the controls, and with the **highest dose of imidacloprid** the percentage of bees that arrived back at the hive **decreased by almost two thirds** ($\\checkmark$) compared to the controls.\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 124% **OR** 124. 4% **OR** 124.39% [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    percentage increase = ((new value - original value) ÷ original value) x 100 %\n\n    percentage increase = ((27.6 - 12.3) ÷ 12.3) x 100 % = **124** % ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the number of bees that arrive or do not arrive back at the hive [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nThe dependent variable in this study is the **number of bees** that **arrive** or **do not arrive** back at the hive ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5.\n    1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ decrease **OR** detrimental effect **OR** negative impact on health **OR** productivity [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Exposing the honey bees to neonicotinoids will **decrease** ($\\checkmark$) the fitness of the colony.\n\n5.\n    2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ less bees return to the hive lowering the number of matings [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ less bees return to the hive, so less nectar **OR** pollen **OR** food is brought back to the colony [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ less resources could impact the fertility and survival rates of queen bees [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ colony may be more susceptible to other stressors such as diseases **OR** parasites, **OR** environmental challenges [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    If less bees return to the hive, then there will be a **lower number of matings** ($\\checkmark$), lowering the fitness of the hive.\n\n6. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    *Answer must include at least one positive and one negative statement to achieve full marks.*\n\n    *Agree because…*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ neonicotinoids affect the ability of honey bees to return to their hives [1 mark]\n\n    *Disagree because…*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ only tested one species of honey bees [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ small sample size [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ only two types of neonicotinoids were tested [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Neonicotinoids should not be used as pesticides because they **affect the ability of honey bees to return to their hive** ($\\checkmark$), which decreases the fitness of the colony and may also affect their ability to pollinate. However, the study only tested a **small sample of honey bees** ($\\checkmark$), therefore, it doesn’t mean that neonicotinoids should not be used."},{"ref":"BI0811","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0811\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nIn guinea pigs (*Cavia porcellus*), long hair (L) is dominant to short hair (l), and curly hair (C) is dominant to straight hair (c). A test cross was carried out between male heterozygous long, curly-haired guinea pigs and female short, straight-haired guinea pigs and a chi-squared test (χ2) was carried out on the offspring.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0811A.png\" alt=\"BI0811A\">\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0811B.png\" alt=\"BI0811B\">\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0811C.png\" alt=\"BI0811C\">\n\n1.\n 1. Using a genetic diagram, state the expected ratio of the cross between the two parents. [3 marks]\n\n 2. Identify the recombinants. [1 mark]\n\n2.\n 1. Calculate the ratio of the observed phenotypes. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Suggest a reason for the difference between the expected and observed ratios. [1 mark]\n\n3.\n 1. State the null hypothesis in this study. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Deduce with reasons whether the null hypothesis should be accepted. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ parental gamete genotypes identified in the punnett square; male gametes: LC, Lc, lC, lc **AND** female gametes: lc [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ offspring genotypes identified as LlCc, Llcc, llCc, llcc [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 1:1:1:1 [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Father´s genotype = LlCc\n\n Mother’s genotype = llcc\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0811ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0811ANSA\">\n\n There is a **1:1:1:1** ratio ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Llcc **AND** llCc [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ long straight **AND** short curly [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The recombinants are **Llcc** and **llCc** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 4:2:1:3 **OR** 0.4: 0.2: 0.1 : 0.3 **OR** 0.368: 0.194: 0.110: 0.328 **OR** 0.37: 0.19: 0.11: 0.33 [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n 184 + 97 + 55 + 164 = 500\n\n Curly long hair = 184 ÷ 500 = 0.368\n\n Curly short hair = 97 ÷ 500 = 0.194\n\n Straight long hair = 55 ÷ 500 = 0.110\n\n Straight curly hair = 164 ÷ 500 = 0.328\n\n 0.368 : 0.194 : 0.110 : 0.328\n\n **0.4 : 0.2 : 0.1 : 0.3** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ genes located on the same chromosomes **OR** genes linked [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ frequent crossing over, during meiosis **OR** prophase 1 **OR** production of gametes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ multiple genes **OR** gene interactions, may influence expression of trait [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ environmental factors influencing gene expression [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The expected and observed phenotypic ratios may be different due to the two **genes being linked** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ there is no significant difference between the observed and expected frequencies of offspring phenotypes **OR** the observed offspring ratios are consistent with the expected ratios based on Mendelian genetics [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the genes are unlinked [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The null hypothesis is that there is **no significant difference** ($\\checkmark$) between the observed offspring ratios, and the expected ratios that are based on Mendelian genetics.\n\n3.\n 2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ null hypothesis should be rejected [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ expected ratio differs significantly from the observed ratio [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at three degrees of freedom **AND** the 0.05 significance level [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chi squared value **OR** 95.6 is higher than the critical value **OR** 7.815 [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n At a significance level of **0.05** and **three degrees of freedom** ($\\checkmark$), the calculated chi-squared value **exceeds the critical value** ($\\checkmark$). This indicates a **significant difference** between the observed and expected ratios ($\\checkmark$) and so the **null hypothesis should be rejected** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0812","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0812\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe table shows the results from a study on the percentage of cholesterol found in the cell membrane of epithelial skin cells of different animals.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0812A.png\" alt=\"BI0812A\">\n\n1. Calculate the mean percentage of cholesterol found in the cell membrane across all the animals listed in the table. [1 mark]\n\n2. State one role of cholesterol in the cell membrane. [1 mark]\n\n3. Suggest why arctic foxes have a greater mean percentage of cholesterol in their cell membranes than kangaroos. [2 marks]\n\n4. Estimate, with a reason, the percentage of cholesterol that would be found in the cell membranes of polar bears. [1 mark]\n\n5. Explain why the mass of cholesterol in the cell membranes is calculated as a percentage rather than a whole number. [2 marks]\n\n6. Suggest why the cells studied may contain a higher percentage of cholesterol than shown in the table. [1 mark]\n\n7.\n 1. Based on the data provided, suggest which animal in the study exhibits the highest fluidity in its epithelial skin cell membrane. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Explain your answer to part (i). [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 17.3 % **OR** 17.29 % **OR** 17.286 % [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n mean = sum of percentages / number of animals\n\n (10 + 12 + 15 + 18 + 28 + 8 + 30) ÷ 7 = **17.3** % ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to regulate the fluidity of the membrane **OR** to reduce rigidity of the membrane at lower temperatures **OR** to prevent excessive fluidity at higher temperatures [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ reduces the permeability of the membrane to hydrophilic particles **OR** reduces the permeability of the cell membrane to ions **OR** small polar molecules [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n One role of cholesterol in the cell membrane is to **regulate the fluidity** ($\\checkmark$) of the membrane.\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ arctic foxes live in cooler environments **OR** kangaroos live in warmer environments [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ a higher percentage of cholesterol will lower the temperature at which the membrane freezes **OR** becomes too rigid [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ so the membrane will be able to carry out normal functions efficiently **OR** maintain fluidity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ arctic foxes have a higher dietary intake of cholesterol than kangaroos **OR** kangaroos have a diet lower in cholesterol than arctic foxes [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Arctic foxes have more cholesterol in their cell membrane than kangaroos as **arctic foxes live in cooler environments** ($\\checkmark$) and a higher percentage of cholesterol in their membranes will **prevent** the membrane from **freezing** ($\\checkmark$), thus **maintaining the fluidity** of the membrane ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **one** of the following:\n\n *Answer must include a value in the correct range and a reason for the mark*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ value must be between 25% and 35% **BECAUSE** it lives in a similar climate to the arctic fox **OR** penguin [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ value must be between 25% and 35% **BECAUSE** they have a similar diet to the arctic fox **OR** a diet high in marine animals, which tend to have a higher cholesterol content [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **30%** ($\\checkmark$) because the polar bear lives in a similar, **cold** environment to the **arctic fox** and the **penguin** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cell membrane may be of different length, **OR** width, in different organisms [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to make the values comparable [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ easier to determine percent cholesterol from a sample of cells than to determine the mass of cholesterol in a single cell [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The mass of cholesterol is calculated as a percentage rather than a whole number, as the cell membrane may be of **different length and width** ($\\checkmark$) in different organisms and so it allows the values to be **compared** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the table only takes into account cholesterol present in the cell membrane [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ organelles **OR** endoplasmic reticulum **OR** nucleus **OR** Golgi apparatus **OR** mitochondria **OR** vacuoles **OR** lysosomes are membrane-bound and so also contain cholesterol [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The cells studied may contain a higher percentage of cholesterol than shown in the table, as the table **only** takes into account the **cholesterol that is present in the cell membrane** ($\\checkmark$) rather than the whole cell.\n\n7.\n 1. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ African elephant [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Arctic fox [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **African elephant** ($\\checkmark$) has the most fluid cell membrane.\n\n7.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n *African elephant because:*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at high temperatures cholesterol reduces ability of phospholipids to move, making the membrane more rigid **OR** less fluid **AND** African elephant has the lowest cholesterol [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cholesterol reduces the fluidity of plasma membrane at high temperatures **AND** African elephant has the lowest cholesterol and lives in warm environments [1 mark]\n\n *Actic fox because:*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at low temperatures cholesterol increases ability of phospholipids to move, making the membrane more fluid **AND** Arctic fox has the highest cholesterol and lives in cold environments [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Cholesterol **limits the fluidity** of the plasma membrane at **high temperatures**, and the African elephant, which lives in **warm environments**, has the **lowest percentage of cholesterol** in the epithelial cell membrane ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0813","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0813\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nA study has been conducted on the effect of hormonal contraception pollution on the mating calls of the adult male African clawed frog (*Xenopus laevis*). The spectrogram shows the frequency and pitch of clicks (mating call) of control *X.laevis* and those exposed to oestrogen.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0813ai.png\" alt=\"BI0813ai\">\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0813aii.png\" alt=\"BI0813aii\">\n[Source: Adapted from Hoffmann, F., & Kloas, W. (2012, February 15). *Estrogens can disrupt amphibian mating behavior*. PLOS ONE. Retrieved May 13, 2023, from https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0032097.  Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n1. Distinguish between the clicks in the spectrogram between the control *X. laevis* and the *X. laevis* treated with oestrogen in 1 second.            [2 marks]\n\nThe graph shows the average duration of the *X. laevis* mating calls when exposed to the hormone oestrogen.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0813b.png\" alt=\"BI0813b\">\n[Source: Adapted from Hoffmann, F., & Kloas, W. (2012, February 15). *Estrogens can disrupt amphibian mating behavior*. PLOS ONE. Retrieved May 13, 2023, from https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0032097.  Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n2. Describe the effect of oestrogen on the duration of the mating call shown in this study.        [2 marks]\n\n3. Explain why it is important to have the *X.laevis* control group.        [1 mark]\n\n4. Identify the independent variable in this study.    [1 mark]\n\n5.\n    1. Predict the relative reproductive success of *X.laevis* exposed to 29.6 ngL:sup[-1] of oestrogen.            [1 mark]\n\n    2. Outline the possible consequences of oestrogen exposure on *X. laevis*.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ control *X.laevis* have a more regular click pattern **WHEREAS** *X.laevis* treated with oestrogen have an irregular click pattern        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ control *X.laevis* have a lower pitch of clicks **OR** a pitch of approximately 2.5 kHz **WHEREAS** *X.laevis* have a higher pitch of clicks **OR** a pitch of approximately 3 kHz    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the *X.laevis*  exposed to oestrogen have longer clicks **COMPARED** to the control *X.laevis* **OR** the control *X.laevis* has more frequent clicks **OR** faster clicks than the *X.laevis* exposed to oestrogen        [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n   <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0813ci.png\" alt=\"BI0813ci\">\n   <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0813cii.png\" alt=\"BI0813cii\">\n    [Source: Adapted from Hoffmann, F., & Kloas, W. (2012, February 15). *Estrogens can disrupt amphibian mating behavior*. PLOS ONE. Retrieved May 13, 2023, from https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0032097.  Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n    The control *X.laevis* have a more **regular click pattern compared** to the *X.laevis* treated with oestrogen, which have an **irregular click pattern** ($\\checkmark$). The control *X.laevis* have a **lower pitch** of clicks at approximately **2.5 kHz**, whereas the *X.laevis* that were exposed to oestrogen have a **higher pitch** of approximately **3 kHz** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 29.6 ngL:sup[-1] of oestrogen **OR** the highest concentration of oestrogen has the shortest mean mating call duration        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the lowest level of oestrogen OR 0.296 ngL:sup[-1] of oestrogen has the longest mean mating call duration    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at the middle concentration of oestrogen OR at ngL:sup[-1], the mean mating call duration was similar to the control OR only 1s longer than the control        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as oestrogen levels increase the mean duration of the mating call decreases            [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **longest** mean mating **call** of 33s was seen at the **lowest concentration of oestrogen** (0.296 ngL:sup[-1]) ($\\checkmark$).  *X.laevis* exposed to an **oestrogen concentration of 2.96 ngL:sup[-1]** had a mean mating call **duration similar** to the **control** ($\\checkmark$), just 1s longer.  However, at the **highest concentration of oestrogen** (29.6 ngL:sup[-1]), *X.laevis* had a much **lower** mean mating call **duration**, 4 seconds less than the control ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to ensure that any effect being seen on the mating call duration **OR** frequency of clicks **OR** pitch of clicks is only due to the effect of hormones **OR** oestrogen    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to allow for a baseline comparison            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows for establishing causality            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ helps to minimise the impact of confounding variables    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    It is important to have a control group because it helps establish that **any observed changes in mating call duration are due to oestrogen** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the concentration of oestrogen that *X.laevis* is exposed to [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The independent variable in this study is the **concentration of oestrogen** ($\\checkmark$) that the *X.laevis* is exposed to.\n\n5.\n    1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lower reproductive success than the control **OR** reduced successful matings    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The *X.laevis* exposed to oestrogen may have **lower** reproductive success than the control ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5.\n    2. Any **three** of the following\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *X.laevis* exposed to oestrogen have a higher pitch of clicks **OR** longer clicks **OR** a shorter mating call duration, so may not be recognised by female *X.laevis*    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ resulting in reduced mating opportunities and so reduced reproductive success    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *X.laevis*  exposed to oestrogen may have shorter life spans and die without passing their genes onto the next generation reducing population size and gene pool        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ this can result in the population being more vulnerable to disease **OR** environmental changes **OR** limit the population’s adaptive potential [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if the altered mating call resembles calls of a different species of frog **OR** attracts females from another species, this can result in hybridisation of the species    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ altered calls may disrupt the social dynamics **OR** may reduce ability of male to establish dominance over competitors **OR** defend territory [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Exposure to oestrogen in  *X.laevis* frogs could result in **reduced reproductive success due to reduced mating opportunities** ($\\checkmark$) as a result of females **not recognising the altered mating call** ($\\checkmark$). The population size and gene pool may also decrease if oestrogen pollution **reduces the lifespan of the frogs** ($\\checkmark$). Altered calls may also reduce the ability of the males to defend their territories against competitors and so may **disrupt the social dynamics** of the population ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0814","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0814\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nData was collected on the protein composition of venom samples taken from the Malayan Krait (*B. candidus*) found in three localities; Malaysia, Indonesia and Thailand. All samples were carefully transported and preserved at temperatures below 20 :sup[o]C. The graph shows the number of different types of protein found in each venom sample.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0814A.png\" alt=\"BI0814A\">\n[Source: Adapted from Rusmili, M., Othman, L., Abidin, SA., Yusof, FA., Ratanabanangkoon, K., Chanhome, L., Hodgson, W., Chaisakul, J. (2019). *Variations in neurotoxicity and proteome profile of Malayan krait (Bungarus candidus) venoms*. PLoS ONE 14(12): e0227122. Retrieved May 14, 2023, from https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0227122.Copyright-free]\n\n1.\n 1. Identify the independent and dependent variables in this investigation. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Venom samples were collected from different snakes, in different localities. Suggest two controlled variables, other than temperature, that would support the collection of comparable samples. [1 mark]\n\nBelow are the results from gel electrophoresis of one group of venom proteins.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0814B.png\" alt=\"BI0814B\">\n\n2.\n 1. Identify the group of venom proteins shown. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Explain how proteins are separated using gel electrophoresis. [2 marks]\n\nDifferent mice were envenomed with samples from each of the three localities. It was observed that the venom with the lowest neurotoxicity was from the Malayan krait sampled in Malaysia.\n\n3. Suggest why it was important to transport venom samples at temperatures below 20 :sup[o]C. [2 marks]\n\n4. List two advantages of using mice as model organisms for humans [1 mark]\n\nThe venom samples were further analysed, and it was suggested that the Three Finger Toxins group (3FTx) of proteins contributed significantly to the neurotoxicity of the venom. This prompted a closer analysis of the different types of 3FTx proteins found in each venom sample. The data was presented in a graph.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0814C.png\" alt=\"BI0814C\">\n[Source: Adapted from Rusmili, M., Othman, L., Abidin, SA., Yusof, FA., Ratanabanangkoon, K., Chanhome, L., Hodgson, W., Chaisakul, J. (2019).*Variations in neurotoxicity and proteome profile of Malayan krait (Bungarus candidus) venoms*. PLoS ONE 14(12): e0227122. Retrieved May 14, 2023, from https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0227122.Copyright-free]\n\n5. Based on the data, suggest which type of 3FTx protein is most likely responsible for differences in the levels of toxicity observed between the different venom samples. Justify your answer. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1. **BOTH** of the following (Students must indicate which is the independent and dependent variable):\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the locality of Malayan krait (independent variable) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ protein composition of venom samples (dependent variable) [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The independent variable is the **locality** of the Malayan krait (Malaysia, Thailand, or Indonesia), and the dependent variable is the **protein composition** of the venom sample ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ samples collected from snakes of similar sizes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ samples collected from snakes of similar ages [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ samples collected from snakes of the same sex [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ samples collected using the same equipment **OR** apparatus [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ samples stored using the same methods [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ samples collected at the same time of day [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ samples collected from snakes in a similar feeding condition **OR** samples not collected from snakes who have recently fed [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n In order to collect valid samples from snakes in different localities, you could collect samples from snakes of a **similar size** ($\\checkmark$) or **age** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ PI **OR** PI proteins [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **PI** proteins ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 2.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ gel electrophoresis separates proteins based on charge **OR** size **OR** molecular weight **OR** conformation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ proteins move toward the positive end of the gel [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ smaller proteins move further **OR** larger proteins move less [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Gel electrophoresis separates proteins based on their **size** ($\\checkmark$). Proteins with a smaller molecular weight will travel **further** than proteins with a heavier molecular weight ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ proteins can denature at high temperatures **OR** temperatures above 20 :sup[o]C may denature the proteins [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ denatured proteins may affect the neurotoxicity **OR** affect the potency of venom [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ venom components may include enzymes, so keeping them at a lower temperature could reduce the degradation of venom [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ prevention of bacterial growth **OR** decomposition [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Proteins **denature** ($\\checkmark$) at high temperatures, which could **impact the neurotoxicity** ($\\checkmark$) of the samples which need to be used in the experiments.\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fewer ethical considerations when using mice [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mice are easier to keep in a lab **OR** require less space **OR** fewer resources **OR** are more cost-effective [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mice reproduce quickly [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ share similar physiology to humans **OR** mice have a similar genome to humans [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mice can be genetically modified to study neurotoxins [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Mice are good model organisms to use for humans in research because there are **fewer ethical considerations** when using them; they also require **less space** ($\\checkmark$) and resources to keep them, so they are more cost-effective.\n\n5. **BOTH** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ non-conventional [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** they are the only type of 3FTx proteins that vary amongst the different localities **OR** because the other types of 3FTx proteins are the same amongst the three localities **OR** samples from Malaysia had the lowest neurotoxicity and the Malaysian samples had the lowest level of non-conventional proteins [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0814ANS.png\" alt=\"BI0814ANS\">\n\n **Non-conventional proteins** ($\\checkmark$) are most likely the cause of the differences in toxicity because they are the **only type of 3FTx** proteins that vary between the **different localities** ($\\checkmark$). In the Malaysian samples with the lowest neurotoxicity, the levels of non-conventional proteins were also the **lowest between all three localities** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0815","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0815\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe diagram shows the separation of photosynthetic pigments using the thin-layer chromatography (TLC) technique in the solvent propanone.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0815A.png\" alt=\"BI0815A\">\n\n1. Identify a pigment that is present in cabbage but **not** in spinach. [1 mark]\n\n2. Outline the method used to extract the pigments from the plant leaves. [2 marks]\n\n3.\n 1. Outline what information the Rf value provides in this experiment. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Calculate the Rf value of Xanthophyll, showing your working. [2 marks]\n\n4. Spinach also contains Lycopene, an orange/red pigment. Suggest why it is absent from the chromatogram. [1 mark]\n\n5. Deduce, with a reason, what pigment X is. [1 mark]\n\n6. Explain why the origin must be above the solvent at the start of the experiment. [1 mark]\n\n7. Explain why it is important that the TLC plate is held by its edges when setting up this experiment. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ xanthophyll [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n A photosynthetic pigment that is present in cabbage but not in spinach is **xanthophyll** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ grind the leaves up finely in propanone **OR** add propanone after grinding leaves up finely **OR** chop or finely cut the plant material into small pieces, add propanone and allow the mixture to stand for several hours [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ filter mixture to remove ground leaves **OR** separate propanone from leaf debris [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allow pigment to dry and rehydrate it using propanone [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n To extract pigment from the plant leaves, the plant leaves are **ground up finely in propanone** ($\\checkmark$) and then the mixture is **filtered** to remove the ground leaves ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ preliminary indication of the name of the pigment **OR** name of the pigment when compared to a reference table [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ relative mobility of a substance within a chromatographic system [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The Rf value indicates the **name of the pigment** when compared to a reference table ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n *1 mark can be awarded for the correct working, but an incorrect answer*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 2.1 cm ÷ 3.4 cm **OR** Rf value = distance moved by pigment from origin ÷ distance moved by solvent [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 0.6 **OR** 0.62 [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0815ansA.png\" alt=\"BI0815ansA\">\n\n Rf value = distance moved by pigment from origin ÷ distance moved by solvent\n\n Distance moved by pigment from origin = 2.7 - 0.6 = 2.1 cm \\\n Distance moved by solvent front from origin = 4.0 - 0.6 = 3.4 cm\n\n Rf value = 2.1 ÷ 3.4 = **0.6** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the pigment did not dissolve **OR** is insoluble in propanone **OR** pigment may have degraded during extraction [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Lycopene may be absent from the chromatogram as it is **insoluble** ($\\checkmark$) in propanone.\n\n5. Any **one** of the following:\n\n *Answer must include a pigment and a reason to gain the mark*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carotene **BECAUSE** it moved the same distance as X **OR** same Rf value as X [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Pigment X is likely to be **carotene** as it moved the **same distance** as X ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ so that the pigment only runs in one direction **OR** so that the pigment moves with the solvent [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to stop the pigment from dissolving into the propanone **OR** solvent [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it promotes even separation **OR** achieves better resolution between different pigments [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The origin is placed above the solvent at the start of the experiment to **stop the pigment from dissolving into the solvent** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n7. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ contamination from skin **OR** gloves can alter the results [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ minimises the risk of damaging or scratching the stationary phase, which could affect separation of pigments [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n It is important that TLC plates are held by the edges as **contamination from the skin** can **alter** the results ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0816","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0816\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe micrograph shows an image of a plant cell taken with a light microscope.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0816A.png\" alt=\"BI0816A\">\n[Source: Adapted from CNX OpenStax. (2016, May 27). *File:Figure 04 00 00new.jpg.* Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 14, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Figure_04_00_00new.jpg . Copyright free]\n\n1. Calculate the magnification of the image, showing your work. [2 marks]\n\n2. Label, with the letter N, a nucleus in the image. [1 mark]\n\n3. The image was taken using a light microscope. State how you deduce this from the image. [1 mark]\n\n4. Draw and label a diagram to show how the same type of plant cell shown in the micrograph would appear when examined with an electron microscope. [3 marks]\n\n5. State two differences that would be seen when looking at an electron micrograph of a bacterium cell compared to this plant cell. [2 marks]\n\n6. At first, the student who took this image could not see the cell when focusing the microscope. Suggest what may have caused this problem. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 4000 ÷ 50 **OR** size of image/ size of specimen [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 80 x [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0816ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0816ANSA\">\n [Source: Adapted from CNX OpenStax. (2016, May 27). *File:Figure 04 00 00new.jpg*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 14, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Figure_04_00_00new.jpg. Copyright free]\n\n Magnification = Image size ÷ Actual size\n\n Image size = 39 - 35 = 4 mm\n\n 1mm = 1000 µm\n\n 4mm = 4000 µm\n\n Actual size = 50 µm\n\n Magnification = **4000 µm ÷ 50 µm** ($\\checkmark$) = **80 x** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ labelled arrow N pointing to a nucleus [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0816ANSB.png\" alt=\"BI0816ANSB\">\n [Source: Adapted from CNX OpenStax. (2016, May 27). *File:Figure 04 00 00new.jpg*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 14, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Figure_04_00_00new.jpg. Copyright free]\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cannot see the ultrastructure of the cell **OR** ribosomes **OR** mitochondria **OR** rough endoplasmic reticulum **OR** Golgi apparatus **OR** vacuoles [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ low resolution [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n It is possible to deduce that the image was taken with a light microscope as the **ultrastructure** of the cell is **not visible** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ diagram must contain a large angular shape, with a labelled nucleus, cell membrane **AND** cell wall [1 mark]\n\n **Labels:**\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 80S ribosomes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mitochondria [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Golgi apparatus [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ rough endoplasmic reticulum [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ vacuole [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ smooth endoplasmic reticulum [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleolus [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0816ANSCOR.png\" alt=\"BI0816ANSCOR\">\n\n5. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 70s ribosomes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ flagellum [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleoid region [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ pili [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Two different structures that could be seen when looking at a bacterium as opposed to the plant cells through an electron microscope are **pili** ($\\checkmark$) and the **nucleoid region** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the light was off **OR** not bright enough **OR** diaphragm was not open enough [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the cell was not directly below the lens [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the microscope was not focused sufficiently [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the slide **OR** coverslip was too dirty [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the objective lens was not clicked in correctly **OR** not appropriately housed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the lens **OR** eyepiece was dirty [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The student may not have been able to see the cell as it was **not placed directly below the lens** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0817","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0817\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nCreatine is a naturally occurring chemical produced in the body which supplies energy to muscles. It’s also a supplement used widely in the fitness industry to reduce recovery times and improve athletic performance.\n\nCreatine is broken down into creatinine which is toxic in high concentrations. A study was conducted on the effect of creatine supplementation on the creatinine concentration in the urine. Urine samples were collected from humans, and the creatinine concentration was measured.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0817A.png\" alt=\"BI0817A\">\n[Source: Adapted from Yang, C-Y., Chen, F-A., Chen, C-F., Liu, W-S., Shih, C-J., Ou, S-M., et al. (2015) *Diagnostic Accuracy of Urine Protein/Creatinine Ratio Is Influenced by Urine Concentration*. PLoS ONE 10(9): e0137460. Retrieved May 29th, 2023 from https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0137460. Copyright free.]\n\nThe results from the study were used to make up corresponding creatine solutions to mimic the urine samples. These creatine solutions were then given to students to analyse. The specific gravity of each sample was measured using a hydrometer, a small graduated float used to compare the density of a liquid between two samples.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0817B.png\" alt=\"BI0817B\">\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0817C.png\" alt=\"BI0817C\">\n[Source: Adapted from Yang, C-Y., Chen, F-A., Chen, C-F., Liu, W-S., Shih, C-J., Ou, S-M., et al. (2015) *Diagnostic Accuracy of Urine Protein/Creatinine Ratio Is Influenced by Urine Concentration*. PLoS ONE 10(9): e0137460. Retrieved May 29th, 2023 from https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0137460. Copyright free]\n\n1.\n    1. Describe how urea enters the urine from the blood.  [2 marks]\n\n    2. The urine samples were collected from the subjects after the same intensity of exercise. Suggest how increased intensity of exercise could change creatinine concentration in the samples.      [2 marks]\n\n2. Suggest a variable that would need to be controlled during the student’s experiment. [1 mark]\n\n3. Suggest one advantage and one disadvantage of using model urine samples instead of human urine.         [2 marks]\n\n4. Calculate, as a percentage, how much greater the urine sample specific gravity with 10g of supplemental creatine intake was compared to that with 5g of supplemental intake. [1 mark]\n\n5. Describe **TWO** reasons why mice were used in this experiment and not humans.     [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ blood enters the kidney through the afferent arteriole  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ afferent arteriole is wider than efferent arteriole so pressure in glomerulus is high        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ high-pressure filtration **OR** ultrafiltration forces urea from blood **OR** plasma into Bowman’s capsule            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ urea is small **OR** water-soluble so passes freely through basement membrane into filtrate  [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Blood enters the nephron through the **afferent arteriole**, creating **high pressure** in the **glomerulus ** ($\\checkmark$). **High-pressure filtration** forces urea from the blood plasma into the **Bowman’s capsule** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    2. any **two** of the following:\n\n    ***increased creatinine concentration***:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased creatinine production **OR** increased breakdown of creatine in muscles due to more energy required for muscle activity    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ creatinine becomes more concentrated in nephron due to kidneys conserving water during exercise [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ exercise-induced muscle damage can increase creatine breakdown         [1 mark]\n\n     ***decreased creatinine concentration***:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reduced blood flow to the kidneys OR lower glomerular filtration rate during exercise  [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    During exercise, the **increased breakdown of creatine** in muscles to meet **higher energy demands** leads to **more creatinine** production, which can increase its concentration in the urine ($\\checkmark$). However, **reduced blood flow** to the **kidneys** during exercise **lowers the glomerular filtration rate**, which could **decrease** the **concentration** of creatinine in the urine ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the temperature of the artificial sample     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the same type of hydrometer                 [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ thorough mixing of samples (to prevent solute settlement)     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ time elapsed since sample was created and when hydrometer reading was taken     [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The the **type of hydrometer** used for obtaining the results would have to be the **same** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **one** of the following:\n\n    *Advantage*:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ artificial samples are more sanitary **OR** are safer for students to use [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ artificial samples can be recreated many times [1 mark]\n\n    Any **one** of the following:\n\n    *Weakness*:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ artificial samples only act as a model **OR** may not be exactly the same as\n    the urine sample **OR** are simplified versions of urine samples    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lack of interference factors                         [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The artificial samples are safer for students to use as they are **more sanitary** ($\\checkmark$) and have less risk of containing harmful bacteria or other pathogens. The artificial samples are acting as **models**, so they are more **simplified** ($\\checkmark$) versions of the urine samples, so they might not contain everything the urine sample has.\n\n4.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 66.7 (accept answers in the range of 66.6 - 66.7)     [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0817ANSC.png\" alt=\"BI0817ANSC\">\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Percentage Increase = $\\left(\\dfrac{\\text{ final value-initial value}}{\\text{initial value}}\\right) $ X 100%\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Specific gravity for 5g supplement intake = 15\\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Specific gravity for 10g supplement intake =25\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 25 - 15 = 10\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 10/15 x 100%\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ = **66.7%** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n5. any **two** of the following\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ less ethical considerations     [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ require less space **OR** resources **OR** easier to keep in a lab **OR** cheaper [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ most model organisms have shorter lifespans **OR** life cycles **OR** grow quickly (so can be studied over generations)    [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ many model organisms have physiological similarities to humans [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ more control over the experimental environment                 [1 mark]\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Mice are advantageous because they require **less space** ($\\checkmark$) and resources. \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ They also grow more quickly, which means they can be studied over **many generations** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0818","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0818\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nDuring a field day, students were asked to investigate the effect of soil salinity on the distribution of plant species. Students designed an experiment that measured the number of plant species found up the shore at varying distances from the high tide line. Three groups of students investigated three sites on the same shore. The distance from the high tide line was taken as an indication of the salinity levels. The results are shown in the table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0818A.png\" alt=\"BI0818A\">\n\n1.\n    1. Identify the independent variable. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Outline the steps students could take, and the equipment they could use, to collect their results.                                [3 marks]\n\n2.\n    1. Suggest a reason for the discrepancy in the number of species sampled in areas 1, 2, and 3.                                     [1 mark]\n\n    2. Describe a way the students could reduce the impact of discrepancies on their collected data.                             [1 mark]\n\n3. The different species of plants found in each area were recorded in a table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0818B.png\" alt=\"BI0818B\">\n\nCalculate how many times greater the number of plant species found in Area 1 was compared to Area 2.                                 [1 mark]\n\n4. Evaluate the students’ investigation into salinity's effect on plant species distribution.                                 [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. (units are required for the mark):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ distance from the high tide line            [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **distance from the high tide line** ($\\checkmark$) is the independent variable.\n\n1.\n    2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use a measuring tape to measure 15m from the high tide line towards inland **OR** up shore                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use a belt transect (to mark out the area of investigation)    **OR**  transect line **OR** quadrats placed next to a measuring tape    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use a quadrat to sample plants every 3m **OR** at regular distances along the transect     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the distance between the transect and the quadrat should be kept constant [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ count the number of different plant species found within each quadrat     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use a clipboard **OR** notepad **OR** device for recording data   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use a dichotomous key **OR** plant identification guide **OR** expert to identify plant species [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    To carry out their investigation, students could use a measuring tape to **measure 15 m** up the shore away **from the high tide line** ($\\checkmark$). Place a **quadrat every 3 m** along the tape to **form a belt transect** ($\\checkmark$). Record the **number** of different **plant species** found in **each quadrat** ($\\checkmark$) in a results table.\n\n2.\n    1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some areas may have different soil **OR** soil structure **OR** more fertile soil **OR** more suitable soil pH **OR** salinity **OR** depth of soil **OR** mineral availability            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ human disturbances (footpaths, for example)        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light intensity                                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water availability                                 [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plant density                                     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ herbivory                                         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ accept any other valid description of habitat variation **OR** disturbance     [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    There may be discrepancies in the results because the areas sampled might have different soil types, with some being more **fertile** than others ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    2.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase the sampling frequency OR more quadrats OR more transects    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ decrease the distance between quadrats, so more taken within 15m [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase expertise **OR** use better resources to identify species [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ avoid using sites that clearly had foot trails                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ control more variables **OR** use areas with same angle of slope (accept any suitable example) [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Students could reduce the impact of discrepancies by increasing sampling frequency by adding more quadrats ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 1.29 **OR** 1.286            [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0818ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0818ANSA\">\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Area 1 - No. of plant species  = 9\\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Area 2 - No. of plant species = 7\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 9/7 = **1.29** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4. Any **three** of the following (must include strengths and weaknesses):\n\n    **Strengths**\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ students sampled three areas **OR** more than one area, **WHICH MEANS** they can calculate a mean from their results **OR** increases the reliability of their results        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ all areas sampled were at the same beach, **WHICH** decreases the effects of confounding variables between trials        [1 mark]\n\n    **Limitations**\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the salinity of the soil wasn’t measured, **OR** distance from the high tide line was used as an indirect measurement, **WHICH** means the research question is not addressed, **OR** a conclusion cannot be reached        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large discrepancies in students' results **MEANS** that the data isn’t reliable    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ more areas should be sampled to improve reliability    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lack of controlled variables, so students should identify variables such as soil moisture **OR** angle of slope **OR** soil pH **OR** light intensity, **OR** other valid controlled variable (example must be given to award mark)  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ all areas sampled were at the same beach, **WHICH** means the results may not be valid for other beaches            [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The students sampled **more than three** areas so a mean could be taken, and this increases the **reliability** ($\\checkmark$) of the results. However, there were **large discrepancies** between student results ($\\checkmark$), so **more areas need to be sampled** ($\\checkmark$). Also, the **salinity** of the soil was **not measured**; instead, they relied on **indirect measurements**, which means a conclusion can only be supported to a limited degree ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0820","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0820\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nChitin is an abundant polysaccharide in nature and is a major component of arthropod exoskeletons. In an experiment, scientists tested the effect of various chitin synthesis inhibitors - diflubenzuron (DFB), hexaflumuron (HX) and teflubenzuron (TFB) - on the moulting process of the salmon louse (*Lepeophtheirus salmonis*) which is a known parasite of salmon.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0820A.png\" alt=\"BI0820A\">\n[Source: Adapted from Harðardóttir, HM., Male, R., Nilsen, F., Dalvin, S.(2019). *Effects of chitin synthesis inhibitor treatment on Lepeophtheirus salmonis (Copepoda, Caligidae) larvae*. PLoS ONE 14(9): e0222520. Retrieved May 27, 2023.  https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0222520. Copyright free]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0820B.png\" alt=\"BI0820B\">\n[Source: Adapted from Harðardóttir, HM., Male, R., Nilsen, F., Dalvin, S.(2019). *Effects of chitin synthesis inhibitor treatment on Lepeophtheirus salmonis (Copepoda, Caligidae) larvae*. PLoS ONE 14(9): e0222520. Retrieved May 27, 2023.  https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0222520. Copyright free]\n\n1.\n    1. State, which is the independent variable and dependent variable. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Suggest which chitin synthesis inhibitor is most effective at reducing the moulting success of salmon louse. Justify your answer. [1 mark]\n\n    3. Suggest why the chitin synthesis inhibitors were administered at different concentrations.    [1 mark]\n\n2.\n    1. Exoskeletons provide an anchorage for muscles to attach to. In mammals, which type of tissue attaches muscle to the bone?     [1 mark]\n\n    2. Describe another function of the exoskeleton.     [1 mark]\n\n3. Outline the relationship between the type of chitin inhibitor, concentration and the percentage of incomplete moultings in salmon lice (*Lepeophtheirus salmonis*).     [3 marks]\n\n4. As shown, chitin is visible as dark areas present on the surface of arthropods. Suggest one apparatus that could have been used to view the exoskeletons of the moulting salmon lice accurately.     [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. (students must identify the type of variable to achieve the mark):\n\n    **independent variable**:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type of chitin inhibitor at different concentrations [1 mark]\n\n    **dependent variable**:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ % of animals with incomplete moulting  [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The independent variable is the **type of inhibitor** at different concentrations ($\\checkmark$) and the dependent variable is the **percentage of animals with incomplete moultings** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    2.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ HX **BECAUSE** at the lowest concentrations, it causes the greatest increase in incomplete moulting, **OR** it is the most effective at reducing moulting success at lower concentrations    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **HX** is the most effective chitin synthesis inhibitor because it’s the most effective at reducing moulting success at **lower concentrations** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some inhibitors may be lethal **OR** harmful at specific concentrations so data cannot be included for dead lice                            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some inhibitors may be ineffective at some concentrations    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to determine the lowest concentration necessary to achieve the desired percentage of incomplete moultings     [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The concentrations used may have been different because some of the inhibitors used might be **lethal** and kill the salmon louse larvae **at specific concentrations** , and therefore, **data cannot be included** for measurements of the percentage of animals with incomplete moulting ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    1.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tendons        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Tendons** ($\\checkmark$) attach muscle to bone.\n\n2.\n    2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ protection **OR** physical barrier to predators **OR** toxins **OR** pathogens    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prevents excessive water uptake **OR** loss     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ acts as a lever                                 [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sensory perception                                 [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ camouflage                                         [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The exoskeleton provides **protection** ($\\checkmark$) for the larvaes’ vital organs.\n\n3.\n    Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as the concentration of all inhibitors increases, the percentage of incomplete moultings increases **OR** positive relationship between the concentration of inhibitor and the percentage of incomplete moultings     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hexaflumuron is the most effective inhibitor at lower concentrations      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ diflubenzuron is required in relatively higher concentrations to have the same effect as other inhibitors       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at the relative highest concentration of each inhibitor, the percentage of incomplete moultings is approximately 90%       [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nFor all inhibitors, as the concentration increases, so does the percentage of incomplete moultings. This is a **positive relationship** ($\\checkmark$). Hexaflumuron impacts the percentage of incomplete moultings at **lower concentrations** relative to the other inhibitors ($\\checkmark$). Diflubenzuron is required in relatively **higher concentrations** compared to the other inhibitors in order to have a similar effect ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ viewed using a light microscope **OR** magnifying glass **OR** stereoscope     [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The salmon lice could be viewed using a **light microscope** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0854","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0854\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the varied forms of genetic material found in viruses.    [4 marks]\n\n2. Discuss the evidence supporting that the diversity of viruses exists as a result of convergent evolution.    [5 marks]\n\n3. Compare the structure of the coronavirus with the human immunodeficiency virus, HIV. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ single-stranded RNA     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ single-stranded DNA        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ double-stranded RNA        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ double-stranded DNA        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleic acid **OR** DNA **OR** RNA     [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nThere are many different types of viruses. The types of genetic information found in different types of viruses include **single-stranded RNA** ($\\checkmark$), **double-stranded RNA** ($\\checkmark$), **single-stranded DNA** ($\\checkmark$), and **double-stranded DNA** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ convergent evolution refers to the appearance of similar traits in unrelated organisms as a response to similar selection pressures     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ viruses exhibit a wide range of forms **OR** structures **OR** mechanisms to adapt to different hosts **OR** environments            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ presence of a capsid surrounding genetic material **OR** nucleocapsid is a shared feature that evolved independently in different viral lineages                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ presence of specific protein receptors or spikes have evolved independently to facilitate host cell attachment **OR** suitable example such as similar spike proteins in coronaviruses and influenza viruses    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ independent evolution of similar replication enzymes **OR** reverse transcriptase        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ viruses utilise either DNA **OR** RNA as their genetic material, demonstrating molecular convergence in adapting to different replication strategies         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ convergent traits enhance viral fitness by aiding in host cell attachment **OR** immune evasion **OR** efficient replication        [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Convergent evolution occurs when **unrelated organisms** evolve **similar traits** due to **similar selection pressures** ($\\checkmark$). In viruses, a wide **range** of **structures** and **shapes** are observed, which enable them to multiply in **different hosts**($\\checkmark$). One example is the **independent evolution** of **protein spikes** in **coronaviruses** and **influenza viruses** to aid in **host cell attachment** ($\\checkmark$). Similarly, unrelated viruses have independently evolved **reverse transcriptase** for **efficient replication**  ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, viruses use either **DNA or RNA** as genetic material, showing **molecular convergence** in adapting to various **replication needs** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following (*answer must include at least **two** similarities and **two** differences to gain full marks*):\n\n    ***Similarities***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are spherical in shape [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contain (viral) envelope, **OR** both are enveloped with a lipid bilayer    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contain RNA as the genetic material    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contain a capsid    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contain spikes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** spikes are made of glycoprotein [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ HIV contains reverse transcriptase, **WHILE** coronaviruses do not        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ HIV contains two (single) strands of RNA, **WHILE** the coronavirus contains one single strand of RNA **OR** HIV genome is shorter or about 10,000 nucleotides, **WHILE** coronavirus is longer (about 30,000 nucleotides)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ HIV has an enveloped capsid, **WHILE** coronavirus contains a helical (nucleo)capsid  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ HIV genome is within enveloped capsid, **WHILE**  coronavirus genome is a nucleocapsid/not in envelope         [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    In comparing the coronavirus and the human immunodeficiency virus (HIV), there are several similarities and differences.  Both the coronavirus and HIV are **spherical in shape** ($\\checkmark$), the genetic information is enclosed by a **capsid** ($\\checkmark$), and both types of viruses contain **RNA as the genetic material** ($\\checkmark$). However, **HIV** is a retrovirus and **contains the enzyme reverse transcriptase**, while the **coronavirus does not contain reverse transcriptase** ($\\checkmark$). Both viruses **contain spikes** ($\\checkmark$) for attachment to cells, **and both HIV spikes and coronavirus spikes are made of glycoprotein** ($\\checkmark$). **HIV contains two** single **strands of RNA; however, the coronavirus contains one single strand of RNA** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0870","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0870\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Describe how geographic isolation can lead to the formation of a new species from a single interbreeding population.        [5 marks]\n\n2. Outline what is meant by sympatric speciation.        [4 marks]\n\n3. Describe how sexual and asexual reproduction can lead to the formation of new species with different numbers of copies of each chromosome.    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **five** of the following:\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ gene flow is prevented **OR** reduced between the separated groups  [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ different selection pressures act on each isolated population due to different environments [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ natural selection favours different alleles **OR** phenotypes in each population [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ mutations arise independently in each population **OR** increase genetic variation in each population  [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ genetic drift changes allele frequencies randomly     [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ allele frequencies diverge over generations [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ reproductive isolating mechanisms evolve  [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ pre-zygotic isolation **OR** different mating behaviour **OR** breeding seasons **OR** mechanical **OR** gamete incompatibility may occur [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ post-zygotic isolation **OR** reduced hybrid viability **OR** hybrid sterility **OR** reduced hybrid fertility may occur [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ if secondary contact occurs, the populations do not interbreed successfully **OR**  do not produce fertile offspring [1 mark]\n\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nGeographic isolation prevents **gene flow** between the separated populations ($\\checkmark$). Different selection pressures, or genetic drift, cause **allele frequencies to diverge** over time in each population ($\\checkmark$). **Mutations also arise independently** in each population, increasing genetic variation between them ($\\checkmark$). As genetic differences accumulate, **reproductive isolating mechanisms** evolve ($\\checkmark$).  If the populations later come into **secondary contact**, they **cannot interbreed** successfully to produce fertile offspring, so a new species forms ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ occurs without geographic isolation **OR** populations remain in the same area [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ reproductive isolation develops within a single population [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ gene flow between subgroups is reduced **OR** prevented [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ polyploidy creates immediate reproductive isolation [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ ecological niche differentiation occurs so subgroups use different resources **OR** microhabitats [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ disruptive selection favours extreme phenotypes **OR** selects against intermediates [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ assortative mating occurs so individuals preferentially mate with similar individuals [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ sexual selection causes divergence in mate preferences **OR** mating signals [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ temporal isolation occurs due to different breeding **OR** flowering times in the same area [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ behavioural isolation occurs due to different courtship behaviours **OR** mating calls [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ a host shift occurs so individuals feed **OR** mate on different host species [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ chromosomal rearrangements reduce successful interbreeding between subgroups [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nSympatric speciation occurs when new species form in the **same geographic area** ($\\checkmark$). Reproductive isolation evolves within a population, so **gene flow is reduced** between subgroups ($\\checkmark$). This can occur through **polyploidy**, especially in plants, causing immediate reproductive isolation ($\\checkmark$). It can also occur through **sexual selection** or **assortative mating**, where differences in mate choice reduce interbreeding and lead to divergence ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    ***Asexual reproduction:***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chromosome number of daughter species is an odd number [1 mark]\n\n    ***Sexual reproduction:***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the hybridisation of closely related species [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ new hybrid species has one set of chromosomes from each parent species     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ self-fertilisation **OR** autogamy occurs, and chromosome number is doubled **OR** halved    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fusion of two diploid gametes **OR** another valid example of fertilisation leading to a change in chromosome copy number    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Both asexual and sexual reproduction:***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reproductive isolation **OR** new species are sometimes brought about by differences in chromosome copy number        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ abnormal meiotic division in parent species [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ daughter species is reproductively isolated from the parent species     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ daughter species reproduces asexually because meiosis is no longer possible     [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Both sexually and asexually reproducing species can be produced when **two closely related species hybridise** ($\\checkmark$) to produce a daughter species that can carry out mitosis and is **reproductively isolated from the parent species** ($\\checkmark$).  Sometimes reproductive isolation in both sexually and asexually reproducing species is brought about by a **change in chromosome copy number** ($\\checkmark$).  This is often due to **abnormal meiotic divisions** ($\\checkmark$).  When an **odd number of chromosomes** is present ($\\checkmark$) in the daughter species, for example, 3n, **meiosis is impossible** ($\\checkmark$), and the daughter species can only reproduce asexually through mitosis.  If the daughter species **reproduces through sexual reproduction, the chromosome copy number must be even** ($\\checkmark$) so that **meiosis is possible** ($\\checkmark$).  Sometimes a single, **self-fertilizing species may undergo abnormal meiotic divisions** ($\\checkmark$), which result in the production of a **new species that has half** ($\\checkmark$) or **double** ($\\checkmark$) the number of chromosomes and thus becomes reproductively isolated."},{"ref":"BI0911","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0911\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline why scientists must have several approaches to conserve biodiversity.   [4 marks]\n\n2. There are approximately 80 Javan Rhinoceros (*Rhinoceros sondaicus*) in the world. Discuss the environmental and economic implications the Indonesian government must consider before volunteering the Javan Rhinoceros to the EDGE programme.        [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain why rapid human population growth is linked to the current biodiversity crisis.   [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ no single approach is sufficient **OR** establishing conservation areas is often effective, but without reintroduction of lost species it will have limited success **OR** other suitable example showing that when one approach is used, another is required for effective conservation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ different species **OR** ecosystems require different measures        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ not all methods are affordable for different countries **OR** organisations, **OR** governments    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ biodiversity encompasses a vast range of organisms, habitats, and ecosystems with different interdependencies [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ socio-economic factors need to be considered to ensure that conservation efforts take into account local communities' needs and interests   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ongoing changes in biodiversity may mean that strategies need to be adapted accordingly      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ multiple approaches can create a more resilient and effective conservation strategy   [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Scientists must have multiple approaches to conserve biodiversity as **no single approach** is sufficient ($\\checkmark$), different species require **different methods** ($\\checkmark$) and not all methods are **affordable for different governments** ($\\checkmark$).  In addition, biodiversity encompasses a vast range of organisms and habitats with **different interdependencies** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following (*implications must be stated*):\n\n    ***Environmental negative considerations***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ disruption of current food chains **OR** webs   [1 mark]\n\n    ***Environmental positive considerations***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ restoration of food webs **OR** chains  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ habitats **OR** forests will have to be restored     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the Javan Rhinoceros is a keystone species, so conserving this species would indirectly protect the ecosystem      [1 mark]\n\n    ***Economic negative considerations***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can be expensive to put rhinos in breeding programmes **OR** zoos, **OR** EDGE program would require financial investments in research **OR** monitoring, **OR** conservation initiatives            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ land that has been used for urbanisation **OR** businesses **OR** agriculture  will have to be reforested            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ local communities might rely on the same resources as the rhinoceros, **OR** local communities' livelihoods might be impacted by conservation measures       [1 mark]\n\n    ***Economic positive considerations***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase tourism in the area      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ may improve international cooperation **OR** trade agreements, **OR** foreign investments      [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Before volunteering the Javan rhinoceros to the EDGE programme, economic considerations must be taken into account.  For example, protecting the Javan rhinoceros would likely **increase tourism** in the area ($\\checkmark$) as people will travel to the country to see the rhinos. However, it can be **expensive** to put rhinos into **breeding programmes** ($\\checkmark$) or **reforest** land that has been **urbanised** ($\\checkmark$), and this may be **unaffordable** for the Indonesian government. A positive environmental consideration is that **reforestation will increase biodiversity** ($\\checkmark$) in the area. However, it may also result in **disruption of current, stable food chains** ($\\checkmark$)in the area.\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ more hunting **OR** poaching **OR** for food **OR** resources, which endangers animals [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ loss of habitats due to urbanisation **OR** agriculture [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased agriculture can lead to a monoculture **OR** a loss in diversity  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ deforestation for building infrastructure **OR** resources such as paper **OR** agriculture   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased pollution due to increased vehicles **OR** global transport **OR** industry **OR** factories can cause damage to organisms **OR** contribute to global warming   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased use of fertilisers leading to eutrophication      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased spread of diseases to plants **OR** animals due to increased global transport [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased risk of spread of invasive species due to increased global transport     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased spread of pests due to increased air travel damaging agriculture **OR** forest ecosystems     [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Rapid human population growth is linked to the current biodiversity crisis because as the global population grows, there is an increased need for resources which can result in **loss of habitat** due to **increased agriculture** ($\\checkmark$), an increase in the use of **fertilisers** which can result in **eutrophication** ($\\checkmark$) and more **hunting** of animals ($\\checkmark$) which lowers animal populations.  An increase in agriculture which utilises **monocultures** lowers species diversity in the area ($\\checkmark$).  An increase in the human population is also correlated with **increased global transport**, which can result in increased **pollution**, which contributes to **global warming** ($\\checkmark$), which can have a detrimental effect on ecosystems and also can result in an increased **spread of invasive species** ($\\checkmark$) and **pests** ($\\checkmark$), which lowers biodiversity within ecosystems."},{"ref":"BI0925","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0925\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Define the terms species, population, and community.    [3 marks]\n\n2. Explain the conditions required for coral reef formation. [6 marks]\n\n3. Using three named organisms, describe the adaptations they have to different\nenvironments.     [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a species is a group of organisms capable of interbreeding to produce fertile offspring         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a population is a group of individuals of the same species occupying the same area        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a community is all the populations of different species living in the same geographical area       [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    A species is often defined as a **group of organisms** that are capable of **interbreeding to produce fertile offspring** ($\\checkmark$). A population is a group of individuals of the **same species** which occupy the **same geographical area** ($\\checkmark$). A community is all the **populations of different species** living in the **same geographical area** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **six** of the following (maximum three marks for named conditions):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ suitable water temperature **ALLOWS** symbiotic algae to grow **OR** increased temperatures cause the algae to produce substances that are toxic to the coral so corals become stressed and expel zooxanthellae **OR** symbiotic algae, causing bleaching of the coral and making them more vulnerable to starvation and disease    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ clearwater **OR** water clarity **ALLOWS** sunlight through for photosynthesis     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ shallow water **OR** suitable water depth **ALLOWS** sunlight to reach symbiotic algae         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ suitable pH **ALLOWS** algae to survive **OR** skeleton to form **OR** as the pH drops, the availability of carbonate ions in seawater decreases, making it more challenging for corals to build their calcium carbonate skeletons **OR** calcium carbonate dissolves in acidic solutions, so the lowered pH of the water erodes the skeleton, removing its support and protection, leading to death **OR** increased acidity can cause coral bleaching, where corals expel the zooxanthellae    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ suitable salinity **OR** saltwater **ALLOWS** coral to survive and grow OR if the salinity is too high or too low, it can disrupt the osmotic balance, leading to dehydration or water overload within the coral cells **OR** the concentration of calcium and carbonate ions in the water is influenced by salinity **OR** the correct salinity plays a role in the chemical cues that attract larvae to appropriate settlement areas on the reef        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ coral larvae need a hard substrate on which they can attach and begin their growth        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gentle currents help to deliver nutrients and oxygen to the corals while flushing away sediment and waste        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adequate nutrients **BECAUSE** although corals have a symbiotic relationship with zooxanthellae, they also depend on capturing plankton and organic matter from the water for additional nutrition  [1 mark]\n    [6 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Corals require **suitable temperatures**, which allow the symbiotic algae to **survive** and grow ($\\checkmark$). They need to be in relatively **shallow water**, which allows the **sunlight** to reach the symbiotic algae ($\\checkmark$). Corals also need **clear water**, which also allows sunlight to reach them so that the symbiotic algae can **photosynthesise** ($\\checkmark$). Coral larvae also need a **hard substrate**, such as rock, on which they can **attach** and begin to grow ($\\checkmark$).Finally, Corals also need a suitable **salinity and pH**. Salinity directly influences **calcium carbonate** ion concentration ($\\checkmark$), and pH can influence the development of the **skeleton** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following (maximum **three marks** for named organisms and where it's found):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 3 named organisms and their correct habitat **OR** environment, e.g. Fennec fox found in the desert                 [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adaptation and advantage of adaptation for each organism, e.g. large vascular ears to dissipate heat             [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **Fennec fox** is adapted to live in **desert** ($\\checkmark$) environments because it has **large vascular ears** which allow it to **dissipate heat** ($\\checkmark$). It can also use its large ears to **detect small insects**, which are its prey ($\\checkmark$). **Marram grass** is adapted to live in **sand dunes** ($\\checkmark$) because it has **dense shallow roots** which allow it to **collect water** when it rains ($\\checkmark$). It can also **close the stomata** in its leaves to reduce water loss when water is in short supply ($\\checkmark$). The **kapok tree** is found in **tropical rainforests** ($\\checkmark$) it has **buttress roots** that extend above ground. This can help it to **grow tall** in shallow soil ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0944","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0944\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Explain how the shape of erythrocytes and cells lining the proximal convoluted tubule make these cells more adapted to the functions they perform.  [4 marks]\n\n2. Compare and contrast the structure of striated muscle cells and cardiac muscle fibres.              [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain how the structure of egg and sperm cells are adapted to their functions.    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  erythrocytes are biconcave, which increases their surface area    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  increased surface area increases rate of gas exchange between erythrocyte and its surroundings **OR** blood plasma [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  PCT cells are columnar - allowing close packing of cells / efficient use of space [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  cells of the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) have microvilli which increase their surface area [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  increased surface area of PCT cells provides more places for aquaporins **OR** amino acid transport proteins **OR** glucose transport proteins **OR** ion transport proteins [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  greater number of aquaporins **OR** other correctly named transport protein increases the rate substances **OR** water **OR** amino acids **OR** glucose **OR** ions are reabsorbed from the filtrate [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  invaginations on the outer cell membrane, **OR** membrane on opposite side to lumen, of PCT cells provide a large SA for transport of substances out of the cell [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Erythrocytes are **biconcave**, which **increases** their **surface area** ($\\checkmark$).  Having a larger surface area **increases the rate** at which **gas exchange** occurs between the cell and its surroundings ($\\checkmark$).  Cells lining the proximal convoluted tubule have **microvilli**, increasing their **surface area** ($\\checkmark$).  This increased surface area means the cell can have **more aquaporins** and other **transport proteins** in its cell membrane ($\\checkmark$), which **increases the rate** at which substances such as water, glucose and amino acids are **reabsorbed** from the filtrate ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following (must contain at least 1 similarity and one difference for full marks):\n\n    **Differences:**\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ striated muscle cells have multiple nuclei **WHEREAS** cardiac muscle cells do not        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cardiac muscle cells are branched **WHEREAS** striated muscle cells are not        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ striated muscle cells are arranged side by side **WHEREAS** cardiac muscles are arranged side to side **AND** end to end [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ striated muscle fibres can be much larger than cardiac muscle cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cardiac muscle cells have intercalated discs **WHEREAS** striated muscle fibres do not [1 mark]\n\n    **Similarities:**\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contain contractile myofibrils [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contain sarcoplasm **OR** sarcoplasmic reticulum **OR** T-tubules [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Both** cardiac muscle cells and striated muscle fibres contain **contractile myofibrils** ($\\checkmark$), **T-tubules** and a **sarcolemma** ($\\checkmark$). However, **cardiac** muscle cells are **branched** and connected by **intercalated discs**, whereas **striated muscle** fibres are **not branched** ($\\checkmark$) and are formed from **fused cells** ($\\checkmark$).  Finally, **striated muscle** fibres have **multiple nuclei**, whereas **cardiac muscle** cells have only **one nucleus** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ egg cells are large, **OR** have a large volume of cytoplasm, which supplies the egg with nutrients **OR** food stores **OR** lipid stores **OR** makes it possible for multiple, rapid rounds of cell division after fertilisation  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ egg cells contain many mitochondria for energy production  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ eggs **OR** sperm cells are haploid, which allows them to fuse with another gamete to become diploid [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the zona pellucida prevents multiple sperm from fusing with the egg **OR** polyspermy **OR** prevents sperm from another species from fusing with the egg [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ corona radiata of egg protects the egg [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ egg cells contain cortical granules which undergo exocytosis after fertilisation which prevents polyspermy **OR** modifies zona pellucida **OR** results in the breakdown of sperm binding proteins [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ egg cells secrete chemicals which attract sperm [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sperm cells are streamlined, **OR** have few organelles, for swimming [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sperm cells have receptors for chemicals released by egg cells which guide sperm to the egg [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sperm cells have an acrosome which breaks down the zona pellucida [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sperm cells have receptor proteins which bind to the egg’s cell membrane [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sperm cells have a tail **OR** flagellum for locomotion [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sperm cells have many mitochondria which supply the energy for locomotion [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Egg** cells are large, so they have a **large volume of cytoplasm** containing **mitochondria** and **fat droplets** ($\\checkmark$) to supply the egg with energy.  Because it is large, egg cells are also prepared for relatively **rapid** rounds of **division** after they are fertilised ($\\checkmark$).  It is important that the zygote, formed after fertilisation, is diploid, so the **sperm and egg** are both **haploid** ($\\checkmark$).  The egg's **zona pellucida** also **prevents polyspermy** ($\\checkmark$) by hardening after fertilisation in response to the exocytosis of **enzymes** contained in **cortical granules** ($\\checkmark$).  Sperm are also adapted to their function, which is to fertilise egg cells.  They have **receptors** that bind to **chemicals released by egg** cells which help them move towards egg cells ($\\checkmark$).   Sperm cells also have an **acrosome** at the tip of their head, which **breaks down the zona pellucida** ($\\checkmark$), allowing receptors on the head of the sperm to **bind** to receptors on the cell **membrane of the egg** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0964","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0964\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nTwo different mesocosms were used for a study to understand the population dynamics of two ecosystems, one without fish (C ) and one with fish (F). Each mesocosm had the following added to it: two different species of snails and the same (one) species of phytoplankton. The snails feed on the phytoplankton and are, in turn, preyed upon by the fish.\n\nThe following diagrams show the mesocosms used for the study. C is the control without fish, and F is the experimental mesocosm with one fish added. Both mesocosms were maintained at the same temperature and light conditions.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/mesocosms%20phytoplankton%2C%20snails%2C%20fish%20black%20and%20white%20created%20in%20BR.png\" alt=\"mesocosms phytoplankton, snails, fish black and white created in BR\">\n\nAfter a period of time, as shown in the graph below, the density of phytoplankton was seen to vary in the two mesocosms used in the study.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI0964B.png\" alt=\"BI0964B\">\n[Source: adapted from Cheng, H., Feng, M., Zhang, P., Zhang, H., Wang, H., Xu, J., & Zhang, M. (2022, December 30). *Effects of warming on aquatic snails and periphyton in freshwater ecosystems with and without predation by common carp*. MDPI. Retrieved January 10, 2023, from https://www.mdpi.com/2073-4441/15/1/153, Copyright under CC-BY 4.0]\n\n1. Outline a reason for the variation seen in the density of the phytoplankton in the two mesocosms, as shown above. [1 mark]\n\n2. Explain whether this is indicative of a top-down or bottom–up control of the simulated ecosystem. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the snails are eaten by the fish in mesocosm F, and therefore there is an increase in phytoplankton density [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in the absence of fish, as in mesocosm C, the snails eat the phytoplankton, and the density of phytoplankton is controlled and remains lower than in mesocosm F. [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n In **mesocosm F**, the **snails are eaten by the fish** ($\\checkmark$); therefore, the phytoplankton density is higher than in mesocosm C.\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the fish control the abundance **OR** density of the snail, which controls the density of the producer [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ this is indicative of a top-down control [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in the case of bottom-up control, with increasing light **OR** temperature **OR** nutrients, increasing algae will cause growth of more snails, thus more fish can be supported [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ no data on nutrient content in either mesocosm, so cannot determine if there are bottom-up limiting factors **OR** if there is bottom-up control [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n In mesocosm F, the **fish reduce** the abundance of **snails** ($\\checkmark$), and therefore phytoplankton density increases in the presence of the fish. This is indicative of **top-down control of the ecosystem** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0972","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0972\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast the structure of transmembrane and intracellular receptors. [3 marks]\n\n2. Explain why a variety of chemically different substances are used as hormones and neurotransmitters in humans. [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe how adrenaline (epinephrine) brings about glycogen breakdown in liver cells and how progesterone acts on endometrial cells. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following (*must include at least **one** similarity and at least **one** difference to gain full marks; differences must refer to both receptor types*):\n\n    ***Similarities***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are proteins   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have binding sites for specific chemicals **OR** ligands   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can undergo conformational changes when their ligand binds   [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ binding site of transmembrane receptors is on the extracellular region **OR** faces the outside of the cell **WHEREAS** binding site of intracellular receptors is inside cell   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transmembrane receptors span the plasma membrane **OR** have hydrophobic amino acid side chains in the membrane-spanning region **WHEREAS** intracellular receptors do not span the membrane **OR** have predominantly hydrophilic amino acid side chains exposed on their surfaces   [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n**Both** transmembrane and intracellular receptors are **proteins** ($\\checkmark$) with **specific binding sites** for their ligands ($\\checkmark$). **Transmembrane** receptors have **hydrophobic amino acid side chains** exposed in their **membrane-spanning regions**, whereas **intracellular** receptors have predominantly **hydrophilic amino acid side chains** exposed on their surfaces ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following (*answer must link each reason to a type of chemical or named example*):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ steroid hormones/named steroid hormone are lipid soluble so diffuse through the plasma membrane to bind intracellular receptors   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ peptide/protein hormones/catecholamines/suitable named molecule are water soluble so bind transmembrane receptors in the plasma membrane   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ steroids alter gene expression so responses are slower/longer-lasting   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water-soluble hormones act through transmembrane receptors that trigger intracellular signalling pathways, so responses are rapid   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water-soluble hormones dissolve in plasma **WHEREAS** steroids need carrier proteins **OR** this affects duration of action   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chemically different signalling molecules bind to specific receptors, allowing target cells to distinguish between different signals   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ different neurotransmitters can produce excitatory **OR** inhibitory effects depending on the receptors activated, e.g. glutamate is usually excitatory **WHEREAS** GABA **OR** glycine is usually inhibitory   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ different neurotransmitters are removed from the synaptic cleft by enzymatic breakdown **OR** reuptake so signals can be terminated quickly   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ neuropeptides/endorphins have slower/longer-lasting/modulatory effects   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nitric oxide/NO is a gas so diffuses freely across membranes **OR** is not stored in vesicles **OR** acts locally on nearby cells, whereas hormones travel in the blood to distant target cells, allowing communication over different distances   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ different signalling molecules are synthesised from different precursors **OR** by different pathways, allowing their production and regulation in different tissues   [1 mark]\n\n    *(adrenaline / noradrenaline may be used as a hormone or neurotransmitter example, but credit the same idea only once)*\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n**Steroid** hormones such as **testosterone** are **lipid soluble**, allowing them to **diffuse through** the plasma **membrane** and bind to **intracellular receptors** ($\\checkmark$). In contrast, **protein hormones** such as **insulin** are **water soluble** and bind **transmembrane receptors** ($\\checkmark$). **Steroids** alter **gene expression**, so their effects are slower but **longer-lasting** ($\\checkmark$). Different **neurotransmitters** can produce **excitatory** or **inhibitory** effects depending on the **receptors** activated; for example, **glutamate** is usually excitatory while **GABA** is usually inhibitory ($\\checkmark$). **Nitric oxide** is a **gas**, so it **diffuses** freely **across membranes** and is **not stored in vesicles** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following (*maximum four marks for each hormone*):\n\n    ***Epinephrine/Adrenaline: G-protein-coupled receptor***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adrenaline/epinephrine binds to a G-protein-coupled receptor/GPCR in plasma membrane (of liver cells)   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ binding activates a G protein   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ activated G protein activates adenylyl/adenylate cyclase   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adenylyl/adenylate cyclase converts ATP to cAMP, which acts as a second messenger   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cAMP activates protein kinase A/PKA, which activates phosphorylase kinase **OR** initiates a phosphorylation cascade **OR** amplifies the signal   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glycogen phosphorylase/enzymes involved in glycogen breakdown are activated, increasing glucose availability in liver cells   [1 mark]\n\n    ***Progesterone: intracellular receptor***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ progesterone is lipid soluble **OR** hydrophobic and diffuses through the plasma membrane of endometrial cells   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ progesterone binds to a specific intracellular receptor in the cytoplasm **OR** nucleus, activating the receptor   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the receptor–hormone complex enters the nucleus if the receptor was in the cytoplasm   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the complex binds to specific DNA sequences **OR** hormone response elements   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the complex regulates **OR** promotes transcription of specific genes   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA is translated into proteins that contribute to the maintenance **OR** development of the endometrium/uterine lining   [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n**Epinephrine** binds to a **G-protein-coupled receptor** (GPCR) in the **plasma membrane** of liver cells ($\\checkmark$). This **activates** a **G protein** ($\\checkmark$), which activates **adenylate cyclase**. Adenylate cyclase converts **ATP into cAMP**, which acts as a **second messenger** ($\\checkmark$). cAMP activates **protein kinase A**, starting a **phosphorylation cascade** that activates **glycogen phosphorylase**, resulting in the **breakdown of glycogen** and increasing the **availability of glucose** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    **Progesterone** is **lipid soluble**, so it **diffuses through the plasma membrane** of endometrial cells ($\\checkmark$). It binds to a specific **intracellular receptor**, **activating** the **receptor** ($\\checkmark$). The **receptor–hormone complex** binds to **specific DNA sequences** in the nucleus, promoting **transcription** of specific genes ($\\checkmark$). The **mRNA** is **translated** into **proteins** that help **maintain the endometrium** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1002","marks":"17","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1002\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Adaptive radiation results in a single ancestral species evolving into multiple, closely related new species which may live in close proximity to each other.  Outline 3 mechanisms by which closely related species living in close proximity may be reproductively isolated.    [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline the process by which two species may be formed from an ancestral population through the process of allopatric speciation.                        [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain what polyploidy is and how it can lead to speciation.        [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ behavioural isolation **OR** failure to recognise members of different species as possible mates        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ temporal isolation **OR** reproduction occurs at different times [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ postzygotic barriers        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ habitat isolation, whereby a subsection of one population occupies a different ecological niche [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hybrid offspring are sterile         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mechanical isolation **OR** different morphological structures evolve, resulting in physical incompatibility            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ two closely related species may have different numbers of chromosomes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ incompatibility of gametes **OR** inability of sperm to fertilise egg [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Species living close to one another may be **reproductively isolated** due to **behavioural isolation** ($\\checkmark$), which is a **failure** to **recognise** potential **mates**.  **Two species** may also be **reproductively isolated** by **postzygotic barriers** ($\\checkmark$). For example, **differences in chromosome number** between closely related species ($\\checkmark$) may result in **hybrid offspring** that are **sterile** because their chromosomes cannot pair correctly during meiosis ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ geographic isolation **OR** ancestral population is separated into two areas **OR** any named example, e.g.  mountain range, isthmus [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ different random mutations accrue in the two different populations **OR** different alleles are produced in the two different populations    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ different phenotypes are produced in the two different populations    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ different selection pressures in two different areas    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ different traits selected for in two different areas  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic drift            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reproductive isolation **OR** behavioural isolation **OR** temporal isolation **OR** post-zygotic barriers        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gene flow cannot occur        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ changes occur over many generations    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The ancestral population could have been divided into **two populations** by the formation of a **mountain range** ($\\checkmark$), which means **gene flow** between the two populations **does not occur** ($\\checkmark$).  **Random mutations** then build up ($\\checkmark$) between the two populations leading to **new phenotypes** ($\\checkmark$).  **Selection** pressures may **differ** ($\\checkmark$) between the two populations which means **different phenotypes** would be selected for in the **two different environments**.  Eventually, the two different populations may become **reproductively isolated** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic condition in which an organism has more than two complete sets of chromosomes in its cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ more common in plants            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ formation of new species is through two main mechanisms: autopolyploidy and allopolyploidy  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ autopolyploidy occurs due to failure of chromosomes to separate during meiosis        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ autopolyploidy occurs within one species    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reproductive isolation of polyploid individuals from diploid population    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reduced **OR** cessation of gene flow in population due to polyploids being incompatible with diploids                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polyploid population begins to evolve independently from the diploid population **OR** genetic divergence occurs    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allopolyploidy occurs when two different species interbreed and produce hybrid offspring [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can result in an entire extra set of chromosomes from each parent species    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allopolyploid individuals often reproduce asexually due to the mismatched chromosomes from different species        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ asexual reproduction acts as a reproductive barrier between the allopolyploid and its parent species [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reduced gene flow allows for independent evolution **OR** genetic divergence    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ over time, the allopolyploid population may accumulate genetic differences and adapt to specific ecological niches, leading to the formation of a new species        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polyploidy can result in odd ploidy number **OR** 3n, 5n, etc. in daughter species        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ individuals with odd ploidy number cannot undergo meiosis which acts as a reproductive barrier     [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Polyploidy is the condition of having **more** than **two sets** of **chromosomes** ($\\checkmark$). **Autopolyploidy** is due to the failure of chromosomes to **separate** during **meiosis** ($\\checkmark$).  Often, autopolyploid individuals are **reproductively isolated** ($\\checkmark$) from the parent species due to their **gametes** being **incompatible** with the diploid parent’s gametes.\n\n    **Allopolyploid** species are produced when two different **species hybridise** ($\\checkmark$) resulting in a daughter species with an **extra set of chromosomes** from each parent species ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    In both **allopolyploids and autopolyploids** ($\\checkmark$), occasionally individuals are produced with **odd ploidy numbers** (ex: 3n, 5n) ($\\checkmark$).  These individuals will generally be **unable** to undergo **meiosis** ($\\checkmark$), will **reproduce asexually** and thus be **reproductively isolated** from their parent species ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    Once two populations are reproductively isolated there is **no gene flow** ($\\checkmark$) and a new species is produced."},{"ref":"BI1016","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1016\n\nAn experiment investigating tidal volume in the lungs was conducted using a spirometer. In the experiment, the volume of air inhaled and exhaled with each breath was measured for one minute while the student was at rest. The results obtained from the spirometer are displayed in the graph below. The measurement was repeated for the student during exercise.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1016-SLHL.png\" alt=\"BI1016-SLHL\">\n\n1. Identify the independent and dependent variables in this experiment. [2 marks]\n\n2. State what is measured to give the tidal volume of the student. [1 mark]\n\n3. Calculate the mean tidal volume for one minute, giving the units. [2 marks]\n\n4. Suggest how the tidal volume would differ during exercise. [1 mark]\n\n5. State which cells of the alveoli are specialised to allow gaseous exchange to occur. [1 mark]\n\n6. Describe an experimental method, not involving a spirometer, that could be used by students to measure ventilation rate. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. independent variable: \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ effect of exercise **OR** at rest and exercise **OR** level of activity [1 mark]\n\n dependent variable: \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ tidal volume **OR** volume of air inhaled **OR** exhaled in one breath [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The independent variable is the effect **of exercise** ($\\checkmark$), and the dependent variable is the **volume of air** inhaled ($\\checkmark$) in the lungs.\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ measure the volume of air that moves into or out of the lungs during a normal breath, **OR** at rest [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The tidal volume can be measured by measuring the **volume of air** that flows **into the lungs** with a **normal breath** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ total volume in one minute / number of breaths in one minute **OR** 15.2 dm:sup[3] / 12 breaths [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 1.3 dm:sup[3] breath:sup[-1] [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1016-SLHLA.png\" alt=\"BI1016-SLHLA\">\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ total volume of air (calculated by adding together volume of each peak as indicated by the red marks on the graph above) dm:sup[3] / number of breaths per min\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ = 1.5 dm:sup[3] + 1.5 dm:sup[3] + 1.0 dm:sup[3] + 1.4 dm:sup[3] + 1.3 dm:sup[3] + 1.0 dm:sup[3] + 1.5 dm:sup[3] + 1.2 dm:sup[3] + 1.0 dm:sup[3] + 1.5 dm:sup[3] + 1.0 dm:sup[3] + 1.3 dm:sup[3]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ = **15.2 dm:sup[3] / 12 breaths** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ = **1.3** ($\\checkmark$) dm:sup[3] breath:sup[-1]\n\n4. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ greater **OR** increased, total volume, **OR** total volume of air inhaled **OR** exhaled, would be higher [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The tidal volume would be **greater** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ type 1 pneumocyte [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ **Type I pneumocytes** ($\\checkmark$) are specialised cells that allow gaseous exchange to take place.\n\n6. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ventilation rate is the number of breaths per given time **OR** per minute **OR** second [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ using a stopwatch [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ count the number of times air is breathed in **OR** breathed out through observation **OR** using differential pressure sensor **OR** inflatable chest belt around the thorax [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ measure **OR** repeat several times **OR** collect at least three sets of results **OR** calculate mean from several sets of results [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ any relevant controlled variable, such as using the same person to conduct the activity over a period of time **OR** sitting position **OR** breathing techniques are controlled that can affect the ventilation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ baseline measurement for comparison [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **ventilation rate** is the **number of breaths** over a **set period of time**, usually the number of **breaths per minute** ($\\checkmark$). This can be measured by **counting** the number of times air is **inhaled** and then **exhaled** in a **minute** at rest, through **observation** of **chest movements** ($\\checkmark$). A **stopwatch** is used to measure the time ($\\checkmark$). This is then **repeated** so that at least **three results** are recorded to **calculate the mean**. ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1019","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1019\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nMuch of plastic pollution is non-biodegradable, which means it persists in the environment for long periods of time. Macroplastic pollution is defined as plastic debris that is larger than 1 mm and microplastic pollution less than 1mm.\n\nOn a field trip to a local beach in a temperate climate, students were asked to collect samples of sand and measure the number of pieces of plastic debris present. To do this, 5g samples of sand were collected and placed in a beaker. 50 ml of a concentrated salt solution was then added to the samples, which caused plastic debris to float to the surface. Plastic debris was removed from the samples, measured, and categorised, and the results were recorded in a table. The experiment was repeated in another school in a tropical climate, and the results were compiled.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1019%20table.png\" alt=\"BI1019 table\">\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1019a.png\" alt=\"BI1019a\">\n\n1.\n 1. Identify one possible controlled variable not already mentioned in the question. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Suggest the importance of having a standard classification for plastic debris in this investigation. [1 mark]\n\n2.\n 1. Calculate how many times greater the mean number of microplastic debris sampled was in the tropical climate compared to the temperate climate. [1 mark]\n\n 2. State which uncontrolled variable is most likely responsible for the higher numbers of microplastics sampled compared to macroplastics in the tropical climate. Give a reason for your answer. [2 marks]\n\n3. Students in both climates noted the varying sand particle size of the sand samples taken at each beach.\n\n 1. Describe a limitation to the methodology based on this information. [2 marks]\n\n 2. Suggest a method to reduce the variability in particle size. [1 mark]\n\n4. Evaluate the use of microplastic and macroplastic categories in this investigation. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ concentration of salt solution used to float the plastic debris [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ size **OR** diameter of beaker used to collect the sample [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ time allowed for debris to float before collection [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sand collected from same tide line **OR** distance up the shore [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ depth sand sample taken from [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ any other suitable collection method **OR** consistent sampling technique [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n A controlled variable would be the **concentration of the salt solution**, used to float the plastic debris ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ standard classification ensures that all researchers categorise plastic debris in the same way, which allows for uniform data collection **OR** consistency across data collection [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ reduces errors in identifying **OR** categorising plastic debris, leading to more accurate results [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ensures data can be easily compared to other studies on plastic pollution **OR** makes data comparable [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ enables trends to be analysed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allows for clearer communication of results **OR** allows understanding of results without confusion [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allows for replication of experiment **OR** repeatability [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ helps maintain the validity of the investigation **OR** reduces the risk of biased or subjective classification [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Having a standard size for the different categories of plastic supports the collection of **valid results** from students in the different climates sampled ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1.\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 2.18 (accept in the range of 2.18 - 2.2) [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1019msb.png\" alt=\"BI1019msb\">\n\n Calculation\n\n $\\dfrac{\\text{mean number in tropical climate}} {\\text{mean number in temperate climate}}$\n\n $\\dfrac{12} {5.5}$\n\n = **2.18** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2.\n 2. any **two** of the following (*answer must include cause with corresponding reason for full marks*)\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ light **OR** sunlight **OR** UV rays [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** it causes the breakdown of macroplastic into microplastic **OR** degrades plastic [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ greater abundance of diverse marine and terrestrial species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **WHICH** results in the fragmentation and breakdown of larger plastics through physical and biological processes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** higher temperatures could increase thermal degradation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ wave action **OR** ocean currents [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** stronger wave action **OR** ocean currents can break down macroplastics into smaller microplastic fragments more quickly [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Sunlight** ($\\checkmark$) is most likely responsible for the higher levels of microplastic in the tropical climate because there would be more sunlight, and light can **break down macroplastic into microplastic** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 1. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ different sand particle sizes may affect the separation of plastic debris [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the varying sand particle size between the different samples is an uncontrolled variable, meaning that extraneous factors could affect the results [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ different sand particle sizes introduces inconsistencies in the data making it difficult to compare the results accurately between samples [1 mark]\n\n **and** any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ finer sand may trap microplastics more effectively, leading to higher recorded numbers [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ coarser sand may allow microplastics to escape detection leading to underrepresentation of plastic debris [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **varying sand particle size** between the different samples is an **uncontrolled variable** that affects the methodology. **Finer sand** may **trap more microplastics**, leading to higher counts ($\\checkmark$), while **coarser sand** may allow **microplastics** to **escape detection** ($\\checkmark$). This introduces **inconsistencies** in the data and makes it **difficult to compare** the **results accurately** ($\\checkmark$) between samples collected at different beaches.\n\n3.\n 2.\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ use a filter **OR** sieve **OR** strainer to remove larger sand grains only before weighing the sample [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Students could use a **sieve** to remove some of the larger sand particles, but not the plastic debris ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **two** of the following (*must include at least **one** strength and **one** weakness to gain full marks*):\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ **Strengths** \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ categories help increase the validity of results **OR** allow different students to record valid results [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ having only two categories increases the rate at which plastic debris can be counted and sorted [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ allows a systematic approach **OR** provides clear **OR** consistent criteria **OR** allows the collection of accurate results [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ **Weaknesses** \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ the macroplastic category has a much larger range than the microplastic category [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ some microplastic debris may be hard, **OR** too small, to count [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ not all microplastic can be seen by the eye [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Having two categories helps to **increase the rate** at which plastic can be **sorted** or organised ($\\checkmark$). However, having only two categories may make it difficult to record all plastic debris, as some plastic, such as nano plastic, may be **too small** to see with only the **naked eye** ($\\checkmark$) and may need specialist equipment like a microscope or magnifying glass, otherwise it's not counted."},{"ref":"BI1035","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1035\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast the biological species concept and the morphological concept.[4 marks]\n\n2. Explain the difficulties with applying the biological species and morphological concept to all organisms.    [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe the similarities and differences within and between species. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** similarity and **one** difference to achieve full marks*):\n\n    ***Similarities***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** concepts seek to define a species    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** concepts acknowledge that there can be variation within species    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** concepts are connected to the broader concept of evolution    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the biological species concept defines a species as a group of organisms which can interbreed to produce fertile offspring, **WHEREAS** the morphological concept defines a species as a group of organisms with shared traits        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Mayr proposed the biological species concept **WHEREAS** the morphological concept was proposed by Linnaeus        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the morphological species concept was proposed before the biological species concept    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the morphological species concept is applicable to sexual and asexual organisms, **WHEREAS** the biological species concept is applicable only to sexually reproducing organisms     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the biological species concept reflects the evolutionary relationship OR speciation OR gene flow, **WHEREAS** the morphological species concept may not represent genetic relatedness or evolutionary relationships [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Both concepts are used to **define species** ($\\checkmark$), but Mayr proposed the biological species concept, whereas the morphological concept was proposed by **Linnaeus** ($\\checkmark$). The morphological concept also came **first** ($\\checkmark$). The biological species concept defines a species as a group of organisms that can interbreed to produce **fertile offspring**; however, the morphological concept defines a species as a group of organisms with **shared traits** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the morphological species concept cannot be applied to species that show variation due to reproductive isolation **OR** the morphological concept cannot be applied to species with different forms **OR** polymorphism **OR** named example of a species with different forms     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the morphological species concept does not take into account DNA **OR** genetic information   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the biological species concept is defined in terms of sexual reproduction **HOWEVER**, not all organisms reproduce sexually     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ example of species that reproduce asexually, e.g. budding in yeast **OR** horizontal gene transfer in bacteria                     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the biological species concept relies on the ability to observe and study interbreeding in real-time, which is not possible with extinct species **OR** fossilised organisms              [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some different species can interbreed and produce viable offspring, but these hybrids might have reduced fitness or be sterile, which challenges the clear-cut boundaries proposed by the biological species concept     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ convergent evolution can make it difficult to accurately group organisms based solely on morphological characteristics     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rapid evolution and morphological changes in response to the environment might make individuals appear distinct, even if they share a recent common ancestor, which challenges the reliability of morphological traits as indicators of species relationships         [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The biological species concept defines a species as a group of organisms that can interbreed to produce fertile offspring, however, not all species reproduce **sexually** ($\\checkmark$), for example, yeast reproduce asexually by **budding** ($\\checkmark$). It also relies on the ability to see species interbreeding, which cannot be done with **extinct** species or **fossils** ($\\checkmark$). The morphological concept defines a species as a group of organisms with shared traits, however, due to variation caused by **reproductive isolation** and mutation, not all members of a species **look the same** ($\\checkmark$). In addition, **rapid evolution** might make individuals appear distinctly related when in fact, they shared a recent common ancestor. This could make the morphological species concept **less reliable** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ species can be defined as a group of organisms that are capable of interbreeding to produce fertile offspring    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ species usually share the same number of chromosomes      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ species usually share identical karyotypes             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ example of chromosome complement of a species, e.g. humans have 46 chromosomes           [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the chromosome complement of a species can vary due to genetic conditions such as Down syndrome     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the size genome of a species can vary within a species   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the base sequence of the genome can vary within a species     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ due to mutations or single-nucleotide polymorphisms [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous genes **OR** similarities in gene pathways **OR** similarities in the functions of genes can be found between species that share evolutionary origins     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ species-specific genes are not found in other closely related species     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ horizontal gene transfer between different species, often through mechanisms like viral infections or symbiotic relationships, leads to the acquisition of genes in the recipient that may not be shared by other members of the species         [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    According to the biological species concept, a species can be defined as a group of organisms that are capable of interbreeding to produce **fertile offspring** ($\\checkmark$). They can produce fertile offspring as a species usually have the **same chromosome number** ($\\checkmark$), for example, in humans, this is **46** ($\\checkmark$) unless genetic conditions such as **Down syndrome** affect their karyotype ($\\checkmark$). The **size** of a species' genome can vary slightly within a species ($\\checkmark$), as can the **base sequence** of the genome ($\\checkmark$) and these are both due to **mutations** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1047","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1047\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nDNA and RNA are nucleic acids that are essential to all living organisms.\n\n1. Draw and label a diagram of a nucleotide. $^*$ [4 marks]\n\n2. Explain how the variation in base sequences leads to the ability of DNA to have an enormous capacity for storing genetic information. [5 marks]\n\n3. DNA is a polymer of nucleotides. Describe the structure of DNA. [7 marks]\n\n$^*$ *Note: Starting May 2028, this question may be assessed differently due to changes to the Biology guide, including the use of a data booklet.*","markScheme":"1. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ phosphate group drawn as a circle in correct place. [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ribose sugar drawn as a pentagon in correct place [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nitrogenous base drawn as a rectangle in correct place [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ all three parts labelled correctly [1 mark]\n\n *Bonds and names of nitrogenous bases do not need to be labelled*\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1047.png\" alt=\"BI1047\">\n\n2. any **five** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** similarity and **one** difference given to gain full marks*):\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic code is stored in the sequence of nitrogenous bases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ three bases **OR** a codon, codes for one amino acid [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 64 codons but only 20 amino acids **OR** degenerate code [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ complementary base pairing [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ for each extra nucleotide along a DNA strand, the number of possible combinations of bases increases exponentially [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mutations introduce new genetic information [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ certain sequences are conserved across species because they encode vital information [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA molecules can be hundreds of millions of base pairs long **OR** DNA molecules are very long [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ non-coding regions have regulatory functions, contributing to the complexity of gene expression and overall genetic information [1 mark]\n [5 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The genetic code resides within the **sequences** of nitrogenous **bases** ($\\checkmark$) along a DNA molecule. Amino acids are coded for by **codons** ($\\checkmark$), and the code is **degenerate** ($\\checkmark$), in other words, some amino acids are coded for by more than one codon. The longer the DNA molecule, the greater the number of possible combinations of bases along the DNA strand, in fact there is an **exponential increase** in possibilities ($\\checkmark$) for each single nucleotide added to the polymer. **Mutations** also add to the variety ($\\checkmark$) of the genetic code.\n\n3. any **seven** of the following: \\\n*(appropriately labelled and correctly drawn DNA segment can be used in the answer)*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA is a double helix [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ two strands are anti-parallel **OR** parallel but in opposite directions **OR** one strand is in the 5’ to 3’ direction and the other strand is in the 3’ to 5’ direction [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ strands are twisted **OR** right-handed and turn into a double helix with around 10 base pairs per turn [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleotides consist of one phosphate group, one deoxyribose sugar and one nitrogenous base [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ phosphate group is bound to the 5’ carbon of the sugar OR deoxyribose **OR** pentose [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ phosphate group is bound to the 3’ carbon OH group of deoxyribose (of the next nucleotide) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ covalent **OR** sugar-phosphate bond between phosphate and sugar [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nitrogenous base **OR** base is bound to the 1’ carbon of the sugar **OR** deoxyribose **OR** pentose sugar [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ covalent bond between nitrogenous base and deoxyribose **OR** sugar **OR** pentose sugar [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ two strands are held together by hydrogen bonds between nitrogen bases of two nucleotides **OR** 3 hydrogen bonds between guanine and cytosine **OR** 2 hydrogen bonds between adenine and thymine [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ A pairs up with T **AND** C pairs up with G [1 mark]\n [7 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n DNA is a nucleic acid, also known as a polymer of nucleotides. Many nucleotides are joined together to form it. **One nucleotide** consists of three parts; a **phosphate group, deoxyribose sugar and a nitrogenous base** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n **One end of the phosphate group is bonded to the 5’ carbon of the sugar** ($\\checkmark$) through a condensation reaction. On the other side of the nucleotide, **the phosphate group is bonded to the 3’ carbon sugar** ($\\checkmark$) of another nucleotide through a condensation reaction. Both bonds are **covalent bonds** ($\\checkmark$). Repeated alternating sugar-phosphate bonds form the sugar-phosphate backbone. The **1’ carbon of the sugar is covalently bonded to the nitrogenous base** ($\\checkmark$)also through a condensation reaction to make up one complete nucleotide.\n\n **DNA is a double helix** ($\\checkmark$) in which nitrogenous bases form the centre of the structure, and **both strands are held together by hydrogen bonds** ($\\checkmark$) between the complementary bases from each strand. **A pairs up with T, and C always pairs up with G** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1049","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1049\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline one way unicellular organisms can carry out each of nutrition, movement and excretion. [3 marks]\n\n2. Draw a single mitochondrion, and annotate it to outline the role of four labelled structures. [5 marks]\n\n3. Compare and contrast the structure of E. coli and a palisade mesophyll cell. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nutrition by diffusion through cell membrane **OR** engulfment **OR** phagocytosis **OR** endocytosis **OR** photosynthesis **OR** digestion by enzymes in vacuoles [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ movement by cilia **OR** flagella **OR** pseudopodia [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ excretion by simple diffusion **OR** active transport **OR** exocytosis **OR** contractile vacuoles **OR** vacuoles storing **OR** isolating waste before removal [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Unicellular organisms carry out the processes of life in varying ways. They can carry out **nutrition** by **engulfing** the nutrients ($\\checkmark$) from their surroundings, they can **move** using **flagella** ($\\checkmark$), and they can **remove wastes** from the cell by **simple diffusion** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Diagram must include four of the following labels and annotations:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ outer membrane protective barrier **OR** contains transport proteins that allow specific molecules to enter the mitochondrion [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ inner membrane **OR** cristae increase surface area for reactions **OR** contains proteins essential for reactions **OR** ATP production [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA encodes genes essential for mitochondrial functioning [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 70S ribosomes for synthesis of proteins required by mitochondrion [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ matrix contains enzymes for chemical reactions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ intermembrane space - allows build-up of protons **OR** ion gradient for ATP synthesis **OR** chemiosmosis **OR** contains enzymes for chemical reactions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ clear drawing with no sketchy lines and of suitable size [1 mark]\n [5 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1049.png\" alt=\"BI1049\">\n\n3. any **eight** of the following (*must include at least **two** similarity and **two** differences to gain full marks*):\n\n ***Similarities***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** *E.coli* and palisade mesophyll cells have cell **OR** plasma membranes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** *E.coli* and palisade mesophyll cells have cytoplasm [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** *E.coli* and palisade mesophyll cells have a cell wall [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** *E.coli* and palisade mesophyll cells contain ribosomes [1 mark]\n\n ***Differences***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ *E.coli* cell wall is made up of peptidoglycan **WHEREAS** palisade mesophyll cell walls are made of cellulose [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ palisade mesophyll cells have a compartmentalised structure **WHEREAS** *E.coli* do not [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ palisade mesophyll cells have 80S ribosomes **WHEREAS** *E.coli* have 70S [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ palisade mesophyll cells have nuclei **WHEREAS** *E.coli* have a nucleoid [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ *E.coli* can have plasmids **OR** circular DNA **WHEREAS** palisade mesophyll cells have linear DNA [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ *E.coli* have external structures such as flagella **OR** pili **BUT** palisade mesophyll cells do not [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ palisade mesophyll cells have membrane bound organelles such as golgi apparatus, rough endoplasmic reticulum, chloroplasts, mitochondria **WHEREAS** *E.coli* do not [1 mark]\n [8 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Palisade mesophyll cells and *E.coli* both have **cell membranes** ($\\checkmark$) and **cytoplasm** ($\\checkmark$). They also both can have **cell walls** ($\\checkmark$), however the cell wall in *E.coli* is composed of **peptidoglycan**, whereas the cell wall in palisade mesophyll cells is composed of **cellulose** ($\\checkmark$). Palisade mesophyll cells are full of **membrane bound** organelles such as the golgi apparatus, which gives them a **compartmentalised** structure, whereas *E.coli* **do not** have a compartmentalised structure or membrane bound organelles ($\\checkmark$). Both palisade mesophyll cells and *E.coli* have **ribosomes** ($\\checkmark$), but *E.coli* have **70S** ribosomes and palisade mesophyll cells have **80S** ribosomes ($\\checkmark$). DNA is also stored differently in palisade mesophyll cells and *E.coli*. Palisade mesophyll cells store their DNA inside the **nucleus** and it has a **linear** structure, whereas *E.coli* have DNA floating freely in the cytoplasm as a **nucleoid** ($\\checkmark$) and can sometimes contain extra DNA in the form of a **plasmid** ($\\checkmark$). E.coli can also have external structures such as **pili** or flagella ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1050","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1050\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Distinguish between a colony of algal cells forming a multicellular sphere and a multicellular plant such as a water lily. [4 marks]\n\n2. Draw a labelled diagram which shows the structure of an animal cell. [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe the evidence for the endosymbiotic theory. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ colony of algal cells are generally undifferentiated **WHEREAS** multicellular plants like water lilies are composed of different types **OR** differentiated of cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ no **OR** minimal organisation **OR** minimal division of labour of cells in algal colony, **WHEREAS** in multicellular plant cells arranged into tissues, organs and organ systems, **OR** well-defined structures carry out specific roles in plants [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ each individual algal cell is capable of performing all necessary functions for life **OR** survival independently **WHEREAS** most individual cells of multicellular plants cannot survive independently **OR** away from the main body of the plant [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cells in an algal colony show limited coordination **OR** communication, **WHEREAS** plant cells are connected by plasmodesmata and coordinated by plant hormones **OR** signalling [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ development of colonies is often simple **WHEREAS** plants undergo complex developmental processes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ reproduction in an algal colony typically involves asexual methods **WHEREAS** in multicellular plants such as the water lily typically involves sexual reproduction **OR** multicellular plants use both sexual and asexual reproduction [1 mark]\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Unlike true multicellular organisms like water lilies, algal colonies **lack cellular differentiation** and **specialised tissues**. **Individual cells** within the colony are largely independent, being able to perform all vital functions, and show little coordination with one another. In contrast, cells in the multicellular plant communicate through plasmodesmata and hormones ($\\checkmark$) and usually **do not survive** when **separated** from the plant ($\\checkmark$). These cells become **specialised** ($\\checkmark$) and form **tissues, organs**, and **organ systems** within the plant. This intricate **division of labour** ($\\checkmark$) allows plants to achieve greater size and complexity.\n\n2. any **four** of the following labelled correctly in an appropriate drawing of an animal cell:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleus (shown with double membrane) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cytoplasm [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cell **OR** plasma membrane [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mitochondrion [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ribosome [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ golgi apparatus [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ rough endoplasmic reticulum [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ smooth endoplasmic reticulum [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ lysosome [1 mark]\n\n and the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ diagram drawn without sketchy lines and acceptable size [1 mark]\n [5 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1050.png\" alt=\"BI1050\">\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ larger prokaryotes **OR** cells engulfed smaller prokaryotes **OR** cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ingested cell was kept alive [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ symbiotic relationship between both cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ingested cell acquired a second membrane [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ingested cell is ancestor of chloroplasts **OR** mitochondria [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chloroplast **OR** mitochondrial structures provide evidence for the endosymbiotic theory [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chloroplasts **OR** mitochondria contain 70S ribosomes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chloroplasts **OR** mitochondria contain their own DNA **OR** looped DNA [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chloroplasts **OR** mitochondria can conduct transcription **OR** translation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chloroplasts **OR** mitochondria divide in a similar manner to prokaryotic cells **OR** by binary fission [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chloroplasts **OR** mitochondria are similar in size to prokaryotes [1 mark]\n [7 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The endosymbiotic theory explains the origin of some structures in eukaryotic cells, including chloroplasts and mitochondria. The **ancestors** of chloroplasts and mitochondria were prokaryotes that **were engulfed by larger prokaryotes** ($\\checkmark$). The ingested cells were **kept alive** ($\\checkmark$) and both cells eventually developed a **symbiotic** relationship ($\\checkmark$) with the cell that engulfed them. When the larger cells engulfed the smaller cells, the smaller cells gained a **second membrane** ($\\checkmark$) as they were engulfed via endocytosis. Further evidence for the theory is that chloroplasts and mitochondria contain their own **70s ribosomes** ($\\checkmark$) so can **make their own proteins**, and also contain **their own DNA** ($\\checkmark$) so can carry out **transcription** ($\\checkmark$). Chloroplasts and mitochondria are also able to **divide in a similar manner to prokaryotic cells** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1051","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1051\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the binomial naming system and its importance.     [4 marks]\n\n2. Explain how a sample of cells taken from a foetus can be used to determine whether the foetus will be biologically male or biologically female at birth.        [5 marks]\n\n3. Outline the outcomes of the human genome project and their impacts on different fields of science. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ binomial naming system as the system used to name species     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genus name and species name     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ species with the same genus share many characteristics **OR** are closely related    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ internationally agreed        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ facilitates understanding and communication between scientists     [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The binomial naming system is the system which scientists use to **name species** ($\\checkmark$) and this is **internationally agreed** ($\\checkmark$), which means it’s used all over the world, which aids in the **communication** between scientists ($\\checkmark$) in different countries. The name of each species has two parts, the first highlights the **genus** to which it belongs and the second part refers to the **species** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cells grown in a culture medium [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cells stained [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ observed under a light microscope [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ photomicrograph taken during mitosis **OR** mitotic metaphase [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chromosomes arranged according to size **OR** position of centromeres [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ look at chromosome pair 23 [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ two large chromosomes mean foetus is XX [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ one large chromosome and one small chromosome means foetus is XY [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ XX is biologically female **AND** XY is biologically male [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Once the sample of cells has been taken from the foetus they are grown in a **culture medium** ($\\checkmark$). They are then **stained** ($\\checkmark$) and observed under a **light microscope** ($\\checkmark$). A **photomicrograph** is taken ($\\checkmark$), and the image is cut up so that the **chromosomes** can be **arranged by size** and position of the **centromere** ($\\checkmark$). A foetus with **two large X** chromosomes will be **biologically female** ($\\checkmark$), and a foetus with **one large X** and **one small Y** chromosome will be **biologically male** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the Human Genome Project (HGP) sequenced the entire human genome    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it determined the total number of base pairs in the human genome    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it estimated the number of genes in the human genome             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ identified the loci of genes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ identified repeat sequences     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ identified locations of single nucleotide polymorphisms            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ identified gene sequences associated with certain diseases **OR** biological processes **OR** traits    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ HGP stimulated further development **OR** reduced the price of sequencing technology    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ technology developed for the HGP is now used to diagnose diseases **OR** identify micro-organisms OR DNA matching in forensics and medicine **OR** genetic modification  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contributed to the field by identifying genetic markers that affect how individuals metabolise and respond to medications **OR** contribute information for personalised medicine        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ insights into evolutionary relationships **OR** genetic similarities **OR** genetic differences [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ identification of regulatory elements **OR** non-coding RNAs **OR** components that play essential roles in gene expression **OR** cellular function  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ emphasised importance of consideration of ethical **OR** legal **OR** social implications of genomic research  [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The Human Genome Project (HGP) **sequenced** the entire human genome ($\\checkmark$), **identified the loci** of genes ($\\checkmark$) and identified gene sequences that are connected to some **diseases** ($\\checkmark$) such as breast cancer. Locations of **single nucleotide polymorphisms** ($\\checkmark$) were discovered which are variations in individuals DNA base pairs. The results have contributed to the development of **personalised medicine** ($\\checkmark$) as genetic markers have been identified that affect how individuals respond to medications. The technology developed for the HGP has led to further advancements in technology, **reducing the cost** of genetic sequencing ($\\checkmark$).  The HGP also emphasised how important it is to consider the **ethical implications** ($\\checkmark$) of genomic research and the importance of informed consent."},{"ref":"BI1052","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1052\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Palmitic acid is an example of a saturated fatty acid with a 16 carbon chain. Draw the structure of a palmitic acid. $^*$ [3 marks]\n\n2. Testosterone is a steroid hormone, which is carried in the blood. Explain how, when testosterone reaches its target cells, it brings about its effect on the DNA of that cell. [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain how the structure of cellulose and glycogen are related to their functions. [8 marks]\n\n$^*$ *Note: Starting May 2028, this question may be assessed differently due to changes to the Biology guide, including the use of a data booklet.*","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ only single bonds in the carbon chain [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ a carboxyl group on the 1st or 16th carbon [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ no double bonds present (except in carboxyl group) and all carbons in the chain have four bonds [1 mark]\n\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1052i.png\" alt=\"BI1052i\">\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nonpolar, so diffuses through the cell membrane\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ testosterone binds to a specific intracellular receptor in cytoplasm [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hormone-receptor complex is formed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ conformational change occurs allowing entry into nucleus **OR** complex enters nucleus [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ binds to specific DNA sequences of, **OR** to promoter regions of, target genes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ changes transcriptional activity of target genes\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ newly synthesised mRNA exits nucleus and enters cytoplasm for translation\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ synthesised proteins bring about response [1 mark]\n\n [5 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nWhen testosterone reaches its target cell it **diffuses** through the **cell membrane** ($\\checkmark$) into the cytoplasm of the cell. Once there it attaches to a **specific intracellular receptor** ($\\checkmark$), forming a **hormone-receptor complex** ($\\checkmark$). The conformational change of this complex enables it to **enter the nucleus** ($\\checkmark$) where it **binds** to the **promoter region** of the target genes ($\\checkmark$), bringing about **transcription**, and enabling the production of specific **proteins** to bring about the **response** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following (should include **AT LEAST ONE** for cellulose and **AT LEAST ONE** for glycogen):\n\n *Cellulose:*\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cellulose is made up of the monomer $\\beta$-glucose [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cellulose forms straight chains because the $\\beta$-glucose are oriented alternately upwards and downwards with $\\beta$-1,4 linkages [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the straight chains in cellulose provide structural support [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ $\\beta$-glucose molecules in cellulose form hydrogen bonds between polymers, which form microfibrils [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cellulose microfibrils have a high tensile strength which makes the cell wall strong [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ high tensile strength of cellulose prevents the cell from bursting under osmotic pressure [1 mark]\n\n *Glycogen:*\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycogen is made up of $\\alpha$-glucose molecules [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ $\\alpha$-glucose molecules are oriented the same way, resulting in a curved/twisted structure [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the compact structure of glycogen means a lot can be stored in a small space [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycogen is branched so that glucose can quickly be released for respiration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycogen does not have a fixed size so extra molecules of glucose can be added or taken away as needed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** cellulose and glycogen are insoluble in water due to their sizes so do not affect the osmotic pressure of the cell [1 mark]\n\n [8 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nCellulose is made up of **$\\beta$-glucose** ($\\checkmark$) molecules oriented alternately upwards and downwards which results in a **straight chain** ($\\checkmark$) and the **formation of hydrogen bonds**, contributing to the formation of **microfibrils** ($\\checkmark$). Microfibrils have a **high tensile strength** which contributes to a **strong cell wall** ($\\checkmark$). The **high tensile strength** ($\\checkmark$) of microfibrils prevents the cell from bursting **under osmotic pressure** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n Glycogen is made up of **$\\alpha$-glucose molecules** ($\\checkmark$), all oriented the same way, giving it a **twisted structure** ($\\checkmark$), which means it can be **stored in a small space** ($\\checkmark$). **Glycogen is branched** so that it can quickly be **digested by enzymes** and does not have a **fixed size** ($\\checkmark$), so glucose molecules can easily be **added and taken away** ($\\checkmark$). Both cellulose and glycogen are **insoluble in water** so do not affect the **osmotic pressure** of the cell ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1053","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1053\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Draw a diagram of a generalised amino acid, and label the functional groups. $^*$ [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline how a dipeptide is formed. [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain how extreme pH and extreme temperature affect the structure and function of proteins. [7 marks]\n\n$^*$ *Note: Starting May 2028, this question may be assessed differently due to changes to the Biology guide, including the use of a data booklet.*","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ amine group drawn correctly to one side of central carbon [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carboxyl group drawn correctly to other side of central carbon. [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ‘R’ group drawn correctly above or below central C and H drawn on opposite side. [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carboxyl group, amino groups and ‘R’ group all labelled correctly. [1 mark]\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1053a.png\" alt=\"BI1053a\">\n\n2. any **five** of the following (accept a correctly labelled structural drawing showing the reaction between two amino acids):\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ dipeptide is composed of two amino acids [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ joined by a peptide bond [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ condensation reaction [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ water formed from hydroxyl group **OR** OH from carboxyl group of one amino acid and hydrogen ion **OR** H:sup[+] from the other amino acid [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sharing of electrons between C of carboxyl group of one amino acid and N of amino group of other amino acid [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ covalent bond [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carboxyl group of one amino acid **AND** amine OR amino group of the other [1 mark]\n [5 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Dipeptides are formed from **two amino acids** ($\\checkmark$). The **carboxyl group** from one amino acid joins the **amino group** of another amino acid ($\\checkmark$). This is a **condensation reaction** ($\\checkmark$) which results in the formation of a **peptide bond** ($\\checkmark$), a **covalent** bond ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperatures which are too high, **OR** above the optimum, can lead to denaturation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ pH levels which are too high **OR** too low, **OR** too different from the optimum pH, can lead to denaturation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ denaturation is a change in the 3-dimensional structure **OR** shape of a protein [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature increases motion **OR** kinetic energy of molecules in protein [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increased motion **OR** kinetic energy puts stress on bonds holding structure together [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ break hydrogen bonds so structure breaks down [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ change in pH can affect the charges holding protein structure together [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ protein cannot maintain **OR** take on normal shape [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ proteins shape **OR** structure is specific to its function [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ enzymes can’t bind to substrates when denatured **OR** other suitable example of a protein and its function being affected [1 mark]\n [7 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n When **temperature** increases **too far** above the optimal temperature for a protein, it **increases** the **kinetic energy of molecules** ($\\checkmark$) in the protein, putting **stress** on the **bonds** holding the protein together ($\\checkmark$), so **hydrogen bonds** begin to **break** ($\\checkmark$) and the **3-dimensional structure breaks** down ($\\checkmark$). A **pH** which is **too high** above, or **too low** below the optimum pH **affects the charges** ($\\checkmark$) holding the protein structure together. The protein can **no longer** form its **normal shape** ($\\checkmark$). As the structure of a protein is **specific to its function** ($\\checkmark$), a change in the structure will not permit it to carry out its function. When the normal protein shape is disrupted, leading to loss of activity, the protein is said to be **denatured** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1055","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1055\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Draw and label the fluid mosaic model of a cell’s plasma membrane. [4 marks]\n\n2. Distinguish between integral and peripheral proteins. [4 marks]\n\n3. Compare and contrast the structure and function of glycoproteins and glycolipids in the plasma membrane. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ integral protein across the bilayer [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ protein channel across the bilayer [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ phospholipid bilayer with phosphate heads pointing outside and lipid tails pointing inwards [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ peripheral protein (on surface of the bilayer) **OR** embedded protein (half-way into the membrane) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycoprotein **OR** glycolipid [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cholesterol between the phospholipid tails [1 mark]\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1055.png\" alt=\"BI1055\">\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ integral proteins are permanently attached to the cell membrane **WHILE** peripheral proteins are temporarily attached [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ integral proteins are embedded across the cell membrane **WHILE** peripheral proteins are attached to one surface of the membrane only [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ integral proteins have both a hydrophobic region and a hydrophilic region **WHILE** peripheral proteins are only/mostly hydrophilic [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ integral proteins mainly serve as channels, transporters, or pumps **WHILE** peripheral proteins do not transport materials across the cell membrane [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ integral proteins are held in the cell membrane by hydrophobic interactions **WHILE** peripheral proteins held in place by integral proteins of the membrane, or cytoskeleton [1 mark]\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n | Integral Proteins | Peripheral Proteins | |\n | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n | Integral proteins are **embedded** across the cell membrane | Peripheral proteins are either attached to the **outer** surface of the membrane | $\\hspace{2em}$ ($\\checkmark$) $\\hspace{2em}$ |\n | Integral proteins have **both** a hydrophobic and a hydrophilic region | Peripheral proteins are **hydrophilic** | $\\hspace{2em}$ ($\\checkmark$) $\\hspace{2em}$ |\n | Integral proteins function as **channel** proteins, carrier proteins and protein pumps |peripheral proteins have **no transport** function across the cell membrane | $\\hspace{2em}$ ($\\checkmark$) $\\hspace{2em}$ |\n | Integral proteins are held in the membrane by **electrostatic bonds** (hydrophobic) | Peripheral proteins on the inside surface of the plasma membrane are **held by the cytoskeleton** | $\\hspace{2em}$ ($\\checkmark$) $\\hspace{2em}$ |\n\n3. any **eight** of the following (must include at least **two** similarities and **two** differences to gain full marks):\n\n ***Similarities:*** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have carbohydrate chains as part of their structure [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** glycoproteins and glycolipids are involved in cell recognition [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contribute to cell adhesion [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbohydrate chains on **BOTH** molecules act as unique markers that allow cells to interact with their environment [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** types of molecules contribute to the glycocalyx, providing protection and lubrication for the cell surface [1 mark]\n\n ***Differences:*** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycoproteins consist of a protein backbone with attached carbohydrate chains, **WHEREAS** glycolipids consist of phospholipid with attached carbohydrate chains [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycoproteins have a variety of roles **WHEREAS** glycolipids primarily contribute to membrane stability and lubrication [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycoproteins act as hormone receptors, **WHEREAS** glycolipids do not [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycoproteins can have enzymatic roles **WHEREAS** glycolipids do not [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycolipids play a more direct role **THAN** glycoproteins in affecting membrane fluidity due to their lipid component [1 mark]\n [7 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Both** glycoproteins and glycolipids have **carbohydrate chains** ($\\checkmark$) as part of their structure, which act as **unique markers** that allow **interaction** with the environment ($\\checkmark$). However, in **glycoproteins** the carbohydrate is attached to a protein whereas, in **glycolipids** it is attached to a **phospholipid** ($\\checkmark$). **Both** glycolipids and glycoproteins are involved in **cell recognition** ($\\checkmark$) and contribute to **cell adhesion** ($\\checkmark$). However, **glycoproteins** have a **greater variety** of roles than glycolipids ($\\checkmark$), which are **primarily** involved in **membrane stability** and lubrication ($\\checkmark$). **Glycoproteins** can also act as **hormone receptors** ($\\checkmark$) and **enzymes** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1056","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1056\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline how gel electrophoresis separates molecules and suggest a use for this separation technique. [3 marks]\n\n2. Explain how compartmentalisation is advantageous to eukaryotic cells. [5 marks]\n\n3. In eukaryotic cells, transcription occurs in the nucleus and translation occurs in the cytoplasm. Explain the benefits to the cell of this physical separation.     [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ separates molecules by passing them through a gel using an electrical charge    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ separates molecules by size **OR** charge, **OR** smaller **OR** shorter molecules move further than larger **OR** longer ones                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ separating DNA **OR** protein **OR** RNA,  **OR** DNA profiling **OR** DNA fingerprinting     [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Gel electrophoresis separates molecules by **size or charge** ($\\checkmark$). The molecules are placed into wells in the gel, and an **electrical charge** is applied ($\\checkmark$). This process can be used to **separate DNA fragments**, in DNA profiling ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows for division of labour    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enzymes and substrates involved in the same processes can be in close proximity to each other          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$   reactions can happen faster **OR** more efficiently        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ optimal pH can be maintained in areas of the cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ substances that can cause damage to the cell can be safely stored **OR** reduces risk of damage from digestive **OR** hydrolytic enzymes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organelles can be moved around the cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ post-transcriptional modifications such as splicing can take place    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lower chance of non-specific interactions    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Eukaryotes having a compartmentalised structure means that enzymes and substrates involved in the same processes can be in close **proximity** ($\\checkmark$) to each other. **Optimal pH** ($\\checkmark$) can be maintained in certain areas of the cell so reactions can happen **faster** or more efficiently ($\\checkmark$) . It also means that substances that can damage the cell such as digestive enzymes can be **safely stored** ($\\checkmark$) , and organelles such as ribosomes can **move** around the cell ($\\checkmark$). It also allows important processes to occur such as **splicing** of pre-mRNA ($\\checkmark$), which increases the variety of polypeptides that can be made.\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleus provides a controlled environment for transcription        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA protected within nucleus    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transcription factors **OR** regulatory proteins can enter nucleus     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ this separation allows for precise control over when and where genes are transcribed    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ribosomes are located in the cytoplasm        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ newly transcribed RNA can be processed and modified in the nucleus before it is transported to the cytoplasm (for translation)         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ this spatial separation prevents potential conflicts between transcription and translation machinery    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ modifications of mRNA in nucleus are essential for the maturation and stability of the RNA before it is transported to the cytoplasm     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ribosomes can simultaneously synthesise multiple polypeptides from a single mRNA molecule, increasing efficiency             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA molecules that are not correctly processed **OR** modified in the nucleus can be prevented from entering the cytoplasm for translation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ helps maintain the accuracy of gene expression        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ regulatory proteins **OR** other factors can interact with mRNA molecules in the cytoplasm to influence their translation        [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The DNA is **protected inside the nucleus** ($\\checkmark$) and the nucleus provides a **controlled environment** for transcription ($\\checkmark$) to occur. **Transcription factors** can **enter** the nucleus ($\\checkmark$) but DNA cannot leave, so this provides **precise control** over where and when the genes are **transcribed** ($\\checkmark$). The **mRNA** is **modified** ($\\checkmark$) before leaving the nucleus, and this is essential for its **stability** ($\\checkmark$) and use in translation. **Ribosomes** are located in the **cytoplasm** ($\\checkmark$) and this **spatial separation prevents conflict** ($\\checkmark$) between transcription and translation machinery. Cytoplasmic ribosomes can **simultaneously synthesise multiple polypeptide** chains ($\\checkmark$) from one mRNA strand, **increasing efficiency** ($\\checkmark$). mRNA that has **not been properly processed** can be **prevented from leaving** the nucleus ($\\checkmark$) **preventing incorrect** proteins from being formed by **translation** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1057","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1057\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Describe how the use of stem cells may be used to treat type I diabetes in the future.     [5 marks]\n\n2. Explain the factors that limit cell size in humans.     [4 marks]\n\n3. Explain how red blood cells and striated muscle fibres are adapted to their function.    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ embryonic stem cells **OR** induced pluripotent stem cells **OR** iPSC used     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stem cells contain desired, **OR** insulin, allele     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stem cells differentiate to become pancreatic **OR** β-cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cells are implanted **OR** inserted into the pancreas    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ insulin produced in response to increase in blood glucose    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ immunity suppressing drugs used        [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Embryonic stem cells** ($\\checkmark$) with the **insulin gene** ($\\checkmark$) are stimulated to divide and **differentiate into β-cells** ($\\checkmark$) then **injected into the pancreas** ($\\checkmark$).  Implanted cells **produce insulin** ($\\checkmark$) in response to increasing blood glucose. **Immunosuppression drugs** ($\\checkmark$) may need to be taken if using embryonic stem cells.\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ surface area affects the rate at which materials enter and leave the cell     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ volume affects the rate at which materials are used and produced by the cell  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the surface area to volume ratio affects the rate of metabolism in a cell     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the bigger the cell, the smaller the SA to volume ratio is    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cells having a low SA to volume ratio cannot exchange materials fast enough\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some cells, **OR** motor neurons, **OR** muscle cells, **OR** other named example in humans, are long and thin, which maintains a high SA to volume ratio        [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **surface area** of a cell affects the **rate** at which **materials enter** and **leave** cells ($\\checkmark$). On the other hand, the cell’s **volume** affects the rate at which **materials** are **used and produced** ($\\checkmark$). A **small surface area to volume ratio** is not ideal for human cells because it **limits** the cell’s ability to **export waste from the cytoplasm** rapidly enough ($\\checkmark$) to meet the cytoplasm’s **metabolic needs** ($\\checkmark$), such as the availability of oxygen. A **large cell** has a **small surface area to volume ratio** ($\\checkmark$). Therefore, the transport of the substances is more efficient in small cells.\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    ***red blood cells:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ small and flexible to fit through narrow capillaries [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contain haemoglobin to bind to oxygen **OR** to carry oxygen [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ biconcave to increase surface area        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ no nucleus and lack other organelles to provide more space for haemoglobin    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Striated muscle fibres:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell membrane capable of electrical impulse **OR** action potential propagation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ long cells so produce movement when work together        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lots of mitochondria to supply ATP for contraction        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contain contractile proteins to bring about movement    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ multinucleate supports large size of cell **OR** aids in controlling protein synthesis **OR** repair sarcoplasmic reticulum to store and release calcium ions    [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Red blood cells** are adapted for **oxygen transport** ($\\checkmark$). They are **small** and **flexible** to squeeze through capillaries ($\\checkmark$) and have a **biconcave** shape to **increase** their **surface area** ($\\checkmark$). They **lack** a **nucleus** and most **other organelles** ($\\checkmark$) to make room for the **haemoglobin** which **binds**, and carries, **oxygen** ($\\checkmark$) around the body.\n\n    **Striated muscle fibres** are adapted for **voluntary movement** ($\\checkmark$). They contain **contractile proteins** ($\\checkmark$) and are **long** so when the fibres **contract together** they bring about significant **movement** ($\\checkmark$). They contain **many mitochondria** to provide **ATP** for contraction ($\\checkmark$) and also have **sarcoplasmic reticulum** which stores and releases **calcium ions** ($\\checkmark$) to control muscle contraction. The **many nuclei** are a result of the fusing of cells to produce the long fibres, and these **support** the large cell and **control protein production** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1059","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1059\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nGas exchange is very important for both plants and animals.\n\n1. Outline the properties of gas-exchange surfaces.    [4 marks]\n\n2. Describe how muscles bring about pressure changes during inspiration in humans.        [4 marks]\n\n3. Transpiration is a result of gas exchange in plants. Explain how different environmental factors affect the rate of transpiration.     [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ thin **OR** one cell thick for short diffusion distance     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large surface area for maximum diffusion rate                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ moist for gasses to dissolve, facilitating movement            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ often supplied with good transport system to maintain steep diffusion gradient    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ partially permeable membranes **OR** permeable to gases        [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Gas-exchange surfaces have a **large surface area** to maximise **diffusion** rate ($\\checkmark$). They are **thin**, often one cell thick, to make the **diffusion distance** as **short** as possible ($\\checkmark$). They are usually **moist** so the **gases** can **dissolve** in them ($\\checkmark$), to facilitate movement. The membranes are **permeable** to gases to **diffuse** through them ($\\checkmark$), and they often have a **good transport system** ($\\checkmark$) to maintain a **steep diffusion gradient** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ diaphragm contracts and moves downwards     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ external intercostal muscles contract **OR** internal intercostal muscles relax    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ribcage is pulled upwards and outwards        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increasing the volume of the thorax            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ air particles become more spread out **OR** lower pressure inside the thorax      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ air flows into the lungs     [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    During inspiration, the **diaphragm contracts** and moves downwards ($\\checkmark$).  **External intercostal muscles** contract ($\\checkmark$) and pull the ribcage **upwards** and outwards ($\\checkmark$) which **increases the volume** ($\\checkmark$) of the thoracic cavity. When the volume increases, the **pressure** inside the thoracic cavity **decreases** ($\\checkmark$) moving **air into** the lungs ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transpiration involves the loss of water through stomata    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ environmental factors that affect water loss through transpiration are temperature, humidity **and** wind **OR** air movement        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at higher temperatures, the rate of transpiration increases        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ because heat increases rate of evaporation in leaves **OR** rate water diffuses out of stomata **OR** the kinetic energy of the water molecules is greater    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at extremely high temperatures, the rate of transpiration may decrease    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ because stomata will close to prevent further water loss            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at higher humidity, the rate of transpiration decreases        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ because when surrounding humidity is high, the rate water evaporates in leaves decreases **OR** the rate water vapour diffuses out of stomata decreases **OR** the concentration gradient between the inside of the leaf and the surrounding air is lower [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at higher light intensity, the rate of transpiration increases        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ because light intensity stimulates stomata to open allowing water to diffuse out    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased wind speed increases the rate of transpiration            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ because wind carries the water vapour away from the stomata increasing the concentration gradient around the pores            [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Transpiration involves the loss of water from leaves through **stomata** ($\\checkmark$). The rate of transpiration is affected by environmental factors such as **temperature, humidity, and air movement** ($\\checkmark$). **Higher temperatures** cause the rate of transpiration to **increase** ($\\checkmark$) because **heat energy** absorbed by water in the inner air space of leaves causes water to **evaporate more rapidly** ($\\checkmark$). **Higher humidity** causes the rate of transpiration to **decrease** ($\\checkmark$). This is because air that is more humid carries more water particles in it which reduces the **concentration gradient** ($\\checkmark$) between the inside of the stomata and outside, thus reducing the rate water diffuses out of the stomata.  Lastly, **increases in wind speed** can **increase** the rate of transpiration ($\\checkmark$).  Wind carries away water vapour from the surface of leaves, which **increases the concentration gradient** ($\\checkmark$) of water between the inside and outside of the stomata."},{"ref":"BI1064","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1064\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline how the sperm is adapted for its role of fertilising the egg. [4 marks]\n\n2. Describe what happens when a sperm reaches the egg in the oviduct to when fertilisation is complete.    [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain the role of hormones in the control of the ovarian and uterine cycles.     [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ haploid nucleus **OR**  nucleus contains half of the genetic material to fuse with nucleus of egg during fertilisation [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ acrosome contains enzymes that help penetrate the egg        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ midpiece packed with mitochondria **OR** lots of mitochondria to provide energy for movement [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tail **OR** flagellum propels the sperm towards the egg    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ streamlined shape **OR** very small to reduce resistance as it swims     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chemotaxis **OR** can detect chemical signals released by the egg for navigation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ short lifespan ensures only most viable sperm have a chance to fertilise egg    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The sperm is very **small** and **streamlined** to **reduce** the **resistance** as it swims ($\\checkmark$) through fluids. It has a **flagellum** to **propel** itself forward ($\\checkmark$) and **many mitochondria** in the midpiece to provide the **energy**  ($\\checkmark$) for swimming movements. The **acrosome** contains digestive **enzymes** to help the sperm **penetrate** the protective outer **layers of the egg** ($\\checkmark$) and a **haploid nucleus** ($\\checkmark$) to provide half the genetic material during fertilisation.\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enzymes in sperm head help penetrate plasma membrane **OR** zona pellucida of egg    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sperm plasma membrane fuses with egg plasma membrane    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles released from egg break down sperm flagellum **OR** mid-piece    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sperm haploid nucleus enters egg    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ haploid nucleus of sperm forms pronucleus    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA in pronucleus of egg and pronucleus of sperm **OR** pronuclei replicates    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pronuclei fuse **OR** nucleus of the sperm fuses with the nucleus of the egg    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ forms diploid zygote **OR** zygote contains complete set of chromosomes     [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    When the sperm reaches the egg, it releases **enzymes** from the **acrosome** to help **penetrate** the egg’s cell **membrane** ($\\checkmark$). The plasma **membrane** of the head of the **sperm fuses** with the plasma **membrane** of the **egg** ($\\checkmark$), and the **haploid nucleus enters** the egg’s cytoplasm ($\\checkmark$). **Vesicles** released from the egg **destroy** the sperm’s **flagellum** and **midpiece** ($\\checkmark$). The sperm’s nucleus **forms** a **pronucleus** ($\\checkmark$). The **DNA** in the pronucleus of sperm and pronucleus of egg **replicates** ready for mitosis ($\\checkmark$), and then they **fuse** to form a **diploid zygote** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hypothalamus secretes Gonadotropin-Releasing Hormone (GnRH)        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ GnRH stimulates the anterior pituitary gland to release gonadotropin hormones    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gonadotropin hormones are follicle-stimulating hormone **OR** FSH and luteinising hormone **OR** LH [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ LH  **OR** luteinising hormone triggers ovulation [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ovarian cycle involves follicular phase **OR** development of follicles, ovulation, **AND** luteal phase **OR** corpus luteum    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ uterine cycle controls preparation and maintenance of uterine lining **OR** endometrium  ready for fertilised egg        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ FSH **OR** follicle-stimulating hormone stimulates follicular phase **OR** development of follicles\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ follicles produce oestradiol as they grow    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oestradiol promotes the thickening **OR** increases blood vessel density of the uterine lining **OR** endometrium     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ positive feedback loop as oestradiol increases FSH and LH levels        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in levels of FSH and LH results in development of Graafian follicle     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ surge in LH (and FSH) leads to ovulation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oestradiol inhibits further release of FSH     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ inhibiting FSH is a negative feedback loop **OR** prevents excessive follicular development    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ remaining follicular cells transform into the corpus luteum        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ corpus luteum produces progesterone (and oestradiol) to maintain the uterine lining    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ high levels of progesterone and oestradiol prevent production of GnRH **OR** has negative feedback effect on GnRH **OR** hypothalamus    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if pregnancy doesn't occur, corpus luteum degenerates, therefore decreasing progesterone and oestradiol levels, triggering menstruation        [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **gonadotrophin** hormones, **luteinising hormone** (LH) and **follicle-stimulating hormone** (FSH) ($\\checkmark$) are released from the **pituitary** ($\\checkmark$) when **GnRH** levels rise ($\\checkmark$). **FSH** triggers the **development** of the **follicles** ($\\checkmark$), and as they develop, they **release oestradiol** ($\\checkmark$). The **rising** levels of **oestradiol** cause a further **increase** in **FSH** and **LH**, so this is an example of a **positive feedback** ($\\checkmark$) loop. A **surge in LH**, and to a lesser extent, **FSH**, brings about **ovulation** ($\\checkmark$). **Oestradiol** now **inhibits** further release of **FSH** ($\\checkmark$) to prevent more follicles from developing, an example of a **negative feedback** loop ($\\checkmark$). The remaining cells of the ruptured Graafian follicle now develop in the **corpus luteum**, an endocrine gland in its own right. This **secretes progesterone** and **oestradiol** to **maintain** the thickened **endometrium** ($\\checkmark$), brought about by the **increase** in **oestradiol** before ovulation ($\\checkmark$). If pregnancy does not occur, the **corpus luteum breaks down, progesterone** and **oestradiol levels drop**, and **menstruation** begins ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1065","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1065\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the stages of solvation when water acts as the solvent.        [5 marks]\n\n2. Describe what you would see down a microscope if an onion cell is first put into pure water and then into a strong salt solution.    [4 marks]\n\n3. Explain the movement of water across the cell surface membrane when an animal cell is placed in different solute concentrations.    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ separation of solute particles **OR** molecules **OR** ions [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ breaking of intermolecular forces OR ionic OR hydrogen bonds holding solute particles together [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ disruption of hydrogen bonds between water molecules to accommodate solute particles [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ attractive forces **OR** hydrogen bonds between solute and water molecules form  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water molecules surround solute particles **OR** \"hydration shell\" surrounds each solute particle, preventing  re-aggregating [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ over time, a dynamic equilibrium is established between solute particles leaving the solid and water molecules joining the hydration shells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at maximum solubility of the solute in the water, solution is saturated [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Solvation is the interaction of water molecules with **solute particles**. First, the **solute particles** must **separate** ($\\checkmark$), which often involves the **breaking of intermolecular bonds** ($\\checkmark$)  holding the solute particles together. Some **hydrogen bonds** between **water molecules** also **break**  ($\\checkmark$) . The **water molecules** then form **hydrogen bonds** or electrostatic attractions with the **solute** ($\\checkmark$). These **water molecules** surround the **solute** particles, forming **hydration shells** that **prevent** them from **re-joining** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following (*must include at least **one** from pure water and **one** from strong salt solution to gain full marks*):\n\n    ***pure water:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water moves into the cell, **AND** the cell contents swell  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell membrane pushes against the cell wall [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the cell has become turgid, **OR** cell exerts turgor pressure [1 mark]\n\n    ***strong salt solution:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water moves out of the cell, **AND** the cell contents begin to shrink **OR** reduce in volume [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell membrane pulls away from cell wall [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plasmolysis occurs [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    When a plant cell is put into **pure water** and is observed under a microscope, the **cell contents** can be seen to **swell** ($\\checkmark$), and the **cell membrane pushes** out against the **cell wall** ($\\checkmark$), exerting **turgor pressure** ($\\checkmark$). If the plant cell is then put into a strong salt solution, the **cell contents shrink** ($\\checkmark$), the **cell membrane pulls away** from the **cell wall** ($\\checkmark$), and the cell is said to be **plasmolysed** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following (*must include at least **one** of the first 3 to gain full marks*):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water moves across the membrane by osmosis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water molecules move by diffusion    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ membrane is selectively permeable    **OR** membrane is impermeable to some solutes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water moves through phospholipid bilayer **OR** membrane, which is not a barrier to water    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ net movement of water until equilibrium is reached **OR** until solute concentrations are the same    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water diffuses **OR** moves towards area of higher solute concentration    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ passive transport **OR** no ATP needed    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water moves into cell if placed in hypotonic solution    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell swells, **OR** lysis occurs **OR** cell bursts in hypotonic solution    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water moves out of cell if placed in hypertonic solution    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell shrivels, **OR** crenation occurs in hypertonic solution    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ no net movement of water if cell is placed in isotonic solution     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell maintains normal shape in isotonic solutions                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rate of diffusion **OR** osmosis **OR** water movement is increased by water channels **OR** specific proteins in membranes **OR** aquaporins    [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Water diffuses** ($\\checkmark$) directly **across** the membrane **phospholipid bilayer** ($\\checkmark$) in a process called **osmosis** ($\\checkmark$).  When there is a difference in solute concentration, **water** will **move** to areas of **higher solute** concentration ($\\checkmark$). **Water moves into** an animal **cell** if placed in a **hypotonic solution** ($\\checkmark$), which may lead to **lysis** ($\\checkmark$) of the cell. **Water moves out** of a cell if placed in a **hypertonic solution** ($\\checkmark$), which may lead to **crenation** ($\\checkmark$). There is **no net movement** of water if the cell is placed in an **isotonic solution** ($\\checkmark$), and so the cell will maintain its **normal shape** ($\\checkmark$). The movement of water is a type of **passive transport** ($\\checkmark$) and does not involve ATP."},{"ref":"BI1066","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1066\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Explain how parents with blood type A and blood type B can have a child with blood type O.    [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline how polygenic traits can show a normal distribution.        [4 marks]\n\n3. Explain the role of inheritance in genetic diseases.        [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following (accept any marking point clearly shown in a genetic diagram):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the allele for blood group A **OR** allele I:sup[A], is dominant over the allele for blood group O **OR** allele i     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the allele for blood group B **OR** allele I:sup[B], is dominant over the allele for blood group O **OR** allele i      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ parents with blood group A or blood group B could be heterozygous **OR** I:sup[A]i and I:sup[B]i      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a parent who is heterozygous for blood group A can pass on the allele for blood group O **OR** allele i        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a parent who is heterozygous for blood group B can pass on the allele for blood group O **OR** allele i        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ offspring that receive the allele for blood type O **OR** i allele from both parents will be blood group O    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The alleles for blood type A and blood type B are **dominant** ($\\checkmark$) to the allele for blood type O. Parents with blood type A or B could be **heterozygous** ($\\checkmark$) and possess the recessive allele for blood type O. If the offspring **inherits the recessive allele** ($\\checkmark$) from both parents, then they will have blood type O.\n\n   <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polygenic traits are caused by two or more genes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the greater the number of genes that are responsible for a trait, the greater the number of phenotypes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polygenic traits often show continuous variation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ which is a continuous range of variation rather than distinct categories    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is a large range of phenotypes, where the majority of organisms are in the middle of the normal distribution curve            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polygenic traits are often influenced by the environment    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Polygenic traits are caused by **two or more genes** ($\\checkmark$). The **higher the number of genes** ($\\checkmark$) that are responsible for a trait, the **higher the number of possible phenotypes** ($\\checkmark$) which means that the traits show **continuous variation** ($\\checkmark$). Continuous variation is when a phenotype shows a **continuous range of variation** ($\\checkmark$), rather than having distinct categories and usually shows a normal distribution, where the majority of organisms are in the **middle of the normal distribution curve** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic diseases are controlled by genes **OR** alleles        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ alleles are passed from parents to offspring **OR** gametes have one allele for each characteristic from each parent        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at fertilization the alleles from both parents come together **OR** the combination of alleles determines the characteristic in the offspring    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ diseases caused by dominant alleles only need the allele from one parent to cause the disease in the offspring        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Huntington’s is an autosomal dominant condition **OR** any other valid autosomal dominant condition example            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ diseases caused by recessive alleles need the allele from both parents to cause the disease in the offspring        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phenylketonuria is an autosomal recessive condition **OR** any other valid autosomal recessive condition example        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phenylketonuria caused by mutation of gene that codes for enzyme needed to convert phenylalanine to tyrosine **OR** other suitable example        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (most) sex-linked characteristics are controlled by alleles found on the X chromosome            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ haemophilia is a sex-linked recessive condition **OR** any other valid sex-linked recessive condition example            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sex-linked genetic diseases are more common in males than females     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if a mother is affected by a sex-linked characteristic all of her sons will have the condition        [1 mark]\n    [9 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Genetic diseases are **controlled by alleles** ($\\checkmark$), inherited from biological parents. During meiosis, gametes are produced, which contain **one allele for each characteristic** ($\\checkmark$), and these come together during **fertilization**. The **combination** of alleles passed to offspring can determine whether the offspring is affected by a genetic disease or not ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    Some genetic diseases, such as **Huntington’s disease** ($\\checkmark$), are caused by **dominant alleles** ($\\checkmark$), so one copy of the allele needs to be inherited for the disease to be expressed and therefore affected by the disease. Other genetic diseases, such as **cystic fibrosis**, are caused by **recessive alleles** ($\\checkmark$), which means two copies need to be inherited, one from each biological parent, in order to be expressed and affected with the disease.\n\n    Sex-linked genetic disorders, such as **haemophilia** ($\\checkmark$), are mostly controlled by genes found on the **X chromosome** ($\\checkmark$). If a male inherits an allele that codes for a genetic disorder from his biological mother, he will be affected; however, if a female inherits the same allele, she won’t always be affected, depending on the allele she inherited on the X chromosome from her biological father. It’s because of this that males have a **higher chance** ($\\checkmark$) of being affected by sex-linked genetic diseases."},{"ref":"BI1067","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1067\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Distinguish between RNA and DNA.         [3 marks]\n\n2. Explain the importance of orientation and directionality of DNA and RNA.          [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe how the Hershey and Chase experiment demonstrated that DNA carries the genetic information .       [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  RNA is single-stranded  **WHEREAS** DNA is double-stranded    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA has a variety of shapes **WHEREAS** DNA is a double helix     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA contains ribose sugar  **WHEREAS** DNA contains deoxyribose        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA contains the nitrogenous base uracil **OR** U **WHEREAS** DNA contains thymine **OR** T [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA only contains permanent genetic code in RNA viruses **WHEREAS** DNA contains permanent genetic code in all cells (except red blood cells)        [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    RNA is a **single stranded** molecule containing ribose sugar and uracil as one of its four nitrogenous bases. DNA on the other hand is **double-stranded** ($\\checkmark$), contains **deoxyribose** instead of **ribose** sugar ($\\checkmark$), and has **thymine** instead of **uracil** ($\\checkmark$) as one of its four nitrogenous bases. DNA forms a regular **double helix**, whereas RNA can form **different shapes**  ($\\checkmark$) depending on its role, such as the clover-shaped tRNA and long-stranded mRNA.\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 5’ **OR** 5 prime end  refers to the end with a phosphate group bound to the 5th carbon of the deoxyribose **OR** pentose sugar, but not bound to another nucleotide, the 3’ **OR** 3 prime end refers to the end where the 3rd carbon of the deoxyribose **OR** pentose sugar is not bound to another nucleotide            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA has antiparallel strands **OR** one strand is in the 5’ to 3’ direction, and another strand is in the 3’ to 5’ strand         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ single DNA strand is tilted; therefore, two DNA strands can only bind together if they are in antiparallel orientation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in DNA replication, one lagging strand and one leading strand are produced     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in DNA replication DNA polymerase only adds free nucleotides to the 3’ end of the growing DNA strand **OR** adds free nucleotides in the 5’ to 3’ direction        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in transcription RNA polymerase can only add nucleotides in the 5’ to 3’ direction to form mRNA        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ translation must start nearest to the 5’ end of the mRNA    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Both RNA and DNA have directionality they have a **5’** and **3’** end. The **5’ end** refers to the end with a **phosphate group** attached to carbon 5 of the deoxyribose, but **not attached** to another **nucleotide**, the **3’** end refers to the end where **carbon 3** of the deoxyribose is **not attached** to another **nucleotide** ($\\checkmark$). DNA forms a double helix with **antiparallel** strands ($\\checkmark$). During **replication DNA polymerase** can only **add free nucleotides** in the **5’ to 3’** direction ($\\checkmark$). During **transcription RNA polymerase** can only **add nucleotides** in the **5’ to 3’** direction ($\\checkmark$) to form a strand of mRNA. **Translation** of the mRNA must **start at the 5’** end ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ bacteriophages **OR** phage virus was used         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ bacteriophage contains an inner core that has DNA and a protein coat    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ experiments tested the hypothesis that DNA and not protein carries the genetic information    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ bacteriophage **OR** virus can attach to the surface of bacterial cell **OR** host and inject substance into the cell        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ substance contains information for bacteria cell **OR** host to duplicate **OR** produce more bacteriophages **OR** viruses        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ bacteriophages **OR** virus were labeled with radioactive phosphorus (:sup[32]P) and radioactive sulfur (:sup[35]S) **OR** :sup[32]P was incorporated into DNA core and :sup[35]S was incorporated into protein coat        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phosphorus is found only in DNA and not protein        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sulfur is found only in protein and not DNA    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *Escherichia coli* **OR** bacterial cells were infected with bacteriophage **OR** virus  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ used centrifuge to separate bacteriophage **OR** virus from bacteria        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ virus in liquid **OR** supernatant did not contain radioactive :sup[32]P but bacterial sediment **OR** pellet contained radioactive :sup[32]P            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ :sup[32]P DNA entered the bacteria **OR** host cell for replication          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ virus in liquid **OR** supernatant contained radioactive :sup[35]S but bacterial sediment **OR** pellet did not contain radioactive :sup[35]S            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ :sup[35]S protein structure did not enter the bacteria **OR** host cell for replication     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic material needs to enter the bacterial cell in order to produce new viruses     [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Hershey and Chase's experiment was conducted to investigate whether DNA or protein carries the genetic material. In their experiment, they used **bacteriophages** ($\\checkmark$) to infect bacteria cells. **Bacteriophage** contains an **outer coat made of protein** and an **inner core containing DNA** ($\\checkmark$) which makes it suitable for comparing DNA and protein. The bacteriophage can then **attach itself to the surface of the bacteria cell** and **inject the substance** ($\\checkmark$) which carries important genetic information into the bacteria cell.\n\n    The bacteriophages were then cultured under two different conditions — **one was incorporated with a radioactive phosphorus isotope, :sup[32]P** and another with a radioactive **sulfur isotope, :sup[35]S** ($\\checkmark$). Radioactive elements 32P and 35S were used because **phosphorus is found in the DNA structure** but **absent in protein** ($\\checkmark$), whereas **sulfur**, is **found in** the **protein** structure but **absent** in the **DNA** ($\\checkmark$) structure.\n\n    The bacteria cells were then cultured together with the bacteriophages and were **infected with the bacteriophages** ($\\checkmark$). After centrifugation, it was found that the pellets containing **bacteria cultured** with the **radioactive :sup[32]P bacteriophages contained radioactivity**, whereas the **bacteriophage protein coats** in the supernatant **didn’t** ($\\checkmark$).  The pellets containing the **bacteria cultured** with the **radioactive :sup[35]S bacteriophages did not contain any radioactivity**, whereas the **bacteriophage protein coats** in the supernatant **did** ($\\checkmark$). These provided evidence that DNA labelled with the **:sup[32]P entered the bacteria cells**, whereas the **protein** which was labelled with the **:sup[35]S did not** ($\\checkmark$) enter the bacteria cells."},{"ref":"BI1069","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1069\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Distinguish between a myofibril, the sarcoplasmic reticulum and the sarcolemma. [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline the different proteins and their position in the sarcomere when the muscle is relaxed.    [4 marks]\n\n3. Describe the process of muscle contraction.        [9 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myofibrils are the basic unit inside the muscle fibres **OR** they are composed of  end-to-end sarcomeres **OR** they are long contractile fibres **OR** they are bundles of protein filaments, mainly actin and myosin        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sarcolemma is the plasma membrane of the muscle fibre **OR** cell, **OR** it envelops the muscle fibre        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sarcoplasmic reticulum is a specialised endoplasmic reticulum that stores calcium, **OR** releases calcium ions when stimulated by a nerve impulse **OR** action potential        [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Myofibrils, sarcolemma and sarcoplasmic reticulum are all found in muscle fibres. **Myofibrils** are the basic units of muscle fibres ($\\checkmark$); they are made of **repeating sarcomeres** laid end-to-end and are responsible for muscle contraction. The **sarcolemma** is the membrane that surrounds each **muscle fibre**, similar to the **plasma membrane** ($\\checkmark$) in any other cell. The **sarcoplasmic** reticulum is a **specialised endoplasmic reticulum**. When a nerve impulse reaches the muscle fibre, depolarisation spreads to the sarcoplasmic reticulum. This activates voltage-sensitive calcium channels in the sarcoplasmic reticulum causing them to **release calcium ions** ($\\checkmark$) from the sarcoplasmic reticulum storage. This initiates the process of muscle contraction.\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ five types of protein involved in muscle contraction: titin, myosin, actin, troponin and tropomyosin    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tropomyosin binds to **OR** wraps around, actin, covering binding sites        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ actin, troponin and tropomyosin make up thin filaments            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myosin makes up majority of  thick filaments        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ titin holds myosin fibres in place in sarcomere     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ titin is an immense protein        [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    There are **five types of protein** involved in muscle contraction: **titin, troponin, tropomyosin, myosin and actin** ($\\checkmark$). They all together make up a sarcomere unit that is part of a muscle fibre. **Actin**, troponin and tropomyosin make up the **thin filaments** ($\\checkmark$) and **myosin** makes up the **thick filaments** ($\\checkmark$) in the sarcomere. **Titin** is an immense protein that **holds** the **myosin fibres** in place ($\\checkmark$) in the sarcomere.\n\n    **Tropomyosin is bound to the actin** and troponin ($\\checkmark$), and when the muscle is relaxed, troponin covers myosin binding sites on actin.\n\n3. any **nine** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sarcomeres are the repeating functional units of myofibrils        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sarcomeres can shorten    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a nerve impulse reaches the myofibril bringing about an action potential in the sarcolemma **OR** action potential passes down T-tubules of sarcolemma    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ calcium ions are released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ calcium ions bind to troponin     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ calcium attachment to troponin causes a movement of tropomyosin exposing the binding sites on actin    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myosin heads attach to the binding sites on actin **OR** cross-bridges form between actin and myosin        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myosin heads release ADP and rotate **OR** carry out power stroke, pulling the actin filament inwards    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP attaches to the myosin head and breaks the cross-bridge        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrolysis of ATP moves the myosin head into the cocked **OR** ready position    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ repeat of the process shortens the sarcomere        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the Z lines are pulled closer together        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ shortening of the sarcomeres in a myofibril causes muscle contraction    [1 mark]\n    [9 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The structure of sarcomeres allows them to **shorten, which causes muscle contraction** ($\\checkmark$). When a nerve impulse reaches the sarcoplasmic reticulum of myofibril, **calcium ions** are released ($\\checkmark$). **Calcium ions bind** to a protein called **troponin** in the sarcomere ($\\checkmark$). **Troponin moves** another protein called **tropomyosin** away from the binding sites on actin ($\\checkmark$). **Myosin heads** are in the **cocked position** and contain stored energy from **ATP hydrolysis** ($\\checkmark$). They bind to the **binding sites on actin** to form a **cross bridge** ($\\checkmark$). The stored energy and **ADP are released** ($\\checkmark$). When they are released, the **myosin heads move inwards** (“power stroke”) and pull on the actin ($\\checkmark$). **ATP attaches** to myosin heads and breaks the cross-bridge ($\\checkmark$). **Hydrolysis of ATP moves the myosin heads into the cocked position** ($\\checkmark$). This process repeats until the calcium ions are no longer available. **Repeating** this process **shortens the sarcomeres** ($\\checkmark$) in a myofibril and causes **muscle contraction** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1079","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1079\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Explain why a resting potential is negative.    [3 marks]\n\n2. Explain how the structure of a neuron is adapted to its function. [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe the events that occur to propagate an action potential within a neuron and between two neurons.        [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sodium-potassium pumps move 3 Na$^+$ ions out of the cell and 2 K$^+$ ions into the cell     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ membrane is more permeable to  K$^+$ ions, so  K$^+$ ions leak back out of the cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there are negative ions **OR** anions, **OR** substances carrying negative charge,  inside the cell than outside    [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    A resting potential is negative because **sodium-potassium pumps** in the membrane pump **3 Na$^+$** ions **out** of the cell for every **2 K$^+$** ions that are pumped **in** ($\\checkmark$), making the outside of the cell more positive and the inside less positive. Additionally, the membrane is **more permeable to  K$^+$ ions**, so  K$^+$ ions **leak back out** of the cell ($\\checkmark$), adding further to the charge imbalance. Finally, there is higher concentration of **negative charge**, such as Cl$^-$ ions, **inside the cell** ($\\checkmark$), which make the inside of the cell more negative.\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ long axon fibre to carry electrical impulses over relatively long distances in the body    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ many branched dendrites to receive **OR** send electrical impulses from many neurons    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ myelination **OR** Schwann cells provide insulation so that electrical impulses are conducted faster    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ saltatory conduction **OR** nodes of Ranvier allow impulses to jump from node to node so that the impulse is transported faster        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contain many mitochondria which provide energy to construct neurotransmitters    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell body processes and integrates incoming signals from dendrites before determining whether to generate an action potential     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at the end of the axon, terminal branches form synapses with other neurons **OR** target cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the presence of synaptic vesicles within axon terminals enables the storage and release of neurotransmitters, facilitating efficient communication        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ neurons possess a variety of ion channels, including voltage-gated channels, which regulate the movement of ions across the cell membrane    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ postsynaptic membrane contains receptors that specifically bind to neurotransmitters released by other neurons        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ neurons can undergo structural and functional changes **OR** neuronal plasticity in response to experience or learning        [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The structure of a neuron is adapted to its function by having **long** axon fibres to carry electrical impulses over relatively **long distances in the body** ($\\checkmark$). Neurons have many-branched **dendrites** to receive multiple signals from **other neurons** ($\\checkmark$). In the axons **myelination** provides **insulation** so that electrical impulses are conducted **faster** ($\\checkmark$).  Inbetween the myelin sheaths there are myelin-free gaps called **nodes of Ranvier** which allow action potential to move **faster** ($\\checkmark$) by. **jumping from node to node**.\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stimulus, such as change in membrane voltage or neurotransmitter binding to a receptor, triggers the opening of sodium channels in the axon’s initial segment    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ leads to depolarising of membrane    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if change in potential reaches threshold potential **OR** -55 to -50mV, it triggers the opening of voltage-gated sodium channels    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rapid influx of sodium ions, causing action potential        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ positive feedback loop **OR** greater change in potential results in more sodium voltage-gated channels opening        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ after reaching peak positive potential **OR** +40 mV, voltage-gated sodium channels start to close, and voltage-gated potassium channels open    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ potassium ions move out of the cell, repolarising the membrane and bringing it back towards the resting potential    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hyperpolarisation **OR** refractory period prevents further action potential in that region until sodium-potassium pump restores the resting membrane potential and ionic balance    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ action potential in one region means sodium ions diffuse along inside of axon to neighbouring regions, which changes membrane potential in this region    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ causing this region to surpass the threshold potential        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ action potential cannot go backwards, despite diffusion of sodium ions as sodium voltage-gated channels can’t open during absolute refractory period        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ action potential reaching the presynaptic knob, leading to the opening of voltage-gated calcium channels        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ influx of  Ca:sup[2+] ions causes vesicles containing neurotransmitter to fuse with the presynaptic membrane        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ neurotransmitter is released into the synapse by exocytosis    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ neurotransmitter diffuses across the synapse **AND** binds to receptors on the postsynaptic membrane    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ triggers the opening of sodium channels in the postsynaptic membrane    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if postsynaptic potential reaches threshold potential (-55 mV to -50 mV), an action potential is generated in the next neuron [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Local currents control the propagation of an action potential within a neuron. This occurs when **sodium channels open**, letting Na:sup[+] ions into the cell ($\\checkmark$), which raises the membrane potential so that it meets the **threshold potential** ($\\checkmark$), this triggers an **action potential** ($\\checkmark$). However, due to the presence of a higher concentration of Na:sup[+] ions within the axon, **Na:sup[+] ions diffuse inside** the axon to the next region of the axon, which is at resting potential ($\\checkmark$), this causes the next region to depolarise and **reach the threshold potential** ($\\checkmark$), so that the voltage-gated **sodium channels open** ($\\checkmark$), and the process repeats. Once the wave of **depolarisation** reaches the presynaptic membrane, it **causes the voltage-gated calcium channels** in the presynaptic knob to **open** ($\\checkmark$). **Calcium ions** therefore **diffuse into the cell**, which triggers **vesicles containing neurotransmitters** to **fuse with** the **presynaptic membrane** ($\\checkmark$).  The neurotransmitters **diffuse** across the **synapse and bind to receptors** on the postsynaptic membrane ($\\checkmark$), which triggers sodium channels to open, and the process begins again."},{"ref":"BI1089","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1089\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the chemical properties of amino acid R-groups based on their charge.[4 marks]\n\n2. With reference to bonding and association, compare and contrast conjugated and non-conjugated proteins.     [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe the four levels of protein structure.    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ non-polar R-groups are hydrophobic    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polar R-groups are hydrophilic         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ positively charged  R-groups are basic     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ negatively charged R-groups are acidic    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ amphoteric R-groups can be positively, negatively or neutrally charged **OR** change charge depending on pH            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ neutral polar R-groups are partially positively and partially negatively charged **OR** have polar functional groups        [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Non-polar R-groups are **hydrophobic** ($\\checkmark$), and polar R-groups are **hydrophilic** ($\\checkmark$). Hydrophilic R-groups can be **acidic (-'vely charge) ($\\checkmark$) or basic (+'vely charged)** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following (*must include at least **one** similarity and **one** difference to gain full marks*):\n\n    ***Similarities*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are composed of amino acids joined together by peptide bonds    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can contain ionic bonds         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can contain alpha-helices **OR** beta-pleated sheets     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can contain disulfide covalent bonds [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can contain hydrogen bonds         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can contain hydrophobic interactions [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can be fibrous **OR** globular             [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ conjugated proteins are associated with other chemical **OR** non-protein **OR** prosthetic groups **WHEREAS** non-conjugated proteins are not associated with other chemical **OR** non-protein **OR** prosthetic groups            [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    There are many similarities in the bonding of conjugated and non-conjugated proteins because they can both contain **ionic bonds** ($\\checkmark$), **disulfide covalent bonds** ($\\checkmark$), **hydrogen bonds** ($\\checkmark$), and **hydrophobic interactions** ($\\checkmark$). However, there are some differences in their associations because conjugated proteins are associated with other **chemical groups**, such as carbohydrates, whereas non-conjugated proteins are not ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ primary structure is the sequence of amino acids **OR** precise position of each amino acid       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ amino acids held together by covalent **OR** peptide bonds [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sequence of amino acids determines overall 3D shape of protein due to different R-groups   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ secondary structure has pleating and coiling **OR** local folding of polypeptide chain        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ alpha helices **OR** beta-pleated sheets formed by hydrogen bonding [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ all hydrogen bonding of secondary structure between non-adjacent amine and carboxyl groups in peptide bonds                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tertiary structure as a result of interactions between R-groups **OR** adjacent parts of the polypeptide chain            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ionic bonding as a result of charged R-groups [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pairs of cysteines can form disulfide bonds     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrophobic interactions responsible for protein folding **OR** hydrophobic regions are oriented towards the inside of the protein molecule **OR** hydrophobic amino acids can be found clustered together in the centre of globular proteins            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in integral proteins, hydrophilic amino acids can be found exposed to the cytoplasm or extracellular matrix, **OR** hydrophilic amino acids can be found exposed to aqueous solution     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in integral proteins, hydrophobic amino acids can be found embedded within the membrane    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ quaternary structure is the association of two or more polypeptide chains     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ quaternary structure stabilised by ionic **OR** hydrogen **OR** disulfide **OR** similar interactions to tertiary structure, but between different polypeptide chains            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ conjugated proteins are associated with other chemical **OR** non-protein **OR** prosthetic groups **OR** non-conjugated proteins are not associated with other chemical **OR** non-protein **OR** prosthetic groups [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The primary structure of a protein refers to the **sequence of amino acids** ($\\checkmark$) that make up the polypeptide chain and their precise position. The secondary structure of a protein is a result of hydrogen bonding between **non-adjacent carboxyl** and **amine groups** ($\\checkmark$), resulting in the formation of **alpha-helices and beta-pleated sheets** ($\\checkmark$). The tertiary structure of a protein is the result of interactions between the **R-groups** of amino acids ($\\checkmark$). Charged R-groups can form **ionic bonds** ($\\checkmark$). **Hydrophobic interactions** due to the presence of non-polar R-groups can also cause the protein to fold into different shapes ($\\checkmark$). In integral proteins, hydrophobic amino acids help to **embed** them in the plasma membrane ($\\checkmark$). The quaternary structure of a  protein is when it is composed of **more than one** polypeptide chain ($\\checkmark$). These proteins are referred to as **conjugated** if they are associated with other chemical groups, such as carbohydrates or **non-conjugated** if they are not associated with other chemical groups ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1092","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1092\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast intracellular and extracellular chemical reactions, given one example of each type of reaction.     [5 marks]\n\n2. Enzymes are regulated by inhibitors that are classified as competitive or non-competitive.  Distinguish between these two types of inhibitors.     [4 marks]\n\n3. Describe, using an example, how metabolic pathways can be regulated by end-product inhibition.         [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **five** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** similarity and **one** difference **AND** **one** example of **each** type of reaction to gain full marks*):\n\n    ***Similarities*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are controlled by enzymes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** types of reactions are tightly regulated    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ intracellular reactions occur inside cells **WHEREAS** extracellular reactions occur outside a cell        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ intracellular reactions are more focused on the specific needs of the cell, **WHEREAS** extracellular reactions often involve interactions between cells **OR**, tissues **OR** contribute to broader physiological processes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ intracellular reactions are primarily controlled by cellular mechanisms, **WHEREAS**  extracellular reactions might involve the integration of signals from multiple cells **OR** systems [1 mark]\n\n    ***Examples*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ intracellular example is glycolysis **OR** Krebs cycle **OR** photosynthesis **OR** any other suitable example        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ extracellular example is chemical breakdown in digestive system **OR** any other suitable example [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Intracellular and extracellular chemical reactions are **both tightly regulated** ($\\checkmark$) by **enzymes** ($\\checkmark$), however, **intracellular** reactions go on **inside cells**, and **extracellular** reactions go on **outside** of cells ($\\checkmark$). **Glycolysis** is an example of an **intracellular** reaction ($\\checkmark$) as it occurs in the cytoplasm of cells. The **chemical breakdown of food** is an example of **extracellular** reactions ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ competitive inhibitors have a shape similar to that of the substrate **WHILE** non-competitive inhibitors have a completely different shape from the substrate        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ competitive inhibitors bind to the active site **WHILE** non-competitive inhibitors bind to a site other than the active site, **OR** allosteric site     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ competitive inhibitors have no effect on **OR** do not change the shape of the active site **WHILE** binding of non-competitive inhibitors changes the shape of the active site     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in competitive inhibition, increasing substrate concentration can overcome inhibition **WHILE** in non-competitive inhibition increasing substrate concentration will not have this effect     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ example of competitive inhibitor is disulfiram inhibition of alcohol dehydrogenase **WHILE** example of non-competitive inhibitor is isoleucine end-product inhibition of threonine deaminase, **OR** any other valid examples of corresponding inhibitors        [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    | competitive inhibition | non-competitive inhibition |\n    | :-: | :-: |\n    | substrate and inhibitor have a **similar** shape | substrate and inhibitor have **different** shapes ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | the inhibitor can **bind to the active** site | inhibitor **binds to an allosteric** site ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | inhibitor has **no effect on the shape** of the active site | inhibitor **changes the shape of the active site** after binding ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | increasing substrate concentration **reduces** inhibition | increasing substrate concentration **does not reduce** inhibition ($\\checkmark$) |\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the product of the last reaction will inhibit an enzyme at the beginning of the pathway **OR** a clearly drawn and labelled diagram     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the end-product binds to the allosteric site of the enzyme        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the end-product acts as a non-competitive inhibitor        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when bound the inhibitor changes the shape of the active enzyme so the substrate can no longer bind        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ end-product inhibition is reversible         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as the concentration of the end-product decreases inhibition decreases as well, so the pathway can resume             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ end product inhibition is an example of negative feedback inhibition     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ intermediate products **OR** substrates do not build up if there is an excess of the end-product [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ threonine is converted to isoleucine in a series of enzyme-controlled steps **OR** any other valid example of end-product inhibition    [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1092.png\" alt=\"BI1092\">\n\n    In some metabolic pathways, regulation is achieved by end-product inhibition. The product of the last reaction will **inhibit the first enzyme** ($\\checkmark$) in the pathway. **Threonine is converted to isoleucine**, and this pathway is an example\n($\\checkmark$) of end-product inhibition\n\n    The end-product (isoleucine) **binds to the allosteric site** ($\\checkmark$) of the first enzyme and changes the shape of the active site of the enzyme so the **substrate can no longer bind** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    Isoleucine acts as a **non-competitive inhibitor** ($\\checkmark$). The binding of the **end product is reversible** ($\\checkmark$). Once the end-product detaches from the enzyme, the enzyme can once again catalyse reactions.\n\n    End-product inhibition is an **example of negative feedback inhibition** ($\\checkmark$). If there is an excess of the end product, **intermediate products do not build up** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1095","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1095\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Describe the mutualistic relationship between insect-pollinated plants and pollinators.[4 marks]\n\n2. Outline the self-incompatibility mechanisms that some plants have to prevent fertilisation resulting from self-pollination.    [4 marks]\n\n3. Explain how insect-pollinated flowers are adapted to their function.    [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following (*must include at least **one** statement for insect-pollinated plants and at least **one** for pollinators*)\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ both benefit from the relationship        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ insect-pollinated plants need pollinators to transfer pollen from the anther to the stigma **OR** from the male reproductive structures to the female reproductive structures    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  insect-pollinated plants need pollinators for fertilisation **OR** seed production **OR** to reproduce        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  pollinators increase genetic variation of plant species through cross-polllination    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  pollinators obtain food from the nectar and pollen of flowers [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  nectar provides carbohydrate **OR** is an energy source        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  pollen provides proteins and other nutrients [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The relationship between insect-pollinated plants and pollinators is a mutualistic relationship because **both benefit** ($\\checkmark$). Pollinators obtain **food** from the **nectar** and **pollen** ($\\checkmark$) of flowers. Pollinators are necessary for insect-pollinated plants **to reproduce** ($\\checkmark$). Pollinators **transfer pollen** from the **anther** to the **stigma** ($\\checkmark$). Pollinators **increase** the **genetic variation** of the plant species through **cross-pollination** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ S-gene system **OR** genetic mechanisms control self-incompatibility in many flowering plants\n [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ S-locus contains many different alleles across a population [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ each plant expresses specific S-allele **OR** S-alleles **OR** have specific alleles in BOTH pollen and stigma **OR** style **OR** carpel [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when pollen lands on a stigma, the plant compares the alleles **OR** S-allele of the pollen with the allele **OR** S-allele of stigma **OR** style **OR** carpel [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if  alleles **OR** S-alleles match **OR** are the same, the pollen is recognised as \"self\"  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gametophytic self-incompatibility **OR** GSI **OR** pollen tube growth inhibited within the style **OR** pollen tube is stopped during its growth towards ovule **OR** pollen tube forms but growth of it is arrested **OR** it does not grow down style **OR** carpel [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sporophytic self-incompatibility **OR** SSI **OR** pollen germination blocked [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ recognition of self-pollen activates biochemical pathways **OR** degradation of pollen RNA **OR** inhibition of pollen tube enzymes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ male gamete **OR** pollen nucleus breaks down before entering ovule **OR** ovary **OR** fusing with ovum [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ embryo formed but breaks down before seed formed [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Plants can prevent self-pollination through genetic mechanisms. If the pollen is recognised as ‘self’ by the plant the **germination** of the **pollen grain** to form a **pollen tube** can be **halted** ($\\checkmark$). If the **pollen tube** does manage to germinate it is **prevented** from **growing down to the ovary** ($\\checkmark$). The **pollen nucleus** may also **break down**, ($\\checkmark$) preventing fertilisation if it reaches the ovary. If fertilisation is successful the **embryo** may be **prevented from developing** ($\\checkmark$) into a seed.\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ flowers are used for sexual reproduction    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ insect-pollinated flowers have coloured petals to attract pollinators    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ insect-pollinated flowers have nectar, to attract **OR** reward, pollinators    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ anthers produce pollen that is consumed by pollinators     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the nectary is inside the flower so that pollinators touch the anthers and stigma when feeding            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pollen grains have spines which stick to insects **OR** pollinator            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the ovule develops into the seed, which is used for dispersal            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the ovary develops into the fruit, which is used for dispersal **OR** attracts animals to eat it for seed dispersal        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a large amount of pollen is produced to increase the likelihood of pollination    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some species of flowers are structured so that only one species can pollinate them        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ flowers attract insects using smell **OR** gases         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sepal protects bud **OR** developing flower             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ filament holds anther in best position to touch pollinators [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ top of stigma is sticky so holds pollen in place for germination    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ flowering occurs at same time **OR** after certain night length for the same species to increase chance of cross-pollination        [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Flowers are adapted to their function in several ways. The structure and shape of flowers are adapted for reproduction. **Coloured petals** ($\\checkmark$) and the **nectary** attract pollinators ($\\checkmark$). When pollinators **feed** on nectar and **pollen** on one plant ($\\checkmark$), some of the **pollen** from the anther **sticks** to the insect due to **spikes** on the pollen ($\\checkmark$).  Then, when the same pollinator **feeds** on another plant's nectar or pollen, some of the pollen lands on the **stigma** ($\\checkmark$) because the nectary is inside the flower. This is known as pollination and is used for **sexual reproduction** ($\\checkmark$) in flowering plants. Both the **seed**, which develops **from the ovule** ($\\checkmark$), and the **fruit**, which develops from the **ovary**, are used for seed dispersal ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1096","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1096\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Describe how a mutation may be a silent mutation. [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline five causes of gene mutations.    [5 marks]\n\n3. Compare and contrast somatic and germ cell mutations including their biological implications [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ silent mutation doesn't alter the amino acid sequence of the polypeptide chain encoded by the gene [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ brought about by a point mutation [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ occurs because of degenerate OR redundant genetic code  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ multiple codons can code for the same amino acid [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ change in nucleotide may not affect amino acid it codes for [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ protein's structure OR function may remain unchanged [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    A **silent mutation** is a type of point mutation that **doesn't alter** the **amino acid sequence** of the protein encoded by the gene ($\\checkmark$). This occurs because of the **degenerate genetic code** ($\\checkmark$), where **multiple codons** can code for the **same amino acid** ($\\checkmark$). Therefore, a **change** in the third position of a codon can result in a different **nucleotide without changing** the **amino acid** it codes for ($\\checkmark$). As a result, the **protein's structure** and function remain **unchanged** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ during DNA replication, mistakes can occur [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA polymerase may insert the wrong nucleotide, **OR** DNA polymerase may slip during replication, resulting in the addition or deletion of nucleotides [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ errors in the cellular machinery responsible for correcting DNA mismatches during replication can result in mutations [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ spontaneous loss of amino groups **OR** deamination from bases can lead to the conversion of one base to another [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reactive oxygen species generated during normal cellular processes can damage DNA bases [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chemical mutagens **OR** suitable example can directly alter DNA bases [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ radiation **OR** ionising radiation **OR** X-rays **OR** gamma rays **OR** ultraviolet **OR** UV radiation can damage DNA [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ biological agents **OR** some viruses can integrate their genetic material into the host DNA, potentially disrupting or altering host genes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ exposures to environmental toxins **OR** pollutants **OR** industrial chemicals **OR** carcinogens [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transposons **OR**  \"jumping genes,\" **OR** DNA sequences that can move around within the genome can disrupt genes  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mechanical **OR** physical damage to DNA **OR** breaks **OR** lesions caused by mechanical forces can lead to mutations if not repaired correctly [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ errors in the cellular machinery responsible for correcting DNA mismatches during replication can result in mutations [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Causes of mutations can be split into spontaneous and induced mutations. An example of a spontaneous mutation is an **error during DNA replication**, such as slipping of DNA polymerase leading to the **insertion or deletion** of nucleotides ($\\checkmark$). Then, there can be **errors** in the cellular machinery responsible for **correcting DNA mismatches** during replication, which can also result in mutations ($\\checkmark$).  **Deamination of bases** can occur, which leads to the change of one base into another ($\\checkmark$). On the other hand, induced mutations result from external factors, including exposure to **chemical mutagens** like ethidium bromide ($\\checkmark$) and **radiation** sources such as **ionising** or **UV** radiation ($\\checkmark$), which can damage DNA directly. Additionally, **biological agents** like certain viruses can integrate their genetic material into host DNA, potentially disrupting genes ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    **similarities** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** arise from changes in the DNA sequence **OR** involve substitutions **OR** insertions **OR** deletions **OR** rearrangements of nucleotides [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can lead to changes in the encoded proteins or RNA molecules [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can have biological effects [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can be influenced by environmental factors [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have DNA repair mechanisms that attempt to correct mutations [1 mark]\n\n    **differences** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ somatic cell mutation occurs in body cells **WHEREAS** germ cell mutation occurs in reproductive organs [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mutations in somatic cells are not passed on to offspring **OR** do not affect the genetic makeup of future generations **OR** mutations in somatic cells can lead to health issues **OR** diseases in the affected individual **WHEREAS** mutations in germ cells can be inherited by offspring **OR** can be passed onto next generation **OR** mutations in germ cells directly affect offspring **OR** next generation [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ somatic mutations are relatively common **OR** can occur throughout an individual's life **OR** readily exposed to mutagens **WHEREAS** germ cell mutations are relatively rare in comparison **OR** are isolated in the reproductive organs **OR** are not as exposed to the same mutagenic factors as somatic cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ somatic cells have more DNA repair mechanisms **OR** can correct more types of mutations **OR** trigger cell death to prevent the spread of mutated cells **WHEREAS** germ cells have fewer DNA repair mechanisms [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ somatic mutations primarily impact the health **OR** functioning of the individual in which they occur **OR** are not directly related to evolutionary changes **WHEREAS** germ cell mutations can contribute to genetic diversity within a population **OR** can drive evolutionary changes  [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Somatic** cell mutations can occur in most of the **cells of the body**, whereas **germ** cell mutation can only occur in **reproductive organs** ($\\checkmark$). **Both** somatic and germ cell mutations come about by **changes in the base sequences** of the DNA ($\\checkmark$), which can lead to **changes** in the **resulting proteins** ($\\checkmark$) and therefore can have **biological effects** in the organisms ($\\checkmark$). However, **mutations in somatic** cells are **not passed on to offspring**, they primarily **impact** the **health** of the **individual** in which they occur, whereas mutations in **germ** cells can be **inherited by offspring** ($\\checkmark$) and so can **drive evolutionary changes** ($\\checkmark$). Mutations in **somatic** cells are **more common** than those in germ cells, as they are more readily exposed to **environmental pollutions**, whereas **germ** cells are relatively **isolated** in the reproductive organs ($\\checkmark$); and when mutations do occur **somatic cells** have **more DNA repair mechanisms** than germ cells ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1099","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1099\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Logging trees from forests and fishing from marine waters are two forms of income derived from natural resources. Outline the factors that need to be addressed to make both forms of income sustainable.    [5 marks]\n\n2. Describe how choosing to consume locally grown foods can reduce the environmental impact of agriculture, making it more sustainable.    [3 marks]\n\n3. Discuss the causes and impacts of eutrophication on water bodies due to agricultural practices.         [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **five** of the following:(*answer must includes at least **one** statement from each section*)\n\n    ***Harvesting trees OR timber as natural income*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sustainability of harvesting trees can be monitored by surveying soil characteristics of logged and unlogged sites in the forest     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ selective logging practices that only target mature trees **OR** target overpopulated trees **OR** ensure rate of logging does not exceed growth rate    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ promote reforestation **OR** afforestation **OR** replacing **OR** replanting trees [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ protect **OR** conserve biodiversity within forest        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ leave enough trees to produce fruit **OR** seeds for next season    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ monitor **OR** collect data to compare volume of trees removed with volume of growth    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ encourage certification programmes like the Forest Stewardship Council [1 mark]\n\n    ***Harvesting fish from marine waters as natural income*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sustainability fishing can be achieved by catching fish that do not diminish the fish stock    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the population size of the fish can be monitored     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the number of juveniles in the population of fish can be monitored, **OR** population of fish can be monitored to see if it has more younger or more older fish           [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ more younger fish in the population indicates a growing population, so more sustainable **OR** more older fish in the population indicates a declining population, so not sustainable    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the increase in number of fish annually will provide an estimate of what can be caught        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ maximum sustainable yield (MSY) is the amount of harvest **OR** the maximum average catch without reducing the potential for growth of the fish in the future    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ harvesting at lower than the MSY or at the rate of the MSY is favourable [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use fishing techniques **OR** equipment that minimise bycatch **OR** non-target species    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ designate marine protected areas **OR** breeding grounds    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enforce regulations to prevent overfishing **OR** illegal fishing practices    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ promote international cooperation in managing shared marine resources    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The sustainability of harvesting trees can be achieved by **replanting trees** ($\\checkmark$) that are cut down. The **volume** or mass of **timber** can be **monitored** and replaced by **planting saplings** that will **replace** the timber when **fully grown** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    Fishing practices need to be carried out so that by catching fish, the **original stock** is **not diminished** ($\\checkmark$). **Maximum sustainable yield** is the amount of harvest or catch that **does not decrease** the **potential** for **growth** ($\\checkmark$) in the future. **Harvesting fish** at a rate **lower than the maximum sustainable yield** ($\\checkmark$) will make fishing sustainable.\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ agricultural crops are usually grown in rural areas and transported to the cities        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transportation uses fossil fuels, which release carbon dioxide    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ release of carbon dioxide causes global warming     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ release of carbon dioxide increases the carbon footprint of crops    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ consumption of locally grown crops reduces carbon footprint     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ demand for unseasonal foods increases carbon footprint    **OR** consumption of seasonal foods will reduce carbon footprint      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reducing carbon footprint will make crops more sustainable        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ locally grown produce minimises need for packaging, as it is not being transported long distances and will therefore reduce waste        [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Transportation** of agricultural crops from rural areas to cities **uses fossil fuels** ($\\checkmark$). This causes the release of carbon dioxide, a greenhouse gas, **increasing** the **carbon footprint** ($\\checkmark$) of crops. **Locally grown produce** has a **smaller carbon footprint**, and because it is not being transported long distances, it needs **minimal packaging**, so there is **less waste** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    Also, eating foods grown **seasonally will reduce the carbon footprint** ($\\checkmark$) because some unseasonal foods, like strawberries, have to be brought in from where they are grown. In the long run, this is not sustainable.\n\n3. any **eight** of the following (at least one from each section):\n\n    ***Causes*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ excessive fertiliser used **OR** applying fertiliser before heavy rainfall        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ excess nutrient runoff from fertilisers     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ improper management of manure can lead to its runoff into water bodies        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nitrogen **OR** phosphates carried in runoff    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ erosion from agricultural fields can carry sediment into water bodies, which carries nutrients        [1 mark]\n\n    ***Impacts*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ excess nutrients to the water bodies will cause algae to grow rapidly **OR** algal blooms    [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ growth of algae will block sunlight to underwater plants, causing them to die    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plant and algae die under surface due to lack of light        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in nutrients **OR** dead organic matter leads to more algal growth    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in dead organic matter decomposed    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ aerobic bacteria are decomposers [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ greater oxygen demand **OR** biochemical oxygen demand **OR** BOD    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen-depleted water can lead to fish **OR** other aquatic organisms dying    [1 mark]\n\n    ***As eutrophication takes place, leads to further effects*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in net productivity    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ decrease in biodiversity **OR** diversity of organisms    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ shorter food chains    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ toxic cyanobacteria can take over    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ changes in fish communities        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some types of algae that thrive in eutrophic conditions can produce toxins harmful to humans and wildlife, which can enter the food chain and pose health risks    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ economic impacts on tourism **OR**, fisheries **OR** water treatment [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The runoff from using **fertilisers** ($\\checkmark$) in agricultural fields can contaminate local water bodies, leading to an **increase** in the **growth of algae** ($\\checkmark$) due to added nutrients. The algal growth will **block light** from penetrating the water bodies, so the **underwater plants** die and decompose ($\\checkmark$). The death of underwater plants will cause an **increase** in **dead organic matter** ($\\checkmark$), promoting the **growth** of **bacteria** ($\\checkmark$). These aerobic bacteria will cause a **decrease** in **dissolved oxygen** ($\\checkmark$) in the water as they respire and may lead to the **death** of the **fish** and other **organisms** ($\\checkmark$) in the water.\n\n    This can further lead to a **decrease in biodiversity** ($\\checkmark$) and the **growth of toxic algae** or bacteria ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1111","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1111\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the primary sources of variation in a species.     [4 marks]\n\n2. Describe the possible outcomes of a population producing more offspring than available resources can support.         [5 marks]\n\n3. John Endler carried out a series of experiments in the 1970s on the selection pressures that influenced the colour of guppy fish. State the independent variable in Endler’s experiments and explain the selective pressures exerted on male guppies. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mutations result in changes to the base sequence of DNA        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mutations can be neutral, advantageous or detrimental          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ recombination of genes during sexual reproduction        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ random orientation of chromosomes in metaphase 1 of meiosis        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ crossing over during meiosis [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Genetic variation within a species arises primarily from several sources. First, **mutations** are spontaneous **changes in the DNA sequence**,($\\checkmark$) which can result in new alleles or gene variants. **Recombination of chromosomes** during **sexual reproduction** ($\\checkmark$) results in unique combinations of alleles in offspring. Then, in meiosis, **crossing over** ($\\checkmark$) can occur during prophase 1, and **random orientation of chromosomes** ($\\checkmark$) does occur in metaphase 1 of meiosis.\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in population increases genetic diversity **OR** variation **OR** increases gene pool        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ individuals with favourable characteristics OR advantageous traits **OR** better adapted, survive and reproduce     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ species may become better adapted over time    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in population causes an increase in competition for resources     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ depletion in essential resources lowers overall fitness of population **OR** reduces reproductive success **OR** struggle for survival **OR** increases mortality rates **OR** increased suffering of individuals less adapted **OR** with unfavourable characteristics  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ higher population densities increase disease transmission **OR** environmental degradation [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ emigration reduces gene pool **OR** changes population dynamics    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Overproducing offspring in an environment with limited resources can have the advantageous effect of **increasing** the **genetic diversity** ($\\checkmark$) of the population. This increased diversity can lead to more individuals with **favourable characteristics** surviving to reproductive age ($\\checkmark$). This could eventually lead to a **better adapted population** ($\\checkmark$) over the generations.  However, this population rise also has disadvantages, as there is **greater competition for resources** ($\\checkmark$), and if **essential resources** are **depleted** too much, the **death rate may increase** ($\\checkmark$). There is also an **increased risk** of the spread of **disease** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ independent variable is presence or absence of predators    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ predation pressure from visual predators **OR** birds **OR** larger fish     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ brighter colours make guppies more vulnerable to predation **OR** more conspicuous [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sexual selection pressure [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ female guppies prefer males with vibrant colours **OR** that are more colourful     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ female guppies prefer males with more elaborate courtship displays    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in low-predation environments **OR** areas with fewer predators, guppies have brighter colours **OR** in high-predation areas, males have duller **OR** less bright colours    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ environmental conditions can influence health **OR** size of male guppies, indirectly affecting attractiveness to females    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ male-male competition can affect size **OR** aggressiveness of male guppies    [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **independent variable** in Endler’s experiments is the presence or absence of **predators** ($\\checkmark$). **Predation pressure** from visual predators ($\\checkmark$) poses a shared risk to males with bright colours, leading to **duller colours** in **high predation** environments ($\\checkmark$) to reduce detection. **Sexual selection** ($\\checkmark$), driven by female mate choice, favours **more colourful traits** ($\\checkmark$) and more **elaborate courtship displays** ($\\checkmark$) if they are to reproduce successfully. **Environmental conditions**, such as **water temperature**, also affect the **health** and **size** of males ($\\checkmark$) and indirectly **influence their attractiveness** to females. Additionally, selection pressures stemming from **male-male competition** for access to females can result in variations in traits like **size or weaponry** ($\\checkmark$), particularly in areas with high population density or resource scarcity."},{"ref":"BI1114","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1114\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the differences between mixotrophic and holozoic nutrition.  [4 marks]\n\n2. Describe how herbivores are adapted to eating plants. [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain the differences between obligate anaerobes, facultative anaerobes and obligate aerobes.    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ holozoic nutrition is a form of heterotrophic nutrition    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ holozoic nutrition involves ingesting an organism **OR** part of an organism, **OR** digesting an organism **OR** part of an organism,  internally **OR** energy source is ingestion of organism **OR** part of an organism        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mixotrophs are autotrophic and heterotrophic    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy **OR** nutrient source is sunlight **OR** photosynthesis **AND** ingestion of organisms **OR** parts of organisms  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mixotrophs can use whichever nutrient or energy source is most readily available **OR** require both forms of nutrition    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Holozoic nutrition** is a form of **heterotrophic** nutrition ($\\checkmark$). Organisms that obtain their food in this way are consumers and obtain their nutrients by **ingesting other organisms** ($\\checkmark$). **Mixotrophs** are both **autotrophs and heterotrophs** ($\\checkmark$). They obtain their nutrients by **photosynthesis** or by **ingesting** other organisms ($\\checkmark$). One type of mixotroph uses sunlight energy when it is available to make their own food, or if there is **little sunlight** energy available, they can **consume their food** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some are ruminants **OR** have fermentation chambers **OR** rumens in digestive system    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ long digestive systems **OR** large caecum for breakdown of cellulose **OR** plant material    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ coprophagy **OR** eat own faeces to re-digest plant material **OR** cellulose    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ back teeth **OR** molars **OR** premolars of herbivores are broad **OR** flat for grinding    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ bacteria **OR** microorganisms in digestive system help digest cellulose **OR** plant material    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tough tongue **OR** lips **OR** mouth to survive prickles **OR** thorns **OR** stings    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ modified mouth parts **OR** stylet to pierce phloem **OR** stem to obtain sap    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mandibles  of insects to cut leaves **OR** plant material        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ long necks **OR** legs to reach branches    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ detoxification mechanisms **OR** enzymes to remove plant toxins        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ specialised digestive enzymes to digest cellulose **OR** lignin **OR** cell walls    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Herbivores have many adaptations that allow them to consume and extract nutrients from plant material. Some possess **specialised teeth**, particularly **molars** and **premolars**, which are **flat** for **grinding** and **crushing** plant tissue ($\\checkmark$), as opposed to the sharp teeth used for tearing. Herbivores often have **complex digestive systems**, complete with **fermentation chambers** like the **rumen** in **ruminants** ($\\checkmark$). Within these chambers, **symbiotic microorganisms** help **break down** plant matter ($\\checkmark$), such as cellulose, in plant cell walls. Insects have evolved physiological adaptations such as the **stylets** of aphids that **pierce** the **phloem** to **extract sap** ($\\checkmark$) and the **mandibles** of the grasshopper, which can **cut through leaves** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **seven** of the following (at least two mark points needed for each type):\n\n    ***Obligate anaerobes*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ microorganisms **OR** unicellular organisms **OR** single-celled organisms that cannot survive or grow in the presence of oxygen    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen is toxic to them        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ obtain their energy through anaerobic respiration or fermentation processes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *Clostridium botulinum* **OR** *Clostridium tetani* **OR** other suitable example    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Facultative anaerobes*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ microorganisms OR unicellular organisms, **OR** single-celled organisms that can survive and grow in the presence or absence of oxygen    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can switch between aerobic respiration (using oxygen if available) and anaerobic respiration or fermentation (when oxygen is scarce)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *Escherichia coli* **OR** *Staphylococcus aureus* **OR** yeast **OR** other suitable example    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Obligate aerobes*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms that require oxygen for their growth and survival        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rely on aerobic respiration    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *Mycobacterium tuberculosis* **OR** *Pseudomonas aeruginosa* **OR** fish **OR** humans **OR** other suitable example    [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Microorganisms can be classified based on their oxygen requirements and how they produce ATP. First, there are **obligate anaerobes**, like ***Clostridium botulinum*** ($\\checkmark$), which **cannot survive** in the presence of **oxygen** ($\\checkmark$). They rely on **anaerobic respiration** ($\\checkmark$), or fermentation, for the production of ATP. Then, there are **facultative anaerobes**, such as ***Saccharomyces cerevisiae*** ($\\checkmark$), which are flexible. They can use **both aerobic and anaerobic** ($\\checkmark$) respiration depending on the available oxygen levels. Finally, **obligate aerobes** like ***Mycobacterium tuberculosis*** ($\\checkmark$) **cannot survive** for long **without oxygen** ($\\checkmark$) and primarily use **aerobic respiration** ($\\checkmark$) to release energy from organic compounds."},{"ref":"BI1115","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1115\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Describe the characteristics of the genetic code. [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline, with examples, the importance of complementary base pairing. [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain the roles of  RNA and ribosomes in the formation of a polypeptide chain once mRNA has exited the nucleus. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1.    any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic code consists of sequence of three nucleotides **OR** three bases **OR** triplet code **OR** codons on mRNA [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 64 **OR** 4$^3$ possible combinations [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ each of the 20 amino acids is coded for by at least one codon [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  more codons exist than there are amino acids to be coded for **OR** most amino acids coded for by more than one codon     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ degenerate code [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ code is conservative **OR** the first two bases of a codon are often more critical than the third in determining the amino acid specified **OR** wobble effect allows some flexibility in third base [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ universal **OR** nearly universal across all organisms [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ non-overlapping **OR** codons are read sequentially without overlapping [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ specific codons start **OR** stop protein synthesis [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ directional **OR** codons read in a specific direction [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The genetic code consists of **sequences of three nucleotides** ($\\checkmark$), known as **codons** which make up mRNA. As there are **64** different **codons** ($\\checkmark$) most of the **20 amino acids** are coded for by **more than one** ($\\checkmark$); this is known as a **degenerate code** ($\\checkmark$). The code includes **specific start** and **stop** codons ($\\checkmark$) that initiate and terminate translation respectively. Additionally, the genetic code is **nearly universal** applying across **almost all living organisms** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.    any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  allows for semi-conservative replication [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in DNA replication, each parent DNA strand acts as a template for a new one so new DNA molecules produced are identical to the parent DNA strands [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adenine pairs with thymine **OR** uracil and cytosine pairs with guanine **OR** A pairs with T **OR** U  and C with G [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  to provide structural stability to the DNA molecule    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ complementary bases pair up through hydrogen bonds **OR** A forms two hydrogen bonds with T, and C forms three hydrogen bonds with G  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to synthesize complementary RNA from a DNA template [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  in transcription **OR** formation of tRNA OR formation of rRNA **OR** formation of mRNA, complementary base pairing between free RNA nucleotides and the DNA strand forms RNA    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ U OR uracil is found in RNA instead of  T **OR** thymine [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enables synthesis of polypeptides OR proteins from mRNA [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ensures accuracy **OR** less or no mistakes **OR** errors [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  in translation, mRNA codons are complementary to anticodons of tRNA, which carry amino acids **OR** enabling correct amino acids to be brought into place    [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n    Complementary base pairing between nitrogenous bases of nucleotides occurs between **cytosine and guanine**, and between **adenine and thymine** (in DNA) or **uracil** (in RNA) ($\\checkmark$). This allows for the **semi-conservative replication** of  DNA ($\\checkmark$). Here **parental strands** of DNA act as **templates** for the new strands to be formed, so that new DNA molecules are **identical to the parental** ones ($\\checkmark$). It is also important for the **synthesis of RNA** and **proteins** ($\\checkmark$). This can be seen in **transcription**, where bases of the **DNA** molecule serve as a template for the complementary pairing of bases in **RNA** ($\\checkmark$) and in **translation** where **mRNA codons pair** up with the **anticodons** found on tRNA ($\\checkmark$) molecules which carry the amino acids.\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA **OR** messenger RNA serves as a template for the synthesis of polypeptides **OR** for translation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA **OR** messenger RNA carries the code from the DNA in the nucleus to the ribosomes in the cytoplasm [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rRNA **OR** ribosomal RNA, along with ribosomal proteins, forms ribosomes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tRNA **OR** transfer RNA carries the correct amino acid to the correct codon on the mRNA\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA binds with  small subunit of ribosome [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ribosome binds at 5’ end of mRNA [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tRNA carrying correct amino acid, binds to large subunit [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ anticodon to codon on mRNA **OR** through complementary base pairing **OR** through G to C and U to A [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ second, tRNA brings in next amino acids and attaches to large subunit [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ peptide bond forms between adjacent amino acids **OR** condensation reactions form bond between the amino acids **OR** enzyme catalyses formation of covalent bond between amino acids [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ first tRNA then released to collect its next amino acids    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ribosome moves along mRNA to help bring in next tRNA     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ process repeats until stop codon **OR** until polypeptide complete     [1 mark]\n\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n   The **mRNA** carries the **genetic code** for the formation of the polypeptide from the DNA **into the cytoplasm** ($\\checkmark$) to act as a **template for transcription** ($\\checkmark$). The **mRNA binds** with the **small unit** of the **ribosome** ($\\checkmark$). The **first tRNA**, carrying the corresponding amino acid, then **binds** to the **large subunit** of the ribosome ($\\checkmark$), bringing the **correct amino acid** into the correct place along the messenger RNA, due to **complementary base pairing** between the **anticodon** on the tRNA and **codon** ($\\checkmark$)on the mRNA. A **second tRNA**, also **binds** to the **large subunit** of the ribosome and brings in the **next amino acid** ($\\checkmark$). A strong **covalent peptide bond** forms between the **two amino acids** ($\\checkmark$), and the first tRNA is released to collect its next amino acid. This **process continues until** a **stop codon** on the mRNA is reached ($\\checkmark$), and then the polypeptide is released."},{"ref":"BI1116","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1116\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline how treading on a drawing pin can bring about the reflex of lifting the foot.    [5 marks]\n\n2. Describe how the cerebellum exerts overall control over the voluntary movements of the body.    [4 marks]\n\n3. Explain how melatonin plays a part in the control of circadian rhythms.    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ specialised pain receptors **OR** nociceptors, are activated by drawing pin entering skin [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ activation of nociceptors **OR** sensory receptors generates action potentials [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ action potentials travel along sensory neurons (located in the foot) [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ axons enter spinal cord, transmitting action potentials to spinal cord [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ synapse with interneurons **OR** relay neurons within spinal cord [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ interneurons synapse with **OR** transmit information **OR** action potentials to motor neurons  [1 mark]\n\n     $\\ce{ -> }$ motor neurons synapse with **OR** transmit information **OR** action potentials to leg muscles **OR** effectors  [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ leg muscles **OR** effectors which bring about response **OR** lift the foot [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    When you step on a drawing pin, specialised **pain receptors**, called **nociceptors, are activated** ($\\checkmark$). These trigger **action potentials** in **sensory neurons** ($\\checkmark$), and the **axons** of these neurons **enter** the **spinal cord** ($\\checkmark$), carrying the action potential with them. The sensory neurons **synapse with interneurons** ($\\checkmark$), which in turn **synapse with motor neurons** ($\\checkmark$). The **motor neurons carry** the **information to** the **effectors** ($\\checkmark$), the leg muscles in this case, which bring about the **response** ($\\checkmark$) of lifting the leg.\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cerebellum receives sensory input from sensory receptors when body moves [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ provides information about current position of body **OR** tension of muscles [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cerebellum processes this information to form model of body's position in space [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ compares the intended movement, instructed by the cerebral cortex, with the actual sensory feedback from the body **OR** allows detection of errors in motor execution [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sends corrective signals to the motor centres in the brainstem **OR** cerebral cortex [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ controls timing and sequencing of movement **OR** controls precise sequence of muscle contractions required for complex motor tasks [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ important for maintaining balance and posture [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ involved in motor learning and memory **OR** muslce memory **OR** improved motor performance through practice and repetition  [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The cerebellum is important in **controlling** the **balance** ($\\checkmark$) of the body and the **coordination of voluntary movements** ($\\checkmark$). It **receives information from sensory receptors** around the body which allow it to work out the **current position** ($\\checkmark$) of the body. It then **processes this information**, compares it with the instruction from the cerebral cortex, and **detects any errors** ($\\checkmark$). It then sends out signals to the correct these errors. It is the cerebellum that allows us to **improve** our **motor performance** through **practice and repetition** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ melatonin is secreted **OR** produced by the pineal gland [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ production is controlled by an area in the hypothalamus [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ secretion is controlled by light and dark [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ controls sleep cycle [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cells in retina **OR** photoreceptors in retina are sensitive to light [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ triggering these cells **OR** presence of light causes signal from these cells to cause pineal gland to adjust melatonin production [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ for diurnal animals **OR** animals that are active in day melatonin levels are high at night **OR** for nocturnal animals melatonin levels are hiigh in day [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as light increases, melatonin levels drop and the animal becomes active **OR** alert  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ helps synchronise circadium rhythms as is cue for other physiological processes  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ E.g.  hormone release **OR** mating **OR** hibernation **OR** other suitable example  [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Melatonin is a hormone produced by the **pineal gland** ($\\checkmark$) which is involved in regulating the **sleep-wake cycle** ($\\checkmark$). Melatonin production is **controlled by the hypothalamus** ($\\checkmark$). **Photoreceptors in the retina** detect **light** and send impulses to the brain that **controls** the **secretion** of the melatonin ($\\checkmark$). In diurnal animals, such as humans, the **absence of light increases melatonin production** ($\\checkmark$), and this promotes a state of sleepiness, contributing to the onset of sleep. Conversely, as **morning approaches** and melatonin **levels decline**, the body receives a signal to wake up and become **alert** ($\\checkmark$). Beyond the sleep-wake cycle, melatonin helps **synchronise** various **circadian rhythms**  throughout the body, ensuring that different **physiological processes** ($\\checkmark$) occur at the correct time, such as **mating** and **hibernation** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1117","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1117\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Define the term phenology, and describe the impact of climate change on phenological events associated with a named species.        [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline the anthropogenic causes and positive feedback cycles associated with climate change.                                   [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain the impacts of climate change on organisms and ecosystems, other than those already mentioned.        [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phenology is the study of the timing of biological events    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phenological events refer to recurring, seasonal events or stages in the life cycles of organisms that are influenced by environmental factors  [1 mark]\n\n    **AND** any **one** named example with **one** correct impact of climate changefrom  the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Arctic mouse-ear chickweed **OR** *Cerastiun arcticun*    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ spring growth is advancing **OR** grows earlier in the year due to a warmer climate     [1 mark]\n\n    *Reindeer* \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reindeer **OR** *Rangifer tarandus*  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ migration  is advancing **OR** occurs earlier due to reduced snow cover **OR** warmer temperatures                         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ not enough food as increased temperature means growth of mouse-ear chickweed OR vegetation **OR** plants is out of synch with migration patterns [1 mark]\n\n    *Great tits* \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ great tits **OR** *Parus major*  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ breeding earlier **OR** laying eggs earlier due to earlier peak mass of caterpillars as a result of warmer temperatures         [1 mark]\n\n    *Bees* \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Bees **OR** *Apidae* sp. [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ higher temperatures mean they break hibernation early before there are enough flowers open to provide food [1 mark]\n\n    *Spruce bark beetle* \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ spruce bark beetle **OR** *Ips typographus* **OR** *Dendroctonus micans* [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ higher temperatures increase number of life cycles per year [1 mark]\n\n    *Any other correct example* \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ correctly named organism  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ example of disruption to phenological events due to temperature **OR** photoperiod            [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Phenology is the study of the **timing** of biological events which are **affected by environmental changes** ($\\checkmark$). Climate change can disrupt the synchronisation of events such as the arrival of **migrating reindeer** ($\\checkmark$)  to their **breeding ground** which **arrive earlier** due to **higher temperatures** ($\\checkmark$); the earlier availability of vegetation due to **higher temperatures** means **less food** is available ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following (*answer must include reference to anthropogenic causes and positive feedback cycles to gain full marks*):\n\n    ***Anthropogenic Causes*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ combustion of fossil fuels **OR** anthropogenic increase in carbon dioxide concentrations     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ land use change **OR** deforestation **OR** increased cattle farming [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ many industrial processes release greenhouse gases, contributing to climate changes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ landfills **OR** waste treatment facilities generate methane as organic waste decomposes in anaerobic conditions         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ urbanisation as urban areas generate heat **OR** urban heat island effect, contributes to localised temperature increases and can impact local weather patterns     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the destruction of natural carbon sinks **OR** forests **OR** wetlands **OR** peatlands, reduces the Earth's ability to absorb carbon dioxide [1 mark]\n\n    ***Positive Feedback Cycles*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ocean temperatures increase and they release stored carbon dioxide into the atmosphere. This release of CO:sub[2] amplifies the greenhouse effect     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is a loss of snow **OR** ice cover **OR** arctic ice **OR** glaciers **OR** arctic ice-albedo feedback leading to an increase in the absorption of solar radiation, which contributes to global warming **OR** as polar ice sheets melt due to warming temperatures, sea levels rise, which contributes to further melting of ice sheets          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased rate of peat **OR** undecomposed organic matter decomposition in permafrost, which releases CO:sub[2] into the atmosphere, further enhancing the greenhouse effect          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ methane is released into the atmosphere from melting permafrost, which further enhances the greenhouse effect       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased incidence of forest fires, which release carbon dioxide into the atmosphere, which further enhances the greenhouse effect, **OR** there is an increased incidence of droughts, which can increase the chance of forest fires, which release carbon dioxide into the atmosphere when they burn    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the air becomes warmer, which can hold more water vapour, a greenhouse gas, contributing to further warming [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The major anthropogenic causes of climate change are the **combustion of fossil fuels** ($\\checkmark$) and the change in land use, such as **deforestation** ($\\checkmark$), to clear land for cattle farming. Positive feedback cycles that enhance climate change include the release of **carbon dioxide** from the oceans due to increasing ocean temperatures ($\\checkmark$), the increase in the rate of **decomposition of peat** leading to the release of carbon dioxide into the atmosphere ($\\checkmark$), and the melting of permafrost which releases **methane** into the atmosphere ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ climate change is the long-term change in weather patterns    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased temperatures **OR** reduced snowfall increases the incidence of drought **OR** forest fires in boreal forests **OR** taiga    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **WHICH** reduces primary productivity (in boreal forests **OR** taiga) [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased temperatures lead to the melting of land-fast ice and sea ice [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **WHICH** reduces the breeding grounds of emperor penguins (*Aptenodytes forsteri*) **OR** habitat for walruses (*Odobenus rosmarus*)     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased surface water temperatures can prevent nutrient upwelling     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **WHICH** reduces ocean primary productivity **OR** energy flow [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased global average temperatures can affect the distribution of habitats **OR** ecosystems **OR** communities             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **WHICH** could lead to an upslope shift for montane bird species **OR** range contraction **OR** northward spread of tree species found in North America     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in carbon dioxide concentrations causes ocean acidification     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **WHICH** can lead to the suppression of calcification in corals [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in ocean temperatures can cause coral bleaching     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **WHICH** can lead to the reef ecosystems collapsing [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ shifts in the geographical distribution of species could lead to species disappearing from previously inhabited areas **OR** local extinctions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ species might struggle to adapt to rapidly changing conditions, leading to decreased biodiversity [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rising sea levels threaten coastal habitats        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ expanding range of disease vectors enabling spread of diseases    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased temperatures **OR** droughts can lead to more frequent forest fires **OR** tree diseases  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reduced water availability can lead to reduced habitat for aquatic organisms    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ecosystem changes can affect human communities that rely on them for resources     [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Climate change is the long-term change in **weather patterns** ($\\checkmark$), which can have different impacts on different ecosystems. In boreal forests, increasing temperatures leads to an increased incidence of **drought** and forest fires ($\\checkmark$). This leads to a reduction in **primary productivity** ($\\checkmark$) and a net carbon loss due to the combustion of carbon compounds. Increased temperatures also have an impact on habitats such as sea ice as they reduce the coverage of sea ice because it **melts** ($\\checkmark$), which **reduces the available habitat** for organisms such as the walrus ($\\checkmark$). Increased temperatures also warm the surface of the ocean waters, which can cause **coral bleaching** ($\\checkmark$) and prevent **nutrient upwelling** ($\\checkmark$). A reduction in available nutrients near the surface can reduce the **primary productivity** of ocean ecosystems ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1120","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1120\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nFlu vaccinations are administered to millions of people every year and are often targeted at vulnerable members of the population, such as the elderly. To ascertain the effectiveness of vaccinations at reducing the spread of viruses, a study was conducted in which researchers investigated the effect of vaccination on the viral load of aerosols from pigs.\n\nIn the experiment, pigs were categorised and treated according to the following table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1120.png\" alt=\"BI1120\">\n[Source: Adapted from Neira, V., Allerson, M., Corzo, C., Culhane, M., Rendah,l A., Torremorell, M. (2018). *Detection of influenza A virus in aerosols of vaccinated and non-vaccinated pigs in a warm environment*. PLoS ONE 13(5): e0197600. Retrieved May 24, 2023, from https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0197600. Copyright free]\n\nAt daily intervals after inoculation with the Influenza A virus, the viral load of aerosols from different orifices was sampled. Results are shown.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1120b.png\" alt=\"BI1120b\">\n[Source: Adapted from Neira, V., Allerson, M., Corzo, C., Culhane, M., Rendah,l A., Torremorell, M. (2018). *Detection of influenza A virus in aerosols of vaccinated and non-vaccinated pigs in a warm environment*. PLoS ONE 13(5): e0197600. Retrieved May 24, 2023, from https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0197600. Copyright free]\n\nThe proportion of Influenza A negative pigs was also measured throughout the duration of the experiment.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1120c.png\" alt=\"BI1120c\">\n[Source: Adapted from Neira, V., Allerson, M., Corzo, C., Culhane, M., Rendah,l A., Torremorell, M. (2018). *Detection of influenza A virus in aerosols of vaccinated and non-vaccinated pigs in a warm environment*. PLoS ONE 13(5): e0197600. Retrieved May 24, 2023, from https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0197600. Copyright free]\n\n1.\n    1. Suggest one environmental variable which could influence viral transmission between pigs that should be kept constant.     [1 mark]\n\n    2. State two ways in which the vaccination could be administered.     [1 mark]\n\n    3. Describe how the pig's non-specific immune system would recognise the inert viruses present in the vaccine as foreign molecules.     [2 marks]\n\n2.\n    1. Suggest two safety precautions that investigators could take to limit their health risks during sample collection.          [1 mark]\n\n    2. Describe an advantage to using pigs instead of humans in this experiment. [1 mark]\n\n3. State the advantage of increasing the number of oral aerosol samples taken to 9.      [1 mark]\n\n4. From the data, discuss whether vaccinating pigs with different strains of virus effectively reduces the transmission of viral particles in aerosols. Justify your answer.          [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  temperature  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  humidity    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  airflow **OR** ventilation        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  ground substrate             [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **temperature** of the environment should be kept constant ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ orally **OR** in food    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ intranasally **OR** nasal spray    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ injection **OR** intravenously    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The vaccine could be **injected intravenously** ($\\checkmark$) or given to the pigs **orally** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    3.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ antigens are present on the surface of viruses **OR** viral particles **OR** viral protein coats [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ immune system **OR** immune cells **OR** macrophages **OR** phagocytes recognise the antigen as foreign **OR** non-self [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Within the vaccine, the attenuated virus particles have **antigens** ($\\checkmark$) on the surface of their protein coats. Once administered, the cells of the immune system, such as **macrophages**, recognise the antigens as being **non-self** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ wear gloves **OR** a mask **OR** protective clothing **OR** personal protective equipment (PPE)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ dispose of needles **OR** medical equipment safely     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ aseptic techniques **OR** sanitise equipment **OR** investigators         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vaccinate investigators [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Precautions could include wearing **protective clothing** ($\\checkmark$), such as a mask or gloves, and then safely **disposing of the equipment** ($\\checkmark$), such as needles, after administration.\n\n2.\n    2.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fewer ethical considerations compared to humans        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pigs are genetically **OR** physiologically similar to humans **OR** both are mammals     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ less resources required to care for pigs     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ susceptible to many of the same diseases **OR** illnesses, **OR** viruses         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ shorter life cycles so can be studied throughout their life        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Pigs share many **similarities** ($\\checkmark$) with humans and are **susceptible** to many of the **same viruses** ($\\checkmark$), such as influenza.\n\n3.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ increases the reliability of the results **OR** the experiment    [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Increasing the number of samples to 9 would increase the **reliability** of the results ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **three** of the following (answer must include effective, not effective **AND** conclusion):\n\n    **Not Effective**    \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** VAC-HETMU groups still test positive in oral samples **OR** nasal samples [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** VAC-HETMU group had a greater proportion of positive oral **OR** nasal samples compared to VAC-HETMO [1 mark]\n\n    **Effective** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** VAC-HETMU groups have fewer positive samples compared to the NON-VAC group [1 mark]\n\n    **Conclusion** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Mark awarded for the concluding statement with justification [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Treating pigs with different strains is **not effective** because there are still **positive oral and nasal samples in the VAC-HETMU group** ($\\checkmark$). However, there are **fewer positive samples** in these groups compared to the **NON-VAC group** ($\\checkmark$). In conclusion, the treatment is **not effective** because there are more positive samples in the **VAC-HETMU group** compared to the **VAC-HOMO group** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1121","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1121\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThis image shows an aphid with its stylet inserted into the stem of a plant, enabling it to feed.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1121.png\" alt=\"BI1121\">\n[Source: https://www.flickr.com/photos/62295966@N07/24812950688/, 27th November 2017. *Banana aphid* (Pentalonia nigronervosa) (24812950688).jpg. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 27th June 2023 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: *Banana_aphid_(Pentalonia_nigronervosa)_-_24812950688.jpg*. Copyright under Creative Commons CC0 1.0]\n\n1. Name the tissue that aphids insert their stylets into. [1 mark]\n\n2. Draw a plan diagram to show the distribution of the vascular tissues in a transverse section of a dicotyledonous plant stem. [2 marks]\n\n3. Explain why the rate of flow of sap is faster at the sources than at the sinks.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ Phloem [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ This tissue is **phloem** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ vascular bundles arranged in ring shape around outer edge of stem [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ phloem labelled correctly in outer region of bundle **AND** xylem labelled correctly in inner region [1 mark]\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1121ms.png\" alt=\"BI1121ms\">\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at sources sucrose **OR** organic compounds loaded into phloem AND at sinks sucrose **OR** organic compounds removed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ high sucrose **OR** organic compound concentration at source lowers water potential **OR** increases solute potential so water enters by osmosis **OR** low sucrose **OR** organic compound concentration at sink increases water potential **OR** decreases solute potential so water leaves by osmosis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ water entering phloem at source causes high hydrostatic pressure which increases rate of flow **OR** water leaving phloem at sinks causes low hydrostatic pressure reducing flow [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Sucrose is **actively transported** into the **phloem** at the **source**, which **increases** the **solute potential** (decreases water potential) ($\\checkmark$) of the sap, so **water follows** by **osmosis** ($\\checkmark$). This increase in water content of the sap causes a **high hydrostatic pressure**, **increasing** rate of **flow** ($\\checkmark$). At the **sink**, **sucrose leaves** the phloem ($\\checkmark$), so **solute potential decreases** (water potential increases), and **water leaves** the phloem by **osmosis**. This causes a **lower hydrostatic pressure** and so a **lower** rate of **flow** at the sinks."},{"ref":"BI1122","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1122\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nXylem and phloem are adapted for their functions. The image below is a longitudinal section of a stem showing phloem sieve tubes.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1122.png\" alt=\"BI1122\">\n[Source: Berkshire Community College Bioscience Image Library. 10th February 2014. *Herbaceous Dicot Stem Tangential Section Phloem Plugs in Cucurbita (36826670444).jpg*. Retrieved 27th June 2023 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Herbaceous_Dicot_Stem_Tangential_Section_Phloem_Plugs_in_Cucurbita_%2836826668514%29.jpg Copyright under Creative Commons CC0 1.0]\n\n1.\n    1. Explain how phloem is adapted for its function.    [2 marks]\n\n    2. Identify the structures labelled I and II in this micrograph.     [1 mark]\n\n    3. Identify the features that you used to identify I and II.  [2 marks]\n\nBelow is a micrograph which shows a transport structure from a plant stem.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1122b%20i.png\" alt=\"BI1122b i\">\n[Source: Adapted from Eugeenia Wen, 31st December 2019. File: Stem_of_Dicotyledon_C.S.png. Retrieved 13th May 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Stem_of_Dicotyledon_C.S.png. CC BY 4.0 ]\n\n2.\n    1. Name the group of tissues labelled X.    [1 mark]\n\n    2. Compare and contrast xylem and phloem tissue. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phloem    sieve tubes have a small amount of cytoplasm **OR** lack most cellular components, which gives space for transport of phloem sap **OR** to facilitate the flow of sap         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sieve plates between sieve tubes allow easy movement of phloem sap **OR** allow sap to flow freely between adjacent cells        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ companion cells carry out cellular functions **OR** provide metabolic support for the sieve tubes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell walls of cellulose and lignin **OR** sclerenchyma cells **OR** arrangement of cells, provide mechanical support and prevent collapse under high hydrostatic pressure [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ active transport mechanisms **OR** indirect active transport **OR**  sucrose-proton symport allow for the movement of nutrients **OR** sucrose against concentration gradients for loading at source    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Phloem sieve tubes have a **small amount of cytoplasm**, which gives **space for the transport of phloem sap** ($\\checkmark$) and **sieve plates** ($\\checkmark$) between the sieve tubes **allow the phloem sap to move easily** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    2. **BOTH** answers are required:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ I is a sieve plate **OR** sieve tube element, and II is a companion cell    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    I is a **sieve plate**, and II is a **companion cell** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    3. Answer must include **one** point for I and **one** point for II:\n\n    Any **one** of the following for I:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ I is located between sieve tube elements    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ I is perforated     [1 mark]\n\n    Any **one** of the following for II:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ II is adjacent to a sieve tube element        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ II is smaller in size than the sieve tube element        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ visible cytoplasm in II        [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Structure I is a sieve plate because it is **located between sieve tube elements** ($\\checkmark$) and II is a companion cell because it is **adjacent to a sieve tube element** and is smaller in size than the sieve tube element ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    1.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vascular bundle [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    X is a **vascular bundle** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    2. any **four** of the following (answer **must** include at least **one** difference **and one** similarity):\n\n    *Similarities*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are composed of tubes **OR** vascular tissues        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are both adapted for transport                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contain lignin in cell walls [1 mark]\n\n    *Differences*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ xylem is a non-living **OR** dead at maturity tissue, but phloem is a living tissue    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ xylem cells lack cell contents whereas in phloem sieve tube elements contain some cellular components **OR** companion cells contain normal cellular components    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ xylem contains more lignin **THAN** phloem        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ xylem vessels have a wider diameter than phloem tubes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ primary function of xylem is to transport water and minerals, whereas primary function of phloem is to transport organic compounds **OR** sucrose **OR** sugars    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ unidirectional transport in xylem, whereas bidirectional transport in phloem    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ main cell types in xylem are tracheids and vessel elements, whereas main cell types in phloem are sieve tube elements and companion cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transport through the xylem mainly relies on cohesion, adhesion and tension **OR** driven by transpiration, whereas transport through  phloem relies on active transport **OR** indirect active transport **OR**  sucrose-proton symport **OR** hydrostatic pressure [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Xylem and phloem are **both tubular structures**, or **vascular tissue**, ($\\checkmark$) and **adapted** to **transport substances** around the plant ($\\checkmark$). However, **xylem** is a **non-living tissue** that transports **water** around the plant in **one direction**, whereas **phloem** is a **living tissue** ($\\checkmark$) that transports **organic compounds** ($\\checkmark$)  both **up and down** the plant ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1123","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1123\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nBelow is a graph showing the gestation periods and body masses of four different animals.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n1. Describe the relationship between body mass and the gestation period. [1 mark]\n\n2. Using the data, deduce, with a reason, whether a rabbit would have a more similar gestational period to a hamster or an elephant. [1 mark]\n\n3. State one method, other than body mass, to measure body size of animals. [1 mark]\n\n4. The foetus of a mammal that develops a placenta can remain in the uterus to a later stage of development than in mammals that do not. State one substance that passes from the biological mother to the foetus that helps their development. [1 mark]\n\n5. Humans have a longer gestation period than organisms with similar body mass. State the term used to indicate extreme data points that stand out and do not fit the pattern of a graph. [1 mark]\n\n6. Determine the gestation period for giraffes from the graph. [1 mark]\n\n7. Explain the roles of the main hormones that, when secreted, help bring about childbirth in humans. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ the smaller **OR** lower the body mass, the shorter the gestation period **OR** the larger **OR** higher the body mass, the longer the gestation period [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The **smaller the body mass**, the **shorter the gestation period** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ hamster; as closer in size to hamster than elephant [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The rabbit would have a gestational period that is more similar to that of the **hamster** as it is **much closer in size** to the hamster than the elephant. \n ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ body height **OR** length **OR** width **OR** girth **OR** circumference **OR** linear measurements **OR** limb length **OR** head width **OR** skull size **OR** surface area **OR** wing span [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ One other way of measuring the growth in body size is by measuring the animal’s **body length** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nutrients **OR** amino acids **OR** carbohydrates **OR** lipids **OR** vitamins **OR** minerals **OR** other suitable named nutrient [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ antibodies [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hormones **OR** porgesterone **OR** oestrogen **OR** hCG [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n One substance that passes from mother to foetus is **oxygen** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ outlier **OR** anomaly **OR** deviation **OR** extreme value **OR** aberration **OR** outlying data [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ A human’s gestation period and body mass data would be an **outlier** ($\\checkmark$) on the graph.\n\n6.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ any value in between 410 - 430 days [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The giraffe gestation period is **420 days** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n7. Any **four** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ oxytocin stimulates **OR** induces **OR** regulates **OR** causes more frequent **OR** more vigorous uterine contractions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ prostaglandins soften cervix **OR** prepare cervix for labour **OR** help cause dilation of cervix [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ prostaglandins help initiate uterine contractions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ relaxin relaxes the ligaments in the pelvis **OR** helps soften **OR** widen cervix [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ oestrogen enhances sensitivity of uterine muscle cells to oxytocin **OR** increases number of oxytocin receptors in uterus wall [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as contractions become more frequent **OR** intense, they help dilate the cervix **OR** aid descent of baby [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ positive feedback loop continues until baby born [1 mark]\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Oxytocin** ($\\checkmark$) and **prostaglandins** stimulate **contractions** of the **uterine wall** ($\\checkmark$). **Prostaglandins** also help **soften** the **cervix** allowing it to **dilate** ($\\checkmark$). **Oestrogen** enhances the **sensitivity** of the **uterine muscle** cells to **oxytocin** ($\\checkmark$). As **contractions** become more **intense**, they help **dilate** the **cervix** and aid the **descent** of the **baby** through the birth canal ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1161","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1161\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Draw a labelled diagram of the fluid mosaic model of the plasma membrane of an animal cell.   [4 marks]\n\n2. Explain how type I and type II pneumocytes are adapted to their functions.    [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe how cell surface membranes regulate the movement of substances into and out of animal cells.     [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following, (drawing showing the following labels correctly marked on a clearly drawn diagram):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phospholipid bilayer with phosphate heads pointing outside and lipid tails pointing inwards        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ peripheral protein shown on the surface        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ integral protein shown within the phospholipid bilayer    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glycoprotein **OR** glycolipid shown with carbohydrate chain attached    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cholesterol in bilayer        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ protein channel across the bilayer    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1161.png\" alt=\"BI1161\">\n\n2. any **five** of the following (*must include at least **one** from each section to gain full marks*):\n\n    ***Type I pneumocytes*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ for gas exchange    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large surface area to maximise gas exchange        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ thin to reduce diffusion distance **OR** to reduce distance between capillaries and inside alveoli [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lack many organelles to reduce barriers for gas exchange     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tight junctions between adjacent cells to prevent fluid **OR** gas **OR** air leakage    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Type II pneumocytes*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ secrete **OR** produce surfactant **OR** a fluid to reduce surface tension in alveoli    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cuboidal to provide space for organelles **OR** numerous organelles **OR** secretory granules for production **OR** storage **OR** secretion of surfactant    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can differentiate into type 1 pneumocytes **OR** can regenerate type 1 cells [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n    **Type 1 pneumocytes** are **thin** so there is a **short diffusion distance** between the capillaries and the inside of the alveoli ($\\checkmark$). Their shape **increases** their **surface area**, and they have **fewer organelles** so there are **fewer barriers** to diffusion ($\\checkmark$). All these adaptations enable **rapid exchange of gases** ($\\checkmark$). The type 1 cells have **fewer organelles**, so there are fewer barriers, enabling rapid **exchange** of **gases** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    **Type II** pneumocytes secrete **surfactant** to reduce **surface tension** inside the alveoli ($\\checkmark$). **Numerous organelles** are found inside the type II cells to produce and store **surfactant** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    ***Passive transport*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ small molecules **OR** gases can move through the membrane **OR** pass across the bilayer by (simple) diffusion [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nonpolar **OR** lipid-soluble substances move through the membrane **OR** phospholipid bilayer by (simple) diffusion    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water molecules can pass across the membrane **OR** bilayer by osmosis    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ channel proteins allow facilitated diffusion of ions **OR** polar molecules **OR** water molecules **OR** water-soluble molecules **OR** named example of type of channel protein and molecules that pass through it        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gated proteins **OR** voltage-gated channels **OR** ligand-gated channels **OR** other suitable example control movement of substances due to changes in environment **OR** by specific signals    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ diffusion is the net movement of molecules down their concentration gradient **OR** from a higher concentration to a lower concentration, without use of energy **OR** is a passive process    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Active transport*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ protein pumps **OR** carrier proteins are involved in active transport of ions (but not channel proteins )        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ active transport of substances against a concentration gradient, which requires energy **OR** ATP                  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ indirect active transport **OR** secondary active transport when the movement of one substance against its concentration gradient is coupled to the movement of another substance down its concentration gradient         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ endocytosis is active transport of substances into cell by formation of vesicle from cell surface membrane                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phagocytosis is endocytosis of solid particles **AND** pinocytosis is endocytosis of dissolved substances **OR** liquid      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ exocytosis is the release of substances from the cell via vesicles fusing with the cell surface membrane                     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ different types of cells have different proteins in their cell membrane for transport of different substances                [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Nonpolar** ($\\checkmark$) and **small molecules**, such as **gases**, can pass across the phospholipid bilayer by **simple diffusion** ($\\checkmark$). **On** the other hand, polar molecules and ions tend to pass through **channel proteins** by **facilitated diffusion** ($\\checkmark$). These **channel proteins** may be **gated** to control when these substances pass through them, depending on the **environment** ($\\checkmark$). Whilst **diffusion** is a **passive process**, with the molecules passing **down** their **concentration gradient** ($\\checkmark$), some molecules need to be moved **against** their **concentration gradient** by **active transport**, which requires **energy** ($\\checkmark$). **Protein pumps** or **carrier proteins** embedded in the membrane are required for **active transport** ($\\checkmark$). Molecules may also **enter** the cell via **vesicles** pinched off from the cell membrane, a process known as **endocytosis** ($\\checkmark$). **Exocytosis** allows the **secretion** of substances **from cells**, when **vesicles** containing the substance **fuse with the cell membrane** and release their contents to the outside ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1162","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1162\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast anaerobic and aerobic respiration in animal cells, in terms of substrates, products and yield of ATP.     [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline the process of negative feedback, referring to either temperature regulation or control of blood glucose concentration, in humans as an example.    [4 marks]\n\n3. Define an ecosystem, and explain how the flow of energy and recycling of nutrients make a terrestrial ecosystem sustainable. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:( at least one from each section)\n\n    ***Similarities*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** use glucose as a substrate for energy release     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in **BOTH**, pyruvate is the end product of glycolysis    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** processes release energy **OR** produce ATP    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in aerobic respiration, oxygen is required **WHEREAS**  anaerobic respiration occurs in the absence of oxygen        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ aerobic respiration can use substrates other than glucose **OR** can use fatty acids **OR** amino acids **WHEREAS** in anaerobic respiration only substrate is glucose\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ end products of aerobic respiration are carbon dioxide and water, **WHEREAS** the end product of anaerobic respiration is lactic acid         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ aerobic respiration is more efficient at generating ATP **OR** generates more molecules of ATP per glucose molecule **THAN** anaerobic respiration    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Aerobic** and anaerobic respiration use **glucose** as the **substrate** ($\\checkmark$) to **release energy** in the form of **ATP** ($\\checkmark$).  In **aerobic** respiration, **oxygen** is required, whereas **anaerobic respiration** occurs in the **absence of oxygen** ($\\checkmark$). The **end products of aerobic respiration** in animal cells are **carbon dioxide and water**, whereas the end product of **anaerobic** respiration is **lactic acid** ($\\checkmark$). **Aerobic respiration** produces many **more** molecules of **ATP per glucose molecule** than anaerobic respiration ($\\checkmark$) (approximately 15 times more).\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ negative feedback is involved in maintaining an equilibrium **OR** involved in homeostasis, since it brings the value back to the original    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when there is a change from a set value **OR** optimal level, brings about change to return to set value **OR** reduce changes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ involves receptors detecting change and sending information to control centre **OR** brain    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ control centre **OR** brain sends information to effector **OR** muscle **OR** gland to carry out response [1 mark]\n\n    Any **one** of the following examples (max two marks):\n\n    ***Blood glucose*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in blood glucose detected by the pancreatic **OR** β-cells cells which release insulin, **OR** fall in blood glucose detected by the pancreatic **OR** α-cells which release of glucagon      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ insulin causes glucose to be converted to **OR** stored as glycogen in liver **OR** muscle, so decreasing blood glucose, **OR** glucagon causes glycogen to be broken down to glucose **OR** amino acids to be converted to glucose in liver, so increasing blood glucose [1 mark]\n\n    ***Body temperature*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase **OR** decrease in body temperature signals to hypothalamus in the brain      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (increase in temperature) leads to vasodilation **OR** arterioles supplying blood to the skin dilate **OR** sweat glands increase sweat production (do not allow hairs lie flat as not significant in humans) and so cooling **OR** decrease in body temperature, **OR** (decrease in body temperature) leads to vasoconstriction **OR** arterioles supplying blood to the skin constrict **OR** sweat glands decrease  sweat production (do not allow hairs stand up as not significant in humans) **OR** shivering, to help raise body temperature    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    When there is a change from the normal, **negative feedback** mechanisms are a form of **control** that brings the value back to **within a normal range** ($\\checkmark$). The **change** is **detected by receptors**, which send **signals** to the **brain** ($\\checkmark$) and then signals from the brain to the **effector** to bring about the **response** ($\\checkmark$).  In the control of blood glucose concentration, an **increase** in **blood glucose** will be detected by the **β-cells** and lead to the **release of insulin** ($\\checkmark$), causing **glucose** to be **converted** to and stored as **glycogen** in **liver** and skeletal **muscle** cells. This then restores the **blood glucose** concentration to **within normal levels** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ an ecosystem is a community interacting with its physical environment     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy from the Sun is used for photosynthesis **OR** light energy is converted to chemical energy by autotrophs **OR** plants        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ this initial capture of energy then moves through the trophic levels **OR** from producers to consumers in a food chain            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ only 5 to 10% of the energy is transferred from one trophic level to the next    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy transfer from one level to the next trophic level is not 100% efficient    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy is lost as heat  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy flow is depicted in a pyramid shape and shows the loss of energy   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms absorb nutrients from food that they consume        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ decomposers break down organic matter after death, releasing nutrients    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (saprophytic) bacteria and fungi are decomposers and help release nutrients back to the soil             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nutrients in soil are in simpler compounds and are absorbed by plants    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ recycling of nutrients in ecosystems takes place    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy needs to be supplied continuously for initial capture by plants    [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    An **ecosystem** is a **community** of living organisms interacting with its **physical environment** ($\\checkmark$). **Energy** from the Sun is used by plants for **photosynthesis** ($\\checkmark$) to make organic compounds, and this **energy moves** from **one trophic level** to the **next** ($\\checkmark$) when organisms consume plants or other organisms. This **transfer** of energy is not efficient, only about **5 to 10%** is transferred ($\\checkmark$) to the next level, much is **lost as heat** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    **Organisms** absorb the **nutrients** from the food they consume ($\\checkmark$). After their death, **decomposers break down** the **organic matter** ($\\checkmark$), releasing the nutrients. The **nutrients** are in **simpler compounds** and can be **absorbed by plants** ($\\checkmark$) from the soil to be used to make further organic compounds in plants."},{"ref":"BI1219","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1219\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast anaerobic and aerobic respiration in humans.        [5 marks]\n\n2. Outline the roles of NAD:sup[+] **or** FAD in aerobic respiration.    $^*$ [3 marks]\n\n3. Describe the key biochemical reactions that occur in the mitochondrial matrix during aerobic respiration, excluding the roles of NAD and FAD.            [8 marks]\n\n$^*$ *Note: Starting May 2028, this question may be assessed differently due to changes to the Biology guide.*","markScheme":"1. any **five** of the following: \\\n*Answer must include at least **one** similarity and **one** difference to achieve full marks*\n\n    **Similarities** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** produce ATP    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** use glucose as a substrate **OR** reactant    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** involve glycolysis **OR** the oxidation of glucose **OR** involve the breakdown of one glucose molecule into 2 pyruvates        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are multi-step processes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** use enzymes     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** use NAD as a coenzyme **OR** electron carrier            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** initially take place in the cytoplasm    [1 mark]\n\n    **Differences** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ aerobic respiration requires oxygen **WHEREAS** anaerobic respiration does not    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ aerobic respiration produces 36-38 ATPs **OR** a high number of ATPs **WHEREAS** anaerobic respiration produces 2 ATPs **OR** a low number of ATPs    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the products of aerobic respiration are carbon dioxide and water, **WHEREAS** the product of anaerobic respiration is lactate    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mitochondria are required for aerobic respiration **WHEREAS** they are not for anaerobic respiration        [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Anaerobic and aerobic respiration are similar in that they **both** produce **ATP** ($\\checkmark$), they both use **glucose** as a **substrate** ($\\checkmark$), and both use **enzymes** ($\\checkmark$) to catalyse many reactions. However, aerobic respiration **requires oxygen**, whereas anaerobic respiration **does not** ($\\checkmark$), and aerobic respiration produces **36 ATPs**, whereas anaerobic respiration only produces **2 ATPs** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\n\n    ***NAD***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ NAD:sup[+] acts as electron carrier between metabolic pathways [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ involved  in glycolysis **OR** link reaction **OR** Krebs cycle [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reduced to NADH by accepting electrons and hydrogen ions so oxidation reactions can take place [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reduced NADH donates electrons to electron transport chain **OR** ETC where it is oxidised back to NAD:sup[+] [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ NADH contributes to the formation of ATP **OR** oxidative phosphorylation by generating the proton gradient across the inner mitochondrial membrane [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxidised NAD:sup[+] is used repeatedly in earlier stages **OR** glycolysis **OR**  link reaction **OR** Krebs cycle to accept electrons and continue cellular respiration [1 mark]\n\n    ***FAD***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ FAD is involved in the Krebs cycle    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reduced to FADH:sub[2] by accepting electrons and hydrogen ions, so oxidation reactions can take place[1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ FADH:sub[2] donates electrons to electron transport chain **OR** ETC, where it is oxidised back to FAD [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ FADH:sub[2] contributes to the formation of ATP **OR** oxidative phosphorylation by generating the proton gradient across the inner mitochondrial membrane [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxidised FAD is regenerated and used in the Krebs cycle to continue metabolic reactions    [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n   **NAD:sup[+]** is involved in **glycolysis**, the **link reaction**, and the **Krebs cycle** ($\\checkmark$), where it plays a crucial role in **oxidation reactions** by **accepting electrons** and **hydrogen ions**, **reducing** to **NADH** ($\\checkmark$). The reduced NADH then **donates electrons** to the **electron transport chain (ETC)**, where it is **oxidised** back to **NAD:sup[+]** ($\\checkmark$), providing **electrons** that help **generate** the **proton gradient** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ link reaction **AND** Krebs cycle    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as pyruvate enters matrix it is oxidised **AND** decarboxylated        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ an acetyl group **OR** acetate is formed        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the acetyl group **OR** acetate is combined with coenzyme A **OR** coA, forming acetyl-coenzyme A    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ acetyl-coenzyme A combines with a four-carbon compound **OR** oxaloacetate in the Krebs cycle    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ forming a six-carbon compound **OR** citrate         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ releasing coenzyme A **OR** coA to be reused in the link reaction        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the six-carbon compound **OR** citrate is decarboxylated **AND** oxidised into a five-carbon compound **OR** α-ketoglutarate    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the five-carbon compound **OR** α-ketoglutarate is decarboxylated **AND** oxidised into a four-carbon compound **OR**  succinate    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ through additional steps, oxaloacetate **OR** original four-carbon compound is regenerated        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ one molecule of ATP is generated per turn **OR** two per glucose molecule via substrate-level phosphorylation             [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The two reactions that occur in the mitochondrial matrix as a part of aerobic respiration are the **link reaction** and the **Krebs cycle**($\\checkmark$). The **pyruvate** from glycolysis **enters** the **link reaction** and is **oxidised** and **decarboxylated** ($\\checkmark$) to  form an **acetyl group** ($\\checkmark$). The acetyl group then **combines with coenzyme A** to form **acetyl coenzyme A** ($\\checkmark$). **Acetate** from acetyl coenzyme A acts as a **substrate** for the **Krebs cycle**, where it **combines** with the **four-carbon molecule, oxaloacetate** ($\\checkmark$), forming **citrate, a six-carbon molecule** ($\\checkmark$), This is then **decarboxylated and oxidised** into  **α-ketoglutarate**, a **five-carbon molecule** ($\\checkmark$), which is further **oxidised and decarboxylated**, to form **succinate, a four-carbon molecule**  ($\\checkmark$), before **oxaloacetate** is **reformed** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1221","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1221\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nDNA and RNA are nucleic acids that are essential to all living organisms. ATP is a type of nucleotide.\n\n1. Distinguish between ATP and the monomers of DNA in humans. [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline the roles of the different types of RNA involved in translation.     [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe the structure of the DNA double helix.  [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP contains three phosphate groups **WHEREAS** each DNA nucleotide contains only one phosphate group         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP has adenine for a nitrogenous base **WHEREAS** a DNA nucleotide can contain adenine, cytosine, guanine or thymine    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a component of ATP is ribose **WHEREAS** DNA nucleotides have deoxyribose instead        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP stores energy **OR** provides energy, **WHEREAS** DNA carries **OR** transmits genetic information                          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP can perform its function as a single monomer **WHEREAS** DNA can only perform its function, **OR** carry genetic material as a polymer            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP is found throughout the cell **OR** primarily in the cytoplasm **OR** mitochondria **WHEREAS** DNA is found primarily in the nucleus **OR** only found in the nucleus AND mitochondria            [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Both ATP and DNA molecules are nucleic acids which contain a phosphate group, a sugar and a base. The structural difference between a molecule of ATP and a molecule of DNA is that one ATP molecule contains **three phosphate groups** whereas one DNA nucleotide contains **one phosphate group** ($\\checkmark$). In addition, the nitrogenous base in ATP is **adenine** whereas there are four possible nitrogenous bases in one nucleotide of DNA which are **adenine, cytosine, guanine and thymine** ($\\checkmark$). The functional difference is that ATP **stores energy**, whereas DNA **carries genetic material** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following (there must be at least one statement for each of mRNA, tRNA and rRNA to achieve maximum marks):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA carries the genetic information from DNA to the ribosome            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA serves as a template for protein synthesis        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ codons of mRNA  specify positioning of  amino acids along polypeptide **OR** codons guide the assembly of amino acids into a polypeptide         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tRNA molecules carry amino acids to mRNA **OR** ribosome    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ anticodons on tRNA complementary to codons on mRNA allow amino acids to be positioned correctly along the polypeptide **OR** anticodons bind to codons to bring corresponding amino acid to the ribosome             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rRNA provides the ribosome's structural framework **OR** forms ribosomes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rRNA catalyses the formation of peptide bonds between adjacent amino acids     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rRNA facilitates binding of mRNA and tRNA        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA, tRNA, and rRNA orchestrate protein synthesis during translation    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Together, mRNA, tRNA, and rRNA** orchestrate the process of **protein synthesis** ($\\checkmark$) during translation. **mRNA carries** the genetic **information** from **DNA** to the **ribosome**  ($\\checkmark$). **Codons** on the mRNA **specify the positioning** of **amino acids** along the **polypeptide** chain  ($\\checkmark$). Each **tRNA** molecule has a specific **anticodon** sequence that is **complementary** to a **codon** on the mRNA  ($\\checkmark$). This base-pairing specificity allows tRNA to bring the **corresponding amino acid** to the **correct position** to form the polypeptide  ($\\checkmark$). The rRNA **catalyses** the formation of **peptide bonds** between adjacent amino acids  ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following: \\\n    *(appropriately labelled and correctly drawn DNA segment can be used in the answer)*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ each strand of DNA is a polymer of nucleotides         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ each strand has a sugar-phosphate backbone            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ four nitrogenous bases A **OR** adenine **AND** T **OR** thymine **AND** C **OR** cytosine **AND** G **OR** guanine              [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ two strands are anti-parallel **OR** parallel but in opposite directions **OR** one strand is in the 5’ to 3’ direction and the other strand is in the 3’ to 5’ direction    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ strands are twisted **OR** right-handed and turn into a double helix with around 10 base pairs per turn          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleotides consist of one phosphate group, one deoxyribose sugar and one nitrogenous base                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phosphate group is bound to the 5’ carbon of the sugar **OR** deoxyribose **OR** pentose                           [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phosphate group is bound to the 3’ carbon OH group of deoxyribose (of the next nucleotide)                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ covalent **OR** sugar-phosphate bond between phosphate and sugar        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nitrogenous base **OR** base is bound to the 1’ carbon of the sugar **OR** deoxyribose **OR** pentose sugar             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ covalent bond between nitrogenous base and deoxyribose **OR** sugar **OR** pentose sugar                     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ two strands are held together by hydrogen bonds between nitrogen bases of two nucleotides **OR** 3 hydrogen bonds between guanine and cytosine **OR** 2 hydrogen bonds between adenine and thymine    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ complementary base pairing between nitrogenous bases    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ A pairs up with T AND C pairs up with G        [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    DNA is a nucleic acid, also known as a **polymer of nucleotides** ($\\checkmark$) . Many nucleotides are joined together to form it. **One nucleotide** consists of three parts; a **phosphate group, deoxyribose sugar and a nitrogenous base** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    One end of the **phosphate group** is bonded to the **5’ carbon** of the **deoxyribose sugar** ($\\checkmark$). On the other side of the nucleotide, **the phosphate group is bonded to the 3’ carbon sugar** ($\\checkmark$) of another nucleotide. Both bonds are covalent **sugar-phosphate bonds** ($\\checkmark$). Repeated alternating sugar-phosphate bonds form the **sugar-phosphate backbone** ($\\checkmark$).  The **1’ carbon of the sugar is covalently bonded to the nitrogenous base** ($\\checkmark$) also through a condensation reaction to make up one complete nucleotide.\n\n    **DNA is a double helix** ($\\checkmark$) in which nitrogenous bases form the centre of the structure, and **both strands are held together by hydrogen bonds** ($\\checkmark$) between the complementary bases from each strand. **A** pairs up **with T**, and **C** always pairs up **with G** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1243","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1243\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Suggest reasons why some DNA sequences are highly conserved.        [3 marks]\n\n2. Describe how CRISPR-Cas9 can be used to remove a gene from a section of DNA.        [5 marks]\n\n3. Discuss the economic, social and ethical issues surrounding the use of CRISPR-Cas9 technology to edit genes in humans.        [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they have slower rates of mutation than other sequences        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they regulate genes that are vital for life **OR** they are DNA sequences that control gene expression **OR** promoters OR enhancers **OR** they are binding sites **OR** transcription factor binding sites [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they code for proteins that are vital for life **OR** they code for proteins with crucial functions **OR** required for basic metabolism **OR** reproduction **OR** embryonic development  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they code for structural and functional RNA    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they provide structural stability      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ required for immune system recognition        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mitochondrial DNA **OR** encodes essential components for respiration in mitochondria     [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The hypotheses that account for highly conserved sequences in genes, are that they have **slower** rates of **mutation** than other sequences ($\\checkmark$), they **regulate genes** or code for transcription factors that are **vital for life** ($\\checkmark$), or they **code for genes** that are functionally **important** ($\\checkmark$), such as those involved in cell structure or metabolism.\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA guide **OR** gRNA sequence made that is complementary to a specific DNA sequence **OR** DNA strand with target gene is used as a template to make guide RNA **OR** gRNA **OR** guide RNA **OR** gRNA produced to identify gene of interest **OR** that matches gene of interest        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA guide **OR** gRNA and Cas9 enzyme bind together **OR** Cas9 enzyme is attached to guide RNA **OR** gRNA        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ guide RNA **OR** gRNA and Cas9 injected **OR** delivered into the cell        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA guide **OR** gRNA sequence binds to **OR** identifies target DNA sequence **OR** gene of interest to be cut    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ PAM sequence **OR** protospacer adjacent motif assists Cas9 to attach to DNA        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Cas9 cuts both strands of DNA **OR** cuts out gene of interest **OR** mutated DNA **OR** DNA sequence    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell’s own DNA repair machinery silences gene of interest **OR** targeted gene        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ removed **OR** mutated gene can be replaced by correct gene        [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    CRISPR-Cas9 can be used to remove genes from cells by researchers making a **RNA guide**  that is **complementary** to the specific target DNA sequence ($\\checkmark$). The **RNA guide binds** to the **Cas9 enzyme** ($\\checkmark$), as it will **direct** the enzyme to the correct DNA sequence to be cleaved. The  **RNA guide binds** to the **target DNA** sequence ($\\checkmark$) and **Cas9 cuts both strands** of the DNA ($\\checkmark$). Finally, the cell’s own **DNA repair machinery** will **silence the targeted gene** ($\\checkmark$) and repair the strand.\n\n3. any **eight** of the following (at least **one** from each area):\n\n    **Ethical** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ any changes to genes in embryos **OR** gametes will be inherited by offspring    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ has only been used in a few clinical trials    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ humans are ‘playing God’ [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ could lead to unexpected mutations **OR** off-target effects that radically change humans **OR** potential consequences may not be seen until later generations        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ could lead to ‘designer babies’ **OR** designing aesthetically pleasing humans    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ensuring informed consent from individuals or parents is challenging        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there must be international governance **OR** coordination to address ethical standards and safe usage    [1 mark]\n\n    **Economic** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cures for diseases may be too expensive for poorer countries **OR** may strain healthcare budgets        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ could save governments money in the long term as diseases could be eliminated        [1 mark]\n\n    **Social** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ has the potential to eliminate diseases from the human population        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can be used to target molecular cancer pathways which is less stressful to the body than current cancer treatments        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ could introduce genes that improve the human race e.g. genes that boost immunity [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is some public mistrust of genetically modified organisms        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ availability and affordability could be limited to certain socio-economic groups **OR** exacerbate existing social disparities        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ could create unrealistic expectations about genetic enhancement **OR** discrimination against people with genetic diseases        [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    CRISPR-Cas9 technology can be used to edit genes, however, there are many issues surrounding its use in humans. Some of the ethical issues include that any changes to genes in **embryos** will be **inherited by offspring** ($\\checkmark$) who have no say over these changes. As humans are genetically complex and CRISPR has only been used in a **few clinical trials** ($\\checkmark$),  there may be **off target effects** ($\\checkmark$) introduced to people which may pose a risk to the population. Economic issues include that the technology may be too **expensive for poorer countries** ($\\checkmark$) but could also **save** governments or charities a **large amount of money** in the long term ($\\checkmark$), as **diseases could be eliminated** from the population ($\\checkmark$). Socially, there is already some **public mistrust** of GMOs ($\\checkmark$) so people may either be reluctant to receive treatments or people with treatments may be treated differently. CRISPR could be used to introduce genes into the human population that **‘improve’ the human race** ($\\checkmark$) for example, genes that protect people from catching flu. A positive outcome is if it could be used to **target pathways involved in cancer progression** ($\\checkmark$), which may be a less stressful treatment to the body than current cancer treatments such as chemotherapy."},{"ref":"BI1263","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1263\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline how some eukaryotic cells have an atypical cell structure.            [3 marks]\n\n2. Use named examples to explain how unicellular organisms carry out processes of life.    [5 marks]\n\n3. Compare and contrast the structure of prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells.        [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **three** from the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mature red blood cells do not have a nucleus    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ striated muscle cells have many nuclei    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ aseptate fungal hyphae **OR** fungal hyphae **OR** *Mucor* are multinucleate     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phloem sieve tube elements lack nuclei **OR** do not have many organelles **OR** have continuous cytoplasm flowing through adjacent cells **OR** have sieve tube elements to provide metabolic support **OR** are interconnected cells.    [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Mature red blood cells **do not have a nucleus** ($\\checkmark$), mature sieve tube elements **do not have a nucleus** ($\\checkmark$) and striated muscle cells and fungal hyphae have **many nuclei** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **five** of the following characteristics and a correct way the chosen unicellular organism carries out that process:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cilia allow movement in *Paramecium* **OR** flagella allow movement in *Chlamydomonas* **OR** any other suitable mode of movement relevant to a named unicellular organism    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contractile vacuole helps to regulate water potential of the cell **OR** contractile vacuole has a role in homeostasis in *Paramecium* **OR** *Chlamydomonas* **OR** any other mechanism of homeostasis that is relevant to a named unicellular organism    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enzyme-catalysed metabolic reactions such as respiration occur in the cytoplasm and mitochondria of *Paramecium* **OR** photosynthesis occurs in the chloroplast of *Chlamydomonas* **OR** correct example of metabolism for a named unicellular organism    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oral groove allows nutrition in *Paramecium* **OR** nutrition is by photosynthesis in *Chlamydomonas* **OR** correct mode of nutrition for a named unicellular organism    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *Paramecium* can reproduce asexually by binary fission **OR** *Paramecium* can reproduce by conjugation **OR** *Chlorella* reproduces asexually by mitosis    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ metabolic wastes such as CO$_2$ and ammonia diffuse out across the cell membrane in *Paramecium* **OR** metabolic wastes diffuse out across the cell membrane in *Chlamydomonas* **OR** correct mode of excretion for a named unicellular organism (do not accept removal of undigested food through the anal pore, as this is egestion)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ growth of *Paramecium* occurs after cell division **OR** *Chlamydomonas* increases in size by synthesising new cell components between divisions **OR** correct mode of growth for a named unicellular organism    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *Paramecium* sense their environment and respond by movement due to the movement of cilia **OR** *Chlamydomonas* has an eyespot to detect light and responds by swimming towards it using its flagella (phototaxis) **OR** correct example of sensitivity for a named unicellular organism    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    *Paramecia* are unicellular organisms that can carry out all functions of life. *Paramecia* have **cilia that allow movement** ($\\checkmark$). **Metabolic reactions** ($\\checkmark$) happen in mitochondria and the cytoplasm. The contractile vacuole helps to **regulate the water concentration** ($\\checkmark$) of *Paramecium* by releasing excess water. The oral groove takes in food, so it is responsible for **nutrition** ($\\checkmark$). *Paramecium* reproduces asexually by **binary fission** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **eight** of the following: \\\n*Answer **must** include at least **one** similarity and **one** difference to gain full marks*\n\n    **Similarities:** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic **AND** eukaryotic cells have plasma membranes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic **AND** eukaryotic cells have cytoplasm        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic **AND** eukaryotic cells have ribosomes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic **AND** eukaryotic cells have genetic material **OR** DNA **OR** RNA    [1 mark]\n\n    **Differences:** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells are not compartmentalised  **BUT** eukaryotic cells are compartmentalised    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells do not have membrane-bound organelles **BUT** eukaryotic cells do have membrane-bound organelles            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells have DNA in a loop **OR** plasmid **BUT** eukaryotes have linear DNA **OR** chromosomes         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells have 70S ribosomes **BUT** eukaryotic cells have 80S ribosomes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells do not have a true nucleus **OR** have a nucleoid region **BUT** eukaryotic cells do have a true nucleus    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ all prokaryotic cells have a cell wall **BUT** not all eukaryotic cells have a cell wall    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic DNA is naked **OR** not associated with proteins **BUT** eukaryotic DNA is associated with histone proteins        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells are smaller **THAN** eukaryotic cells        [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells have **genetic material** ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells have **plasma membranes** ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells have **cytoplasm** ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells have **ribosomes** ($\\checkmark$), but **prokaryotes have 70S ribosomes, whereas eukaryotic cells have 80S ribosomes** ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotic cells **do not have a true nucleus**, but eukaryotic cells do have a true nucleus ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotic cells do not have membrane-bound organelles but **eukaryotic cells do have membrane-bound organelles** such as mitochondria ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotic cells have **DNA in a loop** but eukaryotic cells have linear DNA ($\\checkmark$). Not all eukaryotic cells have a cell wall but **all prokaryotic cells do have a cell wall** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n*A quality mark is awarded if each part of the question (a, b, c) is answered to earn at least half of the points available, answers do not require re-reading to be understood, and there are almost no errors and little irrelevant information.* [1 mark]"},{"ref":"BI1270","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1270\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nOrganelles are adapted for their functions.\n\n1. Outline the structure and function of vesicles.    [4 marks]\n\n2. Compare and contrast the structure and function of the rough endoplasmic reticulum and the Golgi apparatus.    [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain how the membranes of the mitochondria, chloroplasts and the nucleus are adapted for their functions.    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles are membrane-bound organelles    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles transport substances within cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles store substances    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles are used in exocytosis **OR** release of neurotransmitters **OR** any other suitable example of exocytosis    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles are used in endocytosis **OR** phagocytosis **OR** pinocytosis    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lysosomes are vesicles that contain digestive enzymes **OR** lysosomes digest material taken in by endocytosis **OR** phagocytosis **OR** lysosomes break down damaged cell organelles    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles are formed by the endoplasmic reticulum **OR** the Golgi apparatus    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the vesicle membrane fuses with the plasma membrane    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles are moved along microtubules by motor proteins    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ secretory vesicles transport substances to be released from the cell **OR** secretory vesicles transport substances such as hormones to the cell membrane to be released    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ clathrin is a protein that is important for vesicle formation **OR** clathrin coats the membrane and helps it bud inwards to form vesicles in endocytosis    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Vesicles are **membrane bound organelles** ($\\checkmark$). A protein called **clathrin has a role in the formation of vesicles** ($\\checkmark$). Vesicles **transport and store substances** ($\\checkmark$). Secretory vesicles **transport substances for release from the cell** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** similarity and **one** difference to gain full marks*):\n\n    ***Similarities:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the rough endoplasmic reticulum **AND** the Golgi apparatus are made of flattened membrane sacs **OR** **BOTH** the rough endoplasmic reticulum and the Golgi apparatus are membrane-bound organelles    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** the rough endoplasmic reticulum and the Golgi apparatus are involved in modification **OR** processing of proteins **OR** post-translational modification    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are involved in the transport of proteins after translation    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the Golgi apparatus membrane sacs are not interconnected **BUT** the rough endoplasmic reticulum is interconnected    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ribosomes are attached to the rough endoplasmic reticulum **BUT** ribosomes do not attach to the Golgi apparatus    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rough endoplasmic reticulum processes polypeptides formed by the ribosomes **WHEREAS** the Golgi apparatus processes proteins received from the rough endoplasmic reticulum    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the Golgi apparatus modifies (e.g. glycosylates), sorts and packages proteins into vesicles for secretion    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the rough endoplasmic reticulum synthesises proteins for secretion **OR** for membranes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the membrane of the rough endoplasmic reticulum is continuous with the nuclear envelope **BUT** the Golgi apparatus is not connected to the nuclear envelope    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Both** the rough endoplasmic reticulum and the Golgi apparatus are made of **flattened membrane sacs** ($\\checkmark$). The rough endoplasmic reticulum and the Golgi apparatus **both** have a **role in processing proteins** ($\\checkmark$) in cells, but the **rough endoplasmic reticulum processes polypeptides produced by the ribosomes**, whereas the **Golgi apparatus processes proteins** received **from** the **rough endoplasmic reticulum** ($\\checkmark$). The **rough endoplasmic reticulum** has **ribosomes** attached and its membrane is **continuous** with the **nuclear envelope**, whereas the **Golgi apparatus** is **not associated with ribosomes** ($\\checkmark$) and is **not connected to** the **nuclear envelope** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** mark point for each of the organelles to gain full marks*):\n\n    ***Mitochondria:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ membrane infoldings of mitochondria provide a large surface area    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electron transport chain proteins are located on the inner membrane (cristae) of mitochondria    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ inner membrane contains ATP synthases for ATP production    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ small intermembrane space between the inner and outer membranes allows a proton gradient to be established quickly    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ inner membrane of mitochondria is impermeable to most ions allowing for a proton gradient to be established    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ outer membrane of mitochondria separates the contents of the mitochondria from the rest of the cell    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Chloroplasts:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ thylakoid membranes give a large surface area in chloroplasts **OR** give a large surface area for the absorption of light    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ membranes of thylakoids in chloroplasts contain photosystems for converting light energy to chemical energy    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electron transport chain proteins in the thylakoid membranes pump protons into the thylakoid space, establishing a proton gradient    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP synthase in the thylakoid membranes produces ATP by chemiosmosis **OR** NADP is reduced to NADPH at the end of the electron transport chain, for use in the light-independent reactions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the small volume of fluid inside the thylakoids (thylakoid space) allows a proton gradient to be established quickly    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Nuclear membrane:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nuclear pores in the nuclear membrane allow substances to pass into and out of the nucleus **OR** nuclear pores allow mRNA to exit the nucleus    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the double membrane separates transcription from translation, allowing mRNA to be modified before translation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the nuclear membrane breaks down into vesicles during mitosis **OR** meiosis **AND** the vesicles fuse to reform the nuclear envelope afterwards    [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The membranes of **cristae** in mitochondria give a **large surface area** for **electron transport chains** ($\\checkmark$), the **small intermembrane** space allows **concentration gradients** to be **established** ($\\checkmark$), and the membranes of the cristae **contain ATP synthases** for the **production** of **ATP** ($\\checkmark$). The **nuclear membrane** is a double membrane that has **pores** for the **movement** of **substances into and out** of the **nucleus** ($\\checkmark$) and the membrane **breaks down into vesicles** during **mitosis** and **meiosis**, which **later fuse** to **reform** the nuclear **envelope** ($\\checkmark$). The membranes of the **thylakoids** in chloroplasts give a **large surface area** for **absorption of light** ($\\checkmark$) and the **small volume** of the **thylakoid space** allows a **proton gradient** to be **established** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1277","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1277\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the concept of homeostasis.    [3 marks]\n\n2. Describe the physiological mechanisms of thermoregulation in humans and their effects on the body.    [5 marks]\n\n3. Compare and contrast type I and type II diabetes.    [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ homeostasis is the maintenance of the internal conditions **OR** internal environment of an organism        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ conditions inside the body are kept within a range of acceptable limits **OR** preset limits **OR** optimal range **OR** safe range                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ changes in the external environment **OR** conditions outside the body **OR** external conditions are not reflected                        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ receptors detect changes **OR** receive stimulus and pass to control centre **OR** CNS **OR** brain [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ control centre **OR** CNS **OR** brain sends information **OR** nerve impulses to effector **OR** muscle or gland         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use of negative feedback loops to return homeostatic variables to set point        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ named example, such as blood pH, body temperature, blood glucose concentration, or blood osmotic concentration        [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Homeostasis is the body’s way of **maintaining** stable **internal conditions** ($\\checkmark$). Homeostatic variables are **kept within an optimal range** ($\\checkmark$) through the use of **negative feedback loops**, which return internal conditions to the **set point** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following (*must include the mechanism **and** effect on the body to receive each mark*):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vasodilation **OR** relaxation of smooth muscle in arterioles supplying the skin **WHICH** increases blood flow near the skin surface so more heat is lost by radiation **OR** lowers body temperature **OR** cools the body    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vasoconstriction **OR** contraction of smooth muscle in arterioles supplying the skin **WHICH** diverts blood away from the skin surface so less heat is lost by radiation **OR** prevents heat loss **OR** keeps the body warm    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ shivering **WHICH** increases cellular respiration in muscles, releasing heat **OR** raises the body temperature **OR** warms the body    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sweating **WHICH** removes heat as the water in sweat evaporates **OR** lowers body temperature **OR** cools the body    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ arrector pili muscles relax **OR** hairs lie flat **WHICH** reduces the insulating layer of trapped air, increasing heat loss **OR** lowers body temperature **OR** cools the body    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ arrector pili muscles contract **OR** hairs stand erect **WHICH** traps a layer of air that insulates the skin **OR** prevents heat loss **OR** keeps the body warm    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ uncoupled respiration in brown adipose tissue **WHICH** releases energy as heat instead of producing ATP **OR** raises body temperature **OR** warms the body    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Shivering** and **sweating** are both used by humans to adjust the core body temperature. When humans **shiver**, rapid involuntary muscle contractions increase cellular respiration, releasing heat, which **raises** the **body temperature** slightly ($\\checkmark$). Conversely, **sweating** releases water onto the skin; as this water evaporates it removes heat from the body, lowering the **body temperature** ($\\checkmark$). Hairs on the skin are also involved in thermoregulation. When thermoreceptors detect a rise in body temperature, the arrector pili muscles relax, causing the hairs to lie flat. This reduces the layer of trapped air, increasing heat loss and cooling the body ($\\checkmark$). On the other hand, when body temperature falls, the arrector pili muscles contract, causing the hairs to stand erect. This traps a layer of insulating air next to the skin, reducing heat loss and keeping the body warm ($\\checkmark$). Blood vessels also respond: when body temperature rises, arterioles supplying the skin dilate (vasodilation), increasing blood flow near the skin surface so that more heat is lost by radiation ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following (must include at least **one** similarity and at least **one** difference to receive full marks):\n\n    *similarities:* \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** types are characterised by high blood sugar **OR** blood glucose concentrations **OR** hyperglycaemia    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** types can lead to excessive urination **OR** thirst (although more severe for type I)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glucose can be excreted in urine in **BOTH** types of diabetes when blood glucose levels exceed renal threshold    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ patients with type I **AND** type II must monitor and adjust their diets carefully    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** conditions, if untreated, can lead to serious health effects, such as renal failure **OR** heart attacks **OR** strokes **OR** blindness **OR** death    [1 mark]\n\n    *differences:* \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type I diabetes is rare **WHEREAS** type II diabetes is common (~90% of cases)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type I diabetes is also known as ‘early onset diabetes’ **OR** more common in young people **OR** people under the age of 20 **WHILE** type II diabetes is also known as ‘late onset diabetes’ **OR** historically associated with older adults but is now increasingly found in younger people    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type I is an autoimmune disorder **WHILE** type II is not    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type I patients produce very little **OR** no insulin **WHEREAS** type II patients may produce insulin but in insufficient amounts **OR** in a form the body cannot use effectively **OR** body does not respond to it    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type I is caused by the destruction of **OR** failure of beta (β) cells in the pancreas to produce insulin **WHEREAS** type II is caused by insulin resistance **OR** a failure of receptors to respond to insulin **OR** impaired insulin secretion    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type I must be treated by insulin injection **OR** insulin pumps **WHILE** type II can often be treated with diet **OR** weight management **OR** exercise **OR** may require medication **OR** insulin as the disease progresses    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type I can develop rapidly **OR** over days or weeks **WHEREAS** type II develops gradually **OR** over years    [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Both type I and type II diabetes are characterised by **high blood glucose levels** ($\\checkmark$) due to problems with insulin regulation, with **glucose** being **excreted** in the **urine** when blood **glucose levels exceed** the **renal threshold** ($\\checkmark$). Both sets of patients must modify their **diets** ($\\checkmark$) carefully to avoid long-term **health effects** such as **renal failure** and **stroke** ($\\checkmark$). Type I diabetes is considered an **autoimmune disorder**, while type II diabetes is not ($\\checkmark$). This is because type I diabetes is caused by the **destruction of pancreatic beta cells**, which produce **insulin**, whereas type II diabetes is caused by **insulin resistance**, where **cells do not respond properly** to insulin, even though it may still be produced ($\\checkmark$). Type I diabetes typically requires **lifelong insulin therapy**, whereas type II diabetes can often be **managed with lifestyle changes** such as **diet** and **exercise**, though **medication** may be necessary as the **condition progresses** ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, type I diabetes **develops rapidly**, often **over weeks**, while type II **progresses slowly** over **months or years** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1291","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1291\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe pale and dark forms of the peppered moth (Biston betularia) are a classic example of natural selection. The graph shows the changes in the population of pale (mostly white) and dark (mostly black) peppered moths (*Biston betularia*) during and post the industrial revolution.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1291.png\" alt=\"BI1291\">\n\nThe image shows the phenotype of pale and dark moths on white tree bark.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1291b.png\" alt=\"BI1291b\">\n[Source: Adapted from Bedin, T. (2020, May 26). *Tilpasning og evolusjon - natural science (PB) - NDLA. ndla.no*. Retrieved October 02, 2023, from https://ndla.no/en/subject:1:bb834c76-d1e4-46c4-8c0a-8f978bd2c956/topic:6:f604b380-5cdb-4227-885e-bc9b57cb3237/topic:3:aa019f6a-a569-4491-b0d2-c8d2df21733f/resource:09fc6c1a-eaa1-4901-b2ae-ee5e7ce1900c. Copyright free]\n\n1. Distinguish between the changes in the numbers of pale and dark moths shown in the graph. [2 marks]\n\n2.\n 1. Outline how the trends in the graph can be explained by natural selection. [3 marks]\n\n 2. Suggest a technique a group of students could use to compare the population sizes of peppered moths between a deciduous (broadleaved) and a coniferous (evergreen) wood. [1 mark]\n\n3. Calculate the percentage increase in dark-coloured moths between 1850 and 1900. [1 mark]\n\n4. Draw a line on the graph, predicting how the number of pale moths would have changed, if the industrial revolution did not happen. [1 mark]\n\n5. In addition to collecting data such as that shown in the graph, scientists rely on various forms of evidence to explore the concept of evolution. State two other forms of evidence that supports the theory of evolution. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ between 1800 and 1850 **OR** around the middle of the Industrial Revolution, the number of pale moths remained high **OR** between 95 and 100% **WHEREAS** the number of dark moths remained low **OR** between 0 and 5% [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ from 1850, the number of pale moths began to rapidly decrease **WHEREAS** the number of dark moths rapidly increased [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ around 1870 **OR** just before 1875, the number of dark moths surpasses the number of pale moths [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ from 1900, the number of pale moths was low **OR** less than 5% **WHEREAS** the number of dark moths was high **OR** around 95% [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Between **1800 and 1850**, the number of **pale** moths was between **95 and 100%**, whereas the number of **dark** moths was between **0 and 5%** ($\\checkmark$). However, **from 1850** the number of **pale** moths **decreased** rapidly, whereas the number of **dark** moths **increased** rapidly ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1. Any **three** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ natural selection explains how individuals with advantageous traits are more likely to survive to reproduce [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ before the Industrial Revolution, lichens **OR** mosses **OR** bark on trees **OR** brickwork were white, so the pale moths were able to camouflage from predators [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increased rates of pollution **OR** soot during the Industrial Revolution caused white lichen and mosses on lichen **OR** moss **OR** bark on trees **OR** brickwork to turn black [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ pale moths were more visible to predators, so their numbers decreased [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ dark moths were able to camouflage from predators, so were better able to survive and breed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the dark moths gained a selective advantage over the pale moths **OR** were selected for **OR** are under directional selection **OR** pale moths faced intense selection pressure due to predation [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Before the Industrial Revolution, **lichen and mosses** on trees were **white**, so the pale moths were able to **camouflage from predators** ($\\checkmark$). However, **post-Industrial Revolution**, there were increased rates of **pollution** which caused trees to turn **black** ($\\checkmark$). This meant that the dark moths were able to **camouflage** from predators, so were better able to **survive and breed** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 2.\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ capture-mark-release-recapture [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n One technique that students could use is the **mark-release-recapture** of **peppered** moths ($\\checkmark$) in coniferous and deciduous woods.\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 1800 % [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1291c.png\" alt=\"BI1291c\">\n\n Percentage increase = ((final value - initial value) ÷ initial value) $\\times$ 100% \\\n Percentage increase = ((95 - 5) ÷ 5 ) $\\times$ 100% \\\n Percentage increase = **1800%** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ line should continue the same trend from before 1850 to 1900 [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1291d.png\" alt=\"BI1291d\">\n\n5. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA sequence **OR** RNA sequencing [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ amino acid sequence of proteins [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ selective breeding **OR** artificial selection [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous structures **OR** comparative anatomy **OR** vestigial structures [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ embryonic development [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the fossil record [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ geographic distribution of species [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **fossil record** ($\\checkmark$) and **DNA sequencing** ($\\checkmark$) provide evidence for the theory of evolution."},{"ref":"BI1299","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1299\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast the principal sources of auxin and cytokinins and their roles in plant growth.        [4 marks]\n\n2. Describe how positive feedback is involved in synchronised and rapid fruit ripening.        [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain how the phototropic response, mediated by auxin in the shoot, triggers changes in the cell wall structure.    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** similarity and **one** difference to gain full marks*):\n\n***Differences***\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ auxin is produced mainly in shoot apical meristems **OR** young leaves, **WHEREAS** cytokinins are produced mainly in root apical meristems      [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ auxin primarily promotes cell elongation, **WHEREAS** cytokinins primarily promote cell division     [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ auxin from the shoot apex promotes apical dominance **OR** inhibits lateral bud growth, **WHEREAS** cytokinins promote lateral bud growth         [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ auxin promotes the initiation of lateral **OR** adventitious roots, **WHEREAS** a relatively high cytokinin concentration promotes shoot formation     [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ auxin delays leaf **OR** fruit abscission, **WHEREAS** cytokinins delay leaf senescence     [1 mark]\n\n***Similarities***\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are chemical messengers that regulate plant growth and development at low concentrations      [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are produced in actively growing plant tissues      [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** regulate the growth and development of roots and shoots      [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ the relative concentrations of **BOTH** hormones help coordinate root and shoot development      [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can be transported from their principal sites of production to other parts of the plant      [1 mark]\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n**Auxin** is produced mainly in **shoot apical meristems and young leaves**, whereas **cytokinins** are produced mainly in **root apical meristems** ($\\checkmark$). **Both hormones** regulate the growth and development of **roots and shoots** ($\\checkmark$). Auxin primarily promotes **cell elongation**, whereas cytokinins primarily promote **cell division** ($\\checkmark$). **Auxin** from the **shoot apex** inhibits **lateral bud growth**, whereas **cytokinins** promote lateral bud growth ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ positive feedback is when a change stimulates further change in the same direction **OR** the output of a process increases the rate of that process        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ethylene **OR** ethene is produced in ripening fruit **OR** maturing fruit        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ethylene **OR** ethene speeds up the ripening process of the fruit that produced it     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as the fruit ripens further, it produces more ethylene    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nearby fruit detect ethylene **OR** ethene        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nearby fruit starts to ripen                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ overall ethylene **OR** ethene production increases        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ more fruit ripen **OR** a large number of fruit ripen **OR** fruit ripening is synchronised due to the amplifying nature of the positive feedback loop        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ synchronised ripening means many fruits are ripe at the same time, attracting seed-dispersing animals **OR** making seed dispersal more efficient    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Positive feedback** is when a **change** stimulates **further change** in the **same direction** ($\\checkmark$). This can occur in synchronised and rapid fruit ripening, where **ethylene** is produced in **ripening fruits**  ($\\checkmark$), and other nearby fruits **detect** the ethylene ($\\checkmark$), so they **also** begin to **ripen** ($\\checkmark$). The ripe fruit then releases its **own ethylene**, so that even **more** nearby fruit **ripens**, so ripening is **rapid** and **synchronised** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ auxin is positively phototropic **OR** is produced in the shoot tip **OR** coleoptile in response to light            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light causes auxin to diffuse into cells on the shaded side of the shoot, **OR** light causes auxin to be actively transported out of cells on the illuminated side of the shoot     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ auxin accumulates on the dark side of the shoot    **OR** auxin redistributes to the shaded side [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ auxin stimulates the activity of proton pumps in the cell membrane    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ proton pumps actively transport protons (H:sup[+]) from the cytoplasm into the cell wall    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ auxin promotes hydrogen ion secretion into  non-living part of the cells **OR** cell walls/cell wall junctions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ acidifying the cell wall    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ low pH activates expansins    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ altering the pattern of hydrogen bonding in polysaccharides in the cell wall **OR** expansins trigger the breakage of crosslinks within cellulose **OR** expansins break hydrogen bonds between cellulose microfibrils and other cell wall components        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the resistance of the cell wall to stretching **OR** cell elongation is decreased, **OR** cell wall becomes more flexible, **OR** cell can take up water more readily, leading to turgor pressure-driven cell expansion or elongation                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell turgor causes stretching **OR** elongation of the cell wall            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ auxin regulates gene expression to promote cell elongation on the shaded side    [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Auxin is a phytohormone, produced in **shoot tips** in response to **light**  ($\\checkmark$). Light causes **auxin** to **diffuse** into cells on the **shaded** side of the shoot ($\\checkmark$) so that it **accumulates** in the **shaded** side ($\\checkmark$). Auxin promotes **hydrogen ion secretion** into the **cell walls and cell wall junctions**  ($\\checkmark$), which **acidifies** the **cell wall** ($\\checkmark$). Low pH activates proteins called **expansins** ($\\checkmark$), and expansins alter the pattern of **hydrogen bonding in polysaccharides** in the cell wall ($\\checkmark$), which **lowers** the resistance of cell walls to **stretching**  ($\\checkmark$). As **water enters** the cell the **turgor pressure** causes the **stretching** and **elongation** of the cell ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1300","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1300\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Describe how the structure of ATP is related to its function. [5 marks]\n\n2. Explain how temperature affects the rate of respiration, with reference to enzymes.    [4 marks]\n\n3. Compare and contrast anaerobic respiration in mice (*Mus musculus*) and yeast.    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **five** of the following (*answer must link structure to function to gain full marks*):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleotide made up of adenine **OR** nitrogenous base, **AND** a ribose **OR** pentose sugar, **AND** three phosphate groups    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cannot pass freely through the plasma membrane **SO** is retained inside the cell where it is needed    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrolysis of the terminal phosphate group releases energy (approximately 30 kJ per mole) **SO** energy is available immediately when needed    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ small relative to macromolecules **AND** soluble **SO** can diffuse freely within the cytoplasm to where energy is needed    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ soluble **OR** highly reactive **SO** can interact readily with other molecules **OR** participate in chemical reactions **OR** can easily be utilised by cells **OR** can react with other metabolites    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can lose a phosphate to make ADP, **AND** ADP can be phosphorylated to make ATP    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrolysis and phosphorylation means it is reusable **OR** recycled    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can transfer a phosphate group to other molecules (phosphorylation) **SO** they become more reactive **OR** less stable    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    ATP is a **nucleotide** consisting of a nitrogenous **base (adenine)**, a **ribose** and **three phosphate groups** ($\\checkmark$). It can easily **lose a phosphate group** via **hydrolysis** to **release energy** ($\\checkmark$), and **ADP** can be **phosphorylated** back to **ATP**, making it **reusable** ($\\checkmark$). The **polar** phosphate groups make ATP **soluble** in water, allowing it to **diffuse freely** ($\\checkmark$) within cells and enabling it to readily **participate in chemical reactions** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as temperature rises, the rate of respiration increases as enzymes **AND** substrates have more kinetic energy **OR** at lower temperatures, the rate of respiration is slower as enzymes **AND** substrates have less kinetic energy    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ so there is a higher chance of successful collisions between enzymes and substrates    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ until an optimum temperature is reached, at which the enzyme exhibits its maximum activity    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at optimum temperature, the kinetic energy of the molecules is sufficiently high to promote a high level of enzyme-substrate interaction **BUT** not so high to cause extensive denaturation of enzyme    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when the temperature is higher than the optimum, the rate of respiration begins to decrease as enzymes involved become denatured **OR** the active site changes shape so the substrate no longer fits    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    At **lower temperatures**, the rate of respiration is **slower** because the **enzymes** involved have **less kinetic energy** ($\\checkmark$), so there is a **lower chance** of successful **collisions** with **substrates** ($\\checkmark$). As **temperatures rise**, the **rate** of respiration **increases**, as the enzymes involved have **more kinetic energy** ($\\checkmark$) until an **optimum** temperature is reached, where the rate of respiration is **highest** ($\\checkmark$). **Above** this **optimum** temperature, the **respiration rate decreases** as the **enzymes** start to become **denatured** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** similarity and **one** difference to gain full marks*):\n\n    *Differences*\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the product of anaerobic respiration in mice is lactic acid **OR** lactate **WHEREAS**, in yeast, it is ethanol and carbon dioxide    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ yeast is better adapted to long-term anaerobic respiration **WHEREAS** mouse cells can perform anaerobic respiration but are limited in endurance    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ yeast converts pyruvate to ethanal, releasing carbon dioxide, then ethanal to ethanol **WHEREAS** mice convert pyruvate directly to lactate with no carbon dioxide released    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lactate can be converted back to pyruvate **OR** glucose when oxygen is available **WHEREAS** ethanol and carbon dioxide are released from yeast cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ products of yeast are used commercially, ethanol in brewing **AND** carbon dioxide in baking, **WHEREAS** lactate in mice is produced in muscles during vigorous activity    [1 mark]\n\n    *Similarities* \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** use glucose as a substrate    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** occur in the cytoplasm    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** occur in the absence **OR** shortage of oxygen    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the products of anaerobic respiration in **BOTH** mice and yeast can be toxic if they build up    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can occur alongside aerobic respiration    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** produce a small amount of ATP **OR** 2 ATPs    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ anaerobic respiration in **BOTH** mice and yeast allows ATP production to continue when oxygen is insufficient    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** begin with glycolysis, in which glucose is converted to pyruvate    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in **BOTH**, reduced NAD is oxidised to regenerate NAD for glycolysis    [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The product of anaerobic respiration in mice is **lactate**, whereas in yeast, it is **ethanol and carbon dioxide** ($\\checkmark$). However, in both the anaerobic respiration of mice and yeast, **glucose** is used as a **substrate** ($\\checkmark$), it occurs in the **cytoplasm** ($\\checkmark$), produces a **small** amount of **ATP** ($\\checkmark$) and in the **absence of oxygen** ($\\checkmark$). Also, in both mice and yeast, if the **products** of anaerobic respiration **build up**, they can be **toxic** to the cell ($\\checkmark$), and anaerobic respiration can occur **alongside aerobic respiration** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1312","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1312\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nEvolution by natural selection was first proposed by Charles Darwin based on his observations of traits in species.\n\n1. Outline the requirements for evolution by natural selection.    [4 marks]\n\n2. Distinguish between inherited and acquired characteristics.    [3 marks]\n\n3. Describe the selection pressures acting on guppies using John Endler’s experiments as an example.            [9 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ overproduction of offspring        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ variation in populations        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ variations must be inheritable **OR** genetic for natural selection    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is a struggle for survival **OR** competition for resources    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ individuals that are best adapted to survive and reproduce **OR** fittest for the environment survive and reproduce     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ over long periods of time **OR** many generations, natural selection leads to genetic change in population    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ natural selection over long periods of time leads to evolution    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Darwin observed **variations** ($\\checkmark$) within populations and that there is an **overproduction of offspring** ($\\checkmark$). Darwin also observed that population sizes tended to remain constant over time, which led him to conclude that there is a **struggle for survival** ($\\checkmark$). Individuals **best adapted to the environment survive, reproduce** ($\\checkmark$), and pass on their genes. This **leads to evolution over long periods of time** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ inherited characteristics are controlled by genes **OR** encoded in DNA, **BUT** acquired characteristics are not controlled by genes **OR** encoded in DNA    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ inherited characteristics are not affected by the environment, **BUT** acquired characteristics are affected by the environment    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ inherited characteristics cannot be learned, **BUT** acquired characteristics can be learned                        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ inherited characteristics can be passed onto the next generation **OR** offspring, **BUT** acquired characteristics cannot                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ an example of an inherited characteristic is blood group **OR** haemoglobin **OR** any other relevant example **OR** an example of an acquired characteristic is language **OR** dyed hair colour **OR** any other relevant example            [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Inherited characteristics are controlled by genes**, but acquired characteristics are not controlled by genes  ($\\checkmark$). **Inherited** characteristics are **not affected** by the **environment**, but **acquired** characteristics **are affected by** the **environment** ($\\checkmark$). **Inherited** characteristics are **not learned**, but **acquired** characteristics **can be learned**  ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **nine** of the following:\\\n    Statements for **both** sexual selection **and** natural selection are required.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sexual selection affects mating and reproduction success, often at the cost of survival        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ natural selection is the process where individuals that are best adapted to their environment are most likely to survive and reproduce                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ male guppies express genes for bright colours                 [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Endler hypothesised that guppies in habitats protected from predators would show brighter colours **OR** brighter spots than those in habitats where they are exposed to predators  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Endler observed guppies in wild **AND** in man-made **OR** artificial ponds     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ independent variable was the presence of predators **OR** cichlids        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ guppies from the wild with varying colours **OR** spots were introduced to tanks OR guppies introduced to tanks and allowed to breed            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ one set of guppies was kept in ponds with predators **OR** cichlids present, **AND** one set with non-predatory **OR** weak-predatory fish present **OR** no predators        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ponds had different sized gravel, **OR** some ponds had fine gravel, **AND** some had course gravel        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in ponds with predators **OR** cichlids, guppies had duller colours **OR** duller spots        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in ponds with coarser gravel, guppies had larger spots **OR** in ponds with finer gravel, guppies had smaller spots     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ more brightly coloured males **OR** those with brighter spots **OR** the more conspicuous males **OR** those that have spots that do not match the gravel size tend to be eaten, so less likely to mate and pass on their alleles to the next generation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ this is predatory selection pressure and leads to a decrease in frequency of alleles for bright colours **OR** spots in the population over time     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in ponds with no predators **OR** non-predatory fish, guppies had brighter colours **OR** brighter spots                     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ brighter coloured guppies **OR** those with brighter spots were more likely to mate and pass on their alleles to the next generation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ this is sexual selection and leads to an increase in frequency of alleles for bright colours **OR** spots in the population over time    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ these experiments show the evolution of guppy colouration involves a dynamic interplay between sexual and natural selection     [1 mark]\n    [9 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Endler hypothesised that **male guppies** would have **brighter colours** in areas where they were **protected from predators** compared to where predators were present ($\\checkmark$). He observed guppies in **natural** and **artificial ponds** ($\\checkmark$). **One set** of male guppies was kept in ponds with **predators**, and **one set** was kept in ponds **without predators** ($\\checkmark$). In the ponds where **predators** were **present**, the **male guppies** were **duller** in colour ($\\checkmark$), which means the males were more **camouflaged** and less likely to be eaten, and so **more likely to survive** to reproductive age and pass on their **alleles** to the **next generation** ($\\checkmark$).  In ponds where there were **no**, or few, **predators**, male guppies were more **brightly coloured** ($\\checkmark$), suggesting **female guppies** were **more attracted** to brightly coloured males, so they were **more likely** to **mate** and **pass on** their **alleles** to the next generation ($\\checkmark$).  Over time, this increases the **frequency** of **alleles** for **bright colours** in these populations ($\\checkmark$). These experiments provide **evidence** that the **evolution** of male guppie colouring is influenced by **natural** and **sexual selection** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1316","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1316\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline three sources which provide evidence for evolution.    [3 marks]\n\n2. Describe how both bats and butterflies have wings.        [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain the process of speciation with at least one named example.     [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA sequence **OR** RNA sequence    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ amino acid sequence of proteins     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ selective breeding **OR** artificial selection    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous structures **OR** comparative anatomy **OR** vestigial structures     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ embryonic development    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the fossil record        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ geographic distribution of species    [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Evidence for evolution can come from **DNA sequences** ($\\checkmark$), **amino acid sequences** ($\\checkmark$), or **homologous structures** ($\\checkmark$) such as the pentadactyl limb.\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ bat wings and butterfly wings  do not share a common ancestor        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ bats share common ancestry with other mammals, whereas butterflies share common ancestry with other insects **OR** bats are mammals **OR** Mammalia, whereas butterflies are insects **OR** Arthropoda     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ wings evolved through convergent evolution     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ convergent evolution is the process where unrelated organisms evolve similar characteristics **OR** traits to perform similar functions     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ wings in bats and butterflies are an example of analogous structures         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ analogous structures share a similar function but are not the result of a common ancestor                         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ wings evolved in response to similar environmental pressures **OR** ecological niches **OR** as a result of natural selection favouring flight     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ wings in bats are modified forelimbs **OR** are made of skin and bone **WHEREAS** butterfly wings are not **OR** are made of chitin         [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Bats and butterflies do not share a recent **common ancestor** ($\\checkmark$), but both evolved wings due to **convergent evolution** ($\\checkmark$). Convergent evolution is the process by which unrelated organisms can **evolve similar traits** due to similar necessities, such as the need to fly ($\\checkmark$). This means that wings in bats and butterflies are an example of **analogous structures** ($\\checkmark$) because they share a **similar function** but do not result from a common ancestor ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ speciation is the process by which a population is split and evolves differently over time to form distinct species [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ diversity exists within a population **OR** genetic variation exists within a population      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ members of a population become reproductively isolated                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sympatric speciation occurs when new species evolve within the same geographic area       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allopatric speciation occurs when new species form in separate geographic areas due to geographical isolation            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reproductive isolation can be due to geographical **OR** physical **OR** behavioural **OR** temporal barriers [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ each population is exposed to different environmental conditions **OR** selective pressures [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ each population evolves differently over time **OR** different adaptations evolve over time **OR** mutations affect each group differently **OR** differential selection **OR** genetic drift affects populations differently    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ these differences act as barriers to gene flow     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ over time, the groups evolve differently and can no longer interbreed, so new species have been formed     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ bonobos and chimpanzees **OR** any other suitable example of speciation     [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Speciation is the process by which a population becomes split, and each group evolves differently over time, leading to the formation of **new species** ($\\checkmark$). This is possible because **variation** exists within a population ($\\checkmark$). When members of a population become **reproductively isolated** ($\\checkmark$) by a geographical barrier like a river ($\\checkmark$), both groups are exposed to different environmental conditions or **selective pressures** ($\\checkmark$). Over time, the two groups evolve differently as different mutations affect both groups, this is referred to as **differential selection** ($\\checkmark$). These differences between the populations act as **barriers to gene flow** ($\\checkmark$). Over time, the groups evolve so differently that they can no longer **interbreed** and are two distinct species ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1319","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1319\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast transcription and translation between eukaryotes and prokaryotes.[5 marks]\n\n2. Describe how vesicles link translation and exocytosis.    [3 marks]\n\n3. Explain how errors in transcription may result in altered enzyme function.    [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **five** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** similarity and **one** difference to achieve full marks*):\n\n    ***Similarities*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ribosomes are the site of translation in **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ translation occurs in the cytoplasm  in **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA is synthesised in the 5’ to 3’ direction during transcription in  **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the mRNA code is read in the 5’ to 3’ direction in  **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** prokaryotes and eukaryotes have promoter regions **OR** sequences which enzymes **OR** transcription factors can bind to initiate **OR** enhance **OR** repress transcription    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA polymerase is involved in transcription in **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transcription involves the use of a DNA template to synthesise RNA in **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ translation involves the use of an RNA template **OR** mRNA to synthesise protein in **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the base pairing rules for DNA and RNA are the same **OR** Adenine (A) pairs with Thymine (T) in DNA **OR** Uracil (U) in RNA, while Cytosine (C) pairs with Guanine (G)  in **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there are specific stop signals that mark the end of transcription and translation in **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** prokaryotes and eukaryotes use transfer RNA (tRNA) molecules in translation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the same codons typically code for the same amino acids in **BOTH** eukaryotes and prokaryotes            [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transcription and translation are coupled **OR** occur simultaneously in prokaryotes **WHEREAS** in eukaryotes transcription occurs before translation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in eukaryotes transcription occurs in the nucleus **AND** translation occurs in the cytoplasm **WHEREAS** in prokaryotes **BOTH** processes occur in the cytoplasm    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleosomes are present in eukaryotic DNA and help to regulate transcription **BUT** are absent from prokaryotic DNA    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$eukaryotic mRNA undergoes post-transcriptional modification **OR** splicing **OR** addition of a poly-A tail **OR** addition of a 3’ cap **WHEREAS** prokaryotic DNA does not **OR** eukaryotes have introns **WHEREAS** prokaryotes do not        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 70S ribosomes carry out translation in prokaryotes **WHEREAS** 80S ribosomes carry out translation in eukaryotes                    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Ribosomes** are the site of **translation** in both eukaryotes and prokaryotes ($\\checkmark$), however, **70S ribosomes** carry out translation in prokaryotes, and **80S ribosomes** carry out translation in eukaryotes ($\\checkmark$). During transcription, **mRNA is synthesised** in the **5’ to 3’** direction in both eukaryotes and prokaryotes ($\\checkmark$), and both involve **RNA polymerase** ($\\checkmark$). However, in eukaryotes, the mRNA undergoes **post-transcriptional modification**, whereas this does not occur in prokaryotes ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polypeptides are translated by ribosomes on the rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER) **OR** after translation, polypeptide chains are folded into proteins **OR** modified inside RER    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles containing polypeptides bud off the RER **AND** are transported to the Golgi apparatus    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polypeptides are processed into their final form in the Golgi apparatus    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles bud off the Golgi apparatus        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vesicles fuse with the plasma membrane of the cell     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ releasing proteins in the process of exocytosis    [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Exocytosis is the process by which **vesicles** (fluid-filled membrane sacs) within cells move towards the **plasma membrane** of the cell and **fuse** with it ($\\checkmark$), which allows them to **release their contents** ($\\checkmark$). This process can be linked to translation, as polypeptides are **translated** by ribosomes on the **RER** ($\\checkmark$), and then transferred in **vesicles** to the **Golgi** apparatus where they are **processed**  ($\\checkmark$) and then are transferred via vesicles to where they are needed outside the cell, via **exocytosis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a substitution mutation **OR** the incorrect RNA nucleotide is incorporated into the growing mRNA chain            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ an extra RNA nucleotide **OR** base is incorporated **OR** inserted **OR** removed **OR** deleted from the growing mRNA chain    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ may result in incorrect splicing of pre-mRNA     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ may result in translation **OR** production of a longer **OR** shorter polypeptide chain    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sequence of amino acids in the polypeptide chain can only be altered if the mutation occurs in the coding region of the gene **OR** exon    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic code dictates the correspondence between codons and specific amino acids [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ may result in the wrong amino acid being incorporated into the polypeptide chain **OR** wrong primary structure during translation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ leads to incorrect folding of the tertiary structure of the polypeptide chain        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as the incorrect amino acid may have an R-group that interacts differently with the rest of the chain            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the incorrect polypeptide chain is incorporated into the tertiary **OR** quaternary structure of the globular enzyme structure        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ results in an altered active site shape **OR** altered allosteric sites **OR** loss of active site [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ therefore substrate is unable to bind **OR** loss of catalytic activity        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ results in potential loss of stability in enzyme structure **OR** enzyme is more easily affected by changes in the environment **OR** enzyme denatures more easily        [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Transcription is the process by which an RNA copy of DNA is made. Errors in transcription can result in the **incorrect RNA** ($\\checkmark$) nucleotide, or an **extra RNA** nucleotide being incorporated into the growing mRNA chain ($\\checkmark$). This may result in **incorrect splicing** of the **pre-mRNA** molecule ($\\checkmark$) before it leaves the nucleus, which can result in the translation of a **truncated polypeptide** chain ($\\checkmark$) or the **wrong amino acid** being incorporated into the polypeptide chain ($\\checkmark$). This can result in the incorrect polypeptide chain being incorporated into the **tertiary structure** of the **globular** enzyme structure ($\\checkmark$) , which may result in an **altered active site** ($\\checkmark$), thereby **preventing** a **substrate** from successfully binding ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1322","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1322\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Distinguish between the structure and location of chromosomes found in eukaryotes and prokaryotes.            [5 marks]\n\n2. Describe the stages of meiosis and how genetically unique, haploid gametes are created during the process.    [8 marks]\n\n3. Explain the information that can be deduced from a human karyogram.        [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ eukaryotic chromosomes are found in the nucleus **WHEREAS** prokaryotic chromosomes are found in the nucleoid region **OR** chromosomes of eukaryotes are contained within a membrane **WHEREAS** prokaryotic chromosomes are not membrane bound     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotes have a circular chromosome **WHEREAS** eukaryotes have linear chromosomes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotes typically have one chromosome **WHEREAS** eukaryotes typically have multiple chromosomes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in eukaryotic chromosomes, DNA is associated with histone proteins **WHEREAS** prokaryotic chromosomes do not contain histones        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic chromosomes do not contain centromeres **WHEREAS** eukaryotic chromosomes do [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic chromosomes do not contain telomeres **WHEREAS** eukaryotic chromosomes do     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ eukaryotic chromosomes carry more genetic information **OR** DNA **OR** genes **THAN** prokaryotic chromosomes    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Eukaryotic chromosomes are found in the **nucleus** whereas prokaryotic chromosomes are found in the **nucleoid** region ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotes typically contain **one, circular** chromosome ($\\checkmark$)  whereas eukaryotes  have **multiple, linear** chromosomes ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotic chromosomes **do not** contain **centromeres** and **telomeres** ($\\checkmark$), whereas eukaryotic chromosomes do ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ all the DNA in a cell is replicated during interphase        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ so cells are diploid **OR** contain homologous chromosomes when they enter meiosis    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous chromosomes pair **AND** become visible **OR** condense  in prophase I    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ crossing over occurs between the non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes during prophase I    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous chromosomes are moved to the equator of the cell by spindle microtubules **AND** randomly orientated OR independently assorted in metaphase I [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous chromosomes are pulled to opposite sides of the cell by spindle microtubules in anaphase I            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ one diploid cell is split into two haploid cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chromatids condense **OR** become visible during prophase II        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pairs of sister chromatids are moved to the equator of the cell by spindle microtubules during metaphase II **AND** randomly orientated **OR** independently assorted    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pairs of sister chromatids are separated by spindle fibres in anaphase II        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nuclear membrane reforms in telophase II **AND** four haploid cells are formed from the two haploid cells of     meiosis         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ crossing over during prophase I **AND** independent assortment **OR** random alignment of homologous chromosomes in metaphase I **OR** metaphase II result in unique gametes    [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    All of the DNA in a cell is **replicated** during **interphase** ($\\checkmark$), so cells are **diploid** when they enter meiosis ($\\checkmark$). **Homologous** chromosomes **pair up**, become **visible** ($\\checkmark$) and **crossing over** of non-sister chromatids occur during **prophase I** ($\\checkmark$). Then, homologous chromosomes are moved to the **equator** of the cell by **spindle microtubules** and **randomly orientated** during **metaphase I** ($\\checkmark$). Homologous chromosomes are pulled to the **opposite poles** of the cell by spindle fibres in **anaphase I** ($\\checkmark$) and one **diploid cell** is split into **two haploid** cells during telophase I ($\\checkmark$). The cells then enter meiosis II. The **pairs** of **sister chromatids** are **independently assorted** to form a single line along the equator of the cell, in **metaphase II** ($\\checkmark$). During **anaphase II** the centromeres split and the **sister chromatids** are pulled to **opposite poles** of the cell ($\\checkmark$). In **telophase II** the **nuclear membrane reforms** around the chromosomes, and after cytokinesis, **4 new haploid, non-identical**,  daughter cells are formed from the **2 haploid cells** that resulted from **meiosis I** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a karyogram is an image showing the positions of centromeres and banding patterns of stained **OR** dyed homologous chromosomes ordered by decreasing length        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ biological sex determined by XX and XY **OR** sex chromosomes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ whether non-disjunction has occurred is determined by the presence or absence of chromosome (s)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ presence of chromosomal abnormalities (such as breakages or translocation) determined by the banding pattern **OR** size of chromosome        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ indication of chromosome disorders such as Down syndrome, which is caused by trisomy 21[1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    A karyogram is an image showing the positions of **centromeres** and **banding patterns** of stained **homologous chromosomes** ordered by decreasing length ($\\checkmark$). A karyogram can be used to deduce **biological sex** ($\\checkmark$) or the presence of **chromosomal abnormalities** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1324","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1324\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nPopulations within a community are interdependent due to the many interactions among organisms.\n\n1. Sketch a sigmoid population growth curve. Annotate each phase with the reason for the change in growth. [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline how the data needed to estimate population size using the Lincoln index are collected. [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain competition and cooperation using examples. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. the following:\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ axes suitably labelled **AND** sigmoid curve drawn [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ exponential phase: rapid growth because resources are plentiful **OR** little competition / predation / disease [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ transitional phase: growth rate slows as resources become limiting **OR** competition / predation / disease increase [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ plateau phase: population is stable because birth rate = death rate / birth + immigration = death + emigration **OR** carrying capacity / K is reached **OR** maximum population the environment can support [1 mark]\n\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1324ams.png\" alt=\"BI1324ams\">\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ first sample of the population is captured **AND** counted [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ captured individuals are marked in a way that does not harm them / affect their behaviour / make them more visible to predators [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ marked individuals are released at the site where they were captured [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ time is allowed for marked individuals to mix randomly back into the population [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ second sample is captured from the same area / using the same method **AND** the total number counted [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ number of marked individuals in the second sample is counted [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ large samples are captured so that the estimate is more representative / less affected by chance **OR** procedure is repeated / several recaptures are carried out **AND** a mean estimate is calculated to improve reliability [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ one assumption stated **OR** no immigration / emigration / births / deaths between samples **OR** marks are not lost **OR** marked and unmarked individuals are equally likely to be caught [1 mark]\n\n [5 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n A **first sample** is **captured** and the number of individuals is **counted** ($\\checkmark$). Each individual is **marked** in a way that **does not harm** it or make it **more visible** to predators, and then **released** ($\\checkmark$). Time is allowed for the **marked individuals** to **mix back** into the population ($\\checkmark$). A **second sample** is then **captured**, and the **total number** is **counted**, along with the **number** of **marked individuals** ($\\checkmark$). To improve **reliability**, the process is **repeated** two more times, and the **mean is calculated** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** statement and **one** example for **both** competition **and** cooperation to gain full marks*)\n:\n\n ***Competition:*** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms compete for a limited resource such as food / space / light / water / mates [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ competition is a cost to one or both organisms because a resource used by one is not available to the other **OR** because it reduces survival / reproduction [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ intraspecific competition is between members of the same species **OR** interspecific competition is between members of different species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ named example of interspecific competition with the resource stated, e.g. grey squirrels compete with red squirrels for food and are more successful, so red squirrel populations decline **OR** any other named example [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ named example of intraspecific competition with the resource stated, e.g. plants of the same species competing for light **OR** male red deer competing for mates [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ competition limits population size **OR** acts as a density-dependent factor as the population nears carrying capacity [1 mark]\n\n ***Cooperation:*** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cooperation is when organisms work together so that they benefit [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cooperation increases the chance of survival **OR** reproduction [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ named example of intraspecific cooperation, e.g. wolves hunting in packs to catch larger prey **OR** worker bees caring for the colony’s young [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mutualism is an interspecific relationship where two species live in close association **AND** both benefit [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ named example of mutualism with the benefit to both partners stated, e.g. *zooxanthellae* provide hard corals with products of photosynthesis and the coral provides a protected habitat **OR** *Rhizobium* in the root nodules of legumes fixes nitrogen and receives sugars **OR** mycorrhizal fungi supply minerals to orchids and receive sugars [1 mark]\n\n [7 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Competition happens when organisms need the same **limited resource**, such as **food, space or mates** ($\\checkmark$). It is a **cost** to one or both organisms, because resources used by one are not available to the other, which reduces survival or reproduction ($\\checkmark$). Competition can be **intraspecific**, between members of the **same species**, or **interspecific**, between members of **different species** ($\\checkmark$). For example, grey squirrels **compete with red squirrels for food**. Grey squirrels are more successful, so red squirrel populations have declined where grey squirrels have been introduced ($\\checkmark$).\n\n Cooperation is when organisms **work together** so that they **benefit** ($\\checkmark$). Mutualism is when organisms of **two species** live in **close association** and **both benefit** ($\\checkmark$). For example, ***zooxanthellae*** live inside **hard corals** and provide them with **nutrients** made by photosynthesis, while the coral provides the *zooxanthellae* with a **protected habitat** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1335","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1335\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Describe how the structure of water molecules gives water its solvent properties. [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline the importance of isotonic solutions in medical applications. [5 marks]\n\n3. A turgid plant cell is placed in a solution with a higher solute concentration than its cell contents.\n Explain the changes in the solute potential, pressure potential and water potential of the plant cell.\n\n*(Assume constant temperature, no movement of solutes across the cell membrane, and atmospheric pressure in the surrounding solution.)* [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ electrons are shared unequally in the covalent bonds between oxygen and hydrogen [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ water molecules are polar **OR** oxygen has a partial negative charge **AND** hydrogen has a partial positive charge [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ positively charged ions are attracted to the oxygen atoms of water molecules [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ negatively charged ions are attracted to the hydrogen atoms of water molecules [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen bonds form between water molecules and polar solute molecules [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ water molecules surround solute particles, allowing them to disperse through the water [1 mark]\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\nWater molecules are **polar**, with a **partial negative charge** on **oxygen** and **partial positive charges** on **hydrogen** ($\\checkmark$). **Positively charged ions** are **attracted** to the **oxygen atoms** of water molecules ($\\checkmark$), whereas **negatively charged ions** are **attracted** to the **hydrogen atoms** ($\\checkmark$). Water molecules surround **solute molecules**, allowing them to **disperse** through aqueous solutions ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ isotonic solutions have the same effective solute concentration as the cell contents [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ no net movement of water into or out of cells [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ used in intravenous fluids [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ used to bathe organs before transplantation [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ animal cells lack a cell wall to prevent bursting [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ prevent cells from swelling and bursting/lysis [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ prevent cells from shrinking/crenation [1 mark]\n\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n**Isotonic** solutions have the **same solute concentration** as the **cells** ($\\checkmark$) and so are used in **intravenous fluids** ($\\checkmark$) and to **bathe organs** before **transplantation** ($\\checkmark$). They **prevent cells** from **swelling** and **bursting** ($\\checkmark$) or **shrinking** and becoming **crenated** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** statement about the change in each of: solute potential, pressure potential **AND** water potential to gain full marks*):\n\n***Water movement***\\\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ the surrounding solution initially has a lower water potential than the cell contents [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ water moves out of the cell by osmosis [1 mark]\n\n***Solute potential***\\\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ the solute concentration within the cell increases [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ solute potential decreases **OR** becomes more negative [1 mark]\n\n***Pressure potential***\\\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ the cell contents exert less pressure against the cell wall **OR** the cell wall exerts less pressure on the cell contents [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ pressure potential decreases [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ the vacuole/cytoplasm shrinks **OR** the volume of the protoplast decreases [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ pressure potential may fall to zero **OR** the cell becomes flaccid/plasmolysed (if the solution is sufficiently concentrated) [1 mark]\n\n***Water potential and equilibrium***\\\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ water potential is equal to solute potential plus pressure potential **OR** $\\psi_w = \\psi_s + \\psi_p$ [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ the water potential of the cell decreases [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ net movement continues until the water potential of the cell equals that of the surrounding solution [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ at equilibrium, water continues to move in both directions at equal rates [1 mark]\n\n[7 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\nWater moves **out** of the cell by **osmosis** ($\\checkmark$). This **increases** the **solute concentration within** the **cell** ($\\checkmark$), making its **solute potential more negative** ($\\checkmark$).\n\nAs **water** is **lost from** the **vacuole**, **pressure potential decreases** ($\\checkmark$). As $\\psi_w = \\psi_s + \\psi_p$, the decreases in solute potential and pressure potential **lower the water potential** of the cell ($\\checkmark$).\n\n**Net water movement continues until** the **water potential** of the **cell equals** that of the surrounding **solution** ($\\checkmark$). Once **equilibrium** has been reached, **net movement** of water in **each direction** is **equal** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1355","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1355\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast type I and type II pneumocytes.        [4 marks]\n\n2. Explain how human gametes are adapted for their reproductive roles.        [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain the properties and location of stem cells and distinguish between totipotent, pluripotent and multipotent stem cells.    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following: \\\nAnswer must include at least one similarity **and** one difference to receive full marks.\n\n    ***Similarities:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type I **AND** type II pneumocytes are **BOTH** found in the alveoli **OR** the lungs **OR** form part of the alveolar epithelium    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type I **AND** type II pneumocytes are **BOTH** adapted for their functions        [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type I pneumocytes have a flattened shape **WHEREAS** type II pneumocytes have a cuboidal **OR** rounded shape            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type I pneumocytes have minimal cytoplasm **OR** organelles **WHEREAS** type II pneumocytes have more cytoplasm **OR** organelles **OR** contain lamellar bodies\ntype I pneumocytes are adapted for diffusion of gases **WHEREAS** type II pneumocytes are not adapted for diffusion    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type II pneumocytes produce surfactants **WHEREAS** type I pneumocytes do not produce surfactants        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type I pneumocytes are approximately 95% of the surface of alveoli **WHEREAS** type II pneumocytes are approximately 5% of the surface of alveoli **OR** type I pneumocytes are the majority of the alveolar lining **WHEREAS** type II pneumocytes are only a small percentage of the alveolar lining            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ type I pneumocytes have limited regenerative capacity **OR** are more vulnerable to damage **WHEREAS** type II pneumocytes can regenerate **OR** can differentiate into type I pneumocytes [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Type I and type II pneumocytes are **both found in the alveoli** ($\\checkmark$). Type **I pneumocytes are thin**, whereas **type II pneumocytes are cuboidal** ($\\checkmark$). This is because **type I** pneumocytes are adapted for **gaseous exchange** ($\\checkmark$), whereas **type II** pneumocytes are adapted to **produce surfactants** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** point for each cell type to gain full marks*):\n\n    ***Sperm cells:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ flagella **OR** tail for movement **OR** sperm cells can swim        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ acrosome containing enzymes for digesting the jelly coat of the egg **OR** for penetrating the egg                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large numbers of mitochondria for release of energy             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ haploid nucleus **OR** half the normal amount of genetic information for fertilisation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ small cells so can be produced in large quantities               [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ streamlined shape to reduce drag **OR** friction **OR** to make it easier to swim to egg    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ little cytoplasm **OR** few organelles OR no Golgi apparatus **OR** no ribosomes to reduce size **OR** weight to make it easier  for them to swim **OR** move        [1 mark]\n\n    ***Egg cells*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large as contain organelles **OR** resources **OR** nutrients  for early development of the zygote **OR** egg cells contain resources for early development of the embryo    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contain enzymes **OR** cortical granules which release enzymes to change the outer layers after fertilisation to prevent polyspermy                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ zona pellucida for sperm-egg recognition **OR** to prevent polyspermy    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ usually only one develops at a time to reduce risk of multiple fertilisations **OR** for more efficient use of resources                     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ haploid nucleus **OR** half the normal amount of genetic information for fertilisation    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Sperm cells have a **flagellum for movement** ($\\checkmark$), **enzymes in the acrosome** to digest the outer layers of the egg ($\\checkmark$) and **large numbers of mitochondria** in the midpiece for energy release for swimming ($\\checkmark$). Egg cells have **a large cytoplasm with the organelles** and **resources** for the early development of the zygote ($\\checkmark$).  **Both** sperm and egg cells have a **haploid nucleus** so that the normal **diploid number** of chromosomes is **restored at fertilisation** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following (*answer must include at least **two** points about properties and location of stem cells and at least **two** differences between totipotent, pluripotent and multipotent stem cells to gain full marks*):\n\n    ***Properties and locations of stem cells:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stem cells have the capacity to keep dividing            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stem cells have the ability to differentiate **OR** can differentiate into different types of cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stem cells are found in specific locations such as embryos **OR** umbilical cord **OR** stem cell niches [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adult stem cells are found in hair follicles **OR** bone marrow **OR** brain **OR** any relevant example of a location of a stem cell niche             [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences between totipotent, pluripotent and multipotent stem cells:*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ totipotent and pluripotent stem cells are found in embryos **BUT** multipotent stem cells are found in adult tissues                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ totipotent stem cells are found in early-stage embryos **OR** totipotent stem cells can develop into both embryonic and extraembryonic cells  **WHEREAS** pluripotent cells are found in embryos at the blastocyst stage **OR** pluripotent cells can only differentiate into embryonic cells                     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ totipotent stem cells are stem cells with the greatest potential for differentiation **WHEREAS** pluripotent stem cells can differentiate into most types of cells OR totipotent stem cells can differentiate into any type of cell **WHEREAS** multipotent stem cells have the least capacity for differentiation              [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ multipotent stem cells can only develop into cells for the tissue where they are found **WHEREAS** totipotent and pluripotent stem cells can develop into cells for different tissues                            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ totipotent stem cells develop into pluripotent stem cells **BUT** multipotent stem cells do not develop into other types of stem cells    [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Stem cells have the capacity to **differentiate** ($\\checkmark$) and the **ability to continue dividing** ($\\checkmark$). Stem cells are found in **embryos and stem cell niches in adult tissue** ($\\checkmark$). Adult stem cells are found in stem cell niches such as **hair follicles and bone marrow** ($\\checkmark$). Totipotent and pluripotent stem cells are **found in embryos** but **multipotent** stem cells are found in **adult tissues** ($\\checkmark$). **Totipotent** cells are found in early-stage embryos and can develop into both **embryonic** and **extraembryonic cells**, but **pluripotent** cells are found in embryos at the **blastocyst stage** and can only differentiate into **embryonic** cells ($\\checkmark$).  **Pluripotent** and **totipotent** can develop into cells for **different tissues** but **multipotent** cells can only develop into **cells for the tissues where they are found** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1361","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1361\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1.  Explain why Okazaki fragments are formed during DNA replication. [4 marks]\n\n2.  Compare and contrast DNA replication on the leading and lagging strands. [7 marks]\n\n3.  Outline the functions of the enzymes involved in prokaryotic DNA replication. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA polymerase can only work in the 5'-3' direction **OR** can only add nucleotides to the 3' end of the growing DNA strand            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA is made up of two complementary strands running in opposite directions **OR** antiparallel strands            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the lagging strand runs in the 5'-3' direction away from the replication fork **OR** DNA polymerase must move in the 5' to 3' direction away from the replication fork **OR** in the opposite direction to helicase            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ this results in discontinuous replication **OR** as the fork moves forward, DNA polymerase must fall off **AND** reattach on the newly exposed DNA strand            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ primase **OR** DNA primase creates many RNA primers on the lagging strand, so many small DNA fragments **OR** Okazaki fragments are formed            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA ligase joins the individual fragments together **OR** catalyses formation of sugar-phosphate bonds between fragments to form continuous lagging strand            [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\n\nOkazaki fragments are formed during DNA replication because **DNA polymerase can only work** in the **5' to 3'** direction away **from the replication fork** ($\\checkmark$); however, the **lagging** strand runs in the **5' to 3'** direction **from the replication fork** ($\\checkmark$).**Primase** synthesises many **RNA primers** on the lagging strand($\\checkmark$) because as the fork moves forward, DNA polymerase must **fall off and reattach** to the newly exposed DNA strand. **DNA ligase** then **joins** the individual **fragments** together ($\\checkmark$). \n\n2. any **seven** of the following (*answer must include at least **two** similarities and **two** differences to gain full marks*):\n\n***Similarities***\\\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** use complementary base pairing [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** require primers **OR** RNA primers to initiate DNA replication    [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** require enzymes **OR** DNA polymerase [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ replication occurs in the 5' to 3' direction on BOTH strands [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ Both have proofreading **OR** repair mechanisms [1 mark]\n\n***Differences***\\\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$\n\n Okazaki fragments are formed on the lagging strand **BUT** not on the leading strand [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$\n\n template for leading strand runs in the 3'$\\ce{ -> }$ 5' direction **WHEREAS** template for lagging strand runs in 5'$\\ce{ -> }$ 3' direction [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$\n\n DNA replication is continuous on the leading strand **BUT** discontinuous on the lagging strand [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$\n\n the leading strand only requires one RNA primer to initiate replication, **BUT** the lagging strand requires many [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$\n\n DNA ligase is required on the lagging strand to stick many fragments together, **BUT** not on the leading strand [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$\n\n replication occurs in opposite directions **OR** towards the replication fork for the lagging strand **AND** away from the replication fork for the lagging strand [1 mark]\n\n [7 marks]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\\\n\nBoth the leading and lagging strands use **complementary base pairing** ($\\checkmark$), require **primers** for the initiation of replication ($\\checkmark$), require **enzymes** ($\\checkmark$) such as DNA polymerase and replication occurs in the **5' to 3' direction** on both strands($\\checkmark$). However, the **leading strand** only requires **one RNA primer**, whilst the **lagging strand** requires **many** ($\\checkmark$).  **Okazaki fragments** are formed on the **lagging strand** but not on the leading strand ($\\checkmark$), so DNA replication is **continuous on the leading strand** but **discontinuous** on the **lagging** strand ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **five** of the following:\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$\n\n helicase unwinds the DNA strand **OR** separates the strands **OR** breaks the hydrogen bonds between the strands [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$\n\n DNA gyrase **OR** gyrase prevents supercoiling of the DNA strands **OR** helps overcome the strains as helix unwinds [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$\n\n DNA primase **OR** primase synthesises RNA primers[1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$\n\n DNA polymerase III forms the DNA strand starting at the RNA primer **OR** adds complementary nucleotides to the 3' end of the growing strand [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$\n\n DNA polymerase I replaces the RNA primer at the start of each Okazaki fragment with DNA nucleotides [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$\n\n ligase joins the DNA fragments **OR** Okazaki fragments on the lagging strand together **OR** catalyses the formation of sugar-phosphate bonds between Okazaki fragments **OR** DNA fragments [1 mark]\n\n [5 marks]\n\n :u[Sample answer]:\\\n\n Several enzymes are involved in DNA replication, including **helicase**, which **unwinds the DNA** strand ($\\checkmark$) to allow other enzymes to access the DNA and carry out replication. DNA **gyrase prevents supercoiling** of the DNA strands ($\\checkmark$). **Primase** creates **RNA primers** ($\\checkmark$), which **DNA polymerase III**  binds to in order to **start replication** ($\\checkmark$) and **DNA polymerase I replaces the RNA primer** at the start of each Okazaki fragment with a DNA strand ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1363","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1363\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nA study was conducted on germinating soybeans (*Glycine max*)  to observe the effects of cold stress conditions (10°C) and osmotic stress conditions on the germination rate. Researchers were also interested in the effects of osmotic stress and cold stress conditions on the concentration of polysomes in the germinating soybeans. The control soybeans were grown at 25 °C, in sterilised water.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1363a.png\" alt=\"BI1363a\">\n[Source: Adapted from Swigonska, S., Badowiec, A., Mostek, A., Krol, A., & Weidner, S. (2013, December 6). *Formation and stability of polysomes and polysomal populations in roots of germinating seeds of soybean (glycine Max L.) under cold, osmotic and combined cold and osmotic stress conditions - Acta Physiologiae plantarum*. SpringerLink. Retrieved June 18, 2023, from  https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11738-013-1443-2 Copyright under CC BY]\n\n1. Identify the independent variable in this study.    [1 mark]\n\n2. Describe the effect of cold and osmotic stress on germination.        [2 marks]\n\nThe mean concentrations of polysomes in the soybean seeds were measured at various times during their germination, in the different stress conditions. The results are shown in the graph below. Polysomes are clusters of ribosomes which simultaneously translate a single mRNA molecule to synthesise multiple polypeptide chains.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1363.png\" alt=\"BI1363\">\n[Source: Adapted from Swigonska, S., Badowiec, A., Mostek, A., Krol, A., & Weidner, S. (2013, December 6). *Formation and stability of polysomes and polysomal populations in roots of germinating seeds of soybean (glycine Max L.) under cold, osmotic and combined cold and osmotic stress conditions - Acta Physiologiae plantarum*. SpringerLink. Retrieved June 18, 2023, from  https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11738-013-1443-2 Copyright under CC BY]\n\n3. State the stress condition that has the greatest effect on polysome formation compared to the controls.    [1 mark]\n\n4. Calculate the difference in polysome concentration between the control and the soybean seeds under osmotic stress at 24 hours.    [1 mark]\n\n5. Explain how an increase in polysome formation could affect germination.    [2 marks]\n\n6. Suggest a method that could be used to put the soybean seeds under osmotic stress.    [1 mark]\n\n7. Explain why it is important that the soybeans under osmotic stress are grown at 25°C.        [1 mark]\n\n8. Predict with a reason what would happen to the average concentration of polysomes in soybeans if they were put under heat stress conditions of 50°C.        [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ the stress conditions that the soybeans are subject to     [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The independent variable is the **stress conditions** ($\\checkmark$) that the soybeans are subject to.\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ both cold stress **AND** osmotic stress caused the root to lengthen more slowly        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at 72 hours, both cold **AND** osmotic stress resulted in a similar root length        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ overall osmotic stress decreased root length more than cold stress **OR** osmotic stress decreased root length more than cold stress except at 72 hours    [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nBoth **cold and osmotic** stress **decreased lengthening** of the root ($\\checkmark$) compared to the control. However, overall **osmotic stress decreased root length more** than cold stress ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ cold stress    [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ **Cold stress** ($\\checkmark$) has the greatest effect on polysome formation compared to the control.\n\n4. Units **MUST** be included to gain the mark\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 1.7 mg g:sup[-1] OR 1.7 mg per g    [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1363b.png\" alt=\"BI1363b\">\n[Source: Adapted from Swigonska, S., Badowiec, A., Mostek, A., Krol, A., & Weidner, S. (2013, December 6). *Formation and stability of polysomes and polysomal populations in roots of germinating seeds of soybean (glycine Max L.) under cold, osmotic and combined cold and osmotic stress conditions - Acta Physiologiae plantarum*. SpringerLink. Retrieved June 18, 2023, from  https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11738-013-1443-2 Copyright under CC BY]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 4.6 mg g:sup[-1] - 2.9 mg g:sup[-1] = 1.7 mg g:sup[-1] ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increases in polysome concentration increase the rate that proteins are translated **OR** increases translation efficiency        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ proteins are needed to stimulate **OR** control germination        [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    An increase in polysome formation could affect germination because as the concentration of **polysomes increases** the rate **proteins** are **translated** also increases ($\\checkmark$).  Proteins are likely needed to **stimulate germination** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ place soybeans in a solvent with a much higher solute concentration than the internal concentration of the soybeans **OR** in a strong salt solution **OR** in strong saline solution    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ place soybeans in less water than the control soybeans, in an environment with low humidity    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ place soybeans in a highly concentrated glucose solution    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    One method that could be used to place the soybeans under osmotic stress would be to place the soybeans in a **solvent with a much higher solute concentration** than the **internal** concentration of the soybeans ($\\checkmark$).\n\n7. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ so that any differences that are observed are only due to osmotic stress [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ helps to minimise the impact of confounding variables    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to allow for a fair comparison with the control        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    It is important that the soybeans under osmotic stress are kept at 25°C so that any **differences** that are observed are **only due to osmotic stress** ($\\checkmark$) and not temperature.\n\n8. Any **one** of the following *(answer must include a correct statement and a reason to gain the mark)*:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ concentration of polysomes would decrease **BECAUSE** ribosomes contain proteins **OR** RNA  which would be denatured        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ concentration of polysomes would decrease **BECAUSE** of denaturation of enzymes involved in translation, which therefore reduces the production of ribosomes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ concentration of polysomes would decrease **BECAUSE** there will be reduced mRNA stability resulting in less mRNA templates for protein synthesis, so formation of polysomes may be limited    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The average concentration of polysomes would **decrease because** ribosomes are partly made up of **protein**, which would **denature** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1372","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1372\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Describe centrifugation and how developments in this technique allowed scientists to improve their understanding of the components of cells.  [4 marks]\n\n2. Distinguish between transcription and translation in prokaryotic and eukaryotic organisms.    [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain the advantages of cell compartmentalisation in eukaryotes.  [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ centrifugation allows the separation of molecular components based on differences in their relative densities **OR** sizes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a centrifuge **OR** ultracentrifuge is a device that spins at high speeds to separate substances in a suspension using centrifugal force    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows the separation of individual organelles            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell contents are homogenised **OR** broken up **OR** plasma membrane is broken to release organelles into the solution                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ substances that are less dense stay at the top **OR** form the supernatant, **WHILE** substances that are more dense settle at the bottom **OR** form the pellet    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ using cell fractionation, a multi-step process that involves successively higher speeds of centrifugation to collect progressively smaller cell components **OR** denser cell components can be separated at lower speeds    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows biologists to study **OR** determine the roles of individual organelles **OR** molecules **OR** specific chemical pathways in a controlled environment without the presence of confounding substances **OR** separate from the other reactions that occur in the cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ used in the famous experiment by Meselson and Stahl to discover the semi-conservative nature of DNA replication **OR** discovery of translation of mRNA **OR** any other example of an experiment or discovery involving centrifugation            [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Centrifugation** is a process used to **separate cell parts based on their relative densities** ($\\checkmark$). An **ultracentrifuge** is a device that **rotates at high speeds** to **separate cell components** using **centrifugal force** ($\\checkmark$). This allows scientists to **isolate** individual **organelles for experimentation** ($\\checkmark$). **Dense organelles settle to the bottom** of the sample following centrifugation, while **less dense organelles stay at the top** ($\\checkmark$). By **successively separating** the **supernatant** and **pellets** formed during centrifugation, many organelles can be isolated in a process known as **cell fractionation** ($\\checkmark$). These techniques have allowed scientists to make many **discoveries** regarding the nature of various organelles and cell processes, such as the **semi-conservative nature of DNA** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in eukaryotes, the genetic material **OR** DNA is separated from the cytoplasm by the nuclear membrane **OR** envelope **WHEREAS**, in prokaryotes, the genetic material is found in the cytoplasm [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in eukaryotes, transcription takes place in the nucleus, and translation occurs in the cytoplasm **WHEREAS** in prokaryotes, both processes take place in the cytoplasm    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transcription and translation are discontinuous **OR** completed separately in eukaryotes **WHEREAS** transcription and translation are continuous **OR** completed simultaneously in prokaryotes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ post-transcriptional modification occurs in eukaryotes **WHEREAS** no post-transcriptional modification **OR** minimal RNA modification occurs in prokaryotes     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ eukaryotic genes contain non-coding segments called introns **WHILE** prokaryotic genes do not **OR** eukaryotes have introns and exons **WHILE** prokaryotes do not [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in eukaryotes, introns are removed to produce mature mRNA **OR** RNA splicing occurs  **WHEREAS**, in prokaryotes, this process does not occur         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ movement of mRNA through nuclear pores into cytoplasm is controlled in eukaryotes, which helps regulate gene expression  **WHEREAS**, in prokaryotes, this form of gene expression regulation cannot happen    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    In **eukaryotic** organisms, the **DNA** is **separated from** the **cytoplasm** by the **nuclear membrane**, whereas the genetic material of **prokaryotes** is **within the cytoplasm** ($\\checkmark$).  In eukaryotes, **transcription** occurs **within the nucleus**, while **translation** occurs **in the cytoplasm**. In prokaryotes, however, **both processes** occur in the **cytoplasm** as these organisms do not have a nucleus ($\\checkmark$). Due to this lack of separation, both **transcription and translation happen simultaneously** in prokaryotes, while in eukaryotes, **transcription** must be **completed first** before **translation** can begin ($\\checkmark$). This allows **post-transcriptional modification** to take place in eukaryotes ($\\checkmark$), whereby the **immature mRNA is edited** through the process of **RNA splicing**, which **removes introns** ($\\checkmark$), sections of a gene that **do not code for proteins**. This **post-transcriptional modification** does not occur in **prokaryotes** as they **do not have introns** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows organelles to have specialised functions                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ such as the enzymatic digestion of substances in lysosomes **OR** the ingestion of foreign particles into phagocytic vacuoles **OR** any other named example of a compartmentalised organelle and its specialised function                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows metabolic processes to be isolated from each other        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ improves efficiency **OR** prevents incompatible reactions from occurring in the same part of the cell **OR** allows different cellular processes to occur simultaneously without interfering with each other **OR** ensures that ATP production is focused and efficient, preventing wasteful reactions in other parts of the cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows the maintenance of concentration gradients **OR** internal conditions that differ from that of the cytoplasm                        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ by controlling the distribution of substances such as enzymes and metabolites **OR** the products of metabolism **OR** nutrients    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ provides increased surface area for reactions                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ such as those that occur in the inner membrane of the mitochondria **OR** electron transport chain **OR** chemiosmosis **OR** other named example of a process within the cell that requires a high surface area    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows nutrients **OR** wastes to be stored separately from other cell components    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ such as starch **OR** cell sap in the vacuoles of plant cells             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows cellular communication **OR** allows precise control of cellular responses    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ using signaling molecules that are released from one compartment and target specific receptors in another            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ protects cellular components from potentially damaging substances **OR** isolates toxic substances, reducing cytotoxicity **OR** risk to the rest of the cell            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ provides an additional layer of protection for important cell structures        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ such as the DNA within the nucleus **OR** any other named example        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows transport **OR** sorting **OR** modification of molecules        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ including the transport of substances in vesicles from the rough endoplasmic reticulum to the Golgi apparatus                            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows the ability to respond to external stimuli using modification of the number **OR** structure of specific organelles **OR** cell compartments    [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Cell **compartmentalisation** uses **membranes** to separate cell components into **specialised organelles** ($\\checkmark$). This allows each organelle to take on unique roles, such as **absorbing foreign particles**, as seen in **phagocytic vacuoles** ($\\checkmark$). Various **cell processes** can be **completed separately** ($\\checkmark$), making them more **efficient** ($\\checkmark$). Compartmentalisation also allows the cell to **control** the production and **distribution** of substances such as **enzymes** ($\\checkmark$), directing them to areas where they are needed. Furthermore, compartmentalisation provides organelles such as the **mitochondria** with vastly **increased surface area** ($\\checkmark$) so that **metabolic reactions** can occur, allowing it to produce much more ATP than would otherwise be possible ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1418","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1418\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nGenetic variation is the root of phenotypic diversity between and within species.\n\n1. Phenotypic expression relies on the genome and proteome. Outline the differences between the genome and the proteome    [4 marks]\n\n2. While genetic variations can be beneficial, others can lead to detrimental health conditions, such as diabetes. Compare and contrast Type I and Type II diabetes.    [4 marks]\n\n3. Describe the approaches used to classify different species.    [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genome is the complete set of genetic material present in an organism **OR** includes all the genes, regulatory elements and non-coding regions of the genetic material of an organism **WHEREAS** proteome is the entire set of proteins produced **OR** expressed by a cell, tissue, or organism at a specific time under defined conditions           [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genome composed of DNA nucleotides **WHEREAS**  proteome composed of amino acids [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genome stores genetic information **OR** is a template for the synthesis of proteome **WHEREAS**  proteome carries out the functions of the organism **OR** is involved in cellular processes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genome relatively stable over time **WHEREAS** proteome is highly dynamic **OR** proteome constantly changing in response to cellular needs **OR** environment **OR** internal signals [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ size of genome is relatively constant within a species **WHEREAS** proteome can be much larger **OR** more diverse than the genome due to alternative splicing, post-translational modifications, and protein-protein interactions    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **genome** is the **complete set of genetic material** present in an organism, including **all** the **genes, regulatory elements** and **non-coding regions**, whereas the **proteome** is the **entire set** of **proteins** produced by a **cell, tissue,** or **organism** at a specific time under defined conditions ($\\checkmark$). The **genome** is made up of **nucleotides**, whereas the **proteome** is composed of **amino acids** ($\\checkmark$). Functionally, the **genome** acts as a source of genetic **instructions**, providing the information necessary for development and regulation, while the **proteome** actively **participates** in **cellular processes** ($\\checkmark$) based on these instructions. The **genome** is relatively **stable** with occasional genetic variations, whereas the **proteome** is highly **dynamic**, responding to environmental factors ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **four** of the following (**AT LEAST ONE** similarity and **AT LEAST ONE** difference must be given):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** Types of diabetes affect blood glucose concentration **OR** affect the body’s ability to control blood glucose concentration        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** types of diabetes cause health complications if blood glucose concentration is not controlled                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Type I is an autoimmune disease **WHEREAS** Type II is linked to genetics **OR** lifestyle **OR** ageing [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Type I diabetes is caused when the beta cells are destroyed by the immune system and do not secrete enough insulin **WHEREAS** Type II diabetes is caused when body cells become insensitive **OR** resistant to insulin    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Type I diabetes usually begins in childhood **WHEREAS** Type II diabetes usually begins in adulthood [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Type I diabetes cannot be controlled by lifestyle changes **WHEREAS** Type II diabetes can often be controlled by lifestyle changes            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Type I diabetes requires frequent measurement of blood glucose concentration **WHEREAS** Type II diabetes does not require frequent measurement of blood glucose concentration                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Type I diabetes requires insulin injections from start **WHEREAS** Type II diabetes is only treated with insulin if the disease progresses        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Type I tends to be diagnosed at early age **WHEREAS** Type II tends to be diagnosed in older people                                [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Both** Type I and Type II diabetes **disrupt** the **regulation** of **normal blood glucose concentration** ($\\checkmark$). **Type I diabetes** is an **autoimmune disease** where the **immune system destroys beta cells in the pancreas**, resulting in a **lack of insulin** production. The causes of **Type II diabetes** are **multifactorial**, including **genetics, lifestyle** and **ageing** ($\\checkmark$), and it manifests when body **cells** become **resistant to insulin** ($\\checkmark$). **Type I diabetes** requires **frequent measurement of blood glucose** concentration, whereas **Type II diabetes** does **not usually require** frequent measurements of blood glucose concentration ($\\checkmark$). **Type I** diabetes requires lifelong **insulin injections**, whereas **Type II** diabetes only requires **insulin** injections if the **condition progresses** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ classification is used to organise organisms into groups             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ binomial nomenclature **OR** binomial system used to name organisms **OR** name is genus and species      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows communication and understanding between scientists all around the world        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there are three domains of life                     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a species is the smallest unit of classification    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hierarchy is used to group species **OR** the taxa are arranged in order of decreasing group size: domain, kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus, species        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ morphological **OR** artificial classification is based on physical characteristics        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ morphology can lead to errors **OR** analogous structures can mislead classification  [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ biochemical classification analyses molecular components **OR** proteins **OR** DNA **OR** RNA  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ base sequences in nucleic acids **OR** amino acid sequences of proteins are analysed     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cladistics is based on shared characteristics traced to most recent common ancestor **OR** cladogram is a visual representation of how species are evolutionarily related, with a node representing a common ancestor        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ modern approach combines evidence **OR** uses all types of classification **OR** uses morphology, biochemistry and genetics      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ natural classification groups organisms based on evolutionary history    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ classification includes fossils        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms with a recent common ancestor are classified more closely together    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ species in the same genus share many characteristics          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms can be reclassified when new evidence is found      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ natural classification allows scientists to predict characteristics of species within a group  [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Classification is the process of **organising organisms** into appropriate categories or **groups** based on their **shared characteristics** ($\\checkmark$). Scientists use **binomial nomenclature** to assign **each organism** a unique two-part name made up of the **genus** and **species** ($\\checkmark$), facilitating **global scientific communication** ($\\checkmark$). Organisms are traditionally classified **hierarchically**, starting with the broadest category, the **domain**, then working downwards -  **kingdom, phylum, class, order, family genus, species** ($\\checkmark$) (which can then be split into sub-species). **Traditional, artificial**, classification has been based on **physical traits. However**, this **morphological** approach ($\\checkmark$) can be **misleading** due to **analogous structures** ($\\checkmark$). **Modern** classification integrates **morphological, biochemica**l, and **genetic** information, ($\\checkmark$). **Biochemical** classification examines the **molecular components** of organisms, including the **sequences** of **bases** of  **DNA** and **amino acids** of **proteins** ($\\checkmark$). **Cladistics** is based on **shared characteristics** traced to the **most recent common ancestor**, and these relationships can be represented visually on **cladograms** ($\\checkmark$). As **new data emerges, reclassification** may occur ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1445","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1445\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Draw a labelled plan to show the distribution of different tissues in a transverse section of a dicotyledonous plant stem. [3 marks]\n\n2. Describe the movement of water in plants beginning with water in the soil and ending with water vapour in the atmosphere. [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain how different environmental factors affect the rate of transpiration. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **three** of the following:\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ correct positioning and labelling of xylem [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ correct positioning and labelling of phloem [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ correct positioning and labelling of cortex [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ correct positioning and labelling of epidermis [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ correct positioning and labelling of pith [1 mark]\n\n[3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/drawing%20of%20TS%20of%20stem%20of%20dicot.png\" alt=\"drawing of TS of stem of dicot\">\n\n2. Any **five** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ concentration of mineral ions in the roots is higher than in the surrounding soil [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ (leads to) movement of water into roots by osmosis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ water is pulled upwards in xylem vessels **OR** the stem against gravity from the roots to the leaves [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ (due to) adhesive and cohesive properties of water [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ transpiration pull moves water towards the leaves **OR** up [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ structure of xylem vessels **OR** long continuous narrow tubes of xylem make capillary action possible [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ water evaporates in the inner air space of leaves **OR** from the surface of mesophyll cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ water diffuses out of stomata [1 mark]\n\n [5 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nWater **evaporates** from the surface of mesophyll cells in the inner air space of leaves ($\\checkmark$) and then **diffuses** out of the **stomata** ($\\checkmark$). This **pulls water upwards** ($\\checkmark$)in the stem because of water's **adhesive** and **cohesive properties** ($\\checkmark$). Water enters the roots by **osmosis** ($\\checkmark$) because the **concentration** of mineral **ions** is **higher** in the roots than in the soil ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **seven** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ transpiration involves the loss of water through stomata [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ environmental factors that affect water loss through transpiration are temperature, humidity and wind **OR** air movement [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at higher temperatures, the rate of transpiration increases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** heat increases rate of evaporation in leaves **OR** rate water diffuses out of stomata **OR** the kinetic energy of the water molecules is greater [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at extremely high temperatures, the rate of transpiration may decrease [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** stomata will close to prevent further water loss [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at higher humidity, the rate of transpiration decreases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** when surrounding humidity is high, the rate water evaporates in leaves decreases **OR** the rate water vapour diffuses out of stomata decreases **OR** the concentration gradient between the inside of the leaf and the surrounding air is lower [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at higher light intensity, the rate of transpiration increases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** light intensity stimulates stomata to open allowing water to diffuse out [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increased wind speed increases the rate of transpiration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BECAUSE** wind carries the water vapour away from the stomata increasing the concentration gradient around the pores [1 mark]\n\n [7 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nTranspiration involves the loss of water from leaves through **stomata** ($\\checkmark$). The rate of transpiration is affected by environmental factors such as **temperature, humidity, and air movement** ($\\checkmark$). **Higher temperatures** cause the rate of transpiration to **increase** ($\\checkmark$) because **heat energy** absorbed by water in the inner air space of leaves causes water to **evaporate more rapidly** ($\\checkmark$). **Higher humidity** causes the rate of transpiration to **decrease** ($\\checkmark$). This is because air that is more humid carries more water particles in it which reduces the **concentration gradient** ($\\checkmark$) between the inside of the stomata and outside, thus reducing the rate water diffuses out of the stomata. Lastly, **increases in wind speed** can **increase** the rate of transpiration ($\\checkmark$). Wind carries away water vapour from the surface of leaves, which **increases the concentration gradient** ($\\checkmark$) of water between the inside and outside of the stomata.\n\n*The quality mark is awarded if each part of the question (a, b, c) is answered to earn at least half of the points available, answers do not require re-reading to be understood, and there are almost no errors and little irrelevant information.* [1 mark]"},{"ref":"BI1461","marks":"5","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1461\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe table shows the recommended daily intake of some essential amino acids for infants aged 4 months. The mean concentrations of the same amino acids in cows’ milk and human milk are also provided. The mean mass of protein laid down by the body is 3.1g day-1 for infants from age 4 months to 12 months.\n\n| Amino acid | Recommended $\\\\$ infant (4 months) $\\\\$ daily intake/mg g$^{-1}$ $\\\\$ body protein gain| Mean $\\\\$ concentration of $\\\\$ amino acids in $\\\\$ cow’s milk/mg $\\\\$ g$^{-1}$ milk protein| Mean $\\\\$ concentration of $\\\\$amino acids in $\\\\$ human milk/mg g$^{-1}$ $\\\\$ milk protein|\n\n| :-- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| Histidine | 16 | 27 | 26 |\n\n| Isoleucine | 40 | 47 | 46 |\n\n| Leucine | 93 | 95 | 93 |\n\n| Methionine | 33 | 33 | 42 |\n\n| Lysine | 60 | 78 | 66 |\n\n| Phenylalanine | 72 | 102 | 72 |\n\n| Threonine | 50 | 44 | 43 |\n\n| Tryptophan | 10 | 14 | 17 |\n\n| Valine | 54 | 64 | 55 |\n\n[Source Adapted from: National Research Council (US) Subcommittee on the Tenth Edition of the Recommended Dietary Allowances. (1989). Protein and amino acids. Recommended Dietary Allowances: 10th Edition. Retrieved March 21, 2024, from https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK234922/. Copyright free]\n\n1. State the definition of an essential amino acid. [1 mark]\n\n2. Using only the data in the table, explain why cow’s milk is used as a replacement for human milk in infant nutrition. [2 marks]\n\nTyrosine, a conditionally essential amino acid, can be formed from phenylalanine by the addition of a hydroxyl group.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/phenylalanine%20to%20tyrosine%20no%20enzyme%20label%20i.png\" alt=\"phenylalanine to tyrosine no enzyme label i\">\n\n3.\n\n 1. Suggest why tyrosine is called a conditionally essential amino acid. [1 mark]\n\n 2. State **one** role of protein in the human body. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ amino acid that cannot be synthesised in cells **OR** tissues **OR** systems [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ need to be consumed **OR** ingested in the diet [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n An essential amino acid **cannot** be **synthesised** by the **organism that requires it** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it has a similar composition **OR** concentration of essential amino acids to human milk [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cows’ milk meets all the same essential amino acid needs of infants that human milk does if more than 3.1g of milk protein is consumed **OR** if consumed in same volume per day [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ lower mass of cows’ milk protein than human milk protein needs to be consumed to reach infants' daily threonine requirements for daily body mass increase [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 7 out of 9 essential amino acids are present in higher concentrations in cows’ milk than in human milk **OR** that valine, phenylalanine, and lysine are significantly higher in cows’ milk than in human milk [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n **Cows’ milk** is a good substitute for **human milk** for infants as it has a **similar concentration** of essential amino acids ($\\checkmark$) and **7 of the 9 essential amino acids** shown are in a **higher concentration** in **cows’ milk** than in human milk($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n\n 1. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ if phenylalanine is not available tyrosine cannot be synthesised [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the synthesis of tyrosine is conditional on **OR** depends on the presence (in the diet) of phenylalanine [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n Under normal circumstances, tyrosine can be synthesised from the essential amino acid phenylalanine, however, if there is **not enough phenylalanine** available then **tyrosine** needs to be **obtained from** the diet ($\\checkmark$).\n\n 2. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ structural support **OR** collagen in connective tissue **OR** any other suitable example [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ enzymes **OR** catalyse reactions **OR** suitable example [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ transport **OR** suitable example [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ antibodies **OR** defence against disease **OR** protect body from infection **OR** suitable example [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hormones **OR** part of endocrine system **OR** regulate blood glucose **OR** any other suitable example [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ movement **OR** contraction of muscles **OR** actin **OR** myosin are contractile proteins **OR** any other suitable example [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cellular communication **OR** receptor proteins in cell membranes **OR** facilitate cellular response [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ storage essential nutrients **OR** ferritin stores iron **OR** any other suitable example [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ regulation of gene expression **OR** transcription factors [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n One role of protein in the body is as **hormones**, such as **insulin** that helps **regulate blood glucose concentration** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1464","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1464\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe micrograph shows cardiac muscle tissue.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1464%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1464 fig1\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Goyitrina. (2007, January 22). File:Musculocardiaco.jpg. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved March 23, 2024 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Musculocardiaco.jpg. Copyright by CC 3.0]\n\n1.  Compare and contrast the **structure** of cardiac muscle cells and skeletal muscle cells.    [4 marks]\n\n2.  Within the right atrium wall is a group of cardiac cells with the ability to initiate an action potential. State the name of those groups of cells.    [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.  Any **four** of the following\\\n\n    *Answer must include at least **one** point from similarities and **one** from differences to gain full marks*\n\n    **Similarities**\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are striated    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have a similar arrangement of myosin **AND** actin    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contain many mitochondria    [1 mark]\n\n    **Differences**\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cardiac muscles are shorter and wider **THAN** skeletal muscle cells **OR** skeletal muscle cells are longer and narrower **THAN** cardiac muscle cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cardiac muscles can have one nucleus **OR** two nuclei per cell **WHEREAS** skeletal muscle cells are multinucleated    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cardiac muscles are branched **WHEREAS** skeletal muscles are unbranched    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ s there are gap junctions **OR** intercalated discs between cardiac muscle cells **WHEREAS** there are no gap junctions **OR** intercalated discs between skeletal muscle cells [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Both skeletal and cardiac muscle cells are **striated** ($\\checkmark$)  and contain **many mitochondria** ($\\checkmark$). However, cardiac muscle cells are **branched**, while skeletal muscle cells are **linear** ($\\checkmark$), and cardiac muscle cells only have **one or two nuclei per cell**, whereas skeletal muscle cells are **multinucleated** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.  Any **one** of the following\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sinoatrial **OR** SA node [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pacemaker [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    \n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    The **sinoatrial node**($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1544","marks":"5","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1544\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nECGs are graphs that plot the electrical activity from the SA and AV nodes. The ECG below is from a person with atrial fibrillation, characterised by rapid, irregular beating of the atrial chambers of the heart.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1544eDIT.png\" alt=\"BI1544eDIT\">\n\n1. Calculate the mean heart rate of this person. [1 mark]\n\n2. Suggest, with a reason, how atrial fibrillation will affect the pumping of the blood from the ventricles. [1 mark]\n\n3. Describe the passage of the electrical impulse and resulting mechanical actions in the heart during the QRS complex of a normal ECG. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. $\\ce{ -> }$ 23 bpm **OR** 23 beats min$^{-1}$ (allow 23 - 30) [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1544AnsA.png\" alt=\"BI1544AnsA\">\n\n Time from end of the first QRS stage to end of last = 13s\n\n Number of beats in 13 seconds = 5\n\n Beats in one second = $(5 \\div 13) \\times 60$ = **23 beats min**$^{-1}$ ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ less blood is pumped from ventricles per beat **OR** decreased cardiac output **BECAUSE** incomplete filling of ventricles [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ inefficient and erratic ventricular contractions **BECAUSE** of irregular atrial signals [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ventricles have to contract more times per minute **OR** rate of ventricular systole increases **BECAUSE** lower volume of blood pumped out per beat [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Atrial fibrillation will **reduce** the **efficiency** of blood **pumping from** the **ventricles** because the **irregular** and **rapid beating** of the **atria** leads to **incomplete filling** of the ventricles ($\\checkmark$), resulting in **decreased cardiac output** ($\\checkmark$) and potentially insufficient blood supply to the body.\n\n3. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ electrical impulse passes through AV **OR** atrioventricular node [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ impulse passes down bundle of His **OR** down Purkinje fibres [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ventricles contract from apex upwards [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ventricular depolarisation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ventricular systole [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ blood pressure rises rapidly [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ atrioventricular valves shut **OR** semi-lunar valves open [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ atrial diastole **OR** atria begin to refill **OR** atrial repolarisation [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The QRS complex represents the **depolarisation** of the **ventricles** ($\\checkmark$), triggered as the **electrical impulse passes through** the **AV node** ($\\checkmark$). It travels down to the **apex** of the heart so the **ventricles** can **contract upwards** to push the blood out of the heart ($\\checkmark$). The phase corresponds to **ventricular systole** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1547","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1547\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA student carried out an experiment to measure the energy content of different foods. Below is a diagram of the apparatus used.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1547ai.png\" alt=\"BI1547ai\">\n\nAt the start of the experiment, the initial temperature of the water and the mass of the food sample were recorded. The food was then set alight and immediately placed under the boiling tube of water. Once the food had finished burning, the final temperature of the water was recorded.\n\nIt takes 4.2J of energy to raise the temperature of 1g (1mL) of water by 1:sup[o]C.\n\nThe increase in the temperature of 10mL of water from 0.5g of potato crisp was 35:sup[o]C.\n\n1. Using the information provided, calculate the energy of the potato snack in kJ g:sup[-1] [2 marks]\n\nPart of the nutrition details from the bag of potato snacks is shown below:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1547b.png\" alt=\"BI1547b\">\n\n2. Assuming the students' repeated trials yielded similar results to those you have calculated, suggest two inaccuracies in the experimental method that could explain the discrepancy between the measured energy content in the experiment and the value listed on the food label. [2 marks]\n\nThe table below shows the energy and fat content on the packs of the other foods the student tested.\n\n| Food Type | Energy / kJ 100 $\\bf{g^{-1}}$ | Fat Content/%|\n| :-- | :-: | :-: |\n| Butterscotch popcorn| 1837 | 11.5 |\n| Pitta bread | 1005 | 2.8 |\n| Digestive biscuit | 2039 | 24.0 |\n| Smoked almonds | 2520 | 52.0 |\n| Oat cracker | 1807 | 14.2 |\n| Tortilla crisp | 2051 | 22.5 |\n\n3. Identify, with a reason, which food investigated by the student is least likely to increase the risk of obesity. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. *Full marks can only be awarded if units included*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ {(10g x 35:sup[o]C) x 4.2J) ÷ 0.5g} ÷ 1000 **OR** 2940J g:sup[-1] ÷ 1000 **OR** 2940J g:sup[-1] [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 2.940 **OR** 2.94 **OR** 2.9 kJ g:sup[-1] [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Energy transferred (J) per gram of food\n\n = {(mass of water (g) x temperature rise (:sup[o]C) x 4.2 (J g:sup[-1] :sup[o]C:sup[-1] ))÷\n Mass of food sample (g)}\n\n = **{(10g x 35:sup[o]C x 4.2J) ÷ 0.5g}** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n = **2940J g:sup[-1]** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n Energy in kJ g:sup[-1] = 2940J g:sup[-1] ÷ 1000\n\n = **2.940 kJ g:sup[-1]** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ heat is transferred to the boiling tube **OR** thermometer **OR** lost to air **OR** lost to surroundings instead of to water [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ incomplete combustion offood **OR** potato snack [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ food may fall off the needle **OR** holder before fully burning [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The energy calculated from the experiment is lower because **not all** the **heat** is **transferred to** the **water** in the boiling tube, **some** is **transferred to** the **apparatus** and **lost to** the surrounding **air** ($\\checkmark$). Also, the food may **not** have **fully combusted**, often indicated by smoke ($\\checkmark$)\n\n3.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ pitta bread [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ because it contains the least energy **AND** fat **OR** has the lowest energy **AND** fat content [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **pitta bread** ($\\checkmark$) is the least likely to lead to obesity if eaten as it has the **lowest energy and fat** ($\\checkmark$) content out of all the foods tested."},{"ref":"BI1553","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1553\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA student wants to investigate how cell size affects the rate of diffusion. They carried out an experiment using alkaline agar cubes of different dimensions infused with pink phenolphthalein indicator, as shown in the diagram below. The phenolphthalein indicator becomes clear at pH values below 9.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1553.png\" alt=\"BI1553\">\n\nThree cubes of each size are submerged in an acetic acid solution and removed every two minutes so that the volume diffused can be calculated. The results of the experiment are summarised in the table below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/Untitled%205.png\" alt=\"Untitled 5\">\n\n1. Explain how the student was able to determine the percentage volume diffused in this experiment. [3 marks]\n\n2. Evaluate, using the data, the student’s hypothesis that the rate of diffusion decreases linearly as cube side length increases. [2 marks]\n\n3. How do the results of this experiment relate to the functional limitations of cells based on their sizes? [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ phenolphthalein indicator turns clear **OR** changes colour **OR** goes from pink to clear when exposed to acetic acid **OR** ethanoic acid [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cubes become progressively clear as acetic acid diffuses in [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ dimensions of pink **OR** clear region can be measured with a ruler [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ volume is calculated by multiplying length, width, and height **OR** length cubed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ divide the volume of the diffusion zone by the total cube volume and multiply by 100% to express it as a percentage [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ repeat readings made **OR** outliers eliminated **AND** mean values calculated\n [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The student needs to **compare the dimensions of the diffused and undiffused portions** ($\\checkmark$) of each cube. This can be observed since **phenolphthalein changes** from **pink to clear** when **exposed to acetic acid** ($\\checkmark$). The pink undiffused region of each cube can be **measured with a ruler** ($\\checkmark$) and its **length cubed** to determine **volume** ($\\checkmark$). This value is then **subtracted from the total volume** ($\\checkmark$) in order to **calculate percentage diffusion**. Repeat readings allow outliers to be identified and eliminated so a **reliable mean can be calculated** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hypothesis rejected OR not supported [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ because The relationship is non-linear **OR** geometric **OR** exponential [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ doubling the dimensions of a cube results in a diffusion percentage **OR** rate of diffusion that is much less than expected for a linear decrease **OR** less than half [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ volume increases faster than surface area **OR** surface area to volume ratio decreases as cube size increases **OR** surface area is squared WHEREAS volume is cubed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ resulting in much slower rates of diffusion for larger cubes [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n As cube size increases, the **rate of diffusion decreases more than expected** ($\\checkmark$) based on the dimensional differences. For example, the 4 cm cube, despite being only twice as wide as the 2 cm cube, has a rate of diffusion and percentage volume diffusion **much less than half**. This would indicate that the student’s **hypothesis would not be supported** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ there is a size limitation on cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ because diffusion is less effective **OR** slower as cell size **OR** volume increases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ because they require nutrients **OR** gas exchange **OR** removal of waste products **OR** the efficient movement of substances into and out of the cytoplasm to survive [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ because surface area increases more slowly than volume **OR** volume increases more quickly than surface area **OR** surface area to volume ratio decreases as cells grow larger [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Cells are **unable to grow beyond a certain size** ($\\checkmark$) because **diffusion would be insufficient** ($\\checkmark$) to allow them to **receive** the **nutrients** they **require** to **survive** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1597","marks":"5","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1597\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n A university student decided to mimic the experiments conducted by the ecologist Joseph Connell to investigate the competition between two species of barnacles (*Sp. A* and *Sp. B*) living in intertidal zones. They specifically wanted to see if one species could outcompete the other for space. The diagram below shows where *Sp. A* and *Sp. B* live, *Sp. A* on the upper intertidal zone and *Sp. B* on the lower intertidal zone.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1597.png\" alt=\"BI1597\">\n\nThe student transferred 3 rocks containing *Sp. A* from the upper intertidal zone to the lower intertidal zone and fastened them down, as shown in the diagram on the left below. They then allowed *Sp. B* to colonise these rocks.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/Joseph%20Connell%20colonisation%20by%20barnacles%20BI1597i.png\" alt=\"Joseph Connell colonisation by barnacles BI1597i\">\n\nAfter the colonisation period was over, the student removed *Sp. B* with a needle from one half of each of the 3 rocks that had been fastened down and left the other half undisturbed, as shown in the right-hand diagram above.\n\nThe student then monitored the percentage mortality of *Sp. A* on each half of these rocks for a year; the results are shown in the table below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1597T.png\" alt=\"BI1597T\">\n[Data adapted from original data obtained by the scientist]\n\n1. Deduce what effect *Sp. B* had on *Sp. A*. [1 mark]\n\n2. Outline the realised niches for the two species of barnacles. [2 marks]\n\nIn a second experiment, during unusually warm weather, the survival of both species was monitored at the margin where they coexisted. When there was no water for several days, mature *Sp. B* experienced a 50% mortality rate, while mature *Sp. A* suffered only about 2%.\n\n3. Using all the data, explain why both species can sometimes coexist in the same tidal zone. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **one** of the following :\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ *Sp. B* causes an increased mortality of *Sp. A* **OR** *Sp. B* has a negative impact on the survival of *Sp. A* [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ *Sp. B* outcompetes *Sp. A* in the lower intertidal zone [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n ***Sp. B*** caused an **increase** in the **death** of ***Sp. A***, as shown in the data ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. **Two** of the following :\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the realised niche for *Sp. A* is the upper intertidal zone [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the realised niche for *Sp. B* is the lower intertidal zone [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **realised niche** for ***Sp. A*** is the **upper intertidal** zone ($\\checkmark$), and for *Sp. B*, it is the **lower intertidal** zone ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **two** of the following :\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at the margins of the lower and upper intertidal zone, when there was a drought *Sp. A* showed lower mortality than *Sp. B* [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ *Sp. B* is not as resistant to desiccation as, **OR** requires more constant moisture than, *Sp. A* therefore can not survive on the upper rocks of the intertidal zone [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the intertidal zone environment varies allowing both species to exploit different microhabitats reducing direct competition and allowing coexistence [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fluctuating environmental conditions **OR** changes in temperature **OR** water exposure can give one species a temporary advantage preventing one species from completely excluding the other [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Based on the second experiment, *Sp. A*'s **higher resilience** to **lack of water**, as evidenced by its significantly **lower mortality rate** during **dry conditions** ($\\checkmark$), allows it to survive in the upper intertidal zone where exposure to air and the risk of desiccation are greater. In contrast, ***Sp. B's*** **higher mortality rate** in the **absence of water** indicates it **requires more constant moisture**, confining it to the **lower intertidal zone** ($\\checkmark$), where it is more frequently submerged."},{"ref":"BI1608","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1608\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nOrganisms that are better adapted to their environment are more likely to survive and thrive.\n\n1.  Describe how the skin and mucous membranes provide both physical and chemical defence against pathogens.    [4 marks]\n\n2.  Explain the importance of concentration gradients and the mechanisms that regulate them in the human ventilation system.    [5 marks]\n\n3.  Discuss to what extent the fossil record provides evidence for evolution.    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1.  any **four**  of the following (*answer must include **one** point from physical defence and **one** point from chemical defence to gain full marks*):\n\n    ***Physical defence***\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ skin provides a physical barrier **OR** is composed of multiple tissue layers preventing pathogens from entering the body    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ skin continuously sheds dead cells which removes any pathogens on the surface    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cells in skin are held together by tight junctions reinforcing the barrier function **OR** preventing pathogens from passing between cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mucus produced by mucous membranes can trap pathogens     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mucus containing trapped pathogens is either expelled by the body or swallowed    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cilia on mucous membrane surfaces move pathogens **OR** foreign particles out of the body    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Chemical defence***\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sebaceous glands in the skin secrete sebum, which lowers the pH of the skin and inhibits the growth of bacteria and fungi    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mucus contains lysozyme **OR** antimicrobial enzymes which break down bacterial cell walls    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sweat contains antimicrobials **OR** acids which inhibit growth of pathogens     [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n    The **skin** provides **physical defence** by forming a **barrier** that **prevents pathogens** from **entering** the **body** ($\\checkmark$). It also provides **chemical defence** against pathogens by having **sebaceous glands** that **secrete sebum**, which **lowers** the **pH** of the skin and so **inhibits** the **growth** of **bacteria** and **fungi** on the skin ($\\checkmark$). Mucous membranes line the internal surfaces of some organs and body cavities and secrete mucus, which can **trap pathogens** ($\\checkmark$). The mucus is then **expelled** by the body or **swallowed** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.  any **five** of the following:\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ steeper the concentration gradient, the greater the rate of diffusion, allowing for faster movement of molecules from areas of high concentration to areas of low concentration    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ inhaled air in alveoli has a higher concentration of oxygen than oxygen-depleted blood in the capillaries surrounding the alveoli **OR** blood has a higher concentration of carbon dioxide than the air in the alveoli. [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ concentration gradients facilitate diffusion of oxygen from alveoli into blood **OR** carbon dioxide from blood into alveoli, which provides cells with oxygen for respiration and removes waste carbon dioxide    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large network of capillaries surrounding alveoli rapidly transport oxygen away from the lungs **OR** rapidly transport carbon dioxide from the body to the lungs    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the processes of inhalation and exhalation ensure that there is a constant flow of air in and out of lungs    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chemoreceptors in carotid artery **OR** aorta **OR** medulla monitor blood pH levels **OR** blood carbon dioxide concentrations [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chemoreceptors send signals to respiratory centre in medulla oblongata OR brain stem     [1 mark]\n\n    respiratory centre **OR** medulla oblongata controls breathing rate **OR** depth of breathing to maintain concentration gradient of oxygen    [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n    Efficient gas exchange in the lungs relies on maintaining concentration gradients, where **inhaled air** in the **alveoli** has a **higher concentration** of **oxygen** than **oxygen-depleted blood** and a **lower concentration** of **carbon dioxide than that in** the surrounding **capillaries** ($\\checkmark$). This gradient allows **oxygen** to **diffuse from** the **alveoli** into the **blood** and **carbon dioxide from** the **blood into** the **alveoli** ($\\checkmark$). An extensive **network of capillaries** surrounding the alveoli rapidly **transports oxygen away** from the **lungs** and **carbon dioxide to** the lungs ($\\checkmark$). The processes of **inhalation** and **exhalation** ensure a **constant flow** of **air** in and out of the lungs, **maintaining** these **gradients** ($\\checkmark$). **Chemoreceptors** in the **carotid artery** and **aorta** monitor **blood pH** ($\\checkmark$), sending **signals to** the **respiratory centre** to **adjust breathing rate** and **depth** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.  any **seven** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** answer from ‘provides evidence’ and **one** answer from ‘doesn’t provide’ evidence to gain full marks*):\n\n    \n\n    ***Provides evidence***\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some organisms have very good fossil records, which allow their evolution to be tracked easily, e.g. horses        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the sequence of fossils that appear in layers of rock matches the sequence in which they would be expected to evolve     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ older organisms appear in deeper rock layers **OR** strata, and more recently evolved organisms appear in the upper layers    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there are fossils of extinct organisms with transitional forms **OR** fossils of extinct organisms that have features that link different stages of evolution, e.g. Archaeopteryx, bird-like dinosaurs that support the hypothesis that birds evolved from reptiles        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the fossil record shows evidence of mass extinction events **OR** there are many more extinct species than living ones        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fossils show evidence of adaptive radiation **OR** divergence of different organisms from a common ancestor **OR** speciation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the fossil record shows evidence of vestigial structures that are no longer present in current organisms    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is fossil evidence for the existence of the last universal common ancestor (LUCA) near hydrothermal vents        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fossils provide chronological evidence that supports the theory of gradualism in evolution, where small changes accumulate over long periods    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fossilised remains of early hominids show a clear progression in brain size **OR** bipedalism, supporting human evolution    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Doesn’t provide evidence***\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fossil record is incomplete, with many gaps **OR** some fossil species appear abruptly in the fossil record without any precursors    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some species in the fossil record appear not to have changed much over long periods of time        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ convergent evolution **OR** fossils of unrelated organisms with similar features makes it difficult to track evolution accurately    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ soft-bodied organisms are rarely fossilised, leading to an incomplete record that skews our understanding of evolutionary history    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ process of fossilisation is rare and biased towards certain environments, meaning that the fossil record may not accurately represent all ecosystems and organisms    [1 mark]\n\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n    The fossil record supports evolution because some organisms have **well-documented fossil records** that allow their evolutionary changes to be **tracked easily** ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the **sequence** in which **fossils appear in rock layers** matches the sequence in which they would be **expected to evolve** ($\\checkmark$). For example, **older** organisms are found in **deeper layers** of rocks, while more **recently evolved organisms** are found in the **upper layers** ($\\checkmark$). There are also fossils of extinct organisms with **transitional features**, such as Archaeopteryx, a bird-like dinosaur, which supports the hypothesis that birds evolved from reptiles and provides **evolutionary links** between present and extinct species ($\\checkmark$). However, there are **gaps** in the fossil record ($\\checkmark$), some species appear not to have **changed over long periods of time** ($\\checkmark$), and **convergent evolution** can make it difficult to track evolution accurately ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1629","marks":"3","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1629\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nThe diagram shows the results of a chromatogram created using pigments extracted from a geranium leaf (*Geranium albanum*).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/1629iii.png\" alt=\"1629iii\">\n\nThe table shows Rf values for photosynthetic pigments.\n\n| Photosynthetic pigment | Rf |\n\n| :-- | :-- |\n\n| Chlorophyll a | 0.68 |\n\n| Chlorophyll b | 0.54 |\n\n| $\\beta$- carotene | 0.94 |\n\n| Fucoxanthine | 0.51 |\n\n| Lutein | 0.43 |\n\n| Neoxanthine | 0.22 |\n\n| Violaxanthine | 0.08 |\n\n[Source: Adapted from: Bhatia, S., Nagpal, K., Bera, T., Sharma, A., & Sharma, K. Evaluation of pharmacognostical, phytochemical and anti-microbial properties of Porphyra vietnamensis (2015). International Journal of Green Pharmacy, 9(2), 131. Retrieved April 28, 2024, from https://doi.org/10.4103/0973-8258.155065. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n1.\n\n 1. Calculate the Rf value of pigment X. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Identify pigment X using the data from the table. [1 mark]\n\n2. Neoxanthine appears light green on the chromatogram. Explain why it appears this colour. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n\n 1. any **one** of the following\\\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 0.69 [1 mark]\n\n *Accept answers in the range of 0.68 - 0.70*\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/1629%20MSii.png\" alt=\"1629 MSii\">\n\n Rf value = distance moved by pigment from origin ÷ distance moved by solvent front from the origin\\\n\n Rf value = (29 - 5) ÷ (40 - 5)\\\n\n Rf value = 24 ÷ 35 = 0.69 ($\\checkmark$)\n\n 2. any **one** of the following\\\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chlorophyll a [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n **Chlorophyll a** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **one** of the following\\\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ it reflects light green light **AND** absorbs all other colours of light **OR** wavelengths of green light are reflected **AND** all other wavelengths of light are absorbed **OR** reflects wavelengths 594 - 570nm and absorbs all other wavelengths [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n Neoxanthine **reflects green** light and **absorbs** all **other colours** of light ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1652","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1652\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe karyogram is from a rat (*Rattus rattus*) cell without any chromosomal abnormalities. Biological sex is determined in the same way in rats and humans.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1652.png\" alt=\"BI1652\">\n[Source: Adapted from Hamanaka, S., Yamaguchi, T., Kobayashi, T., Kato-Itoh, M., Yamazaki, S., Sato, H., Umino, A., Wakiyama, Y., Arai, M., Sanbo, M., Hirabayashi, M., & Nakauchi, H. (2011, July 15). *File: Karyogram of normal rat.png*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 11, 2024 from URL https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Karyogram_of_normal_rat.png. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n1. State, with a reason, the stage of mitosis at which chromosomes are photographed to make a karyogram.                     [1 mark]\n\n2. Deduce, with a reason, the biological sex of the rat.    [1 mark]\n\n3. State how the chromosomes have been arranged to create the karyogram.    [2 marks]\n\n4. Using the karyogram, compare and contrast between the chromosomes of rats and humans.        [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ metaphase **BECAUSE** the chromosomes are visible **OR** condensed **OR** aligned on the equator of the cell so that it is easier to sort them    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The chromosomes are in **metaphase** because the chromosomes are **visible** and aligned on the **equator** of the cell, which makes it **easier to sort** them ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ male **BECAUSE** it has a Y chromosome **OR** has both X and Y chromosomes        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The rat is **male** because it has a  **Y** chromosome ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ by decreasing length    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ using banding patterns **OR** centromere positions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in pairs **OR** homologous pairs **OR** one chromosome from each parent forms a pair    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The chromosomes have been arranged by **decreasing length** ($\\checkmark$) and using their **banding patterns** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **two**  of the following: \\\n    *Answer must include at least **one** similarity and at least **one** difference to gain full marks*\n\n    ***Similarities***\\\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have pairs of chromosomes **OR** homologous chromosomes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have specific banding patterns **OR** genes on the chromosomes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have a pair of sex chromosomes **OR** X and Y chromosomes    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rats have fewer chromosomes **OR** autosomes than humans **OR** humans have more chromosomes **OR** autosomes than rats **OR** rats have 21 pairs of chromosomes **AND** humans have 23 pairs of chromosomes **OR** rats have 42 chromosomes **AND** humans have 46  chromosomes **OR**  rats have 40 autosomes and 2 sex chromosomes **AND** humans have 44 autosomes and 2 sex chromosomes **OR**  rats have 20 pairs of autosomes and 1 pair of sex chromosomes **AND** humans have 22 pairs of autosomes and 1 pair of sex chromosomes    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Both rats and humans have **homologous chromosomes** ($\\checkmark$), however, rats have **fewer chromosomes** (42) than humans (46) ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1716","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1716\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1.  Explain how mitosis is regulated in eukaryotic cells.    [5 marks]\n\n2.  Describe the stages of interphase and their importance to the process of mitosis.    [4 marks]\n\n3.  Discuss the importance of mitosis to living organisms using named examples.     [7 marks]","markScheme":"1.  any **five**  of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cyclins are proteins involved in the regulation of mitosis    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ levels of cyclins rise and fall at specific times in cell cycle        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ kinases  **OR** cyclin-dependent kinases are enzymes activated by cyclins    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ signalling occurs at specific checkpoints **OR** stop points of cell cycle    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ G$_2$ checkpoint controls whether cell goes into mitosis    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as cyclin level rises, it attaches to specific kinase or cyclin-dependent kinase **OR** CDK        [1 mark]\n\n    \n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ acts as mitosis-promoting factor    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ As mitosis-promoting factor **OR** MPF **OR** cyclin-kinase complex builds up switches on mitosis **OR** triggers nuclear membrane breakdown [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    **Cyclins** are **proteins** that **regulate mitosis** ($\\checkmark$), with their **levels rising** and **falling** at **specific times** ($\\checkmark$) in the cell cycle. They **activate cyclin-dependent kinases** ($\\checkmark$) (CDKs), forming a **complex** that acts as a **mitosis-promoting factor** ($\\checkmark$) (MPF). This complex **triggers** processes such as **nuclear membrane breakdown**, enabling **mitosis** to **proceed** ($\\checkmark$). The **G$_2$ checkpoint** ensures **favourable conditions** before the **cell enters mitosis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.  Any **four**  of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in G$_1$ **OR** G$_2$  the cell grows larger    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in G$_1$ the cell performs its normal metabolic functions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in S phase, the cell replicates its DNA [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in S phase, the cell replicates its centrosomes **OR** centrioles    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in G$_2$ phase, the cell duplicates its organelles **OR** synthesises proteins required for cell division    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the replication of DNA ensures that each daughter cell receives an identical set of chromosomes during mitosis    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in animal cells the replication of centrosomes **OR** centrioles is critical for forming the mitotic spindle during mitosis        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the growth and preparation during G$_1$ and G$_2$ phases provide the necessary cellular components and energy for successful cell division during mitosis    [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Interphase is split into three stages. In **G$_1$**, the **cell grows larger** ($\\checkmark$)  and performs its **normal metabolic functions** ($\\checkmark$), while in the **S phase**, it **replicates** its **DNA** ($\\checkmark$)  and **centrosomes** ($\\checkmark$). During **G$_2$**, the cell grows, **duplicates** its **organelles**, and **synthesises proteins** ($\\checkmark$)  necessary for cell division. These stages ensure the cell has **enough components** and an **identical set of chromosomes** to successfully **divide into two daughter cells** ($\\checkmark$)  during mitosis.\n\n3.  Any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ necessary to produce genetically identical cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ happens only in eukaryotes   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ provides new cells for growth    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ suitable examples of growth include embryonic development **OR** organ growth    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tissue repair **OR** wound healing    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tissue repair can protect harmful microorganisms from entering a wound    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ essential for regeneration of limbs in certain organisms  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ asexual reproduction    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ suitable examples of asexual reproduction include budding in hydra **OR** budding in yeast **OR** runners in plants **OR** tubers in plants    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ replacement of old **OR** dying cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ suitable example of replacement of cells include renewing of skin cells **OR** renewing intestinal lining [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ uncontrolled mitosis can result in the growth of tumours    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ maintains chromosome number in somatic cells.    [1 mark]\n\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Mitosis is essential for producing **genetically identical cells** ($\\checkmark$), a process **exclusive to eukaryotes** ($\\checkmark$). It enables **growth** ($\\checkmark$), for example, **embryonic development** and **organ growth** ($\\checkmark$) and **tissue repair**, such as in **wound healing** ($\\checkmark$), which **protects against** harmful **microorganisms** ($\\checkmark$).  **Asexual reproduction** ($\\checkmark$) relies on mitosis, for example, budding in **Hydra** and **yeast**. Mitosis **replaces old** or **dying cells** ($\\checkmark$) and **maintains chromosome number** in **somatic cells** ($\\checkmark$). However, **uncontrolled mitosis** can lead to **tumour** formation ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1720","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1720\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nLouis Pasteur disproved the theory of spontaneous generation by showing that life did not spontaneously arise from non-living matter but came from existing microorganisms. In his experiment, he set up the following flasks:\n\nI. Open straight-neck flask containing boiled broth.\n\nII. Swan-neck flask containing boiled broth (neck allowed air to flow but trapped dust and microbes).\n\nIII.\nControl flask containing boiled broth and sealed from the air.\n\nResearchers carried out a similar experiment to Louis Pasteur’s famous experiment, using the set-up below, and incubating the flasks at 30°C.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/spontaneous%20origin%20of%20cells%20flasks.png\" alt=\"spontaneous origin of cells flasks\">\n\n1. State the purpose of boiling the broth before placing it in some of the flasks. [1 mark]\n\n2.\n 1. State two variables that must be controlled in this experiment. [2 marks]\n\n 2. Explain why it is important to have controlled variables in this study. [1 mark]\n\n3.\n 1. State the letters of three flasks which would show signs of decomposition of broth. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Explain your answer to part (i). [3 marks]\n\n4. Suggest, with reasons, how the results of the study would differ if the flasks were incubated at 4°C. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to kill any pre-existing microorganisms **OR** bacteria in the broth **OR** beofre the experiment [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The purpose of boiling the broth was to **kill** any pre-existing **microorganisms in the broth**. ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ initial volume of broth [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature broth is incubated at [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen concentration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ concentration of nutrients in the broth [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ type **OR** volume of flask [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ time flask is incubated for [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ time that broth is boiled for [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sterilisation of flasks **OR** apparatus before setting up the experiment [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ consistent composition of the broth [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Two variables that must be controlled in this experiment are the initial **volume of broth** ($\\checkmark$) and the **temperature** at which the broth is incubated ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to ensure that any differences in the decomposition of the broth are only due to the independent variable [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to limit the influence of confounding variables [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to allow for a fair comparison between the flasks [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to increase replicability **OR** repeatability [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n It is important to have controlled variables in this study to ensure that any **differences in the decomposition of the broth** are only due to the **independent variable** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 1. Any **three** of the following gives one mark:\n\n *Do NOT award mark if any incorrect flasks are included.*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Q, R, S, T [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The flasks that would show decomposition of broth are **Q, R** and **S** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ flask Q was not boiled so microorganisms would be alive **OR** would reproduce **AND** microorganisms could enter from the air [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ flask R was tilted after being boiled, so microorganisms could still enter the flask at an angle [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ flask S was not boiled, so any microorganisms in the flask were not killed before the flask was sealed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ flask T was sealed with a metal mesh, so microorganisms from the air could enter the broth [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Flask **Q** would show decomposition of broth, as it was not boiled so **microorganisms would be alive** in the broth and could also **enter from the air** ($\\checkmark$). Flask **R** would show decomposition of broth, as even though it was tilted, it was not sealed so **microorganisms can still enter the flask** at an angle ($\\checkmark$). Flask **S** would also show decomposition of the broth, as it was **not boiled before being sealed**, so microorganisms would be alive in the broth ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ there would be less decomposition of the broth **OR** less bacterial growth **OR** less rapid growth of microorganisms in flasks Q, R, S and T [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at lower temperatures, microorganisms have a lower metabolic rate **OR** there is stiffening of phospholipid bilayer which decreases efficiency of membrane transport **OR** have a slower growth rate [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cooler temperatures can reduce the likelihood of contamination [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ there would be no difference in flask P because no bacteria could enter and the broth was boiled killing any bacteria in the broth [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n If the flasks were incubated at 4°C, there would be **less decomposition** ($\\checkmark$) of the broth in flasks Q, R, S and T, as at lower temperatures, microorganisms have a **slower growth rate** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1758","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1758\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1.  Outline how the properties of water contributed to the origin of life on Earth. [5 marks]\n\n2.  Draw **two** water molecules, labelling the partial charges and the bond between them.$^*$  [3 marks]\n\n3.  Explain how the solubility of different types of substances in water affects their transport in the blood, giving named examples. [8 marks]\n\n$^*$ *Note: Starting May 2028, this question may be assessed differently due to changes to the Biology guide, including the use of a data booklet.*","markScheme":"1.  any **five** of the following:\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polarity of water molecules enables formation of hydrogen bonds    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen bonding assisted formation of stable molecules **OR** assembly of complex structures **OR** lipid bilayer **OR** other suitable named structures    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ solvent properties facilitated dissolution **OR** interaction of organic molecules    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enabled formation of more complex structures **OR** protocells **OR** precursors to first living cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ high specific heat capacity prevented rapid temperature changes, enabling chemical reactions to occur    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ provided medium for enzymes **OR** chemical reactions **OR** synthesis of biological molecules        [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Water’s **polarity** enables the formation of **hydrogen bonds** ($\\checkmark$), which are crucial for the **stability** and **assembly** of **complex structures** such as **DNA, proteins** and **lipid bilayers** ($\\checkmark$). As a **universal solvent**, water facilitated the **dissolution** and **interaction** of **organic molecules** ($\\checkmark$), leading to the formation of **protocells** ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, water’s **high specific heat capacity** provided a **stable environment, preventing** rapid **temperature changes** and allowing essential **enzymatic** controlled **reactions** to occur ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.  any **three** of the following:\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ two water molecules each made up of one oxygen atom attached to two hydrogen atoms [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ slight negative charge on oxygen atom and slight positive charge on both hydrogen atoms on at least one of the water molecules    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen bond drawn as dashed lines and labelled    [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1758b.png\" alt=\"BI1758b\">\n\n3.  any **eight** of the following:\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transport of molecules in the blood depends on their solubility in water    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transport occurs in blood plasma and red blood cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrophilic **OR** water-soluble substances travel freely in solution    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ionic compounds **OR** ions transported in plasma    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ NaCl dissociates into Na+ and Cl- ions and carried in plasma **OR** NaCl is transported as free ions in plasma    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glucose is polar, so dissolves in water and travels in plasma    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ solubility of amino acids determined by side chain        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ amino acids are transported in an ionised state    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrophobic **OR** water-insoluble substances can not travel in solution    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen is non-polar **OR** has limited solubility in plasma    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ majority of oxygen binds to haemoglobin **OR** is transported by red blood cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cholesterol **OR** fats **OR** lipids **OR** triglycerides are non-polar **OR** hydrophobic and bind to proteins **OR** form lipoproteins to become soluble        [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n    The **transport** of molecules in the blood is **determined by** their **solubility** in water ($\\checkmark$). **Hydrophilic** substances, such as **glucose** ($\\checkmark$) and **ionised amino acids**  ($\\checkmark$), **dissolve** easily **in plasma** and **travel freely** in **solution** ($\\checkmark$). **Ionic compounds** like **sodium chloride dissociate** and are carried as **free ions**, such as **Na$^+$ and Cl$^-$** ($\\checkmark$), in the **plasma** ($\\checkmark$). In contrast, **non-polar substances** like **oxygen** and **lipids** are **not soluble** in water. Only a **small proportion** of **oxygen** is **dissolved** in the **plasma**; it is **primarily transported** by binding to **haemoglobin**($\\checkmark$)  in **red blood cells** ($\\checkmark$). **Lipids**, including **cholesterol** and **triglycerides**, **bind to proteins** to form **lipoproteins**, which makes them **soluble** and allows their transport in the bloodstream ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1760","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1760\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe enzyme catalase breaks down hydrogen peroxide, a metabolic waste product, into water and oxygen, preventing potential harm to the cell. One catalase molecule can catabolise six million hydrogen peroxide molecules per minute. Catalase is located within membrane-bound organelles called peroxisomes, where it breaks down hydrogen peroxide.\n\nStudents conducted an experiment to investigate how increasing substrate concentration affects catalase activity. They ground 1 g of liver tissue (a tissue rich in peroxisomes) with 5 ml of water and soaked a filter paper disc in this mixture for 5 seconds to absorb catalase. The disc was then placed at the bottom of a tube with 10 ml of 1% hydrogen peroxide solution. The time it took for the disc to rise to the surface of the hydrogen peroxide was recorded. This process was repeated twice more, and the mean time was calculated. The experiment was then repeated with four different hydrogen peroxide concentrations using the same liver mixture. The results are shown in the table below.\n\n| Hydrogen Peroxide Concentration / M| Mean time taken for disc to rise / s | Rate of reaction / s$^{-1}$ |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| 0.4 | 62 | 0.016 |\n\n| 0.8 | 41 | 0.024 |\n\n| 1.2 | 13 |  |\n\n| 1.6 | 8 | 0.125 |\n\n| 2.0 | 7 | 0.143 |\n\n1.  Calculate the rate of reaction for the 1.2 M hydrogen peroxide solution.        [1 mark]\n\n2.  In addition to the controlled variables specified in the method, identify **two** other variables that should be kept constant to ensure a fair test in this experiment.    [1 mark]\n\n3.  Describe the relationship between increasing hydrogen peroxide concentration and catalase activity.        [1 mark]\n\n4.  Suggest why catalase is held in peroxisomes and not released directly into the cytoplasm.    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  the follwoing\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 0.077 s$^{-1}$    [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Rate of reaction with 1.2M hydrogen peroxide = 1/13 s\\\n\n    $\\hspace{19.75em}$ = **0.077 s$^{-1}$**\n\n    The rate of reaction for 1.2 M hydrogen peroxide is **0.077 s$^{-1}$** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.  any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pH        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ surface area **OR** size of paper disc    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ time between preparing hydrogen peroxide and using it    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ any other reasonable answer    [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Two other variables that need to be controlled are the **pH** of the solution and the **temperature** of the hydrogen peroxide. ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.  any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as concentration of hydrogen peroxide increases catalase activity increases [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ results observed suggest direct relationship **OR** positive correlation between hydrogen peroxide concentration and catalase activity     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large **OR** greatest increase in rate of reaction between 0.4 M to 1.2 M and then smaller increase in rate of reaction from 1.2 M  to 2.0 M hydrogen peroxide **OR** reaction rate rises significantly at low substrate concentrations but begins to plateau at higher substrate concentrations    [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    As the **concentration** of **hydrogen peroxide increases**, the rate of reaction, i.e. **catalase activity, increases**, indicating a **positive correlation** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.  any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to prevent too much oxygen being produced at once which could damage cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ only way to control rate of reaction as cells cannot control temperature **OR** substrate concentration **OR** hydrogen peroxide concentration    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to prevent catalase from interacting with other cellular molecules **OR** organelles, potentially disrupting normal cellular processes **OR** to ensure that the breakdown of hydrogen peroxide occurs in a specific location, protecting other parts of the cell from damage    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ peroxisomes provide an optimal environment **OR** optimum pH for catalase activity    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows cells to quickly respond to changes in hydrogen peroxide levels, preventing its accumulation **OR** minimising potential cytotoxic effects    [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Catalase is kept in peroxisomes to **prevent** it from **interacting** with other **cellular molecules** or **organelles**, which could **disrupt** normal **cellular processes** ($\\checkmark$). Peroxisomes also provide an **optimal environment** for **catalase activity**, ensuring efficient breakdown of hydrogen peroxide ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1762","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1762\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nBelow is an electron micrograph of skeletal muscle.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1762%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1762 fig1\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Louisa Howard. Human skeletal muscle tissue 1 - TEM. Retrieved 27 August 2024 from: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Human_skeletal_muscle_tissue_1_-_TEM.jpg. Copyright Public Domain]\n\n1.  Identify the region labelled X.        [1 mark]\n\n2.  Using the ruler that has been placed onto the electron micrograph to measure the image size, calculate the actual length of the sarcomere.    [2 marks]\n\n3.  Explain the role of calcium ions in the shortening of the sarcomere, after their release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum.    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Z$-$line    [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    X is the :answer[Z$-$line] ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.  any **two** of following *(units required to achieve full marks)*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 44,000μm / 20,000 (allow for 43,000 $-$ 45,000μm or equivalent in mm or cm)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 2.2μm (allow any values between 2.1 $-$ 2.3 μm)    [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    **Actual size** = $\\dfrac{\\textbf{image size}}{\\textbf{magnification}}$\n\n       \n\n    Image size = 4.4 cm = 44 mm\n\n    1mm = 1,000 μm\n\n    Image size = 44,000 μm\n\n    Actual size = **44,000 μm / 20,000** ($\\checkmark$)\\\n\n    $\\hspace{4.7em}$ = **2.2 μm** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ calcium ions bind to troponin bringing about conformational change     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ this change in troponin moves tropomyosin away from myosin-binding sites on actin **OR** exposes myosin-binding sites on actin        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allowing myosin to bind to actin **OR** form cross-bridges to enabling power stroke to occur [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Once calcium ions are released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, they **bind to troponin**, causing a **conformational change** in the protein ($\\checkmark$). This change **shifts tropomyosin away from** the **myosin-binding sites** on the **actin** filaments ($\\checkmark$). With these binding sites now exposed, **myosin heads** can **attach to actin**, forming **cross-bridges**, allowing the power stroke to occur ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1763","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1763\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nA study was conducted to investigate the relationship between water temperature and phytoplankton density in a bay along the southwest shoreline of Wales, U.K. This study was motivated by concerns about the potential effects of climate change on marine ecosystems, particularly how rising water temperatures could affect phytoplankton populations in the sea. Phytoplankton, which are the primary producers in marine food webs, play a crucial role in carbon cycling and are sensitive to changes in environmental conditions.\nEach month the chlorophyll concentration of seawater samples was measured, using a spectrophotometer. The concentration of chlorophyll is an indicator of phytoplankton density.\nIn parallel with the in situ measurements, mesocosm experiments, like the one shown below, were conducted in the laboratory using water samples from the bay over the same time period.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI1763.png\" alt=\"BI1763\">\n\nDiscuss the advantages and limitations of carrying out these laboratory mesocosm experiments compared to the monthly measurements of chlorophyll concentration in situ. [3 marks]","markScheme":"any **three** of the following *(answer must include at least one advantage and one limitation to gain full marks):*\n\n***Advantages:***\\\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ ease of collection of continuous data **OR** frequent monitoring of phytoplankton growth **OR** chlorophyll concentration [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ precise manipulation of water temperature in mesocosm compared to fluctuating temperatures of seawater in situ [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ controlling flow of substances into **OR** out of mesocosms reduces risk of external contamination **OR** helps ensure stable **OR** consistent experimental conditions **OR** better control of environmental variables **OR** named environmental variable **OR** minimises influence of external factors **OR** pollution **OR** weather variations **OR** any other environmental disturbances [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ enhances reliability **OR** repeatability of the study **OR** conditions can be replicated across multiple trials [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ facilitates prolonged observation **OR** monitoring of biological responses over time **OR** can be maintained for extended periods under constant conditions **OR** allows for long-term studies [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ reduce the potential for ecological damage that could arise from in situ experiments **OR** minimises environmental impact **OR** ethical concerns associated with manipulating natural habitats [1 mark]\n\n***Limitations:***\\\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ may not fully replicate the complexity **OR** variability of natural conditions in the bay **OR** might not be directly applicable to natural settings [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ may not capture the full scale **OR** range of natural ecosystem interactions **OR** limited size can restrict the range of species **OR** interactions studied [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ can be costly **OR** resource-intensive **OR** can have higher costs **OR** more logistical challenges **OR** need specialised equipment compared to in situ studies [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ smaller scale of mesocosms compared to the actual bay can influence the behaviour **OR** growth patterns of phytoplankton [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ ethical concerns regarding welfare of organisms in mesocosm [1 mark]\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nMesocosm experiments offer the **advantage** of **controlled environmental conditions**, allowing for **precise manipulation** of **variables** like **temperature** ($\\checkmark$), and they **minimise environmental impact** by **avoiding direct interference** with natural ecosystems ($\\checkmark$). However, they may **not fully replicate** the complexity of **natural environments**, potentially leading to results that **lack ecological validity** ($\\checkmark$), and they can be **resource-intensive**, requiring **specialised equipment**($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2002","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2002\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nPlant growth chambers are contained and controllable environments used to strictly control growth conditions. A researcher planted *Arabidopsis thaliana* in several growth chambers at six different carbon dioxide concentrations. After 3 weeks of growth, the plants were dried and massed.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI2002A.png\" alt=\"BI2002A\">\n\n1. State the independent variable. [1 mark]\n\n2. Suggest **one** reason for reporting biomass units as grams per 10 plants. [1 mark]\n\n3. Explain the trend seen in the graph. [3 marks]\n\n4. Discuss a probable result if the $\\ce{CO_2}$ concentration were raised above\n2,000 mg m$^{-3}$. [2 marks]\n\n5. Explain the use of dried biomass in the experiment. [2 marks]\n\n6. State **one** light variable that can be adjusted in the growth chamber to potentially change the biomass produced by the plant. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ $\\ce{CO_2}$ concentration **OR** mg m$^{-3}$ of $\\ce{CO_2}$ [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The independent variable is the **concentration of carbon dioxide in mg m**$^{-3}$ ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ measuring individual plants may be less precise due to the limitations of the scale **OR** instrument used **OR** combining plants into groups improves accuracy [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ reduces impact of outliers **OR** non-germination improving data reliability [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allows larger sample sizes to be used which improves reliability **OR** reduces impact of variability between individual plants [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ provides a more reliable mean by averaging natural variation in growth between individual plants **OR** provides a more representative measure of biomass across plants [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ any other reasonable suggestion [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n This would simplify measurements and **reduce variability** ($\\checkmark$) between individual plants in large-scale experiments, ensuring a more **reliable mean** that accounts for **natural variation** across a population ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as $\\ce{CO_2}$ concentration increases, dry biomass also increases, indicating a possible positive correlation **OR** indicating a positive relationship [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ $\\ce{CO_2}$ is a substrate for photosynthesis, so increasing its availability can enhance rate of photosynthesis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ photosynthesis reactions are catalysed by enzymes, and higher $\\ce{CO_2}$ concentrations can initially increase the rate of these reactions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in biomass suggests increase in $\\ce{CO_2}$ concentration increases rate of photosynthesis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at higher $\\ce{CO_2}$ concentrations, the biomass plateaus, suggesting limiting factor **OR** enzyme concentration **OR** light intensity **OR** temperature is limiting [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n From 600 to 1,200 mg m$^{-3}$ $\\ce{CO_2}$, biomass increases, indicating a **positive relationship** ($\\checkmark$). This occurs because **CO**$_2$ is a **substrate for photosynthesis**, and its availability **enhances** the **rate of photosynthesis** ($\\checkmark$). However, **beyond 1,200 mg m**$^{-3}$, the biomass **plateaus**, suggesting that **enzymes** are **saturated** or another **factor**, such as **light intensity**, has become **limiting** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **two** of the following *(result must be supported by reasoning)*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ biomass production would not change **OR** would stay constant [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ photosynthetic enzymes (rubisco) are saturated **OR** carbon dioxide is no longer the limiting factor [1 mark]\n **OR**\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ biomass production would decrease [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ excessive $\\ce{CO_2}$ could disrupt cellular processes **OR** lead to toxic effects **OR** metabolic imbalances **OR** limit access to oxygen for respiration [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The biomass measurements would stay at the **plateau** ($\\checkmark$) because **carbon dioxide** is **no longer** the **limiting factor** once the photosynthetic **enzymes** are **saturated** ($\\checkmark$) with the substrate.\n\n5. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ dry biomass is an indirect measure of photosynthetic rate [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ drying plants ensures results are based only on organic matter [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ removes variability of water content between plants [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ using gas sensors for weeks to measure photosynthetic rate is impractical **OR** would be labour intensive **OR** require constant monitoring [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ only endpoint data is required to measure three weeks of growth [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ any other reasonable explanation [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Dried biomass is an **indirect measure of photosynthetic rate** ($\\checkmark$), because it reflects the organic molecules produced by the plant. Drying **ensures results** are based only on **organic molecules** by **removing variability** caused by **water content** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ light intensity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ day length [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ wavelength(s) [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n One way the light delivered by the growth chambers can impact the dried biomass produced is if they change the **intensity of the light** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2412","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2412\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nA group of students was investigating the water potential of carrot cells. They cut ten cylinders of tissue from the same carrot, all the same diameter and length. They recorded the mass of each carrot cylinder and then placed each one into different solutions, with varying sucrose concentrations, as shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/eDITa.png\" alt=\"eDITa\">\n\nAfter 1 hr, each carrot cylinder was removed from its solution, dried, and its final mass recorded. The students then calculated the percentage change in mass and recorded this in the graph shown.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/EdiTb.png\" alt=\"EdiTb\">\n\n1. Draw a line of best fit onto the graph provided. [1 mark]\n\n2. Use the graph and the conversion table below to estimate the water potential of the carrot cells ($\\psi_{\\ce{cell}}$). Show your working. [3 marks]\n\n**mol** $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ **to solute potential conversion table**\n\n| Sucrose concentration / mol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ | Solute potential / kPA |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| 0.1 | -248.44 |\n| 0.2 | -496.86 |\n| 0.3 | -745.30 |\n| 0.4 | -993.74 |\n| 0.5 | -1242.11 |\n| 0.6 | -1490.62 |\n| 0.7 | -1739.06 |\n| 0.8 | -1987.50 |\n| 0.9 | -2235.94 |\n| 1.0 | -2484.38 |\n\n3. Explain what happens inside the carrot cells, in terms of pressure, osmotic and water potentials, when immersed in sucrose concentrations lower than 0.4 mol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ (hypotonic solutions). [3 marks]\n\nThe students repeated the experiment using turnip tissue. The solute potential ($\\psi_{\\ce{s}}$) of the turnip cell contents was given as -1550.40 kPa.\n\n$\\psi_{\\ce{cell}} = \\psi_{\\ce{s}} + \\psi_{\\ce{p}}$\n\n4. Use the equation and ‘mol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ to water potential conversion table’ provided to calculate the pressure potential ($\\psi_{\\ce{p}}$) of the turnip cells when they reached equilibrium in a 0.5 mol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ sucrose solution. [1 mark]\n\n5. The students studied the carrot cells under a light microscope after immersing them in the 1.0 mol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ sucrose solution for one hour. Describe what the students would have observed about the carrot cells. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ a straight line drawn so five points are above the line and five points are below the line [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/AnsA.png\" alt=\"AnsA\">\n\n2. any **three** of the following *(max **two** marks for correct answer without showing working, **OR** correct working without final correct answer)*:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ line crosses x-axis **OR** horizontal axis **OR** shows no change in mass at 0.47 mol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ (allow range 0.46 - 0.48) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 0.5 mol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ = -1242.11 kPa and 0.4 mol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ = -993.74 kPa [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 1242.11 - 993.74 = 248.37 kPa [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 0.47 mol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ = -993.74 + (0.7 $\\times$ 248.37) =-1167.60 kPa [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ -1167.60 kPa (allow in range -1140 to -1200) [1 mark]\n\n *Alternative method: identify any concentration value and its corresponding ΔG from the graph, then scale proportionally to the equilibrium concentration\ne.g. reading ΔG = –1242 kJ mol⁻¹ at [conc] = 0.50, then: ΔG (at equilibrium) = (0.47 / 0.50) × –1242 = –1167.6 kJ mol⁻¹\n Award full marks if the method is correct and consistent with read values*\n\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI2412bms.png\" alt=\"BI2412bms\">\n\n Line crosses x-axis at **0.47** mol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ ($\\checkmark$) sucrose solution, indicating no change in mass at this concentration (equilibrium is reached) \\\n At equilibrium water potential of cell (ψcell) equals water potential of external solution ($\\psi_{\\ce{solution}}$).\\\n The pressure potential ($\\psi_{\\ce{p}}$) of the sucrose solution is 0, because it is in an open container \\\n ψsolution = $\\psi_{\\ce{p}} + \\psi_{\\ce{s}} = 0 + \\psi_{\\ce{s}} = \\psi_{\\ce{s}}$ so, water potential of the sucrose solution (ψsolution) equals solute potential of the solution ($\\psi_{\\ce{s}}$)\n\n From table 0.5 mol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ sucrose solution = **-1242.11 kPa** \\\n From table 0.4 mol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ sucrose solution = **-993.74 kPa**\n\n Difference = **1242.11 kPa - 993.74 kPa** = **248.37 kPa** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n Calculating water potential of cell ($\\psi_{\\ce{cell}}$) using interpolation:\\\n 0.47 mol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$ = **-993.74 + (0.7** $\\times$ **248.37**) = **-1167.599 kPa**\\\n = **1167.60 kPa** (to 2 d.p.) ($\\checkmark$)\n\n3. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as water moves in by osmosis, the increase in number of water molecules inside cell, increases pressure potential **OR** increase in water molecules increases turgor pressure inside the cell, leading to increase in pressure potential [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as water moves in by osmosis, number of solute molecules in cell decreases relative to water molecules, making solute potential less negative **OR** increasing solute potential [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ overall water potential of cell becomes less negative, **OR** increases, due to the combined effects of increased pressure potential and less negative **OR** increases solute potential [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n When immersed in hypotonic solutions, **water enters** the cells by **osmosis, turgor pressure increases**, and so there is an **increase in pressure potential** ($\\psi_{\\ce{p}}$) ($\\checkmark$). The influx of water **dilutes** the **solutes** inside the cell, reducing their concentration and making the **solute potential** ($\\psi_{\\ce{s}}$) **less negative** ($\\checkmark$). Overall, the **water potential** of the cell ($\\psi_{\\ce{cell}}$) becomes **less negative** due to the **combined effects** of **increased pressure potential** and **less negative solute potential** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 308.29 kPa [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n $\\psi_{\\ce{cell}} = \\psi_{\\ce{s}} + \\psi_{\\ce{p}}$\n\n $\\psi_{\\ce{s}}$ = -1550.40 kPa\\\n $\\psi_{\\ce{cell}}$ = -1242.11 kPa (from the mol $\\ce{L^{-1}}$to solute potential conversion table)\n\n $\\hspace{2.8em}$ -1242.11 kPa = -1550.40 kPa + $\\psi_{\\ce{p}}$\\\n $\\hspace{2.8em}$ $\\psi_{\\ce{p}}$ -1242.11 kPa + 1550.40 kPa = $\\psi_{\\ce{p}}$\\\n $\\hspace{2.8em}$ $\\psi_{\\ce{p}}$ = 308.29 kPa\n\n5. any **two** of the following *(annotated diagram acceptable)*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ plasmolysed cell [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cytoplasm **OR** cell membrane shrunken away from the cell wall **OR** gaps between the cell wall and cytoplasm [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ shrunken vacuoles **OR** vacuoles appear smaller [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ no loss of cell wall structure **OR** cell walls would still be visible **OR** maintain their shape [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/AnsE.png\" alt=\"AnsE\">"},{"ref":"BI2669","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI2669\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline how light energy is converted into chemical energy during photosynthesis.            [4 marks]\n\n2. Describe the benefits and challenges of FACE experiments for investigating photosynthesis.    [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain how valid results can be obtained when investigating the effect of varying light intensity on the rate of photosynthesis.    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light energy is absorbed by photosynthetic pigments **OR** chlorophyll    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ absorbed light energy excites electrons in chlorophyll        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ excited electrons provide energy to convert carbon dioxide into glucose **OR** carbohydrate [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electrons lost from chlorophyll are replaced by electrons from the splitting of water **OR** photolysis        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ photolysis is the splitting of water molecules in the presence of light [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ photolysis produces electrons **AND** hydrogen ions **AND** oxygen     [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Light energy** is **absorbed** by **chlorophyll** in the chloroplasts ($\\checkmark$). This **energy excites electrons** in the **chlorophyll** molecules ($\\checkmark$). The excited electrons provide energy to **convert carbon dioxide into glucose** ($\\checkmark$). The **electrons lost** from chlorophyll are **replaced** by electrons from the **splitting of water** in a process called **photolysis** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following *(must include at least **one** benefit and **one** challenge to gain full marks):*\n\n    ***Benefits:***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ take place in natural ecosystems, increasing ecological validity    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allow observation of long-term plant responses to elevated $\\ce{CO_2}$        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ simulate future atmospheric $\\ce{CO_2}$ conditions predicted under climate change    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plants experience natural interactions with pests, competitors, and pollinators, giving realistic outcomes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enable study of whole-ecosystem responses, not just individual plants    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Challenges:***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ difficult to control environmental variables like temperature and rainfall        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ expensive **OR** requires large infrastructure    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ differences in the environmental conditions **OR** characteristics of different experimental plots may cause unintended variability in the results        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ wind or weather may disperse added $\\ce{CO_2}$ unevenly        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ monitoring **OR** data collection over large areas is logistically demanding    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ harder to isolate the effect of $\\ce{CO_2}$ alone due to multiple interacting variables        [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    FACE experiments are beneficial because they are conducted in **natural ecosystems**, so they provide **more valid data** about how **plants respond** to **elevated** $\\mathbf{CO_2}$ levels ($\\checkmark$).  They also allow **long-term observations** of plant **growth** and **photosynthesis** under **future climate** conditions ($\\checkmark$).  However, a major challenge is that **environmental variables** like temperature and rainfall are **difficult to control** in open-air settings ($\\checkmark$).  In addition, differences between plots, such as soil type or microclimate, can **introduce variation** in the **data** that is **unrelated** to $\\ce{CO_2}$ levels ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use a light meter to measure light intensity    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allow plant time to acclimatise at each light level before taking measurements        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carry out multiple trials **OR** repeats **OR** collect at least three results at each light intensity    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ take mean of results **OR** remove any anomalous results [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ use  same plant **OR** same species **OR** size **OR** condition of plant            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ maintain a constant $\\ce{CO_2}$ concentration, **OR** use a fixed mass **OR** concentration of sodium hydrogen carbonate        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ keep temperature constant **OR** use a water bath to maintain stable **OR** suitable temperature [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ record data in a consistent **OR** quantifiable way **OR** count  bubbles of oxygen per minute **OR** collect volume of oxygen in same set time            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carry out the experiment in a darkened room to eliminate interference from other light sources [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ calibrate equipment before use to reduce systematic error    [1 mark]\n\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    To ensure valid results a **light meter** should be used to **accurately measure** light intensity ($\\checkmark$). The **plant** should be allowed to **acclimatise** at **each** light **intensity before recording data** ($\\checkmark$). Multiple trials should be carried out at **each light intensity** ($\\checkmark$), and a **mean** should be **calculated** to provide a more reliable estimate of the true photosynthetic rate ($\\checkmark$). The **same species** and **size of plant** should be used in **all trials** ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, **carbon dioxide concentration** must be **kept constant**, for example by using the **same mass** of **sodium hydrogen carbonate** for each trial ($\\checkmark$). **Temperature** should also be **kept constant**, possibly using a **water bath** to maintain a suitable temperature for the plant’s enzymes and to prevent heat from the lamp from affecting results ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI3216","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI3216\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nPhotosynthesis is a process where carbon compounds are produced in the presence of light.\n\n1. Draw and label a plan diagram to show the distribution of the main tissues in a transverse section of a dicotyledonous leaf. [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline how light energy is converted into chemical energy during photosynthesis. [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe an experiment investigating the effect of carbon dioxide concentration on the rate of photosynthesis. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. diagram to show any **four** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ correctly positioned **AND** labelled palisade mesophyll layer [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ correctly positioned **AND** labelled spongy mesophyll layer [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ correctly positioned **AND** labelled upper epidermis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ correctly positioned **AND** labelled lower epidermis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ correctly positioned **AND** labelled vascular bundle **OR** xylem **OR** phloem [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n :u[Sample answer]:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Practical-based%20and%20Extended%20Response%20Questions/Unassigned/images/BI3216_leaf_plan_diagram_v2.png\" alt=\"TS of tissues in dicot leaf plan diagram\">\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ light is absorbed by chlorophyll **OR** pigments arranged in photosystems in the thylakoid membrane [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ absorbed energy excites electrons, which are emitted from the reaction centre of photosystem II [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ excited electrons pass along an electron transport chain **OR** chain of electron carriers [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ energy from the electrons is used to pump protons (H:sup[+]) into the thylakoid space [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ protons (H:sup[+]) diffuse down their concentration gradient through ATP synthase, driving ATP synthesis by chemiosmosis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ photolysis of water produces electrons that replace those lost from photosystem II, together with oxygen **AND** H:sup[+] [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ electrons are re-excited at photosystem I and used to reduce NADP **OR** produce reduced NADP [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP and reduced NADP are used in the Calvin cycle **OR** light-independent reactions to convert glycerate 3-phosphate into triose phosphate (accept to produce triose phosphate **OR** carbohydrate) [1 mark]\n[5 marks]\n\n :u[Sample answer]:\n\n **Light** is **absorbed** by **pigments** in **photosystem II** in the **thylakoid membrane**, **exciting electrons** ($\\checkmark$). The excited electrons pass along an **electron transport chain** ($\\checkmark$), **releasing energy** that **pumps protons** into the **thylakoid space** ($\\checkmark$). The **protons diffuse** back through **ATP synthase**, producing **ATP** by **chemiosmosis** ($\\checkmark$). **Electrons lost** from photosystem II are **replaced** by the **photolysis** of **water**, which releases **oxygen** ($\\checkmark$). At **photosystem I**, electrons are **re-excited** and used to **reduce NADP** ($\\checkmark$). **ATP** and **reduced NADP** are then used in the **Calvin cycle** to convert **glycerate 3-phosphate** into **triose phosphate**, which is used to synthesise **carbohydrates** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ select a range of five different concentrations of sodium hydrogen carbonate/sodium bicarbonate solution/other suitable source of carbon dioxide, including control with no added sodium hydrogen carbonate/no added source of carbon dioxide **OR** prepare 0.000 M, 0.005 M, 0.010 M, 0.015 M and 0.020 M sodium hydrogen carbonate/any acceptable range with consistent intervals provided [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ use aquatic plants/*Elodea*/pondweed **OR** land plants [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ for aquatic plants, submerge the plant in water with the cut end of the stem facing upwards [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ for land-based plants, measure oxygen gas production using a gas sensor **OR** for aquatic plants, count the number of bubbles/water displacement [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ use boiled and cooled water to reduce dissolved carbon dioxide **OR** use a tray with sodium hydroxide to absorb CO:sub[2] from the air in terrestrial plants set-up [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ for land plants, enclose the plant in a sealed chamber and vary CO:sub[2] by adding CO:sub[2] gas **OR** absorbing CO:sub[2] with soda lime/sodium hydroxide, checking concentrations with a CO:sub[2] sensor [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ multiple trials to obtain reliable results **OR** five trials for each concentration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ use a thermometer to check for temperature changes **OR** place the beaker/boiling tube containing aquatic plants in water bath [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ place light source at a constant distance from the plant [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ description of appropriate controlled variable, e.g. plant size in different trials and experimental groups is the same [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ rate of photosynthesis can be calculated as oxygen gas percentage over time **OR** number of bubbles over time **OR** water displacement over time [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbon dioxide concentration is the independent variable **AND** rate of oxygen production is the dependent variable [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allow the plant to acclimatise for a few minutes at each new concentration before measuring [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ collect the gas in a gas syringe **OR** photosynthometer to measure volume, as bubble size varies [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ check pH **OR** use a buffer, as sodium hydrogen carbonate can change the pH of the water [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ plot a graph of rate of photosynthesis against carbon dioxide concentration [1 mark]\n[7 marks]\n\n :u[Sample answer]:\n\n Select **five** different **concentrations** of carbon dioxide (0.000 M to 0.020 M sodium hydrogen carbonate) ($\\checkmark$). For the control with no added sodium hydrogen carbonate, use **boiled** and then **cooled water** to **reduce dissolved carbon dioxide** ($\\checkmark$). Use **pondweed** ($\\checkmark$) and **submerge** it in the **water**, with the **cut end** of the **stem pointing upwards** ($\\checkmark$). The **oxygen** produced can be **collected** in a small **gas syringe** ($\\checkmark$). The **rate of photosynthesis** can then be calculated by **dividing** the **volume** collected **by** the **time** taken ($\\checkmark$). **Repeat** the experiment **five times** at **each concentration** and **calculate** a **mean rate** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n The **other limiting factors**, temperature and light intensity, must be **controlled**. **Temperature** can be controlled by placing the beaker containing the plant in a **thermostatically controlled water bath** ($\\checkmark$), and **light intensity** by placing the **light source** at a **fixed distance** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"​​BI1356","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# ​​BI1356\n\n\\[Maximum marks: 16\\]\n\n1. Distinguish between obligate aerobes, obligate anaerobes, and facultative anaerobes. [3 marks]\n\n2. Describe holozoic nutrition in animals. [5 marks]\n\n3. Using named examples, outline the relationship between dentition, musculature, and diet in herbivorous and omnivorous members of the family *Hominidae*. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. \\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ obligate aerobes require oxygen for respiration **OR** respire aerobically **OR** carry out aerobic respiration, **OR** require oxygen to grow **OR** survive, **OR** rely on aerobic respiration        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ obligate anaerobes do not require oxygen for respiration **OR** respire anaerobically **OR** respire in the absence of oxygen, **OR** are poisoned by oxygen-rich environments, **OR** cannot survive **OR** grow in the presence of oxygen [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ facultative aerobes can respire aerobically or anaerobically **OR** can respire in the presence or absence of oxygen, **OR** can survive **OR** grow in the presence or absence of oxygen [1 mark] \n\n[3 marks]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\n\nObligate aerobes **require oxygen** to respire and grow ($\\checkmark$), whereas obligate anaerobes do **not require oxygen** to respire ($\\checkmark$) and are, in fact, poisoned by oxygen due to a lack of defence mechanisms against oxidation. Facultative aerobes can respire **with or without oxygen** ($\\checkmark$), they have the ability to respire aerobically and anaerobically.\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    \n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ food is ingested            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ food is digested internally [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ food is absorbed [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ food is assimilated [1 mark] \n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ undigested **OR** indigestible food is egested [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ herbivores **OR** primary consumers feed on plant material     [1 mark] \n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ carnivores **OR** omnivores **OR** secondary consumers **OR** any other trophic level above secondary consumers feed on animal material     [1 mark] \n\n[5 marks]\n\n:u[Sample answer]:\n\nHolozoic nutrition refers to a mode of nutrition in which an organism **ingests** ($\\checkmark$) the food and then **digests it internally** ($\\checkmark$). After digestion, the food is **absorbed** ($\\checkmark$) and then **assimilated** ($\\checkmark$). **Herbivores** consume plant **material**($\\checkmark$), and **carnivores** consume **animal material**($\\checkmark$). \n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ herbivorous *Hominids* consume plants **AND** omnivorous hominids eat plants and animals        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ example of herbivorous *Hominids* e.g. gorilla (*Gorrila gorrila*) \n\n    [1 mark] \n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ herbivorous *Hominids* have large masseter muscles to grind food     \n\n[1 mark] \n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ herbivorous *Hominids* have large temporal muscles that allow for forcible bites     \n\n[1 mark]             \n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ herbivorous *Hominids* have larger **OR** broader premolars **OR** molars for grinding plant matter \n\n[1 mark]      \n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ herbivorous Hominids can use their incisors to bite chunks of vegetation \n\n[1 mark] \n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ herbivorous and omnivorous Hominids can have large canines for social interactions (e.g. fighting or ritual displays)\n\n[1 mark] \n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ example of omnivorous *Hominidae* e.g. chimpanzee\n\n[1 mark] \n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ omnivorous *Hominids* have relatively smaller masseter muscles\n\n[1 mark] \n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ omnivorous *Hominids* have relatively smaller temporal muscles \n\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ omnivorous *Hominids* can use canines to grab and tear prey     \n\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ omnivorous *Hominids* can use incisors to bite chunks from prey     \n\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ omnivorous *Hominids* have relatively smaller premolars **OR** molars \n\n[1 mark]\n\n[8 marks]\n\n :u[Sample answer]:\n\n**Herbivorous Hominids** such as the **gorilla** ($\\checkmark$) have **large masseter** muscles ($\\checkmark$) and **molars** to help them **grind vegetation** ($\\checkmark$). They also have **large temporal** muscles, which allow them to **bite down** on chunks of vegetation with force ($\\checkmark$). **Omnivorous** *Hominids*, such as **chimpanzees** ($\\checkmark$), have relatively **smaller** **masseter** muscles ($\\checkmark$) and **molars** ($\\checkmark$) compared to gorillas. Unlike gorillas, chimpanzees can use their **canines** to **hold prey** or **tear flesh** ($\\checkmark$). They can also use their **incisors** to **bite chunks** out of their prey ($\\checkmark$)."}]}]},{"name":"Unity & Diversity","subtopics":[{"name":"Cell Structure","questions":[{"ref":"BI0001","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0001\n\nWhich of the following statements about cells are true?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | All living cells contain cytoplasm enclosed by the plasma membrane. |\n  | II. | Microscopes have helped scientists understand cell structure. |\n  | III. | Prokaryotes do not have genetic material. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because living cells have a plasma membrane that encloses the cytoplasm and separates the cell's internal contents from its surroundings.\n\n**Statement II** is true because microscopes allow scientists to observe cells and cellular structures that are too small to distinguish with the naked eye, helping them understand cell structure.\n\n**Statement III** is false because prokaryotic cells contain DNA as their genetic material. Their chromosomal DNA is located in the nucleoid region of the cytoplasm rather than enclosed within a nucleus. Prokaryotes lack a nucleus, not genetic material.\n\n**Choice A** is therefore correct, as it includes only the true **Statements I** and **II**.\n\n**Choices B, C** and **D** are incorrect because they include one or more incorrect statements."},{"ref":"BI0002","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0002\n\n*Paramecium* produces enzymes that catalyse different chemical reactions taking place in the cytoplasm. What function of life is demonstrated in this situation?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Excretion\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Homeostasis\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Reproduction\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Metabolism","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** - Excretion is the removal of metabolic wastes from the organism. Paramecia expel waste products such as carbon dioxide through the process of diffusion.\n\n**Choice B** - Homeostasis pertains to the ability of living things to maintain a stable internal environment. Paramecia maintain a stable internal condition by expelling excess water using the contractile vacuole.\n\n**Choice C** -   Reproduction is the biological process by which organisms produce offspring that are biologically similar to themselves. Paramecia can reproduce sexually through meiosis and asexually through mitosis.\n\n**Choice D** - Metabolism refers to all the chemical reactions in organisms such as anabolic and catabolic reactions. Paramecia produce enzymes that catalyse different catabolic and anabolic reactions in the cell."},{"ref":"BI0009","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0009\n\nThe photomicrograph shows *Chlorella*, the cells of which contain chlorophyll.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\nMagnification: 100 x $\\hspace{3em}$Scale bar = 10 µm\n\n[Source: Adapted from User: NEON. (2014). *Chlorella vulgaris Beijerinck Nies-2170 / Olympus IX71+DP72.* Wikimedia Common. Retrieved April 6, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Chlorella_vulgaris_NIES2170.jpg. Copyright by CC BY-SA 3.0]\n\nWhich statement best explains how chlorophyll supports a function of life?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ It catalyses the breakdown of glucose into ATP.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$  It absorbs light energy needed to convert inorganic molecules into organic molecules.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ It transports carbon dioxide into the chloroplast for glucose synthesis.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ It stores the energy released during cellular respiration.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** accurately describes chlorophyll’s role in photosynthesis, a process that supports the life function of nutrition in autotrophic organisms (like plants and some unicellular organisms). Chlorophyll absorbs light energy, which is used to convert inorganic carbon dioxide and water into the organic compound glucose.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because this describes cellular respiration, not the function of chlorophyll.\nThe breakdown of glucose into ATP happens in the mitochondria and does not use chlorophyll.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because chlorophyll does not transport carbon dioxide; CO₂ enters the leaf via stomata and diffuses into chloroplasts. Chlorophyll’s role is to absorb light, not transport gases.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because chlorophyll is involved in capturing light energy, not in storing energy from respiration. Energy from respiration is stored in ATP, not in chlorophyll."},{"ref":"BI0072","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0072\n\nCyanobacteria are prokaryotes that can perform photosynthesis. Which structural feature is common to both cyanobacteria and plant cells?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Chloroplast\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cell wall\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Pili\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mitochondria","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nProkaryotes are one of the earliest organisms to have evolved the process of photosynthesis. Plant cells are eukaryotes that can also perform photosynthesis, but they evolved after prokaryotes. Some of the features that distinguish prokaryotes from plant cells (which are eukaryotes) are membrane-bound organelles such as chloroplasts (**Choice A**) and mitochondria (**Choice D**) which are present in plant cells but absent in prokaryotes. According to the endosymbiotic theory, cyanobacteria evolved into chloroplasts during the early evolutionary process of eukaryotic cells.\n\nPili are found in prokaryotes but not in plant cells (**Choice C**). Even though the composition of the cell wall in prokaryotes is different (made of a peptidoglycan polymer) from the cell wall in plant cells (made up of cellulose), the presence of the cell wall is a common feature found in both prokaryotes and plant cells (**Choice B**)."},{"ref":"BI0189","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0189\n\nWhich row correctly describes the DNA and chromosomes of Eubacteria cells?\n\n| | Shape | DNA with histones | Number of chromosomes |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| Circular | Yes | More than one |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Linear | Yes | More than one |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Circular | No | One |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Linear | No | One |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nEubacteria have a single chromosome composed of DNA and not associated with histones(**Choice C**).\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because prokaryotes' DNA is not associated with histones and they only have one chromosome. **Choice B** is incorrect because prokaryotes do not have linear DNA, their DNA is not associated with histones, and they do not have more than one chromosome. **Choice D** is incorrect because prokaryotic chromosomes are not linear."},{"ref":"BI0198","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0198\n\nWhich statement about eukaryotic cells is correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Eukaryotic chromosomes are circular and the DNA is associated with histone proteins\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Eukaryotic cells have looped DNA molecules which are not associated with proteins in the nucleus\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Eukaryotic cells have linear DNA molecules associated with proteins\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Eukaryotic cells have linear DNA molecules that are not associated with histone proteins","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe chromosomes of eukaryotes are linear, and the DNA is coiled around histone proteins (**Choice C**). **Choice A** is incorrect because eukaryotic DNA molecules are not circular. **Choice B**  is incorrect because eukaryotic cells do not have looped DNA in the nucleus. **Choice D**  is incorrect because the linear DNA molecules in eukaryotic cells are coiled around histone proteins."},{"ref":"BI0199","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0199\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nAll organisms contain genetic material. The type, quantity and location of genetic material differ between kingdoms and species.\n\n1. State if the cell shown in the image is a prokaryotic or eukaryotic cell. Give a reason for your answer. [1 mark]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0199.png\" alt=\"BI0199\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Ruiz (16th January 2007). *File: Prokaryote cell diagram international.svg.* Wikimedia commons. Retrieved 18th May 2022 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Prokaryote_cell_diagram_international.svg. Copyright-free]\n\n2. Distinguish between prokaryotic and eukaryotic DNA. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. The following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cell **BECAUSE** chromosome is looped **OR** no nucleus is present     [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    This is a **prokaryotic**($\\checkmark$) cell because the chromosome is **not within a nucleus**($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic DNA is looped, **BUT** the majority of eukaryotic DNA is linear  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic DNA is not contained in a nucleus, **BUT** the majority of eukaryotic DNA is found within a nucleus     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic DNA is found in a chromosome and plasmids **WHEREAS** the majority of eukaryotic DNA is found in chromosomes and not in plasmids [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the majority of eukaryotic DNA is always associated with proteins (histones) **WHILE**  prokaryotic DNA is not bound to proteins (with the exception of *Archae* whose DNA is associated with proteins)  [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nProkaryotic DNA is looped while the majority of eukaryotic **DNA is linear**($\\checkmark$). DNA can be found in **plasmids in some prokaryotes**($\\checkmark$), while the only plasmids found in eukaryotes are present in mitochondria and chloroplasts."},{"ref":"BI0390","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0390\n\nWhich row in the table correctly identifies structures which are present ($\\checkmark$) or absent ($\\times$)  in prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells?\n\n| | Structure | Prokaryotic cells | Eukaryotic cells |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| 80S ribosomes | $\\checkmark$ | $\\times$ |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Plasma membrane | $\\times$ | $\\checkmark$ |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Nucleus | $\\checkmark$| $\\times$ |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Mitochondria | $\\times$ | $\\checkmark$|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nProkaryotic cells do not contain mitochondria but eukaryotic cells do. **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as eukaryotes (as well as *Archae*) contain 80S ribosomes. **Choice B** is incorrect as both eukaryotic and prokaryotic cells contain a plasma membrane. **Choice C** is incorrect as eukaryotic cells contain a nucleus but prokaryotic cells do not."},{"ref":"BI0391","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0391\n\nThese photographs show the same section of a human retina. What is the reason for the difference between the two diagrams?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0391_1.2.png\" alt=\"BI0391_1.2\">\n[Source: Nestl, A. (2012, January 17). *File:Opthalmology AMD Super resolution Cremer.png - Wikimedia Commons*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 16, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Opthalmology_AMD_Super_Resolution_Cremer.png Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The photograph on the right was taken using an electron microscope.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The photograph on the right is at a higher resolution.\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ The photograph on the right is at a higher magnification.\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The photograph on the right shows the ultrastructure of the cells.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe resolution is the ability to distinguish between two objects. **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choices A** and **D** are incorrect because a photograph taken using an electron microscope would allow the ultrastructure of the cells (fine detail of cells, such as organelles) to be observed. **Choice C** is incorrect because these photographs are magnified at the same power."},{"ref":"BI0392","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0392\n\nWhat type of cell is shown in the image?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0392ii.png\" alt=\"BI0392ii\">\n[Source: Adapted from Itayba, Hebrew Wikipedia. (2016, March 1). *Category: Electron Microscope Images*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved May 19, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Electron_microscopy.jpg  Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ A eukaryotic cell because it contains mitochondria\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ A prokaryotic cell because it has a plasma membrane\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ A eukaryotic cell because it does not have a nucleus\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ A prokaryotic cell because it contains ribosomes","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nEukaryotic cells contain mitochondria but prokaryotic cells do not. **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choices B** and **D** are incorrect as the cell is not prokaryotic; it has a nucleus. **Choice C** is incorrect because the cell has a nucleus."},{"ref":"BI0394","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0394\n\nWhich statement(s) is/are **not** true of eukaryotic or prokaryotic cells?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Prokaryotic cells have a compartmentalised structure.\n\n- II.$\\hspace{.8em}$ Only eukaryotic cells have a Golgi apparatus.\n\n- III.$\\hspace{.6em}$ Only eukaryotic cells have membrane-bound organelles.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    I only\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    II and III only\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is **NOT** true of prokaryotic cells because they do not have compartmentalised structures. Compartmentalisation is a defining feature of eukaryotic cells, allowing them to carry out more complex and specialised functions.\n\n**Statement II** is true of prokaryotic cells because they do not have a Golgi apparatus. The Golgi apparatus is a membrane-bound organelle, and only eukaryotic cells possess it.\n\n**Statement III** is true because membrane-bound organelles is a defining feature of eukaryotic cells. Prokaryotic cells lack membrane-bound organelles entirely.\n\n**Choice B** is the correct answer as it only includes **Statement I**.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are incorrect as they all include **Statement II**, **Statement III** or both."},{"ref":"BI0395","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0395\n\nWhich part of the cell is labelled with the letter P?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0395%20image.png\" alt=\"BI0395 image\">\n[Source: Adapted from Itayba, Hebrew Wikipedia. (2016, March 1). *Category: Electron Microscope Images.* Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved May 19, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Electron_microscopy.jpg. Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Vacuole\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    Lysosome\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    Nucleoid\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    Nucleus","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is incorrect, as the photo shows an animal cell, which can contain smaller, single membraned vacuoles, but the nucleus is much larger. **Choice B** is incorrect as lysosomes are smaller and have a single membrane. **Choice C** is incorrect as nucleoids are only present in prokaryotes and are not membrane bound."},{"ref":"BI0397","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0397\n\nWhat do the two labelled organelles have in common?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0397i_1.2.png\" alt=\"BI0397i_1.2\">\n[Source: Adapted from Itayba, Hebrew Wikipedia. (2016, March 1). *Category: Electron Microscope Images.* Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved May 19, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Electron_microscope_images Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ They have a single outer membrane\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    They contain cristae\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    They are surrounded by a double membrane\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    They contain starch grains","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nX is pointing to the nucleus and Y is pointing to a mitochondrion. **Choice A** is incorrect as neither organelle has a single membrane. **Choice B** is incorrect as only mitochondria contain cristae, the nucleus does not. **Choice D** is incorrect as neither the nucleus or mitochondria contain starch grains."},{"ref":"BI0398","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0398\n\nWhich organelle is the site of protein synthesis?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |   |   |B.|\n|<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0398_A.png\" alt=\"BI0398_A\"> |   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0398_B.png\" alt=\"BI0398_B\">|\n|C. |   |   |  |D. |\n|<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0398_C.png\" alt=\"BI0398_C\">|   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0398_D.png\" alt=\"BI0398_D\">|\n\n[Source A: Adapted from Itayba, Hebrew Wikipedia. (2016, March 1). *Category: Electron Microscope Images*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved May 19, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Electron_microscopy.jpg Copyright under CC BY 3.0.\nSource B: Adapted from: Openstax. (2016, May 18). *File:0313 endoplasmic reticulum B en.png - Wikimedia Commons*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 19, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0313_Endoplasmic_Reticulum_b_en.png Copyright under CC BY 3.0. \\\nSource C: and3k, & caper437. (2009, June 25). *File: chloroplast in leaf of anemone sp Tem 85000x.png - Wikimedia Commons*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 19, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chloroplast_in_leaf_of_Anemone_sp_TEM_85000x.png Copyright under CC BY 3.0 \\\nSource D: Murphy, F. (2016, August 10). *Free picture: Thin, section, ebola, virus*. PIXNIO. Retrieved June 19, 2022, from https://pixnio.com/science/microscopy-images/ebola-hemorrhagic-fever-virus/thin-section-containing-the-ebola-virus Copyright free]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because it shows the rough endoplasmic reticulum, which has bound ribosomes that synthesise proteins.\n\n**Choice A** shows the nucleus which contains the genetic information and controls the activity of the cell. **Choice C** shows a chloroplast, the site of photosynthesis. **Choice D** indicates the golgi apparatus which packages and transports proteins."},{"ref":"BI0399","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0399\n\nWhich organelle has a double membrane?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Rough endoplasmic reticulum\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    Golgi apparatus\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    Nucleus\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    Vacuole","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe nucleus is the only organelle among the options listed that has a double membrane. The nuclear envelope consists of two lipid bilayers: the inner membrane and the outer membrane. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choices A**, **B** and **D** are incorrect as they all have a single membrane."},{"ref":"BI0400","marks":"10","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0400\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe photomicrograph below shows a cyanobacterium, *Synechococcus elongatus*.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/nucleoid%20region%20cyanobacter.png\" alt=\"nucleoid region cyanobacter\">\n[Source: Adapted from Gonzalez, R., & Kerfeld, C. (2022, June 13). *Synechococcus elongates*. Wikipedia. Retrieved June 19, 2022, from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Synechococcus_elongatus Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n1.\n    1. State whether the cell shown in the photomicrograph is eukaryotic or prokaryotic. Give a reason for your answer. [2 marks]\n\n    2. State **two** features found in **both** eukaryotic and prokaryotic cell structures. [2 marks]\n\n    3. Identify the type of microscope which was used to produce the microphotograph above. Give a reason for your answer. [2 marks]\n\n    4. Describe the function of X. [2 marks]\n\n2. Compare and contrast the structure of the Golgi apparatus and rough endoplasmic reticulum. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (cell is) prokaryotic **OR** is a prokaryote [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (because it) does not have a nucleus **OR** has nucleoid **OR** no membrane-bound organelles **OR** a named organelle such as a Golgi apparatus, rough endoplasmic reticulum or mitochondria [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nThe cell is **prokaryotic** ($\\checkmark$) because it doesn’t have a **nucleus** ($\\checkmark$) or **membrane bound organelles**.\n\n    2. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **both** have a plasma membrane [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **both** have free ribosomes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **both** eukaryotic plant cells and prokaryotes have cell walls [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **both** have cytoplasm [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nEukaryotic and prokaryotic cells both have **a plasma membrane** ($\\checkmark$) and **free ribosomes** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    3. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electron microscope [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (because it has) higher resolution **OR** ultrastructure **OR** internal structure of the cell is visible  [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nIt is an **electron microscope** ($\\checkmark$), as the image is at a **high resolution** ($\\checkmark$) where you can clearly see the **ultrastructure** of the cell.\n\n    4.    any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contains DNA **OR** the genetic information of the cell  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA controls the activities of the cell **OR** carries instructions for protein synthesis **OR** site of transcription     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ region where DNA replication occurs    [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nX is the **nucleoid region** ($\\checkmark$), it **contains the cell’s DNA** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n    *One difference and one similarity are needed for full marks*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** consist of flattened membrane areas                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rough endoplasmic reticulum has ribosomes bound to it **WHEREAS**  the Golgi apparatus doesn’t [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Golgi apparatus has shorter cisternae **THAN** rough endoplasmic reticulum  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ part of the rough endoplasmic reticulum is continuous with the nuclear membrane **WHEREAS** the Golgi apparatus is not  [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nThe Golgi apparatus and rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER) are both made up of **flattened membrane areas** ($\\checkmark$), however the RER has ribosomes bound to the outside of it, whereas the Golgi apparatus **does not** ($\\checkmark$). Also, the Golgi apparatus has **shorter cisternae** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0414","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0414\n\nWhich statement provides evidence for the endosymbiotic theory?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Phospholipids readily form bilayers.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    Chloroplasts possess a double membrane.\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    RNA is self-replicating.\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    Mitochondria possess a single membrane.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe endosymbiotic theory states that larger prokaryotes engulfed smaller prokaryotes, which were the ancestors of modern chloroplasts and mitochondria. The smaller prokaryotes were engulfed via endocytosis, so they have their original membrane as well as the membrane of the larger prokaryote.  **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it provides evidence for the evolution of plasma membranes not endosymbiotic theory. **Choice C** is incorrect because it provides evidence for the mechanism of inheritance. **Choice D** is incorrect because mitochondria have a double membrane."},{"ref":"BI0416","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0416\n\nThe electron micrograph shows an animal cell.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0416i_1.5.png\" alt=\"BI0416i_1.5\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Itayba, Hebrew Wikipedia. (2016, March 1). *Category: Electron Microscope Images*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved May 19, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Electron_microscopy.jpg. Copyright under CC-SA 3.0]\n\nWhich organelle\\'s origin is due to the endosymbiosis of ancient cells?","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**D** has visible cristae and is oval-shaped; it is a mitochondrion. Mitochondria originated from engulfed prokaryotes via endosymbiosis but have since evolved into specialised organelles that are genetically integrated with and fully dependent on the host eukaryotic cell. **Choice D** is therefore the correct answer.\n\n**Choice A**, the cytoplasm, is not correct as it is simply the internal fluid matrix of the cell. It is part of the original cell structure and not evolved from engulfed bacteria.\n\n**Choice B**, the nucleus, is not correct as it is thought to have formed by invagination (folding inward) of the plasma membrane around the genetic material of an early cell. It does not exhibit the features of an engulfed prokaryote, such as independent circular DNA and 70S ribosomes, and therefore is not considered an endosymbiont.\n\n**Choice C**, a vacuole, is not correct as it is formed through the infolding and fusion of internal membranes within the cell."},{"ref":"BI0417","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0417\n\nWhat feature of chloroplasts provides evidence for the endosymbiotic theory?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ They have their own nucleus.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    They have a single membrane.\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    They contain 70S ribosomes.\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$     They contain a thylakoid membrane.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe endosymbiotic theory states that larger prokaryotes engulfed smaller prokaryotes, which were the ancestors of modern chloroplasts and mitochondria. The smaller prokaryotes contained their own 70S ribosomes which were distinct from the host cell\\'s 80S ribosomes. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choices A** and  **B** are incorrect because chloroplasts do not have a nucleus and have a double membrane. **Choice D**  is incorrect because although chloroplasts do contain a thylakoid membrane, this is not evidence for the endosymbiotic theory."},{"ref":"BI0418","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0418\n\nWhich statement(s) about the endosymbiotic theory is/are true?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1em}$    It provides evidence for the origin of prokaryotes.\n- II. $\\hspace{.7em}$    It provides evidence for the origin of eukaryotes.\n- III. $\\hspace{.5em}$    It provides evidence for spontaneous generation.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    I only\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    II and III only\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe endosymbiotic theory states that larger prokaryotes engulfed smaller prokaryotes, which were the ancestors of modern chloroplasts and mitochondria, eventually evolving into eukaryotes. **Choice A** is correct.\n\nStatement I (**Choices B** and **D**) is incorrect as prokaryotes were not formed by the endosymbiotic theory. Statement III (**Choice C**) is incorrect as spontaneous generation is impossible."},{"ref":"BI0420","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0420\n\nWhich statement applies to both mitochondria and prokaryotes?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ They both contain DNA.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    They both have flagella.\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    They both contain lysosomes.\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    They both have a cell wall.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nMitochondria and prokaryotes both contain their own DNA (**Choice A**).\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because not all prokaryotes have flagella (many are non-motile), and mitochondria never have flagella.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because lysosomes are membrane-bound organelles found only in eukaryotic cells.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because prokaryotes typically have a cell wall made of peptidoglycan, but mitochondria do not have cell walls."},{"ref":"BI0422","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0422\n\nWhich characteristic is **not** true for both mitochondria and chloroplasts?\n\n- A.    $\\hspace{1em}$ They are present in both eukaryotes and prokaryotes.\n\n- B.    $\\hspace{1em}$ They are both able to self replicate.\n- C.    $\\hspace{1em}$ They are both able to synthesise proteins.\n- D.    $\\hspace{1em}$ They both contain circular DNA.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nChloroplasts and mitochondria are present only in eukaryotes. Therefore **Choice A**  is the correct answer.\n\nMitochondria and chloroplasts are both able to self replicate (**Choice B**), synthesise proteins (**Choice C**) and contain circular DNA (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0423","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0423\n\nWhich statements about chloroplasts and mitochondria support the endosymbiotic theory?\n\n| | Chloroplasts | Mitochondria |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| Contain 70S ribosomes | Contain cristae |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Found in prokaryotes | Contain 80S ribosomes |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Have a single membrane | Contain circular DNA |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Contain 70S ribosomes | Have a double membrane |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe endosymbiotic theory states that larger prokaryotes engulfed smaller prokaryotes, which were the ancestors of modern chloroplasts and mitochondria. The smaller prokaryotes were engulfed via endocytosis, so they have their original membrane as well as the membrane of the larger prokaryote.   Both chloroplasts and mitochondria have 70S ribosomes and a double membrane. **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the fact that mitochondria contain cristae is not due to the endosymbiotic theory. **Choice B**  is incorrect because chloroplasts are not found in prokaryotes. **Choice C**  is incorrect because chloroplasts have a double membrane."},{"ref":"BI0424","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0424\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe electron micrograph shows a eukaryotic cell.\n\n   <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/424a.png\" alt=\"424a\">\n    [Source: Adapted from Itayba, Hebrew Wikipedia. (2016, March 1). *Category: Electron Microscope Images*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved May 19, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Electron_microscopy.jpg. Copyright under CC-SA 3.0]\n\n1.\n    1. State the name of an organelle visible in the electron micrograph that provides structural evidence for the endosymbiotic theory. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Label with the letter **S** an example of the organelle that you named in part (i). [1 mark]\n\n    3. Identify three features of the organelle named in part (i) that support the endosymbiotic theory. [3 marks]\n\n2. Contrast the structure of a mitochondrion and typical prokaryote. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mitochondria **OR** mitochondrion [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    2.\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ any mitochondrion labelled correctly with the letter **S** (mitochondria are the darker grey oval structures in the cytoplasm) [1 mark] [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n\n   <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/424b.png\" alt=\"424b\">\n\n    [Source: Adapted from Itayba, Hebrew Wikipedia. (2016, March 1). *Category: Electron Microscope Images*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved May 19, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Electron_microscopy.jpg. Copyright under CC-SA 3.0]\n\n    3. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ has a double membrane [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contains its own genes OR contains circular DNA [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contains (70S) ribosomes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can carry out transcription OR translation [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can self replicate [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n    The mitochondrion has a **double membrane** ($\\checkmark$) as it has evolved from a prokaryote that acquired a second membrane when it was engulfed by a larger prokaryote.  It also contains **circular DNA** ($\\checkmark$) and **70S ribosomes** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **two** of the following (the answer can be in a table format):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mitochondria have a double membrane **BUT** prokaryotes have a single membrane\n [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mitochondria lack a cell wall **BUT** prokaryotes have a cell wall\nt [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  mitochondria have cristae  **BUT** prokaryotes do not [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  prokaryotes may have flagella **OR** pili **BUT** mitochondria do noto [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\\\n    **Mitochondria** have a **double membrane** whereas prokaryotes do not.\n ($\\checkmark$).  Some prokaryotes have **flagella**, but mitochondria do not ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0492","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0492\n\nWhich structure is found in both eukaryotic and prokaryotic cells?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Lysosome\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ribosome\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Golgi apparatus\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Nucleoid","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nUnlike eukaryotes, prokaryotes lack a nucleus and membrane-bound organelles. However, like eukaryotes, prokaryotes contain ribosomes, a non-membrane-bound organelle required for protein synthesis. So, ribosomes are found in both prokaryotes and eukaryotes.\n\nLysosomes (**Choice A**) and the Golgi apparatus (**Choice C**) are only found in eukaryotes. The nucleoid (**Choice D**) is only found in prokaryotes."},{"ref":"BI0494","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0494\n\nThe electron micrograph below shows a cell of the bacterium *Vibrio cholerae*. Which of the labelled structures maintains the cell’s shape and protects the cell from lysing during osmosis?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0494.png\" alt=\"BI0494\">\n[Source: Blokesch, M. & Schoolnik, G.K. (2007). *Serogroup Conversion of Vibrio cholerae in Aquatic Reservoirs*. PLoS Pathog 3(6): e81. Retrieved March 15, 2022, from https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.ppat.0030081.Copyright under CC BY 4.0]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe cell membrane of prokaryotes is surrounded by a rigid cell wall. The cell wall provides shape and structure to the organism. It also prevents the cell from bursting when the cell takes up water through osmosis.  The cell wall is located between the cell membrane and the capsule, a sticky outer layer of polysaccharide. The cell wall is shown in the image below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0494A.png\" alt=\"BI0494A\">"},{"ref":"BI0531","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0531\n\nWhich structure found in a eukaryote has a double membrane?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Golgi apparatus\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mitochondrion\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Lysosome\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Endoplasmic reticulum","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nOrganelles of a eukaryotic cell are enclosed by a single or double membrane. Double membrane-bound organelles include the nucleus, mitochondria, and chloroplasts. So, a mitochondrion has a double membrane.\n\nThe Golgi apparatus (**Choice A**), lysosome (**Choice C**), and endoplasmic reticulum (**Choice D**) all have a single membrane."},{"ref":"BI0532","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0532\n\nThe following electron micrograph shows part of an exocrine pancreas cell. Which of the labelled structures is responsible for the production of vesicles for exocytosis?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0532.png\" alt=\"BI0532\">\n[Source: Adapted from Howard, L.  (n.d.). *File: Pancreatic cells - TEM.jpg*. Wikipedia.  Retrieved January, 3, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Pancreatic_cells_-_TEM.jpg. Copyright under Public Domain]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe Golgi apparatus produces vesicles that contain substances destined for exocytosis from the cell. The Golgi apparatus is composed of stacks of flattened pouches called cisternae. These appear in a micrograph as the stacked, flattened discs indicated by **Choice C**.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0532A.png\" alt=\"BI0532A\">\n[Source: Adapted from Howard, L.  (n.d.). *File: Pancreatic cells - TEM.jpg*. Wikipedia.  Retrieved January, 3, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Pancreatic_cells_-_TEM.jpg. Copyright under Public Domain]\n\n**Choice A** is the plasma membrane, **Choice B** is a lysosome and **Choice D** is the nucleus."},{"ref":"BI0533","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0533\n\nWhich organism contains DNA in just two different types of organelle?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ A tree\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ A mushroom\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ An Archaeon\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ A seaweed","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAll eukaryotic organisms contain organismal DNA in the cell nucleus and mitochondrial DNA in the mitochondria. Photosynthetic organisms additionally contain DNA in the chloroplasts. So, an organism that is a eukaryote but not photosynthetic contains DNA in exactly two organelles. A mushroom is a non-photosynthetic eukaryote, so a mushroom contains DNA in exactly two organelles.\n\nA tree (**Choice A**) and a seaweed (**Choice D**) each contain DNA in three organelles (nucleus, mitochondria, and chloroplasts). An Archaeon (**Choice C**) lacks membrane-bound organelles; its DNA is contained within the cytoplasm."},{"ref":"BI0534","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0534\n\nWhich organelle is formed by a vesicle derived from the cell membrane?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Food vacuole\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ribosome\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Golgi apparatus\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Smooth endoplasmic reticulum","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWhen a cell takes in material through endocytosis, it produces a vesicle derived from the cell membrane, which is how a food vacuole is formed.\n\nRibosomes are involved in translation (**Choice B**), the golgi apparatus sorts, packages and modifies proteins (**Choice C**) and the smooth endoplasmic reticulum, among other functions, synthesizes lipids (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0560","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0560\n\nPalisade mesophyll cells produce and release large amounts of carbohydrates. Which characteristics would you expect to observe in such cells?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ A high density of mitochondria and a large central vacuole.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ A high density of chloroplasts and an extensive Golgi apparatus.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ A high density of chloroplasts and a large central vacuole.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ A high density of mitochondria and an extensive Golgi apparatus.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because palisade mesophyll cells are the primary site of photosynthesis. A high density of chloroplasts is required to capture light energy. The large central vacuole is essential because it pushes the chloroplasts to the edges of the cell, minimising the distance carbon dioxide must diffuse and maximising light absorption.\n\n**Choices A** and **D** are incorrect because while palisade cells contain mitochondria for cellular maintenance, their dominant characteristic is the machinery for production (chloroplasts), not just the breakdown of energy.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because although the Golgi apparatus is involved in cell wall carbohydrate packaging, the central vacuole is a more significant and defining structural adaptation for the specific function of light-dependent carbohydrate production in the mesophyll."},{"ref":"BI0562","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0562\n\nThe electron micrograph shows a sarcomere, the smallest functional unit of striated muscle tissue. What is the actual length between the two Z lines?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0562i.png\" alt=\"BI0562i\">\n[Source: Adapted from Loison, O., Weitkunat , M., Kaya-Çopur , A., Nascimento Alves, C., Matzat , T., & Spletter , M. L. (2018). *Category: sarcomeres*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved March 5, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Sarcomeres Copyright under CC BY 3.0][Ruler tool, cm]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 10 µm\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 1 mm\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 1 cm\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 1 µm","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe length between the two Z lines is 4.5 cm, which is equivalent to 45000 µm. Actual length is calculated using the equation; image size divided by magnification. 45000 µm ÷ 45000 x = 1 µm.  **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **C** are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0575","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0575\n\nMesophyll cells are found in plants. Plant cells have unique characteristics that distinguish them from any other cell type.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0575.png\" alt=\"BI0575\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Zhang, N., Zhang, L., Zhao, L., Ren, Y., Cui, D., Chen, J., ... & Chen, F. (2017). *iTRAQ and virus-induced gene silencing revealed three proteins involved in the cold response in bread wheat*. Scientific reports, 7(1), 1-16.Retrieved March 27, 2022. From https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5548720/. Copyright under CC by 4.0]\n\nIn the micrograph above, one of the cell parts is labelled X. What is the name of this structure?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Chloroplast\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Central vacuole\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cell wall\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Starch granule","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** - The cellulose cell wall is the outermost layer of plant cells just outside the plasma membrane.\n\nChloroplasts (**Choice A**) are membrane-bound oval-shaped organelles (labelled Ch in the image) that are responsible for photosynthesis.\n\nThe central vacuole (**Choice B**) is located in the centre of the cell, not towards the outside.  It is evident in the micrograph provided by the white, empty space in the middle of the cell.\n\nStarch granules (**Choice D**) are structures consisting of starch.  Their function is to store starch which is produced during photosynthesis. Starch granules are not visible in the micrgraph above."},{"ref":"BI0577","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0577\n\nWhich process is most likely responsible for the origin of mitochondria as a eukaryotic cell organelle?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Osmosis\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Exocytosis\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Apoptosis\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Endocytosis","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** – Osmosis is an example of passive transport, which involves the movement of water across a semi-permeable membrane.\n\n**Choice B** - Exocytosis is an example of active transport that moves large molecules out of the cell to the cell’s exterior.\n\n**Choice C** – Apoptosis is the process of programmed cell death.\n\n**Choice D** - Endocytosis is an example of active transport that moves large molecules, parts of a cell, or a cell from the cell’s exterior, into a cell’s interior. This is in accordance with the endosymbiotic theory, which is the most likely process by which mitochondria came to be eukaryotic organelles."},{"ref":"BI0752","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0752\n\nWhat is the correct magnification of this image?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/bi0752.png\" alt=\"bi0752\">\n[Source: Adapted from Itayba, Hebrew Wikipedia (2006, March 1). *File:Electron Microscope Siemens, 1960.jpg - wikimedia commons*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved March 4, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Electron_microscope_Siemens,_1960.jpg Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 1000 x\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 100 x\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 0.1 x\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 10000 x","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nMagnification is calculated using the equation image size divided by actual size. Both numbers must be the same units. The image size of the magnification bar is 5 mm, or 5000 µm, the actual size of the magnification bar is 5 µm. 5000 µm ÷ 5 µm = 1000 x. **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choices B, C** and **D** are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0753","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0753\n\nThe electron micrograph shows a prokaryotic cell, *Cyclotella litoralis*. What is the actual diameter of the cell in this image?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/bi0753.png\" alt=\"bi0753\">\n[Source: Adapted from FWC Fish and Wildlife Research Institute. (2011, June 7). *Cyclotella litoralis (scanning electron micrograph)*. Flickr. Retrieved March 5, 2023, from https://www.flickr.com/photos/myfwc/5808259556 Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 3.5 cm\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 350 µm\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 3500 mm\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 35 µm","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe diameter of the cell is the total length, along the widest part of the cell; 3.5 cm. When the scale bar, which represents 10 µm, is measured, it is shown to be 1 cm long in the image. 3.5 multiplied by 10 is 35 µm.  **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **C** are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0756","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0756\n\nWhat feature distinguishes fungal hyphae and striated muscle from the two cell types shown in the images?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/bi0756.png\" alt=\"bi0756\">\n[Source: Adapted from CNX OpenStax. (2016, May 27). *File:Figure 04 00 00.jpg.* Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved March 5, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Figure_04_00_00.jpg Copyright under CC by 3.0]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ They are multinucleate.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ They only have one nucleus.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ They have unusually large cells that contain only one nucleus.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ They contain mitochondria.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nFungal hyphae and striated muscle are both exceptions to the cell theory as they are much larger than typical cells and are multinucleate. **Choice B**  is incorrect as the diagrams depict onion epidermis cells and mast cells, which each contain one nucleus. **Choice C**  is incorrect as both fungal hyphae and striated muscles contain multiple nuclei. **Choice D** is incorrect as fungal hyphae, striated muscle AND the cells in the diagram all contain mitochondria."},{"ref":"BI0757","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0757\n\nWhich statements about unicellular and multicellular organisms are correct?\n\n| | Unicellular organisms | Multicellular organisms|\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | They only reproduce asexually | Contains differentiated cells |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Consist of only one cell | All cellular processes are carried out by one organelle |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Can reproduce sexually | They have emergent properties |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Contains differentiated cells | Can reproduce sexually |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A**  is incorrect, as not all unicellular organisms reproduce asexually. **Choice B** is incorrect, as both unicellular and multicellular organisms contain multiple organelles that carry out different functions. **Choice D**  is incorrect, because unicellular organisms do not contain more than one cell, and so do not contain multiple differentiated cells."},{"ref":"BI0798","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0798\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe micrograph shows a transverse section through a leaf.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0798A.png\" alt=\"BI0798A\">\n[Source:Adapted from Berkshire Community College Bioscience Image Library,2018. *Angiosperm Morphology Mesophyll Arrangement in Ligustrum (36198182664).jpg. Wikimedia Commons*. Retrieved 29th January 2023 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Angiosperm_Morphology_Mesophyll_Arrangement_in_Ligustrum_(36198182664).jpg.Copyright under Creative Commons CC0 1.0]\n\n1.\n    1. Identify an adaptation of this leaf for its function.    [1 mark]\n\n    2. Identify the cell organelle that is found in particularly high numbers in the palisade mesophyll cells. [1 mark]\n\nThis micrograph shows a transverse section through a pancreatic acinar cell. Acinar cells are found in the exocrine tissue of the pancreas.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0798B.png\" alt=\"BI0798B\">\n[Source: Adapted from Howard, L., 2006. *Pancreatic acinar cells.jpg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 29th January 2023 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?search=pancreatic+cells&title=Special:MediaSearch&go=Go&type=image&haslicense=unrestricted. Copyright under Public Domain]\n\n2.\n    1. Explain how the structure of this cell is related to the function of the synthesis of proteins for release from the cell. [2 marks]\n\n    2. State why an electron microscope is needed to view the ultrastructure of the\nacinar cells. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large number of chloroplasts    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large surface area                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ air spaces **OR** spongy mesophyll                        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ vascular tissues **OR** veins **OR** vascular bundles                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stomata **OR** guard cells                            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ waxy cuticle                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ columnar cells in top layer **OR** palisade mesophylll    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The leaf has a **large surface area** ($\\checkmark$) for absorption of light.\n\n1.\n    2.\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chloroplasts    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The organelle in greater abundance in the palisade mesophyll cells is **the chloroplast** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleus separated from cytoplasm by membrane **AND** allowing post-transcriptional modification of mRNA    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large numbers of membrane-bound ribosomes **OR** rough endoplasmic reticulum **AND** for translation **OR** synthesis of proteins for secretion    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large amount of rough endoplasmic reticulum **AND** lumen of rough endoplasmic reticulum is where polypeptides are folded and modified to form proteins    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ many vesicles **AND** used to transport proteins from rough endoplasmic reticulum to Golgi apparatus **OR** to carry proteins to plasma **OR** cell membrane for secretion    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Golgi apparatus **AND** receives proteins from rough endoplasmic reticulum and modifies **OR** packages proteins for release from  cell [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The structure of this cell is adapted to its function because it contains a large amount of **rough endoplasmic reticulum**. The **ribosomes** on the rough endoplasmic reticulum are the site of the **synthesis** of the **polypeptides** ($\\checkmark$) and these **polypeptides** are then **folded and modified into functional proteins** in the **lumen** of the **rough endoplasmic reticulum** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electron microscopes have a higher resolution than light microscopes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electron microscopes have a higher magnification than light microscopes    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Electron microscopes have **higher magnification** ($\\checkmark$) and **higher resolution** ($\\checkmark$)  than light microscopes, so they allow more detail to be seen in smaller objects."},{"ref":"BI0799","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0799\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nThe micrograph below shows *E. coli* cells.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0799A.png\" alt=\"BI0799A\">\n[Source: Adapted from Highton, P., 1968. *E.coli image.jpeg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 29th January 2023 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:E.coli_image.jpg. Copyright under Creative CommonsCC0 1.0]\n\n1. Identify the structure labelled I in the micrograph. [1 mark]\n\n2. If the magnification of the image is x36,000 and the length of cell II in the image is 54 mm, calculate the actual length of cell II in micrometres (µm). [2 marks]\n\n3. State **one** feature, other than the structure labelled I, that can be used to identify the cells in this micrograph as prokaryotic cells. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ Nucleoid **OR** region of circular DNA **OR** region of bacterial chromosome **OR** circular DNA    [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:  \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Structure I is the **nucleoid** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ = $\\left(\\dfrac{\\text{54000 µm}}{\\text{36000}}\\right) $\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ = 1.5µm    [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:  \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Magnification = x36000\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Length in image = 54mm x 1000 = 54000 µm\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Actual length\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ = $\\left(\\dfrac{\\textbf{54000 µm}}{\\textbf{36000}}\\right) $  ($\\checkmark$)\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ = **1.5 µm** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell wall of peptidoglycan    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell in binary fission        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ no compartments in cell **OR** no membrane bound organelles **OR** no nucleus    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:  \\\n    The cell does **not have** a nucleus, or any other **membrane bound organelles** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0804","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0804\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe diagram below shows a cell.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/804.png\" alt=\"804\">\n[Source: Adapted from Gonzalez, R., & Kerfeld, C. (2022, June 13). *Synechococcus elongatus*. Wikipedia. Retrieved June 19, 2022, from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Synechococcus_elongatus Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n1.\n    1. Deduce with a reason, what type of cell this is.    [1 mark]\n\n    2. Calculate the magnification of the cell.            [1 mark]\n\n    3. Outline two functions of life carried out by the cell in the diagram. [2 marks]\n\n2.\n    1. State the type of microscope used to produce the image.    [1 mark]\n\n    2. Explain your choice of microscope for part (i).            [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. Prokaryote *(needed for mark)*\n\n    then any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ no nucleus    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ has a nucleoid    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ no membrane bound organelles **OR** a named organelle such as a Golgi apparatus, rough endoplasmic reticulum or mitochondria    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ size is typical of prokaryote (0.1μm - 5μm)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rod shaped    [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The cell is a **prokaryotic**  cell as it contains **no nucleus** ($\\checkmark$) or **membrane bound organelles** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    2.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 110,000 x    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    magnification= image size ÷  actual size\n\n    image size = 11 mm = 11,000,000 nm\n\n    actual size = 100 nm\n\n    11,000,000nm ÷ 100 nm = **110,000** x ($\\checkmark$)\n\n1.\n    3. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nutrition: consuming food for energy **OR** metabolic processes **OR** growth **OR** reproduction    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reproduction: producing offspring    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ metabolism: chemical reactions that occur within the cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ homeostasis: maintaining a stable internal environment    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ growth: a permanent  increase in number **OR** size of cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ excretion: the removal of metabolic waste    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ response: the ability to react to changes in the environment [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ movement: change in position OR location in relation to the surroundings [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Two functions of life carried out by the cell are **excretion**, which is the **removal of metabolic waste** ($\\checkmark$) and **reproduction** which is **producing offspring** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electron microscope    **OR** transmission electron microscope        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    An **electron microscope** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it allows you to see the ultrastructure of the cell        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it has a high resolution    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it has a high magnification        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    An electron microscope would be used to create the image, as it allows one to see the **ultrastructure** of the cell ($\\checkmark$) due to its **high resolution**  and **magnification** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0805","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0805\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nThe diagram below shows a cell.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0805A.png\" alt=\"BI0805A\">\n[Source: Adapted from Gonzalez, R., & Kerfeld, C. (2022, June 13). *Synechococcus elongatus*. Wikipedia. Retrieved June 19, 2022, from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Synechococcus_elongatus Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n1. Compare and contrast the structure of a mictochondrion with the structure of the cell shown in the diagram. [2 marks]\n\n2. Explain why the cell in the diagram is structurally similar to a mitochondrion.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    **Similarities**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **both** have plasma membranes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **both** contain circular **OR** naked DNA        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mitochondrial ribosomes are more similar in size **OR** structure to prokaryotes than eukaryotes **OR both** contain 70S ribosomes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **both** are similar in size        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **both** are similar shapes **OR** oval **OR** elongated        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **both** have ATPase proteins embedded in surrounding membrane        [1 mark]\n\n    **Differences**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mitochondria do not have a cell wall **OR** a capsule whereas, prokaryotes do        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotes may have flagella **OR** pili but mitochondria do not        [1 mark]\n\n    *Answer must include at least one difference and one similarity to gain full marks*\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The cell in the diagram is a prokaryotic cell and is similar in structure to a mitochondrion, as mitochondrial **ribosomes** are more **similar in size** and **structure** to prokaryotes than eukaryotes, ($\\checkmark$), and they both have **naked circular DNA** ($\\checkmark$). However mitochondria **do not have a cell wall** ($\\checkmark$), whereas **prokaryotic cells do**.\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ endosymbiotic theory    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mitochondria evolved from free-living prokaryotes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ that were engulfed **BUT** not digested by larger prokaryotes    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The prokaryotic cell in the diagram has structural similarities to a mitochondrion, due to the **endosymbiotic theory** ($\\checkmark$) which explains that mitochondria evolved from **free-living prokaryotes** ($\\checkmark$) that were **engulfed though not digested** ($\\checkmark$) by larger cells."},{"ref":"BI0816","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0816\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe micrograph shows an image of a plant cell taken with a light microscope.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0816A.png\" alt=\"BI0816A\">\n[Source: Adapted from CNX OpenStax. (2016, May 27). *File:Figure 04 00 00new.jpg.* Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 14, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Figure_04_00_00new.jpg . Copyright free]\n\n1. Calculate the magnification of the image, showing your work. [2 marks]\n\n2. Label, with the letter N, a nucleus in the image. [1 mark]\n\n3. The image was taken using a light microscope. State how you deduce this from the image. [1 mark]\n\n4. Draw and label a diagram to show how the same type of plant cell shown in the micrograph would appear when examined with an electron microscope. [3 marks]\n\n5. State two differences that would be seen when looking at an electron micrograph of a bacterium cell compared to this plant cell. [2 marks]\n\n6. At first, the student who took this image could not see the cell when focusing the microscope. Suggest what may have caused this problem. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 4000 ÷ 50 **OR** size of image/ size of specimen [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 80 x [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0816ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0816ANSA\">\n [Source: Adapted from CNX OpenStax. (2016, May 27). *File:Figure 04 00 00new.jpg*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 14, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Figure_04_00_00new.jpg. Copyright free]\n\n Magnification = Image size ÷ Actual size\n\n Image size = 39 - 35 = 4 mm\n\n 1mm = 1000 µm\n\n 4mm = 4000 µm\n\n Actual size = 50 µm\n\n Magnification = **4000 µm ÷ 50 µm** ($\\checkmark$) = **80 x** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ labelled arrow N pointing to a nucleus [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0816ANSB.png\" alt=\"BI0816ANSB\">\n [Source: Adapted from CNX OpenStax. (2016, May 27). *File:Figure 04 00 00new.jpg*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 14, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Figure_04_00_00new.jpg. Copyright free]\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cannot see the ultrastructure of the cell **OR** ribosomes **OR** mitochondria **OR** rough endoplasmic reticulum **OR** Golgi apparatus **OR** vacuoles [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ low resolution [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n It is possible to deduce that the image was taken with a light microscope as the **ultrastructure** of the cell is **not visible** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ diagram must contain a large angular shape, with a labelled nucleus, cell membrane **AND** cell wall [1 mark]\n\n **Labels:**\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 80S ribosomes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mitochondria [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Golgi apparatus [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ rough endoplasmic reticulum [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ vacuole [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ smooth endoplasmic reticulum [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleolus [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0816ANSCOR.png\" alt=\"BI0816ANSCOR\">\n\n5. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 70s ribosomes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ flagellum [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleoid region [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ pili [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Two different structures that could be seen when looking at a bacterium as opposed to the plant cells through an electron microscope are **pili** ($\\checkmark$) and the **nucleoid region** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. Any **one** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the light was off **OR** not bright enough **OR** diaphragm was not open enough [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the cell was not directly below the lens [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the microscope was not focused sufficiently [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the slide **OR** coverslip was too dirty [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the objective lens was not clicked in correctly **OR** not appropriately housed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the lens **OR** eyepiece was dirty [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The student may not have been able to see the cell as it was **not placed directly below the lens** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0833","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0833\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nBelow is a diagram of a motor neuron. Neurons are the longest cells in the human body.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI0833A.png\" alt=\"BI0833A\">\n\n1. Explain the factors that limit cell size in humans. [4 marks]\n\nBelow is a diagram of a *Paramecium*, a unicellular organism.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/paramecium%20black.png\" alt=\"paramecium black\">\n\n2. Explain how life functions are performed by *Paramecium*. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **four** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ surface area affects the rate at which materials enter and leave the cell [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ volume affects the rate at which materials are used and produced by the cell [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the surface area to volume ratio affects the rate of metabolism in a cell [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the bigger the cell, the smaller the SA:V ratio is [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cells having a low SA:V ratio cannot exchange materials fast enough [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ some cells, **OR** motor neurons, **OR** muscle cells, **OR** other named example in humans, are long and thin, which maintains a high SA:V ratio [1 mark]\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n The **surface area** of a cell affects the rate at which materials **enter and leave cells** ($\\checkmark$). On the other hand, the cell’s **volume** affects the rate at which materials are **used and produced** ($\\checkmark$). A small surface area to volume ratio is not ideal for human cells because it **limits the cell’s ability to export waste from the cytoplasm** rapidly enough to meet the **cytoplasm’s metabolic needs, such as the availability of oxygen** ($\\checkmark$). **A large cell has a small surface area to volume ratio** ($\\checkmark$). Therefore, the transport of the substances is more efficient in small cells.\n\n2. Any **four** of the following (function of life must be mentioned when explaining how *Paramecium* performs this, to award mark):\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ functions of life are metabolism **AND** growth **AND** reproduction **AND** nutrition **AND** homeostasis **AND** movement **AND** excretion **AND** response to stimuli, **OR** sensitivity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ movement in *Paramecium* is demonstrated by cilia used to propel themselves through water [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ response is through reacting to stimuli in the environment **OR** avoidance of light [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ homeostasis is demonstrated by keeping the internal conditions within set limits **OR** excess water is expelled by the contractile vacuole [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ metabolism is carried out by enzymes in the cytoplasm [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ growth is an irreversible increase in size **OR** dry mass and paramecium achieve this through accumulating organic matter **OR** nutrients from food [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ reproduction function in *Paramecium* is demonstrated by asexual division through binary fission, **OR** mitosis or micronucleus and amitotic division of macronucleus **OR** sexually through conjugation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ excretion is the removal of metabolic waste products from the cell **OR** diffusion of CO:sub[2] out of the cell [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nutrition is performed by ingesting smaller organisms and digesting them in vesicles **OR** through endocytosis [1 mark]\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n *Paramecium* acquires **nutrients** by ingesting bacteria and **digesting them in vesicles**($\\checkmark$). It exhibits **growth** when it **irreversibly increases in size** by accumulating organic matter from ingested **food** ($\\checkmark$). *Paramecium* **responds** to different environmental stimuli. For example, it **changes** the **direction** of its movement once it **touches** a solid object ($\\checkmark$). **Excretion** in *Paramecium* occurs when waste products such as **carbon dioxide diffuse out of the cell** ($\\checkmark$). *Paramecium* **reproduces** both asexually by **binary fission** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1049","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1049\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline one way unicellular organisms can carry out each of nutrition, movement and excretion. [3 marks]\n\n2. Draw a single mitochondrion, and annotate it to outline the role of four labelled structures. [5 marks]\n\n3. Compare and contrast the structure of E. coli and a palisade mesophyll cell. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nutrition by diffusion through cell membrane **OR** engulfment **OR** phagocytosis **OR** endocytosis **OR** photosynthesis **OR** digestion by enzymes in vacuoles [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ movement by cilia **OR** flagella **OR** pseudopodia [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ excretion by simple diffusion **OR** active transport **OR** exocytosis **OR** contractile vacuoles **OR** vacuoles storing **OR** isolating waste before removal [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Unicellular organisms carry out the processes of life in varying ways. They can carry out **nutrition** by **engulfing** the nutrients ($\\checkmark$) from their surroundings, they can **move** using **flagella** ($\\checkmark$), and they can **remove wastes** from the cell by **simple diffusion** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Diagram must include four of the following labels and annotations:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ outer membrane protective barrier **OR** contains transport proteins that allow specific molecules to enter the mitochondrion [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ inner membrane **OR** cristae increase surface area for reactions **OR** contains proteins essential for reactions **OR** ATP production [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA encodes genes essential for mitochondrial functioning [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 70S ribosomes for synthesis of proteins required by mitochondrion [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ matrix contains enzymes for chemical reactions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ intermembrane space - allows build-up of protons **OR** ion gradient for ATP synthesis **OR** chemiosmosis **OR** contains enzymes for chemical reactions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ clear drawing with no sketchy lines and of suitable size [1 mark]\n [5 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI1049.png\" alt=\"BI1049\">\n\n3. any **eight** of the following (*must include at least **two** similarity and **two** differences to gain full marks*):\n\n ***Similarities***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** *E.coli* and palisade mesophyll cells have cell **OR** plasma membranes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** *E.coli* and palisade mesophyll cells have cytoplasm [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** *E.coli* and palisade mesophyll cells have a cell wall [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** *E.coli* and palisade mesophyll cells contain ribosomes [1 mark]\n\n ***Differences***\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ *E.coli* cell wall is made up of peptidoglycan **WHEREAS** palisade mesophyll cell walls are made of cellulose [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ palisade mesophyll cells have a compartmentalised structure **WHEREAS** *E.coli* do not [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ palisade mesophyll cells have 80S ribosomes **WHEREAS** *E.coli* have 70S [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ palisade mesophyll cells have nuclei **WHEREAS** *E.coli* have a nucleoid [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ *E.coli* can have plasmids **OR** circular DNA **WHEREAS** palisade mesophyll cells have linear DNA [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ *E.coli* have external structures such as flagella **OR** pili **BUT** palisade mesophyll cells do not [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ palisade mesophyll cells have membrane bound organelles such as golgi apparatus, rough endoplasmic reticulum, chloroplasts, mitochondria **WHEREAS** *E.coli* do not [1 mark]\n [8 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Palisade mesophyll cells and *E.coli* both have **cell membranes** ($\\checkmark$) and **cytoplasm** ($\\checkmark$). They also both can have **cell walls** ($\\checkmark$), however the cell wall in *E.coli* is composed of **peptidoglycan**, whereas the cell wall in palisade mesophyll cells is composed of **cellulose** ($\\checkmark$). Palisade mesophyll cells are full of **membrane bound** organelles such as the golgi apparatus, which gives them a **compartmentalised** structure, whereas *E.coli* **do not** have a compartmentalised structure or membrane bound organelles ($\\checkmark$). Both palisade mesophyll cells and *E.coli* have **ribosomes** ($\\checkmark$), but *E.coli* have **70S** ribosomes and palisade mesophyll cells have **80S** ribosomes ($\\checkmark$). DNA is also stored differently in palisade mesophyll cells and *E.coli*. Palisade mesophyll cells store their DNA inside the **nucleus** and it has a **linear** structure, whereas *E.coli* have DNA floating freely in the cytoplasm as a **nucleoid** ($\\checkmark$) and can sometimes contain extra DNA in the form of a **plasmid** ($\\checkmark$). E.coli can also have external structures such as **pili** or flagella ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1050","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1050\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Distinguish between a colony of algal cells forming a multicellular sphere and a multicellular plant such as a water lily. [4 marks]\n\n2. Draw a labelled diagram which shows the structure of an animal cell. [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe the evidence for the endosymbiotic theory. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ colony of algal cells are generally undifferentiated **WHEREAS** multicellular plants like water lilies are composed of different types **OR** differentiated of cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ no **OR** minimal organisation **OR** minimal division of labour of cells in algal colony, **WHEREAS** in multicellular plant cells arranged into tissues, organs and organ systems, **OR** well-defined structures carry out specific roles in plants [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ each individual algal cell is capable of performing all necessary functions for life **OR** survival independently **WHEREAS** most individual cells of multicellular plants cannot survive independently **OR** away from the main body of the plant [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cells in an algal colony show limited coordination **OR** communication, **WHEREAS** plant cells are connected by plasmodesmata and coordinated by plant hormones **OR** signalling [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ development of colonies is often simple **WHEREAS** plants undergo complex developmental processes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ reproduction in an algal colony typically involves asexual methods **WHEREAS** in multicellular plants such as the water lily typically involves sexual reproduction **OR** multicellular plants use both sexual and asexual reproduction [1 mark]\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Unlike true multicellular organisms like water lilies, algal colonies **lack cellular differentiation** and **specialised tissues**. **Individual cells** within the colony are largely independent, being able to perform all vital functions, and show little coordination with one another. In contrast, cells in the multicellular plant communicate through plasmodesmata and hormones ($\\checkmark$) and usually **do not survive** when **separated** from the plant ($\\checkmark$). These cells become **specialised** ($\\checkmark$) and form **tissues, organs**, and **organ systems** within the plant. This intricate **division of labour** ($\\checkmark$) allows plants to achieve greater size and complexity.\n\n2. any **four** of the following labelled correctly in an appropriate drawing of an animal cell:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleus (shown with double membrane) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cytoplasm [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cell **OR** plasma membrane [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mitochondrion [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ribosome [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ golgi apparatus [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ rough endoplasmic reticulum [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ smooth endoplasmic reticulum [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ lysosome [1 mark]\n\n and the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ diagram drawn without sketchy lines and acceptable size [1 mark]\n [5 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI1050.png\" alt=\"BI1050\">\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ larger prokaryotes **OR** cells engulfed smaller prokaryotes **OR** cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ingested cell was kept alive [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ symbiotic relationship between both cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ingested cell acquired a second membrane [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ingested cell is ancestor of chloroplasts **OR** mitochondria [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chloroplast **OR** mitochondrial structures provide evidence for the endosymbiotic theory [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chloroplasts **OR** mitochondria contain 70S ribosomes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chloroplasts **OR** mitochondria contain their own DNA **OR** looped DNA [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chloroplasts **OR** mitochondria can conduct transcription **OR** translation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chloroplasts **OR** mitochondria divide in a similar manner to prokaryotic cells **OR** by binary fission [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chloroplasts **OR** mitochondria are similar in size to prokaryotes [1 mark]\n [7 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The endosymbiotic theory explains the origin of some structures in eukaryotic cells, including chloroplasts and mitochondria. The **ancestors** of chloroplasts and mitochondria were prokaryotes that **were engulfed by larger prokaryotes** ($\\checkmark$). The ingested cells were **kept alive** ($\\checkmark$) and both cells eventually developed a **symbiotic** relationship ($\\checkmark$) with the cell that engulfed them. When the larger cells engulfed the smaller cells, the smaller cells gained a **second membrane** ($\\checkmark$) as they were engulfed via endocytosis. Further evidence for the theory is that chloroplasts and mitochondria contain their own **70s ribosomes** ($\\checkmark$) so can **make their own proteins**, and also contain **their own DNA** ($\\checkmark$) so can carry out **transcription** ($\\checkmark$). Chloroplasts and mitochondria are also able to **divide in a similar manner to prokaryotic cells** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1136","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1136\n\nWhat is the actual width of the cell in this image? The scale bar represents 5μm.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI1135.png\" alt=\"BI1135\">\n[Source: Adapted from Itayba, Hebrew Wikipedia (2006, March 1). *File: Electron Microscope Siemens, 1960*.jpg - wikimedia commons. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved March 4, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Electron_microscope_Siemens,_1960.jpg Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 4μm\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 40μm\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 400μm\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 4000μm","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe scale bar is shown to be 5μm in width, which is represented by 0.5cm on the ruler. The ruler indicates a total cell width of 4cm. Therefore, 8 scale bar widths will fit within the cell. Actual cell width = 8 $\\times$ 5μm = 40μm.\n\n**Choices A, C**, and **D** are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1137","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1137\n\nWhich row in the table correctly identifies structures which are present (✓) or absent (✕)  in prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells?\n\n| | Structure | Prokaryotic cells | Eukaryotic cells |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 70S Ribosomes in Cytosol | ✓ | ✕ |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Cell Wall | ✕ | ✓ |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Mitochondria | ✓ | ✕ |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Naked DNA | ✕ | ✓ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nProkaryotic cells contain 70S ribosomes in their cytoplasm, while eukaryotic cells have 80s cytoplasmic ribosomes, so **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect as prokaryotes have a cell wall, but not all eukaryotes do. **Choice C** is incorrect as prokaryotes do not contain membrane-bound organelles such as mitochondria, but eukaryotes do. ***Choice D** is incorrect, as prokaryotic cells have naked DNA, whereas eukaryotic cells contain DNA within a nuclear membrane."},{"ref":"BI1223","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1223\n\nWhich structure in a eukaryotic cell would also be present in a prokaryotic cell?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Nucleus\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Plasma membrane\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 80S ribosomes\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Histones","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nBoth eukaryotic and prokaryotic cells contain a plasma membrane; **Choice B**  is correct.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D**  are incorrect as they are present in eukaryotic cells but not prokaryotic cells."},{"ref":"BI1258","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1258\n\nBelow is a micrograph of a bacterium, *Marinomonas arctica*, which is found in Arctic sea ice.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI1258.png\" alt=\"BI1258\">\n[Source: Gulf of Alaska Seamounts expedition, 2019. *Marinomonas arctica.jpg*. Wikimedia. Retrieved, September 25th 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Marinomonas_arctica.jpg. Copyright under Public Domain]\n\nWhich row contains features of prokaryotic cells such as *Marinomonas arctica*?\n\n| | Ribosomes | Cell wall | DNA associated with proteins\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 80S | Yes | No |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 80S | Yes | Yes |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 70S | No | No |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 70S | Yes | No |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because bacteria are prokaryotes, and prokaryotes have 70S ribosomes and a cell wall; they do not have proteins associated with the DNA. **Choice A** is incorrect because prokaryotes do not have 80S ribosomes. **Choice B** is incorrect because prokaryotes do not have 80S ribosomes or proteins associated with DNA. **Choice C** is incorrect because prokaryotes do have cell walls."},{"ref":"BI1259","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1259\n\nWhich of the following correctly describes the images produced by electron microscopes?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | High resolution |\n    | II. | High magnification |\n    | III. | Coloured |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nElectron micrographs have high resolution (**Statement I**) and high magnification (**Statement II**) **Choice B** is therefore correct.\n\nElectron micrographs are not coloured (**Statement III**). **Choice A** is incorrect as it only includes one correct statement.  **Choices C** and **D** are incorrect because they include **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI1263","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1263\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline how some eukaryotic cells have an atypical cell structure.            [3 marks]\n\n2. Use named examples to explain how unicellular organisms carry out processes of life.    [5 marks]\n\n3. Compare and contrast the structure of prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells.        [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **three** from the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mature red blood cells do not have a nucleus    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ striated muscle cells have many nuclei    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ aseptate fungal hyphae **OR** fungal hyphae **OR** *Mucor* are multinucleate     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phloem sieve tube elements lack nuclei **OR** do not have many organelles **OR** have continuous cytoplasm flowing through adjacent cells **OR** have sieve tube elements to provide metabolic support **OR** are interconnected cells.    [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Mature red blood cells **do not have a nucleus** ($\\checkmark$), mature sieve tube elements **do not have a nucleus** ($\\checkmark$) and striated muscle cells and fungal hyphae have **many nuclei** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **five** of the following characteristics and a correct way the chosen unicellular organism carries out that process:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cilia allow movement in *Paramecium* **OR** flagella allow movement in *Chlamydomonas* **OR** any other suitable mode of movement relevant to a named unicellular organism    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contractile vacuole helps to regulate water potential of the cell **OR** contractile vacuole has a role in homeostasis in *Paramecium* **OR** *Chlamydomonas* **OR** any other mechanism of homeostasis that is relevant to a named unicellular organism    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enzyme-catalysed metabolic reactions such as respiration occur in the cytoplasm and mitochondria of *Paramecium* **OR** photosynthesis occurs in the chloroplast of *Chlamydomonas* **OR** correct example of metabolism for a named unicellular organism    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oral groove allows nutrition in *Paramecium* **OR** nutrition is by photosynthesis in *Chlamydomonas* **OR** correct mode of nutrition for a named unicellular organism    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *Paramecium* can reproduce asexually by binary fission **OR** *Paramecium* can reproduce by conjugation **OR** *Chlorella* reproduces asexually by mitosis    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ metabolic wastes such as CO$_2$ and ammonia diffuse out across the cell membrane in *Paramecium* **OR** metabolic wastes diffuse out across the cell membrane in *Chlamydomonas* **OR** correct mode of excretion for a named unicellular organism (do not accept removal of undigested food through the anal pore, as this is egestion)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ growth of *Paramecium* occurs after cell division **OR** *Chlamydomonas* increases in size by synthesising new cell components between divisions **OR** correct mode of growth for a named unicellular organism    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *Paramecium* sense their environment and respond by movement due to the movement of cilia **OR** *Chlamydomonas* has an eyespot to detect light and responds by swimming towards it using its flagella (phototaxis) **OR** correct example of sensitivity for a named unicellular organism    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    *Paramecia* are unicellular organisms that can carry out all functions of life. *Paramecia* have **cilia that allow movement** ($\\checkmark$). **Metabolic reactions** ($\\checkmark$) happen in mitochondria and the cytoplasm. The contractile vacuole helps to **regulate the water concentration** ($\\checkmark$) of *Paramecium* by releasing excess water. The oral groove takes in food, so it is responsible for **nutrition** ($\\checkmark$). *Paramecium* reproduces asexually by **binary fission** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **eight** of the following: \\\n*Answer **must** include at least **one** similarity and **one** difference to gain full marks*\n\n    **Similarities:** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic **AND** eukaryotic cells have plasma membranes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic **AND** eukaryotic cells have cytoplasm        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic **AND** eukaryotic cells have ribosomes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic **AND** eukaryotic cells have genetic material **OR** DNA **OR** RNA    [1 mark]\n\n    **Differences:** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells are not compartmentalised  **BUT** eukaryotic cells are compartmentalised    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells do not have membrane-bound organelles **BUT** eukaryotic cells do have membrane-bound organelles            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells have DNA in a loop **OR** plasmid **BUT** eukaryotes have linear DNA **OR** chromosomes         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells have 70S ribosomes **BUT** eukaryotic cells have 80S ribosomes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells do not have a true nucleus **OR** have a nucleoid region **BUT** eukaryotic cells do have a true nucleus    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ all prokaryotic cells have a cell wall **BUT** not all eukaryotic cells have a cell wall    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic DNA is naked **OR** not associated with proteins **BUT** eukaryotic DNA is associated with histone proteins        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells are smaller **THAN** eukaryotic cells        [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells have **genetic material** ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells have **plasma membranes** ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells have **cytoplasm** ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells have **ribosomes** ($\\checkmark$), but **prokaryotes have 70S ribosomes, whereas eukaryotic cells have 80S ribosomes** ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotic cells **do not have a true nucleus**, but eukaryotic cells do have a true nucleus ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotic cells do not have membrane-bound organelles but **eukaryotic cells do have membrane-bound organelles** such as mitochondria ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotic cells have **DNA in a loop** but eukaryotic cells have linear DNA ($\\checkmark$). Not all eukaryotic cells have a cell wall but **all prokaryotic cells do have a cell wall** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n*A quality mark is awarded if each part of the question (a, b, c) is answered to earn at least half of the points available, answers do not require re-reading to be understood, and there are almost no errors and little irrelevant information.* [1 mark]"},{"ref":"BI1322","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1322\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Distinguish between the structure and location of chromosomes found in eukaryotes and prokaryotes.            [5 marks]\n\n2. Describe the stages of meiosis and how genetically unique, haploid gametes are created during the process.    [8 marks]\n\n3. Explain the information that can be deduced from a human karyogram.        [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ eukaryotic chromosomes are found in the nucleus **WHEREAS** prokaryotic chromosomes are found in the nucleoid region **OR** chromosomes of eukaryotes are contained within a membrane **WHEREAS** prokaryotic chromosomes are not membrane bound     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotes have a circular chromosome **WHEREAS** eukaryotes have linear chromosomes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotes typically have one chromosome **WHEREAS** eukaryotes typically have multiple chromosomes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in eukaryotic chromosomes, DNA is associated with histone proteins **WHEREAS** prokaryotic chromosomes do not contain histones        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic chromosomes do not contain centromeres **WHEREAS** eukaryotic chromosomes do [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic chromosomes do not contain telomeres **WHEREAS** eukaryotic chromosomes do     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ eukaryotic chromosomes carry more genetic information **OR** DNA **OR** genes **THAN** prokaryotic chromosomes    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Eukaryotic chromosomes are found in the **nucleus** whereas prokaryotic chromosomes are found in the **nucleoid** region ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotes typically contain **one, circular** chromosome ($\\checkmark$)  whereas eukaryotes  have **multiple, linear** chromosomes ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotic chromosomes **do not** contain **centromeres** and **telomeres** ($\\checkmark$), whereas eukaryotic chromosomes do ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ all the DNA in a cell is replicated during interphase        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ so cells are diploid **OR** contain homologous chromosomes when they enter meiosis    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous chromosomes pair **AND** become visible **OR** condense  in prophase I    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ crossing over occurs between the non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes during prophase I    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous chromosomes are moved to the equator of the cell by spindle microtubules **AND** randomly orientated OR independently assorted in metaphase I [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous chromosomes are pulled to opposite sides of the cell by spindle microtubules in anaphase I            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ one diploid cell is split into two haploid cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chromatids condense **OR** become visible during prophase II        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pairs of sister chromatids are moved to the equator of the cell by spindle microtubules during metaphase II **AND** randomly orientated **OR** independently assorted    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pairs of sister chromatids are separated by spindle fibres in anaphase II        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nuclear membrane reforms in telophase II **AND** four haploid cells are formed from the two haploid cells of     meiosis         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ crossing over during prophase I **AND** independent assortment **OR** random alignment of homologous chromosomes in metaphase I **OR** metaphase II result in unique gametes    [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    All of the DNA in a cell is **replicated** during **interphase** ($\\checkmark$), so cells are **diploid** when they enter meiosis ($\\checkmark$). **Homologous** chromosomes **pair up**, become **visible** ($\\checkmark$) and **crossing over** of non-sister chromatids occur during **prophase I** ($\\checkmark$). Then, homologous chromosomes are moved to the **equator** of the cell by **spindle microtubules** and **randomly orientated** during **metaphase I** ($\\checkmark$). Homologous chromosomes are pulled to the **opposite poles** of the cell by spindle fibres in **anaphase I** ($\\checkmark$) and one **diploid cell** is split into **two haploid** cells during telophase I ($\\checkmark$). The cells then enter meiosis II. The **pairs** of **sister chromatids** are **independently assorted** to form a single line along the equator of the cell, in **metaphase II** ($\\checkmark$). During **anaphase II** the centromeres split and the **sister chromatids** are pulled to **opposite poles** of the cell ($\\checkmark$). In **telophase II** the **nuclear membrane reforms** around the chromosomes, and after cytokinesis, **4 new haploid, non-identical**,  daughter cells are formed from the **2 haploid cells** that resulted from **meiosis I** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a karyogram is an image showing the positions of centromeres and banding patterns of stained **OR** dyed homologous chromosomes ordered by decreasing length        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ biological sex determined by XX and XY **OR** sex chromosomes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ whether non-disjunction has occurred is determined by the presence or absence of chromosome (s)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ presence of chromosomal abnormalities (such as breakages or translocation) determined by the banding pattern **OR** size of chromosome        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ indication of chromosome disorders such as Down syndrome, which is caused by trisomy 21[1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    A karyogram is an image showing the positions of **centromeres** and **banding patterns** of stained **homologous chromosomes** ordered by decreasing length ($\\checkmark$). A karyogram can be used to deduce **biological sex** ($\\checkmark$) or the presence of **chromosomal abnormalities** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1341","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1341\n\nWhat is a **disadvantage** to an organism of being multicellular rather than unicellular?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Larger body size\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ability to adapt to their surroundings \n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The need for a complex exchange system\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cell differentiation","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nMulticellular organisms are comprised of numerous specialised cells, enabling them to achieve larger sizes compared to unicellular organisms. This results in them needing exchange systems, such as the circulatory and respiratory systems, to manage waste removal and to acquire essential substances such as oxygen and nutrients. In contrast, unicellular organisms can rely on simple diffusion. **Choice C**  is correct.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect because although multicellular organisms often have a larger body size than unicellular organisms, this can be advantageous to them. **Choice B**  is incorrect, as multicellular organisms adapting to their environment more easily is an advantage. **Choice D**  is incorrect, as cell differentiation, where cells become specialised for specific functions, is an advantage as it allows for the division of labour amongst cells."},{"ref":"BI1342","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1342\n\nWhich of the following supports the theory that multicellularity has evolved repeatedly?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n\n  | | |\n\n  | :-- | :-- |\n\n  | I. | Sexual reproduction is more common in multicellular organisms, allowing for greater genetic diversity. |\n\n  | II. | Modern eukaryotes are believed to have evolved from larger prokaryotes which engulfed but didn't digest beneficial smaller prokaryotes. |\n\n  | III. | Fossil evidence indicates that different taxa developed multicellularity at different times in evolutionary history. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nFossils provide evidence that different taxa developed multicellularity at different times in evolutionary history, indicating that multicellularity developed independently in different taxa; **Statement III** is correct. **Choice A** is correct.\n\nAlthough multicellular organisms more commonly reproduce sexually than single-celled organisms, leading to greater genetic diversity, this does not directly support the idea of multicellularity evolving repeatedly. It highlights one of the advantages of sexual reproduction, however, single-celled organisms tend to reproduce rapidly by asexual reproduction, which can lead to more effective dispersal and the withstanding of harsher conditions. **Statement I** is not true. **Statement II**  is not true as it summarises the theory of endosymbiosis rather than supports multicellularity evolving repeatedly. **Choices B**, **C** and **D**  are incorrect, as they all contain **Statement II** and/or **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI1440","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1440\n\nWhich of the following statements read true for differences between prokaryotes and eukaryotes?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Prokaryotes have DNA associated with histones, while eukaryotic cells have naked DNA. |\n    | II. | Eukaryotes have linear chromosomes, while prokaryotes have circular chromosomes. |\n    | III. | Most prokaryotes have a singular chromosome, whilst most eukaryotic somatic cells contain at least two chromosomes. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nProkaryotes typically have DNA that is not associated with histones and is often referred to as ‘naked’. In eukaryotes, histone proteins help package the DNA into structural units called nucleosomes. This makes **Statement I** false.\n\nEukaryotic chromosomes are typically linear and located within the nucleus, while most prokaryotic organisms have a single circular chromosome found in a nucleoid region without a surrounding nuclear membrane. This makes **Statement II** true.\n\nMost prokaryotes contain a single circular chromosome, while eukaryotic somatic cells are generally diploid with paired chromosomes. Therefore **Statement III** is true.\n\nSeeming as only **Statements II** and **III** are true, this makes **Choice B** the correct answer to the question.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are all incorrect as they include **Statement I**."},{"ref":"BI1471","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1471\n\nThe micrograph shows the ultrastructure of a cell. What type of cell is shown and what process is it undergoing?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI1471%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1471 fig1\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Lewis, M. E., Belland, R. J., AbdelRahman, Y. M., Beatty, W. L., Aiyar, A. A., Zea, A. H., Greene, S. J., Marrero, L., Buckner, L. R., Tate, D. J., McGowin, C. L., Kozlowski, P. A., O’Brien, M., Lillis, R. A., Martin, D. H., & Quayle, A. J. (2014, May 15). Morphologic and molecular evaluation of chlamydia trachomatis growth in human endocervix reveals distinct growth patterns. Frontiers. Retrieved February 11 , 2024, from https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fcimb.2014.00071/full. Copyright free]\n\n| | $\\hspace{1em}$Type of cell$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$Process$\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n| --- | :-- | :-- |\n\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Eukaryotic | Mitosis |\n\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Eukaryotic | Binary fission |\n\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Prokaryotic | Mitosis |\n\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Prokaryotic | Binary fission |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nProkaryotic cells have an approximate diameter of 0.1$-$ 5.0  µm, whereas eukaryotic cells are significantly larger with a diameter range of 10 $-$ 100  µm.  The cells in the micrograph have an approximate diameter of 0.5  µm, so are prokaryotic. Prokaryotic cells do not replicate themselves using mitosis, they use binary fission. **Choice D**  is correct.\n\n**Choices A**  and **B** are incorrect as the cells in the micrograph are prokaryotic, not eukaryotic. **Choice C**  is incorrect as prokaryotic cells lack a mitotic spindle, so do not carry out mitosis, they carry out binary fission."},{"ref":"BI1474","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1474\n\nWhich statement(s) provide evidence for the endosymbiotic theory?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1.6em}$ Mitochondria and chloroplasts have adaptations that increase their surface area\n\n- II. $\\hspace{1.3em}$ Mitochondria and chloroplasts can make ATP\n\n- III. $\\hspace{1em}$ Mitochondria and chloroplasts have their own ribosomes\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nThe endosymbiotic theory explains that modern eukaryotic cells evolved from prokaryotic cells, when larger prokaryotes engulfed, but did not digest, other prokaryotes that are the precursors of modern chloroplasts and mitochondria. Evidence for this includes mitochondria and chloroplasts being the only organelles in eukaryotes that have their own ribosomes (**Statement III**). **Statement II**  is also correct, as this is a key similarity to prokaryotes, which also produce ATP through processes like cellular respiration (mitochondria) and photosynthesis (chloroplasts). This supports the idea that these organelles were once free-living bacteria capable of producing ATP. **Choice C**  is therefore correct.\n\n**Statement I**  is incorrect as the adaptations that increase the surface area of mitochondria and chloroplasts are not evidence for endosymbiosis. \n\n**Choice B** is incorrect as it is missing one of the true statements.\n\n**Choices A** and **D** are incorrect as they contain **Statement I**"},{"ref":"BI1542","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1542\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe micrograph shows a cell of an exocrine gland from the stomach of the European beaver (*Castor fiber*).\nExocrine glands are glands that release their secretions through a duct.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI1542.png\" alt=\"BI1542\">\n[Source: Adapted from Ziółkowska, N., Lewczuk, B., Petryński, W., Palkowska, K., Prusik, M., Targońska, K., Giżejewski, Z., & Przybylska-Gornowicz, B. (2014, April).\n*(PDF) light and electron microscopy of the European beaver* ... Research gate. Retrieved March 21, 2024, https://www.researchgate.net/publication/261609121_Light_and_Electron_Microscopy_of_the_European_Beaver_Castor_fiber_Stomach_Reveal_Unique_Morphological_Features\n_with_Possible_General_Biological_Significance. Copyright by CC 4.0]\n\n1. Suggest one role of exocrine glands.        [1 mark]\n\n2. Using the letter X, label the organelle with the function of producing proteins that are exported outside the cell.        [1 mark]\n\n3. Explain how **two** organelles are related to the function of an exocrine cell.    [2 marks]\n\n4. Pancreatic cells are both exocrine and endocrine glands. Name one hormone produced by the pancreas and state what the stimulus is for its secretion.    [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ digestion **OR** production **OR** secretion of digestive enzymes **OR** saliva **OR** named digestive enzyme\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ bile production **OR** secretion [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sweat production **OR** secretion **OR** thermoregulation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sebum production **OR** secretion **OR** lubrication of skin **OR** hair [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ milk production **OR** secretion [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mucus production **OR** secretion [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ seminal fluid production **OR** secretion [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tear production **OR** secretion [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Endocrine glands secrete hormones directly into the **bloodstream**, whereas exocrine glands secrete substances to the **surfaces of the body** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER) should be labelled with the letter X        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI1542AnsA.png\" alt=\"BI1542AnsA\">\n    [Source: Adapted from Ziółkowska, N., Lewczuk, B., Petryński, W., Palkowska, K., Prusik, M., Targońska, K., Giżejewski, Z., & Przybylska-Gornowicz, B. (2014, April). *(PDF) light and electron microscopy of the European beaver* ... Research gate. Retrieved March 21, 2024, https://www.researchgate.net/publication/261609121_Light_and_Electron_Microscopy_of_the_European_Beaver_Castor_fiber_Stomach_Reveal_Unique_Morphological_Features_with_Possible_General_Biological_Significance. Copyright by CC 4.0]\n\n3. any **two** of the following (must have structure and function to gain mark):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ extensive endoplasmic reticulum to synthesise enzymes **OR** proteins        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ many mitochondria to produce ATP for protein synthesis                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ many secretory vesicles to secrete substances                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ many Golgi apparatus to process and package proteins                [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The exocrine cell has an **extensive endoplasmic reticulum** that **synthesises enzymes** ($\\checkmark$) and **many mitochondria** that produce **ATP** for protein synthesis ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **one**  of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ insulin secreted in response to high **OR** increase in blood glucose **OR** sugar levels        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glucagon secreted in response to low **OR** decrease in blood glucose **OR** sugar levels        [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The pancreas secretes **insulin** in response to an **increase** in **blood glucose** levels  ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1556","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1556\n\nThe diagram shows two kinds of specialised cells found in the same individual.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI1556.png\" alt=\"BI1556\">\n\nWhich of the following statements would be true for both cell types?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n | | |\n | :-- | :-- |\n | I. | They share the same genome. |\n | II. | They develop based on the expression of some genes and not others. |\n | III. | They develop from the same stem cell populations. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because both cells contain the same genes/genome in their diploid nucleus (**Statement I**). They differentiate or become specialised during their development due to the expression of some genes but not others (**Statement II**).\n\n**Statement III** is not true because although both types of cells originate from stem cells, they come from different lineages and therefore develop from different stem cell populations or niches. **Choices B, C**, and **D** are incorrect because they contain **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI1557","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1557\n\nThe electron micrograph shows tobacco leaf mesophyll cells.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI1557.png\" alt=\"BI1557\">\n[Adapted from: Marys, E. (n.d.). *A Ultrathin Section of Vegetal Leaf Mesophyll Cells Showing Chloroplast Structure Stock Image - Image of discovery, botany: 211880559*. Dreamstime.com. Retrieved April 23, 2024, from https://www.dreamstime.com/ultra-thin-section-transmission-electron-microscopy-tobacco-leaf-mesophyll-cells-showing-chloroplast-structure-ultrathin-image211880559. Royalty free image.]\n\nIdentify structures X, Y, and Z in the micrograph.\n\n| | Structure X | Structure Y | Structure Z |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | mitochondrion | nucleus | plasma membrane |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | chloroplast | vacuole | cell wall |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | mitochondrion | vacuole | plasma membrane |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | chloroplast | nucleus | cell wall |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because structure X is a chloroplast, structure Y is the nucleus and structure Z is the cell wall.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because X is a chloroplast, not a mitochondrion. **Choice B** is incorrect because Y is the nucleus, not a vacuole. **Choice C** is incorrect because both structures X and Y are mislabelled."},{"ref":"BI2320","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2320\n\nElectron microscopes allow the ultrastructure of cells and molecules to be viewed.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/Edit.png\" alt=\"Edit\">\n[Source: National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases (NIAID)/NIH (2019, January 4). *Electron micrograph of tau clusters (46603522841).jpg*. Wikimedia. Retrieved December 27, 2024, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Electron_micrograph_of_tau_clusters_(46603522841).jpg. Public Domain]\n\nWhich of these methods is used to analyse protein structure?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Freeze-fracture electron microscopy\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Fluorescent staining\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cryogenic electron microscopy\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Immunofluorescence","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because cryogenic electron microscopy can produce 3D images of biological molecules such as proteins, DNA, and RNA. Samples are rapidly frozen to preserve their natural state before an electron beam is passed through, allowing the structure of individual molecules to be studied.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because freeze-fracture electron microscopy is primarily used to study cellular structures, such as the internal arrangement of membranes. While it involves rapid freezing and can produce 3D images, it does not resolve molecular or atomic details of protein structures.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because fluorescent staining is used in light microscopy to identify specific molecules, such as proteins, in cells. Fluorescent dyes or antibodies emit specific wavelengths of light, highlighting target molecules, but they do not provide detailed structural information about proteins.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because immunofluorescence identifies specific proteins in cells or tissues using fluorescently tagged antibodies. While it is useful for locating proteins, it is not a technique for analysing protein structure at the molecular level."},{"ref":"BI2438","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2438\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nBelow is a diagram of *Saccharomyces cervesiae* commonly referred to as Brewer's yeast.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI2438.png\" alt=\"BI2438\">\n\n1. Distinguish between the cell wall in fungal and plant cells. [1 mark]\n\n2.\n 1. Explain the presence of chloroplast in plant cells, but not animal or fungal cells. [2 marks]\n\n 2. A chloroplast is a type of plastid. Outline the function of one other named type of plastid found in plant cells that is absent in fungal cells. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ fungal cell walls are primarily composed of chitin **WHEREAS** plant cell walls are composed of cellulose [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The major component of fungal cell walls is **chitin**, but in plants, it’s **cellulose** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ plants use chloroplasts for photosynthesis **AND** are autotrophs **OR** allows plants to make their own food **OR** convert light energy to chemical energy [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ animals and fungi do not need chloroplast to photosynthesise because they are heterotrophs **OR** feed on other organisms **OR** obtain energy by consuming organic matter rather than producing it [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Plants are **autotrophs** and make their own food by photosynthesis. **Chloroplasts** contain chlorophyll which absorbs the light energy needed for **photosynthesis** ($\\checkmark$). Animals and fungi are **heterotrophs**, they consume their food and **do not need chloroplast** for photosynthesis ($\\checkmark$).\n\n 2. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chromoplasts synthesise **OR** store pigments (other than chlorophyll) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ leucoplasts are involved in storage **OR** metabolism [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Plant cells have **chromoplasts** which synthesise and **store pigments**, such as carotenoids ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2726","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2726\n\nThe table below compares two specialised cells found in the human body:\n\n| Cell Type | Primary Function | Proteins Expressed |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| Skeletal Muscle | Movement | Actin & Myosin |\n| Neuron | Communication | Neurotransmitters & Ion Channel Proteins |\n\nWhich of the following explains the differences between the specialised cells?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Muscle cells and neurons have different DNA, so they produce different proteins\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Each cell type expresses only the genes needed for its function\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Differentiation permanently deletes unused genes from a cell’s DNA\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Neurons and muscle cells switch functions over time, depending on environmental conditions","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct. Each cell type expresses only the genes needed for its function, while other genes remain inactive. This selective gene expression leads to structural and functional differences between cells.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect. All cells in an organism (except gametes and red blood cells) contain the same DNA. Differences in protein production result from differences in gene expression, not in DNA sequence. **Choice C** is incorrect. Differentiation silences genes but does not remove them. **Choice D** is incorrect. Once differentiated, cells do not differentiate again — a neuron will not become a muscle cell later in life."},{"ref":"BI2727","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2727\n\nThe figure below shows the results of a study on the evolution of multicellularity. Obligate multicellularity means the organism is always multicellular, facultative multicellularity means the organism is usually unicellular but can sometimes form multicellular structures.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/Ed2727.png\" alt=\"Ed2727\">\n[Source: Adapted from Fisher, R. (2013). *Group Formation, Relatedness, and the Evolution of Multicellularity. Current Biology*, 23. Retrieved April 2025  from https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0960982213005678. CC BY 3.0.]\n\nWhat can be deduced from this phylogenetic tree?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | All mammals are obligately multicellular |\n    | II. | Multicellularity evolved once in eukaryotes |\n    | III. | All prokaryotes are unicellular |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because mammals are animals, and from the phylogenetic tree, the word animals is boxed(obligately multicellular). \\\n**Statement II** is false because multicellularity appears on multiple separate branches that do not share a recent common ancestor, suggesting independent evolution. \\\n**Statement III** is false because cyanobacteria are prokaryotes, and the tree shows them boxed, indicating they are multicellular. \\\nTherefore, **Choice A** is correct as it only contains the one true **Statement I**.\n\n**Choices B, C**, and **D** are all incorrect as they include one or more of the false **Statements II** and **III**."},{"ref":"BI2786","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2786\n\nThe table below presents observations made about four newly discovered entities (W, X, Y and Z).\n\n| Entity | Observation |\n| :-: | :-- |\n| W | Contains a cell wall, cytoplasm, and genetic material, but no internal compartments. |\n| X | Consists of a protein coat surrounding RNA. |\n| Y | Contains membrane-bound compartments, each containing DNA. |\n| Z | Exists as free-floating DNA molecules in a liquid medium. |\n\nBased on cell theory, which row of the table below correctly identifies whether each entity is likely to be classified as a living organism?\n\n| | Entity W | Entity X | Entity Y | Entity Z |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Yes  | No | Yes | No\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | No | Yes | No | Yes |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Yes | No | No | Yes |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | No | Yes | Yes | No |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Entity W**, with a cell wall, cytoplasm, and genetic material, strongly indicates that it is a cell, the basic unit of life. It is likely a prokaryotic cell.\\\n**Entity X** is only reported to have RNA with a protein coat surrounding it. This description best matches that of a virus. Viruses are not considered living organisms.\\\n**Entity Y** contains membrane-bound compartments, which are indicative of eukaryotic organelles and DNA. This description is likely describing a eukaryotic cell.\\\n**Entity Z** is only DNA without any barrier to the outside and does not describe a cell.\n\n**Choice A** is correct because it correctly identifies **Entities W** and **Y** as likely to be classified as living, while **X** and **Z are classified** as non-living. **Choice B, Choice C**, and **Choice D** do not have this combination of entities."},{"ref":"BI2787","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2787\n\nA student, learning to use a microscope, observed a pollen grain under a microscope with a magnification of ×100. Using an eyepiece graticule, they measured the diameter of the pollen grain to be 25 divisions. At this magnification, 1 division on the eyepiece graticule equals 3 $\\mu$m. To allow the student to measure more pollen grains and to calculate a mean diameter, which of the following conclusions on the experimental work so far is/are correct?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1em}$    Increasing the magnification of the objective lens would widen the field of view.\n- II. $\\hspace{.7em}$    The actual diameter of the pollen grain is 75 $\\mu$m.\n- III. $\\hspace{.5em}$    If the magnification were increased to ×200, each eyepiece graticule division would represent a smaller actual size.\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is not true. Increasing the magnification will narrow the field of view. This is a common student mistake. As magnification increases, the observer is focusing on a smaller area, like zooming in with a camera.\\\n**Statement II** is true. The actual diameter of the pollen grain is calculated as 25 divisions × 3 µm/division = 75 µm.\\\n**Statement III** is true. If the magnification were increased by switching to a higher power objective, the same number of eyepiece graticule divisions would span a smaller actual distance on the specimen.\\\n**Choice C** is correct because it contains the proper combination of **Statement II** and **Statement III**. **Choice A, Choice B** and **Choice D** do not contain the proper combination of statements."},{"ref":"BI2788","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2788\n\nA researcher labelled two different proteins with molecules that emit light of distinct colours when illuminated with a specific wavelength of light. To determine if these proteins are present in the same location in the cell, which type of microscopy would be most appropriate?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Bright field microscopy\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Scanning electron microscopy\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Fluorescence microscopy\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Transmission electron microscopy","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nFluorescence microscopy uses specific wavelengths of light to excite fluorescent molecules, the labels, causing them to emit light at different wavelengths. Using appropriate filters, the light emitted from each can be detected to determine their locations. Therefore, **Choice C** is correct.\n\nBright field microscopy is the term used to describe a common light microscopy technique where specimens appear dark against a bright background due to the direct transmission of light through the sample. Bright field microscopy would not be able to excite specific fluorescent molecules or distinguish between particular wavelengths of light, so **Choice A** is incorrect.\\\nScanning electron microscopy uses a stream of electrons to produce an image of the surface of an organism and does not use or detect any wavelength of light, so **Choice B** is incorrect.\\\nTransmission electron microscopy passes a stream of electrons through a thin specimen to visualise internal structures and does not use or detect any wavelength of light, so **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI2789","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2789\n\nThe plasma membrane, a structure common to all cells, is primarily composed of a phospholipid bilayer. What is the fundamental reason for this specific lipid arrangement?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ To provide a flexible boundary for the cell and a source of stored energy.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ To create a rigid barrier impermeable to most substances for protection.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ To form a selectively permeable barrier due to the hydrophobic nature of the lipids.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ To actively transport molecules using energy from the lipids.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because it accurately describes the plasma membrane as selectively permeable, allowing some things to pass and some not, and this is because the tails of the phospholipids comprising the membrane are hydrophobic.\\\n**Choice A** is incorrect. While it’s true that the membrane provides a flexible boundary for the cell, it is not a source of energy. If the membrane is broken down to release energy, the boundary of the cell will be destroyed, and the cell will cease to exist.\\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the membrane is not rigid or impermeable. It is flexible and partially permeable.\\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the energy used to transport molecules across the membrane is typically provided by the hydrolysis of ATP."},{"ref":"BI2790","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2790\n\nMatch the labelled structure (Q-T from the diagram) with its primary structure.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/Ed2790.png\" alt=\"Ed2790\">\n\n| | Contains genetic information | Provides support and protection | Regulates the passage of substances |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | S | Q | R |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | T | Q | S |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | T | Q | R |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | T | R | Q |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn the diagram, T indicates naked DNA, and this is the genetic material for the cell. Q indicates the cell wall, which provides structural support and protection. R indicates the cell membrane, which regulates the passage of substances in and out of the cell, and S indicates the cytoplasm, which is the site of metabolic reactions in the cell. Therefore, **Choice C** is the correct answer because it correctly matches the functions of the structures that are indicated by the letters in the diagram.\n\n**Choice A, Choice B**, and **Choice D** do not correctly match the functions of the structures indicated by the letters in the diagram."},{"ref":"BI2791","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2791\n\nWhich of the following options correctly identifies the forms of DNA and the types of ribosomes found in prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells?\n\n| | Prokaryotic DNA | Eukaryotic DNA | Prokaryotic Ribosomes  | Eukaryotic Ribosomes |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |  :-: |  :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Linear | Circular | 80S | 70S |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Circular | Linear | 80S | 80S |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Circular  | Linear | 70S | 80S |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Linear  | Linear | 70S | 70S |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nProkaryotic cells have circular DNA and 70S ribosomes, while eukaryotic cells have linear DNA and 80S ribosomes. Therefore, **Choice C** is correct. **Choices A, B** and **D** are incorrect because they do not have the correct combinations."},{"ref":"BI2792","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2792\n\nThe diagram depicts a single-celled organism living in freshwater that faces water constantly moving into its cell due to osmosis.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI2792.png\" alt=\"BI2792\">\n[Source: Adapted from *File: Paramecium caudatum.svg.* (2024, March 12). Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 04:34, April 23, 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=File:Paramecium_caudatum.svg&oldid=859786930.CC Public Domain]\n\nTo prevent bursting, the cell actively pumps out excess water using specialised structures. This process is an example of:\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Obtaining nutrients\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Eliminating waste products\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Responding to external stimuli\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Maintaining homeostasis","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nHomeostasis is the maintenance of a stable internal environment. The organism pumping out of excess water is a mechanism to keep its osmolarity stable and different from the outside of the cell. Therefore, **Choice D** is correct.\\\nThe organism is not taking in or making any substance for energy and growth, so **Choice A** is incorrect.\\\nExcess water may be considered a waste product. However, the primary purpose of the pump is to regulate osmolarity and prevent lysis. Waste products typically refer to metabolic wastes like carbon dioxide or nitrogenous compounds. Therefore, **Choice B** is incorrect.\\\nThe active pumping out of water is the organism's way of maintaining internal conditions rather than just a direct response to the stimulus, and so **Choice C** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI2793","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2793\n\nWhich of the following statements is true of fungal cell walls?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ They are composed of cellulose.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Fungal cells do not have cell walls.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ They are composed of chitin.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ They are composed of peptidoglycan.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because fungal cell walls are primarily composed of chitin. **Choice A** is incorrect because cellulose is the main carbohydrate that makes up plant cell walls. Peptidoglycan is found in bacterial cell walls, so **Choice D** is incorrect. Fungal cells do have cell walls, so **Choice B** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI2794","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2794\n\nWhich of the following rows correctly pairs the cell type with its nuclear characteristic?\n\n| | Cell type | Nuclear state |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Phloem sieve tube elements | No nucleus |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Skeletal muscle cells | Single nucleus |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Red blood cells | Single nucleus |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Aseptate fungal hyphae | No nucleus |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because phloem sieve tube elements lose their nucleus as they mature to facilitate the transport of sugars. \\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because skeletal muscle cells are multinucleated to accommodate their length. \\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because mature red blood cells lack a nucleus. They jettison the nucleus when mature to increase the haemoglobin capacity of oxygen transport. \\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because aseptate fungal hyphae have a continuous cytoplasm containing many nuclei."},{"ref":"BI2795","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2795\n\nWhich of the following correctly pairs the structure with the type of cell in which it is found?\n\n| | Structure | Type of cell |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Nucleoid region | Animal |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Cell wall | Prokaryotic  |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Chloroplast | Fungal cell |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Sap vacuole  | Prokaryotic  |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because nearly all prokaryotic cells possess a cell wall that provides structure, support, and protection. **Choice A** is incorrect because a nucleoid region is characteristic of a prokaryotic cell, not an animal cell. **Choice C** is incorrect because chloroplasts are found in plants and some algae cells, but not fungi. **Choice D** is incorrect because sap vacuoles are characteristic of plant cells, not prokaryotic cells."},{"ref":"BI2796","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2796\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe light micrographs below show examples of skeletal muscle, phloem sieve tube elements, aseptate fungal hyphae, and red blood cells. These types of cells are sometimes used as atypical examples of cells.\n\n| Skeletal Muscle | Phloem Sieve Tube Elements |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/Aii.png\" alt=\"Aii\"> | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BIII.png\" alt=\"BIII\"> |\n| [*File: Skeletal Muscle Tissue.jpg*. (2024, June 13). Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 02:00, April 9, 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=File:Skeletal_Muscle_Tissue.jpg&oldid=883658940.]| [*File:Herbaceous Dicot Stem Tangential Section Phloem Parenchyma in Cucurbita (35207518570).jpg*. (2020, September 16). Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 02:05, April 9, 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/w /index.php?title=File: Herbaceous_Dicot_Stem.]|\n\n| Aseptate Fungal Hyphae | Red Blood Cells |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/ci.png\" alt=\"ci\"> | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/DII.png\" alt=\"DII\"> |\n| [*File: Yeast cells and short hyphae in Gram stain.jpg*. (2024, September 17). Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 02:10, April 9, 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia .org/w/index.php?title=File:Yeast_cells_and_short_hyphae_in_Gram_stain.jpg&oldid=925217916.]|  [*File: Blood cells 090304-F-5951M-108.jpg*. (2024, October 5). Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 02:14, April 9, 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=File:Blood_cells_090304-F-5951M-108.jpg&oldid=933116797.] |\n\n1. Explain how the number of nuclei in each cell type in the micrograph of each cell relates to the cell’s function.            [4 marks]\n\n2. Contrast the cell wall composition of the aseptate hyphae and phloem sieve tube elements.                [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. to award a mark, the response must include the number of nuclei **AND** a reason for this\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ skeletal muscle cells have multiple nuclei, providing multiple copies of genes to produce the large numbers of proteins needed for muscle contraction    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phloem sieve tube elements lack a nucleus (at maturity), which creates space for the efficient transport of sugars and other organic molecules    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ aseptate fungal hyphae have multiple nuclei to allow for rapid growth **OR** distribution of nutrients **OR** cytoplasmic streaming throughout the extensive network **OR** multinucleation occurs due to nuclear divisions without cytoplasmic division    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ red blood cells lack a nucleus, which increases space for haemoglobin, maximising oxygen transport     [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Skeletal muscle cells** have **many nuclei** because they must make many **copies of proteins** for contractions along the entire length of the cell, which may be **very long** ($\\checkmark$). **Phloem** sieve tube elements have **no nucleus** to allow for more space for transporting **sugars** more efficiently ($\\checkmark$). **Aseptate hyphae** have **many nuclei** throughout the cell to help it grow quickly and **distribute nutrients** ($\\checkmark$). **Red blood cells** have **no nuclei** at maturity for greater volume to **carry haemoglobin** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    ***Note a table showing the comparisons would also be acceptable:***\n\n    | Cell | No. of nuclei | Relation to cell function | |\n    | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n    | Skeletal muscle | **many** | **for many copies of protein synthesis** | ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | Phloem sieve tube elements | **none** | **to allow room for translocation** | ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | Aseptate fungal hyphae | **many** | **to allow rapid transfer of nutrients** | ($\\checkmark$) |\n    | Red blood cell | **none** | **to create more room for haemoglobin** | ($\\checkmark$) |\n\n2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ fungal cell walls are composed of chitin, **WHEREAS** plant cell walls are composed of cellulose [1 mark]\n\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ **Fungal cell walls** are composed of **chitin**, while **plant cell walls** are composed of **cellulose** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2852","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2852\n\nThe diagram shows three dividing eukaryotic cells (not drawn to scale).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Cell%20Structure/images/BI2852%20A2.2%20core%20MCQ.jpeg.JPEG\" alt=\"BI2852 A2.2 core MCQ.jpeg\">\n\nWhich row correctly pairs each cell with its type?\n\n| | Cell X | Cell Y | Cell Z |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Palisade mesophyll | Yeast | Liver |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Liver | Palisade mesophyll | Yeast |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Yeast | Liver | Palisade mesophyll |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Palisade mesophyll | Liver | Yeast |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Cell X** is undergoing budding, a process characteristic of unicellular fungi like yeast. The cell also has a cell wall, a large vacuole and no chloroplasts, reinforcing that this cell is indeed a fungal cell, such as yeast.\n\n**Cell Y** has no cell wall, no visible vacuoles and there are centrioles in the dividing cells, indicating that this is a dividing animal cell, such as a liver cell.\n\n**Cell Z** has is dividing into daughter cells, each of which has a cell wall, vacuole, and chloroplasts. The cell is also dividing evenly by laying down a cell plate between the cells, which will develop into the cell walls of the new cells. These are all features of plant cells, such as palisade mesophyll cells.\n\n**Choice C** is therefore the correct answer to this question."}]},{"name":"Classification & Cladistics","questions":[{"ref":"BI0251","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0251\n\nThe diagram below represents the three domains of life.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Classification%20%26%20Cladistics/images/BI0251ii.png\" alt=\"BI0251ii\">\n\nWhich row has the correct names for the domains?\n\n| | Domain 1 | Domain 2 | Domain 3\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| Archaea | Prokaryotes | Eukaryote |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Protista | Prokaryota | Eukaryota |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Protista | Eubacteria | Eukaryota |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Eubacteria | Archaea | Eukaryota |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because the three domains of organisms are Archaea, Eubacteria and Eukaryota. **Choice A** is incorrect because prokaryote is not the name of one of the domains. **Choice B** is incorrect because Protista and prokaryota are not the names of domains. **Choice C** is incorrect because Protista is not one of the domains."},{"ref":"BI0252","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0252\n\nTo which domain do organisms with membrane-enclosed organelles belong to?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Eukaryota\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    Archaea\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    Eubacteria\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    Prokaryotes","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because Eukaryota are the only group with nuclei and membrane-bound organelles. **Choice B** and **choice C** are incorrect because organisms in these two domains (Archaea and Eubacteria) do not have membrane-enclosed organelles. **Choice D** is incorrect because prokaryotes are not the name of a domain."},{"ref":"BI0253","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0253\n\nWhich of the following represents the correct order for the traditional taxonomic hierarchy?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Class $\\rightarrow$ Order    $\\rightarrow$ Family\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Phylum $\\rightarrow$ Order $\\rightarrow$ Class\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Order $\\rightarrow$ Genus    $\\rightarrow$ Family\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Kingdom    $\\rightarrow$ Order    $\\rightarrow$ Phylum","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because the traditional hierarchy of taxa is a domain, kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus, so class $\\rightarrow$ order $\\rightarrow$ family is in the correct order. **Choice B** is incorrect because the class is above order. **Choice C** is incorrect because the family is above genus. **Choice D** is incorrect because the phylum is above order."},{"ref":"BI0254","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0254\n\nNatural classification groups organisms according to which characteristic?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Morphology\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    Anatomical characteristic\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    Modes of nutrition\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    Common ancestry","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because natural classification groups organisms according to their evolutionary relationships and common ancestry.\n\n**Choice A** and **Choice B** are incorrect because morphology and anatomical characteristics may be used as evidence when classifying organisms, but natural classification is ultimately based on evolutionary relationships rather than just structural similarities.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because mode of nutrition may describe how organisms obtain energy, but it is not the primary basis of natural classification."},{"ref":"BI0255","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0255\n\nWhich of the following would **not** be a reason for reclassification?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Discovery of a new species\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Evidence for a common ancestor\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Evidence of similar bone structures\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Observation of different behaviours","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because behaviours do not indicate common ancestry. **Choice B** is incorrect because natural classification is based on common ancestry so evidence for a common ancestor can lead to reclassification. **Choice A** is incorrect because the discovery of a new species may provide evidence of a common ancestor and lead to reclassification. **Choice C** is incorrect because evidence of similar bone structures may be part of the evidence for a common ancestor and lead to an organism being reclassified."},{"ref":"BI0260","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0260\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n1. Natural classification is used to group organisms with common ancestry together, based on similarities and shared characteristics. All organisms can be classified into one of the three domains.\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Classification%20%26%20Cladistics/images/BI0260.png\" alt=\"BI0260\">\n\n 1. List three characteristics of Eubacteria. [3 marks]\n\n 2. Distinguish between archaeans and eukaryotes. [1 mark]\n\n2. Outline how a case study like the reclassification of the figwort family (*Scrophulariaceae*) demonstrates the limitations of morphology-based taxonomy.[4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ unicellular [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic **OR** no true nucleus **OR** no membrane-bound organelles [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ single chromosome [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA is a loop [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ proteins associated with DNA may or may not be present [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 70S ribosomes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cell wall made of peptidoglycan [1 mark]\n\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nEubacteria are organisms that are **unicellular** ($\\checkmark$) and have no true nucleus. They have **a single chromosome** ($\\checkmark$) that is a **loop** ($\\checkmark$) and **histone proteins may or may not be present** ($\\checkmark$). The **ribosomes of Eubacteria are 70S** ($\\checkmark$) and their **cell walls are made of peptidoglycan** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n 2. any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Archaea do not have membrane-bound organelles **WHEREAS** Eukaryota do have membrane-bound organelles [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Archaea do not have a nucleus **WHEREAS** Eukaryota do have a nucleus [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Archaea have circular chromosomes **WHEREAS** Eukaryota have linear chromosomes [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nArchaea **have circular chromosomes** but Eukaryota **have linear chromosomes** ($\\checkmark$). Archaea **do not have a nucleus** but Eukaryota do ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ morphology-based taxonomy relies on physical similarities in structuress [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ similarities used in morphology-based classification may result from convergent evolution, not common ancestry [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ convergent evolution produces analogous structures that look similar but evolved independently [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the figwort family **OR** *Scrophulariaceae* was originally grouped based on morphological traits [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ molecular and cladistic analyses showed that some species classified in the figwort family were more closely related to species in other families [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ led to the reclassification of many species into different families based on genetic evidence [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ case study shows that relying only on morphology can mislead taxonomy because of homoplasy OR similar traits not due to common ancestry[1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cladistics, using DNA **OR** molecular data, provides a more objective and accurate method for determining evolutionary relationships [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ reclassification highlights how scientific classifications are hypotheses that can be falsified or revised with new evidence [1 mark]\n\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nThe figwort family was **originally classified** based on **morphological traits**, such as **flower shape** ($\\checkmark$). However, **DNA sequencing** and **cladistic analysis** showed that **some species** were more closely related to plants in other families, **not sharing** a **recent common ancestor** with the **figworts** ($\\checkmark$). This finding demonstrated that the **similarities in structure** resulted from **convergent evolution**, where **unrelated species** develop **similar traits** independently ($\\checkmark$). Therefore, **relying solely** on **morphology** can be **misleading in classification**, highlighting the importance of **molecular data** for **more accurate** evolutionary relationships ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0262","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0262\n\nWhat information can be deduced from the cladogram?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Classification%20%26%20Cladistics/images/cladogram%20of%20primates%20i.png\" alt=\"cladogram of primates i\">\n\n[Adapted from: Source Bockman, P. (2011, October 3). *File: Primate cladogram.jpg*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 14, 2022, from https:// commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Primate_cladogram.jpg CC-BY-SA-3.0]\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Lorises and Tarsiers share a common ancestor.\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Old world monkeys and New world monkeys have the same DNA sequences.\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Human and Lemur species are the same age.\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Tarsiers and Lemurs do not share a common ancestor.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAll organisms on the same cladogram share a common ancestor. **Choice A** is correct. **Choice B** is incorrect as old world and new world monkeys have evolutionarily split on the cladogram, so there are differences in their DNA sequence. **Choice C** is incorrect as this cladogram does not show the age of the species. **Choice D** is incorrect because lemurs and tarsiers are on the same cladogram so they do share the same ancestor."},{"ref":"BI0263","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0263\n\nAccording to the cladogram, which two primates are the most closely related?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Classification%20%26%20Cladistics/images/BI0263.png\" alt=\"BI0263\">\n[Adapted from: Source Bockman, P. (2011, October 3). *File: Primate cladogram.jpg*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 14, 2022, from https:// commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Primate_cladogram.jpg CC-BY-SA-3.0]\n\n- A.    $\\hspace{1em}$ New world monkeys and old world monkeys\n\n- B.    $\\hspace{1em}$ Lorises and lemurs\n- C.    $\\hspace{1em}$ Lemurs and humans\n- D.    $\\hspace{1em}$ Apes and humans","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nHumans and apes (**Choice D**) are the most closely related because they shared a common ancestor more recently than the other primates, such as new world and old world monkeys (**Choice A**), Lorises and Lemurs (**Choice B**) and Lemurs and Humans (**Choice C**)."},{"ref":"BI0264","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0264\n\nIn cladistics, which is the correct definition of a clade?\n\n- A.    $\\hspace{1em}$ A group of organisms with the same DNA sequence\n\n- B.    $\\hspace{1em}$ A group of organisms that share a common ancestor\n- C.    $\\hspace{1em}$ A group of organisms with different DNA sequences\n- D.    $\\hspace{1em}$ A group of organisms that do not share a common ancestor","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is the correct definition for a clade, a group of organisms that share a common ancestor. **Choice A** and **C** are incorrect as they do not feature the idea of a common ancestor. **Choice D** is incorrect because organisms within a clade do share a common ancestor."},{"ref":"BI0265","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0265\n\nWhat evidence can be, and has been used, to determine the members of the same clade?\n\n- I.    $\\hspace{1em}$ Base sequence of genes\n- II.    $\\hspace{.7em}$ Amino acid sequence of proteins\n- III.    $\\hspace{.4em}$ Physical appearance of organisms\n\n- A.    $\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- B.    $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n- C.    $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III\n- D.    $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nChoice D is correct.\nThe DNA sequence (**Statement I**) and amino acid sequence (**Statement II**) are the usual, present way of determining members of the same clade, however, physical appearance (**Statement III**) was previously, but is now rarely, used."},{"ref":"BI0266","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0266\n\nWhich arrow correctly identifies a node in the cladogram?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Classification%20%26%20Cladistics/images/BI0266%20primate%20cladogram%20i.png\" alt=\"BI0266 primate cladogram i\">\n[Adapted from: Source Bockman, P. (2011, October 3). *File: Primate cladogram.jpg*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 14, 2022, from https:// commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Primate_cladogram.jpg CC-BY-SA-3.0]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA node is the point where species diverge. **Choice D** is correct. **Choices A** and **B** are incorrect as they point to the middle of the line, so they do not represent divergence. **Choice C** is incorrect as it points to a species."},{"ref":"BI0267","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0267\n\nWhich of the following is **not** evidence for organisms being in the same clade?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Chimpanzees (*P. troglodytes*) share 99% of their DNA with humans (*H. sapiens*).\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ African elephants (*L. africana*) and manatees share a common ancestor (*T. manatus*).\n\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Bonobo  (*P. paniscus*) and humans (*H. sapiens*) both choose to play with gender specific toys.\n\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ *E. coli* have a 95% similarity amino acid sequence to *S. enterica*.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nTwo organisms having similar behaviour is not evidence that they belong in the same clade so **Choice C** is correct. A clade is a group of organisms that have evolved from a common ancestor, and is usually determined by similarity in amino acid and DNA sequences, therefore **Choices A**, **B** and **D** could be used as evidence for organisms to be in the same clad and are thus incorrect for this question."},{"ref":"BI0268","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0268\n\nThe table shows the number of differences in amino acid sequences for the Insulin-A protein, between pigs and other organisms.\n\n| $\\hspace{3em}$ Organism $\\hspace{3em}$ | Number of differences in amino acid sequence for Insulin-A |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| Human | 2 |\n| Cow | 5 |\n| Giraffe | 50 |\n| Goat | 4 |\n| Warthog | 1 |\n| Chimpanzee | 10 |\n| Flamingo | 25 |\n\nWhich organism would be furthest from pigs in a cladogram?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Warthogs because they have the least number of differences\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Giraffes because they have the largest number of differences\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Humans because they are most different physically\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Flamingoes because they have a different number of limbs","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nOrganisms with the largest differences in the amino acid sequence are the most evolutionarily diverged, so would be the furthest apart. **Choice B** is correct. **Choice A** is incorrect as warthogs and pigs have the least number of differences so would be the closest organisms on a cladogram. **Choices C** and **D** are incorrect because cladograms are not based on physical appearance."},{"ref":"BI0269","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0269\n\nThe DNA sequence from the same section of DNA but from two different organisms is shown in the table below.\n\nWhich organisms are most likely to have diverged from each other most recently?\n\n| | Sequence 1 | Sequence 2 |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| ATCGGGTCGCTTTCTCG | ATCCGGTCGCTTTGTCG |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | TCGCGCGTAAATCGATG  | TCCCGCGTCAATCGTTA |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | ATCGGGTGTGTCATATG | ATCGCGTGTCTCATCTG |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | TGTCATCATCCCGTACTG | TGTCATCATCGGCATGAC |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because the two sequences have the smallest number of differences between them, only two, therefore they are the most closely related. **Choices B**, **C** and **D** are incorrect, as there are more than 2 differences between the two sequences, 4, 3 and 8, respectively."},{"ref":"BI0271","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0271\n\nWhat is the relationship between the number of differences in DNA base sequences and time since divergence from a common ancestor?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The lower the number of differences, the more recently organisms diverged from each other.\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The number of differences is equal to the number of years since the organisms diverged.\n\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ The higher the number of differences, the more recently organisms diverged from each other.\n\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ There is no relationship between the number of differences in DNA base sequences and time since divergence.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe number of sequence differences acts as a molecular clock, so the lower the number of differences accumulated in the DNA base sequence, the more closely related and more recently the organisms diverged from each other. **Choice A** is correct. **Choice B** is incorrect because the number of differences is not an exact measurement of time. **Choice C** is incorrect as it’s the opposite of **Choice A**. **Choice D** is incorrect as there is a relationship."},{"ref":"BI0272","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0272\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe diagrams below show two cladograms for primates. One cladogram was made using data from DNA sequences and the other using amino acid sequences.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Classification%20%26%20Cladistics/images/BI0272.png\" alt=\"BI0272\">\n[Source: Adapted from Bockman, P. (2011, October 3). *File: Primate cladogram.jpg*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 14, 2022, from https: //commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Primate_cladogram.jpg CC-BY-SA-3.0]\n\n1. Identify the organism(s) most closely related to humans in the cladogram based on…\n\n    1. **DNA** sequences    [1 mark]\n\n    2. **amino acid** sequences    [1 mark]\n\n2. Describe the advantage of using DNA sequences to construct cladograms compared to amino acid sequences. [2 marks]\n\n3. Suggest why cladograms made using physical features are not reliable predictors of phylogenetic relationships.     [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. $\\ce{ -> }$ apes [1 mark]\n\n        [1 mark]\n\n    2. $\\ce{ -> }$ old world monkeys **AND** new world monkeys [1 mark]\n\n        [1 mark]\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the nucleotide sequence of a gene contains more information than the amino acid sequence of the corresponding polypeptides [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ silent mutations alter the DNA sequence but do not affect the amino acid sequence [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ degeneracy in the genetic code means a mutation does not necessarily change the amino acid sequence    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ greater information can be obtained by DNA sequence analysis because variability in both the coding and non-coding regions of the genome can be examined [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA samples can be easily sequenced **OR** DNA is cheaper to sequence **OR** DNA sequencing yields more data whereas the approach to sequencing of proteins varies with type of protein [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA sequence can be used to establish amino acid sequence, but not the other way around [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    Using DNA sequences to construct cladograms is advantageous because due to the existence of **coding and non-coding regions** ($\\checkmark$) of DNA, **more information** ($\\checkmark$) is held in the nucleotide sequence than the amino acid sequence . Also mutations may occur in the DNA which do not show up in the amino acid sequence due to the **degenerative code** ($\\checkmark$) leading to **silent mutations** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any two  of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adaptive radiation can result in closely related species showing very different structural features [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ convergent evolution means that distantly related organisms may show very similar structural features [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ analogous structures are derived through separate evolutionary pathways and are not indicators of evolutionary relationships  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA and amino acid analysis have shown that some classification of organisms based on shared structural features was incorrect    [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nCladograms are no longer based on structural features due to the presence of **analogous structures**, which are the result of **convergent evolution** ($\\checkmark$) and so are **not indicators of evolutionary relationships** ($\\checkmark$). DNA and amino acid analysis have shown that many cladograms based solely on **shared structural features** are incorrect ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0273","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0273\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nThe organisms in the table below all share a common ancestor.\n\n| Organism | Vertebrae | Two pairs of limbs | Sweat Glands |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| Trout | Present | Absent | Absent |\n| Black bear | Present | Present | Absent |\n| Human | Present | Present | Present |\n\n1.\n    1. Use the data in the table to draw a cladogram. [2 marks]\n\n    2. State the source of variation which allowed organisms in the cladogram to diverge into different species  [1 mark]\n\n2. In cladistics, state the name used to describe a group of organisms which have all evolved from a common ancestor. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ a node for a common ancestor [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms in the correct order (trout, black bear, human) [1 mark]\n\n        [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Classification%20%26%20Cladistics/images/273ai.png\" alt=\"273ai\">\n    **Only** names of organisms expected, features added just for explanation.\n\n    2.\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ mutations [1 mark]\n\n        [1 mark]\n\n2.\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ clade [1 mark]\n\n[1 mark]"},{"ref":"BI0510","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0510\n\nOrganisms may be reclassified using evidence from cladistics. Cladogram 1, based on DNA comparisons of several species, represents the historical view of relationships among arachnids. Cladogram 2, based on DNA comparisons of many more species than Cladogram 1, challenges the historical view.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Classification%20%26%20Cladistics/images/BI0510.png\" alt=\"BI0510\">\n[Source: Adapted from Ballesteros, J.A., Santibáñez-López, C.E., Baker, C.M.,  Benavides, L.R., Cunha T.J., Gainett, G., Ontano A.Z., Setton, E.V.W, Arango, C.P., Gavish-Regev, E., Harvey, M.S., Wheeler, W.C., Hormiga, G., Giribet, G., & Sharma, P.P. (2022). *Comprehensive Species Sampling and Sophisticated Algorithmic Approaches Refute the Monophyly of Arachnida*. Molecular Biology and Evolution, Volume 39, Issue 2, Retrieved March 15, 2022, from https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msac021. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\nWhat is suggested by Cladogram 2 that is not suggested by Cladogram 1?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Horseshoe crabs are a sister group of arachnids.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Horseshoe crabs are a group within arachnids.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sea spiders are an ancestor of arachnids.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sea spiders are not an ancestor of horseshoe crabs.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nBoth cladograms show the relationships among arachnids and organisms related to arachnids. Cladogram 1 shows sea spiders and horseshoe crabs as related to, but not members, of the arachnids. Horseshoe crabs therefore form a sister group to the arachnids (A sister group is the closest relative to a given unit in a cladogram). Cladogram 2 shows horseshoe crabs as a group within the arachnids.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Classification%20%26%20Cladistics/images/BI0510A.png\" alt=\"BI0510A\">\n\nSo, Cladogram 2 indicates that horseshoe crabs are a group within the arachnids, a relationship not indicated by Cladogram 1.\n\nCladogram 1, not Cladogram 2, indicates that horseshoe crabs are a sister group of arachnids (**Choice A**). Both cladograms show that sea spiders were an ancestor of arachnids (**Choices C** and **D**)."},{"ref":"BI0551","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0551\n\nEarly cladograms were constructed by comparing structures of organisms. The cladogram on the left is based on structure and the cladogram on the right is based on comparing the DNA sequence of a particular gene.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Classification%20%26%20Cladistics/images/BI0551.png\" alt=\"BI0551\">\n\n[Source: Left: Adapted from Milinkovitch, M., Thewissen, J. (1997). *Even-toed fingerprints on whale ancestry*. Nature 388, (622–623).  Retrieved January 4, 2023, from https://doi.org/10.1038/41650. Copyright under open access; Right: Adapted from Masato Nikaido, Alejandro P. Rooney, Norihiro Okada. (1999, August 31). *Phylogenetic relationships among cetartiodactyls based on insertions of short and long interpersed elements: Hippopotamuses are the closest extant relatives of whales*. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences. Retrieved January 4, 2023, from https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.96.18.10261.  Copyright-free.]\n\nWhat is the reason that the cladogram based on the DNA sequences is a more reliable representation of the sequence of divergence among clades than the cladogram based on structures?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Structures are not inherited when species diverge.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ DNA does not change over time while structures can change over time.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Similar structures can be coded for by different genes in genetically distant organisms.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ There are many different structures that are coded for by the same DNA sequence.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nMorphological traits, or structures, are useful for creating cladograms. However, cladograms created by comparing such traits may be inaccurate because distantly related organisms can have structures that appear similar but have different genetic bases. The appearance of similar structures with a different evolutionary basis is not evidence of relatedness, but rather is evidence of convergent evolution. So, one reason the cladogram based on the DNA sequences is more reliable than the cladogram based on structures is that similar structures can be coded for by different genes in genetically distant organisms.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because structures are inherited when species diverge, and the inheritance of structures is beneficial for constructing cladograms.  This is because shared structures are sometimes, but not always, evidence of genetic relatedness.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because DNA does change over time. Scientists look at sequences of changes in DNA to determine evolutionary relatedness.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because different structures are generally coded for by different DNA sequences."},{"ref":"BI0565","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0565\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe cladogram below shows three possible evolutionary origins of the platypus (Platypus X, Y, and Z), an egg-laying mammal with webbed feet. The italicised taxa are extinct.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Classification%20%26%20Cladistics/images/bi0565.png\" alt=\"bi0565\">\n\n[Sources: adapted from Celik, M.A. and Phillips, M.J. (2020). *Conflict Resolution for Mesozoic Mammals: Reconciling Phylogenetic Incongruence Among Anatomical Regions*. Front. Genet. 11:0651. Retrieved January 4, 2023 from https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32774343/. Copyright  under CC BY 4.0]\n\n1.\n    1. State the taxa most closely related to marsupials. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Based on the taxa shown in the cladogram, deduce one reason for the difficulty in identifying the true divergence of the platypus.[1 mark]\n\n2.\n    1. The cladogram shown is based on morphological traits. Describe **one** other type of evidence that can be used to deduce evolutionary relationships.     [2 marks]\n\n    2. Suggest **one** type of morphological evidence that could be used to provide support for Platypus Z over Platypus X as the origin of the platypus.\n [1 mark]\n\n3. Taxonomists classify organisms according to their evolutionary origins using a hierarchical classification system.\n\n    Outline the principal taxonomic ranks in the modern classification system.\n [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n    $\\ce{->}$ placental mammals [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Placental mammals** ($\\checkmark$) are most closely related to marsupials. They are both members of Theria.\n\n    2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ most species are extinct [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ must rely on fossil evidence [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Most of the species** in the cladogram **are extinct** ($\\checkmark$), so scientists **must rely on fossils** ($\\checkmark$) of these organisms to identify relatedness and determine the divergence of the platypus.\n\n2.\n    1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ DNA sequence **OR** gene sequences **OR** amino acid sequences **OR** protein sequences [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ compare sequences for shared gene **OR** protein in different species [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ number of differences in sequences indicates degree of relatedness **OR** the more differences there are between species the more distantly related they are [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Scientists can use **DNA sequences** ($\\checkmark$) to determine evolutionary relationships. For example, scientists might **compare** the nucleotide **sequence of a shared gene** across species ($\\checkmark$). By comparing the sequences of nucleotides in the same gene, scientists can infer **the order in which the changes most likely occurred**. This allows them to determine the most likely **divergence order** ($\\checkmark$) among the organisms.\n\n    2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ identification **OR** comparison of homologous structures [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ identification of vestigial structures [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Scientists could **identify and compare homologous structures** ($\\checkmark$) in the platypus to those of other mammals. For example, if the webbed feet of the platypus are more like the feet of marsupials than the feet of ancestral species like *Docodonta*, the platypus may be more closely related to Theria than it is to *Docodonta*.\n\n    **OR Alternative answer**\n\n    Scientists could **identify vestigial structures** ($\\checkmark$) in the platypus and other mammals. For example, if the platypus and marsupials have similar vestigial structures that are also similar to functional structures that were present in earlier groups of mammals, the platypus likely emerged after *Docodonta* in the evolution of mammals.\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ domain is the largest rank [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ the three domains are Archaea, Eubacteria, and Eukarya [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ domain Eukarya is split into kingdoms  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ from largest to smallest, kingdoms are divided into phyla **OR** phylum, classes, orders, families, genera **OR** genuses, and species     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ species are sometimes divided into subspecies  [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **largest rank** in the classification system is the **domain** ($\\checkmark$). There are three domains – **Archaea, Eubacteria, and Eukarya** ($\\checkmark$). The **domain Eukarya is divided into 4 kingdoms** ($\\checkmark$) – Protista, Fungi, Plantae, and Animalia. Each **kingdom is further divided, from largest to smallest into phyla, class, order, family, genus, and species** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0946","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0946\n\nWhich technique estimates the divergence time between species based on genetic differences?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Radioactive dating\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Molecular clock dating\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Comparative anatomy\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Studying fossil layers","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nMolecular clock dating uses the genetic differences between species to estimate their times of divergence. This method relies on the assumption that mutations accumulate at a relatively constant rate over time. By comparing the DNA sequences of different organisms, scientists can estimate how long ago their last common ancestor lived. **Choice B** is, therefore, correct.\n\nRadioactive dating is a method used to date rocks and other materials based on the known decay rates of radioactive isotopes. **Choice A** is, therefore, not correct.\n\nComparative anatomy involves comparing the structures of different organisms to understand their evolutionary relationships. While it can provide evidence of common ancestry and evolutionary trends, it does not offer a precise method for estimating the time of divergence between species based on genetic differences. **Choice C** is, therefore, not correct.\n\nStudying the distribution of fossils within sedimentary rock layers establishes relative ages and the chronological order of geological and evolutionary events. Whilst it is essential for constructing the timeline of life on Earth, it does not use genetic differences to estimate divergence times between species. **Choice D** is, therefore, not correct."},{"ref":"BI1155","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1155\n\nWhich taxonomic group of organisms represents a domain?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Eukaryota\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Plantae\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Protoctista\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Animalia","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nEukaryota is one of only three domains - the other two being Archaea and Eubacteria.\n\n**Choice B** (Plantae) is one of the five kingdoms, not a domain. **Choice C** (Protoctista) and  **Choice D** (Animalia) are also kingdoms."},{"ref":"BI1156","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1156\n\nThe table shows part of the amino acid sequence for the Haemoglobin Alpha (HBA1) gene in a number of different organisms.\n\n| Organism | Amino acid sequence for HBA1 |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| Human | AQVKGHGKKVADAL |\n| Shark | PSIKAHGAKVVTAL |\n| Duck | AQIKAHGKKVAAAL |\n| Bat | AQVKGHGKKVGDAL |\n|Cobra | KDIIHQGEKVGKAL |\n\nWhich cladogram would best represent the relationship between these organisms, using just the information provided in the table?\n\n| | | | |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Classification%20%26%20Cladistics/images/BI1156A.png\" alt=\"BI1156A\"> | B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Classification%20%26%20Cladistics/images/cladogram%20iii.png\" alt=\"cladogram iii\">|\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Classification%20%26%20Cladistics/images/BI1156C.png\" alt=\"BI1156C\"> | D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Classification%20%26%20Cladistics/images/BI1156d.png\" alt=\"BI1156d\"> |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nFirst, compare the sequences of the different organisms to the human sequence:\n\n| Organism | Amino acid sequence for HBA1 |Number of differences from human\n| :-: | :-: |:-: |\n| Human | AQVKGHGKKVADAL |0 (reference)|\n| Shark | PSIKAHGAKVVTAL |6|\n| Duck | AQIKAHGKKVAAAL |3|\n| Bat | AQVKGHGKKVGDAL |1|\n|Cobra | KDIIHQGEKVGKAL |9|\n\nThen rank the organisms by similarity in amino acid sequences for HBA1 to humans:\n\nMost similar is the bat, then the duck, then the shark, and then the one with the least similar sequence is the cobra\n\nThe correct cladogram is **Choice D** as it reflects this hierarchy.\n\nIn real life, cobra (a reptile) and human (a mammal) are both amniotes, sharing a more recent common ancestor than either does with shark, which is a cartilaginous fish. The observed pattern in the table, where the shark appears more similar to humans than the cobra based on HBA1 sequences, is an artefact of analysing a single gene and is not reflective of the broader evolutionary relationship.\n\n**Choices A, B**, and **C** are incorrect because they do not follow the relationship order suggested by the amino acid differences in the table."},{"ref":"BI1188","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1188\n\nThe cyanobacteria *Leptolyngbya nigrescens*  has recently been reclassified as *Phormidesmis nigrescens*. What is a  possible reason for this?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Observation of similar sensitivities to light between bacteria in the *Phormidesmis* genus and the newly classified *Phormidesmis nigrescens*\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Discovery of structural similarities of cyanobacteria to other bacteria in the *Leptolyngbya* genus\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Discovery of similarities in ribosomal RNA sequences between *Phormidesmis nigrescens* and other bacteria in the *Phormidesmis* genus\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ New evidence that *Phormidesmis nigrescens*  share a common ancestor with other cyanobacteria in the *Leptolyngbya* genus","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSimilarities between ribosomal RNA sequences between *Phormidesmis nigrescens*  and other bacteria in the *Phormidesmis* genus indicates that they are likely to share a common ancestor, belong in the same genus and therefore should be reclassified. **Choice C** is correct.\n\nSimilarities in behaviour do not indicate that *Phormidesmis nigrescens*  share a common ancestor with other bacteria in the *Phormidesmis* genus; **Choice A**  is incorrect. To be reclassified, the *Phormidesmis nigrescens* should be more similar to bacteria in the Phormidesmis genus than the *Leptolyngbya* genus, also structural similarities are not grounds for reclassification; **Choice B** is incorrect. To be reclassified, the *Phormidesmis nigrescens* should share a common ancestor with cyanobacteria in the *Phormidesmis* genus rather than the *Leptolyngbya* genus; **Choice D**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1390","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1390\n\nThe following image shows a member of the *Scrophulariaceae* (figwort) family drawn in 1774. Although this family was once among the largest groups of flowering plants, it has since been reclassified and is now less than half of its former size. Which of the following explains why reclassification of taxonomic groups may be required?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Classification%20%26%20Cladistics/images/BI1390.png\" alt=\"BI1390\">\n[Source: Bouchard, M. (1774). *Figwort (Scrophularia sp): flowering stem with separate flower*. Look and Learn. Retrieved December 30, 2023, from https://www.lookandlearn.com/history-images/YW042762VEL/Figwort-Scrophularia-sp-flowering-stem-with-separate-flower. Copyright BY CC 4.0]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Genomic sequencing\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Improvements in dichotomous keys\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Genetically modified species\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Discovery of new species","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWhen the taxonomic system was developed, species were largely classified according to physical similarities. With the discovery of DNA and gene sequencing techniques, it became possible to compare the genomes of organisms. Genomic data allows the evolutionary relationships between organisms to be determined much more accurately and thus can lead to the reclassification of some taxa. **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because dichotomous keys are used to identify organisms based on observable characteristics, not for reclassifying taxonomic groups. **Choice C** is incorrect because genetic modification is when the genetic material of an organism has been altered through human intervention, however, this does not lead to the reclassification of species. **Choice D** is incorrect since the discovery of a new species should not affect previously classified organisms, especially if genome sequencing is used as the primary means of classification."},{"ref":"BI1439","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1439\n\nA biology class was given the following information:\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n | | |\n | :-- | :-- |\n | I. | **W, X, Y** and **Z** are all closely related animal species. |\n | II. | **g, W** and **Y** all evolved from animal species **X**. |\n | III. | Species **g** is an extinct recent common ancestor of **W** and **Y**. |\n | IV. | Animal species **Z** is the least related to the others, with extinct species **h** serving as its most recent phylogenetic connection to the others. |\n\nBelow are cladograms drawn by four different students to represent the evolutionary relationships described.\n\nWhich student drew the cladogram correctly?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Classification%20%26%20Cladistics/images/BI1439.png\" alt=\"BI1439\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAnimal species **Z** needs to be in a different clade to **W** and **Y** as it is the least closely related. However, **Z** is still linked to **W** and **Y** via animal species **h**. **W** and **Y** are linked via a recent common ancestor, **g**, and **W, Y** and **g** are all linked via animal species **X**. This makes student **Choice B** correct.\nThe cladogram drawn by student A is incorrect in many ways, including placing extinct species **h** as the most distantly related species of **W** and **Y**, and placing **Z** as the common ancestor connecting all the other species. **Choice A** is, therefore, not the correct answer to this question.\n\nThe cladogram drawn by student C is incorrect in several places, including putting **h** as the most recent extinct ancestor of **W** and **X** and putting **g** as the common ancestor connecting all the other species. **Choice C** is, therefore, not the correct answer to this question.\n\nThe cladogram drawn by student D puts **X** and **Y** the wrong way round. **Choice D** is therefore also not the correct answer."},{"ref":"BI1487","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1487\n\nIn a cladogram, what is represented by a node?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ A new trait that is shared by two organisms\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The most recent common ancestor between species\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ A group of organisms with a common ancestor\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The most genetically distant organisms","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nNodes on a cladogram are a splitting point that represent the most recent ancestor between species; **Choice B** is correct.\n\nA node does not represent a new trait shared by two organisms; **Choice A** is incorrect. A group of organisms with a common ancestor is a clade, not a node; **Choice C**  is incorrect. The most genetically distant organism on a cladogram is an outgroup, not a node; **Choice D**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1522","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1522\n\nThe image shows the skulls of a gorilla, chimpanzee, and human. What conclusion can be drawn from the anatomies of the skulls?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Classification%20%26%20Cladistics/images/BI1522%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1522 fig1\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Zhang, K. (2015, May 1). Wikimedia Commons. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved February 29, 2024.https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: The_Skulls_of_the_gorilla,chimpanzee_and_humans_-_Kaiwei_Zhang.jpg. Copyright free]\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Gorillas and chimpanzees are more closely related to each other than they are to humans\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Humans are more intelligent than chimpanzees and gorillas \n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Gorillas, chimpanzees and humans share a recent common ancestor\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Chimpanzees and gorillas have a diploid chromosome number of 48, whereas humans have a diploid chromosome number of 46","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nThe similar anatomies of the gorilla, chimpanzee and human skull suggest they have a common ancestor; **Choice C**  is correct.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect, as similar anatomy is not enough to say that gorillas and chimpanzees are more closely related to each other than humans due to convergent evolution. **Choice B** is incorrect, as skull size is not necessarily correlated to intelligence. **Choice D**  is incorrect as skull size and shape do not provide any information on chromosome number."},{"ref":"BI2376","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2376\n\nWhich evidence led biologists to classify Archaea in a separate domain from Bacteria?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The presence of a cell wall\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The presence of 70s ribosomes\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The presence of circular chromosomes\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Differences in ribosomal RNA base sequences","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nThe ribosomal RNA (rRNA) base sequencing carried out by Carl Woese and George Fox (1977) revealed that Archaea and Bacteria form distinct evolutionary groups. The molecular differences provided evidence that organisms previously grouped together as prokaryotes should be classified in separate domains. This evidence supported the development of the three-domain classification system. **Choice D** is, therefore, correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because cell walls are also found in Bacteria. The presence of a cell wall, rather than its composition, does not distinguish Archaea from Bacteria.\n\n**Choice B**  is incorrect because both Archaea and Bacteria have 70S ribosomes. Differences in their rRNA base sequences, rather than the presence of this type of ribosome, provide evidence for their evolutionary separation.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because circular chromosomes occur in both Archaea and Bacteria. Their presence, therefore, does not provide a distinguishing feature that supports classification into separate domains."}]},{"name":"Conservation of Biodiversity","questions":[{"ref":"BI0901","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0901\n\nWhich definitions are matched to the correct form of diversity?\n\n| | Ecosystem diversity | Genetic diversity | Species diversity\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Different habitats, communities and ecological processes | The type of alleles in a population | Genetic variation within a species |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | The number of species and their abundance in an area | Phenotypic variation in a community | Genetic variability within a species |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Variety and abundance of different ecosystems in a geographical area| Genetic variability within a species or population | The number of species and their abundance in an area |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Different habitats, communities and ecological processes | The total number and type of epigenetic markers in a population | The variety of all life on Earth|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nEcosystem diversity is defined as the variety and abundance of different ecosystems present in a geographical area. Genetic diversity is the genetic variability within a species or population. Species diversity is the number and relative abundance of species found in a specific area. Therefore **Choice C** is correct.\n :br**Choice A** is incorrect because genetic diversity does not only refer to the type of alleles in a population but the number of different alleles in a population. **Choice B**  is incorrect because phenotypic variation refers to differences in observable characteristics, not genetic diversity, and the definition of ecosystem diversity shown in row B is actually the definition for species diversity. **Choice D**  is incorrect as genetic diversity does not relate to the control of the expression of genes (epigenetics) within a population and the variety of all life on Earth is the definition for biodiversity."},{"ref":"BI0902","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0902\n\nWhich of the following provides evidence for a higher level of biodiversity now compared to any time in the past?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Changing levels of atmospheric carbon dioxide\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The fossil record\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ A decrease in the rate of evolution\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Decreased levels of speciation","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe number of organisms found via the fossil record for any era is much lower compared to the number of organisms found on Earth now and those that continue to be found, so **Choice B**  is correct. **Choice A**  is incorrect as changing levels of atmospheric carbon dioxide provide evidence for humans' impact on carbon dioxide levels. **Choices C** and **D**  are incorrect as evidence suggests that the rate of evolution and speciation are increasing, which provides evidence for increasing biodiversity."},{"ref":"BI0903","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0903\n\nWhich is **not** a cause of anthropogenic species extinction?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Overhunting\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Deforestation\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Introduction of new diseases\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Volcanic eruptions","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAnthropogenic species extinction is extinction caused by human activities. Volcanic eruptions are natural events and are not caused by human activities. However, they can still contribute to species extinction by releasing ash, gases, and lava that disrupt ecosystems and reduce biodiversity. **Choice D** is therefore correct.\n\nWhen humans overhunt (**Choice A**) it can lead to a decline in populations and, in some cases, extinction. Deforestation (**Choice B**) is another human activity. The clearing of forests for purposes such as agriculture, logging or urban development, can destroy habitats and disrupt ecosystems, leading to extinction of species that rely on those habitats. When humans introduce new diseases to ecosystems (**Choice C**), either accidentally or deliberately, it can have devastating effects on native species and bring about extinction.  **Choices A**, **B**  and **C**  are therefore incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0904","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0904\n\nThe image shows changes in coverage to mixed dipterocarp forests in Southeast Asia over the last 100 years.\nWhich is not a factor that contributed to these changes?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Conservation%20of%20Biodiversity/images/BI0904_A4.2.png\" alt=\"BI0904_A4.2\">\n[Source: Adapted from San Jose. (2006, February 8). File: map of east asia.png - wikimedia commons. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved July 10, 2023 from URL https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Map_of_East_Asia.png. Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Building of new roads to mining sites\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Poaching of endangered species\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Planting of rubber plantations\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Building of hydroelectric dams","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe image shows the loss of forests in Southeast Asia over 100 years. Poaching of endangered species does not result in deforestation; **Choice B**  is correct.\n\nBuilding new roads to mining sites involves clearing land to mine sites, so **Choice A**  is incorrect. Planting rubber plantations requires clearing forests to plant rubber trees, so **Choice C**  is incorrect.  Building hydroelectric dams can lead to flooding of forests and irreparable damage to trees, so **Choice D** is also incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0905","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0905\n\nWhich statement(s) are possible solutions to the biodiversity crisis?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Promoting rewilding projects |\n    | II. | Recycling and reusing products |\n    | III. | Increase meat consumption |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nRewilding projects attempt to restore and rehabilitate ecosystems by reintroducing native species and allowing natural processes to become re-established.  **Statement I** is therefore correct. Recycling and reusing products means fewer resources will be extracted from the Earth which is a process that can destroy natural habitats and lead to excessive landfill use, so **Statement II** is correct. **Choice C**  is therefore correct (Statement I and II).\n\nIncreasing meat consumption (**Statement III**) results in more land being cleared for animals such as cattle to be bred, decreasing biodiversity. **Choice D** is therefore incorrect as it includes this statement. **Choices A** and **B** are missing one of the possible solutions to the crisis and are therefore incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0906","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0906\n\nWhich is **not** a cause of the current biodiversity crisis?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Decreased urbanisation\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The widespread use of human vaccines\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increased air travel\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increased transmission of diseases","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nUrbanisation is not decreasing, it is increasing all over the world, which is contributing to the current biodiversity crisis. **Choice A**  is correct.\n\nThe widespread use of vaccines has led to fewer people dying of diseases before reproductive age, contributing to worldwide population growth, which contributes to the current biodiversity crisis, so **Choice B** is incorrect. Increased air travel results in more pollution and makes it easier for diseases to spread worldwide which sometimes negatively impacts species, contributing to the biodiversity crisis, so **Choices C** and **D** are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0907","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0907\n\nHow does introducing new invasive species, such as Japanese knotweed (*Reynoutria japonica*), to ecosystems affect biodiversity?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Conservation%20of%20Biodiversity/images/BI0907.png\" alt=\"BI0907\">\n[Source: Adapted from Carter, W. (2019, September 11). File: reynoutria japonica in Brastad 1.jpg - Wikimedia Commons. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved July 10, 2023 from URL https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Reynoutria_japonica_in_Brastad_1.jpg. Copyright free]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increases biodiversity because there are more species in the ecosystem\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Decreases biodiversity as the invasive species outcompetes native species for resources\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Decreases biodiversity as new plants encourage new insects and animals to immigrate to the ecosystem\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Increases biodiversity as the invasive species brings new pests into the ecosystem","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nInvasive species are species that arrive in an ecosystem to which they are not endemic to and they often lack natural population controls such as herbivores or predators.  They therefore sometimes outcompete native species for resources which can decrease biodiversity.  **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect, as introducing an invasive species usually reduces biodiversity. There are many reasons for this, including competition with native species, predation, herbivory, habitat alteration and the introduction of disease. **Choice C** is incorrect as although new plants may encourage new insects and plants to immigrate into an area, this would increase biodiversity, not decrease it.  **Choice D** is incorrect because although invasive species can bring new pests, these pests often reduce biodiversity by decimating native populations."},{"ref":"BI0908","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0908\n\nThe table shows the number of tree species in two similarly sized forests. Which statement about the two communities is correct?\n\n| | Tree species | Forest 1 | Forest 2\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$ | *Quercus alba* | 30 | 25 |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$ | *Acer rubrum* | 12 | 25 |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$ | *Fagus sylvatica*| 50 | 25 |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$ | *Betula occidentalis* | 8 | 25|\n| $\\hspace{1em}$ | **Total number of trees** | 100 | 100|\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Forest 2 has the same species richness as Forest 1\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Forest 1 has a higher species evenness than Forest 2\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Forest 1 has a higher species richness than Forest 2\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Forest 2 has an equal species evenness to Forest 1","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nSpecies richness is the number of different species present in an area. There are 4 species present in both forests so **Choice A** is correct.\n\nSpecies evenness is the relative abundance of different species in an area. **Choices B** and **D** are incorrect as Forest 2 has a higher species evenness than Forest 1 as there is an equal number of each tree species. **Choice C** is incorrect as species richness is the same in both forests."},{"ref":"BI0909","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0909\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe image shows the changes to mixed dipterocarp forests in Southeast Asia over 100 years. Researchers have concluded that the changes are mainly due to anthropogenic factors.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Conservation%20of%20Biodiversity/images/BI0904bi.png\" alt=\"BI0904bi\">\n[Source: Adapted from San Jose. (2006, February 8). File: map of east asia.png - wikimedia commons. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved July 10, 2023 from URL https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Map_of_East_Asia.png. Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n1. Distinguish between the forest coverage in 1900 and 2000  [1 mark]\n\n2. State the definition for ‘anthropogenic’.    [1 mark]\n\n3. Explain two possible anthropogenic reasons for differences in forest coverage between 1900 and 2000.    [2 marks]\n\n4. Suggest what evidence scientists would need to collect from the forests to provide evidence for a biodiversity crisis in South East Asia.    [1 mark]\n\n5. Suggest why it might be important to increase biodiversity in the area.    [1 mark]\n\n6. Conservationists in the area have started a reforestation project in the area, in an attempt to restore the forest to 1900 coverage levels. Discuss what effects this would have on biodiversity.    [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is less forest coverage in the year 2000 **COMPARED TO** the year 1900, **OR** there is more forest coverage in the year 1900 to the year 2000     [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nThere is **less forest** coverage in the year 2000 compared to the year 1900 ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ environmental **OR** ecological change caused by human activity     [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nThe term anthropogenic refers to an **environmental change** caused, directly or indirectly, by **human activity** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ deforestation for agriculture OR urbanisation     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pollution from factories OR vehicles weakening trees     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ human population growth resulting in more trees being cut to build infrastructure **OR** provide fuels **OR** make resources such as paper     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ spread of pests **OR** diseases **OR** introduction of invasive species causes damage to trees     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ forest fires caused by human activities     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mining **OR** oil drilling **OR** other resource extraction     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ specific examples related to climate change such as changing rainfall patterns **OR** increased frequency of extreme weather *(do **NOT** allow climate change without a specific example)*     [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nTwo anthropogenic reasons for the loss of forests between 1900 and 2000 are **deforestation** to grow **crops**  ($\\checkmark$) and **human population growth** resulting in more trees being cut down for the **building of infrastructure** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ surveys of plant and animal species in forested **AND** deforested areas to compare species richness **OR** abundance **OR** diversity over time     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ analysis of species population trends     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ amassing data from citizen scientists on species richness **OR** abundance, **OR** diversity     [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nTo provide evidence for the biodiversity crisis in South East Asia, scientists would need to conduct surveys on **species diversity in the area over time**. ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ maintain food chains **OR** webs     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ trees carry out carbon capture **OR** release oxygen into the atmosphere **OR** climate change mitigation     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prevent extinction of organisms     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ promote tourism in the area **OR** aesthetic reasons     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ slow down the spread of zoonotic diseases (as loss of habitat means that animals will live closer together and closer to humans)     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ potential medical resources **OR** webs     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ preservation of cultural heritage of indigenous communities     [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ethical responsibility to preserve biodiversity     [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nIt is important to increase biodiversity in the area, as **trees** carry out **carbon capture** ($\\checkmark$) which contributes to mitigating climate change and promotes **tourism** in the area, which would **boost the economy** of South East Asia ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. Any **three** of the following (**must** state what effect it has on biodiversity for mark):\n\n    ***Increase biodiversity because***...\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ provides more habitats     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ provides places for organisms to hide from predators **OR** hunters         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prevents floods **OR** landslides due to increased transpiration     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ provides more food for organisms     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ encourages the establishment of complex ecological interactions **OR** an example such as an increase in number of pollinators for plant reproduction     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increases species richness     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increases genetic diversity **OR** gene flow     [1 mark]\n\n    ***Decrease biodiversity because***...\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ invasive species are introduced     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ native species are displaced during deforestation         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is competition between newly planted trees and existing vegetation         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ species competition is influenced by edge effects     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ soil disturbance during planting impacts other species negatively     [1 mark]\n\n    ***Have little effect on biodiversity because***...\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ more than one approach is needed to change biodiversity in the area     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ limited species of trees **OR** monoculture are planted         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tree species are not selected for their suitability to the area     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reforested areas are isolated **OR** lack connections to the existing forest     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they are not monitored **OR** managed properly     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reforested areas are too close to human settlements, potentially leading to conflict     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ trees are planted but animals are not reintroduced    [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nReforesting may increase biodiversity in the area as it will provide more habitats for organisms ($\\checkmark$) and provide organisms with a place to hide from hunters or predators ($\\checkmark$). However, it may decrease biodiversity if only limited species of trees are replanted in the area ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0910","marks":"10","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0910\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe image shows the extinct North Island giant moa (*Dinornis novaezealandiae*), a flightless bird that was native to New Zealand. The Moa became extinct in the last 600 years, during the current sixth mass extinction.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Conservation%20of%20Biodiversity/images/BI0910.png\" alt=\"BI0910\">\n[Source: Adapted from Michael, B. H. (2012). File: Euryapteryx.jpg - Wikimedia Commons. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved July 21, 2023 from URL https://\ncommons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Euryapteryx.jpg. Copyright under CC BY 3.0\n]\n\n1.\n    1. State the key difference between the current sixth mass extinction and the previous mass extinctions.  [1 mark]\n\n    2. State one possible cause of the extinction of the Moa.  [1 mark]\n\n2.\n    1. Outline the physical evidence scientists have collected indicating that the Moa existed.  [1 mark]\n\n    2. In the past, scientists thought that the kiwi (*Apteryx haastii*), a flightless bird based in New Zealand, was the Moa’s closest living relative.  However, this has been disproven. State what evidence scientists may have found to disprove this relationship.  [1 mark]\n\n    3. The Moa’s closest living relatives are the South American Tinamou (*Tinamus major*), which have the ability to fly. Explain why these birds are less likely to go extinct than the Moa.  [1 mark]\n\n3. Evidence suggests that the Tinamou and Moa shared a common ancestor, but they evolved independently from each other when they were geographically isolated millions of years ago. Explain the evolution of the Tinamou with reference to natural selection.    [3 marks]\n\n3. Another animal at risk of extinction is the Javan rhinoceros (*Rhinoceros sondaicus*), shown in the image below. There are thought to be fewer than 80 in the world. Suggest two methods scientists could use to prevent its extinction, with a reason for each.    [2 marks]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Conservation%20of%20Biodiversity/images/BI0910b.png\" alt=\"BI0910b\">\n[Source: Adapted from Nelson, S. (2012, July 20). Category: Rhinoceros Sondaicus. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved July 21, 2023 from URL https://\ncommons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category: Rhinoceros_sondaicus. Copyright under CC BY 3.0\n]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sixth mass extinction is mostly anthropogenic **OR** mainly caused by human activity **WHEREAS** the previous ones were (primarily) caused by natural events     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the sixth mass extinction event is **FASTER** than the previous ones\n     [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nThe current sixth mass extinction is mostly **anthropogenic**, whereas the **previous** ones were primarily caused by **natural events** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hunting    for food         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fragility of eggs means that they were prone to damage\n     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ introduction of predators **OR** Polynesian dogs **OR** invasive **OR** non-native species\n     [1 mark]\n\n     $\\ce{ -> }$ loss of habitat due to human activity\n     [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nOne possible cause of the extinction of the Moa is them being **hunted** ($\\checkmark$) for food by humans.\n\n2.\n    1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fossils     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rock art **OR** artefacts depicting Moa\n     [1 mark]\n\n     $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA analysis from Moa remains         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ isotopic analysis from Moa remains\n     [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n **Fossils** ($\\checkmark$) provide physical evidence for the existence of the Moa.\n\n    2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ dissimilar DNA **OR** amino acid sequences     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ detailed morphological comparisons show notable differences in skeletal structures **OR** body size, **OR** other anatomical features\n     [1 mark]\n\n     $\\ce{ -> }$ phylogenetic analysis placed the Kiwi and Moa on separate branches of the phylogenetic tree         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fossil evidence **OR** fossil dating has shown different ancestral lineages\n     [1 mark]\n\n     $\\ce{ -> }$ another species discovered that appears more closely related to the Moa\n     [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nScientists may have found that the Kiwi had **dissimilar DNA sequences** ($\\checkmark$) to the Moa, and more similar DNA sequences to another organism.\n\n    3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Tinamous can fly so can escape predators **OR** hunters more easily    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Tinamous can build their nests high up in trees so that their young are less vulnerable\n     [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nTinamous are less likely to go extinct than the Moa, as they can **build their nests high** up in trees so that their **young are less vulnerable** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ geographic isolation of the Tinamou **OR** Moa lineage meant that different populations were subjected to varying environmental conditions         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ random mutations lead to genetic  variation\n     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic variation may have resulted in various physical and behavioural traits\n     [1 mark]\n\n     $\\ce{ -> }$ certain traits among Tinamous **OR** Moas would have been more advantageous than others\n     [1 mark]\n\n     $\\ce{ -> }$ for example, camouflage **OR** flight ability **OR** specific feeding adaptations **OR** any other suitable example of advantageous trait          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ therefore greater chance of survival **OR** more organisms with these advantageous traits survived to reproductive age\n     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reproduced and passed on alleles\n     [1 mark]\n\n     $\\ce{ -> }$ repeated for many generations\n     [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nThe **geographic isolation** of the Tinamou lineage meant that different populations were subjected to **different environmental conditions** ($\\checkmark$).  **Random mutations** in the populations resulted in **genetic variation** ($\\checkmark$), which may have resulted in **different physical and behavioural traits** ($\\checkmark$). Some traits among Tinamous, such as camouflage or flight abilities, would have been more **advantageous** than others ($\\checkmark$), resulting in **higher survival rates**($\\checkmark$). Therefore, these organisms with advantageous traits were able to **pass on their alleles** to **successive generations** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ breeding programmes **OR** carefully selecting individuals for breeding, **REASON** to increase numbers of rhino **OR** population size **OR** genetic diversity **OR** increase chance of survival of offspring **OR** produce offspring that can later be released into the wild; **OR** freeze eggs and sperm **OR** gametes **REASON** to preserve genetic diversity **OR** to use IVF and a closely related rhino species to produce more Javan rhinos         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ habitat protection **OR** restoration projects, **REASON** to provide safe spaces for rhino **OR** to provide enough resources for rhino in natural area\n     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ place rhinos into zoos **OR** nature reserves, **REASON** to protect from poaching, **OR** hunting, **OR** habitat loss, **OR** for research **OR** for monitoring **OR** public awareness **OR** education **OR** funding **OR** to have animals that can be released into the wild after catastrophic events, **OR** severe disease, in natural habitat\n     [1 mark]\n\n     $\\ce{ -> }$ change laws to protect rhinos, **REASON** to reduce hunting **OR** poaching **OR** export of rhino horn **OR** use of rhino horn\n     [1 mark]\n\n     $\\ce{ -> }$ sequence DNA of as many Javan rhinos as possible **REASON** to preserve genetic diversity **OR** different alleles          [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nScientists could prevent the extinction of the Javan rhinoceros by introducing **breeding programmes** to **increase** the rhino **population** ($\\checkmark$) or start **habitat restoration projects** to provide enough area with enough **resources to support** the rhino ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0911","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0911\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline why scientists must have several approaches to conserve biodiversity.   [4 marks]\n\n2. There are approximately 80 Javan Rhinoceros (*Rhinoceros sondaicus*) in the world. Discuss the environmental and economic implications the Indonesian government must consider before volunteering the Javan Rhinoceros to the EDGE programme.        [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain why rapid human population growth is linked to the current biodiversity crisis.   [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ no single approach is sufficient **OR** establishing conservation areas is often effective, but without reintroduction of lost species it will have limited success **OR** other suitable example showing that when one approach is used, another is required for effective conservation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ different species **OR** ecosystems require different measures        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ not all methods are affordable for different countries **OR** organisations, **OR** governments    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ biodiversity encompasses a vast range of organisms, habitats, and ecosystems with different interdependencies [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ socio-economic factors need to be considered to ensure that conservation efforts take into account local communities' needs and interests   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ongoing changes in biodiversity may mean that strategies need to be adapted accordingly      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ multiple approaches can create a more resilient and effective conservation strategy   [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Scientists must have multiple approaches to conserve biodiversity as **no single approach** is sufficient ($\\checkmark$), different species require **different methods** ($\\checkmark$) and not all methods are **affordable for different governments** ($\\checkmark$).  In addition, biodiversity encompasses a vast range of organisms and habitats with **different interdependencies** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following (*implications must be stated*):\n\n    ***Environmental negative considerations***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ disruption of current food chains **OR** webs   [1 mark]\n\n    ***Environmental positive considerations***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ restoration of food webs **OR** chains  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ habitats **OR** forests will have to be restored     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the Javan Rhinoceros is a keystone species, so conserving this species would indirectly protect the ecosystem      [1 mark]\n\n    ***Economic negative considerations***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can be expensive to put rhinos in breeding programmes **OR** zoos, **OR** EDGE program would require financial investments in research **OR** monitoring, **OR** conservation initiatives            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ land that has been used for urbanisation **OR** businesses **OR** agriculture  will have to be reforested            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ local communities might rely on the same resources as the rhinoceros, **OR** local communities' livelihoods might be impacted by conservation measures       [1 mark]\n\n    ***Economic positive considerations***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase tourism in the area      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ may improve international cooperation **OR** trade agreements, **OR** foreign investments      [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Before volunteering the Javan rhinoceros to the EDGE programme, economic considerations must be taken into account.  For example, protecting the Javan rhinoceros would likely **increase tourism** in the area ($\\checkmark$) as people will travel to the country to see the rhinos. However, it can be **expensive** to put rhinos into **breeding programmes** ($\\checkmark$) or **reforest** land that has been **urbanised** ($\\checkmark$), and this may be **unaffordable** for the Indonesian government. A positive environmental consideration is that **reforestation will increase biodiversity** ($\\checkmark$) in the area. However, it may also result in **disruption of current, stable food chains** ($\\checkmark$)in the area.\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ more hunting **OR** poaching **OR** for food **OR** resources, which endangers animals [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ loss of habitats due to urbanisation **OR** agriculture [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased agriculture can lead to a monoculture **OR** a loss in diversity  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ deforestation for building infrastructure **OR** resources such as paper **OR** agriculture   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased pollution due to increased vehicles **OR** global transport **OR** industry **OR** factories can cause damage to organisms **OR** contribute to global warming   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased use of fertilisers leading to eutrophication      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased spread of diseases to plants **OR** animals due to increased global transport [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased risk of spread of invasive species due to increased global transport     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased spread of pests due to increased air travel damaging agriculture **OR** forest ecosystems     [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Rapid human population growth is linked to the current biodiversity crisis because as the global population grows, there is an increased need for resources which can result in **loss of habitat** due to **increased agriculture** ($\\checkmark$), an increase in the use of **fertilisers** which can result in **eutrophication** ($\\checkmark$) and more **hunting** of animals ($\\checkmark$) which lowers animal populations.  An increase in agriculture which utilises **monocultures** lowers species diversity in the area ($\\checkmark$).  An increase in the human population is also correlated with **increased global transport**, which can result in increased **pollution**, which contributes to **global warming** ($\\checkmark$), which can have a detrimental effect on ecosystems and also can result in an increased **spread of invasive species** ($\\checkmark$) and **pests** ($\\checkmark$), which lowers biodiversity within ecosystems."},{"ref":"BI1458","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1458\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nMountain gorillas (*Gorilla beringei beringei*)  were reclassified from \"Critically Endangered\" to \"Endangered\" by the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) in November 2018. This change in status was a significant conservation success story, reflecting the positive impact of concerted efforts by governments, conservation organisations, and local communities to protect and increase the population of this subspecies of the eastern gorilla. \n\nThe table below shows the changes in the number of mountain gorillas from 2010 to 2018.\n\n| Year | Number of gorillas |\n\n| :-: | :-: |\n\n| 2010 | 480 |\n\n| 2016 | 604 |\n\n| 2018 | 1004 |\n\n1.  Calculate the percentage increase in the number of gorillas from 2010 to 2018.  [1 mark]\n\n2.  Although the mountain gorilla is rarely seen in zoos, another gorilla subspecies, the western lowland gorilla (*Gorilla gorilla gorilla*) is. Explain how zoos play a critical role in the conservation of endangered species. [3 marks]\n\nStudies have shown that the invasive plant species *Sericostachys scandens* affects forest composition and may affect the Grauer’s gorilla (*Gorilla beringei graueri*) recovery in Congo. \n\n3.  Outline two precautions that should be taken before considering biological control of invasive plants such as these.    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The following\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 109.16% **OR** 109.2%    [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    $\\dfrac{(1004 - 480)}{480} \\times 100\\%$                                                 \n\n    = :answer[109.16%] ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2.  Any **three** of the following\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ animal husbandry facilities to help prevent death from disease or injury    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ maintain detailed records **OR** pedigrees **OR** known studbooks to track genetic lineage used to choose breeding pairs to maintain genetic diversity **OR** minimise inbreeding    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ exchange animals between different institutions to introduce new genetic diversity    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ captive breeding **OR** artificial insemination **OR** in vitro fertilisation increases chance of successful breeding **OR** means they do not have to find a mate    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  reintroduction programs **OR** release animals into wild    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ scientists **OR** researchers can study the species up close **OR** in detail for greater understanding of how to protect it *in-situ*    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ educational programs to educate the public about the importance of animal conservation    [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Zoos facilitate the **exchange** of **animals** between institutions ($\\checkmark$) and maintain **detailed records** to track **genetic lineage** used to choose **breeding pairs** to introduce **new genetic diversity** ($\\checkmark$).  **Artificial insemination** also increases the **success rate** of breeding ($\\checkmark$) endangered species. Moreover, zoos are instrumental in **reintroducing programs** that release **animals** back into the **wild**, directly contributing to restoring **natural populations** ($\\checkmark$) and ecosystems.\n\n3.  Any **two** of the following \n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ research to make sure control organism specifically targets invasive species **OR** does not affect native species **OR** does not damage ecosystem **OR** habitats **OR** food chains    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ conditions in the target environment should support the survival of the control species    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ control species should have a natural control **OR** predator    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ control species should not spread outside the targeted area **OR** should not become an invasive species themselves    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ long-term monitoring plans to observe the effects of the biocontrol species    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ possible socio-economic impacts **OR** consider ethical **OR** cultural **OR** social implications of control species should be assessed    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plan should be developed in case species need to be controlled **OR** eradicated [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Before using biological control, it is crucial to conduct **thorough research** to ensure that the control organism **specifically targets** the **invasive** species **without impacting native** species or the **ecosystem** ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the **conditions** in the target environment must **support** the **survival** of the **control** species ($\\checkmark$)\n\n, ensuring it can effectively reduce the invasive population **without becoming invasive** itself($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1599","marks":"3","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1599\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nConservationists must consider the size and arrangement of in situ nature parks when creating and managing them. The diagram below shows the theoretical design of three sets of nature reserves.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Conservation%20of%20Biodiversity/images/nature%20reserve%20design%20BI1599ii.png\" alt=\"nature reserve design BI1599ii\">\n\nFor each pair of designs labelled A and B, compare the two and determine which design is better suited for the long-term preservation and management of animal populations. For each comparison, provide a reason to justify your choice. [3 marks]","markScheme":"**AI vs BI**\n\nany **one** of the following:\n\n*AI is better than BI sincee a larger reserve*:\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ supports more species **OR** a wider variety of species [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ ensures a more stable food chain **OR** provides more resources [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ reduction in the chance of inbreeding **OR** extinction [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ can better buffer species against environmental changes because it includes a variety of microhabitats **OR** climates [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ more likely to support keystone species, critical for structure of community [1 mark]\n\n**AII vs BII**\n\nany **one** of the following:\n\n*AII is better than BII since a less fragmented reserve*:\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ has better connectivity for breeding **OR** gene flow [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ has reduced edge effect so fewer disturbances **OR** predators **OR** invasive species **OR** reduces habitat degradation preserving the core habitat for sensitive species [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ provides enough resources to sustain viable populations in the long term [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ might reduce human-wildlife conflict, as animals less likely to stray into human-populated areas [1 mark]\n\n**AIII vs BIII**\n\nany **one** of the following:\n\n*AIII is better than BIII since corridors*:\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ facilitate movement of animals **OR** migration which is crucial for breeding **OR** finding food **OR** escaping predators **OR** for maintaining genetic diversity [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ can make the reserve more accessible for maintenance and management purposes [1 mark]\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n**AI is better** than BI because the **larger reserve** area in AI can **support more species**, as it provides **more resources** ($\\checkmark$).\n**AII is better** than BII because it is **less fragmented**. This **minimises** the **edge effect**, which **reduces exposure** to threats such as **poaching**, **predation**, and **invasive species**. ($\\checkmark$).\n**AIII is better** than BIII because the **corridors** in AIII **facilitate animal movement**, which is essential for **maintaining genetic diversity**, accessing **resources**, and **escaping predators**. ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1684","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1684\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe Eurasian beaver (*Castor fiber*) has been described as a species that promotes biodiversity. Beavers are herbivores that feed on freshwater plants and tree bark. They use their sharp front teeth to fell trees and use the logs and branches to build dams that create pools in which they can build their lodge. A beaver lodge is a construction in which they nest and raise their young, providing protection from predators.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Conservation%20of%20Biodiversity/images/BI1684%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1684 fig1\">\n\n[Source: Jerzy Strzelecki, retrieved July 2024 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Castor_fiber_07(js),_Narew_River,\n\n_Poland.jpg, copyright under CC BY-SA 3.0]\n\n1. Suggest ways in which the reintroduction of beavers can increase biodiversity. [4 marks]\n\nThe effect of introducing beavers on the species diversity of birds and bats was studied in four areas: on river banks, one without any beaver activity and one where beavers are present, and then ponds, again, one without beaver activity and one where beavers were present. The graph below shows the calculated Simpson’s reciprocal index of Diversity for each area.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Conservation%20of%20Biodiversity/images/BI1684%20fig2.png\" alt=\"BI1684 fig2\">\n\n2. State two necessary measurements to calculate Simpson’s reciprocal index of diversity. [1 mark]\n\n3. Calculate the percentage increase in the species diversity of bats in the pond after the introduction of the beaver. [2 marks]\n\nBeavers became extinct in the UK over 400 years ago. Reintroduction programmes are now being carried out in various regions of the UK.\n\n4. Suggest **three** anthropogenic activities that could have led to the extinction of the beavers in the UK. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ many different habitats **OR** niches are created by beaver activity [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ dams create pools that slow the run-off of rivers, benefiting plant rooting [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ dams generate wetlands that harbour insect **OR** invertebrate species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ this promotes a food chain with consumers such as birds [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ pools are habitats for aquatic plants **OR** fish [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ felling trees allows sunlight onto the forest floor, benefiting ground-level plants and shrubs [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fallen trees are a source of food for microorganisms **OR** detritivores [1 mark]\n\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n The activity of beavers **creates many habitats** for other organisms ($\\checkmark$), with an overall increase in biodiversity. For example, the felling of trees opens up patches of ground to sunlight, **promoting ground-level vegetation** ($\\checkmark$). The pools created by beaver dams **attract insects** ($\\checkmark$) and the consumers (such as birds) that **feed on those insects** ($\\checkmark$). Dams slow the run-off of rainwater which has a **beneficial effect on plant roots** which are not washed away by fast-flowing water ($\\checkmark$). The pools also attract **fish species and aquatic plants** that can oxygenate the pond water ($\\checkmark$); beavers do not eat fish, so there is no harm to the fish from them.\n\n2. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ total number of different species in the area **OR** species richness **AND** total number of organisms of each species **OR** species evenness [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n Two measurements needed to calculate Simpson’s Diversity Index are the **total number of species in the area** and the **total number of organisms of each species**($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ percentage increase = {(final volume - initial volume)/initial volume} $\\times$ 100\\% [1 mark]\n\n **OR** {(0.81-0.51) / 0.51} $\\times$ 100\\%\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 58.8\\% (allow numbers between 52 and 64%) [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n Percentage increase = **{(0.81$-$0.51) / 0.51} x 100\\%** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n = **58.8** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hunting for fur **OR** castoreum [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ habitat destruction **OR** the draining of wetlands **OR** other hydrological changes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ deforestation **OR** logging **OR** increase in agricultural land [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ water pollution [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ fragmentation of landscape [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ urbanisation **OR** industrial development **OR** road construction [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ introduction of alien species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ trade **OR** travel leading to introduction of disease [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ use of pesticides **OR** herbicides **OR** agricultural chemicals [1 mark]\n\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n The extinction of beavers in the UK may have been caused by **overhunting** for their valuable **fur** and **castoreum** ($\\checkmark$), combined with **extensive habitat destruction** due to the **draining of wetlands** ($\\checkmark$) and **deforestation** for **agricultural expansion** ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the **introduction of non-native species** ($\\checkmark$) and **diseases**, through international **travel**, ($\\checkmark$) further contributed to their decline."},{"ref":"BI2323","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2323\n\nWhich species are prioritised for conservation in the EDGE of Existence programme?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species that are genetically distinct from other living species, and are in danger of extinction.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species that are evolutionarily distinct, and are found in vulnerable ecosystems.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species that are closely related to keystone species, and are in danger of extinction.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species that are keystone species, and are found in vulnerable ecosystems.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because the EDGE of Existence program highlights and focuses on species that are evolutionarily distinct with few living relatives and, therefore, represent a disproportionately high level of genetic diversity. Additionally, the EDGE of Existence program only prioritises species on the endangered and vulnerable IUCN lists, which are, therefore, in danger of extinction.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the EDGE of Existence program does not use vulnerable ecosystems as a criterion. It focuses solely on species that are evolutionarily distinct and threatened with extinction.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because while keystone species play a crucial role in maintaining ecosystem structure and function, they are not necessarily evolutionarily distinct. The EDGE programme does not prioritise species based on their ecological role.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because neither keystone species nor vulnerable ecosystems are criteria for the EDGE of Existence program."},{"ref":"BI2378","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2378\n\nThe graph shows the global human population since 1700 CE, with a projection made to 2086.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Conservation%20of%20Biodiversity/images/QuesEdit2.png\" alt=\"QuesEdit2\">\n\nAccording to the projection, what will be the percentage population increase between 2000 and 2050 CE?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 27%\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 38%\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 62%\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 88%","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because the global human population in 2000 was 6.0 billion, projected to increase to 9.7 billion by 2050. \\\nThe increase is:\n9.7 billion − 6.0 billion = 3.7 billion\n\nTo calculate the percentage increase:\\\nPercentage increase = ($\\dfrac{\\ce{increase}}{\\ce{population in 2000}}) \\times 100$%\n\n= ($\\dfrac{\\ce{3.7 billion}}{\\ce{6.0 billion}}) \\times 100$% = 61.67%\n\nAll the choices are stated to 2 significant figures, so rounding this answer to two significant figures gives **62%**.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Conservation%20of%20Biodiversity/images/AnsEdit2.png\" alt=\"AnsEdit2\">\n\n**Choices A, B** and **D** are incorrect because they all contain calculation errors."},{"ref":"BI2714","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2714\n\nBelow is a list of conservation strategies:\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Establishing a zoo to breed endangered species in controlled environments |\n    | II. | National parks and protected areas where species live in their natural habitat |\n    | III. | Seed banks that store genetic material from endangered plant species |\n\nWhich of the statements describes an example of in-situ conservation?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is false because a zoo is an example of ex-situ conservation (when species are protected outside of their natural habitat). \\\n**Statement II** is true because a National park is an example of in-situ conservation, focusing on protecting species in their natural habitats.\\\n**Statement III** is false because a seed bank is an example of ex-situ conservation.\n\n**Choice B** is therefore correct as it only gives the true **Statement II**.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are all incorrect as they include one or more of the false **Statements I** and **III**."},{"ref":"BI2715","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2715\n\nThe following image shows the restoration of an old quarry site into a rehabilitated park.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Conservation%20of%20Biodiversity/images/BI2715.png\" alt=\"BI2715\">\n[Source: Tzeferis, P. (2016, March 11). *Qyarry Rehabillitation. Wikipedia*. Retrieved April 30, 2025, from: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Quarry_Rehabillitation.JPG. CC BY-SA 4.0\n]\n\nWhat term describes this process?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Rewilding\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Reclamation\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sustainable development\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ In-situ conservation","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because reclamation is the process of restoring disturbed land, such as an old quarry, to a functional ecosystem or other land use (e.g., a park). This often involves soil restoration, planting vegetation, and reconstructing habitats.\\\n**Choice A** is incorrect because rewilding refers to the restoration of ecosystems by reintroducing native species and minimising human intervention. While rewilding can occur in some restoration projects, quarry rehabilitation typically involves more active human efforts.  \\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because sustainable development refers to using resources in a way that meets present needs without compromising the needs of future generations. \\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because in-situ conservation focuses on protecting species in their natural habitats. This term does not describe the process of rehabilitating a disturbed land area, such as a quarry."},{"ref":"BI2716","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2716\n\nWhich of the following has/have directly contributed to biodiversity loss?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | The overhunting of African elephants for their ivory |\n    | II. | The expansion of cities like São Paulo, Brazil |\n    | III. | The spread of the invasive zebra mussels in North America through ballast water from ships |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because overhunting is one cause of the biodiversity crisis. \\\n**Statement II** is true because urbanisation is a cause of the biodiversity crisis. \\\n**Statement III** is true because the introduction/spread of pests, diseases and alien species due to global transport are causes of the biodiversity crisis.\n\nTherefore, **Choice D** is the correct answer as **Statements I, II** and **III** are true."},{"ref":"BI2808","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2808\n\nThe electric blue tarantula (*Chilobrachys natanicharum*, pictured below) is a newly-listed (2023) species found in the mangrove swamps of south-western Thailand. The vivid blue colour is created by nanostructures on the spider’s hairs that produce an iridescent effect.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Conservation%20of%20Biodiversity/images/BI2808.png\" alt=\"BI2808\">\n\nThe electric blue tarantula *Chilobrachys natanicharum*\n\n[Source: Chomphuphuang, Narin *et al* (2024 March, 28), File: *Chilobrachys natanicharum2* [image]. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 2025 April, 30 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Chilobrachys_natanicharum2.jpg. CC BY 4.0]\n\nWhich statement about the electric blue tarantula is most likely to be correct?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The electric blue tarantula would be able to mate successfully with another tarantula found in Thailand, *Chilobrachys huahini*, and produce fertile offspring.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The electric blue tarantula has recently evolved from darker-coloured species of tarantula.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The electric blue tarantula was previously classified in the class Insecta (insects).\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The electric blue tarantula has existed for a long time, but was not discovered until recently.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because, like the vast majority of animal species that live on Earth today, this species had been unknown to humans. It has existed in remote areas away from human habitation, in a niche that makes it a rare global species. As such, it was not discovered until 2023, when human intervention to prevent habitat loss to palm oil farming identified the species. \\\n**Choice A** is incorrect because *Chilobrachys huahini* is a distinct species; only members of the same species can reproduce successfully and produce fertile offspring. \\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because there is no evidence of the recent emergence of this species; it is more likely to have been undiscovered for a long time. \\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because all spiders, including the tarantula, belong to the class *Arachnida* (arachnids). The key difference is the number of pairs of legs; insects have 3 pairs, whereas arachnids have 4 pairs."},{"ref":"BI2809","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2809\n\nThe image shows Aotearoa’s (New Zealand) North Island giant moa (*Dinornis novaezealandiae*), which became extinct in the period 1300 - 1500 A.D.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Conservation%20of%20Biodiversity/images/BI2809.png\" alt=\"BI2809\">\n[Source: Adapted from Frederick William Frohawk (16 July 1861 - 10 December 1946). File: *Dinornis novaezealandiae* [image]. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 2025 April 30 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dinornis_novaezealandiae.png. Public domain]\n\nWhich of the following factors was **not** a contributing factor to the extinction of the giant moa during that period?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Habitat loss caused by human encroachment\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Anthropogenic climate change\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Hunting\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Predation by non-human species","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct (not a factor in the moa’s extinction) because this is an example of anthropogenic extinction, but not a direct result of climate change. In the period 1300-1500 A.D., human-caused climate change had not yet occurred; such climate changes began to occur in the mid-20th century and have continued to the present. \\\n**Choice A** is incorrect (a factor in the moa’s extinction) because human forest clearance would have taken place to create living and farming space for humans, their crops and livestock. This will have reduced the moa’s habitat area, impacting its population.\\\n**Choice C** is incorrect (a factor in the moa’s extinction) hunting by humans was the single largest factor impacting the moa’s population. They were hunted mainly as a food source for the early settlers of Aotearoa. \\\n**Choice D** is incorrect (a factor in the moa’s extinction) because the moa was not an apex predator; the Haast’s eagle (*Hieraaetus moorei*) was a natural predator of the moa, so it played a role in reducing the moa’s population size."},{"ref":"BI2810","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2810\n\nWhat is the main cause of Southeast Asia's loss of dipterocarp forest ecosystems?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Plantations of palm oil (*Elaeis guineensis*)\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Fungal disease affecting trees\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Nutrient depletion in the soil\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Anthropogenic climate change","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because palm oil plantations now cover large areas previously inhabited by dipterocarp species. Palm oil is used widely in the food and cosmetics industries.\\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because, whilst fungal disease does occur in all natural rainforests, it has not caused widespread loss of dipterocarp species. \\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because soil nutrient depletion will have occurred, but as a result of biodiversity loss, not a cause of it. \\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because, whilst anthropogenic (human-caused) climate change is a cause of ecosystem loss, it is not the main cause in this case."},{"ref":"BI2811","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2811\n\nWhich is the most significant factor in the loss of global biodiversity being experienced in the 21st century?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Industrialisation\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Human population growth\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Invasive plant species\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Deforestation","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because the rapid growth in human population (which has included a doubling in the period 1960-2020) has put pressure on all the Earth’s resources that humans rely upon. In doing so, habitat loss has had a major negative effect on thousands of species across all five kingdoms. \\\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are all incorrect; whilst they are all factors contributing to global biodiversity loss, they are not the overarching cause. The question asks for the ‘most significant factor’, which is human population growth."},{"ref":"BI2812","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2812\n\nWhich approach, carried out by conservationists in zoos and botanical gardens, defines the transition between *ex-situ* conservation and *in-situ* conservation?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Building more capacity in zoos and botanical gardens.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Carrying out artificial selection within zoos and botanical gardens.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Building the population of captive-bred species across zoos and botanical gardens in many countries.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Release of captive-bred species from zoos and botanical gardens into nature reserves.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because the transfer of ex-situ to in-situ means the return of species from captivity to areas that could become their natural habitats again. Nature reserves increase the chances of successful re-introduction, although many other measures would have to be taken to ensure success. \\\n**Choice A** is incorrect because building more captive breeding areas will not increase the chances of being able to reintroduce species back into the wild. \\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because, whilst artificial selection would allow desirable traits to be selected for in captivity, this would not necessarily guarantee that species would thrive or even survive in a wild habitat again. Artificial selection would also take a long time (measured in years and decades for some species in particular), as successive generations are bred. \\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because having more animals in captivity and plants in botanical gardens would do little to increase the chances of those species being successfully reintroduced to the wild. A large captive population serves no natural purpose to conservation unless a species is released into the wild at some stage. Keeping species in captive breeding might be called ‘preservation’, but it is not ‘conservation’ in its truest sense."},{"ref":"BI2813","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2813\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe graph below shows Biodiversity Intactness, a long-term indicator that measures how much original biodiversity remains within terrestrial communities on the continents, based on levels from around 1500 A.D., when records began.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Conservation%20of%20Biodiversity/images/BI2808%20-%20Bi2813%20A4.2%20PQ%20MCQ%20and%20SAQ%20PhilCh.jpeg.JPEG\" alt=\"BI2808 - Bi2813 A4.2 PQ MCQ and SAQ PhilCh.jpeg\">\n[Source: adapted from WWF (2024) *Living Planet Report 2024 – A System in\nPeril*. WWF, Gland, Switzerland http://bit.ly/4jzPxrE. Retrieved 30 April 2025, Public Domain]\n\n1.\n    1. Using data from the graph, compare and contrast biodiversity in Asia and Europe.  [2 marks]\n\n    2. Suggest reasons for the difference(s) you highlighted in part (a) (i)   [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline **two** reasons why the graph might give an inexact picture of global biodiversity loss.  [2 marks]\n\n3. Certain Asian species, including the Chinese Crocodile Lizard (*Shinisaurus crocodilurus*), have been selected for the EDGE of Existence programme in Asia, whereas another endangered species, the Asian elephant (*Elephas maximus*), is not on the EDGE of Existence list. Suggest why.  [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. any **two** of the following (*must include **one** similarity and **one** difference to gain full marks*)\n\n        ***Similarity:***\\\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** showed a high level (close to 1.0) in 1800    [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** declined from 1800 to 1950  [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** showed an overall decline 1800 - 2024 **OR** in the period of study    [1 mark]\n\n        ***Difference:***\\\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ Asia fell steeply from 1950-2000 **WHEREAS** Europe recovered in that period  [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ Asia continues to decline (in 2024) **WHEREAS** Europe is stable **OR** increasing   [1 mark]\n\n        [2 marks]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n        A similarity is that **both** continents’ biodiversity **fell from 1800 to 1950** ($\\checkmark$). By contrast, Europe’s biodiversity **increased** from 1950 onwards, whereas Asia’s **continues to decline** in the present day ‘any two of the following (must include one similarity and one difference to gain full marks) ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    2. any **three** of the following:\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ better awareness **OR** education in Europe   [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ weaker environmental protection law in Asia [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ less poverty **OR** more access to money to fund biodiversity work in Europe   [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ more reliance on subsistence farming in Asia   [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ less human population growth in Europe since 1950 **OR** high population density in Asia   [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ more deforestation **OR** urbanisation in Asia since 1950    [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ European **OR** American companies operating in Asia; accept palm oil plantation [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ theft **OR** trafficking **OR** poaching **OR** illegal wildlife trading **OR** of Asian native species   [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ conflict **OR** political instability in some Asian regions limits long-term biodiversity policy implementation   [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ higher rate of industrialisation **OR** infrastructure development in Asia since 1950  [1 mark]\n\n        *accept converse arguments*\n\n        [3 marks]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n        Asia’s rapid **economic growth** and **population growth** have put pressure on natural resources ($\\checkmark$). An example could be the draining of wetlands and deforestation to create city space for housing ($\\checkmark$). **Weaker laws and law enforcement** in Asia ($\\checkmark$) have led to a rise in **poaching and trafficking** of endangered species, and this can lead to eventual extinction. Non-Asian companies have made **large agricultural investments** in Asia such as large-scale palm oil plantations, which damage the ecosystem in native tropical forest areas ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ only terrestrial ecosystems **OR** land are covered in the data **OR** there is no information on aquatic **OR** seas **OR** ocean ecosystems   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is no distinction between subcontinents such as North and South America    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is no distinction between kingdoms **OR** between plants and animals **OR** microorganisms     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ data collection in 1800 will have been less sophisticated **OR** accurate than 2024    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ biodiversity in 1500 **OR** baseline data was already influenced by human activity   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ indicator may rely on modelled **OR** estimated data rather than direct field measurements   [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The data **only** covers **terrestrial ecosystems**, so takes no account of aquatic organisms ($\\checkmark$). Also, **methods** of measuring biodiversity in 2024 will be a lot **more accurate** than those used in previous centuries ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the elephant has close genetic relatives **OR** more close relatives (than the crocodile)   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the lizard is more evolutionarily unique (than the elephant)   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ conservation data on the elephant is insufficient **OR** not up-to-date (to qualify for EDGE)      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the elephant may not be globally endangered **OR** it may be endangered in one country only **OR** have a healthy population in other countries   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is better ex-situ conservation **OR** better global zoo **OR** captive populations of the elephant than the  lizard    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lizard may occupy a more restricted **OR** fragmented habitat, increasing extinction risk   [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The Chinese crocodile lizard may be **very distinct genetically from its nearest relative**, which gives it priority for selection into the EDGE programme, whereas the elephant has more close relatives ($\\checkmark$). The elephant is a popular species for zoos and conservation projects, so there will be **higher populations in captivity** ($\\checkmark$), making its extinction risk lower. Also, the elephant may be endangered in one country but may **exist in large populations elsewhere**, lowering its risk of global extinction danger ($\\checkmark$)."}]},{"name":"Diversity of Organisms","questions":[{"ref":"BI0081","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0081\n\nThis table shows the estimated number of genes in *E. coli* bacteria, fruit flies, dogs, humans, and rice.\n\n| Species | Estimated number of genes |\n| :-- | :-- |\n| *E.coli* (bacteria species) | 4,000 |\n| *Drosophila melanogaster* (fruit fly) | 13,000 |\n| *Canis lupis familiaris* (dog) | 20,000 |\n| *Homo sapiens* (humans) | 30,000 |\n| *Oryza sativa* (rice) | 40,000 |\n\nWhat can you conclude from the above information?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species in the same kingdom of life have a similar number of genes\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Different species have different numbers of genes\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ More complex species have more genes\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The number of genes is not a characteristic of a species","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is the correct answer.  This is a small sample of species, but the process of elimination means **Choice B** is the only possible correct answer. Fruit flies, humans and dogs are all in the animal kingdom but have different numbers of genes (**Choice A**).  This information shows that the number of genes does not determine the complexity, as rice has more genes than humans (**Choice C**). It is not possible to conclude that the number of genes is not a characteristic of a species from this small sample of data (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0101","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0101\n\nThe okapi looks like a small-sized giraffe and has stripes that resemble the zebra. It lives in the Ituri Forest in the Democratic Republic of the Congo. Okapis eat the leaves and buds of trees they can reach. They also graze on grass and ferns and eat fruit. Okapis tend to be solitary animals and the mother gives birth to one offspring which continues to be dependent on the mother’s milk for six months. When they are four to five years old, they are mature and can breed, but only with other okapis.\n\nWhich of the following statements is applicable to the okapi?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | Okapi is a hybrid of a giraffe and a zebra |\n  | II. | Okapi is an herbivore |\n  | III. | Okapi is its own species |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nOkapi is a herbivore **(II)** because it only eats plants. Okapi is a species **(III)** since it can interbreed and produce fertile offspring.\n\nThey are not a hybrid of a giraffe and a zebra (**I**), even though they look like one."},{"ref":"BI0102","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0102\n\nWhat is the best definition for a species?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ A group of individuals living in a defined area.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ A group of individuals of the same species living and interacting with one another in a given area.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ A group of individuals living in a given area and interacting within the environment.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ A group of individuals that can interbreed and produce fertile offspring.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA group of individuals living in a defined area is not a biological definition (**Choice A**).  A group of individuals of the same species living in a given area at the same time who are interacting with each other is a population (**Choice B**) and the environment in which they live is a habitat (**Choice C**). It is only when they can interbreed to produce fertile offspring that they can be considered a species. (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0190","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0190\n\nWhat type of human cell does the following karyogram represent?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Diversity%20of%20Organisms/images/BI0190.png\" alt=\"BI0190\">\n[Source: Adapted from Abedi M., Salmaninejad A. and Sakhinia E. (13th November 2017). *File: Peripheral-blood-karyotype-result-belonging-to-the-subject-compatible-with-48-XYYY.png.* Wikimedia commons. Retrieved 15th May 2022 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Peripheral-blood-karyotype-result-belonging-to-the-subject-compatible-with-48-XYYY.png. Copyright under Creative Commons 4.0 International ]\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Sperm\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Egg\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Brain cell\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Skin cell","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because there are 23 chromosomes, so it is a haploid cell, and one of the sex chromosomes is the ‘Y’ chromosome.  Egg cells do not have the Y chromosome. **Choice B** is incorrect because all egg cells have one ‘X’ sex chromosome. **Choice C** and **choice D** are incorrect because skin and brain cells are diploid, so they have two of each chromosome: 46 in total."},{"ref":"BI0197","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0197\n\nThe following table shows the diploid chromosome number of a range of species.\n\n| Organism | Diploid chromosome number\n| :-: | :-: |\n| Yellow fever mosquito \\(*Aedes egypti*) | 6 |\n| Fruit fly (*Drosophila melanogaster*) | 8 |\n| Spinach (*Spinacia oleracea*)  | 12 |\n| Carrot (*Daucus carota*) | 18 |\n| Rice (*Oryza sativa*) | 24 |\n| Giraffe (*Giraffa camelopardalis*) | 30 |\n| Giant panda (*Ailuropoda melanoleuca*)| 42 |\n\nWhich of these statements is correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The giant panda has more genes than the other species\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The yellow fever mosquito has the fewest genes from these species\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ The gametes of carrots have 18 chromosomes\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The chromosome number is a characteristic of a species","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAll species have a characteristic number of chromosomes (**Choice D**).\n\nGene number is not determined by chromosome number because chromosomes are not all the same size and do not hold the same number of genes (**Choice A** and **Choice B**). **Choice C** is incorrect because gametes have a haploid number of chromosomes which means gametes of carrot plants have 9 chromosomes."},{"ref":"BI0200","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0200\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nThe table below shows the genome size and diploid chromosome numbers of five different species.\n\n| Organism | Chromosome number | Genome size (millions of base pairs)\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| T4 phage (virus) | Single piece of DNA | 0.17 |\n| *Escherichia coli* (bacteria) | 1 | 5 |\n| *Drosophila melanogaster* (fruit fly) | 8 | 140|\n| *Homo sapiens* (human)| 46 | 3,000 |\n| *Paris japonica* (woodland plant)| 40 | 150,000 |\n\n1. Evaluate the evidence from the table in relation to the statement that “organisms with a higher number of chromosomes do not always have more base pairs”. [2 marks]\n\n2. Discuss the data as evidence for the notion that increased genome size means that the organism is more complex. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (comparison of) *Paris japonica* **AND** *Homo sapiens* data support the statement    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *P. japonica* has fewer chromosomes than *H. sapiens* but has more base pairs **OR** *P. japonica* has 40 chromosomes with 150,000 million base pairs while *H. sapiens* has 46 chromosomes with 3,000 million base pairs.[1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (comparison of) *E.coli*, *D. melanogaster* and *P. japonica* **OR** *H. sapiens* data does not support the statement                            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ (because) as the number of chromosomes increases, so does the genome size, **OR** the number of base pairs [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The data shows that for all organisms apart from *Paris japonica* that higher chromosome number has a larger genome (greater number of base pairs). *Paris japonica* has **fewer chromosomes** ($\\checkmark$) than *Homo sapiens* but has a **larger genome** ($\\checkmark$). ***P. japonica*** ($\\checkmark$) has 40 chromosomes which are less than ***Homo sapiens*** ($\\checkmark$) but has 150,000 million base pairs compared to 3,000 million base pairs in *Homo sapiens*.\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    **Supporting the statement**:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *Escherichia coli* is the simplest one-cell organism and has the smallest genome size **OR** has 5 million base pairs compared to more complex multicellular organisms [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ T4 phage is not a living organism and has a smaller genome than any of the living organisms [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fruit flies are simpler organisms than humans and have a smaller genome [1 mark]\n\n    **Not supporting the statement**:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *Homo sapiens* is considered the most complex organism in the table but does not have the largest genome, **OR** *H. sapiens* has 3,000 million base pairs compared to 150,000 in *P. japonica*. [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a larger genome does not necessarily mean that an organism has more genes  [1 mark]\n\n    *If only one side of the argument is mentioned (but not both), the maximum mark is one.*\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nThe data **does not fully support** the statement that more complex organisms have a larger genome. \\\nFor example, ***Homo sapiens*** is the most complex organism in the table but does not have the largest genome ($\\checkmark$). ***Paris japonica*** , a woodland plant, has 150,000 million base pairs compared to 3,000 in *Homo sapiens*, which does not support the statement that more complex organisms have larger genomes because plants are simpler organisms than humans and achieve bigger genomes mostly due to polyploidy. ($\\checkmark$) \\\nOn the other hand, ***Escherichia coli*** is the simplest living organism in the table and has the smallest genome size, 5 million base pairs. The other organisms are more complex and have larger genomes, e.g. *Homo sapiens* has 3,000 million base pairs.  ($\\checkmark$)  T4 page is not considered to be a living organism. The above statements **support** the notion that the increased genome size means that the organism is more complex."},{"ref":"BI0250","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0250\n\nBiologists use the binomial naming system to name every organism on the planet. The binomial name for the platypus is *Ornithorhynchus anatinus*. Which statement(s) below are true for the binomial naming system and the platypus?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1em}$    The genus for the platypus is *Ornithorhynchus*.\n- II. $\\hspace{.7em}$    The binomial naming system is based on an international agreement\nbetween scientists.\n- III. $\\hspace{.4em}$     The name, *Ornithorhynchus anatinus* is unique to platypus species.\n\n-  A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    I and II only\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    II and III only\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAll three statements are correct. The first part of the binomial name is the genus, the second part is the species which is unique to platypus species. The binomial system and binomial names are agreed between biologists at international congresses."},{"ref":"BI0712","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0712\n\nThe table shows factors that can result in speciation. Which word is matched to the correct definition?\n\n| | Word | Definition |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Polyploidy | Having two complete sets of chromosomes |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Sexual reproduction | The combining of genetic information from two individuals |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Reproductive isolation | Species breeding to produce infertile offspring |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Mutations | Random changes to the amino acid sequence |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSexual reproduction involves the fusion of gametes from two individuals, resulting in offspring with a combination of genetic material. This process increases genetic variation, which is essential for evolution and natural selection. **Choice B** is the correct answer.\n\nPolyploidy occurs when an organism has more than two complete sets of chromosomes (e.g., triploid 3n, tetraploid 4n). The definition given in the table describes diploidy (2n), which is the standard chromosome number in most sexually reproducing organisms; **Choice A** incorrect.\n\nInterbreeding between different species can sometimes produce infertile offspring, such as a horse × donkey = mule. This is an example of reproductive isolation because the hybrid offspring is sterile, preventing gene flow between the two species. However, because horses and donkeys are already separate species, this does not result in speciation—it simply reinforces their reproductive barrier. For reproductive isolation to lead to speciation, it must occur within a single species, preventing interbreeding between different populations.  **Choice C** is incorrect.\n\nMutations are random changes in DNA sequences, not in the amino acid sequence; **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0840","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0840\n\nThe table shows the nucleotide base sequence and genome size (number of base pairs, bp) for different species.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Diversity%20of%20Organisms/images/BI0840.png\" alt=\"BI0840\">\n\nWhich conclusion can be drawn, based on the table and your own knowledge?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Genome size is proportional to the size of the species.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The more closely related two species are, the more similar their base sequences.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Humans have more genes than chimpanzees, mice and horses.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The base sequence in the table for humans and chimpanzees is the same, therefore they have an identical genetic code.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSpecies that share a recent common ancestor, such as humans and chimpanzees, are more likely to have similar base sequences. Over time, as species diverge and evolve, mutations accumulate in their base sequences. The longer two species have been separated from their common ancestor, the more mutations will have accumulated in their genomes. Therefore, species that are closely related have fewer accumulated mutations and more similar base sequences (**Choice B**).\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because genome size is not necessarily proportional to the size of the species. The genome size can vary significantly even among organisms of similar size, and organisms with similar-sized genomes, such as the horse and mouse vary greatly in size. **Choice C** is incorrect because it is possible to have a larger genome size with a comparable, or even smaller, number of genes, depending on the amount of non-coding DNA and repetitive sequences present in the genome. Humans have a larger genome but a similar number of genes to the chimpanzee, mouse and horse. **Choice D** is incorrect because there is a difference of one nucleotide base in the table between humans and chimpanzees. The genome of humans and chimpanzees is similar but not the same."},{"ref":"BI1021","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1021\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n*Ammophila arenaria* (Marram grass), pictured below, is plant species that is often found on sand dunes.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Diversity%20of%20Organisms/images/BI1021A.png\" alt=\"BI1021A\">\n[Source: Agnieszka K., Nova. (2020). *File: Ammophila arenaria Piaskownica zwyczajna* 2020-06-29 04.jpg\n.Wikimediacommons. Retrieved July 29, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Ammophila_arenaria_Piaskownica_zwyczajna_2020-06-29_04.jpg.  Copyright under CC BY-SA 4.0\n]\n\n1.\n    1. Identify the genus to which *Ammophila arenaria* belongs. [1 mark]\n\n    2. State the naming system used to name *Ammophila arenaria*. [1 mark]\n\n    3. State the term used to describe the differences between organisms, which is used as the basis for the naming system in part (ii).  [1 mark]\n\n    4. Describe how the genome within a species could vary.     [2 marks]\n\n2. Compare and contrast the morphological and biological species concepts.     [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *Ammophila*                                                [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    ***Ammophila*** ($\\checkmark$) is the genus to which Marram grass belongs.\n\n1.\n    2.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ binomial naming system    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **binomial naming system** ($\\checkmark$) is used to name organisms.\n\n1.\n    3.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ variation **OR** biodiversity **OR** characteristics **OR** phylogeny **OR** morphology        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Variation** ($\\checkmark$) refers to the differences between organisms, which is the basis for the binomial naming system.\n\n1.\n    4. **BOTH** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the genome can vary in size **OR** base sequence [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **WHICH** is a result of mutations **OR** single-nucleotide polymorphisms **OR** insertions **OR** deletions **OR** tandem repeats **OR** other named mutations        [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Single-nucleotide polymorphisms** ($\\checkmark$) could result in changes to the **base sequence** ($\\checkmark$) of DNA between organisms of the same species.\n\n2. **BOTH** of the following (answer must include at least one similarity and one difference to achieve full marks):\n\n    **Similarities** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** seek to define a species     [1 mark]\n\n    **Differences** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the morphological species concept refers to species being groups of organisms with shared physical traits, **WHEREAS** the biological species concept refers to species being groups of organisms that can interbreed to produce fertile offspring        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Linnaeus proposed the morphological species concept **WHEREAS** the biological species concept was proposed by Mayr     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the morphological species concept is applicable to sexual and asexual organisms **HOWEVER**, the biological species concept is only applicable to sexually reproducing organisms     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the morphological species concept was proposed before the biological species concept [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ The biological species concept reflects the evolutionary relationship, **WHEREAS** morphological species concept may not represent genetic relatedness or evolutionary relationships     [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Both** the morphological and biological species concepts **aim to define** what constitutes a **species** ($\\checkmark$) in the field of biology. However, the **morphological** species concept defines species based on their **shared physical characteristics**, whereas the **biological** species concept defines species based on the ability of individuals to **interbreed and produce fertile offspring** under natural conditions ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1022","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1022\n\nWhich of the following is the correct format for species names according to the binomial naming system?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Genus + Species\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Genus + species\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ *Genus* + *species*\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ *Genus* + *Species*","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because only the genus name should be capitalised, it should come before the species name, and both should be in italics. **Choices A** and **D** are incorrect because only the genus name should be capitalised. **Choice B** is incorrect, as the genus and species name should be in italics."},{"ref":"BI1023","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1023\n\nWhich of the following is true for the biological species concept?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Organisms within the same species can interbreed to produce fertile offspring\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Organisms within the same species can interbreed to produce infertile offspring\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species are groups of organisms with shared traits\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species are groups of organisms without shared traits","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAccording to the biological species concept, a species is a group of organisms that can interbreed to produce fertile offspring. **Choice A** is correct. **Choice B** is incorrect as it refers to infertile offspring. **Choices C** and **D** are incorrect because they refer to the morphological species concept."},{"ref":"BI1024","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1024\n\nWhich of the following statement(s) is/are true for the genome?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | The genome is all the genetic information of an organism.  |\n    | II. | Genomes within a species are identical in size. |\n    | III. | Genomes within a species vary in base sequence. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe genome is all the genetic information in an organism (**Statement I**). The base sequence can vary within a species due to single-nucleotide polymorphism (**Statement III**). Therefore, **Choice C** is correct as it contains both true statements.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as it only includes one true statement.\nThe size of the genome within a species can vary, this can be due to mutations, such as insertions and deletions, so **Statement II** is not true. **Choices B** and **D** are, therefore, incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1026","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1026\n\nWhich row in the table is correct for either the biological or morphological species concept?\n\n| | Concept | Species Definition | Proposed by… |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Biological | A group of organisms that can interbreed to produce fertile offspring | Linnaeus |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Biological | A group of organisms with shared traits | Mayr |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Morphological | A group of organisms that can interbreed to produce fertile offspring | Mayr |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Morphological | A group of organisms with shared traits | Linnaeus |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe morphological concept refers to a species as a group of organisms with shared traits. It assumes that individuals that look alike are part of the same species. This was proposed by Linnaeus. **Choice D** is correct. **Choice A** is incorrect because although the definition of the biological species concept is correct, it was not proposed by Linnaeus, it was proposed by Mayr. **Choices B** and **C** are incorrect because neither the definitions nor the proposers are correct."},{"ref":"BI1027","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1027\n\nWhich of the following highlight(s) difficulties in applying the biological species concept?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Budding in yeast |\n    | II. | Runners in spider plants |\n    | III. | Bacterial conjugation  |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because all statements are examples of either asexual reproduction (**Statements I and II**) or horizontal gene transfer (**Statement III**), which makes applying the biological species concept difficult. The biological species concept defines a species as a group of organisms that can interbreed and produce fertile offspring under natural conditions. This concept is formulated for sexual reproduction, not asexual or horizontal genetic transfer."},{"ref":"BI1028","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1028\n\nWhich of the following would provide the strongest evidence for two populations belonging to different species?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Differences in genome base sequences across all members of two populations |\n    | II. | Differences in genome size across all members of two populations |\n    | III. | Difference in chromosome number across all members of two populations\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A**  is correct because only **Statement III** provides the strongest evidence that two populations belong to different species. A consistent difference in chromosome number across all members of two populations is a strong indicator of reproductive isolation and speciation, as different chromosome numbers typically prevent successful interbreeding and the production of fertile offspring.\n\n **Statements I** and **II** (differences in genome base sequences and genome size) can arise through mutation and vary within a single species, making them insufficient on their own as evidence of different species."},{"ref":"BI1029","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1029\n\nThe images below represent haploid or diploid cells from different individuals. Based on their cells, which pairs could produce fertile offspring if they interbreed?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Diversity%20of%20Organisms/images/BI1029A.png\" alt=\"BI1029A\">|\n    | II. | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Diversity%20of%20Organisms/images/BI1029B.png\" alt=\"BI1029B\"> |\n    | III. | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Diversity%20of%20Organisms/images/BI1029C.png\" alt=\"BI1029C\"> |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because only **pairs II** and **III** could produce fertile offspring. This is because **pair II** shares the same haploid number, and **pair III** share the same diploid number. **Choices A, B** and **D** are incorrect because they include **pair I. Pair I** is not likely to produce fertile offspring as the haploid number of one individual is NOT half the diploid number of the other individual, which means they have different numbers of chromosomes."},{"ref":"BI1030","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1030\n\nWhich of the following could be used to identify different species?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Dichotomous key |\n    | II. | Karyograms |\n    | III. | Binomial naming system |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because only **Statements I** and **II** are correct. Dichotomous keys could be used to identify two different species based on their characteristics, and karyograms could be used to identify two different species based on their chromosome number and size. **Choices A, C**, and **D** are incorrect because they contain **Statement III**, which is incorrect. The Binomial naming system can be used to give species a name but does not identify species."},{"ref":"BI1031","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1031\n\nEnvironmental DNA can be used to form which of the following?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Barcodes\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Karyotypes\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Dichotomous keys\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Binomial naming system","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because environmental DNA is genetic material shed by organisms into their environment and can be used to produce barcodes for each species.  **Choice C** is incorrect because environmental DNA does not directly lend itself to the construction of dichotomous keys, as these keys are usually based on observable physical characteristics rather than genetic material. **Choice D** is incorrect as while eDNA analysis can help identify species, it doesn't play a direct role in assigning scientific names, so is not used as the basis for the binomial naming system. **Choice B** is incorrect, as environmental DNA does not provide enough detailed information about individual chromosomes to construct karyotypes."},{"ref":"BI1034","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1034\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe image below shows three different forms of *Ensatina eschscholtzii* species.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Diversity%20of%20Organisms/images/3%20types%20of%20lizards%20Ensatina%20eschscholtzii.png\" alt=\"3 types of lizards Ensatina eschscholtzii\">\n[Source: Adapted from Devitt, T, J. Stuart J.E. Moritz, B. and Moritz, C. (2011). *File: Ensatina eschscholtzii ring species.jpg*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved August 3, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Ensatina_eschscholtzii_ring_species.jpg. Copyright under CC 2.0]\n\n1.\n    1. Construct a dichotomous key to identify the three forms of *Ensatina eschscholtzii*.   [3 marks]\n\n    2. Identify the genus to which *Ensatina eschscholtzii* belongs.             [1 mark]\n\n    3. All three forms of *Ensatina eschscholtzii* belong to the same species. State a property of their genome that they will share.         [1 mark]\n\n2. Explain why the biological species concept is more appropriate to identify the three forms of *Ensatina eschscholtzii* as the same species compared to the morphological species concept.             [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. (students may use a linked or branched dichotomous key)\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 1 mark is awarded for each pair of appropriate defining features identifying each individual form, in any suitable form of dichotomous key  (maximum of two marks)                   [2 marks]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 1 mark is awarded for all three forms identified      [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Diversity%20of%20Organisms/images/BI1034ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI1034ANSA\">\n\n1.\n    2.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *Ensatina*         [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    They belong to the genus ***Ensatina*** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n    3.\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the same number of chromosomes        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    They will share the **same number of chromosomes** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ the biological species concept defines a species as a group of organisms that can interbreed to produce fertile offspring, **WHEREAS** the morphological concept refers to species as groups of organisms having shared characteristics    [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ as the three vary significantly in their physical characteristics, the morphological concept wouldn’t identify them as the same species, **OR** they look different but can still interbreed to produce fertile offspring    [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The **biological species concept defines species as groups of organisms that can interbreed to produce fertile offspring**; however, the **morphological concept refers only to species with shared physical characteristics** ($\\checkmark$). So, because the **three forms of *Ensatina eschscholtzii* look different**, the morphological species concept **would not define them as the same species** ($\\checkmark$), but if they interbreed, they can produce fertile offspring, so they are the same species according to the biological species concept."},{"ref":"BI1035","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1035\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Compare and contrast the biological species concept and the morphological concept.[4 marks]\n\n2. Explain the difficulties with applying the biological species and morphological concept to all organisms.    [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe the similarities and differences within and between species. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** similarity and **one** difference to achieve full marks*):\n\n    ***Similarities***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** concepts seek to define a species    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** concepts acknowledge that there can be variation within species    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** concepts are connected to the broader concept of evolution    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the biological species concept defines a species as a group of organisms which can interbreed to produce fertile offspring, **WHEREAS** the morphological concept defines a species as a group of organisms with shared traits        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Mayr proposed the biological species concept **WHEREAS** the morphological concept was proposed by Linnaeus        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the morphological species concept was proposed before the biological species concept    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the morphological species concept is applicable to sexual and asexual organisms, **WHEREAS** the biological species concept is applicable only to sexually reproducing organisms     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the biological species concept reflects the evolutionary relationship OR speciation OR gene flow, **WHEREAS** the morphological species concept may not represent genetic relatedness or evolutionary relationships [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Both concepts are used to **define species** ($\\checkmark$), but Mayr proposed the biological species concept, whereas the morphological concept was proposed by **Linnaeus** ($\\checkmark$). The morphological concept also came **first** ($\\checkmark$). The biological species concept defines a species as a group of organisms that can interbreed to produce **fertile offspring**; however, the morphological concept defines a species as a group of organisms with **shared traits** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the morphological species concept cannot be applied to species that show variation due to reproductive isolation **OR** the morphological concept cannot be applied to species with different forms **OR** polymorphism **OR** named example of a species with different forms     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the morphological species concept does not take into account DNA **OR** genetic information   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the biological species concept is defined in terms of sexual reproduction **HOWEVER**, not all organisms reproduce sexually     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ example of species that reproduce asexually, e.g. budding in yeast **OR** horizontal gene transfer in bacteria                     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the biological species concept relies on the ability to observe and study interbreeding in real-time, which is not possible with extinct species **OR** fossilised organisms              [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some different species can interbreed and produce viable offspring, but these hybrids might have reduced fitness or be sterile, which challenges the clear-cut boundaries proposed by the biological species concept     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ convergent evolution can make it difficult to accurately group organisms based solely on morphological characteristics     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rapid evolution and morphological changes in response to the environment might make individuals appear distinct, even if they share a recent common ancestor, which challenges the reliability of morphological traits as indicators of species relationships         [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The biological species concept defines a species as a group of organisms that can interbreed to produce fertile offspring, however, not all species reproduce **sexually** ($\\checkmark$), for example, yeast reproduce asexually by **budding** ($\\checkmark$). It also relies on the ability to see species interbreeding, which cannot be done with **extinct** species or **fossils** ($\\checkmark$). The morphological concept defines a species as a group of organisms with shared traits, however, due to variation caused by **reproductive isolation** and mutation, not all members of a species **look the same** ($\\checkmark$). In addition, **rapid evolution** might make individuals appear distinctly related when in fact, they shared a recent common ancestor. This could make the morphological species concept **less reliable** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ species can be defined as a group of organisms that are capable of interbreeding to produce fertile offspring    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ species usually share the same number of chromosomes      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ species usually share identical karyotypes             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ example of chromosome complement of a species, e.g. humans have 46 chromosomes           [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the chromosome complement of a species can vary due to genetic conditions such as Down syndrome     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the size genome of a species can vary within a species   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the base sequence of the genome can vary within a species     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ due to mutations or single-nucleotide polymorphisms [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous genes **OR** similarities in gene pathways **OR** similarities in the functions of genes can be found between species that share evolutionary origins     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ species-specific genes are not found in other closely related species     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ horizontal gene transfer between different species, often through mechanisms like viral infections or symbiotic relationships, leads to the acquisition of genes in the recipient that may not be shared by other members of the species         [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    According to the biological species concept, a species can be defined as a group of organisms that are capable of interbreeding to produce **fertile offspring** ($\\checkmark$). They can produce fertile offspring as a species usually have the **same chromosome number** ($\\checkmark$), for example, in humans, this is **46** ($\\checkmark$) unless genetic conditions such as **Down syndrome** affect their karyotype ($\\checkmark$). The **size** of a species' genome can vary slightly within a species ($\\checkmark$), as can the **base sequence** of the genome ($\\checkmark$) and these are both due to **mutations** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1051","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1051\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the binomial naming system and its importance.     [4 marks]\n\n2. Explain how a sample of cells taken from a foetus can be used to determine whether the foetus will be biologically male or biologically female at birth.        [5 marks]\n\n3. Outline the outcomes of the human genome project and their impacts on different fields of science. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ binomial naming system as the system used to name species     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genus name and species name     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ species with the same genus share many characteristics **OR** are closely related    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ internationally agreed        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ facilitates understanding and communication between scientists     [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The binomial naming system is the system which scientists use to **name species** ($\\checkmark$) and this is **internationally agreed** ($\\checkmark$), which means it’s used all over the world, which aids in the **communication** between scientists ($\\checkmark$) in different countries. The name of each species has two parts, the first highlights the **genus** to which it belongs and the second part refers to the **species** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cells grown in a culture medium [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cells stained [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ observed under a light microscope [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ photomicrograph taken during mitosis **OR** mitotic metaphase [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chromosomes arranged according to size **OR** position of centromeres [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ look at chromosome pair 23 [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ two large chromosomes mean foetus is XX [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ one large chromosome and one small chromosome means foetus is XY [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ XX is biologically female **AND** XY is biologically male [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Once the sample of cells has been taken from the foetus they are grown in a **culture medium** ($\\checkmark$). They are then **stained** ($\\checkmark$) and observed under a **light microscope** ($\\checkmark$). A **photomicrograph** is taken ($\\checkmark$), and the image is cut up so that the **chromosomes** can be **arranged by size** and position of the **centromere** ($\\checkmark$). A foetus with **two large X** chromosomes will be **biologically female** ($\\checkmark$), and a foetus with **one large X** and **one small Y** chromosome will be **biologically male** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the Human Genome Project (HGP) sequenced the entire human genome    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it determined the total number of base pairs in the human genome    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ it estimated the number of genes in the human genome             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ identified the loci of genes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ identified repeat sequences     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ identified locations of single nucleotide polymorphisms            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ identified gene sequences associated with certain diseases **OR** biological processes **OR** traits    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ HGP stimulated further development **OR** reduced the price of sequencing technology    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ technology developed for the HGP is now used to diagnose diseases **OR** identify micro-organisms OR DNA matching in forensics and medicine **OR** genetic modification  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contributed to the field by identifying genetic markers that affect how individuals metabolise and respond to medications **OR** contribute information for personalised medicine        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ insights into evolutionary relationships **OR** genetic similarities **OR** genetic differences [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ identification of regulatory elements **OR** non-coding RNAs **OR** components that play essential roles in gene expression **OR** cellular function  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ emphasised importance of consideration of ethical **OR** legal **OR** social implications of genomic research  [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The Human Genome Project (HGP) **sequenced** the entire human genome ($\\checkmark$), **identified the loci** of genes ($\\checkmark$) and identified gene sequences that are connected to some **diseases** ($\\checkmark$) such as breast cancer. Locations of **single nucleotide polymorphisms** ($\\checkmark$) were discovered which are variations in individuals DNA base pairs. The results have contributed to the development of **personalised medicine** ($\\checkmark$) as genetic markers have been identified that affect how individuals respond to medications. The technology developed for the HGP has led to further advancements in technology, **reducing the cost** of genetic sequencing ($\\checkmark$).  The HGP also emphasised how important it is to consider the **ethical implications** ($\\checkmark$) of genomic research and the importance of informed consent."},{"ref":"BI1227","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1227\n\nThe scientific name for the little spotted kiwi is *Apteryx owenii*. Which of the species below is most closely related to the little spotted kiwi?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Diversity%20of%20Organisms/images/BI1227.png\" alt=\"BI1227\">\n[Source: Adapted from Lapsley Miller, J. (2023, August 28). *Little Spotted Kiwi*. Wikipedia. Retrieved September 13, 2023, from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_spotted_kiwi.  Copyright under CC BY-SA 4.0]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ *Apteryx australis*\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Little spotted pardalote\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ *Owenii apteryx*\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ *Neognathae owenii*","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSpecies names are given using the binomial system, where the genus comes first, and the species follows. Species with the same genus have similar traits and are closely related;  **Choice A**  is correct.\n\n**Choice B**  is incorrect as it is not a scientific name, so conveys no information about the relatedness of species. **Choice C** is incorrect as it is just a reversal of the scientific name of the little spotted kiwi, and is in a different genus. **Choice D**  is incorrect as it is not in the same genus as the little spotted kiwi."},{"ref":"BI1375","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1375\n\nThe image shows a cladogram for several species of hamster.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Diversity%20of%20Organisms/images/BI1375.png\" alt=\"BI1375\">\n[Source: Adapted from Michaplot. (2012, January 4). *File: PhodopusCladogram.jpg*. Wikipedia. Retrieved December 04, 2023 from URL  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:PhodopusCladogram.jpg. Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n1. State what is represented by the letter **X**. [1 mark]\n\n2. Other than their positions on the cladogram, explain what information indicates that *Phodopus campbelli* and *Phodopus sungorus* are closely related.    [1 mark]\n\n3. Modern hamsters have small, furry ears. The common ancestor of the hamsters in the cladogram has been hypothesised to have large, furry ears. Suggest how natural selection explains these changes.        [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  node    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  the last common ancestor of *Phodopus campbelli* **AND** *Phodopus sungorus*        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Letter X represents a **node** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they are from the same genus **OR** in the *Phodopus* genus        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    They are in the same **genus** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ natural selection is the process by which species change over time in response to changes in the environment **OR** competition between organisms in order for species to survive [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ random mutation resulted in new allele for smaller ears        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increases genetic variation **OR** diversity within the  population        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mutation **OR** new allele **OR** allele for smaller ears proved beneficial **OR** led to greater chance of survival        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased reproductive success    **OR** more chance of having offspring **OR** having more offspring        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the offspring inherited the allele for smaller ears **OR** beneficial alleles passed onto next generation            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ over many generations the allele for shorter ears increased in frequency in the population **OR** small-eared hamsters increased in number in the population [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n   Many years ago, **random mutations** occurred in some hamsters that gave them **new alleles** for smaller ears ($\\checkmark$), the allele for smaller ears gave those hamsters a **survival advantage** ($\\checkmark$). These hamsters were **more likely** to have **offspring** ($\\checkmark$), and the **beneficial allele** for small ears was **passed onto** the **offspring** ($\\checkmark$). Over **many generations**, there was an **increase** in the number of hamsters with **smaller ears** and a **decrease** in the number of hamsters with **larger ears** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1384","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1384\n\nThe following table shows the approximate number of protein-encoding genes found in various species. What can be concluded from these findings?\n\n| Species | Approximate number of protein-encoding genes|\n| :-: | :-: |\n| *Drosophila melanogaster* (fruit fly)| 14,000 |\n| *Oryza sativa* (rice) | 51,000 |\n| *Gallus gallus* (chicken) |20,000-23,000 |\n| *Homo sapiens* (human) | 20,000-25,000 |\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | The number of genes is not a good indicator of organism complexity. |\n    | II. | Chickens may have more genes than humans. |\n    | III. | The number of base pairs in the rice genome is greater than in the other species. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe number of genes found in a species does not serve as a good indicator of complexity (**Statement I**). If it did, the data here would suggest that chickens and humans are equally complex, and that rice is far more complex than either of them. Due to the overlapping ranges in the number of genes, it is entirely possible for chickens to have a greater number of genes than humans (**Statement II**). It is impossible to determine the size of the genome (measured in base pairs) of a species based on the number of genes it has. In reality, rice has far fewer base pairs than chickens or humans despite having approximately twice as many genes (**Statement III**). **Choice A** is, therefore, correct.\n\n**Choices B, C**, and **D** are incorrect as they all contain **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI1395","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1395\n\nHow are the chromosomes in a karyogram ordered?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ By analogous pairs\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ By number of genes\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ By size\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ By position of the centromeres","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because the chromosomes in a karyogram are ordered in homologous pairs from largest to smallest.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because chromosomes are grouped in homologous pairs (one from each parent), not analogous pairs. **Choice B** is incorrect because the number of genes on each chromosome is highly variable, and genes do not have a consistent length. Although the chromosomes are aligned with their centromeres in a karyogram, the question only asks about the order of the chromosomes, which is not dependent on centromere position; thus, **Choice D** is also incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1479","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1479\n\nWhat makes up the genome of all eukaryotes but not prokaryotes?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1.6em}$ Chromosomes and mitochondrial DNA\n\n- II. $\\hspace{1.3em}$ Chloroplast DNA\n\n- III. $\\hspace{1em}$ Plasmids and ribosomal DNA\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nThe genome is the whole of the genetic information of an organism. In eukaryotes, chromosomes and mitochondrial DNA (**Statement I**) make up the genome. **Choice A**  is correct.\n\nChloroplast DNA is not found in all eukaryotes (**Statement II**). It is primarily found in plant cells but not animal cells; **Statement II** is incorrect. Plasmids are found in prokaryotes but not eukaryotes, and ribosomes do not contain DNA, **Statement III**  is incorrect. **Choices B, C** and **D**  are incorrect as they all contain **Statements II** and/or **III**."},{"ref":"BI1486","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1486\n\nWhat is the purpose of a biological dichotomous key?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ To identify how closely organisms are related\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ To decide on the binomial name of an organism\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ To identify species\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ To find the last common ancestor of two species","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nDichotomous keys are tools that are used to identify different species based on their physical characteristics; **Choice C**  is correct.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect, as dichotomous keys are not used to identify how closely organisms are related. **Choice B** is incorrect as dichotomous keys may help in identifying the binomial name of an organism, but they do not help with the decision of what to name a species. **Choice D**  is incorrect, as dichotomous keys do not allow scientists to identify the last common ancestor of two species."},{"ref":"BI1570","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1570\n\nThe table shows the number of chromosomes found in the gametes of various species.\n\n| Species | Haploid chromosome number (n) |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| *Parascaris equorum* (horse threadworm)| 2 |\n| *Vicia faba* (field bean) | 6 |\n| *Oryza sativa* (rice) | 12 |\n| *Homo sapiens* (human) | 23 |\n| *Pan troglodytes* (chimpanzee) | 24 |\n| *Canis familiaris* (dog) | 39 |\n\nWhat can be concluded from the data in the table?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Diploid cells usually have an even number of chromosomes. |\n    | II. | Species with more chromosomes have a larger genome size. |\n    | III. | Humans are most closely related to *Pan troglodytes*. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because diploid cells have an even number of chromosomes (**Statement I**) unless affected by errors such as non-disjunction. This can be obtained by doubling the haploid number in the table and recognising that an odd number of diploid chromosomes cannot be evenly distributed to form gametes.\n\n**Statement II** is false because the number of chromosomes an organism has is not an accurate reflection of its genome size. *Oryza sativa*, for example, has a much larger genome than any of the other species in the table despite having fewer chromosomes. **Statement III** is false because although humans are closely related to chimpanzees, this cannot be concluded from the data in the table alone. *Stipa spartea* (porcupine grass), for example, has the same number of chromosomes as humans but is not closely related. **Choices B, C**, and **D** are incorrect because they contain one or more false statements."},{"ref":"BI1579","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1579\n\nThe diagram shows an unidentified species of crab.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Diversity%20of%20Organisms/images/BI1579.png\" alt=\"BI1579\">\n\nUse the dichotomous key provided to identify this species.\n\n| | | |\n| --- | :-- | :-- |\n| 1.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Smooth shell … . | Go to 2. |\n| | Bumps or spikes on shell … . | Go to 3. |\n| 2.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Eyestalks … . | Go to 4. |\n| | No eyestalks … . | *Menippe mercenaria.* |\n| 3.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Pear-shaped shell … . | Go to 5. |\n| | Non-pear-shaped shell … . | Go to 6. |\n| 4.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Antennae visible … . | *Birgus latro.* |\n| | No antennae visible … . | *Ocypode quadrata.* |\n| 5.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Six walking legs … . | *Paralithodes camtschaticus.* |\n| | Eight walking legs … . | *Macrocheira kaempferi.* |\n| 6.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Tail present … . | *Limulus polyphemus.* |\n| | Tail absent … . | *Callinectes sapidus.* |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ *Menippe mercenaria*\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ *Ocypode quadrata*\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ *Paralithodes camtschaticus*\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ *Callinectes sapidus*","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because this species has a rough, non-pear-shaped shell and no tail, making it a blue crab (*Callinectes sapidus*).\n\n**Choices A** is incorrect because the species in the diagram has eyestalks and a rough shell. **Choice B** is incorrect because the species in the diagram has a rough shell. **Choice C** is incorrect because the species in the diagram has a non-pear-shaped shell."},{"ref":"BI1652","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1652\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe karyogram is from a rat (*Rattus rattus*) cell without any chromosomal abnormalities. Biological sex is determined in the same way in rats and humans.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Diversity%20of%20Organisms/images/BI1652.png\" alt=\"BI1652\">\n[Source: Adapted from Hamanaka, S., Yamaguchi, T., Kobayashi, T., Kato-Itoh, M., Yamazaki, S., Sato, H., Umino, A., Wakiyama, Y., Arai, M., Sanbo, M., Hirabayashi, M., & Nakauchi, H. (2011, July 15). *File: Karyogram of normal rat.png*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 11, 2024 from URL https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Karyogram_of_normal_rat.png. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n1. State, with a reason, the stage of mitosis at which chromosomes are photographed to make a karyogram.                     [1 mark]\n\n2. Deduce, with a reason, the biological sex of the rat.    [1 mark]\n\n3. State how the chromosomes have been arranged to create the karyogram.    [2 marks]\n\n4. Using the karyogram, compare and contrast between the chromosomes of rats and humans.        [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ metaphase **BECAUSE** the chromosomes are visible **OR** condensed **OR** aligned on the equator of the cell so that it is easier to sort them    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The chromosomes are in **metaphase** because the chromosomes are **visible** and aligned on the **equator** of the cell, which makes it **easier to sort** them ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ male **BECAUSE** it has a Y chromosome **OR** has both X and Y chromosomes        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The rat is **male** because it has a  **Y** chromosome ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ by decreasing length    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ using banding patterns **OR** centromere positions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in pairs **OR** homologous pairs **OR** one chromosome from each parent forms a pair    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The chromosomes have been arranged by **decreasing length** ($\\checkmark$) and using their **banding patterns** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **two**  of the following: \\\n    *Answer must include at least **one** similarity and at least **one** difference to gain full marks*\n\n    ***Similarities***\\\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have pairs of chromosomes **OR** homologous chromosomes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have specific banding patterns **OR** genes on the chromosomes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have a pair of sex chromosomes **OR** X and Y chromosomes    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences*** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rats have fewer chromosomes **OR** autosomes than humans **OR** humans have more chromosomes **OR** autosomes than rats **OR** rats have 21 pairs of chromosomes **AND** humans have 23 pairs of chromosomes **OR** rats have 42 chromosomes **AND** humans have 46  chromosomes **OR**  rats have 40 autosomes and 2 sex chromosomes **AND** humans have 44 autosomes and 2 sex chromosomes **OR**  rats have 20 pairs of autosomes and 1 pair of sex chromosomes **AND** humans have 22 pairs of autosomes and 1 pair of sex chromosomes    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Both rats and humans have **homologous chromosomes** ($\\checkmark$), however, rats have **fewer chromosomes** (42) than humans (46) ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2321","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2321\n\nWhole genome sequencing has been used to sequence the genomes of many species. Which of the following are potential future uses of whole genome sequencing?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Identifying disease-causing alleles.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sequencing the genomes of bacteria and fungi.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Advancing personalised medicine.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Identifying evolutionary relationships between species.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because personalised medicine involves using an individual's entire genetic profile to predict their response to specific drugs and tailor treatments accordingly. While the technology exists, its integration into routine clinical practice for most patients is considered a primary future goal of genomics.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the identification of disease-causing alleles (such as the BRCA1 gene or those for cystic fibrosis) is a well-established current use of genome sequencing.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the sequencing of prokaryotes (bacteria) and fungi was among the earliest successes of the Human Genome Project era and is currently performed routinely in pathology and research labs.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because comparing whole genomes to construct phylogenetic trees and determine evolutionary relationships is a standard contemporary method in modern cladistics."},{"ref":"BI2373","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2373\n\nWhen constructing a dichotomous key for classifying bird species, which characteristic would be most appropriate for the first division in the key?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Colour of feathers\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Habitat (forest, grassland, urban)\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Presence or absence of webbed feet\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Diet (carnivorous, herbivorous, omnivorous)","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because binary traits (presence/absence) work best for early divisions in a dichotomous key.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect because colour is too variable within a species.\n\n**Choice B**  is incorrect because habitat is a variable characteristic, so not suitable for classification.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because diet varies within species and requires direct observation."},{"ref":"BI2648","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2648\n\nWhich statement(s) below are challenges associated with the biological species concept (BSC)?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | The BSC assumes reproductive isolation, but some species are reproductively isolated only in certain environments. |\n    | II. | Fossil species cannot be assessed using the BSC due to the lack of observable mating behaviour. |\n    | III. | The BSC relies on morphological features, which can vary widely within a species. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because reproductive isolation may be context-dependent (e.g. due to geography or behaviour), making it unclear whether two populations are separate species under BSC.\n\n**Statement II** is true because the BSC depends on observing whether organisms can interbreed. This is impossible with fossils, which are studied based on morphology and location, not reproductive behaviour.\n\n**Statement III** is false because the BSC specifically does not define species based on morphology; that is the morphological species concept. BSC focuses instead on reproductive capability, so this is not a valid criticism of BSC.\n\n**Choice B** is correct because it includes only the two true **Statements I** and **II**.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as it is missing the true **Statement II**.\n\n**Choices C** and **D** are incorrect as they include the false **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI2649","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2649\n\nWhich of the following statements explain why classifying diverging populations as species can be arbitrary?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Traits may diverge at different rates in different populations |\n    | II. | Some populations may remain reproductively compatible despite divergence |\n    | III. | Morphological changes always occur before genetic differences appear |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because, during speciation, some traits (e.g., behaviour, physiology, morphology) can evolve faster than others. This means two populations might look different but still interbreed, or might appear similar but already be reproductively isolated. Because divergence doesn’t follow a fixed pattern, deciding when to define them as separate species becomes subjective.\n\n**Statement II** is true because Populations might develop noticeable differences in traits (such as colouration or mating calls), but still produce fertile offspring when they interbreed. According to the biological species concept, they’re technically the same species, but these differences can confuse classification.\n\n**Statement III** is false because often, the opposite is true. Genetic differences (mutations, allele frequency changes) may accumulate before any visible morphological change occurs. For example, two populations may look identical but carry distinct genetic markers and be reproductively isolated.\n\nSaying morphological change “always” comes first is incorrect and overly simplistic.\n\n**Choice A** is correct because it includes only the two true **Statements I** and **II**.\n\n**Choices B, C** and **D** are incorrect as they include the false **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI2655","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2655\n\nMarbled crayfish, *Procambarus virginalis* (shown below) only reproduce through parthenogenesis, a type of asexual reproduction.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Diversity%20of%20Organisms/images/BI2655.png\" alt=\"BI2655\">\n[Adapted from Lyko, F. (2017). *The marbled crayfish (Decapoda: Cambaridae) represents an independent new species. Zootaxa 4363(4): 544–552*. Retrieved 10/03/2025, DOI: 10.11646/zootaxa.4363.4.6. CC PY]\n\nWhich of the following statements about classifying these crayfish is/are correct?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | They produce genetically identical offspring, making species classification impossible|\n    | II. | They cannot be classified using the biological species concept |\n    | III. | They can produce many offspring so cannot be classified using the biological species concept |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is false. Although marbled crayfish produce genetically identical offspring, this does not make classification impossible; it just means the biological species concept (which relies on interbreeding) isn’t applicable. Other species concepts can be used.\n\n**Statement II** is true. The biological species concept defines species based on their ability to interbreed and produce fertile offspring, with reproductive isolation preventing gene flow between different species. However, it does not apply to organisms that reproduce exclusively asexually.\n\n**Statement III** is false. The number of offspring does not affect the applicability of the biological species concept. The issue is the absence of sexual reproduction, not the offspring count.\n\n**Choice A** is correct because it contains only the true statement and none of the false ones.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because it contains the false **Statement III**.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because it contains the false **Statement I**.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because it contains the false **Statements I** and **III**."},{"ref":"BI2656","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2656\n\nWhich of the following statements best describes how horizontal gene transfer (HGT) challenges the biological species concept in bacteria?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ HGT occurs only between closely related bacteria, reinforcing species boundaries.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ HGT is a mechanism of vertical inheritance, where genetic material is passed directly from parent to offspring.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ HGT enables bacteria to acquire genes from distantly related organisms, blurring the boundaries defined by reproductive isolation.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ HGT increases the mutation rate within a bacterial population, leading to rapid speciation.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because horizontal gene transfer allows bacteria to incorporate genetic material from unrelated species. This confounds the biological species concept, which relies on reproductive isolation to define species boundaries.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because HGT is not limited to closely related bacteria; it can occur between very different bacterial species.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect as HGT is a form of lateral (non-vertical) gene transfer, not the typical parent-to-offspring inheritance.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because while HGT can introduce new genes, it does not inherently increase the mutation rate or directly lead to rapid speciation."},{"ref":"BI2658","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2658\n\nWhy does chromosome number affect hybrid fertility?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Species with different chromosome numbers can always produce fertile offspring because meiosis is not affected by chromosome pairing.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Hybrids with mismatched chromosome numbers have increased genetic diversity, making them more fertile.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Chromosome number only affects physical traits, not reproductive ability.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Hybrids with different chromosome numbers cannot produce functional gametes because homologous chromosomes fail to pair during meiosis.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because meiosis requires homologous chromosomes to pair correctly. If the chromosome numbers do not match, pairing cannot occur properly, leading to errors in gamete formation and often resulting in sterility.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because mismatched chromosomes disrupt meiosis, preventing successful gamete production and leading to sterility in many hybrids.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because increased genetic diversity does not necessarily lead to higher fertility—chromosome mismatches usually cause sterility rather than increased reproductive success.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because chromosome number directly affects meiosis and fertility."},{"ref":"BI2660","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2660\n\nThe image on the left shows a cross-section of a symmetrical flower. The image on the right shows the same flower but looking upwards from below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Diversity%20of%20Organisms/images/BI2660.png\" alt=\"BI2660\">\n\nUse the following key to identify the plant which this flower is from:\n\n| | | |\n| :-- | :-- | :-- |\n| 1. | Animal pollinated …. | Go to 2|\n| | Wind pollinated …. | Plant P |\n| 2. | Flower with 10 stamens …. | Plant Q |\n| | Flower with more than 10 stamens …. | Go to 3 |\n| 3. | Ovary as tall as wide…. | Plant R |\n| | Ovary taller than wide…. | Go to 4 |\n| 4. | Flower has more sepals than petals ….| Plant S |\n| | Flower has fewer sepals than petals…. |Plant T|\n\nWhich plant does the flower belong to?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Plant P\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Plant Q\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Plant T\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Plant R","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct. The plant is animal-pollinated (the flower has large petals and internal reproductive structures (stigma and anthers), indicating animal pollination). It also has more than 10 stamens and an ovary that is taller than it is wide. This takes us to step 4. From the diagram on the right, we can see that the flower has fewer sepals than petals which means it must be plant T.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the flower in the diagram is animal-pollinated as it has anthers and a stigma held inside the large, colourful petals.\n\nStep 2: The image only shows half the flower, which has 11 stamens visible. This means the full flower has more than eleven stamens; **Choice B** is, therefore, incorrect.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because, in step 3, the ovary is taller than it is wide, so it can't be flower **R**."},{"ref":"BI2662","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2662\n\nWhy is the cytochrome oxidase I gene the region most commonly used for DNA barcoding in animal species?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Because this gene is highly conserved, resulting in nearly identical sequences across all animal species.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Because this gene is sufficiently different between species while remaining relatively constant within a species.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Because this gene is found exclusively in animals and not in any other organisms.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Because this gene is part of the nuclear DNA and evolves rapidly, ensuring distinct differences even among closely related species.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct.  The cytochrome oxidase I gene has the ideal balance of variability. Its sequence differs enough between species to allow clear differentiation, yet it remains consistent within individuals of the same species. This makes it highly effective for DNA barcoding.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect: If the cytochrome oxidase I gene were too highly conserved (i.e., nearly identical across species), it wouldn’t provide enough variation to distinguish one species from another.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect. The cytochrome oxidase I gene is not exclusive to animals; it is found in the mitochondria of many eukaryotes. Its utility for animal barcoding comes from its pattern of variability, not its exclusivity.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect. The cytochrome oxidase I gene is mitochondrial, not nuclear, and its evolutionary rate is moderate rather than extremely rapid. A gene that evolves too quickly might accumulate too many mutations, complicating species-level identification."},{"ref":"BI2663","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2663\n\nThe diagram below shows the results obtained from the genetic fingerprinting of environmental DNA in a soil sample.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Diversity%20of%20Organisms/images/BI2663.png\" alt=\"BI2663\">\n\nHow many different species are present in this sample?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 9\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 8\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 4\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 6","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because there are four different types of DNA profiles with the same banding patterns visible. The well on the left contains the DNA ladder and so should not be counted as a species. Samples 1, 7, and 8 are all the same species, as are samples 3 and 6 and samples 4 and 5. Sample 2 is a unique species.\n\n**Choice A** is not correct because many of the DNA profiles are identical so they must be from the same species, not different ones. It also includes the DNA ladder in the count\n\n**Choice B** is not correct because it counts repeating DNA profiles as different species.\n\n**Choice D** is not correct because it also counts organisms with the same profiles as different species."},{"ref":"BI2801","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2801\n\nWhat determines the overall size of a eukaryotic genome?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The number of genes\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The number of chromosomes\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The amount of protein\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The total amount of DNA","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe number of genes contributes to the overall size of the genome, but it is not the sole determinant. Eukaryotic genomes contain a significant amount of noncoding DNA.  The amount of this non-coding DNA is a major contributing factor to the overall size of a eukaryotic genome.  **Choice A** is incorrect.\\\nChromosomes vary greatly in the number of genes and noncoding DNA they contain. Simply knowing the number of chromosomes in a genome does little to describe the size of the genome.  **Choice B** is incorrect.\\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because a genome is a measure of only the amount of DNA and does not consider protein.  \\\n**Choice D** is the correct choice because it is the total amount of DNA in a genome that determines the size of that genome, regardless of the function of the DNA."},{"ref":"BI2802","marks":"11","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2802\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nThe table shows genome sizes for several different species.\n\n|Organism | Genome Size (base pairs) |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| *Escherichia coli* (Bacterium) | 5 million |\n| *Saccharomyces cerevisiae* (Yeast) | 12 million |\n| *Drosophila melanogaster* (Fruit Fly) | 140 million |\n| *Xenopus laevis* (Frog) | ???? |\n| *Homo sapiens* | 3 billion |\n\n1. Describe the challenges of predicting the size of the genome of *Xenopus laevis* based on the information provided in the table.     [2 marks]\n\nSingle-cell genome sequencing allows researchers to analyse the genetic material of individual cells. Cancer is characterised by significant genomic instability, leading to massive variations in genome size when compared to noncancerous cells.\n\n2. Explain how single-cell genome sequencing can be more useful than traditional genome sequencing for developing effective cancer treatments.    [3 marks]\n\nThe diagram below depicts nanopore DNA sequencing technology.  This portable technology involves passing a single piece of DNA through a tiny pore in a membrane in an electrical field in a manner such that the current can reveal the DNA sequence.  This allows for the analysis of environmental DNA (eDNA), which consists of DNA fragments shed by organisms into their surroundings (e.g., skin cells, waste).  It can also give almost immediate results for shorter fragments, and sequence entire genomes in as little as 3 days.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Diversity%20of%20Organisms/images/2802a.jpg\" alt=\"2802a\">\n[Source: Adapted from *DNA sequencing method* [Infographic], by National Institute of Standards and Technology, 2012 Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 5 May 5 2025 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=File:DNA_Sequencing_Method_ (8051510835).jpg&oldid=958458868.Public Domain]\n\n3. Discuss **one** potential benefit and one potential risk associated with the widespread use of portable DNA sequencing technologies.     [2 marks]\n\n4. Suggest ways whole-genome sequencing can contribute to our understanding of evolutionary relationships between species, and also to personalised medicine.     [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. the following (award zero marks for a numerical prediction):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genome size does not increase with complexity in a predictable manner     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **WHICH MEANS** it is not possible to predict a genome size based on the information in the table       [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The genome size of Xenopus laevis **cannot be predicted** ($\\checkmark$) from the table because there is **no correlation** between organism **complexity and genome size** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ traditional whole-genome sequencing provides an average genomic profile of the whole organism **OR** not just the tumour     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ single-cell sequencing can show differences between the typical genome of that organism and the tumor     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ single-cell sequencing can show differences within the tumour itself     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ heterogeneity within the tumour can provide clinicians **OR** researchers with clues as to the best treatment options      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ comparison of single-cell genome sequencing of tumour with originating tissue can help clinicians predict which treatment option will best allow healthy tissue to survive     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ single-cell sequencing facilitates the development of more personalised and effective therapies     [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Traditional whole-genome** sequencing provides an overall genomic view of a **whole organism** ($\\checkmark$), giving us an average of the genetic information across all cells. **Single-cell sequencing** offers a more **detailed** perspective by revealing **differences** ($\\checkmark$) between the typical genome of that organism and the tumour cells.  Heterogeneity within the tumour can provide clinicians and researchers with crucial clues for determining the **most effective treatment options** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **two** of the following *(must include **one** benefit and **one** risk)*:\n\n    ***benefits***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enables rapid identification of pathogens in remote or resource-limited areas     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ faster response time for public health interventions     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tracking of endangered **OR** invasive **OR** elusive species by eDNA without the need for lab facilities     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ identification of species present in an ecosystem based on eDNA without the need for lab facilities  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic analysis at the point of care **OR** on-site for rapid treatment response     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ individual empowerment **OR** accountability over their own health     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased speed of identification of criminal suspects **OR** disaster victims     [1 mark]\n\n    ***risks***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ privacy concerns due to easy access to genetic data     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ misuse of genetic information **OR** genetic discrimination      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ misinterpretation of results by non-experts may lead to incorrect medical or legal decisions     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ potential for higher error rate  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ potential for bioterrorism   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ technological advancements moving faster than legal regulatory framework leads to misuse and abuse [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    One potential benefit of portable DNA sequencing is that it can provide **faster results** ($\\checkmark$) because the sequencing can be done on-site rather than sent to a lab, which can take days.  One possible risk is that DNA samples can be collected from the environment **without the consent of the people**, raising important **ethical concerns** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **four** of the following, at least one must be presented for each application\n\n    ***evolutionary relationships***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows for comparison of whole genomes across species    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ identifies conserved regions and genes     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ facilitates the study of evolutionary genetic changes such as gene duplication, loss, chromosomal alterations and horizontal gene transfer    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ generates data for analysing genome patterns of evolution    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ extensive genetic data enables the creation of detailed and accurate phylogenetic trees    [1 mark]\n\n    ***personalised medicine***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ identifies genetic predispositions to disease    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ predict individual responses to drugs and medicines    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ characterise the genetic make-up of tumours to guide cancer treatment    [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Whole-genome sequencing can contribute to the study of evolutionary relationships by identifying differences in **genetic patterns of evolution**, such as **gene duplication or loss** and **chromosomal duplication or loss** ($\\checkmark$).  It can also **identify conserved genes** or regulatory sequences between species ($\\checkmark$).  Whole-genome sequencing can contribute to personalised medicine through the **identification of genetic diseases** ($\\checkmark$) and predispositions to disease, as well as probable individual physiological **responses to treatment** options ($\\checkmark$)."}]},{"name":"Evolution & Speciation","questions":[{"ref":"BI0226","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0226\n\nWhich statement best defines evolution?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Humans breed organisms to produce desired traits\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Two populations become reproductively isolated\n\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ A change in the heritable characteristics of a population over generations\n\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Individuals change their characteristics during their lifetime in response to the environment","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because evolution is defined as a change in heritable characteristics (genetic traits) within a population over generations. The key ideas are population-level change and inheritance across generations.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because this describes selective breeding (artificial selection), where humans choose which organisms reproduce to increase desired traits. It can cause evolutionary change, but it is not the definition of evolution.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because reproductive isolation (no interbreeding or gene flow) can be a mechanism leading to speciation, but isolation itself is not the definition of evolution. Evolution is about changes in heritable traits in a population.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because this describes acquired characteristics or acclimatisation (changes during an individual’s lifetime). These changes are generally not inherited genetically, so they do not define evolution."},{"ref":"BI0227","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0227\n\nThe forelimbs of a human, bird, and whale have the same basic bone pattern but different functions.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0227ii.png\" alt=\"BI0227ii\">\n\nWhat does this provide evidence for?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The differences are caused by acquired characteristics that are passed on\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The limbs formed due to selective breeding by humans\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ The limbs are analogous, showing convergent evolution from unrelated ancestors\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The limbs are homologous, suggesting a common ancestor and divergent evolution","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA shared underlying structure with different functions is evidence of homologous structures. Homology supports common ancestry, and the differences in function are explained by divergent evolution (adaptation to different environments). **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because this is a Lamarck-style idea, which says that acquired characteristics are passed on. Evolutionary change occurs through inheritance of alleles and changes in allele frequencies, not traits acquired during an individual’s lifetime being inherited.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because selective breeding requires humans intentionally choosing parents to reproduce. The limb similarities across humans, bats, and whales are not explained by human-directed breeding; they are found in natural lineages over long timescales.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because analogous structures have similar function but different underlying structure and do not come from a common ancestor (e.g., bird wing vs insect wing). In this case, the underlying bone plan is very similar, so they are not analogous."},{"ref":"BI0228","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0228\n\nWhich of the following is/are example(s) of selective breeding?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1em}$ A dog is trained to sit on command\n- II. $\\hspace{0.7em}$ Farmers repeatedly breed cattle with the highest milk yield over many generations\n\n- III. $\\hspace{0.5em}$A bacterial population becomes antibiotic-resistant after exposure to antibiotics\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$    I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    II only\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    II and III only\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is not true. Training a dog changes behaviour through learning. It does not involve choosing parents to reproduce, and the learned behaviour is not a genetic trait passed on through reproduction.\n**Statement II** is true. This is classic artificial selection, where humans choose which cows reproduce based on a desired inherited trait (high milk yield). Over many generations, the alleles linked to that trait become more common.\n**Statement III** is not true. Antibiotics act as an environmental selection pressure. Resistant bacteria survive and reproduce more successfully, so resistance increases in the population. This is natural selection, not humans selecting parents to breed for a desired trait.\n\n**Choice B** is correct because it includes the only true **Statement II**.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are all incorrect as they include one or more of the untrue **Statements I** and **III**."},{"ref":"BI0229","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0229\n\nWhich of the following best describes an analogous structure?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ A structure in two different species that serves a similar function and was inherited from a common ancestor.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    A structure in two different species that serves a similar function but evolved independently, without a common ancestor.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    A structure found in all members of a species that performs the same function.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    A structure found in all members of a species but serves different functions.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAnalogous structures are traits found in different species that perform similar functions but do not originate from a common ancestor. Instead, they arise through convergent evolution, where species adapt to similar environments or ecological roles independently. **Choice B** is correct as it describes analogous structures, which evolve due to similar selective pressures rather than shared ancestry.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it describes homologous structures, which share a common evolutionary origin but may have different functions (e.g., a whale's flipper and a human's arm).\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because this refers to universal structures within a species, which is unrelated to analogous structures.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because this describes structural variation within a species, not similarities between different species."},{"ref":"BI0230","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0230\n\nBased on the structure of each forelimb shown below, which one is likely to be analogous?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |   |   |B.|\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0230_A.png\" alt=\"BI0230_A\">|   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0230_B.png\" alt=\"BI0230_B\">|\n|C. |   |   |  |D. |\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0230_C.png\" alt=\"BI0230_C\"> |   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0230_D.png\" alt=\"BI0230_D\">|\n\n[Adapted from: Source Владислав Петрович , В. (2011). *File:homology vertebrates-en.svg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved June 8, 2022, from https ://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homology_vertebrates-en.svg copyright free \\\nGraham, V. B. (2016, May 31). *File:analogous & homologous structures.svg.* Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved June 8, 2022, from https ://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Analogous_%26_Homologous_Structures.svg CC-BY-SA-4.0]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAn analogous structure is one that has a similar function to other structures but does not come from a common ancestor. **Choice B** is a forelimb, but does not have a structure similar to the other three limbs, which indicates that it does not come from a common ancestor, so is likely to be analogous.\n**Choices A**, **C** and **D** are incorrect as they are all homologous to each other i.e. they share a common ancestor, evidenced by the similarities in their structures."},{"ref":"BI0232","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0232\n\nWhich of the following is **not** an example of speciation?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Evolution of different beak shapes in finches (*C. olivacea*)\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    Breeding varieties of green apples (*M. domestica*) with desirable characteristics\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    Squirrels on the North and South rims of the grand canyon, who cannot interbreed\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    Buffalo grass (*B. dactyloides*) is able to grow in polluted soil but other species of grass cannot","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because it refers to selective breeding, not speciation. **Choices A**, **C** and **D** are all examples of speciation."},{"ref":"BI0233","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0233\n\nWhich option lists examples of evidence that can be used to support the theory of evolution?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Sudden appearance of complex organisms, fossil record, homologous structures\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    Fossil record, homologous structures, size of organisms\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    Homologous structures, speciation, fossil record\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    Speciation, the complexity of organisms, increasing size of organisms","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe fossil record provides evidence of evolution, as the sequence in which fossils appear matches the sequence that they would be expected to evolve. Homologous structures provide evidence of evolution as they provide evidence that organisms shared a common ancestor. Speciation provides evidence of evolution as when populations become reproductively isolated, then natural selection can act differently on those populations, resulting in the formation of new species. **Choice C** is correct. **Choices A** and **D**  are incorrect as the organisms becoming more complex does not necessarily correlate with evolution. **Choice B** is incorrect, as the increasing size of organisms does not necessarily correlate with evolution."},{"ref":"BI0235","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0235\n\nHow does gradual divergence provide evidence for evolution?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ There is variation between different species across geographical areas.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    There is no variation between different species across geographical areas.\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    There is variation between populations of the same species across geographical areas.\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    There is no variation between populations of the same species across geographical areas.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nGradual divergence describes gradual changes in allele frequencies and phenotypes across a species’ geographical range, so neighbouring populations are similar but become increasingly different with distance while still belonging to the same species. Therefore,  **Choice C** is correct: there is variation within a species across geographical areas.\n\n**Choices B** and **D** are incorrect because “no variation” does not support gradual divergence.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because gradual divergence refers to differences within a species, not variation between different species."},{"ref":"BI0236","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0236\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe diagram represents the evolution of features in the forefoot of horses from the oldest ancestor (a) to modern day horses (e).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0236.png\" alt=\"BI0236\">\n[Source: Heard, J.M. (2015, August 8). *Image from page 65 of \"breeding, training, management and diseases of the horse and other domestic animals*\" (1893). Flickr. Retrieved June 7, 2022, from https://www.flickr.com/photos/internetarchivebookimages/20385840566/. CC0 1.0]\n\n1.\n    1. Distinguish between the earliest and most recent forefoot. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Explain the mechanism through which the horse forefoot has changed over time. [4 marks]\n\n    3. Suggest what would happen if ancestral horses were brought back and introduced into the wild today. Justify your answer. [1 mark]\n\n2. State the definition of evolution. [1 mark]\n\n3. Different horse breeds exist in varying geographical locations. Suggest **one** possible way to show that these horses living in different geographical locations were from the same species. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  any **two** of the following:\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ more recent forefoot is longer **THAN** the older forefoot (or vice versa) [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ more recent forefoot is more narrow **THAN** the older forefoot (or vice versa) [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ more recent forefoot has **FEWER** toes **OR** only one toe **COMPARED** to the older forefoot **OR** more recent foreleg has **FEWER** individual **OR** fused bones **COMPARED** to the older forelegs [1 mark]\n\n        [2 marks]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    The more recent forefoot is **longer than the earlier forefoot** ($\\checkmark$) and has fewer **toes** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    2. any **four** of the following:\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ heritable variation in toe number OR forefoot length existed in the population    [1 mark]\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ random mutation produced individuals with fewer toes OR more weight on the central toe OR longer forefoot                                    [1 mark]\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ environmental selection pressure meant this phenotype gave a fitness advantage OR better survival OR greater reproductive success                        [1 mark]\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ individuals with the advantageous trait survived and reproduced more than others    [1 mark]\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ trait was inherited by offspring                            [1 mark]\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ over many generations, the frequency of the advantageous allele increased in the population                                        [1 mark]\n\n        [4 marks]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    **Random mutations** and recombination produced individuals with **variation** in forefoot structure, including **reduced toe number**  and a **longer central toe** ($\\checkmark$). In **changing environments**, individuals with these traits had a **fitness advantage** ($\\checkmark$), and their **offspring inherited the alleles** ($\\checkmark$). Over **many generations, allele frequencies shifted** ($\\checkmark$), and the reduced-toe forefoot became common.\n\n    3. **one** of the following:\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ wouldn\\'t survive **OR** would die **BECAUSE** they are less adapted to the environment **OR** would be outcompeted **OR** would be selected against **OR** other reasonable justification [1 mark]\n        [1 mark]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    Ancestral horses **would not survive**  ($\\checkmark$) in the wild because they are not as well **adapted to the environment** ($\\checkmark$) as modern horses.\n\n2. $\\ce{ -> }$ heritable characteristics of a species change over time [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    Evolution is when the **heritable characteristics** ($\\checkmark$) of a species change over time.\n\n3. any **two** of the following (*must be from the same pair of statements to gain full marks*):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cross-breed the horses [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if produce fertile offspring they are same species [1 mark]\n\n    **OR**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ analyse horses' DNA [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ look for very similar base sequences **OR** same barcode [1 mark]\n\n    **OR**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ compare chromosome number **OR** karyotypes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ same chromosome number supports being the same species [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    **Cross-breed** the **horses** from different locations ($\\checkmark$). If they **produce fertile offspring** then they are of the same species ($\\checkmark$), ."},{"ref":"BI0270","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0270\n\nWhich is a key difference between homologous and analogous structures?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Homologous structures are similar due to ancestry, but analogous structures are similar due to convergent evolution.\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Homologous structures are similar due to ancestry, but analogous structures are similar due to divergent evolution.\n\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Analogous structures are similar due to ancestry, but homologous structures are similar due to convergent evolution.\n\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Analogous structures always look similar but homologous structures do not.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because homologous structures are due to common ancestry, but analogous structures are not due to common ancestry and instead are the result of convergent evolution. **Choice B** is incorrect because analogous structures are similar due to convergent evolution, where structures evolve independently but have similar functions due to the needs of the organisms. **Choice C** is incorrect because analogous structures are not due to common ancestry. **Choice D** is incorrect because homologous structures and analogous structures can sometimes look different."},{"ref":"BI0526","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0526\n\nWhat can result in speciation among organisms living in the same location?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Behavioural isolation\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Geographic isolation\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Stabilising selection\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Directional selection","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSpeciation comes about through reproductive isolation which can result from behavioural isolation, in which a population of a species can no longer interbreed with other populations of the species. For example, some segments of a population may respond to a particular mating call or mating behaviour not expressed by another segment of the population.\n\nGeographic isolation (**Choice B**) occurs when populations are prevented from interbreeding because they are in different physical locations. In this question they are in the same location, therefore this is incorrect. Stabilising selection (**Choice C**) is a type of natural selection that promotes an intermediate phenotype, so extremes are reduced, and it does not result in speciation. Directional selection (**Choice D**) is a type of natural selection that promotes an extreme phenotype, but the selection is in one direction so it does not divide the population and so does not lead to speciation. Only if selection occurs in both directions (divergent selection) does it tend to lead to speciation."},{"ref":"BI0706","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0706\n\n*Triticum monococcum*  is an einkorn wheat and was the first domesticated crop. It has a diploid number of 14 chromosomes. *Triticum turgidum* is a durum wheat with a total of 28 chromosomes. It is a result of the hybridization of *Triticum monococcum* and another grass *Aegilops speltoides* which also has a diploid number of 14. What is the correct term for *Triticum turgidum* in comparison to *Triticum monococcum*?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Haploid\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Triploid\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Hexaploid\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Tetraploid","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe normal diploid number (2n) means an organism has two sets of chromosomes, one from each parent. *Triticum turgidum* arose from a hybridisation event between *Triticum monococcum* (2n = 14) and *Aegilops speltoides* (2n = 14). Following chromosome doubling, the resulting organism inherited two full sets of chromosomes from each parent, making it tetraploid (4n = 28). **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** (Haploid) is incorrect because haploid (n) refers to having one set of chromosomes, which would be 7 in this case.\n\n**Choice B** (Triploid) is incorrect because triploid (3n) means three sets, totalling 21 chromosomes.\n\n**Choice C** (Hexaploid) is incorrect because hexaploid (6n) refers to six sets, or 42 chromosomes."},{"ref":"BI0714","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0714\n\nThe diagram shows the haploid number of chromosomes in a gamete. What could be the polyploid number of chromosomes for this organism?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0714.png\" alt=\"BI0714\">\n[Adapted from: Source Ehamberg. (2010, May 10). *File:haploid vs diploid.svg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved June 30, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haploid_vs_diploid.svg Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 6\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 24\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 3\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 4","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nPolyploidy is when an organism develops a mutation where they have more than two sets of the haploid number of chromosomes. **Choice B** is correct as it has 8 sets of 3 chromosomes.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect, as it is the diploid number, which is double the haploid number. **Choice C** is incorrect, as it is the haploid number of chromosomes. **Choice D** is incorrect because it is not a multiple of the haploid number."},{"ref":"BI0715","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0715\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe male firefly (*P. pyralis*) signals to potential female mates by flashing its lights in specific patterns. Females will only respond to light patterns produced by their own species.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0715A.png\" alt=\"BI0715A\">\n[Source: Adapted from Gallagher, J. (2014, June 3). *File: Firefly - Photinus pyralis, Woodbridge, Virginia.jpg - Wikimedia ...* Wikimedia commons. Retrieved July 1, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Firefly_-_Photinus_pyralis,_Woodbridge,_Virginia.jpg Copyright under CC BY 2.0]\n\n1. Identify the form of reproductive isolation that fireflies exhibit. Justify your answer.     [2 marks]\n\n2. Suggest **one** possible advantage of this mating ritual.  [1 mark]\n\n3. All fireflies seen today have diverged from the same ancestral species. Explain how speciation is responsible for the large variety of firefly species.  [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$  behavioural type [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$  the different light patterns **OR** the mating behaviour **OR** rituals  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$  prevents the fireflies from mating with different species **OR** interspecies breeding  [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The fireflies are exhibiting **behavioural** isolation ($\\checkmark$), as the specific **mating rituals** that they perform ($\\checkmark$), **prevent interspecies breeding** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$  allows females to find males of the same species **OR** allows fireflies to identify members of the opposite sex     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$  allows females to select the fittest males **OR** mates      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$  brighter **OR** faster flashes can increase the chance of mating **OR** be more attractive to females      [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    An advantage to the firefly of exhibiting this type of behaviour is that it allows females to find **males** of the **same species**. ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$  speciation is the formation of a new species **OR** the process by which populations diverge **OR** evolve over time to become distinct species     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$  members of a population are isolated from one another **OR** barriers prevent interbreeding      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$  allopatric speciation involves a physical barrier separating groups **OR** sympatric speciation involves a non-physical barrier separating groups         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$  over time selective pressures affect each group differently **OR** different mutations occur within each group **OR** over time each group can develop different behaviours **OR** different characteristics develop due to natural selection         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$  allele frequencies change in each population **OR** genetic drift         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$  groups are unable to interbreed **OR** are reproductively isolated when the barrier is removed **OR** cannot reproduce to produce fertile offspring         [1 mark]\n[3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Speciation is when a group of organisms change so much that they can no longer mate together to produce **fertile offspring** ($\\checkmark$). This can come about by a group of fireflies being **separated or isolated** ($\\checkmark$) from the original population, say by being blown to an island. Over time, the separated group will change. For example the climate may be colder so those individuals with a larger abdomen have **more chance of surviving** ($\\checkmark$) as they lose less heat, and so they pass on their favourable alleles and the **allele frequency** in the population **changes** ($\\checkmark$). When the separated group encounter fireflies from the original population, they can **no longer reproduce together** ($\\checkmark$) as their abdomens are so different."},{"ref":"BI0813","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0813\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nA study has been conducted on the effect of hormonal contraception pollution on the mating calls of the adult male African clawed frog (*Xenopus laevis*). The spectrogram shows the frequency and pitch of clicks (mating call) of control *X.laevis* and those exposed to oestrogen.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0813ai.png\" alt=\"BI0813ai\">\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0813aii.png\" alt=\"BI0813aii\">\n[Source: Adapted from Hoffmann, F., & Kloas, W. (2012, February 15). *Estrogens can disrupt amphibian mating behavior*. PLOS ONE. Retrieved May 13, 2023, from https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0032097.  Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n1. Distinguish between the clicks in the spectrogram between the control *X. laevis* and the *X. laevis* treated with oestrogen in 1 second.            [2 marks]\n\nThe graph shows the average duration of the *X. laevis* mating calls when exposed to the hormone oestrogen.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0813b.png\" alt=\"BI0813b\">\n[Source: Adapted from Hoffmann, F., & Kloas, W. (2012, February 15). *Estrogens can disrupt amphibian mating behavior*. PLOS ONE. Retrieved May 13, 2023, from https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0032097.  Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n2. Describe the effect of oestrogen on the duration of the mating call shown in this study.        [2 marks]\n\n3. Explain why it is important to have the *X.laevis* control group.        [1 mark]\n\n4. Identify the independent variable in this study.    [1 mark]\n\n5.\n    1. Predict the relative reproductive success of *X.laevis* exposed to 29.6 ngL:sup[-1] of oestrogen.            [1 mark]\n\n    2. Outline the possible consequences of oestrogen exposure on *X. laevis*.[3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ control *X.laevis* have a more regular click pattern **WHEREAS** *X.laevis* treated with oestrogen have an irregular click pattern        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ control *X.laevis* have a lower pitch of clicks **OR** a pitch of approximately 2.5 kHz **WHEREAS** *X.laevis* have a higher pitch of clicks **OR** a pitch of approximately 3 kHz    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the *X.laevis*  exposed to oestrogen have longer clicks **COMPARED** to the control *X.laevis* **OR** the control *X.laevis* has more frequent clicks **OR** faster clicks than the *X.laevis* exposed to oestrogen        [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n   <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0813ci.png\" alt=\"BI0813ci\">\n   <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0813cii.png\" alt=\"BI0813cii\">\n    [Source: Adapted from Hoffmann, F., & Kloas, W. (2012, February 15). *Estrogens can disrupt amphibian mating behavior*. PLOS ONE. Retrieved May 13, 2023, from https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0032097.  Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n    The control *X.laevis* have a more **regular click pattern compared** to the *X.laevis* treated with oestrogen, which have an **irregular click pattern** ($\\checkmark$). The control *X.laevis* have a **lower pitch** of clicks at approximately **2.5 kHz**, whereas the *X.laevis* that were exposed to oestrogen have a **higher pitch** of approximately **3 kHz** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 29.6 ngL:sup[-1] of oestrogen **OR** the highest concentration of oestrogen has the shortest mean mating call duration        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the lowest level of oestrogen OR 0.296 ngL:sup[-1] of oestrogen has the longest mean mating call duration    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ at the middle concentration of oestrogen OR at ngL:sup[-1], the mean mating call duration was similar to the control OR only 1s longer than the control        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ as oestrogen levels increase the mean duration of the mating call decreases            [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **longest** mean mating **call** of 33s was seen at the **lowest concentration of oestrogen** (0.296 ngL:sup[-1]) ($\\checkmark$).  *X.laevis* exposed to an **oestrogen concentration of 2.96 ngL:sup[-1]** had a mean mating call **duration similar** to the **control** ($\\checkmark$), just 1s longer.  However, at the **highest concentration of oestrogen** (29.6 ngL:sup[-1]), *X.laevis* had a much **lower** mean mating call **duration**, 4 seconds less than the control ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to ensure that any effect being seen on the mating call duration **OR** frequency of clicks **OR** pitch of clicks is only due to the effect of hormones **OR** oestrogen    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ to allow for a baseline comparison            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows for establishing causality            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ helps to minimise the impact of confounding variables    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    It is important to have a control group because it helps establish that **any observed changes in mating call duration are due to oestrogen** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the concentration of oestrogen that *X.laevis* is exposed to [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The independent variable in this study is the **concentration of oestrogen** ($\\checkmark$) that the *X.laevis* is exposed to.\n\n5.\n    1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lower reproductive success than the control **OR** reduced successful matings    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The *X.laevis* exposed to oestrogen may have **lower** reproductive success than the control ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5.\n    2. Any **three** of the following\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *X.laevis* exposed to oestrogen have a higher pitch of clicks **OR** longer clicks **OR** a shorter mating call duration, so may not be recognised by female *X.laevis*    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ resulting in reduced mating opportunities and so reduced reproductive success    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *X.laevis*  exposed to oestrogen may have shorter life spans and die without passing their genes onto the next generation reducing population size and gene pool        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ this can result in the population being more vulnerable to disease **OR** environmental changes **OR** limit the population’s adaptive potential [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ if the altered mating call resembles calls of a different species of frog **OR** attracts females from another species, this can result in hybridisation of the species    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ altered calls may disrupt the social dynamics **OR** may reduce ability of male to establish dominance over competitors **OR** defend territory [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Exposure to oestrogen in  *X.laevis* frogs could result in **reduced reproductive success due to reduced mating opportunities** ($\\checkmark$) as a result of females **not recognising the altered mating call** ($\\checkmark$). The population size and gene pool may also decrease if oestrogen pollution **reduces the lifespan of the frogs** ($\\checkmark$). Altered calls may also reduce the ability of the males to defend their territories against competitors and so may **disrupt the social dynamics** of the population ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0855","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0855\n\nWhich statement best illustrates the process of evolution?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ A horse that increases its maximum running speed has faster offspring.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ A random mutation gives resistance to a disease and spreads in the population.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mutations occur as needed to increase fitness in individuals and populations.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The most complex organisms are the most evolved.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because evolution is the change in heritable characteristics of a population, and random mutations alter heritable traits.  In this case the mutation confers resistance to a disease, making these individuals more likely to survive and pass on the mutation to their offspring.  Over time resistance to the disease becomes more prevalent in the population.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because traits acquired through an organism's lifetime cannot be passed on to the next generation.  **Choice C** is incorrect because mutations are random and do not occur because an individual or population requires a different trait.  **Choice D** is incorrect because complexity is not a measure of evolutionary progress, as evolution refers to change over time."},{"ref":"BI0856","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0856\n\nWhich of the following provides evidence for evolution through selective breeding?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Phenotypic variation in the domesticated dog (*Canis familiaris*) has increased significantly as a result of humans selecting desirable traits. |\n    | II. | Crop yield in domesticated crop plants is significantly greater than their wild relatives. |\n    | III. | *Homo sapiens* in many parts of the world have alleles that provide resistance to diseases endemic to their region. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because **Statements I** and **II** are correct.  **Statement I** supports the theory of evolution because an increase in phenotypes is a component of the process of evolution.  **Statement II** supports the theory of evolution because it exemplifies that humans, selecting for desirable traits, can cause directional selection in crop plants.\n\nAlthough **Statement III** does support the theory of evolution, it does not do so as an example of selective breeding.  Humans have not been selectively bred.  **Choices B, C** and **D** do not exclusively include **Statements I and II**, so they are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0857","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0857\n\nShown in diagrams A and B are two pairs of similar structures.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0857.png\" alt=\"BI0857\">\n\nWhich of the following statements correctly explain the structural similarities shown in diagrams A and B?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n | | |\n | :-- | :-- |\n | I. | Structural similarities in diagram A are primarily due to random mutations, not common ancestry. |\n | II. | Structural similarities in diagram B are due to the fact they both perform similar functions. |\n | III. | Diagram A depicts analogous structures, and diagram B depicts homologous structures. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because only **statement II** correctly explains the structural similarities.\n\nThe bird and insect wings are similar due to convergent evolution. **Statement III** is incorrect because diagram A depicts homologous structures, and diagram B depicts analogous structures. **Statement I** is incorrect because humans and bats evolved from a common ancestor, and random mutations slowly gave rise to differences in the pentadactyl limb between different species. **Choices A, B** and **D** are all incorrect because they include either **statements I** or **III**, or both."},{"ref":"BI0858","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0858\n\nWhat sequence of events usually occur during the process of speciation?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Reproductive isolation → one species becomes two species → random mutations\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Random mutations → reproductive isolation → one species becomes another species\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Geographic isolation → reproductive isolation → one species becomes another species\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Geographic isolation → reproductive isolation → one species becomes two species","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because geographic isolation leads to a buildup of different mutations between two populations, leading to reproductive isolation and the production of two species from one.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect, as reproductive isolation cannot occur prior to a buildup of random mutations.  **Choices B** and **C** are incorrect because speciation involves one species becoming two, not one."},{"ref":"BI0859","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0859\n\nIt is thought that the Congo River divided a population of the common ancestor of bonobos (*Pan paniscus*) and chimpanzees (*Pan troglodytes*), resulting in geographic isolation. The south side of the river has more stable food availability, while the north side experiences more frequent food shortages.\n\nWhich correctly pairs theoretical selection pressures and consequent adaptations of the two populations?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0859.png\" alt=\"BI0859\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because a scarcity of food (alongside competition with gorillas) led to increased aggression in the ancestral population of chimpanzees. Plentiful food on the south side of the river (as well as an absence of gorillas) led to increased cooperation in the ancestral population of bonobos.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because there was and is no difference in solar radiation on either side of the Congo River. **Choice C** is incorrect because the selection pressures and adaptations are reversed in terms of where they existed (south side vs. north side).  **Choice D** is incorrect because food scarcity led to more aggression, and plentiful food led to more cooperation."},{"ref":"BI0860","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0860\n\nThe base sequence between two species was found to be 78% the same, while the order of amino acids found in the same cell types between two species was 82% the same.  What may be the reason for this?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The genetic code is degenerate.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mutations occur more frequently in DNA that codes for proteins.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Techniques used to analyse base sequences of DNA are less reliable.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The genetic code is universal.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because what is meant by “the genetic code is degenerate” is multiple codons sometimes code for the same amino acid, which means that sometimes a mutation will change the genetic code, but the same amino acid is still coded for.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect as mutations generally occur more frequently in DNA that does not code for proteins. **Choice C** is incorrect as techniques used to analyse the base sequence of DNA are more reliable than those used to determine the order of amino acids.  **Choice D** is incorrect as the universality of the genetic code refers to the fact that all organisms use the same genetic code."},{"ref":"BI0861","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0861\n\nThe overall structure of the flipper of a whale, in terms of bones and muscles, is similar to the overall structure of the limb of a horse.  What are these structures referred to as?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ancestral structures\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Convergent structures\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Analogous structures\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Homologous structures","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because homologous structures refer to structures inherited from a common ancestor which are similar but often used for different functions.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect, as ancestral structures refer to structures present in an ancestor species, not structures found in present day species.  **Choice B** is incorrect as convergent structures are not a term used in biology.  Convergent evolution, on the other hand, leads to analogous structures. **Choice C** is incorrect as analogous structures refer to structures used for the same function, which are significantly different when closely inspected.  Analogous structures also have different evolutionary origins."},{"ref":"BI0862","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0862\n\nWhich of the following is the strongest evidence supporting the hypothesis that two populations are two different species?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Allele frequencies differ between the two populations.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The two populations are reproductively isolated.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The DNA sequence of some genes was found to be different.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The two populations are geographically isolated.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because two reproductively isolated populations are a key component of the definition of a species.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because allele frequencies often differ between different populations of the same species. **Choice C** is incorrect because varying DNA sequences in genes confirms the existence of different alleles, not the existence of two species.  **Choice D** is incorrect because even when two populations are geographically isolated, they may still be the same species."},{"ref":"BI0863","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0863\n\nWhen a horse, which has 64 chromosomes, and a donkey, which has 62 chromosomes reproduce, a mule offspring is produced.  Which are correct descriptions of mules?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Mules frequently produce offspring with other mules. |\n    | II. | Cells in mules cannot undergo meiosis but can undergo mitosis. |\n    | III. | Mule gametes have 63 chromosomes. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement II** is correct because mules possess an odd number of chromosomes (63), which means they cannot form the homologous pairs necessary for successful meiosis. This prevents the production of viable gametes. However, mitosis can still occur because it involves the replication and separation of individual sister chromatids, which does not require the pairing of homologous chromosomes.\n\n**Statement I** is incorrect because mules are infertile hybrids. Since they cannot produce functional gametes, they cannot reproduce with other mules.\n\n**Statement III** is incorrect because 63 is the diploid (2n) number of chromosomes found in the mule's somatic (body) cells. If a mule were able to produce gametes, the chromosome number would be approximately half that (approx 31.5), which is impossible as chromosomes must exist as whole units.\n\n**Choice C** is correct as it only includes the one true Statement II.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **D** are incorrect as they include one or more of the incorrect Statements I and III."},{"ref":"BI0864","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0864\n\nWhat is adaptive radiation?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ When a new species is produced through natural selection\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ When new ecological niches are exploited, resulting in speciation\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ When a species moves to a new location and adapts to new conditions\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ When speciation occurs, and analogous traits remain","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because adaptive radiation refers to the rapid diversification and speciation of a single ancestral species into multiple descendent species, taking advantage of different niches within a community. **Choice A** is incorrect as natural selection refers to organisms with advantageous traits having a greater chance of survival and so a greater chance to reproduce and pass on those traits to future generations within that population, leading to the evolution of species over time.  **Choice C** is incorrect as it refers to the phenomenon where an organism changes in response to new environmental conditions through natural selection and random mutations, while adaptive radiation involves the rapid evolution of more than one species from a single ancestral species. **Choice D** is incorrect, as analogous traits do not result from common ancestry."},{"ref":"BI0865","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0865\n\nWhich describes the  differences between the main isolating mechanisms in sympatric and allopatric speciation?\n\n| | Sympatric Speciation | Allopatric Speciation\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Behavioural isolation | Polyploidy |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Geographic isolation | Temporal isolation |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Temporal isolation | Geographic isolation |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Geographic isolation  | Polyploidy |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAllopatric speciation occurs when populations of the same species become geographically isolated, preventing them from interbreeding. This can be caused by various factors such as rivers, mountains or volcanoes erupting. Allopatric actually means ‘different geographical area’’.  Sympatric speciation is the evolution of a new species from an existing one without geographical isolation (sympatric actually means ‘same geographical area’). The factors that can cause this type of speciation include behavioural and temporal isolation.  **Choice C** is, therefore, correct because temporal isolation is common in sympatric speciation, and geographic isolation leads to allopatric speciation.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because although behavioural isolation can lead to sympatric speciation, polyploidy does not lead directly to allopatric speciation.  **Choice B** is incorrect because geographical isolation is not part of the process of sympatric speciation, and, temporal isolation does not directly lead to allopatric speciation. **Choice D** is incorrect because geographic isolation is not part of the process of sympatric speciation, and polyploidy does not lead directly to allopatric speciation."},{"ref":"BI0866","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0866\n\nTwo species of smartweed (genus *Persicaria*) hybridise and form a new species.  Which of the examples of parent chromosome number would result in a barrier to reproduction?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | 2n |\n    | II. | 3n |\n    | III. | 4n |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and IIII","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because 3n cells cannot undergo meiosis and, therefore, they would be a barrier to reproduction.\nIf both parents have the regular diploid chromosome number (2n) then the resulting offspring would also be 2n, due to normal meiotic cell division and formation of viable gametes. A parent with a diploid number of chromosomes would, therefore, not be a barrier to reproduction.\nEven though tetraploidy (4n) can lead to more complex pairing of chromosomes during meiosis, the even number of chromosomes allows meiosis to occur and viable gametes can be produced. A tetraploid parent would, therefore, not necessarily be a barrier to reproduction.\n**Choices A**, **C** and **D** do not include 3n, and so are not correct answers."},{"ref":"BI0867","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0867\n\nWhich of the following are often barriers to hybridisation between species?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | A given animal hybrid species has an even, as opposed to an odd number of chromosomes. |\n    | II. | Two species of flowering plants produce gametes at different times of the year.|\n    | III. | Behavioural barriers exist due to variations in bird song between species. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct.  Cross-pollination is unlikely if two species of flowering plants produce gametes at different times of year (**Statement II**).  Mating, and thus hybridisation, is unlikely or impossible if members of one bird species fail to recognise the mating calls of another bird species (**Statement III**).  **Statement I** is not a barrier to hybridisation because, typically, animals with even numbers of chromosomes in their somatic cells can reproduce sexually, but those with odd numbers are usually sterile due to an inability of their cells to undergo meiosis."},{"ref":"BI0868","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0868\n\nWhich letter in the Venn diagram correctly matches a description of a speciation event?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0868%20A4.1.11%20png.png\" alt=\"BI0868 A4.1.11 png\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Bonobos (*Pan paniscus*) and chimpanzees (*Pan troglodytes*) evolved from a common ancestor.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ A hybrid species (3n) was produced by two smartweed species (genus *Persicaria*) living in the same geographical region.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Sub-species of tortoise (*C. nigra becki* and *C. nigra guntheri*) in the Galapagos have evolved due to lava flow forming lava fields and impassable terrain.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The tetraploid grey treefrog emerged as a new species distinct from its diploid relative leading to reproductive isolation despite occupying the same geographical area.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because speciation through polyploidy is common in smartweed; if the resulting chromosome number is odd (here 3n), then the plants will become reproductively isolated from the 2n population, even though they are in the same geographic location. This is, therefore, an example of polyploidy and sympatric speciation.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because, although bonobos and chimpanzees were geographically isolated from each other (allopatric speciation), there was no increase in the chromosome number during the speciation event. **Choice C** is incorrect because the lava fields geographically isolated the tortoises, leading to sub-species forming. This is, therefore, an ongoing allopatric speciation event, not sympatric, and it does not involve polyploidy. The tetraploid frog resulted from a polyploidy speciation event that resulted in reproductive isolation. This did not involve spatial separation, so cannot be regarded as allopatric speciation; therefore, **Choice D** is also incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0869","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0869\n\nThe diagram below shows the feeding areas of four species of warbler (genus *Setophaga*). What is the best explanation for how these warblers came to coexist in such close proximity?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI0869.png\" alt=\"BI0869\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Polyploidy resulted from multiple sympatric speciation events.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Adaptive radiation reduced competition between the species.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The presence of four species increases biodiversity and ecological stability.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ One species evolved over time to exploit new ecological niches.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because adaptive radiation involves exploiting new ecological niches and speciation, here resulting in 4 new species from the original ancestral species which compete less with one another. **Choice A** is incorrect, as polyploidy is rare in vertebrates and animals generally. Although greater biodiversity generally increases ecological stability, **Choice C** does not explain how the warblers came to coexist in such close proximity. **Choice D** is incorrect because if only one species evolved to exploit new ecological niches, there would be only one warbler species, not four."},{"ref":"BI0870","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0870\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Describe how geographic isolation can lead to the formation of a new species from a single interbreeding population.        [5 marks]\n\n2. Outline what is meant by sympatric speciation.        [4 marks]\n\n3. Describe how sexual and asexual reproduction can lead to the formation of new species with different numbers of copies of each chromosome.    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **five** of the following:\n\n   $\\ce{ -> }$ gene flow is prevented **OR** reduced between the separated groups  [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ different selection pressures act on each isolated population due to different environments [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ natural selection favours different alleles **OR** phenotypes in each population [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ mutations arise independently in each population **OR** increase genetic variation in each population  [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ genetic drift changes allele frequencies randomly     [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ allele frequencies diverge over generations [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ reproductive isolating mechanisms evolve  [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ pre-zygotic isolation **OR** different mating behaviour **OR** breeding seasons **OR** mechanical **OR** gamete incompatibility may occur [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ post-zygotic isolation **OR** reduced hybrid viability **OR** hybrid sterility **OR** reduced hybrid fertility may occur [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ if secondary contact occurs, the populations do not interbreed successfully **OR**  do not produce fertile offspring [1 mark]\n\n[5 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nGeographic isolation prevents **gene flow** between the separated populations ($\\checkmark$). Different selection pressures, or genetic drift, cause **allele frequencies to diverge** over time in each population ($\\checkmark$). **Mutations also arise independently** in each population, increasing genetic variation between them ($\\checkmark$). As genetic differences accumulate, **reproductive isolating mechanisms** evolve ($\\checkmark$).  If the populations later come into **secondary contact**, they **cannot interbreed** successfully to produce fertile offspring, so a new species forms ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ occurs without geographic isolation **OR** populations remain in the same area [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ reproductive isolation develops within a single population [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ gene flow between subgroups is reduced **OR** prevented [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ polyploidy creates immediate reproductive isolation [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ ecological niche differentiation occurs so subgroups use different resources **OR** microhabitats [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ disruptive selection favours extreme phenotypes **OR** selects against intermediates [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ assortative mating occurs so individuals preferentially mate with similar individuals [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ sexual selection causes divergence in mate preferences **OR** mating signals [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ temporal isolation occurs due to different breeding **OR** flowering times in the same area [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ behavioural isolation occurs due to different courtship behaviours **OR** mating calls [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ a host shift occurs so individuals feed **OR** mate on different host species [1 mark]\n$\\ce{ -> }$ chromosomal rearrangements reduce successful interbreeding between subgroups [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nSympatric speciation occurs when new species form in the **same geographic area** ($\\checkmark$). Reproductive isolation evolves within a population, so **gene flow is reduced** between subgroups ($\\checkmark$). This can occur through **polyploidy**, especially in plants, causing immediate reproductive isolation ($\\checkmark$). It can also occur through **sexual selection** or **assortative mating**, where differences in mate choice reduce interbreeding and lead to divergence ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    ***Asexual reproduction:***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chromosome number of daughter species is an odd number [1 mark]\n\n    ***Sexual reproduction:***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the hybridisation of closely related species [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ new hybrid species has one set of chromosomes from each parent species     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ self-fertilisation **OR** autogamy occurs, and chromosome number is doubled **OR** halved    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fusion of two diploid gametes **OR** another valid example of fertilisation leading to a change in chromosome copy number    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Both asexual and sexual reproduction:***\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reproductive isolation **OR** new species are sometimes brought about by differences in chromosome copy number        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ abnormal meiotic division in parent species [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ daughter species is reproductively isolated from the parent species     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ daughter species reproduces asexually because meiosis is no longer possible     [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Both sexually and asexually reproducing species can be produced when **two closely related species hybridise** ($\\checkmark$) to produce a daughter species that can carry out mitosis and is **reproductively isolated from the parent species** ($\\checkmark$).  Sometimes reproductive isolation in both sexually and asexually reproducing species is brought about by a **change in chromosome copy number** ($\\checkmark$).  This is often due to **abnormal meiotic divisions** ($\\checkmark$).  When an **odd number of chromosomes** is present ($\\checkmark$) in the daughter species, for example, 3n, **meiosis is impossible** ($\\checkmark$), and the daughter species can only reproduce asexually through mitosis.  If the daughter species **reproduces through sexual reproduction, the chromosome copy number must be even** ($\\checkmark$) so that **meiosis is possible** ($\\checkmark$).  Sometimes a single, **self-fertilizing species may undergo abnormal meiotic divisions** ($\\checkmark$), which result in the production of a **new species that has half** ($\\checkmark$) or **double** ($\\checkmark$) the number of chromosomes and thus becomes reproductively isolated."},{"ref":"BI1002","marks":"17","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1002\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Adaptive radiation results in a single ancestral species evolving into multiple, closely related new species which may live in close proximity to each other.  Outline 3 mechanisms by which closely related species living in close proximity may be reproductively isolated.    [4 marks]\n\n2. Outline the process by which two species may be formed from an ancestral population through the process of allopatric speciation.                        [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain what polyploidy is and how it can lead to speciation.        [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ behavioural isolation **OR** failure to recognise members of different species as possible mates        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ temporal isolation **OR** reproduction occurs at different times [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ postzygotic barriers        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ habitat isolation, whereby a subsection of one population occupies a different ecological niche [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hybrid offspring are sterile         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mechanical isolation **OR** different morphological structures evolve, resulting in physical incompatibility            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ two closely related species may have different numbers of chromosomes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ incompatibility of gametes **OR** inability of sperm to fertilise egg [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Species living close to one another may be **reproductively isolated** due to **behavioural isolation** ($\\checkmark$), which is a **failure** to **recognise** potential **mates**.  **Two species** may also be **reproductively isolated** by **postzygotic barriers** ($\\checkmark$). For example, **differences in chromosome number** between closely related species ($\\checkmark$) may result in **hybrid offspring** that are **sterile** because their chromosomes cannot pair correctly during meiosis ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ geographic isolation **OR** ancestral population is separated into two areas **OR** any named example, e.g.  mountain range, isthmus [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ different random mutations accrue in the two different populations **OR** different alleles are produced in the two different populations    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ different phenotypes are produced in the two different populations    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ different selection pressures in two different areas    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ different traits selected for in two different areas  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic drift            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reproductive isolation **OR** behavioural isolation **OR** temporal isolation **OR** post-zygotic barriers        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gene flow cannot occur        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ changes occur over many generations    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The ancestral population could have been divided into **two populations** by the formation of a **mountain range** ($\\checkmark$), which means **gene flow** between the two populations **does not occur** ($\\checkmark$).  **Random mutations** then build up ($\\checkmark$) between the two populations leading to **new phenotypes** ($\\checkmark$).  **Selection** pressures may **differ** ($\\checkmark$) between the two populations which means **different phenotypes** would be selected for in the **two different environments**.  Eventually, the two different populations may become **reproductively isolated** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic condition in which an organism has more than two complete sets of chromosomes in its cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ more common in plants            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ formation of new species is through two main mechanisms: autopolyploidy and allopolyploidy  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ autopolyploidy occurs due to failure of chromosomes to separate during meiosis        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ autopolyploidy occurs within one species    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reproductive isolation of polyploid individuals from diploid population    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reduced **OR** cessation of gene flow in population due to polyploids being incompatible with diploids                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polyploid population begins to evolve independently from the diploid population **OR** genetic divergence occurs    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allopolyploidy occurs when two different species interbreed and produce hybrid offspring [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ can result in an entire extra set of chromosomes from each parent species    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allopolyploid individuals often reproduce asexually due to the mismatched chromosomes from different species        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ asexual reproduction acts as a reproductive barrier between the allopolyploid and its parent species [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reduced gene flow allows for independent evolution **OR** genetic divergence    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ over time, the allopolyploid population may accumulate genetic differences and adapt to specific ecological niches, leading to the formation of a new species        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polyploidy can result in odd ploidy number **OR** 3n, 5n, etc. in daughter species        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ individuals with odd ploidy number cannot undergo meiosis which acts as a reproductive barrier     [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Polyploidy is the condition of having **more** than **two sets** of **chromosomes** ($\\checkmark$). **Autopolyploidy** is due to the failure of chromosomes to **separate** during **meiosis** ($\\checkmark$).  Often, autopolyploid individuals are **reproductively isolated** ($\\checkmark$) from the parent species due to their **gametes** being **incompatible** with the diploid parent’s gametes.\n\n    **Allopolyploid** species are produced when two different **species hybridise** ($\\checkmark$) resulting in a daughter species with an **extra set of chromosomes** from each parent species ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    In both **allopolyploids and autopolyploids** ($\\checkmark$), occasionally individuals are produced with **odd ploidy numbers** (ex: 3n, 5n) ($\\checkmark$).  These individuals will generally be **unable** to undergo **meiosis** ($\\checkmark$), will **reproduce asexually** and thus be **reproductively isolated** from their parent species ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    Once two populations are reproductively isolated there is **no gene flow** ($\\checkmark$) and a new species is produced."},{"ref":"BI1154","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1154\n\nWhich pair of structures would be considered analogous?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI1154.png\" alt=\"BI1154\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAnalogous structures refer to structures found in different species which serve a similar function, but are not the result of common ancestry. Sharks are cartilaginous fish, and their fins are modified pectoral fins made of cartilage. Whales are mammals, and their fins are modified forelimbs, which are homologous to the forelimbs of land-dwelling mammals. Whale and shark fins serve the same purpose, are similar in structure, and are the product of convergent evolution.\n\nThe structures in **Choices A** and **B** share similar functions, but they also share a common ancestry, making them homologous, not analogous. **Choice C** cannot be correct as they share a common ancestry but are quite different in terms of structure and function, also making them homologous."},{"ref":"BI1199","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1199\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nBelow is a diagram that outlines the bone structure of four different animal species. These structures all evolved from a common ancestor with a pentadactyl (5-digit) limb.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI1199-SL.png\" alt=\"BI1199-SL\">\n[Source: Adapted from Волков Владислав Петрович.  2011, January, 1. *Homology*.  Retrieved December 9, 2022 from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Homology_(biology)#/media/File: Homology_vertebrates-en.svg. CC BY-SA 4.0]\n\n1. State the term used to describe anatomical features found in different species that share a common ancestor.  [1 mark]\n\n2. Bones are living tissues that require nutrients that are transported in the blood.  Outline how the structure of capillaries makes them well-adapted for the exchange of nutrients.  [2 marks]\n\n3. Explain how adaptive radiation brought about the differences between the limbs of the whale and the bird.  [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous  structures        [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Homologous structures** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ capillary walls are one cell thick    [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ results in short diffusion distance for substances **OR** correctly named substance being exchanged        [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ have pores which increase permeability [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ are highly branched which increases the surface area available for the exchange of substances    [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ narrow diameter **OR** lumen which reduces diffusion distance     [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Capillaries have a **narrow diameter**, and their **walls** are **one cell thick** ($\\checkmark$).  Both of these features **reduce the distance** substances being exchanged need to **diffuse** ($\\checkmark$) ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ whales and birds share a common ancestor     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ shared common ancestor had a limb similar to both whales and birds **OR** had pentadactyl limb                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ change in environment **OR** change in circumstances causes different selection pressures    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ divergent evolution **OR** ancestral species begins to diversify into different lines        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reproductive isolation **OR** no gene flow between ancestors of birds and whales    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ random mutation resulted in new phenotypes **OR** new alleles **OR** bone structures        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ populations can no longer interbreed, so form new species **OR** speciation occurs        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ new species develop traits specialised to their environment **OR** ecological niche **OR** different selection pressures selected for different limb structures **OR** aerial locomotion **OR** description of selection pressure favouring wing structures **AND** aquatic life **OR** description of selection pressure favoured flipper structures    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ differences in limb structure between whales and birds developed over long time periods    [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Whales and birds **share a common ancestor** ($\\checkmark$), and as a result, they have **inherited many of the same genes** from this common ancestor, which **determine limb structure** ($\\checkmark$).  The limbs are different now due to **reproductive isolation** ($\\checkmark$), **long periods of time** ($\\checkmark$), and the fact that, for the most part, **ancestors** of whales and birds lived in **different environments**, which means **different selection pressures** ($\\checkmark$) acted on the ancestors of whales and birds favouring different traits."},{"ref":"BI1232","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1232\n\nWhich of the following statement concerning analogous structures is/are correct?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | They are formed through convergent evolution |\n    | II. | They perform the same or similar functions in different organisms |\n    | III. | They evolve from a common ancestor |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAnalogous structures refer to structures found in different species which serve a similar function (**Statement II**) but are not the result of common ancestry, they are formed through convergent evolution (**Statement I**), which is when different organisms evolve similar structures but have not arisen from a common ancestor. **Choice B**  is correct.\n\nAnalogous structures do not evolve from a common ancestor (**Statement III**); **Choices C** and **D**  are incorrect. **Choice A**  is incorrect because it only involves **statement II**."},{"ref":"BI1291","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1291\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe pale and dark forms of the peppered moth (Biston betularia) are a classic example of natural selection. The graph shows the changes in the population of pale (mostly white) and dark (mostly black) peppered moths (*Biston betularia*) during and post the industrial revolution.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI1291.png\" alt=\"BI1291\">\n\nThe image shows the phenotype of pale and dark moths on white tree bark.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI1291b.png\" alt=\"BI1291b\">\n[Source: Adapted from Bedin, T. (2020, May 26). *Tilpasning og evolusjon - natural science (PB) - NDLA. ndla.no*. Retrieved October 02, 2023, from https://ndla.no/en/subject:1:bb834c76-d1e4-46c4-8c0a-8f978bd2c956/topic:6:f604b380-5cdb-4227-885e-bc9b57cb3237/topic:3:aa019f6a-a569-4491-b0d2-c8d2df21733f/resource:09fc6c1a-eaa1-4901-b2ae-ee5e7ce1900c. Copyright free]\n\n1. Distinguish between the changes in the numbers of pale and dark moths shown in the graph. [2 marks]\n\n2.\n 1. Outline how the trends in the graph can be explained by natural selection. [3 marks]\n\n 2. Suggest a technique a group of students could use to compare the population sizes of peppered moths between a deciduous (broadleaved) and a coniferous (evergreen) wood. [1 mark]\n\n3. Calculate the percentage increase in dark-coloured moths between 1850 and 1900. [1 mark]\n\n4. Draw a line on the graph, predicting how the number of pale moths would have changed, if the industrial revolution did not happen. [1 mark]\n\n5. In addition to collecting data such as that shown in the graph, scientists rely on various forms of evidence to explore the concept of evolution. State two other forms of evidence that supports the theory of evolution. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ between 1800 and 1850 **OR** around the middle of the Industrial Revolution, the number of pale moths remained high **OR** between 95 and 100% **WHEREAS** the number of dark moths remained low **OR** between 0 and 5% [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ from 1850, the number of pale moths began to rapidly decrease **WHEREAS** the number of dark moths rapidly increased [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ around 1870 **OR** just before 1875, the number of dark moths surpasses the number of pale moths [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ from 1900, the number of pale moths was low **OR** less than 5% **WHEREAS** the number of dark moths was high **OR** around 95% [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Between **1800 and 1850**, the number of **pale** moths was between **95 and 100%**, whereas the number of **dark** moths was between **0 and 5%** ($\\checkmark$). However, **from 1850** the number of **pale** moths **decreased** rapidly, whereas the number of **dark** moths **increased** rapidly ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1. Any **three** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ natural selection explains how individuals with advantageous traits are more likely to survive to reproduce [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ before the Industrial Revolution, lichens **OR** mosses **OR** bark on trees **OR** brickwork were white, so the pale moths were able to camouflage from predators [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ increased rates of pollution **OR** soot during the Industrial Revolution caused white lichen and mosses on lichen **OR** moss **OR** bark on trees **OR** brickwork to turn black [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ pale moths were more visible to predators, so their numbers decreased [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ dark moths were able to camouflage from predators, so were better able to survive and breed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the dark moths gained a selective advantage over the pale moths **OR** were selected for **OR** are under directional selection **OR** pale moths faced intense selection pressure due to predation [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Before the Industrial Revolution, **lichen and mosses** on trees were **white**, so the pale moths were able to **camouflage from predators** ($\\checkmark$). However, **post-Industrial Revolution**, there were increased rates of **pollution** which caused trees to turn **black** ($\\checkmark$). This meant that the dark moths were able to **camouflage** from predators, so were better able to **survive and breed** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 2.\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ capture-mark-release-recapture [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n One technique that students could use is the **mark-release-recapture** of **peppered** moths ($\\checkmark$) in coniferous and deciduous woods.\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 1800 % [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI1291c.png\" alt=\"BI1291c\">\n\n Percentage increase = ((final value - initial value) ÷ initial value) $\\times$ 100% \\\n Percentage increase = ((95 - 5) ÷ 5 ) $\\times$ 100% \\\n Percentage increase = **1800%** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n4. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ line should continue the same trend from before 1850 to 1900 [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI1291d.png\" alt=\"BI1291d\">\n\n5. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA sequence **OR** RNA sequencing [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ amino acid sequence of proteins [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ selective breeding **OR** artificial selection [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous structures **OR** comparative anatomy **OR** vestigial structures [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ embryonic development [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the fossil record [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ geographic distribution of species [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **fossil record** ($\\checkmark$) and **DNA sequencing** ($\\checkmark$) provide evidence for the theory of evolution."},{"ref":"BI1316","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1316\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline three sources which provide evidence for evolution.    [3 marks]\n\n2. Describe how both bats and butterflies have wings.        [5 marks]\n\n3. Explain the process of speciation with at least one named example.     [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA sequence **OR** RNA sequence    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ amino acid sequence of proteins     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ selective breeding **OR** artificial selection    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ homologous structures **OR** comparative anatomy **OR** vestigial structures     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ embryonic development    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the fossil record        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ geographic distribution of species    [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Evidence for evolution can come from **DNA sequences** ($\\checkmark$), **amino acid sequences** ($\\checkmark$), or **homologous structures** ($\\checkmark$) such as the pentadactyl limb.\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ bat wings and butterfly wings  do not share a common ancestor        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ bats share common ancestry with other mammals, whereas butterflies share common ancestry with other insects **OR** bats are mammals **OR** Mammalia, whereas butterflies are insects **OR** Arthropoda     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ wings evolved through convergent evolution     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ convergent evolution is the process where unrelated organisms evolve similar characteristics **OR** traits to perform similar functions     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ wings in bats and butterflies are an example of analogous structures         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ analogous structures share a similar function but are not the result of a common ancestor                         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ wings evolved in response to similar environmental pressures **OR** ecological niches **OR** as a result of natural selection favouring flight     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ wings in bats are modified forelimbs **OR** are made of skin and bone **WHEREAS** butterfly wings are not **OR** are made of chitin         [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Bats and butterflies do not share a recent **common ancestor** ($\\checkmark$), but both evolved wings due to **convergent evolution** ($\\checkmark$). Convergent evolution is the process by which unrelated organisms can **evolve similar traits** due to similar necessities, such as the need to fly ($\\checkmark$). This means that wings in bats and butterflies are an example of **analogous structures** ($\\checkmark$) because they share a **similar function** but do not result from a common ancestor ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ speciation is the process by which a population is split and evolves differently over time to form distinct species [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ diversity exists within a population **OR** genetic variation exists within a population      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ members of a population become reproductively isolated                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sympatric speciation occurs when new species evolve within the same geographic area       [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allopatric speciation occurs when new species form in separate geographic areas due to geographical isolation            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ reproductive isolation can be due to geographical **OR** physical **OR** behavioural **OR** temporal barriers [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ each population is exposed to different environmental conditions **OR** selective pressures [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ each population evolves differently over time **OR** different adaptations evolve over time **OR** mutations affect each group differently **OR** differential selection **OR** genetic drift affects populations differently    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ these differences act as barriers to gene flow     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ over time, the groups evolve differently and can no longer interbreed, so new species have been formed     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ bonobos and chimpanzees **OR** any other suitable example of speciation     [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Speciation is the process by which a population becomes split, and each group evolves differently over time, leading to the formation of **new species** ($\\checkmark$). This is possible because **variation** exists within a population ($\\checkmark$). When members of a population become **reproductively isolated** ($\\checkmark$) by a geographical barrier like a river ($\\checkmark$), both groups are exposed to different environmental conditions or **selective pressures** ($\\checkmark$). Over time, the two groups evolve differently as different mutations affect both groups, this is referred to as **differential selection** ($\\checkmark$). These differences between the populations act as **barriers to gene flow** ($\\checkmark$). Over time, the groups evolve so differently that they can no longer **interbreed** and are two distinct species ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1377","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1377\n\nWhich characteristic(s) is/are observed in both natural selection and selective breeding?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Favourable traits are selected|\n    | II. | Increase in variation |\n    | III. | Change is driven by the environment |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is correct because in both selective breeding and natural selection, favourable characteristics are “selected” by either the environment (natural selection) or humans (selective breeding). **Statement II** is incorrect because natural selection and selective breeding don’t increase variation, it influences the proportion of existing variants. **Statement III** is incorrect because selective breeding is not driven by the environment, it is driven by humans. **Choice A** is, therefore, correct.\n\n**Choices B**, **C** and **D** are incorrect as they include **Statement II** and/or **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI1407","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1407\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe *Allium* genus contains leeks, garlics and chives. The images below show two members of the *Allium* genus that are native to Lithuania and their associated chromosome numbers. Polyploidy events have occurred to produce *Allium oleraeceum*.\n\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI1407A.png\" alt=\"BI1407A\"> | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI1407B.png\" alt=\"BI1407B\"> |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| *Allium angulosum* | *Allium oleraeceum* |\n| 2n = 16 | 4n = 32 |\n\n[Source: Adapted from Lefnaer, S. (2015, July 26). *Category: allium angulosum*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved January 8, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Allium_angulosum_sl3.jpg. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n[Source: Adapted from Leidus, I. (2013, July 7). *File: allium oleraceum - rohulauk.jpg* - wikimedia commons. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved January 8, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:  Allium_oleraceum_-_rohulauk.jpg. Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n1. Suggest how polyploidy may occur in plants.    [1 mark]\n\n2. Outline how polyploidy in a species can lead to speciation.        [2 marks]\n\n3. With reference to plant hormones, explain the directional growth observed in the image of *Allium oleraceum*.        [3 marks]\n\n4. Plants within the *Allium* genus often have bulbs which grow underground. Outline how bulbs can be considered sinks **and** sources in the process of translocation.        [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **one**  of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hybridisation events between closely related plant species **OR** gametes of two closely related plant species fuse        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ extra set of chromosomes formed during DNA replication stage in interphase **OR** cell cycle [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a diploid gamete fuses with a haploid gamete **OR** two diploid gametes fuse together [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ failed spindle formation **OR** non-disjunction **OR** failed separation of chromosomes **IN** meiosis leading to gametes with extra set of chromosomes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chromosomal duplications in cells involved in vegetative **OR** asexual reproduction [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Polyploidy can occur in plants if there are **hybridisation** events between **closely related plant** species ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms with a different number of chromosomes **OR** new tetraploid and original diploid cannot breed together to produce fertile offspring    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ results in reproductive isolation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polyploid plants may be larger **OR** more fertile **OR** better adapted than original populations allowing them to exploit different ecological niches leading to geographical isolation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ once isolated polyploid population diverges genetically from original populations    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The new polyploid organism has a different number of chromosomes than the original plant and so they **cannot breed** together to produce **fertile offspring** ($\\checkmark$). This can result in **reproductive isolation** ($\\checkmark$), which could lead to the formation of a new species.\n\n3. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plant stems **OR** *Allium oleraceum* stems respond to light by growing towards it **OR** plant stems are positively phototropic            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ auxin is produced by actively dividing cells in the tip of the stem **OR** in the meristem     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light causes auxin to accumulate on the shaded side of the stem     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ auxin efflux pumps bring about increased concentration on shaded side    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ this causes cells on the shaded side to elongate                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ auxin causes protons to be pumped from cytoplasm of cells into cell wall lowering pH    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lowering of pH loosens linkages between cellulose fibres so when water enters cell wall stretches and cell elongates    [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    *Allium oleraceum* has a **positive phototropic** response to light ($\\checkmark$).  **Auxin** is released from the **actively dividing cells** of the **meristem** ($\\checkmark$). Light stimulates auxin to **accumulate** on the **shaded** side of the **stem** ($\\checkmark$), so that **cells** on the **shaded side elongate** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **two**  of the following:\\\n    *Answer must include statements supporting bulbs being a sink and supporting bulbs being a source to gain full marks*\n\n    **Bulbs are a source because…**\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when the plant is not actively photosynthesising, it uses sucrose **OR** starch **OR** carbohydrate **OR** nutrients **OR** amino acids stored in bulb        [1 mark]\n\n    **Bulbs are a sink because…**\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ when the plant is actively photosynthesising it will provide carbohydrates **OR** glucose **OR** sucrose **OR** starch, for storage in **OR** growth of, bulb    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    At the **beginning** of the **growing** season, the bulb is a **source** because the plant is **not** actively **photosynthesising**, so it needs to use the **sucrose and amino acids stored in the bulbs** for **growth** ($\\checkmark$).   However, during the **middle** of the growing season, when the plant is **actively photosynthesising**, **sucrose** is **transported** from the **leaves to the bulb** to **store** the **excess carbohydrate** and so the bulb becomes a **sink** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1577","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1577\n\nWhich of the following pairs of limbs are homologous?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A. | | $\\hspace{2cm}$ | |B.|\n| |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI1577A.png\" alt=\"BI1577A\"> | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI1577B.png\" alt=\"BI1577B\">|\n|C. | | | |D. |\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI1577C.png\" alt=\"BI1577C\"> | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI1577D.png\" alt=\"BI1577D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because the horse and sea lion limbs have the same bones in the same order. This is the result of common ancestry between the two species, making these structures homologous despite their different functions.\n\n**Choices A, B,** and **C** are incorrect because these are examples of analogous structures as a result of convergent evolution. Analogous structures share a common function but do not share a common ancestry. A further clue is that horse and sea lion are both mammals, whereas the other pairs of animals belong to different taxonomic groups (**Choice A** = mammal, insect; **Choice B** = mammal, crustacean; **Choice C**= mollusc, echinoderm)."},{"ref":"BI2377","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2377\n\nWhich of the following is **NOT** a true statement of allopolyploidy in plants?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ It results in an increased chance of hybrid vigour.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Plant breeders can select varieties that are best suited to local conditions.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ It results in the duplication of chromosomes within a single species.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ It results in an Increased ability of plants to colonise new habitats.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAllopolyploidy arises from hybridisation between two different species, followed by chromosome doubling. The key point is that it introduces a new set of chromosomes from a different species rather than simply doubling an existing diploid genome.\n\nIn contrast, autopolyploidy occurs within a single species and results in an exact doubling (or higher multiple) of its own chromosome set.  **Choice C** is, therefore, the correct answer.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because hybrid vigour is often a direct result of allopolyploidy. New alleles are introduced into a species, giving an immediate selective advantage to that plant species.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because allopolyploidy allows new varieties that are well adapted to different local abiotic and biotic conditions. This characteristic can explain the widespread global cultivation of wheat (*Triticum aestivum*) as a staple crop.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the new alleles brought in from new species allow a species to compete in a territory or habitat that it had not otherwise been successful in, allowing an invasive species to outcompete the native species in many cases."},{"ref":"BI2803","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2803\n\nWhich statement is true for both Lamarckism and epigenetic inheritance?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Both involve changes in the DNA sequence that are inherited and lead to evolution.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Both involve inherited changes caused by the environment, but the effect of epigenetics may only last a few generations, so it is not a type of evolution.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Both operate by the use and disuse of traits, leading to evolutionary change.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Only epigenetics leads to changes in the DNA sequence and, therefore, evolution.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because both Lamarckism and epigenetic inheritance propose that environmental factors can cause inherited changes, while Lamarckism suggested these changes were permanent and led to evolution through traits acquired during an organism's lifetime. Epigenetic changes involve reversible chemical modifications to DNA or histone proteins that affect gene expression without altering the DNA sequence. These epigenetic changes can be passed to offspring, but often only persist for a few generations and typically do not lead to long-term evolutionary change.\\\n**Choice A** is incorrect because neither Lamarckism nor epigenetic inheritance involves changes to the DNA sequence itself. Epigenetics modifies gene expression through chemical tags, while Lamarckism assumed physical or behavioural changes were inherited.\\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because only Lamarckism proposed that the use or disuse of traits directly causes evolutionary change. Epigenetic inheritance does not involve the use or disuse of structures but rather chemical changes to gene regulation influenced by environmental factors. Furthermore, epigenetics does not lead to evolution.\\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because epigenetic inheritance does not involve changes to the DNA sequence itself and therefore does not cause long-term evolutionary change."},{"ref":"BI2804","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2804\n\nThe table below shows the percentage of the amino acid sequences of cytochrome c shared between a human and four other species.\n\n| Species Pair | Cytochrome c sequence shared (%) |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| Human–chimpanzee | 99 |\n| Human–mouse | 87 |\n| Human–chicken | 75 |\n| Human–zebrafish | 50 |\n\nWhich conclusion is best supported by these data?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ A human and a chimpanzee shared a common ancestor more recently than a human and a mouse.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cytochrome c has evolved faster in the chicken lineage than in the mouse lineage.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The human–zebrafish divergence occurred most recently.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The molecular clock runs at different rates in mammals and fish.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because there is a higher percentage similarity for the cytochrome c amino acid sequence between the human and chimpanzee (99%) compared to the human and mouse (87%). This indicates less time for mutations to accumulate, meaning humans and chimpanzees shared a common ancestor more recently than humans and mice.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because although the cytochrome c percentage similarity is lower in chickens (75%) than in mice (87%), this alone does not confirm that cytochrome c evolved faster in the chicken lineage. To determine evolutionary rates, both sequence similarity and known divergence times are needed.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because the lowest sequence similarity (50%) between human and zebrafish suggests the longest time since divergence, not the most recent.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because differences in sequence similarity alone cannot demonstrate variation in molecular clock rates. The molecular clock is a concept based on the idea that mutations accumulate at a relatively constant rate over time in most species. To conclude rate differences, both the time since divergence and the number of accrued changes must both be known."},{"ref":"BI2805","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2805\n\nWhich breeding practice most directly illustrates artificial selection of a polygenic trait?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Allowing the organisms with traits that fit the environment best to pass them on to the next generation.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Breeding plants which are susceptible to a specific bacteria with those that contain the resistance gene.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Crossing only the tallest wheat plants each generation for an increase in yield.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Introducing wild genes to increase genetic diversity in a breed.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because artificial selection on a polygenic trait involves humans selecting individuals with desirable values for a trait that shows continuous variation, such as plant height, which is typically controlled by multiple genes. By repeatedly crossing the tallest wheat plants, breeders shift the population’s average height upwards over successive generations.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it describes natural selection, where environmental pressures—not human choice—determine which traits are passed on. In this scenario, humans are letting the environment dictate which individuals breed more successfully, so it is not an example of artificial selection.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because crossing a disease-resistant plant with those that are susceptible to the disease is an example of artificial selection, but not on a polygenic trait, as it only involves one gene.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because introducing wild genes to increase genetic diversity adds variation to the gene pool, but does not involve selecting for a specific trait."},{"ref":"BI2806","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2806\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nChimpanzees (*Pan troglodytes*) and bonobos (*Pan paniscus*) diverged from a common ancestor around 1.25 million years ago. This event is thought to have been influenced by the formation of the Congo River.\n\nThe diagram shows the ranges of chimpanzees and bonobos.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Evolution%20%26%20Speciation/images/BI2806.png\" alt=\"BI2806\">\n[Source: Adapted from Guy, Cp. (2016, May 24). *File: Bonobo vs chimp.png* - Wikimedia Commons. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved January 12 , 2024 from URL https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Bonobo_vs_chimp.png. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n1. Chimpanzees and bonobos are two different species that share a recent common ancestor. State **three** similarities or differences between them that would result from this shared ancestry and subsequent divergence.    [3 marks]\n\n2.\n    1. Name and explain which type of speciation is likely to have occurred between chimpanzees and bonobos. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Describe the role of differential selection in the speciation event between chimpanzees and bonobos.                [2 marks]\n\n3.\n    1. Explain how the process of speciation affects biodiversity.                 [1 mark]\n\n    2. Suggest why gradual evolutionary change within a single species is not considered speciation in the absence of reproductive isolation.        [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following *(at least **one** from each section)*:\n\n    ***Similarities:***\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ similar DNA base sequences **OR** similar mitochondrial DNA sequences             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ similar amino acid sequences **OR** cytochrome c **OR** named example         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ similar features **OR** morphology **OR** homologous structures **OR** named example     [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences:***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ differences in some gene sequences **OR** proteins due to gene mutations since divergence  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ differences in specific behaviours **OR** social structures      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ differences in morphology due to adaptations since divergence          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ unable to breed and produce fertile offspring         [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Chimpanzees and bonobos have **similar DNA base sequences** ($\\checkmark$) and **amino acid sequences** in **proteins** ($\\checkmark$) because they share a recent common ancestor. However, they are now **unable to breed and produce fertile offspring** ($\\checkmark$), which is why they are not the same species.\n\n2.\n    1. any **two** of the following:\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ allopatric speciation          [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ a geographical barrier **OR** a river separates the two populations, preventing gene flow between them         [1 mark]\n\n        [2 marks]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n        It is likely to be **allopatric** speciation ($\\checkmark$) because the **Congo River separates** the two populations, **preventing gene flow** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    2. any **two** of the following:\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ different environmental conditions **OR** selection pressures in the separated populations favoured different phenotypes         [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ natural selection acted differently on each population, increasing the frequency of different alleles in each group             [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ over time, genetic differences accumulated        [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ this led to reproductive isolation as the populations became too genetically distinct to interbreed     [1 mark]\n\n        [2 marks]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n        DIfferential selection helped lead to speciation because there were **different environmental conditions** ($\\checkmark$) in the different places, This meant **different alleles** were selected and **increased in frequency** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n    1.\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ speciation increases biodiversity **BECAUSE** it raises the total number of species on Earth    [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ Speciation leads to two species where there was previously one, so it will **increase** the total **number of species**, which **increases biodiversity** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    2. any **two** of the following:\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ reproductive isolation is needed to prevent interbreeding **OR** to separate gene pools    [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ without this, individuals can still mate and produce fertile offspring **OR** alleles are shared within the population                    [1 mark]\n\n        $\\ce{ -> }$ gradual changes within a species without reproductive isolation represent variation within a species **NOT** the formation of a new species        [1 mark]\n\n        [2 marks]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n        Reproductive isolation is needed to **prevent interbreeding** ($\\checkmark$). If individuals can still interbreed, they can **share alleles across the gene pool** ($\\checkmark$). This means there is no accumulation of differences, which leads to speciation."},{"ref":"BI2807","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2807\n\nWhich of the following statements accurately describes characteristics of homologous structures?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Homologous structures are defined as functionally similar anatomical features that arise through divergent evolution. |\n    | II. | Although bats and dolphins exhibit distinct external morphology, their forelimbs evolved from a common ancestor. |\n    | III. | Bird wings and insect wings originate from the same embryonic tissues and are therefore homologous. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWhile homologous structures do arise via divergent evolution, they are not defined by functional similarity. In fact, their function may differ considerably, for example, the forelimbs of bats for flight versus the flippers of dolphins for swimming. Homology is established based on shared structural origin, not shared function; **Statement I** is false.\\\nHomologous structures are anatomical features found in different species that share a common evolutionary origin, even if they have diverged in form or function. Both bats and dolphins have a pentadactyl limb structure in their forelimbs, despite a differing external appearance and function. This reflects divergent evolution from a shared ancestor; **Statement II** is true.\\\nBat wings and insect wings are analogous structures. Although they perform a similar function (flight), they evolved independently in different evolutionary lineages and do not share embryological origin. Bat wings derive from the vertebrate limb, while insect wings arise from outgrowths of the arthropod exoskeleton. **Statement III** is false.\n\n**Choice B** is the correct answer because it only includes the one true **Statement II**.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are incorrect as they include one or more of the false **Statements I** and **III**."}]},{"name":"Nucleic Acids","questions":[{"ref":"BI0038","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0038\n\nThe diagram shows the structure of a DNA fragment.\n\nWhich letter points to the bonds that easily break and reform?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI0038.png\" alt=\"BI0038\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from User: Madprime. (2010, March). *File: DNA chemical structure jp.svg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved May 15, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: DNA_chemical_structure_jp.svg. Copyright under CC-SA 3.0]","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe bonds between the nitrogenous bases holding the two strands together are hydrogen bonds. Hydrogen bonds typically break and reform easily; therefore  **Choice B** is the correct answer to the question.\n\nThe nitrogenous bases consist of single and double covalent bonds (**Choice A**) between nitrogen and carbon atoms. Deoxyribose sugars have single covalent bonds (**Choice C**) between the carbon atoms forming the pentose sugar. There are covalent bonds between the phosphate and sugar and between the phosphate and base (**Choice D**). Covalent bonds do not typically break and reform easily."},{"ref":"BI0039","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0039\n\nThe diagram below shows transcription.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI0039.png\" alt=\"BI0039\">\n\nWhat do structures X and Y have in common?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Both are made up of phosphate groups, ribose sugar and nitrogenous bases.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Both contain hydrogen bonds between adenine and thymine.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Both contain sugar-phosphate bonds.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Both have six-carbon monosaccharides in their structure.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nStructure X is DNA, and structure Y is RNA. Both structures are made up of phosphate groups, sugars and nitrogenous bases. Both structures contain sugar-phosphate bonds, a type of covalent bond, between phosphate groups and sugars. **Choice C** is the correct answer.\n\nThe sugar in DNA is deoxyribose and the sugar in RNA is ribose (**Choice A**). The sugar molecule in both DNA and RNA is a pentose sugar which means it has five carbon atoms, not six (**Choice D**).\n\nRNA does not contain thymine (**Choice B**) and it is single-stranded."},{"ref":"BI0042","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0042\n\nWhich of the following are correct percentages of bases in a sample of DNA?\n\n| | Adenine | Cytosine | Guanine | Thymine |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 10% | 25% | 30% | 35% |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 10% | 15% | 15% | 10% |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 20% | 30% | 30% | 20% |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | 20% | 20% | 30% | 30% |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDNA molecules consist of 4 nitrogenous bases: adenine (A), cytosine (C), guanine (G) and thymine (T). Chargaff's rule states that A will pair with T and C will pair with G. The percentages of all 4 bases should add up to 100% because there are no other bases present.\n\n**Choices A and D** are incorrect because the amount of A is not equal to the amount of T, and the amount of C is not equal to the amount of G.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the sum of all percentages does not add up to 100%."},{"ref":"BI0043","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0043\n\nWhich statements are characteristics of a DNA molecule?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | The two strands are identical. |\n  | II. | The two strands are antiparallel to one another. |\n  | III. | The sugar-phosphate backbone is held together by covalent bonds. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA DNA molecule is made up of two antiparallel strands of nucleotides. Antiparallel means that both strands are geometrically parallel but oriented in opposite directions (**Option II**). One of the strands is in the direction of 5' to 3', whereas the other strand is in the direction of 3' to 5'. The backbones, made of alternating sugar and phosphate groups, are held together by covalent sugar-phosphate bonds (**Option III**). Both of these strands are joined together by the hydrogen bonds between the complementary base pairs.\n\nDNA strands are not identical (**Option I**) because identical would mean that they have the same nucleotide arrangement, instead they are complementary to each other."},{"ref":"BI0045","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0045\n\nWhat distinguishes uracil from thymine?\n\n| | Uracil | Thymine |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Joined to a pentose sugar | Joined to a hexose sugar |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Found in RNA | Found in DNA |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Two-ring structure  | Single-ring structure |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Complementary to cytosine | Complementary to adenine |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nUracil and thymine are nitrogenous bases found in two types of nucleic acids: DNA and RNA.\n\nUracil is found in RNA, whereas thymine is found in DNA. (**Choice B**)\n\nIn RNA, Uracil is not complementary to cystine, it is complementary with adenine, and thymine is also complementary to adenine\n (**Choice D**).\n\nIn both DNA and RNA, these nitrogenous bases are joined to a five-carbon sugar, also called a pentose sugar which has a ring structure, so **Choice A** is incorrect.\n\nBoth uracil and thymine are pyrimidines which are single-ring structures (**Choice C**). Adenine and guanine are purines which consist of two rings."},{"ref":"BI0047","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0047\n\nWhich of the following processes is most directly involved in the formation of nucleic acids such as DNA?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrolysis\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Condensation\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Catabolism\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Translation","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nNucleotides are linked together by condensation reactions to form molecules of DNA (**Choice B**). The formation of DNA requires the removal of water for the joining of smaller molecules to form a larger molecule.\n\nThe joining of nucleotides together can also be classified as an anabolic reaction but not as a catabolic reaction (**Choice C**). Catabolism is the breaking down of a large molecule into a smaller molecule. An example of catabolism is hydrolysis which is a reaction that breaks down a large molecule into a smaller molecule by adding water (**Choice A**).\n\nTranslation is a process which follows the transcription of DNA (**Choice D**). It is where mRNA is used as a template by ribosomes to synthesize polypeptides."},{"ref":"BI0049","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0049\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe image below shows a short fragment of the DNA molecule.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI0049.png\" alt=\"BI0049\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from User: Madprime. (2010, March). *File: DNA chemical structure jp.svg.* Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved May 15, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: DNA_chemical_structure_jp.svg. Copyright under CC-SA 3.0]\n\n1. Define the term ‘antiparallel’ in the context of a DNA molecule. [1 mark]\n\n2. Compare and contrast the structures of DNA and RNA.  [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ two DNA strands run in opposite directions **OR** one strand is oriented 5' to 3', while the other is 3' to 5' [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n   Antiparallel is the arrangement of the **two strands** in the **double helix**, where they run in **opposite directions**, with **one strand oriented 5' to 3'** and the **other 3' to 5'**  ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **four** of the following:\n\n    *At least **one** statement for compare and **one** statement for contrast*\n\n    **compare**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are nucleic acids **OR**  composed of nucleotides [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contain adenine **OR** A **AND** cytosine **OR** C **AND** guanine **OR** G [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have a sugar-phosphate backbone [1 mark]\n\n    **contrast**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA is double-stranded **WHEREAS** RNA is single-stranded [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA contains thymine **OR** T  **WHEREAS** RNA has uracil **OR** U [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA has deoxyribose **WHEREAS** RNA contains ribose [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ structure of DNA is more stable **THAN** RNA [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n    **Both** DNA and RNA are **nucleic acids** composed of **nucleotides**  ($\\checkmark$) and they both have a **sugar-phosphate backbone** ($\\checkmark$). They also contain the nitrogenous bases **adenine** (A), **cytosine** (C), and **guanine** (G) ($\\checkmark$), however, **DNA** contains **thymine** (T), while **RNA** has **uracil** (U) ($\\checkmark$). **DNA** is **double-stranded**, whereas **RNA** is **single-stranded**  ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0313","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0313\n\nWhich of the following shows the correct components of one nucleosome?\n\n| | Number of histone molecules | Number of loops the DNA formed |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ A. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | eight | single |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ B. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | eight | double |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ C. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | four | single |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ D. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | four | double |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nIn one nucleosome, there are eight histone molecules which are basic in pH. The DNA would supercoil around the nucleosome to form a double loop."},{"ref":"BI0321","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0321\n\nThe graph below shows the results of an experiment conducted by a team of researchers investigating the relationship between the composition and melting point of different DNA samples. At the melting point, the DNA double helix separates into two individual strands.\n\nTwo DNA samples were compared, one consisting of only PQ pairs and another one consisting of only RS pairs. Letters P, Q, R and S represent different nitrogenous bases found in the DNA.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI0321.png\" alt=\"BI0321\">\n\nBased on the results presented in the graph, and your knowledge of nucleotide base pairing, deduce which nitrogenous bases correspond to the letters P, Q, R and S.\n\n| | $\\hspace{2.5em}$ P $\\hspace{2.5em}$ | $\\hspace{2.5em}$ Q $\\hspace{2.5em}$ | $\\hspace{2.5em}$ R $\\hspace{2.5em}$ | $\\hspace{2.5em}$ S $\\hspace{2.5em}$ |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ A. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Cytosine | Guanine | Thymine | Adenine |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ B. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Adenine | Cytosine | Guanine | Thymine |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ C. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Guanine | Adenine | Thymine | Cytosine |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ D. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Thymine | Adenine | Cytosine | Guanine |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nAdenine pairs up with thymine and cytosine pairs up with guanine. Hence, PQ or RS would either be adenine with thymine or cytosine with guanine (**Choices A** and **D**), but definitely not **Choices B** or **C**.\n\nCytosine and guanine pairing has three hydrogen bonds, whereas the adenine and thymine pairing has only two hydrogen bonds. At $95^oC$, there is a smaller percentage of RS-rich DNA melted content compared to PQ-rich DNA.\n\nThis shows that RS would be cytosine and guanine pairing (**Choice D**) and PQ is adenine and thymine. Because the extra hydrogen bond between C and G makes it more temperature resistant compared to A and T."},{"ref":"BI0322","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0322\n\nWhat feature of the DNA molecule was utilised by Hershey and Chase in order to identify that viral DNA is able to enter host cells, but most of their protein is not?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ DNA nucleotides contain phosphorus.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ DNA can supercoil around nucleosomes.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ DNA contains a short repetitive nucleotide sequence.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ DNA strands are held together by hydrogen bonds.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nIn Hershey and Chase's experiment, the radioactive isotope phosphorus, $^{32}$P and radioactive isotope sulfur, $^{35}$S were used to determine whether DNA or protein is responsible for carrying the genetic material.\n\n$^{32}$P was incorporated into the DNA core because the nucleotides of DNA contain a phosphate group containing the element phosphorus (**choice A**). Hydrogen bonding in DNA molecules is unrelated to Hershey and Chase experiment (**choice D**). \n\nDNA being able to supercoil around nucleosomes and having short repetitive nucleotide sequences are not features that were used by Hershey and Chase (**choice B** and **choice C**)."},{"ref":"BI0498","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0498\n\nHershey and Chase conducted experiments using the radioactive isotopes :sup[35]S and :sup[32]P which demonstrated that DNA is the genetic material. What would they have observed about the distribution of isotopes in the centrifuged sample if protein, rather than DNA, were the genetic material?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ High proportion of :sup[35]S in the bacterial pellet relative to the supernatant.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ High proportion of :sup[32]P in the bacterial pellet relative to the supernatant.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ High proportion of both :sup[35]S and :sup[32]P in the bacterial pellet relative to the supernatant.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Low proportion of both :sup[35]S and :sup[32]P in the bacterial pellet relative to the supernatant.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nHershey and Chase grew bacteriophages in the presence of the radioactive isotope :sup[35]S or :sup[32]P, then allowed them to infect bacteria. If proteins, rather than DNA, had been the genetic material transferred to bacteria, the bacteria would have contained :sup[35]S (the radiolabel in proteins) and not  :sup[32]P (the radiolabel in DNA). Therefore, Hershey and Chase would have observed a high proportion of  :sup[35]S in the bacterial pellet relative to the supernatant.\n\nA high proportion of :sup[32]P in the bacterial pellet relative to the supernatant (**Choice B**) indicates that DNA, not protein, was the genetic material. Hershey and Chase did not grow bacteria in the presence of both isotopes at the same time and so could not have observed either a higher (**Choice C**) or lower (**Choice D**) proportion of both :sup[35]S and :sup[32]P in the bacterial pellet relative to the supernatant."},{"ref":"BI0541","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0541\n\nA strand of nucleotides reads CCUAGAGCUUACCG. Which type of pentose sugar could be found in the nucleotides in the natural composition of this strand?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Deoxyribose only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ribose only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Both deoxyribose and ribose\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Neither deoxyribose nor ribose","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe nucleotide strand CCUAGAGCUUACCG contains the bases cytosine (C), uracil (U), adenine (A), and guanine (G). While C, A, and G are found in both DNA and RNA, the base uracil is only found in RNA. So, the strand is an RNA strand.\n\nRNA contains the pentose sugar ribose. Therefore, ribose is found in the nucleotides in the strand.\n\n**Choices A** and **C** are incorrect because deoxyribose is only found in DNA, not RNA. The nucleotide strand cannot be DNA because it contains uracil in place of thymine (T).\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the strand is RNA which contains ribose."},{"ref":"BI0605","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0605\n\nWhich of the following is a similarity between transcription and translation?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Both use DNA as a template\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$  Both occur in the 5\\' to 3\\' direction\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Both involve binding to a ribosome\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Both involve mRNA splicing","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nBoth transcription and translation synthesize strands from 5\\' to 3\\'. \\\nOnly transcription uses DNA as a template, whereas translation uses mRNA as a template (**Choice A**).\\\nBinding to ribosomes takes place only in translation but not in transcription (**Choice C**).\\\nmRNA splicing takes place only during post-transcriptional modifications and not in post-translational modifications (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0841","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0841\n\nHow does the genetic code provide evidence of universal common ancestry?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The genetic code of mitochondria is identical to the genetic code of the nucleus in eukaryotes.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Each amino acid is coded for by only one codon.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Mutations cannot cause changes in the sequence of bases in the genome.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The genetic code is the same across all three domains of life, with just a few exceptions.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe genetic code is largely the same in all known organisms. There are a few exceptions such as mitochondrial DNA which can vary slightly in codon assignments for specific amino acids, and some bacterial species. Therefore **Choice D** is correct .\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the genetic code used in mitochondria is similar to the genetic code used in the nucleus of eukaryotic cells but not entirely the same. **Choice B** is incorrect because the genetic code is degenerate. There are 64 possible codons in the genetic code, but only 20 amino acids. As a result, some amino acids are coded for by more than one codon, providing redundancy and flexibility in protein synthesis. **Choice C** is incorrect because mutations are changes in the genetic code. As a result, mutations play a significant role in generating genetic diversity and are a driving force in the process of evolution."},{"ref":"BI0891","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0891\n\nWhich of the labelled structures shows the correct directionality of the DNA molecule?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI0891A.png\" alt=\"BI0891A\">\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI0891B.png\" alt=\"BI0891B\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because the left strand is running in the 5’ - 3’ direction with the phosphate group attached to the 5th carbon at the 5’ end, and the 3rd carbon of the terminating deoxyribose sugar is free. The right strand is running antiparallel in the 3’-5’ direction.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because both strands are labelled as running in the 5’ - 3’ direction, and the carbons of the deoxyribose sugars are incorrectly labelled. **Choice B** is incorrect because the strands are incorrectly labelled with the 5’ and 3’ ends, and the carbons of the deoxyribose sugars are incorrectly labelled on both strands. **Choice D** is incorrect because both strands are labelled as running in the 3’ - 5’ direction, and the carbons of the deoxyribose sugars on both strands are incorrectly labelled."},{"ref":"BI0892","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0892\n\nWhat would make one DNA molecule more stable than another DNA molecule?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | More G-C base pairs than A-T base pairs |\n    | II. | More hydrogen bonds between base pairs |\n    | III. | Greater distance between A-T base pairs than G-C base pairs |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because the stability of the DNA helix molecule is affected by the nucleotide composition. There are 3 hydrogen bonds between the G-C base pairs, and only 2 between the A-T base pairs, therefore, more G-C pairs (**Statement I**)  means a greater number of hydrogen bonds (**Statement II**).\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as it does not include both correct statements.\n\nThe distance between the A-T base pairs and the G-C base pairs is equal, and even if the distance between A-T were greater, it would not add to stability (**Statement III**).  **Choices B** and **D** are therefore incorrect as they include **Statement III**."},{"ref":"BI0893","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0893\n\nThe diagram depicts nucleosomes.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI0893A.png\" alt=\"BI0893A\">\n[Source: Adapted from CNX OpenStax, (2016, May). *File: Figure 16 03 02.png.* Wikimedia commons. Retrieved May 28, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Figure_16_03_02.png. Copyright under CC-BY-4.0]\n\nWhat is the role of X?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Joins nucleosomes together\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Packages DNA into nucleosomes through coiling\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ DNA replication\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Carries genetic material","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because X labels the core histone proteins which help package the DNA into nucleosomes through coiling.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because X is not linker DNA which extends from one nucleosome to the next, connecting nucleosomes with one another. **Choice C** is incorrect because enzymes carry out DNA replication, not histone proteins. **Choice D** is incorrect because DNA carries genetic material, not the core histone proteins."},{"ref":"BI0894","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0894\n\nHershey and Chase conducted an experiment to investigate whether DNA or protein serves as the genetic material. They cultured 2 batches of viruses, one in a medium containing radioactive :sup[35]S and one in radioactive :sup[32]P. They then introduced each batch of viruses to separate cultures of bacteria. After allowing time for the viruses to infect the bacteria, the culture solutions were centrifuged to separate the viral remains from the bacterial cells, forming two layers, the pellet and the supernatant.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI0894A.png\" alt=\"BI0894A\">\n\nWhich component of the tube would provide evidence that DNA is the genetic material?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Supernatant with radioactive :sup[35]S\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Pellet with radioactive :sup[35]S\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Supernatant with radioactive :sup[32]P\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Pellet with radioactive :sup[32]P","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because the bacterial cells form the pellet, and they would contain :sup[32]P as DNA contains phosphorus but not sulphur. The viruses cultured in :sup[32]P incorporated it into their DNA, and they infected the bacteria with this radioactive DNA.\n\n**Choices A** and **C** are incorrect as the bacteria with the radioactive DNA should be found in the pellet, whereas the supernatant contains the viral remains. **Choice B** is incorrect as if the bacteria in the pellet contained :sup[35]S then this would indicate that protein, which contains sulphur, and not phosphorus, serves as the genetic material."},{"ref":"BI0895","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0895\n\nThe diagram below shows the structure based on the tetranucleotide hypothesis proposed by Levene that DNA was made up of equal amounts of adenine, guanine, cytosine and thymine. This hypothesis was a problem of induction, because it was based on a limited set of data. Subsequent evidence and data revealed a more complex and accurate understanding of the structure, leading to the falsification of the initial hypothesis.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI0895A.png\" alt=\"BI0895A\">\n[Source: JWSchmidt (2006). *Tetranucleotide*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved July 6th, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Tetranucleotide.png. CC-BY-SA-3.0-migrated-with-disclaimers]\n\nWhich scientist is credited with falsifying the tetranucleotide hypothesis?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Erwin Chargaff\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Rosalind Franklin\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Alfred Hershey\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ James Watson","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because Chargaff used paper chromatography to show that the proportions of adenine (A) and thymine (T) are the same, as are the proportions of guanine (G) and cytosine (C). There are not equal proportions of A, T, G and C.\n\nFranklin (**Choice B**) and Watson (**Choice D**) are credited for their contributions to the overall structure of DNA structure. Hershey (**Choice C**), along with Chase, is famous for his research which provided evidence for the idea that DNA, rather than protein, carries the genetic code."},{"ref":"BI0897","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0897\n\nWhat bases are P, Q, and R if S is a pyrimidine?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI0897A.png\" alt=\"BI0897A\">\n\n| | P | Q | R |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Guanine $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Cytosine $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Thymine $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Guanine | Adenine | Cytosine |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Cytosine | Guanine | Adenine|\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Adenine | Thymine | Cytosine |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAdenine and guanine are purines. Cytosine and thymine are pyrimidines.\n\nIf S is a pyrimidine (~20%), it is either cytosine or thymine. Purines pair with pyrimidines, so Q, which also makes up about 20% of the molecule, must be guanine or adenine. This means **Choices A** and **D** can be eliminated as correct answers.\n\nP and R must pair up as they make up approximately the same proportion of the DNA. So P and R must either be adenine and thymine or guanine and cytosine. This means **Choice C** is incorrect.\n\nThis leaves us with the correct answer of **Choice B**. P is guanine, which pairs with R, cytosine, and Q is adenine, which pairs with S, which must be thymine."},{"ref":"BI0898","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0898\n\nHow did radioisotopes help make the investigations by Hershey and Chase possible?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ They identified the helical shape of DNA.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ They tracked elements that are incorporated into cells.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ They separated mixtures according to size and charge.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ They stopped DNA replication for base sequencing.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because radioisotopes are used to track elements. Hershey and Chase used radioactive :sup[35]S and :sup[32]P to provide evidence that DNA is the genetic material.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because X-ray diffractions were used to identify the helical shape of DNA and not radioisotopes. Although Hershey ad Chase did use centrifugation to separate the bacteria from the viral particles, **Choice C** is incorrect because centrifugation does not require radioisotopes and it does not separate mixtures according to charge.  Gel electrophoresis is the process that tends to be used to separate mixtures according to size and charge. **Choice D** is incorrect because dideoxyribonucleotides are used to stop DNA replication and not radioisotopes."},{"ref":"BI0899","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0899\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe diagram below shows the structure of DNA nucleotides.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI0899A.png\" alt=\"BI0899A\">\n[Source: Adapted from OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology, (2016, May). *File:0322 DNA Nucleotides.jpg*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved July 6, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0322_DNA_Nucleotides.jpg. Copyright under CC-BY-4.0]\n\n1. State the component of DNA that is attached at position X.      [1 mark]\n\n2. Identify the type of bonding shown as dotted lines in the diagram. [1 mark]\n\n3. The 5’ and 3’ linkages in DNA play an important role in replication, transcription and translation.\n\n    1. On the diagram, label the 5’ prime and 3’ prime ends on both strands to show the direction of the DNA.                      [1 mark]\n\n    2. Outline how the processes of transcription and translation occur with regards to the formation of  5’ and 3’ linkages.             [2 marks]\n\n4. Explain how the DNA helix maintains its stability.      [2 marks]\n\n5. Erwin Chargaff’s data on the relative amounts of purine and pyrimidine bases played an important role in determining the structure of DNA.\n\n    1. Outline how Chargaff’s data contributed to our understanding of the structure of DNA.                      [2 marks]\n\n    2. State a proposed model of DNA that was falsified by Chargaff's data.    [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ phosphate **OR** phosphate group                    [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ A **phosphate group** is found at X ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen **OR** hydrogen bonds                [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ **Hydrogen** bonding ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n    1.\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ both strands are labelled correctly with 5’ at the available 5th carbon and 3’ at the available 3rd carbon                [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI0899ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0899ANSA\">\n\n3.\n    2. any **two** of the following (AT LEAST **ONE** from transcription and **ONE** from translation):\n\n    ***transcription***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA polymerase adds nucleotides to the 3’ end of the growing mRNA strand        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA polymerase creates the RNA strand in the 5’ to the 3’ direction        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA polymerase reads the DNA template strand in the 3’ to 5’ direction        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the new nucleotides are added to the 3’ end through sugar-phosphate bonds between the 3’ carbon of the last nucleotide and the phosphate group of the incoming nucleotide        [1 mark]\n\n    ***translation***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ribosome moves along the mRNA from the 5’ end to the 3’ end         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ codons of the mRNA are read from the 5’ end to the 3’ end to add amino acids that form the polypeptide             [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n    In transcription, RNA polymerase **reads the DNA template strand in the 3’ to 5’ direction** ($\\checkmark$) and creates the **RNA strand in the opposite direction** ($\\checkmark$). In translation, the **ribosome moves along the mRNA in the 5’ to 3’ direction** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ purine-to-pyrimidine bonding **OR** complementary base pairing    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ adenine-thymine **OR** A-T and cytosine-guanine **OR** C-G have equal lengths    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen bonding between complementary bases  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrophobic interaction between adjacent bases     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sugar-phosphate bonds link the 3’ carbon of one deoxyribose sugar to the phosphate group on the 5’ carbon of the next, forming a strong sugar-phosphate backbone    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ by winding around central histone proteins in eukaryotes to form nucleosomes        [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n    The DNA double helix maintains its stability through **complementary base pairing of purine to pyrimidine** ($\\checkmark$). This base pairing is held together by 2 **hydrogen bonds** between adenine to thymine and 3 **hydrogen bonds** ($\\checkmark$) between cytosine and guanine.\n\n5.\n    1. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ showed the ratio of adenine to thymine and guanine to cytosine are equal [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ showed that the four nucleotide bases are not in repeating sequences **OR** equally distributed                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ suggested that DNA consists of two complementary strands    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ showed that the composition of DNA varies across diverse life forms    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ helped Watson and Crick in building the double helix shape of their DNA model    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ base pairing data suggested A pairs with T and G pairs with C            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ base pairing data suggested  mechanisms whereby DNA could replicate **OR** be transcribed     [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n    Chargaff’s data showed that the molar **ratio of adenine to thymine** and the **ratio of guanine to cytosine** are close to 1:1 ($\\checkmark$). This data then helped Watson and Crick to deduce that **DNA consists of two complementary strands** ($\\checkmark$) and is **helical in shape** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5.\n    2. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tetranucleotide hypothesis **OR** theory of four nucleotide bases in repeating sequence **OR** repeating tetramer nucleic acid         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the purely protein-based genetic material    [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n    The **tetranucleotide hypothesis** ($\\checkmark$) was falsified by Chargaff’s data."},{"ref":"BI0900","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0900\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe Hershey-Chase experiment in 1952 involved cultured viruses that were radioactively labelled with either sulfur (:sup[35]S) or phosphorus (:sup[32]P) in two separate flasks. Each batch of viruses was then added to separate cultures of bacteria. After allowing time for the viruses to infect the bacteria, the culture solutions were centrifuged to separate the viral remains from the bacterial cells, forming two layers, the pellet and the supernatant.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI0900.png\" alt=\"BI0900\">\n\n1. Explain why radioactive sulfur (:sup[35]S) and radioactive phosphorus (:sup[32]P) were used in the Hershey–Chase experiment to distinguish between DNA and protein. [2 marks]\n\n2. Suggest one technological development, other than radioisotopes, that helped open up possibilities in understanding the overall structure of DNA. [1 mark]\n\n3.\n 1. Outline the results of the experiment that provided evidence to support the conclusion that DNA is the genetic material. [2 marks]\n\n 2. Describe how the 4 nitrogenous bases of DNA enables polypeptides to be coded for. [2 marks]\n\n4. Explain how DNA can be packaged into compact units to form chromatin. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following :\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ :sup[35]S is used to label protein because sulfur is found in protein but not in DNA [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ :sup[32]P is used to label DNA because phosphorus is found in DNA but not in protein [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n :sup[35]S is used because **sulphur is found in protein and not in DNA** ($\\checkmark$), and :sup[32]P is used because **phosphorus is found in DNA and not protein** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ X-ray crystallography **OR** diffraction (by Rosalind Franklin) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ paper chromatography (by Erwin Chargaff) [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Another technological development used was **X-ray diffraction** ($\\checkmark$) by Rosalind Franklin which provided evidence that DNA has a helical shape.\n\n3.\n 1.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ :sup[35]S **OR** radioactively labelled protein was found in the supernatant **OR** not found in the pellet **OR** no radioactivity detected in bacteria infected with viruses labelled with :sup[35]S [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ :sup[32]P **OR** radioactively labelled DNA was found in the pellet **OR** not found in the supernatant **OR** radioactivity was detected in bacteria infected with viruses labelled with :sup[32]P [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The results from the Hershey-Chase experiment showed that **:sup[35]S**, used to **label protein, was found in the supernatant** ($\\checkmark$), and the **:sup[32]P**, used to label DNA, was found in the pellet ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the four nitrogenous bases are A, T, G, C **OR** adenine, thymine, guanine, cytosine [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ polypeptides are coded for by genes OR a sequence of nucleotides [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ three nitrogenous bases **OR** codon **OR** triplet of bases, code for a specific amino acid **OR** start **OR** stop [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ degenerative code **OR** several codons can code for one amino acid [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA as a template **OR** antisense strand to form mRNA strand [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ complementary base pairing between DNA nucleotides and RNA nucleotides **OR** between codons on mRNA and amino acid [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA is used as template to form polypeptide chains [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Polypeptides are coded for by **genes** ($\\checkmark$) made up of a specific sequence of nucleotides. A nucleotide consists of a ribose sugar, a phosphate group and one of four nitrogenous bases - **adenine, guanine, cytosine and thymine** ($\\checkmark$). DNA is used as a **template to form the mRNA strand** ($\\checkmark$). A triplet of bases, **a codon**, along the mRNA strand codes for a **specific amino acid** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA tightly wraps around a core of eight histone proteins [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ held together by an additional histone protein **OR** H1 linker protein [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ H1 histone proteins binds the nucleosomes together to form a 30 nm chromatin fibre [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ long DNA molecules are packed into a small space [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the negatively charged DNA is attracted to the positively charged histones [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ form structures called nucleosomes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleosomes are attached to other nucleosomes by linker DNA [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n DNA coils tightly around a core of **eight histone proteins** ($\\checkmark$) which are held together by **H1 histone proteins** ($\\checkmark$) to form structures known as **nucleosomes** ($\\checkmark$). The **nucleosomes** are bundled **together** to form the **30nm fibre chromatin** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1047","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1047\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nDNA and RNA are nucleic acids that are essential to all living organisms.\n\n1. Draw and label a diagram of a nucleotide. $^*$ [4 marks]\n\n2. Explain how the variation in base sequences leads to the ability of DNA to have an enormous capacity for storing genetic information. [5 marks]\n\n3. DNA is a polymer of nucleotides. Describe the structure of DNA. [7 marks]\n\n$^*$ *Note: Starting May 2028, this question may be assessed differently due to changes to the Biology guide, including the use of a data booklet.*","markScheme":"1. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ phosphate group drawn as a circle in correct place. [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ribose sugar drawn as a pentagon in correct place [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nitrogenous base drawn as a rectangle in correct place [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ all three parts labelled correctly [1 mark]\n\n *Bonds and names of nitrogenous bases do not need to be labelled*\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI1047.png\" alt=\"BI1047\">\n\n2. any **five** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** similarity and **one** difference given to gain full marks*):\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic code is stored in the sequence of nitrogenous bases [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ three bases **OR** a codon, codes for one amino acid [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 64 codons but only 20 amino acids **OR** degenerate code [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ complementary base pairing [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ for each extra nucleotide along a DNA strand, the number of possible combinations of bases increases exponentially [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ mutations introduce new genetic information [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ certain sequences are conserved across species because they encode vital information [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA molecules can be hundreds of millions of base pairs long **OR** DNA molecules are very long [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ non-coding regions have regulatory functions, contributing to the complexity of gene expression and overall genetic information [1 mark]\n [5 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The genetic code resides within the **sequences** of nitrogenous **bases** ($\\checkmark$) along a DNA molecule. Amino acids are coded for by **codons** ($\\checkmark$), and the code is **degenerate** ($\\checkmark$), in other words, some amino acids are coded for by more than one codon. The longer the DNA molecule, the greater the number of possible combinations of bases along the DNA strand, in fact there is an **exponential increase** in possibilities ($\\checkmark$) for each single nucleotide added to the polymer. **Mutations** also add to the variety ($\\checkmark$) of the genetic code.\n\n3. any **seven** of the following: \\\n*(appropriately labelled and correctly drawn DNA segment can be used in the answer)*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA is a double helix [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ two strands are anti-parallel **OR** parallel but in opposite directions **OR** one strand is in the 5’ to 3’ direction and the other strand is in the 3’ to 5’ direction [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ strands are twisted **OR** right-handed and turn into a double helix with around 10 base pairs per turn [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleotides consist of one phosphate group, one deoxyribose sugar and one nitrogenous base [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ phosphate group is bound to the 5’ carbon of the sugar OR deoxyribose **OR** pentose [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ phosphate group is bound to the 3’ carbon OH group of deoxyribose (of the next nucleotide) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ covalent **OR** sugar-phosphate bond between phosphate and sugar [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nitrogenous base **OR** base is bound to the 1’ carbon of the sugar **OR** deoxyribose **OR** pentose sugar [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ covalent bond between nitrogenous base and deoxyribose **OR** sugar **OR** pentose sugar [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ two strands are held together by hydrogen bonds between nitrogen bases of two nucleotides **OR** 3 hydrogen bonds between guanine and cytosine **OR** 2 hydrogen bonds between adenine and thymine [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ A pairs up with T **AND** C pairs up with G [1 mark]\n [7 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n DNA is a nucleic acid, also known as a polymer of nucleotides. Many nucleotides are joined together to form it. **One nucleotide** consists of three parts; a **phosphate group, deoxyribose sugar and a nitrogenous base** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n **One end of the phosphate group is bonded to the 5’ carbon of the sugar** ($\\checkmark$) through a condensation reaction. On the other side of the nucleotide, **the phosphate group is bonded to the 3’ carbon sugar** ($\\checkmark$) of another nucleotide through a condensation reaction. Both bonds are **covalent bonds** ($\\checkmark$). Repeated alternating sugar-phosphate bonds form the sugar-phosphate backbone. The **1’ carbon of the sugar is covalently bonded to the nitrogenous base** ($\\checkmark$)also through a condensation reaction to make up one complete nucleotide.\n\n **DNA is a double helix** ($\\checkmark$) in which nitrogenous bases form the centre of the structure, and **both strands are held together by hydrogen bonds** ($\\checkmark$) between the complementary bases from each strand. **A pairs up with T, and C always pairs up with G** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1048","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1048\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe image below shows a short fragment of the DNA molecule.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI1048A.png\" alt=\"BI1048A\">\n[Source: Adapted from User: Madprime. (2010, March). *File: DNA chemical structure jp.svg.* Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved May 15, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: DNA_chemical_structure_jp.svg. Copyright under CC-SA 3.0]\n\n1. Suggest **one** possible problem that could arise if DNA consisted of two parallel strands instead of two antiparallel strands.      [1 mark]\n\n2. State one place where DNA is found in cells. [1 mark]\n\n3. Compare and contrast the structure of DNA and RNA.        [5 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ no hydrogen bonding between base pairs        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ two strands are not held together **OR** no double-stranded DNA    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ two strands would be geometrically the same, thus unable to combine     [1 mark]\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    If DNA were made of two parallel strands, both strands would be oriented in the same 3’ to 5’ direction. Since the DNA strand is tilted and not straight, that would mean that two DNA strands **would not be able to combine** ($\\checkmark$) to form a double helix.\n\n2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleus **OR** nucleoid **OR** mitochondria **OR** chloroplasts **OR** plasmids    [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ One place where DNA is found in the cell is in the **nucleus** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **five** of the following (there must be at least **one** similarity and **one** difference given to gain full marks):\n\n    **Similarities:** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** DNA and RNA are composed of nucleotides **OR** sugar, phosphate **AND** base [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** DNA and RNA contain four types of nitrogenous bases [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** DNA and RNA have covalent bonds holding nucleotides together [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** DNA and RNA exhibit base pairing **OR** complementary bases are connected through hydrogen bonds **OR** have hydrogen bonds between the complementary nitrogenous bases [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** DNA and RNA have a backbone made up of alternating sugar and phosphate groups [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** DNA and RNA are polymers, composed of repeating monomer units **OR** nucleotides, linked together in a chain [1 mark]\n\n    **Differences:** \\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA contains deoxyribose sugar **WHEREAS** RNA contains ribose  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA contains the nitrogenous bases adenine, thymine, cytosine and  guanine **OR** ATCG **WHEREAS** RNA contains adenine, uracil, cytosine and guanine **OR** AUCG, **OR** in DNA adenine pairs with thymine and cytosine pairs with guanine **WHEREAS** in RNA adenine pairs with uracil and cytosine pairs with guanine  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA typically consists of two (antiparallel) strands **WHEREAS** RNA is usually single-stranded [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in DNA adenine pairs with thymine and cytosine pairs with guanine **WHEREAS** in RNA adenine pairs with uracil and cytosine pairs with guanine  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA typically forms a double-stranded helical structure with base pairing between the two strands  **WHEREAS** RNA can fold into complex secondary structures due to intra-strand base pairing, creating loops, stems, and hairpin structures [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Both DNA and RNA are made up of **nucleotides** ($\\checkmark$), however, the sugar in DNA is **deoxyribose**, whilst that in RNA is **ribose** ($\\checkmark$). Both DNA and RNA contain **four** different **nitrogenous bases** ($\\checkmark$), however, DNA contains adenine, guanine, cytosine and thymine, whilst RNA contains **uracil instead of thymine** ($\\checkmark$). Both DNA and RNA are **polymers** of nucleotides ($\\checkmark$) held together by **covalent bonds** ($\\checkmark$) between the sugar of one nucleotide and a phosphate group of the next, however, DNA is typically **double-stranded**, and RNA is typically **single-stranded** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1067","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1067\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Distinguish between RNA and DNA.         [3 marks]\n\n2. Explain the importance of orientation and directionality of DNA and RNA.          [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe how the Hershey and Chase experiment demonstrated that DNA carries the genetic information .       [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  RNA is single-stranded  **WHEREAS** DNA is double-stranded    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA has a variety of shapes **WHEREAS** DNA is a double helix     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA contains ribose sugar  **WHEREAS** DNA contains deoxyribose        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA contains the nitrogenous base uracil **OR** U **WHEREAS** DNA contains thymine **OR** T [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA only contains permanent genetic code in RNA viruses **WHEREAS** DNA contains permanent genetic code in all cells (except red blood cells)        [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    RNA is a **single stranded** molecule containing ribose sugar and uracil as one of its four nitrogenous bases. DNA on the other hand is **double-stranded** ($\\checkmark$), contains **deoxyribose** instead of **ribose** sugar ($\\checkmark$), and has **thymine** instead of **uracil** ($\\checkmark$) as one of its four nitrogenous bases. DNA forms a regular **double helix**, whereas RNA can form **different shapes**  ($\\checkmark$) depending on its role, such as the clover-shaped tRNA and long-stranded mRNA.\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 5’ **OR** 5 prime end  refers to the end with a phosphate group bound to the 5th carbon of the deoxyribose **OR** pentose sugar, but not bound to another nucleotide, the 3’ **OR** 3 prime end refers to the end where the 3rd carbon of the deoxyribose **OR** pentose sugar is not bound to another nucleotide            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ DNA has antiparallel strands **OR** one strand is in the 5’ to 3’ direction, and another strand is in the 3’ to 5’ strand         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ single DNA strand is tilted; therefore, two DNA strands can only bind together if they are in antiparallel orientation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in DNA replication, one lagging strand and one leading strand are produced     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in DNA replication DNA polymerase only adds free nucleotides to the 3’ end of the growing DNA strand **OR** adds free nucleotides in the 5’ to 3’ direction        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in transcription RNA polymerase can only add nucleotides in the 5’ to 3’ direction to form mRNA        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ translation must start nearest to the 5’ end of the mRNA    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Both RNA and DNA have directionality they have a **5’** and **3’** end. The **5’ end** refers to the end with a **phosphate group** attached to carbon 5 of the deoxyribose, but **not attached** to another **nucleotide**, the **3’** end refers to the end where **carbon 3** of the deoxyribose is **not attached** to another **nucleotide** ($\\checkmark$). DNA forms a double helix with **antiparallel** strands ($\\checkmark$). During **replication DNA polymerase** can only **add free nucleotides** in the **5’ to 3’** direction ($\\checkmark$). During **transcription RNA polymerase** can only **add nucleotides** in the **5’ to 3’** direction ($\\checkmark$) to form a strand of mRNA. **Translation** of the mRNA must **start at the 5’** end ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ bacteriophages **OR** phage virus was used         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ bacteriophage contains an inner core that has DNA and a protein coat    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ experiments tested the hypothesis that DNA and not protein carries the genetic information    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ bacteriophage **OR** virus can attach to the surface of bacterial cell **OR** host and inject substance into the cell        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ substance contains information for bacteria cell **OR** host to duplicate **OR** produce more bacteriophages **OR** viruses        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ bacteriophages **OR** virus were labeled with radioactive phosphorus (:sup[32]P) and radioactive sulfur (:sup[35]S) **OR** :sup[32]P was incorporated into DNA core and :sup[35]S was incorporated into protein coat        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phosphorus is found only in DNA and not protein        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sulfur is found only in protein and not DNA    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ *Escherichia coli* **OR** bacterial cells were infected with bacteriophage **OR** virus  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ used centrifuge to separate bacteriophage **OR** virus from bacteria        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ virus in liquid **OR** supernatant did not contain radioactive :sup[32]P but bacterial sediment **OR** pellet contained radioactive :sup[32]P            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ :sup[32]P DNA entered the bacteria **OR** host cell for replication          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ virus in liquid **OR** supernatant contained radioactive :sup[35]S but bacterial sediment **OR** pellet did not contain radioactive :sup[35]S            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ :sup[35]S protein structure did not enter the bacteria **OR** host cell for replication     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic material needs to enter the bacterial cell in order to produce new viruses     [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Hershey and Chase's experiment was conducted to investigate whether DNA or protein carries the genetic material. In their experiment, they used **bacteriophages** ($\\checkmark$) to infect bacteria cells. **Bacteriophage** contains an **outer coat made of protein** and an **inner core containing DNA** ($\\checkmark$) which makes it suitable for comparing DNA and protein. The bacteriophage can then **attach itself to the surface of the bacteria cell** and **inject the substance** ($\\checkmark$) which carries important genetic information into the bacteria cell.\n\n    The bacteriophages were then cultured under two different conditions — **one was incorporated with a radioactive phosphorus isotope, :sup[32]P** and another with a radioactive **sulfur isotope, :sup[35]S** ($\\checkmark$). Radioactive elements 32P and 35S were used because **phosphorus is found in the DNA structure** but **absent in protein** ($\\checkmark$), whereas **sulfur**, is **found in** the **protein** structure but **absent** in the **DNA** ($\\checkmark$) structure.\n\n    The bacteria cells were then cultured together with the bacteriophages and were **infected with the bacteriophages** ($\\checkmark$). After centrifugation, it was found that the pellets containing **bacteria cultured** with the **radioactive :sup[32]P bacteriophages contained radioactivity**, whereas the **bacteriophage protein coats** in the supernatant **didn’t** ($\\checkmark$).  The pellets containing the **bacteria cultured** with the **radioactive :sup[35]S bacteriophages did not contain any radioactivity**, whereas the **bacteriophage protein coats** in the supernatant **did** ($\\checkmark$). These provided evidence that DNA labelled with the **:sup[32]P entered the bacteria cells**, whereas the **protein** which was labelled with the **:sup[35]S did not** ($\\checkmark$) enter the bacteria cells."},{"ref":"BI1145","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1145\n\nThe graphs below show the nucleotide base distribution for the genome of human mitochondria.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI1145.png\" alt=\"BI1145\">\n\nWhat conclusion can be made based on the data shown above?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The genome contains an equal distribution of adenine, thymine, cytosine, and guanine.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ There is no relationship between the four nucleotide bases.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Adenine and guanine are less common than thymine and cytosine.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ There is complementary base pairing between guanine and cytosine, and between adenine and thymine.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWhen comparing the two strands, the proportion of adenine bases equals that of thymine, and the percentage of guanine equals that of cytosine. This is due to complementary base pairing.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect, as there is an unequal distribution of the four bases in the genome. **Choice B** is incorrect as a direct relationship exists between A-T and C-G bases due to complementary base pairing. **Choice C** cannot be correct due to the complementary base pairing between adenine and thymine, and between guanine and cytosine."},{"ref":"BI1166","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1166\n\nWhich statement is correct concerning the structure of DNA?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ A phosphate within a nucleotide is covalently bonded to a nitrogenous base\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ DNA molecules are made up of two parallel strands running in the same 3'-5' direction, linked by hydrogen bonds between complementary bases\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Bonding between adjacent phosphates and deoxyribose sugars forms the backbone of DNA\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Adenine is complementary to uracil, and cytosine is complementary to guanine","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCovalent bonding between adjacent phosphates and deoxyribose sugars forms the backbone of DNA; **Choice C** is correct.\n\nWithin nucleotides, the phosphate is covalently bonded to the deoxyribose sugar and the sugar is attached to the base; **Choice A**  is incorrect. DNA molecules are made up of two antiparallel strands (they are parallel but run in opposite directions, one runs 3’-5’ and the other 5’-3’), linked by hydrogen bonds between complementary bases; **Choice B**  is incorrect. In DNA, adenine is complementary to thymine; **Choice D**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1171","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1171\n\nThe diagram shows loose chromatin, also termed euchromatin. Which letter represents a histone protein?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI1171ii.png\" alt=\"BI1171ii\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA histone is one of 8 proteins that DNA winds around so that it can be packaged efficiently into a cell’s nucleus. **Choice A** is correct.\n\nLine B is pointing to the DNA wrapped around 8 histones; **Choice B** is incorrect. Line C is pointing to a short section of DNA (linker DNA) that connects one nucleosome to the next; **Choice C** is incorrect. Line D is pointing to a nucleosome, which is DNA wrapped around 8 histone proteins; **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1221","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1221\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nDNA and RNA are nucleic acids that are essential to all living organisms. ATP is a type of nucleotide.\n\n1. Distinguish between ATP and the monomers of DNA in humans. [3 marks]\n\n2. Outline the roles of the different types of RNA involved in translation.     [5 marks]\n\n3. Describe the structure of the DNA double helix.  [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP contains three phosphate groups **WHEREAS** each DNA nucleotide contains only one phosphate group         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP has adenine for a nitrogenous base **WHEREAS** a DNA nucleotide can contain adenine, cytosine, guanine or thymine    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a component of ATP is ribose **WHEREAS** DNA nucleotides have deoxyribose instead        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP stores energy **OR** provides energy, **WHEREAS** DNA carries **OR** transmits genetic information                          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP can perform its function as a single monomer **WHEREAS** DNA can only perform its function, **OR** carry genetic material as a polymer            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ATP is found throughout the cell **OR** primarily in the cytoplasm **OR** mitochondria **WHEREAS** DNA is found primarily in the nucleus **OR** only found in the nucleus AND mitochondria            [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Both ATP and DNA molecules are nucleic acids which contain a phosphate group, a sugar and a base. The structural difference between a molecule of ATP and a molecule of DNA is that one ATP molecule contains **three phosphate groups** whereas one DNA nucleotide contains **one phosphate group** ($\\checkmark$). In addition, the nitrogenous base in ATP is **adenine** whereas there are four possible nitrogenous bases in one nucleotide of DNA which are **adenine, cytosine, guanine and thymine** ($\\checkmark$). The functional difference is that ATP **stores energy**, whereas DNA **carries genetic material** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following (there must be at least one statement for each of mRNA, tRNA and rRNA to achieve maximum marks):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA carries the genetic information from DNA to the ribosome            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA serves as a template for protein synthesis        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ codons of mRNA  specify positioning of  amino acids along polypeptide **OR** codons guide the assembly of amino acids into a polypeptide         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ tRNA molecules carry amino acids to mRNA **OR** ribosome    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ anticodons on tRNA complementary to codons on mRNA allow amino acids to be positioned correctly along the polypeptide **OR** anticodons bind to codons to bring corresponding amino acid to the ribosome             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rRNA provides the ribosome's structural framework **OR** forms ribosomes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rRNA catalyses the formation of peptide bonds between adjacent amino acids     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ rRNA facilitates binding of mRNA and tRNA        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mRNA, tRNA, and rRNA orchestrate protein synthesis during translation    [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Together, mRNA, tRNA, and rRNA** orchestrate the process of **protein synthesis** ($\\checkmark$) during translation. **mRNA carries** the genetic **information** from **DNA** to the **ribosome**  ($\\checkmark$). **Codons** on the mRNA **specify the positioning** of **amino acids** along the **polypeptide** chain  ($\\checkmark$). Each **tRNA** molecule has a specific **anticodon** sequence that is **complementary** to a **codon** on the mRNA  ($\\checkmark$). This base-pairing specificity allows tRNA to bring the **corresponding amino acid** to the **correct position** to form the polypeptide  ($\\checkmark$). The rRNA **catalyses** the formation of **peptide bonds** between adjacent amino acids  ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following: \\\n    *(appropriately labelled and correctly drawn DNA segment can be used in the answer)*\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ each strand of DNA is a polymer of nucleotides         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ each strand has a sugar-phosphate backbone            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ four nitrogenous bases A **OR** adenine **AND** T **OR** thymine **AND** C **OR** cytosine **AND** G **OR** guanine              [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ two strands are anti-parallel **OR** parallel but in opposite directions **OR** one strand is in the 5’ to 3’ direction and the other strand is in the 3’ to 5’ direction    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ strands are twisted **OR** right-handed and turn into a double helix with around 10 base pairs per turn          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleotides consist of one phosphate group, one deoxyribose sugar and one nitrogenous base                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phosphate group is bound to the 5’ carbon of the sugar **OR** deoxyribose **OR** pentose                           [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phosphate group is bound to the 3’ carbon OH group of deoxyribose (of the next nucleotide)                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ covalent **OR** sugar-phosphate bond between phosphate and sugar        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nitrogenous base **OR** base is bound to the 1’ carbon of the sugar **OR** deoxyribose **OR** pentose sugar             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ covalent bond between nitrogenous base and deoxyribose **OR** sugar **OR** pentose sugar                     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ two strands are held together by hydrogen bonds between nitrogen bases of two nucleotides **OR** 3 hydrogen bonds between guanine and cytosine **OR** 2 hydrogen bonds between adenine and thymine    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ complementary base pairing between nitrogenous bases    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ A pairs up with T AND C pairs up with G        [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    DNA is a nucleic acid, also known as a **polymer of nucleotides** ($\\checkmark$) . Many nucleotides are joined together to form it. **One nucleotide** consists of three parts; a **phosphate group, deoxyribose sugar and a nitrogenous base** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    One end of the **phosphate group** is bonded to the **5’ carbon** of the **deoxyribose sugar** ($\\checkmark$). On the other side of the nucleotide, **the phosphate group is bonded to the 3’ carbon sugar** ($\\checkmark$) of another nucleotide. Both bonds are covalent **sugar-phosphate bonds** ($\\checkmark$). Repeated alternating sugar-phosphate bonds form the **sugar-phosphate backbone** ($\\checkmark$).  The **1’ carbon of the sugar is covalently bonded to the nitrogenous base** ($\\checkmark$) also through a condensation reaction to make up one complete nucleotide.\n\n    **DNA is a double helix** ($\\checkmark$) in which nitrogenous bases form the centre of the structure, and **both strands are held together by hydrogen bonds** ($\\checkmark$) between the complementary bases from each strand. **A** pairs up **with T**, and **C** always pairs up **with G** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1382","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1382\n\nWhich set of bonds found in nucleic acids are correctly identified in the table below?\n\n| | Bonds between phosphates and pentose sugars | Bonds between nucleotide bases |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ |Covalent | Hydrogen |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Hydrogen | Covalent |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Covalent | Covalent |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Hydrogen | Hydrogen |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because the nucleic acid backbone is held together by covalently bonded sugars and phosphates, while the nucleotide bases are held together by hydrogen bonds.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the types of bonds are reversed. **Choice C** is incorrect because the bonds between nucleotides are hydrogen bonds, not covalent bonds. **Choice D** is incorrect because the bonds between phosphates and pentose sugars are covalent, not hydrogen bonds."},{"ref":"BI1394","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1394\n\nWhich of the following shows the correct structure of a DNA nucleotide?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/1394ii.png\" alt=\"1394ii\">\n\n*Note: Starting May 2028, this question may be assessed differently due to changes to the Biology guide, including the use of a data booklet.*","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because it shows the nitrogenous base bonded to the 1’ carbon of the pentose sugar and the phosphate bonded to the 5’ carbon. Even though the structure is inverted the bonding remains accurate, as this is how it would appear on the antiparallel DNA strand.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it shows the phosphate bonded to the oxygen on the pentose sugar, which is not possible. **Choice C** is incorrect as it shows incorrect bonding for the nucleotide base, which is attached to the 2’ carbon. The phosphate is shown bonded to the 3’ end of the sugar, which would be part of the next nucleotide subunit. **Choice D** is incorrect as it shows incorrect bonding for the nucleotide base, again connected to the 2’ carbon."},{"ref":"BI1565","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1565\n\nWhich of the following represents the correct structure of RNA?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A. | | $\\hspace{2cm}$ | |B.|\n| |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI1565A.png\" alt=\"BI1565A\"> | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI1565B.png\" alt=\"BI1565B\">|\n|C. | | | |D. |\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI1565C.png\" alt=\"BI1565C\"> | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI1565D.png\" alt=\"BI1565D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because RNA is a single-stranded nucleic acid that contains adenine (A), cytosine (C), uracil (U), and guanine (G) nucleotide bases, while DNA is a double-stranded nucleic acid containing thymine (T) instead of uracil. The nucleotide bases are covalently bonded to a pentose sugar, which is covalently bonded to a phosphate at the 5’ and 3’ ends.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect as this is the structure of a DNA molecule, including a thymine base. **Choice B** is incorrect because RNA does not contain thymine. **Choice D** is incorrect because the diagram shows a double-stranded molecule similar to DNA, but the strands are incorrectly depicted as parallel instead of the correct anti-parallel orientation."},{"ref":"BI1717","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1717\n\nSeveral models of DNA were proposed before the current model was accepted in the 1950s. What was one limitation of the earlier DNA models?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ It was composed of repeating units of four nucleotides.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The amount of adenine was the same as the amount of thymine.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The sugar-phosphate backbone was oriented outwards.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Early models showed the bases oriented inwards.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\n**Choice A** refers to the tetranucleotide hypothesis, which proposed that DNA was made up of repeating units of four nucleotides in a fixed sequence. This model failed to explain the diversity and complexity of genetic information since it suggested a monotonous, non-variable structure for DNA.\n\nChargaff’s findings that the amount of adenine is equal to thymine and the amount of cytosine is equal to guanine were not a limitation.  It is a correct statement about the current model of DNA structure **(Choice B)**. \n\nThe sugar-phosphate backbone is oriented outwards, so this is not a limitation, but rather a true statement about the structure of DNA **(Choice C)**.\n\nThe bases are oriented inwards, so this too is a true statement about the current model of DNA structure **(Choice D)**"},{"ref":"BI1849","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1849\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nBelow is a diagram showing a DNA and RNA molecule, both of which are made of nucleotides.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/1849.png\" alt=\"1849\">\n\n1. Draw a labelled diagram of a nucleotide.$^*$ [3 marks]\n\n2. Describe the structural difference between the sugars found in DNA and RNA nucleotides.\n [1 mark]\n\n3. Outline how an RNA molecule is formed through the joining of nucleotide monomers. [2 marks]\n\n$^*$ *Note: Starting May 2028, this question may be assessed differently due to changes to the Biology guide, including the use of a data booklet.*","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ pentose sugar **OR** ribose **OR** deoxyribose drawn in the centre and correctly labelled [1 mark]\n $\\ce{ -> }$ phosphate group drawn attached to C5 of pentose sugar and labelled (C5 does not need to be labelled) [1 mark]\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nitrogenous base drawn correctly on C1 of the pentose sugar and labelled (C1 does not need to be labelled) [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI1849MSii.png\" alt=\"BI1849MSii\">\n\n2. *answer can be as annotated diagram*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ deoxyribose **OR** DNA sugar has a hydrogen atom **OR** H attached to C2 **WHEREAS** ribose **OR** RNA sugar has a hydroxyl group **OR** OH attached to C2 [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI1849MS2i.png\" alt=\"BI1849MS2i\">\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ joined together through condensation reactions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sugar-phosphate bond **OR** covalent bond forms between the nucleotides [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ bond forms between phosphate group of one nucleotide **AND** the 3' hydroxyl group **OR** C3 of the sugar **OR** ribose in next nucleotide [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n RNA is formed through **condensation reactions** ($\\checkmark$) where **sugar-phosphate bonds** join the **phosphate group** of **one nucleotide** to the **3' hydroxyl** group of the sugar in **another nucleotide** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1850","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1850\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/1850i.png\" alt=\"1850i\">\n\n1. Describe the role of DNA in storing genetic information. [2 marks]\n\n2. Outline the importance of the antiparallel arrangement of DNA strands in the structure of DNA. [3 marks]\n\n3. Outline the importance of the diversity of DNA base sequences in the process of evolution. [3 marks]\n\n4. Discuss the importance of the conservation of the genetic code in understanding universal common ancestry. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic code is stored in the sequence of nucleotide bases **OR** adenine, thymine, cytosine, guanine within the DNA molecule [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ groups of three nucleotides **OR** bases form codons, which correspond to specific amino acids [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sequence of codons determines the primary structure of proteins, which controls the organism’s traits and functions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ double-helix structure ensures stability of genetic information and allows for accurate replication during cell division [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic code is degenerate **OR** multiple codons can code for same amino acid providing some protection against mutations [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ genes can be turned on or off **OR** are used when needed **OR** can be expressed and repressed [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n DNA stores genetic information in the form of **codons**, which are **sequences** of **three nucleotide bases** ($\\checkmark$) that **code** for specific **amino acids** ($\\checkmark$). These codons **determine** the **structure of proteins**, which are essential for the **organism’s traits** and **functions** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ two DNA strands run in opposite directions **OR** one strand runs 5' to 3', while the other runs 3' to 5' [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allows the complementary base pairs **OR** adenine-thymine AND guanine-cytosine to align properly and form hydrogen bonds [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen bonding stabilises the double helix [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ antiparallel strands allow for the twisting of the DNA molecule [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ensures that the DNA molecule is stable and can store genetic information effectively [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The antiparallel arrangement means that **one DNA strand** runs **5' to 3'**, while the **other runs 3' to 5'** ($\\checkmark$). This allows the **complementary base pairs** to **align** correctly and form **hydrogen bonds** between adenine-thymine and guanine-cytosine ($\\checkmark$). These **hydrogen bonds** help **stabilise** the **double helix** and allow the **DNA to twist** into its characteristic structure ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ leads to genetic variation between individuals within a population [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ provides the basis for natural selection, as certain traits may offer a selective advantage in specific environments [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Individuals with advantageous traits are more likely to survive and reproduce, passing their favourable alleles **OR** genes to the next generation [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ over time, advantageous traits become more common, driving evolution and the development of new species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allows populations to adapt to changing environments, contributing to species' survival [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The diversity of DNA base sequences leads to **genetic variation** between **individuals** in a **population** ($\\checkmark$). This variation provides the basis for **natural selection**, where **certain traits** offer a selective **advantage** in **specific environments** ($\\checkmark$). Over time, **individuals** with **advantageous traits** are **more likely** to **survive, reproduce**, and **pass on** their **favourable genes** ($\\checkmark$), driving the process of evolution and the development of new species.\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ genetic code is nearly universal across all living organisms **OR** there are only a few minor variations in the genetic code observed in certain species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in nearly all organisms the same triplet codons specify the same amino acids [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ universality of the genetic code implies a shared ancestral origin for all organisms [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ used in phylogenetic analysis to reconstruct evolutionary relationships, providing strong evidence for universal common ancestry [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ conservation of the genetic code helps maintain the integrity of proteins **OR** reduces risk of mutations that could disrupt essential cellular functions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ despite evolutionary changes in species, the genetic code remains largely unchanged over time [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The conservation of the genetic code across all organisms suggests that all life shares a common ancestor, as the **same triplet codons** specify the **same amino acids** in **all species** ($\\checkmark$). This universality indicates that the **genetic code** has been **passed down** through **generations** with **little change** ($\\checkmark$), providing evidence for universal common ancestry."},{"ref":"BI2065","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2065\n\nHow many hydrogen bonds are there between the bases in a section of a strand of DNA with the base sequence GGC TAC CAC TCT and the bases on its complementary strand?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 24\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 31\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 30\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 36","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because a G-C base pairing uses 3 hydrogen bonds, whereas an A-T base pairing uses only 2. In GGCTACCACTCT there are $7 \\times 3 = 21$ bonds between C and G, plus $5 \\times 2 = 10$ bonds between A and T. 21 + 10 = 31.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it shows seven fewer than the correct number of hydrogen bonds.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because it shows one fewer than the correct number of hydrogen bonds.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because it shows five more than the correct number of hydrogen bonds."},{"ref":"BI2319","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2319\n\nWhich statement explains why DNA is evidence of common ancestry?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ All living organisms have an identical DNA base sequence.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ All living organisms contain DNA.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Closely related species contain the same alleles.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ More recently evolved species have more similar DNA.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because the presence of DNA in all organisms reflects a universal lineage, providing evidence of common ancestry. All organisms use the same four nucleotides (adenine, thymine, cytosine, and guanine) and the same genetic code to translate DNA into proteins, which supports the idea of a shared evolutionary origin.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because while all organisms share common sequences in their DNA, their base sequences are not identical. Differences in DNA sequences accumulate over time, and species with more recent common ancestors have more similar DNA.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because closely related species do not necessarily contain the same alleles. They share homologous genes and may have similar alleles, but their alleles often differ due to mutation and divergence after their lineages split. Also, this does not directly explain why DNA is evidence of common ancestry—rather, it describes a consequence of common ancestry.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because evolution does not progress in a linear ladder from “older” to “more recently evolved” species. All extant species are equally evolved. DNA similarity reflects how recently two species share a common ancestor, not how “recently evolved” they are. The wording is conceptually flawed, so it cannot explain why DNA provides evidence of common ancestry."},{"ref":"BI2567","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2567\n\nWhich of the following equations is true for the number of bases in a double-stranded DNA molecule?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ $1=\\dfrac{\\ce{A + T}}{\\ce{C + G}}$\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{\\ce{A}}{\\ce{C}}=\\dfrac{\\ce{G}}{\\ce{T}}$\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{A \\times T = C \\times G}$\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{\\ce{A}}{\\ce{T}}=\\dfrac{\\ce{C}}{\\ce{G}}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct. Chargaff’s rule states that the ratio of A:T and of C:G is 1:1. Therefore A/T = C/G as both sides of the equation should be 1.\n\n**Choice A** is not correct because within a double-strand of DNA different types of bases will exist in an equal percentage as their complementary base pair (e.g. % of C= % of G). However, the two sets of pairs will not necessarily be equal to each other.\\\n**Choice B** is not correct because A and C / T and G are not complementary base pairs, so will not exist in equal numbers to each other. Therefore it is unlikely that both sides of the equation are equal.\\\n**Choice C** is not correct because within a double-strand of DNA different types of bases will exist in an equal percentage as their complementary base pair (e.g. % of C = % of G). However, the two sets of pairs will not necessarily be equal to each other. Therefore, both sides of the equation are likely to be different."},{"ref":"BI2568","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2568\n\nThe table shows the number of bases in a single DNA strand that codes for a strand of mRNA. The strand contains no introns.\n\nThe letters X, Y and Z represent the percentage of each type of base in the mRNA.\n\n| Bases | Number of kilobases (kb) in the DNA strand | Percentage of same bases in the mRNA |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| Adenine | 31 | 23.3 |\n| Cytosine | 22 | X |\n| Guanine | 26 | Y |\n| Thymine | 24 | Z |\n\nWhich option shows the correct percentages of bases in the mRNA synthesised?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ **X** = 25.2%, **Y**= 21.4%, **Z** = 30.1%\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ **X** = 21.4%, **Y** = 25.2%, **Z** = 23.3%\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ **X** = 25.2%, **Y** = 21.4%, **Z** = 0%\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ **X** = 21.4%, **Y** = 25.2%, **Z** = 0%","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe correct answer is **Choice C** because there are 103 kb in total in the DNA strand. This means that in the DNA strand, 30.1% of bases are adenine ($\\dfrac{31}{103} \\times 100$%), 21.4% of bases are cytosine (22/103 x 100), 25.2% of bases are guanine ($\\dfrac{26}{103} \\times 100$%) and 23.3% of bases are thymine ($\\dfrac{24}{103} \\times 100$%). In the mRNA, these bases will be matched with their complementary base pair, so that the percentage value in the cytosine row will be the same as the percentage of guanine, so X = 25.2%. Likewise, Y will be the cytosine percentage (21.4%). Z is 0%, as there are no thymine bases found in mRNA, only uracil.\n\n**Choice A** is not correct because even though X and Y are showing the right percentages, Z is incorrect as it is showing the percentage of uracil, not thymine. **Choice B** is not correct because the percentages shown are for the DNA not for the complementary base pair of that base (which would be found in the mRNA), Z is also incorrect as it is showing the percentage of uracil, not thymine. **Choice D** is not correct because the percentages shown are for the DNA not for the complementary base pair of that base (which would be found in the mRNA)."},{"ref":"BI2573","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2573\n\nThe graph shows the results of an experiment by Hershey and Chase. Bacteria (*E. coli*) were infected with virus particles labelled with either (radioactive phosphorus) $\\ce{^{32}P}$ or (radioactive sulfur) $\\ce{^{35}S}$. A blender was used to remove attached viruses from the bacterial cells and samples collected from the suspension were centrifuged to record the percentage of isotope remaining in the cells. \\\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Infected bacteria” refers to the percentage of live bacteria that survived the blending process.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/2573a.png\" alt=\"2573a\">\n\nWhich of the following statements explain(s) why the extracellular $\\ce{^{32}P}$ increased as the running time in the blender increased?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n | | |\n | :-- | :-- |\n | I. | When bacteriophages infect bacteria, not all viral particles successfully inject their DNA into the host cells. Some phages remain attached to the cell surface or fail to transfer their DNA. |\n | II. | During blending, the infected bacteria take up more $\\ce{^{32}P}$. |\n | III. | During blending, some of the infected bacterial cells are lysed (the reason the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$infected bacteria” percentage decreases). The lysed cells release their $\\ce{^{32}P}$. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because not all bacteriophages successfully inject their DNA into the bacterial host cells during infection. Some phages may remain attached to the bacterial surface or fail to inject their DNA. During blending, these surface-bound phages are detached, and their $\\ce{^{32}P}$-labelled DNA is released into the extracellular medium, contributing to the increase in extracellular $\\ce{^{32}P}$ in the graph.\n\n**Statement II** is false. Bacteria do not \"take up more” $\\ce{^{32}P}$ during blending. The blending process is designed to remove surface-bound phages and shear unattached components, not to facilitate further DNA uptake by bacterial cells.\n\n**Statement III** is true. Some of the infected bacterial cells can be lysed during the blending process. When this happens, the $\\ce{^{32}P}$-labelled viral DNA inside these lysed cells is released into the extracellular environment, further increasing extracellular $\\ce{^{32}P}$.\n\n**Choice A** is correct because it is the only choice that includes both the true **Statements I** and **III**.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because it only includes one true statement.\n\n**Choice B** and **D** are incorrect because they include the false **Statement II**."},{"ref":"BI2644","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2644\n\nThe following dichotomous key can be used to identify unknown molecules:\n\n1\\. Does the molecule have a sugar linked to a nitrogenous base?\n\n-   -Yes: Go to Step 2\\\n    -No: It is molecule V\n\n2\\. Does the sugar have a hydroxyl group (-OH) at the 2' carbon?\n\n-   -Yes: Go to Step 3\\\n    -No: It is a type of molecule W\n\n3\\. Is the number of phosphate groups present three?\n\n-   -Yes: It is molecule Y\\\n    -No: It is molecule Z\n\nWhich row shows the correct identification of the molecules?\n\n| | Molecule V | Molecule W | Molecule Y | Molecule Z |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Ribonucleotide | Deoxyribonucleotide | ATP | ADP |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Deoxyribonucleotide | Ribonucleotide | ATP | ADP |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Ribose sugar |Ribonucleotide | ATP | Deoxyribonucleotide |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ |  Ribose sugar | Deoxyribonucleotide | ATP | ADP |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because all four identifications follow the dichotomous key.\n\nMolecule V is free ribose. The “No” branch at Step 1 identifies a molecule that does not have a sugar linked to a nitrogenous base. All nucleotides contain a sugar linked to a nitrogenous base, so free ribose is the only possible identification within the stated set.\n\nMolecule W is a deoxyribonucleotide. It contains a sugar linked to a nitrogenous base, but its sugar lacks a hydroxyl group (–OH) at the 2′ carbon. Deoxyribose has a hydrogen atom at this position instead of the hydroxyl group found in ribose. Therefore, W is a nucleotide containing deoxyribose.\n\nMolecule Y is ATP. Its sugar has a hydroxyl group at the 2′ carbon, identifying it as ribose. The stem states that all ribose-containing nucleotides in this set contain adenine. With three phosphate groups, Y is therefore adenosine triphosphate (ATP).\n\nMolecule Z is ADP. Like Y, it contains ribose and adenine, but it does not contain three phosphate groups. Since the stem restricts these nucleotides to either two or three phosphate groups, Z must contain two and is therefore adenosine diphosphate (ADP).\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because molecule V should be ribose sugar (not a ribonucleotide), and molecule W should be a deoxyribonucleotide (not a ribonucleotide).\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because molecule V should be ribose sugar (not a deoxyribonucleotide), and molecule W should be a deoxyribonucleotide (not a ribonucleotide).\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because molecule W should be a deoxyribonucleotide (not a ribonucleotide), and molecule Z should be ADP (not a deoxyribonucleotide)."},{"ref":"BI2645","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2645\n\nRefer to the diagram showing a section of a DNA strand.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/Picture1.png\" alt=\"Picture1\">\n\nWhich carbon on the deoxyribose sugar is bonded to the phosphate group of the adjacent nucleotide, in the sugar-phosphate bond?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Carbon 1'\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Carbon 2'\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Carbon 3'\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Carbon 4'","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/ANS.png\" alt=\"ANS\">\n\nThe psugar-phosphate bond forms between the 3' carbon of the sugar in one nucleotide (which has an –OH group), and the 5' phosphate group of the next nucleotide. So, the 3' carbon is directly involved in forming the bond that links nucleotides within a single strand of DNA or RNA, making **Choice C** correct.\n\nThe 1' carbon of the sugar is the attachment point for the nitrogenous base, not for the phosphate group; **Choice A** is incorrect.\n\nIn deoxyribose, the 2' carbon lacks an –OH group (it has just –H), distinguishing DNA from RNA. It is not involved in bonding to phosphate, so **Choice B** is incorrect.\n\nThe 4' carbon connects to the 3' carbon and the 5' carbon within the sugar ring, but does not form bonds with the phosphate group; **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI2646","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2646\n\nWhich of the following best supports the idea that DNA is the universal genetic material in living organisms?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ All organisms have the same number of chromosomes\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ All organisms translate proteins using ribosomes\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ All organisms use DNA to pass on genetic information\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ All organisms reproduce sexually","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because the use of DNA is universal across all living organisms.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because chromosome number varies widely across different species.\n\n**Choice B** is true, but it is not a statement specific to DNA being the genetic material.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because not all organisms reproduce sexually."},{"ref":"BI2647","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2647\n\nWhich of the following is the correct pairing of a nucleotide component and its description?\n\n| | Component | Description |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Sugar | Varies between RNA and DNA to form genetic code |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Base | Provides structure to the backbone |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Phosphate | Forms bonds with the base and sugar |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Base | Varies along nucleic acid to form genetic code|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because the sequence of nitrogenous bases is what encodes genetic information in DNA and RNA. This is the only row with a fully correct and precise description.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because, while the sugar differs between DNA (deoxyribose) and RNA (ribose), it does not form the genetic code. The code is carried in the base sequence, not the sugar.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because Bases are not part of the backbone (which is made of sugar–phosphate units). Bases project from the backbone and form the coding region, not the structure.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because phosphate forms bonds with sugars, not with bases. Bases attach to the sugar (at carbon 1'), not to phosphate."},{"ref":"BI2666","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2666\n\nWhat characteristic of DNA allows it to serve as a template for both DNA replication and mRNA synthesis?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The genetic code in DNA is universal across all organisms\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrogen bonding enables the correct alignment of deoxyribonucleotides and ribonucleotides in newly synthesised strands\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ DNA’s double helix structure means one strand can be replicated at the same time as the other is being transcribed\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The strong sugar-phosphate backbone","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct. During both replication and transcription, nucleotides are aligned along the DNA template strand according to base-pairing rules. Hydrogen bonds form between the nitrogenous bases of the template DNA strand and the incoming deoxyribonucleotides (during replication) or ribonucleotides (in transcription).\\\nThe genetic code being universal means that the same codons code for the same amino acids in all organisms. However, this relates to protein synthesis, not to how DNA acts as a template for replication or transcription; **Choice A** is incorrect.\\\n**Choice C** is incorrect because replication and transcription can’t occur on opposite strands simultaneously. \\\n**Choice D** is incorrect because while the sugar-phosphate backbone is a feature of both DNA and RNA strands that provides structural stability, it does not explain how DNA can interact with both DNA and RNA nucleotides."},{"ref":"BI2667","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2667\n\nWhich of the following molecules are classified as nucleic acids?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | ATP |\n    | II. | rRNA |\n    | III. | DNA |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is false. ATP (adenosine triphosphate) is a nucleotide, not a nucleic acid. It is a single molecule, not a long chain (polymer) of nucleotides, which is what defines nucleic acids like DNA and RNA. \\\n**Statement II** is true. Ribosomal RNA (rRNA) is a type of RNA, which is one of the two major types of nucleic acids (the other being DNA). It is a polymer of ribonucleotides, synthesised during transcription, and plays a role in protein synthesis. \\\n**Statement III** is true. DNA is a polymer of deoxyribonucleotides. It serves as the hereditary material in most organisms.\\\nNote that the correct answer includes the two molecules whose names end in ‘NA’, which stands for nucleic acid - DNA and RNA.\\\n**Choice C** is, therefore, the correct answer as it only includes the two true **Statements II** and **III**.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **D** are incorrect as they include the false **Statement I**."},{"ref":"BI2842","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2842\n\nWhich of the following features of DNA most directly underpins its virtually limitless capacity to encode genetic information?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The use of four chemically distinct nitrogenous bases arranged in long chains.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Semiconservative replication via complementary base pairing, which preserves sequence diversity.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The covalent sugar-phosphate backbone’s antiparallel orientation, which fixes strand directionality.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Coiling of DNA into chromosomes, which aids efficient storage.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because the linear sequence of four different nucleotides (A, T, C and G) joined by sugar-phosphate bonds can form an almost infinite number of unique combinations, providing DNA with its enormous information-storage capacity. The human genome is about 3,000,000,000 nucleotides long, so there would be** $\\mathbf{4^{3,000,000,000}}$ theoretical combinations of nucleotides.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because complementary base pairing ensures faithful replication, but does not itself generate new sequences.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because the antiparallel backbone ensures the strands are arranged in opposite directions, but does not contribute to the sequence diversity.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because coiling allows DNA to be compacted and stored efficiently within the nucleus, but it does not directly contribute to the *capacity* to encode genetic information. While necessary for packaging long DNA molecules, coiling does not influence the number of unique sequences that DNA can carry or its ability to store diverse instructions."},{"ref":"BI2851","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2851\n\nThe table shows the base composition of three DNA samples from different organisms:\n\n| Sample | %A | %T | %G | %C |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| X | 24 | 24 | 26 | 26 |\n| Y | 21 | 21 | 49 | 49 |\n| Z | 20 | 30 | 20 | 30 |\n\nWhich sample(s) most likely represent double-stranded DNA molecules?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ X only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ X and Y only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Y and Z only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ X, Y and Z","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nTo identify double-stranded DNA, two rules must be satisfied:\n\n-   1\\. Base pairing rule: %A = %T and %G = %C\n\n-   2\\. Total base composition must add up to 100%\n\nSample **X** has equal proportions of adenine and thymine (24% each) and equal proportions of guanine and cytosine (26% each). The total adds up to 100%, which satisfies all criteria for double-stranded DNA. This sample is therefore most likely double-stranded.\n\nSample **Y** also has equal proportions of A and T (21%) and G and C (49%), but the total adds up to 140%, which is biologically impossible. This does not follow the base composition rule for double-stranded DNA.\n\nSample **Z** has mismatched base pairs: A $\\not=$ T and G $\\not=$ C. Although the total adds up to 100%, the base-pairing rules are not followed. This suggests that Z is likely single-stranded DNA.\n\n**Choice A** is therefore the correct answer because only sample **X** likely represents a double-stranded DNA molecule.\\\n**Choices B, C** and **D** are all incorrect as they include one or more samples that are not double-stranded."},{"ref":"BI2997","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2997\n\nWhich statement correctly describes the significance of the 5' $\\rarr$ 3' directionality in biological processes?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | DNA replication can only synthesise new strands in the 5' $\\rarr$ 3' direction. |\n    | II. | RNA polymerase moves along the template strand in the 3' $\\rarr$ 5' direction during transcription. |\n    | III. | Ribosomes translate mRNA by reading codons sequentially from the 5' end to the 3' end. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nDNA polymerase can only add nucleotides to the 3' hydroxyl end, meaning all new DNA strands are synthesised in the 5' $\\rarr$ 3' direction. This is why the leading strand is continuous and the lagging strand forms Okazaki fragments. **Statement I** is true.\n\nDuring transcription, RNA polymerase moves along the DNA template strand in the 3' $\\rarr$ 5' direction, so that it can synthesise RNA in the 5' $\\rarr$ 3' direction, matching the required biochemical polarity of nucleotide addition. **Statement II** is true.\n\nRibosomes translate an mRNA molecule by reading codons from the 5' end toward the 3' end, beginning at the start codon (AUG). This directionality ensures correct polypeptide elongation and alignment with the template. **Statement III** is true.\n\nSince all three statements are true, **Choice D** is the correct answer."},{"ref":"BI3204","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI3204\n\nA single, linear RNA polymer contains 300 nucleotide monomers.\n\nHow many condensation reactions were required to form the polymer, and how many water molecules were released?\n\nA. $\\hspace{1em}$ 300 condensation reactions; 300 water molecules\n\nB. $\\hspace{1em}$ 300 condensation reactions; 600 water molecules\n\nC. $\\hspace{1em}$ 150 condensation reactions; 150 water molecules\n\nD. $\\hspace{1em}$ 299 condensation reactions; 299 water molecules","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nJoining *n* monomers into a single linear polymer requires *n−1* bonds to be formed. Therefore, joining 300 nucleotides requires 299 condensation reactions. In the simplified condensation model, each reaction releases one water molecule, so 299 water molecules are released. **Choice D** is correct because 299 links are required, resulting in 299 condensation reactions and 299 water molecules.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the number of links formed is one fewer than the number of monomers.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because each condensation reaction releases one water molecule in this model, not two.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because there is no reason to divide the number of nucleotides by two."},{"ref":"BI3205","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI3205\n\nDNA stores genetic information using a 4-symbol system, because each position in the sequence can contain one of four bases: A, T, C, or G.\n\nThe graph compares the number of possible sequences in a 2-symbol system and a 4-symbol system at sequence lengths of 2, 5 and 10 symbols. The graph uses a logarithmic scale on the y-axis.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI3205.png\" alt=\"BI3205\">\n\nWhat does the graph suggest about the capacity of DNA for storing information?\n\nA. $\\hspace{1em}$ A two-symbol system stores more information because fewer symbols are used.\n\nB. $\\hspace{1em}$ The two systems have the same information capacity because both use a finite number of symbols.\n\nC. $\\hspace{1em}$ Using four symbols rather than two symbols produces more possible sequences of the same length, giving DNA a greater capacity for storing information.\n\nD. $\\hspace{1em}$ Using four symbols produces only four times as many possible sequences as using two symbols, regardless of sequence length.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nFor a sequence of length *n*, a 2-symbol system can produce $2^n$ possible sequences, whereas a 4-symbol system can produce $4^n$. At a sequence length of 10, these systems produce 1,024 and 1,048,576 possible sequences, respectively. Therefore, the 4-symbol DNA system produces 1,024 times as many sequences of this length, demonstrating how four bases arranged in sequences give DNA an enormous capacity for storing information. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because using fewer possible symbols at each position produces fewer, not more, possible sequences.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the number of symbols available at each position affects the number of possible sequences.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the difference increases with sequence length; the 4-symbol system produces 1,024 times as many possible sequences at a length of 10, not four times as many."},{"ref":"BI3206","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI3206\n\nThe diagram below shows a replication fork with exposed bases on one template strand.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI3206.png\" alt=\"BI3206\">\n\nWhat is the sequence of the new DNA strand shown by the empty boxes, written from 5' to 3'?\n\nA. $\\hspace{1em}$ 5'–ATGCCT–3'\n\nB. $\\hspace{1em}$ 5'–TACGGA–3'\n\nC. $\\hspace{1em}$ 5'–UACGGA–3'\n\nD. $\\hspace{1em}$ 5'–AUGCCU–3'","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe template strand runs from 3' to 5' and has the sequence 3'–TACGGA–5'. Complementary base pairing in DNA places A opposite T and G opposite C. Because the new strand is antiparallel to the template, its sequence is 5'–ATGCCT–3'. Therefore, **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because it repeats the template sequence rather than providing its complementary sequence.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because it is not complementary to the template and contains uracil, which occurs in RNA rather than DNA.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because it is a complementary RNA sequence, as shown by the presence of uracil; it is not the new DNA strand produced during DNA replication."},{"ref":"BI3207","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI3207\n\nWhich of the following are differences between DNA and RNA?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | DNA contains uracil, whereas RNA contains thymine. |\n    | II. | DNA contains deoxyribose, whereas RNA contains ribose.  |\n    | III. | DNA is usually double-stranded, whereas RNA is usually single-stranded.  |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n**Statement I** is false because the bases have been reversed: DNA contains thymine, whereas RNA contains uracil.\n\n**Statement II** is true because DNA nucleotides contain deoxyribose, whereas RNA nucleotides contain ribose.\n\n**Statement III** is true because DNA is usually double-stranded, whereas RNA is usually single-stranded.\n\nTherefore, **Choice C** is correct because it includes both true **Statements II** and **III**.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it omits the true **Statement III**.\n\n**Choices B** and **D** are incorrect because they include the false **Statement I**."},{"ref":"BI3208","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI3208\n\nOne strand of a DNA molecule has the following base composition:\n\nA = 22%, T = 36%, G = 15%, C = 27%\n\nWhich principle can be used to determine the percentage of guanine in **the other strand** of the DNA molecule, and what is this percentage?\n\nA. $\\hspace{1em}$ Antiparallel orientation; 27%\n\nB. $\\hspace{1em}$ Complementary base pairing; 27%\n\nC. $\\hspace{1em}$ Complementary base pairing; 15%\n\nD. $\\hspace{1em}$ Antiparallel orientation; 15%","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nDuring complementary base pairing, guanine pairs with cytosine. Because cytosine makes up 27% of the original strand, guanine must make up 27% of the other strand. Therefore, **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it gives the correct percentage but identifies the wrong principle. Antiparallel orientation means that the two DNA strands run in opposite directions: one runs from 5′ to 3′, while the other runs from 3′ to 5′ alongside it. This describes the orientation of the sugar–phosphate backbones, not which bases pair together. Therefore, antiparallel orientation alone cannot be used to determine the percentage of guanine.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because it identifies the correct principle but gives the wrong percentage. The 15% guanine in the original strand pairs with cytosine, so it determines the percentage of cytosine, not guanine, in the other strand. To determine the guanine percentage, we must use the original strand’s 27% cytosine.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because both the principle and the percentage are wrong. Antiparallel orientation describes strand direction rather than the relationship between base percentages. Complementary base pairing determines that guanine makes up 27% of the other strand, not 15%."},{"ref":"BI3209","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI3209\n\nA gene encoding green fluorescent protein (GFP) from a jellyfish is inserted into a bacterium by gene transfer. The bacterium’s ribosomes successfully produce functional GFP.\n\nWhat does this demonstrate about the genetic code?\n\nA. $\\hspace{1em}$ Bacteria and jellyfish have identical sets of genes, allowing the protein to be produced.\n\nB. $\\hspace{1em}$ The genetic code is conserved because the same codons generally specify the same amino acids in bacteria and jellyfish, supporting universal common ancestry.\n\nC. $\\hspace{1em}$ The bacterium evolved the ability to read jellyfish codons after exposure to the jellyfish gene.\n\nD. $\\hspace{1em}$ The genetic code differs between bacteria and jellyfish, but their ribosomes translate codons into the same amino acids.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe bacterium’s protein-synthesis machinery can correctly translate the jellyfish gene because the same codons generally specify the same amino acids in both organisms. This conservation of the genetic code across different life forms supports the conclusion that they inherited the code from a universal common ancestor. **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because bacteria and jellyfish share the same genetic code, but they do not have identical sets of genes.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because the bacterium did not acquire a new ability after exposure to the gene. Its existing translation machinery could already interpret the codons.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because if the codons are translated into the same amino acids, the genetic code is conserved rather than different."},{"ref":"BI3210","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI3210\n\nWhich of the following statements about nucleic acids are correct?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | The coding of genetic information depends on the sequence of bases.|\n    | II. | Adenine, cytosine and guanine occur in both DNA and RNA. |\n    | III. | Covalent bonds form between the phosphate group of one nucleotide and the pentose sugar of a neighbouring nucleotide.|\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n**Statement I** is true because genetic information is encoded by the sequence of nitrogenous bases.\n\n**Statement II** is true because adenine, cytosine and guanine occur in both DNA and RNA. DNA contains thymine as its fourth base, whereas RNA contains uracil.\n\n**Statement III** is true because covalent bonds between phosphate groups and pentose sugars in neighbouring nucleotides produce the continuous sugar–phosphate backbone of a nucleic acid strand.\n\nTherefore, **Choice D** is correct.\n\n**Choices A, B,** and **C** are all incorrect because they omit one or more of the true statements."},{"ref":"BI3211","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI3211\n\nThe table compares characteristics of bonding in the sugar–phosphate backbone and between complementary bases in DNA.\n\n| Property | Sugar–phosphate backbone | Cross-strand base pairing |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| Type of bond | **P** | **Q** |\n| Relative strength of individual bonds | Strong | **R** |\n| Role | **S** | Links complementary bases |\n\nWhat are P, Q, R and S?\n\n| | P | Q | R | S |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Covalent | Hydrogen | Weak | Forms the structural framework of each strand |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Hydrogen | Covalent | Strong | Forms the structural framework of each strand |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Covalent | Hydrogen | Strong | Encodes genetic information |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Hydrogen | Covalent | Weak | Encodes genetic information|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nCovalent bonds link the sugars and phosphate groups of neighbouring nucleotides, forming a strong, continuous sugar-phosphate backbone. Therefore, P is covalent, and S is “forms a continuous structural backbone.”\n\nComplementary bases on opposite strands are linked by hydrogen bonds. Individual hydrogen bonds are relatively weak compared with covalent bonds, although many hydrogen bonds collectively help to stabilise the DNA molecule. Therefore, Q is hydrogen and R is weak. **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because it reverses the bond types: the backbone contains covalent bonds, while complementary bases are linked by hydrogen bonds.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because individual hydrogen bonds are relatively weak, and genetic information is encoded by the base sequence rather than by the sugar–phosphate backbone.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because it reverses the bond types and incorrectly states that the backbone encodes genetic information."},{"ref":"BI3212","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI3212\n\nA student states, “ATP molecules and nucleic acid monomers are nucleotides.”\n\nWhich response correctly evaluates this statement?\n\nA. $\\hspace{1em}$ Correct because all nucleotides contain more than one phosphate group.\n\nB. $\\hspace{1em}$ Correct because both ATP and nucleic acid monomers consist of a nitrogenous base, a pentose sugar and one or more phosphate groups.\n\nC. $\\hspace{1em}$ Incorrect because ATP contains a hexose sugar, whereas nucleic acid monomers contain pentose sugars.\n\nD. $\\hspace{1em}$ Incorrect because ATP contains an amino acid rather than a nitrogenous base.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nATP consists of the nitrogenous base adenine, the pentose sugar ribose and three phosphate groups. The monomers from which DNA and RNA are formed also contain a nitrogenous base, a pentose sugar and phosphate. Therefore, ATP and nucleic acid monomers can both be classified as nucleotides. **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because nucleotides do not all contain more than one phosphate group. A nucleotide consists of a nitrogenous base, a pentose sugar and one or more phosphate groups. ATP contains three phosphate groups, but the nucleotides incorporated into nucleic-acid polymers each contribute one phosphate group to the sugar–phosphate backbone.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because ATP contains ribose, which is a pentose sugar rather than a hexose sugar.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because ATP contains the nitrogenous base adenine, not an amino acid."},{"ref":"BI3213","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI3213\n\nThe diagram shows three domains of living organisms - bacteria, archaea and eukaryotes, together with a separate group of RNA viruses.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Nucleic%20Acids/images/BI3213.png\" alt=\"BI3213\">\n\nWhat conclusion is supported by the diagram?\n\nA. $\\hspace{1em}$ RNA viruses should be classified as living organisms because they contain heritable genetic information.\n\nB. $\\hspace{1em}$ DNA evolved from RNA, so all early living organisms used RNA as their genetic material.\n\nC. $\\hspace{1em}$ All living organisms use DNA as their genetic material, whereas some viruses use RNA and are not considered living.\n\nD. $\\hspace{1em}$ The presence of genetic material is sufficient for an entity to be considered living.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nBacteria, archaea and eukaryotes represent the three domains of living organisms, and all use DNA as their genetic material. Some viruses, including influenza viruses and HIV, use RNA as their genetic material. However, viruses are not considered living organisms. Therefore, the occurrence of RNA genomes in some viruses does not contradict the statement that DNA is the genetic material of all living organisms. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because containing heritable genetic information is not, by itself, sufficient for an entity to be considered living.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the diagram does not show that DNA evolved from RNA or that the earliest living organisms used RNA as their genetic material.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because viruses contain genetic material but are not considered living organisms."},{"ref":"BI3214","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI3214\n\nA newly discovered entity  has RNA rather than DNA as its genetic material and can replicate only inside host cells.\n\nWhich statement best evaluates this discovery in relation to the principle that DNA is the genetic material of all living organisms?\n\nA. $\\hspace{1em}$ It disproves the principle because any entity capable of replication is classified as a living organism.\n\nB. $\\hspace{1em}$ It disproves the principle because living organisms can use either DNA or RNA as their genetic material.\n\nC. $\\hspace{1em}$ It does not disprove the principle because the entity could be an RNA virus, which is not considered a living organism.\n\nD. $\\hspace{1em}$ It does not disprove the principle because RNA cannot function as genetic material.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe entity has characteristics consistent with an RNA virus: it contains RNA and depends on host cells for replication. Viruses are not considered living organisms, so the discovery does not contradict the principle that DNA is the genetic material of all living organisms. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the ability to replicate inside host cells is not sufficient for an entity to be considered living.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because the presence of an RNA virus does not prove that living organisms can use RNA as their genetic material.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because some viruses use RNA as their genetic material. These viruses do not contradict the principle because they are not considered living organisms—not because RNA cannot carry genetic information."}]},{"name":"Origin of Cells","questions":[{"ref":"BI0419","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0419\n\nWhy is it difficult for scientists to test hypotheses about the origin of the first cells?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The conditions on pre-biotic Earth cannot be accurately replicated\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    The first cells are still alive, but are hard to study\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    Fossil evidence of protocells is abundant but hard to interpret\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    Protocells did not contain nucleic acids","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nScientists face significant challenges replicating the exact environmental conditions of early Earth because our understanding of its atmosphere, temperature, pressure, and chemical composition billions of years ago is limited; **Choice A** is correct.\n\nThe first cells appeared billions of years ago and are now extinct. Modern cells are descendants but not the original first cells; **Choice B** is incorrect.\nThere is very limited or no direct fossil evidence of the first protocells because they were microscopic, soft-bodied, and lacked durable structures that fossilise well. The lack, rather than the abundance, of fossil evidence is a key challenge to testing the hypothesis about the origin of the first cells; **Choice C** is incorrect.\nCurrent scientific understanding strongly supports that protocells contained early forms of genetic material, such as RNA molecules. RNA is believed to have played a dual role as both a genetic material and a catalyst (ribozymes), making it central to hypotheses like the RNA world hypothesis.\nNucleic acids are crucial because they enable self-replication and heredity, essential for the evolution of life. **Choice D** contradicts the accepted hypotheses and is therefore incorrect."},{"ref":"BI0421","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0421\n\nWhat provides evidence that all life on Earth evolved from the same population of cells?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Eukaryotic cells evolved from prokaryotic cells.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    Both mitochondria and prokaryotes have a cell wall.\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    Both eukaryotes and bacteria contain histones.\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    The genetic code is universal.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAll 64 codons of the genetic code are present in all organisms, apart from minor variations indicating that all life evolved from the same original cells  (**Choice D**).\n\nEukaryotic cells evolved from prokaryotic cells (**Choice A**) is incorrect as this is the endosymbiotic theory. **Choice B** is incorrect as mitochondria do not have a cell wall. **Choice C** is incorrect as bacteria do not have histones."},{"ref":"BI0425","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0425\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nThe diagram below shows the apparatus used in the experiments conducted by Miller and Urey.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Origin%20of%20Cells/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n[Source: Carny. (2008, February 4). File: Miller-Urey Experiment-EN.SVG. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved May 20, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Miller-Urey_experiment-en.svg.  Copyright free]\n\nDescribe how this experiment showed that the origin of life was possible on early Earth. [3 marks]","markScheme":"Any **three**  of the following:\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ ammonia (NH$_3$), methane (CH$_4$) and Hydrogen (H$_2$) were the major gases present in the early atmosphere  [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ heat was used to simulate higher temperatures [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ steam was passed through ammonia (NH$_3$), methane (CH$_4$) and Hydrogen (H$_2$) **OR**  through a mixture of compounds     [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ electricity was used to simulate lightning [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ resulting mixture was cooled down [1 mark]\n\n$\\ce{ -> }$ the amino acids **OR** organic carbon compounds needed for life were produced [1 mark]\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nMiller and Urey conducted experiments which showed that the compounds present in the **early atmosphere**  (**methane, ammonia and hydrogen** ($\\checkmark$)) could be used to make the organic carbon compounds necessary for life.  They did this by heating up water and **passing the steam** ($\\checkmark$) through ammonia, methane and hydrogen and by using **electricity to simulate the lightning** ($\\checkmark$).  They found that these conditions **created amino acids** ($\\checkmark$) and other organic carbon compounds."},{"ref":"BI0832","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0832\n\nIn the early 19th century, vitalism was a widely accepted theory. This theory suggested that organic compounds, those found in living organisms, could only be produced through a \"vital force\" present in living matter.\n\nWhat was the role of urea in falsifying the theory of vitalism?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Urea is produced by kidneys, indicating that living things can make organic compounds.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Urea can be produced in laboratories, indicating that living things cannot synthesize organic compounds.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Urea can be produced in laboratories, indicating that not only living things can synthesize organic compounds.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Urea is produced by the kidneys, indicating that not only laboratories can make organic compounds.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe theory of vitalism states that only living things can synthesise organic molecules. In 1953 Miller and Urey set up apparatus to simulate the conditions believed to be present on early Earth. Using these apparatus, they managed to produce urea, an organic compound, in the laboratory, which falsified this theory of vitalism. Therefore **Choice C** is correct.\n\nThe fact that urea is produced by the kidneys (**Choices A** and **D**) does not falsify the theory of vitalism but rather supports it. The production of urea in laboratories does not mean it cannot be synthesised by living things (**Choice B**)."},{"ref":"BI0947","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0947\n\nWhich of the following statements support the hypothesis that RNA was the first genetic material?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | RNA nucleotides can polymerise under certain prebiotic conditions. |\n    | II. | RNA is chemically more stable than DNA.|\n    | III. | Some RNA molecules can catalyse chemical reactions. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is correct because RNA nucleotides can polymerise to form RNA under certain prebiotic conditions.\n\n**Statement II** is incorrect because RNA is generally less chemically stable than DNA. The hydroxyl group attached to the 2′ carbon of ribose makes RNA more susceptible to hydrolysis. Greater stability, therefore, helps explain why DNA subsequently became the principal long-term store of genetic information; it does not support the claim that RNA is chemically more stable.\n\n**Statement III** is correct because some RNA molecules, called ribozymes, can catalyse chemical reactions. RNA could therefore potentially both store genetic information and catalyse reactions before DNA and protein enzymes evolved.\n\n**Choice B** is correct because it only includes the true **Statement I** and **III**.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because only one of the true statements, **Statement I** is included.\n**Choices C** and **D** are incorrect because they both include the false **Statement II**."},{"ref":"BI0948","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0948\n\nWhich row correctly describes the formation of vesicles when phospholipids are mixed with water?\n\n| | Structure of vesicle | Feature of fatty acid tail | Formation in water |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | monolayer | hydrophilic | spontaneous |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | bilayer | hydrophobic | spontaneous |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | monolayer | hydrophilic | induced |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | bilayer | hydrophobic | induced |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWhen phospholipids are mixed with water, they can spontaneously assemble into bilayers because they are amphipathic. Their hydrophilic phosphate heads face the surrounding water and the vesicle's aqueous contents, while their hydrophobic fatty acid tails are positioned together within the membrane's centre, away from water. The bilayer can close to form a spherical vesicle without requiring external induction. Therefore, **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choices A** and **C** are incorrect because vesicle membranes are phospholipid bilayers rather than monolayers, and fatty acid tails are hydrophobic rather than hydrophilic.\n**Choice C** is incorrect because, although the membrane is a bilayer and its fatty acid tails are hydrophobic, its assembly is spontaneous rather than induced."},{"ref":"BI1068","marks":"11","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1068\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nBelow is a diagram of *Euglena* a photosynthetic protist. It is a living cell which can carry out all the functions of life.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Origin%20of%20Cells/images/BI1068.png\" alt=\"BI1068\">\n\n1.\n 1. State which function of life the flagellum is adapted for. [1 mark]\n\n 2. State which function of life is enabled by the contractile vacuole. [1 mark]\n\n2. Outline why viruses are not regarded as living organisms. [2 marks]\n\n3. State two main principles of the cell theory [2 marks]\n\n4. Explain how the properties of phospholipids mean they naturally form vesicles when in aqueous solution. [2 marks]\n\n5. Explain how the formation of vesicles was important in the evolution of cells. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ movement [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The flagellum allows the *Euglena* to **move** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n1.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ homeostasis **OR** control of a constant internal environment [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The contractile vacuole allows the Euglena to carry out the **homeostatic process** ($\\checkmark$) of osmoregulation.\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ consist of genetic material enclosed in a protein coat **OR** capsid **OR** lack cellular structures **OR** organelles **OR** examples of organelles [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cannot carry out metabolic processes on their own **OR** cannot carry out homeostasis [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ depend on host cells to replicate **OR** to produce new viral particles **OR** cannot reproduce independently [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ lack the ability to respond to stimuli **OR** adapt to changes in their environment on their own [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Viruses are regarded as non-living as they **lack cellular structures** ($\\checkmark$), such as organelles and cell membranes. They are **unable** to carry out **metabolic reactions** ($\\checkmark$), such as respiration. They also **cannot reproduce independently** ($\\checkmark$), they have to enter a cell and use the host’s cell machinery to form new viral particles.\n\n3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cells are the smallest units of life **OR** the cell is the basic unit of structure and function in living organisms [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ all living organisms are composed of one or more cells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ all cells arise from pre-existing cells **OR** through cell division [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Two principles of the cell theory are that **all organisms are composed of one or more cells** ($\\checkmark$), and **all cells arise from pre-existing cells** ($\\checkmark$) through cell division.\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ phospholipids have a hydrophilic OR polar head OR polar phosphate group which is attracted to water [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ phospholipids have a hydrophobic **OR** non-polar tail that is repelled by water [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ phospholipids are amphipathic [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ form double layer in aqueous solution **OR** water, with hydrophobic tails in centre and hydrophilic heads facing outwards into aqueous environment [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ spontaneous process driven by the tendency to minimise interactions between hydrophobic regions and water [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as lipid bilayer forms it curves creating a closed spherical structure **OR** vesicle [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **amphipathic** ($\\checkmark$) nature of phospholipids, with **hydrophilic phosphate heads** and **hydrophobic fatty acid tails**, causes them to self-organise into **bilayers in aqueous solutions** ($\\checkmark$). The **phosphate heads** stick outwards **into** the **aqueous environment** as they are attracted to water ($\\checkmark$), and the **hydrophobic tails** arrange themselves in the **centre of the bilayer** as they are repelled by water ($\\checkmark$). As the **bilayer** forms it **curves** ($\\checkmark$) to form **closed vesicles**.\n\n5. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ probably led to formation of protocells **OR** formation of early vesicles may have led to protocells [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ formation of vesicles **OR** lipid bilayer structure led to development of cell membranes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ plasma membranes separate internal cellular environment from external surroundings, allowing for controlled interactions with the environment [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ allow compartmentalisation of different cellular functions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ as cells became more complex, different vesicles or membrane-bound organelles evolved to carry out specific tasks within the cell [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ this specialisation led to increased efficiency and complexity in cellular processes [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ facilitated concentration of molecules and enzymes in specific areas, which enhanced the efficiency of biochemical reactions [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ acted as a barrier, preventing reactive molecules from interacting with the surroundings and protecting the delicate molecular structures within [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ this isolation provided stability in diverse environments [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ protection of genetic material from the external environment [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n It is believed that, early in the evolution of life, before the existence of fully developed cells, simple lipid structures similar to vesicles could have formed **protocells**($\\checkmark$), likely precursors to modern cells. The formation of vesicles and the lipid bilayer structure provided a basis for the **development of cell membranes** ($\\checkmark$). These membranes **separated** the **internal** cellular environment from the **external** surroundings, allowing for **controlled interactions** with the environment ($\\checkmark$). As cells became more complex, **different vesicles** or membrane-bound **organelles** could have **evolved** to carry out **specific tasks** within the cell ($\\checkmark$). As cellular life evolved, vesicles provided a means to **protect genetic material** ($\\checkmark$) from the external environment, which was crucial for the stability and transmission of genetic information across generations."},{"ref":"BI1164","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1164\n\nWhich statement(s) is/are a necessary requirement for the evolution of the first cells?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | DNA having the ability to catalyse reactions |\n    | II. | Polymerisation of carbon compounds |\n    | III. | Spontaneous formation of vesicles |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThere is evidence that simple carbon-containing molecules were present on Earth, millions of years ago, however, for early cells to evolve, they would have needed these simple molecules to form more complex structures such as polysaccharides and polynucleotides (**Statement II**). Phospholipids can spontaneously form vesicles (**Statement III**) or microspheres (microscopic hollow spheres made from a phospholipid bilayer), which resemble the plasma membrane of a small cell, this would allow for early cells to develop a different internal chemical environment from their surroundings. **Choice B**  is correct.\n\nDNA cannot catalyse reactions, **Statement I**  is incorrect. **Choices A, C** and **D**  are incorrect as they include **Statement I**."},{"ref":"BI1392","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1392\n\nEnzymes are needed to replicate and transcribe DNA. Ribosomes are needed to translate the DNA into enzymes. According to the “RNA world” hypothesis, what problem does this present for the first lifeforms to evolve on Earth?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Early life forms could not catalyse reactions.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Early life forms could not self-replicate.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Early life forms were unable to produce proteins.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Early lifeforms did not have cell membranes.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because early life forms likely had neither ribosomes nor enzymes and therefore could not produce proteins. The “RNA world” hypothesis suggests that early life forms were believed to have used ribozymes (RNA molecules with catalytic activity) to catalyse essential reactions.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the ability to catalyse metabolic reactions is thought to be a requirement for early life forms. **Choice B** is incorrect because self-replication is also a basic requirement for all living things. **Choice D** is incorrect because early life forms would have been unicellular, and all cells need a membrane of some kind to maintain structure and chemical gradients."},{"ref":"BI1444","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1444\n\nBelow is a diagram of an experimental setup in which boiled nutrient broth was placed into three different swan-necked flasks. Flask X was open to the air, but the swan neck trapped dust and microorganisms. Flask Y was sealed. Flask Z had its neck broken off, exposing it directly to the air.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Origin%20of%20Cells/images/BI1444%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1444 fig1\">\n\nHow do the results of this experiment support the idea that cells arise from pre-existing cells?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Broth in flask X remained clear because dust and microbes were trapped in the neck, preventing contamination.\n\n- II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ Broth in flask Y remained clear, indicating no microbial growth, as the sealed environment prevented any external contamination.\n\n- III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ Broth in flask Z remained clear, proving that exposure to air alone does not lead to microbial growth.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\n**Statement I** is true because in flask X the swan neck traps dust and microorganisms, preventing them from reaching the broth. As a result, the broth remains clear, showing that microorganisms in the air must physically enter the broth for growth to occur. This supports the idea that new cells arise from existing cells rather than forming spontaneously.\n\n**Statement II** is true because flask Y is sealed after sterilisation, so no microorganisms can enter. The broth remains clear, indicating that no microbial growth occurs in the absence of contamination. This provides strong evidence that cells do not arise spontaneously from non-living matter.\n\n**Statement III** is false because in flask Z, where the neck is broken, airborne microorganisms can directly enter the broth. This would cause the broth to become cloudy due to microbial growth. Therefore, the statement is incorrect in claiming the broth remained clear.\n\nAs only **Statements I** and **II** are true, answer **Choice C** is the correct answer to this question."},{"ref":"BI1521","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1521\n\nWhich role of RNA supports the hypothesis that it acted as the first genetic material?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ It functions as a primer during transcription\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ It functions as an enzyme used by ribosomes\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ It serves as a template for polypeptide synthesis\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ It transports amino acids during translation","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nRNA has the ability to self-replicate and can also act as an enzyme. These two properties contribute to the hypothesis that RNA was the first genetic material, rather than DNA. **Choice B** is correct.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect, as transcription does not involve primers. **Choice C**  is incorrect, as RNA’s role as a template for polypeptide synthesis does not contribute to the hypothesis that it was the first genetic material. **Choice D**  is wrong, as RNA’s role in transporting amino acids during translation does not contribute to the hypothesis that it was the first genetic material."},{"ref":"BI1523","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1523\n\nWhen early lifeforms first emerged on Earth, the levels of UV radiation were much higher than they are today. How did higher levels of UV radiation affect the conditions of the early Earth?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Provided energy for photosynthesis, so plants produced higher levels of oxygen\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Broke down gases into smaller molecules\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Mutated DNA nucleotide sequences so that a variety of polypeptides were formed\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Caused volcanoes to release gases into the atmosphere","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nThe high levels of UV radiation broke down gases in the early atmosphere, which provided smaller, highly reactive molecules that eventually formed the building blocks of life, such as amino acids, peptides and ribose. **Choice B**  is correct.\n\nBillions of years ago, there were no plants, and high levels of UV can affect plants adversely by damaging DNA; **Choice A**  is incorrect. Although UV radiation affects DNA, DNA was not thought to be present when the first life forms appeared; **Choice C**  is incorrect. UV radiation did not cause volcanoes to release gases into the atmosphere, this was caused by Earth cooling down after the Hadean aeon; **Choice D**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1524","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1524\n\nThe diagram shows the phylogenetic tree of life. Which method **CANNOT** be used to estimate the ages of the first living cells?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Origin%20of%20Cells/images/BI1524%20fig1.png\" alt=\"BI1524 fig1\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Croft, J. D. (2014, November 4). File: phylogenic tree-en.svg. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved February 26, 2024 from URL https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Phylogenic_Tree-en.svg. Copyright under CC BY 3.0]\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Dating fossils using radioactive techniques\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Genomic analysis of mutation rate\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Analysis of stromatolites\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Analysing fossils of LUCA from ancient volcanic rocks","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nWhile some of the oldest known microfossils have been found in volcanic sediments and have been used to estimate the age of early life, there are no direct fossils of LUCA. LUCA is inferred from molecular and phylogenetic analyses rather than from physical fossil evidence. Therefore, **Choice D** provides a method that **cannot** be used, and so is the correct answer to this question.\n\nis used to determine the age of ancient rocks and fossils by measuring the decay of radioactive isotopes. It provides an estimate of when organisms were alive on Earth, making it a valid method for dating early life; **Choice A** provides a method that can be used, and so is an incorrect answer to this question.\n\nGenomic analysis of mutation rate, known as the molecular clock, tracks changes in DNA sequences over time to estimate divergence points in evolutionary history. It can be used to infer when the first living cells emerged; **Choice B**  provides a method that can be used, and so is an incorrect answer to this question. \n\nStromatolites are layered rock formations created by the accumulation of sediment trapped by photosynthetic bacteria. By analysing the layering and geochemical signatures of these formations, scientists can estimate the age of the rock, thereby providing indirect dating by identifying potential habitats for early life. **Choice C** provides a method that can be used, so it is an incorrect answer to this question."},{"ref":"BI1525","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1525\n\nEvidence suggests that the last universal common ancestor was nitrogen-fixing. Which biological molecule might it have incorporated nitrogen into?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Starch\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ DNA nucleotide\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Ribose sugar\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Fatty acid","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nA DNA nucleotide is made up of a phosphate group, a nitrogenous base and a pentose sugar. Nitrogen is an essential component of the bases. **Choice B**  is correct.\n\nStarch, ribose sugar, and fatty acids do not contain nitrogen. **Choices A, C** and **D**  are incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1526","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1526\n\nAll organisms evolve through natural selection. Which of the following is a direct effect of natural selection on living organisms?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ It promotes the accumulation of beneficial traits in organisms\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ It allowed the last universal common ancestor to outcompete other organisms within one generation\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ It allows organisms that are best adapted to their environment to survive\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ It explains how life began on Earth","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\nNatural selection allows organisms that are best adapted to their environment to survive and pass on their genes; **Choice C** is correct.\n\nPromoting the accumulation of beneficial traits in organisms is a consequence of natural selection, not the direct effect. As organisms with beneficial traits survive and reproduce more, those traits become more common in the population over time. Natural selection is driven by random mutations that may or may not benefit an organism; **Choice A**  is incorrect. Although natural selection via evolution did allow the last universal common ancestor to outcompete other organisms, it is likely to have been a gradual process that did not occur within one generation; **Choice B** is incorrect. Natural selection does not address the origin of life itself; it explains how life changes and evolves after it has already begun. ; **Choice D**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1608","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1608\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nOrganisms that are better adapted to their environment are more likely to survive and thrive.\n\n1.  Describe how the skin and mucous membranes provide both physical and chemical defence against pathogens.    [4 marks]\n\n2.  Explain the importance of concentration gradients and the mechanisms that regulate them in the human ventilation system.    [5 marks]\n\n3.  Discuss to what extent the fossil record provides evidence for evolution.    [7 marks]","markScheme":"1.  any **four**  of the following (*answer must include **one** point from physical defence and **one** point from chemical defence to gain full marks*):\n\n    ***Physical defence***\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ skin provides a physical barrier **OR** is composed of multiple tissue layers preventing pathogens from entering the body    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ skin continuously sheds dead cells which removes any pathogens on the surface    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cells in skin are held together by tight junctions reinforcing the barrier function **OR** preventing pathogens from passing between cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mucus produced by mucous membranes can trap pathogens     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mucus containing trapped pathogens is either expelled by the body or swallowed    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cilia on mucous membrane surfaces move pathogens **OR** foreign particles out of the body    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Chemical defence***\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sebaceous glands in the skin secrete sebum, which lowers the pH of the skin and inhibits the growth of bacteria and fungi    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mucus contains lysozyme **OR** antimicrobial enzymes which break down bacterial cell walls    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sweat contains antimicrobials **OR** acids which inhibit growth of pathogens     [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n    The **skin** provides **physical defence** by forming a **barrier** that **prevents pathogens** from **entering** the **body** ($\\checkmark$). It also provides **chemical defence** against pathogens by having **sebaceous glands** that **secrete sebum**, which **lowers** the **pH** of the skin and so **inhibits** the **growth** of **bacteria** and **fungi** on the skin ($\\checkmark$). Mucous membranes line the internal surfaces of some organs and body cavities and secrete mucus, which can **trap pathogens** ($\\checkmark$). The mucus is then **expelled** by the body or **swallowed** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.  any **five** of the following:\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ steeper the concentration gradient, the greater the rate of diffusion, allowing for faster movement of molecules from areas of high concentration to areas of low concentration    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ inhaled air in alveoli has a higher concentration of oxygen than oxygen-depleted blood in the capillaries surrounding the alveoli **OR** blood has a higher concentration of carbon dioxide than the air in the alveoli. [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ concentration gradients facilitate diffusion of oxygen from alveoli into blood **OR** carbon dioxide from blood into alveoli, which provides cells with oxygen for respiration and removes waste carbon dioxide    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ large network of capillaries surrounding alveoli rapidly transport oxygen away from the lungs **OR** rapidly transport carbon dioxide from the body to the lungs    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the processes of inhalation and exhalation ensure that there is a constant flow of air in and out of lungs    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chemoreceptors in carotid artery **OR** aorta **OR** medulla monitor blood pH levels **OR** blood carbon dioxide concentrations [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ chemoreceptors send signals to respiratory centre in medulla oblongata OR brain stem     [1 mark]\n\n    respiratory centre **OR** medulla oblongata controls breathing rate **OR** depth of breathing to maintain concentration gradient of oxygen    [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n    Efficient gas exchange in the lungs relies on maintaining concentration gradients, where **inhaled air** in the **alveoli** has a **higher concentration** of **oxygen** than **oxygen-depleted blood** and a **lower concentration** of **carbon dioxide than that in** the surrounding **capillaries** ($\\checkmark$). This gradient allows **oxygen** to **diffuse from** the **alveoli** into the **blood** and **carbon dioxide from** the **blood into** the **alveoli** ($\\checkmark$). An extensive **network of capillaries** surrounding the alveoli rapidly **transports oxygen away** from the **lungs** and **carbon dioxide to** the lungs ($\\checkmark$). The processes of **inhalation** and **exhalation** ensure a **constant flow** of **air** in and out of the lungs, **maintaining** these **gradients** ($\\checkmark$). **Chemoreceptors** in the **carotid artery** and **aorta** monitor **blood pH** ($\\checkmark$), sending **signals to** the **respiratory centre** to **adjust breathing rate** and **depth** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.  any **seven** of the following (*answer must include at least **one** answer from ‘provides evidence’ and **one** answer from ‘doesn’t provide’ evidence to gain full marks*):\n\n    \n\n    ***Provides evidence***\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some organisms have very good fossil records, which allow their evolution to be tracked easily, e.g. horses        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the sequence of fossils that appear in layers of rock matches the sequence in which they would be expected to evolve     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ older organisms appear in deeper rock layers **OR** strata, and more recently evolved organisms appear in the upper layers    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there are fossils of extinct organisms with transitional forms **OR** fossils of extinct organisms that have features that link different stages of evolution, e.g. Archaeopteryx, bird-like dinosaurs that support the hypothesis that birds evolved from reptiles        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the fossil record shows evidence of mass extinction events **OR** there are many more extinct species than living ones        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fossils show evidence of adaptive radiation **OR** divergence of different organisms from a common ancestor **OR** speciation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the fossil record shows evidence of vestigial structures that are no longer present in current organisms    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there is fossil evidence for the existence of the last universal common ancestor (LUCA) near hydrothermal vents        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fossils provide chronological evidence that supports the theory of gradualism in evolution, where small changes accumulate over long periods    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fossilised remains of early hominids show a clear progression in brain size **OR** bipedalism, supporting human evolution    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Doesn’t provide evidence***\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fossil record is incomplete, with many gaps **OR** some fossil species appear abruptly in the fossil record without any precursors    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some species in the fossil record appear not to have changed much over long periods of time        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ convergent evolution **OR** fossils of unrelated organisms with similar features makes it difficult to track evolution accurately    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ soft-bodied organisms are rarely fossilised, leading to an incomplete record that skews our understanding of evolutionary history    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ process of fossilisation is rare and biased towards certain environments, meaning that the fossil record may not accurately represent all ecosystems and organisms    [1 mark]\n\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n    The fossil record supports evolution because some organisms have **well-documented fossil records** that allow their evolutionary changes to be **tracked easily** ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, the **sequence** in which **fossils appear in rock layers** matches the sequence in which they would be **expected to evolve** ($\\checkmark$). For example, **older** organisms are found in **deeper layers** of rocks, while more **recently evolved organisms** are found in the **upper layers** ($\\checkmark$). There are also fossils of extinct organisms with **transitional features**, such as Archaeopteryx, a bird-like dinosaur, which supports the hypothesis that birds evolved from reptiles and provides **evolutionary links** between present and extinct species ($\\checkmark$). However, there are **gaps** in the fossil record ($\\checkmark$), some species appear not to have **changed over long periods of time** ($\\checkmark$), and **convergent evolution** can make it difficult to track evolution accurately ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1715","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1715\n\nWhich statement(s) provide evidence for the common ancestry of all existing forms of cellular life?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n\n    | | |\n\n    | :-- | :-- |\n\n    | I. | The universality of the genetic code |\n\n    | II. | The machinery for protein synthesis |\n\n    | III. | Use of ATP as a common energy currency |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\n**Statements I, II** and **III** are true, making **Choice D** the correct answer to the question.\n\nThe universality of the genetic code **(Statement I)** suggests that all modern organisms inherited this code from a single common ancestor. The nearly identical genetic code across all known forms of life. implies that the code was established very early in the history of life and has been conserved through billions of years of evolution. If different forms of life had arisen independently, it is highly unlikely that they would all use the same genetic code.  \n\nThe machinery for protein synthesis,  **(Statement II)** which includes ribosomes, transfer RNA (tRNA), and messenger RNA (mRNA), is remarkably conserved across all forms of cellular life. This high degree of similarity suggests that these complex molecular mechanisms were inherited from a common ancestor, known as the Last Universal Common Ancestor (LUCA). \n\nATP as a common energy currency  **(Statement III)** used by organisms as diverse as bacteria, archaea, and eukaryotes suggests that this energy system was established early in the evolutionary history of life and inherited from a single ancestral organism. If different forms of life had evolved independently, it is unlikely that they would all rely on the same molecule for energy, reinforcing the idea of a shared origin."},{"ref":"BI1720","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI1720\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nLouis Pasteur disproved the theory of spontaneous generation by showing that life did not spontaneously arise from non-living matter but came from existing microorganisms. In his experiment, he set up the following flasks:\n\nI. Open straight-neck flask containing boiled broth.\n\nII. Swan-neck flask containing boiled broth (neck allowed air to flow but trapped dust and microbes).\n\nIII.\nControl flask containing boiled broth and sealed from the air.\n\nResearchers carried out a similar experiment to Louis Pasteur’s famous experiment, using the set-up below, and incubating the flasks at 30°C.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Origin%20of%20Cells/images/spontaneous%20origin%20of%20cells%20flasks.png\" alt=\"spontaneous origin of cells flasks\">\n\n1. State the purpose of boiling the broth before placing it in some of the flasks. [1 mark]\n\n2.\n 1. State two variables that must be controlled in this experiment. [2 marks]\n\n 2. Explain why it is important to have controlled variables in this study. [1 mark]\n\n3.\n 1. State the letters of three flasks which would show signs of decomposition of broth. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Explain your answer to part (i). [3 marks]\n\n4. Suggest, with reasons, how the results of the study would differ if the flasks were incubated at 4°C. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to kill any pre-existing microorganisms **OR** bacteria in the broth **OR** beofre the experiment [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The purpose of boiling the broth was to **kill** any pre-existing **microorganisms in the broth**. ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ initial volume of broth [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature broth is incubated at [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen concentration [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ concentration of nutrients in the broth [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ type **OR** volume of flask [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ time flask is incubated for [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ time that broth is boiled for [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ sterilisation of flasks **OR** apparatus before setting up the experiment [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ consistent composition of the broth [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Two variables that must be controlled in this experiment are the initial **volume of broth** ($\\checkmark$) and the **temperature** at which the broth is incubated ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to ensure that any differences in the decomposition of the broth are only due to the independent variable [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to limit the influence of confounding variables [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to allow for a fair comparison between the flasks [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ to increase replicability **OR** repeatability [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n It is important to have controlled variables in this study to ensure that any **differences in the decomposition of the broth** are only due to the **independent variable** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 1. Any **three** of the following gives one mark:\n\n *Do NOT award mark if any incorrect flasks are included.*\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Q, R, S, T [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The flasks that would show decomposition of broth are **Q, R** and **S** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ flask Q was not boiled so microorganisms would be alive **OR** would reproduce **AND** microorganisms could enter from the air [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ flask R was tilted after being boiled, so microorganisms could still enter the flask at an angle [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ flask S was not boiled, so any microorganisms in the flask were not killed before the flask was sealed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ flask T was sealed with a metal mesh, so microorganisms from the air could enter the broth [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Flask **Q** would show decomposition of broth, as it was not boiled so **microorganisms would be alive** in the broth and could also **enter from the air** ($\\checkmark$). Flask **R** would show decomposition of broth, as even though it was tilted, it was not sealed so **microorganisms can still enter the flask** at an angle ($\\checkmark$). Flask **S** would also show decomposition of the broth, as it was **not boiled before being sealed**, so microorganisms would be alive in the broth ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **two** of the following\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ there would be less decomposition of the broth **OR** less bacterial growth **OR** less rapid growth of microorganisms in flasks Q, R, S and T [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ at lower temperatures, microorganisms have a lower metabolic rate **OR** there is stiffening of phospholipid bilayer which decreases efficiency of membrane transport **OR** have a slower growth rate [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cooler temperatures can reduce the likelihood of contamination [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ there would be no difference in flask P because no bacteria could enter and the broth was boiled killing any bacteria in the broth [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n If the flasks were incubated at 4°C, there would be **less decomposition** ($\\checkmark$) of the broth in flasks Q, R, S and T, as at lower temperatures, microorganisms have a **slower growth rate** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1721","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1721\n\nLouis Pasteur disproved the theory of spontaneous generation by showing that life did not spontaneously arise from non-living matter but came from existing microorganisms. In his experiment, he set up the following flasks:\n\nOpen straight-neck flask containing boiled broth.\nSwan-neck flask containing boiled broth (neck allowed air to flow but trapped dust and microbes).\nControl flask containing boiled broth and sealed from the air.\n\nThe experiment was replicated as shown below:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Origin%20of%20Cells/images/Pasteur%20flask.png\" alt=\"Pasteur flask\">\n\nWhich two flasks would show the presence of microorganisms in the results?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ R and S\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ S and Q\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Q and R\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ P and Q","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nFlasks R and S (**Choice A**) would show the presence of microorganisms as Flask R is exposed to the air, so bacteria can enter and Flask S wasn't boiled, so microorganisms originally in the flask were not killed before the flask was sealed.\n\nFlask P (**Choice D**) is incorrect as it was boiled first, killing any microorganisms and sealed so no more microorganisms can enter.\nFlask Q (**Choices B**, **C**, **D**) is incorrect because it was boiled, killing any microorganisms present. Swan-necked flasks prevent dust particles from carrying bacteria into the flask."},{"ref":"BI2374","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2374\n\nWhich property of ribozymes is believed to have identified RNA as the first genetic material, ahead of DNA?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Structural\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Synthetic\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Catalytic\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Physical","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because ribozymes are lengths of RNA that have catalytic properties (hence the ‘-zymes’ suffix, like the in word ‘en-zymes’). Some reactions that are thought to have been catalysed by ribozymes are the formation of peptide bonds during protein synthesis, a feature of ribosomal RNA in all species, not just in early life forms. This catalytic property also helps to explain how DNA first formed and became the basic unit of inheritance, with proteins carrying out most of the catalytic activity in cells.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because RNA does not give any structural properties to cells or organelles that contain it.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because RNA has a role in protein synthesis, but it does not explain how RNA may have pre-dated DNA as the first genetic material.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the physical properties of RNA and ribozymes are irrelevant to their role in early life development."},{"ref":"BI2413","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2413\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nHaem is the non-protein, iron-binding component found in haemoglobin.  Haem and chlorophyll are similar in structure but chlorophyll contains a magnesium ion instead of iron. The biochemical pathways that make haem and chlorophyll involve enzymes that are highly conserved across many distantly related species.  The diagram shows an amino acid sequence alignment for part of one such enzyme, delta-aminolevulinate dehydratase. Each amino acid is represented by a single-letter code.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Origin%20of%20Cells/images/Edit1.png\" alt=\"Edit1\">\n[Source: Alignment created with and adapted from Cobalt with Refseq and Genbank sequences from National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI)[Internet]. *Bethesda (MD): National Library of Medicine (US), National Center for Biotechnology Information; [1988] – [cited 2024]*. Available from: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/]\n\n1. The diagram suggests that certain metabolic pathways are conserved across diverse organisms. Discuss how these conserved pathways provide evidence for LUCA.      [2 marks]\n\n2. Suggest two environmental conditions found in hydrothermal vents that may have facilitated the evolution of early metabolic pathways.        [2 marks]\n\n3. Explain how genomic analysis can be used to estimate the time period in which the last universal common ancestor (LUCA) existed.        [2 marks]\n\n4. Scientists discovered a bacterial enzyme with a significantly different amino acid sequence from its human counterpart but nearly identical function.  Suggest possible reasons why this function is conserved despite sequence divergence.     [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ conserved metabolic pathways **OR** similar enzymes in conserved pathways suggest a shared evolutionary origin **OR** fundamental processes like glycolysis **OR** ATP synthesis **OR** protein translation are found in all life forms    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ highly conserved pathways demonstrate biochemical efficiency favoured in natural selection **OR** strong selective pressure     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the lack of any other widespread metabolic mechanisms suggests competition and extinction of other forms of life    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Many **fundamental processes** like **glycolysis** are **conserved** across **all known life forms** ($\\checkmark$).  This indicates a shared evolutionary origin and supports the idea of LUCA (the last universal common ancestor).  The **lack of other metabolic mechanisms suggests** that they may have existed in early species but were outcompeted and **became extinct** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ heat energy to increase the rate of chemical reactions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ high pressure at thermal vents stabilised complex chemical reactions that could not occur in less extreme environments    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ minerals to serve as catalysts promoting biochemical reactions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mineral-rich vents may have mimicked cell membranes, concentrating molecules and enabling the development of metabolic pathways    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ proton gradients **OR** pH gradient for ATP synthesis via chemiosmosis    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ energy sources such as hydrogen **OR** methane **OR** sulfide gases as electron donors for metabolic reactions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ highly reduced compounds from vents coming into contact with the ocean’s oxidised compounds provided redox gradients driving early metabolism    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lack of oxygen prevented oxidative damage to early organic molecules **OR** supported anaerobic metabolism development    [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The heat and high-pressure environment of the hydrothermal vents may have enabled the evolution of metabolic pathways as **heat energy increases** the **rate** of **chemical reactions** ($\\checkmark$) and **high pressure stabilises complex chemical reactions** ($\\checkmark$) that may not have been able to occur in less extreme environments.\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phylogenetic trees constructed from genomic data illustrate evolutionary relationships    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ comparing conserved genetic sequences helps infer evolutionary timelines, as more conserved sequences indicate older ancestry    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genomic evidence combined with other evidence **OR** fossils helps establish LUCA’s timefram    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ molecular clocks estimate divergence time by assuming mutations accumulate at a constant rate\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ highly conserved ribosomal RNA **OR** rRNA sequences are useful for estimating divergence events\n [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Phylogenetic trees**, constructed from genomic data, illustrate **evolutionary relationships**, helping estimate when LUCA existed by identifying **divergence points** ($\\checkmark$)  **Molecular clocks** further refine this by comparing **genetic differences** and applying a **known mutation rate** to infer the **timing** of common ancestry ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ proteins with different primary sequences can fold into similar three-dimensional conformations **OR** structures    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ an enzyme's function depends on its 3-D structure    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ variations in sites other than the active site may not affect function    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ convergence in evolution can lead to functionally similar enzymes despite a difference in sequence    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ some amino acid substitutions are neutral and do not alter protein folding **OR** stability **OR** active site configuration    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ other structural adaptations **OR** flexible binding sites **OR** other suitable named adaptation allow variations in sequence without affecting overall enzyme function    [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Proteins with **different primary sequences** fold into **similar three-dimensional structures** ($\\checkmark$). Since an **enzyme’s function** depends on its **3D structure, especially** the shape of the **active site** ($\\checkmark$), as long as it has a **similar structure**, it should be able to perform a **similar function** ($\\checkmark$)."}]},{"name":"Unassigned","questions":[{"ref":"BI0011","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0011\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe image below is a micrograph of blood.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/micrograph%20of%20blood%20with%20phagocytes%20phagocytic%20white%20blood%20cells%20lymphocytes%20i.png\" alt=\"micrograph of blood with phagocytes phagocytic white blood cells lymphocytes i\">\nMagnification: 800x\n\n[Source: Adapted from Berkshire Community College Bioscience Image Library. (2018). *Connective Tissue: Human Blood Leukocyte Survey*. Flickr. Retrieved April 4, 2022, from\nhttps://www.flickr.com/photos/146824358@N03/26921278147. Copyright free]\n\n1. Identify the part of the cells labelled A and B. [2 marks]\n\n2. Outline why blood is classified as a tissue. [1 mark]\n\n3. State where the stem cells needed to produce red blood cells are located. [1 mark]\n\n4. Progenitor cells are produced by dividing stem cells. These cells will eventually differentiate even further. Blood cells have a common progenitor cell. Describe the process that happens to these progenitor cells. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ arrow A points to the plasma membrane   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ arrow B points to the nucleus (in white blood cell) [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ consists of different types of cells **OR** consists of specialised cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ consists of red blood cells, white blood cells and platelets   [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\nThe definition of tissue is **different types of cells** working together ($\\checkmark$). Blood consists of **red blood cells, white blood cells and platelets**,($\\checkmark$) which each have a different role and work together.\n\n3.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ bone marrow [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ through selective gene expression **OR** expression of only tissue-specific genes **OR** expression of specific genes needed for the development of the blood cells     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in response to changes in the environment  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ become specialised cells **OR** become cells with a specific role **OR** function  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ differentiate into red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets **OR** become the different types of blood cells   [1 mark]\n[2 marks]\n\n    :u[Sample answer]:\n\n**Through selective gene expression** ($\\checkmark$) common progenitor cells **become specialised cells** ($\\checkmark$). In the case of blood, these cells **differentiate into red blood cells, white blood cells and platelets** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0274","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0274\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline how mitochondrial DNA can be used to construct a cladogram.     [4 marks]\n\n2. Explain why organisms classified in the past may be reclassified. [4 marks]\n\n3. Compare and contrast cladograms made using molecular evidence and structural evidence. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1.    any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mitochondrial DNA from different organisms can be sequenced [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mutations occur at a roughly constant rate **OR** can act as a molecular clock [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the lower the number of differences in the base sequence between organisms, the more closely related the organisms are [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the number of differences in the base sequence can be used to determine how long ago species diverged from a common ancestor [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ which can be used to deduce where the nodes are on cladograms [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nSections of mitochondrial DNA from **different organisms can be sequenced** ($\\checkmark$), which allows scientists to observe differences in the base sequence. The **lower the number of differences** in the base sequence between organisms, the **more closely related** the organisms are ($\\checkmark$). The **number of differences** in the sequence of mitochondrial DNA can also be used to determine **how long ago species diverged** from a common ancestor ($\\checkmark$), which can be used to deduce **position of nodes** on cladograms ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.    any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms (used to be) classified based on shared anatomical characteristics **OR** structures **OR** physical features [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ shared anatomical characteristics **OR** structures **OR** physical features may be analogous **OR** not due to common ancestry [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ analogous structures as a result of convergent evolution [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms (are now) classified using amino acid **OR** DNA sequences [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increased accuracy **OR** reliability of cladograms made using DNA **OR** amino acid sequences [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nIn the past, organisms were classified based on shared **physical features** ($\\checkmark$), however **physical features could be analogous** and therefore not the result of common ancestry ($\\checkmark$). Cladograms which are constructed using **DNA or amino acid sequences** ($\\checkmark$) are more **reliable** ($\\checkmark$) and can highlight new evolutionary relationships which would lead to organisms potentially being reclassified.\n\n3.    any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** seek to demonstrate **OR** deduce evolutionary relationships [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contain a root **OR** ancestor common to all organisms within the cladogram [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contain nodes **OR** points at which species diverged from one another [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contain clades **OR** an ancestor and all of its evolutionary descendants [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** can contain outgroups **OR** the most distantly related species [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ species in **BOTH** may be reclassified (in response to new evidence) [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ species organised based on anatomical structures **OR** physical characteristics **WHEREAS** species are organised based on amino acid **OR** DNA sequence [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in the past cladograms were constructed using similarities in physical features **WHEREAS** now DNA **OR** amino acid sequences are used [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ molecular evidence **OR** DNA sequences **OR** amino sequences more reliable **OR** accurate than structural evidence **OR** physical characteristics [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ analogous structures can be an issue in cladograms made using structural similarities **BUT** not in cladograms using molecular evidence **OR** DNA **OR** amino acid sequences [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cladograms made using structural evidence require less technology **COMPARED** to cladograms using molecular evidence **OR** DNA or amino acid sequences [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nCladograms which are made using structural evidence and those made using molecular evidence have several things in common, for example, **both have** features such as a **root** ($\\checkmark$), **nodes** ($\\checkmark$) and **clades** ($\\checkmark$), they **both** also attempt to show **evolutionary relationships** between species ($\\checkmark$). There are also some differences in these cladograms. In cladograms made using structural evidence, the **physical characteristics** of species are used to organise them into groups, which **does not require much technology**. However, in cladograms using molecular evidence, which requires more **advanced technology** ($\\checkmark$), scientists use the number of differences in the DNA or **amino acid sequence** ($\\checkmark$), which is known to be the more **reliable** ($\\checkmark$) method and is more commonly used now, compared to structural features being used more in the **past** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0314","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0314\n\nWhich statement highlights the importance of nucleosomes in DNA supercoiling ?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ To fit the length of DNA into the nucleus\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ To access specific DNA sequences\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ To form a circular chromosome\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ To hold the information needed for coding amino acids","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nA nucleosome helps to supercoil the DNA in order to package the DNA into a more condensed and compact structure. With this packaging, a length of DNA, totalling 2m, would be able to fit into the nucleus. **Choice A** is correct.\n\nEven though the DNA is packed into the nucleus, nucleosomes can still allow access to specific DNA sequences for the transcription process (**Choice B**). DNA can be accessed with or without the nucleosomes present.\n\nThe circular chromosome found in bacterial cells is shorter than the chromosomes of  eukaryotic cells so they do not require any DNA packaging, hence the absence of nucleosomes (**Choice C**). \n\nDNA acts as a template for RNA transcription, which then is used to code for amino acids. A nucleosome is used to supercoil the DNA and does not hold the information to code for amino acids (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0450","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0450\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe micrograph shows a part of a plant cell from the leaf of *Anemone sp.*\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/EM%20of%20chloroplast%20no%20labels.png\" alt=\"EM of chloroplast no labels\">\n[Source: and3k, & caper437. (2009, June 25). *File: chloroplast in leaf of Anemone sp Tem 85000x.png - Wikimedia Commons*. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved June 13, 2022, from https ://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Chloroplast_in_leaf_of_Anemone_sp_TEM_12000x.png Copyright free]\n\n1. Label structures in the plant cell above:\n 1. using the letter R where starch can be found; [1 mark]\n\n 2. using the letter S where lipids can be found; [1 mark]\n\n 3. using the letter T where cellulose can be found. [1 mark]\n\n2. Compare and contrast the structure and functions of glycogen and starch. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n 1. $\\ce{ -> }$ arrow labelled R pointing to a lighter oval [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/450ai%201.png\" alt=\"450ai 1\">\n\n 2. $\\ce{ -> }$ arrow labelled S pointing to the cell membrane **OR** membrane around chloroplast **OR** thylakoid membrane **OR** lipid droplet **OR** vacuolar membrane [1 mark]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/450aii.png\" alt=\"450aii\">\n\n 3. $\\ce{ -> }$ arrow labelled T pointing to the cell wall\n\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$:\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/450aiii.png\" alt=\"450aiii\">\n\n2. Any **four** of the following (should include **AT LEAST ONE** for compare and **AT LEAST ONE** for contrast):\n\n *Compare:*\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are polysaccharides [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are made up of $\\alpha$- glucose molecules [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** have 1,4 and 1,6 linkages [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are energy storage molecules [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** amylopectin in starch and glycogen are branched molecules [1 mark]\n\n *Contrast:*\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ glycogen is more branched **IN CONTRAST TO** starch, **OR** amylose of starch is unbranched, amylopectin has few branched chains **WHEREAS** glycogen is more branched [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ Starch is composed of two different polymers, amylose and amylopectin **WHEREAS** glycogen is composed of only one polymer [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ starch is found in plant cells **WHEREAS** glycogen is found in animal cells [1 mark]\n\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nBoth starch and glycogen are **polysaccharides** ($\\checkmark$) made of **$\\alpha$- glucose molecules** ($\\checkmark$), which have **1,4 and 1,6 linkages** ($\\checkmark$). Starch is composed of **two different** polymers; **amylose** (unbranched and forms a helix) and **amylopectin** (branched), whereas **glycogen** is composed of only **one type of polymer** ($\\checkmark$). Both amylopectin, in starch, and glycogen are **branched** molecules ($\\checkmark$), but **glycogen is more branched than starch** ($\\checkmark$). Both are **energy storage** ($\\checkmark$)molecules but **starch** is found in **plant cells** whereas **glycogen** is found in **animal cells** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{For additional detail}}$: see 4:30 in Carbohydrates Key Concept Video"},{"ref":"BI0572","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0572\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n1. Outline the steps in calculating the magnification of a micrograph.  [4 marks]\n\n2. Describe the steps taken to focus on a specimen using the 40x objective lens of a light microscope.      [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ measure (using a ruler) the scale bar on the drawing **OR** micrograph   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ note the stated length which the scale bar represents [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ convert the represented and real length or scale bar into the same units [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ M= I/A **OR** any correct example of the calculation     [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    To calculate the magnification of a microphotograph, the first step is to measure **the scale bar using a ruler** ($\\checkmark$). The second step is **to note the length the scale bar represents** ($\\checkmark$). This gives you the actual size of the image. These two measurements should be converted to **the same units** ($\\checkmark$). The last step is to **divide the length measured by the ruler by the length represented by the scale bar** ($\\checkmark$). During this calculation, units cancel out.  Therefore, the **magnification does not have units**.\n\n2. Any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{->}$  use the lowest power **OR** 4X objective lens first        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ place the specimen **OR** slide directly below the objective lens **OR** on the centre of the microscope stage   [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$  turn the coarse focus knob to focus on the specimen at 4X  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ adjust the turret **OR** change the objective lens so the medium power **OR** 10X objective lens is used     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ refocus on the specimen using a fine focus knob at 10X and 40X [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$  adjust the turret **OR** change the objective lens, so the high power **OR** 40X objective lens is used [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{->}$ while fine focusing always move the lens away from the slide [1 mark]\n[4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nPlace the specimen **in the centre** of the microscope stage  ($\\checkmark$).  Use the **4x objective lens first**  ($\\checkmark$) and use the **coarse focus knob**  ($\\checkmark$) to focus on the specimen.  Adjust the position of the specimen so it is in the middle of the field of view and then **switch to the 10x**  ($\\checkmark$) objective lens and **refocus using the fine focus knob**, ($\\checkmark$) by moving lens **away from the specimen**  ($\\checkmark$) to prevent lens breakage.  Finally, switch to the **40x objective lens** ($\\checkmark$) and use the **fine focus knob** to refocus on the specimen."},{"ref":"BI0769","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI0769\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nAn investigation was carried out into the effects of household detergents on aquatic habitats. Scientists tested the effect of different household detergents on the surface tension of water in order to better understand the impact they may have on organisms living on the surface.\n\nA paper clip was folded slightly on one side and rested on the surface of the water, maintained at a constant temperature and contained within a water tank. Drops of detergent solution were slowly added to the water tank until the paperclip sank to the bottom. The volume of detergent solution taken to sink the paperclip was recorded.\n\nDetergent solutions were made by adding 5 ml of detergent to 15 ml of water.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/BI0769A.png\" alt=\"BI0769A\">\n\nThe results are shown in the table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/BI0769B.png\" alt=\"BI0769B\">\n\n1. State the relationship between the volume of detergent solution added and the surface tension of water. [1 mark]\n\n2.\n 1. For the **Easy Clean** detergent, calculate the mean volume of detergent needed to sink the paper clip. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Estimate the volume of **Easy Clean** detergent solution it would take to sink 5 paper clips. [1 mark]\n\n3.\n 1. State **one** variable, other than those given, which would need to be controlled in this experiment in order to achieve a valid result. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Suggest **one** method that could be used to keep the water at a constant temperature. [1 mark]\n\n 3. Explain the importance of maintaining water at a constant temperature. [2 marks]\n\n 4. Identify **one** potential source of error in the methodology. [1 mark]\n\n4. Suggest why different numbers of drops of detergent solution were needed to sink the paperclip between each type of detergent. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ surface tension decreases as detergent solution volume increases [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ As the volume of detergent solution **increases** the **surface tension** of the water **decreases** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1. (units must be included in order to be awarded the mark)\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 9.25ml [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/BI0769ANSA.png\" alt=\"BI0769ANSA\">\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n Mean = total/number of trials\n\n = (8 ml+10 ml+10 ml+9 ml) / 4\n\n = 37 ml / 4\n\n = **9.25ml** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n2.\n 2. (units must be included in order to be awarded the mark)\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ 9.25ml [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n If it takes 9.25 ml of detergent solution to reduce the surface tension enough to sink one paper clip, then the **same volume** would sink 5 paper clips ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 1. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ volume of the water (in the water tank) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ size **OR** mass **OR** brand **OR** material of paper clip **OR** way it is folded [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ distance of burette from water surface [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The **size of the paper clip** ($\\checkmark$) in the experiment would need to be controlled.\n\n3.\n 2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ water bath [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ heating/cooling blocks [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n A **water bath** ($\\checkmark$) could be used to maintain a constant temperature.\n\n3.\n 3. Any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the cohesive properties of water create surface tension **OR** surface tension is due to hydrogen bonding between water molecules [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ cohesive forces **OR** hydrogen bonding decrease with increasing temperature **BECAUSE** there is more kinetic energy so molecules move more and interact less **OR** less attractive forces form between them, **OR** cohesive forces **OR** hydrogen bonding increase with decreasing temperature **BECAUSE** there is less kinetic energy so molecules move less and interact more **OR** more attractive forces form between them [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n It’s important to maintain a constant temperature in this experiment because surface tension is due to **cohesion** ($\\checkmark$) between water molecules which is a result of hydrogen bonding. High temperatures can break hydrogen bonds and **reduce the cohesive properties** ($\\checkmark$) of water which in turn reduces surface tension.\n\n3.\n 4.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ surface tension broken by the droplets of detergent solution **OR** ripples created by droplets of detergent [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ paper clips folded incorrectly **OR** differently (if different paper clips used for each trial) [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ paper clip has residual detergent between trials (if same paper clip is used for each trial) [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ paper clips are different materials **OR** brands **OR** texture (if different paper clips are used for each trial) [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ A source of error could be that the surface tension of the water is broken by the **droplets of detergent** ($\\checkmark$) which create ripples. This could result in the paperclip sinking.\n\n4.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ detergents are made using different chemicals **OR** detergents have different starting concentrations **OR** different concentrations of chemical ingredients [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ some chemicals **OR** detergents affect the cohesion between water molecules more **AND** therefore reduce surface tension more [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ There may be a difference between results because each detergent is a different brand which may be made using **different chemicals** ($\\checkmark$). Some of the chemicals found in some detergents may have a **greater effect on cohesion** between water molecules which means they could reduce the **surface tension** more ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1197","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1197\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe micrograph below shows *Cyanobacteria*, which contain chlorophyll.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/BI1197-SL.png\" alt=\"BI1197-SL\">\n[Source: Adapted from Thompson L. and Whatson N. (2007, January 1).  *Cyanobacteria*.  Wikipedia.  Retrieved on September 6, 2023, from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyanobacteria#. Copyright under CCO]\n\n1. Using your knowledge and the micrograph, state two features that would indicate that this is a bacterium.     [2 marks]\n\n2. Outline the structural differences between animal and plant cells.  [3 marks]\n\n3. Explain how light energy is used in photosynthesis to carry out anabolic reactions. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **two** of the following\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell wall of peptidoglycan            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ naked DNA **OR** DNA not associated with histones **OR** proteins    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleoid **OR** DNA free in cytoplasm                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ no membrane bound organelles                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ 70s ribosomes                                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cell smaller than 10μm                            [1 mark]\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Two features that would indicate that this is a bacterium are it has **no membrane-bound organelles** ($\\checkmark$) and **70s ribosomes** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plant cells have a cell wall, **WHEREAS** animal cells do not    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plant cells generally have a central vacuole **WHEREAS** animal cells do not        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ plant cells have chloroplasts **WHEREAS** animal cells do not    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ animal cells may have lysosomes **WHEREAS** plant cells do not    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ animal cells may have cilia **OR** flagella **WHEREAS** plant cells do not    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ animal cells have centrioles **WHEREAS** plant cells do not        [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Plant cells have a **cell wall** ($\\checkmark$), **central vacuole** ($\\checkmark$) and **chloroplasts**, whereas **animal** cells **do not** ($\\checkmark$) .\n\n3. any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light energy is absorbed by chlorophyll            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light energy is transferred to electrons **OR** is used to excite electrons    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light energy splitting water molecules **OR** photolysis releases electrons for the electron transport chain **OR** hydrogen ions for use in ATP production    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light energy is used to synthesise ATP from ADP and inorganic phosphate **OR** NADPH from electrons and one proton            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ NADPH **OR** ATP is used to make larger organic compounds **OR** glucose **OR** starch **OR** other named organic compound from carbon dioxide        [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Light energy is **absorbed** by **chlorophyll** ($\\checkmark$) and is used to **excite electrons** ($\\checkmark$).  These electrons reduce **NADP:sup[+]** to form **NADPH**, which is an anabolic reaction ($\\checkmark$).  Energy present in **NADPH** is then used to **produce glucose** from **carbon dioxide** ($\\checkmark$).  This is also an anabolic reaction."},{"ref":"BI1220","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1220\n\nWhich statement is **not** true about the genome?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The human genome is organised into chromosomes.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Each liver cell contains a full copy of the genome.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Organisms of the same species share most of their genome.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ All the information carried by the genome is expressed in each cell.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nNot all genes are expressed in each cell. Only the necessary genes are expressed based on the specialised function of the cell. **Choice D** is **not** a true statement, and so is the correct answer.\n\nThe genome is all the genetic information of an organism, including the genes and non-coding DNA. In humans, the genome is organised into linear chromosomes in the nucleus, and circular chromosomes in the mitochondria. **Choice A** is a true statement and so an incorrect answer. Organisms of the same species have most of their genome in common; however, variations give rise to some diversity between individuals. **Choice C** is a true statement and so an incorrect answer. Each cell in a multicellular organism typically contains a full copy of the genome, including liver cells, an exception is red blood cells as they do not have a nucleus. **Choice B** is a true statement and so an incorrect answer."},{"ref":"BI1418","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1418\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\nGenetic variation is the root of phenotypic diversity between and within species.\n\n1. Phenotypic expression relies on the genome and proteome. Outline the differences between the genome and the proteome    [4 marks]\n\n2. While genetic variations can be beneficial, others can lead to detrimental health conditions, such as diabetes. Compare and contrast Type I and Type II diabetes.    [4 marks]\n\n3. Describe the approaches used to classify different species.    [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genome is the complete set of genetic material present in an organism **OR** includes all the genes, regulatory elements and non-coding regions of the genetic material of an organism **WHEREAS** proteome is the entire set of proteins produced **OR** expressed by a cell, tissue, or organism at a specific time under defined conditions           [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genome composed of DNA nucleotides **WHEREAS**  proteome composed of amino acids [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genome stores genetic information **OR** is a template for the synthesis of proteome **WHEREAS**  proteome carries out the functions of the organism **OR** is involved in cellular processes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genome relatively stable over time **WHEREAS** proteome is highly dynamic **OR** proteome constantly changing in response to cellular needs **OR** environment **OR** internal signals [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ size of genome is relatively constant within a species **WHEREAS** proteome can be much larger **OR** more diverse than the genome due to alternative splicing, post-translational modifications, and protein-protein interactions    [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The **genome** is the **complete set of genetic material** present in an organism, including **all** the **genes, regulatory elements** and **non-coding regions**, whereas the **proteome** is the **entire set** of **proteins** produced by a **cell, tissue,** or **organism** at a specific time under defined conditions ($\\checkmark$). The **genome** is made up of **nucleotides**, whereas the **proteome** is composed of **amino acids** ($\\checkmark$). Functionally, the **genome** acts as a source of genetic **instructions**, providing the information necessary for development and regulation, while the **proteome** actively **participates** in **cellular processes** ($\\checkmark$) based on these instructions. The **genome** is relatively **stable** with occasional genetic variations, whereas the **proteome** is highly **dynamic**, responding to environmental factors ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **four** of the following (**AT LEAST ONE** similarity and **AT LEAST ONE** difference must be given):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** Types of diabetes affect blood glucose concentration **OR** affect the body’s ability to control blood glucose concentration        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** types of diabetes cause health complications if blood glucose concentration is not controlled                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Type I is an autoimmune disease **WHEREAS** Type II is linked to genetics **OR** lifestyle **OR** ageing [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Type I diabetes is caused when the beta cells are destroyed by the immune system and do not secrete enough insulin **WHEREAS** Type II diabetes is caused when body cells become insensitive **OR** resistant to insulin    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Type I diabetes usually begins in childhood **WHEREAS** Type II diabetes usually begins in adulthood [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Type I diabetes cannot be controlled by lifestyle changes **WHEREAS** Type II diabetes can often be controlled by lifestyle changes            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Type I diabetes requires frequent measurement of blood glucose concentration **WHEREAS** Type II diabetes does not require frequent measurement of blood glucose concentration                    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Type I diabetes requires insulin injections from start **WHEREAS** Type II diabetes is only treated with insulin if the disease progresses        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ Type I tends to be diagnosed at early age **WHEREAS** Type II tends to be diagnosed in older people                                [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Both** Type I and Type II diabetes **disrupt** the **regulation** of **normal blood glucose concentration** ($\\checkmark$). **Type I diabetes** is an **autoimmune disease** where the **immune system destroys beta cells in the pancreas**, resulting in a **lack of insulin** production. The causes of **Type II diabetes** are **multifactorial**, including **genetics, lifestyle** and **ageing** ($\\checkmark$), and it manifests when body **cells** become **resistant to insulin** ($\\checkmark$). **Type I diabetes** requires **frequent measurement of blood glucose** concentration, whereas **Type II diabetes** does **not usually require** frequent measurements of blood glucose concentration ($\\checkmark$). **Type I** diabetes requires lifelong **insulin injections**, whereas **Type II** diabetes only requires **insulin** injections if the **condition progresses** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. Any **eight** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ classification is used to organise organisms into groups             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ binomial nomenclature **OR** binomial system used to name organisms **OR** name is genus and species      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ allows communication and understanding between scientists all around the world        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there are three domains of life                     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a species is the smallest unit of classification    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hierarchy is used to group species **OR** the taxa are arranged in order of decreasing group size: domain, kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus, species        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ morphological **OR** artificial classification is based on physical characteristics        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ morphology can lead to errors **OR** analogous structures can mislead classification  [1 mark]\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ biochemical classification analyses molecular components **OR** proteins **OR** DNA **OR** RNA  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ base sequences in nucleic acids **OR** amino acid sequences of proteins are analysed     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cladistics is based on shared characteristics traced to most recent common ancestor **OR** cladogram is a visual representation of how species are evolutionarily related, with a node representing a common ancestor        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ modern approach combines evidence **OR** uses all types of classification **OR** uses morphology, biochemistry and genetics      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ natural classification groups organisms based on evolutionary history    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ classification includes fossils        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms with a recent common ancestor are classified more closely together    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ species in the same genus share many characteristics          [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ organisms can be reclassified when new evidence is found      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ natural classification allows scientists to predict characteristics of species within a group  [1 mark]\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Classification is the process of **organising organisms** into appropriate categories or **groups** based on their **shared characteristics** ($\\checkmark$). Scientists use **binomial nomenclature** to assign **each organism** a unique two-part name made up of the **genus** and **species** ($\\checkmark$), facilitating **global scientific communication** ($\\checkmark$). Organisms are traditionally classified **hierarchically**, starting with the broadest category, the **domain**, then working downwards -  **kingdom, phylum, class, order, family genus, species** ($\\checkmark$) (which can then be split into sub-species). **Traditional, artificial**, classification has been based on **physical traits. However**, this **morphological** approach ($\\checkmark$) can be **misleading** due to **analogous structures** ($\\checkmark$). **Modern** classification integrates **morphological, biochemica**l, and **genetic** information, ($\\checkmark$). **Biochemical** classification examines the **molecular components** of organisms, including the **sequences** of **bases** of  **DNA** and **amino acids** of **proteins** ($\\checkmark$). **Cladistics** is based on **shared characteristics** traced to the **most recent common ancestor**, and these relationships can be represented visually on **cladograms** ($\\checkmark$). As **new data emerges, reclassification** may occur ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1527","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1527\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1.  Describe how the primordial soup hypothesis (an aqueous mix of simple organic molecules) explains the origin of carbon compounds on Earth. [3 marks]\n\n2.  Mitochondria are organelles that are thought to have evolved from early prokaryotes. Discuss whether mitochondria can be considered living or nonliving. [4 marks]\n\n3.  Discuss the evidence and limitations for the existence of a last universal common ancestor. [8 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ early atmosphere **OR** oceans contained a mixture of gases **OR** carbon dioxide AND methane **OR** water vapour **OR** ammonia **OR** hydrogen gas [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ high levels of UV **OR** ultraviolet light **OR** electrical discharges **OR** lightning catalysed reactions in these gases    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the gases broke down into smaller, highly reactive components    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the smaller components reacted to form larger carbon compounds **OR** more complex organic molecules    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ abiogenesis **OR** chemical evolution    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ in water **OR** pools **OR** oceans    [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    The primordial soup hypothesis states that billions of years ago, there were much **higher levels of UV light** on Earth which **catalysed** reactions in the **gases** of the early atmosphere ($\\checkmark$). The UV radiation **broke down** inorganic molecules into **smaller, highly reactive** components ($\\checkmark$). The smaller, highly reactive components reacted to **form larger carbon compounds** ($\\checkmark$).    \n\n2.  Any **four** of the following:\\\n\n    *Answers must include at least one argument for living and one argument for non-living to gain full marks*\\\n\n    **Living because…**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they can replicate independently of the host cell **OR** carry our binary fission    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they contain their own ribosomes to make their own proteins    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they contain their own DNA **OR** genetic material    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they can make their own ATP **OR** release energy from organic molecules **OR** carry out a series of metabolic reactions independent of the host cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they have their own membranes    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they can grow larger    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they can excrete waste **OR** carbon dioxide    [1 mark]\n\n    **Non-living because…**\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they cannot survive independently outside of a host cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the majority of proteins needed for mitochondrial function are synthesised in the host cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they do not regulate homeostasis independently of the host cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they cannot respond to stimuli **OR** adapt to changing environments    [1 mark]\n\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Mitochondria can be thought of as non-living because they **cannot survive independently** outside the host cell ($\\checkmark$) and the majority of **proteins** needed for mitochondrial function are **made in the host cell** ($\\checkmark$). However, it could be argued that they are living as they carry out several life processes, such as **reproduction, independently** of the host cell ($\\checkmark$), and contain their own **ribosomes** which they use to **make** their own **proteins** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.  Any **eight** of the following:\\\n\n    *Answers must include at least one argument in support of a last universal common ancestor (LUCA) and at least one gap in the evidence to gain full marks*\n\n    ***Evidence that exists for the existence of a last universal common ancestor***\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ universal genetic code **OR** all organisms have the same nucleotide bases **OR** genetic code     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ conserved molecular structures **OR** all organisms have the same degenerate amino acid code **OR** the same codons code for the same amino acids in all organisms **OR** there are highly conserved DNA sequences that are common across many domains of life    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ genomic analysis of fossils shows that there are many similarities in the DNA sequences between modern and extinct organisms    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ specific proteins produced by different organisms can be used interchangeably between species without any major side effects    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ all organisms have a similar process for gene transcription and protein synthesis **OR** transcription and translation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ mutations in DNA **OR** amino acid sequences can be tracked over time and used as a ‘molecular clock’ to estimate the age of the last universal common ancestor    [1 mark]\n\n    **Gaps in the evidence for the existence of a last universal common ancestor**\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ incomplete fossil record makes it difficult to link all species together anatomically    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ very early organisms were unicellular **OR** made of soft tissues that do not readily fossilise    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lack of direct fossil evidence for the last universal common ancestor    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the last universal common ancestor does not exist today    [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]        \n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    The last universal common ancestor (LUCA) is proposed to be the evolutionary link between the abiotic phase of Earth’s history and the biotic phase. It is the last ancestor that links all biological life on Earth. Evidence for the LUCA’s existence comes from the fact that organisms are all biochemically similar; they share the **same DNA bases** ($\\checkmark$). Furthermore, the **same codons** code for the **same amino acids** in all organisms ($\\checkmark$), and all organisms have a similar process for **gene transcription and protein synthesis**($\\checkmark$). Also, there are some **highly conserved sequences** across the majority of organisms such as **ribosomal RNA** which is common across all domains of life ($\\checkmark$). **Genomic analysis** of fossils also shows that there are many similar DNA sequences between **extinct and modern organisms** ($\\checkmark$). However, there **isn’t** a **complete fossil record** which makes it difficult to link all species anatomically ($\\checkmark$), there is **no direct fossil evidence** for the existence of the LUCA ($\\checkmark$) and the LUCA does **not exist** on Earth **today** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2798","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI2798\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Electron microscopy provides images of cells with significantly higher resolution when compared to light microscopy. Evaluate the implications and limitations of relying primarily on electron micrographs to understand cell function.            [3 marks]\n\n2. Explain how advances in microscopy have contributed to our understanding of cell structure and function.                                [5 marks]\n\n3. Discuss the evidence for and against the idea that prokaryotic cells are “simple” compared to eukaryotic cells.                             [8 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **three** of the following, *must include at least **one** implication and **one** limitation*\n\n    ***Implications***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fine detail resolution has led to a more detailed understanding of structure-function relationships    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ discovery of new organelles and structures    [1 mark]\n\n    ***Limitations***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ requires complex sample preparation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ sample preparation can leave artefacts    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ samples must be fixed, dead, meaning no dynamic cellular processes can be observed    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ pigmentation or colour is not detected    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ maximum understanding comes from combining information from both light and electron microscopy    [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Electron microscopy gives us very **detailed images**, which let us see how the tiny parts of a cell are **structured to fulfil** their **functions** ($\\checkmark$). It has also led to the discovery of **new things inside** cells ($\\checkmark$). Unfortunately, to **prepare specimens** for electron microscopy, they must be treated in such a way that they are **killed**, and so we **cannot observe processes** happening in real time ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ light microscopy allowed for the initial visualisation of cells and basic structures    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ staining techniques have enhanced the visibility of cell components    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ fluorescence microscopy enables visualisation and localisation of specific molecules     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ live-imaging techniques allow dynamic processes to be observed    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ electron microscopy provides significantly higher resolution than light microscopy    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transmission electron microscopy reveals details of the internal structure of organelles    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ scanning electron microscopy provides 3-D images of cell surfaces **OR** cell shape    [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Light microscopy first allowed us to see **cells** and their **basic** parts ($\\checkmark$). Later, **staining** made it easier to see these parts more **clearly** ($\\checkmark$). **Fluorescence** microscopy has allowed scientists to visualise and pinpoint the **locations** of specific molecules within cells ($\\checkmark$).  **Electron** microscopy provides **far greater resolution** than light microscopy ($\\checkmark$), making it possible to see **internal structures** like mitochondria and membranes in **fine detail** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **eight** of the following:\n\n    ***In favour***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells lack membrane-bound organelles **OR** have less compartmentalisation        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells have a single, circular chromosome; eukaryotic cells have multiple linear chromosomes        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells are generally smaller        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells reproduce by binary fission, which is less complex than mitosis/meiosis        [1 mark]\n\n    ***Against***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells efficiently carry out all life processes within a single cell, demonstrating functional sophistication        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells exhibit diverse and complex metabolic pathways        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells exhibit unique metabolic pathways, ie chemosynthesis        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells can engage in cell signaling        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells have highly specialised adaptations (ie flagellae)        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells have a high surface area to volume ratio, increasing the exchange rate with the surroundings         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ prokaryotic cells are structurally simpler but functionally complex        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ evaluation of the application of the word “simple”        [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Prokaryotes are generally **smaller** ($\\checkmark$) than most eukaryotic cells, and they reproduce by **binary fission** ($\\checkmark$) instead of mitosis or meiosis. Prokaryotic cells are often called more simple than eukaryotic cells, but it is important to **distinguish between structure and function** ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotic cells may appear simple, but they still carry out all of the **functions of life** ($\\checkmark$) and have some that are unique to prokaryotes. Although prokaryotes **do not have membrane-bound organelles** ($\\checkmark$), they still have **complex metabolic** pathways like cellular respiration and even pathways eukaryotes do not have, like **chemosynthesis** ($\\checkmark$). Prokaryotes can communicate with each other via **cell signalling** pathways like quorum sensing ($\\checkmark$) and also have highly **specialised structures** such as flagellae for motility ($\\checkmark$). The term “simple” is an oversimplification of a superficial observation."},{"ref":"BI2799","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2799\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe transmission electron micrograph shows a mammalian lung mitochondrion.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/BI2799.png\" alt=\"BI2799\">\n[Source: Adapted from Howard, L (n.d.). *Mitochondria,_mammalian_lung_-_TEM* [Photograph]. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved 5 Sept. 2024 from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Mitochondria,_mammalian_lung_-_TEM.jpg. Copyright Public Domain]\n\n1. Draw an annotated diagram of the mitochondrion to scale.                [4 marks]\n\n2. Calculate the magnification of this image.                            [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ drawing shape resembles the image, including overall outline and internal structures    [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ single lines, no sketched lines, no shading            [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ matrix correctly labelled **AND** site of Krebs cycle **OR** contains enzymes for Krebs cycle **OR** metabolic reactions **OR** provides medium for substrate-enzymes interaction **OR** any other suitable function of matrix [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ crista correctly labelled **AND** increases surface area for electron transport chain **OR** ATP synthases **OR** oxidative phosphorylation **OR** site of ATP production  [1 mark]\n\n[4 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/Ans.png\" alt=\"Ans\">\n\n2.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ measured length of scale bar divided by 50 nm for magnification calculation **OR** $\\dfrac{7,000,000}{\\ce{50 nm}}$ (accept image line length in range 6,000,000 - 8,000,000 nm)    [1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ 140,000 $\\times$ (accept answers in range 120,000 - 160,000 x)        [1 mark]\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Length of scale bar in image = 7 mm \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 1mm = 1,000,000 nm \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ 7mm = 7,000,000 nm\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Actual length scale bar represents = 50 nm\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Magnification  = $\\dfrac{\\mathbf{7,000,000}}{\\mathbf{50\\ nm}}$ ($\\checkmark$) = **140,000** $\\times$ ($\\checkmark$)"},{"ref":"BI2835","marks":"8","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"PBQ","body":"# BI2835\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n| | | |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| **Rose of Sharon** ***(Hibiscus syriacus)*** $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/BI2835i.png\" alt=\"BI2835i\">$\\\\$ | **Singapore Graveyard Flower** ***(Plumeria obtusa)*** $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/BI2835ii.png\" alt=\"BI2835ii\">$\\\\$ | **Purple Loosestrife** ***(Lythrum salicaria)*** $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/BI2835iii.png\" alt=\"BI2835iii\">$\\\\$ |\n| **Daffodil** ***(Narcissus pseudonarcissus)*** $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/BI2835iv.png\" alt=\"BI2835iv\"> $\\\\$ | **Primrose** ***(Primula vulgaris)*** $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/BI2835v.png\" alt=\"BI2835v\"> $\\\\$ | **Balkan Anemone** ***(Anemone blanda)*** $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/BI2835vi.png\" alt=\"BI2835vi\"> $\\\\$ |\n| **Rose** ***(Rosa hybrid)*** $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/BI2835vii.png\" alt=\"BI2835vii\"> $\\\\$ | **Sunflower** ***(Helianthus annuus)*** $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/BI2835viii.png\" alt=\"BI2835viii\"> $\\\\$ | **Garden Peony** ***(Paeonia lactiflora)*** $\\\\$ <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/BI2835ix.png\" alt=\"BI2835ix\"> $\\\\$\n\n1. State **two** qualities that make a characteristic suitable for use as the divisions of a dichotomous key. [2 marks]\n\n2. Evaluate the use of flower colour in the construction of a dichotomous key. [3 marks]\n\n3. Construct a dichotomous key for four of the flowers in the diagram. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ clearly observable [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ binary **OR** providing a clear either/or choice **OR** two mutually exclusive options [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the characteristic should be consistently present in the organism it aims to group and consistently absent in the alternative group [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the two states of the characteristic should not be overlapping **OR** ambiguous [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ the characteristic used should be present **OR** interpretable across all items being identified by the key [1 mark]\n\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n A good characteristic for a dichotomous key should be **clearly visible** ($\\checkmark$) so that it can be easily observed in all organisms being identified. It should also offer a clear **either/or option**, such as presence or absence, so the user can make a **definitive choice** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. **three** of the following (*must include at least **one** advantage and **one** disadvantage to gain full marks*):\n\n ***advantages***\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ease of observation of colour [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ there are a wide variety of flower colours, increasing the possibility of differentiating between species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ can be a genetically fixed trait in some species [1 mark]\n\n ***disadvantages***\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ subjective perception of colour [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ variability within a species [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ environmental influence [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ image limitations (black and white) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ colour change with flower developmental stage [1 mark]\n\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Using colour for a dichotomous key has advantages and disadvantages. On the positive side, colour is **easy to observe** ($\\checkmark$) and there is a **wide variety of colours** so that it is easy to distinguish between species ($\\checkmark$). Colour can also be a disadvantage because different people may describe or **interpret the same colour differently** ($\\checkmark$), leading to inconsistent results. Also, **flower colour can vary** within a single plant ($\\checkmark$), and the colour of a single flower can change as it grows.\n\n3. any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ use of appropriate observable characteristics **OR** each step uses clear binary, and non-overlapping characteristics [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ structure of the key is logical and dichotomous **OR** the key follows a binary format at each step [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ accurate identification and separation of all four flowers [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ clear formatting and correct labelling of the key **OR** steps are numbered or lettered correctly and lead logically to species names [1 mark]\n\n *students may use any observable traits visible in the diagram*\n\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n **1a**. Flower head with a prominent central disk surrounded by many petals— **Sunflower**\\\n **1b**. ($\\checkmark$) Flower without a prominent central disk… **go to 2** ($\\checkmark$)\\\n 2a. Flower with petals fused into a distinct central trumpet or funnel shape… **Daffodil**\\\n 2b Flower without petals fused into a distinct central trumpet or funnel shape… go to 3\\\n 3a Numerous and overlapping petals… **Garden Peony**\\\n 3b Flowers clustered together on the stem… **Purple Loosestrife** ($\\checkmark$)\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Additional Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Unassigned/images/BI2835cms.png\" alt=\"BI2835cms\">"}]},{"name":"Viruses","questions":[{"ref":"BI0842","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0842\n\nBacteriophages and coronaviruses are both viruses that affect living organisms. Which structures are present in both bacteriophages and coronaviruses?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | cytoplasm |\n    | II. | capsid |\n    | III. | genetic material |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because bacteriophages and coronavirus have a capsid and genetic material, and do not have cytoplasm (**Choice A, Choice B**, and **Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0843","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0843\n\nWhich feature is present in coronaviruses but absent from T4 bacteriophages?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Genetic information\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ A protein capsid\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ A phospholipid envelope\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Structures for attachment to a host cell","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because coronaviruses have a phospholipid envelope derived from host-cell membranes, whereas T4 bacteriophages do not have an envelope.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because both contain genetic material. T4 bacteriophages contain DNA, whereas coronaviruses contain RNA.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because both have protein components that package their genetic material. In T4 bacteriophages, DNA is enclosed within the protein capsid of the head. In coronaviruses, RNA is associated with nucleocapsid proteins.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because both have structures for attachment to host cells. T4 bacteriophages use tail fibres, whereas coronaviruses use spike proteins."},{"ref":"BI0844","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0844\n\nWhich of the rows below shows the correct characteristics of viruses, prokaryotes and eukaryotes?\n\n| | Virus | Prokaryote | Eukaryote\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Genome is DNA or RNA | Genome is DNA only | Genome is DNA only |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Rely on host cell for nutrition | Rely on host cell for nutrition | Does not rely on host cell for nutrition |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Synthesises ATP for energy supply| Synthesises ATP for energy supply | Synthesises ATP for energy supply |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Capsid present | Capsid present | Capsid present|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because viruses contain DNA or RNA as their genome. Viruses rely on host cells for nutrition, but prokaryotes and eukaryotes do not (**Choice B**). Prokaryotes and eukaryotes synthesise ATP, but viruses do not (**Choice C**). Capsids are present in viruses but not in prokaryotes or eukaryotes (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0845","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0845\n\nSome bacteriophages infect host bacteria using the lytic cycle or the lysogenic cycle.\nWhich process is characteristic of the lysogenic cycle but not the lytic cycle?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Phage DNA is injected into the host cell.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The phage inserts DNA into host DNA, forming a prophage.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Host bacterium is killed by cell lysis.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Phage proteins bind to receptors on the host bacterium cell.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because phage DNA is inserted into the host cell DNA in the lysogenic cycle, but not the lytic cycle. During the lysogenic cycle and lytic cycle, phage proteins bind to specific receptors on the surface of the host bacterium (**Choice D**). Phage DNA is then inserted into the host bacterium (**Choice A**) in both the lysogenic cycle and lytic cycle.  Host bacterial cells are killed by lysis in the lytic cycle (**Choice C**)."},{"ref":"BI0846","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0846\n\nWhich is characteristic of both prokaryotes and viruses?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Contain genetic code  |\n    | II. | Rely on host cell for metabolism |\n    | III. | Surrounded by cell wall |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because both prokaryotes and viruses contain a genetic code. Only viruses rely on a host cell for metabolism, and only prokaryotes have a cell wall (**Choice B, Choice C**, and **Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0847","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0847\n\nWhich is not a factor in the rapid evolution of the human immunodeficiency virus?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Genetic recombination\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ High mutation rate\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Slow replication rate\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Genetic diversity of host population","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C** is correct because a slow replication rate is not a factor in the rapid evolution of HIV. Genetic recombination occurs when many strains of the virus infect the same host, and a new combination of genetic material is observed, which leads to changes in the virus (evolution) over time (**Choice A**).   Mutations occur rapidly in HIV because it is a single-stranded RNA virus and lacks a mechanism to proofread the genome. The introduction of this variation leads to evolution (**Choice B**). The genome of humans and the HIV host population, has high genetic diversity. Such diversity in the genome may provide a selective advantage for some HIV strains (**Choice D**)."},{"ref":"BI0848","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0848\n\nThe origin of viruses is an area of active research and exploration. Which statement is not evidence that viruses have origins in other organisms?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Similarities between genome sequence in viral and cellular genes.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Coevolution of viruses and host.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Retroviral enzymes such as reverse transcriptase convert RNA to DNA.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Rate of mutation is rapid in viruses.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because the rapid mutation rate is not evidence of viral origins, but rather a characteristic of some viruses.   Similarities between genome sequences in viral and cellular genes (**Choice A**), the coevolution of viruses and hosts (**Choice B**), and retroviral enzymes converting RNA to DNA (**Choice C**),  are all evidence of viral origins from other organisms."},{"ref":"BI0849","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0849\n\nViruses, compared to living organisms, are very small. Which other characteristic listed below distinguishes viruses from unicellular living organisms?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Contain genetic material\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Contain cytoplasm\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Lack enzymes\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Genetic material is surrounded by a capsid","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because The structure of all viruses includes the presence of a capsid, which surrounds the genetic material (**Choice D**). Both viruses and living organisms contain genetic material (**Choice A**). Unicellular living organisms contain cytoplasm (**Choice B**).  Some viruses lack enzymes, but not all viruses lack enzymes (**Choice C**)."},{"ref":"BI0850","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0850\n\nA diagram of HIV is shown below. Which structure is composed of glycoproteins?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Viruses/images/BI0850.png\" alt=\"BI0850\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because HIV spikes are glycoproteins. Reverse transcriptase (**Choice A**) is an enzyme, and made of protein, not glycoprotein. The matrix (**Choice D**) and capsid (**Choice C**) are made of protein but not glycoprotein."},{"ref":"BI0851","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0851\n\nWhich is **not** characteristic of HIV?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The viral RNA encodes for reverse transcriptase\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Spike glycoproteins allow for entry into CD4 cells\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Two single strands of RNA are encased in a capsid\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Integrase proteins are required for entry into a host cell","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is correct because integrase proteins are not required to enter the host cell; integrase proteins are needed to integrate viral genetic information into host DNA. All other answer options (**Choice A, Choice B**, and **Choice C**) are characteristics of HIV."},{"ref":"BI0852","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0852\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe image below shows a virus which reproduces using the lytic or lysogenic cycle.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Viruses/images/lambda%20bacteriophage%20servier.png\" alt=\"lambda bacteriophage servier\">\n[Source: Adapted from File: bacteriophage-virus. Retrieved 10 December 2024 from: https://smart.servier.com/smart_image/bacteriophage-virus. CC BY 4.0]\n\n1. State two structural features common to all viruses. [2 marks]\n\n2.\n 1. Identify the name of this virus. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Label the capsid on the image. [1 mark]\n\n3. Compare and contrast the lytic and lysogenic cycles. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ small size relative to living organisms **OR** size is fixed [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ contains nucleic acids as genetic material **OR** contain genetic material **OR** contain DNA or RNA (both needed for mark) [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ surrounded by a capsid **OR** protein coat [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ do not have cytoplasm **OR** do not have organelles [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n Two structural features common to all viruses are that they contain either **DNA or RNA** ($\\checkmark$), surrounded by a **capsid** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ bacteriophage **OR** lambda bacteriophage **OR** lambda phage (phage on its own is not accepted) [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n 2. see the image below\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Viruses/images/BI0852%20mark%20scheme.png\" alt=\"BI0852 mark scheme\">\n [Source: Adapted from File: bacteriophage-virus. Retrieved 10 December 2024 from: https://smart.servier.com/smart_image/bacteriophage-virus. CC BY 4.0]\n\n [1 mark]\n\n3. any **four** of the following, but answer must include at least one similarity and one difference to earn full marks:\n\n **similarities**\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** cycles are mechanisms for phage, **OR** bacteriophage, reproduction [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** cycles require phage proteins to bind to specific receptors on the surface of the host bacterium [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** require phage DNA, **OR** phage genetic information **OR** phage genome, to be inserted into the host bacterium [1 mark]\n\n **differences**\\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in lytic cycle, host bacterial cells are killed **OR** lysed, **WHILE** host cells are not destroyed **OR** lysed in lysogenic cycle [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in lytic cycle, phage genome **OR** DNA remains separate to bacterial DNA within the host cell, **WHILE** phage DNA is inserted into host cell in lysogenic cycle [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in lytic cycle, phage genes, **OR** DNA, **OR** genetic information is expressed immediately, **WHILE** in the lysogenic cycle, genes are not transcribed, **OR** expressed, **OR** genetic information lies dormant [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ in lytic cycle host cells are lysed rapidly, leading to immediate harm to the host bacterium, **WHILE** in lysogenic cycle, there is no harm to host for extended period of time, **OR** cycle is not immediate [1 mark]\n [4 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n **Both** lytic and lysogenic cycles are processes of **phage reproduction** ($\\checkmark$). **Both** cycles start with **phage proteins binding** to bacterial host cell **receptors** ($\\checkmark$), and then the **genetic material** is **injected** ($\\checkmark$) into the host cell. In the **lytic cycle, phage DNA remains separate** to the **bacterial DN** within the host cell, while in the **lysogenic cycle, viral DNA** is **inserted into the host chromosome** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0853","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0853\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe image below shows the human immunodeficiency virus (HIV).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Viruses/images/BI0853A.png\" alt=\"BI0853A\">\n\n1. Explain the function of structure X. [2 marks]\n\n2.\n 1. Identify structure Y. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Identify structure Z. [1 mark]\n\n3. State the host of this virus. [1 mark]\n\n4. Describe the metabolic functions of reverse transcriptase. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **two** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ host cell attachment [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ specificity, **OR** specific spikes, bind to specific host cell receptors [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ facilitate viral entry into host cell [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ facilitate fusion of the viral envelope with the host cell membrane [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Structure X, the viral glycoprotein spikes, facilitate **attachment to the host cell** ($\\checkmark$) by binding to specific receptors on its surface. This allows the virus to **enter the host cell** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ capsid [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n 2.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ RNA **OR** single stranded RNA [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n3.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ humans **OR** *Homo sapiens* [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n4. Any **three** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ reverse transcriptase is an enzyme used in HIV replication [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ uses viral RNA as template [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ synthesises viral DNA from RNA **OR** reverses transcription to make viral DNA from RNA [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ viral DNA is integrated into host chromosome with the help of reverse transcriptase [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ integrated viral DNA is transcribed by host cell to synthesise viral proteins [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n HIV is categorised as a retrovirus. The enzyme reverse transcriptase is contained in the virus's capsid and **is needed for HIV replication** ($\\checkmark$). The enzyme synthesises **a complementary DNA strand** ($\\checkmark$) **from the HIV genetic material, RNA** ($\\checkmark$). Reverse transcriptase **facilitates the integration of viral DNA into the host chromosome** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0854","marks":"16","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI0854\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1. Outline the varied forms of genetic material found in viruses.    [4 marks]\n\n2. Discuss the evidence supporting that the diversity of viruses exists as a result of convergent evolution.    [5 marks]\n\n3. Compare the structure of the coronavirus with the human immunodeficiency virus, HIV. [7 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **four** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ single-stranded RNA     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ single-stranded DNA        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ double-stranded RNA        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ double-stranded DNA        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleic acid **OR** DNA **OR** RNA     [1 mark]\n    [4 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nThere are many different types of viruses. The types of genetic information found in different types of viruses include **single-stranded RNA** ($\\checkmark$), **double-stranded RNA** ($\\checkmark$), **single-stranded DNA** ($\\checkmark$), and **double-stranded DNA** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **five** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ convergent evolution refers to the appearance of similar traits in unrelated organisms as a response to similar selection pressures     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ viruses exhibit a wide range of forms **OR** structures **OR** mechanisms to adapt to different hosts **OR** environments            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ presence of a capsid surrounding genetic material **OR** nucleocapsid is a shared feature that evolved independently in different viral lineages                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ presence of specific protein receptors or spikes have evolved independently to facilitate host cell attachment **OR** suitable example such as similar spike proteins in coronaviruses and influenza viruses    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ independent evolution of similar replication enzymes **OR** reverse transcriptase        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ viruses utilise either DNA **OR** RNA as their genetic material, demonstrating molecular convergence in adapting to different replication strategies         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ convergent traits enhance viral fitness by aiding in host cell attachment **OR** immune evasion **OR** efficient replication        [1 mark]\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Convergent evolution occurs when **unrelated organisms** evolve **similar traits** due to **similar selection pressures** ($\\checkmark$). In viruses, a wide **range** of **structures** and **shapes** are observed, which enable them to multiply in **different hosts**($\\checkmark$). One example is the **independent evolution** of **protein spikes** in **coronaviruses** and **influenza viruses** to aid in **host cell attachment** ($\\checkmark$). Similarly, unrelated viruses have independently evolved **reverse transcriptase** for **efficient replication**  ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, viruses use either **DNA or RNA** as genetic material, showing **molecular convergence** in adapting to various **replication needs** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **seven** of the following (*answer must include at least **two** similarities and **two** differences to gain full marks*):\n\n    ***Similarities***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** are spherical in shape [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contain (viral) envelope, **OR** both are enveloped with a lipid bilayer    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contain RNA as the genetic material    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contain a capsid    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** contain spikes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ **BOTH** spikes are made of glycoprotein [1 mark]\n\n    ***Differences***\\\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ HIV contains reverse transcriptase, **WHILE** coronaviruses do not        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ HIV contains two (single) strands of RNA, **WHILE** the coronavirus contains one single strand of RNA **OR** HIV genome is shorter or about 10,000 nucleotides, **WHILE** coronavirus is longer (about 30,000 nucleotides)    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ HIV has an enveloped capsid, **WHILE** coronavirus contains a helical (nucleo)capsid  [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ HIV genome is within enveloped capsid, **WHILE**  coronavirus genome is a nucleocapsid/not in envelope         [1 mark]\n    [7 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    In comparing the coronavirus and the human immunodeficiency virus (HIV), there are several similarities and differences.  Both the coronavirus and HIV are **spherical in shape** ($\\checkmark$), the genetic information is enclosed by a **capsid** ($\\checkmark$), and both types of viruses contain **RNA as the genetic material** ($\\checkmark$). However, **HIV** is a retrovirus and **contains the enzyme reverse transcriptase**, while the **coronavirus does not contain reverse transcriptase** ($\\checkmark$). Both viruses **contain spikes** ($\\checkmark$) for attachment to cells, **and both HIV spikes and coronavirus spikes are made of glycoprotein** ($\\checkmark$). **HIV contains two** single **strands of RNA; however, the coronavirus contains one single strand of RNA** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2375","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2375\n\nWhich statement correctly describes the genetic material changes (using reverse transcriptase)  that occur in a retrovirus inside a host cell?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Viral DNA is used to produce viral RNA\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Viral RNA is used to produce viral DNA\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Viral RNA is converted directly into host cell RNA\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Viral RNA replaces host cell DNA","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because retroviruses use reverse transcriptase to produce a DNA copy from their RNA genome. The viral RNA therefore acts as a template for the production of viral DNA inside the host cell.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because this describes the production of RNA from DNA, which is transcription, not reverse transcription.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because reverse transcriptase produces DNA from RNA rather than converting viral RNA directly into host cell RNA.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because the viral RNA does not replace the host cell DNA. Instead, a DNA copy of the viral genome is produced and may become integrated into the host cell’s DNA."},{"ref":"BI2939","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2939\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe diagram shows the lysogenic and lytic life cycles of a bacteriophage.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Viruses/images/BI2939a.png\" alt=\"BI2939a\">\n[Source: Adapted from CNX OpenStax. (2016, May 27). *File: Figure 21 02 03*.png. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved June 18, 2025 from https:://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Figure_21_02_03.png. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n1. Outline the event occurring in **Step X** of the bacteriophage lifecycle.        [1 mark]\n\n2.\n    1. Define a prophage.  [1 mark]\n\n    2. Using the letter **P**, label a prophage in the diagram.        [1 mark]\n\n3. Explain why viruses are termed ‘obligate intracellular parasites’.            [2 marks]\n\n4. Suggest **two** environmental conditions that could cause a bacteriophage to switch from the lysogenic to the lytic cycle.                [2 marks]\n\n5. Suggest **one** advantage for the bacteriophage in undergoing the lysogenic cycle instead of the lytic cycle.                [1 mark]\n\n6. Explain how integrating bacteriophage DNA into the bacterial host genome can influence bacterial evolution.                [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the host cell is bursting **OR** lysing **AND** phages **OR** bacteriophages **OR** virions are being released        [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    In Step X, the host cell undergoes\n    **lysis** as newly formed **bacteriophages** are **released** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n    1.\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ inactive form of  bacteriophage **OR** phage in which the phage genome **OR** DNA has been incorporated into the bacterial genome **OR** chromosomes   [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ A prophage is the **inactive** form of a  **bacteriophage** in which the **phage genome** is **integrated** into the **bacterial cell’s chromosome** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n    2. any **one** of the following\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ letter **P** should be used to indicate where the ​​viral DNA is physically connected to **OR** fused with the bacterial chromosome in step 2 **OR** step 3 of the lysogenic life cycle        [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Viruses/images/b2ans.png\" alt=\"b2ans\">\n    [Source: Adapted from CNX OpenStax. (2016, May 27). *File: Figure 21 02 03*.png. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved June 18, 2025 from https:://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Figure_21_02_03.png. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n3. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cannot complete life cycle **OR** reproduce **OR** replicate outside of a living **OR** host cell    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lack own metabolic machinery **OR** enzymes to produce ATP **OR** release energy **OR** cannot carry out metabolic processes independently        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ dependent on **OR** must use host cell’s ribosomes **OR** enzymes **OR** cellular components for protein synthesis **OR** replication    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cause harm to the host cell during lysis    [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Viruses are described as obligate parasites because they **cannot** carry out **metabolic processes independently** ($\\checkmark$) and can only **reproduce inside** a **host cell** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ exposure to antibiotics        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ exposure to UV light **OR** radiation     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ exposure to mutagenic chemicals        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ exposure to high **OR** extreme temperatures    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ damage **OR** stress to the host cell **OR** damage to host cell’s DNA    [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Exposure to **antibiotics** ($\\checkmark$) or **UV radiation** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n5. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phage DNA is passed on to all daughter cells when the host cell replicates, leading to widespread infection **OR** propagation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phage can remain dormant **OR** latent and persist through unfavourable **OR** stressful  conditions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ host cell is not killed immediately, allowing for the phage to spread without destroying its reservoir        [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    The bacteriophage can remain **dormant** within the host genome, allowing ot to **persist through unfavourable conditions** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phage **OR** bacteriophage DNA  introduces new genes **OR** alleles into bacterial genome     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ this process is a form of horizontal gene transfer **OR** HGT **OR**  a form of transduction        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ new genes may confer selective advantage **OR** new traits **OR** antibiotic resistance **OR** increased pathogenicity    **OR** other suitable example            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increases genetic variation in the bacterial population for natural selection to act upon        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ phage DNA is excised **OR** removed and may carry adjacent bacterial genes to a new host     [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Integrating viral DNA into a bacterial genome **introduces new genes** into the bacterial genome ($\\checkmark$), which may result in the host cell having **new traits** such as **antibiotic resistance** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2941","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2941\n\nThe diagrams show the external structures of a coronavirus and a bacteriophage.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Viruses/images/2941a.png\" alt=\"2941a\">\n\nWhat is the difference in their structure?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The coronavirus has a tail to attach to host cells, whereas the bacteriophage lambda does not.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The bacteriophage lambda has an RNA genome, whereas the coronavirus has a DNA genome.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The coronavirus has an envelope surrounding its capsid, whereas the bacteriophage lambda does not.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Bacteriophage lambda does not have a capsid, whereas the coronavirus does.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nBacteriophage lambda infects prokaryotes, while coronaviruses infect eukaryotic cells. Coronaviruses use the host cell’s membrane to form an envelope, protecting the capsid and aiding infection through glycoproteins that bind to host receptors. In contrast, bacteriophage lambda lacks an envelope and uses its tail to attach to host cells. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the coronavirus does not have a tail for attachment, whereas the bacteriophage lambda does. **Choice B** is incorrect because the coronavirus has an RNA genome, and the bacteriophage lambda has a DNA genome. **Choice D** is incorrect because all viruses possess a capsid to protect their genome."},{"ref":"BI2943","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2943\n\nThe image shows the lysogenic life cycle of a bacteriophage.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Viruses/images/BI2943.png\" alt=\"BI2943\">\n\nWhat process is occurring in Step 4?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The host cell divides, and the viral DNA is copied into the daughter cells.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Bacteriophage DNA is removed from the host cell DNA, and the cell is destroyed.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The host cell transfers the viral DNA into another cell using horizontal gene transfer.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Bacteriophage DNA is removed from the host cell DNA, ready to enter the lytic cycle.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nIn the lysogenic cycle, the viral DNA is integrated into the host chromosome as a prophage. Step 4 represents induction of the lysogenic cycle. This is when the prophage (viral DNA integrated in the host genome) is excised (removed) from the host DNA, and the phage is ready to switch into the lytic cycle (viral genes become active, leading to replication and assembly). **Choice D** is, therefore, correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because host cell division with the prophage being copied into daughter cells happens during binary fission (vertical transmission) and is represented by the dividing cell stage (Step 3), not Step 4.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because, although prophage DNA can be removed during induction, the host cell is not destroyed immediately at this point. Cell destruction occurs later in the lytic cycle during lysis, after viral DNA replication and phage assembly.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because this diagram shows the prophage being inherited through vertical transmission (cell division), not the host transferring viral DNA to another cell via horizontal gene transfer. Horizontal gene transfer would involve the movement of DNA between different cells (e.g., transduction), which is not what Step 4 depicts."},{"ref":"BI2945","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2945\n\nThe graph compares the base mutation rates of different DNA and RNA viruses during replication in one host cell.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Viruses/images/BI2945.png\" alt=\"BI2945\">\n[Source: Adapted from Cuevas, J. M., Geller, R., Garijo, R., López-Aldeguer, J., & Sanjuán, R. (2015, September 16). *Extremely high mutation rate of HIV-1 in vivo*. PLOS Biology. Retrieved 17 June 2025 from, https://journals.plos.org/plosbiology/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pbio.1002251. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\nWhat can be concluded from this graph?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ DNA viruses have a higher mutation rate than RNA viruses\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ All RNA viruses have a higher mutation rate than DNA viruses\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The polio virus has a mutation rate approximately 10 $\\times$ higher than the HIV-1 virus\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Herpes simplex has a higher mutation rate than lambda phage","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe polio virus has a mutation rate of 0.0001, and the HIV-1 virus has a mutation rate of 0.00001, which is 10 $\\times$ less. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because the graph shows that RNA viruses have a much higher mutation rate than DNA viruses. This is due to the fact that RNA-dependent RNA polymerase lacks proofreading capability.\\\n**Choice B** is incorrect because while all RNA viruses shown in the graph have higher rates, you cannot conclude 'All RNA viruses' globally from just four examples. **Choice D** is incorrect because the herpes simplex virus has a lower mutation rate than the lambda phage."}]},{"name":"Water","questions":[{"ref":"BI0177","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0177\n\nWhich of the following statements about the dipolarity of the water molecule are true?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Oxygen attracts the shared electrons stronger than hydrogen.\n- II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ Hydrogen atoms are partially negative.\n- III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ The shared electrons spend more time closer to oxygen.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$  II and III\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is correct. Oxygen has a higher electronegativity compared to hydrogen. This means that oxygen will attract shared electrons more strongly.\n\n**Statement II** is false. Since the shared electrons between oxygen and hydrogen spend more time near oxygen,  the hydrogen in the water molecule becomes partially positive.\n\n**Statement III** is true because oxygen attracts the shared electrons more strongly resulting in the shared electrons spending more time closer to the oxygen atom."},{"ref":"BI0178","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0178\n\nWhat intermolecular bond is responsible for water's cohesive, adhesive and thermal properties?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Covalent bond\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrogen bond\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Ionic bond\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Metallic bond","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice B** is correct because water is a polar molecule (dipolar). Hydrogen bonds form due to the attraction between the partially positive (δ:sup[+]) hydrogen atom of one molecule and the partially negative (δ:sup[-]) oxygen atom of another. These intermolecular forces are responsible for:\n\n- Cohesion: Water molecules sticking to each other.\n- Adhesion: Water molecules sticking to other polar/charged surfaces.\n- Thermal Properties: High specific heat capacity and latent heat of vaporisation due to the energy required to break these bonds.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because covalent bonds are intramolecular; they hold the hydrogen and oxygen atoms together within a single water molecule, but do not hold different water molecules to one another.\n\n**Choices C** and **D** are incorrect because water is a molecular compound; it does not contain metals (metallic bonding) nor does it involve the complete transfer of electrons between ions (ionic bonding)."},{"ref":"BI0181","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0181\n\nWhat substances are attracted to water and form intermolecular bonds with water molecules?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Insoluble substances\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrophobic substances\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrophilic substances\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Non-polar substances","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nHydrophilic (**Choice C**) substances dissolve in water through the formation of hydrogen bonds with water. \\\nNon-polar (**Choice D**) substances are not attracted to water molecules and do not form bonds with them. These substances are hydrophobic (**Choice B**), making them insoluble (**Choice A**) in water."},{"ref":"BI0182","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0182\n\nWhat property of water helps living organisms maintain their internal body temperature?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Thermal properties\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Cohesive properties\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Adhesive properties\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Solvent properties","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWater's thermal properties (**Choice A**) include a high heat of vaporisation which results in the absorption of a large amount of heat by water before changing its physical state. This means that changing the temperature of the water is slow, and this helps to maintain the internal temperature of organisms between set limits. \\\nWater has cohesive properties (**Choice B**) which means it sticks to other water molecules. \\\nWater has adhesive properties (**Choice C**) which means it adheres to charged surfaces. \\\nWater is an excellent solvent (**Choice D**) which means it dissolves a wide array of polar and ionic substances."},{"ref":"BI0183","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0183\n\nWhich of the following is not soluble in water?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Glucose\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Amino Acids\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Cholesterol\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Sodium Chloride","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCholesterol (**Choice C**) is a non-polar hydrophobic molecule. Therefore, it is insoluble in water. \\\nGlucose (**Choice A**), amino acids (**Choice B**), and sodium chloride (**Choice D**) are soluble in water and are hydrophilic."},{"ref":"BI0186","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0186\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n1. Outline how hydrogen bonds form between water molecules. [2 marks]\n\n2. State one of the ways that hydrophilic substances interact with water. [1 mark]\n\n3.  State one of the ways that non-polar molecules interact with water.[1 mark]\n\n4. Distinguish between cohesion and adhesion.[2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen bonds are formed between the hydrogen atom of one water molecule and the oxygen atom of another water molecule [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen atoms in water molecules are partially positive [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen atoms in water molecules are partially negative [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen bonds are a polar attraction and electrons are not shared [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    In water molecules, hydrogen bonds are formed between the **partially positive** ($\\checkmark$) hydrogens of one water molecule and the **partially negative** ($\\checkmark$) oxygens of other water molecules.\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  they are attracted to and form intermolecular bonds with water            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ they form hydrogen bonds                            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  they dissolve in water **OR** are soluble in water                    [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    Hydrophilic substances are **attracted to water**($\\checkmark$)  and form intermolecular bonds. They are said to be **soluble** ($\\checkmark$) in water.\n\n3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  do not freely associate with water [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  hydrophobic are repelled by water [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  do not dissolve in water OR insoluble in water [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    Non-polar molecules are **hydrophobic** ($\\checkmark$) and **do not dissolve** ($\\checkmark$)  in water.\n\n4. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  in cohesion, water molecules stick to each other **WHILE** in adhesion, water molecules adhere to the polar, **OR** charged, surfaces of other molecules [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  cohesion explains water’s surface tension properties  **WHILE** adhesion explains capillary action    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  in plants, adhesion allows water to travel by capillary forces  **WHILE** cohesion is responsible for maintaining an uninterrupted water column [1 mark]\n\n[2 marks]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\nWater molecules are polar, with one end being partially positive and the other end being partially negative. This polarity allows water to form hydrogen bonds with other molecules. When **hydrogen bonds form between two water molecules, this is cohesion**($\\checkmark$), whereas hydrogen bonds forming between water and other polar molecules is **adhesion**. An example of adhesion and cohesion working together would be in plants, where water travels up xylem vessels against gravity. This is possible because **cohesion maintains an uninterrupted water column**($\\checkmark$), and the adhesion of water to the cellulose walls of xylem vessels drives the water upwards through **capillary action**($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0187","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0187\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nBelow is a picture of a water strider ($\\textit{Gerris sp.}$).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Water/images/BI0187i_2.2.png\" alt=\"BI0187i_2.2\">\n[Source: Schnobby. (2010). *Water Strider*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved April 27, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Water_strider_in_a_pond.jpg. Copyright by CC BY-SA 3.0]\n\n1. Explain why the water strider ($\\textit{Gerris sp.}$) does not sink. [2 marks]\n\n2. Describe, using an example, the importance of water as a solvent in living organisms. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  cohesion, **OR** cohesive properties, of water is responsible [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  water molecules bind to other water molecules [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  water molecules form intermolecular hydrogen bonds [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  cohesion results in high surface tension of water [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  legs of the insect are hydrophobic [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    **Cohesion**($\\checkmark$) is the water property that is responsible for this phenomenon. Water **molecules bind with other water molecules** ($\\checkmark$) through **hydrogen bonds** ($\\checkmark$)to create **high surface tension**($\\checkmark$). The legs of the water strider are covered in a secreted wax, making them **hydrophobic**($\\checkmark$), which allows the water strider to move on top of the water.\n\n2. Any **three** of the following (maximum of 2 marks if no example is provided):\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  metabolic reactions can only happen in solutions [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  water dissolves hydrophilic substances needed for life processes [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  substances can be transported through the water-based solutions [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  blood plasma dissolves and transports sodium chloride **OR** glucose **OR** amino acids **OR** any other valid example in animals [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$  water dissolves and transports nutrients **OR** minerals needed by plants **OR** any other valid example in plants [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    **Water dissolves hydrophilic substances**($\\checkmark$)\nrequired for **metabolic reactions** ($\\checkmark$) which can only occur in solution.  Dissolved substances can react with each other and also be **transported**($\\checkmark$)to other parts of an organism. For example, water dissolves and transports glucose in the **blood plasma**($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0668","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0668\n\nThe cohesive and adhesive properties of water allow xylem vessels to transport water inside plants.\n\nWhich of the following are correct descriptions of how these properties are related to water transport in plants?\n\n| | Cohesive | Adhesive |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$| Polar water molecules are attracted to one another and form hydrogen bonds. | Polar water molecules are attracted to the hydrophilic cell walls in xylem.\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Water molecules are polar, so are able to dissolve ions | Polar water molecules are attracted to one another and form hydrogen bonds. |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Polar water molecules are attracted to one another and form oxygen bonds. | Water molecules are polar, so are able to dissolve ions |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Polar water molecules are attracted to the hydrophilic cell walls in xylem. | Polar water molecules are attracted to one another and form hydrogen bonds. |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWater is cohesive because it is a polar molecule. The oxygen atom in one water molecule forms a hydrogen bond with the hydrogen atom of another water molecule. In xylem vessels water is attracted to the hydrophilic regions of the cell wall and hydrogen bonds are formed.  This an accurate description of the adhesive properties of water in the context of water transport in plants (**Choice A**). These statements are reversed in **Choice D**.\n\nIons dissolve in water, but this is not related to the cohesive (**Choice B**) or adhesive (**Choice C**) properties of water."},{"ref":"BI0834","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0834\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe image shows a cross-section of the vascular tissue of a woody dicot stem, showing the annual rings of phloem and xylem.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Water/images/BI0834.jpg\" alt=\"BI0834\">\n[Source: Adapted from: Berkshire Community College Bioscience Image Library. (2014, February 10). File:Woody Dicot stem primary xylem in late one year  - wikimedia. Wikimedia commons. Retrieved July 07, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Woody_Dicot_Stem_Ray_System_in_Two_Year_Tilia_%2834997659262%29.jpg. Copyright free]\n\n1. Explain how the properties of water allow it to be transported in the xylem.  [3 marks]\n\n2. Describe a physical property of water, other than those that are important for the movement of water through the plant, that makes water essential for the survival of plants.    [1 mark]\n\n3. Outline why plants cannot use water to transport lipids.    [1 mark]\n\n4. If the temperature drops to -1:sup[o]C for a few hours suggest a reason why the water in xylem may not freeze.    [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Any **three** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water is cohesive **OR** hydrogen bonding between water molecules **OR** strong intermolecular forces so can form a continuous column of water **OR** counteract gravity     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water is adhesive **OR** water molecules can adhere to other polar surfaces **OR** water adheres to walls of xylem vessels **OR** to lignin and cellulose of xylem vessels, so prevent collapse of water column **OR** separation from xylem vessel walls     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ capillary action occurs as water moves upwards against gravity in the narrow xylem vessels         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the hydrogen bonds between water molecules mean that as water evaporates  from the leaf  negative pressure **OR** tension occurs, so water moves upwards against gravity         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ low viscosity reduces resistance to flow within the xylem         [1 mark]\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\nWater can be transported efficiently through the xylem as the water molecules adhere to the inside of the xylem vessels ($\\checkmark$) and cohere to each other, therefore forming a continual column of water ($\\checkmark$). As the water evaporates from the leaves, the hydrogen bonds between the molecules mean that a negative pressure occurs at the top of this column of water, enabling the water to move upwards, against gravity ($\\checkmark$).  This is the cohesion-tension theory.\n\n2. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water is a solvent so can dissolve nutrients which can be used by the plant **OR** provides a medium for various biochemical reactions     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ high specific heat capacity prevents extreme temperature fluctuations that could damage plant tissues **OR** maintain optimal enzyme activity and metabolic processes in plants     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transparency allows light to penetrate and reach submerged plants for photosynthesis         [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Water is known as a universal solvent, so nutrients can be dissolved in it and be transported to where they are needed ($\\checkmark$).\n\n 3. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ lipids are hydrophobic **OR** non-polar, so cannot dissolve in water     [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Water cannot be used to transport lipids in plants as they are hydrophobic  ($\\checkmark$).\n\n 4. Any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water has a high specific heat capacity, **OR** it has to lose a lot of energy to freeze     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ due to the high solute concentration of the contents of the xylem         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ increase in pressure in the xylem raises the freezing point of water             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ high specific heat capacity means water can release stored heat slowly, maintaining a stable temperature above freezing point                 [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Water in the xylem may not freeze when the temperature drops below -1:sup[o]C, as it has a high specific heat capacity  ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0835","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI0835\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nThe image shows a ringed seal (*Pusa hispida*) that lives in the Arctic Sea.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Water/images/BI0835.png\" alt=\"BI0835\">\n[Source: Adapted from: Kingfisher. (2021, May 29). Pusa hispida. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved from July 07, 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Pusa_hispida. Copyright free]\n\n1. Distinguish between the physical properties of air and water that allow the seal to mostly live in the sea.      [2 marks]\n\n2. Ringed seals glide into the water head first from ice shelves, rather like humans diving into a pool head first. Explain the advantage of entering the water in this way rather than ‘belly-flopping’ into the water.    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water has a high specific heat capacity **COMPARED TO** air, so its temperature remains relatively stable compared to air **OR** so water remains relatively warmer during the winter     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water has a higher density **COMPARED TO** air, and this supports the seal’s body weight as the water exerts buoyant forces on the seal     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water has a higher viscosity **COMPARED TO** air, providing the seal with more resistance to movement **OR** motion, which provides the seal with thrust and propulsion for swimming     [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    The ringed seal is mostly able to live in water rather than air as water has a **high specific heat capacity** compared to air, so its **temperature remains relatively stable** so that the seal does not get too cold ($\\checkmark$). Water has a **higher density** compared to air, and this **supports** the seal’s **body weight** as the water exerts **buoyant forces** on the seal ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. Any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water has a high surface tension due to hydrogen bonds **THEREFORE**, ‘belly-flopping’ will cause pain due to the hydrogen bonds breaking forcefully **OR** gliding/diving causes hydrogen bonds between water molecules to be broken more gently **OR** entering head first reduces the body surface area that initially comes into contact with the water **THEREFORE** reducing water resistance         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gliding in is more energy-efficient for the seal as they use momentum generated from sliding on ice      [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gliding is more stealthy OR quiet, which allows the seal to avoid predators     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ gliding is more controlled, so the seal is at less risk of injury     [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample Answer}}$: \\\n    Gliding in head first’ is advantageous as water has a **high surface tension** due to the high number of hydrogen bonds between water molecules, therefore,  ‘belly-flopping’ may cause **pain** from **breaking the hydrogen bonds** forcefully ($\\checkmark$). Gliding is more **stealthy** so the seal has more chance of catching prey or **escaping predators** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI0836","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI0836\n\nThe black-throated loon (*Gavia arctica*) is a bird that lives near large bodies of water in cold climates.  Which of the following is a correct description of how the properties of water may make winter temperatures more moderate for the black-throated loon?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Water has a relatively high specific heat capacity which buffers the environmental temperature.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ice floats on water so insulates the liquid water beneath the ice.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Water has a low specific heat capacity which warms the surrounding air.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Water is dense which enables the black-throated loon to float.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice A** is correct because water’s ability to absorb and store a significant amount of heat without undergoing large temperature changes provides thermal cushioning.  In summer water absorbs heat energy and then in winter this heat energy is lost to the surrounding air helping to create milder microclimates. This enables the black-throated loon to inhabit areas where temperature might otherwise be too extreme.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because it does not explain how winter air temperatures are made more moderate, only that ice insulates water that is below it which is not where the black-throated loon lives.  **Choice C** is incorrect because water does not have a low specific heat capacity.  **Choice D** is incorrect because, although it is a true statement, it does not explain how winter temperatures are made more moderate."},{"ref":"BI1006","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1006\n\nWhich of the following is a reason why water is retained on Earth and does not escape into space?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Variations in global precipitation and hydrological cycling\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Tidal forces exerted by the Moon on Earth’s oceans\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Atmospheric temperatures too low for significant escape of water vapour into space\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Earth’s gravitational strength preventing the escape of water molecules into space","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nEarth's gravitational field exerts a force strong enough to retain gases and liquids, including water vapour, in the atmosphere and hydrosphere. Without gravity, water molecules would have sufficient energy to escape into space. Gravity is the key reason why Earth can retain an atmosphere and surface water.  **Choice D** is, therefore, correct.\n\nPrecipitation patterns determine where water falls, not whether water remains on the planet.\nThe hydrological cycle redistributes water but does not influence Earth’s ability to retain it.\nRainfall patterns do not prevent atmospheric gases or water vapour from escaping into space.\n**Choice A**  is, therefore, incorrect.\n\nThe Moon’s gravity causes tides, not water retention. Tidal forces move water horizontally (rise and fall of sea levels) but do not affect whether water can escape Earth’s gravitational field. **Choice B** is, therefore, incorrect.\n\nWhile temperature influences the kinetic energy of gas molecules, Earth’s atmospheric temperatures are not the main reason water is retained. Even in warmer regions, most water vapour still lacks enough velocity to escape; gravity is the controlling factor, not temperature.  **Choice C**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1007","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1007\n\nWhat is the most accepted theory for the origin of water on Earth?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Asteroids from space containing ice arrived on Earth billions of years ago\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Respiration from early organisms released water into the atmosphere\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Heat from ancient deep-sea vents catalysed the reaction between hydrogen and oxygen atoms\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Early volcanic eruptions, released steam into the atmosphere","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAsteroids from space, which contained ice, arrived on Earth. This is the most accepted theory for the origin of water on Earth. This theory, often referred to as the \"Late Heavy Bombardment\" theory, suggests that during the early stages of the Solar System's formation, a significant number of asteroids and comets, some of which contained water in the form of ice, bombarded the Earth. Over time, as these icy bodies collided with Earth, they contributed to the accumulation of water on the planet's surface. **Choice A** is correct.\n\n**Choice B**  is incorrect, as water was present on Earth before early organisms were present. **Choice C** is incorrect because free oxygen atoms were absent in the early atmosphere. **Choice D** is incorrect because liquid water would need to be present on Earth before it could be converted into steam."},{"ref":"BI1008","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1008\n\nThe \"Goldilocks zone\" is a critical concept in the search for extraterrestrial life. Which statement best explains why Earth is classified as being within this zone, while Saturn is excluded?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Earth is large enough to keep an atmosphere, whereas Saturn is a gas giant with gravity that is too strong.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Earth reflects light that shows a clear water signature, whereas Saturn does not.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Earth is at a distance where solar radiation allows water to remain liquid, whereas Saturn is too far away, so water is frozen.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Earth has water in all three states (solid, liquid, gas), whereas Saturn only has ice in its rings.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe Goldilocks zone is defined as the region around a star where temperatures allow liquid water to exist on a planet’s surface. Earth receives enough solar radiation to maintain water in liquid form, which is essential for life as we know it. Saturn orbits far outside this temperature range; it receives too little solar energy, so water cannot exist as a liquid and instead remains frozen. **Choice C** is correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because planet size and gravity influence atmospheric retention, but they do not define the Goldilocks zone. Saturn actually has **much stronger** gravity and easily retains a massive atmosphere. Its exclusion from the Goldilocks zone is due to insufficient solar radiation, not its atmospheric or gravitational properties.\n\n **Choice B**  is incorrect because detecting a **water signature** uses transit spectroscopy, but this does not determine whether a planet is in the Goldilocks zone. The zone is defined purely by distance from the star and temperature suitability for liquid water, not the detection of water vapour or ice.\n\n **Choice D**  is incorrect because the presence of multiple states of water on Earth is a **consequence** of being in the Goldilocks zone, not the reason Earth is classified within it. Saturn’s icy rings do not influence its habitability. The key criterion is whether liquid water can exist stably at the surface, which depends on temperature and solar energy, not the number of water states present."},{"ref":"BI1009","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1009\n\nWhich of the following is **not** a reason that scientists look for the presence of water on planets as a sign of extraterrestrial life?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Water is a necessary resource for most living organisms\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Most enzymes catalyse reactions in solutions containing water\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Water molecules can stick to each other\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Water is a universal solvent","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice C**  is correct because the ability of water molecules to stick to each other, known as cohesion, is important for some biological processes, but is not a primary reason scientists search for water as evidence of extraterrestrial life.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because water is required by most known living organisms for metabolic processes and survival.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because enzymes generally function in aqueous environments, where water acts as the medium for biochemical reactions.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because water is a universal solvent due to its polarity, which allows many substances to dissolve and participate in chemical reactions essential to life."},{"ref":"BI1010","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1010\n\nWhich of the following is **not** a real-life example of adhesion?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Surface tension on the surface of a lake\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Dew drops hanging off the tip of a blade of grass\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Water coating the surface of the alveoli\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Water sticking to cellulose in cell walls","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSurface tension on the surface of the lake, describes the property of cohesion, the ability of water molecules to form hydrogen bonds with other water molecules. **Choice A** is correct.\n\nAdhesion is the ability of water to form hydrogen bonds with other polar molecules. **Choices B, C** and **D**  are incorrect as they are all examples of water is sticking to surfaces that are not water molecules, demonstrating adhesion."},{"ref":"BI1011","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1011\n\nWhich is the correctly annotated diagram of a molecule of water?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Water/images/BI1011iii.png\" alt=\"BI1011iii\">\n\n*Note: Starting May 2028, this question may be assessed differently due to changes to the Biology guide, including the use of a data booklet.*","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA water molecule consists of 2 hydrogens and one oxygen connected by single covalent bonds. Water molecules are polar, with a slight negative charge on the oxygen (δ-) and a slight positive charge on the hydrogens (δ+) due to unequal sharing of electrons. **Choice B**  is correct.\n\n**Choice A**  is incorrect as it consists of one hydrogen bonded to two oxygens. **Choice C**  is incorrect, as water molecules are not linear due to the sharing of electrons. **Choice D**  is incorrect as oxygen is not slightly positive (δ+) and hydrogen is not slightly negative (δ-)."},{"ref":"BI1012","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1012\n\nWhich term to describe a property of water is matched to its correct definition?\n\n| | Term | Definition |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Exerts buoyant forces | Much energy is required to turn water into a vapour |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | High specific heat capacity | Much energy is required to raise the temperature of 1kg water by 1 °C |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | High boiling point | Turns from ice into steam at 100 °C |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Low viscosity | Easily allows objects to float on the surface |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWater has a high specific heat capacity, meaning it can absorb or lose a lot of heat with only a small temperature change. This property helps maintain stable temperatures in organisms and aquatic environments. **Choice B** is correct.\n\nWater exerts buoyant forces on objects, which means that it allows them to float easily on its surface. Objects that are less dense than the water they displace will experience an upward force greater than their weight, causing them to float.  **Choice A** is incorrect.\n\nA high boiling point means that liquid water turns into steam at 100°C; **Choice C** is incorrect.\n\nLow viscosity means that water flows easily; **Choice D**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1013","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1013\n\nThe image shows a water strider (*Aquarius paludum*) standing on the surface of a pond. Which property of water allows the water strider to stand on the surface without sinking?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Water/images/BI1013.png\" alt=\"BI1013\">\n[Source adapted from: Zinogre. (2021, July 11). *File: Aquarius paludum 142592609.jpg*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved July, 04 2023, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: Aquarius_paludum_142592609.jpg. Copyright free]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Adhesion\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cohesion\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ High specific heat capacity\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Buoyancy","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWater molecules are polar and form hydrogen bonds with each other. This cohesion creates surface tension, which acts like a thin, elastic film that supports the water strider's low body weight. **Choice B** is, therefore, correct.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because adhesion is when water forms hydrogen bonds with other polar substances. The water strider's legs are covered in water-repellent hairs, so water does not stick to them.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because high specific heat capacity helps keep water temperature stable, but it has no part in holding up the water strider.\n\n**Choice D** is incorrect because buoyancy is the upward force a fluid exerts on an object placed in it. The water strider stands on the surface rather than floating in the water, so it is held up by surface tension, not buoyancy."},{"ref":"BI1014","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1014\n\nWhich of the following statement(s) about the role of water as a solvent is correct?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Salt can dissolve in water because it is hydrophilic|\n    | II. | Lipids cannot dissolve in water because they are non-polar |\n    | III. | Cellulose can dissolve in water, as it is able to form hydrogen bonds |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSalt, an ionic compound, dissolves readily in water because water is a polar solvent and can interact with the charged ions in salt through electrostatic attractions. Substances that have an affinity for water and can form interactions with water molecules are referred to as hydrophilic (**Statement I**). Lipids are non-polar molecules, and water is a polar solvent. Polar and non-polar substances do not readily mix because water molecules are attracted to each other through hydrogen bonding and other dipole-dipole interactions. Lipids are hydrophobic, meaning they repel water and do not dissolve well in it (**Statement II**). **Choice C** is, therefore, correct.\n\nAlthough cellulose can form hydrogen bonds, it is insoluble (**Statement III**) as it is too large to dissolve in water; **Choices B**  and  **D**  are therefore incorrect as they include this statement. **Choice A** is incorrect as it does not include **statement II**."},{"ref":"BI1015","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1015\n\nWhich of the following statements explains how water has facilitated the evolution of life on Earth?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Water acts as a catalyst for chemical reactions\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Water acts as a solvent allowing chemical reactions to occur faster within it\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Water acts in hydrolysis reactions allowing for the synthesis of large biological molecules\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Water has a high latent heat of vaporisation so is able to convert between states easily","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWater's unique ability to act as a solvent is crucial for life's evolution. Many biochemical reactions that are fundamental to life occur within aqueous environments. Water's solvent properties facilitate the dissolution and interaction of molecules, enabling chemical reactions to occur more rapidly and efficiently. **Choice B** is, therefore, correct.\n\nWater acts as a reaction medium for many chemical reactions, but does not catalyse the chemical reactions that facilitate evolution, **Choice A** is incorrect. **Choice C**  is incorrect as hydrolysis reactions break down larger molecules into smaller ones. **Choice D**  is incorrect as water’s high latent heat of vaporisation means that it requires a lot of energy to convert liquid water into a gas."},{"ref":"BI1018","marks":"9","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI1018\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nThe diagram shows a solar system very similar to ours, where five planets orbit a central star.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Water/images/BI1018.png\" alt=\"BI1018\">\n\n1. Define the term “Goldilocks zone”. [1 mark]\n\n2.\n 1. Label with the letter R the planet that is least likely to have extraterrestrial life present. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Explain reasons for your answer to part (i). [3 marks]\n\n3.\n 1. Some of the planets in this solar system have water present on them. Suggest where this water might have come from. [1 mark]\n\n 2. Explain why the planets referred to in part (i) have retained water, whereas others in this solar system have not.[2 marks]\n\n4. Suggest one other biological molecule, other than water, that may be a sign of life on a planet. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{ -> }$ the zone around a star where temperatures allow for water to exist in a liquid state on the surface of planets [1 mark]\n[1 mark]\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The Goldilocks zone is the zone **around a star** where **temperatures** allow **water** to exist in a **liquid** state on the surface of planets ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.\n 1.\n $\\ce{ -> }$ line labelled with letter R, pointing to the furthest planet from the star **OR** line labelled with letter R, pointing to the closest planet to the star [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Water/images/BI1018ansa.png\" alt=\"BI1018ansa\">\n\n2.\n 2. Any **three** of the following:\n\n **If R is the planet furthest from the star:** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature is too cold [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chemical reactions may be slower **OR** not happen at all due to the lack of water as a solvent **OR** enzymes are unable to catalyse chemical reactions [1 mark]\n\n **If R is the planet closest to the star:** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperature is too hot [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ chemical reactions may not happen at all due to the lack of water as a solvent, **OR** enzymes are denatured **OR** unable to catalyse chemical reactions [1 mark]\n\n **Applies to either answer:** \\\n $\\ce{ -> }$ unlikely to have liquid water on the surface of the planet **OR** is outside the Goldilocks zone **AND** water is a necessary resource for the survival of all known organisms [1 mark]\n [3 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n The planet furthest from the star is the least likely to have extraterrestrial life, as it is furthest from the heat and light source, so it may be **too cold** for organisms to survive ($\\checkmark$). It is outside the Goldilocks zone, which means that it is **unlikely to have liquid water** and water is a **necessary resource** for the survival of all known organisms ($\\checkmark$). The lack of liquid water also means that any **chemical reactions** necessary for life may be slower or not happen at all due to the **lack of a solvent** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3.\n 1.\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ asteroids **OR** comets [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Water on some of the planets may have come from **asteroids** ($\\checkmark$) which contained ice and collided with the planet.\n\n3.\n 2. **BOTH** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ gravity stops water from escaping [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ temperatures low enough to condense water [1 mark]\n [2 marks]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n Some planets in the solar system retain water because the force of **gravity** on those planets prevents water from escaping those planets ($\\checkmark$), and the distance from the star keeps the **temperatures** on those planets **low** enough to allow water to **condense** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n4. Any **one** of the following:\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ nucleic acids [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ proteins **OR** amino acids [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ carbohydrates [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ lipids **OR** fats [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ methane - indicates methanogens [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen- indicates photosynthetic organisms [1 mark]\n\n $\\ce{ -> }$ ammonia - indicates biological process, including the nitrogen cycle [1 mark]\n [1 mark]\n\n $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n One other biological molecule that may suggest the presence of life on a planet is **carbohydrates** ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1046","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1046\n\nThe micrograph below shows the longitudinal cross-section of a stem from a plant in the *Pinus* genus.  Xylem vessels are clearly visible.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Water/images/BI1046.png\" alt=\"BI1046\">\nMagnification: 100X\n\n[Source: From Berkshire Community College Bioscience Image Library. (2014). *Radial section: Pinus stem magnification: 100x iron-alum hematoxylin and safranin stain*. Wikimedia Commons. Retrieved April 29, 2022, from https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gymnosperm_Stem_Soft_Wood_in_Pinus_(36087426450).jpg.\n Copyright by CC BY CC0 1.0\n]\n\nThe xylem is composed of hollow, tube-like tissues that transport water through capillary action. What property of water explains capillary action?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Melting point\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Adhesive properties\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Specific heat capacity\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Buoyant forces","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAdhesive properties (**Choice B**) refer to water’s ability to stick to charged surfaces or polar molecules. This, together with cohesion, allows water to move against gravity up the stem through capillary action.\n\nWater's melting point (**Choice A**) is the temperature at which it changes its state from solid to liquid.\n\nThe specific heat capacity (**Choice C**) of water pertains to the amount of heat energy needed to raise the temperature of a unit mass (usually one gram) of water by 1 °C.\n\nThe buoyant force of water (**Choice D**) is the upward force on an object submerged or partially immersed in water, equal to the weight of the displaced water."},{"ref":"BI1224","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1224\n\nThe image shows the vascular tissue in the leaves of a corn plant (*Zea mays*). What property of water is not demonstrated in the vascular tissue of the plant?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Water/images/BI1224.png\" alt=\"BI1224\">\n\n[Source: Adapted from Berkshire Community College  Bioscience Image Library. (2017, October 3). *Angiosperm morphology: The closed and collateral vascular bundle in Zea*. Flickr. Retrieved September 11, 2023, from https://www.flickr.com/photos/146824358@N03/37215761790. Copyright free]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Cohesion\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Adhesion\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Universal solvent\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ High thermal conductivity","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe vascular tissue of a plant contains the xylem, which transports water from the roots to the leaves of the plant and the phloem, which transports soluble organic compounds that are made during photosynthesis. Although water has a high thermal conductivity, which means that it can transfer heat easily, this is not demonstrated by the movement of water in the xylem; **Choice D**  is correct.\n\nCohesion is the ability of water molecules to stick to each other via their hydrogen bonds, this allows a continuous column of water to be transferred up the xylem; **Choice A**  is incorrect. Adhesion is the ability of water to stick to surfaces, this allows water to stick to the inner surface of the xylem, which helps to maintain the integrity of the continuous water column; **Choice B**  is incorrect. Water is a universal solvent, which means that many nutrients can be dissolved in it and be transported via the xylem and phloem to where they are needed in the plant; **Choice C**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI1537","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1537\n\nWhich of the following thermal properties of water is **incorrectly** matched?\n\n| | Property | Water |\n\n| --- | :-- | :-: |\n\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Melting Point | 0 $\\degree$C |\n\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Specific Heat Capacity | 2.2 J $\\text{g}^{-1}$ $\\degree \\text{C}^{-1}$ |\n\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Latent Heat of Vaporisation | 2260 J $\\text{g}^{-1}$ |\n\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Boiling Point | 100 $\\degree$C |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation:}}$\\\n\n**Choice B** is incorrectly matched. The specific heat capacity of water is 4.2 J $\\text{g}^{-1} \\degree \\text{C}^{-1}$, not 2.2  J $\\text{g}^{-1} \\degree \\text{C}^{-1}$.\n\n**Choice A**, **C**, and **D** are correctly matched."},{"ref":"BI1758","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"ERQ","body":"# BI1758\n\n**Maximum mark: 16**\n\n1.  Outline how the properties of water contributed to the origin of life on Earth. [5 marks]\n\n2.  Draw **two** water molecules, labelling the partial charges and the bond between them.$^*$  [3 marks]\n\n3.  Explain how the solubility of different types of substances in water affects their transport in the blood, giving named examples. [8 marks]\n\n$^*$ *Note: Starting May 2028, this question may be assessed differently due to changes to the Biology guide, including the use of a data booklet.*","markScheme":"1.  any **five** of the following:\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ polarity of water molecules enables formation of hydrogen bonds    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen bonding assisted formation of stable molecules **OR** assembly of complex structures **OR** lipid bilayer **OR** other suitable named structures    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ solvent properties facilitated dissolution **OR** interaction of organic molecules    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ enabled formation of more complex structures **OR** protocells **OR** precursors to first living cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ high specific heat capacity prevented rapid temperature changes, enabling chemical reactions to occur    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ provided medium for enzymes **OR** chemical reactions **OR** synthesis of biological molecules        [1 mark]\n\n    [5 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n\n    Water’s **polarity** enables the formation of **hydrogen bonds** ($\\checkmark$), which are crucial for the **stability** and **assembly** of **complex structures** such as **DNA, proteins** and **lipid bilayers** ($\\checkmark$). As a **universal solvent**, water facilitated the **dissolution** and **interaction** of **organic molecules** ($\\checkmark$), leading to the formation of **protocells** ($\\checkmark$). Additionally, water’s **high specific heat capacity** provided a **stable environment, preventing** rapid **temperature changes** and allowing essential **enzymatic** controlled **reactions** to occur ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2.  any **three** of the following:\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ two water molecules each made up of one oxygen atom attached to two hydrogen atoms [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ slight negative charge on oxygen atom and slight positive charge on both hydrogen atoms on at least one of the water molecules    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrogen bond drawn as dashed lines and labelled    [1 mark]\n\n    [3 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Water/images/BI1758b.png\" alt=\"BI1758b\">\n\n3.  any **eight** of the following:\\\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transport of molecules in the blood depends on their solubility in water    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ transport occurs in blood plasma and red blood cells    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrophilic **OR** water-soluble substances travel freely in solution    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ ionic compounds **OR** ions transported in plasma    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ NaCl dissociates into Na+ and Cl- ions and carried in plasma **OR** NaCl is transported as free ions in plasma    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ glucose is polar, so dissolves in water and travels in plasma    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ solubility of amino acids determined by side chain        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ amino acids are transported in an ionised state    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ hydrophobic **OR** water-insoluble substances can not travel in solution    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ oxygen is non-polar **OR** has limited solubility in plasma    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ majority of oxygen binds to haemoglobin **OR** is transported by red blood cells [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ cholesterol **OR** fats **OR** lipids **OR** triglycerides are non-polar **OR** hydrophobic and bind to proteins **OR** form lipoproteins to become soluble        [1 mark]\n\n    [8 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$:\\\n\n    The **transport** of molecules in the blood is **determined by** their **solubility** in water ($\\checkmark$). **Hydrophilic** substances, such as **glucose** ($\\checkmark$) and **ionised amino acids**  ($\\checkmark$), **dissolve** easily **in plasma** and **travel freely** in **solution** ($\\checkmark$). **Ionic compounds** like **sodium chloride dissociate** and are carried as **free ions**, such as **Na$^+$ and Cl$^-$** ($\\checkmark$), in the **plasma** ($\\checkmark$). In contrast, **non-polar substances** like **oxygen** and **lipids** are **not soluble** in water. Only a **small proportion** of **oxygen** is **dissolved** in the **plasma**; it is **primarily transported** by binding to **haemoglobin**($\\checkmark$)  in **red blood cells** ($\\checkmark$). **Lipids**, including **cholesterol** and **triglycerides**, **bind to proteins** to form **lipoproteins**, which makes them **soluble** and allows their transport in the bloodstream ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI1810","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1810\n\nWhich of the following are a reason why water's properties would have influenced early cellular evolution in aquatic environments?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | High surface tension allowing formation of proto-cell membranes. |\n    | II. | Polar solvent properties enabling interaction of biomolecules. |\n    | III. | High density causing rapid sedimentation of organic material. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWater's ability to dissolve biomolecules and, therefore, enabling them to interact (**Statement II**) would have been crucial to early cellular evolution. This property allows for the formation of a \"primordial soup,\" where simple organic molecules could interact, form more complex compounds, and eventually lead to the development of proto-cells. **Choice B** is the correct answer to this question.\n\nWhile surface tension is a property of water, it does not directly contribute to the formation of proto-cell membranes (**Statement I**) in the context of early cellular evolution. Proto-cell membranes are primarily formed by the self-assembly of amphipathic molecules like phospholipids, which align themselves in a bilayer in water due to their hydrophilic and hydrophobic regions.\n\nOrganic molecules needed to remain in suspension for interactions, rather than settling quickly (**Statement III**). Water's density is well-suited for suspending organic particles, allowing them to remain available for biochemical reactions, while enabling gradual sedimentation over time.\n\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are incorrect as they include one or more untrue statements."},{"ref":"BI1811","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI1811\n\nWhy is water considered a \"universal solvent,\" and why is this important for the origin of life?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | --: | :-- |\n    | I. | $\\hspace{1em}$ It dissolves a wider variety of substances than any other liquid. |\n    | II. | $\\hspace{1em}$ It can dissolve both polar and non-polar molecules. |\n    | III. | $\\hspace{1em}$ It enables complex biochemical reactions by facilitating the interaction of reactants. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWater is known as the \"universal solvent\" because it can dissolve a wider range of substances, than any other liquid (**Statement I**). This property is due to water's polarity and ability to form hydrogen bonds with various molecules. Water’s ability to dissolve various substances means that it can bring reactants together in a solution, enabling the complex biochemical reactions that are necessary for life (**Statement III**). **Choice C** is correct.\n\nWater is a polar solvent, which means it is very effective at dissolving other polar substances and ionic compounds. However, water is not good at dissolving non-polar molecules because there is no attraction between water molecules and non-polar molecules. **Statement II** is therefore not true.\n\n**Choices A, B** and **D** are incorrect as they all include **Statement II**."},{"ref":"BI2318","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2318\n\nWhich substance is insoluble in water and suitable for energy storage in animals?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Starch\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Glucose\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Ribose\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Glycogen","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Choice D** is the correct answer because glycogen is the primary carbohydrate used for short- to medium-term energy storage in animals. It is insoluble in water, so it does not affect the water potential of the cytoplasm, making it ideal for storage.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because starch is the carbohydrate storage molecule in plants, not animals. While starch is insoluble and suitable for energy storage in plants, it is not used by animals.\n\n**Choice B** is incorrect because glucose is hydrophilic, soluble in water, and used for immediate energy, not long-term storage.\n\n**Choice C** is incorrect because ribose is hydrophilic and is not used for energy storage. Instead, it is a component of RNA and nucleotides like ATP."},{"ref":"BI2778","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2778\n\nWhich statement supports the theory that meteorites are a source of water on Earth?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Meteorites contain ice with the same water isotopes as those found in Earth’s oceans.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Meteorites contain ice and simple polypeptides that promote cohesion between water molecules.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Meteorites contain liquid water that has a similar salinity to Earth’s oceans.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Meteorites contain ice and compounds that can be dehydrated to release water.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe oldest meteorites in our solar system have been found to contain ice and hydrated minerals. Hydrated minerals contain water in their crystal structures. Hydrated minerals originating from meteorites can become dehydrated through heating, releasing water vapour into the surroundings and leaving behind anhydrous minerals; **Choice D** is correct.\n\nWater molecules contain hydrogen and oxygen atoms, each of which can exist as different isotopes. In planetary science, scientists compare the ratios of these isotopes (such as the deuterium-to-hydrogen (D/H) ratio or the $\\ce{^{18}O/^{16}O}$ ratio) to trace the origin of water.\n\n**Choice A** is incorrect because it implies that meteorites contain ice with exactly the same isotopes as those in Earth’s oceans. In reality, all water contains the same possible isotopes, but the key evidence lies in the relative proportions of these isotopes. Some meteorites contain water with a similar isotopic ratio to Earth's oceans, not an identical isotopic composition. Therefore, the claim is misleading.\n\nCohesion is a process that does not require anything but water molecules, it is due to the formation of hydrogen bonds between water molecules. Some evidence of amino acids has been found in meteorites, but no evidence of polypeptides. **Choice B** is incorrect.\n\nMeteorites travel through the vacuum of space, where temperatures are far too low to sustain liquid water. Also, no compelling evidence exists that meteorite-bound water has similar salinity to Earth's oceans.; **Choice C** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI2779","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2779\n\nWhich factor determined primarily whether water from asteroids remained on Earth rather than escaping into space?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The presence of greenhouse gases which insulates Earth and prevents the loss of water vapour to space.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The presence of autotrophic prokaryotes which photolysed water from the atmosphere in photosynthesis.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The strength of Earth’s gravitational field which prevents water from diffusing into space.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The favourable range of temperatures on Earth allowing water to exist in solid, liquid and gaseous states.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nEarth’s gravity pulls all matter, including water, toward the centre of the Earth, preventing water from escaping into space. **Choice C** is correct.\n\nGreenhouse gases such as carbon dioxide insulate the Earth, however, greenhouse gases trap heat, not water; **Choice A** is incorrect. \\\nAutotrophic prokaryotes appeared on Earth after the Earth had already retained a large amount of water; **Choice B** is incorrect. \\\nAlthough the temperature on Earth is favourable for the condensation and freezing of water, temperature alone is not sufficient to keep water on Earth. Without gravitational force, water would not remain on Earth; **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI2780","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2780\n\nWhich property describes water’s ability to associate with a wide range of other compounds, and how did this property help retain water on early Earth before the emergence of life?\n\n| | Property of water | How did this property help retain water on Earth? |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | High heat capacity | Minimised temperature fluctuations that would evaporate water |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Universal solvent | Allowed a wide range of condensation reactions to occur, so water is trapped in biological polymers |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Polarity | Allowed water to interact with minerals so that it is stored in hydrated rocks |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Low density | Prevented water from sinking through the surface of the Earth into the mantle |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nWater is a polar molecule, meaning it has partial positive and negative charges. This polarity allows water to form chemical bonds with rock-forming minerals, leading to hydrated compounds such as gypsum ($\\ce{CaSO_4.H_2O}$) and hydrated feldspars. These minerals incorporate water into their crystal structures (known as water of crystallisation), helping retain water in the Earth's crust before the appearance of life; **Choice C** is correct.\n\nHigh heat capacity refers to water’s ability to store heat without large temperature changes, which helps to regulate Earth’s climate, but doesn’t explain why water is retained on Earth; **Choice A** is incorrect. \\\nWater is described as a universal solvent as it can dissolve a wide range of substances; however, while water is required for condensation reactions, the process of condensation does not trap water in a manner that would keep it on Earth. Also, biological polymers did not exist until after life emerged. **Choice B** is incorrect. \\\nIf water had entered the mantle, the heat there would cause it to evaporate instantly, and therefore, water would not be retained on Earth. Water's ability to sink into the mantle would be due to rock porosity and cracks, not its density; **Choice D**  is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI2781","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2781\n\nThe table provides information on planets within a distant galaxy that are a similar size to Earth. Which planet is most likely to be able to support life as we know it on Earth?\n\n| | Gases in the atmosphere | Surface temperature / $\\degree$C | Is there evidence of water being present? |\n| --- | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Oxygen and carbon dioxide | - 45 | Yes |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Nitrogen and sulfur dioxide | 15 | Yes |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Oxygen and hydrogen |100 | Yes |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | Helium and hydrogen | 450 | No |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe gases present in a planet’s atmosphere can influence whether life is supported, as different organisms may evolve to interact with different gases. For example, extremophiles on Earth can survive in low-oxygen (hypoxic) environments and derive energy from compounds such as hydrogen sulfide or hydrogen gas. However, while life can exist in a range of atmospheric conditions, the most critical factor for supporting life as we know it is the presence of liquid water at a suitable temperature. Atmospheric composition plays a role, but it is secondary to temperature and water availability.\n\nPlanet B has a surface temperature of 15 $\\degree$C, which is well within the range where water remains in the liquid state, and evidence of water is present; **Choice B** is correct.\n\nAlthough there is water present on Planet A, the surface temperature is -45$\\degree$C, which means that the water would be frozen; **Choice A** is incorrect.\n\nAlthough there is water present on Planet C, the surface temperature is 100$\\degree$C, which is the boiling point of water at 1 atm pressure, therefore water would be in a gaseous form. **Choice C** is incorrect.\n\nThere is no evidence of water being present on Planet D, and its surface temperature is 450 $\\degree$C, which is far too high for any known form of life to survive. **Choice D** is incorrect."},{"ref":"BI2782","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2782\n\nWhich statement(s) explain why evidence of liquid water is essential for identifying the possible existence of life on other planets, as found on Earth?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Water is the only molecule that can exist as a solid, liquid and gas in the ‘Goldilocks’ zone (approximately -50$\\degree$C to 80$\\degree$C). |\n    | II. | Liquid water acts as a universal solvent, so it facilitates the biochemical reactions necessary for life. |\n    | III. | Water is necessary for the polymerisation reaction in polynucleotides and polypeptides. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe ‘Goldilocks zone’ is the area around a star where the temperature is neither too hot nor too cold for liquid water to exist on the surface of planets.\n\nMany molecules exist as liquids in the temperature range of Earth’s Goldilocks zone. For example, ethanol, benzene, sulfur trioxide and bromine can exist in solid, liquid and gaseous states within the temperature range of approximately -50$\\degree$C to 80$\\degree$C found on Earth (the Goldilocks zone). Therefore, water is not the only molecule that can exist in all three states in the Goldilocks zone. **Statement I** is false. \\\nThe polar nature of water and its ability to form hydrogen bonds mean that it can dissolve a range of molecules and enable enzymatic reactions. As such, water forms the basis of all life as found on Earth. **Statement II** is true. \\\nNucleotides form polymers such as DNA or RNA, and amino acids form polypeptides using condensation reactions. However, water is not required for condensation reactions, but rather, is a product of such reactions. By contrast, water is needed for hydrolysis reactions, which break down polymers into monomers. **Statement III** is false.\n\n**Choice B** is correct because it only includes the true **Statement II**.\\\n**Choices A, C** and **D** are incorrect as they all include the false **Statements I** and **III**."},{"ref":"BI2783","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# BI2783\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe pie chart shows the chemical composition of the Murchison meteorite, which fell in Australia in 1969. It belongs to a class of meteorites called carbonaceous chondrites, some of the solar system's oldest meteorites.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Water/images/BI2783.png\" alt=\"BI2783\">\n[Source: Adapted from Braukmuller, N., Wombacher, F., Hezel, D. C., Escoube, R., & Munker, C. (2018, July 24). The chemical composition of carbonaceous chondrites: Implications for volatile element depletion, complementarity and alteration. ScienceDirect. Retrieved March 24, 2025 from https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0016703718304095. Copyright under CC BY 4.0]\n\n1. Explain why analysis of carbonaceous chondrites has led to the hypothesis that meteorites are most likely to be the source of water on Earth.            [2 marks]\n\n2. Explain **two** properties of water that make it an essential molecule for life.        [2 marks]\n\n3. Other than water, deduce with a reason **one other substance** found on the Murchison meteorite that is essential for life.                        [1 mark]\n\nThe ‘Goldilocks’ zone is the area around a star, where the conditions are optimal for the presence of liquid water.\n\n4. Other than liquid water, state **two** properties that a planet in the Goldilocks zone may need for life to evolve.                                    [2 marks]\n\n5. Carbonaceous chondrites have also collided with planets in the solar system that are outside the ‘Goldilocks zone’. Suggest why life may not have appeared on those planets.                                        [2 marks]\n\n6. If a planet within the ‘Goldilocks zone’ **does not** have an atmosphere, suggest how this would affect the retention of liquid water.                    [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contain water with a similar composition to Earth’s oceans **OR** with a similar ratio of hydrogen isotopes to ocean water            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contain liquid water         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ contain crystallised **OR** hydrated minerals that contain water    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ arrived at a time in the Earth’s formation that allowed the water to enter **OR** stay in a liquid state        [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Carbonaceous chondrites are thought to be the most likely source of water on Earth because they contain water with a **similar ratio of hydrogen isotopes** to **ocean** water ($\\checkmark$) and they have been found to contain **liquid water** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n2. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water is a universal solvent **OR**  dissolves a wide range of substances **WHICH** facilitates cellular metabolic reactions **OR** transport of dissolved substances        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water is cohesive **OR** water molecules can stick to each other due to hydrogen bonding **WHICH** allows for droplet formation **OR** capillary action **OR** surface tension in plants    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water is adhesive **OR** water molecules stick to other surfaces due to hydrogen bonding **WHICH** allows for processes such as the transpiration stream in plants            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water has a high specific heat capacity **OR** it resists sudden changes in temperature **WHICH** helps organisms maintain homeostasis **OR** stabilises temperatures in large bodies of water [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ water has a high heat of vaporisation **OR** it requires a large amount of energy to convert liquid water into gas **WHICH**  allows organisms to regulate body temperature through sweating or transpiration        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ solid water (ice) is less dense than liquid water therefore, ice can float **WHICH** means that aquatic ecosystems are insulated from large fluctuations in temperature            [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Water is a **universal solvent** which facilitates a range of **cellular metabolic reactions** ($\\checkmark$), and it is **cohesive**, which allows for processes such as **water droplet formation** or the formation of **large bodies** of water ($\\checkmark$).\n\n3. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ amino acids **BECAUSE** they are used to build proteins which can be used to make enzymes, build tissues or regulate processes            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ purines **OR** pyrimidines **BECAUSE** they can be used to build RNA **OR** DNA nucleotides        [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    **Amino acids**, because they are the **building blocks of all proteins** ($\\checkmark$), such as enzymes.\n\n4. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ presence of organic molecules **OR** presence of key chemical elements **OR** three named key elements from carbon, oxygen, hydrogen, nitrogen, phosphorus, sulfur    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a large enough size **OR** sufficient planetary massto generate enough gravitational pull to retain an atmosphere                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a solid surface **OR** lithosphere **OR** crust                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a source of energy **OR** a central star **OR** a nearby Sun **OR** solar energy **OR** lightning **OR** geothermal **OR** hydrothermal activity to drive metabolic **OR** prebiotic chemical reactions    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ a magnetic field to shield the surface from solar wind **OR** cosmic radiation **OR** preserve DNA integrity **OR** to protect life from harmful radiation    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ stable climate  orbital stability to allow sufficient time for chemical evolution and life to emerge            [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ presence of an atmosphere to support temperature regulation, pressure for liquid water, **OR**  protection from radiation    [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Two factors that a planet in the ‘Goldilocks zone’ may need for life to evolve are  the **presence of organic molecules** ($\\checkmark$) and  a **solid surface** ($\\checkmark$) that allows the accumulation and organisation of complex compounds.\n\n5. any **two** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the planet may be too small so may not have sufficient gravity to sustain an atmosphere **OR** retain liquid water                     [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the planet may be too large resulting in excessive gravity **OR** a dense atmosphere which results in surface temperatures which are too high for chemical **OR** metabolic reactions         [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the planet may be too cold **OR** too far from the star **OR** the Sun for photosynthesis **OR** liquid water to exist        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the planet may be too warm **OR** too close to the star **OR** the Sun for  liquid water to exist **OR** chemical **OR** metabolic reactions to take place **OR** for complex molecules to be stable        [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ The planet’s atmosphere may contain toxic gases that prevent the development of stable life forms             [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the planet may not have a strong enough magnetic field to protect the planet **OR** organic molecules **OR** surface conditions from harmful radiation                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ the planet may not have a solid surface **OR** be mainly made up of gas, so there are no suitable surfaces **OR** habitats for organisms            [1 mark]\n\n    [2 marks]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    Although carbonaceous chondrites may have delivered water and organic compounds, life may not have evolved if the planet was **too small**  to sustain an **atmosphere** due to **low gravity** ($\\checkmark$). Also, the planet may be **too warm** or **close to the Sun** for **liquid water to be present** ($\\checkmark$).\n\n6. any **one** of the following:\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there would be no retention of liquid water **BECAUSE** water would evaporate quickly **OR** sublime                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there would be no water retention **OR** liquid water **BECAUSE** UV **OR** ultraviolet radiation could break down water molecules                [1 mark]\n\n    $\\ce{ -> }$ there would be no retention of liquid water BECAUSE water would quickly freeze due to heat not being trapped by the atmosphere            [1 mark]\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Sample answer}}$: \\\n    There would be **no water retention** because water would **evaporate quickly** due to the lack of atmosphere ($\\checkmark$)."},{"ref":"BI2865","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1","type":"MCQ","body":"# BI2865\n\nThe diagram below shows a water column inside a xylem vessel:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Biology%20HL/Unity%20%26%20Diversity/Water/images/BI2865%20A1.1%20core%20MCQ.jpeg.JPEG\" alt=\"BI2865 A1.1 core MCQ.jpeg\">\n[Sources: Adapted from Chan, T (26 May 2025), *File: Capillary Action in Plants [image]*. Retrieved 5 June 2025 from https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9c/Capillary_Action_in_Plants.jpg. CC BY-SA 4.0]\n\nWhich of the following best explains how the property illustrated contributes to plant survival?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n    | | |\n    | :-- | :-- |\n    | I. | Cohesion between molecules allows tension from transpiration to be transmitted up the xylem. |\n    | II. | Cohesion enables    water to evaporate more easily. |\n    | III. | Adhesion prevents the water column from breaking. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n**Statement I** is true because cohesion refers to the attraction between water molecules due to hydrogen bonding. This cohesive property allows the water column to behave as a continuous stream, enabling tension created by transpiration at the leaves to be transmitted all the way down the xylem. This is central to the cohesion-tension theory of water transport.\\\n**Statement II** is false because cohesion actually resists evaporation. The hydrogen bonds between water molecules make it harder for individual molecules to escape into the air. Evaporation is more closely influenced by temperature and humidity, not directly by cohesion.\\\n**Statement III** is true because adhesion is the attraction between water molecules and the walls of the xylem vessel. This helps to counteract gravity and supports the column, reducing the chance of it breaking, especially under tension during transpiration. So this contributes to the integrity of the water column.\n\n**Choice C** is therefore the correct answer because it only includes the two true **Statements I** and **III**.\\\n**Choice A** is incorrect as it is missing the true **Statement III**.\\\n**Choices B** and **D** are incorrect as they include the false **Statement II**."}]}]}]};
